Parts of Alconbury Weston (including Hamerton Road, North Road and High Street) experienced internal residential flooding, outbuilding and garden flooding, and prolonged displacement of residents (periods >6 months) due to river–driven and associated sewer flooding. [TAB] (p13)
Residential areas experienced flooding during December 2020; residents were identified as 'impacted by flooding' and visited by the LLFA. Flooding also caused erosion of the brook bank beneath the A1 flyover on High Street requiring discussion of stabilisation works. [PAG] (p16)
Alconbury Brook's limited conveyance caused flooding in Alconbury and Alconbury Weston during the December 2020 event. Partners have undertaken and plan further actions (including hydraulic modelling) and the Environment Agency and the local Flood Group are working to improve conveyance; the LLFA is supporting residents and community flood action measures are being delivered in 2021–22. (p21)","36 properties; 32 residential properties flooded internally; 4 properties with damage to outbuildings/garages/gardens [PAG] (p10)
95 properties; 59 properties; 36 properties; 46 residential properties; 32 residential properties; 13 properties; 4 properties [IMG] (p11)
17 residential properties (Hamerton Road); 7 residential properties (North Road); 8 residential properties (High Street); displacement periods >6 months [TAB] (p13)",GB-CAM_12_2020-12-01.html,GB-CAM_12_2020-12-01,Alconbury flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Alconbury and Alconbury Weston,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Alconbury-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Version-1.1.pdf
GB-NNH,29,2020-08-16,52.5553439,-0.4260397,"<div id=""html_e3a254c201843de9aeb054da32d60da5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road, Nassington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of at least one building on Station Road during the event; repeated internal flooding of a building on more than one occasion in the last five years. Surface water drainage systems were overwhelmed, with runoff flowing to local low points around properties. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of an occupied building occurred on 16 August 2020; the same building has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. The affected property reported the flood via the Flood Toolkit. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater entered properties on Station Road, recorded as 'flooding through garage'. [TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed along the highway from south to north; drainage (road gullies/surface water sewer) was exceeded or ineffective, causing water to pool at the low point at the road edge and overtop the dropped kerb and flow onto the adjacent property and into an integral garage. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Photographs taken as part of the flood investigation show a residential property on Station Road with a roadside gully adjacent and a footpath that falls towards the garage door; during the 16 August 2020 flood incident surface water flowed along the carriageway toward the garage entrance, with the gully and the footpath gradient directing runoff toward the property. A parked vehicle is shown partially over the gully, which could have affected drainage access. [IMG] (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property reported via Flood Toolkit (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Station Road, Nassington",England,"Internal flooding of at least one building on Station Road during the event; repeated internal flooding of a building on more than one occasion in the last five years. Surface water drainage systems were overwhelmed, with runoff flowing to local low points around properties. (p5)
Internal flooding of an occupied building occurred on 16 August 2020; the same building has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. The affected property reported the flood via the Flood Toolkit. (p7)
Floodwater entered properties on Station Road, recorded as 'flooding through garage'. [TAB] (p10)
Surface water runoff flowed along the highway from south to north; drainage (road gullies/surface water sewer) was exceeded or ineffective, causing water to pool at the low point at the road edge and overtop the dropped kerb and flow onto the adjacent property and into an integral garage. (p16)
Photographs taken as part of the flood investigation show a residential property on Station Road with a roadside gully adjacent and a footpath that falls towards the garage door; during the 16 August 2020 flood incident surface water flowed along the carriageway toward the garage entrance, with the gully and the footpath gradient directing runoff toward the property. A parked vehicle is shown partially over the gully, which could have affected drainage access. [IMG] (p32)",1 property reported via Flood Toolkit (p7),GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16,Nassington flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Nassington, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Station Road, Nassington.pdf"
GB-NNH,29,2020-08-16,52.5528975,-0.4303509,"<div id=""html_9f1715ed55d24b726d42765535f4246f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Street, Nassington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the direction of Church Lane (sic) overwhelmed a road gully, forming a large puddle that overtopped a dropped kerb, flowed across the footpath and entered an integral garage, contributing to internal flooding of a property. The footpath slope directed overtopping water toward the property frontage. (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Church Street, Nassington",England,"Surface water runoff from the direction of Church Lane (sic) overwhelmed a road gully, forming a large puddle that overtopped a dropped kerb, flowed across the footpath and entered an integral garage, contributing to internal flooding of a property. The footpath slope directed overtopping water toward the property frontage. (p15)",1 property (p15),GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16,Nassington flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Nassington, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Station Road, Nassington.pdf"
GB-NNH,29,2020-08-16,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_d42b0b22374b5bbcd81d39f0d7900af3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The county (Nassington area) experienced surface water flooding on 16th August 2020, prompting county&ndash;level actions including asset planning, maintenance/cleansing of streets and open spaces, and coordination between highways, planning and the LLFA to manage flood risk. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"The county (Nassington area) experienced surface water flooding on 16th August 2020, prompting county–level actions including asset planning, maintenance/cleansing of streets and open spaces, and coordination between highways, planning and the LLFA to manage flood risk. (p23)",,GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16,Nassington flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Nassington, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Station Road, Nassington.pdf"
GB-NNH,29,2020-08-16,52.55266,-0.4302511,"<div id=""html_aa2223d1161f2d1e4c21a350bd5edae0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nassington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding caused by intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall overwhelmed drainage, producing excess surface water that flowed to low points around properties in Nassington and led to internal flooding of at least one building on Station Road. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Station Road (within Nassington) caused internal flooding of at least one property on 16 August 2020; the affected property has experienced internal flooding on multiple occasions in the last five years and reported the incident to the Flood Toolkit. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>In Nassington one property was reported to have flooded internally when surface water (from nearby Church Lane) exceeded drainage capacity, overtopped a dropped kerb and flowed into and through an integral garage. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense heavy rainfall over a short period caused widespread surface&ndash;water/flash flooding across this area of Nassington. The Local Lead Flood Authority (NCC Flood and Water Management Team) received multiple formal and anecdotal reports of flooding from the area and individual reports continued to be received in the weeks after the event. Emergency response was largely reactive; fluvial (main river) warnings were not issued because river level triggers were not reached. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense heavy rainfall on 16th August 2020 caused surface water flooding across the area. Surface water drainage systems across the catchment were unable to collect and convey the rainfall, so excess water flowed over ground following local levels and accumulated in low points around properties. There were no effective overland flow routes to safe areas, resulting in reliance on buried drainage systems whose design capacity was exceeded. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property reported via Flood Toolkit (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Nassington,England,"Surface water flooding caused by intense, short–duration rainfall overwhelmed drainage, producing excess surface water that flowed to low points around properties in Nassington and led to internal flooding of at least one building on Station Road. (p5)
Flooding at Station Road (within Nassington) caused internal flooding of at least one property on 16 August 2020; the affected property has experienced internal flooding on multiple occasions in the last five years and reported the incident to the Flood Toolkit. (p7)
In Nassington one property was reported to have flooded internally when surface water (from nearby Church Lane) exceeded drainage capacity, overtopped a dropped kerb and flowed into and through an integral garage. (p15)
Intense heavy rainfall over a short period caused widespread surface–water/flash flooding across this area of Nassington. The Local Lead Flood Authority (NCC Flood and Water Management Team) received multiple formal and anecdotal reports of flooding from the area and individual reports continued to be received in the weeks after the event. Emergency response was largely reactive; fluvial (main river) warnings were not issued because river level triggers were not reached. [PAG] (p17)
Intense heavy rainfall on 16th August 2020 caused surface water flooding across the area. Surface water drainage systems across the catchment were unable to collect and convey the rainfall, so excess water flowed over ground following local levels and accumulated in low points around properties. There were no effective overland flow routes to safe areas, resulting in reliance on buried drainage systems whose design capacity was exceeded. (p18)","1 property reported via Flood Toolkit (p7)
1 property (p15)",GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_29_2020-08-16,Nassington flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Nassington, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Station Road, Nassington.pdf"
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9364373,-0.2280794,"<div id=""html_7ab289e40a4125226f6a112de97d8001"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wymondley Priory</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Staff observed flood water flowing from an adjacent field to the Priory entrance onto Priory Lane as surface water flowed into Little Wymondley. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The online pond at The Priory fed an unnamed watercourse that was heavily silted, narrow and overgrown, reducing channel capacity and contributing to banks overtopping during the storm on 7 February 2014. [PAG] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Wymondley Priory,England,"Staff observed flood water flowing from an adjacent field to the Priory entrance onto Priory Lane as surface water flowed into Little Wymondley. (p21)
The online pond at The Priory fed an unnamed watercourse that was heavily silted, narrow and overgrown, reducing channel capacity and contributing to banks overtopping during the storm on 7 February 2014. [PAG] (p28)",,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9331526,-0.2122706,"<div id=""html_b1091a6e66b5f06a86d0fc30450bc797"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ash Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland and surface water flows entered and filled the Ash Brook; blocked trash screens and culverts too small for the channel caused water to back up and the channel to be overwhelmed, contributing to flooding of Little Wymondley (internal property flooding) and roads becoming impassable. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Little Wymondley lies within the catchment of Ash Brook; flooding on 7 February 2014 affected the village centre and access roads, consistent with Ash Brook (catchment/overflow) being the watercourse associated with the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Ash Brook caused river flooding that affected Little Wymondley on 7 February 2014, contributing to inundation of the village centre, Stevenage Road (up to Elms Close) and access roads such as Priory Lane. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ash Brook is identified as the primary watercourse in the catchment and is the location where an unnamed, partly open channel (which is open for a short length before entering a culvert) discharges. The confluence at the corner of Priory Lane and Stevenage Road is specifically noted as the meeting point, indicating this reach conveyed flows during the investigated event on 7 February 2014. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ash Brook reached the top of its bank and exited the watercourse on the right&ndash;hand bank at Stevenage Road, flooding properties lower than the road. A blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane impeded flow and caused water to back up upstream, contributing to higher water levels and flooding. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blockage at a downstream trash screen caused water to back up, creating higher water levels upstream than downstream; water tried to re&ndash;enter the channel at a traffic&ndash;calming location and then receded later the same day. [TAB,PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A private attenuation pond designed to receive surface water from the Stevenage Bypass had overtopped and overflowed into Ash Brook, bypassing the formal outfall. Downstream inlet to Ash Brook was heavily silted and partially blocked with debris; trash screen had been recently cleared with debris deposited on the bank, indicating flood&ndash;related blockage and overtopping into the watercourse. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the storm on 7 February 2014 the Ash Brook had high water levels and banks overtopped in places. The open channel was heavily overgrown and silted, reducing conveyance capacity. Debris (garden waste, leaf litter, twigs and branches) that had accumulated along frontages north of Stevenage Road was washed downstream during the heavy rainfall, increasing the risk of culvert/blockage and overbank flows. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ash Brook had high water levels and in places its banks overtopped during the 7 February 2014 event. A blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane caused Ash Brook to back up and exit the channel, flooding surrounding land and flowing down Stevenage Road. The channel is described as heavily silted and overgrown in numerous locations (reducing capacity) and there is a collapsing revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ash Brook was running high; a blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane caused the brook to back up and exit its channel, flooding surrounding land and contributing flows into the village (including down Stevenage Road). The channel was noted as silted/overgrown in places and had culverts of limited capacity, reducing conveyance and contributing to overtopping during the event. A collapsing revetment outside Elms Close was also observed. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blockage of a trash screen at Chantry Lane caused water in the Ash Brook to back up, exit the channel and flood surrounding land and flow down Stevenage Road; sections of the channel were heavily silted and overgrown, reducing capacity and contributing to overflow. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Ash Brook during the event submerged drainage outfalls from roads, preventing flows entering the channel and causing backing up of water onto highways. Material left in the channel could become mobile during high flows and potentially block downstream culverts/trash screens. A section of revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site was observed collapsing into the channel. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Ash Brook during the 7 February 2014 event likely submerged road drainage outfalls, preventing flows from the highway entering the channel and causing water to back up onto the roads. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert and trash&ndash;screen route of Ash Brook from Chantry Lane down to the entrance of Wymondley Nursing Home was implicated in the Little Wymondley flooding on 7th February 2014. HCC confirmed responsibility for this culvert but it is not recorded on the HCC asset register and therefore not under a maintenance programme, and HCC were investigating whether A1(M) highway run&ndash;off discharges to Ash Brook as a potential contributing factor to the flooding. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Corey's Mill water meadow is online and part of Ash Brook; its outfall had a blocked trash screen that restricted flow and contributed to flooding at Little Wymondley. The outfall and access were inspected and the trash screen and access were cleared to restore flow; Anglian Water committed to a maintenance plan. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ash Brook was implicated in the flooding affecting Little Wymondley on 7 February 2014; high flows in the brook and its associated conveyance structures (culverts, trash screens) contributed to or exacerbated local inundation. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in Ash Brook (which receives surface water run&ndash;off from the A1(M)) contributed to flooding in Little Wymondley; assets along Ash Brook were identified by CCTV as being in poor condition and implicated in the flood impacts, requiring remedial works/maintenance. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ash Brook received surface water run&ndash;off from the A1(M) and was implicated in the flooding affecting Little Wymondley; the report identifies sections of the watercourse (including culverted sections, bridges and vegetation) as potentially impeding flows and requires riparian landowners to inspect and clear obstructions to reduce flood risk. [PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mapped as the main watercourse (red lines) through the village; contributed to observed flooding where surface&ndash;water flow paths converged with the watercourse at low&ndash;lying crossings and nearby areas. [IMG] (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least four residential properties and one commercial property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (reported) [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three to four meters of open channel [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one trash screen (Chantry Lane) became blocked [PAG] (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Ash Brook,England,"Overland and surface water flows entered and filled the Ash Brook; blocked trash screens and culverts too small for the channel caused water to back up and the channel to be overwhelmed, contributing to flooding of Little Wymondley (internal property flooding) and roads becoming impassable. (p7)
Little Wymondley lies within the catchment of Ash Brook; flooding on 7 February 2014 affected the village centre and access roads, consistent with Ash Brook (catchment/overflow) being the watercourse associated with the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
The Ash Brook caused river flooding that affected Little Wymondley on 7 February 2014, contributing to inundation of the village centre, Stevenage Road (up to Elms Close) and access roads such as Priory Lane. [PAG] (p11)
Ash Brook is identified as the primary watercourse in the catchment and is the location where an unnamed, partly open channel (which is open for a short length before entering a culvert) discharges. The confluence at the corner of Priory Lane and Stevenage Road is specifically noted as the meeting point, indicating this reach conveyed flows during the investigated event on 7 February 2014. [IMG,PAG] (p15)
Ash Brook reached the top of its bank and exited the watercourse on the right–hand bank at Stevenage Road, flooding properties lower than the road. A blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane impeded flow and caused water to back up upstream, contributing to higher water levels and flooding. (p21)
Blockage at a downstream trash screen caused water to back up, creating higher water levels upstream than downstream; water tried to re–enter the channel at a traffic–calming location and then receded later the same day. [TAB,PAG] (p22)
A private attenuation pond designed to receive surface water from the Stevenage Bypass had overtopped and overflowed into Ash Brook, bypassing the formal outfall. Downstream inlet to Ash Brook was heavily silted and partially blocked with debris; trash screen had been recently cleared with debris deposited on the bank, indicating flood–related blockage and overtopping into the watercourse. [PAG] (p23)
During the storm on 7 February 2014 the Ash Brook had high water levels and banks overtopped in places. The open channel was heavily overgrown and silted, reducing conveyance capacity. Debris (garden waste, leaf litter, twigs and branches) that had accumulated along frontages north of Stevenage Road was washed downstream during the heavy rainfall, increasing the risk of culvert/blockage and overbank flows. [PAG] (p28)
Ash Brook had high water levels and in places its banks overtopped during the 7 February 2014 event. A blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane caused Ash Brook to back up and exit the channel, flooding surrounding land and flowing down Stevenage Road. The channel is described as heavily silted and overgrown in numerous locations (reducing capacity) and there is a collapsing revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
Ash Brook was running high; a blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane caused the brook to back up and exit its channel, flooding surrounding land and contributing flows into the village (including down Stevenage Road). The channel was noted as silted/overgrown in places and had culverts of limited capacity, reducing conveyance and contributing to overtopping during the event. A collapsing revetment outside Elms Close was also observed. [PAG] (p30)
Blockage of a trash screen at Chantry Lane caused water in the Ash Brook to back up, exit the channel and flood surrounding land and flow down Stevenage Road; sections of the channel were heavily silted and overgrown, reducing capacity and contributing to overflow. [PAG] (p31)
High water levels in Ash Brook during the event submerged drainage outfalls from roads, preventing flows entering the channel and causing backing up of water onto highways. Material left in the channel could become mobile during high flows and potentially block downstream culverts/trash screens. A section of revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site was observed collapsing into the channel. [PAG] (p32)
High water levels in Ash Brook during the 7 February 2014 event likely submerged road drainage outfalls, preventing flows from the highway entering the channel and causing water to back up onto the roads. [PAG] (p33)
The culvert and trash–screen route of Ash Brook from Chantry Lane down to the entrance of Wymondley Nursing Home was implicated in the Little Wymondley flooding on 7th February 2014. HCC confirmed responsibility for this culvert but it is not recorded on the HCC asset register and therefore not under a maintenance programme, and HCC were investigating whether A1(M) highway run–off discharges to Ash Brook as a potential contributing factor to the flooding. (p35)
The Corey's Mill water meadow is online and part of Ash Brook; its outfall had a blocked trash screen that restricted flow and contributed to flooding at Little Wymondley. The outfall and access were inspected and the trash screen and access were cleared to restore flow; Anglian Water committed to a maintenance plan. [PAG] (p36)
Ash Brook was implicated in the flooding affecting Little Wymondley on 7 February 2014; high flows in the brook and its associated conveyance structures (culverts, trash screens) contributed to or exacerbated local inundation. (p37)
Flows in Ash Brook (which receives surface water run–off from the A1(M)) contributed to flooding in Little Wymondley; assets along Ash Brook were identified by CCTV as being in poor condition and implicated in the flood impacts, requiring remedial works/maintenance. (p40)
Ash Brook received surface water run–off from the A1(M) and was implicated in the flooding affecting Little Wymondley; the report identifies sections of the watercourse (including culverted sections, bridges and vegetation) as potentially impeding flows and requires riparian landowners to inspect and clear obstructions to reduce flood risk. [PAG] (p41)
Mapped as the main watercourse (red lines) through the village; contributed to observed flooding where surface–water flow paths converged with the watercourse at low–lying crossings and nearby areas. [IMG] (p50)","at least four residential properties and one commercial property (p7)
4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (reported) [IMG,PAG] (p10)
three to four meters of open channel [IMG,PAG] (p15)
one trash screen (Chantry Lane) became blocked [PAG] (p31)",GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9324375,-0.2176214,"<div id=""html_b9c96dfb3f58e921afcc453d50446606"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Corey's Mill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents raised concerns that the outfall from Corey's Mill water meadow (an asset built to take additional flows from the Stevenage Bypass) may have released additional flows during the 7 February 2014 flood, potentially contributing to flooding in Little Wymondley; the asset has not received regular maintenance since a change of ownership and this contribution was recorded as requiring verification by Anglian Water. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Corey's Mill water meadow attenuation area (built to accept A1(M) run&ndash;off and part of Ash Brook) was inspected after the flooding; its outfall had a blocked trash screen which was cleared and access to the outfall cleared to ensure free flow. The feature is implicated in handling surface water/run&ndash;off from the A1(M). (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Ash Brook flows through Corey's Mill water meadow (an Anglian Water riparian area). The watercourse was implicated in the flooding affecting Little Wymondley associated with the 7 February 2014 event. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Corey's Mill and its associated water meadow were implicated in the flood response; a maintenance plan for the mill was requested and the meadow's capacity and need for upgrade or Reservoirs Act listing were to be assessed because of surface water run&ndash;off related to the flooding. (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Corey's Mill,England,"Residents raised concerns that the outfall from Corey's Mill water meadow (an asset built to take additional flows from the Stevenage Bypass) may have released additional flows during the 7 February 2014 flood, potentially contributing to flooding in Little Wymondley; the asset has not received regular maintenance since a change of ownership and this contribution was recorded as requiring verification by Anglian Water. [TAB] (p29)
The Corey's Mill water meadow attenuation area (built to accept A1(M) run–off and part of Ash Brook) was inspected after the flooding; its outfall had a blocked trash screen which was cleared and access to the outfall cleared to ensure free flow. The feature is implicated in handling surface water/run–off from the A1(M). (p36)
The Ash Brook flows through Corey's Mill water meadow (an Anglian Water riparian area). The watercourse was implicated in the flooding affecting Little Wymondley associated with the 7 February 2014 event. (p37)
Corey's Mill and its associated water meadow were implicated in the flood response; a maintenance plan for the mill was requested and the meadow's capacity and need for upgrade or Reservoirs Act listing were to be assessed because of surface water run–off related to the flooding. (p41)",,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9353564,-0.2522896,"<div id=""html_8b3415e9837ba34e1c62dea858093ea7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stevenage Road, Little Wymondley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road became impassable due to floodwater; vehicles that attempted to pass generated bow waves that increased the impact of the flooding; police closed the road. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flowing water made its way along Stevenage Road rather than through the culverted channel because of blocked trash screens and undersized culverts; water was discharged back onto the road and contributed to flooding in the village centre. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Stevenage Road in the village centre suffered internal flooding on 7 February 2014. The main road into and out of Little Wymondley (Stevenage Road) was closed by Hertfordshire Constabulary because it became impassable; vehicles attempting to pass created bow waves that increased flooding impacts to properties. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Stevenage Road (east&ndash;west access) and properties in the centre of the village up to Elms Close. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water flows along Stevenage Road rendered the road impassable and led Hertfordshire Constabulary to close the road; flows extended from Chantry Lane to Elms Close and vehicle bow waves increased flooding to nearby properties. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water was observed and heard flowing along Stevenage Road in the early hours; residents on Stevenage Road awoke to water within their properties and flows beneath under&ndash;floor voids. Flows from the east along Stevenage Road joined water from Priory Lane, contributing to roads becoming impassable. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water flowed along Stevenage Road (joining flows from Priory Lane), causing the road to become impassable; vehicles driving through created bow waves that worsened flooding; properties lower than the road were flooded where Ash Brook overtopped the right bank. Water also flowed through the Plume of Feathers car park and some re&ndash;entered Ash Brook downstream of the trash screen. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A trash screen/inlet to the Ash Brook adjacent to Stevenage Road became blocked with debris so water could not flow through the screen, causing water to back up and create higher water levels upstream than downstream; flood waters subsequently receded and were able to flow back into the watercourse by the end of the day. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Outfall located north of Stevenage Road opposite the junction with Chantry Lane was choked with vegetation, heavily silted and difficult to access; the blocked/outfall condition and associated debris indicate localized flow blockage contributing to flood flow bypass and reduced conveyance into Ash Brook. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Along the frontage of properties north of Stevenage Road garden waste and tree debris accumulated and then was washed downstream during heavy rainfall, reflecting flood flows in the Ash Brook and contributing to hazard and potential blockages during the storm on 7 February 2014. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 7 February 2014 water from the Ash Brook backed up and exited the channel and flowed down Stevenage Road (linked to a blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane). Gullies in Stevenage Road were reported found to be blocked and outfalls were likely submerged, preventing road drainage and causing water to back up onto the road. Residents also reported vehicle bow waves on 7 February pushed flood water towards properties on the road. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from Ash Brook backed up and exited the channel and flowed down Stevenage Road. Residents reported vehicle&ndash;generated bow waves on 7 February 2014 that pushed water toward properties, reaching over door thresholds and entering low airbricks. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies in Stevenage Road were found to be blocked during the flood; with Ash Brook at high level the road outfalls would have been submerged, preventing drainage into the channel and causing water to back up onto the road on 7 February 2014. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies were found to be blocked during the event and, with Ash Brook at high levels submerging outfalls, road drainage could not discharge to the channel. Water backed up onto and along the road, causing significant highway flooding. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts carrying ordinary watercourses under Stevenage Road were identified for CCTV survey to confirm their condition and potential role in flooding during the 7 February 2014 event. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Stevenage Road experienced surface water/urban flooding; trash screens on the road require technical assessment and gullies/drainage need review and maintenance to manage road flooding and reduce flood impacts. (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (reported) (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Stevenage Road, Little Wymondley",England,"Road became impassable due to floodwater; vehicles that attempted to pass generated bow waves that increased the impact of the flooding; police closed the road. (p7)
Flowing water made its way along Stevenage Road rather than through the culverted channel because of blocked trash screens and undersized culverts; water was discharged back onto the road and contributed to flooding in the village centre. [PAG] (p8)
Properties on Stevenage Road in the village centre suffered internal flooding on 7 February 2014. The main road into and out of Little Wymondley (Stevenage Road) was closed by Hertfordshire Constabulary because it became impassable; vehicles attempting to pass created bow waves that increased flooding impacts to properties. (p10)
Flooding affected Stevenage Road (east–west access) and properties in the centre of the village up to Elms Close. [PAG] (p11)
Flood water flows along Stevenage Road rendered the road impassable and led Hertfordshire Constabulary to close the road; flows extended from Chantry Lane to Elms Close and vehicle bow waves increased flooding to nearby properties. (p16)
Water was observed and heard flowing along Stevenage Road in the early hours; residents on Stevenage Road awoke to water within their properties and flows beneath under–floor voids. Flows from the east along Stevenage Road joined water from Priory Lane, contributing to roads becoming impassable. [PAG] (p20)
Flood water flowed along Stevenage Road (joining flows from Priory Lane), causing the road to become impassable; vehicles driving through created bow waves that worsened flooding; properties lower than the road were flooded where Ash Brook overtopped the right bank. Water also flowed through the Plume of Feathers car park and some re–entered Ash Brook downstream of the trash screen. [PAG] (p21)
A trash screen/inlet to the Ash Brook adjacent to Stevenage Road became blocked with debris so water could not flow through the screen, causing water to back up and create higher water levels upstream than downstream; flood waters subsequently receded and were able to flow back into the watercourse by the end of the day. [TAB] (p22)
Outfall located north of Stevenage Road opposite the junction with Chantry Lane was choked with vegetation, heavily silted and difficult to access; the blocked/outfall condition and associated debris indicate localized flow blockage contributing to flood flow bypass and reduced conveyance into Ash Brook. (p23)
Along the frontage of properties north of Stevenage Road garden waste and tree debris accumulated and then was washed downstream during heavy rainfall, reflecting flood flows in the Ash Brook and contributing to hazard and potential blockages during the storm on 7 February 2014. [PAG] (p28)
On 7 February 2014 water from the Ash Brook backed up and exited the channel and flowed down Stevenage Road (linked to a blocked trash screen at Chantry Lane). Gullies in Stevenage Road were reported found to be blocked and outfalls were likely submerged, preventing road drainage and causing water to back up onto the road. Residents also reported vehicle bow waves on 7 February pushed flood water towards properties on the road. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
Floodwater from Ash Brook backed up and exited the channel and flowed down Stevenage Road. Residents reported vehicle–generated bow waves on 7 February 2014 that pushed water toward properties, reaching over door thresholds and entering low airbricks. [PAG] (p30)
Gullies in Stevenage Road were found to be blocked during the flood; with Ash Brook at high level the road outfalls would have been submerged, preventing drainage into the channel and causing water to back up onto the road on 7 February 2014. (p32)
Highway gullies were found to be blocked during the event and, with Ash Brook at high levels submerging outfalls, road drainage could not discharge to the channel. Water backed up onto and along the road, causing significant highway flooding. [PAG] (p33)
Culverts carrying ordinary watercourses under Stevenage Road were identified for CCTV survey to confirm their condition and potential role in flooding during the 7 February 2014 event. (p39)
Stevenage Road experienced surface water/urban flooding; trash screens on the road require technical assessment and gullies/drainage need review and maintenance to manage road flooding and reduce flood impacts. (p40)",4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (reported) (p10),GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9370021,-0.2310333,"<div id=""html_67967ba5b5e06d093d17030cffe7dc6c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Priory Lane, Wymondley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland and surface water flows reached the unnamed watercourse along Priory Lane via field drains and surface water discharge points, contributing to the village flooding. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the recurring locations that flooded (including the event on 7 February 2014). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road access from the north along Priory Lane was affected by flooding on 7 February 2014; the road became impassable in places as the village centre and access roads were inundated. [IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road access along Priory Lane from the north was flooded, affecting access to the village. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water flows along Priory Lane rendered the road impassable and led Hertfordshire Constabulary to close the road; flows extended from Gravely Lane to the junction with Stevenage Road and vehicle bow waves worsened flooding to nearby properties. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water flowed down Priory Lane onto Stevenage Road, adding to flows in the village; roads became impassable. Water entered/flowed under properties' under&ndash;floor voids and threatened a local business on Priory Lane (Hertfordshire Fire and Rescue pumped water away to prevent entry). (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage and conveyance beneath and alongside Priory Lane were compromised: an open channel beside the east verge contained leaf litter and debris, and culverts beneath/adjacent to the lane were blocked or silted so flow from the unnamed watercourse discharges into the open channel at the north boundary of Priory Farm land. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The unnamed watercourse running to/along Priory Lane is described as heavily overgrown, silted and of limited capacity; with saturated ground and the storm on 7 February 2014 watercourses would have been high and banks could overtop, producing overland flow and flooding onto/around Priory Lane. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 7 February 2014 event the Priory Lane channel was extremely narrow, overgrown and heavily silted which reduced its capacity and caused it to react quickly to run&ndash;off. Highway gullies along Priory Lane were buried/blocked by verge collapse and the highway drainage was likely overwhelmed, resulting in water flowing down the highway. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Numerous buried gullies (caused by verge collapse from vehicles) prevented highway drainage from entering the system so runoff flowed down the carriageway to the lowest point, contributing to roadway flooding during the 7 February 2014 event. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Buried and blocked gullies (caused by collapsed verges covering manhole covers and open gullies) prevented highway drainage entering the drainage system, causing water to flow along the highway to low points during the heavy rainfall on 7 February 2014. CCTV surveys of culverts along Priory Lane were later undertaken by the LLFA. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts carrying ordinary watercourses beneath Priory Lane were subject to CCTV inspection after the 7 February 2014 flood to confirm their condition and assess whether culvert condition or blockage had potential to influence flooding at the location. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An unnamed watercourse and drainage assets along Priory Lane were identified in poor condition from a CCTV survey after the 7 February 2014 flood; works were recommended to program repairs/replacement, and the maintenance regime for gullies and drainage on Priory Lane was to be assessed. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An unnamed watercourse along Priory Lane (connected to the Ash Brook system) was implicated in the flooding response; riparian sections including culverts, bridges, vegetation and debris were identified as potentially impeding flows and requiring inspection and clearance following the event. (p41)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>150mm culvert blocked with debris; 1 x 300mm and 1 x 150mm plastic culverts present; 300mm+ culvert; brick&ndash;arch culvert 450mm noted downstream; downstream outlet culvert 600mm at junction with Stevenage Road. [PAG] (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Priory Lane, Wymondley",England,"Overland and surface water flows reached the unnamed watercourse along Priory Lane via field drains and surface water discharge points, contributing to the village flooding. (p7)
Identified as one of the recurring locations that flooded (including the event on 7 February 2014). (p8)
Road access from the north along Priory Lane was affected by flooding on 7 February 2014; the road became impassable in places as the village centre and access roads were inundated. [IMG] (p10)
Road access along Priory Lane from the north was flooded, affecting access to the village. [PAG] (p11)
Flood water flows along Priory Lane rendered the road impassable and led Hertfordshire Constabulary to close the road; flows extended from Gravely Lane to the junction with Stevenage Road and vehicle bow waves worsened flooding to nearby properties. (p16)
Flood water flowed down Priory Lane onto Stevenage Road, adding to flows in the village; roads became impassable. Water entered/flowed under properties' under–floor voids and threatened a local business on Priory Lane (Hertfordshire Fire and Rescue pumped water away to prevent entry). (p21)
Drainage and conveyance beneath and alongside Priory Lane were compromised: an open channel beside the east verge contained leaf litter and debris, and culverts beneath/adjacent to the lane were blocked or silted so flow from the unnamed watercourse discharges into the open channel at the north boundary of Priory Farm land. [PAG] (p25)
The unnamed watercourse running to/along Priory Lane is described as heavily overgrown, silted and of limited capacity; with saturated ground and the storm on 7 February 2014 watercourses would have been high and banks could overtop, producing overland flow and flooding onto/around Priory Lane. [PAG] (p28)
During the 7 February 2014 event the Priory Lane channel was extremely narrow, overgrown and heavily silted which reduced its capacity and caused it to react quickly to run–off. Highway gullies along Priory Lane were buried/blocked by verge collapse and the highway drainage was likely overwhelmed, resulting in water flowing down the highway. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
Numerous buried gullies (caused by verge collapse from vehicles) prevented highway drainage from entering the system so runoff flowed down the carriageway to the lowest point, contributing to roadway flooding during the 7 February 2014 event. (p32)
Buried and blocked gullies (caused by collapsed verges covering manhole covers and open gullies) prevented highway drainage entering the drainage system, causing water to flow along the highway to low points during the heavy rainfall on 7 February 2014. CCTV surveys of culverts along Priory Lane were later undertaken by the LLFA. [PAG] (p33)
Culverts carrying ordinary watercourses beneath Priory Lane were subject to CCTV inspection after the 7 February 2014 flood to confirm their condition and assess whether culvert condition or blockage had potential to influence flooding at the location. (p39)
An unnamed watercourse and drainage assets along Priory Lane were identified in poor condition from a CCTV survey after the 7 February 2014 flood; works were recommended to program repairs/replacement, and the maintenance regime for gullies and drainage on Priory Lane was to be assessed. (p40)
An unnamed watercourse along Priory Lane (connected to the Ash Brook system) was implicated in the flooding response; riparian sections including culverts, bridges, vegetation and debris were identified as potentially impeding flows and requiring inspection and clearance following the event. (p41)",150mm culvert blocked with debris; 1 x 300mm and 1 x 150mm plastic culverts present; 300mm+ culvert; brick–arch culvert 450mm noted downstream; downstream outlet culvert 600mm at junction with Stevenage Road. [PAG] (p25),GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.927861,-0.2272171,"<div id=""html_e804b9caec9c7f441fc71928cbc5bd7c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chantry Lane, Wymondley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A trash screen on Chantry Lane was blocked, diverting water over open land before it discharged back onto Stevenage Road; flows nonetheless travelled along Stevenage Road and contributed to inundation and access loss. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water flows on Stevenage Road extended from Chantry Lane to Elms Close, indicating the flood extent reached the Chantry Lane area. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Trash screen at Chantry Lane became obstructed with debris, preventing flow through a box culvert; water backed up upstream of the screen, flowed into fields south of Stevenage Road and through allotments and contributed to flooding of Stevenage Road and adjacent lower&ndash;lying properties. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A culvert at Chantry Lane was identified as being too small and liable to cause water to back up and flow outside the channel; high winter antecedent flows and the storm on 7 February 2014 would have contributed to banks overtopping in places, causing flooding associated with the Ash Brook at or near Chantry Lane. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The trash screen at Chantry Lane became blocked during the 7 February 2014 event, causing water within the Ash Brook to back up, exit the channel and flood the surrounding land and flow down Stevenage Road. The Chantry Lane culvert is also identified as being undersized and able to cause backing up if overwhelmed. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The trash screen at Chantry Lane became blocked during the event, causing Ash Brook to back up, overtop/exit the channel and flood the surrounding land; the backed&ndash;up water then flowed down towards Stevenage Road. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The trash screen at Chantry Lane was to be reviewed and assessed following the 7 February 2014 flooding to determine whether it trapped debris or affected flows and whether it should be retained, redesigned or removed. (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 trash screen blocked [PAG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Chantry Lane, Wymondley",England,"A trash screen on Chantry Lane was blocked, diverting water over open land before it discharged back onto Stevenage Road; flows nonetheless travelled along Stevenage Road and contributed to inundation and access loss. (p7)
Flood water flows on Stevenage Road extended from Chantry Lane to Elms Close, indicating the flood extent reached the Chantry Lane area. (p16)
Trash screen at Chantry Lane became obstructed with debris, preventing flow through a box culvert; water backed up upstream of the screen, flowed into fields south of Stevenage Road and through allotments and contributed to flooding of Stevenage Road and adjacent lower–lying properties. (p21)
A culvert at Chantry Lane was identified as being too small and liable to cause water to back up and flow outside the channel; high winter antecedent flows and the storm on 7 February 2014 would have contributed to banks overtopping in places, causing flooding associated with the Ash Brook at or near Chantry Lane. [PAG] (p28)
The trash screen at Chantry Lane became blocked during the 7 February 2014 event, causing water within the Ash Brook to back up, exit the channel and flood the surrounding land and flow down Stevenage Road. The Chantry Lane culvert is also identified as being undersized and able to cause backing up if overwhelmed. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
The trash screen at Chantry Lane became blocked during the event, causing Ash Brook to back up, overtop/exit the channel and flood the surrounding land; the backed–up water then flowed down towards Stevenage Road. [PAG] (p30)
The trash screen at Chantry Lane was to be reviewed and assessed following the 7 February 2014 flooding to determine whether it trapped debris or affected flows and whether it should be retained, redesigned or removed. (p39)",1 trash screen blocked [PAG] (p30),GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9326112,-0.236922,"<div id=""html_df2d7eeaf5236ec89483f22fb9a76057"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Elms Close, Wymondley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected properties on Stevenage Road up to Elms Close on 7 February 2014, indicating inundation reached the area near Elms Close. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water flows on Stevenage Road extended to Elms Close, placing Elms Close within the area affected by flood flows and road impassability. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water attempting to re&ndash;enter the Ash Brook immediately upstream of a trash screen/box culvert located under Elms Close was impeded by debris on the trash screen. The blockage caused water to back up, producing a higher water level upstream of the screen than downstream; water levels receded through the day and the flood waters returned to the watercourse by the end of the day. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the flood event on 7 February 2014 two trash screens at Elms Close became partially blocked and residents observed upstream water levels higher than downstream. A culvert running from the Elms Close industrial estate to Siccut Road has been identified as undersized and the report notes a revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site is collapsing into the Ash Brook, affecting channel capacity. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two trash screens at the Elms Close industrial area became partially blocked during the 7 February 2014 flood event, with residents observing higher water upstream than downstream. A section of revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site was observed collapsing into the Ash Brook (noted as a channel condition that can worsen flooding). [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two trash screens at Elms Close became partially blocked during the flood, with residents observing upstream water higher than downstream, indicating backwater and reduced conveyance in the channel during the event. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The trash screen at Elms Close was to be reviewed and assessed following the 7 February 2014 flooding to determine whether it affected flow or debris accumulation and whether it should be retained, redesigned or removed. (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two trash screens at Elms Close became partially blocked [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two trash screens [PAG] (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Elms Close, Wymondley",England,"Flooding affected properties on Stevenage Road up to Elms Close on 7 February 2014, indicating inundation reached the area near Elms Close. (p10)
Flood water flows on Stevenage Road extended to Elms Close, placing Elms Close within the area affected by flood flows and road impassability. (p16)
Water attempting to re–enter the Ash Brook immediately upstream of a trash screen/box culvert located under Elms Close was impeded by debris on the trash screen. The blockage caused water to back up, producing a higher water level upstream of the screen than downstream; water levels receded through the day and the flood waters returned to the watercourse by the end of the day. [TAB] (p22)
During the flood event on 7 February 2014 two trash screens at Elms Close became partially blocked and residents observed upstream water levels higher than downstream. A culvert running from the Elms Close industrial estate to Siccut Road has been identified as undersized and the report notes a revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site is collapsing into the Ash Brook, affecting channel capacity. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
Two trash screens at the Elms Close industrial area became partially blocked during the 7 February 2014 flood event, with residents observing higher water upstream than downstream. A section of revetment wall outside the Elms Close industrial site was observed collapsing into the Ash Brook (noted as a channel condition that can worsen flooding). [PAG] (p30)
Two trash screens at Elms Close became partially blocked during the flood, with residents observing upstream water higher than downstream, indicating backwater and reduced conveyance in the channel during the event. [PAG] (p31)
The trash screen at Elms Close was to be reviewed and assessed following the 7 February 2014 flooding to determine whether it affected flow or debris accumulation and whether it should be retained, redesigned or removed. (p39)","two trash screens at Elms Close became partially blocked [PAG] (p30)
two trash screens [PAG] (p31)",GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9419293,-0.2226323,"<div id=""html_827a62f2f59ab7ba421727a470abb709"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Graveley Lane, Wymondley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water flows on Priory Lane extended from Gravely Lane to the junction with Stevenage Road, indicating the flood extent included the Gravely Lane area. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surrounding land and the unnamed watercourse running from Graveley Lane to Stevenage Road had little remaining capacity following the prolonged wet winter, contributing to overland flow and banks overtopping on 7 February 2014. (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Graveley Lane, Wymondley",England,"Flood water flows on Priory Lane extended from Gravely Lane to the junction with Stevenage Road, indicating the flood extent included the Gravely Lane area. (p16)
Surrounding land and the unnamed watercourse running from Graveley Lane to Stevenage Road had little remaining capacity following the prolonged wet winter, contributing to overland flow and banks overtopping on 7 February 2014. (p28)",,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.840052299999996,-0.09785519224523963,"<div id=""html_7e4b4be17e8587199a62100bc13ddbe5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hertfordshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the county (notably Little Wymondley) experienced surface water flooding that inundated roads and affected residential properties. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hertfordshire was hit by a severe storm with heavy rainfall on the evening of 6 February into early 7 February 2014; saturated ground led to runoff and localised flooding, including road closures and flooded properties in Little Wymondley. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Hertfordshire,England,"Parts of the county (notably Little Wymondley) experienced surface water flooding that inundated roads and affected residential properties. (p1)
Hertfordshire was hit by a severe storm with heavy rainfall on the evening of 6 February into early 7 February 2014; saturated ground led to runoff and localised flooding, including road closures and flooded properties in Little Wymondley. (p16)",,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HRT,1,2014-02-07,51.9328384,-0.2344971,"<div id=""html_9f8c87ce18ea4f7bf859bb1f668905f3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Wymondley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A residential street was flooded with water covering the entire road and extending onto sidewalks and property entrances; road access was impeded and properties were at risk of inundation. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties causing internal damage to residential and commercial buildings; village access cut off as main road (Stevenage Road) became impassable; vehicle bow waves increased flood impacts and prompted police to close the road. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village centre was flooded on 7 February 2014 after watercourses were overwhelmed and water flowed outside the channel into the centre; blocked trash screens and undersized culverts contributed to overland flow into the village (vegetation in open watercourses may have reduced severity). [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding occurred in residential properties and a commercial property; the main road into and out of the village became impassable and was closed, and vehicles attempting to pass through floodwater created bow waves that increased flooding impacts to properties. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The centre of Little Wymondley was flooded on 7 February 2014: several residential properties and one commercial property suffered internal flooding; access roads from Priory Lane and Stevenage Road were inundated and the main road was closed by police because it became impassable, with vehicle bow waves worsening property flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the centre of the village. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village was the subject of the flood investigation for the 7 February 2014 extreme weather period; flooding in the local watercourse network (Ash Brook and unnamed tributaries) is noted, with culverted sections and the confluence at Priory Lane / Stevenage Road identified as locations of interest where flood flows were conveyed. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential and commercial properties were flooded in the village; roads into and out of Little Wymondley became impassable due to flood water. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of the village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014 (photos of the flooding on that date were obtained from HCC Highways and local residents). The village is also shown on EA maps as lying in 'high risk' zones for both surface water and river flooding. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected the village on 7 February 2014: residents reported water flowing from surrounding land and watercourses into the settlement; flooding began in the early hours (around 05:00) and water entered properties, including flows beneath under&ndash;floor voids; roads became impassable. [IMG,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding as Ash Brook reached the top of its bank and overtopped onto Stevenage Road and lower&ndash;lying properties; runoff from fields and flows down Priory Lane added to flooding. Roads were impassable and closed by police; residents reported internal and under&ndash;floor flooding in properties. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with Ash Brook occurred in the Little Wymondley area when a trash screen became blocked causing upstream water levels to rise and temporary local inundation before levels receded later the same day. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014; the subsequent investigation documented drainage and culvert issues (blocked/silted outlets, damaged culvert tops and debris) along Priory Lane and near the railway bridge. [PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ash Brook and associated surface water flows passed through Corey's Mill water meadow beneath the A602; inlet to the water meadow and culvert/pipe structures were observed with standing water and partially submerged pipes, and map evidence shows surface water and a watercourse converging in the area, indicating flooding of watercourse/channel and local surface water inundation affecting the village vicinity. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Ash Brook and the unnamed Priory Lane watercourse were reported as heavily silted, overgrown and of restricted capacity; with the wet winter and the storm in the early hours of 7 February 2014 water levels were high and banks would have overtopped in places, producing overland flows and local flooding in the village and contributing to culvert backup at locations such as near Little Wymondley Hall and the Elms Close outfall. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 7 February 2014 event the village experienced flooding from overland flows and from watercourses overtopping/backing up. Residents reported properties being impacted by bow waves generated by vehicles driving through floodwater, which drove water toward properties and could breach door thresholds and airbricks. Water slowed on reaching the village, increasing depths and prolonging inundation. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village, at the lowest point in the Ash Brook catchment, experienced floodwater accumulation as overland flows and drainage converged and slowed; residents reported properties were affected by bow waves from vehicles driving through floodwater which pushed water over door thresholds and into low airbricks. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Village sits at lowest point of the Ash Brook catchment so overland flows, watercourses and highway drainage converged and slowed in the village leading to deeper flood levels. Residents reported on 7 February 2014 that vehicle bow waves pushed floodwater towards properties, breaching door thresholds and entering low airbricks. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the heavy rainfall on 7 February 2014, blocked/buried gullies on adopted roads (Stevenage Road and Priory Lane) and runoff from surrounding fields overwhelmed highway drainage; submerged outfalls to Ash Brook caused further back&ndash;up, producing surface water/road flooding within the village. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014, prompting an LLFA&ndash;led assessment and interventions (inspections and clearance of watercourse/outfall by Anglian Water and enquiries into highway drainage by HCC). [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014 driven by intense, prolonged rainfall. Floodwaters affected the community and land in Little Wymondley (overland and fluvial inundation). (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Little Wymondley experienced flooding on 7 February 2014 driven by the extreme rainfall; multiple contributing causes combined (as identified in the report) and, because the watercourses are confined through the village, the flooding affected the area and requires a collaborative approach to resolve. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village was the location of the 7 February 2014 flood; impacts prompted recommendations to assess and improve road resilience (signage, closure procedures) for roads in and out of Little Wymondley and to review/repair drainage and watercourse assets. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in the village associated with local watercourses (Ash Brook and an unnamed watercourse along Priory Lane), resulting in flood risk and impacts to the community; the report judged there to be limited options for flood alleviation due to confined channels. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed surface&ndash;water flow paths and watercourse flows converged on low&ndash;lying points in the village. Photographs document flooding at multiple locations (roads and watercourse crossings). Flood flow directions followed roads and natural contours into the main watercourses, producing concentrated flooding at key spots. [IMG,PAG] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village was affected by flooding on 7 February 2014; map and photographs show surface water flow across the area and convergence of a watercourse near an unknown culvert, with culvert infrastructure and submerged pipes photographed, indicating local inundation and flow paths through the settlement. [PAG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread inundation across the village on 07 February 2014: roads, driveways and underpasses were submerged; water encroached on properties and building entrances; vehicles and public infrastructure were affected. [IMG,PAG] (p52)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four residential properties; one commercial property [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (reported) [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 homes [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 photographed flood locations [IMG,PAG] (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Little Wymondley,England,"A residential street was flooded with water covering the entire road and extending onto sidewalks and property entrances; road access was impeded and properties were at risk of inundation. [IMG] (p1)
Internal flooding of properties causing internal damage to residential and commercial buildings; village access cut off as main road (Stevenage Road) became impassable; vehicle bow waves increased flood impacts and prompted police to close the road. (p7)
The village centre was flooded on 7 February 2014 after watercourses were overwhelmed and water flowed outside the channel into the centre; blocked trash screens and undersized culverts contributed to overland flow into the village (vegetation in open watercourses may have reduced severity). [PAG] (p8)
Internal flooding occurred in residential properties and a commercial property; the main road into and out of the village became impassable and was closed, and vehicles attempting to pass through floodwater created bow waves that increased flooding impacts to properties. [PAG] (p9)
The centre of Little Wymondley was flooded on 7 February 2014: several residential properties and one commercial property suffered internal flooding; access roads from Priory Lane and Stevenage Road were inundated and the main road was closed by police because it became impassable, with vehicle bow waves worsening property flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
Flooding affected the centre of the village. [PAG] (p11)
The village was the subject of the flood investigation for the 7 February 2014 extreme weather period; flooding in the local watercourse network (Ash Brook and unnamed tributaries) is noted, with culverted sections and the confluence at Priory Lane / Stevenage Road identified as locations of interest where flood flows were conveyed. (p15)
Residential and commercial properties were flooded in the village; roads into and out of Little Wymondley became impassable due to flood water. [PAG] (p16)
Areas of the village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014 (photos of the flooding on that date were obtained from HCC Highways and local residents). The village is also shown on EA maps as lying in 'high risk' zones for both surface water and river flooding. (p19)
Surface water flooding affected the village on 7 February 2014: residents reported water flowing from surrounding land and watercourses into the settlement; flooding began in the early hours (around 05:00) and water entered properties, including flows beneath under–floor voids; roads became impassable. [IMG,PAG] (p20)
The village experienced flooding as Ash Brook reached the top of its bank and overtopped onto Stevenage Road and lower–lying properties; runoff from fields and flows down Priory Lane added to flooding. Roads were impassable and closed by police; residents reported internal and under–floor flooding in properties. (p21)
Flooding associated with Ash Brook occurred in the Little Wymondley area when a trash screen became blocked causing upstream water levels to rise and temporary local inundation before levels receded later the same day. (p22)
The village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014; the subsequent investigation documented drainage and culvert issues (blocked/silted outlets, damaged culvert tops and debris) along Priory Lane and near the railway bridge. [PAG] (p26)
Ash Brook and associated surface water flows passed through Corey's Mill water meadow beneath the A602; inlet to the water meadow and culvert/pipe structures were observed with standing water and partially submerged pipes, and map evidence shows surface water and a watercourse converging in the area, indicating flooding of watercourse/channel and local surface water inundation affecting the village vicinity. [PAG] (p27)
The Ash Brook and the unnamed Priory Lane watercourse were reported as heavily silted, overgrown and of restricted capacity; with the wet winter and the storm in the early hours of 7 February 2014 water levels were high and banks would have overtopped in places, producing overland flows and local flooding in the village and contributing to culvert backup at locations such as near Little Wymondley Hall and the Elms Close outfall. [PAG] (p28)
During the 7 February 2014 event the village experienced flooding from overland flows and from watercourses overtopping/backing up. Residents reported properties being impacted by bow waves generated by vehicles driving through floodwater, which drove water toward properties and could breach door thresholds and airbricks. Water slowed on reaching the village, increasing depths and prolonging inundation. [TAB] (p29)
The village, at the lowest point in the Ash Brook catchment, experienced floodwater accumulation as overland flows and drainage converged and slowed; residents reported properties were affected by bow waves from vehicles driving through floodwater which pushed water over door thresholds and into low airbricks. [PAG] (p31)
Village sits at lowest point of the Ash Brook catchment so overland flows, watercourses and highway drainage converged and slowed in the village leading to deeper flood levels. Residents reported on 7 February 2014 that vehicle bow waves pushed floodwater towards properties, breaching door thresholds and entering low airbricks. [PAG] (p32)
During the heavy rainfall on 7 February 2014, blocked/buried gullies on adopted roads (Stevenage Road and Priory Lane) and runoff from surrounding fields overwhelmed highway drainage; submerged outfalls to Ash Brook caused further back–up, producing surface water/road flooding within the village. [PAG] (p33)
The village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014, prompting an LLFA–led assessment and interventions (inspections and clearance of watercourse/outfall by Anglian Water and enquiries into highway drainage by HCC). [PAG] (p36)
The village experienced flooding on 7 February 2014 driven by intense, prolonged rainfall. Floodwaters affected the community and land in Little Wymondley (overland and fluvial inundation). (p37)
Little Wymondley experienced flooding on 7 February 2014 driven by the extreme rainfall; multiple contributing causes combined (as identified in the report) and, because the watercourses are confined through the village, the flooding affected the area and requires a collaborative approach to resolve. (p38)
The village was the location of the 7 February 2014 flood; impacts prompted recommendations to assess and improve road resilience (signage, closure procedures) for roads in and out of Little Wymondley and to review/repair drainage and watercourse assets. (p40)
Flooding occurred in the village associated with local watercourses (Ash Brook and an unnamed watercourse along Priory Lane), resulting in flood risk and impacts to the community; the report judged there to be limited options for flood alleviation due to confined channels. (p42)
Observed surface–water flow paths and watercourse flows converged on low–lying points in the village. Photographs document flooding at multiple locations (roads and watercourse crossings). Flood flow directions followed roads and natural contours into the main watercourses, producing concentrated flooding at key spots. [IMG,PAG] (p50)
The village was affected by flooding on 7 February 2014; map and photographs show surface water flow across the area and convergence of a watercourse near an unknown culvert, with culvert infrastructure and submerged pipes photographed, indicating local inundation and flow paths through the settlement. [PAG] (p51)
Widespread inundation across the village on 07 February 2014: roads, driveways and underpasses were submerged; water encroached on properties and building entrances; vehicles and public infrastructure were affected. [IMG,PAG] (p52)","at least 4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (p7)
four residential properties; one commercial property [PAG] (p9)
4 residential properties; 1 commercial property (reported) [IMG,PAG] (p10)
4 homes [PAG] (p16)
3 photographed flood locations [IMG,PAG] (p50)",GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_1_2014-02-07,Little Wymondley flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Wymondley, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/CD88 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.5782863,-2.4300367,"<div id=""html_d8d3d93d862acd1ee7584c631b46b231"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bolton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Smiths Road (described as being to the south east of Bolton centre, mixed residential and commercial). Smiths Road is listed as a location for the 9 February 2020 flooding event and is the subject of the investigation. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell flooding in the Bolton area (notably Prestolee and Ringley Bridges) generated Flood Warnings and reports of property flooding from approximately 12:45pm on 9th February 2020. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Bolton,England,"Flooding affected Smiths Road (described as being to the south east of Bolton centre, mixed residential and commercial). Smiths Road is listed as a location for the 9 February 2020 flooding event and is the subject of the investigation. [IMG] (p6)
River Irwell flooding in the Bolton area (notably Prestolee and Ringley Bridges) generated Flood Warnings and reports of property flooding from approximately 12:45pm on 9th February 2020. (p13)",,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.6023223,-2.2999892,"<div id=""html_a7acd0114f7fbec7e882a4bac64739d8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Irwell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the Rivers Irwell and Croal (after significant rainfall on saturated ground) caused internal flooding of properties and businesses in Riverside Drive, Prestolee and Smiths Road. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 9 February 2020 the River Irwell rose to a peak of 1.31m at 13:30 (likely the fifth highest since 1948). A Flood Warning for the River Irwell at Prestolee and Ringley Bridges was issued at 09:05 on 9 February and reports of flooding to property in the area were made from approximately 12:45 (Community Flood Group). Historically the River Irwell has overflowed at Prestolee causing internal flooding (20 September 1946) and on 26 December 2015 (Storm Eva) flooding from the River Irwell caused internal flooding to residential properties on Riverside Drive and to properties on the opposite bank at Stoneclough Rise; Smiths Road also recorded internal flooding on 26 December 2015. [MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell rose and flooded over its banks; rising river levels filled the surface water sewer system at the outfall serving Riverside Drive, causing backflow into highway gullies and resulting in highway flooding before the river bank was overtopped. Floating debris from the river impacted and damaged the brickwork outfall structure. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell overtopped/impacted its embankment adjacent to Riverside Drive, leaving a sandy mix of debris (including man&ndash;made material) on the embankment and contributing to marginal embankment stability and downstream flooding of Riverside Drive. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell at Riverside Drive swelled on 9th February 2020 and flooded into its floodplain, inundating property; a Flood Alert and Flood Warning were issued for the Irwell (Prestolee and Ringley area) with a targeted Flood Warning issued at 09:05 and the first threshold crossed at 10:30. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell rose to levels that triggered a Flood Alert and a Flood Warning for the Prestolee/Stoneclough/Ringley area; the first Flood Warning threshold for the area was crossed on the day of the event. After the event the EA Operations team removed sediment from the river in the area, assessed the viability of temporary defences and treated invasive riverbank vegetation to enable assessment of a potential flood wall; the EA also surveyed and re&ndash;modelled the river as part of a Strategic Business Case and progressed to an Outline Business Case for a proposed flood scheme. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 properties; 5 businesses; 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>36 properties; 9 properties; 5 residential properties; 2 businesses [MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>first Flood Warning threshold crossed; 81 properties within GM25 Flood Warning Area; 74 properties potentially received the flood warning; 52 properties registered with Floodline; 22 properties via Extended Direct Warnings (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Irwell,England,"Fluvial flooding from the Rivers Irwell and Croal (after significant rainfall on saturated ground) caused internal flooding of properties and businesses in Riverside Drive, Prestolee and Smiths Road. (p3)
On 9 February 2020 the River Irwell rose to a peak of 1.31m at 13:30 (likely the fifth highest since 1948). A Flood Warning for the River Irwell at Prestolee and Ringley Bridges was issued at 09:05 on 9 February and reports of flooding to property in the area were made from approximately 12:45 (Community Flood Group). Historically the River Irwell has overflowed at Prestolee causing internal flooding (20 September 1946) and on 26 December 2015 (Storm Eva) flooding from the River Irwell caused internal flooding to residential properties on Riverside Drive and to properties on the opposite bank at Stoneclough Rise; Smiths Road also recorded internal flooding on 26 December 2015. [MUL] (p13)
The River Irwell rose and flooded over its banks; rising river levels filled the surface water sewer system at the outfall serving Riverside Drive, causing backflow into highway gullies and resulting in highway flooding before the river bank was overtopped. Floating debris from the river impacted and damaged the brickwork outfall structure. (p16)
The River Irwell overtopped/impacted its embankment adjacent to Riverside Drive, leaving a sandy mix of debris (including man–made material) on the embankment and contributing to marginal embankment stability and downstream flooding of Riverside Drive. (p17)
The River Irwell at Riverside Drive swelled on 9th February 2020 and flooded into its floodplain, inundating property; a Flood Alert and Flood Warning were issued for the Irwell (Prestolee and Ringley area) with a targeted Flood Warning issued at 09:05 and the first threshold crossed at 10:30. [PAG] (p19)
River Irwell rose to levels that triggered a Flood Alert and a Flood Warning for the Prestolee/Stoneclough/Ringley area; the first Flood Warning threshold for the area was crossed on the day of the event. After the event the EA Operations team removed sediment from the river in the area, assessed the viability of temporary defences and treated invasive riverbank vegetation to enable assessment of a potential flood wall; the EA also surveyed and re–modelled the river as part of a Strategic Business Case and progressed to an Outline Business Case for a proposed flood scheme. (p20)","25 properties; 5 businesses; 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)
36 properties; 9 properties; 5 residential properties; 2 businesses [MUL] (p13)
first Flood Warning threshold crossed; 81 properties within GM25 Flood Warning Area; 74 properties potentially received the flood warning; 52 properties registered with Floodline; 22 properties via Extended Direct Warnings (p20)",GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.5739664,-2.4499115,"<div id=""html_f3c841e36af71c9666589d08636128e4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Croal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the Rivers Irwell and Croal (after significant rainfall on saturated ground) caused internal flooding of properties and businesses in Riverside Drive, Prestolee and Smiths Road. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Croal at Farnworth experienced very high river levels on 9th February 2020 (highest level that day 1.31 m at 1:30pm) and is associated with flooding in the area; the monitoring location is approximately 1 km downstream of properties that flooded on Smiths Road. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Croal at Smiths Road swelled and flooded into its floodplain on 9th February 2020, inundating property and depositing river silt into external areas and highway drainage gullies. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 properties; 5 businesses; 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Croal,England,"Fluvial flooding from the Rivers Irwell and Croal (after significant rainfall on saturated ground) caused internal flooding of properties and businesses in Riverside Drive, Prestolee and Smiths Road. (p3)
The River Croal at Farnworth experienced very high river levels on 9th February 2020 (highest level that day 1.31 m at 1:30pm) and is associated with flooding in the area; the monitoring location is approximately 1 km downstream of properties that flooded on Smiths Road. (p12)
The River Croal at Smiths Road swelled and flooded into its floodplain on 9th February 2020, inundating property and depositing river silt into external areas and highway drainage gullies. (p19)",25 properties; 5 businesses; 1 metre depth in some properties (p3),GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.6091886,-2.185937,"<div id=""html_4dc9ddbf1ea21ee0ecbd391a19b9f57b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Roch</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavier rainfall in the upstream catchments of the Rivers Roch and Croal on 9th February 2020 caused much greater runoff, contributing to rivers swelling and subsequent flooding of floodplains and properties in the area. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Roch,England,"Heavier rainfall in the upstream catchments of the Rivers Roch and Croal on 9th February 2020 caused much greater runoff, contributing to rivers swelling and subsequent flooding of floodplains and properties in the area. (p19)",,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.56336847105238,-2.3994468814572296,"<div id=""html_d6ff87eadedbe61934c8495cf4c91ae7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hacken Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the Smiths Road corridor affected by poor gullies and carriageway drainage resulting in surface water pooling on the road; included in recommendations to replace gullies and improve drainage. [TAB] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Hacken Bridge,England,Part of the Smiths Road corridor affected by poor gullies and carriageway drainage resulting in surface water pooling on the road; included in recommendations to replace gullies and improve drainage. [TAB] (p21),,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.5626694,-2.4000653,"<div id=""html_a92d0e8b3795927de185d63fb25eba9b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Smith's Road, Darcy Lever</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties and businesses on 9 February 2020 caused by fluvial flooding from the Rivers Irwell and Croal. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map identifies a flood location at the downstream edge of the catchment upstream of Smiths Road, indicating river flooding occurred at or immediately upstream of the area near Smiths Road. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Smiths Road were flooded on 9th February 2020 (the River Croal monitoring station at Moses Gate is approx. 1 km downstream of these flooded properties). (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Historic and recorded flooding occurred at Smith Road: river/surface&ndash;water related inundation affected residential and commercial properties; anecdotal evidence indicates areas served by sewers drained away after the river flooding associated with the 9 February event. [MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage performed poorly during the flood, with gully gratings having become proud of the carriageway and the sag point served by only a single gully, reducing interception and flow capacity; gullies needed resetting and additional capacity to intercept water during the event. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location where the River Croal swelled on 9th February 2020 and flooded into the floodplain, resulting in properties being inundated. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding and potential backflow into surface water sewer outfalls affecting the road and nearby properties; actions suggested include more frequent gully maintenance and examination of non&ndash;return valves on outfalls. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding affected properties at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road, with overflow risk into private property (specifically risk of overflow into the vehicle access and into no. 3 Smiths Road). The locations experienced issues with potential backflow from surface water/sewer outfalls during flood events. (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 properties; 5 businesses (Riverside Drive and Smiths Road) (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 residential properties; 2 businesses [MUL] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Smith's Road, Darcy Lever",England,"Internal flooding of properties and businesses on 9 February 2020 caused by fluvial flooding from the Rivers Irwell and Croal. (p3)
Map identifies a flood location at the downstream edge of the catchment upstream of Smiths Road, indicating river flooding occurred at or immediately upstream of the area near Smiths Road. [IMG] (p11)
Properties on Smiths Road were flooded on 9th February 2020 (the River Croal monitoring station at Moses Gate is approx. 1 km downstream of these flooded properties). (p12)
Historic and recorded flooding occurred at Smith Road: river/surface–water related inundation affected residential and commercial properties; anecdotal evidence indicates areas served by sewers drained away after the river flooding associated with the 9 February event. [MUL] (p14)
Highway drainage performed poorly during the flood, with gully gratings having become proud of the carriageway and the sag point served by only a single gully, reducing interception and flow capacity; gullies needed resetting and additional capacity to intercept water during the event. (p17)
Location where the River Croal swelled on 9th February 2020 and flooded into the floodplain, resulting in properties being inundated. (p19)
Surface water flooding and potential backflow into surface water sewer outfalls affecting the road and nearby properties; actions suggested include more frequent gully maintenance and examination of non–return valves on outfalls. [TAB] (p21)
Surface water and sewer–related flooding affected properties at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road, with overflow risk into private property (specifically risk of overflow into the vehicle access and into no. 3 Smiths Road). The locations experienced issues with potential backflow from surface water/sewer outfalls during flood events. (p22)","25 properties; 5 businesses (Riverside Drive and Smiths Road) (p3)
5 residential properties; 2 businesses [MUL] (p14)",GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.54732477345806,-2.3694483548703427,"<div id=""html_eacb69502a3559637f9c82fad6db8eab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Riverside Drive, Prestolee</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties at around 1.30pm on 9 February 2020; flood depths were significant with around 1 metre depth in some properties. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Riverside Drive, a residential area; the location is one of the two specific flood locations investigated in the report. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundation during the river flooding caused siltation of highway drainage gullies; gullies were found with high silt levels and were cleaned after the flood event. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the flood the River Irwell rose and back&ndash;filled the surface water sewer outfall serving Riverside Drive, causing water to issue from highway gullies and resulting in flooding of the highway before the river overtopped its bank. Debris (a rolled&ndash;up section of chain link fencing) was found trapped at the sewer outfall hindering discharge; the outfall brickwork showed structural damage likely from impact by floating debris. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters from the River Irwell spilled onto Riverside Drive (via the left bank), depositing a sandy mix of debris with a high proportion of man&ndash;made material on the embankment and making the embankment marginally stable; the road and adjoining embankment were directly affected by the river flooding. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River water spilled onto Riverside Drive via the left bank during the storm, resulting in flooding affecting the Riverside Drive community. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell swelled and flooded into its floodplain at Riverside Drive, inundating properties. A Forward Incident Officer was deployed to Riverside Drive at ~11:00 on 9 February to carry out door&ndash;knocking and advise evacuation; some residents evacuated while others remained. Follow&ndash;up visits and clean&ndash;up were carried out, with river silt and debris deposited on external areas and in highway gullies. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Riverside Drive: the surface water outfall was identified as structurally impaired and there was risk of backflow into the surface water sewer during flood events. This led to continued development of a flood defence project for Riverside Drive and recommendations to consider reconstruction of the outfall and installation of non&ndash;return valves to reduce property flooding. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surface water outfall at Riverside Drive is structurally impaired, contributing to flooding risk and allowing potential backflow into the drainage system during flood events; this has led RMAs to recommend reconstruction of the outfall and consideration of non&ndash;return valves to reduce property flooding. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 properties; 5 businesses; 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 Adult Social Care Officers; 2 Housing Officers; 2 Forward Incident Officers (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Riverside Drive, Prestolee",England,"Internal flooding of properties at around 1.30pm on 9 February 2020; flood depths were significant with around 1 metre depth in some properties. (p3)
Flooding occurred at Riverside Drive, a residential area; the location is one of the two specific flood locations investigated in the report. [IMG] (p6)
Inundation during the river flooding caused siltation of highway drainage gullies; gullies were found with high silt levels and were cleaned after the flood event. [PAG] (p14)
During the flood the River Irwell rose and back–filled the surface water sewer outfall serving Riverside Drive, causing water to issue from highway gullies and resulting in flooding of the highway before the river overtopped its bank. Debris (a rolled–up section of chain link fencing) was found trapped at the sewer outfall hindering discharge; the outfall brickwork showed structural damage likely from impact by floating debris. (p16)
Floodwaters from the River Irwell spilled onto Riverside Drive (via the left bank), depositing a sandy mix of debris with a high proportion of man–made material on the embankment and making the embankment marginally stable; the road and adjoining embankment were directly affected by the river flooding. [PAG] (p17)
River water spilled onto Riverside Drive via the left bank during the storm, resulting in flooding affecting the Riverside Drive community. (p18)
River Irwell swelled and flooded into its floodplain at Riverside Drive, inundating properties. A Forward Incident Officer was deployed to Riverside Drive at ~11:00 on 9 February to carry out door–knocking and advise evacuation; some residents evacuated while others remained. Follow–up visits and clean–up were carried out, with river silt and debris deposited on external areas and in highway gullies. (p19)
Flooding affected Riverside Drive: the surface water outfall was identified as structurally impaired and there was risk of backflow into the surface water sewer during flood events. This led to continued development of a flood defence project for Riverside Drive and recommendations to consider reconstruction of the outfall and installation of non–return valves to reduce property flooding. [TAB] (p21)
The surface water outfall at Riverside Drive is structurally impaired, contributing to flooding risk and allowing potential backflow into the drainage system during flood events; this has led RMAs to recommend reconstruction of the outfall and consideration of non–return valves to reduce property flooding. [PAG] (p22)","25 properties; 5 businesses; 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)
10 Adult Social Care Officers; 2 Housing Officers; 2 Forward Incident Officers (p19)",GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.54799,-2.3722131,"<div id=""html_c141845318f9e1034762ed93a684f313"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Prestolee</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prestolee (including Riverside Drive and Smiths Road) was directly impacted by river flooding on 9 February 2020, resulting in internal flooding of properties and businesses; properties on Riverside Drive experienced significant inundation (around 1 metre depth in some cases). (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell flooding at Prestolee/Ringley Bridges led to reports of flooding to property starting at approximately 12:45pm following the Flood Warning issued on 9th February 2020. Prestolee has also been the location of past River Irwell overflows (20th September 1946) and the Boxing Day 2015 event associated with Storm Eva. [MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Prestolee community was specifically targeted by a Flood Warning issued on the morning of the event; EA staff provided real&ndash;time forecasting information to the local community flood group during the incident and used the group to organise a post&ndash;event community drop&ndash;in. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 properties; 5 businesses; up to 1 metre depth (in some Riverside Drive properties) (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Prestolee,England,"Prestolee (including Riverside Drive and Smiths Road) was directly impacted by river flooding on 9 February 2020, resulting in internal flooding of properties and businesses; properties on Riverside Drive experienced significant inundation (around 1 metre depth in some cases). (p3)
River Irwell flooding at Prestolee/Ringley Bridges led to reports of flooding to property starting at approximately 12:45pm following the Flood Warning issued on 9th February 2020. Prestolee has also been the location of past River Irwell overflows (20th September 1946) and the Boxing Day 2015 event associated with Storm Eva. [MUL] (p13)
The Prestolee community was specifically targeted by a Flood Warning issued on the morning of the event; EA staff provided real–time forecasting information to the local community flood group during the incident and used the group to organise a post–event community drop–in. (p20)",25 properties; 5 businesses; up to 1 metre depth (in some Riverside Drive properties) (p3),GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.5401383,-2.3779588,"<div id=""html_e6087015299e89f5ee52d09a6221e0d4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kearsley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Riverside Drive in Prestolee (a residential area within Kearsley). This location is specifically identified in the report as a site of flooding on 9 February 2020 and is the subject of the investigation. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell monitoring station at Prestolee (located at Kearsley) recorded 5.33 m at 14:45 on 9 February 2020, indicating high river levels that were relevant to flooding in the Kearsley area. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5.33 m (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Kearsley,England,"Flooding occurred at Riverside Drive in Prestolee (a residential area within Kearsley). This location is specifically identified in the report as a site of flooding on 9 February 2020 and is the subject of the investigation. [IMG] (p6)
The River Irwell monitoring station at Prestolee (located at Kearsley) recorded 5.33 m at 14:45 on 9 February 2020, indicating high river levels that were relevant to flooding in the Kearsley area. (p12)",5.33 m (p12),GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,3,2020-02-09,53.54752648680763,-2.395746160839627,"<div id=""html_4a9cfcb4481fb16e8aa8750de505bcda"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Farnworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Farnworth saw elevated River Croal levels on 9th February 2020 (river level at the Farnworth station reached 1.31 m that day, likely the fifth highest since 1948) with associated property flooding nearby (Smiths Road). (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Farnworth,England,"Farnworth saw elevated River Croal levels on 9th February 2020 (river level at the Farnworth station reached 1.31 m that day, likely the fifth highest since 1948) with associated property flooding nearby (Smiths Road). (p12)",,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09.html,GB-BOL_3_2020-02-09,Bolton flooding 09 Feb 2020,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Riverside Drive and Smiths Road on 9th February 2020 – Storm Ciara,010/Bolton/010/2020/Bolton/Storm/Ciara/2020///Flood/report.pdf
GB-LAN,23,2018-06-01,53.7663092,-2.7594263,"<div id=""html_3271b2d99a8dd76df2600a7c4e6f45d8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Greaves Town Lane, Preston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roadway in Greaves Town was flooded (public highway) and required emergency response, as shown in Fig.2 (photograph of flooded street and a 'Flood' temporary sign). (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,"Greaves Town Lane, Preston",England,"Roadway in Greaves Town was flooded (public highway) and required emergency response, as shown in Fig.2 (photograph of flooded street and a 'Flood' temporary sign). (p15)",,GB-LAN_23_2018-06-01.html,GB-LAN_23_2018-06-01,Lancashire flooding 01 Jun 2018,1 June 2018 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation Report,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-june-2018-flooding.pdf
GB-LAN,23,2018-06-01,53.86117,-2.5650887,"<div id=""html_de6a47773f6e559f3dcedbd0ce4dfefd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancashire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the county were affected by flooding during the event on 1 June 2018. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On the evening of 1 June 2018, very localised high&ndash;intensity downpours temporarily overwhelmed urban drainage systems across parts of Lancashire. Emergency responder records show flooding incidents in Leyland, Lea (Preston), Pilling (Wyre), Bacup, Accrington, Barnoldswick, Earby and Longridge. The event led to flooded highways and domestic property flooding. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of Lancashire experienced flooding on 1 June 2018. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, concentrated rainfall on 1 June 2018 exceeded the capacity of local urban drainage (public and private) across the county, producing surface water flooding of highways, homes, gardens and garages and deep water running downhill on main roads. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple reported flooding incidents across the county on 1 June 2018, affecting public highways and private property. Clusters of reports were noted particularly around Preston, Pendle and Rossendale; emergency services and water companies responded on the night. [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On the night of 1 June 2018 intense rainfall overwhelmed many urban drainage networks across the county, producing standing water on public highways and causing towns and villages to be badly affected; people experienced damage to their homes and belongings and concern for personal safety. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall on 1 June 2018 overwhelmed many urban drainage networks in parts of the county, producing surface water / urban flooding that entered homes and caused damage to people's homes and belongings and concern for personal safety. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>60 homes; 19 homes flooded internally to at least one habitable room (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lancashire,England,"Parts of the county were affected by flooding during the event on 1 June 2018. (p1)
On the evening of 1 June 2018, very localised high–intensity downpours temporarily overwhelmed urban drainage systems across parts of Lancashire. Emergency responder records show flooding incidents in Leyland, Lea (Preston), Pilling (Wyre), Bacup, Accrington, Barnoldswick, Earby and Longridge. The event led to flooded highways and domestic property flooding. (p3)
Part of Lancashire experienced flooding on 1 June 2018. (p10)
Intense, concentrated rainfall on 1 June 2018 exceeded the capacity of local urban drainage (public and private) across the county, producing surface water flooding of highways, homes, gardens and garages and deep water running downhill on main roads. [PAG] (p13)
Multiple reported flooding incidents across the county on 1 June 2018, affecting public highways and private property. Clusters of reports were noted particularly around Preston, Pendle and Rossendale; emergency services and water companies responded on the night. [IMG,PAG] (p14)
On the night of 1 June 2018 intense rainfall overwhelmed many urban drainage networks across the county, producing standing water on public highways and causing towns and villages to be badly affected; people experienced damage to their homes and belongings and concern for personal safety. [PAG] (p17)
