Internal flooding affected properties around Pednormead End. [PAG] (p20)","3 properties (p19)
3 properties [PAG] (p20)",GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-BKM,2,2014-09-20,51.7036297,-0.6132259,"<div id=""html_0080b4c2a3d7228316098b2cdedf8a21"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Chesham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of mainly business properties in the town centre &mdash; flooding extended from Market Square along High Street north to The Broadway. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Building occupiers reported manhole surcharging within at least three shops along the High Street and Broadway. A shop owner on The Broadway observed a tidemark of water and dirt after the event; the visible water had dissipated via the surface water drainage system. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The southern end of the High Street experienced surface water flooding when slot drains were exceeded; water flowed overland southward for about an hour. Vale Brook culvert manholes located within shops along the High Street and Broadway surcharged during the event. Floodwater entered shops a few metres to depths of approximately 2 to 5 cm. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water entered shops (mostly via the street&ndash;facing front) causing internal shallow flooding (typically 2&ndash;5 cm and a few metres into shops). Some properties on the east side were flooded from the rear via East Street. Businesses reported loss of floor&ndash;level stock, replacement of damaged carpets and reduced trading hours to clean up. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water was channelled along the High Street by its levels and camber; the capacity of surface water drainage was exceeded causing a backup of surface water at ground level. A pipe beneath the High Street (approx. 300mm) was noted as possibly affecting drainage capacity if blocked. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High Street drainage and highway drainage capacity/state of repair were identified as problematic during the flood event; recommendations call for improved understanding, gully and highway drainage cleansing and prioritised repairs to address flow restrictions. [TAB] (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 3 shops (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 properties; at least 3 shops' manholes surcharged; depth approximately 2 to 5 cm [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 properties; 4 properties from the rear via East Street [PAG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"High Street, Chesham",England,"Flooding of mainly business properties in the town centre — flooding extended from Market Square along High Street north to The Broadway. (p8)
Building occupiers reported manhole surcharging within at least three shops along the High Street and Broadway. A shop owner on The Broadway observed a tidemark of water and dirt after the event; the visible water had dissipated via the surface water drainage system. (p18)
The southern end of the High Street experienced surface water flooding when slot drains were exceeded; water flowed overland southward for about an hour. Vale Brook culvert manholes located within shops along the High Street and Broadway surcharged during the event. Floodwater entered shops a few metres to depths of approximately 2 to 5 cm. [PAG] (p19)
Surface water entered shops (mostly via the street–facing front) causing internal shallow flooding (typically 2–5 cm and a few metres into shops). Some properties on the east side were flooded from the rear via East Street. Businesses reported loss of floor–level stock, replacement of damaged carpets and reduced trading hours to clean up. [PAG] (p20)
Surface water was channelled along the High Street by its levels and camber; the capacity of surface water drainage was exceeded causing a backup of surface water at ground level. A pipe beneath the High Street (approx. 300mm) was noted as possibly affecting drainage capacity if blocked. (p21)
High Street drainage and highway drainage capacity/state of repair were identified as problematic during the flood event; recommendations call for improved understanding, gully and highway drainage cleansing and prioritised repairs to address flow restrictions. [TAB] (p27)","at least 3 shops (p18)
18 properties; at least 3 shops' manholes surcharged; depth approximately 2 to 5 cm [PAG] (p19)
18 properties; 4 properties from the rear via East Street [PAG] (p20)",GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-BKM,2,2014-09-20,51.70371828020141,-0.6160613637653843,"<div id=""html_faa27b7da93cd75d4e7cb17214067ed0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Street, Chesham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Church Street was internally flooded during the event. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property on Church Street (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Church Street, Chesham",England,One property on Church Street was internally flooded during the event. (p19),1 property on Church Street (p19),GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-BKM,2,2014-09-20,51.7000384,-0.6268577,"<div id=""html_50fef500de268ae5ee99dffacaad5c13"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Missenden Road, Chesham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Chess culvert located under Missenden Road is known to be of insufficient capacity during extreme events and may have caused water to back up upstream, contributing to flooding of properties on Pednormead End. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Missenden Road, Chesham",England,"The River Chess culvert located under Missenden Road is known to be of insufficient capacity during extreme events and may have caused water to back up upstream, contributing to flooding of properties on Pednormead End. (p19)",,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-BKM,2,2014-09-20,51.7024694,-0.614243,"<div id=""html_c46236a452c2c45a81e478d0fde6ba0b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Germain Street, Chesham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Germain Street was internally flooded during the event. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property on Germain Street (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Germain Street, Chesham",England,One property on Germain Street was internally flooded during the event. (p19),1 property on Germain Street (p19),GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-BKM,2,2014-09-20,51.7095035,-0.611689,"<div id=""html_8ea2dc02b65f6738f949e0cd704f9f8b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Townsend Road, Chesham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A temporary logger at the Vale Brook culvert on Townsend Road recorded the watercourse responding to the intense overnight rainfall: water levels rose rapidly and reached the highest recorded since the logger was installed. The Vale Brook at this culvert rose to around 500 mm depth (the channel usually runs dry), showing a rapid rise and fall consistent with a flash&ndash;flooding pulse. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A temporary logger at the entrance to the Vale Brook culvert recorded the highest water levels since monitoring began during the 20 September 2014 event. The typically dry watercourse rose rapidly and fell quickly, reaching a substantial depth at the culvert entrance. [IMG,PAG] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 500 mm water depth [PAG] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Townsend Road, Chesham",England,"A temporary logger at the Vale Brook culvert on Townsend Road recorded the watercourse responding to the intense overnight rainfall: water levels rose rapidly and reached the highest recorded since the logger was installed. The Vale Brook at this culvert rose to around 500 mm depth (the channel usually runs dry), showing a rapid rise and fall consistent with a flash–flooding pulse. [PAG] (p16)
A temporary logger at the entrance to the Vale Brook culvert recorded the highest water levels since monitoring began during the 20 September 2014 event. The typically dry watercourse rose rapidly and fell quickly, reaching a substantial depth at the culvert entrance. [IMG,PAG] (p17)",around 500 mm water depth [PAG] (p16),GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-BKM,2,2014-09-20,51.7034138,-0.6126659,"<div id=""html_6a3c34069a124c393595c5a57b9ab9e7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Street, Chesham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage on East Street was overwhelmed, causing surface water to flow downhill into the rear of properties on the east side of the High Street and contributing to internal flooding of those properties. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The capacity of the highway drainage on East street was exceeded, causing surface water to flow downhill to the rear of properties on the east side of the High Street. (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"East Street, Chesham",England,"Highway drainage on East Street was overwhelmed, causing surface water to flow downhill into the rear of properties on the east side of the High Street and contributing to internal flooding of those properties. (p20)
The capacity of the highway drainage on East street was exceeded, causing surface water to flow downhill to the rear of properties on the east side of the High Street. (p21)",4 properties (p20),GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-BKM,2,2014-09-20,51.70935527066246,-0.6106056414162012,"<div id=""html_ad6846b608209b89312fd42ab0351113"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chesham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The rainfall&ndash;accumulation radar for 20 September 2014 shows very high, localized rainfall over Chesham and its immediate west/southwest surroundings. This heavy accumulation is presented as part of a Flood Investigation Report for Chesham, indicating the intense rainfall was linked to flooding that was investigated. Specific impacts to properties, infrastructure or people are not detailed in the provided context. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20 September 2014 intense rainfall caused surface water runoff and increased flows in the River Chess and its tributary the Vale Brook. In parts of the town these increased flows exceeded the capacity of infrastructure (notably the Vale Brook culvert), resulting in flooding of parts of Chesham. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An intense rainfall event produced surface water runoff and markedly increased flows in the River Chess and its tributary the Vale Brook. In parts of town the increased flow exceeded the capacity of infrastructure (notably the Vale Brook culvert), causing internal flooding to residential properties (including basements). [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple areas within the town experienced property flooding during the incident (areas A, B and C are highlighted as locations where residential and business properties flooded). [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Town centre was affected by flooding driven by the Vale Brook receiving surface water from much of Chesham's urban area and limited channel capacity; flood and surface&ndash;water risk prompted mapping of fluvial and surface water flood extents in the town centre. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected an extensive area of the town during the investigated event, with residential and commercial streets within the town shown to be at risk; a band of fluvial flooding also occurred along the River Chess in the south of the town. The valley topography funnelled runoff towards the Vale Brook and the River Chess, concentrating flows through low&ndash;lying parts of Chesham and increasing flood impacts. [IMG,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A very localised, intense overnight storm (19&ndash;20 Sept 2014) produced heavy rainfall over the town; radar shows highest accumulation over Chesham (particularly High Street and Pednormead End neighbourhoods). The intense rainfall caused rapid rises in local watercourses (see Vale Brook) and contributed to acute urban/flash flooding conditions in the town. Water levels rose and fell quickly in response to the storm. [IMG,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At least 34 properties were internally flooded (5 residential, 29 businesses). Surface&ndash;water ponding in Market Square and along the High Street exceeded slot drains causing overland flows (southern High Street flowed overland for about an hour); most shop flooding penetrated only a few metres to depths of about 2&ndash;5 cm. Vale Brook culvert manholes within at least three High Street/Broadway shops surcharged. Market Square section of the Vale Brook culvert experienced sand compaction from the event, causing collapse of brick pavers at the surface (surface damage) though the culvert performance was not affected. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At least 34 properties were flooded internally (5 residential properties and 29 businesses). Town&ndash;centre shops were generally flooded from the street&ndash;facing front for a few metres with shallow depths of about 2&ndash;5 cm; some properties were flooded from the rear via East Street. Businesses reported loss of floor&ndash;level stock, damaged carpets and reduced trading while cleaning and repairing. The culverted Vale Brook capacity was exceeded, causing backing up of water through manholes and preventing rapid drainage of surface water. [PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An intense, highly localised rainfall event (approx. 03:00&ndash;05:00) produced widespread surface water flooding across Chesham. Up to around Pednormead End and the town centre the event led to overwhelmed drainage, backed&ndash;up culverts/manholes, and surface water entering properties; property owners cleaned floors, removed damaged carpet tiles and used dehumidifiers. Surface water had largely drained away by about 06:00, and no immediate multi&ndash;agency incident response occurred because agencies were unaware until the following Monday. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced flooding on 20 September 2014; a number of issues contributed to the event (including problems associated with the Vale Brook culvert). (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced flooding that affected the town centre; issues with drainage and culvert restrictions (Vale Brook culvert under Missenden Road) increased flood risk and prompted post&ndash;event actions (CCTV surveys, temporary defences review and the Chesham Flood Alleviation Scheme). [TAB,PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced flooding associated with the investigated event (20 September 2014). Community and local authority response actions are recorded: residents were advised to sign up for Environment Agency Floodline warnings, to document and report flood incidents to Chiltern District Council, Buckinghamshire County Council and the Environment Agency, and the Chesham Flood Action Group coordinated local response and support. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five residential properties within 1km2 [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>34 properties; 5 residential properties; 29 businesses; at least 3 shop manholes surcharged [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>34 properties; 5 residential properties; 29 businesses; 5 properties in Market Square; 18 properties along the High Street; 2 properties on The Broadway; 4 properties on Broad Street [PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Chesham,England,"The rainfall–accumulation radar for 20 September 2014 shows very high, localized rainfall over Chesham and its immediate west/southwest surroundings. This heavy accumulation is presented as part of a Flood Investigation Report for Chesham, indicating the intense rainfall was linked to flooding that was investigated. Specific impacts to properties, infrastructure or people are not detailed in the provided context. [IMG] (p1)
On 20 September 2014 intense rainfall caused surface water runoff and increased flows in the River Chess and its tributary the Vale Brook. In parts of the town these increased flows exceeded the capacity of infrastructure (notably the Vale Brook culvert), resulting in flooding of parts of Chesham. (p4)
An intense rainfall event produced surface water runoff and markedly increased flows in the River Chess and its tributary the Vale Brook. In parts of town the increased flow exceeded the capacity of infrastructure (notably the Vale Brook culvert), causing internal flooding to residential properties (including basements). [PAG] (p5)
Multiple areas within the town experienced property flooding during the incident (areas A, B and C are highlighted as locations where residential and business properties flooded). [IMG,PAG] (p8)
Town centre was affected by flooding driven by the Vale Brook receiving surface water from much of Chesham's urban area and limited channel capacity; flood and surface–water risk prompted mapping of fluvial and surface water flood extents in the town centre. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
Surface water flooding affected an extensive area of the town during the investigated event, with residential and commercial streets within the town shown to be at risk; a band of fluvial flooding also occurred along the River Chess in the south of the town. The valley topography funnelled runoff towards the Vale Brook and the River Chess, concentrating flows through low–lying parts of Chesham and increasing flood impacts. [IMG,PAG] (p12)
A very localised, intense overnight storm (19–20 Sept 2014) produced heavy rainfall over the town; radar shows highest accumulation over Chesham (particularly High Street and Pednormead End neighbourhoods). The intense rainfall caused rapid rises in local watercourses (see Vale Brook) and contributed to acute urban/flash flooding conditions in the town. Water levels rose and fell quickly in response to the storm. [IMG,PAG] (p16)
At least 34 properties were internally flooded (5 residential, 29 businesses). Surface–water ponding in Market Square and along the High Street exceeded slot drains causing overland flows (southern High Street flowed overland for about an hour); most shop flooding penetrated only a few metres to depths of about 2–5 cm. Vale Brook culvert manholes within at least three High Street/Broadway shops surcharged. Market Square section of the Vale Brook culvert experienced sand compaction from the event, causing collapse of brick pavers at the surface (surface damage) though the culvert performance was not affected. [PAG] (p19)
At least 34 properties were flooded internally (5 residential properties and 29 businesses). Town–centre shops were generally flooded from the street–facing front for a few metres with shallow depths of about 2–5 cm; some properties were flooded from the rear via East Street. Businesses reported loss of floor–level stock, damaged carpets and reduced trading while cleaning and repairing. The culverted Vale Brook capacity was exceeded, causing backing up of water through manholes and preventing rapid drainage of surface water. [PAG,MUL] (p20)
An intense, highly localised rainfall event (approx. 03:00–05:00) produced widespread surface water flooding across Chesham. Up to around Pednormead End and the town centre the event led to overwhelmed drainage, backed–up culverts/manholes, and surface water entering properties; property owners cleaned floors, removed damaged carpet tiles and used dehumidifiers. Surface water had largely drained away by about 06:00, and no immediate multi–agency incident response occurred because agencies were unaware until the following Monday. [PAG] (p21)
The town experienced flooding on 20 September 2014; a number of issues contributed to the event (including problems associated with the Vale Brook culvert). (p25)
The town experienced flooding that affected the town centre; issues with drainage and culvert restrictions (Vale Brook culvert under Missenden Road) increased flood risk and prompted post–event actions (CCTV surveys, temporary defences review and the Chesham Flood Alleviation Scheme). [TAB,PAG] (p27)
The town experienced flooding associated with the investigated event (20 September 2014). Community and local authority response actions are recorded: residents were advised to sign up for Environment Agency Floodline warnings, to document and report flood incidents to Chiltern District Council, Buckinghamshire County Council and the Environment Agency, and the Chesham Flood Action Group coordinated local response and support. [TAB,PAG] (p29)","five residential properties within 1km2 [PAG] (p5)
34 properties; 5 residential properties; 29 businesses; at least 3 shop manholes surcharged [PAG] (p19)
34 properties; 5 residential properties; 29 businesses; 5 properties in Market Square; 18 properties along the High Street; 2 properties on The Broadway; 4 properties on Broad Street [PAG,MUL] (p20)",GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20.html,GB-BKM_2_2014-09-20,Chesham flooding 20 Sep 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Chesham 20 September 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/150629-chesham-s19-report/final.pdf
GB-HEF,29,2020-02-16,51.9503616,-2.7192109,"<div id=""html_bfade976a52ed4e1d369b2c8778a82f5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Llanwarne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding in Llanwarne affected two properties and a parish church (episodes during each reported storm, lasting up to 6 hours). There was also a report of internal flooding at one other property in Llanwarne (noted as being in Flood Zone 1). [TAB,MUL] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties; 1 parish church; 1 additional internally flooded property (in Flood Zone 1) [TAB,MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Llanwarne,England,"Fluvial flooding in Llanwarne affected two properties and a parish church (episodes during each reported storm, lasting up to 6 hours). There was also a report of internal flooding at one other property in Llanwarne (noted as being in Flood Zone 1). [TAB,MUL] (p4)","2 properties; 1 parish church; 1 additional internally flooded property (in Flood Zone 1) [TAB,MUL] (p4)",GB-HEF_29_2020-02-16_etc.html,GB-HEF_29_2020-02-16_etc,,,
GB-HEF,29,2020-02-16,51.8861918,-2.6829994,"<div id=""html_1c077c2744a203f8679c53e4dbd4c5a1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Llangarron</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal fluvial flooding of properties in Llangarron caused by the nearby brook. Reported internal flooding events affected properties and adjacent gardens/outbuildings; one property experienced repeated flooding across multiple storm events. Descriptive accounts attribute flooding both to surface water runoff and an overflowing watercourse where a bridge caused flow backup and water to flow around a wall. [MUL] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 accounts of internal flooding; 1 property flooded in all three reported events; flood depths recorded from less than 2 ft to 4 ft (internal); garden/outbuilding depths up to 5 ft; flood duration persisted up to 2 days [MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Llangarron,England,Internal fluvial flooding of properties in Llangarron caused by the nearby brook. Reported internal flooding events affected properties and adjacent gardens/outbuildings; one property experienced repeated flooding across multiple storm events. Descriptive accounts attribute flooding both to surface water runoff and an overflowing watercourse where a bridge caused flow backup and water to flow around a wall. [MUL] (p4),4 accounts of internal flooding; 1 property flooded in all three reported events; flood depths recorded from less than 2 ft to 4 ft (internal); garden/outbuilding depths up to 5 ft; flood duration persisted up to 2 days [MUL] (p4),GB-HEF_29_2020-02-16_etc.html,GB-HEF_29_2020-02-16_etc,,,
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.2972301,-0.1061029,"<div id=""html_ed038f9c494f780ee32c4d31b03a78fb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coulsdon Common</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flow pathways from Coulsdon Common fed into the main flowpath to Caterham Drive and contributed to the surface water flooding seen on 7th June 2016. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified on the RoFSW map as part of a major flow pathway on 7 June 2016 conveying surface water northwards toward Caterham Drive; acted as a route for runoff contributing to flooding at Caterham Drive. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The main storm drain from Coulsdon Common terminates in a soakaway on the Common; that soakaway has been observed to surcharge during heavy rainfall, lifting a manhole cover and allowing water to flow down the grass verge onto Stites Hill Road and via a drainage ditch through Rydon's wood onto Caterham Drive. The constraint and surcharging at this soakaway may have contributed to the severity of downstream flooding on Caterham Drive. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A soakaway at Coulsdon Common is identified as being prone to surcharge; the report recommends this be investigated further as part of addressing local surface water drainage problems that contributed to flooding. [TAB] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 7 June 2016 event surface water runoff overwhelmed or threatened the capacity of the existing surface water sewer and drainage infrastructure at Coulsdon Common (adjacent to the Tandridge/Croydon boundary). This led to concern about drainage capacity and prompted recommendation to assess sewer capacity and consider upstream SuDS/land interventions to reduce overland flow. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 soakaway surcharged (Soakaway A); 1 manhole cover lifted (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Coulsdon Common,England,"Surface water flow pathways from Coulsdon Common fed into the main flowpath to Caterham Drive and contributed to the surface water flooding seen on 7th June 2016. (p12)
Identified on the RoFSW map as part of a major flow pathway on 7 June 2016 conveying surface water northwards toward Caterham Drive; acted as a route for runoff contributing to flooding at Caterham Drive. (p15)
The main storm drain from Coulsdon Common terminates in a soakaway on the Common; that soakaway has been observed to surcharge during heavy rainfall, lifting a manhole cover and allowing water to flow down the grass verge onto Stites Hill Road and via a drainage ditch through Rydon's wood onto Caterham Drive. The constraint and surcharging at this soakaway may have contributed to the severity of downstream flooding on Caterham Drive. (p16)
A soakaway at Coulsdon Common is identified as being prone to surcharge; the report recommends this be investigated further as part of addressing local surface water drainage problems that contributed to flooding. [TAB] (p20)
During the 7 June 2016 event surface water runoff overwhelmed or threatened the capacity of the existing surface water sewer and drainage infrastructure at Coulsdon Common (adjacent to the Tandridge/Croydon boundary). This led to concern about drainage capacity and prompted recommendation to assess sewer capacity and consider upstream SuDS/land interventions to reduce overland flow. [TAB] (p22)",1 soakaway surcharged (Soakaway A); 1 manhole cover lifted (p16),GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.3550556,-0.0643103753173489,"<div id=""html_8a65d4ca2e71b50c05dc32f656bd9053"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The London Borough of Croydon was particularly affected on 7 June 2016 by localised deep, fast&ndash;flowing water which submerged/floated vehicles and entered properties at several locations. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple surface water and groundwater flood events impacted areas within the borough: Purley town centre suffered significant property flooding and disruption to the local transport network during the intense flash floods of 20 July 2007; Kenley and Purley experienced extensive groundwater&ndash;related flooding in winter 2013&ndash;14; intense rainfall in 2015 caused deep surface water flooding in areas of Purley; properties in the Caterham Drive area flooded on 7 June 2016, located in a topographic low where surface water flow paths accumulate. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Croydon borough experienced flooding on 7 June 2016: residents reported flooding to the council, prompting follow&ndash;up and advice on property&ndash;level protection. The event exposed issues with surface water drainage capacity and unrecorded sewer connections (including a sewer between Stites Hill Road and Caterham Drive), and led to actions to investigate mis&ndash;connections and internal sewer flooding risks. [TAB,PAG] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Croydon,England,"The London Borough of Croydon was particularly affected on 7 June 2016 by localised deep, fast–flowing water which submerged/floated vehicles and entered properties at several locations. (p5)
Multiple surface water and groundwater flood events impacted areas within the borough: Purley town centre suffered significant property flooding and disruption to the local transport network during the intense flash floods of 20 July 2007; Kenley and Purley experienced extensive groundwater–related flooding in winter 2013–14; intense rainfall in 2015 caused deep surface water flooding in areas of Purley; properties in the Caterham Drive area flooded on 7 June 2016, located in a topographic low where surface water flow paths accumulate. [MUL] (p9)
Croydon borough experienced flooding on 7 June 2016: residents reported flooding to the council, prompting follow–up and advice on property–level protection. The event exposed issues with surface water drainage capacity and unrecorded sewer connections (including a sewer between Stites Hill Road and Caterham Drive), and led to actions to investigate mis–connections and internal sewer flooding risks. [TAB,PAG] (p22)",,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.3008137,-0.1023436,"<div id=""html_3b33fc24406fda774b9cb874c12c1efb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Caterham Drive, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties located in the topographic low along Caterham Drive flooded when surface water runoff accumulated and formed prominent flow paths; local residents provided photographic evidence of flooding in 2007 and 2015. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff accumulates in a topographic low along Caterham Drive, forming prominent flow paths that led to properties being flooded. The local topography directs runoff along the road from southeast to northwest, causing inundation of properties located in the low point. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water / urban runoff flowed from the vicinity of Caterham&ndash;on&ndash;the&ndash;Hill onto Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event. Properties adjacent to the flow path reported flooding on 7 June 2016 and are located within areas the Environment Agency maps as 'high' risk (annual probability &gt;3.3%). Numerous soakaways along Caterham Drive were identified and several local soakaways were inspected (site visit 05/08/2016; condition assessments by Flowline in Appendix B); soakaway condition (siltation/blocking) affects the ability to drain runoff and is relevant to the flooding experienced on Caterham Drive. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on and adjacent to Caterham Drive reported flooding during the 7th June 2016 event caused by overland surface water flow routed from higher ground and by surcharging foul sewers following heavy rainfall; these properties lie adjacent to RoFSW 'high' risk flow paths. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff (likely originating from Rydon's Lane) propagated north down the road, producing fast&ndash;flowing floodwater along Caterham Drive and causing property flooding at both the northern and southern ends of the road. Properties experienced complete ground&ndash;floor and garden inundation, partial ground&ndash;floor flooding/damp creep, and internal flooding; at least one resident had to move out while a house dried. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Caterham Drive experienced flooding from surface water and from sewers during the event on 7 June 2016. The report identifies surface water and sewer sources as the reported causes of flooding at this location. [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 7 June 2016 the surface water drainage and soakaway network failed to drain the intense rainfall, causing rapid onset of surface water flooding across roads and into surrounding properties. Properties A and B experienced the deepest flooding (water reached thresholds and inundated the ground floor). Residents observed the foul sewer surcharging with lifted manhole covers, indicating surface water entry to the combined sewer. A CCTV survey found blocked gullies (9 and 10) opposite the severely flooded properties. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water/overland flow from intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage on Caterham Drive. Gullies were found blocked (gullies 9 and 10) and some gullies had suspected collapsed outlet pipes (gullies 4 and 10), reducing drainage capacity. Two properties (A and B) experienced severe internal flooding. A manhole on the carriageway was surcharging and there was physical damage to the carriageway around the manhole cover; video shows runoff from Rydon's Lane onto Caterham Drive. The drainage network was later inspected/cleaned after the event. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from Rydon's Lane flowed onto Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event, causing damage to the carriageway around a manhole cover and depositing flood debris on a recently tarmacked pathway leading onto Caterham Drive. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road and adjacent properties flooded; surface water entered and hydraulically overloaded the foul sewer causing sewer surcharge and foul sewage to enter properties; standing water and debris on the carriageway; sandbags were delivered to affected residents; remedial works undertaken including gully cleaning, CCTV surveys and repair of identified defects. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overwhelmed the drainage and entered the foul sewer, hydraulically overloading the sewer network and causing flooding into properties on Caterham Drive. Flood depths exceeded 900mm in places. Residents at the northern end (properties A&ndash;D) used personal property protection; at the southern end properties E&ndash;F were affected and attended by the fire service. A builder removed an earth bund on 7 June which subsequently allowed surface water to flow off the road into woodland in the following days. [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 7th June 2016 Caterham Drive experienced flooding that prompted resident complaints. Lanes for Drains were deployed to pump, suck and sweep floodwater and debris. Subsequent CCTV inspections of drains identified blockages and collapsed pipes that may have been caused by the flooding. The sewer network surcharged during the extreme event as surface water entered the system. [TAB,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Caterham Drive during the event on 7th June 2016; residents reported incidents and provided photographic and video evidence of flooding, and blockages were noted as contributing factors to the flooding. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report describes overland surface water flooding affecting the northern end of Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event. It recommends enhancing nearby land (Dollypers Hill) and installing a brow ditch or interceptor drain to intercept runoff from steep slopes and reduce the volume of overland flooding reaching Caterham Drive. [TAB,PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced overland / surface&ndash;water flooding on 7th June 2016 when runoff from adjacent steep slopes reached the road; the report identifies this as the Caterham Drive flood event and proposes mitigation (brow ditch/interceptor drain, SuDS upslope) to reduce overland flows into the street. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Caterham Drive was the subject of a flood investigation and a Flowline CCTV survey following flood impacts in June 2016; the report headings indicate the road experienced flooding during the June 2016 event. (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties; up to 1m in some areas; two foot of fast&ndash;flowing water reported along the road [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (A and B) inundated; water depth approx 900 mm (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties flooded (A and B); 2 gullies blocked (9 and 10); 2 gullies with suspected collapsed outlet pipes (4 and 10); 1 surcharging manhole; damage to carriageway around 1 manhole [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flooding in excess of 900mm deep; two collapsed gullies (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flooding in excess of 900mm deep; 4 properties (A&ndash;D); 2 properties (E&ndash;F) [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Caterham Drive, Croydon",England,"Properties located in the topographic low along Caterham Drive flooded when surface water runoff accumulated and formed prominent flow paths; local residents provided photographic evidence of flooding in 2007 and 2015. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
Surface water runoff accumulates in a topographic low along Caterham Drive, forming prominent flow paths that led to properties being flooded. The local topography directs runoff along the road from southeast to northwest, causing inundation of properties located in the low point. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p10)
Surface water / urban runoff flowed from the vicinity of Caterham–on–the–Hill onto Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event. Properties adjacent to the flow path reported flooding on 7 June 2016 and are located within areas the Environment Agency maps as 'high' risk (annual probability >3.3%). Numerous soakaways along Caterham Drive were identified and several local soakaways were inspected (site visit 05/08/2016; condition assessments by Flowline in Appendix B); soakaway condition (siltation/blocking) affects the ability to drain runoff and is relevant to the flooding experienced on Caterham Drive. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
Properties on and adjacent to Caterham Drive reported flooding during the 7th June 2016 event caused by overland surface water flow routed from higher ground and by surcharging foul sewers following heavy rainfall; these properties lie adjacent to RoFSW 'high' risk flow paths. [PAG] (p12)
Surface water runoff (likely originating from Rydon's Lane) propagated north down the road, producing fast–flowing floodwater along Caterham Drive and causing property flooding at both the northern and southern ends of the road. Properties experienced complete ground–floor and garden inundation, partial ground–floor flooding/damp creep, and internal flooding; at least one resident had to move out while a house dried. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Caterham Drive experienced flooding from surface water and from sewers during the event on 7 June 2016. The report identifies surface water and sewer sources as the reported causes of flooding at this location. [TAB,PAG] (p14)
On 7 June 2016 the surface water drainage and soakaway network failed to drain the intense rainfall, causing rapid onset of surface water flooding across roads and into surrounding properties. Properties A and B experienced the deepest flooding (water reached thresholds and inundated the ground floor). Residents observed the foul sewer surcharging with lifted manhole covers, indicating surface water entry to the combined sewer. A CCTV survey found blocked gullies (9 and 10) opposite the severely flooded properties. (p15)
Surface water/overland flow from intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage on Caterham Drive. Gullies were found blocked (gullies 9 and 10) and some gullies had suspected collapsed outlet pipes (gullies 4 and 10), reducing drainage capacity. Two properties (A and B) experienced severe internal flooding. A manhole on the carriageway was surcharging and there was physical damage to the carriageway around the manhole cover; video shows runoff from Rydon's Lane onto Caterham Drive. The drainage network was later inspected/cleaned after the event. [PAG] (p16)
Surface water runoff from Rydon's Lane flowed onto Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event, causing damage to the carriageway around a manhole cover and depositing flood debris on a recently tarmacked pathway leading onto Caterham Drive. [IMG] (p17)
Road and adjacent properties flooded; surface water entered and hydraulically overloaded the foul sewer causing sewer surcharge and foul sewage to enter properties; standing water and debris on the carriageway; sandbags were delivered to affected residents; remedial works undertaken including gully cleaning, CCTV surveys and repair of identified defects. (p18)
Surface water overwhelmed the drainage and entered the foul sewer, hydraulically overloading the sewer network and causing flooding into properties on Caterham Drive. Flood depths exceeded 900mm in places. Residents at the northern end (properties A–D) used personal property protection; at the southern end properties E–F were affected and attended by the fire service. A builder removed an earth bund on 7 June which subsequently allowed surface water to flow off the road into woodland in the following days. [TAB,PAG] (p19)
On 7th June 2016 Caterham Drive experienced flooding that prompted resident complaints. Lanes for Drains were deployed to pump, suck and sweep floodwater and debris. Subsequent CCTV inspections of drains identified blockages and collapsed pipes that may have been caused by the flooding. The sewer network surcharged during the extreme event as surface water entered the system. [TAB,PAG] (p20)
Flooding occurred on Caterham Drive during the event on 7th June 2016; residents reported incidents and provided photographic and video evidence of flooding, and blockages were noted as contributing factors to the flooding. (p21)
The report describes overland surface water flooding affecting the northern end of Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event. It recommends enhancing nearby land (Dollypers Hill) and installing a brow ditch or interceptor drain to intercept runoff from steep slopes and reduce the volume of overland flooding reaching Caterham Drive. [TAB,PAG] (p22)
Experienced overland / surface–water flooding on 7th June 2016 when runoff from adjacent steep slopes reached the road; the report identifies this as the Caterham Drive flood event and proposes mitigation (brow ditch/interceptor drain, SuDS upslope) to reduce overland flows into the street. [PAG] (p23)
Caterham Drive was the subject of a flood investigation and a Flowline CCTV survey following flood impacts in June 2016; the report headings indicate the road experienced flooding during the June 2016 event. (p26)","6 properties; up to 1m in some areas; two foot of fast–flowing water reported along the road [TAB,PAG] (p13)
2 properties (A and B) inundated; water depth approx 900 mm (p15)
2 properties flooded (A and B); 2 gullies blocked (9 and 10); 2 gullies with suspected collapsed outlet pipes (4 and 10); 1 surcharging manhole; damage to carriageway around 1 manhole [PAG] (p16)
flooding in excess of 900mm deep; two collapsed gullies (p18)
flooding in excess of 900mm deep; 4 properties (A–D); 2 properties (E–F) [TAB,PAG] (p19)",GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.30685412479924,-0.1122607055865419,"<div id=""html_785011bb0111a6eb0e7ea36bcc4f5e79"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Glade, Coulsdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Smaller flow pathways from The Glade fed into the main RoFSW flowpath to Caterham Drive and contributed to the surface water flooding observed on 7th June 2016. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"The Glade, Coulsdon",England,Smaller flow pathways from The Glade fed into the main RoFSW flowpath to Caterham Drive and contributed to the surface water flooding observed on 7th June 2016. (p12),,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.3012613,-0.1045028,"<div id=""html_21d555989e1837f07906f0cf5477fdd7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rydon's Lane, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff originating from Rydon's Lane during the intense storm flowed downhill and is reported to have influenced flooding of properties to the south of Caterham Drive. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed from Rydon's Lane onto Caterham Drive during the intense rainfall, forming part of the major flow path and contributing to rapid surface water flooding on 7 June 2016. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff was conveyed through Rydon's wood (and along Rydon's Lane) onto Caterham Drive; video footage shows runoff from Rydon's Lane contributing to flooding downstream. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff originated on Rydon's Lane and flowed onto Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event; this runoff is recorded on video. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flash flooding on 7th June 2016 prompted residents to contact emergency services; calls were received from residents on Caterham Drive and the adjoining Rydon's Lane and the London Fire Brigade were alerted and took action to mitigate floodwater impacts. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three calls (from Caterham Drive and adjoining Rydon's Lane) (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Rydon's Lane, Croydon",England,"Surface water runoff originating from Rydon's Lane during the intense storm flowed downhill and is reported to have influenced flooding of properties to the south of Caterham Drive. (p13)
Surface water runoff flowed from Rydon's Lane onto Caterham Drive during the intense rainfall, forming part of the major flow path and contributing to rapid surface water flooding on 7 June 2016. (p15)
Surface water runoff was conveyed through Rydon's wood (and along Rydon's Lane) onto Caterham Drive; video footage shows runoff from Rydon's Lane contributing to flooding downstream. [PAG] (p16)
Surface water runoff originated on Rydon's Lane and flowed onto Caterham Drive during the 7 June 2016 event; this runoff is recorded on video. (p17)
Flash flooding on 7th June 2016 prompted residents to contact emergency services; calls were received from residents on Caterham Drive and the adjoining Rydon's Lane and the London Fire Brigade were alerted and took action to mitigate floodwater impacts. (p19)",three calls (from Caterham Drive and adjoining Rydon's Lane) (p19),GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.2977529,-0.1100513,"<div id=""html_8b9c1fe327e8514697397b8b01e36f4f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stites Hill Road, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from Stites Hill Road flowed onto Caterham Drive, forming a major flow path that contributed to rapid surface water flooding during the intense rainfall on 7 June 2016. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water from a surcharging soakaway on Coulsdon Common lifted a manhole cover and flowed down the grass verge onto Stites Hill Road, flooding the carriageway. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed onto Stites Hill Road (adjacent to the park) during the 7 June 2016 event. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Evidence of surface water flowing onto Stites Hill Road adjacent to City of London Park was recorded. A previously unrecorded sewer was identified connecting Stites Hill Road (in Tandridge) to Caterham Drive, which may have carried significant extra flow to Caterham Drive during the event. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Soakaways along Stites Hill Road have previously surcharged; the report identifies two soakaways that have surcharged and recommends further investigation as part of addressing the surface&ndash;water drainage failures linked to the event. [TAB] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An unrecorded sewer connection between Stites Hill Road and Caterham Drive is specifically highlighted for further exploration as part of the flood investigation, indicating sewer infrastructure linked to flooding at the time of the event. RMAs were advised to detect unknown pipelines/infrastructure that may have contributed to flooding. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 soakaways [TAB] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Stites Hill Road, Croydon",England,"Surface water runoff from Stites Hill Road flowed onto Caterham Drive, forming a major flow path that contributed to rapid surface water flooding during the intense rainfall on 7 June 2016. (p15)
Water from a surcharging soakaway on Coulsdon Common lifted a manhole cover and flowed down the grass verge onto Stites Hill Road, flooding the carriageway. (p16)
Surface water flowed onto Stites Hill Road (adjacent to the park) during the 7 June 2016 event. (p17)
Evidence of surface water flowing onto Stites Hill Road adjacent to City of London Park was recorded. A previously unrecorded sewer was identified connecting Stites Hill Road (in Tandridge) to Caterham Drive, which may have carried significant extra flow to Caterham Drive during the event. [PAG] (p18)
Soakaways along Stites Hill Road have previously surcharged; the report identifies two soakaways that have surcharged and recommends further investigation as part of addressing the surface–water drainage failures linked to the event. [TAB] (p20)
An unrecorded sewer connection between Stites Hill Road and Caterham Drive is specifically highlighted for further exploration as part of the flood investigation, indicating sewer infrastructure linked to flooding at the time of the event. RMAs were advised to detect unknown pipelines/infrastructure that may have contributed to flooding. [TAB] (p22)",2 soakaways [TAB] (p20),GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.28197,-0.0771032,"<div id=""html_6946a8102d79d437bbcaa42d882f7f27"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Caterham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A surface water flow path is described as originating in the vicinity of Caterham&ndash;on&ndash;the&ndash;Hill and routed northwest onto Caterham Drive, contributing overland runoff that led to surface water flooding downslope during the 7th June 2016 event. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense local storm on 7 June 2016 caused surface water runoff and overland flow from steep slopes and Rydon's Lane, plus surcharging foul sewers, which propagated down Caterham Drive. Six properties (labelled A&ndash;F) were flooded: complete ground floor and garden flooding at properties A and B (up to 1 m in places), complete garden flooding and partial ground&ndash;floor flooding/damp at properties C and D, and internal flooding at properties E and F (one resident had to move out while the house dried). Bus drivers reported fast&ndash;flowing water (about two foot) along Caterham Drive and traffic diversions were put in place. Over 80 calls relating to flooding in the area were received by Thames Water Utilities on 7&ndash;8 June. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff overloaded the sewer network in Caterham on the Hill, causing hydraulic overload, sewer surcharge and flooding with reports of foul sewage entering properties. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Caterham area was affected by overland surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding (event of 7th June 2016). Specific local impacts included flooding on/near Caterham Drive and Stites Hill Road, identification of an unrecorded sewer connection between Stites Hill Road and Caterham Drive, and community&ndash;level response including formation of the Caterham and Old Coulsdon Flood Action Group. Mitigation options were proposed for land at the northern end of Caterham Drive (Dollypers Hill) to reduce overland runoff to the area. [TAB,PAG] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Over 80 calls; 6 properties (A&ndash;F) flooded (4 properties in the north, 2 in the south); up to 1 m flood depth in some areas; ~2 foot of fast&ndash;flowing water reported by bus drivers. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flooding in excess of 900mm deep (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Caterham,England,"A surface water flow path is described as originating in the vicinity of Caterham–on–the–Hill and routed northwest onto Caterham Drive, contributing overland runoff that led to surface water flooding downslope during the 7th June 2016 event. [PAG] (p12)
Intense local storm on 7 June 2016 caused surface water runoff and overland flow from steep slopes and Rydon's Lane, plus surcharging foul sewers, which propagated down Caterham Drive. Six properties (labelled A–F) were flooded: complete ground floor and garden flooding at properties A and B (up to 1 m in places), complete garden flooding and partial ground–floor flooding/damp at properties C and D, and internal flooding at properties E and F (one resident had to move out while the house dried). Bus drivers reported fast–flowing water (about two foot) along Caterham Drive and traffic diversions were put in place. Over 80 calls relating to flooding in the area were received by Thames Water Utilities on 7–8 June. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Surface water runoff overloaded the sewer network in Caterham on the Hill, causing hydraulic overload, sewer surcharge and flooding with reports of foul sewage entering properties. (p18)
The Caterham area was affected by overland surface water and sewer–related flooding (event of 7th June 2016). Specific local impacts included flooding on/near Caterham Drive and Stites Hill Road, identification of an unrecorded sewer connection between Stites Hill Road and Caterham Drive, and community–level response including formation of the Caterham and Old Coulsdon Flood Action Group. Mitigation options were proposed for land at the northern end of Caterham Drive (Dollypers Hill) to reduce overland runoff to the area. [TAB,PAG] (p22)","Over 80 calls; 6 properties (A–F) flooded (4 properties in the north, 2 in the south); up to 1 m flood depth in some areas; ~2 foot of fast–flowing water reported by bus drivers. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
flooding in excess of 900mm deep (p18)",GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.3199716,-0.1393506,"<div id=""html_820d8d3f883761aee788ede3fd8be9c9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coulsdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported across the Coulsdon area (including Caterham Drive and nearby parkland): standing water on roads, blocked gullies and two collapsed gullies were identified; floodwaters subsided then required sweepers and gully&ndash;suckers to clear debris; foul sewage entered properties where surface water entered the foul sewer system; sandbags were delivered to affected residents. [MUL] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two collapsed gullies; approximately 40 calls on 7 June 2016; approximately 40 calls on 8 June 2016; flooding in excess of 900 mm deep [MUL] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Coulsdon,England,"Flooding was reported across the Coulsdon area (including Caterham Drive and nearby parkland): standing water on roads, blocked gullies and two collapsed gullies were identified; floodwaters subsided then required sweepers and gully–suckers to clear debris; foul sewage entered properties where surface water entered the foul sewer system; sandbags were delivered to affected residents. [MUL] (p18)",two collapsed gullies; approximately 40 calls on 7 June 2016; approximately 40 calls on 8 June 2016; flooding in excess of 900 mm deep [MUL] (p18),GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CRY,4,2016-06-07,51.2908971,-0.097909,"<div id=""html_44852fb70754dd25f6d3a00a7391f6ac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Caterham On the Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported in Caterham on the Hill from both the surface and foul sewer networks; surface water appears to have entered sewer assets and may have contributed additional flows toward Caterham Drive via a previously unrecorded sewer. [PAG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Caterham On the Hill,England,Flooding was reported in Caterham on the Hill from both the surface and foul sewer networks; surface water appears to have entered sewer assets and may have contributed additional flows toward Caterham Drive via a previously unrecorded sewer. [PAG] (p18),,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07.html,GB-CRY_4_2016-06-07,Croydon flooding 07 Jun 2016,Caterham Drive Flood Investigation Report – 7th June 2016,030/Croydon/030/2017/Croydon/Caterham Drive S19 Report Final.pdf
GB-CAM,30,2023-10-20,52.355125677488616,0.03755234082856641,"<div id=""html_a3483ee9aa6c27dd4228386520187825"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Meadowlands Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A private piped drainage system from nearby properties connects to an inspection chamber on Meadowlands Park; the outfall and current configuration are unrecorded. The public open space's surface water system is to be CCTV surveyed and targeted for remedial/riparian works, implying it was implicated in the area's poor drainage during the flood events. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Meadowlands Park,England,"A private piped drainage system from nearby properties connects to an inspection chamber on Meadowlands Park; the outfall and current configuration are unrecorded. The public open space's surface water system is to be CCTV surveyed and targeted for remedial/riparian works, implying it was implicated in the area's poor drainage during the flood events. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p4)",,GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc.html,GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc,Earith flooding 20 Oct 2023 and Earith flooding 05 Jan 2024,Flood Investigation Report: Earith,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2023/Cambridgeshire County/Earith-October-2023-and-January-2024-Section-19-Flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-CAM,30,2023-10-20,52.1278302,-0.4250206,"<div id=""html_920019ca1e1b29a09c4de1b146e34001"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Great Ouse</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported impacted areas and the highest surface&ndash;water flood risk are concentrated adjacent to the Great Ouse; surface water flooding affected properties on Bridge End and Vermuyden with significant external flooding and compromised sanitation. [IMG,MUL] (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 internal property flooded; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties contacted the LLFA in January 2024 [IMG,MUL] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,River Great Ouse,England,"Reported impacted areas and the highest surface–water flood risk are concentrated adjacent to the Great Ouse; surface water flooding affected properties on Bridge End and Vermuyden with significant external flooding and compromised sanitation. [IMG,MUL] (p1)","4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 internal property flooded; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties contacted the LLFA in January 2024 [IMG,MUL] (p1)",GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc.html,GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc,Earith flooding 20 Oct 2023 and Earith flooding 05 Jan 2024,Flood Investigation Report: Earith,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2023/Cambridgeshire County/Earith-October-2023-and-January-2024-Section-19-Flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-CAM,30,2023-10-20,52.3545996,0.0366307,"<div id=""html_08db82c37d06c62871f2ac851c11fbb8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridge End, Earith</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Bridge End and Vermuyden experienced surface water flooding that significantly affected external premises. One report included internal property flooding; sanitation and ability to use toilets were compromised. Some households deployed pumps to prevent internal flooding. [MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20th October 2023 surface water accumulated at the low point on Bridge End (reported depth ~230mm). Gardens and outbuildings were flooded with surface water that included foul elements (e.g. toilet paper). Residents reported the local Anglian Water pumping station threshold had an issue on that date, affecting pumping performance. [MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, repeated rainfall overwhelmed the sewerage network and accumulated at topographic low points on Bridge End. Road gullies may have been partially silted, carriageway drainage was a focus for inspection, and previous installation of non&ndash;return valves to protect properties on Bridge End is being reviewed &mdash; indicating properties and the highway were affected by flooding during the events. [TAB,MUL] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 report with internal property flooding; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties reported further flooding (January 2024) [MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>230mm depth [MUL] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Bridge End, Earith",England,"Properties on Bridge End and Vermuyden experienced surface water flooding that significantly affected external premises. One report included internal property flooding; sanitation and ability to use toilets were compromised. Some households deployed pumps to prevent internal flooding. [MUL] (p1)
On 20th October 2023 surface water accumulated at the low point on Bridge End (reported depth ~230mm). Gardens and outbuildings were flooded with surface water that included foul elements (e.g. toilet paper). Residents reported the local Anglian Water pumping station threshold had an issue on that date, affecting pumping performance. [MUL] (p3)
Intense, repeated rainfall overwhelmed the sewerage network and accumulated at topographic low points on Bridge End. Road gullies may have been partially silted, carriageway drainage was a focus for inspection, and previous installation of non–return valves to protect properties on Bridge End is being reviewed — indicating properties and the highway were affected by flooding during the events. [TAB,MUL] (p4)","4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 report with internal property flooding; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties reported further flooding (January 2024) [MUL] (p1)
230mm depth [MUL] (p3)",GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc.html,GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc,Earith flooding 20 Oct 2023 and Earith flooding 05 Jan 2024,Flood Investigation Report: Earith,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2023/Cambridgeshire County/Earith-October-2023-and-January-2024-Section-19-Flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-CAM,30,2023-10-20,52.3558597,0.0388136,"<div id=""html_2b64060fcae6b8517d3c1a7a7c9e32f9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Vermuyden, Earith</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Bridge End and Vermuyden experienced surface water flooding that significantly affected external premises. One report included internal property flooding; sanitation and ability to use toilets were compromised. Some households deployed pumps to prevent internal flooding. [MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20th October 2023 surface water accumulated at the low point on Vermuyden (reported depth ~230mm). Foul inspection chambers on Vermuyden were overwhelmed by surface water ingress and were reported as surcharging; residents described the surcharging water as appearing &#8220;clean&#8221; (i.e. surface water ingress into foul chambers). [MUL] (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 report with internal property flooding; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties reported further flooding (January 2024) [MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>230mm depth [MUL] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Vermuyden, Earith",England,"Properties on Bridge End and Vermuyden experienced surface water flooding that significantly affected external premises. One report included internal property flooding; sanitation and ability to use toilets were compromised. Some households deployed pumps to prevent internal flooding. [MUL] (p1)
On 20th October 2023 surface water accumulated at the low point on Vermuyden (reported depth ~230mm). Foul inspection chambers on Vermuyden were overwhelmed by surface water ingress and were reported as surcharging; residents described the surcharging water as appearing “clean” (i.e. surface water ingress into foul chambers). [MUL] (p3)","4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 report with internal property flooding; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties reported further flooding (January 2024) [MUL] (p1)
