GB-CAM,30,2023-10-20,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_040189bc82ef661281fa275a39725597"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding in Earith significantly affected external premises of properties; one report included internal property flooding; sanitation and ability to use toilets were compromised. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 internal property flooded; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties contacted the LLFA in January 2024 [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"Surface water flooding in Earith significantly affected external premises of properties; one report included internal property flooding; sanitation and ability to use toilets were compromised. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)","4 flood reports (October 2023); 1 internal property flooded; 3 properties deployed pumps; 5 properties contacted the LLFA in January 2024 [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)",GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc.html,GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc,Earith flooding 20 Oct 2023 and Earith flooding 05 Jan 2024,Flood Investigation Report: Earith,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2023/Cambridgeshire County/Earith-October-2023-and-January-2024-Section-19-Flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-CAM,30,2023-10-20,52.35550901479053,0.03320716254148328,"<div id=""html_6aa8313ad43104a1add8f3e18ea78bad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Earith</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village of Earith experienced surface water flooding concentrated in southern and central parts, with reported impacts to properties (external premises significantly affected) and compromised sanitation/toilet use. The map and report note a history of surface water flooding in this location. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred in the Earith area during heavy rainfall (19&ndash;20 October 2023 and 3&ndash;5 January 2024). The affected area is low&ndash;lying with a distinct depression (shown in the LiDAR DTM), which led to water accumulation and higher&ndash;than&ndash;modelled incidence of flooding; residents were visited by the LLFA on 31 October 2023. The report notes that observed flooding exceeded the mapped surface&ndash;water risk and that some areas marked as high risk were less severely affected, indicating extremely localised factors influenced where flooding occurred. [PAG,MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High&ndash;intensity rainstorms caused surface water flooding in Earith, flooding at least four properties. Foul inspection chambers were reported surcharging with ingress of surface water (foul elements present in some flooded gardens/outbuildings), indicating interaction between surface water and the foul sewer network. [MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, repeated rainfall on 20 October 2023 and 5 January 2024 overwhelmed the sewerage network and caused water to accumulate at topographic low points in Earith. Local carriageway drainage and gullies may have been compromised by silt/debris (last cleaned Sept 2023), there is a private piped drainage connection into an inspection chamber on Meadowlands Park whose outfall is unknown, and flooding is a recurring issue requiring further investigation (including non&ndash;return valve effectiveness on Bridge End). [TAB,MUL] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 flood reports (October 2023); 5 properties reported further flooding (January 2024); 1 report included internal property flooding; 3 properties deployed pumps [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties [MUL] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Earith,England,"The village of Earith experienced surface water flooding concentrated in southern and central parts, with reported impacts to properties (external premises significantly affected) and compromised sanitation/toilet use. The map and report note a history of surface water flooding in this location. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)
Surface water flooding occurred in the Earith area during heavy rainfall (19–20 October 2023 and 3–5 January 2024). The affected area is low–lying with a distinct depression (shown in the LiDAR DTM), which led to water accumulation and higher–than–modelled incidence of flooding; residents were visited by the LLFA on 31 October 2023. The report notes that observed flooding exceeded the mapped surface–water risk and that some areas marked as high risk were less severely affected, indicating extremely localised factors influenced where flooding occurred. [PAG,MUL] (p2)
High–intensity rainstorms caused surface water flooding in Earith, flooding at least four properties. Foul inspection chambers were reported surcharging with ingress of surface water (foul elements present in some flooded gardens/outbuildings), indicating interaction between surface water and the foul sewer network. [MUL] (p3)
Intense, repeated rainfall on 20 October 2023 and 5 January 2024 overwhelmed the sewerage network and caused water to accumulate at topographic low points in Earith. Local carriageway drainage and gullies may have been compromised by silt/debris (last cleaned Sept 2023), there is a private piped drainage connection into an inspection chamber on Meadowlands Park whose outfall is unknown, and flooding is a recurring issue requiring further investigation (including non–return valve effectiveness on Bridge End). [TAB,MUL] (p4)","4 flood reports (October 2023); 5 properties reported further flooding (January 2024); 1 report included internal property flooding; 3 properties deployed pumps [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)
4 properties [MUL] (p3)",GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc.html,GB-CAM_30_2023-10-20_etc,Earith flooding 20 Oct 2023 and Earith flooding 05 Jan 2024,Flood Investigation Report: Earith,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2023/Cambridgeshire County/Earith-October-2023-and-January-2024-Section-19-Flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-LAN,13,2016-09-13,53.9158869,-2.1432568,"<div id=""html_871b2b3a8e65be1b896dc8bd9a8e8c54"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Earby Beck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Earby Beck burst its banks, flooding the adjacent road and leaving silt and other debris. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Earby Beck burst its banks due to intense rainfall, causing significant flash flooding in Earby: roads were closed by police, 9 properties were internally flooded and firefighters rescued 10 people stranded in a restaurant. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties; 10 people (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Earby Beck,England,"Earby Beck burst its banks, flooding the adjacent road and leaving silt and other debris. (p3)
Earby Beck burst its banks due to intense rainfall, causing significant flash flooding in Earby: roads were closed by police, 9 properties were internally flooded and firefighters rescued 10 people stranded in a restaurant. (p12)",9 properties; 10 people (p12),GB-LAN_13_2016-09-13.html,GB-LAN_13_2016-09-13,Lancashire flooding 13 Sep 2016,13 September 2016 Flood In Lancashire: Section 19 Report,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-13-september-2016-flooding.pdf
GB-LAN,13,2016-09-13,53.86117,-2.5650887,"<div id=""html_f68ba81cc6d906280a18ac7246f8ea23"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancashire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy overnight rainfall on the night of 13 September 2016 caused surface water flooding across the county; local urban drainage networks were overwhelmed and did not have capacity to accommodate the run&ndash;off, resulting in widespread flooding of homes, roads and footways. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Across Lancashire 42 properties were reported flooded as a result of the intense rainfall on 13 September 2016; flooding was reported across the listed districts (Burnley, Chorley, Pendle, Rossendale, South Ribble, Wyre). (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 13 September 2016 rainfall event overwhelmed natural and man&ndash;made drainage networks across locations in Lancashire, producing particularly damaging flooding in parts of the county (notably Pendle and Lancaster districts). [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The extreme rainfall event of 13 September 2016 overwhelmed natural and man&ndash;made drainage networks across locations in Lancashire, producing surface water/flash flooding and particularly damaging flooding in some districts (notably Pendle and Lancaster). Reported effects of the intense rainfall included traffic driving through standing water producing bow waves that washed onto private property, vehicles abandoned in flooded roads impeding access, and water ingress at low&ndash;lying properties. (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties flooded; 11 homes internally flooded [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lancashire,England,"Heavy overnight rainfall on the night of 13 September 2016 caused surface water flooding across the county; local urban drainage networks were overwhelmed and did not have capacity to accommodate the run–off, resulting in widespread flooding of homes, roads and footways. [PAG] (p3)
Across Lancashire 42 properties were reported flooded as a result of the intense rainfall on 13 September 2016; flooding was reported across the listed districts (Burnley, Chorley, Pendle, Rossendale, South Ribble, Wyre). (p12)
The 13 September 2016 rainfall event overwhelmed natural and man–made drainage networks across locations in Lancashire, producing particularly damaging flooding in parts of the county (notably Pendle and Lancaster districts). [PAG] (p14)
