GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.3494416,0.2603378,"<div id=""html_f739c54d2970348b7ff67ddd8634cd86"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hever Wood Road, West Kingsdown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding reported at the lowest point of the road affecting one property. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Hever Wood Road, West Kingsdown",England,Flooding reported at the lowest point of the road affecting one property. [TAB] (p2),1 property [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.3477086,0.2517348,"<div id=""html_a460d491ab0d14579ba6398f1fa30058"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Botsom Lane, West Kingsdown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the dip of the road; residents reported 'water pouring off the adjacent business park' onto Botsom Lane and at least one property was affected. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property affected [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Botsom Lane, West Kingsdown",England,Flooding affected the dip of the road; residents reported 'water pouring off the adjacent business park' onto Botsom Lane and at least one property was affected. [TAB] (p2),1 property affected [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.3505151,0.2535894,"<div id=""html_8fbbfad83dd741623c57178236cfa9ec"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Primmett Close, West Kingsdown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property in the cul&ndash;de&ndash;sac was confirmed to have flooded near the lowest point of the road. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Primmett Close, West Kingsdown",England,One property in the cul–de–sac was confirmed to have flooded near the lowest point of the road. [TAB] (p2),1 property flooded [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.3440154,0.2664397,"<div id=""html_8ae1062b42416786caaab8d5e83ba356"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Warland Road, West Kingsdown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of one property at the lowest point of the road. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Warland Road, West Kingsdown",England,Internal flooding of one property at the lowest point of the road. [TAB] (p2),1 property [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.2070749,0.7210362,"<div id=""html_9cf9faabffabda03b54b1f9eb56a733b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Very heavy rainfall on 10 June 2019 led to surface water flooding across the county; areas such as West Kingsdown, Vigo, Snodland and Swanley were particularly badly affected. In the West Kingsdown area around a dozen properties are reported to have been flooded by surface water, many affected internally. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows from farmland south of the A20 London Road caused muddy surface&ndash;water runoff onto the A20 and into residential streets in West Kingsdown. Floodwater flowed from fields across the A20 into Ash Tree Close and then north towards Southfields Road. Multiple properties experienced internal and external flooding (including kitchens), part of a conservatory wall collapsed, waist&ndash;high flooding was reported on Neal Road and one resident was evacuated by KFRS. A commercial property was internally flooded and local roads were affected by significant muddy runoff. [IMG] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around a dozen properties (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 properties; 1 commercial property; 5 properties in Ash Tree Close/Ash Tree Drive; 2 properties internally flooded in Neal Road; 1 property externally flooded in Neal Road; 1 resident evacuated; 1 part of a conservatory wall collapsed; 1 property flooded on A20 London Road; 1 property flooded in Southfields Road; 1 property in Heaver Wood Road; 1 property in Phelps Close; 1 property in Botsom Lane; 1 property in Primmett Close; 1 property in Warland Road [IMG] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Kent,England,"Very heavy rainfall on 10 June 2019 led to surface water flooding across the county; areas such as West Kingsdown, Vigo, Snodland and Swanley were particularly badly affected. In the West Kingsdown area around a dozen properties are reported to have been flooded by surface water, many affected internally. (p1)
Overland flows from farmland south of the A20 London Road caused muddy surface–water runoff onto the A20 and into residential streets in West Kingsdown. Floodwater flowed from fields across the A20 into Ash Tree Close and then north towards Southfields Road. Multiple properties experienced internal and external flooding (including kitchens), part of a conservatory wall collapsed, waist–high flooding was reported on Neal Road and one resident was evacuated by KFRS. A commercial property was internally flooded and local roads were affected by significant muddy runoff. [IMG] (p2)","around a dozen properties (p1)
15 properties; 1 commercial property; 5 properties in Ash Tree Close/Ash Tree Drive; 2 properties internally flooded in Neal Road; 1 property externally flooded in Neal Road; 1 resident evacuated; 1 part of a conservatory wall collapsed; 1 property flooded on A20 London Road; 1 property flooded in Southfields Road; 1 property in Heaver Wood Road; 1 property in Phelps Close; 1 property in Botsom Lane; 1 property in Primmett Close; 1 property in Warland Road [IMG] (p2)",GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.342944665244204,0.2627640552737047,"<div id=""html_37aae4e5be2c6167a994c91b355cd71f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Kingsdown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Around a dozen properties in the West Kingsdown area were flooded by surface water; many of those properties were affected internally. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across the town experienced overland/surface water flooding (overland flow from adjacent farmland and runoff from a business park). Impacts included internal flooding to residential and at least one commercial property, external flooding, a conservatory wall collapse, and at least one evacuation. [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A heavy, approximately 1&ndash;in&ndash;100&ndash;year storm overwhelmed drainage, producing overland flows from the rural catchment and urban area that collected at local low points and flow paths and caused flooding to properties in the vicinity. Highway drainage was overwhelmed but drained once rainfall ceased. (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around a dozen properties (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>A20 London Road: 1 residential property flooded; 1 commercial property internally flooded; Ash Tree Close/Drive: 5 properties internally flooded; Southfields Road: 1 property; Neal Road: 2 properties internally flooded and 1 property externally flooded; Heaver Wood Road: 1 property; Phelps Close: 1 property; Botsom Lane: 1 property; Primmett Close: 1 property; Warland Road: 1 property; Evacuations: 1 resident; Conservatory wall collapse: 1 [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 report of flooding received during the event (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,West Kingsdown,England,"Around a dozen properties in the West Kingsdown area were flooded by surface water; many of those properties were affected internally. (p1)
Multiple locations across the town experienced overland/surface water flooding (overland flow from adjacent farmland and runoff from a business park). Impacts included internal flooding to residential and at least one commercial property, external flooding, a conservatory wall collapse, and at least one evacuation. [IMG] (p2)
A heavy, approximately 1–in–100–year storm overwhelmed drainage, producing overland flows from the rural catchment and urban area that collected at local low points and flow paths and caused flooding to properties in the vicinity. Highway drainage was overwhelmed but drained once rainfall ceased. (p7)","around a dozen properties (p1)
A20 London Road: 1 residential property flooded; 1 commercial property internally flooded; Ash Tree Close/Drive: 5 properties internally flooded; Southfields Road: 1 property; Neal Road: 2 properties internally flooded and 1 property externally flooded; Heaver Wood Road: 1 property; Phelps Close: 1 property; Botsom Lane: 1 property; Primmett Close: 1 property; Warland Road: 1 property; Evacuations: 1 resident; Conservatory wall collapse: 1 [IMG] (p2)
1 report of flooding received during the event (p7)",GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.3303854,0.3590695,"<div id=""html_68959e2eabb35eb561dfe3616f4d2f08"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Vigo Village</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report notes Vigo was particularly badly affected by the very heavy rainfall and associated flooding during the event. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Vigo Village,England,The report notes Vigo was particularly badly affected by the very heavy rainfall and associated flooding during the event. (p1),,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.3349376,0.4326667560161199,"<div id=""html_b8b05de176752b14da51ecbcce275fea"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Snodland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report notes Snodland was particularly badly affected by the very heavy rainfall and associated flooding during the event. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Snodland,England,The report notes Snodland was particularly badly affected by the very heavy rainfall and associated flooding during the event. (p1),,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-KEN,11,2019-06-10,51.39750895,0.18022632679075817,"<div id=""html_0dcada6133079f3c9e666af6610cb346"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swanley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report notes Swanley was particularly badly affected by the very heavy rainfall and associated flooding during the event. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Swanley,England,The report notes Swanley was particularly badly affected by the very heavy rainfall and associated flooding during the event. (p1),,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_11_2019-06-10,West Kingsdown flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: West Kingsdown,063/Kent/Section-19-Flood-Investigation-West-Kingsdown.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.71329082464435,-2.546640338251686,"<div id=""html_a9c1fb9d7b69a38150d1fd2867379184"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Chef</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow spilled rapidly off the hills to the south and flowed across the Little Chef car park during the July 2012 event, contributing to flooding of downstream properties (Valley Cottages). (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 event flood water flowed overland across the hills and across the car park. (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Little Chef,England,"Overland flow spilled rapidly off the hills to the south and flowed across the Little Chef car park during the July 2012 event, contributing to flooding of downstream properties (Valley Cottages). (p28)
During the July 2012 event flood water flowed overland across the hills and across the car park. (p43)",,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.6903411,-2.4545215,"<div id=""html_dd54a18134387c4f1295417e16c7aedc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River South Winterbourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall and a rapid 9.7 m rise in groundwater (7&ndash;9 July 2012) increased flow in the South Winterbourne, causing river levels to exceed channel capacity along much of its length, flooding at channel restrictions and producing overland flow and spring emergence that affected properties and infrastructure. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river experienced coincident increases in flow and stage during the early July 2012 groundwater rise, with a pronounced peak in early July. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Received augmentation discharges from Wessex Water and reportedly very large volumes of water (including from a released reservoir), increasing flows in the watercourse and contributing to downstream flooding of properties. [PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The South Winterbourne experienced episodic very high flows in 2012 that coincided with flood events: a sharp spike in mean daily flow in July 2012 associated with the July 2012 flood, and further elevated peaks in November 2012 and December 2012. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 event groundwater volumes and pressure were very high, with groundwater emerging through concrete floors in the area. In November 2012 river levels rose (the report notes the river level rise during that event); the Wessex Water augmentation was suspended before the November river rise and did not contribute to that flooding. [MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The South Winterbourne channel has limited capacity through the study area, which was identified as a key contributor to fluvial flooding risk affecting the local villages; blockages (including low bridges and other permanent obstructions) increase upstream flood risk and constrained channel alignment (by properties and the road) limits options for widening or diversion. [PAG,MUL] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The South Winterbourne watercourse alongside the A35 is identified for annual clearance and investigation of highway drainage discharge because it receives highway runoff and is a key feature in the local flood pathway associated with the July 2012 overland flooding. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme high flows in the South Winterbourne watercourse during 6&ndash;8 July 2012 led to overtopping of the watercourse and contributed to flooding of downstream properties; the event was driven by an extreme rise in groundwater and the watercourse remained at elevated flows contributing to further flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The South Winterbourne watercourse was central to the investigation; community management and maintenance were proposed to reduce flood risk, and household&ndash;level measures were recommended to protect properties against groundwater, surface water and fluvial flooding affecting the villages served by the catchment. [PAG,MUL] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Conveyance through the village was poor with risk of blockage at numerous small bridges and along the watercourse; surface water from the north of the B3159 was entering the South Winterbourne due to drainage/road camber issues. Report recommends alterations to bridges, improved drainage connections and road camber to reduce blockage risk and improve conveyance of surface water into the watercourse. [PAG,MUL] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse overflowed and caused flooding to downstream properties; residents raised concerns about an augmentation discharge by Wessex Water around the July 2012 floods and numerous WW tankers operating downstream were reported. Flooding severely affected three properties in the South Winterbourne area, which remained unoccupied six months after the event; flows were diverted to a rear sump and pumped into the channel during the event. [PAG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 flood event residents reported very large volumes of water entering the South Winterbourne associated with a Wessex Water augmentation discharge and rumours of a reservoir release; Wessex Water tankers were observed operating downstream of the watercourse. [PAG] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The South Winterbourne stream experienced very high flows associated with the extreme rainfall in June/July 2012. Groundwater emergence through the South Winterbourne valley and high flows contributed to flooding of the villages; an agreement exists to suspend Wessex Water discharges to the stream when flow exceeds 400 l/s. [PAG] (p78)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9.7 m rise in groundwater; flood waters remained high for approximately two weeks (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak flow at gauging location 1.33 m3/s on 08/07/2012 (highest recorded in the record). [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>peak flow just below 1.4 m3/s in July 2012; secondary peak just below 1.0 m3/s in December 2012 [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [PAG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flow threshold 400 l/s (suspension of discharge) [PAG] (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,River South Winterbourne,England,"Heavy rainfall and a rapid 9.7 m rise in groundwater (7–9 July 2012) increased flow in the South Winterbourne, causing river levels to exceed channel capacity along much of its length, flooding at channel restrictions and producing overland flow and spring emergence that affected properties and infrastructure. (p23)
The river experienced coincident increases in flow and stage during the early July 2012 groundwater rise, with a pronounced peak in early July. [PAG] (p27)
Received augmentation discharges from Wessex Water and reportedly very large volumes of water (including from a released reservoir), increasing flows in the watercourse and contributing to downstream flooding of properties. [PAG] (p29)
The South Winterbourne experienced episodic very high flows in 2012 that coincided with flood events: a sharp spike in mean daily flow in July 2012 associated with the July 2012 flood, and further elevated peaks in November 2012 and December 2012. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p30)
During the July 2012 event groundwater volumes and pressure were very high, with groundwater emerging through concrete floors in the area. In November 2012 river levels rose (the report notes the river level rise during that event); the Wessex Water augmentation was suspended before the November river rise and did not contribute to that flooding. [MUL] (p31)
The South Winterbourne channel has limited capacity through the study area, which was identified as a key contributor to fluvial flooding risk affecting the local villages; blockages (including low bridges and other permanent obstructions) increase upstream flood risk and constrained channel alignment (by properties and the road) limits options for widening or diversion. [PAG,MUL] (p34)
The South Winterbourne watercourse alongside the A35 is identified for annual clearance and investigation of highway drainage discharge because it receives highway runoff and is a key feature in the local flood pathway associated with the July 2012 overland flooding. (p43)
Extreme high flows in the South Winterbourne watercourse during 6–8 July 2012 led to overtopping of the watercourse and contributed to flooding of downstream properties; the event was driven by an extreme rise in groundwater and the watercourse remained at elevated flows contributing to further flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)
The South Winterbourne watercourse was central to the investigation; community management and maintenance were proposed to reduce flood risk, and household–level measures were recommended to protect properties against groundwater, surface water and fluvial flooding affecting the villages served by the catchment. [PAG,MUL] (p47)
Conveyance through the village was poor with risk of blockage at numerous small bridges and along the watercourse; surface water from the north of the B3159 was entering the South Winterbourne due to drainage/road camber issues. Report recommends alterations to bridges, improved drainage connections and road camber to reduce blockage risk and improve conveyance of surface water into the watercourse. [PAG,MUL] (p48)
The watercourse overflowed and caused flooding to downstream properties; residents raised concerns about an augmentation discharge by Wessex Water around the July 2012 floods and numerous WW tankers operating downstream were reported. Flooding severely affected three properties in the South Winterbourne area, which remained unoccupied six months after the event; flows were diverted to a rear sump and pumped into the channel during the event. [PAG] (p76)
During the July 2012 flood event residents reported very large volumes of water entering the South Winterbourne associated with a Wessex Water augmentation discharge and rumours of a reservoir release; Wessex Water tankers were observed operating downstream of the watercourse. [PAG] (p77)
The South Winterbourne stream experienced very high flows associated with the extreme rainfall in June/July 2012. Groundwater emergence through the South Winterbourne valley and high flows contributed to flooding of the villages; an agreement exists to suspend Wessex Water discharges to the stream when flow exceeds 400 l/s. [PAG] (p78)","9.7 m rise in groundwater; flood waters remained high for approximately two weeks (p23)
Peak flow at gauging location 1.33 m3/s on 08/07/2012 (highest recorded in the record). [PAG] (p27)
peak flow just below 1.4 m3/s in July 2012; secondary peak just below 1.0 m3/s in December 2012 [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p30)
3 properties [PAG] (p76)
flow threshold 400 l/s (suspension of discharge) [PAG] (p78)",GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.712726,-2.5459448,"<div id=""html_102f78d9efc5468022142e5a3950bc41"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Valley Cottages, Winterbourne Abbas</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Valley Cottages were flooded when overland flow from hills passed across the Little Chef car park and into the cottages during the July 2012 event. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Valley Cottages were flooded during the July 2012 overland flow event; temporary alleviation was provided using sandbags. (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,"Valley Cottages, Winterbourne Abbas",England,"Valley Cottages were flooded when overland flow from hills passed across the Little Chef car park and into the cottages during the July 2012 event. (p28)
Properties in Valley Cottages were flooded during the July 2012 overland flow event; temporary alleviation was provided using sandbags. (p43)",,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.7080974,-2.5149141,"<div id=""html_1115cce8b4e24457a77fb7455e86fae2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Rew Lane, Dorset</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents identified heavily silted runoff from agricultural land in the Rew Lane (sic) area as contributing to runoff and local flooding problems during the July 2012 event. (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,"North Rew Lane, Dorset",England,Residents identified heavily silted runoff from agricultural land in the Rew Lane (sic) area as contributing to runoff and local flooding problems during the July 2012 event. (p28),,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.711414228638596,-2.5376648204606567,"<div id=""html_f8428f8f0dfb33e46680c100f9c01b8e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Steepleton Water, Winterbourne Steepleton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents in the Steepleton Water area reported surface runoff from the A35 spilling across properties; existing drainage was not maintained and proved inadequate to manage the large flows during the July 2012 flood. [PAG] (p76)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,"Steepleton Water, Winterbourne Steepleton",England,Residents in the Steepleton Water area reported surface runoff from the A35 spilling across properties; existing drainage was not maintained and proved inadequate to manage the large flows during the July 2012 flood. [PAG] (p76),,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.6993004,-2.4995713,"<div id=""html_117078324ea40e8d92660e6b16dad9e9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Martinstown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced incidences of flooding which prompted an investigation to understand causes and identify possible flood alleviation measures. [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties were flooded as groundwater emerged through springs and from overland flow and drainage backup/overflow caused by the elevated groundwater and river levels. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from surrounding hills, groundwater emerging from springs and fluvial overflow caused overland flows through the village. Restricted channel capacity from low bridges and lack of watercourse maintenance increased flooding. Vehicles on the B3159 generated bow&ndash;waves that pushed floodwater above thresholds and into properties. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 flood event bow waves from traffic along the B3159 through Martinstown exacerbated flood impacts; impacts were increased by diverted traffic following the A35 closure. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties were flooded during the 6&ndash;8 July 2012 event due to an extreme rise in groundwater causing groundwater emergence into properties and high watercourse flows causing overtopping; the persistently high groundwater led to additional flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of inadequate surface water drainage were identified and improvement works are recommended to reduce local flood risk. [PAG,MUL] (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 properties (combined Martinstown and Winterbourne Monkton) (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Martinstown,England,"The village experienced incidences of flooding which prompted an investigation to understand causes and identify possible flood alleviation measures. [MUL] (p8)
Properties were flooded as groundwater emerged through springs and from overland flow and drainage backup/overflow caused by the elevated groundwater and river levels. (p23)
Surface water runoff from surrounding hills, groundwater emerging from springs and fluvial overflow caused overland flows through the village. Restricted channel capacity from low bridges and lack of watercourse maintenance increased flooding. Vehicles on the B3159 generated bow–waves that pushed floodwater above thresholds and into properties. (p29)
During the July 2012 flood event bow waves from traffic along the B3159 through Martinstown exacerbated flood impacts; impacts were increased by diverted traffic following the A35 closure. [TAB] (p37)
Properties were flooded during the 6–8 July 2012 event due to an extreme rise in groundwater causing groundwater emergence into properties and high watercourse flows causing overtopping; the persistently high groundwater led to additional flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)
Areas of inadequate surface water drainage were identified and improvement works are recommended to reduce local flood risk. [PAG,MUL] (p47)","42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) (p23)
20 properties (combined Martinstown and Winterbourne Monkton) (p29)
42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p46)",GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.70915958098221,-2.533455946001463,"<div id=""html_cd077756483ec627a77d5c2a4a01958c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Winterbourne Steepleton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced incidences of flooding which prompted an investigation to understand causes and identify possible flood alleviation measures. [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Historic episodes of flooding reported (e.g. 14 April 1936 parish records) where the river overflowed into the upper pond and caused storm flows down the main road, flooding the road between Boxen Hedge Farm and Dairy House and posing danger to children; accounts are similar to residents' reports after the 2012 floods. [PAG,MUL] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties were flooded due to spring emergence of groundwater beneath properties and overland flow/drainage backup associated with the high groundwater and river levels. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Eight properties flooded in the July 2012 event; some of these properties were flooded again in November and December 2012. Flooding resulted from direct groundwater emergence, overland flow from groundwater emerging outside properties, and fluvial flooding due to overtopping of the watercourse. Restricted watercourse capacity and proximity of properties increased vulnerability. [MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village is identified as one of the affected villages where properties experienced fluvial and groundwater&ndash;related flooding; the report notes potential to utilise existing disused channels in Winterbourne Steepleton for localised secondary channel works to reduce flood risk. [MUL] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 flood event bow waves from traffic moving along the B3159 through the village exacerbated flooding impacts; this was worsened when the A35 was closed and traffic was diverted through the village. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties were flooded during the 6&ndash;8 July 2012 event as rising groundwater emerged into and around buildings (including through floors/basements and via doorways) and high flows caused overtopping; continued high groundwater produced further flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Alterations to the watercourse are recommended to reduce blockage risk and improve conveyance; works to channel structures to lower local river levels and reuse of the mill leat/secondary channels were proposed to reduce local flooding and downstream risk from blockages at small bridges. [MUL] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface runoff from the A35 spilled across properties due to inadequate/unmaintained drainage; severe flooding impacted three properties in the South Winterbourne area (flows diverted to a sump and pumped to the watercourse), with those properties still unoccupied six months after the event. [PAG] (p76)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties [MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [PAG] (p76)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Winterbourne Steepleton,England,"The village experienced incidences of flooding which prompted an investigation to understand causes and identify possible flood alleviation measures. [MUL] (p8)
Historic episodes of flooding reported (e.g. 14 April 1936 parish records) where the river overflowed into the upper pond and caused storm flows down the main road, flooding the road between Boxen Hedge Farm and Dairy House and posing danger to children; accounts are similar to residents' reports after the 2012 floods. [PAG,MUL] (p18)
Properties were flooded due to spring emergence of groundwater beneath properties and overland flow/drainage backup associated with the high groundwater and river levels. (p23)
Eight properties flooded in the July 2012 event; some of these properties were flooded again in November and December 2012. Flooding resulted from direct groundwater emergence, overland flow from groundwater emerging outside properties, and fluvial flooding due to overtopping of the watercourse. Restricted watercourse capacity and proximity of properties increased vulnerability. [MUL] (p28)
The village is identified as one of the affected villages where properties experienced fluvial and groundwater–related flooding; the report notes potential to utilise existing disused channels in Winterbourne Steepleton for localised secondary channel works to reduce flood risk. [MUL] (p34)
During the July 2012 flood event bow waves from traffic moving along the B3159 through the village exacerbated flooding impacts; this was worsened when the A35 was closed and traffic was diverted through the village. [TAB] (p37)
Properties were flooded during the 6–8 July 2012 event as rising groundwater emerged into and around buildings (including through floors/basements and via doorways) and high flows caused overtopping; continued high groundwater produced further flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)
Alterations to the watercourse are recommended to reduce blockage risk and improve conveyance; works to channel structures to lower local river levels and reuse of the mill leat/secondary channels were proposed to reduce local flooding and downstream risk from blockages at small bridges. [MUL] (p47)
Surface runoff from the A35 spilled across properties due to inadequate/unmaintained drainage; severe flooding impacted three properties in the South Winterbourne area (flows diverted to a sump and pumped to the watercourse), with those properties still unoccupied six months after the event. [PAG] (p76)","42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) (p23)
8 properties [MUL] (p28)
42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p46)
3 properties [PAG] (p76)",GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.714402500000006,-2.5603476556571554,"<div id=""html_35646c17e4e664e1210800e1ab6cd033"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Winterbourne Abbas</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced incidences of flooding which prompted an investigation to understand causes and identify possible flood alleviation measures. [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Historical records (parish records referenced in the report) indicate instances of flooding in the village; accounts are described as consistent with groundwater and surface&ndash;water flooding and similar to those given after the 2012 floods. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties were flooded when groundwater emerged through springs beneath properties and by overland flow and drainage backup/overflow resulting from the high groundwater and river levels. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fourteen properties suffered internal flooding in July 2012 from overland surface water flow and direct groundwater emergence. Flooding north of the A35 was perceived to be linked to damaged drainage beneath the highway. The A35 was closed for several days due to flooding. Residents reported surcharging of foul and surface water drainage and emergence from utility access chambers. Overland flow from hills also flowed across the Little Chef car park and caused flooding to Valley Cottages; properties at the A35/B3159 junction were affected by overland flow and by the watercourse exceeding capacity. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 event groundwater volumes and pressure were very high, with groundwater emerging through concrete floors in Winterbourne Abbas. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Suffered flooding in July 2012 and further flooding in November 2012 and December 2012 due to sustained high groundwater levels; Wessex Water infrastructure at Winterbourne Abbas had no discharge via its consented outfall during the July 2012 event. [MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>In the July 2012 event overland flow crossed the hills in the south&ndash;west of the village, flowed across the Little Chef car park and flooded properties in Valley Cottages; temporary sandbagging was used as alleviation. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties were flooded during the 6&ndash;8 July 2012 event when an extreme rise in groundwater resulted in groundwater emerging through floors and basements and entering properties, and very high watercourse flows caused overtopping; groundwater remained high leading to additional flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and highway drainage were inadequate causing localised flooding risk; recommendations include investigating drainage connections north of the A35, improving maintenance of surface water/highway drains and considering a permanent flood barrier at the Little Chef car park to direct overland flow into the watercourse. [MUL] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 event residents reported surface runoff (including from the A35) and poor or blocked surface&ndash;water drainage/gulls leading to runoff spilling across properties and reduced channel capacity; these drainage failures contributed to flooding in the village. Residents also reported concerns about an augmentation discharge and tankers operating downstream around the July 2012 floods. [PAG] (p76)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 properties (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Winterbourne Abbas,England,"The village experienced incidences of flooding which prompted an investigation to understand causes and identify possible flood alleviation measures. [MUL] (p8)
Historical records (parish records referenced in the report) indicate instances of flooding in the village; accounts are described as consistent with groundwater and surface–water flooding and similar to those given after the 2012 floods. (p18)
Properties were flooded when groundwater emerged through springs beneath properties and by overland flow and drainage backup/overflow resulting from the high groundwater and river levels. (p23)
Fourteen properties suffered internal flooding in July 2012 from overland surface water flow and direct groundwater emergence. Flooding north of the A35 was perceived to be linked to damaged drainage beneath the highway. The A35 was closed for several days due to flooding. Residents reported surcharging of foul and surface water drainage and emergence from utility access chambers. Overland flow from hills also flowed across the Little Chef car park and caused flooding to Valley Cottages; properties at the A35/B3159 junction were affected by overland flow and by the watercourse exceeding capacity. (p28)
During the July 2012 event groundwater volumes and pressure were very high, with groundwater emerging through concrete floors in Winterbourne Abbas. (p31)
Suffered flooding in July 2012 and further flooding in November 2012 and December 2012 due to sustained high groundwater levels; Wessex Water infrastructure at Winterbourne Abbas had no discharge via its consented outfall during the July 2012 event. [MUL] (p32)
In the July 2012 event overland flow crossed the hills in the south–west of the village, flowed across the Little Chef car park and flooded properties in Valley Cottages; temporary sandbagging was used as alleviation. (p43)
Properties were flooded during the 6–8 July 2012 event when an extreme rise in groundwater resulted in groundwater emerging through floors and basements and entering properties, and very high watercourse flows caused overtopping; groundwater remained high leading to additional flooding in November and December 2012. [MUL] (p46)
Surface water and highway drainage were inadequate causing localised flooding risk; recommendations include investigating drainage connections north of the A35, improving maintenance of surface water/highway drains and considering a permanent flood barrier at the Little Chef car park to direct overland flow into the watercourse. [MUL] (p47)
During the July 2012 event residents reported surface runoff (including from the A35) and poor or blocked surface–water drainage/gulls leading to runoff spilling across properties and reduced channel capacity; these drainage failures contributed to flooding in the village. Residents also reported concerns about an augmentation discharge and tankers operating downstream around the July 2012 floods. [PAG] (p76)","42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) (p23)
14 properties (p28)
42 properties (combined across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p46)",GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.689517,-2.4602515,"<div id=""html_72e32a50a623d398e7e6b1776a8438ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Winterborne Monkton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the total of twenty properties flooded in July 2012. Flooding resulted from surface water runoff from surrounding hills and groundwater emerging in springs, with constrained channel capacity (low bridges, lack of maintenance) exacerbating impacts. (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 properties (combined Martinstown and Winterbourne Monkton) (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Winterborne Monkton,England,"Part of the total of twenty properties flooded in July 2012. Flooding resulted from surface water runoff from surrounding hills and groundwater emerging in springs, with constrained channel capacity (low bridges, lack of maintenance) exacerbating impacts. (p29)",20 properties (combined Martinstown and Winterbourne Monkton) (p29),GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,6,2012-07-01,50.7083205,-2.6120017,"<div id=""html_6997c7a22c0f332c7e862dc9581902ba"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Long Bredy</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced groundwater&ndash;related flooding in July 2012 (noted as the worst since 1955) and further flooding in November 2012 and December 2012 as groundwater levels remained high; the village remained on extended flood alert periods. [MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents reported rumours that the below&ndash;ground Wessex Water reservoir at Long Bredy was 'let go' during the July 2012 flood, resulting in very large volumes of water entering the South Winterbourne; a large number of Wessex Water tankers were observed operating downstream of the watercourse. (p77)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Long Bredy,England,"Experienced groundwater–related flooding in July 2012 (noted as the worst since 1955) and further flooding in November 2012 and December 2012 as groundwater levels remained high; the village remained on extended flood alert periods. [MUL] (p32)
Residents reported rumours that the below–ground Wessex Water reservoir at Long Bredy was 'let go' during the July 2012 flood, resulting in very large volumes of water entering the South Winterbourne; a large number of Wessex Water tankers were observed operating downstream of the watercourse. (p77)",,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc.html,GB-DOR_6_2012-07-01_etc,"Dorset flooding July 2012, Dorset flooding November 2012 and Dorset flooding December 2012",South Winterbourne Flood Investigation: Final,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/South Winterbourne Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-MIK,1,2018-05-27,52.0249064,-0.7344045,"<div id=""html_2b64af586dd870183bc7052fb059fb6d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Milton Keynes Hospital</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the flood event, indicators of flooding were observed in these areas during the site inspection. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The hospital was flooded during the event; indicators of surface&ndash;water flooding were observed around the hospital and along its access road. Flooding at the hospital has been reported on multiple previous occasions. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Milton Keynes,Milton Keynes Hospital,England,"Following the flood event, indicators of flooding were observed in these areas during the site inspection. (p21)
The hospital was flooded during the event; indicators of surface–water flooding were observed around the hospital and along its access road. Flooding at the hospital has been reported on multiple previous occasions. (p30)",,GB-MIK_1_2018-05-27.html,GB-MIK_1_2018-05-27,Milton Keynes Unitary Authority flooding 27 May 2018,FWMA Section 19 Report: Flood Event: May 2018,081/Milton Keynes/081/2018/Milton Keynes/Milton Keynes/10/18.pdf
GB-MIK,1,2018-05-27,52.0406502,-0.7594092,"<div id=""html_23a23c81696be0e466a6389733f0f4b8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Milton Keynes Unitary Authority</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported internal property flooding affected at least 315 houses, the Hospital and a number of business premises. Debris and leaf litter were observed on gully inlets, which may have restricted gully functioning. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall overwhelmed the drainage network, causing surface water to gravitate to local low points and resulting in internal flooding of residential properties. Evidence of sewer surcharging was observed, damaging the overlying pavement. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On the evening of 27 May 2018 significant rainfall across parts of Milton Keynes caused flooding across multiple areas, resulting in ingress to residential properties and commercial premises across the borough. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Numerous residential estates experienced internal and external flooding during the event; household items and carpets were left outside showing water damage, rack marks from floating debris and wicking on brickwork were observed during post&ndash;event site visits indicating internal inundation of properties. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 27 May 2018 an intense, short&ndash;duration convective storm tracked over central Milton Keynes producing surface&ndash;water/urban flooding. Runoff accumulated in local topographic depressions, with properties located in those depressions being inundated; Property Level Protection (PLP) was identified as potentially beneficial to reduce impacts. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall overwhelmed the drainage network causing surface water to gravitate to low points in the topography and enter properties; evidence of sewer surcharging was observed causing pavement damage. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant widespread flooding occurred across Milton Keynes, producing multiple incidents that met the thresholds for formal flood investigation and triggering site meetings, data collection (photos, video, eyewitness statements) and the production of Flood Investigation Reports to determine contributing factors and responsibilities. (p45)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>315 houses (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 315 residential properties (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 315 residential properties; 1 hospital [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Bletchley: 6 properties; Bradwell Common: 1 property; Cmk: 2 properties; Conniburrow: 1 property; Downs Barn: 2 properties; Eaglestone: 2 properties; Fishermead: 2 properties; Galley Hill: 1 property; Granby: 1 property; Great Linford: 4 properties; Heelands: 1 property; Kents Hill: 1 property; Lakes Estate: 11 properties; Leadenhall: 4 properties; Neath Hill: 16 properties; Peartree Bridge: 2 properties; Pennyland: 1 property; Springfield: 1 property; Stony Stratford: 1 property; Tinkers Bridge: 4 properties [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 315 houses [PAG] (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Milton Keynes,Milton Keynes Unitary Authority,England,"Reported internal property flooding affected at least 315 houses, the Hospital and a number of business premises. Debris and leaf litter were observed on gully inlets, which may have restricted gully functioning. (p9)
Heavy rainfall overwhelmed the drainage network, causing surface water to gravitate to local low points and resulting in internal flooding of residential properties. Evidence of sewer surcharging was observed, damaging the overlying pavement. (p10)
On the evening of 27 May 2018 significant rainfall across parts of Milton Keynes caused flooding across multiple areas, resulting in ingress to residential properties and commercial premises across the borough. [PAG] (p13)
Numerous residential estates experienced internal and external flooding during the event; household items and carpets were left outside showing water damage, rack marks from floating debris and wicking on brickwork were observed during post–event site visits indicating internal inundation of properties. [PAG] (p16)
On 27 May 2018 an intense, short–duration convective storm tracked over central Milton Keynes producing surface–water/urban flooding. Runoff accumulated in local topographic depressions, with properties located in those depressions being inundated; Property Level Protection (PLP) was identified as potentially beneficial to reduce impacts. (p35)
Heavy rainfall overwhelmed the drainage network causing surface water to gravitate to low points in the topography and enter properties; evidence of sewer surcharging was observed causing pavement damage. [PAG] (p40)
Significant widespread flooding occurred across Milton Keynes, producing multiple incidents that met the thresholds for formal flood investigation and triggering site meetings, data collection (photos, video, eyewitness statements) and the production of Flood Investigation Reports to determine contributing factors and responsibilities. (p45)","315 houses (p9)
over 315 residential properties (p10)
over 315 residential properties; 1 hospital [PAG] (p13)
