GB-MIK,1,2018-05-27,52.029148,-0.7665514,"<div id=""html_9a6c2580220dcc6cb97ec79047ea427e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cairngorm Gate, Milton Keynes Unitary Authority</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of a pipe burst where escaped water caused localised collapse and subsidence of a paved area; paving was displaced and substrate exposed. [IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The pipe burst undermined an area of interlocking paving, causing subsidence, displacement of paving blocks and localized structural damage requiring repair. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road gully inlets at Cairngorm Gate were observed to be covered by leaf litter, twigs and other detritus, which reduced gully capacity and may have impeded drainage. This partial or complete blockage is identified as a contributing factor to surface water / urban flooding during the flood event, potentially increasing runoff and the likelihood of nearby property flooding. [IMG] (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300 mm deep floodwater within retail units [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Milton Keynes,"Cairngorm Gate, Milton Keynes Unitary Authority",England,"Location of a pipe burst where escaped water caused localised collapse and subsidence of a paved area; paving was displaced and substrate exposed. [IMG] (p8)
The pipe burst undermined an area of interlocking paving, causing subsidence, displacement of paving blocks and localized structural damage requiring repair. (p33)
Road gully inlets at Cairngorm Gate were observed to be covered by leaf litter, twigs and other detritus, which reduced gully capacity and may have impeded drainage. This partial or complete blockage is identified as a contributing factor to surface water / urban flooding during the flood event, potentially increasing runoff and the likelihood of nearby property flooding. [IMG] (p34)","300 mm deep floodwater within retail units [IMG,PAG] (p33)",GB-MIK_1_2018-05-27.html,GB-MIK_1_2018-05-27,Milton Keynes Unitary Authority flooding 27 May 2018,FWMA Section 19 Report: Flood Event: May 2018,081/Milton Keynes/081/2018/Milton Keynes/Milton Keynes/10/18.pdf
GB-MIK,1,2018-05-27,52.0229677,-0.7436754,"<div id=""html_e2b20455a1d208d9becc2ef07614f6e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Daniels Welch, Coffee Hall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Indicators of surface water flooding were observed on these roads; Daniels/Welch in particular showed significant evidence of flooding affecting properties (e.g. carpets removed and placed in front gardens to dry/dispose). (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater entered front gardens and exceeded door thresholds on the left&ndash;hand side properties; carpets were removed and placed in front gardens to dry/dispose; water levels exceeded the damp&ndash;proof course and, given front&ndash;facing air bricks, internal water ingress into properties is likely. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding concentrated in the low ground at the front of residential properties on the low (eastern) side of the road. Observed evidence shows four distinct clusters of flooding along the street, with flooding on both sides in the northern cluster. An interim drainage channel was observed adjacent to house number 141. [IMG,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred within the low ground at the front of residential properties on the low side of the road, inundating front gardens/entrances and causing local surface water accumulation. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced an extreme short&ndash;duration rainfall event on 27 May 2018 that is considered very likely to have produced surface water flooding at/around the street (data point reported for the event). [TAB] (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>100 mm to 200 mm exceedance of door thresholds (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Addresses 121&ndash;141; 145&ndash;175; 213&ndash;233; four clusters [IMG,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Return Period 1 in 602 years [TAB] (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Milton Keynes,"Daniels Welch, Coffee Hall",England,"Indicators of surface water flooding were observed on these roads; Daniels/Welch in particular showed significant evidence of flooding affecting properties (e.g. carpets removed and placed in front gardens to dry/dispose). (p21)
Floodwater entered front gardens and exceeded door thresholds on the left–hand side properties; carpets were removed and placed in front gardens to dry/dispose; water levels exceeded the damp–proof course and, given front–facing air bricks, internal water ingress into properties is likely. (p22)
Surface–water flooding concentrated in the low ground at the front of residential properties on the low (eastern) side of the road. Observed evidence shows four distinct clusters of flooding along the street, with flooding on both sides in the northern cluster. An interim drainage channel was observed adjacent to house number 141. [IMG,PAG] (p24)
Flooding occurred within the low ground at the front of residential properties on the low side of the road, inundating front gardens/entrances and causing local surface water accumulation. (p25)
