GB-BKM,8,2013-12-01,51.59618453945747,-0.6284318784524542,"<div id=""html_3aabd8572ebbcee32b014079a98da745"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harrias House Residential Care Home</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Access to the care home was impeded because Hedgerley Lane was flooded by contaminated foul sewer water (a broken foul sewer and closed Pyebush Lane pumping station caused the foul sewer to surcharge). Staff required off&ndash;road transport to reach the building. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9&ndash;12 inches (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Harrias House Residential Care Home,England,Access to the care home was impeded because Hedgerley Lane was flooded by contaminated foul sewer water (a broken foul sewer and closed Pyebush Lane pumping station caused the foul sewer to surcharge). Staff required off–road transport to reach the building. (p11),9–12 inches (p11),GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01,Beaconsfield flooding December 2013 - January 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Hedgerley Lane, Beaconsfield December 2013 - January 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/hedgerley-lane-beaconsfield.pdf
GB-BKM,8,2013-12-01,51.5918982,-0.6290156,"<div id=""html_4a497ceb60b735ad5e6695f6ff6bd8bd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hedgerley Lane, Beaconsfield</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carriageway heavily waterlogged with multiple large puddles and standing water; potholes and muddy, uneven surface indicating drainage failure and impaired road usability. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames Water sewers surcharged (after Pyebush Lane pumping station was turned off and groundwater infiltrated pipes), discharging contaminated water from manholes onto Hedgerley Lane. The road became impassible to vehicles and on foot, creating environmental and public health risks and cutting off access to nearby properties including a care home and a nursery. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water inundation affected Hedgerley Lane (predicted to flood in a 1 in 30 year event); the January 2014 flood (beginning of January) was the worst on record at this location and is described as probably a combination of surface water flooding and foul sewer surcharge. Further floods occurred in February and March 2014. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The foul sewer surcharged and contaminated floodwater covered the road, making it impassable. Contractors tankered the water day and night until tankering stopped on 14 Jan 2014, after which contaminated water collected on the road. Thames Water cleared the floodwater on 16 Jan 2014 and completed road decontamination the following week. [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer on Hedgerley Lane surcharged (due to extreme/prolonged rainfall combined with the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station), flooding the lane with contaminated (sewage) water; the road was covered, access to cut&ndash;off properties was made almost impossible, and the event posed an environmental and public health risk. Report states properties were not flooded internally. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9&ndash;12 inches (flood depth) [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Hedgerley Lane, Beaconsfield",England,"Carriageway heavily waterlogged with multiple large puddles and standing water; potholes and muddy, uneven surface indicating drainage failure and impaired road usability. (p1)
Thames Water sewers surcharged (after Pyebush Lane pumping station was turned off and groundwater infiltrated pipes), discharging contaminated water from manholes onto Hedgerley Lane. The road became impassible to vehicles and on foot, creating environmental and public health risks and cutting off access to nearby properties including a care home and a nursery. (p4)
Surface water inundation affected Hedgerley Lane (predicted to flood in a 1 in 30 year event); the January 2014 flood (beginning of January) was the worst on record at this location and is described as probably a combination of surface water flooding and foul sewer surcharge. Further floods occurred in February and March 2014. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
The foul sewer surcharged and contaminated floodwater covered the road, making it impassable. Contractors tankered the water day and night until tankering stopped on 14 Jan 2014, after which contaminated water collected on the road. Thames Water cleared the floodwater on 16 Jan 2014 and completed road decontamination the following week. [PAG,MUL] (p11)
Foul sewer on Hedgerley Lane surcharged (due to extreme/prolonged rainfall combined with the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station), flooding the lane with contaminated (sewage) water; the road was covered, access to cut–off properties was made almost impossible, and the event posed an environmental and public health risk. Report states properties were not flooded internally. [TAB] (p16)","9–12 inches (flood depth) [PAG,MUL] (p11)",GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01,Beaconsfield flooding December 2013 - January 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Hedgerley Lane, Beaconsfield December 2013 - January 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/hedgerley-lane-beaconsfield.pdf
GB-BKM,8,2013-12-01,51.5977158,-0.6229312,"<div id=""html_56a636389e32d4b4a7bcf17f541f208f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pyebush Lane, Beaconsfield</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pyebush Lane pumping station was turned off for a period, which prevented normal removal of flows. This contributed to Thames Water pipes being overwhelmed (with groundwater infiltration noted), leading to surcharging out of manholes and contaminated floodwater on Hedgerley Lane that made the road impassable and posed environmental and public health risks, cutting off access to properties including a care home and a nursery. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pyebush Lane pumping station was closed (from 31 Dec 2013) because of the broken sewer and associated flows; tankers were used to manage flows at the pumping station but capacity was exceeded due to groundwater and surface water infiltration, contributing to sewer surcharge elsewhere. [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Pyebush Lane, Beaconsfield",England,"The Pyebush Lane pumping station was turned off for a period, which prevented normal removal of flows. This contributed to Thames Water pipes being overwhelmed (with groundwater infiltration noted), leading to surcharging out of manholes and contaminated floodwater on Hedgerley Lane that made the road impassable and posed environmental and public health risks, cutting off access to properties including a care home and a nursery. (p4)
The Pyebush Lane pumping station was closed (from 31 Dec 2013) because of the broken sewer and associated flows; tankers were used to manage flows at the pumping station but capacity was exceeded due to groundwater and surface water infiltration, contributing to sewer surcharge elsewhere. [PAG,MUL] (p11)",,GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01,Beaconsfield flooding December 2013 - January 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Hedgerley Lane, Beaconsfield December 2013 - January 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/hedgerley-lane-beaconsfield.pdf
GB-BKM,8,2013-12-01,51.6104378,-0.6524466,"<div id=""html_2b8d539b9cb5f3a019ce8d395b665118"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gregories Road, Beaconsfield</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A broken foul sewer was reported in Gregories Road during the flood event; this sewer failure contributed to downstream operational impacts (closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station). (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A broken foul sewer occurred in Gregories Road on 31 Dec 2013; this failure led to the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station and contributed to downstream sewer surcharge and flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A broken sewer in Gregories Road caused contaminated flood water which led to the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station and required tanker removal of contaminated flood water. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Gregories Road, Beaconsfield",England,"A broken foul sewer was reported in Gregories Road during the flood event; this sewer failure contributed to downstream operational impacts (closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station). (p10)
A broken foul sewer occurred in Gregories Road on 31 Dec 2013; this failure led to the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station and contributed to downstream sewer surcharge and flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p11)
A broken sewer in Gregories Road caused contaminated flood water which led to the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station and required tanker removal of contaminated flood water. (p12)",,GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01,Beaconsfield flooding December 2013 - January 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Hedgerley Lane, Beaconsfield December 2013 - January 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/hedgerley-lane-beaconsfield.pdf
GB-BKM,8,2013-12-01,51.6082881,-0.6571233,"<div id=""html_0405ec7e8f5e0244dd12f9d188389152"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Beaconsfield</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected a road in Beaconsfield (Hedgerley Lane), resulting in standing water and waterlogged road conditions. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on Hedgerley Lane within Beaconsfield caused by pumping station closure and groundwater infiltration led to surcharging of sewers and contaminated discharge onto the road, making it impassible, creating environmental and public health risks, and isolating properties (including a care home and nursery). (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local area (including Hedgerley Lane) experienced surface water flooding combined with foul sewer surcharge; the early January 2014 event was described as the worst on record and led to repeated flooding at the location afterwards. [PAG,IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within Beaconsfield a broken foul sewer and the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station led to contaminated sewer floodwater covering Hedgerley Lane and making routes impassable. Local impacts included assistance required to access Harrias House care home (4 Jan 2014) and the closure of a nursery on 16 Jan 2014 to avoid children walking through contaminated water. Road decontamination was completed the week after 16 Jan 2014. [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contaminated flood water in the area led South Bucks District Council environmental health to close a nursery on 16 January 2014 to prevent children walking through contaminated water and bringing it into buildings; SBDC staff were on site throughout the flooding to provide advice. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9&ndash;12 inches (flood depth) [MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Beaconsfield,England,"Surface water flooding affected a road in Beaconsfield (Hedgerley Lane), resulting in standing water and waterlogged road conditions. (p1)
Flooding on Hedgerley Lane within Beaconsfield caused by pumping station closure and groundwater infiltration led to surcharging of sewers and contaminated discharge onto the road, making it impassible, creating environmental and public health risks, and isolating properties (including a care home and nursery). (p4)
Local area (including Hedgerley Lane) experienced surface water flooding combined with foul sewer surcharge; the early January 2014 event was described as the worst on record and led to repeated flooding at the location afterwards. [PAG,IMG] (p9)
Within Beaconsfield a broken foul sewer and the closure of the Pyebush Lane pumping station led to contaminated sewer floodwater covering Hedgerley Lane and making routes impassable. Local impacts included assistance required to access Harrias House care home (4 Jan 2014) and the closure of a nursery on 16 Jan 2014 to avoid children walking through contaminated water. Road decontamination was completed the week after 16 Jan 2014. [MUL] (p11)
Contaminated flood water in the area led South Bucks District Council environmental health to close a nursery on 16 January 2014 to prevent children walking through contaminated water and bringing it into buildings; SBDC staff were on site throughout the flooding to provide advice. (p12)",9–12 inches (flood depth) [MUL] (p11),GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_8_2013-12-01,Beaconsfield flooding December 2013 - January 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Hedgerley Lane, Beaconsfield December 2013 - January 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/hedgerley-lane-beaconsfield.pdf
GB-KEN,2,2012-06-03,51.14879944651296,0.2789621194323351,"<div id=""html_39cd30faebe78297a68f3020f0a2573f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Taylor Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the adjacent open channel caused flooding into Taylor Court, resulting in damage to ground&ndash;floor properties. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood water depth approximately 0.6 m (reported for adjacent car park); no precise count of properties given [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Taylor Court,England,"Overtopping of the adjacent open channel caused flooding into Taylor Court, resulting in damage to ground–floor properties. [TAB] (p2)",flood water depth approximately 0.6 m (reported for adjacent car park); no precise count of properties given [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03.html,GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03,Tunbridge Wells flooding 03 Jun 2012,Flood Investigation Report: Brook Road – High Brooms,"063/Kent/Brook-Road,-Tunbridge-Wells-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf"
GB-KEN,2,2012-06-03,51.1488894,0.2796412,"<div id=""html_af9dded3c9ef74b4d44d0260586146f5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oak Road, High Brooms</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood from a section of open channel of a watercourse next to a private car park near Oak Road damaged properties and vehicles. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At Oak Road the open channel section (approx. 30 m) and the upstream culvert inlet are part of the flooded watercourse. A trash screen at the culvert inlet was observed clear in one photo and heavily obstructed by large debris (wood, metal, rubbish) in another &mdash; the debris was lodged against the screen and blocking flow into the culvert, reducing drainage capacity and contributing to local hydraulic obstruction. [IMG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An ordinary watercourse (open channel and culvert opening with a trash screen) at Oak Road was implicated in flooding. The June 2012 incident prompted remedial actions to reduce future flood risk, including debris removal, replacement/improvement of the trash screen, erection of 'No Flytipping' signs and regular inspections/maintenance. [TAB,MUL] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 trash screen obstructed; ~30 m open channel; ~1 km of downstream culvert [IMG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two similar flood incidents; one incident resulted in internal damage to properties [TAB,MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Oak Road, High Brooms",England,"A flood from a section of open channel of a watercourse next to a private car park near Oak Road damaged properties and vehicles. (p1)
At Oak Road the open channel section (approx. 30 m) and the upstream culvert inlet are part of the flooded watercourse. A trash screen at the culvert inlet was observed clear in one photo and heavily obstructed by large debris (wood, metal, rubbish) in another — the debris was lodged against the screen and blocking flow into the culvert, reducing drainage capacity and contributing to local hydraulic obstruction. [IMG] (p3)
An ordinary watercourse (open channel and culvert opening with a trash screen) at Oak Road was implicated in flooding. The June 2012 incident prompted remedial actions to reduce future flood risk, including debris removal, replacement/improvement of the trash screen, erection of 'No Flytipping' signs and regular inspections/maintenance. [TAB,MUL] (p4)","three properties (p1)
1 trash screen obstructed; ~30 m open channel; ~1 km of downstream culvert [IMG] (p3)
two similar flood incidents; one incident resulted in internal damage to properties [TAB,MUL] (p4)",GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03.html,GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03,Tunbridge Wells flooding 03 Jun 2012,Flood Investigation Report: Brook Road – High Brooms,"063/Kent/Brook-Road,-Tunbridge-Wells-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf"
GB-KEN,2,2012-06-03,51.1481526,0.2805555,"<div id=""html_64c38e2bdbb409dfc6a2ec237216d5ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brook Road, High Brooms</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The residents' car parking area accessed via Brook Road was inundated when the open channel overtopped its banks, causing damage to vehicles. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood water depth in car parking area approximately 0.6 m [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Brook Road, High Brooms",England,"The residents' car parking area accessed via Brook Road was inundated when the open channel overtopped its banks, causing damage to vehicles. [TAB] (p2)",flood water depth in car parking area approximately 0.6 m [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03.html,GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03,Tunbridge Wells flooding 03 Jun 2012,Flood Investigation Report: Brook Road – High Brooms,"063/Kent/Brook-Road,-Tunbridge-Wells-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf"
GB-KEN,2,2012-06-03,51.14958039738272,0.2733030786084968,"<div id=""html_62bb8155cc2d927b26222f749f96cdf6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Brooms</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from an open channel of a watercourse next to a private car park in the High Brooms area caused damage to properties and vehicles. (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,High Brooms,England,Flooding from an open channel of a watercourse next to a private car park in the High Brooms area caused damage to properties and vehicles. (p1),three properties (p1),GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03.html,GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03,Tunbridge Wells flooding 03 Jun 2012,Flood Investigation Report: Brook Road – High Brooms,"063/Kent/Brook-Road,-Tunbridge-Wells-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf"
GB-KEN,2,2012-06-03,51.1371483,0.2673446,"<div id=""html_b1012128d7be37697b16aebe9291cbbf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tunbridge Wells</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood event in Tunbridge Wells (High Brooms area) on 3 June 2012, caused by an open channel of a watercourse next to a private car park, resulted in damage to properties and vehicles within the borough. (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Tunbridge Wells,England,"A flood event in Tunbridge Wells (High Brooms area) on 3 June 2012, caused by an open channel of a watercourse next to a private car park, resulted in damage to properties and vehicles within the borough. (p1)",three properties (p1),GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03.html,GB-KEN_2_2012-06-03,Tunbridge Wells flooding 03 Jun 2012,Flood Investigation Report: Brook Road – High Brooms,"063/Kent/Brook-Road,-Tunbridge-Wells-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf"
GB-LAN,21,2021-07-01,53.7409458,-2.7049799,"<div id=""html_be10411a8ab9869f9ddfdfbd2c0554e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Factory Lane, Penwortham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lancashire Factory Lane was flooded during the July 2021 event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Three separate floodings between 3 July and 12 July 2021 disrupted access along the lane for residents and businesses, encroached into drives and garden areas, and caused repeated damage to one particular home. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with defects in the local culverted watercourse occurred downstream of Factory Lane; investigations link these defects to reported flooding that affected a home (on 3, 10 and 12 July 2021) and other nearby premises. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Factory Lane associated with the organised, heavy and persistent rain on 3 July 2021; the street area was highlighted as a key flooding location in the investigation map (localised inundation from intense rainfall and/or overflow from the nearby watercourse). [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked gullies caused flooding of the highway and adjacent private property. The Council's local Highways team attended to clear gullies between 3 and 12 July 2021, but flooding recurred in further heavy rain. Subsequent investigation identified defects in a downstream carrier drain / culverted watercourse; tree roots and trapped silt formed a significant obstruction that prevented flows draining away in heavy rainfall. [PAG,MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following clearance of an upstream blockage in May 2021, the drainage systems around Factory Lane became inundated during heavy rainfall in early July 2021. A shallow&ndash;gradient culverted watercourse had trapped silt and tree roots, creating a significant obstruction to flows; the culvert was subsequently cleared/repaired. Separate new reports of flooding at a different location on Factory Lane were made in May 2023 and September 2023 (under investigation). [MUL] (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three flooding events; one home (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 home [PAG] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,"Factory Lane, Penwortham",England,"Lancashire Factory Lane was flooded during the July 2021 event. (p1)
Three separate floodings between 3 July and 12 July 2021 disrupted access along the lane for residents and businesses, encroached into drives and garden areas, and caused repeated damage to one particular home. (p3)
Flooding associated with defects in the local culverted watercourse occurred downstream of Factory Lane; investigations link these defects to reported flooding that affected a home (on 3, 10 and 12 July 2021) and other nearby premises. [PAG] (p4)
Flooding occurred on Factory Lane associated with the organised, heavy and persistent rain on 3 July 2021; the street area was highlighted as a key flooding location in the investigation map (localised inundation from intense rainfall and/or overflow from the nearby watercourse). [IMG,PAG] (p5)
Blocked gullies caused flooding of the highway and adjacent private property. The Council's local Highways team attended to clear gullies between 3 and 12 July 2021, but flooding recurred in further heavy rain. Subsequent investigation identified defects in a downstream carrier drain / culverted watercourse; tree roots and trapped silt formed a significant obstruction that prevented flows draining away in heavy rainfall. [PAG,MUL] (p6)
Following clearance of an upstream blockage in May 2021, the drainage systems around Factory Lane became inundated during heavy rainfall in early July 2021. A shallow–gradient culverted watercourse had trapped silt and tree roots, creating a significant obstruction to flows; the culvert was subsequently cleared/repaired. Separate new reports of flooding at a different location on Factory Lane were made in May 2023 and September 2023 (under investigation). [MUL] (p7)","three flooding events; one home (p3)
1 home [PAG] (p4)",GB-LAN_21_2021-07-01.html,GB-LAN_21_2021-07-01,Penwortham flooding 01 Jul 2021 - 31 Jul 2021,"July 2021 Flooding In Lancashire Factory Lane, Penwortham, South Ribble",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-factory-lane-penwortham-july-2021.pdf
GB-LAN,21,2021-07-01,53.7271527,-2.7330066,"<div id=""html_333a933edab35af9439302acc91018b7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>South Ribble</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>South Ribble (the local authority area) was the administrative area where the July 2021 flooding occurred. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,South Ribble,England,South Ribble (the local authority area) was the administrative area where the July 2021 flooding occurred. (p1),,GB-LAN_21_2021-07-01.html,GB-LAN_21_2021-07-01,Penwortham flooding 01 Jul 2021 - 31 Jul 2021,"July 2021 Flooding In Lancashire Factory Lane, Penwortham, South Ribble",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-factory-lane-penwortham-july-2021.pdf
GB-LAN,21,2021-07-01,53.74143315,-2.7292367175458985,"<div id=""html_bc8f7aa295cf4f14f07f11d793dd5ba1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Penwortham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Penwortham experienced flooding during the July 2021 event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An historic culverted watercourse in Penwortham had fallen into disrepair, preventing proper drainage of the watercourse and connected highway surface&ndash;water drainage. This contributed to inundation of local drainage systems in heavy rainfall in early July 2021. The culvert was later cleared/repaired and no repeat of the flooding at that location has been reported since those works. (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Penwortham,England,"Penwortham experienced flooding during the July 2021 event. (p1)
An historic culverted watercourse in Penwortham had fallen into disrepair, preventing proper drainage of the watercourse and connected highway surface–water drainage. This contributed to inundation of local drainage systems in heavy rainfall in early July 2021. The culvert was later cleared/repaired and no repeat of the flooding at that location has been reported since those works. (p7)",,GB-LAN_21_2021-07-01.html,GB-LAN_21_2021-07-01,Penwortham flooding 01 Jul 2021 - 31 Jul 2021,"July 2021 Flooding In Lancashire Factory Lane, Penwortham, South Ribble",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-factory-lane-penwortham-july-2021.pdf
GB-NNH,8,2016-03-09,52.2983377,-0.7997433,"<div id=""html_780c000aa06bce1c08b91b06124f47d7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stonelea Road, Sywell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment, together with insufficient or absent drainage, caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours to low points around private property. This resulted in internal flooding of a property on Stonelea Road; internal flooding of one property has been experienced on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property (water entered the building). The incident on 9th March 2016 caused internal flooding of one property; the same property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the previous five years. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area is identified on NCC Flood Toolkit as being within an area of varying surface water flood risk; the street (residential area including later housing on Palmer Grove) was within the scope of the local flood investigation relating to the 9th March 2016 event. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from agricultural fields to the north flowed under the rear garden fence across the rear garden into the house and garage, causing internal flooding of a property. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from agricultural fields flowed under the rear garden fence across the garden towards the house and garage, ponded at the rear of the building and entered air bricks. Water flowed under the ground floor (audible from inside), rose over the rear door threshold causing damage to the door frame and flooring, and caused internal flooding of the garage. Floodwaters later receded by continuing to flow into the airbricks. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding associated with inadequate or ineffective surface water drainage from recent developments affected properties on Stonelea Road, creating flood risk to those properties. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland surface water flow converged from the west and southwest toward a central low point adjacent to Stonelea Road, producing a schematic flood area on the map. Historic ditches and possible outlet pipes connected to the area but were indicated as insufficient during the incident, contributing to localized inundation at/near Stonelea Road. [IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater pooled and flowed along the side and rear of a residential property, entering the garden and reaching the house threshold; water encroached onto the patio and interior threshold, outdoor furniture was submerged and a submersible pump was deployed to remove water. [IMG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Field at the rear of Stonelea Road experienced waterlogging and soil compaction that damaged crop growth; photographed areas show large bare or thinly vegetated patches consistent with localized pooling/runoff adjacent to residential properties. [IMG,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear garden of an affected residential property was saturated; air brick located just below the door threshold and a linear drainage channel present directly in front of the rear door on the patio, indicating water accumulation at the base of the property. [IMG,PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear entrance/patio area of a residential property on Stonelea Road showed water staining and evidence of surface water affecting external thresholds and drainage features (air brick and drain noted at rear of the affected property). [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Saturated ground at the rear of a garden of an affected property on Stonelea Road, indicating soil saturation and shallow inundation of garden area. [IMG,PAG] (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property [IMG] (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Stonelea Road, Sywell",England,"Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment, together with insufficient or absent drainage, caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours to low points around private property. This resulted in internal flooding of a property on Stonelea Road; internal flooding of one property has been experienced on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p7)
Internal flooding of a property (water entered the building). The incident on 9th March 2016 caused internal flooding of one property; the same property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the previous five years. (p9)
The area is identified on NCC Flood Toolkit as being within an area of varying surface water flood risk; the street (residential area including later housing on Palmer Grove) was within the scope of the local flood investigation relating to the 9th March 2016 event. (p10)
Surface water runoff from agricultural fields to the north flowed under the rear garden fence across the rear garden into the house and garage, causing internal flooding of a property. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p12)
Surface water from agricultural fields flowed under the rear garden fence across the garden towards the house and garage, ponded at the rear of the building and entered air bricks. Water flowed under the ground floor (audible from inside), rose over the rear door threshold causing damage to the door frame and flooring, and caused internal flooding of the garage. Floodwaters later receded by continuing to flow into the airbricks. (p13)
Surface water flooding associated with inadequate or ineffective surface water drainage from recent developments affected properties on Stonelea Road, creating flood risk to those properties. (p22)
Overland surface water flow converged from the west and southwest toward a central low point adjacent to Stonelea Road, producing a schematic flood area on the map. Historic ditches and possible outlet pipes connected to the area but were indicated as insufficient during the incident, contributing to localized inundation at/near Stonelea Road. [IMG] (p30)
Floodwater pooled and flowed along the side and rear of a residential property, entering the garden and reaching the house threshold; water encroached onto the patio and interior threshold, outdoor furniture was submerged and a submersible pump was deployed to remove water. [IMG] (p38)
Field at the rear of Stonelea Road experienced waterlogging and soil compaction that damaged crop growth; photographed areas show large bare or thinly vegetated patches consistent with localized pooling/runoff adjacent to residential properties. [IMG,PAG] (p46)
Rear garden of an affected residential property was saturated; air brick located just below the door threshold and a linear drainage channel present directly in front of the rear door on the patio, indicating water accumulation at the base of the property. [IMG,PAG] (p48)
Rear entrance/patio area of a residential property on Stonelea Road showed water staining and evidence of surface water affecting external thresholds and drainage features (air brick and drain noted at rear of the affected property). [IMG,PAG] (p49)
Saturated ground at the rear of a garden of an affected property on Stonelea Road, indicating soil saturation and shallow inundation of garden area. [IMG,PAG] (p50)","1 property (p7)
one property (p9)
one property [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p12)
1 residential property [IMG] (p38)",GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09,Sywell flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Stonelea Road, Sywell, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22711/FIR-Sywell/Rev03/141116.pdf
GB-NNH,8,2016-03-09,52.298039,-0.8009112,"<div id=""html_fb239a59e8a4e9697d6f93af55f38ffc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Westlea Road, Sywell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area is identified on NCC Flood Toolkit as being within an area of varying surface water flood risk; Westlea Road was included in the local assessment and investigation following the flooding incident on 9th March 2016. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from agricultural fields caused flood water to affect rear gardens and garages on Westlea Road; three properties had gardens and garages affected in November 2012, and a garage of a property flooded on 09/03/2016. [TAB,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fields to the rear of Westlea Road were waterlogged with standing water and muddy, waterlogged tracks; this surface water was adjacent to and influenced the boundary with nearby residential properties. [IMG,PAG] (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties; one garage [TAB,MUL] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Westlea Road, Sywell",England,"The area is identified on NCC Flood Toolkit as being within an area of varying surface water flood risk; Westlea Road was included in the local assessment and investigation following the flooding incident on 9th March 2016. (p10)
Runoff from agricultural fields caused flood water to affect rear gardens and garages on Westlea Road; three properties had gardens and garages affected in November 2012, and a garage of a property flooded on 09/03/2016. [TAB,MUL] (p12)
Fields to the rear of Westlea Road were waterlogged with standing water and muddy, waterlogged tracks; this surface water was adjacent to and influenced the boundary with nearby residential properties. [IMG,PAG] (p39)","three properties; one garage [TAB,MUL] (p12)",GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09,Sywell flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Stonelea Road, Sywell, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22711/FIR-Sywell/Rev03/141116.pdf
GB-NNH,8,2016-03-09,52.29915753104671,-0.7996622535178609,"<div id=""html_b3fa1993f30cfd56f04349996eed2807"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Palmer Grove, Sywell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding associated with inadequate or ineffective surface water drainage from recent developments affected properties on Palmer Grove, creating flood risk to those properties. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Palmer Grove, Sywell",England,"Surface water flooding associated with inadequate or ineffective surface water drainage from recent developments affected properties on Palmer Grove, creating flood risk to those properties. (p22)",,GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09,Sywell flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Stonelea Road, Sywell, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22711/FIR-Sywell/Rev03/141116.pdf
GB-NNH,8,2016-03-09,52.3047815,-0.806767,"<div id=""html_eec3b478e2d8142630d8121f2a475493"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holcot Lane, Sywell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A piped watercourse on Holcot Lane is shown connecting to the schematic flood area and formed part of the flow network during the incident; pipe/culvert sections and outlet routes from Holcot Lane are implicated in conveying water toward the flooded area and may have been overwhelmed or contributed to inundation. [IMG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Holcot Lane, Sywell",England,A piped watercourse on Holcot Lane is shown connecting to the schematic flood area and formed part of the flow network during the incident; pipe/culvert sections and outlet routes from Holcot Lane are implicated in conveying water toward the flooded area and may have been overwhelmed or contributed to inundation. [IMG] (p30),,GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09,Sywell flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Stonelea Road, Sywell, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22711/FIR-Sywell/Rev03/141116.pdf
GB-NNH,8,2016-03-09,52.2971155,-0.7982256,"<div id=""html_4be3b7a598b83249ea0de4ff309622ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sywell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At Stonelea Road in Sywell, heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment with insufficient drainage led to excess surface water flowing to low points around private property and caused internal flooding of at least one property in the village. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents in the village were affected by the flooding incident on 9th March 2016; affected residents and a parish representative were interviewed as part of a Flood Incident Investigation on 14th June 2016. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow overland from agricultural fields to low points around private properties in the village. Several properties backing onto the fields experienced garden and garage flooding, including the affected property on Stonelea Road. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The investigation map locates a schematic flood area within Sywell where multiple overland flow routes and historic drainage features converged, indicating localized surface water flooding in the village (central low point near Stonelea Road) during the incident. [IMG] (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Sywell,England,"At Stonelea Road in Sywell, heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment with insufficient drainage led to excess surface water flowing to low points around private property and caused internal flooding of at least one property in the village. (p7)
Residents in the village were affected by the flooding incident on 9th March 2016; affected residents and a parish representative were interviewed as part of a Flood Incident Investigation on 14th June 2016. [PAG] (p10)
Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow overland from agricultural fields to low points around private properties in the village. Several properties backing onto the fields experienced garden and garage flooding, including the affected property on Stonelea Road. [PAG] (p13)
The investigation map locates a schematic flood area within Sywell where multiple overland flow routes and historic drainage features converged, indicating localized surface water flooding in the village (central low point near Stonelea Road) during the incident. [IMG] (p30)",1 property (p7),GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_8_2016-03-09,Sywell flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Stonelea Road, Sywell, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22711/FIR-Sywell/Rev03/141116.pdf
GB-DUD,1,2016-06-08,52.51108,-2.0816813,"<div id=""html_3f2f4c77526550592f85e4ff853765fe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dudley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding occurred across parts of Dudley borough due to extreme rainfall, causing surface water to inundate streets and residential areas and generating large numbers of support requests to the Council; emergency response measures (road closures/vehicle blockades) were used to protect public safety. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dudley experienced exceptionally intense rainfall (notably on 16 June 2016) that overloaded the local drainage network and caused significant flooding across the town on 8 and 16 June 2016; impacts described as of a magnitude unseen in recent times. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dudley Council activated its Pre Flood Action Plan in response to ongoing poor weather and an AMBER forecast for 14 June indicating a medium risk of significant localised surface water flooding; the regional 16 June rainfall map also shows Dudley received moderate to high 24&ndash;hour rainfall accumulations, consistent with conditions that produced localised surface water/urban flooding during the June events. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas across the borough experienced surface water flooding during the incident (scattered but far reaching). Flooding incidents included occurrences at individual properties and clusters of properties; investigations found a concentration of incidents in the Brierley Hill area. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Unprecedented intense rainfall on 16 June 2016 caused widespread flooding across the borough. Local drainage and sewer networks were overwhelmed, producing widespread surface&ndash;water/urban flooding, some river/watercourse incidents and internal property flooding. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 300 requests for support; in the order of 75 internal flooding incidents of residential properties [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 300 requests for support; 75 internal residential properties flooded [PAG] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dudley,Dudley,England,"Widespread flooding occurred across parts of Dudley borough due to extreme rainfall, causing surface water to inundate streets and residential areas and generating large numbers of support requests to the Council; emergency response measures (road closures/vehicle blockades) were used to protect public safety. [IMG] (p1)
Dudley experienced exceptionally intense rainfall (notably on 16 June 2016) that overloaded the local drainage network and caused significant flooding across the town on 8 and 16 June 2016; impacts described as of a magnitude unseen in recent times. (p4)
Dudley Council activated its Pre Flood Action Plan in response to ongoing poor weather and an AMBER forecast for 14 June indicating a medium risk of significant localised surface water flooding; the regional 16 June rainfall map also shows Dudley received moderate to high 24–hour rainfall accumulations, consistent with conditions that produced localised surface water/urban flooding during the June events. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
Areas across the borough experienced surface water flooding during the incident (scattered but far reaching). Flooding incidents included occurrences at individual properties and clusters of properties; investigations found a concentration of incidents in the Brierley Hill area. (p13)
Unprecedented intense rainfall on 16 June 2016 caused widespread flooding across the borough. Local drainage and sewer networks were overwhelmed, producing widespread surface–water/urban flooding, some river/watercourse incidents and internal property flooding. [PAG] (p16)","more than 300 requests for support; in the order of 75 internal flooding incidents of residential properties [IMG] (p1)
