GB-KHL,2,2014-08-10,53.7435722,-0.3394758,"<div id=""html_2601f462dc7d883daa5aa0cbb78097a8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kingston Upon Hull</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Urban streets were inundated with standing water after heavy rain; vehicles were forced to drive through flooded roads, producing large splashes and indicating transport disruption and hazards for road users and pedestrians. [PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread highway flooding across the city; two properties flooded during the intense storms; many areas flooded before the receiving drainage infrastructure could convey flows. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As Lead Local Flood Authority, Hull City Council received advance warnings for the 10 August 2014 surface&ndash;water event and carried out pre&ndash;event actions: targeting identified problem gullies and hotspots for gully&ndash;clearing and checking/cleaning council&ndash;owned trash screens on ordinary watercourses to reduce the risk of gully blockage. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water (pluvial) flooding across the city on 10th August 2014 caused by intense, localised rainfall and high winds. Multiple areas experienced flooding; residents undertook self&ndash;help measures while others looked to the council for response. The event highlighted lack of warning for this flood source. [TAB,PAG] (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Kingston Upon Hull,England,"Urban streets were inundated with standing water after heavy rain; vehicles were forced to drive through flooded roads, producing large splashes and indicating transport disruption and hazards for road users and pedestrians. [PAG] (p1)
Widespread highway flooding across the city; two properties flooded during the intense storms; many areas flooded before the receiving drainage infrastructure could convey flows. (p4)
As Lead Local Flood Authority, Hull City Council received advance warnings for the 10 August 2014 surface–water event and carried out pre–event actions: targeting identified problem gullies and hotspots for gully–clearing and checking/cleaning council–owned trash screens on ordinary watercourses to reduce the risk of gully blockage. [PAG] (p24)
Surface water (pluvial) flooding across the city on 10th August 2014 caused by intense, localised rainfall and high winds. Multiple areas experienced flooding; residents undertook self–help measures while others looked to the council for response. The event highlighted lack of warning for this flood source. [TAB,PAG] (p27)",2 properties (p4),GB-KHL_2_2014-08-10.html,GB-KHL_2_2014-08-10,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Aug 2014,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Heavy Rain August 2014,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/HeavyRainAugust2014.pdf"
