GB-CAM,5,2014-08-01,52.30094024852415,-0.3900466266328446,"<div id=""html_b9248bc0ded46cebcad97b24f67acd5d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kimbolton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The reported flood incident occurred within Kimbolton (section of Thrapston Road), indicating a localised flooding event in the town; no further town&ndash;wide impacts are described. [IMG] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Kimbolton,England,"The reported flood incident occurred within Kimbolton (section of Thrapston Road), indicating a localised flooding event in the town; no further town–wide impacts are described. [IMG] (p2)",,GB-CAM_5_2014-08-01.html,GB-CAM_5_2014-08-01,Kimbolton flooding August 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Kimbolton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Flood/Investigation/Report//Kimbolton.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4106161,-0.2221013,"<div id=""html_4df461e662af4b6c491e3db0d980595a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dundonald Church</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included within the Abbott Avenue / Cottenham Park area affected by flooding caused by foul and surface water sewer network issues; the location was identified for review and incorporation into drainage modelling and Thames Water asset records. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location (Abbott Avenue including Dundonald Church) experienced sewer flooding that met the Section 19 investigation threshold. Flooding occurred during the June 2016 and July 2017 events (recorded sewer return periods 1 in 24 yr for June 2016 and 1 in 3 yr for July 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding occurred in June 2016 caused by a surcharging manhole during the event. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded episodes of intense short&ndash;duration rainfall on 23 June 2016 led to localised surface water flooding affecting the church grounds and access routes. [TAB] (p88)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Dundonald Church,England,"Included within the Abbott Avenue / Cottenham Park area affected by flooding caused by foul and surface water sewer network issues; the location was identified for review and incorporation into drainage modelling and Thames Water asset records. (p4)
Location (Abbott Avenue including Dundonald Church) experienced sewer flooding that met the Section 19 investigation threshold. Flooding occurred during the June 2016 and July 2017 events (recorded sewer return periods 1 in 24 yr for June 2016 and 1 in 3 yr for July 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Internal flooding occurred in June 2016 caused by a surcharging manhole during the event. (p42)
Recorded episodes of intense short–duration rainfall on 23 June 2016 led to localised surface water flooding affecting the church grounds and access routes. [TAB] (p88)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.15344,-0.181805,"<div id=""html_e5c635e38c8ce4bd8f436c94d20f7f02"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gatwick</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy showers on 29&ndash;30 July 2017 led to delayed flights at Gatwick Airport due to flooding&ndash;related disruption to operations. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Gatwick,England,Heavy showers on 29–30 July 2017 led to delayed flights at Gatwick Airport due to flooding–related disruption to operations. (p16),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4382776,-0.2066459,"<div id=""html_655a45141c1f49335dcbdb66aaf063ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wimbledon Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Wimbledon Park ward experienced flooding in June 2016 affecting Mount Road, Acuba Road and Brooklands Avenue, as recorded on TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Wimbledon Park ward location experienced a surface water/sewer flooding incident on 23 June 2016 that lasted about 12 hours and caused internal flooding (including basement flooding) and reports of foul sewage on the road. (p65)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than two properties (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Wimbledon Park,England,"The Wimbledon Park ward experienced flooding in June 2016 affecting Mount Road, Acuba Road and Brooklands Avenue, as recorded on TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)
The Wimbledon Park ward location experienced a surface water/sewer flooding incident on 23 June 2016 that lasted about 12 hours and caused internal flooding (including basement flooding) and reports of foul sewage on the road. (p65)",more than two properties (p65),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4038921,-0.2181221,"<div id=""html_9b7de456c176dc561d8b7c325cc5de0e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Joseph Hood Recreation Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from Joseph Hood Recreation Ground slopes down to Whatley Avenue and increased flows into the local drainage network, contributing to the sewer surcharge and flooding at the junction with Martin Way. (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Joseph Hood Recreation Ground,England,"Runoff from Joseph Hood Recreation Ground slopes down to Whatley Avenue and increased flows into the local drainage network, contributing to the sewer surcharge and flooding at the junction with Martin Way. (p27)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4007176,-0.2213205,"<div id=""html_a650a119ca3ec11ca5496f78e948217e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cannon Hill Common</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface runoff from Cannon Hill Common slopes down towards Whatley Avenue, contributing additional flow into the local drainage network and exacerbating the sewer surcharge and flooding at the Martin Way/Whatley Avenue junction. (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Cannon Hill Common,England,"Surface runoff from Cannon Hill Common slopes down towards Whatley Avenue, contributing additional flow into the local drainage network and exacerbating the sewer surcharge and flooding at the Martin Way/Whatley Avenue junction. (p27)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.39991886030812,-0.21253423147239492,"<div id=""html_50b20c718a5dd136376a0399e9b2b632"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cannon Hill Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Cannon Hill ward contained locations (Whatley Avenue and Martin Way) that flooded during the June 2016 event. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 flooded location (Whatley Avenue and Martin Way listed as Flooding Location 1) [TAB] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Cannon Hill Ward,England,The Cannon Hill ward contained locations (Whatley Avenue and Martin Way) that flooded during the June 2016 event. [TAB] (p21),1 flooded location (Whatley Avenue and Martin Way listed as Flooding Location 1) [TAB] (p21),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.41498080896432,-0.19117607564863762,"<div id=""html_b3e4ee4c67c3680c9117597a783464e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Abbey Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Abbey ward experienced flooding (notably at Merantun Way and Morden Road) during the reported storm events. [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Merantun Way and Morden Road, located in Abbey ward, experienced repeated surface water flooding leading to closure of this critical highway within the ward. [MUL] (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three occasions (one in 2016 and two in 2017); 300 to 600 mm and 600 to 1000 mm depth ranges as recorded at the junction [MUL] (p70)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Abbey Ward,England,"The Abbey ward experienced flooding (notably at Merantun Way and Morden Road) during the reported storm events. [TAB,MUL] (p21)
The junction of Merantun Way and Morden Road, located in Abbey ward, experienced repeated surface water flooding leading to closure of this critical highway within the ward. [MUL] (p70)",three occasions (one in 2016 and two in 2017); 300 to 600 mm and 600 to 1000 mm depth ranges as recorded at the junction [MUL] (p70),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.424159566202334,-0.194406632473065,"<div id=""html_33e11b96521f222de91d6576218dc69b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Trinity Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ward experienced flooding at Effra Road on 23 June 2016 (recorded sewer return period 1 in 21 years). [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contains Effra Road where the June 2016 surface water / sewer flooding event occurred; the incident reached the council's Section 19 investigation threshold and caused internal flooding to residential properties within the ward. (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two residential properties (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Trinity Ward,England,"The ward experienced flooding at Effra Road on 23 June 2016 (recorded sewer return period 1 in 21 years). [TAB] (p21)
Contains Effra Road where the June 2016 surface water / sewer flooding event occurred; the incident reached the council's Section 19 investigation threshold and caused internal flooding to residential properties within the ward. (p55)",two residential properties (p55),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.41499274173446,-0.21041370263625875,"<div id=""html_82b8a42e379183a645fdd410de397dda"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dundonald Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ward contains Abbott Avenue and a significant pumping station; foul sewers surcharged in the ward during the June 2016 and July 2017 events, producing internal flooding at low points. [MUL] (p42)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 incidents reached Section 19 threshold [MUL] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Dundonald Ward,England,"The ward contains Abbott Avenue and a significant pumping station; foul sewers surcharged in the ward during the June 2016 and July 2017 events, producing internal flooding at low points. [MUL] (p42)",2 incidents reached Section 19 threshold [MUL] (p42),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4037983,-0.2433798,"<div id=""html_93e3ff98955f6e36cdbd024ca3e1e886"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pyl Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted Pyl Brook runs behind properties on West Barnes Lane (and behind properties on the Westway) and received/transported surface water runoff during the incidents. The culvert and associated surface water sewer connections formed part of the flow path that contributed to flooding of rear gardens and properties on the eastern side of West Barnes Lane and adjacent private access land. [PAG,MUL] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted Pyl Brook (650 m culverted length in this location) runs behind properties and is the local low point; limited capacity/flow within the culvert and connections from surface water sewers led to ponding and inundation of adjacent properties and private land during the 2016 and 2017 incidents. Flows progress northwest towards the A3 and into the Beverley Brook at the borough boundary. [PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted Pyl Brook is identified as the local low point where surface&ndash;water runoff would collect; this contributed to surface&ndash;water flooding of adjacent streets/properties during the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water sewers that discharge to the Pyl Brook contributed to surcharges and backflows in the local sewer network, which in turn contributed to flooding at the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner underpass. [MUL] (p79)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 in 30 year; 1 in 2 year; 1 in 4 year return periods (TWUL&ndash;estimated incidents at Shannon Corner) [MUL] (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Pyl Brook,England,"The culverted Pyl Brook runs behind properties on West Barnes Lane (and behind properties on the Westway) and received/transported surface water runoff during the incidents. The culvert and associated surface water sewer connections formed part of the flow path that contributed to flooding of rear gardens and properties on the eastern side of West Barnes Lane and adjacent private access land. [PAG,MUL] (p59)
The culverted Pyl Brook (650 m culverted length in this location) runs behind properties and is the local low point; limited capacity/flow within the culvert and connections from surface water sewers led to ponding and inundation of adjacent properties and private land during the 2016 and 2017 incidents. Flows progress northwest towards the A3 and into the Beverley Brook at the borough boundary. [PAG,MUL] (p60)
The culverted Pyl Brook is identified as the local low point where surface–water runoff would collect; this contributed to surface–water flooding of adjacent streets/properties during the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p61)
Surface water sewers that discharge to the Pyl Brook contributed to surcharges and backflows in the local sewer network, which in turn contributed to flooding at the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner underpass. [MUL] (p79)",1 in 30 year; 1 in 2 year; 1 in 4 year return periods (TWUL–estimated incidents at Shannon Corner) [MUL] (p79),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3900424,-0.2087805,"<div id=""html_11af63b65c65d91893a689d3c0e9a748"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Pyl Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A blocked TWUL surface water sewer adjacent to Hatfeild Primary School prevented runoff discharging into the East Pyl Brook, contributing to surface&ndash;water/sewer&ndash;related flooding at Lower Morden Lane that was constrained to the highway (no properties affected). [MUL] (p78)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three events; no properties affected [MUL] (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,East Pyl Brook,England,"A blocked TWUL surface water sewer adjacent to Hatfeild Primary School prevented runoff discharging into the East Pyl Brook, contributing to surface–water/sewer–related flooding at Lower Morden Lane that was constrained to the highway (no properties affected). [MUL] (p78)",three events; no properties affected [MUL] (p78),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4096275,-0.3062621,"<div id=""html_eb45f448fb9ff295016705d246166db0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London Borough of Kingston Upon Thames</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected polling stations in the Royal Borough of Kingston upon Thames, causing disruption and impacting polling locations on 23 June 2016. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,London Borough of Kingston Upon Thames,England,"Flooding affected polling stations in the Royal Borough of Kingston upon Thames, causing disruption and impacting polling locations on 23 June 2016. (p16)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.5073219,-0.1276474,"<div id=""html_68e22adf418a7d60d7c350e484665ad8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London Region</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface&ndash;water/flash flooding during the three rainfall events caused disruption to roads and railway lines across south London, led to station closures and flight/highway delays, and resulted in multiple locations in the London Borough of Merton meeting the Section 19 investigation threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 locations; 5 locations; 3 locations [PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,London Region,England,"Widespread surface–water/flash flooding during the three rainfall events caused disruption to roads and railway lines across south London, led to station closures and flight/highway delays, and resulted in multiple locations in the London Borough of Merton meeting the Section 19 investigation threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p16)","9 locations; 5 locations; 3 locations [PAG,MUL] (p16)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4570271,-0.1932607,"<div id=""html_9da756d7170a86d40ce62b33750394e7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London Borough of Wandsworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The London Borough of Wandsworth experienced rail disruption including the closure of Balham Station during heavy showers on 29&ndash;30 July 2017 related to flooding. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the intense rainfall event crossed the borough boundary and affected areas within the London Borough of Wandsworth. (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies on Acuba Road are maintained annually by the borough; the authority recorded no actions during or after the incident despite sewer surcharging at the location contributing to local flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,London Borough of Wandsworth,England,"The London Borough of Wandsworth experienced rail disruption including the closure of Balham Station during heavy showers on 29–30 July 2017 related to flooding. (p16)
Flooding from the intense rainfall event crossed the borough boundary and affected areas within the London Borough of Wandsworth. (p65)
Gullies on Acuba Road are maintained annually by the borough; the authority recorded no actions during or after the incident despite sewer surcharging at the location contributing to local flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p69)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.443024376747985,-0.15202507304724897,"<div id=""html_1132223dadea96f1f73ac6ef5ca135d9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Balham Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Closure of Balham Station on 29&ndash;30 July 2017 caused by heavy showers / flooding, disrupting rail services. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Balham Station,England,"Closure of Balham Station on 29–30 July 2017 caused by heavy showers / flooding, disrupting rail services. (p16)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4181385,-0.17784,"<div id=""html_d39c5787f35437839f83d8b896f8d620"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Colliers Wood Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area around Colliers Wood Station is recorded as having experienced flooding in the report (return period recorded for the June 2016 event). [TAB] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Colliers Wood Station,England,The area around Colliers Wood Station is recorded as having experienced flooding in the report (return period recorded for the June 2016 event). [TAB] (p21),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.408966,-0.23054,"<div id=""html_67a330c826af1a141d813810d541cabe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Raynes Park Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Adjacent to the station the main surface water sewers and highway gullies around the Raynes Park railway bridge were associated with flood incidents; four gullies directly underneath the railway bridge (adjacent to the station) were not accessible for cleaning prior to being addressed in April 2017, and these gullies were implicated in local surface&ndash;water drainage failures that contributed to transport risk during flood events. (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Raynes Park Station,England,"Adjacent to the station the main surface water sewers and highway gullies around the Raynes Park railway bridge were associated with flood incidents; four gullies directly underneath the railway bridge (adjacent to the station) were not accessible for cleaning prior to being addressed in April 2017, and these gullies were implicated in local surface–water drainage failures that contributed to transport risk during flood events. (p37)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4050242,-0.2977428,"<div id=""html_bf17b6f4ea2f9bfa2ad62a3d53112f9a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Hogsmill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded one of the largest short&ndash;duration rainfall peaks during the 29/30 July 2017 event; this intense, concentrated rainfall was associated with localised surface water flooding at the monitored Hogsmill location and contributed to local flood impacts. [IMG,MUL] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,River Hogsmill,England,"Recorded one of the largest short–duration rainfall peaks during the 29/30 July 2017 event; this intense, concentrated rainfall was associated with localised surface water flooding at the monitored Hogsmill location and contributed to local flood impacts. [IMG,MUL] (p19)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.5254987,-0.4985196,"<div id=""html_25424ebfd86f4df2e1ba9b74615630b4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>M25</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 09 Aug 2017 heavy thunderstorms caused significant problems on major highways including the M25, with flooding&ndash;related disruption to traffic. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,M25,England,"On 09 Aug 2017 heavy thunderstorms caused significant problems on major highways including the M25, with flooding–related disruption to traffic. (p16)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4051686,-0.2195869,"<div id=""html_eceeb117ff73fbf8d3018416283b36f8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Whatley Avenue, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer at the junction and the combined sewer network adjacent to Bushey Road experienced hydraulic overflow / capacity issues likely caused by misconnections or private drainage, contributing to flooding at Whatley Avenue. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Street experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding during the June 2016 event; recorded sewer gauge indicated a 1&ndash;in&ndash;24&ndash;year return period for that storm. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding concentrated at the local low point at the junction with Martin Way: internal flooding of properties where flows were channelled down Whatley Avenue towards the junction. Flooding started at about 09:00 on 23 June 2016 and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ponding/channelled to a local low point at the junction with Martin Way causing flooding at that junction; nearby properties set lower than the highway were affected. Highway gullies on Whatley Avenue (excluding the eastern part) were on Merton's high priority cleansing programme and had last been cleaned in January&ndash;February 2016 prior to the incident. [PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on/near Whatley Avenue experienced internal flooding (confirmed by contractors on 23 June 2016); the main trunk sewer serving the area was running at full capacity during the event; as part of the response the foul sewer on Whatley Avenue (either side of the junction with Martin Way) was cleaned on 28 July 2016. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from higher ground (Cannon Hill Common and Joseph Hood Recreation Ground) and surcharge of local surface water and foul sewers caused flooding at low&ndash;level locations near the junction with Martin Way; gullies were overwhelmed and properties flooded. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A 750 mm surface water sewer and the foul sewer were running full upstream of the junction with Martin Way; additional flow backed up into gullies and caused flooding of low&ndash;level properties at the junction with Martin Way. Runoff from Cannon Hill Common and Joseph Hood Recreation Ground slopes down to Whatley Avenue, increasing flows into the local drainage. [PAG,MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall during the 22&ndash;24 June 2016 period associated with the June 2016 flood investigation produced surface water flooding at Whatley Avenue as part of the event. [TAB] (p87)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded extreme short&ndash;duration (5&ndash;minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016, associated with surface&ndash;water / flash&ndash;flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 in 24 yr (recorded sewer return period) [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately ten properties; peak depth between 300 and 450 mm [PAG] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Whatley Avenue, Raynes Park",England,"Foul sewer at the junction and the combined sewer network adjacent to Bushey Road experienced hydraulic overflow / capacity issues likely caused by misconnections or private drainage, contributing to flooding at Whatley Avenue. (p4)
Street experienced sewer–related flooding during the June 2016 event; recorded sewer gauge indicated a 1–in–24–year return period for that storm. [TAB] (p21)
Flooding concentrated at the local low point at the junction with Martin Way: internal flooding of properties where flows were channelled down Whatley Avenue towards the junction. Flooding started at about 09:00 on 23 June 2016 and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p23)
Surface water ponding/channelled to a local low point at the junction with Martin Way causing flooding at that junction; nearby properties set lower than the highway were affected. Highway gullies on Whatley Avenue (excluding the eastern part) were on Merton's high priority cleansing programme and had last been cleaned in January–February 2016 prior to the incident. [PAG,MUL] (p24)
Properties on/near Whatley Avenue experienced internal flooding (confirmed by contractors on 23 June 2016); the main trunk sewer serving the area was running at full capacity during the event; as part of the response the foul sewer on Whatley Avenue (either side of the junction with Martin Way) was cleaned on 28 July 2016. [TAB,MUL] (p26)
Runoff from higher ground (Cannon Hill Common and Joseph Hood Recreation Ground) and surcharge of local surface water and foul sewers caused flooding at low–level locations near the junction with Martin Way; gullies were overwhelmed and properties flooded. [PAG] (p27)
A 750 mm surface water sewer and the foul sewer were running full upstream of the junction with Martin Way; additional flow backed up into gullies and caused flooding of low–level properties at the junction with Martin Way. Runoff from Cannon Hill Common and Joseph Hood Recreation Ground slopes down to Whatley Avenue, increasing flows into the local drainage. [PAG,MUL] (p28)
Intense rainfall during the 22–24 June 2016 period associated with the June 2016 flood investigation produced surface water flooding at Whatley Avenue as part of the event. [TAB] (p87)
Recorded extreme short–duration (5–minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016, associated with surface–water / flash–flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)","1 in 24 yr (recorded sewer return period) [TAB] (p21)
approximately ten properties; peak depth between 300 and 450 mm [PAG] (p23)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.40455199463844,-0.21529058460816144,"<div id=""html_10a5dea607005db7f2e526ccb767facb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Martin Way, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding linked to hydraulic overflow / capacity issues in the foul and combined sewer network at the junction with Whatley Avenue and adjacent to Bushey Road, potentially due to misconnections from the surface water network or private drainage. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Martin Way experienced flooding during the June 2016 event as recorded in the report. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding concentrated at the local low point at the junction with Whatley Avenue: internal flooding of properties where surface water and sewer flows accumulated. Flooding started at about 09:00 on 23 June 2016 and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at the junction with Whatley Avenue where a local low point ponded flows; properties close to the junction (set lower than the highway) were affected. Much of Martin Way is identified as being at medium risk of surface water flooding. [MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Junction area with Whatley Avenue was affected during the 23 June 2016 flood &mdash; flows could not drain due to sewer capacity issues; the council later implemented carriageway level changes and installed two SuDS tree pits near the junction as post&ndash;event mitigation. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At the junction with Whatley Avenue the 750 mm surface water sewer and the foul sewer were running full, causing backup into gullies and flooding of nearby low&ndash;level properties. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall during the 22&ndash;24 June 2016 period associated with the June 2016 flood investigation produced surface water flooding at Martin Way as part of the event. [TAB] (p87)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded extreme short&ndash;duration (5&ndash;minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016, associated with surface&ndash;water / flash&ndash;flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 in 24 yr (recorded sewer return period) (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately ten properties; peak depth between 300 and 450 mm [PAG] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Martin Way, Raynes Park",England,"Flooding linked to hydraulic overflow / capacity issues in the foul and combined sewer network at the junction with Whatley Avenue and adjacent to Bushey Road, potentially due to misconnections from the surface water network or private drainage. (p4)
Martin Way experienced flooding during the June 2016 event as recorded in the report. [TAB] (p21)
Flooding concentrated at the local low point at the junction with Whatley Avenue: internal flooding of properties where surface water and sewer flows accumulated. Flooding started at about 09:00 on 23 June 2016 and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p23)
Surface water flooding occurred at the junction with Whatley Avenue where a local low point ponded flows; properties close to the junction (set lower than the highway) were affected. Much of Martin Way is identified as being at medium risk of surface water flooding. [MUL] (p24)
Junction area with Whatley Avenue was affected during the 23 June 2016 flood — flows could not drain due to sewer capacity issues; the council later implemented carriageway level changes and installed two SuDS tree pits near the junction as post–event mitigation. [TAB] (p26)
At the junction with Whatley Avenue the 750 mm surface water sewer and the foul sewer were running full, causing backup into gullies and flooding of nearby low–level properties. (p27)
Intense rainfall during the 22–24 June 2016 period associated with the June 2016 flood investigation produced surface water flooding at Martin Way as part of the event. [TAB] (p87)
Recorded extreme short–duration (5–minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016, associated with surface–water / flash–flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)","1 in 24 yr (recorded sewer return period) (p21)
approximately ten properties; peak depth between 300 and 450 mm [PAG] (p23)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4048687,-0.2375022,"<div id=""html_b3fb10af212b6321210766e3421d4169"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bushey Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The combined sewer/trunk main adjacent to Bushey Road receives multiple flows from north and south; capacity and hydraulic overflow issues in this network contributed to flooding in the surrounding area (Apostles area experienced significant flooding in the 2016 event and lesser flooding during 2017 events). CCTV of the combined sewer near Bushey Road was recommended to identify misconnections. [PAG,MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding in the Apostles area drained toward and into the Bushey Road trunk sewer; the local roads (north&ndash;south roads between Kingston Road, Grand Drive and Bushey Road) became inaccessible to vehicular traffic during the event. The worst affected road, Dupont Road, experienced internal property flooding and widespread inundation visible to wheel&ndash;rim height on parked vehicles. [PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows from the blocked Dupont Road passed across Bushey Road and there was highway ponding at the junction with Grand Drive. The 2000 by 1524 mm trunk surface&ndash;water sewer on Bushey Road acted as a hydraulic throttle, contributing to the ponding. TWUL inspected this trunk sewer and found it running clear on 26 June 2016. [TAB] (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately ten properties; 36 hours; maximum depth 300&ndash;450 mm [PAG] (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Bushey Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"The combined sewer/trunk main adjacent to Bushey Road receives multiple flows from north and south; capacity and hydraulic overflow issues in this network contributed to flooding in the surrounding area (Apostles area experienced significant flooding in the 2016 event and lesser flooding during 2017 events). CCTV of the combined sewer near Bushey Road was recommended to identify misconnections. [PAG,MUL] (p28)
Surface water flooding in the Apostles area drained toward and into the Bushey Road trunk sewer; the local roads (north–south roads between Kingston Road, Grand Drive and Bushey Road) became inaccessible to vehicular traffic during the event. The worst affected road, Dupont Road, experienced internal property flooding and widespread inundation visible to wheel–rim height on parked vehicles. [PAG] (p29)
Flows from the blocked Dupont Road passed across Bushey Road and there was highway ponding at the junction with Grand Drive. The 2000 by 1524 mm trunk surface–water sewer on Bushey Road acted as a hydraulic throttle, contributing to the ponding. TWUL inspected this trunk sewer and found it running clear on 26 June 2016. [TAB] (p33)",approximately ten properties; 36 hours; maximum depth 300–450 mm [PAG] (p29),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.408999,-0.2213303,"<div id=""html_31a8c24bcbfcf58775b3138699b9057f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dupont Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the 'Apostles' area that experienced flooding driven by interactions between surface water runoff and the sewer network; flooding was linked to a hydraulic 'throttle' effect in the main surface water sewer and its connections, contributing to surface water/sewer overflow onto side roads including Dupont Road. CCTV investigation of the main surface water sewer and connections was recommended. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dupont Road (included within the Apostles area) experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding during the June 2016 event with a recorded sewer return period of 1&ndash;in&ndash;24 years. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dupont Road was the worst&ndash;affected road in the Apostles area during the June 2016 event: the street was inundated, vehicles partially submerged and pedestrian/vehicular access disrupted; flooding contributed to internal flooding of properties in the affected area. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dupont Road was the worst&ndash;affected road in the event: flooding reached internal parts of properties. The report links the mapped extent in Dupont Road to the June 2016 event and notes it as the worst affected road in the area. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred as a result of a blockage identified in the surface water sewer. Non&ndash;scheduled gully cleansing on 22 Jun 2016 identified the issue; on 23 Jun 2016 a Merton jetting tanker cleared the blockage and resolved the flooding. Thames Water attended subsequently to check the sewer. [TAB] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blockage of a 225 mm surface water sewer at the junction with Dorien Road caused water to back up and flood the low point of Dupont Road, producing internal flooding of properties at lower levels. The blockage was cleared by Merton using a jetting tanker. [TAB,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dupont Road (within the Apostles area) recorded intense rainfall during 22&ndash;24 June 2016 associated with the June 2016 event and was affected by surface water flooding during that event. [TAB] (p87)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded extreme short&ndash;duration (5&ndash;minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016 (listed as part of the Apostles area), associated with surface&ndash;water / flash&ndash;flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 in 24 yr (recorded sewer return period) [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately ten properties; 300&ndash;450 mm [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 10 properties; maximum flood depth between 300 and 450 mm; incident duration about 36 hours [PAG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Dupont Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"Part of the 'Apostles' area that experienced flooding driven by interactions between surface water runoff and the sewer network; flooding was linked to a hydraulic 'throttle' effect in the main surface water sewer and its connections, contributing to surface water/sewer overflow onto side roads including Dupont Road. CCTV investigation of the main surface water sewer and connections was recommended. (p4)
Dupont Road (included within the Apostles area) experienced sewer–related flooding during the June 2016 event with a recorded sewer return period of 1–in–24 years. [TAB] (p21)
Dupont Road was the worst–affected road in the Apostles area during the June 2016 event: the street was inundated, vehicles partially submerged and pedestrian/vehicular access disrupted; flooding contributed to internal flooding of properties in the affected area. [IMG,PAG] (p29)
Dupont Road was the worst–affected road in the event: flooding reached internal parts of properties. The report links the mapped extent in Dupont Road to the June 2016 event and notes it as the worst affected road in the area. [PAG] (p30)
Flooding occurred as a result of a blockage identified in the surface water sewer. Non–scheduled gully cleansing on 22 Jun 2016 identified the issue; on 23 Jun 2016 a Merton jetting tanker cleared the blockage and resolved the flooding. Thames Water attended subsequently to check the sewer. [TAB] (p32)
Blockage of a 225 mm surface water sewer at the junction with Dorien Road caused water to back up and flood the low point of Dupont Road, producing internal flooding of properties at lower levels. The blockage was cleared by Merton using a jetting tanker. [TAB,PAG] (p33)
Dupont Road (within the Apostles area) recorded intense rainfall during 22–24 June 2016 associated with the June 2016 event and was affected by surface water flooding during that event. [TAB] (p87)
Recorded extreme short–duration (5–minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016 (listed as part of the Apostles area), associated with surface–water / flash–flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)","1 in 24 yr (recorded sewer return period) [TAB] (p21)
approximately ten properties; 300–450 mm [IMG,PAG] (p29)
approximately 10 properties; maximum flood depth between 300 and 450 mm; incident duration about 36 hours [PAG] (p30)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4095154,-0.2329622,"<div id=""html_a5129deea6cdd182900f5cf4b462d015"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coombe Lane, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area by Raynes Park railway bridge experienced flooding concerns related to the condition and operation of pumps beneath the bridge; pump issues were identified as contributing to surface water/sewer flood behaviour and recommended for investigation and inclusion in flood modelling. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Lane (by Raynes Park railway bridge) experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding on all three reported events: June 2016, July 2017 and August 2017; recorded sewer return periods were 1&ndash;in&ndash;24 yr (June 2016), 1&ndash;in&ndash;3 yr (July 2017) and 1&ndash;in&ndash;6 yr (Aug 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location&ndash;wide flooding on Coombe Lane caused internal flooding of commercial premises and disruption to local transport; the area (a low point beneath Raynes Park railway bridge) reached Merton's Section 19 threshold in the reported events. [MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local low point for the local drainage catchment; multiple surface water and foul sewers discharge towards Coombe Lane and the local highway gullies in Coombe Lane were part of the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and subject to reactive clearing before and after the recorded flooding incidents, indicating the location was implicated in surface&ndash;water sewer surcharge and highway flooding during the area incidents. [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at the local low point around Coombe Lane, causing road drainage/gully issues. Highway gullies on Coombe Lane were part of the council's high&ndash;priority cleansing programme (cleaned December 2015 and via reactive call&ndash;outs before and after the incidents) in response to the flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Lane (by Raynes Park railway bridge) recorded intense rainfall during 22&ndash;24 June 2016 associated with the June 2016 event and experienced surface water flooding as part of the investigated event. [TAB] (p87)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded extreme short&ndash;duration (5&ndash;minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016 (noted at location by Raynes Park Railway Bridge), associated with surface&ndash;water / flash&ndash;flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 commercial properties [MUL] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Coombe Lane, London Borough of Merton",England,"Area by Raynes Park railway bridge experienced flooding concerns related to the condition and operation of pumps beneath the bridge; pump issues were identified as contributing to surface water/sewer flood behaviour and recommended for investigation and inclusion in flood modelling. (p4)
Coombe Lane (by Raynes Park railway bridge) experienced sewer–related flooding on all three reported events: June 2016, July 2017 and August 2017; recorded sewer return periods were 1–in–24 yr (June 2016), 1–in–3 yr (July 2017) and 1–in–6 yr (Aug 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Location–wide flooding on Coombe Lane caused internal flooding of commercial premises and disruption to local transport; the area (a low point beneath Raynes Park railway bridge) reached Merton's Section 19 threshold in the reported events. [MUL] (p35)
Local low point for the local drainage catchment; multiple surface water and foul sewers discharge towards Coombe Lane and the local highway gullies in Coombe Lane were part of the high–priority cleansing programme and subject to reactive clearing before and after the recorded flooding incidents, indicating the location was implicated in surface–water sewer surcharge and highway flooding during the area incidents. [PAG] (p37)
Surface water flooding occurred at the local low point around Coombe Lane, causing road drainage/gully issues. Highway gullies on Coombe Lane were part of the council's high–priority cleansing programme (cleaned December 2015 and via reactive call–outs before and after the incidents) in response to the flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p38)
Coombe Lane (by Raynes Park railway bridge) recorded intense rainfall during 22–24 June 2016 associated with the June 2016 event and experienced surface water flooding as part of the investigated event. [TAB] (p87)
Recorded extreme short–duration (5–minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016 (noted at location by Raynes Park Railway Bridge), associated with surface–water / flash–flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)",4 commercial properties [MUL] (p35),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.414624,-0.2202144,"<div id=""html_94647b32040ffe7b14443b8f6b24c965"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Worple Road, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Worple Road experienced flooding linked to surface water runoff and sewers reaching hydraulic capacity; Thames Water were recommended to carry out CCTV investigation of the surface water sewer network to establish sewer condition and identify blockages or defects contributing to the flooding. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Worple Road (part of the 'Worple Road and Wimbledon Hill Road' location) experienced sewer flooding reaching the Section 19 threshold across the listed events. Flooding was recorded in June 2016, July 2017 and August 2017 (recorded sewer return periods 1 in 21 yr, 1 in 2 yr and 1 in 5 yr respectively). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the 2016 flooding incident, gullies along Worple Road were added to the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme, showing Worple Road was affected by the 2016 flood and associated sewer/highway drainage failures. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway flooding and inundation of basements. Runoff entered basements of retail units (The Pavement) via basement skylights; flooding extended onto Worple Road Mews. Two residential properties were affected internally. [MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway inundated; basements of some retail units on The Pavement flooded by runoff entering through basement skylights at the footway; flooding extended onto Worple Road Mews; two residential properties affected internally; commercial units recorded internal flooding (30 July 2017 event). [PAG,MUL] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded historic flooding incidents in the vicinity (2007 and 2011) believed to be a combination of surface water and sewer flooding. The SFRA and RoFSW mapping indicate medium risk along Worple Road in front of commercial units; report notes similar consequences occurred in the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies on the road were cleansed between November 2016 and April 2017 as a response following flooding that affected the road and nearby commercial units. [TAB] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Because Worple Road is relatively flat and perpendicular to local slopes, rapid surface water flow ponds on Worple Road and flows downhill toward the town centre; runoff from Worple Road and Worple Road Mews runs towards The Pavement shopping parade causing flooding to some shop units and surcharging of local surface water sewers. (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Short&ndash;duration extreme rainfall during 23 June 2016 resulted in localised surface water flooding of the road and surrounding areas, affecting carriageway drainage and access. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; flood depths 300&ndash;600 mm (recorded for the July 2017 incident) [MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties [PAG,MUL] (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Worple Road, Raynes Park",England,"Worple Road experienced flooding linked to surface water runoff and sewers reaching hydraulic capacity; Thames Water were recommended to carry out CCTV investigation of the surface water sewer network to establish sewer condition and identify blockages or defects contributing to the flooding. (p4)
Worple Road (part of the 'Worple Road and Wimbledon Hill Road' location) experienced sewer flooding reaching the Section 19 threshold across the listed events. Flooding was recorded in June 2016, July 2017 and August 2017 (recorded sewer return periods 1 in 21 yr, 1 in 2 yr and 1 in 5 yr respectively). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Following the 2016 flooding incident, gullies along Worple Road were added to the high–priority cleansing programme, showing Worple Road was affected by the 2016 flood and associated sewer/highway drainage failures. (p37)
Highway flooding and inundation of basements. Runoff entered basements of retail units (The Pavement) via basement skylights; flooding extended onto Worple Road Mews. Two residential properties were affected internally. [MUL] (p49)
Highway inundated; basements of some retail units on The Pavement flooded by runoff entering through basement skylights at the footway; flooding extended onto Worple Road Mews; two residential properties affected internally; commercial units recorded internal flooding (30 July 2017 event). [PAG,MUL] (p50)
Recorded historic flooding incidents in the vicinity (2007 and 2011) believed to be a combination of surface water and sewer flooding. The SFRA and RoFSW mapping indicate medium risk along Worple Road in front of commercial units; report notes similar consequences occurred in the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p52)
Gullies on the road were cleansed between November 2016 and April 2017 as a response following flooding that affected the road and nearby commercial units. [TAB] (p53)
Because Worple Road is relatively flat and perpendicular to local slopes, rapid surface water flow ponds on Worple Road and flows downhill toward the town centre; runoff from Worple Road and Worple Road Mews runs towards The Pavement shopping parade causing flooding to some shop units and surcharging of local surface water sewers. (p54)
Short–duration extreme rainfall during 23 June 2016 resulted in localised surface water flooding of the road and surrounding areas, affecting carriageway drainage and access. [TAB] (p88)","2 residential properties; flood depths 300–600 mm (recorded for the July 2017 incident) [MUL] (p49)
2 residential properties [PAG,MUL] (p50)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4239224,-0.2119785,"<div id=""html_e7b36eadf139d694d8f0a52707985196"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wimbledon Hill Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wimbledon Hill Road was included among streets where surface water sewer condition is suspected to have contributed to flooding; the report recommends CCTV of surface water sewers to establish condition and potential causes of blockages or failures. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The street was inundated by sewer&ndash;related flooding recorded at the local Thames Water sewer gauge. Flooding occurred on 23 June 2016, 29&ndash;30 July 2017 and 09 August 2017 with recorded sewer return periods of 1 in 21 years (June 2016), 1 in 2 years (July 2017) and 1 in 5 years (August 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the town&ndash;centre area that experienced flooding during the events; contributed to highway and basement inundation affecting commercial and residential premises in the local area. [MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The steep slope of Wimbledon Hill Road channels surface water runoff down towards the local low point at the junction with Alexandra Road and St George's Road, contributing to increased surface water flooding extents and channelling water that causes ponding on adjacent roads during the flooding incidents. [MUL] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Historic flooding incidents recorded (2007 and 2011). Mapping and report text identify increased surface water flooding extents predicted on Wimbledon Hill Road and a high&ndash;risk junction with St George's Road/Alexandra Road; records indicate similar impacts in the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Responders attended Wimbledon Hill Road (26 June 2016) and checked the lateral sewer; attended again on 28&ndash;29 June 2016 and pumped out the basement of a property. A CCTV sewer survey was later carried out (12 July 2016). [TAB,PAG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement flooding at a property was pumped out following the June 2016 incident; surface water runoff from Wimbledon Hill Road contributed to overland flow towards The Pavement shopping parade during the Aug 2017 event, increasing flood risk to nearby shops; lateral sewer was inspected and found clear in June 2016. [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Short&ndash;duration extreme rainfall during 23 June 2016 produced localised surface water flooding on the road and adjacent areas, impacting drainage and access. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one basement [TAB,PAG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (basement pumped out) [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Wimbledon Hill Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"Wimbledon Hill Road was included among streets where surface water sewer condition is suspected to have contributed to flooding; the report recommends CCTV of surface water sewers to establish condition and potential causes of blockages or failures. (p4)
The street was inundated by sewer–related flooding recorded at the local Thames Water sewer gauge. Flooding occurred on 23 June 2016, 29–30 July 2017 and 09 August 2017 with recorded sewer return periods of 1 in 21 years (June 2016), 1 in 2 years (July 2017) and 1 in 5 years (August 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Part of the town–centre area that experienced flooding during the events; contributed to highway and basement inundation affecting commercial and residential premises in the local area. [MUL] (p49)
The steep slope of Wimbledon Hill Road channels surface water runoff down towards the local low point at the junction with Alexandra Road and St George's Road, contributing to increased surface water flooding extents and channelling water that causes ponding on adjacent roads during the flooding incidents. [MUL] (p51)
Historic flooding incidents recorded (2007 and 2011). Mapping and report text identify increased surface water flooding extents predicted on Wimbledon Hill Road and a high–risk junction with St George's Road/Alexandra Road; records indicate similar impacts in the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p52)
Responders attended Wimbledon Hill Road (26 June 2016) and checked the lateral sewer; attended again on 28–29 June 2016 and pumped out the basement of a property. A CCTV sewer survey was later carried out (12 July 2016). [TAB,PAG] (p53)
Basement flooding at a property was pumped out following the June 2016 incident; surface water runoff from Wimbledon Hill Road contributed to overland flow towards The Pavement shopping parade during the Aug 2017 event, increasing flood risk to nearby shops; lateral sewer was inspected and found clear in June 2016. [TAB,MUL] (p54)
Short–duration extreme rainfall during 23 June 2016 produced localised surface water flooding on the road and adjacent areas, impacting drainage and access. [TAB] (p88)","one basement [TAB,PAG] (p53)
1 property (basement pumped out) [TAB,MUL] (p54)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.421968,-0.2093775,"<div id=""html_12038705f5ae6f4ae3df3d93a8723940"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Worple Road Mews, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater extended onto Worple Road Mews causing highway inundation and inundation of basements and commercial premises. On 23 June 2016 runoff entered basements of The Pavement retail units through basement skylights on the footway and two residential properties were internally flooded. On 30 July 2017 commercial units were internally flooded with flood depths estimated between 300 and 600 mm. [MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed downhill from adjacent roads (including Wimbledon Hill Road) toward The Pavement shopping parade; surcharging of the smaller surface water sewers at Worple Road Mews caused ponding and contributed to internal flooding of some commercial units. Debris in a nearby gully reduced flow into the sewer and worsened the flooding. [MUL] (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; flood depth 300&ndash;600 mm [MUL] (p49)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Worple Road Mews, London Borough of Merton",England,"Floodwater extended onto Worple Road Mews causing highway inundation and inundation of basements and commercial premises. On 23 June 2016 runoff entered basements of The Pavement retail units through basement skylights on the footway and two residential properties were internally flooded. On 30 July 2017 commercial units were internally flooded with flood depths estimated between 300 and 600 mm. [MUL] (p49)
Surface water runoff flowed downhill from adjacent roads (including Wimbledon Hill Road) toward The Pavement shopping parade; surcharging of the smaller surface water sewers at Worple Road Mews caused ponding and contributed to internal flooding of some commercial units. Debris in a nearby gully reduced flow into the sewer and worsened the flooding. [MUL] (p54)",2 residential properties; flood depth 300–600 mm [MUL] (p49),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4206698,-0.2087736,"<div id=""html_d988de75138bd9e5b7a6040f739ca130"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St George's Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ponding occurred on St George's Road during the reported flooding incidents; runoff was channelled and ponded on this road contributing to local highway flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Relatively flat; described as receiving channelled runoff that causes water to pond on the road during the flooding incidents. The south&ndash;eastern parts are highlighted as high risk of surface water flooding and predicted to experience increased flood extents. [MUL] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Historic incident records list flooding on St George's Road (2007) believed to be a combination of surface water and sewer flooding; records indicate similar consequences occurred during the June 2016 and July/August 2017 events, i.e. local surface water/sewer flooding affecting the street. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St. George's Road floods first during surface water events and floods internally into commercial units; surface water sewers on the road become overwhelmed and required jetting/cleaning (silt present) as part of the response. [TAB] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"St George's Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"Surface water ponding occurred on St George's Road during the reported flooding incidents; runoff was channelled and ponded on this road contributing to local highway flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p50)
Relatively flat; described as receiving channelled runoff that causes water to pond on the road during the flooding incidents. The south–eastern parts are highlighted as high risk of surface water flooding and predicted to experience increased flood extents. [MUL] (p51)
Historic incident records list flooding on St George's Road (2007) believed to be a combination of surface water and sewer flooding; records indicate similar consequences occurred during the June 2016 and July/August 2017 events, i.e. local surface water/sewer flooding affecting the street. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)
St. George's Road floods first during surface water events and floods internally into commercial units; surface water sewers on the road become overwhelmed and required jetting/cleaning (silt present) as part of the response. [TAB] (p54)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4229404,-0.1907155,"<div id=""html_951a4d4a53c6c5ac1262dc7033232588"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Effra Road, Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water accumulated at a local low point affecting the carriageway of Effra Road; Merton recommended a topographical survey and potentially raising kerb levels to keep surface water on the carriageway. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Effra Road experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding on 23 June 2016, recorded at the local TWUL sewer gauge with a recorded sewer return period of 1 in 21 years. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water / surcharged surface water sewer flooding that caused internal flooding to residential properties. Flooding reportedly entered properties from the rear; one resident reported damage to kitchen and flooring. Highway gullies were noted as part of the local drainage system and gully cleaning history is recorded. [PAG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Effra Road with Haydon's Road is the local low point where surface water runoff accumulated during the recorded flooding incident; Effra Road was subsequently added to the council's high priority gully cleansing programme. [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the June 2016 incident, Thames Water attended and jetted the foul sewer, clearing an identified blockage; subsequently they jet&ndash;cleaned 60 m of foul sewer and lateral connections in Effra Road. The assessment records both sewer and surface&ndash;water sources for the flooding, noting water entered properties from rear gardens rather than from highway surface water. [TAB] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Short&ndash;duration intense rainfall on 23 June 2016 produced localised surface water flooding of the road and adjacent areas, causing rapid inundation of the carriageway and nearby land. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two residential properties; one property with damage to kitchen and flooring [PAG] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Effra Road, Merton",England,"Surface water accumulated at a local low point affecting the carriageway of Effra Road; Merton recommended a topographical survey and potentially raising kerb levels to keep surface water on the carriageway. (p4)
Effra Road experienced sewer–related flooding on 23 June 2016, recorded at the local TWUL sewer gauge with a recorded sewer return period of 1 in 21 years. [TAB] (p21)
Surface water / surcharged surface water sewer flooding that caused internal flooding to residential properties. Flooding reportedly entered properties from the rear; one resident reported damage to kitchen and flooring. Highway gullies were noted as part of the local drainage system and gully cleaning history is recorded. [PAG] (p55)
The junction of Effra Road with Haydon's Road is the local low point where surface water runoff accumulated during the recorded flooding incident; Effra Road was subsequently added to the council's high priority gully cleansing programme. [PAG] (p56)
Following the June 2016 incident, Thames Water attended and jetted the foul sewer, clearing an identified blockage; subsequently they jet–cleaned 60 m of foul sewer and lateral connections in Effra Road. The assessment records both sewer and surface–water sources for the flooding, noting water entered properties from rear gardens rather than from highway surface water. [TAB] (p58)
Short–duration intense rainfall on 23 June 2016 produced localised surface water flooding of the road and adjacent areas, causing rapid inundation of the carriageway and nearby land. [TAB] (p88)",two residential properties; one property with damage to kitchen and flooring [PAG] (p55),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4224908,-0.1896058,"<div id=""html_8895177f62183a69071820228573eab4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Edith Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a potential cause of the flooding due to a possible low point on Edith Road that could allow surface water to flow toward affected properties; a topographical survey was recommended and raising kerb levels at the low point was suggested to keep surface water on the carriageway. (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Edith Road, London Borough of Merton",England,Identified as a potential cause of the flooding due to a possible low point on Edith Road that could allow surface water to flow toward affected properties; a topographical survey was recommended and raising kerb levels at the low point was suggested to keep surface water on the carriageway. (p58),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4040355,-0.2361652,"<div id=""html_40aa929d0112b842935268017ff00fa5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Barnes Lane, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties adjacent to West Barnes Lane were affected by preferential surface water flows and issues in the local surface water sewer network (including blockages), with flows directed from West Barnes Lane toward neighbouring streets contributing to flooding. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>West Barnes Lane experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding on 23 June 2016 and 29&ndash;30 July 2017; recorded sewer return periods were 1 in 30 years (June 2016) and 1 in 2 years (July 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff overtopped the raised table at the junction with Adela Avenue and flowed along West Barnes Lane toward properties on the eastern side and the adjacent access gate to private land behind those properties. The flooding at this location reached Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold during the incidents listed. [PAG,MUL] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and surface&ndash;water sewer surcharge led to flooding affecting properties on the eastern side of West Barnes Lane. Runoff that could not enter the sewer network drained towards the culverted Pyl Brook behind properties, causing inundation of rear gardens/driveways and increasing flood risk to adjacent homes. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and surcharge towards the low point at the culverted Pyl Brook caused flooding along West Barnes Lane; eastern&ndash;side properties and driveways back&ndash;slope toward the highway and were within the flood&ndash;prone area. Gullies on West Barnes Lane were on the council's high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and had last been cleaned in December 2015/January 2016 prior to the flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding recorded between junctions with Estella Avenue and Seaforth Avenue on 20 July 2007; attended on 23 June 2016 when a gully&ndash;cleansing tanker removed surface water but the surface water sewer was found not to be flowing. Subsequent gully cleans and highway/gully improvements were implemented to reduce runoff to properties. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on West Barnes Lane were flooded because they sit slightly lower than the raised table at the Adela Avenue junction and runoff that could not enter gullies was channelled toward back&ndash;sloping driveways. Highway gullies at the junction were cleaned as part of the response. [TAB,MUL] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties and the junction near the raised table experienced flooding during 2016 and 2017. Highway gullies around the junction may have surcharged, runoff was observed to be poorly draining, and debris suggests blockages remain in the surface water sewer. There is potential interaction with the culverted Pyl Brook if that culvert is at capacity. [MUL] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Short&ndash;duration intense rainfall on 23 June 2016 produced localised surface water flooding of the lane and adjacent areas, affecting drainage and access. [TAB] (p88)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"West Barnes Lane, London Borough of Merton",England,"Properties adjacent to West Barnes Lane were affected by preferential surface water flows and issues in the local surface water sewer network (including blockages), with flows directed from West Barnes Lane toward neighbouring streets contributing to flooding. (p5)
West Barnes Lane experienced sewer–related flooding on 23 June 2016 and 29–30 July 2017; recorded sewer return periods were 1 in 30 years (June 2016) and 1 in 2 years (July 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Surface water runoff overtopped the raised table at the junction with Adela Avenue and flowed along West Barnes Lane toward properties on the eastern side and the adjacent access gate to private land behind those properties. The flooding at this location reached Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold during the incidents listed. [PAG,MUL] (p59)
Surface water and surface–water sewer surcharge led to flooding affecting properties on the eastern side of West Barnes Lane. Runoff that could not enter the sewer network drained towards the culverted Pyl Brook behind properties, causing inundation of rear gardens/driveways and increasing flood risk to adjacent homes. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p60)
Surface water runoff and surcharge towards the low point at the culverted Pyl Brook caused flooding along West Barnes Lane; eastern–side properties and driveways back–slope toward the highway and were within the flood–prone area. Gullies on West Barnes Lane were on the council's high–priority cleansing programme and had last been cleaned in December 2015/January 2016 prior to the flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p61)
Flooding recorded between junctions with Estella Avenue and Seaforth Avenue on 20 July 2007; attended on 23 June 2016 when a gully–cleansing tanker removed surface water but the surface water sewer was found not to be flowing. Subsequent gully cleans and highway/gully improvements were implemented to reduce runoff to properties. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p62)
Properties on West Barnes Lane were flooded because they sit slightly lower than the raised table at the Adela Avenue junction and runoff that could not enter gullies was channelled toward back–sloping driveways. Highway gullies at the junction were cleaned as part of the response. [TAB,MUL] (p63)
Properties and the junction near the raised table experienced flooding during 2016 and 2017. Highway gullies around the junction may have surcharged, runoff was observed to be poorly draining, and debris suggests blockages remain in the surface water sewer. There is potential interaction with the culverted Pyl Brook if that culvert is at capacity. [MUL] (p64)
Short–duration intense rainfall on 23 June 2016 produced localised surface water flooding of the lane and adjacent areas, affecting drainage and access. [TAB] (p88)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3980177,-0.2357391,"<div id=""html_9f96fc1d70bc9277127ee1a18ec49dd6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Adela Avenue, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed north&ndash;eastwards along Adela Avenue and ponded either side of the carriageway before overtopping the raised table on West Barnes Lane, contributing to overland flow toward properties at the eastern side of West Barnes Lane. [PAG,MUL] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water collected by a 225 mm surface water sewer on Adela Avenue drained to a manhole and discharged into the culverted Pyl Brook immediately north&ndash;west of affected properties; this runoff contributed to the surface water/sewer flooding of properties adjacent to West Barnes Lane during the 2016 and 2017 incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on the south side of the Adela Avenue junction lie within Flood Zone 3 and Flood Zone 2 extends approximately 50 m southwest up Adela Avenue; gullies on Adela Avenue were on the council's high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and had last been cleaned in December 2015/January 2016 prior to the flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding/ponding on the highway was attended on 23 June 2016; a gully&ndash;cleansing tanker removed as much surface water as possible but the surface water sewer was not flowing. Subsequent mitigation works included jetting of main sewers and connections, and installation of two additional highway gullies on Adela Avenue; kerb and footway/raised&ndash;table level adjustments and an additional gully were installed nearby in 2018 to reduce runoff toward properties. [TAB,PAG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff could not enter the highway gullies and the local 225 mm surface water sewer had insufficient capacity, causing runoff to back up and surcharge out of gullies at the raised table. Remedial works were carried out: footway and raised table levels adjusted and an additional highway gully installed outside property 168 in 2018; two additional gullies were installed on Adela Avenue and the main sewers and connections were jetted when this work occurred. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents in 2016 and 2017 are believed to have been caused by the Adela Avenue 225 mm surface water sewer; gullies at Adela Avenue drain into that sewer and evidence of poor drainage and potential sewer blockage was observed. [PAG,MUL] (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one highway gully outside property 168; two additional gullies on Adela Avenue [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p63)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Adela Avenue, London Borough of Merton",England,"Surface water runoff flowed north–eastwards along Adela Avenue and ponded either side of the carriageway before overtopping the raised table on West Barnes Lane, contributing to overland flow toward properties at the eastern side of West Barnes Lane. [PAG,MUL] (p59)
Surface water collected by a 225 mm surface water sewer on Adela Avenue drained to a manhole and discharged into the culverted Pyl Brook immediately north–west of affected properties; this runoff contributed to the surface water/sewer flooding of properties adjacent to West Barnes Lane during the 2016 and 2017 incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p60)
Properties on the south side of the Adela Avenue junction lie within Flood Zone 3 and Flood Zone 2 extends approximately 50 m southwest up Adela Avenue; gullies on Adela Avenue were on the council's high–priority cleansing programme and had last been cleaned in December 2015/January 2016 prior to the flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p61)
Surface–water flooding/ponding on the highway was attended on 23 June 2016; a gully–cleansing tanker removed as much surface water as possible but the surface water sewer was not flowing. Subsequent mitigation works included jetting of main sewers and connections, and installation of two additional highway gullies on Adela Avenue; kerb and footway/raised–table level adjustments and an additional gully were installed nearby in 2018 to reduce runoff toward properties. [TAB,PAG] (p62)
Surface water runoff could not enter the highway gullies and the local 225 mm surface water sewer had insufficient capacity, causing runoff to back up and surcharge out of gullies at the raised table. Remedial works were carried out: footway and raised table levels adjusted and an additional highway gully installed outside property 168 in 2018; two additional gullies were installed on Adela Avenue and the main sewers and connections were jetted when this work occurred. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p63)
Flooding incidents in 2016 and 2017 are believed to have been caused by the Adela Avenue 225 mm surface water sewer; gullies at Adela Avenue drain into that sewer and evidence of poor drainage and potential sewer blockage was observed. [PAG,MUL] (p64)","one highway gully outside property 168; two additional gullies on Adela Avenue [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p63)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3986174,-0.2362487,"<div id=""html_3a64a441fce9adf13f649b6be707045e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Douglas Avenue, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from Douglas Avenue drains into the same sewer network/manhole that discharges into the culverted Pyl Brook; this runoff contributed to the surface water/sewer flooding affecting properties on the eastern side of West Barnes Lane in the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Douglas Avenue, London Borough of Merton",England,"Surface water from Douglas Avenue drains into the same sewer network/manhole that discharges into the culverted Pyl Brook; this runoff contributed to the surface water/sewer flooding affecting properties on the eastern side of West Barnes Lane in the 2016 and 2017 events. [PAG,MUL] (p60)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4103076,-0.1731577,"<div id=""html_5ca360d9be4622309271497ac795ded7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mount Road, Colliers Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred as a result of interactions between surface water runoff and the sewer network; Thames Water and Merton recommended re&ndash;benching of the sewer on Acuba Road / Mount Road to address hydraulic capacity / flow issues. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Mount Road (Wimbledon Park) during the June 2016 event as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the location where the surface water/sewer flooding event occurred on 23 June 2016; contributed to internal property flooding within the location. [PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High point/low point topology at the junction with Acuba Road and Brooklands Avenue led to surface water flooding at Mount Road; highway gullies on Mount Road were part of the cleansing programme and were noted in relation to the flooding incident (last cleaned March 2016 prior to the incident). (p66)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than two properties [PAG] (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Mount Road, Colliers Wood",England,"Flooding occurred as a result of interactions between surface water runoff and the sewer network; Thames Water and Merton recommended re–benching of the sewer on Acuba Road / Mount Road to address hydraulic capacity / flow issues. (p5)
Flooding occurred on Mount Road (Wimbledon Park) during the June 2016 event as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)
Part of the location where the surface water/sewer flooding event occurred on 23 June 2016; contributed to internal property flooding within the location. [PAG] (p65)
High point/low point topology at the junction with Acuba Road and Brooklands Avenue led to surface water flooding at Mount Road; highway gullies on Mount Road were part of the cleansing programme and were noted in relation to the flooding incident (last cleaned March 2016 prior to the incident). (p66)",more than two properties [PAG] (p65),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4404999,-0.1943572,"<div id=""html_feb6f28fdbbbd2cf20f49b7dd5b3d0d6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Acuba Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding linked to sewers at hydraulic capacity and sewer configuration; recommendation to re&ndash;bench the sewer on Acuba Road / Mount Road to address the hydraulic issues contributing to flooding. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Acuba Road (Wimbledon Park) during the June 2016 event as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water/sewer flooding affected the location on 23 June 2016; foul sewage was reported on the road at the junction of Acuba Road and Ravensbury Road during the incident. [PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Acuba Road, Mount Road and Brooklands Avenue is a topographic low where surface water accumulated; the junction and Acuba Road are identified as locations at high risk of surface water flooding and were implicated in the flooding incident (gullies at the Acuba/ Mount Road junction were added to high priority cleansing afterwards). [PAG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames Water attended the location (25 June 2016) and inspected foul sewer manholes on Acuba Road (no blockages found); attended again on 10 July 2016 to clean up and disinfect the road. Gullies at the junction of Mount Road and Acuba Road were cleansed (March 2017). Thames Water have proposed rebenching a sewer on Acuba Road to improve discharge into a larger sewer. [TAB,PAG] (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer surcharging occurred at the junction with Ravensbury Road. Thames Water (TWUL) identified the 300 mm diameter sewer from Mount Road as a potential constraint and proposed re&ndash;benching to allow easier discharge into a larger sewer. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than two properties [PAG] (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Acuba Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"Flooding linked to sewers at hydraulic capacity and sewer configuration; recommendation to re–bench the sewer on Acuba Road / Mount Road to address the hydraulic issues contributing to flooding. (p5)
Flooding occurred on Acuba Road (Wimbledon Park) during the June 2016 event as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)
Surface water/sewer flooding affected the location on 23 June 2016; foul sewage was reported on the road at the junction of Acuba Road and Ravensbury Road during the incident. [PAG] (p65)
The junction of Acuba Road, Mount Road and Brooklands Avenue is a topographic low where surface water accumulated; the junction and Acuba Road are identified as locations at high risk of surface water flooding and were implicated in the flooding incident (gullies at the Acuba/ Mount Road junction were added to high priority cleansing afterwards). [PAG] (p66)
Thames Water attended the location (25 June 2016) and inspected foul sewer manholes on Acuba Road (no blockages found); attended again on 10 July 2016 to clean up and disinfect the road. Gullies at the junction of Mount Road and Acuba Road were cleansed (March 2017). Thames Water have proposed rebenching a sewer on Acuba Road to improve discharge into a larger sewer. [TAB,PAG] (p68)
Foul sewer surcharging occurred at the junction with Ravensbury Road. Thames Water (TWUL) identified the 300 mm diameter sewer from Mount Road as a potential constraint and proposed re–benching to allow easier discharge into a larger sewer. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p69)",more than two properties [PAG] (p65),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4395847,-0.1940455,"<div id=""html_f1ad5f2fc1c568c073b5cc70c22e9e3f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brooklands Avenue, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included among local roads where sewer condition and configuration contributed to flooding; recommended continued dialogue between Merton and Thames Water regarding sewer works to reduce future flooding risk. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Brooklands Avenue (Wimbledon Park) during the June 2016 event as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential basements on Brooklands Avenue were flooded as part of the surface water/sewer flooding event that began on 23 June 2016; the location experienced internal flooding of properties. (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Brooklands Avenue with Acuba Road and Mount Road is the local topographic low where surface water ponding/flooding occurred; the junction is described as impacted by the flooding incident and identified as high risk in mapping. (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basements of properties flooded where a local topographic low point collects water. The flooding is likely to have been caused by surface&ndash;water sewer surcharging with water finding its way to the natural low point. [MUL] (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than two properties (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Brooklands Avenue, London Borough of Merton",England,"Included among local roads where sewer condition and configuration contributed to flooding; recommended continued dialogue between Merton and Thames Water regarding sewer works to reduce future flooding risk. (p5)
Flooding occurred on Brooklands Avenue (Wimbledon Park) during the June 2016 event as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB] (p21)
Residential basements on Brooklands Avenue were flooded as part of the surface water/sewer flooding event that began on 23 June 2016; the location experienced internal flooding of properties. (p65)
The junction of Brooklands Avenue with Acuba Road and Mount Road is the local topographic low where surface water ponding/flooding occurred; the junction is described as impacted by the flooding incident and identified as high risk in mapping. (p66)
Basements of properties flooded where a local topographic low point collects water. The flooding is likely to have been caused by surface–water sewer surcharging with water finding its way to the natural low point. [MUL] (p69)",more than two properties (p65),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4135373,-0.1864805,"<div id=""html_35f5b72ffcc19e298093be2c1529f731"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Merantun Way, Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Merantun Way (and adjacent Morden Road) during the June 2016, late July 2017 and early August 2017 events as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction with Morden Road was inundated by surface water runoff and flooding. The highway was closed during each event due to deep floodwater; no internal property flooding was reported at this location. [PAG,MUL] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Concerns raised about functionality of the combined sewer system in Merantun Way due to siltation, which may have contributed to flooding at the junction with Morden Road. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Merantun Way was one of the locations examined for the 23 June 2016 event; intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall produced very high rainfall intensity that contributed to surface water runoff and sewer interactions, overwhelming drainage capacity and causing flooding at this location. (p82)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one location (included among the nine locations examined that reached Merton's Section 19 threshold) (p82)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Merantun Way, Merton",England,"Flooding occurred on Merantun Way (and adjacent Morden Road) during the June 2016, late July 2017 and early August 2017 events as recorded by TWUL sewer gauges. [TAB,MUL] (p21)
The junction with Morden Road was inundated by surface water runoff and flooding. The highway was closed during each event due to deep floodwater; no internal property flooding was reported at this location. [PAG,MUL] (p70)
Concerns raised about functionality of the combined sewer system in Merantun Way due to siltation, which may have contributed to flooding at the junction with Morden Road. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p73)
Merantun Way was one of the locations examined for the 23 June 2016 event; intense, short–duration rainfall produced very high rainfall intensity that contributed to surface water runoff and sewer interactions, overwhelming drainage capacity and causing flooding at this location. (p82)",one location (included among the nine locations examined that reached Merton's Section 19 threshold) (p82),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4089702,-0.1931478,"<div id=""html_9991c41eebfeefcb6d6936e3779bf8de"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Morden Road, Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Merantun Way / Morden Road area was flooded during the reported storm events; flooding was recorded on sewer gauges and associated with recorded sewer return periods at the location. [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Morden Road (the low point at the junction with Merantun Way) received runoff and was flooded to depths that caused the road to be closed; the topography of Morden Road was identified as the main reason for flooding at this location. No internal flooding of properties was reported here. [PAG,MUL] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced significant surface water flooding at the junction with Merantun Way which led to the road being closed (closure reported from the South Wimbledon station). Runoff from surcharging gullies on Morden Road contributed to the flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Morden Road, paired with Merantun Way in the report, experienced intense short&ndash;duration rainfall during the 23 June 2016 event that led to surface water runoff overwhelming drainage and sewer systems, contributing to flooding at this location. (p82)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>depth 300 to 600 mm (23rd June 2016); depth 600 to 1000 mm (30th July 2017 and 9th August 2017); durations ~12 hours (23rd June 2016) and ~6 hours (30th July 2017 and 9th August 2017) [MUL] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one location (included among the nine locations examined that reached Merton's Section 19 threshold) (p82)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Morden Road, Merton",England,"The Merantun Way / Morden Road area was flooded during the reported storm events; flooding was recorded on sewer gauges and associated with recorded sewer return periods at the location. [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Morden Road (the low point at the junction with Merantun Way) received runoff and was flooded to depths that caused the road to be closed; the topography of Morden Road was identified as the main reason for flooding at this location. No internal flooding of properties was reported here. [PAG,MUL] (p70)
Experienced significant surface water flooding at the junction with Merantun Way which led to the road being closed (closure reported from the South Wimbledon station). Runoff from surcharging gullies on Morden Road contributed to the flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p73)
