GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.403813,-0.1914687,"<div id=""html_9eae3021372fa01882f42da4f74b9a64"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kenley Road, Morden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding recorded at Kenley Road car park as indicated by the nearest TWUL sewer gauge. Estimated maximum storm return periods: 1 in 21 year (June 2016), 1 in 2 year (July 2017), 1 in 5 year (August 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The car park experienced flooding during all three reported events; limited information is available so the precise impacts to the car park (extent, depth, damage) are not determined in the record. [MUL] (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Kenley Road, Morden",England,"Flooding recorded at Kenley Road car park as indicated by the nearest TWUL sewer gauge. Estimated maximum storm return periods: 1 in 21 year (June 2016), 1 in 2 year (July 2017), 1 in 5 year (August 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
The car park experienced flooding during all three reported events; limited information is available so the precise impacts to the car park (extent, depth, damage) are not determined in the record. [MUL] (p78)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4199878,-0.2199647,"<div id=""html_26913efec99d9daea512369538c3b512"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hillside, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred within the ward associated with Worple Road and Wimbledon Hill Road on 23 June 2016, 29&ndash;30 July 2017 and 09 August 2017 (recorded sewer return periods: 1 in 21 yr; 1 in 2 yr; 1 in 5 yr respectively). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Hillside, Raynes Park",England,"Flooding occurred within the ward associated with Worple Road and Wimbledon Hill Road on 23 June 2016, 29–30 July 2017 and 09 August 2017 (recorded sewer return periods: 1 in 21 yr; 1 in 2 yr; 1 in 5 yr respectively). [TAB,MUL] (p21)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4116812,-0.2000799,"<div id=""html_cee0f7e2dcf3aa2535bb21fabf8bb22d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dorset Road, Merton Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dorset Road experienced flooding as recorded in the report (sewer gauge&ndash;derived return periods are reported for the relevant storm events). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ponding across the carriageway on the section between Kingston Road and Erridge Road, with greatest depths adjacent to several dropped&ndash;kerb crossovers. Flooding occurred during each of the three reported rainfall events; sewer and highway drainage issues (including tree root ingress and broken pipes) are believed to have contributed. Local gullies were targeted for priority cleansing (southern section added after 2016) and CCTV surveys have been recommended. [MUL] (p75)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three rainfall events; TWUL&ndash;estimated return periods of 1 in 21 year, 1 in 2 year and 1 in 5 year for the incidents [MUL] (p75)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Dorset Road, Merton Park",England,"Dorset Road experienced flooding as recorded in the report (sewer gauge–derived return periods are reported for the relevant storm events). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Surface water ponding across the carriageway on the section between Kingston Road and Erridge Road, with greatest depths adjacent to several dropped–kerb crossovers. Flooding occurred during each of the three reported rainfall events; sewer and highway drainage issues (including tree root ingress and broken pipes) are believed to have contributed. Local gullies were targeted for priority cleansing (southern section added after 2016) and CCTV surveys have been recommended. [MUL] (p75)","three rainfall events; TWUL–estimated return periods of 1 in 21 year, 1 in 2 year and 1 in 5 year for the incidents [MUL] (p75)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4181341,-0.178677,"<div id=""html_2eca8487a76d3d977d3f04397a507d9b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Baltic Close, Colliers Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Baltic Close is recorded in the report as a flooded location (recorded sewer return period shown for the June 2016 event). [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Baltic Close during the June 2016 incident; the location flooded once but did not meet Merton's Section 19 threshold. (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding of the basement of a commercial property caused by combined sewer surcharge following increased surface water runoff from High Street Colliers Wood. Flooding began ~09:00 on 23 Jun 2016 and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Combined sewer network back&ndash;flow and surface water/runoff diverted from the highway entered the basement of a private/commercial property on Baltic Close, causing basement flooding. [PAG] (p77)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 incident (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one commercial property; maximum depth approximately 600 mm; lasted about 12 hours [PAG] (p76)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Baltic Close, Colliers Wood",England,"Baltic Close is recorded in the report as a flooded location (recorded sewer return period shown for the June 2016 event). [TAB] (p21)
Flooding occurred at Baltic Close during the June 2016 incident; the location flooded once but did not meet Merton's Section 19 threshold. (p75)
Significant flooding of the basement of a commercial property caused by combined sewer surcharge following increased surface water runoff from High Street Colliers Wood. Flooding began ~09:00 on 23 Jun 2016 and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p76)
Combined sewer network back–flow and surface water/runoff diverted from the highway entered the basement of a private/commercial property on Baltic Close, causing basement flooding. [PAG] (p77)","1 incident (p75)
one commercial property; maximum depth approximately 600 mm; lasted about 12 hours [PAG] (p76)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.41139,-0.2206129,"<div id=""html_96b43fa92a200d728711e59219279a29"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Abbott Avenue, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Abbott Avenue (including Dundonald Church) experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding on 23 Jun 2016 and 29&ndash;30 Jul 2017; recorded sewer return periods were 1&ndash;in&ndash;24 yr (Jun 2016) and 1&ndash;in&ndash;3 yr (Jul 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer surcharged at the lowest point of the catchment causing internal flooding of properties at the western end adjacent to the pumping station; during the July 2017 incident the pump failed, prolonging flooding and resulting in internal property damage. [MUL] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A manhole surcharged causing internal flooding to properties at the western end of Abbott Avenue adjacent to the pumping station; Dundonald Church experienced internal flooding. Post&ndash;event imagery (approx. one week after June 2016) shows residual foul water, mud and debris on the carriageway. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local surface water and foul sewer network routed significant flows along Abbott Avenue during the recorded incidents. A 225 mm surface water sewer and a storage tank (500 mm x 1250 mm) convey flow north&ndash;east along Abbott Avenue before being diverted south into Kingston Road via an existing footpath. Two foul sewers (each 450 mm) join at Abbott Avenue and connect into a rectangular sewer (660 mm x 1015 mm); there is an approximately 30 m section adjacent to the Dundonald Church with dimensions 991 mm x 661 mm. Following a flooding incident in 2016, gullies on Abbott Avenue were added to the higher&ndash;priority cleansing programme and cleaned at least once per year for 2016/17 and 2017/18; gullies had last been cleaned in February 2017 prior to the 2017 incident. [PAG,MUL] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Abbott Avenue experienced flooding incidents as part of local surface&ndash;water / urban flooding. The area slopes toward Abbott Avenue and parts of the south side are shown at high/medium surface&ndash;water flood risk. LLFA discussions confirmed flooding incidents in the area in three consecutive years (2016&ndash;2018), the most significant in 2016. Gullies on Abbott Avenue were not on the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme prior to the 2016 incident (cleaned at least once every two years under the standard contract) and were added to annual cleansing programmes for 2016/17 and 2017/18; gullies were last cleaned in February 2017 prior to the 2017 incident. [PAG,MUL] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Subject to surface water/sewer flooding incidents: contractor clean&ndash;up works and confirmation the pumping station was operational (attended 28 Jun 2016); added to high&ndash;priority gully cleansing programme; gullies cleaned (Jan/Feb 2017); post&ndash;July 2017 network investigations including CCTV survey, manhole inspections ('lift and look') and sewer cleaning; pump station arrangement reviewed and network modelling undertaken. [TAB,MUL] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer network backed up and surcharged at the lowest point of the catchment at Abbott Avenue, with surcharging discharging out of a foul manhole where flows from under the railway converge with those from the pumping station. Debris (including ladders and bricks) was found in the foul sewer between the pumping station and Abbott Avenue causing blockages. The local sewage pumping station experienced an electrical failure and was out of service for 12 hours during the July 2017 incident. [TAB] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration rainfall on 23 June 2016 caused localised surface water flooding on Abbott Avenue, affecting the roadway and immediate surroundings. [TAB] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 incidents reached Section 19 threshold (2016 and 2017); pump failure for 12 hours; flooding lasted about six hours (July 2017) [MUL] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Abbott Avenue, Raynes Park",England,"Abbott Avenue (including Dundonald Church) experienced sewer–related flooding on 23 Jun 2016 and 29–30 Jul 2017; recorded sewer return periods were 1–in–24 yr (Jun 2016) and 1–in–3 yr (Jul 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Foul sewer surcharged at the lowest point of the catchment causing internal flooding of properties at the western end adjacent to the pumping station; during the July 2017 incident the pump failed, prolonging flooding and resulting in internal property damage. [MUL] (p42)
A manhole surcharged causing internal flooding to properties at the western end of Abbott Avenue adjacent to the pumping station; Dundonald Church experienced internal flooding. Post–event imagery (approx. one week after June 2016) shows residual foul water, mud and debris on the carriageway. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p43)
Local surface water and foul sewer network routed significant flows along Abbott Avenue during the recorded incidents. A 225 mm surface water sewer and a storage tank (500 mm x 1250 mm) convey flow north–east along Abbott Avenue before being diverted south into Kingston Road via an existing footpath. Two foul sewers (each 450 mm) join at Abbott Avenue and connect into a rectangular sewer (660 mm x 1015 mm); there is an approximately 30 m section adjacent to the Dundonald Church with dimensions 991 mm x 661 mm. Following a flooding incident in 2016, gullies on Abbott Avenue were added to the higher–priority cleansing programme and cleaned at least once per year for 2016/17 and 2017/18; gullies had last been cleaned in February 2017 prior to the 2017 incident. [PAG,MUL] (p44)
Abbott Avenue experienced flooding incidents as part of local surface–water / urban flooding. The area slopes toward Abbott Avenue and parts of the south side are shown at high/medium surface–water flood risk. LLFA discussions confirmed flooding incidents in the area in three consecutive years (2016–2018), the most significant in 2016. Gullies on Abbott Avenue were not on the high–priority cleansing programme prior to the 2016 incident (cleaned at least once every two years under the standard contract) and were added to annual cleansing programmes for 2016/17 and 2017/18; gullies were last cleaned in February 2017 prior to the 2017 incident. [PAG,MUL] (p45)
Subject to surface water/sewer flooding incidents: contractor clean–up works and confirmation the pumping station was operational (attended 28 Jun 2016); added to high–priority gully cleansing programme; gullies cleaned (Jan/Feb 2017); post–July 2017 network investigations including CCTV survey, manhole inspections ('lift and look') and sewer cleaning; pump station arrangement reviewed and network modelling undertaken. [TAB,MUL] (p46)
Foul sewer network backed up and surcharged at the lowest point of the catchment at Abbott Avenue, with surcharging discharging out of a foul manhole where flows from under the railway converge with those from the pumping station. Debris (including ladders and bricks) was found in the foul sewer between the pumping station and Abbott Avenue causing blockages. The local sewage pumping station experienced an electrical failure and was out of service for 12 hours during the July 2017 incident. [TAB] (p47)
Intense short–duration rainfall on 23 June 2016 caused localised surface water flooding on Abbott Avenue, affecting the roadway and immediate surroundings. [TAB] (p88)",2 incidents reached Section 19 threshold (2016 and 2017); pump failure for 12 hours; flooding lasted about six hours (July 2017) [MUL] (p42),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3995208,-0.2424677,"<div id=""html_4607d795904708202fb05a2afffb98da"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Burlington Road, West Barnes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Burlington Road / Shannon Corner underpass was flooded during all three rainfall events due to heavily silted surface&ndash;water sewers and a limited sewer gradient that caused sewer surcharge and backflow. Location is known to flood regularly; Merton recorded no properties as affected. [PAG,MUL] (p79)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>No properties affected; TWUL estimated the three incidents as having return periods of 1 in 30, 1 in 2 and 1 in 4 years. [PAG,MUL] (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Burlington Road, West Barnes",England,"The Burlington Road / Shannon Corner underpass was flooded during all three rainfall events due to heavily silted surface–water sewers and a limited sewer gradient that caused sewer surcharge and backflow. Location is known to flood regularly; Merton recorded no properties as affected. [PAG,MUL] (p79)","No properties affected; TWUL estimated the three incidents as having return periods of 1 in 30, 1 in 2 and 1 in 4 years. [PAG,MUL] (p79)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3994765,-0.2437999,"<div id=""html_562cf6a7c06a041853b6aba4baaff760"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Shannon Corner, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction beneath the A3 flyover at Shannon Corner flooded during all three events as surface&ndash;water sewers there were heavily silted (notably under the central island), producing surcharge and backflow issues. Cleansing and further sewer investigation were recommended. [PAG,MUL] (p79)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>No properties affected. [PAG,MUL] (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Shannon Corner, London Borough of Merton",England,"The junction beneath the A3 flyover at Shannon Corner flooded during all three events as surface–water sewers there were heavily silted (notably under the central island), producing surcharge and backflow issues. Cleansing and further sewer investigation were recommended. [PAG,MUL] (p79)","No properties affected. [PAG,MUL] (p79)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.391338,-0.2227162,"<div id=""html_a8f0257c4c5ad4d4b8e9a33a95ebce32"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grand Drive, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway ponding was observed on Bushey Road at the junction with Grand Drive during the event, indicating local surface&ndash;water flooding and a potential network capacity or hydraulic overload at that junction. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Grand Drive, London Borough of Merton",England,"Highway ponding was observed on Bushey Road at the junction with Grand Drive during the event, indicating local surface–water flooding and a potential network capacity or hydraulic overload at that junction. (p33)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4159021,-0.2331404,"<div id=""html_c14f1ac84dc3438318ebaa8eb2a5650e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pepys Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers flow north&ndash;south through Pepys Road and converge with other pipes at the Worple Road junction; gullies on Pepys Road were included in the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme following previous flooding. The road was within the area affected by the 2016 and 2017 incidents which caused transport risk and flooding of commercial properties. [MUL] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the 23 June 2016 surface water flooding incident, gullies along Pepys Road were added to Merton's high&ndash;priority gully cleansing programme. Gullies had last been cleaned in December 2015 and were subsequently cleaned in January 2017 (with reactive call&ndash;outs before and after the incidents). [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Pepys Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"Sewers flow north–south through Pepys Road and converge with other pipes at the Worple Road junction; gullies on Pepys Road were included in the high–priority cleansing programme following previous flooding. The road was within the area affected by the 2016 and 2017 incidents which caused transport risk and flooding of commercial properties. [MUL] (p37)
Following the 23 June 2016 surface water flooding incident, gullies along Pepys Road were added to Merton's high–priority gully cleansing programme. Gullies had last been cleaned in December 2015 and were subsequently cleaned in January 2017 (with reactive call–outs before and after the incidents). [IMG,PAG] (p38)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4102003,-0.2266002,"<div id=""html_c6a2c481d2358f41c0bb3c57f8eb03c6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wyke Road, Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A surface water sewer from Wyke Road discharges into the main 1050 mm sewer near Raynes Park railway bridge; gullies along Wyke Road were added to the cleansing programme after the 2016 incident. Wyke Road lay within the drainage catchment affected by the 2016 and 2017 flooding incidents. [MUL] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Wyke Road, Cottenham Park",England,A surface water sewer from Wyke Road discharges into the main 1050 mm sewer near Raynes Park railway bridge; gullies along Wyke Road were added to the cleansing programme after the 2016 incident. Wyke Road lay within the drainage catchment affected by the 2016 and 2017 flooding incidents. [MUL] (p37),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4126591,-0.2271848,"<div id=""html_655ebda70da1313abfe4c223c537e484"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Langham Road, Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding during the 2016 incident; gullies on Langham Road were subsequently added to Merton's high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and were cleaned in January 2017 in advance of the 2017 incidents. [MUL] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Langham Road, Cottenham Park",England,Experienced flooding during the 2016 incident; gullies on Langham Road were subsequently added to Merton's high–priority cleansing programme and were cleaned in January 2017 in advance of the 2017 incidents. [MUL] (p37),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.414066,-0.2208575,"<div id=""html_a3fab641c6dbd6366c1cf923a3578d1a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Downs Road, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in gully cleaning actions carried out in Jan/Feb 2017 as part of the response to the June 2016 flooding; cleaned gullies to reduce surface water runoff and mitigate future flooding. [TAB] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Lower Downs Road, Raynes Park",England,Included in gully cleaning actions carried out in Jan/Feb 2017 as part of the response to the June 2016 flooding; cleaned gullies to reduce surface water runoff and mitigate future flooding. [TAB] (p46),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4145072,-0.2251223,"<div id=""html_e1a07ce7345d56370bafcf1e65c2a2bc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Arterberry Road, Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Upstream sewer surveys and cleaning works were carried out (October 2018) as remedial action following the sewer surcharging and flooding associated with the July 2017 incident. [TAB] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Arterberry Road, Cottenham Park",England,Upstream sewer surveys and cleaning works were carried out (October 2018) as remedial action following the sewer surcharging and flooding associated with the July 2017 incident. [TAB] (p47),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4143137,-0.2233892,"<div id=""html_843b0b92acb44f5d2248a2e035ab7cff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Crescent Road, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Upstream sewer surveys and cleaning works were carried out (October 2018) as remedial action following the sewer surcharging and flooding associated with the July 2017 incident. [TAB] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Crescent Road, Raynes Park",England,Upstream sewer surveys and cleaning works were carried out (October 2018) as remedial action following the sewer surcharging and flooding associated with the July 2017 incident. [TAB] (p47),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4258196,-0.2014429,"<div id=""html_3b28e43889bf62283b7d9860f467f1c4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alexandra Road, Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Forms the local low point at the junction with St George's Road and Wimbledon Hill Road and is identified as part of a high&ndash;risk area where surface water runoff collects, contributing to ponding during flooding incidents. [MUL] (p51)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Alexandra Road, Merton",England,"Forms the local low point at the junction with St George's Road and Wimbledon Hill Road and is identified as part of a high–risk area where surface water runoff collects, contributing to ponding during flooding incidents. [MUL] (p51)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4227608,-0.2098911,"<div id=""html_44023b4a08b33d654b0e2df85726f426"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mansel Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A historic flooding incident is recorded (1981) believed to be a combination of surface water and sewer flooding. The report notes that records suggest similar consequences occurred in the 2016 and 2017 events (implying Mansel Road was affected in those episodes). [MUL] (p52)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Mansel Road, London Borough of Merton",England,A historic flooding incident is recorded (1981) believed to be a combination of surface water and sewer flooding. The report notes that records suggest similar consequences occurred in the 2016 and 2017 events (implying Mansel Road was affected in those episodes). [MUL] (p52),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4190503,-0.1873187,"<div id=""html_2eba5f78a222fff88354834e44a4e6a4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Haydon's Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Haydon's Road gullies were part of the council's high priority cleansing programme and were last cleaned in March 2016 prior to the flooding incident; surface water runoff collected toward the Effra Road/Haydon's Road low point during the incident. [PAG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Haydon's Road, London Borough of Merton",England,Haydon's Road gullies were part of the council's high priority cleansing programme and were last cleaned in March 2016 prior to the flooding incident; surface water runoff collected toward the Effra Road/Haydon's Road low point during the incident. [PAG] (p56),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3995497,-0.2354986,"<div id=""html_f1fdc3b48638a9874e77fbbd7649eef3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Estella Avenue, West Barnes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A 225 mm sewer under Estella Avenue connected into the local surface water sewer network and discharged into the Pyl Brook culvert immediately northwest of the properties that flooded; these connections contributed to surcharge/backflow into the culverted watercourse during the 2016 and 2017 flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Estella Avenue, West Barnes",England,"A 225 mm sewer under Estella Avenue connected into the local surface water sewer network and discharged into the Pyl Brook culvert immediately northwest of the properties that flooded; these connections contributed to surcharge/backflow into the culverted watercourse during the 2016 and 2017 flooding incidents. [PAG,MUL] (p60)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.400709,-0.233575,"<div id=""html_2a4bf530808f84c5a3a77ea71a5b3ada"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Westway, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties adjacent to the Westway were identified as being at risk from, and potentially affected by, capacity issues in the culverted Pyl Brook behind the properties. If the culvert was at capacity it may have been interconnected with the surface water sewer network and contributed to flooding during the 2016 and 2017 incidents. The EA were recommended to survey the culvert (dimensions and invert levels) to establish its condition and capacity. [MUL] (p64)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Westway, London Borough of Merton",England,"Properties adjacent to the Westway were identified as being at risk from, and potentially affected by, capacity issues in the culverted Pyl Brook behind the properties. If the culvert was at capacity it may have been interconnected with the surface water sewer network and contributed to flooding during the 2016 and 2017 incidents. The EA were recommended to survey the culvert (dimensions and invert levels) to establish its condition and capacity. [MUL] (p64)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4406227,-0.1955395,"<div id=""html_30b86d7c9715f5e1d05e69924f454d61"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ravensbury Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reports of foul sewage on the carriageway at the junction with Acuba Road (indicative of sewer&ndash;related flooding/contamination on the road). (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer surcharging occurred at the junction with Acuba Road, causing local flooding at that junction. [MUL] (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Ravensbury Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"Reports of foul sewage on the carriageway at the junction with Acuba Road (indicative of sewer–related flooding/contamination on the road). (p65)
Foul sewer surcharging occurred at the junction with Acuba Road, causing local flooding at that junction. [MUL] (p69)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4113547,-0.1897518,"<div id=""html_a5c38ddc2b4ce66beb684db3abbf914a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Jubilee Way, Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding associated with overcapacity highway gullies at the Morden Road junction with Jubilee Way (runoff and surcharge from gullies contributing to flooding); prompted consideration of a SuDS retrofit to reduce surface water impact. [TAB] (p73)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Jubilee Way, Merton",England,Surface water flooding associated with overcapacity highway gullies at the Morden Road junction with Jubilee Way (runoff and surcharge from gullies contributing to flooding); prompted consideration of a SuDS retrofit to reduce surface water impact. [TAB] (p73),,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4177698,-0.1785501,"<div id=""html_6125463d0748e56d0ffc96f0e6a61836"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street Colliers Wood, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Increased surface water runoff and combined sewer surcharge in this street contributed to the combined sewer flooding incident that affected Baltic Close on 23 Jun 2016. Historic flooding incidents along High Street Colliers Wood occurred in 2000 and 2007; the 2007 event led to closure of Colliers Wood London Underground station and internal damage to a nearby library. [MUL] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Existing highway flow path and carriageway levels on High Street Colliers Wood diverted surface water runoff toward a private property (junction with Baltic Close), contributing to the basement flooding incident. [PAG] (p77)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"High Street Colliers Wood, London Borough of Merton",England,"Increased surface water runoff and combined sewer surcharge in this street contributed to the combined sewer flooding incident that affected Baltic Close on 23 Jun 2016. Historic flooding incidents along High Street Colliers Wood occurred in 2000 and 2007; the 2007 event led to closure of Colliers Wood London Underground station and internal damage to a nearby library. [MUL] (p76)
Existing highway flow path and carriageway levels on High Street Colliers Wood diverted surface water runoff toward a private property (junction with Baltic Close), contributing to the basement flooding incident. [PAG] (p77)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.41086985,-0.18809708858824303,"<div id=""html_c8656f9835ec058c7034c9691e5d9744"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during three intense rainfall events; the LLFA investigated threshold&ndash;reaching sites and analysed additional flooded locations across the borough. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations within the borough were heavily affected by intense rainfall leading to localised surface&ndash;water / urban flooding, causing disruption to local roads, rail and properties. These incidents triggered a Section 19 investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Three locations within the borough were heavily affected by intense thunderstorms and associated surface water/flash flooding, reaching the Section 19 investigation threshold. [IMG,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall produced peak hourly depths across local gauges and led to surface&ndash;water / urban flooding across parts of the borough, causing localized flooding of streets and properties. [PAG,MUL] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the three rainfall events nine locations within the borough reached the council's flood investigation threshold; multiple localities experienced localised inundation and investigations/actions were recorded by responsible managing authorities. [PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Across the borough there were multiple flood incidents during the three rainfall events: nine locations reached Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold on at least one occasion; additional locations also flooded. The events triggered investigations, chaptered analysis of flood mechanisms and collaborative recommendations between risk management authorities. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during the reported events; sewer gauge data was used to estimate storm return periods for fourteen flooded locations. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area around the junction of Whatley Avenue and Martin Way within the borough experienced surface water flooding, with ponding at a local low point and properties near the junction affected; highway gullies on the roads were on a high priority cleansing programme and had been last cleaned in January&ndash;February 2016 prior to the incident. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the junction of Martin Way and Whatley Avenue caused by a 750 mm surface water sewer running full and the local foul sewer network running full; additional flow backed up into gullies and low&ndash;level properties. Runoff from Cannon Hill Common and Joseph Hood Recreation Ground slopes down to Whatley Avenue, exacerbating the local drainage surcharge. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The council is responsible for implementing recommended actions (detention basin, SuDS, new gullies, increased street cleansing and planting features) to reduce runoff and local sewer overload that caused flooding of properties and roads. [PAG,MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced significant surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding in the Apostles area (Raynes Park ward) during the June 2016 event: local drainage and trunk sewers were overwhelmed, contributing to prolonged inundation and internal property flooding within the affected neighbourhood. [PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations within the borough reached the council's Section 19 threshold for flooding, indicating localised surface&ndash;water/sewer&ndash;related flooding across the area. [IMG,PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area of Merton (Bushey Mead/Dupont Road area) experienced surface water/highway flooding associated with the June 2016 event; the council undertook gully cleansing as part of its response. [IMG,PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred in the borough (notably Dupont Road) due to a blockage in the surface water sewer; response actions included gully cleansing and jetting to clear the blockage and resolve the flooding. Thames Water attended multiple times to check the sewer. [TAB,PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough contained a flooding hotspot at Coombe Lane by Raynes Park railway bridge that reached the investigation (Section 19) threshold in all three events. Local topography, a large contributing sewer catchment draining down to Coombe Lane and low levels beneath the railway bridge contributed to repeated inundation. The council carried out/led CCTV investigations, collaborative drainage modelling with TWUL, and identification of SuDS and storm&ndash;relief/bypass options to reduce inflows to the main surface water sewer and provide overflow safety. [PAG,MUL] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood incidents across the borough reached the Section 19 threshold: internal flooding of commercial properties and transport disruption (including closure of the Raynes Park railway bridge underpass). [PAG,MUL] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced localised surface&ndash;water and sewer&ndash;related flooding during the incidents. Highway gullies in Coombe Lane and the south part of Pepys Road were a focus for cleansing before and after the incidents; four gullies directly beneath Raynes Park railway bridge were initially inaccessible and were not cleaned prior to the 2016/2017 incidents but were cleaned in April 2017. Flooding incidents caused risk to transport and flooding of commercial properties within the borough. [PAG,MUL] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding in the Cottenham Park critical drainage area prompted gully cleansing, council liaison with TWUL and subsequent sewer cleaning and CCTV inspections by Thames Water after the July 2017 event. [TAB,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The local sewer network in the borough received rainfall that probably exceeded its design capacity. The foul sewer surcharged at the lowest point of the catchment at Abbott Avenue (Dundonald ward) during the 2016 and 2017 events; during the 2017 incident an existing pump at Abbott Avenue failed for 12 hours, leading to local flooding. The borough is recommended to investigate pumps and sewer capacities in the affected catchment. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across the borough experienced sewer surcharge and surface water runoff that exceeded the sewer network capacity during the reported events, leading to internal flooding of properties and several locations reaching Merton's Section 19 threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at multiple locations in the borough reached the council's Section 19 reporting threshold; Abbott Avenue was one of the affected locations where sewer surcharge caused internal property flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The local authority (Merton) changed the gully cleansing priority for Abbott Avenue after the 2016 flooding incident: gullies that had previously been on a standard (at least once every two years) cleansing regime were moved to an at&ndash;least&ndash;annual cleansing programme for 2016/17 and 2017/18. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Merton area (drainage catchment DC27) experienced surface&ndash;water flooding incidents in consecutive years (2016&ndash;2018), with the most significant incident in 2016. Flood&ndash;risk mapping (Figure 7.3) identifies parts of the local area, including Abbott Avenue and parts of Lower Downs Road, as high/medium risk of surface&ndash;water flooding. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced surface water and sewer flooding affecting streets including Abbott Avenue and Lower Downs Road, prompting gully cleansing, pump station checks and sewer investigations and cleaning. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the borough (the Hillside/Wimbledon town centre area including Worple Road and Wimbledon Hill Road and adjacent streets) were affected by flooding during multiple events, reaching Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the borough (central Wimbledon area) experienced surface water and associated sewer flooding; mapping shows predominant local risk from surface water runoff and predicted increased extents on key streets. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Town&ndash;centre surface water flooding occurred during prolonged rainfall (Aug 2017), with rapid overland flow down steep adjacent roads causing ponding on Worple Road and St. George's Road, runoff from a multi&ndash;storey car park reaching pavements and entering shops, and surface water sewers becoming overwhelmed/surcharging. [PAG,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The local area experienced a surface&ndash;water flooding incident driven by topography that routes runoff to the Effra Road/Haydon's Road low point; parts of Merton (Effra Road, Haydon's Road and nearby properties) were affected or identified in connection with the incident. [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at West Barnes Lane and adjoining streets reached the threshold for a Section 19 investigation for the June 2016 and July 2017 incidents; flooding was focused around the north&ndash;eastern end of Adela Avenue and across the highway junction with West Barnes Lane. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The London Borough of Merton carried out a high&ndash;priority highway gully cleansing programme for Adela Avenue and West Barnes Lane (gullies cleaned Dec 2015/Jan 2016); despite this, surface water/sewer flooding occurred in the locality during the 2016 and 2017 incidents and is the subject of the Section 19 investigation. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough's historic incident records include surface&ndash;water and fluvial&ndash;interaction flooding at local streets (for example Seaforth Avenue and West Barnes Lane on 20 July 2007) and records a response to surface&ndash;water flooding on 23 June 2016. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The local authority carried out cleansing of highway gullies at the affected junction (Adela Avenue and West Barnes Lane) between December 2016 and March 2017 and again in February&ndash;March 2018 as part of the response to local surface water flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas within the borough reached the Section 19 threshold (e.g. Mount Road / Acuba Road / Brooklands Avenue in June 2016). The council carried out kerb and highway drainage improvement works (including adding gullies and carriageway resurfacing) which have reduced local flood risk. [PAG,MUL] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Wimbledon Park ward (covering Mount Road, Acuba Road and Brooklands Avenue) reached the council's Section 19 threshold. The event started at approximately 09:00 BST and lasted about 12 hours; internal flooding affected properties (including residential basements) and foul sewage was reported on the road at the junction of Acuba Road and Ravensbury Road. [PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The council's highway gullies in the area were implicated in a local flooding incident; gullies were part of the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and, after the flooding incident, gullies at the junction of Mount Road and Acuba Road were added to the high priority cleansing programme for 2016/17 and 2017/18. [IMG,PAG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced multiple incidents of sewer and surface&ndash;water flooding (including foul sewer surcharging at Acuba/Ravensbury junction and basement flooding on Brooklands Avenue). Merton carried out assessment and made recommendations including engaging with TWUL on sewer re&ndash;benching and advising property&ndash;level resilience for affected basements. [PAG,MUL] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Merantun Way / Morden Road location in the borough reached the Council's Section 19 threshold on three occasions (one event on 23 Jun 2016 and two events in 2017), producing highway inundation and closures at a critical TfL&ndash;managed junction. [PAG,MUL] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported the flooding to TfL and Thames Water during the incidents; significant flooding in the area led to a closure of Morden Road (from South Wimbledon station). [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced surface water flooding across multiple locations: Morden Road was closed due to significant flooding; Dorset Road (between Kingston Road and Erridge Road in Merton Park ward) experienced surface water ponding across the carriageway during the events, with greatest depths adjacent to dropped kerb crossovers. The council raised concerns about sewer functionality and asset maintenance contributing to flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p74)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 23 June 2016 combined sewer flooding following increased surface water runoff from High Street, Colliers Wood led to significant flooding of the basement of a commercial property within the borough. [PAG] (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced a combined sewer surcharge / surface water flooding incident on 23 Jun 2016 that produced basement flooding of a commercial property in Baltic Close (max depth ~600 mm). The borough also records historic flooding incidents in 2000 and 2007 (the 2007 event disrupted Colliers Wood London Underground station). [PAG,MUL] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the borough experienced combined sewer backflow and surface water runoff incidents that caused property basement flooding (for example the Baltic Close property) and prompted sewer inspections. [PAG] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Numerous flooding incidents occurred across the borough during the reported events &mdash; affecting communities and infrastructure across multiple locations. Nine locations experienced incidents that met Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold and a further five locations flooded without reaching that threshold; incidents prompted actions by Risk Management Authorities before, during and after the events and led to recommendations for drainage improvements and communication. [PAG,MUL] (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Merton (the borough) experienced numerous surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding incidents in the three events. Sewers reached hydraulic capacity and were overwhelmed by the extreme rainfall; misconnections and blockages aggravated flooding in local drainage networks and resulted in multiple locations meeting the Section 19 threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p82)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough's sewer/drainage network was unable to handle the extreme rainfall and was overwhelmed, resulting in sewer and surface&ndash;water flooding across the borough. [PAG] (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local sewer/drainage capacity was exceeded during the June 2016 extreme rainfall, with the sewer network unable to handle the flows and thus being overwhelmed; this prompted recommendations to re&ndash;evaluate the drainage network on a catchment basis. (p84)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>nine locations reached Section 19 threshold on 23 June 2016; five locations reached Section 19 threshold on 29&ndash;30 July 2017; three locations reached Section 19 threshold on 9 August 2017; five additional locations flooded but did not reach Section 19 thresholds [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 locations reached Section 19 threshold (23 Jun 2016); 5 locations (29 Jul 2017 &ndash; 30 Jul 2017); 3 locations (09 Aug 2017) [PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 locations [IMG,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 locations [PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 locations; 5 locations; 4 TWUL sewer gauges [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 flooding locations [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately ten properties; 36 hours; 300&ndash;450 mm [PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>nine locations reached Section 19 threshold (including Dupont Road) [IMG,PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately four commercial properties; flood depth 600&ndash;1000 mm (at Raynes Park); June event duration approximately 12 hours [PAG,MUL] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 locations reached Section 19 threshold across the borough; 3 distinct reported incidents [PAG,MUL] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 locations reached Section 19 threshold [PAG,MUL] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than two properties [PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property (basement) flooded; maximum flood depth approximately 600 mm [PAG] (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one commercial property; maximum depth approximately 600 mm; closure of a tube station for nine hours [PAG,MUL] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 locations; 9 locations; 5 locations [PAG,MUL] (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>fourteen locations; nine locations reached Merton's Section 19 threshold; five additional locations flooded but did not reach the threshold [PAG,MUL] (p82)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>designed for up to 1 in 30 year rainfall events; June 2016 was reported as a 1 in 60 year event (p84)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,London Borough of Merton,England,"Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during three intense rainfall events; the LLFA investigated threshold–reaching sites and analysed additional flooded locations across the borough. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)
Multiple locations within the borough were heavily affected by intense rainfall leading to localised surface–water / urban flooding, causing disruption to local roads, rail and properties. These incidents triggered a Section 19 investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p16)
Three locations within the borough were heavily affected by intense thunderstorms and associated surface water/flash flooding, reaching the Section 19 investigation threshold. [IMG,PAG] (p17)
Intense, short–duration rainfall produced peak hourly depths across local gauges and led to surface–water / urban flooding across parts of the borough, causing localized flooding of streets and properties. [PAG,MUL] (p18)
During the three rainfall events nine locations within the borough reached the council's flood investigation threshold; multiple localities experienced localised inundation and investigations/actions were recorded by responsible managing authorities. [PAG,MUL] (p19)
Across the borough there were multiple flood incidents during the three rainfall events: nine locations reached Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold on at least one occasion; additional locations also flooded. The events triggered investigations, chaptered analysis of flood mechanisms and collaborative recommendations between risk management authorities. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p20)
Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during the reported events; sewer gauge data was used to estimate storm return periods for fourteen flooded locations. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p21)
The area around the junction of Whatley Avenue and Martin Way within the borough experienced surface water flooding, with ponding at a local low point and properties near the junction affected; highway gullies on the roads were on a high priority cleansing programme and had been last cleaned in January–February 2016 prior to the incident. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p24)
Flooding occurred at the junction of Martin Way and Whatley Avenue caused by a 750 mm surface water sewer running full and the local foul sewer network running full; additional flow backed up into gullies and low–level properties. Runoff from Cannon Hill Common and Joseph Hood Recreation Ground slopes down to Whatley Avenue, exacerbating the local drainage surcharge. [PAG] (p27)
The council is responsible for implementing recommended actions (detention basin, SuDS, new gullies, increased street cleansing and planting features) to reduce runoff and local sewer overload that caused flooding of properties and roads. [PAG,MUL] (p28)
The borough experienced significant surface water and sewer–related flooding in the Apostles area (Raynes Park ward) during the June 2016 event: local drainage and trunk sewers were overwhelmed, contributing to prolonged inundation and internal property flooding within the affected neighbourhood. [PAG] (p29)
Multiple locations within the borough reached the council's Section 19 threshold for flooding, indicating localised surface–water/sewer–related flooding across the area. [IMG,PAG] (p30)
Area of Merton (Bushey Mead/Dupont Road area) experienced surface water/highway flooding associated with the June 2016 event; the council undertook gully cleansing as part of its response. [IMG,PAG] (p31)
Surface water flooding occurred in the borough (notably Dupont Road) due to a blockage in the surface water sewer; response actions included gully cleansing and jetting to clear the blockage and resolve the flooding. Thames Water attended multiple times to check the sewer. [TAB,PAG] (p32)
The borough contained a flooding hotspot at Coombe Lane by Raynes Park railway bridge that reached the investigation (Section 19) threshold in all three events. Local topography, a large contributing sewer catchment draining down to Coombe Lane and low levels beneath the railway bridge contributed to repeated inundation. The council carried out/led CCTV investigations, collaborative drainage modelling with TWUL, and identification of SuDS and storm–relief/bypass options to reduce inflows to the main surface water sewer and provide overflow safety. [PAG,MUL] (p34)
Multiple flood incidents across the borough reached the Section 19 threshold: internal flooding of commercial properties and transport disruption (including closure of the Raynes Park railway bridge underpass). [PAG,MUL] (p36)
The borough experienced localised surface–water and sewer–related flooding during the incidents. Highway gullies in Coombe Lane and the south part of Pepys Road were a focus for cleansing before and after the incidents; four gullies directly beneath Raynes Park railway bridge were initially inaccessible and were not cleaned prior to the 2016/2017 incidents but were cleaned in April 2017. Flooding incidents caused risk to transport and flooding of commercial properties within the borough. [PAG,MUL] (p37)
Surface water flooding in the Cottenham Park critical drainage area prompted gully cleansing, council liaison with TWUL and subsequent sewer cleaning and CCTV inspections by Thames Water after the July 2017 event. [TAB,PAG] (p40)
The local sewer network in the borough received rainfall that probably exceeded its design capacity. The foul sewer surcharged at the lowest point of the catchment at Abbott Avenue (Dundonald ward) during the 2016 and 2017 events; during the 2017 incident an existing pump at Abbott Avenue failed for 12 hours, leading to local flooding. The borough is recommended to investigate pumps and sewer capacities in the affected catchment. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p41)
Multiple locations across the borough experienced sewer surcharge and surface water runoff that exceeded the sewer network capacity during the reported events, leading to internal flooding of properties and several locations reaching Merton's Section 19 threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p42)
Flooding at multiple locations in the borough reached the council's Section 19 reporting threshold; Abbott Avenue was one of the affected locations where sewer surcharge caused internal property flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p43)
The local authority (Merton) changed the gully cleansing priority for Abbott Avenue after the 2016 flooding incident: gullies that had previously been on a standard (at least once every two years) cleansing regime were moved to an at–least–annual cleansing programme for 2016/17 and 2017/18. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p44)
The Merton area (drainage catchment DC27) experienced surface–water flooding incidents in consecutive years (2016–2018), with the most significant incident in 2016. Flood–risk mapping (Figure 7.3) identifies parts of the local area, including Abbott Avenue and parts of Lower Downs Road, as high/medium risk of surface–water flooding. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p45)
Experienced surface water and sewer flooding affecting streets including Abbott Avenue and Lower Downs Road, prompting gully cleansing, pump station checks and sewer investigations and cleaning. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p46)
Parts of the borough (the Hillside/Wimbledon town centre area including Worple Road and Wimbledon Hill Road and adjacent streets) were affected by flooding during multiple events, reaching Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p48)
Parts of the borough (central Wimbledon area) experienced surface water and associated sewer flooding; mapping shows predominant local risk from surface water runoff and predicted increased extents on key streets. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)
Town–centre surface water flooding occurred during prolonged rainfall (Aug 2017), with rapid overland flow down steep adjacent roads causing ponding on Worple Road and St. George's Road, runoff from a multi–storey car park reaching pavements and entering shops, and surface water sewers becoming overwhelmed/surcharging. [PAG,MUL] (p54)
The local area experienced a surface–water flooding incident driven by topography that routes runoff to the Effra Road/Haydon's Road low point; parts of Merton (Effra Road, Haydon's Road and nearby properties) were affected or identified in connection with the incident. [IMG,PAG] (p56)
Flooding at West Barnes Lane and adjoining streets reached the threshold for a Section 19 investigation for the June 2016 and July 2017 incidents; flooding was focused around the north–eastern end of Adela Avenue and across the highway junction with West Barnes Lane. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p58)
The London Borough of Merton carried out a high–priority highway gully cleansing programme for Adela Avenue and West Barnes Lane (gullies cleaned Dec 2015/Jan 2016); despite this, surface water/sewer flooding occurred in the locality during the 2016 and 2017 incidents and is the subject of the Section 19 investigation. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p60)
The borough's historic incident records include surface–water and fluvial–interaction flooding at local streets (for example Seaforth Avenue and West Barnes Lane on 20 July 2007) and records a response to surface–water flooding on 23 June 2016. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p62)
The local authority carried out cleansing of highway gullies at the affected junction (Adela Avenue and West Barnes Lane) between December 2016 and March 2017 and again in February–March 2018 as part of the response to local surface water flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p63)
Areas within the borough reached the Section 19 threshold (e.g. Mount Road / Acuba Road / Brooklands Avenue in June 2016). The council carried out kerb and highway drainage improvement works (including adding gullies and carriageway resurfacing) which have reduced local flood risk. [PAG,MUL] (p64)
Flooding in the Wimbledon Park ward (covering Mount Road, Acuba Road and Brooklands Avenue) reached the council's Section 19 threshold. The event started at approximately 09:00 BST and lasted about 12 hours; internal flooding affected properties (including residential basements) and foul sewage was reported on the road at the junction of Acuba Road and Ravensbury Road. [PAG] (p65)
The council's highway gullies in the area were implicated in a local flooding incident; gullies were part of the high–priority cleansing programme and, after the flooding incident, gullies at the junction of Mount Road and Acuba Road were added to the high priority cleansing programme for 2016/17 and 2017/18. [IMG,PAG] (p66)
The borough experienced multiple incidents of sewer and surface–water flooding (including foul sewer surcharging at Acuba/Ravensbury junction and basement flooding on Brooklands Avenue). Merton carried out assessment and made recommendations including engaging with TWUL on sewer re–benching and advising property–level resilience for affected basements. [PAG,MUL] (p69)
The Merantun Way / Morden Road location in the borough reached the Council's Section 19 threshold on three occasions (one event on 23 Jun 2016 and two events in 2017), producing highway inundation and closures at a critical TfL–managed junction. [PAG,MUL] (p70)
Reported the flooding to TfL and Thames Water during the incidents; significant flooding in the area led to a closure of Morden Road (from South Wimbledon station). [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p73)
The borough experienced surface water flooding across multiple locations: Morden Road was closed due to significant flooding; Dorset Road (between Kingston Road and Erridge Road in Merton Park ward) experienced surface water ponding across the carriageway during the events, with greatest depths adjacent to dropped kerb crossovers. The council raised concerns about sewer functionality and asset maintenance contributing to flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p74)
On 23 June 2016 combined sewer flooding following increased surface water runoff from High Street, Colliers Wood led to significant flooding of the basement of a commercial property within the borough. [PAG] (p75)
The borough experienced a combined sewer surcharge / surface water flooding incident on 23 Jun 2016 that produced basement flooding of a commercial property in Baltic Close (max depth ~600 mm). The borough also records historic flooding incidents in 2000 and 2007 (the 2007 event disrupted Colliers Wood London Underground station). [PAG,MUL] (p76)
Parts of the borough experienced combined sewer backflow and surface water runoff incidents that caused property basement flooding (for example the Baltic Close property) and prompted sewer inspections. [PAG] (p77)
Numerous flooding incidents occurred across the borough during the reported events — affecting communities and infrastructure across multiple locations. Nine locations experienced incidents that met Merton's Section 19 investigation threshold and a further five locations flooded without reaching that threshold; incidents prompted actions by Risk Management Authorities before, during and after the events and led to recommendations for drainage improvements and communication. [PAG,MUL] (p81)
Merton (the borough) experienced numerous surface water and sewer–related flooding incidents in the three events. Sewers reached hydraulic capacity and were overwhelmed by the extreme rainfall; misconnections and blockages aggravated flooding in local drainage networks and resulted in multiple locations meeting the Section 19 threshold. [PAG,MUL] (p82)
The borough's sewer/drainage network was unable to handle the extreme rainfall and was overwhelmed, resulting in sewer and surface–water flooding across the borough. [PAG] (p83)
Local sewer/drainage capacity was exceeded during the June 2016 extreme rainfall, with the sewer network unable to handle the flows and thus being overwhelmed; this prompted recommendations to re–evaluate the drainage network on a catchment basis. (p84)","nine locations reached Section 19 threshold on 23 June 2016; five locations reached Section 19 threshold on 29–30 July 2017; three locations reached Section 19 threshold on 9 August 2017; five additional locations flooded but did not reach Section 19 thresholds [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)
