designed for up to 1 in 30 year rainfall events; June 2016 was reported as a 1 in 60 year event (p84)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.40819685319756,-0.22832760780359473,"<div id=""html_779e68e0015482051953248d19947d9f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area around Raynes Park railway bridge was implicated in flooding linked to pump condition/operation beneath the bridge, affecting local drainage behaviour and requiring investigation to inform surface water and sewer flood modelling. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Raynes Park ward experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding associated with the Coombe Lane location on 23 Jun 2016, 29&ndash;30 Jul 2017 and 09 Aug 2017 (see Coombe Lane entries and recorded sewer return periods). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Apostles area within Raynes Park (twelve roads between Kingston Road, Grand Drive and Bushey Road) experienced significant flooding in the June 2016 event, resulting in internal flooding of properties and prolonged street inundation. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The location within Raynes Park ward reached Merton's Section 19 threshold in the reported events; impacts in the area included internal flooding of commercial properties and transport disruption (including closure of the railway&ndash;bridge underpass). [PAG,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The underpass beneath the Raynes Park railway bridge was inundated, causing Metropolitan Police to close the highway and making the road impassable; local transport was disrupted and commercial properties were flooded as part of the same incident. [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The sewer network converges near Raynes Park railway bridge (major diameters discharging there) and gullies beneath the railway bridge were implicated in previous flooding incidents; the local sewer configuration and blocked/uncleared gullies contributed to surface&ndash;water surcharge and transport risk in the Raynes Park area during the recorded incidents. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Four highway gullies directly underneath the Raynes Park railway bridge were not cleaned prior to the 2016 flooding incidents due to lack of access; these four gullies were subsequently cleaned in April 2017. The railway bridge also forms the catchment boundary and the area immediately south was identified as high risk of surface water flooding in the report. [PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The road slopes down beneath the Raynes Park railway bridge created a man&ndash;made low spot where significant flooding occurred; the railway bridge underpass became impassable and was closed to pedestrians and vehicles. Pumps beneath the railway bridge were identified for further investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p41)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately ten properties; 36 hours; 300&ndash;450 mm (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 commercial properties; 1 underpass closure [PAG,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately four commercial properties; flood depth 600&ndash;1000 mm; June event duration approximately 12 hours [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 incidents [PAG,MUL] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Raynes Park,England,"The area around Raynes Park railway bridge was implicated in flooding linked to pump condition/operation beneath the bridge, affecting local drainage behaviour and requiring investigation to inform surface water and sewer flood modelling. (p4)
The Raynes Park ward experienced sewer–related flooding associated with the Coombe Lane location on 23 Jun 2016, 29–30 Jul 2017 and 09 Aug 2017 (see Coombe Lane entries and recorded sewer return periods). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
The Apostles area within Raynes Park (twelve roads between Kingston Road, Grand Drive and Bushey Road) experienced significant flooding in the June 2016 event, resulting in internal flooding of properties and prolonged street inundation. (p29)
The location within Raynes Park ward reached Merton's Section 19 threshold in the reported events; impacts in the area included internal flooding of commercial properties and transport disruption (including closure of the railway–bridge underpass). [PAG,MUL] (p35)
The underpass beneath the Raynes Park railway bridge was inundated, causing Metropolitan Police to close the highway and making the road impassable; local transport was disrupted and commercial properties were flooded as part of the same incident. [IMG,PAG] (p36)
The sewer network converges near Raynes Park railway bridge (major diameters discharging there) and gullies beneath the railway bridge were implicated in previous flooding incidents; the local sewer configuration and blocked/uncleared gullies contributed to surface–water surcharge and transport risk in the Raynes Park area during the recorded incidents. (p37)
Four highway gullies directly underneath the Raynes Park railway bridge were not cleaned prior to the 2016 flooding incidents due to lack of access; these four gullies were subsequently cleaned in April 2017. The railway bridge also forms the catchment boundary and the area immediately south was identified as high risk of surface water flooding in the report. [PAG] (p38)
The road slopes down beneath the Raynes Park railway bridge created a man–made low spot where significant flooding occurred; the railway bridge underpass became impassable and was closed to pedestrians and vehicles. Pumps beneath the railway bridge were identified for further investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p41)","approximately ten properties; 36 hours; 300–450 mm (p29)
4 commercial properties; 1 underpass closure [PAG,MUL] (p35)
approximately four commercial properties; flood depth 600–1000 mm; June event duration approximately 12 hours [IMG,PAG] (p36)
3 incidents [PAG,MUL] (p41)",GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4143002,-0.2360857,"<div id=""html_0ea1c19876effefc14b2974b18084e62"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Higher ground in and around Cottenham Park contributed a large proportion of surface water runoff that flowed downhill towards Coombe Lane and the low spot beneath Raynes Park railway bridge, increasing flows that contributed to sewer surcharge and underpass flooding. [MUL] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Cottenham Park,England,"Higher ground in and around Cottenham Park contributed a large proportion of surface water runoff that flowed downhill towards Coombe Lane and the low spot beneath Raynes Park railway bridge, increasing flows that contributed to sewer surcharge and underpass flooding. [MUL] (p41)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4079938,-0.2019654,"<div id=""html_0acb7e9f11e209c9a92901b10fdbaa1d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Merton Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Merton Park ward experienced flooding at locations listed in the report (including Dorset Road and Kenley Road car park). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Merton Park,England,"Merton Park ward experienced flooding at locations listed in the report (including Dorset Road and Kenley Road car park). [TAB,MUL] (p21)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3977576,-0.2383655,"<div id=""html_a662ba8ba4104cc4926e23283473e359"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Barnes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction and along West Barnes Lane; sewer gauges record sewer surcharge events at these locations. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties within West Barnes (notably those backing onto the culverted Pyl Brook along West Barnes Lane) experienced inundation during the 2016 and 2017 incidents; the area is also mapped as Flood Zone 3, covering most of West Barnes Lane and some properties around the Adela Avenue junction. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced surface&ndash;water flooding during the events in 2016 and 2017; runoff collected toward low&ndash;lying parts of the neighbourhood (notably along West Barnes Lane) contributing to inundation of the local study area. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction (under the A3 flyover) in West Barnes was affected during all three reported rainfall events. Flooding at this location is recurrent and during the events was attributable to heavily silted surface water sewers and a limited sewer gradient that produced surcharges and backflows. Network investigations confirmed siltation issues beneath the central island of the roundabout; no properties were recorded by the council as affected. [MUL] (p79)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0 properties recorded as affected [MUL] (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,West Barnes,England,"Flooding occurred at the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction and along West Barnes Lane; sewer gauges record sewer surcharge events at these locations. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p21)
Properties within West Barnes (notably those backing onto the culverted Pyl Brook along West Barnes Lane) experienced inundation during the 2016 and 2017 incidents; the area is also mapped as Flood Zone 3, covering most of West Barnes Lane and some properties around the Adela Avenue junction. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p60)
Area experienced surface–water flooding during the events in 2016 and 2017; runoff collected toward low–lying parts of the neighbourhood (notably along West Barnes Lane) contributing to inundation of the local study area. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p61)
The Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction (under the A3 flyover) in West Barnes was affected during all three reported rainfall events. Flooding at this location is recurrent and during the events was attributable to heavily silted surface water sewers and a limited sewer gradient that produced surcharges and backflows. Network investigations confirmed siltation issues beneath the central island of the roundabout; no properties were recorded by the council as affected. [MUL] (p79)",0 properties recorded as affected [MUL] (p79),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4182747,-0.1778626,"<div id=""html_30757816c464590f96281d208ac1a1d3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Colliers Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer flooding recorded in the Baltic Close / Colliers Wood Station area as measured by TWUL sewer gauges. Estimated maximum storm return periods recorded at the nearest sewer gauge: 1 in 21 year (June 2016), 1 in 2 year (July 2017), 1 in 5 year (August 2017). Section 19 investigation threshold was not reached for these events. [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding affected Colliers Wood on 23 June 2016: a commercial property's basement was flooded, caused primarily by combined sewer flooding following increased surface water runoff from High Street Colliers Wood. Flooding started at about 09:00 BST and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p75)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Colliers Wood,England,"Sewer flooding recorded in the Baltic Close / Colliers Wood Station area as measured by TWUL sewer gauges. Estimated maximum storm return periods recorded at the nearest sewer gauge: 1 in 21 year (June 2016), 1 in 2 year (July 2017), 1 in 5 year (August 2017). Section 19 investigation threshold was not reached for these events. [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Significant flooding affected Colliers Wood on 23 June 2016: a commercial property's basement was flooded, caused primarily by combined sewer flooding following increased surface water runoff from High Street Colliers Wood. Flooding started at about 09:00 BST and lasted about 12 hours. [PAG] (p75)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.3897736,-0.2163635,"<div id=""html_a3c7c7efac2391b9b5e0793e99857275"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Morden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Lower Morden ward experienced flooding at Lower Malden Lane (recorded by TWUL sewer gauges). Estimated maximum storm return periods associated with the location: 1 in 30 year (June 2016), 1 in 2 year (July 2017), 1 in 4 year (August 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ward contained Lower Morden Lane which experienced surface water flooding constrained to the highway with no properties affected; nearby TWUL surface water sewer blockage prevented discharge to East Pyl Brook, contributing to local runoff issues. [MUL] (p78)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0 properties affected [MUL] (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Lower Morden,England,"The Lower Morden ward experienced flooding at Lower Malden Lane (recorded by TWUL sewer gauges). Estimated maximum storm return periods associated with the location: 1 in 30 year (June 2016), 1 in 2 year (July 2017), 1 in 4 year (August 2017). [TAB,MUL] (p21)
The ward contained Lower Morden Lane which experienced surface water flooding constrained to the highway with no properties affected; nearby TWUL surface water sewer blockage prevented discharge to East Pyl Brook, contributing to local runoff issues. [MUL] (p78)",0 properties affected [MUL] (p78),GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4187233,-0.2420669,"<div id=""html_3629964be186f5ce85e2bbf9564c2096"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Copse Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Higher ground in and around Copse Hill contributed surface water runoff that fed flows toward Coombe Lane and the Raynes Park underpass, increasing runoff volumes that contributed to sewer surcharge and local flooding. [MUL] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Copse Hill,England,"Higher ground in and around Copse Hill contributed surface water runoff that fed flows toward Coombe Lane and the Raynes Park underpass, increasing runoff volumes that contributed to sewer surcharge and local flooding. [MUL] (p41)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.41902299447477,-0.2175629037404358,"<div id=""html_edfd357362921f18a796230f27133ccf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wimbledon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wimbledon town centre area experienced repeated flooding across the events, including highway inundation and internal flooding of commercial and residential premises in the affected locality. [MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water (urban) flooding affected central Wimbledon during the reported events; incidents in adjacent streets (Worple Road, Wimbledon Hill Road, St George's Road) are recorded and report text states similar consequences occurred in the 2016 and 2017 events. Transport and commercial hubs in central Wimbledon were identified as vulnerable to surface&ndash;water inundation. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Wimbledon,England,"Wimbledon town centre area experienced repeated flooding across the events, including highway inundation and internal flooding of commercial and residential premises in the affected locality. [MUL] (p49)
Surface–water (urban) flooding affected central Wimbledon during the reported events; incidents in adjacent streets (Worple Road, Wimbledon Hill Road, St George's Road) are recorded and report text states similar consequences occurred in the 2016 and 2017 events. Transport and commercial hubs in central Wimbledon were identified as vulnerable to surface–water inundation. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-MRT,2,2016-06-23,51.4089922,-0.2226123,"<div id=""html_dc5316fbf63639312c6ad494be71d2b6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Apostles</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Apostles area recorded intense rainfall during 22&ndash;24 June 2016 associated with the June 2016 event; this produced surface water flooding in the Apostles area as part of the investigated event. [TAB] (p87)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded extreme short&ndash;duration (5&ndash;minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016 (table lists Apostles area, including Dupont Road), associated with surface&ndash;water / flash&ndash;flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,The Apostles,England,"The Apostles area recorded intense rainfall during 22–24 June 2016 associated with the June 2016 event; this produced surface water flooding in the Apostles area as part of the investigated event. [TAB] (p87)
Recorded extreme short–duration (5–minute) rainfall during 23 June 2016 (table lists Apostles area, including Dupont Road), associated with surface–water / flash–flooding conditions in the locality. [TAB] (p88)",,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc.html,GB-MRT_2_2016-06-23_etc,"London Borough of Merton flooding 23 Jun 2016, London Borough of Merton flooding 29 Jul 2017 - 30 Jul 2017 and London Borough of Merton flooding 09 Aug 2017",Section 19: Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3/final.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.60724859359257,-0.5554467835055225,"<div id=""html_df9074fc9ad0d30480848be75d6530b7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Peters Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverted River Misbourne and highway drainage under the High Street / St Peter's Court were implicated in flooding: connections into the east culvert beneath the High Street were mapped, and the area around TQ 00102 90885 experienced road and property flooding. A nearby sewer overflow onto Gravel Hill was also identified as a related issue. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert beneath St Peter's Court was partly restricted, contributing to road and property flooding in the nearby area (area B around TQ 00102 90885). High River Misbourne levels and the culvert restriction prevented gullies on the High Street from discharging at full capacity; once the culvert was cleared and gullies jetted, surface water cleared rapidly. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A partially restricted culvert under St Peter's Court caused a flow obstruction that prevented highway drainage from discharging into the River Misbourne, resulting in a backup of surface water at ground level. [PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert under St Peter's Court was partially restricted, causing water to back up and preventing surface water from draining into the River Misbourne; this obstruction contributed to a backup of surface water at ground level. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A blocked riparian&ndash;owned culvert at St Peter's Court was identified as causing local flooding; the Environment Agency contacted the riparian owner, authorised and commissioned emergency clearance, carried out site visits and arranged jetting/clearance works to alleviate the flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert under St Peter's Court was partially restricted, causing water to back up and preventing surface water from draining via the surface water drainage infrastructure. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The St Peters Court culvert suffered flow restrictions/obstructions that contributed to surface water/urban flooding in Chalfont St Peter (including the High Street). Recommended actions include CCTV inspection, clearing/repair of outfalls and prioritised maintenance (jetting of gullies and culvert repairs) to reduce future surface water flooding. [TAB] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Problems with surface water outfalls into the St Peter's Court culvert were identified as contributing to surface water flooding in the nearby High Street; recommended actions included CCTV survey, clearing/maintenance of the culvert and jetting of gully connections. [TAB] (p39)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,St Peters Court,England,"Culverted River Misbourne and highway drainage under the High Street / St Peter's Court were implicated in flooding: connections into the east culvert beneath the High Street were mapped, and the area around TQ 00102 90885 experienced road and property flooding. A nearby sewer overflow onto Gravel Hill was also identified as a related issue. (p10)
The culvert beneath St Peter's Court was partly restricted, contributing to road and property flooding in the nearby area (area B around TQ 00102 90885). High River Misbourne levels and the culvert restriction prevented gullies on the High Street from discharging at full capacity; once the culvert was cleared and gullies jetted, surface water cleared rapidly. (p23)
A partially restricted culvert under St Peter's Court caused a flow obstruction that prevented highway drainage from discharging into the River Misbourne, resulting in a backup of surface water at ground level. [PAG] (p29)
The culvert under St Peter's Court was partially restricted, causing water to back up and preventing surface water from draining into the River Misbourne; this obstruction contributed to a backup of surface water at ground level. [PAG] (p30)
A blocked riparian–owned culvert at St Peter's Court was identified as causing local flooding; the Environment Agency contacted the riparian owner, authorised and commissioned emergency clearance, carried out site visits and arranged jetting/clearance works to alleviate the flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p32)
The culvert under St Peter's Court was partially restricted, causing water to back up and preventing surface water from draining via the surface water drainage infrastructure. (p37)
The St Peters Court culvert suffered flow restrictions/obstructions that contributed to surface water/urban flooding in Chalfont St Peter (including the High Street). Recommended actions include CCTV inspection, clearing/repair of outfalls and prioritised maintenance (jetting of gullies and culvert repairs) to reduce future surface water flooding. [TAB] (p38)
Problems with surface water outfalls into the St Peter's Court culvert were identified as contributing to surface water flooding in the nearby High Street; recommended actions included CCTV survey, clearing/maintenance of the culvert and jetting of gully connections. [TAB] (p39)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6080294,-0.5560293,"<div id=""html_e9a70e43e1c3debd578d3cfc83a9f113"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Greyhound</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sustained internal flooding caused by a sewage overflow on 07 February 2014. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,The Greyhound,England,Sustained internal flooding caused by a sewage overflow on 07 February 2014. (p24),,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.60762565309283,-0.5556228934457361,"<div id=""html_cc27d20c94277b945c8af15b9f0710ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>George Inn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A manhole next to the George Inn was identified as a potential flood&ndash;related asset to be sealed; the action requested was to either carry out sealing or report why it could not be done, as part of measures to address local sewer/manhole&ndash;related flooding. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater pooled at the entrance and extended into the building; sandbags and temporary barriers were placed at the threshold and access was restricted to reduce water ingress. [IMG] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,George Inn,England,"A manhole next to the George Inn was identified as a potential flood–related asset to be sealed; the action requested was to either carry out sealing or report why it could not be done, as part of measures to address local sewer/manhole–related flooding. [TAB] (p28)
Floodwater pooled at the entrance and extended into the building; sandbags and temporary barriers were placed at the threshold and access was restricted to reduce water ingress. [IMG] (p47)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.61082003363162,-0.5575758871804786,"<div id=""html_262eb80ad011f424bec9410e8645cb53"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chalfont Scout Hut</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the ground outside the scout hut, encroaching close to the building and scattering debris, indicating disruption and potential damage to the hut's immediate surroundings. [IMG] (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Chalfont Scout Hut,England,"Floodwater covered the ground outside the scout hut, encroaching close to the building and scattering debris, indicating disruption and potential damage to the hut's immediate surroundings. [IMG] (p48)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6480032,-0.5800363,"<div id=""html_9a4549e4cef58f9a6988fd570ea071d0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Misbourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Misbourne catchment experienced high river levels driven by above&ndash;average rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater; catchment issues (including culvert restriction at St Peter's Court) led to local water backing up and surface water flooding. The catchment was later included in Thames Water's information&ndash;gathering exercise into infiltration and groundwater problems. [PAG,MUL] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels in the River Misbourne were high due to above&ndash;average prolonged rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater levels; these high river levels contributed to prevented surface water drainage and to prolonged flooding and repeated sewer overflows in Chalfont St Peter. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Obstructions and culvert flow restrictions on the Misbourne contributed to flooding in Chalfont St Peter (including surface water flooding affecting the High Street). Actions recommended include culvert CCTV surveys, enforcement against landowners who failed to remove obstructions, upstream land&ndash;management and consideration of a telemetered gauge to improve warnings. [TAB,PAG] (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Misbourne,England,"The Misbourne catchment experienced high river levels driven by above–average rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater; catchment issues (including culvert restriction at St Peter's Court) led to local water backing up and surface water flooding. The catchment was later included in Thames Water's information–gathering exercise into infiltration and groundwater problems. [PAG,MUL] (p29)
Water levels in the River Misbourne were high due to above–average prolonged rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater levels; these high river levels contributed to prevented surface water drainage and to prolonged flooding and repeated sewer overflows in Chalfont St Peter. (p37)
Obstructions and culvert flow restrictions on the Misbourne contributed to flooding in Chalfont St Peter (including surface water flooding affecting the High Street). Actions recommended include culvert CCTV surveys, enforcement against landowners who failed to remove obstructions, upstream land–management and consideration of a telemetered gauge to improve warnings. [TAB,PAG] (p38)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6216101,-0.5587725,"<div id=""html_8d56cb0177d0e2faf595e1ab23d41545"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Amersham Road, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Amersham Road (A413) runs adjacent to the River Misbourne and is within the area where road and property flooding was recorded (location B is on/near the main road corridor), indicating it was affected by surface/urban flooding during the event. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Amersham Road, Chalfont St Peter",England,"Amersham Road (A413) runs adjacent to the River Misbourne and is within the area where road and property flooding was recorded (location B is on/near the main road corridor), indicating it was affected by surface/urban flooding during the event. (p8)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6064047,-0.5564089,"<div id=""html_efc1b1b9b064a91f126b8106b1b5e7ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The High Street was closed due to flood impacts (including foul water overflows and drainage backups), causing significant local disruption and affecting access and businesses in the village centre. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverted River Misbourne and highway drainage under the High Street / St Peter's Court were implicated in flooding: connections into the east culvert beneath the High Street were mapped, and the area around TQ 00102 90885 experienced road and property flooding. A nearby sewer overflow onto Gravel Hill was also identified as a related issue. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies on the High Street were unable to discharge to the River Misbourne at full capacity leading to surface water accumulation; manholes overflowed with sewage&ndash;laden water entering the George pub on the High Street during incidents reported in January and February 2014. [MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewage&ndash;laden water from overflowing manholes entered premises on the High Street: The George pub suffered internal flooding on multiple occasions (reported weekend 05/06 January 2014; again 12 February 2014) and the Greyhound Inn suffered internal flooding (07 February 2014). Road and pavement around the nearby A413 zebra crossing were contaminated and required clean&ndash;up; pumping to relieve High Street flooding was considered but not used due to risk to downstream properties. [PAG,MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert downstream of High Street was surveyed and cleared as part of the response; TfB cleared road gullies in the flooded area after culvert clearing. The EA reported no obstructions, culverts of ample capacity and the channel conveying water efficiently along this stretch. [TAB] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced a sewage overflow incident; Thames Water reported the High Street incident as closed on 10 February 2014 and cleanup of sewage from road and pavements was carried out. [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewage overflow onto the road and pavements; Thames Water carried out cleanup of sewage from road and pavements. Transport for Buckinghamshire/BCC closed the High Street, moved sandbags to protect High Street properties and closed the footpath from High Street to the Church Lane parking lot. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred in the High Street, linked to blocked outfalls into the St Peter's Court culvert and gully/connection issues; interventions included clearing the culvert, jetting gully connections and investigating other causes if flooding recurred. [TAB] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging manholes on High Street caused water to bubble up and overflow onto the pavement and road; standing floodwater covered the street and adjacent footpath (to a parking lot), with temporary barriers placed to warn/restrict access. [IMG,MUL] (p46)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 pub (George) flooded [MUL] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"High Street, Chalfont St Peter",England,"The High Street was closed due to flood impacts (including foul water overflows and drainage backups), causing significant local disruption and affecting access and businesses in the village centre. (p5)
Culverted River Misbourne and highway drainage under the High Street / St Peter's Court were implicated in flooding: connections into the east culvert beneath the High Street were mapped, and the area around TQ 00102 90885 experienced road and property flooding. A nearby sewer overflow onto Gravel Hill was also identified as a related issue. (p10)
Gullies on the High Street were unable to discharge to the River Misbourne at full capacity leading to surface water accumulation; manholes overflowed with sewage–laden water entering the George pub on the High Street during incidents reported in January and February 2014. [MUL] (p24)
Sewage–laden water from overflowing manholes entered premises on the High Street: The George pub suffered internal flooding on multiple occasions (reported weekend 05/06 January 2014; again 12 February 2014) and the Greyhound Inn suffered internal flooding (07 February 2014). Road and pavement around the nearby A413 zebra crossing were contaminated and required clean–up; pumping to relieve High Street flooding was considered but not used due to risk to downstream properties. [PAG,MUL] (p25)
The culvert downstream of High Street was surveyed and cleared as part of the response; TfB cleared road gullies in the flooded area after culvert clearing. The EA reported no obstructions, culverts of ample capacity and the channel conveying water efficiently along this stretch. [TAB] (p27)
Experienced a sewage overflow incident; Thames Water reported the High Street incident as closed on 10 February 2014 and cleanup of sewage from road and pavements was carried out. [TAB] (p31)
Sewage overflow onto the road and pavements; Thames Water carried out cleanup of sewage from road and pavements. Transport for Buckinghamshire/BCC closed the High Street, moved sandbags to protect High Street properties and closed the footpath from High Street to the Church Lane parking lot. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p32)
Surface water flooding occurred in the High Street, linked to blocked outfalls into the St Peter's Court culvert and gully/connection issues; interventions included clearing the culvert, jetting gully connections and investigating other causes if flooding recurred. [TAB] (p39)
Surcharging manholes on High Street caused water to bubble up and overflow onto the pavement and road; standing floodwater covered the street and adjacent footpath (to a parking lot), with temporary barriers placed to warn/restrict access. [IMG,MUL] (p46)",1 pub (George) flooded [MUL] (p24),GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6152936,-0.5502646,"<div id=""html_eeae8aef2d20bd05497e617bd060841a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gravel Hill, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A sewer overflow was recorded as discharging onto Gravel Hill (location A), indicating direct sewer&ndash;related surface flooding at this location. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked gullies and suspected downstream sewer/manhole blockage leading to local surface&ndash;water/sewer flooding. Culvert clearing was completed and gullies were jetted to clear blockages; manhole on Gravel Hill was identified as possibly blocked downstream and to be checked. [TAB,MUL] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Gravel Hill, Chalfont St Peter",England,"A sewer overflow was recorded as discharging onto Gravel Hill (location A), indicating direct sewer–related surface flooding at this location. (p8)
Blocked gullies and suspected downstream sewer/manhole blockage leading to local surface–water/sewer flooding. Culvert clearing was completed and gullies were jetted to clear blockages; manhole on Gravel Hill was identified as possibly blocked downstream and to be checked. [TAB,MUL] (p28)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.601225,-0.5534538,"<div id=""html_c0fe70154f8450ae173e81e5d5809163"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kingsway, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the Kingsway/A413 roundabout and adjacent carriageway (including the A413 zebra crossing). Multi&ndash;agency actions included daily de&ndash;blocking and cleansing from the site of discharge to the roundabout, culvert clearing and gully jetting to restore flow and relieve road flooding; consideration was given to reinstating pumping from sewers into the Misbourne with EA approval. [TAB] (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Kingsway, Chalfont St Peter",England,"Flooding affected the Kingsway/A413 roundabout and adjacent carriageway (including the A413 zebra crossing). Multi–agency actions included daily de–blocking and cleansing from the site of discharge to the roundabout, culvert clearing and gully jetting to restore flow and relieve road flooding; consideration was given to reinstating pumping from sewers into the Misbourne with EA approval. [TAB] (p27)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6097088,-0.5917115,"<div id=""html_b96a26ba2d12c01e882b2b494e08b150"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Welders Lane, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Narcot Lane and Welders Lane junction flooded with surface water; passing vehicles splashed pedestrians as a result of the road flooding reported by CDC. [TAB] (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Welders Lane, Chalfont St Peter",England,Narcot Lane and Welders Lane junction flooded with surface water; passing vehicles splashed pedestrians as a result of the road flooding reported by CDC. [TAB] (p31),,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6077952,-0.5573875,"<div id=""html_b5128bf4e1243a74515700a55bb129ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Lane, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Access was constrained after flooding: the footpath from High Street to the Church Lane parking lot was closed and nearby High Street properties were protected with sandbags and subject to road/footpath closures. [TAB] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Church Lane, Chalfont St Peter",England,Access was constrained after flooding: the footpath from High Street to the Church Lane parking lot was closed and nearby High Street properties were protected with sandbags and subject to road/footpath closures. [TAB] (p32),,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6251135,-0.5794525,"<div id=""html_07c53c32f17cfe312a57fb5e12ab852e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Narcot Lane, Chalfont St Giles</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Narcot Lane and Welders Lane junction flooded with surface water; passing vehicles splashed pedestrians as a result of the road flooding reported by CDC. [TAB] (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Narcot Lane, Chalfont St Giles",England,Narcot Lane and Welders Lane junction flooded with surface water; passing vehicles splashed pedestrians as a result of the road flooding reported by CDC. [TAB] (p31),,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6043496,-0.5545674,"<div id=""html_40b800ae42df215f0480bd2bc1b4858f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hither Meadow, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked riparian culvert and associated surface water flooding risk led to Environment Agency flood response: EA delivered and installed sandbags to protect a property in Hither Meadow and authorised emergency culvert clearance following site visits. [TAB] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Hither Meadow, Chalfont St Peter",England,Blocked riparian culvert and associated surface water flooding risk led to Environment Agency flood response: EA delivered and installed sandbags to protect a property in Hither Meadow and authorised emergency culvert clearance following site visits. [TAB] (p32),,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6066401,-0.5549634,"<div id=""html_1986e8f6a4d8b15e09d18e7f1c78b546"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hiljon Crescent, Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Hiljon Crescent were protected during the emergency by an EA&ndash;built sandbag wall while a blocked culvert was jetted. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Hiljon Crescent, Chalfont St Peter",England,Properties on Hiljon Crescent were protected during the emergency by an EA–built sandbag wall while a blocked culvert was jetted. (p33),,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_fb76df4f27d40eb827f3cae0039a638b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The county was the subject of a flood investigation covering a flood event that occurred during January&ndash;March 2014; the report analyses conditions, watercourses, incident response and provides conclusions and recommendations related to that flooding. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Chalfont St Peter (within Buckinghamshire) experienced internal flooding of commercial premises and closure of the High Street, disrupting local businesses and access. [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the county (Chalfont St Peter and nearby villages) were affected by manhole/sewer overflows and culvert&ndash;related high river/ surface water levels that caused sewage&ndash;laden water to enter commercial premises (The George pub, Greyhound Inn), required road closures and clean&ndash;up, caused an upstream Affinity Water pumping station in Chalfont St Giles to cease pumping, and prompted emergency culvert clearance works by the Environment Agency. [MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Organised and led multi&ndash;agency response to high river levels in Chalfont St Peter (meeting on 18 February 2014). Coordinated jetting operations downstream of a culvert; contractor unable to deploy silt nets because of high water. Jetting removed large amounts of silt, pieces of plywood, branches and general refuse and continued until 21 February 2014; remaining silt was expected to be flushed out naturally as river levels fell slowly during the week of the works. [MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced road flooding (including at the Narcot Lane/Welders Lane junction) and multiple sewage overflow incidents affecting roads, pavements and local properties; local authority distributed FloodSax® to affected properties and assisted with site visits and reporting. [TAB,MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding across the county prompted emergency actions: TfB distributed and installed sandbags (including two tonnes in one night); Thames Water removed a blockage that ended a sewage overflow at the A413 zebra crossing; Thames Water installed a temporary pumping and filtration system beside the George pub to discharge water into the River Misbourne. (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two or more business premises; High Street closed for approximately two weeks [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two properties [TAB,MUL] (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"The county was the subject of a flood investigation covering a flood event that occurred during January–March 2014; the report analyses conditions, watercourses, incident response and provides conclusions and recommendations related to that flooding. (p2)
Chalfont St Peter (within Buckinghamshire) experienced internal flooding of commercial premises and closure of the High Street, disrupting local businesses and access. [IMG,PAG] (p6)
Parts of the county (Chalfont St Peter and nearby villages) were affected by manhole/sewer overflows and culvert–related high river/ surface water levels that caused sewage–laden water to enter commercial premises (The George pub, Greyhound Inn), required road closures and clean–up, caused an upstream Affinity Water pumping station in Chalfont St Giles to cease pumping, and prompted emergency culvert clearance works by the Environment Agency. [MUL] (p25)
Organised and led multi–agency response to high river levels in Chalfont St Peter (meeting on 18 February 2014). Coordinated jetting operations downstream of a culvert; contractor unable to deploy silt nets because of high water. Jetting removed large amounts of silt, pieces of plywood, branches and general refuse and continued until 21 February 2014; remaining silt was expected to be flushed out naturally as river levels fell slowly during the week of the works. [MUL] (p26)
Experienced road flooding (including at the Narcot Lane/Welders Lane junction) and multiple sewage overflow incidents affecting roads, pavements and local properties; local authority distributed FloodSax® to affected properties and assisted with site visits and reporting. [TAB,MUL] (p31)
Flooding across the county prompted emergency actions: TfB distributed and installed sandbags (including two tonnes in one night); Thames Water removed a blockage that ended a sewage overflow at the A413 zebra crossing; Thames Water installed a temporary pumping and filtration system beside the George pub to discharge water into the River Misbourne. (p34)","two or more business premises; High Street closed for approximately two weeks [IMG,PAG] (p6)
two properties [TAB,MUL] (p31)",GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.840052299999996,-0.09785519224523963,"<div id=""html_8dc35170e7bba0d98caf9b487c5bb7d0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hertfordshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding occurred in the EA Hertfordshire and North London area, prompting the opening of the Hatfield Area Incident Room to manage the response. (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Hertfordshire,England,"Widespread flooding occurred in the EA Hertfordshire and North London area, prompting the opening of the Hatfield Area Incident Room to manage the response. (p31)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6082069,-0.5543134,"<div id=""html_e33acc0855f2c3a09ec6f7777fbe4aa5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chalfont St Peter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding during January&ndash;March 2014 following prolonged rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater. A culvert flow restriction caused the River Misbourne to back up, contributing to backup of the surface water drainage network. Blockage and groundwater infiltration within the sewer network caused foul water overflows onto the A413 and within the village centre. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>After a prolonged period of above&ndash;average rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater levels, river levels were raised and a culvert flow restriction in the village caused the River Misbourne to back up, which may have caused a backup of the surface water drainage network. A blockage in the sewer network caused an overflow of foul water onto the A413 and groundwater infiltration into the sewer network caused foul water overflows within the village centre. Internal flooding affected business premises within the village. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village contained two identified flood problem locations: a sewer overflow discharging onto Gravel Hill (location A) and road and property flooding at a junction near location B (around TQ 00102 90885). The River Misbourne flows through the village (partly culverted in the centre) and is implicated in local fluvial/drainage&ndash;related flooding that contributed to these issues. [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer overflow discharged onto Gravel Hill (location A) and road and property flooding occurred in the area around TQ 00102 90885 (location B). Flooding was associated with the River Misbourne and a small tributary, and the village centre contains a culverted section of the Misbourne (culvert under St Peter's Court split into two pipes) that is implicated in local drainage/flooding behaviour. [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road and property flooding in the village centre (area B). A sewer overflow discharged onto Gravel Hill (area A). Culverting of the River Misbourne in the centre and culvert connections beneath the High Street/St Peter's Court were implicated in the flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the January&ndash;March 2014 event groundwater incursion affected Chalfont St Peter by entering the foul sewer network, contributing to sewer&ndash;related flooding problems in the village. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater incursion entered the foul sewer network, causing sewer flooding in the village during the reported event. [IMG,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater incursion into the foul sewer network during the event described in the report (January&ndash;March 2014). Historic fluvial and surface&ndash;water flooding of the village centre (November 2000&ndash;February 2001) with sewage often combining with floodwater, causing road closures (including one week in February 2001) and business disruption (the Greyhound Inn repeatedly flooded and closed from February 2001). The village centre was flooded to a depth of 45 cm in February 2001. [PAG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Manholes overflowed with sewage&ndash;laden water causing sewage to enter the George pub on the High Street; gullies on the High Street were unable to discharge fully due to high River Misbourne levels and culvert restriction, causing surface water accumulation; internal flooding occurred at the Greyhound Inn and a road closure occurred associated with the flooding. [MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in the River Misbourne and exceptionally high groundwater levels contributed to flooding in the village centre; the culvert under St Peter's Court was partially restricted causing water to back up and preventing surface water draining into the river via highway drainage connections. Village&ndash;centre flooding affected parking and pavement use by customers and business activity. At least two business premises flooded internally. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Chalfont St Peter were affected by flooding associated with the River Misbourne and by surface water flooding; the High Street experienced surface water flooding and residents adjacent to the Misbourne were impacted. [TAB,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding affected the High Street in Chalfont St Peter associated with blocked/poorly performing road drainage (St Peter's Court culvert outfalls and gully connections). The event prompted CCTV investigation of the culvert, gully cleansing/jetting and consideration of temporary flood defences and other mitigation measures. [TAB,PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area experienced flooding in January&ndash;March 2014 related to the River Misbourne (and associated culverts/ditches), sewer infiltration and capacity problems, and surface water/urban runoff. This prompted reviews of temporary flood defences, riparian maintenance guidance and proposals to form a local Flood Action Group. [TAB,PAG] (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding of two or more business premises within an area of 1km2; High Street closed for approximately two weeks [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two instances caused sewage entry to the George pub; road closure for at least 10 hours [MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 2 business premises (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Chalfont St Peter,England,"The village experienced flooding during January–March 2014 following prolonged rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater. A culvert flow restriction caused the River Misbourne to back up, contributing to backup of the surface water drainage network. Blockage and groundwater infiltration within the sewer network caused foul water overflows onto the A413 and within the village centre. (p4)
After a prolonged period of above–average rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater levels, river levels were raised and a culvert flow restriction in the village caused the River Misbourne to back up, which may have caused a backup of the surface water drainage network. A blockage in the sewer network caused an overflow of foul water onto the A413 and groundwater infiltration into the sewer network caused foul water overflows within the village centre. Internal flooding affected business premises within the village. [PAG] (p5)
The village contained two identified flood problem locations: a sewer overflow discharging onto Gravel Hill (location A) and road and property flooding at a junction near location B (around TQ 00102 90885). The River Misbourne flows through the village (partly culverted in the centre) and is implicated in local fluvial/drainage–related flooding that contributed to these issues. [IMG,PAG] (p8)
Sewer overflow discharged onto Gravel Hill (location A) and road and property flooding occurred in the area around TQ 00102 90885 (location B). Flooding was associated with the River Misbourne and a small tributary, and the village centre contains a culverted section of the Misbourne (culvert under St Peter's Court split into two pipes) that is implicated in local drainage/flooding behaviour. [IMG,PAG] (p9)
Road and property flooding in the village centre (area B). A sewer overflow discharged onto Gravel Hill (area A). Culverting of the River Misbourne in the centre and culvert connections beneath the High Street/St Peter's Court were implicated in the flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
During the January–March 2014 event groundwater incursion affected Chalfont St Peter by entering the foul sewer network, contributing to sewer–related flooding problems in the village. [IMG,PAG] (p15)
Groundwater incursion entered the foul sewer network, causing sewer flooding in the village during the reported event. [IMG,PAG] (p16)
Groundwater incursion into the foul sewer network during the event described in the report (January–March 2014). Historic fluvial and surface–water flooding of the village centre (November 2000–February 2001) with sewage often combining with floodwater, causing road closures (including one week in February 2001) and business disruption (the Greyhound Inn repeatedly flooded and closed from February 2001). The village centre was flooded to a depth of 45 cm in February 2001. [PAG,MUL] (p17)
Manholes overflowed with sewage–laden water causing sewage to enter the George pub on the High Street; gullies on the High Street were unable to discharge fully due to high River Misbourne levels and culvert restriction, causing surface water accumulation; internal flooding occurred at the Greyhound Inn and a road closure occurred associated with the flooding. [MUL] (p24)
High water levels in the River Misbourne and exceptionally high groundwater levels contributed to flooding in the village centre; the culvert under St Peter's Court was partially restricted causing water to back up and preventing surface water draining into the river via highway drainage connections. Village–centre flooding affected parking and pavement use by customers and business activity. At least two business premises flooded internally. (p29)
Areas of Chalfont St Peter were affected by flooding associated with the River Misbourne and by surface water flooding; the High Street experienced surface water flooding and residents adjacent to the Misbourne were impacted. [TAB,PAG] (p38)
Surface–water flooding affected the High Street in Chalfont St Peter associated with blocked/poorly performing road drainage (St Peter's Court culvert outfalls and gully connections). The event prompted CCTV investigation of the culvert, gully cleansing/jetting and consideration of temporary flood defences and other mitigation measures. [TAB,PAG] (p39)
The area experienced flooding in January–March 2014 related to the River Misbourne (and associated culverts/ditches), sewer infiltration and capacity problems, and surface water/urban runoff. This prompted reviews of temporary flood defences, riparian maintenance guidance and proposals to form a local Flood Action Group. [TAB,PAG] (p40)","internal flooding of two or more business premises within an area of 1km2; High Street closed for approximately two weeks [PAG] (p5)
two instances caused sewage entry to the George pub; road closure for at least 10 hours [MUL] (p24)
at least 2 business premises (p29)",GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,6,2014-01-01,51.6321046,-0.5697912,"<div id=""html_022605f7520e3d811d67ff036a2daf9e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chalfont St Giles</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Affinity Water pumping station in the village ceased pumping because it was flooded. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affinity Water pumping station at Chalfont St Giles resumed pumping on 20 February 2014; the resumption may have produced a small, very localised reduction in groundwater levels and a small local downward effect on water levels in the River Misbourne. (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Chalfont St Giles,England,"The Affinity Water pumping station in the village ceased pumping because it was flooded. (p25)
Affinity Water pumping station at Chalfont St Giles resumed pumping on 20 February 2014; the resumption may have produced a small, very localised reduction in groundwater levels and a small local downward effect on water levels in the River Misbourne. (p29)",,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_6_2014-01-01,Chalfont St Peter flooding January–March 2014,"Chalfont St Peter, January – March 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/chalfont-st-peter/March/2014.pdf
GB-DBY,8,2023-10-18,53.25866753494731,-1.3359710404418947,"<div id=""html_a3e92450f82d1fffd3fae93ce389b731"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Erin Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Outflowing floodwater entered Erin Court and flowed down Staveley Road; an unnamed ditch passing through Erin Court ('Ditch 1') was identified for maintenance to better accommodate floodwater capacity. Investigations were carried out to determine whether drainage arrangements to the south of the southern ordinary watercourse contributed to the outflow into Erin Court. (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Erin Court,England,Outflowing floodwater entered Erin Court and flowed down Staveley Road; an unnamed ditch passing through Erin Court ('Ditch 1') was identified for maintenance to better accommodate floodwater capacity. Investigations were carried out to determine whether drainage arrangements to the south of the southern ordinary watercourse contributed to the outflow into Erin Court. (p3),,GB-DBY_8_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_8_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-8-post-event-response-and-recovery-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,8,2023-10-18,52.9872203,-1.4999681,"<div id=""html_c48dd506687acec01f761fd7de5c42fc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Duffield Meadows Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from ordinary (upstream) watercourses affected the area immediately upstream of the school; Derbyshire County Council are investigating the flooding mechanisms to determine required maintenance actions. (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Duffield Meadows Primary School,England,Flooding from ordinary (upstream) watercourses affected the area immediately upstream of the school; Derbyshire County Council are investigating the flooding mechanisms to determine required maintenance actions. (p6),,GB-DBY_8_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_8_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-8-post-event-response-and-recovery-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
