2 properties; depth approximately 100mm (p12)",GB-NNH_15_2014-02-14.html,GB-NNH_15_2014-02-14,Deenethorpe flooding 14 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Deenethorpe, nr Corby, 14th February 2014",090/North Northamptonshire/Deenethorpe-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,20,2020-08-16,52.2847067,-0.726483,"<div id=""html_d7d6c277f001e2508a8a789cf6430c2a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swanspool Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Acted as an intended receptor for excess surface water during the intense rainfall; lack of effective overland flood flow routes meant excess runoff relied on buried drainage to convey water toward the brook. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Swanspool Brook,England,Acted as an intended receptor for excess surface water during the intense rainfall; lack of effective overland flood flow routes meant excess runoff relied on buried drainage to convey water toward the brook. (p22),,GB-NNH_20_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_20_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Sheep Street / Silver Street, Wellingborough: 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Sheep Street and Silver Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,20,2020-08-16,52.3008377,-0.6928256,"<div id=""html_d59bdefc3cdab4401a99cbce70b6c863"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sheep Street, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding affected properties on/around Sheep Street as part of the single flood incident at Sheep Street / Silver Street. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties on Sheep Street as part of the single flood incident; water entered buildings (crossed thresholds) causing internal flooding. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water runoff and rapid flows entered properties from the street (front and rear). Reported internal flooding included ground&ndash;floor ingress under front doors and basements/cellars filling; one cellar and ground&ndash;floor areas flooded requiring recovery and refurbishment of premises. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid runoff and ponding on Sheep Street; water rushed down the street and collected at the low point around the junction with Doddington Road forming a lake. Water entered properties from the back door and via underground pipework from a neighbouring property, possibly through an air brick. Whole cellar flooded; shallow flooding in rear hall and ground floor entrance hall, rear annexe and basement. Occupant remained in property and later returned on 18 August 2020. Residents and businesses tried to stop vehicles from driving through the flooded street. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple properties on Sheep Street experienced internal flooding; one instance was minor and managed by diverting the flow. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow from Silver Street continued southeast down Sheep Street, affecting properties on both sides. At the low point of Sheep Street, runoff from rear roofs and the rear garden flowed towards the rear doors of a residential property. The cellar flooded due to surcharging and overflow of a drainage system that receives water from neighbouring properties and external areas. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>50mm across ground floor; 400mm depth in cellar; external depths 1200mm to 1800mm at one property [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>400mm depth in cellar; external depth between 1200mm and 1800mm (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Sheep Street, Wellingborough",England,"Internal flooding affected properties on/around Sheep Street as part of the single flood incident at Sheep Street / Silver Street. (p5)
Internal flooding of properties on Sheep Street as part of the single flood incident; water entered buildings (crossed thresholds) causing internal flooding. (p8)
Surface–water runoff and rapid flows entered properties from the street (front and rear). Reported internal flooding included ground–floor ingress under front doors and basements/cellars filling; one cellar and ground–floor areas flooded requiring recovery and refurbishment of premises. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Rapid runoff and ponding on Sheep Street; water rushed down the street and collected at the low point around the junction with Doddington Road forming a lake. Water entered properties from the back door and via underground pipework from a neighbouring property, possibly through an air brick. Whole cellar flooded; shallow flooding in rear hall and ground floor entrance hall, rear annexe and basement. Occupant remained in property and later returned on 18 August 2020. Residents and businesses tried to stop vehicles from driving through the flooded street. (p18)
Multiple properties on Sheep Street experienced internal flooding; one instance was minor and managed by diverting the flow. (p19)
Flow from Silver Street continued southeast down Sheep Street, affecting properties on both sides. At the low point of Sheep Street, runoff from rear roofs and the rear garden flowed towards the rear doors of a residential property. The cellar flooded due to surcharging and overflow of a drainage system that receives water from neighbouring properties and external areas. (p20)","more than five buildings (p5)
