GB-CLD,1,2020-02-09,53.7923511,-2.1055895,"<div id=""html_6f4145c906d19211c46f55dcd7f6c1dc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Widdop Reservoir</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The reservoirs filled during heavy rainfall on 9th February 2020 and spilled/overtopped; the reservoirs upstream of Hebden Bridge spilled at various times between 11:30 and 18:00 on 9th February. (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Calderdale,Widdop Reservoir,England,The reservoirs filled during heavy rainfall on 9th February 2020 and spilled/overtopped; the reservoirs upstream of Hebden Bridge spilled at various times between 11:30 and 18:00 on 9th February. (p48),,GB-CLD_1_2020-02-09.html,GB-CLD_1_2020-02-09,Calderdale flooding 09 Feb 2020,Section 19 Report: Flood Event 9th February 2020,020/Calderdale/020/2020/Calderdale/Item 5 App A - Flood Events of 9th February 2020 and 20th February 2022.pdf
GB-BKM,9,2013-12-01,51.7889167,-0.797117,"<div id=""html_efc4f0177a0bb02c665665c9b40f9914"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Road, Stoke Mandeville</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carriageway covered by deep surface water during winter 2013/2014; vehicles were forced to drive through floodwater with large splashes, making the road difficult and dangerous to traverse and disrupting local transport. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Road (B4443) in Stoke Mandeville experienced flooding during winter 2013/2014. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred during the winter 2013/2014 flood event and prompted a formal flood investigation by Risk Management Authorities (Buckinghamshire CC, AVDC, TfB). The location was highlighted soon after the event as requiring investigation. [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded flooding of Lower Road (road inundation) reported by a local resident; surface water inundation affecting the road and nearby properties in the Lower Road area. [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blockages in the main drainage line on Lower Road contributed to flooding during the heavy rainfall of winter 2013/14. Transport for Buckinghamshire used a heavy duty jetter and gulley crew on several visits, excavated the footway/verge to investigate, and repairs were carried out where blockages could not be cleared by jetter. [MUL] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Lower Road, Stoke Mandeville",England,"Carriageway covered by deep surface water during winter 2013/2014; vehicles were forced to drive through floodwater with large splashes, making the road difficult and dangerous to traverse and disrupting local transport. (p1)
Lower Road (B4443) in Stoke Mandeville experienced flooding during winter 2013/2014. [PAG] (p3)
Surface water flooding occurred during the winter 2013/2014 flood event and prompted a formal flood investigation by Risk Management Authorities (Buckinghamshire CC, AVDC, TfB). The location was highlighted soon after the event as requiring investigation. [IMG,PAG] (p8)
Recorded flooding of Lower Road (road inundation) reported by a local resident; surface water inundation affecting the road and nearby properties in the Lower Road area. [IMG,PAG] (p9)
Blockages in the main drainage line on Lower Road contributed to flooding during the heavy rainfall of winter 2013/14. Transport for Buckinghamshire used a heavy duty jetter and gulley crew on several visits, excavated the footway/verge to investigate, and repairs were carried out where blockages could not be cleared by jetter. [MUL] (p14)",,GB-BKM_9_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_9_2013-12-01,Stoke Mandeville flooding winter 2013/2014,"Lower Road (B4443), Stoke Mandeville, Winter 2013/2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/lower-road-stoke-mandeville-s19-report-final.pdf
GB-BKM,9,2013-12-01,51.78627977542804,-0.7897635899241652,"<div id=""html_d6dab05307dca91fcc864780ffa28d0d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stoke Mandeville</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Road (B4443) in Stoke Mandeville experienced surface water/urban flooding in winter 2013/2014 that covered the roadway and disrupted local transport, forcing vehicles to traverse deep water and reducing road safety. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The settlement experienced surface water flooding during winter 2013/2014 that inundated roads (including Lower Road) as the catchment and road drainage were at or near capacity. [MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Stoke Mandeville,England,"Lower Road (B4443) in Stoke Mandeville experienced surface water/urban flooding in winter 2013/2014 that covered the roadway and disrupted local transport, forcing vehicles to traverse deep water and reducing road safety. (p1)
The settlement experienced surface water flooding during winter 2013/2014 that inundated roads (including Lower Road) as the catchment and road drainage were at or near capacity. [MUL] (p4)",,GB-BKM_9_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_9_2013-12-01,Stoke Mandeville flooding winter 2013/2014,"Lower Road (B4443), Stoke Mandeville, Winter 2013/2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/lower-road-stoke-mandeville-s19-report-final.pdf
GB-BDF,6,2020-12-23,52.1818158,-0.6049976,"<div id=""html_d8950d2363bbe7ae0345a08ccf5a0b57"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>School Lane, Carlton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall generated an overland flood flow route down School Lane that conveyed runoff into the ditch system feeding Turvey Road. This flow path contributed to water backing up across an adjacent field and was a causal factor in internal flooding of a nearby residential property off The Marsh (ground&ndash;floor inundation, damaged furniture). [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall formed an overland flood flow route down School Lane (about 600 m south of The Marsh), which discharged into a ditch feeding the piped/open ditch system toward The Marsh. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; 75 mm flood depth [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bedford,"School Lane, Carlton",England,"Heavy rainfall generated an overland flood flow route down School Lane that conveyed runoff into the ditch system feeding Turvey Road. This flow path contributed to water backing up across an adjacent field and was a causal factor in internal flooding of a nearby residential property off The Marsh (ground–floor inundation, damaged furniture). [IMG,PAG] (p1)
Heavy rainfall formed an overland flood flow route down School Lane (about 600 m south of The Marsh), which discharged into a ditch feeding the piped/open ditch system toward The Marsh. (p2)","1 residential property; 75 mm flood depth [IMG,PAG] (p1)",GB-BDF_6_2020-12-23.html,GB-BDF_6_2020-12-23,Carlton flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Carlton - The Marsh,005/Bedford/005/2021/Bedford/Carlton-The-Marsh/July/2021.pdf
GB-BDF,6,2020-12-23,52.1832661,-0.6191915,"<div id=""html_372253832b4db2289765180475ec1c7b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Turvey Road, Carlton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from School Lane discharged into a ditch along Turvey Road; the ditch is piped beneath Turvey Road and becomes an open ditch flowing toward The Marsh. Partial blockage of the upstream trash screen in the piped system is reported to have caused water to back up across the adjacent field toward the residential property, contributing to internal flooding and property damage. [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff discharged into a ditch along Turvey Road; the ditch is piped beneath Turvey Road and continues east of St Mary&#8217;s Church as an open ditch toward The Marsh. A partially blocked trash screen upstream of the piped system is thought to have caused water to back up across an adjacent field toward properties, contributing to flooding. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; 75 mm flood depth [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bedford,"Turvey Road, Carlton",England,"Runoff from School Lane discharged into a ditch along Turvey Road; the ditch is piped beneath Turvey Road and becomes an open ditch flowing toward The Marsh. Partial blockage of the upstream trash screen in the piped system is reported to have caused water to back up across the adjacent field toward the residential property, contributing to internal flooding and property damage. [IMG,PAG] (p1)
Runoff discharged into a ditch along Turvey Road; the ditch is piped beneath Turvey Road and continues east of St Mary’s Church as an open ditch toward The Marsh. A partially blocked trash screen upstream of the piped system is thought to have caused water to back up across an adjacent field toward properties, contributing to flooding. (p2)","1 residential property; 75 mm flood depth [IMG,PAG] (p1)",GB-BDF_6_2020-12-23.html,GB-BDF_6_2020-12-23,Carlton flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Carlton - The Marsh,005/Bedford/005/2021/Bedford/Carlton-The-Marsh/July/2021.pdf
GB-BDF,6,2020-12-23,52.1890346,-0.610043,"<div id=""html_26926e6162e2e4685e332065d4cc61c4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Marsh, Carlton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of at least one residential property on The Marsh (ground floor flooded, furniture damaged). A second property was close to flooding but residents dug a trench to divert water westwards around the property into the northern ditch system. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; 75mm across the ground floor (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bedford,"The Marsh, Carlton",England,"Internal flooding of at least one residential property on The Marsh (ground floor flooded, furniture damaged). A second property was close to flooding but residents dug a trench to divert water westwards around the property into the northern ditch system. (p2)",1 residential property; 75mm across the ground floor (p2),GB-BDF_6_2020-12-23.html,GB-BDF_6_2020-12-23,Carlton flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Carlton - The Marsh,005/Bedford/005/2021/Bedford/Carlton-The-Marsh/July/2021.pdf
GB-BKM,17,2016-06-15,51.665451,-0.6170104,"<div id=""html_b8df98a74154fccb40bd927ad05b5af3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Old Amersham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High&ndash;intensity, localised rainfall led to rapid surface&ndash;water runoff on urban surfaces, resulting in flooding of homes and businesses; the event had very little warning and was of short duration during business hours. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding occurred such that the incident met the council&#8217;s Section 19 thresholds: multiple residential properties (including basements) were internally flooded; at least one property experienced internal flooding for one week or longer; business premises were internally flooded; one or more items of critical infrastructure were flooded. (p5)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 residential properties; 20 commercial properties (internally flooded) (Old Amersham and Amersham) (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five or more residential properties; 1km2; one property for one week or longer; two or more business premises; one or more items of critical infrastructure (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Old Amersham,England,"High–intensity, localised rainfall led to rapid surface–water runoff on urban surfaces, resulting in flooding of homes and businesses; the event had very little warning and was of short duration during business hours. (p4)
Internal flooding occurred such that the incident met the council’s Section 19 thresholds: multiple residential properties (including basements) were internally flooded; at least one property experienced internal flooding for one week or longer; business premises were internally flooded; one or more items of critical infrastructure were flooded. (p5)","9 residential properties; 20 commercial properties (internally flooded) (Old Amersham and Amersham) (p4)
five or more residential properties; 1km2; one property for one week or longer; two or more business premises; one or more items of critical infrastructure (p5)",GB-BKM_17_2016-06-15.html,GB-BKM_17_2016-06-15,Old Amersham flooding 15 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham 15th June 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-amersham-investigation-final-april-2018.pdf
GB-BKM,17,2016-06-15,51.66688635,-0.624285834301358,"<div id=""html_690285e92c85d8fd391d5f6de03696c9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Amersham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 15 June 2016 event produced extremely localised, intense rainfall with a HYRAD cell centred over Amersham (one pocket estimated at 38&ndash;41.5 mm in 24 hours). This localized intense precipitation is associated with the 15 June 2016 flood event and is likely to have produced localised surface&ndash;water/flash flooding and urban flooding in the Amersham area (report text links the rainfall cell to the 15 June flood event). The text does not give granular property&ndash;level impacts for Amersham on 15 June, so this attribution is made with some uncertainty. [PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Amersham,England,"The 15 June 2016 event produced extremely localised, intense rainfall with a HYRAD cell centred over Amersham (one pocket estimated at 38–41.5 mm in 24 hours). This localized intense precipitation is associated with the 15 June 2016 flood event and is likely to have produced localised surface–water/flash flooding and urban flooding in the Amersham area (report text links the rainfall cell to the 15 June flood event). The text does not give granular property–level impacts for Amersham on 15 June, so this attribution is made with some uncertainty. [PAG] (p11)",,GB-BKM_17_2016-06-15.html,GB-BKM_17_2016-06-15,Old Amersham flooding 15 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham 15th June 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-amersham-investigation-final-april-2018.pdf
GB-CAM,7,2014-01-01,52.4923753,0.0396022,"<div id=""html_3d92dc903287187da9444c556f20a161"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Primrose Hill, Doddington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Indicative flood extent covered Primrose Hill and adjacent farmland; flooding overlapped rural and residential areas southwest of Doddington. [IMG] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Primrose Hill, Doddington",England,Indicative flood extent covered Primrose Hill and adjacent farmland; flooding overlapped rural and residential areas southwest of Doddington. [IMG] (p2),,GB-CAM_7_2014-01-01.html,GB-CAM_7_2014-01-01,Doddington flooding January 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Doddington,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Flood/Investigation/Report/Doddington.pdf
GB-CAM,7,2014-01-01,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_be42faa8ed6f312cbd4aa29e39a0b489"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood incident occurred within Cambridgeshire &mdash; specifically at Primrose Hill, Doddington &mdash; affecting nearby farmland and areas adjacent to Doddington village. [IMG] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"The flood incident occurred within Cambridgeshire — specifically at Primrose Hill, Doddington — affecting nearby farmland and areas adjacent to Doddington village. [IMG] (p2)",,GB-CAM_7_2014-01-01.html,GB-CAM_7_2014-01-01,Doddington flooding January 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Doddington,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Flood/Investigation/Report/Doddington.pdf
GB-CAM,7,2014-01-01,52.49674233228311,0.06387379461720628,"<div id=""html_53649adc95545ff0d0aa193ee2dae744"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Doddington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A substantial area southwest of Doddington was indicated as flooded, including nearby fields and areas adjacent to the village (overlapping rural and residential zones). [IMG] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Doddington,England,"A substantial area southwest of Doddington was indicated as flooded, including nearby fields and areas adjacent to the village (overlapping rural and residential zones). [IMG] (p2)",,GB-CAM_7_2014-01-01.html,GB-CAM_7_2014-01-01,Doddington flooding January 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Doddington,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Flood/Investigation/Report/Doddington.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.01390155849167,-0.23115151800182449,"<div id=""html_dd94aabdcaf9cb41ecb28b13b9a784f6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pig and Whistle (former)</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bridge by the Pig and Whistle was described as not suitable to convey the volume of water; this contributed to blockage/backup of the brook and local flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 inches [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,Pig and Whistle (former),England,"The bridge by the Pig and Whistle was described as not suitable to convey the volume of water; this contributed to blockage/backup of the brook and local flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p19)","3 inches [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p19)",GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.0168398,-0.2433318,"<div id=""html_06d9a426cf85d00825a878ef09f9bc10"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pix Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris was blocking the brook causing water to enter residential properties; flow was restored after debris was cleared. The blockage and associated flooding prompted deployment of responders and mitigation measures. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the intense rainfall event on 4 July 2015 a significant quantity of debris (vegetation, timber and watercress) was mobilised and conveyed along Pix Brook to downstream locations, contributing to blockage risk at the twin culvert and requiring debris management measures. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris blockage of the Pix Brook twin&ndash;culvert caused surface water to accumulate upstream and contributed to internal and external flooding in the affected area. Residents reported being affected internally or externally on 4th July 2015; debris blocking the culvert on 24th July 2015 caused surface water to accumulate on Brook Street/Coppice Mead. IDB undertook maintenance and a 2015 project to reduce culvert blocking and enable safe debris removal. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 4th July 2015 heavy rainfall produced high flows in the Pix Brook; debris (predominantly watercress) accumulated at the trash screen at the Brook Street road junction, overwhelming the screen and blocking the twin&ndash;culvert highway culvert. This caused upstream water levels to rise rapidly, overtopping the brook banks and flooding the local area, resulting in internal and external flooding, risk to life and obstruction of access. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Trash screens at Brook Street, Valerian Way and other structures became blocked and were cleared following the flood; debris removal was necessary to restore flow. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of Pix Brook on 4 July 2015 led to blockages at trash screens and other structures (notably at Brook Street and Valerian Way), contributing to local flooding that prompted emergency attendance by the Internal Drainage Board to clear/remove screens and a Section 19 investigation coordinated by Central Bedfordshire Council. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pix Brook is described as having a significant debris load (sources include a nearby conservation woodland, watercress growth and garden/back&ndash;channel debris adjacent to Brook Street) that can block culverts and reduce channel capacity during flood flows. Specific issues cited include potential debris blocking at the A507 culvert, sediment accumulation at the Brook Street highway bridge reducing discharge capacity, and interaction with the Pix Brook Reservoir flood gates &mdash; all contributing to flooding risk in the locality. [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pix Brook overtopped/caused local flooding in Stotfold on 4 July 2015. Debris from adjacent areas (notably watercress and tree branches) was identified as blocking the channel and contributing to flow obstructions along the banks, increasing flood risk to nearby properties and land. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a prime source of debris (including watercress and woody branches) that was transported and accumulated in the channel and at outfalls, increasing debris loading and contributing to blockages during the 4 July 2015 flood; prompted recommendations for trash screens, maintenance, baffle hedges and other controls to reduce debris entering the channel. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the Pix Brook caused the brook to burst its banks, inundating adjacent woodland and mobilising woody debris which was carried downstream (identified as the source of debris found at Brook Street). (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Vegetation (identified as watercress) from upstream woodland was dislodged and flowed downstream, with similar weed observed having been pulled out at Brook Street &mdash; indicating vegetation/debris transport by the flood flows and potential for local channel/blockage issues. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred along the Pix Brook corridor on 4 July 2015. The post&ndash;event annotated map identifies culverts (Hitchin Road, Brook Street), IDB trash screens (Brook Street, Valerian Way), a standalone reservoir and control gates, and the proximity of Letchworth sewage works as relevant to the event and response. The report notes actions to increase retention in the lower floodplain and the need to intercept debris before the two channels meet. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The brook became blocked by debris and by a recently installed trash screen/bridge, forming a dam that prevented flow and caused the brook to overflow into gardens, garages, vehicles and streets. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The brook course was blocked by debris and a recently installed trash gate/trash screen which collected debris and was not cleared, causing the brook to be unable to convey storm flows. These blockages led to overflow and flooding of adjacent gardens and streets on 04/07/2015 and during heavy rainfall in mid&ndash;July 2015. [PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties; two properties (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,Pix Brook,England,"Debris was blocking the brook causing water to enter residential properties; flow was restored after debris was cleared. The blockage and associated flooding prompted deployment of responders and mitigation measures. (p5)
During the intense rainfall event on 4 July 2015 a significant quantity of debris (vegetation, timber and watercress) was mobilised and conveyed along Pix Brook to downstream locations, contributing to blockage risk at the twin culvert and requiring debris management measures. [PAG] (p6)
Debris blockage of the Pix Brook twin–culvert caused surface water to accumulate upstream and contributed to internal and external flooding in the affected area. Residents reported being affected internally or externally on 4th July 2015; debris blocking the culvert on 24th July 2015 caused surface water to accumulate on Brook Street/Coppice Mead. IDB undertook maintenance and a 2015 project to reduce culvert blocking and enable safe debris removal. [MUL] (p7)
On 4th July 2015 heavy rainfall produced high flows in the Pix Brook; debris (predominantly watercress) accumulated at the trash screen at the Brook Street road junction, overwhelming the screen and blocking the twin–culvert highway culvert. This caused upstream water levels to rise rapidly, overtopping the brook banks and flooding the local area, resulting in internal and external flooding, risk to life and obstruction of access. (p8)
Trash screens at Brook Street, Valerian Way and other structures became blocked and were cleared following the flood; debris removal was necessary to restore flow. [PAG] (p9)
Flooding of Pix Brook on 4 July 2015 led to blockages at trash screens and other structures (notably at Brook Street and Valerian Way), contributing to local flooding that prompted emergency attendance by the Internal Drainage Board to clear/remove screens and a Section 19 investigation coordinated by Central Bedfordshire Council. (p10)
Pix Brook is described as having a significant debris load (sources include a nearby conservation woodland, watercress growth and garden/back–channel debris adjacent to Brook Street) that can block culverts and reduce channel capacity during flood flows. Specific issues cited include potential debris blocking at the A507 culvert, sediment accumulation at the Brook Street highway bridge reducing discharge capacity, and interaction with the Pix Brook Reservoir flood gates — all contributing to flooding risk in the locality. [TAB,PAG] (p12)
The Pix Brook overtopped/caused local flooding in Stotfold on 4 July 2015. Debris from adjacent areas (notably watercress and tree branches) was identified as blocking the channel and contributing to flow obstructions along the banks, increasing flood risk to nearby properties and land. (p13)
Identified as a prime source of debris (including watercress and woody branches) that was transported and accumulated in the channel and at outfalls, increasing debris loading and contributing to blockages during the 4 July 2015 flood; prompted recommendations for trash screens, maintenance, baffle hedges and other controls to reduce debris entering the channel. [PAG] (p14)
High flows in the Pix Brook caused the brook to burst its banks, inundating adjacent woodland and mobilising woody debris which was carried downstream (identified as the source of debris found at Brook Street). (p15)
Vegetation (identified as watercress) from upstream woodland was dislodged and flowed downstream, with similar weed observed having been pulled out at Brook Street — indicating vegetation/debris transport by the flood flows and potential for local channel/blockage issues. [PAG] (p17)
Flooding occurred along the Pix Brook corridor on 4 July 2015. The post–event annotated map identifies culverts (Hitchin Road, Brook Street), IDB trash screens (Brook Street, Valerian Way), a standalone reservoir and control gates, and the proximity of Letchworth sewage works as relevant to the event and response. The report notes actions to increase retention in the lower floodplain and the need to intercept debris before the two channels meet. [IMG] (p18)
The brook became blocked by debris and by a recently installed trash screen/bridge, forming a dam that prevented flow and caused the brook to overflow into gardens, garages, vehicles and streets. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p19)
The brook course was blocked by debris and a recently installed trash gate/trash screen which collected debris and was not cleared, causing the brook to be unable to convey storm flows. These blockages led to overflow and flooding of adjacent gardens and streets on 04/07/2015 and during heavy rainfall in mid–July 2015. [PAG,MUL] (p20)",12 properties; two properties (p5),GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.0763881,-0.2714684,"<div id=""html_9c375f7e7f5dd3aeae848d3233b847ab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Ivel</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment is fast&ndash;responding and vulnerable to flash flooding during significant rainfall, leading to rapid runoff during the 4 July 2015 event. (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,River Ivel,England,"Catchment is fast–responding and vulnerable to flash flooding during significant rainfall, leading to rapid runoff during the 4 July 2015 event. (p6)",,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.01392560851627,-0.2333113506671238,"<div id=""html_f876b6e25f57e24da6edb85c95bd1320"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brook Street Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A newly installed trash screen/strainer at the Brook Street bridge became blocked by debris on 04/07/2015 and acted like a dam, holding back flow. The blockage caused upstream water to build up and overflow into back gardens, garages, houses and along Brook Street. [TAB] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,Brook Street Bridge,England,"A newly installed trash screen/strainer at the Brook Street bridge became blocked by debris on 04/07/2015 and acted like a dam, holding back flow. The blockage caused upstream water to build up and overflow into back gardens, garages, houses and along Brook Street. [TAB] (p19)",,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.0138423,-0.2313026,"<div id=""html_95e487a9eb3dd3f34fd8af79bdc86787"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brook Street, Stotfold</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris blocking Pix Brook at Brook Street caused water to enter residential properties; Fire & Rescue were on site and deployed aqua sacs to limit flood water entering properties; IDB operatives and Highways/grab lorry cleared debris and the brook was reported to be flowing again; Brook Street trash screen was cleared/removed by IDB. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water accumulated on Brook Street (and Coppice Mead) when debris blocked the twin&ndash;culvert downstream, causing obstruction of access (estimated 24 hours) and contributing to internal/external flooding reported by residents on 4th July 2015. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 4th July 2015 the trash screen at the road junction on Brook Street became blocked by debris during high flows, preventing flow into the highway culvert and causing overtopping and flooding of the highway/local area and obstruction of access. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Trash screens at Brook Street became obstructed during the flood; the Internal Drainage Board attended on 4th July to assist in clearing the trash screen(s). (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Accumulation of sediment and debris in the Brook Street highway bridge and the adjacent back channel reduced the discharge capacity of the highway asset and was identified as a debris source that could contribute to flooding of the gardens adjacent to the back channel. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris accumulated in the back channel and at Brook Street (the report states the majority of the debris at Brook Street came from nearby woodland), reducing channel capacity and contributing to flooding and debris entry into gardens during the 4 July 2015 event; recommended inspection, removal of debris sources and surveying of the highway bridge for sediment. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Deposition/accumulation of debris at Brook Street caused by flood flows in the Pix Brook; the report states the majority of debris at Brook Street originated from the nearby woodland washed into the brook. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows dislodged vegetation (identified as watercress) which was pulled out at Brook Street and transported downstream, indicating strong flood flows and debris movement. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Brook Street is shown on the 4 July 2015 annotated map as the location of a highway culvert and an IDB Brook Street trash screen; these features are identified as relevant to flood flow and debris interception during the event. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflow from the brook (exacerbated by a blocked trash/gate at the Brook Street bridge) caused flooding down Brook Street and into adjacent gardens and properties. Reports note water overflowing into Coppice Mead and previous events where water rose high enough to threaten properties; the fire brigade was called to help divert road water on an earlier occasion. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A blocked trash screen/trash strainer at the Brook Street bridge on 04/07/2015 caused the Pixy to overflow directly into back gardens and down Brook Street into Coppice Mead. Heavy rainfall on 16/17 July 2015 also caused overflow into back gardens. The floodplain was ineffective and the brook course was blocked so no relief flow was available. [MUL] (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties; 2 properties (electricity isolated) (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,"Brook Street, Stotfold",England,"Debris blocking Pix Brook at Brook Street caused water to enter residential properties; Fire & Rescue were on site and deployed aqua sacs to limit flood water entering properties; IDB operatives and Highways/grab lorry cleared debris and the brook was reported to be flowing again; Brook Street trash screen was cleared/removed by IDB. (p5)
Surface water accumulated on Brook Street (and Coppice Mead) when debris blocked the twin–culvert downstream, causing obstruction of access (estimated 24 hours) and contributing to internal/external flooding reported by residents on 4th July 2015. [MUL] (p7)
On 4th July 2015 the trash screen at the road junction on Brook Street became blocked by debris during high flows, preventing flow into the highway culvert and causing overtopping and flooding of the highway/local area and obstruction of access. (p8)
Trash screens at Brook Street became obstructed during the flood; the Internal Drainage Board attended on 4th July to assist in clearing the trash screen(s). (p10)
Accumulation of sediment and debris in the Brook Street highway bridge and the adjacent back channel reduced the discharge capacity of the highway asset and was identified as a debris source that could contribute to flooding of the gardens adjacent to the back channel. [TAB] (p12)
Debris accumulated in the back channel and at Brook Street (the report states the majority of the debris at Brook Street came from nearby woodland), reducing channel capacity and contributing to flooding and debris entry into gardens during the 4 July 2015 event; recommended inspection, removal of debris sources and surveying of the highway bridge for sediment. [PAG] (p14)
Deposition/accumulation of debris at Brook Street caused by flood flows in the Pix Brook; the report states the majority of debris at Brook Street originated from the nearby woodland washed into the brook. (p15)
High flows dislodged vegetation (identified as watercress) which was pulled out at Brook Street and transported downstream, indicating strong flood flows and debris movement. (p16)
Brook Street is shown on the 4 July 2015 annotated map as the location of a highway culvert and an IDB Brook Street trash screen; these features are identified as relevant to flood flow and debris interception during the event. [IMG] (p18)
Overflow from the brook (exacerbated by a blocked trash/gate at the Brook Street bridge) caused flooding down Brook Street and into adjacent gardens and properties. Reports note water overflowing into Coppice Mead and previous events where water rose high enough to threaten properties; the fire brigade was called to help divert road water on an earlier occasion. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p19)
A blocked trash screen/trash strainer at the Brook Street bridge on 04/07/2015 caused the Pixy to overflow directly into back gardens and down Brook Street into Coppice Mead. Heavy rainfall on 16/17 July 2015 also caused overflow into back gardens. The floodplain was ineffective and the brook course was blocked so no relief flow was available. [MUL] (p20)",12 properties; 2 properties (electricity isolated) (p5),GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.0084918,-0.2383904,"<div id=""html_4c39d8fb8c2ab8566cd3f34c9290ba88"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Valerian Way, Stotfold</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris was blocking the Valerian Way trash screen and IDB operatives were on site clearing debris to restore flow during the incident. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Trash screens at Valerian Way were obstructed during the flood; the Internal Drainage Board attended on 4th July to assist in clearing the trash screen(s). (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,"Valerian Way, Stotfold",England,"Debris was blocking the Valerian Way trash screen and IDB operatives were on site clearing debris to restore flow during the incident. (p5)
Trash screens at Valerian Way were obstructed during the flood; the Internal Drainage Board attended on 4th July to assist in clearing the trash screen(s). (p10)",,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.0123996,-0.2344371,"<div id=""html_182b06076f586875fde2d47e6bdc9611"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coppice Mead, Stotfold</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water accumulated on Brook Street/Coppice Mead when debris reportedly blocked the twin&ndash;culvert downstream; residents reported internal and external flooding at the flood location on 4th July 2015. Access was obstructed for an estimated 24 hours. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Raised water levels at the point where the Pix's two channels re&ndash;converge backed up along the old channel and into properties on Coppice Mead; properties are at a considerably lower level than surrounding land so experienced inundation from the backed&ndash;up channel. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,"Coppice Mead, Stotfold",England,"Flood water accumulated on Brook Street/Coppice Mead when debris reportedly blocked the twin–culvert downstream; residents reported internal and external flooding at the flood location on 4th July 2015. Access was obstructed for an estimated 24 hours. [MUL] (p7)
Raised water levels at the point where the Pix's two channels re–converge backed up along the old channel and into properties on Coppice Mead; properties are at a considerably lower level than surrounding land so experienced inundation from the backed–up channel. (p17)",,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.0089332,-0.2403982,"<div id=""html_06d8434ab5b3a5c4b3a6bd6cc8d61a00"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hitchin Road, Stotfold</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hitchin Road is labelled on the annotated map of the 4 July 2015 event as a highway culvert on Pix Brook and therefore identified as infrastructure relevant to the flood conveyance during that event. [IMG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,"Hitchin Road, Stotfold",England,Hitchin Road is labelled on the annotated map of the 4 July 2015 event as a highway culvert on Pix Brook and therefore identified as infrastructure relevant to the flood conveyance during that event. [IMG] (p18),,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,51.9980328,-0.4791514,"<div id=""html_19fbf6e55dd684588daf907cc00bb931"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Central Bedfordshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Portions of Central Bedfordshire (specifically the Pix Brook corridor within the district) experienced flooding associated with the 4 July 2015 event; Central Bedfordshire Council produced an annotated post&ndash;event map (dated 15 Sept 2015) documenting infrastructure and flood management considerations for the affected corridor. [IMG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,Central Bedfordshire,England,Portions of Central Bedfordshire (specifically the Pix Brook corridor within the district) experienced flooding associated with the 4 July 2015 event; Central Bedfordshire Council produced an annotated post–event map (dated 15 Sept 2015) documenting infrastructure and flood management considerations for the affected corridor. [IMG] (p18),,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-CBF,2,2015-07-04,52.015001,-0.22513521613427134,"<div id=""html_f8a98cef38c8490aa2e045128bfbe538"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stotfold</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town was affected by flooding associated with the Pix Brook system during the event, with debris loading and potential culvert blockages reducing channel/discharge capacity and contributing to urban flood flows through Stotfold. Recommendations target community resilience and local asset interventions to reduce future flood impacts. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town was the focus of the investigation into the 4 July 2015 flooding; recommendations address debris management, asset performance, community resilience and measures to reduce flood impacts in Stotfold arising from Pix Brook blockages. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Stotfold is included in the annotated map of the 4 July 2015 Pix Brook flood corridor. The map highlights the Stotfold stretch of Pix Brook and associated infrastructure (culverts, trash screens) and notes South Stotfold as a new development within the mapped flood extent. [IMG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Central Bedfordshire,Stotfold,England,"The town was affected by flooding associated with the Pix Brook system during the event, with debris loading and potential culvert blockages reducing channel/discharge capacity and contributing to urban flood flows through Stotfold. Recommendations target community resilience and local asset interventions to reduce future flood impacts. [TAB] (p12)
The town was the focus of the investigation into the 4 July 2015 flooding; recommendations address debris management, asset performance, community resilience and measures to reduce flood impacts in Stotfold arising from Pix Brook blockages. (p14)
Stotfold is included in the annotated map of the 4 July 2015 Pix Brook flood corridor. The map highlights the Stotfold stretch of Pix Brook and associated infrastructure (culverts, trash screens) and notes South Stotfold as a new development within the mapped flood extent. [IMG] (p18)",,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04.html,GB-CBF_2_2015-07-04,Stotfold flooding 04 Jul 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Pix Brook, Stotfold",023/Central Bedfordshire/023/2016/Central Bedfordshire/pix-brook/tcm3-10511.pdf
GB-HEF,12,2020-02-16,52.1310877,-2.7605585,"<div id=""html_774f8c1c17c2271c20a9f4789dd1dec6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wellington Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wellington Brook (and its tributary) is identified as an ordinary watercourse under Herefordshire Council and is associated with the flood event affecting Canon Pyon on 16 Feb 2020; it is therefore a watercourse relevant to the flooding shown on the map. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The tributary feeding Wellington Brook is identified as the fluvial source for the flooding affecting nearby properties; photographic evidence from October 2019 supports a fluvial mechanism linked to this watercourse. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties internally flooded (related to the tributary to Wellington Brook); 3 properties also flooded in October 2019. [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Wellington Brook,England,"Wellington Brook (and its tributary) is identified as an ordinary watercourse under Herefordshire Council and is associated with the flood event affecting Canon Pyon on 16 Feb 2020; it is therefore a watercourse relevant to the flooding shown on the map. (p1)
The tributary feeding Wellington Brook is identified as the fluvial source for the flooding affecting nearby properties; photographic evidence from October 2019 supports a fluvial mechanism linked to this watercourse. [MUL] (p2)",6 properties internally flooded (related to the tributary to Wellington Brook); 3 properties also flooded in October 2019. [MUL] (p2),GB-HEF_12_2020-02-16_etc.html,GB-HEF_12_2020-02-16_etc,,,
GB-HEF,12,2020-02-16,52.1359641,-2.7873941,"<div id=""html_08b1a6de9cbe9e792918f2cca2ef545c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Canon Pyon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding concentrated along the main river channel in and immediately adjacent to Canon Pyon during the key event; area mapped as Flood Zone 3 (highest risk) with some areas shown as benefiting from flood defences. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in and adjacent to Flood Zone 3, and others in Flood Zone 1, experienced internal flooding attributed to fluvial inundation from a tributary and to surface water runoff/blocked highway drainage. Photographic evidence documents October 2019 flooding. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties internally flooded; 3 of these also flooded in October 2019; 3 listed properties located within the Flood Zone. [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Canon Pyon,England,"Flooding concentrated along the main river channel in and immediately adjacent to Canon Pyon during the key event; area mapped as Flood Zone 3 (highest risk) with some areas shown as benefiting from flood defences. [IMG] (p1)
Properties in and adjacent to Flood Zone 3, and others in Flood Zone 1, experienced internal flooding attributed to fluvial inundation from a tributary and to surface water runoff/blocked highway drainage. Photographic evidence documents October 2019 flooding. [MUL] (p2)",6 properties internally flooded; 3 of these also flooded in October 2019; 3 listed properties located within the Flood Zone. [MUL] (p2),GB-HEF_12_2020-02-16_etc.html,GB-HEF_12_2020-02-16_etc,,,
GB-NNH,27,2019-06-13,52.5373734,-0.4803717,"<div id=""html_d48e43c0c6556828e43a2a3db6c7ecf2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willow Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall increased flows in the watercourse (Willow Brook). Restriction at the flow control culvert/weir caused upstream water levels to rise and water to spill sideways into the balancing ponds; when pond capacity continued to rise flows were routed downstream via the emergency spillway toward Pen Green Lane and the railway aqueduct. The aqueduct and its spillways were at risk of being exceeded, threatening conveyance to the railway drainage at track level. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The tributary of the Willow Brook at the location of the Corby flood incident was the watercourse involved in the flooding; it is classified as a Main River. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Willow Brook,England,"Heavy rainfall increased flows in the watercourse (Willow Brook). Restriction at the flow control culvert/weir caused upstream water levels to rise and water to spill sideways into the balancing ponds; when pond capacity continued to rise flows were routed downstream via the emergency spillway toward Pen Green Lane and the railway aqueduct. The aqueduct and its spillways were at risk of being exceeded, threatening conveyance to the railway drainage at track level. (p15)
The tributary of the Willow Brook at the location of the Corby flood incident was the watercourse involved in the flooding; it is classified as a Main River. (p24)",,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13.html,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13,Corby flooding 13 Jun 2019,"Flood Investigation Report: Railway Adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby, 13 June 2019","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby.pdf"
GB-NNH,27,2019-06-13,52.50858395462103,-0.6822355549584499,"<div id=""html_ae19543196691bb8d11403ce64d9294e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Corby Tunnel</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater ran down the cutting, increased in depth around the railway tracks and flowed north toward Corby Tunnel on 13 June 2019; standing water reached depths that would affect the safe operation of trains and a landslip occurred onto the tracks. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water flowing from the cutting and overtopping ponds increased water depth on and around the railway tracks and through Corby Tunnel, affecting safe train operation. A land slip from the cutting slope occurred onto the tracks (around 16:00), disabling a stopped southbound passenger train. Passengers were ultimately escorted off the railway on foot and evacuated by coach; emergency services assisted and Network Rail cleared the landslip and drained water via railway drainage. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Corby Tunnel,England,"Floodwater ran down the cutting, increased in depth around the railway tracks and flowed north toward Corby Tunnel on 13 June 2019; standing water reached depths that would affect the safe operation of trains and a landslip occurred onto the tracks. (p17)
Water flowing from the cutting and overtopping ponds increased water depth on and around the railway tracks and through Corby Tunnel, affecting safe train operation. A land slip from the cutting slope occurred onto the tracks (around 16:00), disabling a stopped southbound passenger train. Passengers were ultimately escorted off the railway on foot and evacuated by coach; emergency services assisted and Network Rail cleared the landslip and drained water via railway drainage. (p18)",,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13.html,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13,Corby flooding 13 Jun 2019,"Flood Investigation Report: Railway Adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby, 13 June 2019","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby.pdf"
GB-NNH,27,2019-06-13,52.5018438,-0.6827108,"<div id=""html_9fd43f740a033103469133db7c9182da"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pen Green Lane, Corby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water from unmanaged overflow flowed over ground to localised low points, flooding the railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane. The railway (critical infrastructure) was affected by flooding for more than three hours from the onset of flooding. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A short access culvert beneath Pen Green Lane was reported to be almost completely blocked at the time of the flood incident, restricting flow and contributing to the flood event affecting downstream conveyance. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping from Pond 2 flowed across Pen Green Lane on 13 June 2019, conveying floodwater east across disused land to the top of the railway cutting. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert under Pen Green Lane was reported almost completely blocked. The blockage diverted excess flow to off&ndash;line balancing ponds, causing the ponds to fill and contributing to downstream overtopping and flow onto the railway cutting. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water spilled over the low point on Pen Green Lane from Pond 2, causing overtopping of the carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water from Pond 2 spilled over at a low point on Pen Green Lane, inundating the road. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Pen Green Lane, Corby",England,"Excess surface water from unmanaged overflow flowed over ground to localised low points, flooding the railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane. The railway (critical infrastructure) was affected by flooding for more than three hours from the onset of flooding. (p5)
A short access culvert beneath Pen Green Lane was reported to be almost completely blocked at the time of the flood incident, restricting flow and contributing to the flood event affecting downstream conveyance. [PAG] (p14)
Overtopping from Pond 2 flowed across Pen Green Lane on 13 June 2019, conveying floodwater east across disused land to the top of the railway cutting. (p17)
The culvert under Pen Green Lane was reported almost completely blocked. The blockage diverted excess flow to off–line balancing ponds, causing the ponds to fill and contributing to downstream overtopping and flow onto the railway cutting. (p18)
Water spilled over the low point on Pen Green Lane from Pond 2, causing overtopping of the carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p33)
Water from Pond 2 spilled over at a low point on Pen Green Lane, inundating the road. [IMG,PAG] (p34)",,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13.html,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13,Corby flooding 13 Jun 2019,"Flood Investigation Report: Railway Adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby, 13 June 2019","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby.pdf"
GB-NNH,27,2019-06-13,52.4946001,-0.6795257,"<div id=""html_86405a87a615a216a8ede4d1da70bef1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Phoenix Parkway, Corby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Phoenix Parkway / A6116 roundabout area is shown on the map overlapping a blue flood&ndash;risk zone and is adjacent to the watercourse/pipeline/aqueduct convergence highlighted by the red circle. It is therefore implicated as being at risk of, and likely affected by, the flood incident described in the report. [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Phoenix Parkway, Corby",England,"The Phoenix Parkway / A6116 roundabout area is shown on the map overlapping a blue flood–risk zone and is adjacent to the watercourse/pipeline/aqueduct convergence highlighted by the red circle. It is therefore implicated as being at risk of, and likely affected by, the flood incident described in the report. [IMG] (p13)",,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13.html,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13,Corby flooding 13 Jun 2019,"Flood Investigation Report: Railway Adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby, 13 June 2019","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby.pdf"
GB-NNH,27,2019-06-13,52.50083,-0.6832309,"<div id=""html_c5ea6632d1dc14915b08f284329a9265"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stephenson Way, Corby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse flows along the north boundary of properties on Stephenson Way and was within the immediate locality of the flood incident; restricted flow and blocked culverts caused elevated upstream levels and flows that affected the area adjacent to Stephenson Way during the incident. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Stephenson Way, Corby",England,The watercourse flows along the north boundary of properties on Stephenson Way and was within the immediate locality of the flood incident; restricted flow and blocked culverts caused elevated upstream levels and flows that affected the area adjacent to Stephenson Way during the incident. (p14),,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13.html,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13,Corby flooding 13 Jun 2019,"Flood Investigation Report: Railway Adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby, 13 June 2019","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby.pdf"
GB-NNH,27,2019-06-13,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_eefbc0a7facd777627e34a7f2a2e1039"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood incident occurred in the north of the county (north of Corby), affecting land adjacent to the Midland Main Line within Northamptonshire. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Northamptonshire were affected by flooding, prompting a Section 19 investigation by the LLFA and the creation/maintenance of an asset register and records of flooding hotspots. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"The flood incident occurred in the north of the county (north of Corby), affecting land adjacent to the Midland Main Line within Northamptonshire. [PAG] (p10)
Properties in Northamptonshire were affected by flooding, prompting a Section 19 investigation by the LLFA and the creation/maintenance of an asset register and records of flooding hotspots. (p23)",,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13.html,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13,Corby flooding 13 Jun 2019,"Flood Investigation Report: Railway Adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby, 13 June 2019","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby.pdf"
GB-NNH,27,2019-06-13,52.487736,-0.7032713,"<div id=""html_b931f35c3a4c9cbbd352922e0c15e8d0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Corby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Relatively heavy rainfall and poorly maintained watercourses/control structures caused an unmanaged overflow; excess surface water flowed to localised low points in Corby, including flooding the railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane. Critical infrastructure (the railway) was affected for more than three hours. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane in Corby, constituting an impact on critical infrastructure within the town. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, concentrated rainfall over 10&ndash;13 June 2019 led to surface&ndash;water/flash flooding in Corby on 13 June 2019. Local river levels in the wider Nene/Witham area had been notably/exceptionally high in the week prior (5&ndash;11 June), preceding the event. The Corby weather station recorded a marked concentration of the week&#8217;s rainfall in the days immediately before and including 13 June, coincident with the flood event. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident occurred in the north of Corby, affecting land on and adjacent to the Midland Main Line railway. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood incident occurred in the immediate locality within Corby where the tributary/main river flows; blocked culverts and poor maintenance caused raised upstream water levels, spillway flows and filling of balancing ponds, contributing to localised flooding. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Corby experienced surface water/urban flooding during heavy rainfall when surface water sewers outfell to the tributary and balancing ponds. The flow control culvert/weir and balancing ponds were overwhelmed/compromised by restricted flows and poor maintenance, producing pond overflow and downstream flood routing toward Pen Green Lane and the railway cutting. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localized flooding on 13 June 2019 driven by overtopping of Pond 2 and subsequent overland flow across Pen Green Lane and down a railway cutting, causing a landslip onto the railway and standing water that affected safe operation of trains. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area around the railway cutting south of Corby Tunnel experienced runoff, standing water on tracks and a landslip onto the railway. Railway operations were disrupted, emergency response was required on site, and Network Rail carried out clearance works. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Corby,England,"Relatively heavy rainfall and poorly maintained watercourses/control structures caused an unmanaged overflow; excess surface water flowed to localised low points in Corby, including flooding the railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane. Critical infrastructure (the railway) was affected for more than three hours. (p5)
Flooding affected the railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane in Corby, constituting an impact on critical infrastructure within the town. (p7)
Intense, concentrated rainfall over 10–13 June 2019 led to surface–water/flash flooding in Corby on 13 June 2019. Local river levels in the wider Nene/Witham area had been notably/exceptionally high in the week prior (5–11 June), preceding the event. The Corby weather station recorded a marked concentration of the week’s rainfall in the days immediately before and including 13 June, coincident with the flood event. (p9)
A flood incident occurred in the north of Corby, affecting land on and adjacent to the Midland Main Line railway. (p10)
The flood incident occurred in the immediate locality within Corby where the tributary/main river flows; blocked culverts and poor maintenance caused raised upstream water levels, spillway flows and filling of balancing ponds, contributing to localised flooding. (p14)
Parts of Corby experienced surface water/urban flooding during heavy rainfall when surface water sewers outfell to the tributary and balancing ponds. The flow control culvert/weir and balancing ponds were overwhelmed/compromised by restricted flows and poor maintenance, producing pond overflow and downstream flood routing toward Pen Green Lane and the railway cutting. (p15)
Localized flooding on 13 June 2019 driven by overtopping of Pond 2 and subsequent overland flow across Pen Green Lane and down a railway cutting, causing a landslip onto the railway and standing water that affected safe operation of trains. (p17)
The area around the railway cutting south of Corby Tunnel experienced runoff, standing water on tracks and a landslip onto the railway. Railway operations were disrupted, emergency response was required on site, and Network Rail carried out clearance works. (p18)",,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13.html,GB-NNH_27_2019-06-13,Corby flooding 13 Jun 2019,"Flood Investigation Report: Railway Adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby, 13 June 2019","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Railway adjacent to Pen Green Lane, Corby.pdf"
GB-WNH,1,2012-12-20,52.56628789856925,-0.2027211766370756,"<div id=""html_fb38707266cd7be959ae10f8180cfef8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Nene</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ordinary watercourse immediately north of the rear gardens is described as the drainage receptor for highway runoff, land drainage and private roof/paved area drainage for the area that experienced flooding; it is identified in the incident as the local watercourse in the drainage system associated with the flooded properties. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,River Nene,England,"The ordinary watercourse immediately north of the rear gardens is described as the drainage receptor for highway runoff, land drainage and private roof/paved area drainage for the area that experienced flooding; it is identified in the incident as the local watercourse in the drainage system associated with the flooded properties. (p8)",2 properties (p8),GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20.html,GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20,Cogenhoe flooding 20 Dec 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Whiston Road, Cogenhoe, 20th December 2012",090/West Northamptonshire/15590-FIR-Cogenhoe-REV04-19012013.pdf
GB-WNH,1,2012-12-20,52.2266427,-0.7697681,"<div id=""html_f6f999f59d70c27e1292fdd795c6d222"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Whiston Road, Denton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basements and outbuildings of two semi&ndash;detached residential dwellings flooded; basements were pumped out by the property owners. [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on this section were identified as being at risk of flooding (land drainage overspill); drainage and sewage arrangements (septic tanks) on these properties were recommended for verification/replacement where inappropriate. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two semi&ndash;detached properties; three additional occasions in 2012 [MUL] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,"Whiston Road, Denton",England,"Basements and outbuildings of two semi–detached residential dwellings flooded; basements were pumped out by the property owners. [MUL] (p8)
Properties on this section were identified as being at risk of flooding (land drainage overspill); drainage and sewage arrangements (septic tanks) on these properties were recommended for verification/replacement where inappropriate. (p17)",two semi–detached properties; three additional occasions in 2012 [MUL] (p8),GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20.html,GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20,Cogenhoe flooding 20 Dec 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Whiston Road, Cogenhoe, 20th December 2012",090/West Northamptonshire/15590-FIR-Cogenhoe-REV04-19012013.pdf
GB-WNH,1,2012-12-20,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_89f6c831eac830f6b57ab9443d8a63b2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement/internal flooding affected properties on Whiston Road; basements of properties on this street were inundated during the incident. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties; 3 additional incidents in 2012 [MUL] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,Basement/internal flooding affected properties on Whiston Road; basements of properties on this street were inundated during the incident. [MUL] (p7),2 properties; 3 additional incidents in 2012 [MUL] (p7),GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20.html,GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20,Cogenhoe flooding 20 Dec 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Whiston Road, Cogenhoe, 20th December 2012",090/West Northamptonshire/15590-FIR-Cogenhoe-REV04-19012013.pdf
GB-WNH,1,2012-12-20,52.22142964466552,-0.8005541592137464,"<div id=""html_31dca9a7f9cc540e0cd1cefa7ca349d7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brafield On the Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two semi&ndash;detached residential properties experienced internal flooding to basements; outbuildings also flooded. Owners pumped out the basements. [MUL] (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [MUL] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,Brafield On the Green,England,Two semi–detached residential properties experienced internal flooding to basements; outbuildings also flooded. Owners pumped out the basements. [MUL] (p8),2 properties [MUL] (p8),GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20.html,GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20,Cogenhoe flooding 20 Dec 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Whiston Road, Cogenhoe, 20th December 2012",090/West Northamptonshire/15590-FIR-Cogenhoe-REV04-19012013.pdf
GB-WNH,1,2012-12-20,52.2370865,-0.7875148,"<div id=""html_0a28b3aeeea9d65ce028eba73cde40de"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cogenhoe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the east section of the village were at risk of flooding from overspill of land drainage linked to historic drainage layout. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from neighbouring land and the emergence of groundwater create flood risk to properties; the village is referenced as the area where the public sewerage system and other interventions are needed to prevent internal and external flooding. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,Cogenhoe,England,"Properties in the east section of the village were at risk of flooding from overspill of land drainage linked to historic drainage layout. (p17)
Surface water runoff from neighbouring land and the emergence of groundwater create flood risk to properties; the village is referenced as the area where the public sewerage system and other interventions are needed to prevent internal and external flooding. (p19)",,GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20.html,GB-WNH_1_2012-12-20,Cogenhoe flooding 20 Dec 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Whiston Road, Cogenhoe, 20th December 2012",090/West Northamptonshire/15590-FIR-Cogenhoe-REV04-19012013.pdf
GB-DER,3,2012-07-01,52.93483,-1.505179,"<div id=""html_c2b44839e77d8eed492d503a96857d98"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Markeaton Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map material and the report describe surface water and watercourse flows being channelled toward Markeaton Park and Markeaton Lake, with flood&ndash;relief culvert inlets at the park's northern boundary. The July 2012 event resulted in Markeaton Lane being impassable to the north of Markeaton village and reduced visitor numbers to a commercial site (attributed to the access restriction). The park is therefore a downstream receptor of the runoff and was indirectly affected via access disruption and reduced visitation; the text does not clearly document internal inundation of park buildings. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Markeaton and Mackworth brooks interact via weirs and high&ndash;level overflows at the west side of Markeaton Park, creating hydraulic interactions that influenced flow paths during heavy rainfall and contributed to the flood behaviour observed in July 2012. (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Markeaton Park,England,"Map material and the report describe surface water and watercourse flows being channelled toward Markeaton Park and Markeaton Lake, with flood–relief culvert inlets at the park's northern boundary. The July 2012 event resulted in Markeaton Lane being impassable to the north of Markeaton village and reduced visitor numbers to a commercial site (attributed to the access restriction). The park is therefore a downstream receptor of the runoff and was indirectly affected via access disruption and reduced visitation; the text does not clearly document internal inundation of park buildings. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
The Markeaton and Mackworth brooks interact via weirs and high–level overflows at the west side of Markeaton Park, creating hydraulic interactions that influenced flow paths during heavy rainfall and contributed to the flood behaviour observed in July 2012. (p9)",,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01.html,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01,Markeaton flooding July 2012,Section 19 Report: Markeaton,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Markeaton-Brook-Section-19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,3,2012-07-01,52.9375596,-1.5235724,"<div id=""html_f3ebe18374cc9106a5822c93bd608338"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mackworth Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels within the watercourses (specifically the ordinary watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton) were identified as contributing to a raised groundwater table and reduced infiltration, limiting the surface water system's ability to discharge and thereby contributing to the flooding experienced in Markeaton during the July 2012 event. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Previous flood&ndash;risk work specifically modelled flooding mechanisms along Mackworth Brook as part of assessing the July 2012 event. The brook is identified as a watercourse flowing from the northwest toward downstream receptors and was therefore implicated in the movement of water toward the Markeaton study area; the report does not explicitly confirm overtopping or a direct record of the brook causing the internal flooding of properties but treats it as a potential contributor to the July 2012 flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mackworth Brook is described as expected to flood following heavy rainfall, but modelling showed predicted river flooding in Mackworth Brook did not match the full extents observed in July 2012; it therefore contributed to flood conditions but did not alone explain the July 2012 extents. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following heavy rainfall Mackworth Brook experienced elevated river flows; hydraulic modelling shows river flooding is expected along Mackworth Brook but these river flows alone could not reproduce the full flood extents observed in July 2012 nor would they have resulted in internal property flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels within Mackworth Brook contributed to overspill during the July 2012 event; this overspill prevented the drainage system discharging to the watercourses and thereby contributed to surface water ponding and road impassability in the locality. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Mackworth Brook were hydraulically linked to the water table; elevated water levels reduced ground infiltration and would have contributed to groundwater and surface water flooding in the area during the July 2012 event. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mackworth Brook contributed to the July 2012 flooding by providing high flows and by limiting the ability of surface water drainage to discharge; hydraulic connectivity between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton aggravated flooding and modelled high flows in the brook matched observed flood extents. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mackworth Brook (and the ditch that discharges to it) is the receiving watercourse for local drainage; the outfall and capacity constraints at the ditch/brook interface limited the drainage system's effectiveness and contributed to surface water flooding impacts in Markeaton during the July 2012 event. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mackworth Brook (referred to in the report) contributed to the flooding dynamics in July 2012: culverted ditches discharge into the brook and high catchment flows meant local outfalls and channels were overwhelmed. The report also records concerns about vegetation management and channel maintenance that could affect flows. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Designated Main River downstream of Markeaton that contributed to the July 2012 flooding due to the catchment's rapid response. The report identifies the watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton as needing inspection to identify restrictions that prevented surface water discharging freely. [PAG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Mackworth Brook,England,"High water levels within the watercourses (specifically the ordinary watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton) were identified as contributing to a raised groundwater table and reduced infiltration, limiting the surface water system's ability to discharge and thereby contributing to the flooding experienced in Markeaton during the July 2012 event. (p5)
Previous flood–risk work specifically modelled flooding mechanisms along Mackworth Brook as part of assessing the July 2012 event. The brook is identified as a watercourse flowing from the northwest toward downstream receptors and was therefore implicated in the movement of water toward the Markeaton study area; the report does not explicitly confirm overtopping or a direct record of the brook causing the internal flooding of properties but treats it as a potential contributor to the July 2012 flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
Mackworth Brook is described as expected to flood following heavy rainfall, but modelling showed predicted river flooding in Mackworth Brook did not match the full extents observed in July 2012; it therefore contributed to flood conditions but did not alone explain the July 2012 extents. [PAG] (p9)
Following heavy rainfall Mackworth Brook experienced elevated river flows; hydraulic modelling shows river flooding is expected along Mackworth Brook but these river flows alone could not reproduce the full flood extents observed in July 2012 nor would they have resulted in internal property flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
High water levels within Mackworth Brook contributed to overspill during the July 2012 event; this overspill prevented the drainage system discharging to the watercourses and thereby contributed to surface water ponding and road impassability in the locality. (p11)
High water levels in Mackworth Brook were hydraulically linked to the water table; elevated water levels reduced ground infiltration and would have contributed to groundwater and surface water flooding in the area during the July 2012 event. (p12)
Mackworth Brook contributed to the July 2012 flooding by providing high flows and by limiting the ability of surface water drainage to discharge; hydraulic connectivity between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton aggravated flooding and modelled high flows in the brook matched observed flood extents. (p14)
Mackworth Brook (and the ditch that discharges to it) is the receiving watercourse for local drainage; the outfall and capacity constraints at the ditch/brook interface limited the drainage system's effectiveness and contributed to surface water flooding impacts in Markeaton during the July 2012 event. (p15)
Mackworth Brook (referred to in the report) contributed to the flooding dynamics in July 2012: culverted ditches discharge into the brook and high catchment flows meant local outfalls and channels were overwhelmed. The report also records concerns about vegetation management and channel maintenance that could affect flows. [PAG] (p16)
Designated Main River downstream of Markeaton that contributed to the July 2012 flooding due to the catchment's rapid response. The report identifies the watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton as needing inspection to identify restrictions that prevented surface water discharging freely. [PAG] (p17)",,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01.html,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01,Markeaton flooding July 2012,Section 19 Report: Markeaton,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Markeaton-Brook-Section-19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,3,2012-07-01,52.9350479,-1.498343,"<div id=""html_6b25b9f3b9db5f853b40a1c2c8752f93"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Markeaton Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flows during July 2012 were modelled (including &gt;1 in 100 annual probability flows and culvert blockages) and while river flooding was expected along Markeaton Brook, the modelling showed river flooding alone would not reproduce the observed July 2012 flood extents nor produce internal property flooding; thus Markeaton Brook contributed but was not the sole cause of the July 2012 flooding. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Markeaton Brook experienced elevated river flows during heavy rainfall; modelling indicated river flooding along Markeaton Brook is expected but that river flooding alone could not recreate the flood extents observed in July 2012 or cause internal property flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Markeaton Brook were hydraulically linked to a raised water table which reduced infiltration and contributed to groundwater emergence and exacerbated surface water flooding in the catchment during the July 2012 event. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Brook (and connected ordinary watercourses) contributed to flooding in Markeaton during the July 2012 extreme event. Channel capacity and the outfall constraints to the ditch between the village and Mackworth Brook limited drainage; vegetation and debris management issues were noted that could exacerbate blockages and worsen flows. [PAG,IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Designated Main River downstream of Markeaton identified as part of the network of watercourses that contributed to the July 2012 flooding; the report calls for inspection and maintenance to allow surface water to discharge into the watercourse. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Markeaton Brook,England,"River flows during July 2012 were modelled (including >1 in 100 annual probability flows and culvert blockages) and while river flooding was expected along Markeaton Brook, the modelling showed river flooding alone would not reproduce the observed July 2012 flood extents nor produce internal property flooding; thus Markeaton Brook contributed but was not the sole cause of the July 2012 flooding. [PAG] (p9)
Markeaton Brook experienced elevated river flows during heavy rainfall; modelling indicated river flooding along Markeaton Brook is expected but that river flooding alone could not recreate the flood extents observed in July 2012 or cause internal property flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
High water levels in Markeaton Brook were hydraulically linked to a raised water table which reduced infiltration and contributed to groundwater emergence and exacerbated surface water flooding in the catchment during the July 2012 event. (p12)
The Brook (and connected ordinary watercourses) contributed to flooding in Markeaton during the July 2012 extreme event. Channel capacity and the outfall constraints to the ditch between the village and Mackworth Brook limited drainage; vegetation and debris management issues were noted that could exacerbate blockages and worsen flows. [PAG,IMG] (p16)
Designated Main River downstream of Markeaton identified as part of the network of watercourses that contributed to the July 2012 flooding; the report calls for inspection and maintenance to allow surface water to discharge into the watercourse. (p17)",,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01.html,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01,Markeaton flooding July 2012,Section 19 Report: Markeaton,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Markeaton-Brook-Section-19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,3,2012-07-01,52.9212617,-1.4761491,"<div id=""html_3825597593649d2e9c13457564bbcd60"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple historical flood incidents within the city boundaries: a major river flood in 1931/32 (which prompted construction of the Northern Relief culvert); a Markeaton Brook&ndash;related flood in November 2000 that flooded a residential property; flooding of Markeaton Lane in January 2008; residents recalled additional local events in 2001 and 2007; and the July 2012 event when intense runoff and high water levels caused surface&ndash;water ponding on Markeaton Lane, making the road impassable. The area flooded again approximately four to five weeks after July 2012. [MUL] (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property [MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Derby,England,"Multiple historical flood incidents within the city boundaries: a major river flood in 1931/32 (which prompted construction of the Northern Relief culvert); a Markeaton Brook–related flood in November 2000 that flooded a residential property; flooding of Markeaton Lane in January 2008; residents recalled additional local events in 2001 and 2007; and the July 2012 event when intense runoff and high water levels caused surface–water ponding on Markeaton Lane, making the road impassable. The area flooded again approximately four to five weeks after July 2012. [MUL] (p11)",1 residential property [MUL] (p11),GB-DER_3_2012-07-01.html,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01,Markeaton flooding July 2012,Section 19 Report: Markeaton,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Markeaton-Brook-Section-19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,3,2012-07-01,52.9385806,-1.5073104,"<div id=""html_d863878766407d3e18f0936dcb6c5e60"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Markeaton Lane, Markeaton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water drainage along Markeaton Lane had limited capacity during the July 2012 event; the drainage arrangement is identified as a contributor to flooding and a potential target for network changes (e.g. allowing flows into the ditch upstream/west of Markeaton) and maintenance (gully checks). (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Markeaton Lane highways were substantially flooded during July 2012, rendering the road impassable to the north of Markeaton village and causing access restrictions that reduced visitor numbers to a nearby commercial site; local properties adjacent to the road were flooded. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Markeaton Lane highways were substantially flooded during the July 2012 event, becoming impassable north of Markeaton village. Flooding led to internal inundation of local properties and restricted access to a commercial site, reducing visitor numbers. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overland flow and drainage system involvement during the July 2012 event &mdash; surface water runoff routes across the lane and into the local ditch network were identified as a significant contributor to the observed July 2012 flood extents. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Markeaton Lane experienced flooding attributable to surface water runoff. A previous assessment concluded that river flooding alone could not reproduce the July 2012 flood extents and identified overland surface water flow paths consistent with the observed July 2012 extents; highway drainage on Markeaton Lane discharges to a ditch which is constrained and can be overwhelmed, contributing to flooding. DCC records also show Markeaton Lane flooded in January 2008. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from surrounding wet fields ponded on the carriageway, making the road impassable to vehicles during the July 2012 event. Floodwaters largely receded after a relatively short period (residents reported most water receding after a few (2&ndash;4) hours). Markeaton Lane was also reported flooded in January 2008. [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Limited surface water drainage west of Markeaton caused runoff to flow along Markeaton Lane during the July 2012 event. A parallel ditch culverted under Markeaton Road could not accept eastward&ndash;flowing water (owing to a stone wall), so flows were directed towards Markeaton, contributing to flooding along the road during the storm on 6 July 2012. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts under Markeaton Lane were blocked during the July 2012 flood, causing flooding at the lane; culverts were subsequently de&ndash;silted as a response to the July 2012 event to prevent later flooding. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff was managed on Markeaton Lane; the drainage system and a culverted ditch under Markeaton Lane were identified as key conveyance features and potential locations for water to be routed. The drainage operation and capacity constrained flows toward Markeaton during the July 2012 extreme event. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and drainage capacity on Markeaton Lane were examined after the July 2012 event. DCC (highways authority) maintained the drainage to its design capacity; options to reduce flow easterly into a culverted ditch under Markeaton Lane were identified as a potential low&ndash;cost mitigation, and improvements to the local drainage outfall were considered but judged unlikely to materially reduce flood severity in extreme events. The assessment concludes the July 2012 flooding was driven by an extremely wet catchment and that even a redesigned surface water system may not have significantly reduced the observed severity. [PAG,IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water drainage along Markeaton Lane was implicated in the July 2012 flooding. The report recommends checking gullies, ensuring drainage connections remain clear, and considering changes to the drainage network along Markeaton Lane to allow water to pass into a ditch upstream and to the west of Markeaton to reduce risk. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; 2 non&ndash;residential properties; 1 commercial site (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; 2 non&ndash;residential properties; 1 commercial site (impacted by access restrictions) [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 recorded flooding incident (January 2008) [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Markeaton Lane, Markeaton",England,"Surface water drainage along Markeaton Lane had limited capacity during the July 2012 event; the drainage arrangement is identified as a contributor to flooding and a potential target for network changes (e.g. allowing flows into the ditch upstream/west of Markeaton) and maintenance (gully checks). (p5)
Markeaton Lane highways were substantially flooded during July 2012, rendering the road impassable to the north of Markeaton village and causing access restrictions that reduced visitor numbers to a nearby commercial site; local properties adjacent to the road were flooded. (p7)
Markeaton Lane highways were substantially flooded during the July 2012 event, becoming impassable north of Markeaton village. Flooding led to internal inundation of local properties and restricted access to a commercial site, reducing visitor numbers. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
Surface water overland flow and drainage system involvement during the July 2012 event — surface water runoff routes across the lane and into the local ditch network were identified as a significant contributor to the observed July 2012 flood extents. (p9)
Markeaton Lane experienced flooding attributable to surface water runoff. A previous assessment concluded that river flooding alone could not reproduce the July 2012 flood extents and identified overland surface water flow paths consistent with the observed July 2012 extents; highway drainage on Markeaton Lane discharges to a ditch which is constrained and can be overwhelmed, contributing to flooding. DCC records also show Markeaton Lane flooded in January 2008. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p10)
Surface water runoff from surrounding wet fields ponded on the carriageway, making the road impassable to vehicles during the July 2012 event. Floodwaters largely receded after a relatively short period (residents reported most water receding after a few (2–4) hours). Markeaton Lane was also reported flooded in January 2008. [PAG,MUL] (p11)
Limited surface water drainage west of Markeaton caused runoff to flow along Markeaton Lane during the July 2012 event. A parallel ditch culverted under Markeaton Road could not accept eastward–flowing water (owing to a stone wall), so flows were directed towards Markeaton, contributing to flooding along the road during the storm on 6 July 2012. (p12)
Culverts under Markeaton Lane were blocked during the July 2012 flood, causing flooding at the lane; culverts were subsequently de–silted as a response to the July 2012 event to prevent later flooding. (p14)
Surface water runoff was managed on Markeaton Lane; the drainage system and a culverted ditch under Markeaton Lane were identified as key conveyance features and potential locations for water to be routed. The drainage operation and capacity constrained flows toward Markeaton during the July 2012 extreme event. (p15)
Surface water runoff and drainage capacity on Markeaton Lane were examined after the July 2012 event. DCC (highways authority) maintained the drainage to its design capacity; options to reduce flow easterly into a culverted ditch under Markeaton Lane were identified as a potential low–cost mitigation, and improvements to the local drainage outfall were considered but judged unlikely to materially reduce flood severity in extreme events. The assessment concludes the July 2012 flooding was driven by an extremely wet catchment and that even a redesigned surface water system may not have significantly reduced the observed severity. [PAG,IMG] (p16)
Surface water drainage along Markeaton Lane was implicated in the July 2012 flooding. The report recommends checking gullies, ensuring drainage connections remain clear, and considering changes to the drainage network along Markeaton Lane to allow water to pass into a ditch upstream and to the west of Markeaton to reduce risk. (p17)","1 residential property; 2 non–residential properties; 1 commercial site (p7)
1 residential property; 2 non–residential properties; 1 commercial site (impacted by access restrictions) [PAG,IMG] (p8)
1 recorded flooding incident (January 2008) [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p10)",GB-DER_3_2012-07-01.html,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01,Markeaton flooding July 2012,Section 19 Report: Markeaton,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Markeaton-Brook-Section-19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,3,2012-07-01,52.9359745,-1.5087161,"<div id=""html_4a53ad507e7c10d3a1da5957f27d56ab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Markeaton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2012 event commercial and residential properties in Markeaton suffered flooding. Flooding was driven by numerous rainfall events producing saturated ground and high runoff, coupled with high water levels in the ordinary watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton and a high groundwater table that limited the surface water system's ability to discharge, overwhelming local drainage capacity. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village of Markeaton experienced substantial flooding in July 2012 that internally flooded local properties (one residential and two non&ndash;residential) and caused access restrictions to the north of the village that reduced visitor numbers to a commercial site. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced substantial flooding in July 2012: Markeaton Lane highways and local properties were flooded. One residential property and two non&ndash;residential properties suffered internal flooding. An additional commercial site was impacted by access restrictions due to the highway being impassable to the north of the village, which reduced visitor numbers. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding in July 2012; the previous study concluded that surface water runoff (overland flow) was a significant contributing factor to the observed July 2012 flood extents, and river flooding from local brooks alone could not recreate the extent of inundation seen in the event. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The July 2012 flood extents in Markeaton are attributed largely to surface water runoff (overland flow following topography) rather than river flooding alone; surface water runoff was identified as a significant contributing factor to the observed flooding extents in July 2012. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents reported multiple flooding incidents affecting the Markeaton area, including flooding during July 2012 (when surface water runoff and overspill from nearby watercourses caused ponding and impassable carriageways). Residents reported the majority of floodwater receded after a few hours. A second flood occurred about four to five weeks after the July 2012 event. Other dated incidents recalled by residents include 2001 and 2007. [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Limited surface water drainage serving Markeaton Lane to the west of Markeaton caused water to flow along the road during the July 2012 event, directing runoff towards Markeaton. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Markeaton area experienced flooding in July 2012 caused by a combination of sources (extremely wet antecedent conditions followed by an approximately 1 in 15 annual probability rainfall event) with hydraulic connectivity between watercourses and groundwater and inadequate discharge from surface water drainage contributing to observed flood extents. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced surface water flooding during the July 2012 extreme event. Limitations in the local surface water drainage (notably the outfall to the ditch between the village and Mackworth Brook) constrained conveyance and contributed to the observed flooding; improvements to drainage were considered unlikely to materially reduce impacts for an event of this magnitude. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Markeaton experienced significant flooding in July 2012 driven by an extremely wet catchment. Surface water runoff overwhelmed the local drainage/outfall arrangements (including limitations of the outfall between the village and Mackworth Brook), causing flooding of the settlement. Concerns about watercourse vegetation and maintenance were raised during the site visit. [PAG,IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>July 2012 flooding affected the village. Flooding arose from a highly responsive catchment and overflowing local watercourses (Markeaton Brook and Mackworth Brook); surface water drainage connections in Markeaton (connecting to the watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton) contributed to the event. Report recommends inspection and maintenance of gullies and the drainage connection and possible changes to drainage routes to reduce future risk. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; 2 non&ndash;residential properties; 1 commercial site (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; 2 non&ndash;residential properties; 1 additional commercial site [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 flood incidents (July 2012 and a second flood ~4&ndash;5 weeks later) [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Markeaton,England,"During the July 2012 event commercial and residential properties in Markeaton suffered flooding. Flooding was driven by numerous rainfall events producing saturated ground and high runoff, coupled with high water levels in the ordinary watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton and a high groundwater table that limited the surface water system's ability to discharge, overwhelming local drainage capacity. (p5)
The village of Markeaton experienced substantial flooding in July 2012 that internally flooded local properties (one residential and two non–residential) and caused access restrictions to the north of the village that reduced visitor numbers to a commercial site. (p7)
The village experienced substantial flooding in July 2012: Markeaton Lane highways and local properties were flooded. One residential property and two non–residential properties suffered internal flooding. An additional commercial site was impacted by access restrictions due to the highway being impassable to the north of the village, which reduced visitor numbers. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
The village experienced flooding in July 2012; the previous study concluded that surface water runoff (overland flow) was a significant contributing factor to the observed July 2012 flood extents, and river flooding from local brooks alone could not recreate the extent of inundation seen in the event. [PAG] (p9)
The July 2012 flood extents in Markeaton are attributed largely to surface water runoff (overland flow following topography) rather than river flooding alone; surface water runoff was identified as a significant contributing factor to the observed flooding extents in July 2012. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
Residents reported multiple flooding incidents affecting the Markeaton area, including flooding during July 2012 (when surface water runoff and overspill from nearby watercourses caused ponding and impassable carriageways). Residents reported the majority of floodwater receded after a few hours. A second flood occurred about four to five weeks after the July 2012 event. Other dated incidents recalled by residents include 2001 and 2007. [PAG,MUL] (p11)
Limited surface water drainage serving Markeaton Lane to the west of Markeaton caused water to flow along the road during the July 2012 event, directing runoff towards Markeaton. (p12)
The Markeaton area experienced flooding in July 2012 caused by a combination of sources (extremely wet antecedent conditions followed by an approximately 1 in 15 annual probability rainfall event) with hydraulic connectivity between watercourses and groundwater and inadequate discharge from surface water drainage contributing to observed flood extents. (p14)
The village experienced surface water flooding during the July 2012 extreme event. Limitations in the local surface water drainage (notably the outfall to the ditch between the village and Mackworth Brook) constrained conveyance and contributed to the observed flooding; improvements to drainage were considered unlikely to materially reduce impacts for an event of this magnitude. (p15)
Markeaton experienced significant flooding in July 2012 driven by an extremely wet catchment. Surface water runoff overwhelmed the local drainage/outfall arrangements (including limitations of the outfall between the village and Mackworth Brook), causing flooding of the settlement. Concerns about watercourse vegetation and maintenance were raised during the site visit. [PAG,IMG] (p16)
July 2012 flooding affected the village. Flooding arose from a highly responsive catchment and overflowing local watercourses (Markeaton Brook and Mackworth Brook); surface water drainage connections in Markeaton (connecting to the watercourse between Mackworth Brook and Markeaton) contributed to the event. Report recommends inspection and maintenance of gullies and the drainage connection and possible changes to drainage routes to reduce future risk. [PAG] (p17)","1 residential property; 2 non–residential properties; 1 commercial site (p7)
1 residential property; 2 non–residential properties; 1 additional commercial site [PAG,IMG] (p8)
2 flood incidents (July 2012 and a second flood ~4–5 weeks later) [PAG,MUL] (p11)",GB-DER_3_2012-07-01.html,GB-DER_3_2012-07-01,Markeaton flooding July 2012,Section 19 Report: Markeaton,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Markeaton-Brook-Section-19-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,14,2020-12-23,52.3979263,-0.1131182,"<div id=""html_abe05fe69fedcb55a9540c9c510f8dae"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bury Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bury Brook is the main river flowing through Broughton and is identified as the source of fluvial flooding in the village; high water levels in the brook exacerbate flooding when contributory watercourses and highway gullies cannot discharge, producing Flood Zone 3 along its immediate banks that affects adjacent properties and farmland. [PAG,IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid runoff from intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020 led to a rapid rise in river levels and flooding from the Bury Brook, contributing to flooding in the village. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels in Bury Brook rose rapidly and exceeded channel capacity, contributing to river and surface water flooding of adjacent roads and properties and causing the highway drainage system to back up. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflow/river&ndash;related flooding contributing to internal property flooding and local drainage issues in Broughton; prompted inspections and planned maintenance (including review of vegetation clearance, dredging feasibility and maintenance schedule). [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bury Brook was inspected visually following the December 2020 flooding; partners planned a review of maintenance along the brook including investigation of dredging and vegetation clearance, and to reinstate damaged/blocked grips between the highway and the brook. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bury Brook was identified as the watercourse for which flood alleviation measures are being considered following the 23 December 2020 flooding; it contributed to flooding in Broughton and is subject to planned mitigation actions. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Bury Brook,England,"Bury Brook is the main river flowing through Broughton and is identified as the source of fluvial flooding in the village; high water levels in the brook exacerbate flooding when contributory watercourses and highway gullies cannot discharge, producing Flood Zone 3 along its immediate banks that affects adjacent properties and farmland. [PAG,IMG] (p5)
Rapid runoff from intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020 led to a rapid rise in river levels and flooding from the Bury Brook, contributing to flooding in the village. (p7)
Water levels in Bury Brook rose rapidly and exceeded channel capacity, contributing to river and surface water flooding of adjacent roads and properties and causing the highway drainage system to back up. (p11)
Overflow/river–related flooding contributing to internal property flooding and local drainage issues in Broughton; prompted inspections and planned maintenance (including review of vegetation clearance, dredging feasibility and maintenance schedule). [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Bury Brook was inspected visually following the December 2020 flooding; partners planned a review of maintenance along the brook including investigation of dredging and vegetation clearance, and to reinstate damaged/blocked grips between the highway and the brook. (p14)
Bury Brook was identified as the watercourse for which flood alleviation measures are being considered following the 23 December 2020 flooding; it contributed to flooding in Broughton and is subject to planned mitigation actions. (p23)",,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23,Broughton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Broughton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Broughton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,14,2020-12-23,52.3798415,-0.1205096,"<div id=""html_0c212a9a039835992334df3d078b65a8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridge Road, Broughton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A combination of river flooding from the adjacent Bury Brook and surface water runoff occurred. Saturated catchment caused rapid runoff into the brook, highway gullies backed up, surface water travelled down Bridge Road into the village centre and pooled. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and rising levels in Bury Brook caused highway drainage to back up; surface water travelled down Bridge Road into the village centre and pooled. Three residential properties, associated outbuildings and gardens were flooded; some residents were displaced and had to leave their homes for over one month. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies on Bridge Road were found blocked as a result of the flooding; cleansing of blocked gullies was ordered and a post&ndash;flooding asset inspection was undertaken. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked/covered gullies on Bridge Road contributed to drainage failure during the December 2020 flooding; the highway authority ordered cleansing of blocked gullies as part of the post&ndash;flood response and an inspection was scheduled. (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 residential properties; maximum flood depth c.600mm; displacement &gt;1 month [TAB] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Bridge Road, Broughton",England,"A combination of river flooding from the adjacent Bury Brook and surface water runoff occurred. Saturated catchment caused rapid runoff into the brook, highway gullies backed up, surface water travelled down Bridge Road into the village centre and pooled. [PAG] (p10)
Surface water runoff and rising levels in Bury Brook caused highway drainage to back up; surface water travelled down Bridge Road into the village centre and pooled. Three residential properties, associated outbuildings and gardens were flooded; some residents were displaced and had to leave their homes for over one month. [TAB] (p11)
Highway gullies on Bridge Road were found blocked as a result of the flooding; cleansing of blocked gullies was ordered and a post–flooding asset inspection was undertaken. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Blocked/covered gullies on Bridge Road contributed to drainage failure during the December 2020 flooding; the highway authority ordered cleansing of blocked gullies as part of the post–flood response and an inspection was scheduled. (p14)",3 residential properties; maximum flood depth c.600mm; displacement >1 month [TAB] (p11),GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23,Broughton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Broughton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Broughton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,14,2020-12-23,52.3848354,-0.1184062,"<div id=""html_b0dca5634ccad235808561eb46430c00"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Causeway Road, Broughton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of three key flood incident locations in the village on 23 December 2020; the bend and adjacent road area were part of the mapped flood incidents and would have experienced road and nearby property/garden flooding associated with the village&ndash;wide event. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies draining to Bury Brook and saturated ground resulted in rapid surface water runoff that travelled down these roads (School Road and Causeway Road) into the village centre, where water pooled and contributed to local flooding. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water run&ndash;off from saturated surrounding fields flowed onto Causeway Road and pooled at the bend. One residential property flooded internally to a maximum depth of approximately 300 mm, with additional outbuilding and garden flooding. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked highway gullies on Causeway Road and School Road associated with the flooding were identified; cleansing was ordered and damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook were flagged for reinstatement. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked/covered gullies on Causeway Road and School Road were identified during the post&ndash;flood response; the highway authority ordered cleansing of blocked gullies and a joint site inspection was scheduled to check for additional blocked/covered gullies. (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Causeway Road, Broughton",England,"Identified as one of three key flood incident locations in the village on 23 December 2020; the bend and adjacent road area were part of the mapped flood incidents and would have experienced road and nearby property/garden flooding associated with the village–wide event. [PAG] (p10)
Highway gullies draining to Bury Brook and saturated ground resulted in rapid surface water runoff that travelled down these roads (School Road and Causeway Road) into the village centre, where water pooled and contributed to local flooding. (p11)
Surface water run–off from saturated surrounding fields flowed onto Causeway Road and pooled at the bend. One residential property flooded internally to a maximum depth of approximately 300 mm, with additional outbuilding and garden flooding. [TAB] (p12)
Blocked highway gullies on Causeway Road and School Road associated with the flooding were identified; cleansing was ordered and damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook were flagged for reinstatement. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Blocked/covered gullies on Causeway Road and School Road were identified during the post–flood response; the highway authority ordered cleansing of blocked gullies and a joint site inspection was scheduled to check for additional blocked/covered gullies. (p14)",1 residential property [TAB] (p12),GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23,Broughton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Broughton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Broughton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,14,2020-12-23,52.3856525,-0.1239005,"<div id=""html_f684c6fe44f06ddbc333b819c8cddef3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>School Road, Broughton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies draining to Bury Brook and saturated ground resulted in rapid surface water runoff that travelled down these roads (School Road and Causeway Road) into the village centre, where water pooled and contributed to local flooding. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked highway gullies on Causeway Road and School Road associated with the flooding were identified; cleansing was ordered and damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook were flagged for reinstatement. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked/covered gullies on Causeway Road and School Road were identified during the post&ndash;flood response; the highway authority ordered cleansing of blocked gullies and a joint site inspection was scheduled to check for additional blocked/covered gullies. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"School Road, Broughton",England,"Highway gullies draining to Bury Brook and saturated ground resulted in rapid surface water runoff that travelled down these roads (School Road and Causeway Road) into the village centre, where water pooled and contributed to local flooding. (p11)
Blocked highway gullies on Causeway Road and School Road associated with the flooding were identified; cleansing was ordered and damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook were flagged for reinstatement. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Blocked/covered gullies on Causeway Road and School Road were identified during the post–flood response; the highway authority ordered cleansing of blocked gullies and a joint site inspection was scheduled to check for additional blocked/covered gullies. (p14)",,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23,Broughton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Broughton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Broughton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,14,2020-12-23,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_1fdc54369b964570e97ebfd52e9d08c2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid runoff on 23 December 2020 caused a rapid rise in Bury Brook and widespread surface water flooding in the village of Broughton. Impacts included internal flooding of homes and external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. At least four properties remained uninhabitable 10 months after the event. [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The highway authority (Cambridgeshire County Council) carried out post&ndash;flooding asset inspections, visual inspection of Bury Brook, arranged cleansing of blocked gullies and committed to look into reinstating damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook as part of the response to the December 2020 flooding. (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 flood reports; 16 properties; 4 properties [IMG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"Rapid runoff on 23 December 2020 caused a rapid rise in Bury Brook and widespread surface water flooding in the village of Broughton. Impacts included internal flooding of homes and external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. At least four properties remained uninhabitable 10 months after the event. [IMG] (p9)
The highway authority (Cambridgeshire County Council) carried out post–flooding asset inspections, visual inspection of Bury Brook, arranged cleansing of blocked gullies and committed to look into reinstating damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook as part of the response to the December 2020 flooding. (p14)",15 flood reports; 16 properties; 4 properties [IMG] (p9),GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23,Broughton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Broughton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Broughton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,14,2020-12-23,52.38040945,-0.11761225529328301,"<div id=""html_e13243c72a7102bcdc718d73b6bcf945"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Broughton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of the village (particularly along Bridge Road and Causeway Road and the village centre) have been flooded multiple times in recent years due to fluvial flooding from Bury Brook and from surface water runoff; flood zones on the map show properties and farmland adjacent to Bury Brook are at risk when water levels are high and drainage is unable to discharge to the brook. [PAG,IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding affected the village on 23 December 2020. Flooding originated from Bury Brook (fluvial) and from surface&ndash;water runoff, causing inundation of low&ndash;lying parts of the settlement. [PAG,IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 23rd December 2020 the village experienced surface&ndash;water flooding throughout the settlement and flooding caused by the Bury Brook following rapid runoff and rising river levels. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding across the village on 23 December 2020: river levels in the Bury Brook rose rapidly and exceeded channel capacity and surface water from intense rainfall led to widespread runoff and ponding. Properties flooded internally and external flooding affected gardens, outbuildings and roads; some properties remained uninhabitable months later. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from surrounding fields and exceedance of Bury Brook channel capacity caused surface water to travel down roads (Bridge Road, School Road, Causeway Road) into the village centre where water pooled, exacerbating flooding in the central area of the village. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Six residential properties were flooded internally to a maximum depth of approximately 900 mm. Additional outbuilding and garden flooding was noted. Some residents reported having to leave their houses for a period of over one month. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties and local highway drainage failure: six residential properties flooded internally to a depth of c.300mm; highway gullies on Bridge Road, Causeway Road and School Road were blocked, contributing to local surface water/road flooding; damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook were reported and to be reinstated. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Broughton experienced flooding on 23rd December 2020. The event prompted Environment Agency review and validation of flood warnings (leading to a lower river&ndash;level trigger and expansion of the flood warning area), and local flood&ndash;risk partners (LLFA, highways) to carry out post&ndash;flood inspections and community engagement. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Broughton were flooded during the December 2020 event. As a result, residents were affected by loss/damage from the flood and subsequent lack of eligibility for the previously activated Household Flood Resilience Grant (which was not activated for the December 2020 flooding). The event prompted a local flood investigation and follow&ndash;up actions by the LLFA and partners. [TAB,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected the settlement during the 23 December 2020 event, leading to identified RMA operational and riparian maintenance issues and prompting LLFA and Cambridgeshire Highways to plan maintenance and upgrades to the highway drainage network to alleviate the problem. [TAB] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 properties; 4 properties [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 residential properties [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 residential properties; c.300mm internal flood depth [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Broughton,England,"Areas of the village (particularly along Bridge Road and Causeway Road and the village centre) have been flooded multiple times in recent years due to fluvial flooding from Bury Brook and from surface water runoff; flood zones on the map show properties and farmland adjacent to Bury Brook are at risk when water levels are high and drainage is unable to discharge to the brook. [PAG,IMG] (p5)
Significant flooding affected the village on 23 December 2020. Flooding originated from Bury Brook (fluvial) and from surface–water runoff, causing inundation of low–lying parts of the settlement. [PAG,IMG] (p6)
On 23rd December 2020 the village experienced surface–water flooding throughout the settlement and flooding caused by the Bury Brook following rapid runoff and rising river levels. (p7)
Significant flooding across the village on 23 December 2020: river levels in the Bury Brook rose rapidly and exceeded channel capacity and surface water from intense rainfall led to widespread runoff and ponding. Properties flooded internally and external flooding affected gardens, outbuildings and roads; some properties remained uninhabitable months later. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Surface water runoff from surrounding fields and exceedance of Bury Brook channel capacity caused surface water to travel down roads (Bridge Road, School Road, Causeway Road) into the village centre where water pooled, exacerbating flooding in the central area of the village. (p11)
Six residential properties were flooded internally to a maximum depth of approximately 900 mm. Additional outbuilding and garden flooding was noted. Some residents reported having to leave their houses for a period of over one month. [TAB] (p12)
Internal flooding of residential properties and local highway drainage failure: six residential properties flooded internally to a depth of c.300mm; highway gullies on Bridge Road, Causeway Road and School Road were blocked, contributing to local surface water/road flooding; damaged/blocked grips between the highway and Bury Brook were reported and to be reinstated. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Broughton experienced flooding on 23rd December 2020. The event prompted Environment Agency review and validation of flood warnings (leading to a lower river–level trigger and expansion of the flood warning area), and local flood–risk partners (LLFA, highways) to carry out post–flood inspections and community engagement. [PAG] (p14)
Properties in Broughton were flooded during the December 2020 event. As a result, residents were affected by loss/damage from the flood and subsequent lack of eligibility for the previously activated Household Flood Resilience Grant (which was not activated for the December 2020 flooding). The event prompted a local flood investigation and follow–up actions by the LLFA and partners. [TAB,PAG] (p16)
Surface water flooding affected the settlement during the 23 December 2020 event, leading to identified RMA operational and riparian maintenance issues and prompting LLFA and Cambridgeshire Highways to plan maintenance and upgrades to the highway drainage network to alleviate the problem. [TAB] (p23)","16 properties; 4 properties [PAG,IMG] (p10)
6 residential properties [TAB] (p12)
6 residential properties; c.300mm internal flood depth [TAB,PAG] (p13)",GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_14_2020-12-23,Broughton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Broughton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Broughton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CON,5,2019-12-19,50.190674961195505,-5.410113144913755,"<div id=""html_aa05f02e1d8fd79def709bf1f5250985"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cornubia Public House</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater was present adjacent to the Cornubis Public House as part of the main road flooding in central Hayle. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,Cornubia Public House,England,Floodwater was present adjacent to the Cornubis Public House as part of the main road flooding in central Hayle. (p8),,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19.html,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19,Hayle flooding 19 Dec 2019,Flood Investigation Report for the Hayle event of 19 December 2019,027/Cornwall/027/2019/Cornwall/section-19-report-for-hayle.pdf
GB-CON,5,2019-12-19,50.1768597,-5.4152571,"<div id=""html_80ee9ea77e3b54b154bd898b8a21993c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>A30 at Hayle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding forced closure of a section of the A30, causing transport disruption between Camborne West and St Erth Roundabout; the road was later reopened. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A 7&ndash;mile stretch of the A30 was flooded and closed westbound due to muddy surface&ndash;water runoff from nearby agricultural fields (fields set to daffodil cultivation); flooding was muddy and required pumping/clearance; the A30 was later reopened around 2pm. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow exceedance from A30 areas fed additional runoff into the town&#8217;s drainage system, contributing to drainage overwhelm and urban flooding in Hayle. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Agricultural runoff increased the severity of flooding that impacted the A30, causing the road to be affected by floodwaters. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A30 trunk road was flooded on 19 December 2019 due to agricultural runoff from ploughed fields downhill, resulting in inundation of the carriageway. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7&ndash;mile stretch [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,A30 at Hayle,England,"Flooding forced closure of a section of the A30, causing transport disruption between Camborne West and St Erth Roundabout; the road was later reopened. [TAB] (p2)
A 7–mile stretch of the A30 was flooded and closed westbound due to muddy surface–water runoff from nearby agricultural fields (fields set to daffodil cultivation); flooding was muddy and required pumping/clearance; the A30 was later reopened around 2pm. (p5)
Flow exceedance from A30 areas fed additional runoff into the town’s drainage system, contributing to drainage overwhelm and urban flooding in Hayle. (p7)
Agricultural runoff increased the severity of flooding that impacted the A30, causing the road to be affected by floodwaters. (p10)
The A30 trunk road was flooded on 19 December 2019 due to agricultural runoff from ploughed fields downhill, resulting in inundation of the carriageway. (p12)",7–mile stretch [TAB] (p2),GB-CON_5_2019-12-19.html,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19,Hayle flooding 19 Dec 2019,Flood Investigation Report for the Hayle event of 19 December 2019,027/Cornwall/027/2019/Cornwall/section-19-report-for-hayle.pdf
GB-CON,5,2019-12-19,50.1770157,-5.4371697,"<div id=""html_2e98f7d9e5705b510c5f8722e300ac97"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Hayle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hayle rose rapidly due to intense rainfall and tide&ndash;locking, reaching its second highest recorded level since 1968 and contributing to town centre flooding in Hayle. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Hayle experienced elevated levels and high flows (influenced by high tide) on 19 December 2019, producing conditions associated with river/tidal flooding risk for the catchment and Hayle. (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,River Hayle,England,"The River Hayle rose rapidly due to intense rainfall and tide–locking, reaching its second highest recorded level since 1968 and contributing to town centre flooding in Hayle. (p5)
River Hayle experienced elevated levels and high flows (influenced by high tide) on 19 December 2019, producing conditions associated with river/tidal flooding risk for the catchment and Hayle. (p7)",,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19.html,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19,Hayle flooding 19 Dec 2019,Flood Investigation Report for the Hayle event of 19 December 2019,027/Cornwall/027/2019/Cornwall/section-19-report-for-hayle.pdf
GB-CON,5,2019-12-19,50.17405174024525,-5.440505067928404,"<div id=""html_6be801ac89d5d142e1b089b449b7f08d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Erth Roundabout</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named as the eastern extent of the A30 section closed due to flooding (part of the Camborne West&ndash;St Erth Roundabout closure). [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,St Erth Roundabout,England,Named as the eastern extent of the A30 section closed due to flooding (part of the Camborne West–St Erth Roundabout closure). [TAB] (p2),,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19.html,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19,Hayle flooding 19 Dec 2019,Flood Investigation Report for the Hayle event of 19 December 2019,027/Cornwall/027/2019/Cornwall/section-19-report-for-hayle.pdf
