GB-CON,5,2019-12-19,50.267276550000005,-5.259483173696909,"<div id=""html_a997555c737f097dea1f07629961b8b4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Portreath</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency crews were sent to clear screens and provide support as part of the flood response. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,Portreath,England,Environment Agency crews were sent to clear screens and provide support as part of the flood response. (p8),,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19.html,GB-CON_5_2019-12-19,Hayle flooding 19 Dec 2019,Flood Investigation Report for the Hayle event of 19 December 2019,027/Cornwall/027/2019/Cornwall/section-19-report-for-hayle.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.3270236,-0.610145,"<div id=""html_f41420df2468d845b11a75440d86d939"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road, Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall caused surface water drainage and watercourses to be unable to convey rainwater, producing excess surface water that flowed to low points around private property and resulted in internal flooding of properties on Station Road. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties occurred on Station Road. The report states internal flooding of more than one property has been experienced on more than one occasion in the five years prior to the investigation. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred near Station Road, close to the roundabout junction with the A6 Finedon Road. Several businesses on the north and south sides of Station Road, immediately west of the A6, were affected by the flood. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Station Road was flooded by flowing surface water (flow from the west) during the same overtopping events, impeding access and requiring placement of sandbags on the kerbline during responses. [TAB,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The carriageway between the businesses was flooded before the overland flow from the watercourse started; water overtopped the lowered kerbs on the south side of the carriageway and flowed off the highway towards private property; attempts were made to unblock road gullies. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Station Road when heavy rainfall and overwhelmed drainage/watercourses caused excess surface water to flow to low points around private properties. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems and watercourses to be unable to collect and convey rainwater, resulting in excess surface water flowing over ground into low points around private properties on Station Road. Sections of upstream watercourse that had not been recently maintained were identified as a significant contributory factor; limited records of underground drainage suggest historic/private systems may have exacerbated the incident. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the carriageway (overland flow) occurred on Station Road; lowered kerbs are noted at the location of carriageway flooding. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Standing floodwater covered the carriageway and part of the footway; responders placed a continuous line of sandbags across the road to control/divert water flow and protect the nearby traffic features, disrupting normal vehicle and pedestrian movement. [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater extended from the building edge across the sidewalk and into the carriageway, covering the pavement and affecting parked vehicles and pedestrian movement on Station Road. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Station Road carried the flow route of flood water during the event; wet pavement is visible, an approximate route of a below&ndash;ground culvert under the carriageway is shown directing flow, and unused lowered kerbs on the south carriageway channel are visible along the flood route. [IMG,PAG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed across the carriageway along Station Road via the identified flow route and kerb channels, inundating the road surface and affecting access between the businesses either side of the road. [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than one property; more than one occasion in the last 5 years (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 businesses (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Station Road, Irthlingborough",England,"Heavy rainfall caused surface water drainage and watercourses to be unable to convey rainwater, producing excess surface water that flowed to low points around private property and resulted in internal flooding of properties on Station Road. (p7)
Internal flooding of properties occurred on Station Road. The report states internal flooding of more than one property has been experienced on more than one occasion in the five years prior to the investigation. [MUL] (p9)
Flooding occurred near Station Road, close to the roundabout junction with the A6 Finedon Road. Several businesses on the north and south sides of Station Road, immediately west of the A6, were affected by the flood. (p10)
Station Road was flooded by flowing surface water (flow from the west) during the same overtopping events, impeding access and requiring placement of sandbags on the kerbline during responses. [TAB,MUL] (p12)
The carriageway between the businesses was flooded before the overland flow from the watercourse started; water overtopped the lowered kerbs on the south side of the carriageway and flowed off the highway towards private property; attempts were made to unblock road gullies. (p14)
Flooding occurred on Station Road when heavy rainfall and overwhelmed drainage/watercourses caused excess surface water to flow to low points around private properties. [PAG] (p15)
Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems and watercourses to be unable to collect and convey rainwater, resulting in excess surface water flowing over ground into low points around private properties on Station Road. Sections of upstream watercourse that had not been recently maintained were identified as a significant contributory factor; limited records of underground drainage suggest historic/private systems may have exacerbated the incident. (p16)
Flooding of the carriageway (overland flow) occurred on Station Road; lowered kerbs are noted at the location of carriageway flooding. [IMG] (p33)
Standing floodwater covered the carriageway and part of the footway; responders placed a continuous line of sandbags across the road to control/divert water flow and protect the nearby traffic features, disrupting normal vehicle and pedestrian movement. [IMG] (p42)
Floodwater extended from the building edge across the sidewalk and into the carriageway, covering the pavement and affecting parked vehicles and pedestrian movement on Station Road. [IMG,PAG] (p43)
Station Road carried the flow route of flood water during the event; wet pavement is visible, an approximate route of a below–ground culvert under the carriageway is shown directing flow, and unused lowered kerbs on the south carriageway channel are visible along the flood route. [IMG,PAG] (p53)
Floodwater flowed across the carriageway along Station Road via the identified flow route and kerb channels, inundating the road surface and affecting access between the businesses either side of the road. [IMG,PAG] (p54)","more than one property; more than one occasion in the last 5 years (p7)
4 businesses (p10)",GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.3293646,-0.6179477,"<div id=""html_b947f23a4a9a4ba6f42232a065477a34"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Finedon Road, Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Finedon Road stream (an ordinary watercourse) flows southeast to Station Road/A6 and immediately upstream of Business 2 enters a culvert that runs beneath Business 2, Station Road and beneath the car parks of Businesses 3 and 4. The culvert/open channel downstream was in poor condition with high silt and debris, affecting flow out of the culvert and contributing to the flood incident that affected adjacent businesses. (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 businesses (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Finedon Road, Irthlingborough",England,"The Finedon Road stream (an ordinary watercourse) flows southeast to Station Road/A6 and immediately upstream of Business 2 enters a culvert that runs beneath Business 2, Station Road and beneath the car parks of Businesses 3 and 4. The culvert/open channel downstream was in poor condition with high silt and debris, affecting flow out of the culvert and contributing to the flood incident that affected adjacent businesses. (p10)",4 businesses (p10),GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.328814,-0.6080475,"<div id=""html_20ebb00af505186adfc85a7771fa132e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Addington Road, Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Entrance to the culvert under Addington Road was photographed with vegetation and debris partially obscuring the inlet; the upstream watercourse immediately downstream of Addington Road was densely vegetated/overgrown. These conditions were documented in relation to the flood incident and indicate restricted conveyance at the culvert during the event, with floodwater following a flow route across nearby Station Road (between Business 2 and Business 3). [IMG] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Addington Road, Irthlingborough",England,"Entrance to the culvert under Addington Road was photographed with vegetation and debris partially obscuring the inlet; the upstream watercourse immediately downstream of Addington Road was densely vegetated/overgrown. These conditions were documented in relation to the flood incident and indicate restricted conveyance at the culvert during the event, with floodwater following a flow route across nearby Station Road (between Business 2 and Business 3). [IMG] (p55)",,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.3283093,-0.6095615,"<div id=""html_8c6acef949f43f32062aac570171b97c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lilley Terrace, Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The outlet of a culvert in public open space immediately north of Lilley Terrace is identified as part of the watercourse upstream of the recorded flood incident; the culvert outlet and adjoining channel were implicated in the upstream flow contributing to the incident. [IMG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Lilley Terrace, Irthlingborough",England,The outlet of a culvert in public open space immediately north of Lilley Terrace is identified as part of the watercourse upstream of the recorded flood incident; the culvert outlet and adjoining channel were implicated in the upstream flow contributing to the incident. [IMG] (p56),,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.3291978,-0.6111355,"<div id=""html_701daaef740826e773fadb03e57fb1e7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Drayton Place, Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert flows west&ndash;east through private property south of Drayton Place and the open watercourse downstream of the Drayton Place culvert is described as upstream of the flood incident, indicating the channel near Drayton Place was involved in the flood event; vegetation and debris were noted in the channel which may have restricted flow. [IMG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Headwall at the entrance to the Drayton Place culvert was heavily obstructed by vegetation, fallen branches and litter, restricting flow and contributing to the upstream flood incident. Obstruction likely increased local flood risk and impeded inspection/access to the culvert. [IMG] (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Drayton Place, Irthlingborough",England,"The culvert flows west–east through private property south of Drayton Place and the open watercourse downstream of the Drayton Place culvert is described as upstream of the flood incident, indicating the channel near Drayton Place was involved in the flood event; vegetation and debris were noted in the channel which may have restricted flow. [IMG] (p56)
Headwall at the entrance to the Drayton Place culvert was heavily obstructed by vegetation, fallen branches and litter, restricting flow and contributing to the upstream flood incident. Obstruction likely increased local flood risk and impeded inspection/access to the culvert. [IMG] (p58)",,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.3298044,-0.6138166,"<div id=""html_a35021201a05c48c8eeeeef1ecaf568d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Robb Close, Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert entrance/exit immediately southeast of Robb Close was partially obstructed by dense vegetation, leaves, branches and litter, restricting flow from the culvert and contributing to upstream surface water flooding during the incident. [IMG] (p57)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Robb Close, Irthlingborough",England,"The culvert entrance/exit immediately southeast of Robb Close was partially obstructed by dense vegetation, leaves, branches and litter, restricting flow from the culvert and contributing to upstream surface water flooding during the incident. [IMG] (p57)",,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.328857,-0.6138401,"<div id=""html_02b350d3900892f0244a6f21ad4c5139"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fernmoor Drive, Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert entrance immediately north of Fernmoor Drive (headwall) was heavily overgrown and blocked by natural debris and litter, restricting the watercourse and contributing to the upstream flooding at the incident location. [IMG] (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Fernmoor Drive, Irthlingborough",England,"The culvert entrance immediately north of Fernmoor Drive (headwall) was heavily overgrown and blocked by natural debris and litter, restricting the watercourse and contributing to the upstream flooding at the incident location. [IMG] (p58)",,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-NNH,10,2016-03-09,52.32619,-0.6128414,"<div id=""html_dacc4be7633f43b2a29c187deb892257"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Irthlingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident on 9 March 2016 (Station Road, Irthlingborough) caused surface water to flow to low points around private properties, resulting in internal flooding of more than one property and repeated internal flooding events within the preceding five years. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Irthlingborough (specifically on Station Road) experienced internal flooding, with internal flooding of more than one property on multiple occasions. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The east of the town (Station Road area adjacent to the A6/Finedon Road) experienced flooding which affected commercial properties; local ordinary watercourse and culvert blockages/surcharge contributed to the incident. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood incident at Station Road in Irthlingborough was caused by heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment, with drainage systems and watercourses unable to convey rainwater effectively; this led to excess surface water flowing overland to low points around private properties within Irthlingborough. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced a flood incident (the report refers to 'this flood incident' affecting Irthlingborough). The event prompted advice and actions for property owners and communities regarding riparian responsibilities, maintenance of ponds/ditches/open drainage and community resilience measures. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents occurred in Irthlingborough that prompted a Section 19 investigation by the LLFA and the recording of assets and flooding hotspots affecting the area. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town was the location of the flood investigated in this report and was affected by flooding during the event on 9th March 2016. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident occurred in the town; the mapped catchment is described as contributing runoff to the flood location within Irthlingborough (map annotation indicates the flood incident location is within the outlined catchment). [IMG] (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than one property; more than one occasion in the last 5 years (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 businesses (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Irthlingborough,England,"A flood incident on 9 March 2016 (Station Road, Irthlingborough) caused surface water to flow to low points around private properties, resulting in internal flooding of more than one property and repeated internal flooding events within the preceding five years. (p7)
Properties in Irthlingborough (specifically on Station Road) experienced internal flooding, with internal flooding of more than one property on multiple occasions. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
The east of the town (Station Road area adjacent to the A6/Finedon Road) experienced flooding which affected commercial properties; local ordinary watercourse and culvert blockages/surcharge contributed to the incident. (p10)
The flood incident at Station Road in Irthlingborough was caused by heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment, with drainage systems and watercourses unable to convey rainwater effectively; this led to excess surface water flowing overland to low points around private properties within Irthlingborough. (p16)
The town experienced a flood incident (the report refers to 'this flood incident' affecting Irthlingborough). The event prompted advice and actions for property owners and communities regarding riparian responsibilities, maintenance of ponds/ditches/open drainage and community resilience measures. (p17)
Flooding incidents occurred in Irthlingborough that prompted a Section 19 investigation by the LLFA and the recording of assets and flooding hotspots affecting the area. (p19)
The town was the location of the flood investigated in this report and was affected by flooding during the event on 9th March 2016. (p21)
A flood incident occurred in the town; the mapped catchment is described as contributing runoff to the flood location within Irthlingborough (map annotation indicates the flood incident location is within the outlined catchment). [IMG] (p32)","more than one property; more than one occasion in the last 5 years (p7)
4 businesses (p10)",GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_10_2016-03-09,Irthlingborough flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Station Road, Irthlingborough, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/23968/FIR/Station-Road-Irthlingborough/Rev-03/190617.pdf
GB-LAN,8,2020-11-01,53.6068931,-2.8324922,"<div id=""html_6eaca6d587a3380e7fe10608af92d75c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Warpers Moss Lane, Burscough Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Warpers Moss Lane was flooded as part of the November 2020 flood event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of a house and road at Warpers Moss Lane caused by an obstructed culvert and capacity constraints / obstructions in local ditched watercourses; connected highway drainage systems failed to function as historically expected. Culvert and highway drainage were cleaned and jetted earlier in the year; remaining risk was addressed when excess silt and vegetation were cleared from the private land watercourses. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water drainage on the public highway was overwhelmed and failed to discharge because downstream ditched watercourses were obstructed by silt and vegetation, causing floodwater to back up into the highway and into at least one home. Repeated reports of flooding were recorded in 2019 and 2020; a resident reported his home flooded on 24 November 2020 and that this had occurred on three separate occasions. The council identified three obstructed ditched watercourses, notified landowners, and remedial works were completed with no further reports of home flooding after clearance. (p5)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 previous incidents; 1 house (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 separate ditched watercourses; 3 separate occasions; 1 home (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,"Warpers Moss Lane, Burscough Bridge",England,"Warpers Moss Lane was flooded as part of the November 2020 flood event. (p1)
Flooding of a house and road at Warpers Moss Lane caused by an obstructed culvert and capacity constraints / obstructions in local ditched watercourses; connected highway drainage systems failed to function as historically expected. Culvert and highway drainage were cleaned and jetted earlier in the year; remaining risk was addressed when excess silt and vegetation were cleared from the private land watercourses. (p3)
Surface water drainage on the public highway was overwhelmed and failed to discharge because downstream ditched watercourses were obstructed by silt and vegetation, causing floodwater to back up into the highway and into at least one home. Repeated reports of flooding were recorded in 2019 and 2020; a resident reported his home flooded on 24 November 2020 and that this had occurred on three separate occasions. The council identified three obstructed ditched watercourses, notified landowners, and remedial works were completed with no further reports of home flooding after clearance. (p5)","3 previous incidents; 1 house (p3)
3 separate ditched watercourses; 3 separate occasions; 1 home (p5)",GB-LAN_8_2020-11-01.html,GB-LAN_8_2020-11-01,Burscough flooding 01 Nov 2020 - 30 Nov 2020,November 2020 Flooding In Lancashire,069/Lancashire County/november-2020-flooding-warpers-moss-lane-burscough.pdf
GB-LAN,8,2020-11-01,53.86117,-2.5650887,"<div id=""html_b024ef58b605cac71a90dfe6dcfa0cac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancashire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lancashire experienced flooding during the November 2020 flood event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding occurred due to an overwhelmed surface water drainage system and capacity constraints/obstructions on local watercourses; the report documents repeated highway flooding on Warpers Moss Lane (2019&ndash;2020) and flooding of a home on 24 November 2020. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Individual lengths of ditched watercourses in Burscough had fallen into disrepair, obstructing highway drainage outfalls. Under heavy rainfall the road flooded and a local house flooded on multiple occasions. The ditched watercourses were later cleared by the responsible parties and there has been no repeat of the flooding since. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lancashire,England,"Lancashire experienced flooding during the November 2020 flood event. (p1)
Localised flooding occurred due to an overwhelmed surface water drainage system and capacity constraints/obstructions on local watercourses; the report documents repeated highway flooding on Warpers Moss Lane (2019–2020) and flooding of a home on 24 November 2020. (p4)
Individual lengths of ditched watercourses in Burscough had fallen into disrepair, obstructing highway drainage outfalls. Under heavy rainfall the road flooded and a local house flooded on multiple occasions. The ditched watercourses were later cleared by the responsible parties and there has been no repeat of the flooding since. [PAG] (p6)","1 property (p4)
1 property [PAG] (p6)",GB-LAN_8_2020-11-01.html,GB-LAN_8_2020-11-01,Burscough flooding 01 Nov 2020 - 30 Nov 2020,November 2020 Flooding In Lancashire,069/Lancashire County/november-2020-flooding-warpers-moss-lane-burscough.pdf
GB-LAN,8,2020-11-01,53.599706114126626,-2.8404031723101193,"<div id=""html_56b797db7919ee95a2ecc3a486e7eddb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Burscough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Burscough was affected by flooding during the November 2020 Lancashire flood event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local ditched watercourses had fallen into disrepair, which prevented proper drainage and obstructed highway drainage outfalls. Under heavy rainfall the road flooded and a local house flooded on a number of occasions directly from the road. The ditched watercourses were subsequently cleared and there have been no repeat reports of flooding to homes since the works were completed. (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Burscough,England,"Burscough was affected by flooding during the November 2020 Lancashire flood event. (p1)
Local ditched watercourses had fallen into disrepair, which prevented proper drainage and obstructed highway drainage outfalls. Under heavy rainfall the road flooded and a local house flooded on a number of occasions directly from the road. The ditched watercourses were subsequently cleared and there have been no repeat reports of flooding to homes since the works were completed. (p6)",1 property (p6),GB-LAN_8_2020-11-01.html,GB-LAN_8_2020-11-01,Burscough flooding 01 Nov 2020 - 30 Nov 2020,November 2020 Flooding In Lancashire,069/Lancashire County/november-2020-flooding-warpers-moss-lane-burscough.pdf
GB-NNH,26,2020-08-16,52.3009694,-0.6163495,"<div id=""html_75b0ddc81addfe0280c4dd870b2a3f9c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northampton Road, Rushden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a building on Northampton Road when surface water drainage was unable to convey intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall and excess water flowed over ground to low points around properties. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a building on Northampton Road; the property reported internal flooding from the event on 16 August 2020 and had experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the previous five years. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The commercial area on Northampton Road (north&ndash;west of the town centre) was recorded as flooded; surface water/urban flooding impacted warehousing, delivery yards and car parking in the localised catchment. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the A5001 Northampton Road flowed across the car park frontage to the front of the affected property. Highway gullies on the A5001 may have been blocked at the time of the event; the road gully immediately adjacent to the vehicle access into the site was observed blocked, contributing to poor drainage and runoff on the highway. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from an adjacent car park flowed to the front of a property, causing water to reach the property frontage and likely ingress. [IMG] (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 building (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Northampton Road, Rushden",England,"Internal flooding of a building on Northampton Road when surface water drainage was unable to convey intense, short–duration rainfall and excess water flowed over ground to low points around properties. (p6)
Internal flooding of a building on Northampton Road; the property reported internal flooding from the event on 16 August 2020 and had experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the previous five years. (p8)
The commercial area on Northampton Road (north–west of the town centre) was recorded as flooded; surface water/urban flooding impacted warehousing, delivery yards and car parking in the localised catchment. (p11)
Surface water runoff from the A5001 Northampton Road flowed across the car park frontage to the front of the affected property. Highway gullies on the A5001 may have been blocked at the time of the event; the road gully immediately adjacent to the vehicle access into the site was observed blocked, contributing to poor drainage and runoff on the highway. (p17)
Surface water runoff from an adjacent car park flowed to the front of a property, causing water to reach the property frontage and likely ingress. [IMG] (p38)",1 building (p6),GB-NNH_26_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_26_2020-08-16,Rushden flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Northampton Road, Rushden, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Northampton Road, Rushden.pdf"
GB-NNH,26,2020-08-16,52.291157,-0.5988177,"<div id=""html_d0a9552192636d8ac913f35dc4178898"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rushden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incident on Northampton Road in Rushden caused internal flooding of a building; intense heavy rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage leading to overland flow to low points around properties. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rushden was the location of the flood incident at Northampton Road on 16 August 2020, where a building suffered internal flooding. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in the town area (including Northampton Road). Runoff from local watercourses and the localised catchment led to surface water/urban flooding affecting commercial areas in the north&ndash;west of the town. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from car park areas to the front of a property and overtopping of a watercourse to the rear caused fast&ndash;flowing floodwater to enter a ground&ndash;floor workshop. Water entered via the main doors, back door and through blockwork walls. Standing water was pumped out, drains were rodded, and the water receded within 24 hours. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 16 August 2020 intense, short&ndash;duration heavy rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage across Rushden. Excess rainwater flowed over ground to local low points around properties because buried drainage and overland flow routes could not convey the water, resulting in surface water pooling and flooding in the affected area. No community or property&ndash;level resilience measures were in place to mitigate impacts. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 building (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 150 mm internal flood depth; 0.3 m external flood depth; receded within 24 hours (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Rushden,England,"Flood incident on Northampton Road in Rushden caused internal flooding of a building; intense heavy rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage leading to overland flow to low points around properties. (p6)
Rushden was the location of the flood incident at Northampton Road on 16 August 2020, where a building suffered internal flooding. (p8)
Flooding occurred in the town area (including Northampton Road). Runoff from local watercourses and the localised catchment led to surface water/urban flooding affecting commercial areas in the north–west of the town. (p11)
Surface water runoff from car park areas to the front of a property and overtopping of a watercourse to the rear caused fast–flowing floodwater to enter a ground–floor workshop. Water entered via the main doors, back door and through blockwork walls. Standing water was pumped out, drains were rodded, and the water receded within 24 hours. (p16)
On 16 August 2020 intense, short–duration heavy rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage across Rushden. Excess rainwater flowed over ground to local low points around properties because buried drainage and overland flow routes could not convey the water, resulting in surface water pooling and flooding in the affected area. No community or property–level resilience measures were in place to mitigate impacts. (p19)","1 building (p6)
1 property; 150 mm internal flood depth; 0.3 m external flood depth; receded within 24 hours (p16)",GB-NNH_26_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_26_2020-08-16,Rushden flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Northampton Road, Rushden, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Northampton Road, Rushden.pdf"
