GB-LAN,9,2015-12-01,53.83634,-2.2116468,"<div id=""html_528697d2a0f476898d31195111590739"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nelson</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties in Nelson on 26 December 2015: 5 properties suffered internal flooding in the Pendle Water area (primary source identified as Pendle Water overtopping its banks) and 2 properties suffered internal flooding in The Green area. [PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two properties are recorded as having suffered internal flooding on 26 December 2015 (MSFW197: Roughlee). [TAB] (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties; 2 properties [PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [TAB] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Nelson,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties in Nelson on 26 December 2015: 5 properties suffered internal flooding in the Pendle Water area (primary source identified as Pendle Water overtopping its banks) and 2 properties suffered internal flooding in The Green area. [PAG] (p29)
Two properties are recorded as having suffered internal flooding on 26 December 2015 (MSFW197: Roughlee). [TAB] (p30)","5 properties; 2 properties [PAG] (p29)
2 properties [TAB] (p30)",GB-LAN_9_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_9_2015-12-01,Pendle flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,Pendle District Flood Report Recommended Actions,069/Lancashire County/pendle-final-report.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.2383776,-0.7000721,"<div id=""html_6a059c86e07659b1ba1da9e3d6de1a89"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grendon Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Grendon Brook is one of the main rivers that converges near Hall Farm/Lower End and contributed to flooding at that confluence. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Grendon Brook (a main river close to Blackmile Lane) is associated with the area of deepest flooding shown on the map; river reaches follow low&ndash;lying zones and contributed to localised flooding when channel capacity was exceeded (recorded at Easter 1998) and during the heavy rainfall event that saturated the catchment prior to 14 July 2012. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Grendon Brook is included in the discussion of the Grendon Brook catchment being saturated by repeated intense rainfall leading up to and including the event on 14 July 2012. [MUL] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Investigations found only two small sections where a shallow depth of water came out of Grendon Brook (shallow out&ndash;of&ndash;bank flow); these were considered too small to account for the overall flooding experienced. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depth just under 1 m at the confluence (deepest point) (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Grendon Brook,England,"Grendon Brook is one of the main rivers that converges near Hall Farm/Lower End and contributed to flooding at that confluence. (p7)
Grendon Brook (a main river close to Blackmile Lane) is associated with the area of deepest flooding shown on the map; river reaches follow low–lying zones and contributed to localised flooding when channel capacity was exceeded (recorded at Easter 1998) and during the heavy rainfall event that saturated the catchment prior to 14 July 2012. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p8)
Grendon Brook is included in the discussion of the Grendon Brook catchment being saturated by repeated intense rainfall leading up to and including the event on 14 July 2012. [MUL] (p22)
Investigations found only two small sections where a shallow depth of water came out of Grendon Brook (shallow out–of–bank flow); these were considered too small to account for the overall flooding experienced. [PAG] (p23)",flood depth just under 1 m at the confluence (deepest point) (p7),GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.2408291,-0.7065902,"<div id=""html_42b6f50500fdc052564eb19324d77ed8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Blackmile Lane, Grendon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater affected the street. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on a saturated catchment produced excess surface water that flowed to Blackmile Lane; resulted in internal flooding of properties on Blackmile Lane and closure of the highway. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding on 14 July 2012: residents reported chest&ndash;deep water on the highway; water came down the highway and pooled at the bottom of Blackmile Lane then came across adjacent fields; a small surge of fast&ndash;moving water was reported prior to the peak; emergency services attended and the highway was closed; internal flooding of nearby properties occurred. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water came down the highway and pooled at the bottom of Blackmile Lane, then came across fields to the southeast. Water rose steadily and some residents reported a small surge of fast&ndash;moving water prior to the peak. 31 properties on Blackmile Lane were evacuated by Northamptonshire Fire and Rescue Service and occupants were temporarily taken to Grendon Hall. [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties (residential and commercial); flooding of grounds and gardens; access to properties was affected. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Channelled surface water flowed across fields onto the lane. Access to about 31 properties (including flooded properties) was obstructed for several hours. The worst affected house at the bottom of Blackmile Lane was internally flooded to a depth of approximately 30cm. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Volume of water pooled on the lane greatly exceeded channel capacity; a small culvert on the Gibbards Arm was completely submerged and there was flooding over a significant length of highway downstream of the structure. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Some houses in Blackmile Lane experienced flooding; ditches between Grendon Brook and Blackmile Lane are noted as important to control surface water flow to protect these houses. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow following natural contours toward the junction of Blackmile Lane and Main Road, producing surface water flooding of some properties; local features such as garden walls channelled flows and altered impacts to individual properties. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Blackmile Lane on 14 July 2012. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on 14 July 2012 after intense short&ndash;duration rainfall on a saturated catchment; high river levels and inability of the ground to absorb water caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours to the junction of Blackmile Lane and Main Road. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water flowed over ground following natural contours to the junction at Blackmile Lane; surface water reached the junction (i.e. inundation along the lane leading to the junction with Main Road). (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 5 properties (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 5 properties flooded internally (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>31 properties [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 residential properties; 1 commercial property; approximately 31 properties had access affected (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 31 properties; 1 property internally flooded to a depth of approximately 30cm (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Blackmile Lane, Grendon",England,"Floodwater affected the street. (p1)
Intense rainfall on a saturated catchment produced excess surface water that flowed to Blackmile Lane; resulted in internal flooding of properties on Blackmile Lane and closure of the highway. (p4)
Significant flooding on 14 July 2012: residents reported chest–deep water on the highway; water came down the highway and pooled at the bottom of Blackmile Lane then came across adjacent fields; a small surge of fast–moving water was reported prior to the peak; emergency services attended and the highway was closed; internal flooding of nearby properties occurred. (p5)
Water came down the highway and pooled at the bottom of Blackmile Lane, then came across fields to the southeast. Water rose steadily and some residents reported a small surge of fast–moving water prior to the peak. 31 properties on Blackmile Lane were evacuated by Northamptonshire Fire and Rescue Service and occupants were temporarily taken to Grendon Hall. [IMG,PAG] (p6)
Internal flooding of properties (residential and commercial); flooding of grounds and gardens; access to properties was affected. (p9)
Channelled surface water flowed across fields onto the lane. Access to about 31 properties (including flooded properties) was obstructed for several hours. The worst affected house at the bottom of Blackmile Lane was internally flooded to a depth of approximately 30cm. (p10)
Volume of water pooled on the lane greatly exceeded channel capacity; a small culvert on the Gibbards Arm was completely submerged and there was flooding over a significant length of highway downstream of the structure. (p12)
Some houses in Blackmile Lane experienced flooding; ditches between Grendon Brook and Blackmile Lane are noted as important to control surface water flow to protect these houses. (p13)
Intense rainfall on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow following natural contours toward the junction of Blackmile Lane and Main Road, producing surface water flooding of some properties; local features such as garden walls channelled flows and altered impacts to individual properties. (p14)
Flooding occurred at Blackmile Lane on 14 July 2012. (p19)
Flooding occurred on 14 July 2012 after intense short–duration rainfall on a saturated catchment; high river levels and inability of the ground to absorb water caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours to the junction of Blackmile Lane and Main Road. [PAG] (p27)
Excess surface water flowed over ground following natural contours to the junction at Blackmile Lane; surface water reached the junction (i.e. inundation along the lane leading to the junction with Main Road). (p28)","more than 5 properties (p4)
more than 5 properties flooded internally (p5)
31 properties [IMG,PAG] (p6)
7 residential properties; 1 commercial property; approximately 31 properties had access affected (p9)
approximately 31 properties; 1 property internally flooded to a depth of approximately 30cm (p10)",GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.236095,-0.7133471,"<div id=""html_461cabc34f1aced2949a3f06c7886d85"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Main Road, Grendon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water from intense rainfall flowed over the ground following natural contours to the junction of Blackmile Lane and Main Road, indicating floodwater reached the junction area. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At the junction of Main Road and Blackmile Lane water pooled, with the maximum depth noted at the junction (pooling to about 1m). A visible water line relative to the road was recorded. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water routed to the junction of Main Road and Blackmile Lane accumulated when river and drainage capacity was exceeded, contributing to the flooding of some properties at that junction. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water flowed over ground following natural contours to the junction at Main Road; surface water reached the junction (i.e. inundation at the junction with Blackmile Lane). (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>water pooled to a depth of approximately 1m (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Main Road, Grendon",England,"Excess surface water from intense rainfall flowed over the ground following natural contours to the junction of Blackmile Lane and Main Road, indicating floodwater reached the junction area. (p4)
At the junction of Main Road and Blackmile Lane water pooled, with the maximum depth noted at the junction (pooling to about 1m). A visible water line relative to the road was recorded. (p10)
Water routed to the junction of Main Road and Blackmile Lane accumulated when river and drainage capacity was exceeded, contributing to the flooding of some properties at that junction. (p14)
Excess surface water flowed over ground following natural contours to the junction at Main Road; surface water reached the junction (i.e. inundation at the junction with Blackmile Lane). (p28)",water pooled to a depth of approximately 1m (p10),GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.2406066,-0.7066239,"<div id=""html_ccd78299f847a24ac109ef3ea38d8389"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower End, Grendon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area of Lower End lies at the confluence of main rivers and ordinary watercourses and is identified on the map as being at or very near the deepest flooding point. [IMG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower End is within the extent of the flood warning area for Grendon; the report notes that there had been flooding in the area on 14 July 2012. (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depth just under 1 m at the deepest point [IMG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Lower End, Grendon",England,"The area of Lower End lies at the confluence of main rivers and ordinary watercourses and is identified on the map as being at or very near the deepest flooding point. [IMG] (p7)
Lower End is within the extent of the flood warning area for Grendon; the report notes that there had been flooding in the area on 14 July 2012. (p26)",flood depth just under 1 m at the deepest point [IMG] (p7),GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.23361570015935,-0.7144605440180205,"<div id=""html_e2f2bf1a514f801877e16f2e5f0d7038"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grendon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood on Blackmile Lane (within Grendon) caused internal flooding of properties and closure of the highway; records indicate similar flooding has occurred at this location in the past. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding during the event of 14 July 2012: significant rainfall over the local catchment produced runoff that caused chest&ndash;deep flooding on Blackmile Lane, required emergency services assistance, closed the highway and led to internal flooding of nearby properties. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blackmile Lane, on the northern edge of Grendon Village, experienced surface water/flash flooding that led to residents being evacuated (31 properties) and temporary sheltering at Grendon Hall. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Grendon (specifically Lower End and the area around Hall Farm) experienced flooding where main rivers and ordinary watercourses converged, creating the deepest flood point in the area. [IMG,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The civil parish area contains the low&ndash;lying catchment around Blackmile Lane where flooding concentrated near the confluence of main rivers (deepest flooding marked on the LiDAR&ndash;derived map). Recorded localised flooding events affecting this locality include Easter 1998 (channel capacity exceeded) and heavy rainfall affecting the catchment on 14 July 2012. [IMG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Assessed the main river and identified out&ndash;of&ndash;bank flows: two upstream locations where Grendon Brook overtopped its channel at natural low points; wrack lines showed out&ndash;of&ndash;bank flows that followed the channel to relatively shallow depths. Concluded a small amount of water left the channel but high water levels and limited channel capacity may have contributed to increased surface water in the catchment. [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Grendon area experienced a short, intense rainfall event between midnight and 04:30 on 14 July 2012 (44 mm reported), which contributed to saturation of the local catchment. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>There had been flooding in the area on 14 July 2012; a flood warning covering Grendon was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 5 properties (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 5 properties flooded internally (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>31 properties (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depth just under 1 m at the deepest point affecting Lower End and Hall Farm [IMG,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 recorded flood event (Easter 1998) [IMG,MUL] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Grendon,England,"The flood on Blackmile Lane (within Grendon) caused internal flooding of properties and closure of the highway; records indicate similar flooding has occurred at this location in the past. (p4)
The village experienced flooding during the event of 14 July 2012: significant rainfall over the local catchment produced runoff that caused chest–deep flooding on Blackmile Lane, required emergency services assistance, closed the highway and led to internal flooding of nearby properties. (p5)
Blackmile Lane, on the northern edge of Grendon Village, experienced surface water/flash flooding that led to residents being evacuated (31 properties) and temporary sheltering at Grendon Hall. (p6)
Parts of Grendon (specifically Lower End and the area around Hall Farm) experienced flooding where main rivers and ordinary watercourses converged, creating the deepest flood point in the area. [IMG,PAG] (p7)
The civil parish area contains the low–lying catchment around Blackmile Lane where flooding concentrated near the confluence of main rivers (deepest flooding marked on the LiDAR–derived map). Recorded localised flooding events affecting this locality include Easter 1998 (channel capacity exceeded) and heavy rainfall affecting the catchment on 14 July 2012. [IMG,MUL] (p8)
Assessed the main river and identified out–of–bank flows: two upstream locations where Grendon Brook overtopped its channel at natural low points; wrack lines showed out–of–bank flows that followed the channel to relatively shallow depths. Concluded a small amount of water left the channel but high water levels and limited channel capacity may have contributed to increased surface water in the catchment. [MUL] (p12)
The Grendon area experienced a short, intense rainfall event between midnight and 04:30 on 14 July 2012 (44 mm reported), which contributed to saturation of the local catchment. (p22)
There had been flooding in the area on 14 July 2012; a flood warning covering Grendon was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)","more than 5 properties (p4)
more than 5 properties flooded internally (p5)
31 properties (p6)
flood depth just under 1 m at the deepest point affecting Lower End and Hall Farm [IMG,PAG] (p7)
1 recorded flood event (Easter 1998) [IMG,MUL] (p8)",GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.203289657763705,-0.7345336398916282,"<div id=""html_ceec6e3a994ccc1b37ff7a93a6faa004"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yardley Hastings</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village stream came out of bank and caused flooding of the road, depositing gravel across the highway. The EA reported that some residential properties appear to have flooded from surface water. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report records that flooding occurred in this location and recommends NCC contact residents to obtain more detail on the flooding that occurred here. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Three flood storage reservoirs at Yardley Hastings operated as designed during the event: penstocks were fixed and water backed up behind the embankments; emergency spillways were not used and there was no manual or automatic operation of the penstocks. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; a flood warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Yardley Hastings,England,"The village stream came out of bank and caused flooding of the road, depositing gravel across the highway. The EA reported that some residential properties appear to have flooded from surface water. (p11)
The report records that flooding occurred in this location and recommends NCC contact residents to obtain more detail on the flooding that occurred here. (p14)
Three flood storage reservoirs at Yardley Hastings operated as designed during the event: penstocks were fixed and water backed up behind the embankments; emergency spillways were not used and there was no manual or automatic operation of the penstocks. (p24)
Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; a flood warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)",,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.2199754,-0.7008502,"<div id=""html_4813cf0a5ee0dd4ef5a53799d7215a36"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Easton Maudit</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The stream came out of bank (EA estimate of 0.5 m depth) and flowed down the main road, causing damage to bank protection measures in the area. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall led to reported damage to assets in Easton Maudit; NCC and the EA are recommended to investigate those reported asset damages. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; a flood warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.5 m depth (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Easton Maudit,England,"The stream came out of bank (EA estimate of 0.5 m depth) and flowed down the main road, causing damage to bank protection measures in the area. (p11)
Flooding from intense, short–duration rainfall led to reported damage to assets in Easton Maudit; NCC and the EA are recommended to investigate those reported asset damages. (p14)
Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; a flood warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)",0.5 m depth (p11),GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.21392470720008,-0.7741561277857512,"<div id=""html_c66a2cbc226d7cdfb74c9fc732436db5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Denton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood storage reservoir at Denton operated as designed during the event: penstocks were fixed, water backed up behind the reservoir embankment, emergency spillways were not used, and there was no manual or automatic operation of control structures. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; the Flood Storage Reservoir at Denton is used as a basis for issuing warnings; a warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Denton,England,"The flood storage reservoir at Denton operated as designed during the event: penstocks were fixed, water backed up behind the reservoir embankment, emergency spillways were not used, and there was no manual or automatic operation of control structures. (p24)
Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; the Flood Storage Reservoir at Denton is used as a basis for issuing warnings; a warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)",,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,34,2012-07-14,52.22200111005708,-0.6753035670033355,"<div id=""html_8318925494a482452009b6a305da6cbb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bozeat</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bozeat hosts a flood storage reservoir which operated as designed during the event (penstocks fixed, water backed up behind the embankment; spillways not used). Separately, the Bozeat gravel workings site was closed in July 2012 and did not discharge water during that month, so it was not a source of additional flows. [PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; the Flood Storage Reservoir at Bozeat is used as a basis for issuing warnings; a warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Bozeat,England,"Bozeat hosts a flood storage reservoir which operated as designed during the event (penstocks fixed, water backed up behind the embankment; spillways not used). Separately, the Bozeat gravel workings site was closed in July 2012 and did not discharge water during that month, so it was not a source of additional flows. [PAG,MUL] (p24)
Included within the Grendon Brook Catchment flood warning area; the Flood Storage Reservoir at Bozeat is used as a basis for issuing warnings; a warning was issued at 06:52 on 14 July 2012. (p26)",,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14.html,GB-NNH_34_2012-07-14,Grendon flooding 14 Jul 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Flooding at Blackmile Lane, Grendon 14th July 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grendon-FIR.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.108475,1.2302219,"<div id=""html_bb8e956f633df17b9f03e327ae356144"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dawkinge Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as the origin of an additional, larger surface water overland flow path which flowed predominantly northeast to southwest toward the junction between Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue. (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Dawkinge Wood,England,"Described as the origin of an additional, larger surface water overland flow path which flowed predominantly northeast to southwest toward the junction between Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue. (p3)",,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.1045298,1.2126652,"<div id=""html_3d06b175d7bea60333ad9fe26f49a2b3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancaster Avenue, Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from arable fields and blocked highway gullies led to surface water flooding affecting properties on Lancaster Avenue. One property flooded internally to approximately 200&ndash;300 mm. Damage in the vicinity to garages and gardens was reported; some flooded properties in the area were evacuated. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows are described as reaching the junction at Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue, indicating Lancaster Avenue is on the route of surface water overland flow. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction with Capel Street forms a topographic depression where surface water accumulates; identified as a high&ndash;risk area and consistent with locations of reported properties affected by the 04&ndash;05 November 2023 surface&ndash;water flooding event. [IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow passed the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue; drainage gullies within Lancaster Avenue failed to drain runoff, contributing to accumulation of floodwater in the adjacent depression. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater accumulated in the local topographic depression at the junction with Capel Street; lifting of a manhole at the junction of Lancaster Avenue and Capel Street allowed floodwater to drain away and reduce local flood levels. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood alleviation drain under Lancaster Avenue connects two high&ndash;capacity gullies to a 750mm diameter pipe that discharges to a soakaway (with a 225mm diameter overflow). The hydraulic performance of the 750mm pipe was impaired by an obstruction (a foul sewer passing through and blocking a significant part of the surface&ndash;water pipe), reducing drainage capacity during the early November 2023 flood and contributing to local surface water flooding. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff collected in a depression between the junctions of Capel Street/Lancaster Avenue and Lancaster Avenue/Elizabeth Drive, overwhelming the highway drainage and causing flooding of the road and nearby properties. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected part of Lancaster Avenue adjacent to Capel Street. The map shows high&ndash;confidence extent on part of the road and a low&ndash;confidence dashed outline extending further into Lancaster Avenue, indicating additional possible road inundation. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Lancaster Avenue and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded internally; approximately 6 properties affected in the area; 5 properties flooded internally (area total) [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 manhole lifted (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two high&ndash;capacity gullies; 750mm diameter pipe; 225mm diameter overflow (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>At least 6 properties affected; 5 properties flooded internally (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 flood surveys sent; 2 flood surveys received [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Lancaster Avenue, Capel-Le-Ferne",England,"Runoff from arable fields and blocked highway gullies led to surface water flooding affecting properties on Lancaster Avenue. One property flooded internally to approximately 200–300 mm. Damage in the vicinity to garages and gardens was reported; some flooded properties in the area were evacuated. [TAB] (p2)
Overland flows are described as reaching the junction at Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue, indicating Lancaster Avenue is on the route of surface water overland flow. (p3)
The junction with Capel Street forms a topographic depression where surface water accumulates; identified as a high–risk area and consistent with locations of reported properties affected by the 04–05 November 2023 surface–water flooding event. [IMG] (p4)
Overland flow passed the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue; drainage gullies within Lancaster Avenue failed to drain runoff, contributing to accumulation of floodwater in the adjacent depression. (p7)
Floodwater accumulated in the local topographic depression at the junction with Capel Street; lifting of a manhole at the junction of Lancaster Avenue and Capel Street allowed floodwater to drain away and reduce local flood levels. (p8)
A flood alleviation drain under Lancaster Avenue connects two high–capacity gullies to a 750mm diameter pipe that discharges to a soakaway (with a 225mm diameter overflow). The hydraulic performance of the 750mm pipe was impaired by an obstruction (a foul sewer passing through and blocking a significant part of the surface–water pipe), reducing drainage capacity during the early November 2023 flood and contributing to local surface water flooding. (p9)
Runoff collected in a depression between the junctions of Capel Street/Lancaster Avenue and Lancaster Avenue/Elizabeth Drive, overwhelming the highway drainage and causing flooding of the road and nearby properties. (p11)
Flooding affected part of Lancaster Avenue adjacent to Capel Street. The map shows high–confidence extent on part of the road and a low–confidence dashed outline extending further into Lancaster Avenue, indicating additional possible road inundation. [IMG] (p13)
Flooding affected Lancaster Avenue and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15)","1 property flooded internally; approximately 6 properties affected in the area; 5 properties flooded internally (area total) [TAB] (p2)
1 manhole lifted (p8)
two high–capacity gullies; 750mm diameter pipe; 225mm diameter overflow (p9)
At least 6 properties affected; 5 properties flooded internally (p11)
14 flood surveys sent; 2 flood surveys received [TAB] (p15)",GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.1118417,1.2148528,"<div id=""html_b72f38fe3cfd5e0da4e208e272ccd4f7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Capel Street, Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from agricultural fields and blocked drains caused surface water flooding. One property reported internal flooding to approximately 300 mm; one property reported external flooding to the driveway and garden. Some flooded properties in the area were evacuated. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A natural surface water overland flow path routes water across the catchment toward Capel Street, delivering overland flows to the junction with Lancaster Avenue. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A topographic depression at the junction with Lancaster Avenue produced a high surface&ndash;water flood risk. This location is consistent with reported properties being affected during the flood event of 04&ndash;05 November 2023, exposing residential buildings at the junction to surface water inundation. [IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed onto and across the carriageway as drainage was overwhelmed. Overland flow passed the junction with Lancaster Avenue and contributed to accumulation in the nearby topographic depression; a blocked local drain (Northern Capel Street Drain) and insufficient capacity in the connected Flood Alleviation Drain network exacerbated the flooding. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>This drain did not receive floodwater from the topographic depression until a manhole at the southern corner of Lancaster Avenue and Capel Street was lifted; once opened it accepted water that bypassed the Northern drain, helping floodwater to drain away (lifting was reported to produce a sucking/vacuum sound as water entered the system). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A blockage downstream of the flooding at the junction between Capel Street and Elizabeth Drive prevented the connected gullies from draining floodwater away during the early November 2023 flood, contributing to local surface water inundation. Gullies to the east (at the entrance to Elizabeth Drive) lay beyond the blockage and could drain, which likely limited the depth and extent of flooding until water entered the unblocked network. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along Capel Street experienced surface water flooding because a 225mm foul sewer obstruction likely restricted flow, preventing gullies and the overflow connection between the Capel Street network and the flood&ndash;alleviation network from draining floodwater away during the event. [MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from saturated agricultural land accumulated in a localised topographic depression between the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue, overwhelming highway drainage; flooding occurred within the road and contributed to property flooding nearby. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High&ndash;confidence flooding of the highway along Capel Street, concentrated at the intersection with Lancaster Avenue. The map shows the road carriageway inundated; the estimated extent covers the highway only and does not mark properties as flooded. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Capel Street and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internal flooding; 1 property external flooding [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 manhole lifted (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300mm diameter concrete pipe (blocked) (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>At least 6 properties affected; 5 properties flooded internally (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 flood surveys sent; 3 flood surveys received [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Capel Street, Capel-Le-Ferne",England,"Runoff from agricultural fields and blocked drains caused surface water flooding. One property reported internal flooding to approximately 300 mm; one property reported external flooding to the driveway and garden. Some flooded properties in the area were evacuated. [TAB] (p2)
A natural surface water overland flow path routes water across the catchment toward Capel Street, delivering overland flows to the junction with Lancaster Avenue. (p3)
A topographic depression at the junction with Lancaster Avenue produced a high surface–water flood risk. This location is consistent with reported properties being affected during the flood event of 04–05 November 2023, exposing residential buildings at the junction to surface water inundation. [IMG] (p4)
Surface water flowed onto and across the carriageway as drainage was overwhelmed. Overland flow passed the junction with Lancaster Avenue and contributed to accumulation in the nearby topographic depression; a blocked local drain (Northern Capel Street Drain) and insufficient capacity in the connected Flood Alleviation Drain network exacerbated the flooding. (p7)
This drain did not receive floodwater from the topographic depression until a manhole at the southern corner of Lancaster Avenue and Capel Street was lifted; once opened it accepted water that bypassed the Northern drain, helping floodwater to drain away (lifting was reported to produce a sucking/vacuum sound as water entered the system). (p8)
A blockage downstream of the flooding at the junction between Capel Street and Elizabeth Drive prevented the connected gullies from draining floodwater away during the early November 2023 flood, contributing to local surface water inundation. Gullies to the east (at the entrance to Elizabeth Drive) lay beyond the blockage and could drain, which likely limited the depth and extent of flooding until water entered the unblocked network. (p9)
Properties along Capel Street experienced surface water flooding because a 225mm foul sewer obstruction likely restricted flow, preventing gullies and the overflow connection between the Capel Street network and the flood–alleviation network from draining floodwater away during the event. [MUL] (p10)
Surface water runoff from saturated agricultural land accumulated in a localised topographic depression between the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue, overwhelming highway drainage; flooding occurred within the road and contributed to property flooding nearby. (p11)
High–confidence flooding of the highway along Capel Street, concentrated at the intersection with Lancaster Avenue. The map shows the road carriageway inundated; the estimated extent covers the highway only and does not mark properties as flooded. [IMG] (p13)
Flooding affected Capel Street and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15)","1 property internal flooding; 1 property external flooding [TAB] (p2)
1 manhole lifted (p8)
300mm diameter concrete pipe (blocked) (p9)
At least 6 properties affected; 5 properties flooded internally (p11)
23 flood surveys sent; 3 flood surveys received [TAB] (p15)",GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.1056054,1.2122095,"<div id=""html_c0e4cb50f246cdbfa6c67c0697bed63b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Elizabeth Drive, Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property experienced external flooding affecting the driveway and garden with an approximate flood depth of 300mm. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding at a property on Elizabeth Drive with water approximately 300 mm deep at the external entrance. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater accumulated in the topographic depression between Capel Street and Elizabeth Drive, exacerbating local flooding adjacent to Elizabeth Drive. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies at the entrance to Elizabeth Drive were located beyond the blockage and remained able to drain some floodwater away during the early November 2023 event; their presence likely reduced the local depth and extent of flooding until floodwater reached the unblocked drainage network. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The localised depression at/near the junction with Lancaster Avenue led to surface water runoff and flooding affecting the highway and adjacent properties; highway drainage was unable to convey the runoff due to obstructions and high volumes. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Elizabeth Drive; map suggests possible road inundation but extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Elizabeth Drive and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property; approximately 300mm external flooding depth [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 300 mm external flooding (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>At least 6 properties affected; 5 properties flooded internally (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 flood surveys sent; 1 flood survey received [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Elizabeth Drive, Capel-Le-Ferne",England,"One property experienced external flooding affecting the driveway and garden with an approximate flood depth of 300mm. [TAB] (p2)
External flooding at a property on Elizabeth Drive with water approximately 300 mm deep at the external entrance. (p3)
Floodwater accumulated in the topographic depression between Capel Street and Elizabeth Drive, exacerbating local flooding adjacent to Elizabeth Drive. (p7)
Gullies at the entrance to Elizabeth Drive were located beyond the blockage and remained able to drain some floodwater away during the early November 2023 event; their presence likely reduced the local depth and extent of flooding until floodwater reached the unblocked drainage network. (p9)
The localised depression at/near the junction with Lancaster Avenue led to surface water runoff and flooding affecting the highway and adjacent properties; highway drainage was unable to convey the runoff due to obstructions and high volumes. (p11)
Low–confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Elizabeth Drive; map suggests possible road inundation but extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13)
Flooding affected Elizabeth Drive and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15)","one property; approximately 300mm external flooding depth [TAB] (p2)
1 property; 300 mm external flooding (p3)
At least 6 properties affected; 5 properties flooded internally (p11)
7 flood surveys sent; 1 flood survey received [TAB] (p15)",GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.1031074,1.212287,"<div id=""html_3a5c5d61e05375c5bbc49a320005bf3a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alexandra Road, Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Alexandra Road; map suggests possible road inundation but the extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Alexandra Road, Capel-Le-Ferne",England,Low–confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Alexandra Road; map suggests possible road inundation but the extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13),,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.1038002,1.2145364,"<div id=""html_a27fe23cab72cd3b303d4fb6015054da"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Beatrice Road, Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Beatrice Road; map suggests possible road inundation but extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Beatrice Road, Capel-Le-Ferne",England,Low–confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Beatrice Road; map suggests possible road inundation but extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13),,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.1066917,1.2109387,"<div id=""html_9944aa921003dbe83e92f774cb9e3aca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Green Lane, Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Green Lane; map suggests possible road inundation but extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Green Lane, Capel-Le-Ferne",England,Low–confidence (approximate) flood extent indicated extending from Capel Street into Green Lane; map suggests possible road inundation but extent is uncertain. [IMG] (p13),,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.1175359,1.2122037,"<div id=""html_fd049b71ba443a0120432935e5be4ec9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cauldham Lane, Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Cauldham Lane and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 flood surveys sent; 0 flood surveys received [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Cauldham Lane, Capel-Le-Ferne",England,Flooding affected Cauldham Lane and flood surveys were sent to residents following the event. [TAB] (p15),3 flood surveys sent; 0 flood surveys received [TAB] (p15),GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,3,2023-11-04,51.102165,1.2116929,"<div id=""html_3ee16cd6ad1e8c15c932460b6b78c010"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Capel-Le-Ferne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was recorded in the settlement during the evening of 04 November into the early morning of 05 November 2023 following a period of prolonged rainfall and a short, intense burst of rain; floodwaters affected Capel&ndash;le&ndash;Ferne. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties near the junction of Lancaster Avenue and Capel Street experienced overland surface water runoff leading to internal and external flooding, causing damage to garages and gardens and some properties being evacuated. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents reported properties were affected by surface water flooding associated with the event on 04&ndash;05 November 2023. The report identifies a topographic depression at the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue as a high&ndash;risk location consistent with where properties were reported flooded; resident testimony about the 04&ndash;05 November 2023 event was collected at a meeting on 05 December 2023. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident occurred in Capel&ndash;le&ndash;Ferne overnight linked to intense local rainfall. The incident took place between 23:00 on 04 November 2023 and 00:45 on 05 November 2023, coinciding with heavy short&ndash;duration rainfall and preceded by high antecedent rainfall in the previous days. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, antecedent rainfall produced large runoff from surrounding agricultural land which flowed southeast into the village. Water entered the village and travelled along the street corridor (past the Baptist Church and onto Capel Street), overwhelming local drainage and causing surface water flooding within the village. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from saturated agricultural fields accumulated in a local topographic depression between the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue and the junction of Lancaster Avenue and Elizabeth Drive. The highway drainage system was overwhelmed and obstructed, resulting in flooding of the carriageway and at least six properties (five of which flooded internally). KCC Highways attended the incident and carried out inspection and jetting of road gullies and mainlines on 09 November 2023. Kent Fire Rescue Service attended with vehicles and pumped floodwater out of affected properties and gardens into the highway drainage network. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the highway network in Capel&ndash;le&ndash;Ferne: a high&ndash;confidence flood extent is shown on Capel Street with flooding concentrated at the intersection with Lancaster Avenue, and a lower&ndash;confidence extent extends into Lancaster Avenue and adjacent residential streets. The map notes the estimated extent covers roads only and does not mark properties as flooded. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately six properties affected; five properties flooded internally (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties; 5 properties internally flooded; 3 fire engines/vehicles (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Capel-Le-Ferne,England,"Flooding was recorded in the settlement during the evening of 04 November into the early morning of 05 November 2023 following a period of prolonged rainfall and a short, intense burst of rain; floodwaters affected Capel–le–Ferne. (p1)
Properties near the junction of Lancaster Avenue and Capel Street experienced overland surface water runoff leading to internal and external flooding, causing damage to garages and gardens and some properties being evacuated. (p2)
Residents reported properties were affected by surface water flooding associated with the event on 04–05 November 2023. The report identifies a topographic depression at the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue as a high–risk location consistent with where properties were reported flooded; resident testimony about the 04–05 November 2023 event was collected at a meeting on 05 December 2023. (p4)
A flood incident occurred in Capel–le–Ferne overnight linked to intense local rainfall. The incident took place between 23:00 on 04 November 2023 and 00:45 on 05 November 2023, coinciding with heavy short–duration rainfall and preceded by high antecedent rainfall in the previous days. (p6)
Intense, antecedent rainfall produced large runoff from surrounding agricultural land which flowed southeast into the village. Water entered the village and travelled along the street corridor (past the Baptist Church and onto Capel Street), overwhelming local drainage and causing surface water flooding within the village. (p7)
Surface water runoff from saturated agricultural fields accumulated in a local topographic depression between the junction of Capel Street and Lancaster Avenue and the junction of Lancaster Avenue and Elizabeth Drive. The highway drainage system was overwhelmed and obstructed, resulting in flooding of the carriageway and at least six properties (five of which flooded internally). KCC Highways attended the incident and carried out inspection and jetting of road gullies and mainlines on 09 November 2023. Kent Fire Rescue Service attended with vehicles and pumped floodwater out of affected properties and gardens into the highway drainage network. (p11)
Flooding affected the highway network in Capel–le–Ferne: a high–confidence flood extent is shown on Capel Street with flooding concentrated at the intersection with Lancaster Avenue, and a lower–confidence extent extends into Lancaster Avenue and adjacent residential streets. The map notes the estimated extent covers roads only and does not mark properties as flooded. [IMG] (p13)","approximately six properties affected; five properties flooded internally (p2)
6 properties; 5 properties internally flooded; 3 fire engines/vehicles (p11)",GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04.html,GB-KEN_3_2023-11-04,Capel-Le-Ferne flooding 04 Nov 2023 - 05 Nov 2023,Flood Investigation Report: Capel-Le-Ferne,063/Kent/Capel-le-Ferne-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-HRT,3,2014-02-07,51.8652558,-0.1934815,"<div id=""html_8c42eeed0628a36fa83d865dd5bb5dca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Orchard Way, Knebworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in Orchard Way suffered internal flooding following the flood event on 7 February 2014. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal and external areas of the property were flooded following surface water / highway drainage failure; a resident reported a surface water drain was constructed through a neighbour's garden after the 1987 flood to try to alleviate the issue. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow entered rear gardens, entered properties through rear doors, and also moved around the sides of properties into front gardens and onto the road, flowing downslope toward garages and the rear gardens of Broom Grove properties. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff was directed to the field opposite the rear gardens of Orchard Way; the estate highway drainage and road gullies were overwhelmed and unable to cope, causing local flooding in the Orchard Way area. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage underperformance and blocked gullies were identified as factors in flooding on Orchard Way during the investigation into the 7 February 2014 event; recommendations included CCTV survey of the drainage and increased gully cleaning to restore performance and reduce future surface water flooding of the highway and nearby properties. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff affected Orchard Way; highway drainage works included cleaning gullies and the connection between Orchard Way and Gypsy Lane to manage runoff. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff reached Orchard Way following Gypsy Lane, causing surface&ndash;water flooding of the street. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Orchard Way, Knebworth",England,"Residential properties in Orchard Way suffered internal flooding following the flood event on 7 February 2014. (p7)
Internal and external areas of the property were flooded following surface water / highway drainage failure; a resident reported a surface water drain was constructed through a neighbour's garden after the 1987 flood to try to alleviate the issue. [MUL] (p9)
Overland flow entered rear gardens, entered properties through rear doors, and also moved around the sides of properties into front gardens and onto the road, flowing downslope toward garages and the rear gardens of Broom Grove properties. (p10)
Surface water runoff was directed to the field opposite the rear gardens of Orchard Way; the estate highway drainage and road gullies were overwhelmed and unable to cope, causing local flooding in the Orchard Way area. (p11)
Highway drainage underperformance and blocked gullies were identified as factors in flooding on Orchard Way during the investigation into the 7 February 2014 event; recommendations included CCTV survey of the drainage and increased gully cleaning to restore performance and reduce future surface water flooding of the highway and nearby properties. [TAB] (p16)
Surface water runoff affected Orchard Way; highway drainage works included cleaning gullies and the connection between Orchard Way and Gypsy Lane to manage runoff. (p18)
Surface water runoff reached Orchard Way following Gypsy Lane, causing surface–water flooding of the street. (p19)",,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07,Knebworth flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report Knebworth, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/hcc-flood-investigation-s19knebworth.pdf
GB-HRT,3,2014-02-07,51.8655265,-0.192047,"<div id=""html_1e5e0c49fd0ca1a64f1fae35eee76bfa"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Broom Grove, Knebworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in Broom Grove suffered internal flooding following the flood event on 7 February 2014. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads and external areas of properties were flooded during historical events and multiple 2014 events (roads and external property areas affected). [TAB,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow continued into rear gardens, entered properties via rear doors, flowed out of front doors into front gardens and the road, and ponded in the road at the entrance to the walkway to Gibbons Way. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Broom Grove is the lowest point in the catchment so runoff accumulated there; its road gullies/soakaways and highway drainage were overwhelmed and unable to cope, leading to flooding at Broom Grove. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies and drainage in Broom Grove were implicated in the flooding; recommendations include CCTV survey, removal of blockages and increasing gully&ndash;cleaning frequency (currently an 18&ndash;month cycle) to reduce flood risk after the event. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff entered Broom Grove; recommended drainage actions include surveying/cleaning gullies and potentially increasing gully cleaning frequency to reduce future surface water flooding. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff reached Broom Grove (subsequent to Gypsy Lane), causing surface&ndash;water flooding of the street. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Broom Grove, Knebworth",England,"Residential properties in Broom Grove suffered internal flooding following the flood event on 7 February 2014. (p7)
Roads and external areas of properties were flooded during historical events and multiple 2014 events (roads and external property areas affected). [TAB,MUL] (p9)
Overland flow continued into rear gardens, entered properties via rear doors, flowed out of front doors into front gardens and the road, and ponded in the road at the entrance to the walkway to Gibbons Way. (p10)
Broom Grove is the lowest point in the catchment so runoff accumulated there; its road gullies/soakaways and highway drainage were overwhelmed and unable to cope, leading to flooding at Broom Grove. (p11)
Highway gullies and drainage in Broom Grove were implicated in the flooding; recommendations include CCTV survey, removal of blockages and increasing gully–cleaning frequency (currently an 18–month cycle) to reduce flood risk after the event. [TAB] (p16)
Surface water runoff entered Broom Grove; recommended drainage actions include surveying/cleaning gullies and potentially increasing gully cleaning frequency to reduce future surface water flooding. (p18)
Surface water runoff reached Broom Grove (subsequent to Gypsy Lane), causing surface–water flooding of the street. (p19)",,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07,Knebworth flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report Knebworth, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/hcc-flood-investigation-s19knebworth.pdf
GB-HRT,3,2014-02-07,51.8687612,-0.2145336,"<div id=""html_0ad098b391ff27f0d4a5c353e5a7bb49"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Park Lane, Knebworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map and image description show the flood&ndash;affected area concentrated in southwest Knebworth adjacent to Park Lane and the railway; residential streets and nearby public areas around Park Lane were within the blue&ndash;shaded/flood&ndash;affected zone. [IMG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Park Lane, Knebworth",England,Map and image description show the flood–affected area concentrated in southwest Knebworth adjacent to Park Lane and the railway; residential streets and nearby public areas around Park Lane were within the blue–shaded/flood–affected zone. [IMG] (p8),,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07,Knebworth flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report Knebworth, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/hcc-flood-investigation-s19knebworth.pdf
GB-HRT,3,2014-02-07,51.8611504,-0.1953505,"<div id=""html_1ce08b461e821b020f73893cac25219a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gipsy Lane, Knebworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gypsy Lane is identified on the map bounding box near the blue&ndash;shaded flood area in southwest Knebworth; the map indicates streets in this vicinity were affected by flooding during the event. [IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from adjacent farmland flooded the lane; water then flowed from the lane into rear gardens of Orchard Way and entered properties through rear doors. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff was funnelled toward the field adjacent to Gypsy Lane; highway drainage (including the single road gully at the rear of Orchard Way) was overwhelmed and unable to cope with the volume of water, contributing to local flooding. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage in Gypsy Lane was implicated in the flooding response; recommendation to CCTV&ndash;survey the drainage and remove blockages to restore performance following the flood event. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff entered Gypsy Lane during the event; drainage&ndash;related remediation carried out including cleaning soakaways and gullies, clearing the connection to Orchard Way, and re&ndash;digging ditches. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff entered Gypsy Lane during the event, causing surface&ndash;water flooding of the street and contributing runoff downstream. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 road gully (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Gipsy Lane, Knebworth",England,"Gypsy Lane is identified on the map bounding box near the blue–shaded flood area in southwest Knebworth; the map indicates streets in this vicinity were affected by flooding during the event. [IMG] (p8)
Surface water runoff from adjacent farmland flooded the lane; water then flowed from the lane into rear gardens of Orchard Way and entered properties through rear doors. (p10)
Runoff was funnelled toward the field adjacent to Gypsy Lane; highway drainage (including the single road gully at the rear of Orchard Way) was overwhelmed and unable to cope with the volume of water, contributing to local flooding. (p11)
Highway drainage in Gypsy Lane was implicated in the flooding response; recommendation to CCTV–survey the drainage and remove blockages to restore performance following the flood event. [TAB] (p16)
Surface water runoff entered Gypsy Lane during the event; drainage–related remediation carried out including cleaning soakaways and gullies, clearing the connection to Orchard Way, and re–digging ditches. (p18)
Surface water runoff entered Gypsy Lane during the event, causing surface–water flooding of the street and contributing runoff downstream. (p19)",1 road gully (p11),GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07,Knebworth flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report Knebworth, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/hcc-flood-investigation-s19knebworth.pdf
GB-HRT,3,2014-02-07,51.8648596,-0.1902436,"<div id=""html_8c0a424de3b752d64a9a02207a254c86"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gibbons Way, Knebworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road ponding occurred at the entrance to the walkway to Gibbons Way as a result of the overland flow from surrounding streets and gardens; the report notes ponding at this location (it is not explicit that properties on Gibbons Way were internally flooded). (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Gibbons Way, Knebworth",England,Road ponding occurred at the entrance to the walkway to Gibbons Way as a result of the overland flow from surrounding streets and gardens; the report notes ponding at this location (it is not explicit that properties on Gibbons Way were internally flooded). (p10),,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07,Knebworth flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report Knebworth, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/hcc-flood-investigation-s19knebworth.pdf
GB-HRT,3,2014-02-07,51.8658893,-0.1844945,"<div id=""html_741f54677168abc24813491394d0368b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Knebworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rural field inundated with standing water covering a significant area and partially submerging a footpath; a residential street was flooded across its full width with water extending into driveways and yards, surrounding parked cars and disrupting access to properties. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from saturated farmland overflowed onto an adjacent road and subsequently flooded properties. Eighteen properties were flooded internally and two properties suffered external flooding; floodwater affected properties in a further two streets. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The western part of Knebworth (site east of Gypsy Lane) was affected by flooding on 7 February 2014, which resulted in internal flooding of residential properties in Orchard Way and Broom Grove. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Southwestern part of Knebworth (near Park Lane and the railway line) was flooded. The mapped flood&ndash;affected area (red circle and blue shading) covers land west of the main settlement including areas adjacent to the Recreation Ground and nearby residential/public areas. [IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Knebworth (notably Orchard Way and Broom Grove) experienced surface water flooding affecting roads and external property areas and causing internal property flooding on 7 February 2014. [TAB,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on saturated ground produced surface water runoff and overland flow across the catchment that flooded local streets and residential properties in the Knebworth area (flow route described from Gypsy Lane into Orchard Way and Broom Grove). (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Knebworth experienced a flooding event that prompted a Section 19 flood investigation by the Lead Local Flood Authority. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Knebworth experienced internal flooding during the 2013&ndash;14 period and the area was the subject of a Section 19 investigation following the flood event on 7 February 2014. (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 properties; 2 properties; 2 streets (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 properties flooded; 18 properties flooded internally (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Knebworth,England,"Rural field inundated with standing water covering a significant area and partially submerging a footpath; a residential street was flooded across its full width with water extending into driveways and yards, surrounding parked cars and disrupting access to properties. (p1)
Surface water runoff from saturated farmland overflowed onto an adjacent road and subsequently flooded properties. Eighteen properties were flooded internally and two properties suffered external flooding; floodwater affected properties in a further two streets. (p5)
The western part of Knebworth (site east of Gypsy Lane) was affected by flooding on 7 February 2014, which resulted in internal flooding of residential properties in Orchard Way and Broom Grove. (p7)
Southwestern part of Knebworth (near Park Lane and the railway line) was flooded. The mapped flood–affected area (red circle and blue shading) covers land west of the main settlement including areas adjacent to the Recreation Ground and nearby residential/public areas. [IMG] (p8)
Parts of Knebworth (notably Orchard Way and Broom Grove) experienced surface water flooding affecting roads and external property areas and causing internal property flooding on 7 February 2014. [TAB,MUL] (p9)
Heavy rainfall on saturated ground produced surface water runoff and overland flow across the catchment that flooded local streets and residential properties in the Knebworth area (flow route described from Gypsy Lane into Orchard Way and Broom Grove). (p10)
Knebworth experienced a flooding event that prompted a Section 19 flood investigation by the Lead Local Flood Authority. (p13)
Properties in Knebworth experienced internal flooding during the 2013–14 period and the area was the subject of a Section 19 investigation following the flood event on 7 February 2014. (p16)","18 properties; 2 properties; 2 streets (p5)
20 properties flooded; 18 properties flooded internally (p10)",GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07.html,GB-HRT_3_2014-02-07,Knebworth flooding 07 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report Knebworth, Hertfordshire",056/Hertfordshire/hcc-flood-investigation-s19knebworth.pdf
GB-KEN,15,2020-08-15,51.3390036,0.7592697,"<div id=""html_a8d096d38ea0e45c7b36219b94640d9a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lansdown Road, Snipeshill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties on Lansdown Road. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported on Lansdown(e) Road during the event; KFRS were sent but on arrival the water had subsided. Soakaways in the area became full during the intense rainfall and contributed to runoff and local flooding. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage was overwhelmed during the intense rainfall; water on the highway drained away once the rainfall ceased, indicating temporary highway inundation during the event. (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Lansdown Road, Snipeshill",England,"Internal flooding of residential properties on Lansdown Road. [TAB] (p2)
Flooding was reported on Lansdown(e) Road during the event; KFRS were sent but on arrival the water had subsided. Soakaways in the area became full during the intense rainfall and contributed to runoff and local flooding. [PAG] (p5)
Highway drainage was overwhelmed during the intense rainfall; water on the highway drained away once the rainfall ceased, indicating temporary highway inundation during the event. (p6)",4 properties [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_15_2020-08-15.html,GB-KEN_15_2020-08-15,Sittingbourne flooding 15 Aug 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Sittingbourne,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Sittingbourne.pdf
GB-KEN,15,2020-08-15,51.3393206,0.7566343,"<div id=""html_5142e2d6c733671ad1cf788025d3cb0e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coombe Drive, Sittingbourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Both internal and external flooding affected multiple residential properties; additional properties experienced external&ndash;only flooding. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage; floodwater entered properties. Kent Fire and Rescue Service pumped water from two internally flooded properties. A tanker was deployed to control flood water on the highway and remained on site until midnight. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from an intense storm overwhelmed highway drainage during the event; water on the highway drained away once rainfall ceased. Replacement sandbags and floodsaxs were provided to residents following the event, indicating property&ndash;level inundation or threat to properties. (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties; 2 properties [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [PAG] (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Coombe Drive, Sittingbourne",England,"Both internal and external flooding affected multiple residential properties; additional properties experienced external–only flooding. [TAB] (p2)
