GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5860159,-2.5225711,"<div id=""html_97f09c6a6fe1b4cc1c602c64ba591153"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Claypool Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Claypool Primary School is shown on the map within the mapped surface&ndash;water flood zones and therefore is identified as lying in an area that experienced surface water flooding during the intense rainfall and thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Claypool Primary School,England,Claypool Primary School is shown on the map within the mapped surface–water flood zones and therefore is identified as lying in an area that experienced surface water flooding during the intense rainfall and thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.580914563652584,-2.5116718446168202,"<div id=""html_4745b53bebac98c8865debf0e41d0eda"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Meadow Hill Tennis Club</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Meadow Hill Tennis Club is identified on the map within or adjacent to medium/low surface&ndash;water flood zones and is therefore within the area that experienced surface water flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Meadow Hill Tennis Club,England,Meadow Hill Tennis Club is identified on the map within or adjacent to medium/low surface–water flood zones and is therefore within the area that experienced surface water flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5850503,-2.5295363455365685,"<div id=""html_278e321ffc716de30cd68d71ddbcfbad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Joseph's RC High School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Joseph's RC Secondary School experienced major internal flooding during the thunderstorm. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Joseph&#8217;s Roman Catholic High School (labelled on the map) lies within or close to medium&ndash;to&ndash;high surface&ndash;water flood zones and is identified as being within the area that experienced flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Joseph&#8217;s (shown on the map as St Joseph&#8217;s Roman Catholic High School) lies within the catchment where observed overland flow routes and predicted surface water flood zones occur. The catchment produced the largest volume of flood water in the storm on 16th May 2021, indicating the school&#8217;s location was within the area subject to overland flows and surface water flood risk. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property with major internal flooding (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,St Joseph's RC High School,England,"St Joseph's RC Secondary School experienced major internal flooding during the thunderstorm. (p10)
St Joseph’s Roman Catholic High School (labelled on the map) lies within or close to medium–to–high surface–water flood zones and is identified as being within the area that experienced flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17)
St Joseph’s (shown on the map as St Joseph’s Roman Catholic High School) lies within the catchment where observed overland flow routes and predicted surface water flood zones occur. The catchment produced the largest volume of flood water in the storm on 16th May 2021, indicating the school’s location was within the area subject to overland flows and surface water flood risk. [IMG] (p41)",1 commercial property with major internal flooding (p10),GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5905733,-2.535346578721808,"<div id=""html_8d1fbdf8ef7ab2f2ea67db1ce2510d55"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Mary's Roman Catholic Primary</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>School building suffered major internal flooding during the thunderstorm, contributing to commercial properties listed as having major internal flood damage. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The school (St Mary&#8217;s Roman Catholic Primary School, shown on the map) lies within medium/high surface&ndash;water risk zones and was within the area that experienced intense rainfall and associated surface&ndash;water flooding on 16 May 2021, indicating potential flooding exposure to the site and access routes. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The primary school experienced internal flooding when overland flows crossed Chorley New Road and moved across the school site, causing flooding to the building. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Mary's RC Primary School was flooded as a result of overland flow when the debris&ndash;laden stream blinded the culvert debris screen, causing water to overtop and flow into the school site. (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property with major internal flooding (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 school building (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,St Mary's Roman Catholic Primary,England,"School building suffered major internal flooding during the thunderstorm, contributing to commercial properties listed as having major internal flood damage. (p10)
The school (St Mary’s Roman Catholic Primary School, shown on the map) lies within medium/high surface–water risk zones and was within the area that experienced intense rainfall and associated surface–water flooding on 16 May 2021, indicating potential flooding exposure to the site and access routes. [IMG] (p17)
The primary school experienced internal flooding when overland flows crossed Chorley New Road and moved across the school site, causing flooding to the building. (p32)
St Mary's RC Primary School was flooded as a result of overland flow when the debris–laden stream blinded the culvert debris screen, causing water to overtop and flow into the school site. (p33)","1 commercial property with major internal flooding (p10)
1 school building (p32)",GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.592697955554804,-2.537301290648487,"<div id=""html_1549784228230e77233705c6d078d497"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Horwich Golf Club</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water / overland flows were observed across the approximately 10&ndash;hectare catchment, draining to a privately owned culvert inlet at the rear of St John&#8217;s Church and then conveyed in a culvert southwest under private property, Chorley New Road and the Horwich Loco Works site to connect to the Nellies Clough watercourse. [IMG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Horwich Golf Course west catchment produced significant overland flows during the event; runoff issued from the golf course onto Victoria Road when culvert capacity was exceeded, contributing to downstream internal flooding. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from the golf course catchment and an overwhelmed culvert inlet (at the rear of St Johns Church on the course) contributed to increased overland flow and downstream internal flooding. [PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overland flows were observed originating within the Horwich Golf Club East catchment; runoff drained to a privately owned culvert inlet at the rear of Middlewich Close and entered a culverted watercourse that conveys flow southwest toward Nellies Clough. [IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant water flows were observed issuing from the Golf Course site onto neighbouring land, initiating overland flow paths that entered the College Drive development, affected the rear of properties on Middlewich Close, reached Victoria Road and contributed to downstream highway damage (Abernethey Street) and property flooding at Chorley New Road. [IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage infrastructure on their land (including culvert inlets) must be adequately maintained and constructed to current best practice so that drainage is operational and to reduce the risk of local flooding. (p65)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 commercial premises; 1 school building [IMG,PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 commercial premises (including 1 school building) [PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties; 3 commercial premises; 1 school building [IMG] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Horwich Golf Club,England,"Surface water / overland flows were observed across the approximately 10–hectare catchment, draining to a privately owned culvert inlet at the rear of St John’s Church and then conveyed in a culvert southwest under private property, Chorley New Road and the Horwich Loco Works site to connect to the Nellies Clough watercourse. [IMG] (p24)
The Horwich Golf Course west catchment produced significant overland flows during the event; runoff issued from the golf course onto Victoria Road when culvert capacity was exceeded, contributing to downstream internal flooding. [IMG] (p25)
Overland flow from the golf course catchment and an overwhelmed culvert inlet (at the rear of St Johns Church on the course) contributed to increased overland flow and downstream internal flooding. [PAG] (p26)
Surface water overland flows were observed originating within the Horwich Golf Club East catchment; runoff drained to a privately owned culvert inlet at the rear of Middlewich Close and entered a culverted watercourse that conveys flow southwest toward Nellies Clough. [IMG] (p27)
Significant water flows were observed issuing from the Golf Course site onto neighbouring land, initiating overland flow paths that entered the College Drive development, affected the rear of properties on Middlewich Close, reached Victoria Road and contributed to downstream highway damage (Abernethey Street) and property flooding at Chorley New Road. [IMG] (p28)
Drainage infrastructure on their land (including culvert inlets) must be adequately maintained and constructed to current best practice so that drainage is operational and to reduce the risk of local flooding. (p65)","12 commercial premises; 1 school building [IMG,PAG] (p25)
12 commercial premises (including 1 school building) [PAG] (p26)
12 properties; 3 commercial premises; 1 school building [IMG] (p28)",GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.59345953397621,-2.547648169747898,"<div id=""html_6d81402b309e40280cb2589f5aea5453"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Loco Works</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted watercourse that drains the Horwich Golf Course West catchment runs southwest and connects to Nellies Clough within the Loco Works / industrial estate. The published plan shows predicted surface water flooding and the route of observed overland flows reaching the Loco Works / Horwich Loco Industrial Estate, indicating the estate was on the flow route and affected by surface water/river&ndash;related flooding during the event. [IMG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A partial blockage of a culvert was identified on land at the former Horwich Locomotive works site caused by debris from the storm; the Environment Agency undertook works to partially relieve this blockage. (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Loco Works,England,"The culverted watercourse that drains the Horwich Golf Course West catchment runs southwest and connects to Nellies Clough within the Loco Works / industrial estate. The published plan shows predicted surface water flooding and the route of observed overland flows reaching the Loco Works / Horwich Loco Industrial Estate, indicating the estate was on the flow route and affected by surface water/river–related flooding during the event. [IMG] (p24)
A partial blockage of a culvert was identified on land at the former Horwich Locomotive works site caused by debris from the storm; the Environment Agency undertook works to partially relieve this blockage. (p58)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.590114650000004,-2.536847989947367,"<div id=""html_fffdb14fb1af3578fe5ee9b422ff4476"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chorley New Road Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>School received major internal flooding during the intense thunderstorm. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive flooding to the ground floor caused considerable damage. A significant component was backflow from the combined sewer in Chorley New Road into the school via toilets; overland flow on the road outside also converged on the school and may have entered via doorways. No anti&ndash;flood valve was fitted to the foul connection between the school and the main sewer. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Backflow from the combined foul&ndash;and&ndash;surface sewer rose through the school&#8217;s foul connection and caused internal flooding. Overland flow on the road outside converged near the school and may have entered via doorways, contributing to the flooding. No anti&ndash;flood valve was fitted on the school&#8217;s foul connection. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer flows contributed to flooding at Chorley New Road Primary School and Medway Drive as intense surface water overland flows overwhelmed drainage; the report recommends drainage improvements at the junction of Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews to reduce highway flooding and prevent overflows reaching downstream properties. (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage on Chorley New Road outside the school was identified as having insufficient capacity during the event, creating overland/surface water flows that interacted with the sewer network and exposed the school to the risk of sewer flooding. The school was advised to install an anti&ndash;flood valve on the foul sewer connection to reduce this sewer flooding risk. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property with major internal flooding (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Chorley New Road Primary School,England,"School received major internal flooding during the intense thunderstorm. (p10)
Extensive flooding to the ground floor caused considerable damage. A significant component was backflow from the combined sewer in Chorley New Road into the school via toilets; overland flow on the road outside also converged on the school and may have entered via doorways. No anti–flood valve was fitted to the foul connection between the school and the main sewer. (p29)
Backflow from the combined foul–and–surface sewer rose through the school’s foul connection and caused internal flooding. Overland flow on the road outside converged near the school and may have entered via doorways, contributing to the flooding. No anti–flood valve was fitted on the school’s foul connection. (p30)
Sewer flows contributed to flooding at Chorley New Road Primary School and Medway Drive as intense surface water overland flows overwhelmed drainage; the report recommends drainage improvements at the junction of Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews to reduce highway flooding and prevent overflows reaching downstream properties. (p53)
Highway drainage on Chorley New Road outside the school was identified as having insufficient capacity during the event, creating overland/surface water flows that interacted with the sewer network and exposed the school to the risk of sewer flooding. The school was advised to install an anti–flood valve on the foul sewer connection to reduce this sewer flooding risk. [TAB] (p61)",1 commercial property with major internal flooding (p10),GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5931318,-2.5377601,"<div id=""html_9b776e8972f5f3c72e282b8f26cc6a14"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St John's Methodist Church</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert inlet at the rear of St Johns Church (on Horwich Golf Course) was overwhelmed and blinded by soil and silt, increasing overland flow; inlet condition and design were found to be poor after inspection. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert inlet at the rear of the church was overwhelmed by water and debris; soil and silt blinded the inlet causing increased overland flow. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed from the church grounds down the entrance steps onto the pavement and road, contributing to local inundation at the church entrance and surrounding street. [IMG] (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,St John's Methodist Church,England,"The culvert inlet at the rear of St Johns Church (on Horwich Golf Course) was overwhelmed and blinded by soil and silt, increasing overland flow; inlet condition and design were found to be poor after inspection. [PAG] (p25)
The culvert inlet at the rear of the church was overwhelmed by water and debris; soil and silt blinded the inlet causing increased overland flow. (p26)
Floodwater flowed from the church grounds down the entrance steps onto the pavement and road, contributing to local inundation at the church entrance and surrounding street. [IMG] (p78)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5977391,-2.5247284,"<div id=""html_299d624d9b684d4e31e89962935fa2b4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ridgmont Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland surface water flow from the Ridgmont Park catchment was routed eastward through the area during the event; the overland flow path reached Ridgmont Park and contributed to downstream flooding impacts observed on adjacent streets and properties. [IMG] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Ridgmont Park,England,Overland surface water flow from the Ridgmont Park catchment was routed eastward through the area during the event; the overland flow path reached Ridgmont Park and contributed to downstream flooding impacts observed on adjacent streets and properties. [IMG] (p23),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.59842486883751,-2.5303324694124103,"<div id=""html_f7d93dbe6d386ea382d4cc68e92917f5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ridgmont Cemetery</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The cemetery (predominantly greenfield) generated significant runoff during the event; overland flow travelled eastward through the cemetery toward Ridgmont Park and onto Fleet Street/Chorley Old Road, producing downstream internal flooding and garage flooding. [IMG] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Ridgmont Cemetery,England,"The cemetery (predominantly greenfield) generated significant runoff during the event; overland flow travelled eastward through the cemetery toward Ridgmont Park and onto Fleet Street/Chorley Old Road, producing downstream internal flooding and garage flooding. [IMG] (p23)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5845745,-2.522381050329433,"<div id=""html_529f11e1183641ae582ba58310743adf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Beehive and Wacky Warehouse</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert conveying runoff continues in a south&ndash;west direction under the Beehive Public House; during the storm the culvert formed part of the flood conveyance route and therefore flood flows passed beneath/adjacent to this building. (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Beehive and Wacky Warehouse,England,The culvert conveying runoff continues in a south–west direction under the Beehive Public House; during the storm the culvert formed part of the flood conveyance route and therefore flood flows passed beneath/adjacent to this building. (p41),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5848139,-2.5258552,"<div id=""html_c33a1ebcaac3b3023c11e5514cd4465a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Paragon Business Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The map and accompanying text indicate surface water flood risk and observed overland flow routes extend into business districts within the Moor Platt Cough catchment, including the area around Paragon Business Park. The catchment produced the greatest volume of flood water during the storm on 16th May 2021, so Paragon Business Park was within the area subject to observed overland flows and predicted 1&ndash;in&ndash;100&ndash;year surface water flood extent. [IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Paragon Business Park,England,"The map and accompanying text indicate surface water flood risk and observed overland flow routes extend into business districts within the Moor Platt Cough catchment, including the area around Paragon Business Park. The catchment produced the greatest volume of flood water during the storm on 16th May 2021, so Paragon Business Park was within the area subject to observed overland flows and predicted 1–in–100–year surface water flood extent. [IMG] (p41)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,54.7023545,-3.2765753,"<div id=""html_c8edb84406062443fe2e451d2dd61a79"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>UK</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A period of substantial rainfall in early May left soils saturated and a sudden thunderstorm downpour on 16th May 2021 produced rapid surface&ndash;water/flash flooding across parts of the UK (particularly north and west). No flood warnings were issued in advance. The Lead Local Flood Authority (LLFA) carried out site visits after the incident and members of the public supplied photographic evidence and testimonies of the storm impacts. [PAG] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,UK,England,A period of substantial rainfall in early May left soils saturated and a sudden thunderstorm downpour on 16th May 2021 produced rapid surface–water/flash flooding across parts of the UK (particularly north and west). No flood warnings were issued in advance. The Lead Local Flood Authority (LLFA) carried out site visits after the incident and members of the public supplied photographic evidence and testimonies of the storm impacts. [PAG] (p21),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5974322,-2.5481621,"<div id=""html_ba202d2665c93d065a85dc59408fbad2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pearl Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The downstream culvert discharges to a tributary of Pearl Brook after passing under the Horwich Loco Works Site; therefore runoff from the Nellies Clough catchment was routed into the Pearl Brook system, increasing flows entering that watercourse. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The brook was restricted by a small bridge (walkway) which acted as a source of flooding to adjacent streets (Mason Street and Vale Avenue) during the event. (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Pearl Brook,England,"The downstream culvert discharges to a tributary of Pearl Brook after passing under the Horwich Loco Works Site; therefore runoff from the Nellies Clough catchment was routed into the Pearl Brook system, increasing flows entering that watercourse. (p31)
The brook was restricted by a small bridge (walkway) which acted as a source of flooding to adjacent streets (Mason Street and Vale Avenue) during the event. (p54)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5723565,-2.4936083,"<div id=""html_20ff0784ab1567944ed32f55fa02fa61"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bessy Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding occurred lower in the catchment at Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close. Flooding at this location has happened on multiple occasions and in this event was caused when the culvert capacity was overwhelmed (previous incidents are generally due to debris&ndash;screen blockage at the culvert inlet or under&ndash;capacity of a culvert section). [IMG,PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bessy Brook presented a flood risk at the Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close area; the report recommends developing a project and undertaking works to reduce flood risk from the brook. (p63)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Bessy Brook,England,"Fluvial flooding occurred lower in the catchment at Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close. Flooding at this location has happened on multiple occasions and in this event was caused when the culvert capacity was overwhelmed (previous incidents are generally due to debris–screen blockage at the culvert inlet or under–capacity of a culvert section). [IMG,PAG] (p47)
Bessy Brook presented a flood risk at the Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close area; the report recommends developing a project and undertaking works to reduce flood risk from the brook. (p63)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5882989,-2.4774277,"<div id=""html_ed7e278596857070adcdc7561d667e56"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Doffcocker Lodge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Outflow from the lodge overwhelmed the downstream culvert, producing overland flow from the lodge/catchment that contributed to flooding downstream. [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris accumulated in a manhole in the culvert downstream of Doffcocker Lodge constrained flow through the culvert and may have contributed to downstream internal flooding. Downstream overflows from the lodge resulted in internal flooding to residential properties, commercial property and a B&ndash;class road; the debris was removed after inspection. (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflows from the lodge (large overflows) have the potential to cause flooding to properties immediately downstream; recommendation to improve the downstream culvert capacity to prevent such property flooding. [TAB] (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Doffcocker Lodge,England,"Outflow from the lodge overwhelmed the downstream culvert, producing overland flow from the lodge/catchment that contributed to flooding downstream. [IMG,PAG] (p49)
Debris accumulated in a manhole in the culvert downstream of Doffcocker Lodge constrained flow through the culvert and may have contributed to downstream internal flooding. Downstream overflows from the lodge resulted in internal flooding to residential properties, commercial property and a B–class road; the debris was removed after inspection. (p50)
Overflows from the lodge (large overflows) have the potential to cause flooding to properties immediately downstream; recommendation to improve the downstream culvert capacity to prevent such property flooding. [TAB] (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5804738,-2.5496335,"<div id=""html_600d9d3c586913df979ec37954be1a40"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Middle Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert discharges to Middle Brook near Horwich Parkway Train Station; during the storm the catchment produced a very large volume of runoff that was conveyed to Middle Brook, increasing flows at the brook's inlet and contributing to downstream flood risk. (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Middle Brook,England,"The culvert discharges to Middle Brook near Horwich Parkway Train Station; during the storm the catchment produced a very large volume of runoff that was conveyed to Middle Brook, increasing flows at the brook's inlet and contributing to downstream flood risk. (p41)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5782863,-2.4300367,"<div id=""html_85d34b7e3e4e372cbdef3b13b520789b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bolton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Bolton lying within the red ellipse received the most intense rainfall on 16 May 2021, leading to surface&ndash;water/flash flooding evidenced by observed flood damage, debris fields and public photographs/videos; the plan shows a corridor of intense rainfall affecting areas between Horwich and Bolton. [PAG,IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Doffcocker Lodge catchment within Bolton produced outflow that overwhelmed a culvert, causing downstream overland flow and internal flooding to residential and commercial properties and to a B&ndash;class road in the Doffcocker area. [IMG] (p49)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Bolton,England,"Parts of Bolton lying within the red ellipse received the most intense rainfall on 16 May 2021, leading to surface–water/flash flooding evidenced by observed flood damage, debris fields and public photographs/videos; the plan shows a corridor of intense rainfall affecting areas between Horwich and Bolton. [PAG,IMG] (p18)
The Doffcocker Lodge catchment within Bolton produced outflow that overwhelmed a culvert, causing downstream overland flow and internal flooding to residential and commercial properties and to a B–class road in the Doffcocker area. [IMG] (p49)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5841068,-2.522870940228249,"<div id=""html_f1918683b44a7c3056fc35216b4a5592"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Beehive Roundabout</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Beehive area is shown adjacent to medium&ndash;to&ndash;high surface&ndash;water flood zones on the map and is therefore identified as being within the area that experienced surface water flooding during the intense rainfall and thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Beehive roundabout area was identified in relation to highway drainage issues where overland flows contributed to highway flooding; investigators recommended examining drainage around the roundabout to reduce flood risk. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Beehive roundabout area was identified as a location where surface water overland flows caused highway flooding issues; recommendations include examining drainage on the approach between Ainsworth Avenue and the roundabout to reduce flood risk. [TAB] (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Beehive Roundabout,England,"The Beehive area is shown adjacent to medium–to–high surface–water flood zones on the map and is therefore identified as being within the area that experienced surface water flooding during the intense rainfall and thunderstorms on 16th May 2021. [IMG] (p17)
The Beehive roundabout area was identified in relation to highway drainage issues where overland flows contributed to highway flooding; investigators recommended examining drainage around the roundabout to reduce flood risk. (p39)
The Beehive roundabout area was identified as a location where surface water overland flows caused highway flooding issues; recommendations include examining drainage on the approach between Ainsworth Avenue and the roundabout to reduce flood risk. [TAB] (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5798427,-2.5308559,"<div id=""html_d0a9f8f1b949ee3848ac3ad2a7c92b5a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>de Havilland Way, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road closed for several hours due to flooding. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>De Havilland Way (at its junction with Mansell Way) was flooded to around 0.75 metres deep on 16th May 2021, preventing vehicle passage and causing its northbound carriageway to be closed for 24 hours due to deposits of debris. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>De Havilland Way is shown on the map within medium&ndash;to&ndash;high surface&ndash;water flood zones and is identified as being within the area that experienced flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16th May 2021; the road corridor was affected by surface water/flash flooding during that event. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed overland flows and drainage from the Cedar Avenue East catchment discharge to the Moor Platt Clough culvert located in De Havilland Way. The predicted 1&ndash;in&ndash;100&ndash;year surface water flood extent and observed flow routes overlap here, and flooding within the catchment routed to this culvert during the event. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert continues alongside De Havilland Way carrying catchment runoff toward Middle Brook; observed overland flows and predicted surface water flood zones are concentrated along main flow routes that align with this corridor, indicating De Havilland Way was affected by surface water runoff and conveyance issues. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow of water caused flooding at the De Havilland Way (A6027) and Mansell Way junction; the road was closed to north&ndash;bound traffic. (p42)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.75 metres deep; northbound closure for 24 hours (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"de Havilland Way, Horwich",England,"Road closed for several hours due to flooding. (p3)
De Havilland Way (at its junction with Mansell Way) was flooded to around 0.75 metres deep on 16th May 2021, preventing vehicle passage and causing its northbound carriageway to be closed for 24 hours due to deposits of debris. (p10)
De Havilland Way is shown on the map within medium–to–high surface–water flood zones and is identified as being within the area that experienced flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16th May 2021; the road corridor was affected by surface water/flash flooding during that event. (p17)
Observed overland flows and drainage from the Cedar Avenue East catchment discharge to the Moor Platt Clough culvert located in De Havilland Way. The predicted 1–in–100–year surface water flood extent and observed flow routes overlap here, and flooding within the catchment routed to this culvert during the event. (p37)
The culvert continues alongside De Havilland Way carrying catchment runoff toward Middle Brook; observed overland flows and predicted surface water flood zones are concentrated along main flow routes that align with this corridor, indicating De Havilland Way was affected by surface water runoff and conveyance issues. (p41)
Flow of water caused flooding at the De Havilland Way (A6027) and Mansell Way junction; the road was closed to north–bound traffic. (p42)",0.75 metres deep; northbound closure for 24 hours (p10),GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5739074,-2.4953746,"<div id=""html_8a2781d324b4225fbbec2381749ef3f6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sudbury Drive, Lostock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from Bessy Brook inundated Sudbury Drive when the culvert inlet/section was overwhelmed; flooding at this location has historically occurred due to debris blockage or under&ndash;capacity culverts, and on 16th May 2021 the culvert capacity was overwhelmed causing flooding of the street and surrounding area. [PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding at the culverted section where the watercourse is culverted, caused when the culvert capacity was overwhelmed; resulted in internal flooding to nearby residential properties. [PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties experienced flooding; Environment Agency officers visited to meet residents, record the impacts at individual properties and collect photographic evidence of flow paths and rack lines. (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with Bessy Brook at Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close placed the Sudbury area at risk during the event; the Environment Agency is advised to develop a business case and undertake works to reduce flood risk at this location. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding / flood risk on Sudbury Drive associated with Bessy Brook; recommended case for works to reduce flood risk and continued project development. (p63)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 residential properties [PAG] (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Sudbury Drive, Lostock",England,"Fluvial flooding from Bessy Brook inundated Sudbury Drive when the culvert inlet/section was overwhelmed; flooding at this location has historically occurred due to debris blockage or under–capacity culverts, and on 16th May 2021 the culvert capacity was overwhelmed causing flooding of the street and surrounding area. [PAG] (p47)
Fluvial (river) flooding at the culverted section where the watercourse is culverted, caused when the culvert capacity was overwhelmed; resulted in internal flooding to nearby residential properties. [PAG] (p48)
Residential properties experienced flooding; Environment Agency officers visited to meet residents, record the impacts at individual properties and collect photographic evidence of flow paths and rack lines. (p58)
Flooding associated with Bessy Brook at Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close placed the Sudbury area at risk during the event; the Environment Agency is advised to develop a business case and undertake works to reduce flood risk at this location. [TAB] (p61)
Flooding / flood risk on Sudbury Drive associated with Bessy Brook; recommended case for works to reduce flood risk and continued project development. (p63)",11 residential properties [PAG] (p48),GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5738503,-2.4952239,"<div id=""html_cea6fdd0f175a4ae5418e76ba1c4e6a0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ashridge Close, Lostock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from Bessy Brook inundated Ashridge Close when the culvert inlet/section was overwhelmed; the location has experienced repeated flooding linked to culvert debris blockage or under&ndash;capacity, and on 16th May 2021 the culvert capacity was overwhelmed causing flooding at this location. [PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding at the culverted section adjacent to Ashridge Close where the culvert capacity was overwhelmed; contributed to internal flooding of nearby residential properties. [PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties experienced flooding; Environment Agency officers visited to meet residents, record the impacts at individual properties and collect photographic evidence of flow paths and rack lines. (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ashridge Close (at Bessy Brook / Sudbury Drive) was identified as an area requiring works to reduce flood risk following flooding associated with the watercourse during the event. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood risk and/or flooding at Ashridge Close linked to Bessy Brook; the report recommends investigating and undertaking works to reduce that flood risk. (p63)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 residential properties [PAG] (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Ashridge Close, Lostock",England,"Fluvial flooding from Bessy Brook inundated Ashridge Close when the culvert inlet/section was overwhelmed; the location has experienced repeated flooding linked to culvert debris blockage or under–capacity, and on 16th May 2021 the culvert capacity was overwhelmed causing flooding at this location. [PAG] (p47)
Fluvial (river) flooding at the culverted section adjacent to Ashridge Close where the culvert capacity was overwhelmed; contributed to internal flooding of nearby residential properties. [PAG] (p48)
Residential properties experienced flooding; Environment Agency officers visited to meet residents, record the impacts at individual properties and collect photographic evidence of flow paths and rack lines. (p58)
Ashridge Close (at Bessy Brook / Sudbury Drive) was identified as an area requiring works to reduce flood risk following flooding associated with the watercourse during the event. [TAB] (p61)
Flood risk and/or flooding at Ashridge Close linked to Bessy Brook; the report recommends investigating and undertaking works to reduce that flood risk. (p63)",11 residential properties [PAG] (p48),GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.586356366579764,-2.526872037126084,"<div id=""html_17b2f24581113be6df6a026554997a68"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chorley New Road, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Large overland flows through the urban area parallel to the north east of Chorley New Road overwhelmed culverted watercourses and caused flooding to properties. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooded to depths that prevented vehicle passage, causing traffic disruption. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sections of Chorley New Road experienced concentrated surface&ndash;water / flash flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, with high&ndash;risk zones shown on the map and flooding reported on main carriageways and junctions. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from the overwhelmed culvert and upstream catchment pooled on the flatter land at Chorley New Road; downstream of this area the overland flow contributed to internal flooding of commercial and residential properties. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows pooled on the flatter land at Chorley New Road where they merged with other flows, resulting in property flooding downstream. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow reached Chorley New Road and a limited&ndash;capacity combined sewer in Chorley New Road rose during the event, contributing to backflow and flooding impacts in the vicinity (including into the nearby school). (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow passed across Chorley New Road and across the adjacent primary school site, contributing to downstream property flooding. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts from the Cedar Avenue catchments run beneath or along Chorley New Road and the mapped/observed overland flow routes follow road corridors; during the 16th May 2021 event overland flows were routed along/adjacent to this road corridor and conveyed via culverts toward Moor Platt Clough (some uncertainty in the text as flooding on the road is indicated by mapped flow routes rather than an explicit statement). (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from blocked channels and culverts pooled on Chorley New Road and contributed to internal flooding of nearby industrial premises. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from Austins Lane ran onto Chorley New Road and contributed to the flooding of numerous industrial premises along the route. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding occurred in the Chorley New Road area; a post&ndash;event CCTV inspection indicated tree roots and other faults in a section of a privately owned watercourse contributed to the flooding. (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Junction (Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews) identified for drainage improvements to reduce highway flooding and to prevent large overflows that could result in flooding to properties immediately downstream. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage failures at the junction leading to highway flooding; recommendation to make improvements to drainage at the junction to reduce flood risk to the highway and nearby properties. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the entire road surface at the junction, with fast&ndash;moving, turbulent flow making the junction impassable and creating hazardous conditions for vehicles and pedestrians. Buildings and parked vehicles at the junction were partially submerged and access was disrupted. [IMG] (p79)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 0.4 m deep (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 commercial premises; 1 school building; 3 residential properties (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Chorley New Road, Horwich",England,"Large overland flows through the urban area parallel to the north east of Chorley New Road overwhelmed culverted watercourses and caused flooding to properties. (p3)
Road flooded to depths that prevented vehicle passage, causing traffic disruption. (p10)
Sections of Chorley New Road experienced concentrated surface–water / flash flooding during the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, with high–risk zones shown on the map and flooding reported on main carriageways and junctions. [IMG] (p17)
Overland flow from the overwhelmed culvert and upstream catchment pooled on the flatter land at Chorley New Road; downstream of this area the overland flow contributed to internal flooding of commercial and residential properties. (p25)
Flows pooled on the flatter land at Chorley New Road where they merged with other flows, resulting in property flooding downstream. (p28)
Overland flow reached Chorley New Road and a limited–capacity combined sewer in Chorley New Road rose during the event, contributing to backflow and flooding impacts in the vicinity (including into the nearby school). (p29)
Overland flow passed across Chorley New Road and across the adjacent primary school site, contributing to downstream property flooding. (p32)
Culverts from the Cedar Avenue catchments run beneath or along Chorley New Road and the mapped/observed overland flow routes follow road corridors; during the 16th May 2021 event overland flows were routed along/adjacent to this road corridor and conveyed via culverts toward Moor Platt Clough (some uncertainty in the text as flooding on the road is indicated by mapped flow routes rather than an explicit statement). (p37)
Overland flow from blocked channels and culverts pooled on Chorley New Road and contributed to internal flooding of nearby industrial premises. (p38)
Overland flow from Austins Lane ran onto Chorley New Road and contributed to the flooding of numerous industrial premises along the route. (p42)
External flooding occurred in the Chorley New Road area; a post–event CCTV inspection indicated tree roots and other faults in a section of a privately owned watercourse contributed to the flooding. (p48)
Junction (Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews) identified for drainage improvements to reduce highway flooding and to prevent large overflows that could result in flooding to properties immediately downstream. (p52)
Drainage failures at the junction leading to highway flooding; recommendation to make improvements to drainage at the junction to reduce flood risk to the highway and nearby properties. [TAB] (p61)
Floodwater covered the entire road surface at the junction, with fast–moving, turbulent flow making the junction impassable and creating hazardous conditions for vehicles and pedestrians. Buildings and parked vehicles at the junction were partially submerged and access was disrupted. [IMG] (p79)","around 0.4 m deep (p10)
12 commercial premises; 1 school building; 3 residential properties (p25)",GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5882995,-2.5242295,"<div id=""html_b162bec0eaf2ba551c05e7686ea9183b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Poplar Avenue, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties at these locations experienced significant inundation during the event, with floodwater entering buildings to substantial depth. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows that did not enter the watercourse travelled through private gardens, pathways and roads and &mdash; in particular at Poplar Avenue &mdash; caused internal flooding to a high number of residential properties. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Adopted footways west of properties were allowing water to flow toward properties; highway drainage on walkways around Poplar Avenue requires routine maintenance to prevent water reaching properties. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Walkways and adopted footways around Poplar Avenue experienced surface water runoff that threatened properties; actions recommended to shed water into the open watercourse and maintain highway drainage to reduce property flood risk. [TAB,PAG] (p61)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Poplar Avenue, Horwich",England,"Properties at these locations experienced significant inundation during the event, with floodwater entering buildings to substantial depth. (p3)
Overland flows that did not enter the watercourse travelled through private gardens, pathways and roads and — in particular at Poplar Avenue — caused internal flooding to a high number of residential properties. (p38)
Adopted footways west of properties were allowing water to flow toward properties; highway drainage on walkways around Poplar Avenue requires routine maintenance to prevent water reaching properties. (p39)
Walkways and adopted footways around Poplar Avenue experienced surface water runoff that threatened properties; actions recommended to shed water into the open watercourse and maintain highway drainage to reduce property flood risk. [TAB,PAG] (p61)",around 1 metre depth in some properties (p3),GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5713987,-2.5321572,"<div id=""html_f47fadb7d23fc6c00ee05bd5eff6ee6f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lostock Lane, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties at these locations experienced significant inundation during the event, with floodwater entering buildings to substantial depth. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed overland flows and predicted surface&ndash;water flooding affected Lostock Lane as part of the mapped overland flow route through the developed catchment. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging of the sewer and surface water flows (combined with sewage) caused internal flooding to properties and gardens; manhole overflows were observed (water spouting from manholes). Sewer surcharge forced water under the carriageway causing severe highway damage and closure of the road until emergency/temporary carriageway repairs were made. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer capacity was exceeded causing surcharge and manholes to spout; water forced under the carriageway causing severe highway damage and closure of the road for several days until temporary carriageway repairs. Internal flooding to properties and external flooding to garages; at a few properties flooding reached up to 1 metre deep. Two properties were rendered uninhabitable and residents displaced. (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred from overland surface water and contributions from sewer flows. A gully inspection at Alexandra Road/Lostock Lane found 7 of 19 gullies full of debris, which reduced water entering the sewer and contributed to increased overland/highway flooding. [PAG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies on the highway were obstructed (7 of 19 gullies full of debris), reducing flow into the sewer and contributing to increased overland flow on the road; the sag point in the highway had only a single gully, limiting drainage capacity and increasing highway flooding. (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lostock Lane was identified as a high surface&ndash;water&ndash;risk location where highway and property flooding occurred/was a concern during the event; recommendations include reviewing sewer network capacity and making highway drainage improvements to reduce highway and property flooding risk. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties; road closed 2 days [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties; 2 properties; up to 1 metre deep (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Lostock Lane, Horwich",England,"Properties at these locations experienced significant inundation during the event, with floodwater entering buildings to substantial depth. (p3)
Observed overland flows and predicted surface–water flooding affected Lostock Lane as part of the mapped overland flow route through the developed catchment. (p44)
Surcharging of the sewer and surface water flows (combined with sewage) caused internal flooding to properties and gardens; manhole overflows were observed (water spouting from manholes). Sewer surcharge forced water under the carriageway causing severe highway damage and closure of the road until emergency/temporary carriageway repairs were made. [IMG,PAG] (p45)
Sewer capacity was exceeded causing surcharge and manholes to spout; water forced under the carriageway causing severe highway damage and closure of the road for several days until temporary carriageway repairs. Internal flooding to properties and external flooding to garages; at a few properties flooding reached up to 1 metre deep. Two properties were rendered uninhabitable and residents displaced. (p46)
Flooding occurred from overland surface water and contributions from sewer flows. A gully inspection at Alexandra Road/Lostock Lane found 7 of 19 gullies full of debris, which reduced water entering the sewer and contributed to increased overland/highway flooding. [PAG] (p53)
Gullies on the highway were obstructed (7 of 19 gullies full of debris), reducing flow into the sewer and contributing to increased overland flow on the road; the sag point in the highway had only a single gully, limiting drainage capacity and increasing highway flooding. (p54)
Lostock Lane was identified as a high surface–water–risk location where highway and property flooding occurred/was a concern during the event; recommendations include reviewing sewer network capacity and making highway drainage improvements to reduce highway and property flooding risk. [TAB] (p61)","around 1 metre depth in some properties (p3)
5 properties; road closed 2 days [IMG,PAG] (p45)
5 properties; 2 properties; up to 1 metre deep (p46)",GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5834864,-2.4803443,"<div id=""html_7aa0b61243f1d36c46d0485d04c1208d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Victoria Road, Markland Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water / flash flooding concentrated along sections of Victoria Road during the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, affecting the carriageway and adjacent low&ndash;lying residential areas. [IMG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Victoria Road, Markland Hill",England,"Surface water / flash flooding concentrated along sections of Victoria Road during the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, affecting the carriageway and adjacent low–lying residential areas. [IMG] (p17)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5915504,-2.5348344,"<div id=""html_e8c2c19957d9e4a0c849eda8690d171c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>College Drive, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding extended into surrounding residential streets such as College Drive during the intense rainfall and thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, affecting low&ndash;lying properties and road surfaces. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows passed through the College Drive development site; a temporary piped drain installed during development diverted flow past the pipework and into the site rather than into the culvert, increasing flows across the development. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow entered the development at Middlewich Close (part of College Drive / Middlewich Close), causing extensive external flooding to gardens and some internal flooding to newly built properties as flow passed the temporary piped drain and into the estate. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding occurred on College Drive when significant overland flows from the culvert inlet at the rear of College Drive discharged onto the surface. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris from upstream erosion and small landslides blinded the debris screen on the culvert inlet at College Drive, causing the culvert to block and generate overland flow that flooded properties on the eastern side of College Drive. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow across the residential carriageway with water and debris visible on the road surface; flowing water affecting the street environment and reaching parked vehicles, indicating disruption to access and local transport along the street. [IMG] (p75)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"College Drive, Horwich",England,"Surface–water flooding extended into surrounding residential streets such as College Drive during the intense rainfall and thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, affecting low–lying properties and road surfaces. [IMG] (p17)
Overland flows passed through the College Drive development site; a temporary piped drain installed during development diverted flow past the pipework and into the site rather than into the culvert, increasing flows across the development. [PAG] (p28)
Overland flow entered the development at Middlewich Close (part of College Drive / Middlewich Close), causing extensive external flooding to gardens and some internal flooding to newly built properties as flow passed the temporary piped drain and into the estate. (p29)
Internal property flooding occurred on College Drive when significant overland flows from the culvert inlet at the rear of College Drive discharged onto the surface. [PAG] (p32)
Debris from upstream erosion and small landslides blinded the debris screen on the culvert inlet at College Drive, causing the culvert to block and generate overland flow that flooded properties on the eastern side of College Drive. (p33)
Overland flow across the residential carriageway with water and debris visible on the road surface; flowing water affecting the street environment and reaching parked vehicles, indicating disruption to access and local transport along the street. [IMG] (p75)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5873473,-2.521185,"<div id=""html_0f565c35ed21f1503daea33f2d824dab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Austin's Lane, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Austins Lane is within the mapped area where medium to high surface&ndash;water flood zones occur and was within the area affected by the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, resulting in surface&ndash;water flooding impacts to the lane and adjacent areas. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris washed into the culvert inlet at St Leonard&#8217;s Avenue / Austins Lane blinded the debris screen and stopped culvert flow; a considerable overland flow then ran down Austins Lane onto Chorley New Road contributing to flooding of industrial premises and leading to downstream road flooding at the De Havilland Way (A6027) / Mansell Way junction. [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At the culvert inlet serving the junction with Austins Lane a considerable amount of debris washed into the debris screen, blinding it and stopping water passing down the culvert; clearance was required to restore flow. (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Austin's Lane, Horwich",England,"Austins Lane is within the mapped area where medium to high surface–water flood zones occur and was within the area affected by the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, resulting in surface–water flooding impacts to the lane and adjacent areas. [IMG] (p17)
Debris washed into the culvert inlet at St Leonard’s Avenue / Austins Lane blinded the debris screen and stopped culvert flow; a considerable overland flow then ran down Austins Lane onto Chorley New Road contributing to flooding of industrial premises and leading to downstream road flooding at the De Havilland Way (A6027) / Mansell Way junction. [PAG] (p42)
At the culvert inlet serving the junction with Austins Lane a considerable amount of debris washed into the debris screen, blinding it and stopping water passing down the culvert; clearance was required to restore flow. (p43)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5904076,-2.5312245,"<div id=""html_d4ab0d411ef907abc5ca36ad77d70fd9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gloucester Avenue, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding affected Gloucester Avenue during the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, with medium/low risk zones shown on the map extending into this residential street. [IMG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Gloucester Avenue, Horwich",England,"Surface–water flooding affected Gloucester Avenue during the intense thunderstorms on 16 May 2021, with medium/low risk zones shown on the map extending into this residential street. [IMG] (p17)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5924797,-2.5359307,"<div id=""html_a5d61016aea58feac48195f789f52a19"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Middlewich Close, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The privately owned culvert inlet located at the rear of Middlewich Close received observed overland flows from the Horwich Golf Club East catchment and conveyed those flows into the downstream culvert during the event. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant overland flows issued from the golf course onto the rear of properties on Middlewich Close during the rainfall event. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from higher golf course land entered the Rivington Grange Estate at Middlewich Close after a temporary piped drain prevented flow entering the downstream culvert. This caused extensive external flooding to gardens and some internal flooding to newly built property within the development. (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Middlewich Close, Horwich",England,"The privately owned culvert inlet located at the rear of Middlewich Close received observed overland flows from the Horwich Golf Club East catchment and conveyed those flows into the downstream culvert during the event. (p27)
Significant overland flows issued from the golf course onto the rear of properties on Middlewich Close during the rainfall event. [PAG] (p28)
Overland flow from higher golf course land entered the Rivington Grange Estate at Middlewich Close after a temporary piped drain prevented flow entering the downstream culvert. This caused extensive external flooding to gardens and some internal flooding to newly built property within the development. (p29)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5940916,-2.5206915,"<div id=""html_b7027d17a1fc89eaf0685d9599c95a03"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>New Chapel Lane, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant overland flow issued from the southern end of New Chapel Lane into the urban area; flows were channelled along the carriageway (most of the lane lacks highway drainage) and caused damage to footways at the junction with Claypool Road, directing water downhill into residential areas and triggering cascading internal flooding of properties. [PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water was channelled along the carriageway into the urban area; informal obstacles (roads, houses, fences) prevented flows entering the watercourse and created a cascade of flooding impacts as water flowed downhill, surrounding properties one after another. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overland flows were occurring along the carriageway, leading to a recommendation to make drainage improvements and interventions to better manage overland surface water along New Chapel Lane. [TAB] (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"New Chapel Lane, Horwich",England,"Significant overland flow issued from the southern end of New Chapel Lane into the urban area; flows were channelled along the carriageway (most of the lane lacks highway drainage) and caused damage to footways at the junction with Claypool Road, directing water downhill into residential areas and triggering cascading internal flooding of properties. [PAG] (p38)
Surface water was channelled along the carriageway into the urban area; informal obstacles (roads, houses, fences) prevented flows entering the watercourse and created a cascade of flooding impacts as water flowed downhill, surrounding properties one after another. [PAG] (p39)
Surface water overland flows were occurring along the carriageway, leading to a recommendation to make drainage improvements and interventions to better manage overland surface water along New Chapel Lane. [TAB] (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5900012,-2.5220326,"<div id=""html_e8b3d274488b0653b9d89852bceebe60"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oakfield Close, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from fields east of New Chapel Lane entered the Oakfield Close area; a culvert whose capacity has been compromised by historic ad hoc alterations restricted flow and contributed to flooding within the area. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Oakfield Close (and Claypool Road) were affected by constrained/blocked watercourse flows at the rear of properties during the event, leading to flooding risk; residents were advised as riparian owners to undertake improvements so the watercourse is free&ndash;flowing to reduce property flooding. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents (as riparian owners) were advised to improve the rear watercourse to remove constraints to flow and ensure it remains free&ndash;flowing, indicating the watercourse had contributed to flooding issues at properties. (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Oakfield Close, Horwich",England,"Overland flow from fields east of New Chapel Lane entered the Oakfield Close area; a culvert whose capacity has been compromised by historic ad hoc alterations restricted flow and contributed to flooding within the area. (p39)
Properties on Oakfield Close (and Claypool Road) were affected by constrained/blocked watercourse flows at the rear of properties during the event, leading to flooding risk; residents were advised as riparian owners to undertake improvements so the watercourse is free–flowing to reduce property flooding. [TAB] (p61)
Residents (as riparian owners) were advised to improve the rear watercourse to remove constraints to flow and ensure it remains free–flowing, indicating the watercourse had contributed to flooding issues at properties. (p65)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5915362,-2.5265276,"<div id=""html_6f576fee1359bf162dac3b31568ce5de"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ainsworth Avenue, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overland flows affected the highway corridor between Ainsworth Avenue and the nearby Beehive roundabout, contributing to highway flooding and prompting recommendations to examine and improve drainage to reduce flood risk. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage between Ainsworth Avenue and the Beehive roundabout experienced surface water overland flows/highway flooding; authorities were advised to examine and improve drainage to reduce highway flooding risk. [TAB] (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Ainsworth Avenue, Horwich",England,"Surface water overland flows affected the highway corridor between Ainsworth Avenue and the nearby Beehive roundabout, contributing to highway flooding and prompting recommendations to examine and improve drainage to reduce flood risk. (p39)
Highway drainage between Ainsworth Avenue and the Beehive roundabout experienced surface water overland flows/highway flooding; authorities were advised to examine and improve drainage to reduce highway flooding risk. [TAB] (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5879595,-2.5159461,"<div id=""html_f5df1f09476db8f85d7b231e103100a7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Barford Grove, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert inlet and debris screen serving the rear of Wimborne Drive were overwhelmed, producing overland flow that ran along a public right of way and onto Barford Grove. (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Barford Grove, Horwich",England,"The culvert inlet and debris screen serving the rear of Wimborne Drive were overwhelmed, producing overland flow that ran along a public right of way and onto Barford Grove. (p42)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5879917,-2.515284,"<div id=""html_f2a5db8cff8c33bf86df0cd9e49dc7d0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wimborne Close, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert inlet and debris screen at the rear of Wimborne Close were overwhelmed by flow, causing overland flow from the culvert outlet area down a footpath and onto Barford Grove; post&ndash;event inspection found the debris screen around 50% blinded and the culvert largely clear on CCTV. (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Wimborne Close, Horwich",England,"The culvert inlet and debris screen at the rear of Wimborne Close were overwhelmed by flow, causing overland flow from the culvert outlet area down a footpath and onto Barford Grove; post–event inspection found the debris screen around 50% blinded and the culvert largely clear on CCTV. (p42)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5864421,-2.5176575,"<div id=""html_61f0fbc95362a47944cb07b66195fe7d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Leonard's Avenue, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The catchment drains into a culvert located within Bolton Council land at the junction of Austins Lane and St Leonard's Avenue; observed overland flow routes were routed to this culvert during the event, producing surface water/overland flow into the culvert and contributing to downstream flooding. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Most flooding and the properties most significantly impacted were located downstream of the St Leonard&#8216;s Avenue debris screen; the culvert inlet at St Leonard&#8217;s Avenue / Austins Lane became blinded by debris, stopping culvert flow and contributing to overland flooding. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Referencing the same culvert inlet location: debris blinded the inlet debris screen at St Leonard&#8217;s Avenue, preventing water discharge through the culvert and requiring clearance to restore flow. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert inlet and debris screen were affected by debris during the flood event, creating a risk of blockage of the culverted system and potential property flooding; the report recommends reviewing inspection and maintenance and possible additional debris screening or monitoring. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris accumulation at the culvert inlet and on the debris screen led to blockages during the flood event; risk of upstream structure collapse producing further debris and exacerbating flooding. Report recommends additional debris screening, CCTV monitoring of debris levels, and enhanced inspection and maintenance to reduce flood impacts. (p63)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"St Leonard's Avenue, Horwich",England,"The catchment drains into a culvert located within Bolton Council land at the junction of Austins Lane and St Leonard's Avenue; observed overland flow routes were routed to this culvert during the event, producing surface water/overland flow into the culvert and contributing to downstream flooding. (p41)
Most flooding and the properties most significantly impacted were located downstream of the St Leonard‘s Avenue debris screen; the culvert inlet at St Leonard’s Avenue / Austins Lane became blinded by debris, stopping culvert flow and contributing to overland flooding. (p42)
Referencing the same culvert inlet location: debris blinded the inlet debris screen at St Leonard’s Avenue, preventing water discharge through the culvert and requiring clearance to restore flow. (p43)
The culvert inlet and debris screen were affected by debris during the flood event, creating a risk of blockage of the culverted system and potential property flooding; the report recommends reviewing inspection and maintenance and possible additional debris screening or monitoring. [TAB] (p61)
Debris accumulation at the culvert inlet and on the debris screen led to blockages during the flood event; risk of upstream structure collapse producing further debris and exacerbating flooding. Report recommends additional debris screening, CCTV monitoring of debris levels, and enhanced inspection and maintenance to reduce flood impacts. (p63)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5835866,-2.5155681,"<div id=""html_e204ea507b6989df20429c95eef3532b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fall Birch Road, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed overland flows and predicted surface&ndash;water flooding affected the Fall Birch Road area as part of the catchment runoff; surface water followed mapped overland flow routes across the developed catchment. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows from the upper reaches (from fields west of High Rid Reservoir) entered the developed area, causing flooding to properties and gardens. Internal flooding to residential properties and external garages was reported within the catchment. (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water risk at Fall Birch Road led to recommendations to alter kerbing, height and/or camber and make highway drainage improvements so surface water is contained in the carriageway and to reduce the risk of property flooding. (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Fall Birch Road, Horwich",England,"Observed overland flows and predicted surface–water flooding affected the Fall Birch Road area as part of the catchment runoff; surface water followed mapped overland flow routes across the developed catchment. (p44)
Overland flows from the upper reaches (from fields west of High Rid Reservoir) entered the developed area, causing flooding to properties and gardens. Internal flooding to residential properties and external garages was reported within the catchment. (p45)
Surface water risk at Fall Birch Road led to recommendations to alter kerbing, height and/or camber and make highway drainage improvements so surface water is contained in the carriageway and to reduce the risk of property flooding. (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5794984,-2.5048973,"<div id=""html_67459948e1c5968a193e3666208c829a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Links Road, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding occurred in the Links Road area; CCTV inspection after the event showed tree roots and faults in a privately owned watercourse contributed to the problem. (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Links Road, Horwich",England,External flooding occurred in the Links Road area; CCTV inspection after the event showed tree roots and faults in a privately owned watercourse contributed to the problem. (p48),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5964826,-2.5515505,"<div id=""html_6a0ccef9cc6bcd87021f6d1064dd2e2a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Greenstone Avenue, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The small bridge carrying the adopted link footway over Pearl Brook creates a restriction on the brook and is identified as a source of flooding to nearby streets (contributed to flooding impacts observed at Mason Street and Vale Avenue). (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Greenstone Avenue, Horwich",England,The small bridge carrying the adopted link footway over Pearl Brook creates a restriction on the brook and is identified as a source of flooding to nearby streets (contributed to flooding impacts observed at Mason Street and Vale Avenue). (p54),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5969844,-2.5523769,"<div id=""html_ad2cf86c06a0f17c217c2733c8fd9ac1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mason Street, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The small bridge carrying the adopted link footway over Pearl Brook creates a restriction on the brook and is identified as a source of flooding to nearby streets (contributed to flooding impacts observed at Mason Street and Vale Avenue). (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Mason Street, Horwich",England,The small bridge carrying the adopted link footway over Pearl Brook creates a restriction on the brook and is identified as a source of flooding to nearby streets (contributed to flooding impacts observed at Mason Street and Vale Avenue). (p54),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5954814,-2.5584977,"<div id=""html_ed9c7174463d0dfa482ffbe33c1ce24c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Vale Avenue, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on these streets as a result of restriction on Pearl Brook (the small bridge over the brook caused flow restriction and led to flooding at Mason Street and Vale Avenue). (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Vale Avenue, Horwich",England,Flooding occurred on these streets as a result of restriction on Pearl Brook (the small bridge over the brook caused flow restriction and led to flooding at Mason Street and Vale Avenue). (p54),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5882609,-2.5224922,"<div id=""html_4658db98f01378769a91a43bac433e4e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Claypool Road, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from the southern end of New Chapel Lane entered the Claypool Road area; flow was sufficiently high to cause damage to footways at the New Chapel Lane / Claypool Road junction and contributed to internal flooding of downstream properties. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert inlet north of Claypool Road and the watercourse at the rear of properties were identified as vulnerable following the event; constrained culvert/watercourse contributed to flood risk to nearby properties and residents were advised (as riparian owners) to improve the watercourse to remove constraints and ensure free flow. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse at the rear of properties is constrained; residents (as riparian owners) are advised to undertake improvements to the watercourse to remove constraints and ensure it is free flowing to reduce the risk of flooding to rear of properties. (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the entire road surface at the junction, with fast&ndash;moving, turbulent flow making the junction impassable and creating hazardous conditions for vehicles and pedestrians. Buildings and parked vehicles at the junction were partially submerged and access was disrupted. [IMG] (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Claypool Road, Horwich",England,"Overland flow from the southern end of New Chapel Lane entered the Claypool Road area; flow was sufficiently high to cause damage to footways at the New Chapel Lane / Claypool Road junction and contributed to internal flooding of downstream properties. (p38)
Culvert inlet north of Claypool Road and the watercourse at the rear of properties were identified as vulnerable following the event; constrained culvert/watercourse contributed to flood risk to nearby properties and residents were advised (as riparian owners) to improve the watercourse to remove constraints and ensure free flow. [TAB] (p61)
The watercourse at the rear of properties is constrained; residents (as riparian owners) are advised to undertake improvements to the watercourse to remove constraints and ensure it is free flowing to reduce the risk of flooding to rear of properties. (p65)
Floodwater covered the entire road surface at the junction, with fast–moving, turbulent flow making the junction impassable and creating hazardous conditions for vehicles and pedestrians. Buildings and parked vehicles at the junction were partially submerged and access was disrupted. [IMG] (p79)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5793317,-2.4801306,"<div id=""html_bcf57c76933006bd1f74502f348155ca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Haslam Hall Mews, Markland Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Junction (Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews) identified for drainage improvements to reduce highway flooding and to prevent large overflows that could result in flooding to properties immediately downstream. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage failures at the junction leading to highway flooding; recommendation to make improvements to drainage at the junction to reduce flood risk to the highway and nearby properties. [TAB] (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Haslam Hall Mews, Markland Hill",England,"Junction (Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews) identified for drainage improvements to reduce highway flooding and to prevent large overflows that could result in flooding to properties immediately downstream. (p52)
Drainage failures at the junction leading to highway flooding; recommendation to make improvements to drainage at the junction to reduce flood risk to the highway and nearby properties. [TAB] (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.597731,-2.5319139,"<div id=""html_a2ba1896398ffbbdeab027863c86b77d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fleet Street, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant overland flow was observed along Fleet Street during the event, with the cemetery's access road draining to a sewer in Fleet Street; this flow progressed on to Chorley Old Road and was linked to downstream internal flooding of properties and garages. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water / highway drainage issues were identified on Fleet Street (gully blockage/insufficient maintenance), prompting the recommendation for more frequent routine gully cleaning to reduce highway flooding and manage overland flows. [TAB] (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Fleet Street, Horwich",England,"Significant overland flow was observed along Fleet Street during the event, with the cemetery's access road draining to a sewer in Fleet Street; this flow progressed on to Chorley Old Road and was linked to downstream internal flooding of properties and garages. (p23)
Surface–water / highway drainage issues were identified on Fleet Street (gully blockage/insufficient maintenance), prompting the recommendation for more frequent routine gully cleaning to reduce highway flooding and manage overland flows. [TAB] (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.591878,-2.5376893,"<div id=""html_f5061072a03c1442a5b883e240f26129"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Telford Street, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow ran down Telford Street and caused significant highway damage. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Chorley New Road flooding between its junctions with Telford Street and Victoria Road highlighted inadequate highway drainage outside the school, indicating surface water flooding affected this road section and junction area. [TAB] (p61)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Telford Street, Horwich",England,"Overland flow ran down Telford Street and caused significant highway damage. (p25)
Chorley New Road flooding between its junctions with Telford Street and Victoria Road highlighted inadequate highway drainage outside the school, indicating surface water flooding affected this road section and junction area. [TAB] (p61)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5819701,-2.4857232,"<div id=""html_b9461131e008ad62f90a6b94b35f6c38"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Victoria Road, Markland Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant overland flows issued from the golf course site onto Victoria Road via the St Johns Church site when the culvert was overwhelmed. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A downstream section of the culvert near Victoria Road had a partial blockage due to tree root ingress identified after the event; the roots were removed and the culvert made fully operational. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed overland flows from the golf course and development site reached Victoria Road during the event. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from the development ran along Victoria Road carrying water downstream towards Chorley New Road and contributed to the downstream flooding impacts. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Chorley New Road flooding between its junctions with Telford Street and Victoria Road highlighted inadequate highway drainage outside the school, indicating surface water flooding affected this road section and junction area. [TAB] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe street flooding across Victoria Road near St Johns Church; water flowed rapidly across the road and from the church grounds, inundating the pavement and road surface and creating hazardous driving conditions (vehicle seen driving through deep water). [IMG] (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Victoria Road, Markland Hill",England,"Significant overland flows issued from the golf course site onto Victoria Road via the St Johns Church site when the culvert was overwhelmed. (p25)
A downstream section of the culvert near Victoria Road had a partial blockage due to tree root ingress identified after the event; the roots were removed and the culvert made fully operational. (p26)
Observed overland flows from the golf course and development site reached Victoria Road during the event. (p28)
Overland flow from the development ran along Victoria Road carrying water downstream towards Chorley New Road and contributed to the downstream flooding impacts. (p29)
Chorley New Road flooding between its junctions with Telford Street and Victoria Road highlighted inadequate highway drainage outside the school, indicating surface water flooding affected this road section and junction area. [TAB] (p61)
Severe street flooding across Victoria Road near St Johns Church; water flowed rapidly across the road and from the church grounds, inundating the pavement and road surface and creating hazardous driving conditions (vehicle seen driving through deep water). [IMG] (p78)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5902494,-2.5345474,"<div id=""html_de0aca2221be91a11f7ac0514a2f9800"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dale Street West, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding occurred on Dale Street West as a result of overland flows originating from the culvert inlet and Nellies Clough catchment. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Dale Street West were flooded by overland flow caused when the culvert inlet debris screen became blinded by debris transported downstream during the storm. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Dale Street West, Horwich",England,"Internal property flooding occurred on Dale Street West as a result of overland flows originating from the culvert inlet and Nellies Clough catchment. (p32)
Properties on Dale Street West were flooded by overland flow caused when the culvert inlet debris screen became blinded by debris transported downstream during the storm. (p33)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5916829,-2.481387,"<div id=""html_2955bf5910caa2920ce40a114180843b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Delph Hill, Doffcocker</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Located within the plan's red shaded area of most intense rainfall on 16 May 2021; the shaded area is associated with observed flood damage and debris fields from that thunderstorm. [PAG,IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Downstream overland flow from an outflow at Doffcocker Lodge overwhelmed a culvert and ran into the built&ndash;up area; this resulted in internal flooding to residential properties and to a B&ndash;class road located along the downstream route through Delph Hill. Debris observed in a downstream manhole constrained culvert flow and may have contributed to the flooding (uncertain). [IMG] (p49)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Delph Hill, Doffcocker",England,"Located within the plan's red shaded area of most intense rainfall on 16 May 2021; the shaded area is associated with observed flood damage and debris fields from that thunderstorm. [PAG,IMG] (p18)
Downstream overland flow from an outflow at Doffcocker Lodge overwhelmed a culvert and ran into the built–up area; this resulted in internal flooding to residential properties and to a B–class road located along the downstream route through Delph Hill. Debris observed in a downstream manhole constrained culvert flow and may have contributed to the flooding (uncertain). [IMG] (p49)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5976055,-2.536523,"<div id=""html_00cd1aa091053f018a197ab3118929c4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Medway Drive, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A sewer surcharge on Medway Drive resulted in flooding to an external garage; the surcharge was attributed to a sewer blockage. This was a downstream impact of overland flow from the Ridgmont Cemetery catchment. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer flows contributed to flooding at Chorley New Road Primary School and Medway Drive as intense surface water overland flows overwhelmed drainage; the report recommends drainage improvements at the junction of Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews to reduce highway flooding and prevent overflows reaching downstream properties. (p53)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 external garage (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Medway Drive, Horwich",England,"A sewer surcharge on Medway Drive resulted in flooding to an external garage; the surcharge was attributed to a sewer blockage. This was a downstream impact of overland flow from the Ridgmont Cemetery catchment. (p23)
Sewer flows contributed to flooding at Chorley New Road Primary School and Medway Drive as intense surface water overland flows overwhelmed drainage; the report recommends drainage improvements at the junction of Chorley New Road and Haslam Hall Mews to reduce highway flooding and prevent overflows reaching downstream properties. (p53)",1 external garage (p23),GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5909092,-2.5364808,"<div id=""html_af4cac6b6acf6cfc09623283459baead"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Abernethy Street, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow ran down Abernethey Street and caused highway damage. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow ran along Abernethey Street as part of the flow path from the development toward Chorley New Road, contributing to downstream flooding. (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Abernethy Street, Horwich",England,"Overland flow ran down Abernethey Street and caused highway damage. (p28)
Overland flow ran along Abernethey Street as part of the flow path from the development toward Chorley New Road, contributing to downstream flooding. (p29)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5868816,-2.5259906,"<div id=""html_b05aee33681a173ea7354d81fb981533"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cedar Avenue, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Cedar Avenue West catchment experienced surface water flooding on 16th May 2021 caused by a significant overland flow from land either side of New Chapel Lane; the overland flow routed into the council&ndash;owned culvert at the western end of Cedar Avenue and continued south&ndash;west under Chorley New Road towards the Moor Platt Clough culvert. [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fast flows in the upper reaches of the Cedar Avenue West catchment caused damage to river training walls leading to their collapse and failure of supporting private gardens and banks; flows then continued overland through the urban area. (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Cedar Avenue, Horwich",England,"The Cedar Avenue West catchment experienced surface water flooding on 16th May 2021 caused by a significant overland flow from land either side of New Chapel Lane; the overland flow routed into the council–owned culvert at the western end of Cedar Avenue and continued south–west under Chorley New Road towards the Moor Platt Clough culvert. [PAG] (p37)
Fast flows in the upper reaches of the Cedar Avenue West catchment caused damage to river training walls leading to their collapse and failure of supporting private gardens and banks; flows then continued overland through the urban area. (p38)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5855073,-2.5210265,"<div id=""html_4bda2090b102b1c89e5132ef32068ffe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Austin's Lane, Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The catchment drains into a culvert located within Bolton Council land at the junction of Austins Lane and St Leonard's Avenue; observed overland flow routes were routed to this culvert during the event, producing surface water/overland flow into the culvert and contributing to downstream flooding. (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Austin's Lane, Horwich",England,"The catchment drains into a culvert located within Bolton Council land at the junction of Austins Lane and St Leonard's Avenue; observed overland flow routes were routed to this culvert during the event, producing surface water/overland flow into the culvert and contributing to downstream flooding. (p41)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.47769049472794,-2.2435460795004105,"<div id=""html_8a8c155afb7600dcf357aa1bdf143e0f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Greater Manchester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding across Greater Manchester on 16th May 2021 prompted Risk Management Authorities to coordinate flood recovery, preparedness and mitigation actions and to prioritise engagement with affected communities. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Greater Manchester,England,"Flooding across Greater Manchester on 16th May 2021 prompted Risk Management Authorities to coordinate flood recovery, preparedness and mitigation actions and to prioritise engagement with affected communities. [TAB,PAG] (p15)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5965362,-2.5409992,"<div id=""html_1f8d29db1b0b52c99d4033535d5995d5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Horwich</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Horwich and Lostock. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The eastern side of Horwich was heavily impacted by a thunderstorm on 16th May 2021 causing significant flash/surface&ndash;water flooding, traffic disruption and internal and external property flooding. The affected area (eastern Horwich / western Lostock) accounted for a total of 105 properties with some internal flooding. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local roads were inundated: Chorley New Road was flooded to around 0.4 m in places preventing vehicle passage. De Havilland Way at its junction with Mansell Way was flooded to around 0.75 metres, preventing vehicle passage and resulting in the northbound carriageway being closed for 24 hours due to deposits of debris. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties experienced internal flooding: some properties suffered major flooding (complete inundation of the whole ground floor) and others experienced minor internal flooding. Flooding affected an area of approximately 4 square kilometres with spatial clustering of affected properties (notably a cluster in the southwest). (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area recorded lightning activity (1730&ndash;1745 BST) and experienced extremely intense rainfall and hail on 16 May 2021; associated flood damage and debris fields were observed within the area shaded as receiving the most intense rainfall. [PAG,IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, highly localised rainfall on 16 May 2021 produced observed flood damage and debris fields in parts of Horwich (public photographs and video evidence). Radar and witness data indicate the area to the south&ndash;east of Horwich town centre received the heaviest rainfall, producing rapid surface water/flash flooding and associated damage. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, localized heavy rainfall occurred to the south&ndash;east of Horwich town centre during the 6&ndash;hour period to 22:15 GMT on 16 May 2021 (radar shows a white/high accumulation area in that location). This intense rainfall is part of the meteorological conditions associated with the 16 May 2021 flood event affecting Horwich and Lostock. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, localised rainfall and overwhelmed culverts caused overland flows that flooded properties and highways within the town as debris&ndash;blocked culverts forced water out of the drainage network. [IMG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Horwich experienced overland surface water flows derived from the Horwich Golf Course west catchment; impacts included overwhelmed culvert inlets, highway damage on Telford Street, pooling at Chorley New Road and downstream internal flooding to commercial and residential premises. [IMG,PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overland flows from the Horwich Golf Club East catchment entered a culverted watercourse and travelled southwest through residential and industrial areas (via Middlewich Close, under Chorley New Road and through the Horwich Loco Works site) into Nellies Clough, contributing to local urban flooding impacts. [IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents of Oakfield Close and Claypool Road (riparian owners) were advised to clear and improve the small watercourse at the rear of their properties to remove constraints to flow and ensure it remains free&ndash;flowing, indicating the watercourse caused or threatened localised flooding to rear gardens/properties. [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and overland flooding on 16th May 2021 affected the Horwich area: observed overland flow routes converged toward the town centre and outlet, and predicted 1&ndash;in&ndash;100&ndash;year surface water flood zones extend into residential and business districts within Horwich. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The catchment within Horwich (approx. 33 hectares, predominantly developed with sewer drainage) produced overland flows and predicted surface&ndash;water flooding; the report highlights culvert inlet and debris&ndash;screen issues (St Leonard&#8217;s Avenue) and recommends inspection/maintenance and upstream structure monitoring to reduce debris during flood events. [IMG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Horwich (notably Lostock Lane within the catchment) experienced surface water and sewer flooding that led to internal flooding of properties and severe highway damage with road closure for repairs. [IMG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area where multiple flooding incidents were reported and where a Forward Incident Officer was deployed on 16 May; subsequent clearance works (culvert inlets, gullies, highway debris) and inspections were carried out in the days after the event. (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sites in Horwich experienced flooding during the 16 May 2021 event; the EA conducted topographical surveys to record flood levels at individual properties, visited affected streets (e.g. Sudbury Drive, Ashridge Close) to gather information, and CCTV inspection of Nellies Clough culvert identified a partial blockage on land at the former Horwich Locomotive works which EA partially relieved. Further culvert surveys were planned. (p58)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>105 properties (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 4 square kilometres; 3 properties (purple dots); 1 property (orange dot) (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 commercial premises; 1 school building; 3 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties; road closed 2 days [IMG] (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Horwich,England,"Flooding occurred at Horwich and Lostock. (p1)
The eastern side of Horwich was heavily impacted by a thunderstorm on 16th May 2021 causing significant flash/surface–water flooding, traffic disruption and internal and external property flooding. The affected area (eastern Horwich / western Lostock) accounted for a total of 105 properties with some internal flooding. (p10)
Local roads were inundated: Chorley New Road was flooded to around 0.4 m in places preventing vehicle passage. De Havilland Way at its junction with Mansell Way was flooded to around 0.75 metres, preventing vehicle passage and resulting in the northbound carriageway being closed for 24 hours due to deposits of debris. [IMG] (p11)
Residential properties experienced internal flooding: some properties suffered major flooding (complete inundation of the whole ground floor) and others experienced minor internal flooding. Flooding affected an area of approximately 4 square kilometres with spatial clustering of affected properties (notably a cluster in the southwest). (p12)
Area recorded lightning activity (1730–1745 BST) and experienced extremely intense rainfall and hail on 16 May 2021; associated flood damage and debris fields were observed within the area shaded as receiving the most intense rainfall. [PAG,IMG] (p18)
Intense, highly localised rainfall on 16 May 2021 produced observed flood damage and debris fields in parts of Horwich (public photographs and video evidence). Radar and witness data indicate the area to the south–east of Horwich town centre received the heaviest rainfall, producing rapid surface water/flash flooding and associated damage. [PAG] (p19)
Intense, localized heavy rainfall occurred to the south–east of Horwich town centre during the 6–hour period to 22:15 GMT on 16 May 2021 (radar shows a white/high accumulation area in that location). This intense rainfall is part of the meteorological conditions associated with the 16 May 2021 flood event affecting Horwich and Lostock. [IMG] (p20)
Intense, localised rainfall and overwhelmed culverts caused overland flows that flooded properties and highways within the town as debris–blocked culverts forced water out of the drainage network. [IMG] (p22)
Parts of Horwich experienced overland surface water flows derived from the Horwich Golf Course west catchment; impacts included overwhelmed culvert inlets, highway damage on Telford Street, pooling at Chorley New Road and downstream internal flooding to commercial and residential premises. [IMG,PAG] (p25)
Surface water overland flows from the Horwich Golf Club East catchment entered a culverted watercourse and travelled southwest through residential and industrial areas (via Middlewich Close, under Chorley New Road and through the Horwich Loco Works site) into Nellies Clough, contributing to local urban flooding impacts. [IMG] (p27)
Residents of Oakfield Close and Claypool Road (riparian owners) were advised to clear and improve the small watercourse at the rear of their properties to remove constraints to flow and ensure it remains free–flowing, indicating the watercourse caused or threatened localised flooding to rear gardens/properties. [IMG,PAG] (p40)
Surface water and overland flooding on 16th May 2021 affected the Horwich area: observed overland flow routes converged toward the town centre and outlet, and predicted 1–in–100–year surface water flood zones extend into residential and business districts within Horwich. [IMG] (p41)
The catchment within Horwich (approx. 33 hectares, predominantly developed with sewer drainage) produced overland flows and predicted surface–water flooding; the report highlights culvert inlet and debris–screen issues (St Leonard’s Avenue) and recommends inspection/maintenance and upstream structure monitoring to reduce debris during flood events. [IMG] (p44)
Parts of Horwich (notably Lostock Lane within the catchment) experienced surface water and sewer flooding that led to internal flooding of properties and severe highway damage with road closure for repairs. [IMG] (p45)
Area where multiple flooding incidents were reported and where a Forward Incident Officer was deployed on 16 May; subsequent clearance works (culvert inlets, gullies, highway debris) and inspections were carried out in the days after the event. (p56)
Sites in Horwich experienced flooding during the 16 May 2021 event; the EA conducted topographical surveys to record flood levels at individual properties, visited affected streets (e.g. Sudbury Drive, Ashridge Close) to gather information, and CCTV inspection of Nellies Clough culvert identified a partial blockage on land at the former Horwich Locomotive works which EA partially relieved. Further culvert surveys were planned. (p58)","105 properties (p10)
approximately 4 square kilometres; 3 properties (purple dots); 1 property (orange dot) (p12)
12 commercial premises; 1 school building; 3 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p25)
5 properties; road closed 2 days [IMG] (p45)",GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5727921,-2.494764,"<div id=""html_eadb4a11eddf01155d4a7b141c1f1837"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lostock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Horwich and Lostock. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The western side of Lostock was heavily impacted by the thunderstorm on 16th May 2021 producing significant flash/surface&ndash;water flooding, traffic disruption and reports of internal and external property flooding as part of the same affected area (eastern Horwich / western Lostock). (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The main area of flooding covered approximately 4 square kilometres. Properties experienced internal flooding, with locations mapped showing complete ground&ndash;floor inundation in some cases (major internal flooding) and minor internal flooding in others. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in Lostock were affected by flooding, shown on the plan of impacted properties. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Lostock area experienced extremely intense rainfall and associated surface&ndash;water / flash flooding on 16 May 2021. The reproduced surface&ndash;water risk plans and the rainfall analysis show medium to high risk zones across parts of Lostock, with flooding concentrated along low&ndash;lying streets and corridors (notably near Chorley New Road and De Havilland Way), affecting residential streets and local infrastructure. [IMG,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extremely intense convective rainfall (with hail) produced localized flash / surface&ndash;water flooding; post&ndash;event observations recorded flood damage and debris fields and photographic/video evidence locating the most intensely affected area within the red&ndash;shaded zone on the plan. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the Horwich and Lostock area experienced very intense, localised rainfall on 16 May 2021 (e.g. up to 38 mm in 60 minutes in the Horwich area), resulting in localized surface water and flash flooding and observed flood damage in the area. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A sudden thunderstorm produced a large volume of runoff that overwhelmed multiple culvert inlets; debris&ndash;blocked culverts caused flow to bypass the drainage system and continue overland, resulting in flooding across the urban area including properties and highways. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow routes and surface water flood extents in the Moor Platt Clough catchment converged through this area (the culvert inlet is at the junction in Lostock), indicating the locality experienced significant surface water/overland flooding during the storm. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from Bessy Brook in the Lostock area (including Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close) resulted in internal flooding to residential properties; additional external flooding occurred in the Chorley New Road / Links Road area, with CCTV evidence showing tree roots and faults in a privately owned watercourse contributed. [PAG] (p48)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>105 properties (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 residential properties [PAG] (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Lostock,England,"Flooding occurred at Horwich and Lostock. (p1)
The western side of Lostock was heavily impacted by the thunderstorm on 16th May 2021 producing significant flash/surface–water flooding, traffic disruption and reports of internal and external property flooding as part of the same affected area (eastern Horwich / western Lostock). (p10)
The main area of flooding covered approximately 4 square kilometres. Properties experienced internal flooding, with locations mapped showing complete ground–floor inundation in some cases (major internal flooding) and minor internal flooding in others. (p11)
Residential properties in Lostock were affected by flooding, shown on the plan of impacted properties. (p12)
The Lostock area experienced extremely intense rainfall and associated surface–water / flash flooding on 16 May 2021. The reproduced surface–water risk plans and the rainfall analysis show medium to high risk zones across parts of Lostock, with flooding concentrated along low–lying streets and corridors (notably near Chorley New Road and De Havilland Way), affecting residential streets and local infrastructure. [IMG,PAG] (p17)
Extremely intense convective rainfall (with hail) produced localized flash / surface–water flooding; post–event observations recorded flood damage and debris fields and photographic/video evidence locating the most intensely affected area within the red–shaded zone on the plan. [PAG] (p18)
Parts of the Horwich and Lostock area experienced very intense, localised rainfall on 16 May 2021 (e.g. up to 38 mm in 60 minutes in the Horwich area), resulting in localized surface water and flash flooding and observed flood damage in the area. (p19)
A sudden thunderstorm produced a large volume of runoff that overwhelmed multiple culvert inlets; debris–blocked culverts caused flow to bypass the drainage system and continue overland, resulting in flooding across the urban area including properties and highways. (p22)
Overland flow routes and surface water flood extents in the Moor Platt Clough catchment converged through this area (the culvert inlet is at the junction in Lostock), indicating the locality experienced significant surface water/overland flooding during the storm. (p41)
Flooding from Bessy Brook in the Lostock area (including Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close) resulted in internal flooding to residential properties; additional external flooding occurred in the Chorley New Road / Links Road area, with CCTV evidence showing tree roots and faults in a privately owned watercourse contributed. [PAG] (p48)","105 properties (p10)
11 residential properties [PAG] (p48)",GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5824926,-2.4792738,"<div id=""html_f1e80ec5b148b1a76ce87fa452f5539f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Markland Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the Markland Hill and Doffcocker areas were flooded; locations are identified on the plan of properties impacted by flooding. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mapped as being within the area assessed to have received the most intense rainfall on 16 May 2021; this intense rainfall is associated with observed flood damage and debris fields in the vicinity, indicating localised flash/surface water flooding impacts. [PAG,IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland surface water flow, with some sewer surcharge, caused flooding within the Markland Hill catchment. Flooding was confined to low spots in the highway or areas of highway runoff and resulted in internal flooding to garages integral to properties. [IMG] (p51)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Markland Hill,England,"Properties in the Markland Hill and Doffcocker areas were flooded; locations are identified on the plan of properties impacted by flooding. (p12)
Mapped as being within the area assessed to have received the most intense rainfall on 16 May 2021; this intense rainfall is associated with observed flood damage and debris fields in the vicinity, indicating localised flash/surface water flooding impacts. [PAG,IMG] (p18)
Overland surface water flow, with some sewer surcharge, caused flooding within the Markland Hill catchment. Flooding was confined to low spots in the highway or areas of highway runoff and resulted in internal flooding to garages integral to properties. [IMG] (p51)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.590324,-2.4730704,"<div id=""html_f0ff2f29108352e2f73e7fbe07b78f40"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Doffcocker</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the Markland Hill and Doffcocker areas were flooded; locations are identified on the plan of properties impacted by flooding. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Outflow from Doffcocker Lodge overwhelmed a culvert and caused overland flow into the adjacent built area, resulting in internal flooding to residential properties and a B&ndash;class road within the downstream Doffcocker area. [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Doffcocker,England,"Properties in the Markland Hill and Doffcocker areas were flooded; locations are identified on the plan of properties impacted by flooding. (p12)
Outflow from Doffcocker Lodge overwhelmed a culvert and caused overland flow into the adjacent built area, resulting in internal flooding to residential properties and a B–class road within the downstream Doffcocker area. [IMG,PAG] (p49)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.591302060476224,-2.535252271530836,"<div id=""html_10bfe8995b76463e94462e7c77e8941c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rivington Grange Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from higher golf course land entered the Rivington Grange Estate (Middlewich Close), producing extensive external garden flooding and some internal flooding to newly built properties; flow then continued overland onto adjacent roads. (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Rivington Grange Estate,England,"Overland flow from higher golf course land entered the Rivington Grange Estate (Middlewich Close), producing extensive external garden flooding and some internal flooding to newly built properties; flow then continued overland onto adjacent roads. (p29)",,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.582367,-2.5355964,"<div id=""html_1b2eaec6166b855fbaa2c506b3c908c7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Middlebrook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow travelled south through the Middlebrook development and contributed to further flooding of property within the development. (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Middlebrook,England,Overland flow travelled south through the Middlebrook development and contributed to further flooding of property within the development. (p32),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BOL,1,2021-05-16,53.5938642,-2.4748329,"<div id=""html_bcc3a7034c644f19367886414f17147d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Johnson Fold</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from outflow at Doffcocker Lodge overwhelmed the downstream culvert and ran east into residential areas; this produced internal flooding to residential properties and to a B&ndash;class road along the downstream route through the Johnson Fold area. [IMG] (p49)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Johnson Fold,England,Overland flow from outflow at Doffcocker Lodge overwhelmed the downstream culvert and ran east into residential areas; this produced internal flooding to residential properties and to a B–class road along the downstream route through the Johnson Fold area. [IMG] (p49),,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16.html,GB-BOL_1_2021-05-16,Bolton flooding 16 May 2021,Bolton Council Flooding Investigation Report: Flooding at Horwich and Lostock on 16th May 2021,010/Bolton/010/2021/Bolton/Flooding/HorwichLostock2021Report.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.0433509,-0.9591638,"<div id=""html_a5d4e7cff60cab719393222bb3dd4cba"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Leck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels in the River Leck rose rapidly on the morning of 9 March, overtopping banks through Church End. Floodwater flowed the full length of the village road (onto which most properties front). Overland flows from saturated valley sides also contributed. Two properties on Church End were affected by overtopping floodwater; residents deployed their own flood prevention measures and no internal property damage was sustained. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in the River Leck produced fast&ndash;flowing floodwater (deepest at the bridge) that reached up to approximately 1 m depth, making the only vehicular access to the village impassable for hours and isolating Leckhampstead. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid river response in the River Leck catchment following intense rainfall on already&ndash;saturated soils. Very little rain was retained in the soil; quick throughflow and some overland flow rapidly routed water into stream channels, producing high peak flows and consequent river flooding across the catchment. [PAG,IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High levels in the River Leck (and its inability to discharge to the Great Ouse when that river is high) contributed to flooding of adjacent infrastructure, notably causing flooding of the A422 and isolating the village. (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Two properties (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx 1 m depth (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Leck,England,"Water levels in the River Leck rose rapidly on the morning of 9 March, overtopping banks through Church End. Floodwater flowed the full length of the village road (onto which most properties front). Overland flows from saturated valley sides also contributed. Two properties on Church End were affected by overtopping floodwater; residents deployed their own flood prevention measures and no internal property damage was sustained. (p13)
High water levels in the River Leck produced fast–flowing floodwater (deepest at the bridge) that reached up to approximately 1 m depth, making the only vehicular access to the village impassable for hours and isolating Leckhampstead. (p15)
Rapid river response in the River Leck catchment following intense rainfall on already–saturated soils. Very little rain was retained in the soil; quick throughflow and some overland flow rapidly routed water into stream channels, producing high peak flows and consequent river flooding across the catchment. [PAG,IMG] (p19)
High levels in the River Leck (and its inability to discharge to the Great Ouse when that river is high) contributed to flooding of adjacent infrastructure, notably causing flooding of the A422 and isolating the village. (p27)","Two properties (p13)
approx 1 m depth (p15)",GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.1278302,-0.4250206,"<div id=""html_0e31ccc65da22fc2a43bd3813fc010b6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Great Ouse</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Great Ouse exhibited elevated flows at Thornborough, rising above the 0.73 m flood threshold on 9 March and peaking at just above 1.0 m by the morning of 10 March, close to historical high levels. [IMG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in the Great Ouse prevented the River Leck from discharging, contributing to flooding of the A422 and compounding the isolation of Leckhampstead. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rising levels on the Great Ouse were the main concern during the 8&ndash;9 March 2016 event; in places the river was on the verge of breaking its banks and sections of road were closed where the river rose significantly (A422 Stratford Road was closed where the river had risen). (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in the Great Ouse prevented the River Leck from discharging, which led to local flooding impacts including inundation and closure of the A422. (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>exceeded 0.73 m flood threshold; peaked just above 1.0 m; highest recorded 2.24 m [IMG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>river rose by 1.6m in places (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Great Ouse,England,"The River Great Ouse exhibited elevated flows at Thornborough, rising above the 0.73 m flood threshold on 9 March and peaking at just above 1.0 m by the morning of 10 March, close to historical high levels. [IMG] (p14)
High water levels in the Great Ouse prevented the River Leck from discharging, contributing to flooding of the A422 and compounding the isolation of Leckhampstead. [TAB] (p15)
Rising levels on the Great Ouse were the main concern during the 8–9 March 2016 event; in places the river was on the verge of breaking its banks and sections of road were closed where the river rose significantly (A422 Stratford Road was closed where the river had risen). (p21)
High water levels in the Great Ouse prevented the River Leck from discharging, which led to local flooding impacts including inundation and closure of the A422. (p27)","exceeded 0.73 m flood threshold; peaked just above 1.0 m; highest recorded 2.24 m [IMG] (p14)
river rose by 1.6m in places (p21)",GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,51.9863293474595,-1.0445634611499166,"<div id=""html_3ce4ac3e030a7a7dbb8889045485285d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tingewick Bypass</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tingewick Bypass (Tingewick) was reported flooded on 9 March 2016; incident logged as '2hr/Flooding' and marked COMPLETE. [TAB] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Tingewick Bypass,England,Tingewick Bypass (Tingewick) was reported flooded on 9 March 2016; incident logged as '2hr/Flooding' and marked COMPLETE. [TAB] (p22),,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.0350523,-0.9404408,"<div id=""html_a307a7ccf37576213e245caa3a8f4d83"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>South End Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris accumulated against the upstream face of the bridge, lodging in the culverts and main arch and impeding water flow; this blockage likely increased local flow resistance and flood risk at the bridge during the event. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,South End Bridge,England,"Debris accumulated against the upstream face of the bridge, lodging in the culverts and main arch and impeding water flow; this blockage likely increased local flow resistance and flood risk at the bridge during the event. (p20)",,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.0350523,-0.9426587,"<div id=""html_894d3ddb8708bc9eb08ad9f8452fa1d5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church End, Leckhampstead</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Leck overtopped its banks through Church End and overland flows affected the area; two properties on Church End were flooded and high&ndash;velocity, deep floodwater across the main access route made the village inaccessible by vehicle for several hours. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Leck rose and overtopped its banks through Church End; floodwater flowed along the road through the settlement. Two properties were affected by overtopping (external impacts only); residents&#8217; measures prevented internal property damage. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Leck overtopped its banks through Church End and overland flows ran off saturated valley sides, resulting in floodwater affecting properties and making the village inaccessible for several hours due to high depths and velocities on the main highway access. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced flooding during the investigated event; the report specifically recommends investigating improved flood warning arrangements for Church End and community&ndash;based flood planning in response to that flooding. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Two properties (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Church End, Leckhampstead",England,"River Leck overtopped its banks through Church End and overland flows affected the area; two properties on Church End were flooded and high–velocity, deep floodwater across the main access route made the village inaccessible by vehicle for several hours. (p5)
The River Leck rose and overtopped its banks through Church End; floodwater flowed along the road through the settlement. Two properties were affected by overtopping (external impacts only); residents’ measures prevented internal property damage. (p13)
River Leck overtopped its banks through Church End and overland flows ran off saturated valley sides, resulting in floodwater affecting properties and making the village inaccessible for several hours due to high depths and velocities on the main highway access. (p26)
Area experienced flooding during the investigated event; the report specifically recommends investigating improved flood warning arrangements for Church End and community–based flood planning in response to that flooding. [TAB] (p28)","2 properties (p5)
Two properties (p13)
2 properties (p26)",GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.0000073,-0.8648243,"<div id=""html_b88f45f6ebd68445730d6cf1163782b2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stratford Road, Nash</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A422 Stratford Road was closed where the adjacent river rise led to flooding of the road surface; Transport for Buckinghamshire closed a number of roads in the area during the heavy rain. (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>river rose by 1.6m in places (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Stratford Road, Nash",England,A422 Stratford Road was closed where the adjacent river rise led to flooding of the road surface; Transport for Buckinghamshire closed a number of roads in the area during the heavy rain. (p21),river rose by 1.6m in places (p21),GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
