GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,51.9953427,-0.8621429,"<div id=""html_1c94eccae1a6f7b5248961fd57e8e2c6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Winslow Road, Nash</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affecting Winslow Road at Padbury and at Nash requiring attendance by response crews; incidents marked complete. [TAB] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Winslow Road, Nash",England,Flooding affecting Winslow Road at Padbury and at Nash requiring attendance by response crews; incidents marked complete. [TAB] (p22),,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,51.9438581,-0.8990175,"<div id=""html_589ad9f56fa424216526c54e64a5342e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Verney Road, Addington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Verney Road at Addington experienced flooding on 9 March 2016 (logged as 2hr/Flooding, CRN 5021243) and was attended by Transport for Buckinghamshire (status COMPLETE). [TAB] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Verney Road, Addington",England,"Verney Road at Addington experienced flooding on 9 March 2016 (logged as 2hr/Flooding, CRN 5021243) and was attended by Transport for Buckinghamshire (status COMPLETE). [TAB] (p22)",,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.016871,-1.0934607,"<div id=""html_a69c4dbde3cdcec679110bc9665ffabf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fulwell Road, Westbury</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fulwell Road (Westbury) was reported flooded on 9 March 2016; incident logged as '2hr/Flooding' and marked COMPLETE. [TAB] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Fulwell Road, Westbury",England,Fulwell Road (Westbury) was reported flooded on 9 March 2016; incident logged as '2hr/Flooding' and marked COMPLETE. [TAB] (p22),,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_181b6f476a9e84e88773c8881a5e8184"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Buckinghamshire within the River Leck catchment experienced intense, concentrated rainfall on saturated ground, which generated rapid runoff and high peak river flows leading to flooding in the catchment. [PAG,IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rain on 08 Mar 2016 &ndash; 09 Mar 2016 caused flooding incidents across the area. Transport for Buckinghamshire closed a number of roads. The Great River Ouse in places approached or threatened to break its banks; the A422 Stratford Road was closed where the river had risen (reported locally up to 1.6 m in places). Other localities including Buckingham and Beachampton also faced flooding incidents. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Transport for Buckinghamshire crews attended multiple road flooding incidents across the county on 9 March 2016. Impacts included flooded carriageways and at least one road closure (Main Street, Beachampton). Roads affected included Stratford Road (Leckhampstead and Buckingham), Grenville Road (Buckingham), Ford Street (Buckingham), Winslow Road (Padbury and Nash), Main Street (Gawcott), Nash Road (Beachampton), Buckingham Road (Newton Purcell), Church Hill and Lillingstone Road (Akeley), Edgcott Road (Grendon Underwood), Verney Road (Addington) and Fulwell Road (Westbury). Responses were recorded as complete for attended incidents. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 transport response sites; 1 road closure; 3 incidents in Buckingham; 2 incidents on Winslow Road; 2 incidents in Beachampton; 2 incidents in Akeley; 1 incident each in Gawcott, Newton Purcell, Grendon Underwood, Nash, Leckhampstead, Addington, Westbury [TAB] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"Parts of Buckinghamshire within the River Leck catchment experienced intense, concentrated rainfall on saturated ground, which generated rapid runoff and high peak river flows leading to flooding in the catchment. [PAG,IMG] (p19)
Heavy rain on 08 Mar 2016 – 09 Mar 2016 caused flooding incidents across the area. Transport for Buckinghamshire closed a number of roads. The Great River Ouse in places approached or threatened to break its banks; the A422 Stratford Road was closed where the river had risen (reported locally up to 1.6 m in places). Other localities including Buckingham and Beachampton also faced flooding incidents. (p21)
Transport for Buckinghamshire crews attended multiple road flooding incidents across the county on 9 March 2016. Impacts included flooded carriageways and at least one road closure (Main Street, Beachampton). Roads affected included Stratford Road (Leckhampstead and Buckingham), Grenville Road (Buckingham), Ford Street (Buckingham), Winslow Road (Padbury and Nash), Main Street (Gawcott), Nash Road (Beachampton), Buckingham Road (Newton Purcell), Church Hill and Lillingstone Road (Akeley), Edgcott Road (Grendon Underwood), Verney Road (Addington) and Fulwell Road (Westbury). Responses were recorded as complete for attended incidents. [TAB] (p22)","18 transport response sites; 1 road closure; 3 incidents in Buckingham; 2 incidents on Winslow Road; 2 incidents in Beachampton; 2 incidents in Akeley; 1 incident each in Gawcott, Newton Purcell, Grendon Underwood, Nash, Leckhampstead, Addington, Westbury [TAB] (p22)",GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.0336802,-0.9402602,"<div id=""html_4a8a4649ee69f0eece790cb0f1cf86e6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Leckhampstead</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leckhampstead was the subject of a formal flood investigation (Flood Investigation Report) dated 9 March 2016, indicating a flood event affected the settlement and prompted the investigation. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels in the River Leck rose rapidly and overtopped the banks, combined with overland flows from saturated valley sides; this resulted in floodwater affecting properties and making the village access route impassable for a number of hours. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid onset flooding with high&ndash;velocity, deep floodwater across the main access route that isolated the village for a few hours. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village of Leckhampstead was affected by flooding associated with the River Leck catchment during the incident; floodwater inundated parts of the settlement. [IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>March 2016 flooding exhibited a rapid onset that caused highway access and egress problems isolating the village, posed a hazard to people due to depth and velocity of floodwater, and resulted in some houses experiencing internal flooding. The text records repeated historical flood occurrences affecting the village in 1932; 1959; 1983; 1986; 1988; 1990; Easter 1998; February 2001; July 2007; October 2007. [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Leck, aided by overland flows from saturated valley sides, flowed the full length of the road through the village, affecting property frontages; residents deployed local flood prevention measures and no internal property damage was sustained. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leckhampstead experienced flooding driven by a rapid river response and high peak flows on the River Leck after intense rainfall fell on an already&ndash;saturated catchment (causing rapid throughflow and some surface saturation). [PAG,IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During 08 Mar 2016 &ndash; 09 Mar 2016 the village was covered by a Flood Alert for the Upper Ouse that was of limited relevance (based on Great Ouse levels rather than the River Leck). Village residents signed up to receive the alert did not receive it due to a delayed update, reducing local preparedness for the flood event. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the Leckhampstead area on 9 March 2016 (Stratford Road reported as flooded); Transport for Buckinghamshire responded to the incident and recorded it as 2hr/Flooding (COMPLETE). [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid rise of the River Leck and overland flows on saturated ground caused floodwater through the village: main highway access routes were impassable for hours, floodwater flowed along the full length of the only street (onto which many properties front), and properties were affected by deep, fast&ndash;flowing water. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall recorded by the HYRAD radar over the area on 09/03/2016 is associated with the flood event affecting Leckhampstead on 08&ndash;09/03/2016. The rainfall led to flooding in the Leckhampstead area. (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Two properties (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Leckhampstead,England,"Leckhampstead was the subject of a formal flood investigation (Flood Investigation Report) dated 9 March 2016, indicating a flood event affected the settlement and prompted the investigation. [IMG] (p1)
Water levels in the River Leck rose rapidly and overtopped the banks, combined with overland flows from saturated valley sides; this resulted in floodwater affecting properties and making the village access route impassable for a number of hours. (p5)
Rapid onset flooding with high–velocity, deep floodwater across the main access route that isolated the village for a few hours. (p6)
The village of Leckhampstead was affected by flooding associated with the River Leck catchment during the incident; floodwater inundated parts of the settlement. [IMG] (p8)
March 2016 flooding exhibited a rapid onset that caused highway access and egress problems isolating the village, posed a hazard to people due to depth and velocity of floodwater, and resulted in some houses experiencing internal flooding. The text records repeated historical flood occurrences affecting the village in 1932; 1959; 1983; 1986; 1988; 1990; Easter 1998; February 2001; July 2007; October 2007. [MUL] (p12)
Floodwater from the River Leck, aided by overland flows from saturated valley sides, flowed the full length of the road through the village, affecting property frontages; residents deployed local flood prevention measures and no internal property damage was sustained. (p13)
Leckhampstead experienced flooding driven by a rapid river response and high peak flows on the River Leck after intense rainfall fell on an already–saturated catchment (causing rapid throughflow and some surface saturation). [PAG,IMG] (p19)
During 08 Mar 2016 – 09 Mar 2016 the village was covered by a Flood Alert for the Upper Ouse that was of limited relevance (based on Great Ouse levels rather than the River Leck). Village residents signed up to receive the alert did not receive it due to a delayed update, reducing local preparedness for the flood event. (p21)
Flooding affected the Leckhampstead area on 9 March 2016 (Stratford Road reported as flooded); Transport for Buckinghamshire responded to the incident and recorded it as 2hr/Flooding (COMPLETE). [TAB] (p22)
Rapid rise of the River Leck and overland flows on saturated ground caused floodwater through the village: main highway access routes were impassable for hours, floodwater flowed along the full length of the only street (onto which many properties front), and properties were affected by deep, fast–flowing water. (p26)
Intense rainfall recorded by the HYRAD radar over the area on 09/03/2016 is associated with the flood event affecting Leckhampstead on 08–09/03/2016. The rainfall led to flooding in the Leckhampstead area. (p39)","2 properties (p5)
Two properties (p13)
2 properties (p26)",GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,51.9955872,-0.9136531,"<div id=""html_140e25bbe2df6bb68282b6b829998953"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thornborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels on the River Great Ouse at Thornborough rose above the flood threshold (0.73 m) from the morning of 9 March and showed a peak around 01:00 on 10 March, reaching just above 1.0 m and approaching record levels. [IMG] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>exceeded 0.73 m flood threshold; peaked just above 1.0 m; normal range 0.10&ndash;0.73 m; highest recorded 2.24 m [IMG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thornborough,England,"River levels on the River Great Ouse at Thornborough rose above the flood threshold (0.73 m) from the morning of 9 March and showed a peak around 01:00 on 10 March, reaching just above 1.0 m and approaching record levels. [IMG] (p14)",exceeded 0.73 m flood threshold; peaked just above 1.0 m; normal range 0.10–0.73 m; highest recorded 2.24 m [IMG] (p14),GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,52.0335093,-0.9680537,"<div id=""html_c3d5daab8b2b7161f241cb077d84758d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Akeley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area received some of the highest rainfall totals within the River Leck catchment during the storm, which contributed to a rapid river response and high peak flows. This likely increased flood levels and flood risk at Akeley and may have led to river flooding or inundation of low&ndash;lying areas (impact inferred from catchment&ndash;scale flooding described in the report). [PAG,IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding affecting Church Hill within Akeley (roadway inundation). [TAB] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 recorded flooding incident (09 Mar 2016) [TAB] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Akeley,England,"The area received some of the highest rainfall totals within the River Leck catchment during the storm, which contributed to a rapid river response and high peak flows. This likely increased flood levels and flood risk at Akeley and may have led to river flooding or inundation of low–lying areas (impact inferred from catchment–scale flooding described in the report). [PAG,IMG] (p19)
Localised flooding affecting Church Hill within Akeley (roadway inundation). [TAB] (p22)",1 recorded flooding incident (09 Mar 2016) [TAB] (p22),GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08.html,GB-BKM_18_2016-03-08,Leckhampstead flooding 08 Mar 2016 - 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Leckhampstead, 9th March 2016",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2016/Buckinghamshire County/s19-investigation-report-march-2016-leckhampstead/final.pdf
GB-BKM,18,2016-03-08,51.998318,-0.9786725,"<div id=""html_ceb815604014f6a65fc8fc864c8dfc30"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckingham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area experienced flooding incidents during the event. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooding incidents reported in Buckingham on 9 March 2016 (e.g. Stratford Road and Ford Street); incidents were logged as flood events with responses recorded as complete. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in Buckingham during the March 2016 flood event, as referenced by the Environment Agency's 'Flooding in Buckingham: March 2016 Flood Event Summary'. [TAB] (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 incidents [TAB] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckingham,England,"The area experienced flooding incidents during the event. (p21)
