GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2345643,-1.4444568,"<div id=""html_80d49db2e401b2ca347d610cd32e4043"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pizza Hut</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding reached Pizza Hut on the north side of Chatsworth Road; it was the furthest east property recorded as flooded, indicating flood depths decreased farther east. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Pizza Hut,England,"Flooding reached Pizza Hut on the north side of Chatsworth Road; it was the furthest east property recorded as flooded, indicating flood depths decreased farther east. (p30)",,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2631081,-1.3441266,"<div id=""html_2610916064eec6d7bc5edb52e87832d9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pools Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pools Brook is an ordinary watercourse to the west of Poolsbrook and lies within the local drainage network shown on the Storm Babet recorded flood mapping. It is located immediately west of the area where residential properties flooded during Storm Babet and is part of the watercourse system linked to the flooded area (via Ditch 1). This places Pools Brook within the vicinity of the recorded flooding and as a likely component of local flow pathways during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Pools Brook,England,"Pools Brook is an ordinary watercourse to the west of Poolsbrook and lies within the local drainage network shown on the Storm Babet recorded flood mapping. It is located immediately west of the area where residential properties flooded during Storm Babet and is part of the watercourse system linked to the flooded area (via Ditch 1). This places Pools Brook within the vicinity of the recorded flooding and as a likely component of local flow pathways during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p4)",,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2180173,-1.4939186,"<div id=""html_473f89599a419fe756d1c05c5332e700"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Old Mill Pond</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Mill Pond overtopped locally at a weir, contributing to local inundation and to River Hipper bank overtopping in the middle of Holymoorside. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Old Mill Pond,England,"Old Mill Pond overtopped locally at a weir, contributing to local inundation and to River Hipper bank overtopping in the middle of Holymoorside. (p18)",,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2505718,-1.4686872,"<div id=""html_6c2952887b382ff20256e8aae3b8db85"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holme Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Holme Brook, which flows through Brampton and joins the Hipper, contributed to flooding in the community during Storm Babet that led to internal flooding of properties. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Holme Brook (including a culverted section beneath Chatsworth Road and Wheatbridge Road of approximately 80 m) contributed to flooding within the recorded flood outline during the event; culverted reaches and adjacent floodplain lie within the mapped inundation affecting the built&ndash;up area south of Chatsworth Road. [IMG,PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A Flood Warning for Holme Brook covered most of the internally flooded properties; the watercourse (and its floodplain) contributed to internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Holme Brook rose on 20 October 2023 (gauge upstream of Purbeck Avenue Bridge reached a very high level) and contributed to the fluvial flooding that caused internal flooding across Brampton. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Holme Brook rose on 20 Oct, beginning to rise at about 02:00 and reaching a peak level of 1.6 m at about 13:00 (the second&ndash;highest level on record). The brook is identified as a primary source of floodwater during the event, overtopping in places and contributing flow routes into the Brampton community. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Holme Brook overtopped its banks causing internal flooding of nearby properties and local infrastructure damage; overtopping is linked to likely internal flooding of properties on Chester Street and collapse of a riparian brick wall adjacent to West Bars roundabout. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Holme Brook corridor flooding contributed to inundation of adjacent areas and properties in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 &ndash; 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>192 properties; 137 homes; 55 businesses (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 properties (total) within the community shown in Table 4&ndash;1; 142 properties had flooded previously (73% of the properties that were internally flooded during Storm Babet). [PAG,IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to internal flooding of 192 properties in Brampton (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.6 m peak level (second highest level ever recorded) [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 homes and businesses (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Holme Brook,England,"Holme Brook, which flows through Brampton and joins the Hipper, contributed to flooding in the community during Storm Babet that led to internal flooding of properties. (p24)
Holme Brook (including a culverted section beneath Chatsworth Road and Wheatbridge Road of approximately 80 m) contributed to flooding within the recorded flood outline during the event; culverted reaches and adjacent floodplain lie within the mapped inundation affecting the built–up area south of Chatsworth Road. [IMG,PAG] (p25)
A Flood Warning for Holme Brook covered most of the internally flooded properties; the watercourse (and its floodplain) contributed to internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG] (p27)
Holme Brook rose on 20 October 2023 (gauge upstream of Purbeck Avenue Bridge reached a very high level) and contributed to the fluvial flooding that caused internal flooding across Brampton. (p28)
Holme Brook rose on 20 Oct, beginning to rise at about 02:00 and reaching a peak level of 1.6 m at about 13:00 (the second–highest level on record). The brook is identified as a primary source of floodwater during the event, overtopping in places and contributing flow routes into the Brampton community. [IMG,PAG] (p29)
Holme Brook overtopped its banks causing internal flooding of nearby properties and local infrastructure damage; overtopping is linked to likely internal flooding of properties on Chester Street and collapse of a riparian brick wall adjacent to West Bars roundabout. (p31)
Holme Brook corridor flooding contributed to inundation of adjacent areas and properties in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 – 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)","192 properties; 137 homes; 55 businesses (p24)
137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 properties (total) within the community shown in Table 4–1; 142 properties had flooded previously (73% of the properties that were internally flooded during Storm Babet). [PAG,IMG] (p27)
Contributed to internal flooding of 192 properties in Brampton (p28)
1.6 m peak level (second highest level ever recorded) [IMG,PAG] (p29)
8 homes and businesses (p31)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.3006462,-1.3322942,"<div id=""html_298e24bcbf1e9c29aab1f0d0ce2c6cab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Smithy Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Smithy Brook and its culverted sections overtopped/linked with recorded flood outlines during Storm Babet, causing flooding adjacent to watercourse crossings and residential streets in Renishaw and contributing to internal flooding of homes. [IMG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Smithy Brook, an ordinary watercourse through the north of Renishaw, was implicated in the Storm Babet event; recorded flood extents align with Smithy Brook and its culverted connections, and its flooding contributed to internal flooding of residential properties in the village. [PAG,IMG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Smithy Brook and its vicinity produced surface&ndash;water flooding during Storm Babet that led to internal flooding of properties; four businesses on Smithy Brook Road were internally flooded. [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopped at its culvert inlet due to exceptional rainfall and culvert blockages; this overtopping contributed to flooding across Renishaw and to flow routes that joined Sheffield Road and Emmet Carr Brook floodwater. [TAB,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping at the Smithy Brook culvert inlet (exacerbated by a blocked culvert) caused flow to travel westwards and join Sheffield Road and Emmett Carr Brook, contributing to internal property flooding in Renishaw. North East Derbyshire District Council reported peak severity on 20 Oct between 12:00 and 14:00. [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert inlet was blocked by debris following Storm Babet causing overtopping. Pooling occurred on derelict land east of Smithy Brook Road; water flowed westwards down Smithy Brook Road flooding four businesses, flowed onto the Main Road (A6135) flooding six homes, then flowed south along Emmett Carr Lane flooding eighteen homes. [PAG] (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>39 properties [IMG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>39 properties [PAG,IMG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 businesses [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four businesses; six homes; eighteen homes; internal flood depths 0.2m to 0.8m (Sheffield Road); up to 1.2m (property on Emmett Carr Lane) [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 businesses; 6 homes; 18 homes [PAG] (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Smithy Brook,England,"Smithy Brook and its culverted sections overtopped/linked with recorded flood outlines during Storm Babet, causing flooding adjacent to watercourse crossings and residential streets in Renishaw and contributing to internal flooding of homes. [IMG] (p38)
Smithy Brook, an ordinary watercourse through the north of Renishaw, was implicated in the Storm Babet event; recorded flood extents align with Smithy Brook and its culverted connections, and its flooding contributed to internal flooding of residential properties in the village. [PAG,IMG] (p39)
Smithy Brook and its vicinity produced surface–water flooding during Storm Babet that led to internal flooding of properties; four businesses on Smithy Brook Road were internally flooded. [IMG,PAG] (p40)
Overtopped at its culvert inlet due to exceptional rainfall and culvert blockages; this overtopping contributed to flooding across Renishaw and to flow routes that joined Sheffield Road and Emmet Carr Brook floodwater. [TAB,PAG] (p41)
Overtopping at the Smithy Brook culvert inlet (exacerbated by a blocked culvert) caused flow to travel westwards and join Sheffield Road and Emmett Carr Brook, contributing to internal property flooding in Renishaw. North East Derbyshire District Council reported peak severity on 20 Oct between 12:00 and 14:00. [IMG,PAG] (p42)
Culvert inlet was blocked by debris following Storm Babet causing overtopping. Pooling occurred on derelict land east of Smithy Brook Road; water flowed westwards down Smithy Brook Road flooding four businesses, flowed onto the Main Road (A6135) flooding six homes, then flowed south along Emmett Carr Lane flooding eighteen homes. [PAG] (p43)","39 properties [IMG] (p38)
39 properties [PAG,IMG] (p39)
4 businesses [IMG,PAG] (p40)
four businesses; six homes; eighteen homes; internal flood depths 0.2m to 0.8m (Sheffield Road); up to 1.2m (property on Emmett Carr Lane) [IMG,PAG] (p42)
4 businesses; 6 homes; 18 homes [PAG] (p43)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2297891,-1.4185365,"<div id=""html_9a5f572babe81685f45ef1783e24de70"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Spital Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Spital Brook, an ordinary watercourse joining the River Rother in central Chesterfield, is within the area that experienced internal flooding (central Chesterfield areas included Spital Lane). [PAG] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping or local runoff associated with Spital Brook contributed to flooding in its corridor, impacting properties on/near Spital Lane and adjacent areas in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 &ndash; 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties adjacent to Spital Brook were identified as affected and were included in the 'River Rother at Central Chesterfield' Flood Warning issued on 20 October 2023; those adjacent properties experienced internal flooding during the 19&ndash;21 October 2023 event. [PAG,IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Spital Brook exceeded channel capacity and contributed to internal flooding of properties adjacent to the brook and River Rother. (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Spital Brook overflowed and delivered floodwater to properties along Spital Lane, causing internal flooding of homes and non&ndash;residential buildings before joining the River Rother. (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties adjacent to Spital Brook in Central Chesterfield were included in the River Rother at Central Chesterfield Flood Warning, indicating Spital Brook was associated with local flooding of adjacent properties. (p58)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 homes; 4 non&ndash;residential properties (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Spital Brook,England,"Spital Brook, an ordinary watercourse joining the River Rother in central Chesterfield, is within the area that experienced internal flooding (central Chesterfield areas included Spital Lane). [PAG] (p50)
Overtopping or local runoff associated with Spital Brook contributed to flooding in its corridor, impacting properties on/near Spital Lane and adjacent areas in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 – 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)
Properties adjacent to Spital Brook were identified as affected and were included in the 'River Rother at Central Chesterfield' Flood Warning issued on 20 October 2023; those adjacent properties experienced internal flooding during the 19–21 October 2023 event. [PAG,IMG] (p53)
Spital Brook exceeded channel capacity and contributed to internal flooding of properties adjacent to the brook and River Rother. (p54)
Spital Brook overflowed and delivered floodwater to properties along Spital Lane, causing internal flooding of homes and non–residential buildings before joining the River Rother. (p56)
Properties adjacent to Spital Brook in Central Chesterfield were included in the River Rother at Central Chesterfield Flood Warning, indicating Spital Brook was associated with local flooding of adjacent properties. (p58)",3 homes; 4 non–residential properties (p56),GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.3187182,-1.4030927,"<div id=""html_5c3744c59b1655aa94d75334769bc9b6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Moss</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Moss watercourse flooded during Storm Babet and caused internal flooding to properties immediately north of the channel in Eckington. [IMG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Moss tributary is identified as the primary source of flooding that affected local properties during Storm Babet; it caused the internal flooding of the five homes and at least one commercial property in the area. It is noted that the River Rother may have contributed by preventing The Moss from discharging, but this is unclear. [PAG,IMG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Moss tributary was identified as the primary source of flooding affecting the community; flood flows from The Moss inundated low&ndash;lying areas north of the watercourse and caused internal flooding to nearby properties. Trees fell and were reported blocking The Moss after the event, creating a continued flood risk. [PAG,IMG] (p67)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties (5 residential, 3 commercial) [IMG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 homes; 1 commercial property [PAG,IMG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five homes; one additional commercial property; flood depth reported around 0.6 m [PAG,IMG] (p67)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,The Moss,England,"The Moss watercourse flooded during Storm Babet and caused internal flooding to properties immediately north of the channel in Eckington. [IMG] (p64)
The Moss tributary is identified as the primary source of flooding that affected local properties during Storm Babet; it caused the internal flooding of the five homes and at least one commercial property in the area. It is noted that the River Rother may have contributed by preventing The Moss from discharging, but this is unclear. [PAG,IMG] (p66)
The Moss tributary was identified as the primary source of flooding affecting the community; flood flows from The Moss inundated low–lying areas north of the watercourse and caused internal flooding to nearby properties. Trees fell and were reported blocking The Moss after the event, creating a continued flood risk. [PAG,IMG] (p67)","8 properties (5 residential, 3 commercial) [IMG] (p64)
5 homes; 1 commercial property [PAG,IMG] (p66)
five homes; one additional commercial property; flood depth reported around 0.6 m [PAG,IMG] (p67)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.23401609034994,-1.420778984944687,"<div id=""html_f9f8e3a4b5f08357c38150aacec035cc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hollis Lane Railway Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Rother accumulated at the low spot beneath the railway underpass/bridge (East Midlands Main Line), causing pooling beneath the structure, preventing safe passage through the underpass and contributing to internal flooding of nearby ground&ndash;floor properties; footage shows people evacuating east of the underpass by wading through floodwater. [IMG] (p58)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four homes and two businesses; flood depths exceeded 1m [IMG] (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Hollis Lane Railway Bridge,England,"Floodwater from the River Rother accumulated at the low spot beneath the railway underpass/bridge (East Midlands Main Line), causing pooling beneath the structure, preventing safe passage through the underpass and contributing to internal flooding of nearby ground–floor properties; footage shows people evacuating east of the underpass by wading through floodwater. [IMG] (p58)",four homes and two businesses; flood depths exceeded 1m [IMG] (p58),GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.4834061,-1.3014261,"<div id=""html_1c92589686dda3353681554cc33b0960"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Don</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced extreme rainfall during Storm Babet (19&ndash;21 October 2023) and associated very high river flows/levels recorded at gauging stations in the sub&ndash;catchment. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During Storm Babet (19&ndash;21 October 2023) heavy rainfall produced extreme river flows in the Don sub&ndash;catchment; gauging stations recorded the highest or second&ndash;highest flows/levels on record and these extreme flows contributed to flooding impacts across the sub&ndash;catchment communities. [IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The mapped Storm Babet recorded flood outline lies inside the Don and Rother management catchment area; watercourses and culverted watercourses within this management catchment intersect the flood zone, indicating the catchment experienced river flooding during the event. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Recorded rainfall equating to 1.1%&ndash;3.4% AEP at stations across the catchment; all gauging stations produced the highest or second&ndash;highest ranked flows or levels on record. [IMG] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Don,England,"Experienced extreme rainfall during Storm Babet (19–21 October 2023) and associated very high river flows/levels recorded at gauging stations in the sub–catchment. (p3)
During Storm Babet (19–21 October 2023) heavy rainfall produced extreme river flows in the Don sub–catchment; gauging stations recorded the highest or second–highest flows/levels on record and these extreme flows contributed to flooding impacts across the sub–catchment communities. [IMG] (p4)
The mapped Storm Babet recorded flood outline lies inside the Don and Rother management catchment area; watercourses and culverted watercourses within this management catchment intersect the flood zone, indicating the catchment experienced river flooding during the event. [IMG] (p25)",Recorded rainfall equating to 1.1%–3.4% AEP at stations across the catchment; all gauging stations produced the highest or second–highest ranked flows or levels on record. [IMG] (p4),GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2718354,-1.377915,"<div id=""html_24b268c8e00c34d652e1e0eb2f7d5872"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Rother</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded longer and slower river responses to the heavy rainfall from Storm Babet and contributed to very high flows/levels (gauging stations produced their highest or second highest ranked flows/levels). Shortest lag time between peak rainfall and peak flow in the sub&ndash;catchment was recorded near Staveley (8 hours) with the longest at Woodhouse Mill. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Whitting (a tributary of the River Rother) overtopped/was implicated in flooding on the floodplain at Station Road, contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Don and Rother management catchment (including the Rother) is shown as the context for the recorded Storm Babet flood outline; watercourses and culverts within this management catchment intersect the recorded flood extent indicating river/urban flooding affected the area. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the River Rother produced the recorded flood outline during Storm Babet that closely followed the river and inundated adjacent residential streets in South Chesterfield, contributing to internal flooding of properties. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother overtopped/was the primary source of flooding in the Chesterfield South community; flooding from the Rother produced most of the flooded homes on the adjacent floodplain (most flooded homes were west of the river). Flood defenses (walls and embankments) are present along the river. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Rother affected the Chesterfield South community. West bank defences (two flood walls and Hawthorne Street embankment) provided protection, while there are no engineered defences on the east bank. The Avenue Flood Balancing Reservoir upstream operated as intended during the event and stored water. [PAG,IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother was the primary source of flooding: it overtopped flood defence structures at several locations causing inundation of low&ndash;lying areas. Overtopping occurred near Derby Road bridge and along the west bank defences, producing flow routes through gardens into streets and causing internal flooding of homes, businesses and an electricity substation. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother overtopped its banks (floodwater reached ~76.0 mAOD in topographic data), causing floodwater to flow over the bank by Derby Road bridge into properties along Riverview, through gardens on Bridge Street and into Hawthorne Street; it also inundated properties at St Giles Close and Healaugh Way and flooded a Yorkshire Water pumping station which failed. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Rother flooded its floodplain during Storm Babet and inundated adjacent properties and infrastructure in the community centred on Tapton Terrace. [PAG,IMG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother corridor flooded, concentrating Flood Zone 3 along its banks; river flooding from the Rother caused inundation of adjacent properties in Chesterfield, contributing to internal flooding of homes and non&ndash;residential buildings during Storm Babet. [IMG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The recorded flood outline closely follows the course of the River Rother; river flooding overlapped predicted surface&ndash;water risk areas and inundated adjacent low&ndash;lying streets and properties. [PAG,IMG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Primary source of flooding that caused internal flooding across the local community, affecting residential and non&ndash;residential properties downstream of Chesterfield Tapton Bridge. [PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the primary source of flooding for this community; high river levels and overflow from the channel caused inundation of adjacent low&ndash;lying areas and properties. [PAG,IMG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding from the River Rother contributed to internal flooding in central Chesterfield; where it passes beneath Hasland Road three homes and one business were internally flooded. The Environment Agency issued a Flood Alert for the Upper River Rother Catchment and a Flood Warning for the River Rother at Tapton during the event. [PAG] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the River Rother and the River Hipper caused internal flooding across central Chesterfield. Flooding occurred at multiple locations along and adjacent to these watercourses, resulting in internal flooding of homes and businesses at Hasland Road, Spital Lane (confluence), Clayton Street, Hollis Lane / Hady Hill, Camlough Walk, Queen's Park, Ravenside Retail Park and locations near Lordsmill Street. [PAG,IMG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Channel flooding from the River Rother affected its floodplain and contributed to flooding of nearby properties and urban areas in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 &ndash; 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother caused river flooding in Central Chesterfield during the event. Affected areas adjacent to the River Rother included Horns Bridge, Alma Retail Park, Clayton Street, Spital Lane, Hasland Road and Hollis Lane. The Environment Agency issued a 'River Rother at Central Chesterfield' Flood Warning on 20 Oct 2023. The Asset Information and Maintenance Programme records no formal flood defences along the River Rother within the community, leaving riparian walls and natural/engineered high ground to provide informal protection. [PAG,IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother exceeded channel capacity and was a main driver of internal flooding to properties adjacent to the watercourse, contributing to the community flood impacts. [PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper overflowed its banks at Queens Park and Ravenside Retail Park, causing internal flooding to businesses in those locations. Floodwater reached approx. 0.7 m depth at a Queens Park business and approx. 0.8 m depth inside an internally flooded business at Ravenside Retail Park; ten businesses at Ravenside/Queens Park were flooded. Hydrology at the Chesterfield Park Road Bridge gauge on the River Hipper recorded an unprecedented peak on 20 October (gauge peak reached 2.93 m). The River Rother overflowed near Hasland Road (about 100 m upstream of its confluence with Spital Brook), causing internal flooding to one commercial property to the west of the river and four residential properties to the east. [IMG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother overflowed its banks at multiple locations in the area, contributing to internal property flooding at Hasland Road, causing flood flows toward the low spot on the A617/Horns Bridge and contributing to flooding of roads, retail parks (including Alma Retail Park) and other infrastructure. (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother overflowed its west bank at Hady Hill and elsewhere, causing internal flooding of the ground floors of The Foundry and Markham Quay apartment blocks, internal flooding of three businesses on Hady Hill between the river and the railway underpass, and internal flooding of properties on Hollis Lane; sewage was reported at some flooded buildings indicating sewer overwhelm. (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Rother accumulated at a low spot beneath the railway underpass on Hollis Lane / Hady Hill, causing pooling and contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties; individuals evacuated the area by wading through floodwater at depths that exceeded 1 m. [PAG] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The community sits on the River Rother floodplain and flooding from the river contributed to internal flooding of properties at Meadow Close (New Whittington). [PAG] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the River Rother during Storm Babet affected its floodplain; the river's flood extent reached the area south of Meadow Close and is described as contributing to the internal flooding of nearby homes. [PAG,IMG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the River Rother produced river flooding that impacted adjacent residential areas, including Meadow Close. The recorded flood outline from Storm Babet overlaps the river and nearby streets, indicating the river was the source of flooding in the locality. [PAG,IMG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels rose from the evening of 19 October, peaking at the Whittington gauge on 20 October 2023 and contributing to the recorded flood extent that affected nearby Meadow Close and Station Lane. [PAG,IMG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Rother levels rose sharply (gauge peak 20 Oct) and contributed to the flood extent in the Meadow Close area; floodwater from the river crossed Station Lane and is assessed as likely the dominant source causing the final extent of flooding that led to internal flooding of homes. [IMG,PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Rother (together with its tributary The Moss) overtopped/caused river flooding that contributed to internal flooding of properties in Eckington; most flooded homes were west of the River Rother and south of The Moss. [PAG,IMG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High levels in the River Rother may have prevented The Moss tributary from discharging, possibly exacerbating flooding on the floodplain and contributing to inundation of adjacent properties. [PAG,IMG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Rother may have contributed indirectly by holding high levels that potentially prevented The Moss from discharging, but its direct contribution to the inundation is unclear. [PAG,IMG] (p67)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 hours (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>103 properties [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two flood walls totalling 170m; Hawthorne Street embankment 170m; reservoir stored about 100m^3 [PAG,IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>103 homes; 3 businesses; 1 electricity substation [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>topographic level reached 76.0 mAOD; flood depths exceeded 0.8 m (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>26 residential properties; 17 non&ndash;residential properties; 1 death [PAG,IMG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 non&ndash;residential properties [IMG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>43 properties affected in total; 26 homes (Tapton Terrace); 17 non&ndash;residential properties (Brimington Road and Holbeck Close) [PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>43 properties flooded (26 residential homes on Tapton Terrace; 17 non&ndash;residential properties in nearby industrial areas) [PAG,IMG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 homes; 1 business [PAG] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Hasland Road: 3 homes; 1 business; Spital Lane (confluence with River Hipper): 3 homes; 4 businesses; Clayton Street: 10 businesses; Hollis Lane / Hady Hill: 4 homes; 5 businesses; Camlough Walk: 39 homes [PAG,IMG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 businesses; 1 commercial property; 4 residential properties; approx. 0.7 m flood depth at Queens Park business; approx. 0.8 m flood depth at an internally flooded business at Ravenside Retail Park [IMG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property; 4 residential properties; 4 businesses; around 200 homes without power (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 apartment blocks; 3 businesses on Hady Hill; 4 homes and 2 businesses on Hollis Lane (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 homes; 2 businesses; flood depths &gt;1 m [PAG] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 properties [PAG] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 properties [PAG,IMG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 internally flooded properties (on Meadow Close) attributable to river flooding in the area [PAG,IMG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>peak level 4.09 m recorded at the Whittington gauge (20 Oct 2023) [PAG,IMG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 internally flooded homes [IMG,PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties (5 residential, 3 commercial) [PAG,IMG] (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Rother,England,"Recorded longer and slower river responses to the heavy rainfall from Storm Babet and contributed to very high flows/levels (gauging stations produced their highest or second highest ranked flows/levels). Shortest lag time between peak rainfall and peak flow in the sub–catchment was recorded near Staveley (8 hours) with the longest at Woodhouse Mill. (p3)
The River Whitting (a tributary of the River Rother) overtopped/was implicated in flooding on the floodplain at Station Road, contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
The Don and Rother management catchment (including the Rother) is shown as the context for the recorded Storm Babet flood outline; watercourses and culverts within this management catchment intersect the recorded flood extent indicating river/urban flooding affected the area. [IMG] (p25)
Flooding of the River Rother produced the recorded flood outline during Storm Babet that closely followed the river and inundated adjacent residential streets in South Chesterfield, contributing to internal flooding of properties. [IMG] (p33)
The River Rother overtopped/was the primary source of flooding in the Chesterfield South community; flooding from the Rother produced most of the flooded homes on the adjacent floodplain (most flooded homes were west of the river). Flood defenses (walls and embankments) are present along the river. [IMG,PAG] (p34)
Flooding from the River Rother affected the Chesterfield South community. West bank defences (two flood walls and Hawthorne Street embankment) provided protection, while there are no engineered defences on the east bank. The Avenue Flood Balancing Reservoir upstream operated as intended during the event and stored water. [PAG,IMG] (p35)
The River Rother was the primary source of flooding: it overtopped flood defence structures at several locations causing inundation of low–lying areas. Overtopping occurred near Derby Road bridge and along the west bank defences, producing flow routes through gardens into streets and causing internal flooding of homes, businesses and an electricity substation. [PAG] (p36)
The River Rother overtopped its banks (floodwater reached ~76.0 mAOD in topographic data), causing floodwater to flow over the bank by Derby Road bridge into properties along Riverview, through gardens on Bridge Street and into Hawthorne Street; it also inundated properties at St Giles Close and Healaugh Way and flooded a Yorkshire Water pumping station which failed. (p37)
The Rother flooded its floodplain during Storm Babet and inundated adjacent properties and infrastructure in the community centred on Tapton Terrace. [PAG,IMG] (p45)
The River Rother corridor flooded, concentrating Flood Zone 3 along its banks; river flooding from the Rother caused inundation of adjacent properties in Chesterfield, contributing to internal flooding of homes and non–residential buildings during Storm Babet. [IMG] (p46)
The recorded flood outline closely follows the course of the River Rother; river flooding overlapped predicted surface–water risk areas and inundated adjacent low–lying streets and properties. [PAG,IMG] (p47)
Primary source of flooding that caused internal flooding across the local community, affecting residential and non–residential properties downstream of Chesterfield Tapton Bridge. [PAG] (p48)
Identified as the primary source of flooding for this community; high river levels and overflow from the channel caused inundation of adjacent low–lying areas and properties. [PAG,IMG] (p49)
River flooding from the River Rother contributed to internal flooding in central Chesterfield; where it passes beneath Hasland Road three homes and one business were internally flooded. The Environment Agency issued a Flood Alert for the Upper River Rother Catchment and a Flood Warning for the River Rother at Tapton during the event. [PAG] (p50)
Fluvial flooding from the River Rother and the River Hipper caused internal flooding across central Chesterfield. Flooding occurred at multiple locations along and adjacent to these watercourses, resulting in internal flooding of homes and businesses at Hasland Road, Spital Lane (confluence), Clayton Street, Hollis Lane / Hady Hill, Camlough Walk, Queen's Park, Ravenside Retail Park and locations near Lordsmill Street. [PAG,IMG] (p51)
Channel flooding from the River Rother affected its floodplain and contributed to flooding of nearby properties and urban areas in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 – 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)
The River Rother caused river flooding in Central Chesterfield during the event. Affected areas adjacent to the River Rother included Horns Bridge, Alma Retail Park, Clayton Street, Spital Lane, Hasland Road and Hollis Lane. The Environment Agency issued a 'River Rother at Central Chesterfield' Flood Warning on 20 Oct 2023. The Asset Information and Maintenance Programme records no formal flood defences along the River Rother within the community, leaving riparian walls and natural/engineered high ground to provide informal protection. [PAG,IMG] (p53)
The River Rother exceeded channel capacity and was a main driver of internal flooding to properties adjacent to the watercourse, contributing to the community flood impacts. [PAG] (p54)
The River Hipper overflowed its banks at Queens Park and Ravenside Retail Park, causing internal flooding to businesses in those locations. Floodwater reached approx. 0.7 m depth at a Queens Park business and approx. 0.8 m depth inside an internally flooded business at Ravenside Retail Park; ten businesses at Ravenside/Queens Park were flooded. Hydrology at the Chesterfield Park Road Bridge gauge on the River Hipper recorded an unprecedented peak on 20 October (gauge peak reached 2.93 m). The River Rother overflowed near Hasland Road (about 100 m upstream of its confluence with Spital Brook), causing internal flooding to one commercial property to the west of the river and four residential properties to the east. [IMG] (p55)
The River Rother overflowed its banks at multiple locations in the area, contributing to internal property flooding at Hasland Road, causing flood flows toward the low spot on the A617/Horns Bridge and contributing to flooding of roads, retail parks (including Alma Retail Park) and other infrastructure. (p56)
The River Rother overflowed its west bank at Hady Hill and elsewhere, causing internal flooding of the ground floors of The Foundry and Markham Quay apartment blocks, internal flooding of three businesses on Hady Hill between the river and the railway underpass, and internal flooding of properties on Hollis Lane; sewage was reported at some flooded buildings indicating sewer overwhelm. (p57)
Floodwater from the River Rother accumulated at a low spot beneath the railway underpass on Hollis Lane / Hady Hill, causing pooling and contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties; individuals evacuated the area by wading through floodwater at depths that exceeded 1 m. [PAG] (p58)
The community sits on the River Rother floodplain and flooding from the river contributed to internal flooding of properties at Meadow Close (New Whittington). [PAG] (p59)
Flooding of the River Rother during Storm Babet affected its floodplain; the river's flood extent reached the area south of Meadow Close and is described as contributing to the internal flooding of nearby homes. [PAG,IMG] (p60)
High flows in the River Rother produced river flooding that impacted adjacent residential areas, including Meadow Close. The recorded flood outline from Storm Babet overlaps the river and nearby streets, indicating the river was the source of flooding in the locality. [PAG,IMG] (p61)
River levels rose from the evening of 19 October, peaking at the Whittington gauge on 20 October 2023 and contributing to the recorded flood extent that affected nearby Meadow Close and Station Lane. [PAG,IMG] (p62)
River Rother levels rose sharply (gauge peak 20 Oct) and contributed to the flood extent in the Meadow Close area; floodwater from the river crossed Station Lane and is assessed as likely the dominant source causing the final extent of flooding that led to internal flooding of homes. [IMG,PAG] (p63)
River Rother (together with its tributary The Moss) overtopped/caused river flooding that contributed to internal flooding of properties in Eckington; most flooded homes were west of the River Rother and south of The Moss. [PAG,IMG] (p65)
High levels in the River Rother may have prevented The Moss tributary from discharging, possibly exacerbating flooding on the floodplain and contributing to inundation of adjacent properties. [PAG,IMG] (p66)
