GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2336369,-1.4443253,"<div id=""html_b14ca4f01d24121c80361a5c525a7f68"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Hipper</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Quicker and earlier river responses to the heavy rainfall from Storm Babet were recorded on the River Hipper (near Chesterfield and Holymoorside); gauging stations produced the highest or second&ndash;highest ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Hipper (ordinary watercourse through Holymoorside) inundated residential properties in the village, causing internal flooding. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows through Holymoorside on its floodplain and contributed to flooding during the event; flood outlines were recorded along the watercourse and at nearby road crossings, associated with internal flooding of local properties. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels at the Holymoorside gauge on 20 October 2023 (rise beginning ~00:00, peaking ~12:00) contributed to the flood extent in Holymoorside and inundation of nearby residential properties and a business. River Hipper levels reached a record gauge reading and are identified as a causal contributor to the internal flooding of properties on The Alders, New Road and Loads Road. [IMG,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper rose rapidly (peak gauge at Holymoorside on 20 Oct) and overtopped its banks at multiple locations through the village and near Old Mill Pond; overtopping entered properties from the rear (including The Alders, New Road and Loads Road) and contributed directly to the internal flooding of homes and the overtopping of Old Mill Pond. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river overtopped its banks at multiple locations near Old Mill Pond and through Holymoorside; Old Mill Pond overtopped local to a weir. Overtopping upstream of Loads Road bridge and restricted bridge arch capacity caused floodwater to flow east along Loads Road and pool at the Loads Road / New Road low spot, contributing to internal flooding of properties (including The Alders and two homes at the New Road/Loads Road junction). [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper flooded during the storm and is identified as the main river flowing through the community; floodwaters from the River Hipper resulted in internal flooding of nearby properties (all flooded properties are within 75 m of the river). The Storm Babet recorded flood outline closely follows the River Hipper corridor. [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Hipper affected the adjacent Chatsworth Road West community; properties on the river floodplain and at similar elevation to the top bank were internally flooded. [PAG,IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Hipper water levels rose during the event, reaching a recorded peak (gauge c.20 m upstream of Walton Road bridge reached 3.59 m, the highest on record) on 20 Oct, with levels rising from early morning and peaking at 13:00; these high river levels caused flooding of the nearby residential properties. [PAG,IMG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper exceeded its channel and breached its banks at multiple locations in the community. At Chapel Lane West the river breached the north bank and flowed north up the street. River water entered five homes on Walton Road through their rear gardens and flooded them internally. River flows are also understood to have been a dominant source of flooding at Chapel Lane West and are likely connected to flooding affecting Grove Cottages (possible flows through drainage holes in the riverside wall). Residents reported waist&ndash;high water at the south end of Chapel Street West adjacent to the River Hipper. [IMG,PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper flowed through Brampton and was a principal flood source during Storm Babet; it contributed to internal flooding across the community. The Hipper is heavily modified and culverted at multiple locations within the community, which is described in the report as part of the flooding mechanism. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Hipper overtopped/caused fluvial flooding across the local floodplain during the event, inundating built&ndash;up areas south of Chatsworth Road (including Wheatbridge Road, Dock Walk and Anna Street West). Topographic data shows flooded properties sit at similar elevation to the top bank, with low spots in the floodplain contributing to inundation. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Hipper is a primary watercourse in the Brampton floodplain and is identified as concentrating the highest flood risk along its banks (Flood Zone 3), contributing to the flooding of adjacent properties and areas. [PAG,IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper is within the community floodplain and its Flood Warning Area ('River Hipper at Brampton') covered the majority of properties that flooded internally during the event. Flooding from the Hipper (and associated surface&ndash;water interaction where culverts are present) led to internal flooding of properties in Brampton. [PAG,IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Hipper rose to very high levels on 20 October 2023 and its rise contributed to fluvial flooding that led to internal flooding in the Brampton community. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopped on the right bank upstream of Factory Street Bridge and was a primary source of floodwater that inundated a derelict brownfield site, flowed onto Factory Street and into the Lidl car park, and generated flow routes into low&ndash;lying areas of the community. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper overtopped near a derelict brownfield site upstream of Factory Street, inundating that site and initiating flooding of Factory Street, the Lidl car park and downstream sections of Chatsworth Road; the river also surcharged a culvert beneath the Lidl entrance allowing river water to join the road network. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper overtopped just upstream of the culvert beneath Boythorpe Road, contributing to localised flooding and infrastructure damage. Overtopping and high flows are linked to internal flooding in the adjacent streets and to structural damage (a riparian&ndash;owned brick wall collapsed upstream of the culvert immediately west of Boythorpe Road). [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood Warning issued for the River Hipper at Brampton on 20/10/2023 (11:15). EA/FIDO reported River Hipper flooding with lots of debris and land drainage blockages, a collapsed wall was reported on the Hipper in Chesterfield, and post&ndash;event channel clearance and inspections were carried out (including identification of defects in retaining/boundary walls and a culvert). Floodwater made some properties inaccessible and prompted sandbag requests from local streets. [TAB,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding along the River Hipper caused internal flooding to businesses at multiple locations as it passed through central Chesterfield. [PAG,IMG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping/channel flooding from the River Hipper inundated adjacent floodplain and contributed to flooding of properties and urban areas in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 &ndash; 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Hipper led to internal flooding of properties adjacent to the watercourse; a Flood Warning for 'The River Hipper at Central Chesterfield' was issued on 20 Oct 2023 covering the internally flooded properties (excluding one business on Park Road). [PAG,IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper overflowed its banks, causing internal flooding to businesses at Queens Park and Ravenside Retail Park; watermarks show substantial internal inundation at affected buildings. [PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper overflowed its banks at Queens Park and Ravenside Retail Park, causing internal flooding to businesses in those locations. Floodwater reached approx. 0.7 m depth at a Queens Park business and approx. 0.8 m depth inside an internally flooded business at Ravenside Retail Park; ten businesses at Ravenside/Queens Park were flooded. Hydrology at the Chesterfield Park Road Bridge gauge on the River Hipper recorded an unprecedented peak on 20 October (gauge peak reached 2.93 m). The River Rother overflowed near Hasland Road (about 100 m upstream of its confluence with Spital Brook), causing internal flooding to one commercial property to the west of the river and four residential properties to the east. [IMG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Hipper overtopped near the Clayton Street Bridge, initiating flooding that flowed north down Clayton Street and contributed to internal flooding of properties on Clayton Street. (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties along the River Hipper in Central Chesterfield during the October 2023 event; Environment Agency issued a Flood Warning for the River Hipper that included the internally flooded properties. (p58)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties; approximately 50 cm internal flood depth indicated by a watermark on one property (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 residential properties flooded internally; 1 business flooded internally; 6 of the 7 flooded homes were within the 'Upper River Rother catchment' Flood Alert; 5 of the 7 flooded homes are in Flood Zone 3 [IMG,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two internally flooded homes at New Road and Loads Road junction; multiple overtopping locations (unspecified); The Alders flooded from multiple sides [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 properties; 8 on the north bank; 10 on the south bank; all internally flooded properties within 75 m of the river [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 internally flooded properties [PAG,IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 properties; eight on north bank; ten on south bank [PAG,IMG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 homes; 18 properties (total area&ndash;wide internal flooding across Chatsworth Road, Walton Road, Chapel Lane West and Grove Cottages) [IMG,PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>192 properties; 137 homes; 55 businesses (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 properties [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 properties (total) within the community shown in Table 4&ndash;1; 142 properties had flooded previously (73% of the properties that were internally flooded during Storm Babet). [PAG,IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to internal flooding of 192 properties in Brampton (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths exceeding 0.5m; up to 1m in some low spots; areas with about 0.4m reported [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths in locations fed by the river overtopping exceeded 0.5m [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one business internally flooded; one riparian&ndash;owned brick wall collapsed [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 lengths of retaining or boundary walls; 1 culvert defect report; 3 culverts inspected (no issues reported for those); 1 abutment defect report [TAB,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one business at Queen's Park; nine business units south of Ravenside Retail Park; two businesses south of Lordsmill Street; contributed to flooding at the Rother&ndash;Hipper confluence (three homes and four businesses at Spital Lane) [PAG,IMG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 businesses [PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 businesses; 1 commercial property; 4 residential properties; approx. 0.7 m flood depth at Queens Park business; approx. 0.8 m flood depth at an internally flooded business at Ravenside Retail Park [IMG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 non&ndash;residential properties (p57)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Hipper,England,"Quicker and earlier river responses to the heavy rainfall from Storm Babet were recorded on the River Hipper (near Chesterfield and Holymoorside); gauging stations produced the highest or second–highest ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3)
Flooding from the River Hipper (ordinary watercourse through Holymoorside) inundated residential properties in the village, causing internal flooding. (p14)
Flows through Holymoorside on its floodplain and contributed to flooding during the event; flood outlines were recorded along the watercourse and at nearby road crossings, associated with internal flooding of local properties. [PAG,IMG] (p15)
High river levels at the Holymoorside gauge on 20 October 2023 (rise beginning ~00:00, peaking ~12:00) contributed to the flood extent in Holymoorside and inundation of nearby residential properties and a business. River Hipper levels reached a record gauge reading and are identified as a causal contributor to the internal flooding of properties on The Alders, New Road and Loads Road. [IMG,PAG] (p17)
The River Hipper rose rapidly (peak gauge at Holymoorside on 20 Oct) and overtopped its banks at multiple locations through the village and near Old Mill Pond; overtopping entered properties from the rear (including The Alders, New Road and Loads Road) and contributed directly to the internal flooding of homes and the overtopping of Old Mill Pond. (p18)
The river overtopped its banks at multiple locations near Old Mill Pond and through Holymoorside; Old Mill Pond overtopped local to a weir. Overtopping upstream of Loads Road bridge and restricted bridge arch capacity caused floodwater to flow east along Loads Road and pool at the Loads Road / New Road low spot, contributing to internal flooding of properties (including The Alders and two homes at the New Road/Loads Road junction). [PAG] (p19)
The River Hipper flooded during the storm and is identified as the main river flowing through the community; floodwaters from the River Hipper resulted in internal flooding of nearby properties (all flooded properties are within 75 m of the river). The Storm Babet recorded flood outline closely follows the River Hipper corridor. [PAG,IMG] (p20)
Flooding from the River Hipper affected the adjacent Chatsworth Road West community; properties on the river floodplain and at similar elevation to the top bank were internally flooded. [PAG,IMG] (p21)
River Hipper water levels rose during the event, reaching a recorded peak (gauge c.20 m upstream of Walton Road bridge reached 3.59 m, the highest on record) on 20 Oct, with levels rising from early morning and peaking at 13:00; these high river levels caused flooding of the nearby residential properties. [PAG,IMG] (p22)
The River Hipper exceeded its channel and breached its banks at multiple locations in the community. At Chapel Lane West the river breached the north bank and flowed north up the street. River water entered five homes on Walton Road through their rear gardens and flooded them internally. River flows are also understood to have been a dominant source of flooding at Chapel Lane West and are likely connected to flooding affecting Grove Cottages (possible flows through drainage holes in the riverside wall). Residents reported waist–high water at the south end of Chapel Street West adjacent to the River Hipper. [IMG,PAG] (p23)
The River Hipper flowed through Brampton and was a principal flood source during Storm Babet; it contributed to internal flooding across the community. The Hipper is heavily modified and culverted at multiple locations within the community, which is described in the report as part of the flooding mechanism. (p24)
River Hipper overtopped/caused fluvial flooding across the local floodplain during the event, inundating built–up areas south of Chatsworth Road (including Wheatbridge Road, Dock Walk and Anna Street West). Topographic data shows flooded properties sit at similar elevation to the top bank, with low spots in the floodplain contributing to inundation. [IMG] (p25)
River Hipper is a primary watercourse in the Brampton floodplain and is identified as concentrating the highest flood risk along its banks (Flood Zone 3), contributing to the flooding of adjacent properties and areas. [PAG,IMG] (p26)
The River Hipper is within the community floodplain and its Flood Warning Area ('River Hipper at Brampton') covered the majority of properties that flooded internally during the event. Flooding from the Hipper (and associated surface–water interaction where culverts are present) led to internal flooding of properties in Brampton. [PAG,IMG] (p27)
River Hipper rose to very high levels on 20 October 2023 and its rise contributed to fluvial flooding that led to internal flooding in the Brampton community. (p28)
Overtopped on the right bank upstream of Factory Street Bridge and was a primary source of floodwater that inundated a derelict brownfield site, flowed onto Factory Street and into the Lidl car park, and generated flow routes into low–lying areas of the community. [IMG] (p29)
The River Hipper overtopped near a derelict brownfield site upstream of Factory Street, inundating that site and initiating flooding of Factory Street, the Lidl car park and downstream sections of Chatsworth Road; the river also surcharged a culvert beneath the Lidl entrance allowing river water to join the road network. [PAG] (p30)
The River Hipper overtopped just upstream of the culvert beneath Boythorpe Road, contributing to localised flooding and infrastructure damage. Overtopping and high flows are linked to internal flooding in the adjacent streets and to structural damage (a riparian–owned brick wall collapsed upstream of the culvert immediately west of Boythorpe Road). [PAG] (p31)
Flood Warning issued for the River Hipper at Brampton on 20/10/2023 (11:15). EA/FIDO reported River Hipper flooding with lots of debris and land drainage blockages, a collapsed wall was reported on the Hipper in Chesterfield, and post–event channel clearance and inspections were carried out (including identification of defects in retaining/boundary walls and a culvert). Floodwater made some properties inaccessible and prompted sandbag requests from local streets. [TAB,MUL] (p32)
Flooding along the River Hipper caused internal flooding to businesses at multiple locations as it passed through central Chesterfield. [PAG,IMG] (p51)
Overtopping/channel flooding from the River Hipper inundated adjacent floodplain and contributed to flooding of properties and urban areas in Central Chesterfield during the 19 Oct 2023 – 21 Oct 2023 event. [IMG] (p52)
Flooding from the River Hipper led to internal flooding of properties adjacent to the watercourse; a Flood Warning for 'The River Hipper at Central Chesterfield' was issued on 20 Oct 2023 covering the internally flooded properties (excluding one business on Park Road). [PAG,IMG] (p53)
The River Hipper overflowed its banks, causing internal flooding to businesses at Queens Park and Ravenside Retail Park; watermarks show substantial internal inundation at affected buildings. [PAG] (p54)
The River Hipper overflowed its banks at Queens Park and Ravenside Retail Park, causing internal flooding to businesses in those locations. Floodwater reached approx. 0.7 m depth at a Queens Park business and approx. 0.8 m depth inside an internally flooded business at Ravenside Retail Park; ten businesses at Ravenside/Queens Park were flooded. Hydrology at the Chesterfield Park Road Bridge gauge on the River Hipper recorded an unprecedented peak on 20 October (gauge peak reached 2.93 m). The River Rother overflowed near Hasland Road (about 100 m upstream of its confluence with Spital Brook), causing internal flooding to one commercial property to the west of the river and four residential properties to the east. [IMG] (p55)
The River Hipper overtopped near the Clayton Street Bridge, initiating flooding that flowed north down Clayton Street and contributed to internal flooding of properties on Clayton Street. (p57)
Internal flooding of properties along the River Hipper in Central Chesterfield during the October 2023 event; Environment Agency issued a Flood Warning for the River Hipper that included the internally flooded properties. (p58)","7 properties; approximately 50 cm internal flood depth indicated by a watermark on one property (p14)
7 properties [PAG,IMG] (p15)
7 residential properties flooded internally; 1 business flooded internally; 6 of the 7 flooded homes were within the 'Upper River Rother catchment' Flood Alert; 5 of the 7 flooded homes are in Flood Zone 3 [IMG,PAG] (p17)
two internally flooded homes at New Road and Loads Road junction; multiple overtopping locations (unspecified); The Alders flooded from multiple sides [PAG] (p19)
18 properties; 8 on the north bank; 10 on the south bank; all internally flooded properties within 75 m of the river [PAG,IMG] (p20)
18 internally flooded properties [PAG,IMG] (p21)
18 properties; eight on north bank; ten on south bank [PAG,IMG] (p22)
5 homes; 18 properties (total area–wide internal flooding across Chatsworth Road, Walton Road, Chapel Lane West and Grove Cottages) [IMG,PAG] (p23)
192 properties; 137 homes; 55 businesses (p24)
137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 properties [IMG] (p25)
137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 properties (total) within the community shown in Table 4–1; 142 properties had flooded previously (73% of the properties that were internally flooded during Storm Babet). [PAG,IMG] (p27)
Contributed to internal flooding of 192 properties in Brampton (p28)
flood depths exceeding 0.5m; up to 1m in some low spots; areas with about 0.4m reported [IMG] (p29)
