10 businesses; 1 commercial property; 4 residential properties; approx. 0.7 m flood depth at Queens Park business; approx. 0.8 m flood depth at an internally flooded business at Ravenside Retail Park [IMG] (p55)
10 non–residential properties (p57)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.239475,-1.3190622,"<div id=""html_d44497ea4b24768c5cb5da19f55a69ef"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Doe Lea</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Historic flooding from the River Doe Lea inundated the area now occupied by the Erin Court housing development in June 2007. During Storm Babet (19&ndash;21 Oct 2023) the recorded flood outline overlaps the River Doe Lea corridor and adjacent residential streets (notably the Erin Court development, The Grove and Cottage Close), indicating inundation of the river corridor and nearby properties during that event. It is likely the river/floodplain contributed to the observed flooding. [IMG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Doe Lea catchment was the subject of a Flood Alert covering parts of Poolsbrook; maintenance of the flap valve where Ditch 1 joins the Doe Lea is being undertaken to ensure continued functionality against flooding. (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Doe Lea,England,"Historic flooding from the River Doe Lea inundated the area now occupied by the Erin Court housing development in June 2007. During Storm Babet (19–21 Oct 2023) the recorded flood outline overlaps the River Doe Lea corridor and adjacent residential streets (notably the Erin Court development, The Grove and Cottage Close), indicating inundation of the river corridor and nearby properties during that event. It is likely the river/floodplain contributed to the observed flooding. [IMG,MUL] (p5)
The River Doe Lea catchment was the subject of a Flood Alert covering parts of Poolsbrook; maintenance of the flap valve where Ditch 1 joins the Doe Lea is being undertaken to ensure continued functionality against flooding. (p9)",,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2649634,-1.4288231,"<div id=""html_d8425c768a8d089215dd473203e5bf70"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Whitting</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the River Whitting during Storm Babet inundated adjacent floodplain and contributed to internal flooding of properties on Station Road. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Whitting overtopped or threatened its banks and caused river flooding of the retail/residential floodplain. Properties that flooded sit at similar elevation to the top of the River Whitting bank and are lower than surrounding ground. Flood Zone 3 covers the properties affected; the October 2023 event (19 Oct 2023 &ndash; 21 Oct 2023) produced more extensive flooding than the June 2007 event. Flood management features (walls, embankments, culverts) are present along the river corridor. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood Warnings were issued for the River Whitting at Old Whittington and Sheepbridge; properties on Station Road and a business on Whitting Valley Road flooded on the banks of the River Whitting. The river corridor and adjacent embankment were implicated in the flooding during Storm Babet. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Whitting was the primary source of flooding: it rose on 20 Oct and overtopped its east bank (south of the Station Road bridge), initiating the overland flow that inundated Whitting Valley Road and Station Road properties. Gauge data at Sheepbridge reached a peak (highest recorded) and coincided with the timing of impacts in the community. [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 residential properties [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties (9 on Station Road; 1 on Whitting Valley Road) internally flooded [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak gauge level 2.56m; 10 properties internally flooded in the affected area (9 on Station Road: 8 homes and 1 business; 1 business on Whitting Valley Road) [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Whitting,England,"Flooding of the River Whitting during Storm Babet inundated adjacent floodplain and contributed to internal flooding of properties on Station Road. [PAG] (p10)
River Whitting overtopped or threatened its banks and caused river flooding of the retail/residential floodplain. Properties that flooded sit at similar elevation to the top of the River Whitting bank and are lower than surrounding ground. Flood Zone 3 covers the properties affected; the October 2023 event (19 Oct 2023 – 21 Oct 2023) produced more extensive flooding than the June 2007 event. Flood management features (walls, embankments, culverts) are present along the river corridor. [IMG] (p11)
Flood Warnings were issued for the River Whitting at Old Whittington and Sheepbridge; properties on Station Road and a business on Whitting Valley Road flooded on the banks of the River Whitting. The river corridor and adjacent embankment were implicated in the flooding during Storm Babet. [PAG] (p12)
The River Whitting was the primary source of flooding: it rose on 20 Oct and overtopped its east bank (south of the Station Road bridge), initiating the overland flow that inundated Whitting Valley Road and Station Road properties. Gauge data at Sheepbridge reached a peak (highest recorded) and coincided with the timing of impacts in the community. [IMG,PAG] (p13)","10 residential properties [PAG] (p10)
10 properties (9 on Station Road; 1 on Whitting Valley Road) internally flooded [PAG] (p12)
Peak gauge level 2.56m; 10 properties internally flooded in the affected area (9 on Station Road: 8 homes and 1 business; 1 business on Whitting Valley Road) [IMG,PAG] (p13)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.26440775529598,-1.4274201345879083,"<div id=""html_7ef6d9ec409644b6167f4564da498d5c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bridge was inundated by floodwater as the River Whitting overtopped; water flowed over the bridge and then westwards down Station Road, causing accumulation at a low spot and subsequent internal flooding of nearby properties. (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flow along Station Road approximately 300 m; 9 properties on Station Road internally flooded (8 homes and 1 business); flood depth at low spot ~30 cm; internal watermark ~50 cm (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Station Road Bridge,England,"The bridge was inundated by floodwater as the River Whitting overtopped; water flowed over the bridge and then westwards down Station Road, causing accumulation at a low spot and subsequent internal flooding of nearby properties. (p13)",flow along Station Road approximately 300 m; 9 properties on Station Road internally flooded (8 homes and 1 business); flood depth at low spot ~30 cm; internal watermark ~50 cm (p13),GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.23324666001673,-1.4471278282075675,"<div id=""html_8fba1185adfe23e1bd16dd1bb43c6b01"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lidl Car Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundated by floodwater from the overtopped River Hipper; the car park and the low spot at the Lidl entrance were submerged and affected by flow routes across Factory Street. (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depth at Lidl entrance exceeded 0.5m (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Lidl Car Park,England,Inundated by floodwater from the overtopped River Hipper; the car park and the low spot at the Lidl entrance were submerged and affected by flow routes across Factory Street. (p29),flood depth at Lidl entrance exceeded 0.5m (p29),GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
