15 properties [IMG] (p60)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.25,-1.41667,"<div id=""html_1731a084adc84726a53ac66bb66734b3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chesterfield</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A retail district (with large&ndash;footprint businesses and some residential properties) located on the River Whitting floodplain was flooded. Properties sit close to the top of the river bank and lower than surrounding ground. Flooding during 19 Oct 2023 &ndash; 21 Oct 2023 was more extensive than the June 2007 river flood. The flooded properties are within Flood Zone 3 and are also mapped as high risk of surface water flooding. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater pooled at a low spot on Station Road with reported depths that impeded the road; at least one property suffered internal flooding as indicated by a ~50 cm watermark. A resident on Station Road made a sandbag request on 20 October at 15:00. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River level exceedance of the River Hipper caused flooding across Chesterfield. Properties on multiple streets requested sandbags on 20/10/2023; some properties were inaccessible due to floodwater. Fire and Rescue attended the community. Debris and land drainage blockages were recorded constraining channel capacity; a collapsed wall and other channel/retaining wall defects were reported. Environment Agency and local authorities carried out post&ndash;event channel clearances and inspections, and assisted removal of debris and PFR scheme applications. [TAB] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties in South Chesterfield concentrated either side of the River Rother; recorded flood outline during Storm Babet inundated the community, causing internal flooding of homes. [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties were flooded on the community floodplain; most of the flooded homes were located west of the River Rother within the Chesterfield South community. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the Chesterfield South community experienced river&ndash;related and surface water flood impacts; the area lies within Flood Zone 3 and has extensive surface water flood risk. Local flood defences on the River Rother (west bank flood walls and embankments) were a feature of the event and the Avenue Flood Balancing Reservoir upstream operated during the event. [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported behind the embankment at Hawthorne/Sherwood Street requiring pumping on Sunday morning. Community Information Officers were deployed to Chesterfield; UPR Operations Patrol reported assets in Chesterfield appeared undamaged following patrols. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Homes in the Chesterfield community (including Tapton Terrace) were internally flooded during Storm Babet (Oct 2023). Seventeen non&ndash;residential properties flooded during the event. The Environment Agency flood mapping shows many affected properties lie within Flood Zone 3 adjacent to the River Rother; one non&ndash;residential property is in Flood Zone 2 and four are in Flood Zone 1. [IMG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Central Chesterfield suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet, affecting streets and retail areas including Camlough Walk, Spital Lane, Hasland Road, Alma Retail Park, Ravenside Retail Park, Clayton Street and Hollis Lane. (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warning/alerting and response actions occurred in Chesterfield (Upper River Rother Flood Alert on 19 Oct; River Rother at Tapton Flood Warning on 20 Oct). Emergency services attended Tapton Terrace; Environment Agency deployed Community Information Officers and sent operatives with two 75mm pumps to Tapton Terrace (one used to empty a manhole chamber). Chesterfield Borough Council provided sandbags and conducted post&ndash;event visits. One resident lost their life in Tapton Terrace and a coroner's inquest was opened. [IMG,PAG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Central Chesterfield were flooded during the reported event. The Environment Agency Historic Flood Map indicates about half of the flooded properties had previously flooded in November 2000 and June 2007 (from river or surface water). Some flooded properties had no prior records, including properties on Camlough Walk and some on Spital Lane and Hasland Road. Several flooded homes lie in Flood Zone 3 (river floodplain) and other areas show high long&ndash;term surface&ndash;water flood risk. [PAG] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Central Chesterfield experienced internal flooding during the reported event; flooding affected properties adjacent to the River Hipper and the River Rother, and parts of the urban area were exposed to surface&ndash;water flooding risk. [PAG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rivers (River Rother and River Hipper) exceeded channel capacity causing widespread flooding across the community; sewers were overwhelmed in places and surface water runoff exacerbated flooding. Internal flooding affected residences and businesses across the community. [PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in central Chesterfield was concentrated along the River Hipper and River Rother. Hydrological records (Chesterfield Park Road Bridge, River Hipper) show a peak level of 2.93 m (highest recorded) on 20 Oct 2023, with levels rising from about 02:00 and peaking at ~14:30. Near the River Rother at Hasland Road, one commercial property (west of the river) and four residential properties (east of the river) suffered internal flooding when the Rother overtopped its banks. [IMG,PAG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Central Chesterfield experienced internal flooding from the River Hipper and River Rother (including properties along Clayton Street, Camlough Walk, Hady Hill and Hollis Lane); Flood Warnings were issued for the River Hipper at Central Chesterfield and River Rother areas during the event. (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Chesterfield area during Storm Babet: collapsed banks, a missing flap valve from an outfall and sections of walls in poor condition recorded at Ravenside Retail Park; inspections along the River Hipper and investigations following surcharging at Horns Bridge which may have contributed to local flooding. [PAG] (p59)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>30 cm floodwater at Station Road low spot; ~50 cm internal flooding watermark on one property; 1 property made a sandbag request (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 streets requested sandbags; 11 lengths of retaining or boundary walls inspected; 6 defect reports completed; 1 culvert defect reported [TAB] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>103 properties [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two flood walls totalling 170m; Hawthorne Street embankment 170m; The Avenue Flood Balancing Reservoir located ~1.7km upstream; reservoir storing about 100m^3 [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 non&ndash;residential properties; 1 non&ndash;residential property in Flood Zone 2; 4 non&ndash;residential properties in Flood Zone 1; 12 of the 17 non&ndash;residential properties had flooded previously in 2007 [IMG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>49 homes and 30 businesses (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (requested sandbags in Tapton Terrace); two 75mm pumps deployed; one death [IMG,PAG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>49 homes; 30 businesses [PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2.93 m peak level on 20 Oct 2023; one commercial property; four residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 homes; 2 businesses; flood depths &gt;1 m (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Chesterfield,England,"A retail district (with large–footprint businesses and some residential properties) located on the River Whitting floodplain was flooded. Properties sit close to the top of the river bank and lower than surrounding ground. Flooding during 19 Oct 2023 – 21 Oct 2023 was more extensive than the June 2007 river flood. The flooded properties are within Flood Zone 3 and are also mapped as high risk of surface water flooding. [IMG] (p11)
Floodwater pooled at a low spot on Station Road with reported depths that impeded the road; at least one property suffered internal flooding as indicated by a ~50 cm watermark. A resident on Station Road made a sandbag request on 20 October at 15:00. (p14)
River level exceedance of the River Hipper caused flooding across Chesterfield. Properties on multiple streets requested sandbags on 20/10/2023; some properties were inaccessible due to floodwater. Fire and Rescue attended the community. Debris and land drainage blockages were recorded constraining channel capacity; a collapsed wall and other channel/retaining wall defects were reported. Environment Agency and local authorities carried out post–event channel clearances and inspections, and assisted removal of debris and PFR scheme applications. [TAB] (p32)
Internal flooding of residential properties in South Chesterfield concentrated either side of the River Rother; recorded flood outline during Storm Babet inundated the community, causing internal flooding of homes. [IMG,PAG] (p33)
Residential properties were flooded on the community floodplain; most of the flooded homes were located west of the River Rother within the Chesterfield South community. [IMG,PAG] (p34)
Properties in the Chesterfield South community experienced river–related and surface water flood impacts; the area lies within Flood Zone 3 and has extensive surface water flood risk. Local flood defences on the River Rother (west bank flood walls and embankments) were a feature of the event and the Avenue Flood Balancing Reservoir upstream operated during the event. [PAG] (p35)
Flooding was reported behind the embankment at Hawthorne/Sherwood Street requiring pumping on Sunday morning. Community Information Officers were deployed to Chesterfield; UPR Operations Patrol reported assets in Chesterfield appeared undamaged following patrols. (p38)
Homes in the Chesterfield community (including Tapton Terrace) were internally flooded during Storm Babet (Oct 2023). Seventeen non–residential properties flooded during the event. The Environment Agency flood mapping shows many affected properties lie within Flood Zone 3 adjacent to the River Rother; one non–residential property is in Flood Zone 2 and four are in Flood Zone 1. [IMG] (p46)
Central Chesterfield suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet, affecting streets and retail areas including Camlough Walk, Spital Lane, Hasland Road, Alma Retail Park, Ravenside Retail Park, Clayton Street and Hollis Lane. (p50)
Flood warning/alerting and response actions occurred in Chesterfield (Upper River Rother Flood Alert on 19 Oct; River Rother at Tapton Flood Warning on 20 Oct). Emergency services attended Tapton Terrace; Environment Agency deployed Community Information Officers and sent operatives with two 75mm pumps to Tapton Terrace (one used to empty a manhole chamber). Chesterfield Borough Council provided sandbags and conducted post–event visits. One resident lost their life in Tapton Terrace and a coroner's inquest was opened. [IMG,PAG] (p51)
Properties in Central Chesterfield were flooded during the reported event. The Environment Agency Historic Flood Map indicates about half of the flooded properties had previously flooded in November 2000 and June 2007 (from river or surface water). Some flooded properties had no prior records, including properties on Camlough Walk and some on Spital Lane and Hasland Road. Several flooded homes lie in Flood Zone 3 (river floodplain) and other areas show high long–term surface–water flood risk. [PAG] (p52)
Properties in Central Chesterfield experienced internal flooding during the reported event; flooding affected properties adjacent to the River Hipper and the River Rother, and parts of the urban area were exposed to surface–water flooding risk. [PAG] (p53)
Rivers (River Rother and River Hipper) exceeded channel capacity causing widespread flooding across the community; sewers were overwhelmed in places and surface water runoff exacerbated flooding. Internal flooding affected residences and businesses across the community. [PAG] (p54)
Flooding in central Chesterfield was concentrated along the River Hipper and River Rother. Hydrological records (Chesterfield Park Road Bridge, River Hipper) show a peak level of 2.93 m (highest recorded) on 20 Oct 2023, with levels rising from about 02:00 and peaking at ~14:30. Near the River Rother at Hasland Road, one commercial property (west of the river) and four residential properties (east of the river) suffered internal flooding when the Rother overtopped its banks. [IMG,PAG] (p55)
Central Chesterfield experienced internal flooding from the River Hipper and River Rother (including properties along Clayton Street, Camlough Walk, Hady Hill and Hollis Lane); Flood Warnings were issued for the River Hipper at Central Chesterfield and River Rother areas during the event. (p58)
Flooding in the Chesterfield area during Storm Babet: collapsed banks, a missing flap valve from an outfall and sections of walls in poor condition recorded at Ravenside Retail Park; inspections along the River Hipper and investigations following surcharging at Horns Bridge which may have contributed to local flooding. [PAG] (p59)","30 cm floodwater at Station Road low spot; ~50 cm internal flooding watermark on one property; 1 property made a sandbag request (p14)
