4 homes; 2 businesses; flood depths >1 m (p58)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2683482,-1.3495224,"<div id=""html_3fd250bc6729d4efa8633e36f8a24f20"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Staveley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area near Staveley recorded the shortest lag time between peak rainfall and peak river flow (8 hours) during Storm Babet; the sub&ndash;catchment produced very high flows/levels at gauging stations. Poolsbrook, a village close to Staveley, experienced internal flooding of seven properties as a result of the event. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 hours; seven properties [PAG] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Staveley,England,"Area near Staveley recorded the shortest lag time between peak rainfall and peak river flow (8 hours) during Storm Babet; the sub–catchment produced very high flows/levels at gauging stations. Poolsbrook, a village close to Staveley, experienced internal flooding of seven properties as a result of the event. [PAG] (p3)",8 hours; seven properties [PAG] (p3),GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2582548,-1.3393794,"<div id=""html_fd24ccad2fbf4a8defa61dbf252e10f4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Poolsbrook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seven residential properties suffered internal flooding in the main group of houses west of the River Doe Lea during Storm Babet; flooding occurred in the area west of the River Doe Lea with nearby ordinary watercourses and ditches (Pools Brook, Ditch 1, Ditch 2) connected through the local drainage network. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seven residential properties suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet. The recorded Storm Babet flood outline (pink) covered central and eastern parts of the village, particularly areas near Staveley Road, The Grove and Cottage Close. Flooding was associated with local ordinary watercourses and ditches (Pools Brook, Ditch 1 and Ditch 2) in the vicinity of the affected properties. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded flood outline from Storm Babet (Oct 2023) overlaps parts of the community; homes within the community were flooded during the event and homes near Ditch 1 have high surface&ndash;water flood risk. [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding occurred to properties within the village during Storm Babet. Six residential properties and one non&ndash;residential property suffered internal flooding across two streets: five properties adjacent to the Staveley Road / Poolsbrook Crescent junction and two newer&ndash;build homes on the Erin Court development. National mapping also shows a high surface&ndash;water flood risk (≥3.3% AEP) for homes near Ditch 1 and along low&ndash;lying areas adjacent to the River Doe Lea. [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood extent at Poolsbrook covered the low spot at the Staveley Road / Poolsbrook Crescent junction and surrounding area; parts of Erin Court and Staveley Road were impacted and land surrounding Ditch 1 flooded. In total six residential properties and one non&ndash;residential property suffered internal flooding across the area. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from woodland and from Ditch 2 flowed into the Erin Court housing development, internally flooding two newer&ndash;build homes. That water then flowed downslope along Staveley Road to the low spot at the Staveley Road / Poolsbrook Crescent junction, resulting in internal flooding of properties at that low spot. The sewer network also discharged at locations including the rear of No.58 Staveley Road, the junction of Staveley Road and Poolsbrook Crescent, and from a trunk sewer adjacent to the eastern end of Ditch 1, which contributed to the event. Land surrounding Ditch 1 also flooded. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed from Ditch 2 and the ordinary watercourse to the south, with a partially blocked headwall identified during investigations as likely contributing to flooding. Overland flow from the ordinary watercourse to the south was also identified as a contributing/exacerbating source. The Environment Agency flood alert covered part of Poolsbrook to the east (though internally&ndash;flooded properties were not located within the alert area). [PAG] (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Seven properties [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>six residential properties; one non&ndash;residential property; five properties on Staveley Road; two properties on the Erin Court development [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 residential properties; 1 non&ndash;residential property [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 residential properties; 1 non&ndash;residential property; 5 properties on Staveley Road; 2 homes on the Erin Court development [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Poolsbrook,England,"Seven residential properties suffered internal flooding in the main group of houses west of the River Doe Lea during Storm Babet; flooding occurred in the area west of the River Doe Lea with nearby ordinary watercourses and ditches (Pools Brook, Ditch 1, Ditch 2) connected through the local drainage network. [PAG] (p3)
Seven residential properties suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet. The recorded Storm Babet flood outline (pink) covered central and eastern parts of the village, particularly areas near Staveley Road, The Grove and Cottage Close. Flooding was associated with local ordinary watercourses and ditches (Pools Brook, Ditch 1 and Ditch 2) in the vicinity of the affected properties. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)
Recorded flood outline from Storm Babet (Oct 2023) overlaps parts of the community; homes within the community were flooded during the event and homes near Ditch 1 have high surface–water flood risk. [IMG,PAG] (p5)
Internal flooding occurred to properties within the village during Storm Babet. Six residential properties and one non–residential property suffered internal flooding across two streets: five properties adjacent to the Staveley Road / Poolsbrook Crescent junction and two newer–build homes on the Erin Court development. National mapping also shows a high surface–water flood risk (≥3.3% AEP) for homes near Ditch 1 and along low–lying areas adjacent to the River Doe Lea. [IMG,PAG] (p6)
Flood extent at Poolsbrook covered the low spot at the Staveley Road / Poolsbrook Crescent junction and surrounding area; parts of Erin Court and Staveley Road were impacted and land surrounding Ditch 1 flooded. In total six residential properties and one non–residential property suffered internal flooding across the area. [PAG] (p7)
Floodwater from woodland and from Ditch 2 flowed into the Erin Court housing development, internally flooding two newer–build homes. That water then flowed downslope along Staveley Road to the low spot at the Staveley Road / Poolsbrook Crescent junction, resulting in internal flooding of properties at that low spot. The sewer network also discharged at locations including the rear of No.58 Staveley Road, the junction of Staveley Road and Poolsbrook Crescent, and from a trunk sewer adjacent to the eastern end of Ditch 1, which contributed to the event. Land surrounding Ditch 1 also flooded. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
Floodwater flowed from Ditch 2 and the ordinary watercourse to the south, with a partially blocked headwall identified during investigations as likely contributing to flooding. Overland flow from the ordinary watercourse to the south was also identified as a contributing/exacerbating source. The Environment Agency flood alert covered part of Poolsbrook to the east (though internally–flooded properties were not located within the alert area). [PAG] (p9)","Seven properties [PAG] (p3)
7 properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)
six residential properties; one non–residential property; five properties on Staveley Road; two properties on the Erin Court development [IMG,PAG] (p6)
6 residential properties; 1 non–residential property [PAG] (p7)
6 residential properties; 1 non–residential property; 5 properties on Staveley Road; 2 homes on the Erin Court development [PAG,IMG] (p8)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2573005,-1.4282674,"<div id=""html_b43880ebaee73be2fe29683298c19268"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Whittington Moor</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ten residential properties on Station Road suffered internal flooding driven by fluvial flooding associated with Storm Babet. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties and streets in the Whittington Moor community were affected by river flooding originating from the River Whitting; internal flooding occurred within the community as floodwater flowed along Station Road and affected properties adjacent to the river. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 residential properties (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties internally flooded; 9 on Station Road (8 homes and 1 business) and 1 business on Whitting Valley Road [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Whittington Moor,England,"Ten residential properties on Station Road suffered internal flooding driven by fluvial flooding associated with Storm Babet. (p9)
Properties and streets in the Whittington Moor community were affected by river flooding originating from the River Whitting; internal flooding occurred within the community as floodwater flowed along Station Road and affected properties adjacent to the river. [IMG] (p13)","10 residential properties (p9)
10 properties internally flooded; 9 on Station Road (8 homes and 1 business) and 1 business on Whitting Valley Road [IMG] (p13)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.2194066,-1.4962475,"<div id=""html_ade1517862fb8bdf5dde077118c68382"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holymoorside</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in the village suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet; the affected area is residential (mostly detached homes). The local primary school did not flood. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seven residential properties (mostly detached homes) suffered internal flooding during the event. The community lies on the floodplain of the River Hipper; five of the seven flooded homes are in Flood Zone 3 and two in Flood Zone 1. All internally flooded homes have a medium surface water flood risk (some are high). The primary school did not flood. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During Storm Babet (19 Oct 2023 &ndash; 21 Oct 2023) seven homes in the Holymoorside community suffered internal flooding. The community lies on the floodplain of the River Hipper; five of the seven flooded homes are located in Flood Zone 3 and two are in Flood Zone 1. The Environment Agency Historic Flood Map indicates the properties that flooded had not flooded previously. All internally flooded homes are shown as medium risk of surface water flooding on the Long Term Flood Risk Map, and some of these internally flooded homes are shown as high surface&ndash;water risk. The local primary school is the only known vulnerable building type in the community but it did not suffer flooding during Storm Babet. [PAG,IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties during Storm Babet. Seven residential properties experienced internal flooding and one business also flooded. The community is on the River Hipper floodplain; six of the seven internally flooded homes were covered by the 'Upper River Rother catchment' Flood Alert. Five of the seven flooded homes are in Flood Zone 3 (river &gt;1% AEP); two are in Flood Zone 1. All internally flooded homes have medium surface&ndash;water flood risk; some have high surface&ndash;water flood risk. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Hipper overtopped banks at multiple locations through the village (including near Old Mill Pond), Old Mill Pond overtopped, and floodwater and runoff flowed through the village (notably along Loads Road) pooling at the Loads Road / New Road low spot. This led to internal flooding of residential properties on The Alders, New Road and Loads Road, internal flooding of one business, highway flooding and significant soil/silt deposits on Loads Road. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 homes; 5 of the 7 homes; 2 of the 7 homes [PAG,IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 residential properties; 1 business; 6 of the 7 homes were within the Flood Alert; 5 of the 7 homes are in Flood Zone 3; 2 of the 7 homes are in Flood Zone 1. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 residential properties; 1 business [PAG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Holymoorside,England,"Residential properties in the village suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet; the affected area is residential (mostly detached homes). The local primary school did not flood. (p14)
Seven residential properties (mostly detached homes) suffered internal flooding during the event. The community lies on the floodplain of the River Hipper; five of the seven flooded homes are in Flood Zone 3 and two in Flood Zone 1. All internally flooded homes have a medium surface water flood risk (some are high). The primary school did not flood. [PAG,IMG] (p15)
During Storm Babet (19 Oct 2023 – 21 Oct 2023) seven homes in the Holymoorside community suffered internal flooding. The community lies on the floodplain of the River Hipper; five of the seven flooded homes are located in Flood Zone 3 and two are in Flood Zone 1. The Environment Agency Historic Flood Map indicates the properties that flooded had not flooded previously. All internally flooded homes are shown as medium risk of surface water flooding on the Long Term Flood Risk Map, and some of these internally flooded homes are shown as high surface–water risk. The local primary school is the only known vulnerable building type in the community but it did not suffer flooding during Storm Babet. [PAG,IMG] (p16)
Internal flooding of residential properties during Storm Babet. Seven residential properties experienced internal flooding and one business also flooded. The community is on the River Hipper floodplain; six of the seven internally flooded homes were covered by the 'Upper River Rother catchment' Flood Alert. Five of the seven flooded homes are in Flood Zone 3 (river >1% AEP); two are in Flood Zone 1. All internally flooded homes have medium surface–water flood risk; some have high surface–water flood risk. [PAG] (p17)
River Hipper overtopped banks at multiple locations through the village (including near Old Mill Pond), Old Mill Pond overtopped, and floodwater and runoff flowed through the village (notably along Loads Road) pooling at the Loads Road / New Road low spot. This led to internal flooding of residential properties on The Alders, New Road and Loads Road, internal flooding of one business, highway flooding and significant soil/silt deposits on Loads Road. [PAG] (p18)","7 properties (p14)
7 properties [PAG,IMG] (p15)
