7 residential properties; 1 business [PAG] (p18)",GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19.html,GB-DBY_4_2023-10-19,Derbyshire flooding 19 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-4-don-and-rother-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,4,2023-10-19,53.23196,-1.458617,"<div id=""html_929001ad2fe2c5dfcf600b303f393327"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A Flood Warning for the River Hipper at Brampton was issued on 20 Oct 2023; the warning area included properties that subsequently flooded internally during the event. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in the community during Storm Babet; the River Hipper and Holme Brook flow through the community and contributed to flooding. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties within the Brampton community. Flooding occurred in the floodplain of the River Hipper and Holme Brook; topography places many flooded properties at similar elevation to top bank level and in low spots of the floodplain. [IMG,PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties across the community; properties sit at similar elevation to top bank level with low spots in the floodplain which contributed to inundation. Some properties had property flood resilience (PFR) measures but many flooded internally. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20 October 2023 rising levels on the River Hipper and Holme Brook caused internal flooding across the Brampton community. A total of 192 properties were internally flooded (137 homes and 55 non&ndash;residential). Street&ndash;level counts from the event: Alma Street West 53 homes; Chatsworth Road 34 homes and 34 non&ndash;residential (78 total); Brook Vale 10 homes; Hipper Street West 26 homes; Chester Street 6 homes and 2 non&ndash;residential (8 total); School Board Lane 3 homes; Hardwicks Yard 4 homes; Welfare Avenue 1 home; Factory Street 1 non&ndash;residential; Wheatbridge Road 9 non&ndash;residential; Boythorpe Road 2 non&ndash;residential; Chatsworth Road Business Park 7 non&ndash;residential. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the River Hipper (and contributions from Holme Brook) produced flow routes through the Brampton community, concentrating flooding in low&ndash;elevation areas. Impacts included inundation of a derelict brownfield site upstream of Factory Street, flooding of Factory Street (low spot and bridge area), inundation of the Lidl car park, and internal flooding of a public house on Factory Street. Flow paths affected business areas and streets and left debris caught on the Factory Street bridge. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Brampton was the location for a Flood Warning for the River Hipper (20/10/2023). The community experienced river flooding with debris and drainage blockages; some properties became inaccessible and multi&ndash;agency response (EA, DCC, Chesterfield Borough Council, Fire & Rescue) attended and provided post&ndash;event assistance including channel clearance, debris removal and drop&ndash;in support events. [TAB,MUL] (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>192 properties; 137 homes; 55 businesses (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 properties flooded total; 142 properties had flooded previously (73%) [IMG,PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>137 homes; 55 businesses; 192 total properties internally flooded; 142 properties previously flooded (73% of those internally flooded) (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>192 properties; 137 homes; 55 non&ndash;residential; Alma Street West 53 homes; Chatsworth Road 78 properties (34 homes, 34 non&ndash;residential); Brook Vale 10 homes; Hipper Street West 26 homes; Chester Street 8 properties (6 homes, 2 non&ndash;residential); School Board Lane 3 homes; Hardwicks Yard 4 homes; Welfare Avenue 1 home; Factory Street 1 non&ndash;residential; Wheatbridge Road 9 non&ndash;residential; Boythorpe Road 2 non&ndash;residential; Chatsworth Road Business Park 7 non&ndash;residential [TAB] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.6m peak level at Chesterfield Ashgate gauge (20 Oct 2023); Lidl entrance flood depth likely &gt;0.5m; flood depths up to 1m in some locations; low spot at 84.6mAOD noted with depths exceeding 50cm [IMG] (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Brampton,England,"A Flood Warning for the River Hipper at Brampton was issued on 20 Oct 2023; the warning area included properties that subsequently flooded internally during the event. [PAG] (p22)
Internal flooding of properties in the community during Storm Babet; the River Hipper and Holme Brook flow through the community and contributed to flooding. (p24)
Internal flooding of properties within the Brampton community. Flooding occurred in the floodplain of the River Hipper and Holme Brook; topography places many flooded properties at similar elevation to top bank level and in low spots of the floodplain. [IMG,PAG] (p26)
Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties across the community; properties sit at similar elevation to top bank level with low spots in the floodplain which contributed to inundation. Some properties had property flood resilience (PFR) measures but many flooded internally. (p27)
On 20 October 2023 rising levels on the River Hipper and Holme Brook caused internal flooding across the Brampton community. A total of 192 properties were internally flooded (137 homes and 55 non–residential). Street–level counts from the event: Alma Street West 53 homes; Chatsworth Road 34 homes and 34 non–residential (78 total); Brook Vale 10 homes; Hipper Street West 26 homes; Chester Street 6 homes and 2 non–residential (8 total); School Board Lane 3 homes; Hardwicks Yard 4 homes; Welfare Avenue 1 home; Factory Street 1 non–residential; Wheatbridge Road 9 non–residential; Boythorpe Road 2 non–residential; Chatsworth Road Business Park 7 non–residential. [TAB] (p28)
Overtopping of the River Hipper (and contributions from Holme Brook) produced flow routes through the Brampton community, concentrating flooding in low–elevation areas. Impacts included inundation of a derelict brownfield site upstream of Factory Street, flooding of Factory Street (low spot and bridge area), inundation of the Lidl car park, and internal flooding of a public house on Factory Street. Flow paths affected business areas and streets and left debris caught on the Factory Street bridge. [IMG] (p29)
Brampton was the location for a Flood Warning for the River Hipper (20/10/2023). The community experienced river flooding with debris and drainage blockages; some properties became inaccessible and multi–agency response (EA, DCC, Chesterfield Borough Council, Fire & Rescue) attended and provided post–event assistance including channel clearance, debris removal and drop–in support events. [TAB,MUL] (p32)","10 properties [PAG] (p22)
