GB-CAM,28,2020-12-23,52.412291392040224,-0.21659327301944808,"<div id=""html_5fb0fe3eda45d999b5ce9492e44ef843"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Braefield, Woodwalton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties was reported at this location (listed as one of three key risk areas where internal property flooding occurred). The village&ndash;level reports also note additional flooding to roads and gardens. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Field runoff routed from fields to the north caused surface water to flood one residential property to a depth of about 60mm; outbuildings associated with the property flooded to about 180mm. Anecdotal evidence indicates the flood water dissipated relatively quickly and the resident did not have to leave the property. [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Braefield experienced flooding reports tied to blocked/poorly maintained ditches and drainage; this prompted meetings with residents, LLFA site visits and subsequent ditch maintenance and gully/culvert cleansing. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Braefield, Woodwalton",England,"Internal flooding of residential properties was reported at this location (listed as one of three key risk areas where internal property flooding occurred). The village–level reports also note additional flooding to roads and gardens. (p9)
Field runoff routed from fields to the north caused surface water to flood one residential property to a depth of about 60mm; outbuildings associated with the property flooded to about 180mm. Anecdotal evidence indicates the flood water dissipated relatively quickly and the resident did not have to leave the property. [TAB,PAG] (p12)
Braefield experienced flooding reports tied to blocked/poorly maintained ditches and drainage; this prompted meetings with residents, LLFA site visits and subsequent ditch maintenance and gully/culvert cleansing. [TAB] (p13)","1 residential property [TAB,PAG] (p12)",GB-CAM_28_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_28_2020-12-23,Woodwalton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Woodwalton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Woodwalton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,28,2020-12-23,52.4617238,0.6975642,"<div id=""html_fb8912dbdc749a181e0ec24b32721414"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Anglia</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged higher&ndash;than&ndash;average rainfall produced saturated ground conditions and, together with intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020, resulted in very high runoff and significant surface water flooding across East Anglia. [PAG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,East Anglia,England,"Prolonged higher–than–average rainfall produced saturated ground conditions and, together with intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020, resulted in very high runoff and significant surface water flooding across East Anglia. [PAG] (p6)",,GB-CAM_28_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_28_2020-12-23,Woodwalton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Woodwalton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Woodwalton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,28,2020-12-23,52.4138762,-0.2108421,"<div id=""html_ea070e74c1a06a329d4987336c2ffee1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woodwalton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding occurred in Woodwalton as a result of very high runoff from the intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Six LLFA flood reports for Woodwalton describing internal flooding to residential properties, plus flooding to some roads and gardens. Three local areas were highlighted where internal property flooding was reported: New Road; The Cross and village green; and Braefield. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the 23 December 2020 flood, drainage systems in Woodwalton required maintenance. Actions taken or arranged included cleansing of highway gullies by Cambridgeshire Highways and riparian maintenance of drainage ditches (Huntingdonshire District Council visited the site and maintenance work is understood to have been carried out). The LLFA offered support to residents to set up a local flood plan and will continue to liaise with Risk Management Authorities until outstanding actions are completed. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>six flood reports; five residential properties; three key risk areas [PAG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Woodwalton,England,"Significant flooding occurred in Woodwalton as a result of very high runoff from the intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020. (p6)
Six LLFA flood reports for Woodwalton describing internal flooding to residential properties, plus flooding to some roads and gardens. Three local areas were highlighted where internal property flooding was reported: New Road; The Cross and village green; and Braefield. [PAG] (p8)
Following the 23 December 2020 flood, drainage systems in Woodwalton required maintenance. Actions taken or arranged included cleansing of highway gullies by Cambridgeshire Highways and riparian maintenance of drainage ditches (Huntingdonshire District Council visited the site and maintenance work is understood to have been carried out). The LLFA offered support to residents to set up a local flood plan and will continue to liaise with Risk Management Authorities until outstanding actions are completed. (p17)",six flood reports; five residential properties; three key risk areas [PAG] (p8),GB-CAM_28_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_28_2020-12-23,Woodwalton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Woodwalton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Woodwalton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.596420489415756,-0.9293689718400323,"<div id=""html_867f3268b6214e2e5dcd8303ef4ce3b8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stonor Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater at Stonor Park rose to exceptionally high levels in early 2014 (peak near January 2014), contributing to the excess groundwater flow raising levels in and around Marlow. [IMG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Stonor Park,England,"Groundwater at Stonor Park rose to exceptionally high levels in early 2014 (peak near January 2014), contributing to the excess groundwater flow raising levels in and around Marlow. [IMG] (p18)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.567301799999996,-0.7772368918016874,"<div id=""html_a0e717fe680d5a01c5c622c0e0b0dc48"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Higginson Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Park was closed because floodwater was contaminated; Wycombe District Council erected signs and barriers at the site. [TAB] (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Higginson Park,England,Park was closed because floodwater was contaminated; Wycombe District Council erected signs and barriers at the site. [TAB] (p27),,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.567625822485354,-0.7737331528614219,"<div id=""html_8bccc7e013d9e66224693601c0e7e0ce"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tierney Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbags were issued to Tierney Court as part of the flood response to protect properties from flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbags distributed to protect properties at Tierney Court in response to flooding. [TAB] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Tierney Court,England,"Sandbags were issued to Tierney Court as part of the flood response to protect properties from flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p26)
Sandbags distributed to protect properties at Tierney Court in response to flooding. [TAB] (p28)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.61726984402831,-0.7321594820691124,"<div id=""html_05550eb8e70ce3af7e581e4e8089faf2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wycombe District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The district contains Marlow and multiple streets/properties recorded as flooded across several events; the report analyses the Jan&ndash;Feb 2014 event (unusually wet winter with particularly wet days on 23 Jan and 6 Feb) which contributed to fluvial and surface&ndash;water flooding across the area. Historical records in the SFRA/SWMP show numerous internal flood incidents across the district. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>30 properties flooded [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Wycombe District,England,"The district contains Marlow and multiple streets/properties recorded as flooded across several events; the report analyses the Jan–Feb 2014 event (unusually wet winter with particularly wet days on 23 Jan and 6 Feb) which contributed to fluvial and surface–water flooding across the area. Historical records in the SFRA/SWMP show numerous internal flood incidents across the district. [TAB,PAG] (p15)","30 properties flooded [TAB,PAG] (p15)",GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5667216,-0.7686876,"<div id=""html_12457da5d516665a0b7d3df1295e9ad7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lock Island</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sand bags distributed (listed as Mill Road (Lock Island) in distribution records). [TAB] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Lock Island,England,Sand bags distributed (listed as Mill Road (Lock Island) in distribution records). [TAB] (p28),,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5575525,-0.781404,"<div id=""html_0bf778b12cf6c9b9f8ce190d9313fc0b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Thames</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames runs adjacent to Marlow and is likely the source or contributor to the flooding that affected Pound Lane and East Marlow in January&ndash;February 2014 (the text does not explicitly state causation, so this is inferred). (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames (a designated Main River draining a catchment of over 6700 km2) rose in response to rainfall and influenced groundwater and gravel water levels around Marlow, contributing to the flooding experienced in the town during the January&ndash;February 2014 event. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding of the River Thames overtopped banks and inundated low&ndash;lying Thames&ndash;valley towns including areas around Marlow; the river rise stopped navigation and caused widespread inundation during major historical floods and is the principal fluvial hazard analysed for January&ndash;February 2014. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding of the River Thames contributed to flooding in Marlow (notably the Pound Lane / Gossmore Lane area) during the January&ndash;February 2014 event, interacting with high groundwater and surface water to cause internal property flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels in the Thames peaked (10 January 2014 and a higher peak 9&ndash;11 February 2014) and these high river levels contributed to raised groundwater and held up water in the Terrace Gravels beneath and around Marlow. [PAG,MUL] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded two pronounced high&ndash;water periods that generated the January and February 2014 flooding episodes in Marlow &mdash; a peak around 10 January 2014 and a higher peak between 9&ndash;11 February 2014, producing elevated river levels upstream of Marlow Lock. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Thames water levels peaked around 10 January 2014 and rose to a higher peak between 9&ndash;11 February 2014. Areas of Marlow close to the Thames and below approximately 28.2m AOD were likely inundated by floodwaters from the Thames; an Environment Agency Flood Warning was issued for the Marlow reach on 6 January 2014 and river peaks caused continuing and then more severe flooding in February. [PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Thames levels and two peaks (around 10 January 2014 and a second peak around 11 February 2014) caused inundation of low&ndash;lying areas close to the river (areas below ~28.2 m AOD). The river going out of bank led to direct flooding of riverside zones and contributed to more severe flooding during the February peak. [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames&ndash;wide flooding led to a major incident declaration by Thames Valley Police and triggered flood warnings for properties along the Marlow reach (flood warnings issued for properties from All Saints Church / Bisham to Little Marlow). [TAB,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency issued Flood Warning for properties closest to the River Thames between All Saints Church, Bisham and Little Marlow, indicating river&ndash;driven flood risk to riverside properties. [TAB] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warning was in force for properties closest to the River Thames between All Saints Church (Bisham) and Little Marlow during the event; the warning was later removed (no longer in force on 21 February 2014). [TAB] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels rose above the bank and directly flooded areas of Marlow adjacent to the river during the January&ndash;February 2014 event; this Thames flood was estimated to be a 5% AEP event. (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 feet above normal at Windsor Bridge (1947); more than 8 feet above normal at Bray Lock (1947) [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Thames,England,"The River Thames runs adjacent to Marlow and is likely the source or contributor to the flooding that affected Pound Lane and East Marlow in January–February 2014 (the text does not explicitly state causation, so this is inferred). (p7)
The River Thames (a designated Main River draining a catchment of over 6700 km2) rose in response to rainfall and influenced groundwater and gravel water levels around Marlow, contributing to the flooding experienced in the town during the January–February 2014 event. [PAG] (p10)
Fluvial flooding of the River Thames overtopped banks and inundated low–lying Thames–valley towns including areas around Marlow; the river rise stopped navigation and caused widespread inundation during major historical floods and is the principal fluvial hazard analysed for January–February 2014. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p15)
Fluvial flooding of the River Thames contributed to flooding in Marlow (notably the Pound Lane / Gossmore Lane area) during the January–February 2014 event, interacting with high groundwater and surface water to cause internal property flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p16)
River levels in the Thames peaked (10 January 2014 and a higher peak 9–11 February 2014) and these high river levels contributed to raised groundwater and held up water in the Terrace Gravels beneath and around Marlow. [PAG,MUL] (p18)
Recorded two pronounced high–water periods that generated the January and February 2014 flooding episodes in Marlow — a peak around 10 January 2014 and a higher peak between 9–11 February 2014, producing elevated river levels upstream of Marlow Lock. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p19)
River Thames water levels peaked around 10 January 2014 and rose to a higher peak between 9–11 February 2014. Areas of Marlow close to the Thames and below approximately 28.2m AOD were likely inundated by floodwaters from the Thames; an Environment Agency Flood Warning was issued for the Marlow reach on 6 January 2014 and river peaks caused continuing and then more severe flooding in February. [PAG,MUL] (p20)
High River Thames levels and two peaks (around 10 January 2014 and a second peak around 11 February 2014) caused inundation of low–lying areas close to the river (areas below ~28.2 m AOD). The river going out of bank led to direct flooding of riverside zones and contributed to more severe flooding during the February peak. [PAG,MUL] (p21)
Thames–wide flooding led to a major incident declaration by Thames Valley Police and triggered flood warnings for properties along the Marlow reach (flood warnings issued for properties from All Saints Church / Bisham to Little Marlow). [TAB,PAG] (p24)
Environment Agency issued Flood Warning for properties closest to the River Thames between All Saints Church, Bisham and Little Marlow, indicating river–driven flood risk to riverside properties. [TAB] (p25)
Flood warning was in force for properties closest to the River Thames between All Saints Church (Bisham) and Little Marlow during the event; the warning was later removed (no longer in force on 21 February 2014). [TAB] (p27)
River levels rose above the bank and directly flooded areas of Marlow adjacent to the river during the January–February 2014 event; this Thames flood was estimated to be a 5% AEP event. (p32)","4 feet above normal at Windsor Bridge (1947); more than 8 feet above normal at Bray Lock (1947) [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p15)",GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5689888,-0.7756424,"<div id=""html_d5bac436e7bae308f42ec510cb2b8611"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pound Lane, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred in the Pound Lane area during January&ndash;February 2014. Surface water in this area is managed by local soakaways, with excess collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames (drainage arrangement relevant to the flooding experienced). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pound Lane area of Marlow was identified as an area where flooding was experienced during January&ndash;February 2014. Local surface water drainage in this area relies on soakaways with excess water collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames, and the area was included in the study of flooded assets. [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Pound Lane and Gossmore Lane experienced internal flooding during the January&ndash;February 2014 event. Flooding is attributed to Thames fluvial influence combined with intense surface water runoff during the exceptionally wet winter. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pound Lane / Pound Crescent / Trout Close / Pike Close / Garnet Court area (Area A) flooded from groundwater and, for some properties, also from the River Thames. Householders used submersible pumps but could only pump to local sumps; permanently sited council pumps in sumps could not cope. Buckinghamshire Fire and Rescue initially pumped the area and later brought in a higher&ndash;capacity pump; this area was the main focus for the fire service response. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pound Lane area experienced flooding impacts during the event; BFRS estimated that properties in this area were affected by flooding in some way (not all internally). (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced flooding that required pumping and community response: pumps in the Pound Lane area were operating to protect properties, sandbags were issued, residents undertook self&ndash;help, and the road was closed due to flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pound Lane experienced flooding requiring pumping and flood response: surface&ndash;water pump overwhelmed, BFRS pumping assistance, road closure, and sandbag distribution; a pump was sited in Pound Lane by TfB. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area used as the main sandbag distribution and pumping hub during the event. A sandbag station was set up and manned 24 hrs/day; pumps and a supersucker operated from the Pound Lane area to remove floodwater from nearby properties; an Incident Control Unit was set up at Marlow Sports Centre in Pound Lane. Groundwater levels in lower Pound Lane dropped during the response. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater flooding affected lower Pound Lane; pumps were deployed in the Pound Lane/Lock Road area to protect properties; Pound Lane was later re&ndash;opened after response activity. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sand bags were distributed and groundwater levels affected the area; military presence moved out and the lane was later re&ndash;opened. Sand/Land drainage and pumping operations were used as part of the response. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>150 properties (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>810 sandbags; 12 sandbag walls; 3 pumps; 1200 sandbags (reported total at one delivery) [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 pumps; 5 properties cleared by Supersucker [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Pound Lane, Marlow",England,"Surface water flooding occurred in the Pound Lane area during January–February 2014. Surface water in this area is managed by local soakaways, with excess collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames (drainage arrangement relevant to the flooding experienced). (p8)
The Pound Lane area of Marlow was identified as an area where flooding was experienced during January–February 2014. Local surface water drainage in this area relies on soakaways with excess water collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames, and the area was included in the study of flooded assets. [IMG] (p9)
Properties on Pound Lane and Gossmore Lane experienced internal flooding during the January–February 2014 event. Flooding is attributed to Thames fluvial influence combined with intense surface water runoff during the exceptionally wet winter. [TAB] (p16)
The Pound Lane / Pound Crescent / Trout Close / Pike Close / Garnet Court area (Area A) flooded from groundwater and, for some properties, also from the River Thames. Householders used submersible pumps but could only pump to local sumps; permanently sited council pumps in sumps could not cope. Buckinghamshire Fire and Rescue initially pumped the area and later brought in a higher–capacity pump; this area was the main focus for the fire service response. [PAG] (p20)
The Pound Lane area experienced flooding impacts during the event; BFRS estimated that properties in this area were affected by flooding in some way (not all internally). (p22)
Area experienced flooding that required pumping and community response: pumps in the Pound Lane area were operating to protect properties, sandbags were issued, residents undertook self–help, and the road was closed due to flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)
Pound Lane experienced flooding requiring pumping and flood response: surface–water pump overwhelmed, BFRS pumping assistance, road closure, and sandbag distribution; a pump was sited in Pound Lane by TfB. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p25)
Area used as the main sandbag distribution and pumping hub during the event. A sandbag station was set up and manned 24 hrs/day; pumps and a supersucker operated from the Pound Lane area to remove floodwater from nearby properties; an Incident Control Unit was set up at Marlow Sports Centre in Pound Lane. Groundwater levels in lower Pound Lane dropped during the response. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p26)
Groundwater flooding affected lower Pound Lane; pumps were deployed in the Pound Lane/Lock Road area to protect properties; Pound Lane was later re–opened after response activity. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p27)
Sand bags were distributed and groundwater levels affected the area; military presence moved out and the lane was later re–opened. Sand/Land drainage and pumping operations were used as part of the response. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p28)","150 properties (p22)
810 sandbags; 12 sandbag walls; 3 pumps; 1200 sandbags (reported total at one delivery) [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p26)
3 pumps; 5 properties cleared by Supersucker [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p27)",GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5776834,-0.7699441,"<div id=""html_2a6eb222f194c8ebe7e3ebc9ec81daeb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Marlow Road, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Little Marlow Road lies within the eastern area of Marlow that experienced flooding in January&ndash;February 2014. Surface water runoff from Little Marlow Road is routed via the local piped drainage network and discharges to the Newt Ditch (partly culverted), and the area suffered surface&ndash;water/urban flooding during the event. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Little Marlow Road, Marlow",England,"Little Marlow Road lies within the eastern area of Marlow that experienced flooding in January–February 2014. Surface water runoff from Little Marlow Road is routed via the local piped drainage network and discharges to the Newt Ditch (partly culverted), and the area suffered surface–water/urban flooding during the event. (p8)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.57033,-0.7587654,"<div id=""html_41d052fe254c27adc190825cc9d8fca0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gossmore Lane, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Pound Lane and Gossmore Lane experienced internal flooding during the January&ndash;February 2014 event. Flooding is attributed to Thames fluvial influence combined with intense surface water runoff during the exceptionally wet winter. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Community payback workers, supervised by Marlow Town Council, were deployed to Gossmore Lane to assist with sandbagging, indicating localised flooding risk/impacts requiring sandbags. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at/near Gossmore (Gossmore Walk/Gossmore Lamne) with BFRS attendance to assess and advise; sandbags issued to protect properties. [TAB] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gossmore Lane was a focus of the sandbag distribution response during January 2014 to protect properties and manage flood risk. [TAB,MUL] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Gossmore Lane, Marlow",England,"Properties on Pound Lane and Gossmore Lane experienced internal flooding during the January–February 2014 event. Flooding is attributed to Thames fluvial influence combined with intense surface water runoff during the exceptionally wet winter. [TAB] (p16)
Community payback workers, supervised by Marlow Town Council, were deployed to Gossmore Lane to assist with sandbagging, indicating localised flooding risk/impacts requiring sandbags. [TAB] (p24)
Flooding at/near Gossmore (Gossmore Walk/Gossmore Lamne) with BFRS attendance to assess and advise; sandbags issued to protect properties. [TAB] (p25)
Gossmore Lane was a focus of the sandbag distribution response during January 2014 to protect properties and manage flood risk. [TAB,MUL] (p28)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5704125,-0.7580288,"<div id=""html_c5e3b66a7c319890c83b01bac9804b25"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fieldhouse Lane, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding occurred where local piped drainage to the Newt Ditch was blocked, so water could not drain away and flooded the area including Fieldhouse Lane. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Businesses on Fieldhouse Lane took protective action to protect their assets during the Jan&ndash;Feb 2014 flooding event (dates and specific measures not recorded in the report). (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carpark flooding on Fieldhouse Lane; BFRS attended and gave advice; sandbags were delivered to the area. [TAB] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carpark in Fieldhouse Lane flooded; BFRS attended and provided advice; sandbags issued to the Fieldhouse Lane area. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Fieldhouse Lane, Marlow",England,"Surface–water flooding occurred where local piped drainage to the Newt Ditch was blocked, so water could not drain away and flooded the area including Fieldhouse Lane. (p22)
Businesses on Fieldhouse Lane took protective action to protect their assets during the Jan–Feb 2014 flooding event (dates and specific measures not recorded in the report). (p23)
Carpark flooding on Fieldhouse Lane; BFRS attended and gave advice; sandbags were delivered to the area. [TAB] (p25)
Carpark in Fieldhouse Lane flooded; BFRS attended and provided advice; sandbags issued to the Fieldhouse Lane area. [TAB,MUL] (p26)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5676421,-0.783125,"<div id=""html_554e26ff7a9b94992040faebd89c96ef"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Garnet Court, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the area driven by groundwater, with some properties also affected by River Thames flooding; local pumping capacity (householder submersible pumps and council sump pumps) was insufficient. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Front and rear gardens and adjacent areas at Garnet Court flooded due to rising groundwater and drainage/storm&ndash;drain issues. Householders used their own pumps to move water into the street to be handled by the underground culvert pump. Fire & Rescue Service (BFRS) used pumps, checked resident welfare and provided flood&ndash;safety leaflets; relief crews worked in shifts on site. BFRS operated pumping from a large storm drain continuously (24 hrs) and provided sustained pumping operations through January and into February. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Garnet Court requiring continuous pumping from a large storm drain; High Street flooding reported nearby. Buckinghamshire Fire & Rescue Service pumped water from the drain to protect the area. [TAB,MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sanitary facilities were provided to address contamination issues related to the flooding: portaloos were sourced and issued for Garnet Court/Garnett Close. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Street&ndash;level flooding across the residential road, with water reaching curbs and impeding vehicle movement; emergency services attended (fire engine present), hoses deployed and sandbags placed at house entrances indicating active pumping/rescue and property protection efforts. [IMG] (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Garnet Court, Marlow",England,"Flooding in the area driven by groundwater, with some properties also affected by River Thames flooding; local pumping capacity (householder submersible pumps and council sump pumps) was insufficient. [PAG] (p20)
Front and rear gardens and adjacent areas at Garnet Court flooded due to rising groundwater and drainage/storm–drain issues. Householders used their own pumps to move water into the street to be handled by the underground culvert pump. Fire & Rescue Service (BFRS) used pumps, checked resident welfare and provided flood–safety leaflets; relief crews worked in shifts on site. BFRS operated pumping from a large storm drain continuously (24 hrs) and provided sustained pumping operations through January and into February. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)
Flooding at Garnet Court requiring continuous pumping from a large storm drain; High Street flooding reported nearby. Buckinghamshire Fire & Rescue Service pumped water from the drain to protect the area. [TAB,MUL] (p25)
Sanitary facilities were provided to address contamination issues related to the flooding: portaloos were sourced and issued for Garnet Court/Garnett Close. [TAB,MUL] (p26)
Street–level flooding across the residential road, with water reaching curbs and impeding vehicle movement; emergency services attended (fire engine present), hoses deployed and sandbags placed at house entrances indicating active pumping/rescue and property protection efforts. [IMG] (p44)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5745369,-0.7622035,"<div id=""html_850fce55891ead17042c90fc7a27dffd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Quarrydale Drive, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding at Quarrydale Drive; Buckinghamshire Fire & Rescue Service (BFRS) attended to assess and give advice on at least 7 February 2014 and 10 February 2014. [TAB,MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gardens and properties experienced groundwater flooding and floodwater became contaminated with raw sewage. BFRS deployed pumps (including a mobile light pump) to protect homes and pumped groundwater from gardens into drains to reduce levels. Public health concerns led to park closures and placement of portaloos. [TAB,MUL] (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 homes; 4 portaloos [TAB,MUL] (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Quarrydale Drive, Marlow",England,"Localised flooding at Quarrydale Drive; Buckinghamshire Fire & Rescue Service (BFRS) attended to assess and give advice on at least 7 February 2014 and 10 February 2014. [TAB,MUL] (p25)
Gardens and properties experienced groundwater flooding and floodwater became contaminated with raw sewage. BFRS deployed pumps (including a mobile light pump) to protect homes and pumped groundwater from gardens into drains to reduce levels. Public health concerns led to park closures and placement of portaloos. [TAB,MUL] (p27)","4 homes; 4 portaloos [TAB,MUL] (p27)",GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5705582,-0.7716621,"<div id=""html_925646791152706bfb95238b351abce3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbags were distributed to protect properties/road during the January 2014 response. [TAB] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Station Road, Marlow",England,Sandbags were distributed to protect properties/road during the January 2014 response. [TAB] (p28),,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5733647,-0.7649119,"<div id=""html_fae9fb5f226595b6a6608f111188d5cc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dedmere Road, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area flooded from surface water that could not drain away because piped drainage to the Newt Ditch was blocked (reported blockages with leaves), causing local flooding of roads and properties. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Dedmere Road; Buckinghamshire Fire & Rescue Service attended and provided advice to residents. [TAB] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Monitored by quick&ndash;response teams during the incident; sandbag station established to protect properties; RAF assisted monitoring and security. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Dedmere Road, Marlow",England,"Area flooded from surface water that could not drain away because piped drainage to the Newt Ditch was blocked (reported blockages with leaves), causing local flooding of roads and properties. (p22)
Flooding at Dedmere Road; Buckinghamshire Fire & Rescue Service attended and provided advice to residents. [TAB] (p25)
Monitored by quick–response teams during the incident; sandbag station established to protect properties; RAF assisted monitoring and security. (p26)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.568072,-0.7736483,"<div id=""html_9771f41b3abc04384df14387e8987050"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marlow Road, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An assisted self&ndash;elected evacuation took place at Ferry Lane in Marlow due to flooding impacts. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Marlow Road, Marlow",England,An assisted self–elected evacuation took place at Ferry Lane in Marlow due to flooding impacts. (p23),,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5660624,-0.7852387,"<div id=""html_fd5cccc89db666d9cd266764502e5a10"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pike Close, Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the area driven by groundwater, with some properties also affected by River Thames flooding; local pumping capacity (householder submersible pumps and council sump pumps) was insufficient. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An assisted self&ndash;elected evacuation took place at Pike Close due to flooding impacts. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Pike Close with BFRS attendance and advice (reported 9 and 10 February); BFRS were also called out to Pike Close to evacuate a resident. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Pound Lane area required a sustained local response: a 24&ndash;hour sandbag station was established; multiple sandbag deliveries and sandbag walls were used to protect properties; pumps and a large vacuum 'supersucker' were deployed to dewater properties; an incident control unit was established at Marlow Sports Centre (in Pound Lane). [TAB] (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>24hrs/day manned sandbag station; 810 sandbags; 12 sandbag walls; 1200 sandbags (total by 12 Feb); 3 pumps; supersucker pumped out 10 properties [TAB] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Pike Close, Marlow",England,"Flooding in the area driven by groundwater, with some properties also affected by River Thames flooding; local pumping capacity (householder submersible pumps and council sump pumps) was insufficient. [PAG] (p20)
An assisted self–elected evacuation took place at Pike Close due to flooding impacts. (p23)
Flooding at Pike Close with BFRS attendance and advice (reported 9 and 10 February); BFRS were also called out to Pike Close to evacuate a resident. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p25)
Flooding in the Pound Lane area required a sustained local response: a 24–hour sandbag station was established; multiple sandbag deliveries and sandbag walls were used to protect properties; pumps and a large vacuum 'supersucker' were deployed to dewater properties; an incident control unit was established at Marlow Sports Centre (in Pound Lane). [TAB] (p26)",24hrs/day manned sandbag station; 810 sandbags; 12 sandbag walls; 1200 sandbags (total by 12 Feb); 3 pumps; supersucker pumped out 10 properties [TAB] (p26),GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
