GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.579679,-0.7168799,"<div id=""html_78650ae0992d33aa968fd16f8a53286a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Drive, Little Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Drive was included in sandbag distributions (entries for Lock Lane / The Drive and Sailing Club Road / The Drive) to protect properties/roads during the January 2014 flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"The Drive, Little Marlow",England,"The Drive was included in sandbag distributions (entries for Lock Lane / The Drive and Sailing Club Road / The Drive) to protect properties/roads during the January 2014 flooding. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p28)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5663068,-0.7098666,"<div id=""html_4d0ebe7161934e482ac5bbd2351746a7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Riversdale, Bourne End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbags were distributed to protect properties during the January 2014 floods. [TAB] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Riversdale, Bourne End",England,Sandbags were distributed to protect properties during the January 2014 floods. [TAB] (p28),,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.57156365870574,-0.7767503540424273,"<div id=""html_db733aa5b2a1424efe9dec5739c1b4f7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged above&ndash;average rainfall caused groundwater levels to rise and land saturation with groundwater emergence. Surface water drainage pumps were overwhelmed; a culvert on an ordinary watercourse had insufficient capacity; the River Thames overtopped its banks twice, flooding low&ndash;lying areas of Marlow; the sewer system became overwhelmed from groundwater infiltration and caused flooding. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following prolonged high groundwater and saturated ground, groundwater emergence and subsequent rainfall caused flooding where surface water drainage pumps were overwhelmed and a culvert on an ordinary watercourse lacked capacity. The River Thames overtopped its banks during two peak events, flooding low&ndash;lying areas of Marlow. Groundwater infiltration overwhelmed the sewer system, causing internal flooding to properties. The incident met the threshold of internal flooding to five or more residential properties within 1 km². [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two areas within Marlow (Pound Lane area and East Marlow) experienced flooding during January&ndash;February 2014. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two areas within Marlow experienced flooding during January&ndash;February 2014 (Pound Lane area and eastern Marlow). The report describes surface water drainage arrangements for these areas: Pound Lane uses soakaways with excess collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames; eastern Marlow routes runoff via a piped network discharging to the Newt Ditch (a culverted ordinary watercourse). [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Marlow experienced flooding during January&ndash;February 2014, specifically the Pound Lane and East Marlow areas. Flooding affected the urban drainage areas where surface water is managed by soakaways, piped networks and discharges to Newt Ditch and the River Thames. [PAG,IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was experienced within Marlow (notably the Pound Lane area and East Marlow). Surface water in Pound Lane is normally managed by soakaways with excess collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames; in East Marlow runoff is routed through a piped network discharging to the Newt Ditch (a culverted ordinary watercourse). These local drainage arrangements were the channels through which floodwaters and runoff were routed during the event. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding recorded at properties across many streets in Marlow (examples listed in the record include Glade Road, The Causeway, Dedmere Road, Gossmore Lane, Garnet Court, Pound Lane, The Croft, Claremont Road, Station Road, Henley Road, High Street, Hillside Road, Maple Rise, Newtown Road, Oak Tree Road, Peacock Road, St Peter Street, West Street, Wycombe Street and Marlow Bottom Road). Surface&ndash;water flooding was recorded at Higginson Park. Groundwater in the underlying Chalk rose to exceptionally high levels and could resurface through the gravels beneath Marlow, contributing to inundation in built areas. [TAB,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels peaked at Marlow (peak 10 January 2014 and a higher peak 9&ndash;11 February 2014) and high Thames levels held up groundwater in the underlying gravels; groundwater levels in and around Marlow rose, contributing to flooding impacts in the town. [PAG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater levels in the Terrace Gravels beneath and around Marlow rose &mdash; caused by excess flow from the Chilterns and being held up by high River Thames levels &mdash; producing elevated groundwater conditions in January&ndash;February 2014. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced elevated River Thames water levels associated with the January and February 2014 high&ndash;water periods; the town was subject to river flooding risk when Thames levels peaked in early January and again in early February 2014. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At the start of January 2014, with groundwater high and the Thames already high, low&ndash;lying parts of Marlow experienced flooding primarily from groundwater. The River Thames peaked in January and again in February, causing further and more severe flooding. Areas below ~28.2m AOD close to the river were likely inundated. Groundwater, surface water and sewer flooding are described as interlinked contributors to the problems. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying parts of Marlow were inundated in January&ndash;February 2014. Areas below about 28.2m AOD close to the River Thames were likely inundated by Thames floodwaters. Area A (Pound Lane, Pound Crescent, Trout Close, Pike Close, Garnet Court) flooded from groundwater and in places from the River Thames; permanently sited council sump pumps could not cope and Buckinghamshire Fire and Rescue Service initially pumped the water then deployed a higher&ndash;capacity pump. Area B flooded directly from the River Thames and had reports of sewage problems; BFRS protected properties in St Peters Street. In Area C (Firview Close) piped drains backed up and rising groundwater threatened properties until residents pumped water over an informal bund in the A404 drainage ditch to prevent Thames waters backing up the ditch and flooding properties. A forward control point was established in Higginson Park to support multi&ndash;agency tactical coordination. [PAG,IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread local flooding across Marlow from blocked piped drains, rising groundwater emergence and surface water that could not drain to the Newt Ditch; emergency response and community pumping limited impacts but properties and some evacuations occurred. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Describes the Jan&ndash;Feb 2014 flooding in Marlow: River Thames flows overtopped banks and flooded the floodplain; unusually high Chalk and gravel groundwater caused groundwater to emerge at the surface; surface water pumps were overwhelmed and piped drainage could not discharge to Newt Ditch (culvert capacity reduced by sediment); groundwater infiltrated foul sewers causing surcharging and flooding of low&ndash;lying areas. Report summarises affected properties, evacuations and estimated flood likelihood. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood impacts across the town during January&ndash;February 2014: Flood Warnings issued for properties close to the River Thames; flooding reported in St Peter Street and basement flooding on the High Street; groundwater flooding affecting Riverpark Drive and front and rear gardens in Garnet Court; road closures (Pound Lane); extensive pumping operations and sandbag distribution to protect properties; relief crews and fire service pumping operations sustained on site for multiple days. [TAB,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread local flooding across Marlow during January&ndash;February 2014: basement flooding on the High Street; flooding at Garnet Court requiring continuous pumping from a large storm drain; Flood Warning issued for properties along the River Thames from All Saints Church, Bisham to Little Marlow; road closures (Pound Lane); pumping operations at Pound Lane and St Peter Street; evacuations and multi&ndash;agency response (BFRS, EA, TfB, WDC, MTC); sandbag distribution and a 24&ndash;hour sandbag station. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood impacts across the town: carpark flooding (Fieldhouse Lane), residential properties flooded (e.g. a flat in Mill Road), groundwater and sewer&ndash;related flooding issues (sewerage contaminating floodwater, Higginson Park closed for contamination), pumping and sandbagging operations across several streets to protect homes. [TAB,PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Town&ndash;wide flooding impacts and response: contaminated floodwater from sewers; public health advice issued; incident control set up at Marlow Sports Centre and Environmental Health/Housing officers located at Court Garden to advise residents; swift&ndash;water rescue team and multiple pump operations deployed; military/RAF and local authorities provided sandbags, portaloos and manpower; flood warnings were issued and later lifted for properties along the Thames near Marlow. [TAB,PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood impacts across Marlow during January&ndash;February 2014: contaminated floodwater (raw sewage) in residential areas (notably Quarrydale Drive), public&ndash;health advice issued, Higginson Park closed due to contamination, pumping operations conducted by fire service and Thames Water to lower groundwater and sewer levels, encouragements to residents to restrict water use, and temporary sanitation provided to affected streets. [TAB,PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged flooding across parts of Marlow in January&ndash;February 2014. Ground was saturated and unusually high groundwater levels caused groundwater to emerge and flood low&ndash;lying areas. Surface&ndash;water drainage that relies on infiltration failed due to high groundwater; permanent surface&ndash;water pumps were unable to cope and failed. Surface water piped to Newt Ditch caused flooding where the ditch was overwhelmed. Infiltration into foul sewers caused surcharging and sewer flooding. Areas close to the River Thames were flooded when the river overtopped its banks. (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five or more residential properties within an area of 1km2; two river level peaks [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>55 to 60 properties flooded internally; 2 assisted self&ndash;elected evacuations [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 150 properties affected; 55 to 60 properties flooded internally; 2 assisted self&ndash;elected evacuations (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 homes protected; 4 portaloos; 250 men (military) to be placed in 7 sectors; 100 sandbags; 500 sandbags; 3 pumps; 4 properties cleared [TAB,PAG] (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Marlow,England,"Prolonged above–average rainfall caused groundwater levels to rise and land saturation with groundwater emergence. Surface water drainage pumps were overwhelmed; a culvert on an ordinary watercourse had insufficient capacity; the River Thames overtopped its banks twice, flooding low–lying areas of Marlow; the sewer system became overwhelmed from groundwater infiltration and caused flooding. (p5)
Following prolonged high groundwater and saturated ground, groundwater emergence and subsequent rainfall caused flooding where surface water drainage pumps were overwhelmed and a culvert on an ordinary watercourse lacked capacity. The River Thames overtopped its banks during two peak events, flooding low–lying areas of Marlow. Groundwater infiltration overwhelmed the sewer system, causing internal flooding to properties. The incident met the threshold of internal flooding to five or more residential properties within 1 km². [PAG] (p6)
Two areas within Marlow (Pound Lane area and East Marlow) experienced flooding during January–February 2014. [PAG] (p7)
Two areas within Marlow experienced flooding during January–February 2014 (Pound Lane area and eastern Marlow). The report describes surface water drainage arrangements for these areas: Pound Lane uses soakaways with excess collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames; eastern Marlow routes runoff via a piped network discharging to the Newt Ditch (a culverted ordinary watercourse). [PAG] (p8)
Parts of Marlow experienced flooding during January–February 2014, specifically the Pound Lane and East Marlow areas. Flooding affected the urban drainage areas where surface water is managed by soakaways, piped networks and discharges to Newt Ditch and the River Thames. [PAG,IMG] (p9)
Flooding was experienced within Marlow (notably the Pound Lane area and East Marlow). Surface water in Pound Lane is normally managed by soakaways with excess collected in sumps and pumped to the River Thames; in East Marlow runoff is routed through a piped network discharging to the Newt Ditch (a culverted ordinary watercourse). These local drainage arrangements were the channels through which floodwaters and runoff were routed during the event. [PAG] (p10)
Internal flooding recorded at properties across many streets in Marlow (examples listed in the record include Glade Road, The Causeway, Dedmere Road, Gossmore Lane, Garnet Court, Pound Lane, The Croft, Claremont Road, Station Road, Henley Road, High Street, Hillside Road, Maple Rise, Newtown Road, Oak Tree Road, Peacock Road, St Peter Street, West Street, Wycombe Street and Marlow Bottom Road). Surface–water flooding was recorded at Higginson Park. Groundwater in the underlying Chalk rose to exceptionally high levels and could resurface through the gravels beneath Marlow, contributing to inundation in built areas. [TAB,PAG] (p16)
River levels peaked at Marlow (peak 10 January 2014 and a higher peak 9–11 February 2014) and high Thames levels held up groundwater in the underlying gravels; groundwater levels in and around Marlow rose, contributing to flooding impacts in the town. [PAG,MUL] (p17)
Groundwater levels in the Terrace Gravels beneath and around Marlow rose — caused by excess flow from the Chilterns and being held up by high River Thames levels — producing elevated groundwater conditions in January–February 2014. [PAG] (p18)
Experienced elevated River Thames water levels associated with the January and February 2014 high–water periods; the town was subject to river flooding risk when Thames levels peaked in early January and again in early February 2014. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p19)
At the start of January 2014, with groundwater high and the Thames already high, low–lying parts of Marlow experienced flooding primarily from groundwater. The River Thames peaked in January and again in February, causing further and more severe flooding. Areas below ~28.2m AOD close to the river were likely inundated. Groundwater, surface water and sewer flooding are described as interlinked contributors to the problems. [PAG] (p20)
Low–lying parts of Marlow were inundated in January–February 2014. Areas below about 28.2m AOD close to the River Thames were likely inundated by Thames floodwaters. Area A (Pound Lane, Pound Crescent, Trout Close, Pike Close, Garnet Court) flooded from groundwater and in places from the River Thames; permanently sited council sump pumps could not cope and Buckinghamshire Fire and Rescue Service initially pumped the water then deployed a higher–capacity pump. Area B flooded directly from the River Thames and had reports of sewage problems; BFRS protected properties in St Peters Street. In Area C (Firview Close) piped drains backed up and rising groundwater threatened properties until residents pumped water over an informal bund in the A404 drainage ditch to prevent Thames waters backing up the ditch and flooding properties. A forward control point was established in Higginson Park to support multi–agency tactical coordination. [PAG,IMG] (p21)
Widespread local flooding across Marlow from blocked piped drains, rising groundwater emergence and surface water that could not drain to the Newt Ditch; emergency response and community pumping limited impacts but properties and some evacuations occurred. [PAG] (p22)
Describes the Jan–Feb 2014 flooding in Marlow: River Thames flows overtopped banks and flooded the floodplain; unusually high Chalk and gravel groundwater caused groundwater to emerge at the surface; surface water pumps were overwhelmed and piped drainage could not discharge to Newt Ditch (culvert capacity reduced by sediment); groundwater infiltrated foul sewers causing surcharging and flooding of low–lying areas. Report summarises affected properties, evacuations and estimated flood likelihood. (p23)
Multiple flood impacts across the town during January–February 2014: Flood Warnings issued for properties close to the River Thames; flooding reported in St Peter Street and basement flooding on the High Street; groundwater flooding affecting Riverpark Drive and front and rear gardens in Garnet Court; road closures (Pound Lane); extensive pumping operations and sandbag distribution to protect properties; relief crews and fire service pumping operations sustained on site for multiple days. [TAB,PAG] (p24)
Widespread local flooding across Marlow during January–February 2014: basement flooding on the High Street; flooding at Garnet Court requiring continuous pumping from a large storm drain; Flood Warning issued for properties along the River Thames from All Saints Church, Bisham to Little Marlow; road closures (Pound Lane); pumping operations at Pound Lane and St Peter Street; evacuations and multi–agency response (BFRS, EA, TfB, WDC, MTC); sandbag distribution and a 24–hour sandbag station. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p25)
Multiple flood impacts across the town: carpark flooding (Fieldhouse Lane), residential properties flooded (e.g. a flat in Mill Road), groundwater and sewer–related flooding issues (sewerage contaminating floodwater, Higginson Park closed for contamination), pumping and sandbagging operations across several streets to protect homes. [TAB,PAG] (p26)
Town–wide flooding impacts and response: contaminated floodwater from sewers; public health advice issued; incident control set up at Marlow Sports Centre and Environmental Health/Housing officers located at Court Garden to advise residents; swift–water rescue team and multiple pump operations deployed; military/RAF and local authorities provided sandbags, portaloos and manpower; flood warnings were issued and later lifted for properties along the Thames near Marlow. [TAB,PAG] (p27)
Multiple flood impacts across Marlow during January–February 2014: contaminated floodwater (raw sewage) in residential areas (notably Quarrydale Drive), public–health advice issued, Higginson Park closed due to contamination, pumping operations conducted by fire service and Thames Water to lower groundwater and sewer levels, encouragements to residents to restrict water use, and temporary sanitation provided to affected streets. [TAB,PAG] (p28)
Prolonged flooding across parts of Marlow in January–February 2014. Ground was saturated and unusually high groundwater levels caused groundwater to emerge and flood low–lying areas. Surface–water drainage that relies on infiltration failed due to high groundwater; permanent surface–water pumps were unable to cope and failed. Surface water piped to Newt Ditch caused flooding where the ditch was overwhelmed. Infiltration into foul sewers caused surcharging and sewer flooding. Areas close to the River Thames were flooded when the river overtopped its banks. (p32)","five or more residential properties within an area of 1km2; two river level peaks [PAG] (p6)
55 to 60 properties flooded internally; 2 assisted self–elected evacuations [PAG] (p22)
approximately 150 properties affected; 55 to 60 properties flooded internally; 2 assisted self–elected evacuations (p23)
4 homes protected; 4 portaloos; 250 men (military) to be placed in 7 sectors; 100 sandbags; 500 sandbags; 3 pumps; 4 properties cleared [TAB,PAG] (p27)",GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5836767,-0.7407652,"<div id=""html_d630fa328b7b42ad6dfad91ffd78b6f9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warning issued for properties closest to the River Thames from All Saints Church, Bisham to Little Marlow. [TAB,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement flooding reported at Little Marlow in conjunction with the Flood Warning for riverside properties. (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Little Marlow,England,"Flood warning issued for properties closest to the River Thames from All Saints Church, Bisham to Little Marlow. [TAB,PAG] (p24)
Basement flooding reported at Little Marlow in conjunction with the Flood Warning for riverside properties. (p25)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5799463,-0.7120777,"<div id=""html_eea7c0ca7cb4be0a196777411d4582c0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bourne End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple streets and neighbourhoods in Marlow and Bourne End experienced flooding during the January&ndash;February 2014 event. Flood ambassadors visited the area; BFRS attended and gave advice at Pound Lane, Trout Close, Dedmere Road, Pike Close, Quarrydale Drive, Gossmore Walk and West Street; two pumps were deployed at St Peter Street; sandbags were distributed and a sandbag station was manned 24 hrs in the Pound Lane area; road closure on Pound Lane; basements and High Street experienced flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Bourne End,England,"Multiple streets and neighbourhoods in Marlow and Bourne End experienced flooding during the January–February 2014 event. Flood ambassadors visited the area; BFRS attended and gave advice at Pound Lane, Trout Close, Dedmere Road, Pike Close, Quarrydale Drive, Gossmore Walk and West Street; two pumps were deployed at St Peter Street; sandbags were distributed and a sandbag station was manned 24 hrs in the Pound Lane area; road closure on Pound Lane; basements and High Street experienced flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p25)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BKM,10,2014-01-01,51.5536495,-0.84037,"<div id=""html_4af93869a49d4f664672a6791b560b44"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Medmenham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbags were distributed in Medmenham (notably at Ferry Lane) to protect properties from flooding; local provision included supplies from the county. [TAB,MUL] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Medmenham,England,"Sandbags were distributed in Medmenham (notably at Ferry Lane) to protect properties from flooding; local provision included supplies from the county. [TAB,MUL] (p28)",,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_10_2014-01-01,Marlow flooding January–February 2014,"Marlow, 9th January to 20th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/marlow-s19-report-2014/final.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.448701534065094,-2.5942483353218897,"<div id=""html_54c97ee0034a65b93f9d6c0bf6f6011f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bristol Harbour</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal floodwater entered the Floating Harbour via overtopping at Bathurst Basin and Junction Lock; tide&ndash;locking and the Northern Stormwater Interceptor allowed River Frome flows into the Harbour. Bristol City Docks pre&ndash;lowered the Harbour to create extra capacity for this tidal inflow. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal overtopping at river structures (Junction Lock and Bathurst Basin Dam) resulted in tidal water entering the Floating Harbour. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced elevated water levels during the high tides; was pre&ndash;lowered to provide additional storage capacity and its control structures were operated to manage water levels. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four Eastville Sluices; two flood events (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Bristol Harbour,England,"Tidal floodwater entered the Floating Harbour via overtopping at Bathurst Basin and Junction Lock; tide–locking and the Northern Stormwater Interceptor allowed River Frome flows into the Harbour. Bristol City Docks pre–lowered the Harbour to create extra capacity for this tidal inflow. (p11)
Tidal overtopping at river structures (Junction Lock and Bathurst Basin Dam) resulted in tidal water entering the Floating Harbour. [TAB] (p14)
Experienced elevated water levels during the high tides; was pre–lowered to provide additional storage capacity and its control structures were operated to manage water levels. (p18)",four Eastville Sluices; two flood events (p11),GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.44830605,-2.621159556928659,"<div id=""html_b254c13bee5afcb001800aaa3213b89d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cumberland Basin</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping at Junction Lock led to tidal water entering the Cumberland Basin area of the Floating Harbour, exposing adjacent infrastructure to floodwater. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Cumberland Basin area adjacent to the Clifton Suspension Bridge experienced elevated tidal water levels during the 11 March 2020 high tide; estimated tide heights were used to calculate maximum flood depths for the basin/adjacent infrastructure. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads around the Cumberland Basin (including the carriageways at/near the Clifton Suspension Bridge junction) were affected by tidal flooding; Cumberland Basin Road is estimated to have experienced up to 0.40 m of flood depth and roads in this area were closed for approximately 4.5 hours during the high tides. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads around the Cumberland Basin were closed during the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March, indicating inundation of highway infrastructure in the basin area. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the road/harbour area actively managed during the high tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020; associated roads (Cumberland Basin Road) were closed and harbour control structures were monitored/operated to manage rising tidal levels. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents of the Sea Mills area were contacted and warned to install flood barriers; Sea Mills was photographed during the high tides on 12 March 2020, indicating direct exposure to the tidal high water. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Photographs show high tide at vulnerable basin infrastructure; harbour actions (pre&ndash;lowering) were advised to manage tidal surge impacts. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>estimated high tide ~8.77&ndash;8.78 mAOD (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Closures 20:00 &ndash; 00:30 (11 Mar) and 07:00 &ndash; 11:00 (12 Mar); approximate flooded durations 3.5 hours and 4 hours [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Cumberland Basin,England,"Overtopping at Junction Lock led to tidal water entering the Cumberland Basin area of the Floating Harbour, exposing adjacent infrastructure to floodwater. [IMG] (p11)
The Cumberland Basin area adjacent to the Clifton Suspension Bridge experienced elevated tidal water levels during the 11 March 2020 high tide; estimated tide heights were used to calculate maximum flood depths for the basin/adjacent infrastructure. (p13)
Roads around the Cumberland Basin (including the carriageways at/near the Clifton Suspension Bridge junction) were affected by tidal flooding; Cumberland Basin Road is estimated to have experienced up to 0.40 m of flood depth and roads in this area were closed for approximately 4.5 hours during the high tides. [PAG] (p14)
Roads around the Cumberland Basin were closed during the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March, indicating inundation of highway infrastructure in the basin area. [TAB] (p15)
Part of the road/harbour area actively managed during the high tides on 11–12 March 2020; associated roads (Cumberland Basin Road) were closed and harbour control structures were monitored/operated to manage rising tidal levels. (p18)
Residents of the Sea Mills area were contacted and warned to install flood barriers; Sea Mills was photographed during the high tides on 12 March 2020, indicating direct exposure to the tidal high water. [PAG] (p24)
Photographs show high tide at vulnerable basin infrastructure; harbour actions (pre–lowering) were advised to manage tidal surge impacts. [PAG] (p25)","estimated high tide ~8.77–8.78 mAOD (p13)
Closures 20:00 – 00:30 (11 Mar) and 07:00 – 11:00 (12 Mar); approximate flooded durations 3.5 hours and 4 hours [TAB] (p15)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.44669564547392,-2.595668300010457,"<div id=""html_5d8a69e14840d8f4e1e14af9f60d2183"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bathurst Basin Dam</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the tidal River Avon affected the Bathurst Dam area on 11&ndash;12 March 2020. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bathurst Basin Dam area was subject to high tidal levels on 11 March 2020; estimated tide heights were used to derive maximum flood depths impacting the basin/dam vicinity. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Estimated tidal levels exceeded the Bathurst Basin dam/defence leading to overtopping into the Floating Harbour. [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>estimated high tide 8.78 mAOD [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Estimated high tide 8.78 m AOD; defence 8.30 m AOD [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Bathurst Basin Dam,England,"Flooding from the tidal River Avon affected the Bathurst Dam area on 11–12 March 2020. [IMG] (p6)
Bathurst Basin Dam area was subject to high tidal levels on 11 March 2020; estimated tide heights were used to derive maximum flood depths impacting the basin/dam vicinity. [TAB] (p13)
Estimated tidal levels exceeded the Bathurst Basin dam/defence leading to overtopping into the Floating Harbour. [TAB,PAG] (p14)","estimated high tide 8.78 mAOD [TAB] (p13)
Estimated high tide 8.78 m AOD; defence 8.30 m AOD [TAB,PAG] (p14)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.47001271371749,-2.6433220311447965,"<div id=""html_879b947ec2f12d79ee752059147610e1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bristol Avon Between Avonmouth and Bristol</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The narrowing of the river channel between Avonmouth and the city centre forced water to increase in height as the tide rose, amplifying the increase in water levels upstream and contributing to underestimation of city&ndash;centre high tides. [MUL] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Bristol Avon Between Avonmouth and Bristol,England,"The narrowing of the river channel between Avonmouth and the city centre forced water to increase in height as the tide rose, amplifying the increase in water levels upstream and contributing to underestimation of city–centre high tides. [MUL] (p10)",,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4538022,-2.5972985,"<div id=""html_c38b45ad5c8821e018edf4ef5e25f092"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bristol</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High astronomical spring tides combined with a storm surge on 11&ndash;12 March 2020 caused tidal flooding across the Bristol region; roads along the River Avon were significantly impacted and one property flooded internally; no essential infrastructure reported flooded. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of the city adjacent to the River Avon experienced tidal flooding during the high tides of 11&ndash;12 March 2020, with multiple streets, basins and a footpath inundated along the river corridor. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Central Bristol was affected by a tidal flood event; flood alerts and warnings were issued and the Environment Agency operated sluices and closed flood gates (e.g. Eastville sluice gates to allow River Frome flows to discharge to the River Avon downstream of Black Rocks, and gates at Shirehampton) with pre/post&ndash;tide checks and clearances to manage tide locking. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 11 March 2020 (and into 12 March 2020) Bristol experienced tidal/coastal flooding caused by an astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge and westerly winds; the event reached a peak of 8.81mAOD and affected several areas across the city, including the Portway and Cattle Market Road. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The city experienced a tidal flood caused by an astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge, reaching a peak of 8.81mAOD and affecting residential and highway infrastructure across multiple areas. (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Bristol,England,"High astronomical spring tides combined with a storm surge on 11–12 March 2020 caused tidal flooding across the Bristol region; roads along the River Avon were significantly impacted and one property flooded internally; no essential infrastructure reported flooded. (p3)
Areas of the city adjacent to the River Avon experienced tidal flooding during the high tides of 11–12 March 2020, with multiple streets, basins and a footpath inundated along the river corridor. [IMG] (p6)
Central Bristol was affected by a tidal flood event; flood alerts and warnings were issued and the Environment Agency operated sluices and closed flood gates (e.g. Eastville sluice gates to allow River Frome flows to discharge to the River Avon downstream of Black Rocks, and gates at Shirehampton) with pre/post–tide checks and clearances to manage tide locking. (p19)
On 11 March 2020 (and into 12 March 2020) Bristol experienced tidal/coastal flooding caused by an astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge and westerly winds; the event reached a peak of 8.81mAOD and affected several areas across the city, including the Portway and Cattle Market Road. [PAG] (p21)
The city experienced a tidal flood caused by an astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge, reaching a peak of 8.81mAOD and affecting residential and highway infrastructure across multiple areas. (p22)",1 property (p3),GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4454809,-2.5920409,"<div id=""html_211688aaa4a139d83ccfe1d6a5a31d4c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bedminster Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bedminster Bridge tide monitor in the city centre recorded higher&ndash;than&ndash;predicted high tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020, indicating an underestimated surge and higher tidal levels affecting the city centre. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded peak high tide levels at Bedminster Bridge during the two high tide events exceeded predictions: on 11 March 2020 the recorded peak was higher than the predicted peak, and on 12 March 2020 the recorded peak was again higher than predicted. The underestimation was linked to surge/wind effects and to the city&ndash;centre uplift from Avonmouth being larger than the standard adjustment. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed higher tide level increases at Bedminster Bridge (relative to Avonmouth) indicated that city&ndash;centre high tides were larger than the adjusted forecasts, contributing evidence of underestimated tidal heights during the March events. [MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bedminster Bridge location recorded a measured high tide of 8.81 mAOD on 11 March 2020 and was included among the flooded locations for which maximum flood depths were estimated. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal gauge at Bedminster Bridge was actively monitored leading up to the high tide and tidal gauge data were downloaded after the event to monitor storm surge and tidal levels. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bedminster Bridge tidal gauge was monitored leading up to the high tide and tidal gauge data were downloaded after the event, recording the storm surge/tidal levels associated with the 11&ndash;12 March high tides. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8.81mAOD recorded; 8.68mAOD predicted (0.13m under estimated) on 11/03/2020; 8.67mAOD recorded; 8.45mAOD predicted (0.22m under estimated) on 12/03/2020; BCC uplift 0.15m; actual Avonmouth→Bedminster increases 0.18m (11/03/2020) and 0.23m (12/03/2020) [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>measured high tide 8.81 mAOD [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Bedminster Bridge,England,"The Bedminster Bridge tide monitor in the city centre recorded higher–than–predicted high tides on 11–12 March 2020, indicating an underestimated surge and higher tidal levels affecting the city centre. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p8)
Recorded peak high tide levels at Bedminster Bridge during the two high tide events exceeded predictions: on 11 March 2020 the recorded peak was higher than the predicted peak, and on 12 March 2020 the recorded peak was again higher than predicted. The underestimation was linked to surge/wind effects and to the city–centre uplift from Avonmouth being larger than the standard adjustment. [PAG] (p9)
Observed higher tide level increases at Bedminster Bridge (relative to Avonmouth) indicated that city–centre high tides were larger than the adjusted forecasts, contributing evidence of underestimated tidal heights during the March events. [MUL] (p10)
Bedminster Bridge location recorded a measured high tide of 8.81 mAOD on 11 March 2020 and was included among the flooded locations for which maximum flood depths were estimated. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Tidal gauge at Bedminster Bridge was actively monitored leading up to the high tide and tidal gauge data were downloaded after the event to monitor storm surge and tidal levels. [PAG] (p24)
The Bedminster Bridge tidal gauge was monitored leading up to the high tide and tidal gauge data were downloaded after the event, recording the storm surge/tidal levels associated with the 11–12 March high tides. [PAG] (p25)","8.81mAOD recorded; 8.68mAOD predicted (0.13m under estimated) on 11/03/2020; 8.67mAOD recorded; 8.45mAOD predicted (0.22m under estimated) on 12/03/2020; BCC uplift 0.15m; actual Avonmouth→Bedminster increases 0.18m (11/03/2020) and 0.23m (12/03/2020) [PAG] (p9)
measured high tide 8.81 mAOD [TAB,PAG] (p13)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4549691,-2.6278068,"<div id=""html_235de0300de78bf156b2fce67238de65"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Clifton Suspension Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area at/adjacent to the Clifton Suspension Bridge (labelled on the map) experienced tidal River Avon inundation; the map identifies this location as part of the linear pattern of flooding. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Portway beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge was affected by the high tide on 11 March 2020, resulting in inundation of the low&ndash;lying road/adjacent areas as part of the tidal flooding event (estimated tide level used for impact assessment). [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Portway beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge was flooded and closed during the high tides on the evening of 11 March and the morning of 12 March, causing road closures and disruption beneath the bridge. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the astronomical high tide and storm surge inundated the area beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge, affecting the bridge environs and the Portway carriageway below. (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>estimated high tide 8.77 mAOD [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4.5 hours; 5 hours (approximate flooding durations during the two closure periods) [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Clifton Suspension Bridge,England,"Area at/adjacent to the Clifton Suspension Bridge (labelled on the map) experienced tidal River Avon inundation; the map identifies this location as part of the linear pattern of flooding. (p6)
The Portway beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge was affected by the high tide on 11 March 2020, resulting in inundation of the low–lying road/adjacent areas as part of the tidal flooding event (estimated tide level used for impact assessment). [TAB] (p13)
The Portway beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge was flooded and closed during the high tides on the evening of 11 March and the morning of 12 March, causing road closures and disruption beneath the bridge. [TAB] (p15)
Flooding from the astronomical high tide and storm surge inundated the area beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge, affecting the bridge environs and the Portway carriageway below. (p22)","estimated high tide 8.77 mAOD [TAB] (p13)
4.5 hours; 5 hours (approximate flooding durations during the two closure periods) [TAB] (p15)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.447471843665525,-2.619495898836688,"<div id=""html_6c6b1a4b342848ff2279e3f0c68c5502"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brunel Lock Carpark</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carpark was actively managed and closed during the high tide event. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Brunel Lock Carpark was identified by highway teams as a location to close in advance of the high tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020 due to predicted tidal inundation. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Brunel Lock Carpark,England,"Carpark was actively managed and closed during the high tide event. (p18)
Brunel Lock Carpark was identified by highway teams as a location to close in advance of the high tides on 11–12 March 2020 due to predicted tidal inundation. (p24)",,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4839345,-2.644169,"<div id=""html_f3f0ba28782c22b826b5261eb253d1b5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sea Mills Lane, Sea Mills</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the tidal River Avon inundated Sea Mills Lane adjacent to the river; site identified as one of multiple flood locations along the River Avon. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Public highway and adjacent residential property inundated; water covered the entire road surface and encroached onto driveways and gardens; multiple parked vehicles were partially submerged, rendering the street impassable. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Estimated to have experienced a high tide of 8.72 mAOD on the 11 March high tide, contributing to the locations identified as flooded in Table 5/6. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tide exceeded ground level (ground 8.00 m AOD) producing roadway inundation by tidal water; road experienced significant flooding and remained closed between the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March. [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced significant flood depths and was closed between the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March; one report of internal residential flooding where tide levels overtopped a demountable barrier and entered the rear of the property via a lower&ndash;lying side gate. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tides on the evening of 11 March and the morning of 12 March 2020 exceeded a property's demountable flood barrier and entered via the side gate into the lower&ndash;lying rear of the property, causing internal residential flooding. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents at Sea Mills Lane were notified and the road was actively managed/closed as part of the response to the high tides. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal flooding inundated Sea Mills Lane, flooding the road, parked cars and one property; many properties in the area deployed flood barriers/gates in response. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Sea Mills area was affected by the astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge; property flood defences largely prevented inundation but one property was flooded. Residential properties and nearby highway infrastructure were impacted. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sea Mills Lane was identified for closure around the high tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020. Residents of Sea Mills were contacted and advised to install flood barriers to protect properties; photographs of the high tide were taken at Sea Mills. (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8.72 mAOD estimated high tide [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.72 m maximum flood depth; 5 hours estimated flood duration [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one internal residential flooding incident; 5 hours (approximate flooding duration inferred) [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of","Sea Mills Lane, Sea Mills",England,"Flooding from the tidal River Avon inundated Sea Mills Lane adjacent to the river; site identified as one of multiple flood locations along the River Avon. [IMG] (p6)
Public highway and adjacent residential property inundated; water covered the entire road surface and encroached onto driveways and gardens; multiple parked vehicles were partially submerged, rendering the street impassable. [IMG] (p12)
Estimated to have experienced a high tide of 8.72 mAOD on the 11 March high tide, contributing to the locations identified as flooded in Table 5/6. [TAB] (p13)
High tide exceeded ground level (ground 8.00 m AOD) producing roadway inundation by tidal water; road experienced significant flooding and remained closed between the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March. [TAB,PAG] (p14)
Experienced significant flood depths and was closed between the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March; one report of internal residential flooding where tide levels overtopped a demountable barrier and entered the rear of the property via a lower–lying side gate. [TAB,PAG] (p15)
High tides on the evening of 11 March and the morning of 12 March 2020 exceeded a property's demountable flood barrier and entered via the side gate into the lower–lying rear of the property, causing internal residential flooding. (p16)
Residents at Sea Mills Lane were notified and the road was actively managed/closed as part of the response to the high tides. [PAG] (p18)
Tidal flooding inundated Sea Mills Lane, flooding the road, parked cars and one property; many properties in the area deployed flood barriers/gates in response. (p20)
The Sea Mills area was affected by the astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge; property flood defences largely prevented inundation but one property was flooded. Residential properties and nearby highway infrastructure were impacted. (p22)
Sea Mills Lane was identified for closure around the high tides on 11–12 March 2020. Residents of Sea Mills were contacted and advised to install flood barriers to protect properties; photographs of the high tide were taken at Sea Mills. (p24)","8.72 mAOD estimated high tide [TAB] (p13)
0.72 m maximum flood depth; 5 hours estimated flood duration [TAB,PAG] (p14)
one internal residential flooding incident; 5 hours (approximate flooding duration inferred) [TAB,PAG] (p15)
one property (p16)
1 property (p20)
1 property (p22)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4631404,-2.6307458,"<div id=""html_bef873f8c5ac89837d54090f616be484"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Portway, Bristol</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundation occurred at the Portway beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge due to high tides on the tidal River Avon. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal flooding encroached onto the roadway and adjacent pedestrian path (photo evidence at 12:55 on 12 Mar 2020); water nearly overtopped the stone barrier separating the road from the water, threatening road access and likely causing disruption to traffic and pedestrians. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Portway under Clifton Suspension Bridge is estimated to have experienced a high tide of 8.77 mAOD on the 11 March high tide and is included among the locations that flooded. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal inundation beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge caused significant roadway flooding; the Portway experienced an estimated maximum flood depth of 0.77 m and was closed during the tidal high periods. [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Portway was closed due to high tides on the evening of 11 March and the morning of 12 March; road closures (20:00&ndash;00:30 on 11th and 07:00&ndash;11:00 on 12th) indicate the road was flooded during those periods. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road was actively managed and closed; officers were in location to manage traffic and the decision was taken to keep the Portway closed overnight on 11 March 2020 as the tide exceeded the forecast. Maintenance contractors were instructed to pump tide water off the highway. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant tidal floodwater inundation led to closure of the Portway, disrupting an important transport route into and out of Bristol. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal floodwater significantly inundated the Portway, forcing closure of the road and disrupting a primary transport route into and out of Bristol. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Portway (beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge) was inundated during the high tide and storm surge, affecting highway infrastructure and prompting operational responses. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020 prompted highway teams to close the Portway (an hour before the high tide on the evening of 11th in both directions and on the morning of 12th in the outbound direction). Following higher than expected tide predictions, the Portway was kept closed in both directions for both high tides. Photographs were taken of the high tide at the Portway. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Higher&ndash;than&ndash;expected tides led to the Portway being closed (an hour before the high tide on the evening of 11 March and closed both directions for both high tides after monitoring); closures were implemented due to tidal inundation risk. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8.77 mAOD estimated high tide [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.77 m; 4.5 hours [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4.5 hours; 5 hours [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of","Portway, Bristol",England,"Inundation occurred at the Portway beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge due to high tides on the tidal River Avon. [IMG] (p6)
Tidal flooding encroached onto the roadway and adjacent pedestrian path (photo evidence at 12:55 on 12 Mar 2020); water nearly overtopped the stone barrier separating the road from the water, threatening road access and likely causing disruption to traffic and pedestrians. [IMG] (p12)
The Portway under Clifton Suspension Bridge is estimated to have experienced a high tide of 8.77 mAOD on the 11 March high tide and is included among the locations that flooded. [TAB] (p13)
Tidal inundation beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge caused significant roadway flooding; the Portway experienced an estimated maximum flood depth of 0.77 m and was closed during the tidal high periods. [TAB,PAG] (p14)
The Portway was closed due to high tides on the evening of 11 March and the morning of 12 March; road closures (20:00–00:30 on 11th and 07:00–11:00 on 12th) indicate the road was flooded during those periods. [TAB] (p15)
Road was actively managed and closed; officers were in location to manage traffic and the decision was taken to keep the Portway closed overnight on 11 March 2020 as the tide exceeded the forecast. Maintenance contractors were instructed to pump tide water off the highway. (p18)
Significant tidal floodwater inundation led to closure of the Portway, disrupting an important transport route into and out of Bristol. [PAG] (p20)
Tidal floodwater significantly inundated the Portway, forcing closure of the road and disrupting a primary transport route into and out of Bristol. [PAG] (p21)
The Portway (beneath the Clifton Suspension Bridge) was inundated during the high tide and storm surge, affecting highway infrastructure and prompting operational responses. (p22)
High tides on 11–12 March 2020 prompted highway teams to close the Portway (an hour before the high tide on the evening of 11th in both directions and on the morning of 12th in the outbound direction). Following higher than expected tide predictions, the Portway was kept closed in both directions for both high tides. Photographs were taken of the high tide at the Portway. [PAG] (p24)
Higher–than–expected tides led to the Portway being closed (an hour before the high tide on the evening of 11 March and closed both directions for both high tides after monitoring); closures were implemented due to tidal inundation risk. [PAG] (p25)","8.77 mAOD estimated high tide [TAB] (p13)
0.77 m; 4.5 hours [TAB,PAG] (p14)
4.5 hours; 5 hours [TAB] (p15)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4484346,-2.578807,"<div id=""html_7806d0d3502333e0e156c2d1c0d95e71"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cattle Market Road, St Philip</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on Cattle Market Road caused by high tides and elevated levels of the River Avon on 11&ndash;12 March 2020. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater accumulated beneath an underpass/rail bridge, covering the carriageway and preventing normal vehicular and pedestrian use; the image shows people wading through the floodwater, indicating water deep enough to impede passage. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundation of Cattle Market Road caused by high tidal levels on the River Avon during the 11 March high tide; the location is listed among those that flooded and was used to estimate maximum flood depths. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tide exceeded the defence/ground level (defence 7.80 m AOD; ground 7.20 m AOD) causing substantial roadway inundation; road was not reopened between the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March. [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the most vulnerable location with one of the lowest levels of protection and significant flood depths; the road was closed through the high tide period and remained closed between the evening tide on 11 March and the morning tide on 12 March, indicating prolonged flooding at this minor road. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road was actively managed and closed as part of the flood response to the high tides. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal floodwater caused significant flooding that required Cattlemarket Road to be closed. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal floodwater significantly affected Cattle Market Road and caused its closure. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in the report's list of locations affected by the 11&ndash;12 March 2020 astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge; the road and adjacent infrastructure were inundated by tidal/coastal flooding. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cattle Market Road was identified for closure in advance of the high tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020 and was included among vulnerable locations where photographs of the high tide were taken. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a vulnerable road expected to flood from the high tide; included in the list of specific roads to close and was photographed during the high tide on 12 March, indicating tidal inundation risk/impacts to the road corridor. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Estimated high tide 8.82 mAOD [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.02 m maximum flood depth; 5 hours estimated flood duration [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>≈5 hours; closed 20:00 (11 Mar) &ndash; 11:30 (12 Mar) [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of","Cattle Market Road, St Philip",England,"Flooding on Cattle Market Road caused by high tides and elevated levels of the River Avon on 11–12 March 2020. [IMG] (p6)
Floodwater accumulated beneath an underpass/rail bridge, covering the carriageway and preventing normal vehicular and pedestrian use; the image shows people wading through the floodwater, indicating water deep enough to impede passage. [IMG] (p12)
Inundation of Cattle Market Road caused by high tidal levels on the River Avon during the 11 March high tide; the location is listed among those that flooded and was used to estimate maximum flood depths. [TAB] (p13)
High tide exceeded the defence/ground level (defence 7.80 m AOD; ground 7.20 m AOD) causing substantial roadway inundation; road was not reopened between the evening high tide on 11 March and the morning high tide on 12 March. [TAB,PAG] (p14)
Identified as the most vulnerable location with one of the lowest levels of protection and significant flood depths; the road was closed through the high tide period and remained closed between the evening tide on 11 March and the morning tide on 12 March, indicating prolonged flooding at this minor road. [TAB,PAG] (p15)
Road was actively managed and closed as part of the flood response to the high tides. (p18)
Tidal floodwater caused significant flooding that required Cattlemarket Road to be closed. (p20)
Tidal floodwater significantly affected Cattle Market Road and caused its closure. (p21)
Included in the report's list of locations affected by the 11–12 March 2020 astronomical high tide combined with a storm surge; the road and adjacent infrastructure were inundated by tidal/coastal flooding. (p22)
Cattle Market Road was identified for closure in advance of the high tides on 11–12 March 2020 and was included among vulnerable locations where photographs of the high tide were taken. [PAG] (p24)
Identified as a vulnerable road expected to flood from the high tide; included in the list of specific roads to close and was photographed during the high tide on 12 March, indicating tidal inundation risk/impacts to the road corridor. [PAG] (p25)","Estimated high tide 8.82 mAOD [TAB] (p13)
1.02 m maximum flood depth; 5 hours estimated flood duration [TAB,PAG] (p14)
≈5 hours; closed 20:00 (11 Mar) – 11:30 (12 Mar) [TAB,PAG] (p15)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4461291,-2.5979516,"<div id=""html_0437e80496ca1480a26295a4c20f6c11"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cumberland Road, Bristol</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cumberland Road is listed and mapped as one of the locations flooded by the tidal River Avon during the high tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cumberland Road was impacted by flooding from the astronomical high tide combined with the storm surge, affecting highway and nearby residential infrastructure. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of","Cumberland Road, Bristol",England,"Cumberland Road is listed and mapped as one of the locations flooded by the tidal River Avon during the high tides on 11–12 March 2020. [IMG] (p6)
Cumberland Road was impacted by flooding from the astronomical high tide combined with the storm surge, affecting highway and nearby residential infrastructure. (p22)",,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4456114,-2.5867098,"<div id=""html_8b9018621f8da7f0e620ba91756be4e3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Clarence Road, Redcliffe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the tidal River Avon inundated Clarence Road on 11&ndash;12 March 2020, causing the roadway and adjacent areas to be flooded. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Estimated to have experienced a high tide of 8.81 mAOD on the 11 March high tide and included among locations used to estimate maximum flood depths. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Shallow tidal inundation impacted Clarence Road briefly; estimated maximum flood depth was around 0.21 m though the road was not closed. [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road remained open during the high tides but may have experienced very shallow, short&ndash;duration flooding around the high tide periods. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported as affected by the high&ndash;tide / storm&ndash;surge flood on 11&ndash;12 March 2020; the road and surrounding area experienced inundation from tidal/coastal flooding. (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8.81 mAOD estimated high tide [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.21 m [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.5 hours [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of","Clarence Road, Redcliffe",England,"Flooding from the tidal River Avon inundated Clarence Road on 11–12 March 2020, causing the roadway and adjacent areas to be flooded. [IMG] (p6)
