GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.44700657438459,-2.5949655612565343,"<div id=""html_8951898872c7ebcfe33b8331b73b72f1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bathurst Basin</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the tidal River Avon inundated Bathurst Basin (and the adjoining Commercial Road area) on 11&ndash;12 March 2020. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal floodwater overtopped basin barriers and flowed into the Floating Harbour at Bathurst Basin; photographed overtopping showed barrier deformation indicating significant hydraulic pressure on the structure. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal inundation during the 11 March 2020 high tide; Table 5 lists Bathurst Basin Dam among locations that flooded and estimates a high tide level for the Basin leading to local tidal flooding. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bathurst Basin (Bathurst Basin Dam/defences) experienced overtopping with tidal water flowing into the Floating Harbour. [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundation of Bathurst Basin as part of the tidal surge event (astronomical high tide combined with storm surge) that peaked at 8.81mAOD, affecting the basin and adjacent infrastructure. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed and photographed high tide at a vulnerable location (pictures taken on/around 12 March), indicating the basin experienced elevated tidal levels during the event. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tide at Bathurst Basin was observed and photographed during the event, indicating elevated water levels and local tidal inundation risk to this basin and adjacent low&ndash;lying areas. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8.78 mAOD [TAB] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Bathurst Basin,England,"Flooding from the tidal River Avon inundated Bathurst Basin (and the adjoining Commercial Road area) on 11–12 March 2020. (p6)
Tidal floodwater overtopped basin barriers and flowed into the Floating Harbour at Bathurst Basin; photographed overtopping showed barrier deformation indicating significant hydraulic pressure on the structure. [IMG] (p11)
Tidal inundation during the 11 March 2020 high tide; Table 5 lists Bathurst Basin Dam among locations that flooded and estimates a high tide level for the Basin leading to local tidal flooding. [TAB] (p13)
Bathurst Basin (Bathurst Basin Dam/defences) experienced overtopping with tidal water flowing into the Floating Harbour. [TAB,PAG] (p14)
Inundation of Bathurst Basin as part of the tidal surge event (astronomical high tide combined with storm surge) that peaked at 8.81mAOD, affecting the basin and adjacent infrastructure. (p22)
Observed and photographed high tide at a vulnerable location (pictures taken on/around 12 March), indicating the basin experienced elevated tidal levels during the event. [PAG] (p24)
High tide at Bathurst Basin was observed and photographed during the event, indicating elevated water levels and local tidal inundation risk to this basin and adjacent low–lying areas. [PAG] (p25)",8.78 mAOD [TAB] (p13),GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4480297,-2.6180624,"<div id=""html_d56024da6a4e55d314bccd52998b8960"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Junction Lock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the tidal River Avon affected the Floating Harbour entrance lock and junction lock, identified as flood sites on the event map. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal floodwater overtopped the lock wall at Junction Lock, allowing water into the Cumberland Basin/Floating Harbour and producing turbulent overflow adjacent to built infrastructure and walkways. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Junction Lock experienced elevated tidal water levels during the 11 March 2020 high tide, and estimated maximum flood depths for the location were calculated from the estimated tide level and local defence/road elevations. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood defence at Junction Lock was overtopped, with tidal water flowing into the Floating Harbour (overtopping into the harbour was reported). [TAB,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Entrance Lock and Junction Lock were affected by the high tide and storm surge, experiencing inundation associated with the event. (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>estimated high tide 8.78 mAOD [TAB] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Junction Lock,England,"Flooding from the tidal River Avon affected the Floating Harbour entrance lock and junction lock, identified as flood sites on the event map. (p6)
Tidal floodwater overtopped the lock wall at Junction Lock, allowing water into the Cumberland Basin/Floating Harbour and producing turbulent overflow adjacent to built infrastructure and walkways. [IMG] (p11)
Junction Lock experienced elevated tidal water levels during the 11 March 2020 high tide, and estimated maximum flood depths for the location were calculated from the estimated tide level and local defence/road elevations. [TAB] (p13)
The flood defence at Junction Lock was overtopped, with tidal water flowing into the Floating Harbour (overtopping into the harbour was reported). [TAB,PAG] (p14)
The Entrance Lock and Junction Lock were affected by the high tide and storm surge, experiencing inundation associated with the event. (p22)",estimated high tide 8.78 mAOD [TAB] (p13),GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4795006,-2.6496514,"<div id=""html_a4a1cac96a012d2d86041640a40a58eb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sea Mills</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal flooding extents were observed during the high&ndash;tide/ storm surge event; visual evidence (videos and photographs) of tidal flood extents at vulnerable areas was gathered, prompting recommendations to investigate flood risk and potential mitigation measures and to improve modelling for Sea Mills. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the tidal River Avon impacted the Sea Mills 2 location adjacent to the river, recorded as a flood site on the event map. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal flooding inundated Sea Mills Lane (photo evidence at 08:30 on 11 Mar 2020) with water covering the road and encroaching onto adjacent gardens and properties; multiple parked vehicles were partially submerged and the road was rendered impassable. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sea Mills was subject to the tidal flood response; a flood warden was appointed for Sea Mills and property flood resilience was arranged/delivered. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Sea Mills area was affected by tidal flooding during high tide events: flooding occurred on Sea Mills Lane (road, parked cars and one property) and many properties deployed flood barriers/gates. The area is identified as particularly vulnerable to tidal flooding and at increased future risk from sea level rise. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flood defences successfully kept out flood water except for one property which was flooded. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents in Sea Mills were directly warned of high tides and advised to install flood barriers to protect properties; the location was photographed during the high tide, indicating local tidal inundation risk/impacts. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Sea Mills,England,"Tidal flooding extents were observed during the high–tide/ storm surge event; visual evidence (videos and photographs) of tidal flood extents at vulnerable areas was gathered, prompting recommendations to investigate flood risk and potential mitigation measures and to improve modelling for Sea Mills. (p4)
Flooding from the tidal River Avon impacted the Sea Mills 2 location adjacent to the river, recorded as a flood site on the event map. (p6)
Tidal flooding inundated Sea Mills Lane (photo evidence at 08:30 on 11 Mar 2020) with water covering the road and encroaching onto adjacent gardens and properties; multiple parked vehicles were partially submerged and the road was rendered impassable. [IMG] (p12)
Sea Mills was subject to the tidal flood response; a flood warden was appointed for Sea Mills and property flood resilience was arranged/delivered. (p19)
The Sea Mills area was affected by tidal flooding during high tide events: flooding occurred on Sea Mills Lane (road, parked cars and one property) and many properties deployed flood barriers/gates. The area is identified as particularly vulnerable to tidal flooding and at increased future risk from sea level rise. (p20)
Property flood defences successfully kept out flood water except for one property which was flooded. (p22)
Residents in Sea Mills were directly warned of high tides and advised to install flood barriers to protect properties; the location was photographed during the high tide, indicating local tidal inundation risk/impacts. [PAG] (p25)","1 property (p20)
one property (p22)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.498926,-2.695544,"<div id=""html_33ad67f4dfc7601425cb76c685c2f3ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Avonmouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Measured high tide levels at the Avonmouth monitor on 11&ndash;12 March 2020 exceeded the EA forecasts, showing a larger&ndash;than&ndash;predicted surge component which contributed to higher tidal levels affecting the area. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Used as the upstream tidal reference point for forecasts; an added adjustment of 0.15m to Avonmouth tide values underestimated actual increases to the city centre, contributing to higher&ndash;than&ndash;predicted city&ndash;centre tide levels during the March events. [MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced the measured high tide at the gauge of 8.63 mAOD on the 11 March high tide and was one of the locations used to assess the tidal inundation that led to flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Avonmouth area was affected by the tidal event; drainage (the rhine network) was managed by the Lower Severn IDB to control flows and reduce flood risk. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tidal water levels at Avonmouth (predicted and observed) produced a tidal flood risk to Bristol city centre that drove operational responses: forecasts and observed peaks were used to assess whether city defences would provide protection and informed actions such as pre&ndash;lowering the floating harbour and opening Eastville Sluices, monitoring tidal gauges, and triggering road closures and warnings to residents and businesses. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8.63 mAOD measured high tide [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Avonmouth,England,"Measured high tide levels at the Avonmouth monitor on 11–12 March 2020 exceeded the EA forecasts, showing a larger–than–predicted surge component which contributed to higher tidal levels affecting the area. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p8)
Used as the upstream tidal reference point for forecasts; an added adjustment of 0.15m to Avonmouth tide values underestimated actual increases to the city centre, contributing to higher–than–predicted city–centre tide levels during the March events. [MUL] (p10)
Experienced the measured high tide at the gauge of 8.63 mAOD on the 11 March high tide and was one of the locations used to assess the tidal inundation that led to flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
The Avonmouth area was affected by the tidal event; drainage (the rhine network) was managed by the Lower Severn IDB to control flows and reduce flood risk. (p19)
High tidal water levels at Avonmouth (predicted and observed) produced a tidal flood risk to Bristol city centre that drove operational responses: forecasts and observed peaks were used to assess whether city defences would provide protection and informed actions such as pre–lowering the floating harbour and opening Eastville Sluices, monitoring tidal gauges, and triggering road closures and warnings to residents and businesses. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p23)","8.63 mAOD measured high tide [TAB,PAG] (p13)",GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4469267,-2.5694717,"<div id=""html_1da3c130a1ce3663dc590dc9ef046166"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Philips Marsh</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping occurred at Bathurst Basin with water flowing over barrier structures into the Harbour (photographic evidence). In St Phillips Marsh, existing formal defences and buildings acted as de&ndash;facto defences and prevented flooding of Albert Road and surrounding areas. (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",St Philips Marsh,England,"Overtopping occurred at Bathurst Basin with water flowing over barrier structures into the Harbour (photographic evidence). In St Phillips Marsh, existing formal defences and buildings acted as de–facto defences and prevented flooding of Albert Road and surrounding areas. (p11)",,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4842773,-2.6789317,"<div id=""html_ee71c489245a026842397a7d419df898"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Shirehampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Environment Agency closed the flood gates on both high&ndash;tide occasions (11 and 12 March 2020), protecting properties from flooding. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood gates at Shirehampton (along the River Avon) were closed and pre&ndash; and post&ndash;tide checks, including clearance of key structures and screens, were undertaken to manage tidal flooding risk. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood gates were closed in response to the high tides/storm surge. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",Shirehampton,England,"The Environment Agency closed the flood gates on both high–tide occasions (11 and 12 March 2020), protecting properties from flooding. (p16)
Flood gates at Shirehampton (along the River Avon) were closed and pre– and post–tide checks, including clearance of key structures and screens, were undertaken to manage tidal flooding risk. (p19)
Flood gates were closed in response to the high tides/storm surge. (p22)",,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BST,1,2020-03-11,51.4530652,-2.5750154,"<div id=""html_a116490518cb6bc040b6349d6a076257"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Philip</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Avon footpath at Crew's Hole Road and St Phillips was impacted by high tides and was actively managed/closed as part of the flood response. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Avon footpath at Crew's Hole Road and St Phillips (between Totterdown bridge and Cattle Market Road) was identified for closure due to high tides on 11&ndash;12 March 2020, reflecting anticipated tidal inundation of the footpath area. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bristol, City of",St Philip,England,"The River Avon footpath at Crew's Hole Road and St Phillips was impacted by high tides and was actively managed/closed as part of the flood response. (p18)
The River Avon footpath at Crew's Hole Road and St Phillips (between Totterdown bridge and Cattle Market Road) was identified for closure due to high tides on 11–12 March 2020, reflecting anticipated tidal inundation of the footpath area. (p24)",,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11.html,GB-BST_1_2020-03-11,Bristol flooding 11 Mar 2020 - 12 Mar 2020,Flood Investigation: March 2020 Tidal Flooding,016/Bristol/016/2020/Bristol/Flood Investigation Report - River Avon - March 2020.pdf
GB-BDF,4,2020-12-23,52.1894467,-0.5850905,"<div id=""html_6bf9c57715d0fbb60df9aaa55a0c1002"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pavenham Road, Chellington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow routes and land drains discharge into the Pavenham Road watercourse; water backing up at culverts causes overland flow into the central shaded flood risk area, leading to flooding of adjacent land, roads and nearby properties. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and an overwhelmed culvert beneath Pavenham Road caused floodwater to back up and follow local topography. The road was rendered impassable and two nearby residential properties experienced internal flooding with damage to floors and carpets; one property had flood depths reported up to 300 mm with sandbags in place. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two residential properties; 300 mm flood depth [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bedford,"Pavenham Road, Chellington",England,"Overland flow routes and land drains discharge into the Pavenham Road watercourse; water backing up at culverts causes overland flow into the central shaded flood risk area, leading to flooding of adjacent land, roads and nearby properties. (p1)
Surface water runoff and an overwhelmed culvert beneath Pavenham Road caused floodwater to back up and follow local topography. The road was rendered impassable and two nearby residential properties experienced internal flooding with damage to floors and carpets; one property had flood depths reported up to 300 mm with sandbags in place. [TAB] (p2)",two residential properties; 300 mm flood depth [TAB] (p2),GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23.html,GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23,Carlton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Carlton - Pavenham Road,005/Bedford/005/2021/Bedford/Carlton-Pavenham-Road/July/2021.pdf
GB-BDF,4,2020-12-23,52.1895833,-0.6040303,"<div id=""html_e6856974957d116198f2dbca536a21a5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Causeway, Carlton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported to be impassable due to floodwater during the event. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bedford,"The Causeway, Carlton",England,Reported to be impassable due to floodwater during the event. (p2),,GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23.html,GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23,Carlton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Carlton - Pavenham Road,005/Bedford/005/2021/Bedford/Carlton-Pavenham-Road/July/2021.pdf
GB-BDF,4,2020-12-23,52.1925669,-0.6018362,"<div id=""html_bd647ecbf848ee29f77aa856987c85a5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridgend, Chellington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported to be impassable due to floodwater during the event. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bedford,"Bridgend, Chellington",England,Reported to be impassable due to floodwater during the event. (p2),,GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23.html,GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23,Carlton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Carlton - Pavenham Road,005/Bedford/005/2021/Bedford/Carlton-Pavenham-Road/July/2021.pdf
GB-BDF,4,2020-12-23,52.19037,-0.6049492,"<div id=""html_607881efa76833433011b7a0ca876deb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Carlton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map shows Carlton within the mapped flood&ndash;risk area where overland flow routes converge and culvert backups can cause water to overflow into adjacent land and infrastructure; roads and properties in the central shaded zone are at risk of inundation from surface water and drainage backup. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pavenham Road watercourse runs culverted for approximately 350 m beneath Carlton; heavy rainfall and an overwhelmed culvert (and identified maintenance issues) contributed to local flooding. Internal flooding occurred at nearby residential properties causing damage to floors and carpets. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two residential properties [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bedford,Carlton,England,"Map shows Carlton within the mapped flood–risk area where overland flow routes converge and culvert backups can cause water to overflow into adjacent land and infrastructure; roads and properties in the central shaded zone are at risk of inundation from surface water and drainage backup. (p1)
The Pavenham Road watercourse runs culverted for approximately 350 m beneath Carlton; heavy rainfall and an overwhelmed culvert (and identified maintenance issues) contributed to local flooding. Internal flooding occurred at nearby residential properties causing damage to floors and carpets. [TAB] (p2)",two residential properties [TAB] (p2),GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23.html,GB-BDF_4_2020-12-23,Carlton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Carlton - Pavenham Road,005/Bedford/005/2021/Bedford/Carlton-Pavenham-Road/July/2021.pdf
GB-NNH,25,2020-12-23,52.5104138,-0.5180799,"<div id=""html_7b5fdbdcd71a36f93d02a438350fbd95"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Southwick Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Southwick Brook exceeded its capacity upstream of a floodwall, contributing to overflow and the subsequent surface water reaching properties. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Brook capacity was exceeded and the channel overtopped about 100m upstream of Main Street bridge; water levels deepened upstream of the bridge and high downstream levels contributed to overland flows that combined with other surface water to flood properties on Main Street. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Brook overtopped before the start of the concrete flood wall and excess water flowed over local ground to low points around properties. Outfall pipes were covered by high water, restricting discharge and exacerbating surface water ponding and flows onto Main Street. Pipes beneath Main Street and at the ditch northwest of Main Street Bridge and at Millennium Cottage were exceeded, contributing to surface water flowing onto Main Street. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 properties (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>depth of water in Main Street exceeded 1 metre in places; external property areas ~300 mm; internal depths between 20 mm and 150 mm (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Southwick Brook,England,"Southwick Brook exceeded its capacity upstream of a floodwall, contributing to overflow and the subsequent surface water reaching properties. (p7)
Brook capacity was exceeded and the channel overtopped about 100m upstream of Main Street bridge; water levels deepened upstream of the bridge and high downstream levels contributed to overland flows that combined with other surface water to flood properties on Main Street. (p15)
