GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.4376577,-1.984486,"<div id=""html_b21e7d51fd57a2f39bb6dc2fc8cd79dc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Senneleys Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Stonehouse Brook and a smaller tributary run through Senneleys Park; high flows and breaches in these watercourses are linked to the reported flooding in the local watercourse corridor. The park lies adjacent to mapped high/medium flood&ndash;risk zones and was likely subject to overbank flow or local inundation during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse through Senneleys Park (including Bartley Brook) was identified for updated hydraulic modelling to assess capacity and inform mitigation following the 16 June flood. [TAB] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Senneleys Park,England,"The Stonehouse Brook and a smaller tributary run through Senneleys Park; high flows and breaches in these watercourses are linked to the reported flooding in the local watercourse corridor. The park lies adjacent to mapped high/medium flood–risk zones and was likely subject to overbank flow or local inundation during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p20)
The watercourse through Senneleys Park (including Bartley Brook) was identified for updated hydraulic modelling to assess capacity and inform mitigation following the 16 June flood. [TAB] (p21)",,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.4518175,-1.9634629,"<div id=""html_73f63460be9418cb78c8fbe989b642a1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harborne Golf Course</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 16 June 2016 storm the rainfall rate exceeded infiltration on the golf course, producing substantial surface water runoff that flowed downslope toward Fredas Grove and contributed to flooding of gardens, the highway and the one property affected. A historic culverted watercourse through the course (now forming part of the sewer) removed a natural discharge route and may have exacerbated uncontrolled runoff. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 16th June 2016 storm the golf course generated uncontrolled surface water runoff (partly because a historic watercourse across the course has been culverted and now forms part of the sewer network). Runoff left the course, following overland flow routes toward Harts Green/Fredas Grove, entering gardens, properties and the highway where drainage/sewer capacity was exceeded. [TAB] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Harborne Golf Course,England,"During the 16 June 2016 storm the rainfall rate exceeded infiltration on the golf course, producing substantial surface water runoff that flowed downslope toward Fredas Grove and contributed to flooding of gardens, the highway and the one property affected. A historic culverted watercourse through the course (now forming part of the sewer) removed a natural discharge route and may have exacerbated uncontrolled runoff. (p41)
During the 16th June 2016 storm the golf course generated uncontrolled surface water runoff (partly because a historic watercourse across the course has been culverted and now forms part of the sewer network). Runoff left the course, following overland flow routes toward Harts Green/Fredas Grove, entering gardens, properties and the highway where drainage/sewer capacity was exceeded. [TAB] (p42)",,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.4592501,-1.9707502,"<div id=""html_85bedfb28f1586a0005f5df47cf7ecb4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Queen's Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High intensity rainfall exceeded infiltration capacity in the park, generating surface water runoff that was directed southwards through the park into downstream streets (source area for flows that led to property and highway flooding); a breach location at the park car park is indicated as the origin of overland flow routes. [IMG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 16th June 2016 exceeded infiltration in the park, producing southward surface water runoff through Queen's Park that flowed into Queen's Court and Harts Green Road and contributed to ponding and internal property flooding at the low point of Harts Green Road. A flood storage area is proposed at the southern park boundary to store runoff and reduce flows to the sewer network. [IMG] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 internal property flooding incidents [IMG] (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Queen's Park,England,"High intensity rainfall exceeded infiltration capacity in the park, generating surface water runoff that was directed southwards through the park into downstream streets (source area for flows that led to property and highway flooding); a breach location at the park car park is indicated as the origin of overland flow routes. [IMG] (p43)
Intense rainfall on 16th June 2016 exceeded infiltration in the park, producing southward surface water runoff through Queen's Park that flowed into Queen's Court and Harts Green Road and contributed to ponding and internal property flooding at the low point of Harts Green Road. A flood storage area is proposed at the southern park boundary to store runoff and reduce flows to the sewer network. [IMG] (p44)",6 internal property flooding incidents [IMG] (p44),GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.5485102,-1.9005711,"<div id=""html_ba3bcb58d745a6033a5921d910b364f5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Aldridge Road Recreation Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified on the figure/map as a breach location and source of potential overland flow routes east and southeast toward Old Oscott. The map and text link ponding/runoff at the recreation ground to flows affecting nearby properties. (Report map shows a potential breach; the text describes ponding and runoff but does not explicitly confirm a physical structural breach.) [IMG] (p49)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Aldridge Road Recreation Ground,England,Identified on the figure/map as a breach location and source of potential overland flow routes east and southeast toward Old Oscott. The map and text link ponding/runoff at the recreation ground to flows affecting nearby properties. (Report map shows a potential breach; the text describes ponding and runoff but does not explicitly confirm a physical structural breach.) [IMG] (p49),,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
