GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.5630444,-1.7793214,"<div id=""html_5f879f062fafd8922272c80f243c8b82"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Churchill Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The brook exceeded its capacity and overtopped its banks (breached at Breach Location 1). Out&ndash;of&ndash;channel flows travelled through open space and gardens and contributed to ponding at the low point near properties on Carhampton Road, adding to internal property flooding. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported to have exceeded capacity with water levels rising beyond the banks; flows reportedly breached the banks at a breach location, left the channel, travelled through open space and gardens and contributed to flooding and ponding near nearby properties. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Churchill Brook,England,"The brook exceeded its capacity and overtopped its banks (breached at Breach Location 1). Out–of–channel flows travelled through open space and gardens and contributed to ponding at the low point near properties on Carhampton Road, adding to internal property flooding. (p28)
Reported to have exceeded capacity with water levels rising beyond the banks; flows reportedly breached the banks at a breach location, left the channel, travelled through open space and gardens and contributed to flooding and ponding near nearby properties. [TAB,PAG] (p29)",2 properties (p28),GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.4459562,-1.9577666,"<div id=""html_6249fea4d01d84900b85ffdc9e5be40f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bourn Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Capacity of the Bourn Brook channel was exceeded during the 16 June 2016 storm, causing rear garden and internal flooding to properties (notably in Osmaston Road and Reservoir Road). The effect was exacerbated by debris, vegetation and silt reducing channel and bridge&ndash;arch capacity. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main river whose channel accumulated debris, vegetation and silt (most notably reducing capacity at the Elford Road bridge arches), contributing to overtopping/overflow and properties flooding from the river at the rear. [PAG,MUL] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bourn forms the downstream watercourse receiving flows from Wood Brook (which came out of channel at the Bristol Road culvert). Excess flows in Wood Brook contributed to increased flows in the Bourn and are part of the Bourn catchment flood response and mapping. [PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The channel capacity was overwhelmed and the brook breached, allowing floodwater to escape the channel and flow south&ndash;eastwards. The breach adjacent to Birmingham Dental Hospital released water that travelled southwards across the Recreation Ground and contributed to overland flow towards the River Rea and urban streets. [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The capacity of Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and it breached adjacent to Birmingham Dental Hospital (Breach Location 1). The breach allowed flows to run southwards through the Recreation Ground and generate overland flow across Pershore Road into properties fronting Riverside Drive and contributed surface water runoff into St John's Road, Third Avenue and Fourth Avenue. [PAG] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows from residential areas and the Country Park routed towards and joined the Bourn/Bourne Brook, indicating increased flows in the watercourse associated with the storm event. (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 flooded properties (total reported in the area on 16 June 2016) [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties (across Pershore Road, Riverside Drive, St John's Road, Third Avenue and Fourth Avenue) [PAG] (p70)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Bourn Brook,England,"Capacity of the Bourn Brook channel was exceeded during the 16 June 2016 storm, causing rear garden and internal flooding to properties (notably in Osmaston Road and Reservoir Road). The effect was exacerbated by debris, vegetation and silt reducing channel and bridge–arch capacity. [IMG,PAG] (p38)
Main river whose channel accumulated debris, vegetation and silt (most notably reducing capacity at the Elford Road bridge arches), contributing to overtopping/overflow and properties flooding from the river at the rear. [PAG,MUL] (p39)
The Bourn forms the downstream watercourse receiving flows from Wood Brook (which came out of channel at the Bristol Road culvert). Excess flows in Wood Brook contributed to increased flows in the Bourn and are part of the Bourn catchment flood response and mapping. [PAG] (p65)
The channel capacity was overwhelmed and the brook breached, allowing floodwater to escape the channel and flow south–eastwards. The breach adjacent to Birmingham Dental Hospital released water that travelled southwards across the Recreation Ground and contributed to overland flow towards the River Rea and urban streets. [IMG,PAG] (p69)
The capacity of Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and it breached adjacent to Birmingham Dental Hospital (Breach Location 1). The breach allowed flows to run southwards through the Recreation Ground and generate overland flow across Pershore Road into properties fronting Riverside Drive and contributed surface water runoff into St John's Road, Third Avenue and Fourth Avenue. [PAG] (p70)
Overland flows from residential areas and the Country Park routed towards and joined the Bourn/Bourne Brook, indicating increased flows in the watercourse associated with the storm event. (p72)","33 flooded properties (total reported in the area on 16 June 2016) [IMG,PAG] (p38)
33 properties [IMG,PAG] (p69)
33 properties (across Pershore Road, Riverside Drive, St John's Road, Third Avenue and Fourth Avenue) [PAG] (p70)",GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
