Surface water flow routes and Perry Brook river flooding affected residential areas in Perry Beeches; the Risk of Flooding from Surface Water map for Perry Beeches correlates with areas flooded on 8th June, with flows funnelled by local topography toward valley low points. [PAG,IMG] (p56)","44 incidents of internal property flooding; 10 roads reported with internal property flooding (p54)
44 properties; 10 roads with internal property flooding; more than 20 roads with garden/highway flooding [PAG,IMG] (p55)",GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.5899478,-1.8005673,"<div id=""html_1c8c029f5e4d898b7b0f6c097cb53993"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Roughley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area&ndash;level flooding during the June 2016 storms: surface water runoff followed local topography and ponded in low points of the highway; sewer and highway drainage capacity was overwhelmed and surcharged, allowing water to overtop kerbs and enter property curtilages and gardens, causing internal flooding and external ponding (including near Weaver Court). [IMG,MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff ponded in the highway, overtopped kerbs and flowed down through rear gardens and into properties; underlying sewer and highway drainage capacity was overwhelmed causing ponding and sewer surcharge; electrical components and pumps failed during the storm; floodwaters later receded as gullies and drainage regained capacity. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p62)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties [IMG,MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p62)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Roughley,England,"Area–level flooding during the June 2016 storms: surface water runoff followed local topography and ponded in low points of the highway; sewer and highway drainage capacity was overwhelmed and surcharged, allowing water to overtop kerbs and enter property curtilages and gardens, causing internal flooding and external ponding (including near Weaver Court). [IMG,MUL] (p61)
Surface water runoff ponded in the highway, overtopped kerbs and flowed down through rear gardens and into properties; underlying sewer and highway drainage capacity was overwhelmed causing ponding and sewer surcharge; electrical components and pumps failed during the storm; floodwaters later receded as gullies and drainage regained capacity. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p62)","8 properties [IMG,MUL] (p61)
8 properties [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p62)",GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.440224,-1.9382689,"<div id=""html_c5206f861c2138a3daa26e76ada27b90"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Selly Oak</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced a combined flooding event: the Wood Brook came out of channel at a culvert inlet, causing overland flow that flooded parkland behind residential streets and resulted in internal flooding to two properties; highway drainage and sewer infrastructure also contributed. (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p64)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Selly Oak,England,"Area experienced a combined flooding event: the Wood Brook came out of channel at a culvert inlet, causing overland flow that flooded parkland behind residential streets and resulted in internal flooding to two properties; highway drainage and sewer infrastructure also contributed. (p64)",2 properties (p64),GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.444046,-1.9204327,"<div id=""html_cc7a2657e36e94f876b2a0a60be8356d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Selly Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the storms on 16th June 2016, 33 properties in Selly Park North reported internal flooding; highways, footpaths and gardens were also flooded. The capacity of Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and it breached adjacent to Birmingham Dental Hospital (Breach Location 1), with a further breach upstream of Pershore Road (Breach Location 2). Floodwater flowed southwards across the Recreation Ground and overland towards the River Rea, directing runoff into local streets. (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Selly Park,England,"Following the storms on 16th June 2016, 33 properties in Selly Park North reported internal flooding; highways, footpaths and gardens were also flooded. The capacity of Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and it breached adjacent to Birmingham Dental Hospital (Breach Location 1), with a further breach upstream of Pershore Road (Breach Location 2). Floodwater flowed southwards across the Recreation Ground and overland towards the River Rea, directing runoff into local streets. (p69)",33 properties (p69),GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-BIR,1,2016-06-08,52.44575246364059,-1.9957047952297056,"<div id=""html_5d642313d58cd85481bd17c6ff22bffb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woodgate Valley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties across Woodgate Valley experienced internal flooding when intense rainfall overwhelmed property and highway drainage, causing ponding, overtopping of kerbs and flow into properties. (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>38 properties (p72)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Woodgate Valley,England,"Residential properties across Woodgate Valley experienced internal flooding when intense rainfall overwhelmed property and highway drainage, causing ponding, overtopping of kerbs and flow into properties. (p72)",38 properties (p72),GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc.html,GB-BIR_1_2016-06-08_etc,"Birmingham flooding 08 Jun 2016, Birmingham flooding 10 Jun 2016 and Birmingham flooding 16 Jun 2016","June 2016 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2016/Birmingham/S19/Flooding/Report///June/2016/R1.pdf
GB-DER,2,2014-07-19,52.9212617,-1.4761491,"<div id=""html_6894ce54a245cb87807e681e75217da1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas within Derby (notably Oakwood/Bishops Drive) experienced intense short&ndash;duration rainfall causing surface water flooding that led to internal property flooding and widespread highway and garden flooding. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The urban surface water sewer network in the Oakwood area of Derby discharges to Oakwood Brook. During the intense rainfall of 19th July 2014 the network capacity would be exceeded and large volumes of runoff from paved urban areas were focused rapidly toward the sewer outfall and the brook, increasing downstream surface water flood risk and concentrating flow toward the base of the local valleys. [IMG,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the city (Oakwood) was impacted by a short&ndash;duration, high&ndash;intensity storm that produced rapid surface water runoff and caused the public surface water sewer serving a large urban catchment to be overwhelmed. This led to internal flooding of residential properties in Oakwood and widespread external flooding of gardens and highways in the affected neighbourhoods. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall overwhelmed drainage and produced rapid runoff and overland flow toward Wood Brook and Oakwood Brook, causing significant surface water flooding of properties and highways across large areas of the east of Derby (including Oakwood). A cluster of streets between Bishops Drive and Wood Brook was particularly affected. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred across many areas when highway drainage capacity was exceeded by a combination of highway and private surface water during the intense, isolated storm on the afternoon of 19th July 2014 (Oakwood was specifically affected). (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 10 properties (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 10 homes [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 10 properties [PAG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Derby,England,"Areas within Derby (notably Oakwood/Bishops Drive) experienced intense short–duration rainfall causing surface water flooding that led to internal property flooding and widespread highway and garden flooding. (p3)
The urban surface water sewer network in the Oakwood area of Derby discharges to Oakwood Brook. During the intense rainfall of 19th July 2014 the network capacity would be exceeded and large volumes of runoff from paved urban areas were focused rapidly toward the sewer outfall and the brook, increasing downstream surface water flood risk and concentrating flow toward the base of the local valleys. [IMG,PAG] (p7)
Part of the city (Oakwood) was impacted by a short–duration, high–intensity storm that produced rapid surface water runoff and caused the public surface water sewer serving a large urban catchment to be overwhelmed. This led to internal flooding of residential properties in Oakwood and widespread external flooding of gardens and highways in the affected neighbourhoods. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Intense, short–duration rainfall overwhelmed drainage and produced rapid runoff and overland flow toward Wood Brook and Oakwood Brook, causing significant surface water flooding of properties and highways across large areas of the east of Derby (including Oakwood). A cluster of streets between Bishops Drive and Wood Brook was particularly affected. [PAG] (p17)
Surface water flooding occurred across many areas when highway drainage capacity was exceeded by a combination of highway and private surface water during the intense, isolated storm on the afternoon of 19th July 2014 (Oakwood was specifically affected). (p18)","at least 10 properties (p3)
at least 10 homes [PAG,IMG] (p12)
at least 10 properties [PAG] (p17)",GB-DER_2_2014-07-19.html,GB-DER_2_2014-07-19,Derby flooding 19 Jul 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bishops Drive, Oakwood 19th July 2014",033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Bishops-Drive-Oakwood-S19.pdf
GB-DER,2,2014-07-19,52.9424664,-1.4327643,"<div id=""html_79e3b202fa51b91e1ecf4d968373b873"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bishops Drive, Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced internal flooding to properties, severe highway flooding and external (garden) flooding; further extensive external flooding and possible additional internal flooding was reported on Bishops Drive. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cluster of reported internal and external property flooding in the Bishops Drive area and highway flooding. [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Homes on the street experienced internal flooding; highways and gardens in the area suffered severe external property flooding, in some cases close to entering ground&ndash;floor living areas. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from an extreme short&ndash;duration storm overwhelmed the urban drainage (road gullies and public surface water sewer), producing overland flows and accumulation in low spots across the Bishops Drive area that led to internal flooding of properties and damage to ground&ndash;floor living areas. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Accumulation of surface water on the highway and reports of one or more surface water sewer manholes surcharging on Bishops Drive, producing increased overland flow downslope toward neighbouring streets and properties. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant highway surface water flooding occurred near the junction with Earls Crescent; wash of soil and sediment from the adjacent recreation ground onto the road was observed; reports indicate up to ten properties in the Bishops Drive area were flooded internally by surface water (reported/unconfirmed). Foul sewer surcharging and evidence of foul&ndash;water flooding were observed in the area shortly after the event. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the Earls Crescent junction &mdash; the road and junction were inundated during the intense rainfall on 19th July 2014, shown in a resident's photograph of the event. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was concentrated in the cluster of streets between Bishops Drive and Wood Brook, with properties and the highway affected by surface water and overland flow from the intense storm. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The local surface water sewer network that serves the Bishops Drive area was considered likely to have been overwhelmed by the intense rainfall, contributing to surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding in the area. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents reported flooding of properties during the storm on 19th July 2014; foul water (sewer) flooding incidents were reported to City Council officers and referenced in Severn Trent Water records. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 10 properties (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>At least 10 homes affected internally across the six listed streets (collectively). (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 10 properties (across the affected Bishops Drive area) (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Up to 10 properties [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 10 properties [PAG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Bishops Drive, Derby",England,"Experienced internal flooding to properties, severe highway flooding and external (garden) flooding; further extensive external flooding and possible additional internal flooding was reported on Bishops Drive. (p3)
Cluster of reported internal and external property flooding in the Bishops Drive area and highway flooding. [IMG] (p5)
Homes on the street experienced internal flooding; highways and gardens in the area suffered severe external property flooding, in some cases close to entering ground–floor living areas. (p11)
Surface water runoff from an extreme short–duration storm overwhelmed the urban drainage (road gullies and public surface water sewer), producing overland flows and accumulation in low spots across the Bishops Drive area that led to internal flooding of properties and damage to ground–floor living areas. (p13)
Accumulation of surface water on the highway and reports of one or more surface water sewer manholes surcharging on Bishops Drive, producing increased overland flow downslope toward neighbouring streets and properties. (p14)
Significant highway surface water flooding occurred near the junction with Earls Crescent; wash of soil and sediment from the adjacent recreation ground onto the road was observed; reports indicate up to ten properties in the Bishops Drive area were flooded internally by surface water (reported/unconfirmed). Foul sewer surcharging and evidence of foul–water flooding were observed in the area shortly after the event. [PAG,IMG] (p15)
Flooding at the Earls Crescent junction — the road and junction were inundated during the intense rainfall on 19th July 2014, shown in a resident's photograph of the event. (p16)
Flooding was concentrated in the cluster of streets between Bishops Drive and Wood Brook, with properties and the highway affected by surface water and overland flow from the intense storm. [PAG] (p17)
The local surface water sewer network that serves the Bishops Drive area was considered likely to have been overwhelmed by the intense rainfall, contributing to surface water and sewer–related flooding in the area. (p18)
Residents reported flooding of properties during the storm on 19th July 2014; foul water (sewer) flooding incidents were reported to City Council officers and referenced in Severn Trent Water records. (p19)","at least 10 properties (p3)
At least 10 homes affected internally across the six listed streets (collectively). (p11)
at least 10 properties (across the affected Bishops Drive area) (p13)
