1 property internally flooded; 1 property externally flooded [PAG] (p11)",GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.56692,-0.1357064,"<div id=""html_6ecfda21efe72476a732e153cde6bfcf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Timberley Drive, Wybers Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers were overwhelmed and surcharged out of highway gullies and manholes, causing external flooding to a property. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property externally flooded (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Timberley Drive, Wybers Wood",England,"Sewers were overwhelmed and surcharged out of highway gullies and manholes, causing external flooding to a property. (p11)",1 property externally flooded (p11),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.568768,-0.127837,"<div id=""html_492fb190c2c167a849a91a009a4d8ec4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Barmouth Drive, The Willows</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred due to water surcharging out of gullies and manholes around the new mini roundabout on Wingate Road, resulting in external flooding at sheltered and care housing on Barmouth Drive. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 care facilities externally flooded (Stratford House and The Willows) (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Barmouth Drive, The Willows",England,"Flooding occurred due to water surcharging out of gullies and manholes around the new mini roundabout on Wingate Road, resulting in external flooding at sheltered and care housing on Barmouth Drive. (p11)",2 care facilities externally flooded (Stratford House and The Willows) (p11),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5661526,-0.1032622,"<div id=""html_21fab2aaf394b68101be7c0d209add3b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cromwell Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main trunk sewer backed up and discharged through gullies and manholes in a low&ndash;spot of the road, leading to external flooding of nearby residential care/sheltered housing. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying section of the road became impassable due to flood water between Marklew Avenue and Wells Street. [MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows from a large catchment to the west ran east down Cromwell Road into the public sewer network; Cromwell Road was one of the affected locations during the events and contributed to the deepest flooding near Lynton Parade. Sewer capacity and siltation issues exacerbated flooding in this street. [PAG,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the affected locations in the events; unlike the other affected locations it does not discharge to the Haycroft Culvert. It is the location of regular Anglian Water high&ndash;pressure jetting operations between Wells Street and Yarborough Road. [PAG,MUL] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding to properties and extensive flooding of the public highway caused by surcharging of the main (combined) sewer which backed up out of domestic and highway gullies; low&ndash;lying topography allowed sewers to back up. [MUL] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The map identifies a red&ndash;outlined 'Area of Flooding' covering a residential cluster immediately north of Cromwell Road (adjacent to the railway). Surface water/urban flooding concentrated in this cluster of housing; nearby industrial and railway areas were also shown as affected to a lesser extent. [IMG,MUL] (p91)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties externally flooded (Chatteris House and Cromwell House) (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [MUL] (p88)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Cromwell Road, Grimsby",England,"Main trunk sewer backed up and discharged through gullies and manholes in a low–spot of the road, leading to external flooding of nearby residential care/sheltered housing. (p11)
Low–lying section of the road became impassable due to flood water between Marklew Avenue and Wells Street. [MUL] (p30)
Flows from a large catchment to the west ran east down Cromwell Road into the public sewer network; Cromwell Road was one of the affected locations during the events and contributed to the deepest flooding near Lynton Parade. Sewer capacity and siltation issues exacerbated flooding in this street. [PAG,MUL] (p32)
Identified as one of the affected locations in the events; unlike the other affected locations it does not discharge to the Haycroft Culvert. It is the location of regular Anglian Water high–pressure jetting operations between Wells Street and Yarborough Road. [PAG,MUL] (p33)
External flooding to properties and extensive flooding of the public highway caused by surcharging of the main (combined) sewer which backed up out of domestic and highway gullies; low–lying topography allowed sewers to back up. [MUL] (p88)
The map identifies a red–outlined 'Area of Flooding' covering a residential cluster immediately north of Cromwell Road (adjacent to the railway). Surface water/urban flooding concentrated in this cluster of housing; nearby industrial and railway areas were also shown as affected to a lesser extent. [IMG,MUL] (p91)","2 properties externally flooded (Chatteris House and Cromwell House) (p11)
2 properties [MUL] (p88)",GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5552241,-0.1300985,"<div id=""html_d0e27c323e84c49a4c2f892b7a024c29"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Broadway, Wybers Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Combined sewer surcharged through a manhole at the front of properties and from the main sewer manhole cover, causing internal and external flooding of properties on Broadway. [MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewage surcharging from the main sewer threatened internal flooding at properties on Broadway; a relief channel/swale was surveyed and excavated through a back garden to the rear boundary ditch, and on&ndash;site works were completed within two weeks, greatly reducing the risk of internal flooding at this property and neighbouring properties. [MUL] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area 3 (Little Coates Road and Broadway) was affected by surface water flooding. Runoff from a low spot and surcharging gullies on Little Coates Road ran across the footpath into gardens and houses in the Area 3/Broadway locality, causing internal flooding to nearby properties. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies surcharged, flooding the highway and then gardens and houses; a small amount of water entered porches and properties were internally flooded. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Survey identified manhole 4302 cover on Broadway corroded and seized, preventing access into the main public sewer; raised rear bank behind properties prevents drainage to the adjacent open ditch (golf course), increasing the risk of surface water/flood levels rising high enough to enter houses. Recommendation to replace seized manhole cover and consider lowering the bank to allow flood water to drain to the open watercourse. [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded; flooded on three occasions [MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties internally flooded (on Little Coates Road) [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Broadway, Wybers Wood",England,"Combined sewer surcharged through a manhole at the front of properties and from the main sewer manhole cover, causing internal and external flooding of properties on Broadway. [MUL] (p17)
Sewage surcharging from the main sewer threatened internal flooding at properties on Broadway; a relief channel/swale was surveyed and excavated through a back garden to the rear boundary ditch, and on–site works were completed within two weeks, greatly reducing the risk of internal flooding at this property and neighbouring properties. [MUL] (p18)
Area 3 (Little Coates Road and Broadway) was affected by surface water flooding. Runoff from a low spot and surcharging gullies on Little Coates Road ran across the footpath into gardens and houses in the Area 3/Broadway locality, causing internal flooding to nearby properties. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p19)
Highway gullies surcharged, flooding the highway and then gardens and houses; a small amount of water entered porches and properties were internally flooded. (p20)
Survey identified manhole 4302 cover on Broadway corroded and seized, preventing access into the main public sewer; raised rear bank behind properties prevents drainage to the adjacent open ditch (golf course), increasing the risk of surface water/flood levels rising high enough to enter houses. Recommendation to replace seized manhole cover and consider lowering the bank to allow flood water to drain to the open watercourse. [PAG,MUL] (p21)","1 property internally flooded; flooded on three occasions [MUL] (p17)
4 properties internally flooded (on Little Coates Road) [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p19)
4 properties (p20)",GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5615228,-0.1197026,"<div id=""html_41ee6742e550bb47ed082950791f1a1e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Coates Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Site survey found two gullies in the low spot outside 10 Little Coates Road with broken and cracked pipework; risk of the public sewer surcharging and sewage backing up out of these gullies. Recommendation to repair gullies and fit non&ndash;return valve to prevent sewer backing up into the road/properties. [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Little Coates Road, Grimsby",England,"Site survey found two gullies in the low spot outside 10 Little Coates Road with broken and cracked pipework; risk of the public sewer surcharging and sewage backing up out of these gullies. Recommendation to repair gullies and fit non–return valve to prevent sewer backing up into the road/properties. [PAG,MUL] (p21)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5611235,-0.1061412,"<div id=""html_c68429daf672e456ce362118cd5ce03a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Carson Avenue, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies in front gardens surcharged and floodwater flowed around houses and across the road; floodwater entered properties (via front door and airbricks) and manholes to the rear of properties were surcharging. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging of the Haycroft Drain and the public sewer led to flooding affecting properties on Carson Avenue. Site investigations were carried out and recommendations made for high&ndash;pressure jetting of the main sewer, assessment of a non&ndash;return valve at the junction with Littlefield Lane, and diversion of the Carson Avenue sewer connection to reduce load. [MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marked on the report map inside the red 'Area of Flooding' box and shown within blue/light&ndash;blue shaded zones &mdash; identified as being affected by surface water flooding during the incident mapping. [IMG,MUL] (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Carson Avenue, Grimsby",England,"Gullies in front gardens surcharged and floodwater flowed around houses and across the road; floodwater entered properties (via front door and airbricks) and manholes to the rear of properties were surcharging. (p27)
Surcharging of the Haycroft Drain and the public sewer led to flooding affecting properties on Carson Avenue. Site investigations were carried out and recommendations made for high–pressure jetting of the main sewer, assessment of a non–return valve at the junction with Littlefield Lane, and diversion of the Carson Avenue sewer connection to reduce load. [MUL] (p28)
Marked on the report map inside the red 'Area of Flooding' box and shown within blue/light–blue shaded zones — identified as being affected by surface water flooding during the incident mapping. [IMG,MUL] (p29)",1 property (p27),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.56443,-0.1003773,"<div id=""html_dc34c45ff7d56d0628622f9d3f41467d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marshall Avenue, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging occurred in this area during the event as a result of the Haycroft Drain and connected sewers reaching maximum capacity. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20th July 2014 one property was internally flooded and one electricity substation was internally flooded (substation flooding caused loss of power to a large number of homes). On 10th August 2014 two properties were internally flooded and one property externally flooded. Flood water containing raw sewage entered properties via surcharging highway gullies and manholes; water also collected and flowed through gardens. [MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging of the Haycroft Culvert and large land drainage inflows reduced sewer system capacity, contributing to sewer flooding/inundation on Marshall Avenue during the flood events. [MUL] (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded; 1 electricity substation internally flooded (20th July 2014); 2 properties internally flooded; 1 property externally flooded (10th August 2014) [MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>in excess of 300 hectares [MUL] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Marshall Avenue, Grimsby",England,"Surcharging occurred in this area during the event as a result of the Haycroft Drain and connected sewers reaching maximum capacity. (p27)
On 20th July 2014 one property was internally flooded and one electricity substation was internally flooded (substation flooding caused loss of power to a large number of homes). On 10th August 2014 two properties were internally flooded and one property externally flooded. Flood water containing raw sewage entered properties via surcharging highway gullies and manholes; water also collected and flowed through gardens. [MUL] (p30)
Surcharging of the Haycroft Culvert and large land drainage inflows reduced sewer system capacity, contributing to sewer flooding/inundation on Marshall Avenue during the flood events. [MUL] (p33)","1 property internally flooded; 1 electricity substation internally flooded (20th July 2014); 2 properties internally flooded; 1 property externally flooded (10th August 2014) [MUL] (p30)
in excess of 300 hectares [MUL] (p33)",GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5669126,-0.093019,"<div id=""html_6c036445e0c24c73b19ccd1631655e35"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lord Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging occurred in the Lord Street area as Haycroft Drain/main sewer were at maximum capacity during the 10th August 2014 event, causing local flooding. (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Lord Street, Grimsby",England,"Surcharging occurred in the Lord Street area as Haycroft Drain/main sewer were at maximum capacity during the 10th August 2014 event, causing local flooding. (p27)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5659445,-0.1000966,"<div id=""html_af453d1021de37c337bc689c2d2e8d0b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chestnut Avenue, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The same property was internally flooded on both 20th July 2014 and 10th August 2014. Flood water containing raw sewage entered the rear and side of the property, surcharging from passageway gullies and a garden drain; affected areas included the garden, kitchen and utility. [MUL] (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded on 20th July 2014 and 10th August 2014 [MUL] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Chestnut Avenue, Grimsby",England,"The same property was internally flooded on both 20th July 2014 and 10th August 2014. Flood water containing raw sewage entered the rear and side of the property, surcharging from passageway gullies and a garden drain; affected areas included the garden, kitchen and utility. [MUL] (p30)",1 property internally flooded on 20th July 2014 and 10th August 2014 [MUL] (p30),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5648049,-0.1019149,"<div id=""html_60216f15ab86737244d20796009885a1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fairfax Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water containing raw sewage entered the front door of one residential property and entered the front and rear of business units. Water arrived from build&ndash;up in Cromwell Road at the front and from a surcharged manhole in the rear car park; ground floors of the business units were flooded. (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 Property (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Fairfax Road, Grimsby",England,Flood water containing raw sewage entered the front door of one residential property and entered the front and rear of business units. Water arrived from build–up in Cromwell Road at the front and from a surcharged manhole in the rear car park; ground floors of the business units were flooded. (p30),1 Property (p30),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.564216,-0.1024662,"<div id=""html_4a07385e870abdf0fd6078cc3fc228d1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wentworth Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An 8 metre long sag was found in the 450mm diameter public sewer that was holding water (about half full); the same length of sewer throttles down to 225mm diameter at the connection to the Haycroft Drain culvert, causing flow restriction and contributing to sewer surcharge and surface water flooding on the road. [MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A sag and an undersized section of sewer in Wentworth Road were identified as issues affecting sewer performance and contributing to the flooding problems in the catchment. [MUL] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Wentworth Road, Grimsby",England,"An 8 metre long sag was found in the 450mm diameter public sewer that was holding water (about half full); the same length of sewer throttles down to 225mm diameter at the connection to the Haycroft Drain culvert, causing flow restriction and contributing to sewer surcharge and surface water flooding on the road. [MUL] (p31)
A sag and an undersized section of sewer in Wentworth Road were identified as issues affecting sewer performance and contributing to the flooding problems in the catchment. [MUL] (p32)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5649799,-0.1034028,"<div id=""html_d65e3a00e0fa8e4e7a5c7307f49735bc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marklew Avenue, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A manhole in Marklew Avenue contains a grill with a 100% blockage; intruding connections and high silt levels were found in the Marklew Avenue sewer. Residents reported flood water (containing raw sewage) entering property fronts, surcharging from highway gullies and in some cases from a manhole in their front garden as road flooding depths increased. [MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A grill and blockage were identified in Marklew Avenue that reduced drainage performance and contributed to flooding during the events. [MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging of the Haycroft Culvert and large land drainage inflows reduced sewer system capacity, contributing to sewer flooding/inundation on Marklew Avenue during the flood events. [MUL] (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>in excess of 300 hectares [MUL] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Marklew Avenue, Grimsby",England,"A manhole in Marklew Avenue contains a grill with a 100% blockage; intruding connections and high silt levels were found in the Marklew Avenue sewer. Residents reported flood water (containing raw sewage) entering property fronts, surcharging from highway gullies and in some cases from a manhole in their front garden as road flooding depths increased. [MUL] (p31)
A grill and blockage were identified in Marklew Avenue that reduced drainage performance and contributed to flooding during the events. [MUL] (p32)
Surcharging of the Haycroft Culvert and large land drainage inflows reduced sewer system capacity, contributing to sewer flooding/inundation on Marklew Avenue during the flood events. [MUL] (p33)",in excess of 300 hectares [MUL] (p33),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5658627,-0.1017428,"<div id=""html_cc7e6964fb282957062ca0a84f6183b4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lynton Parade, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The private drainage serving the car park at Lynton Parade was found to be totally blocked and in very poor condition (contributing to flood impacts); car park surfacing was breaking up and the area is the lowest part of the catchment where the deepest flooding occurred. [PAG,MUL] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Lynton Parade, Grimsby",England,"The private drainage serving the car park at Lynton Parade was found to be totally blocked and in very poor condition (contributing to flood impacts); car park surfacing was breaking up and the area is the lowest part of the catchment where the deepest flooding occurred. [PAG,MUL] (p32)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5651721,-0.0992443,"<div id=""html_1f6badcb203083c17664f0e31a4af931"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Haycroft Avenue, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Haycroft culvert (serving areas discharging to it, including at Haycroft Avenue) was observed to be surcharging during the flood events, reducing capacity for the urban drainage it serves and contributing to flooding of connected areas. [MUL] (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>in excess of 300 hectares of land drainage entering the system [MUL] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Haycroft Avenue, Grimsby",England,"The Haycroft culvert (serving areas discharging to it, including at Haycroft Avenue) was observed to be surcharging during the flood events, reducing capacity for the urban drainage it serves and contributing to flooding of connected areas. [MUL] (p32)",in excess of 300 hectares of land drainage entering the system [MUL] (p32),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5689591,-0.0985287,"<div id=""html_a843ef1aba2f9001dc003224b49c3d4c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lawrence Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying area where surcharging sewers backed up out of highway gullies; additional runoff from higher ground at Boulevard Avenue exacerbated flooding. The location was the lowest part of the catchment and the majority of properties either internally flooded or came very close. [PAG,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lowest&ndash;lying part of the catchment; the majority of properties either internally flooded or came very close to internal flooding. Flooding and surcharging was linked to silted and hydraulically undersized sewers and limited outfall capacity, exacerbated by the higher level of Boulevard Avenue to the west. [PAG,MUL] (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Lawrence Street, Grimsby",England,"Low–lying area where surcharging sewers backed up out of highway gullies; additional runoff from higher ground at Boulevard Avenue exacerbated flooding. The location was the lowest part of the catchment and the majority of properties either internally flooded or came very close. [PAG,MUL] (p35)
Lowest–lying part of the catchment; the majority of properties either internally flooded or came very close to internal flooding. Flooding and surcharging was linked to silted and hydraulically undersized sewers and limited outfall capacity, exacerbated by the higher level of Boulevard Avenue to the west. [PAG,MUL] (p36)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5684335,-0.0998096,"<div id=""html_10758c0feba5046d00a505d845891816"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lister Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of the rear of houses from water entering via the alleyway and rear gardens; kitchens (at lower level) flooded due to water ingress from rear. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Silt accumulations were recorded in the local sewer at Lister Street during the site investigation; this siltation was identified as reducing hydraulic capacity and contributing to poor sewer performance during the reported flood events. [PAG,MUL] (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Lister Street, Grimsby",England,"Internal flooding of the rear of houses from water entering via the alleyway and rear gardens; kitchens (at lower level) flooded due to water ingress from rear. (p35)
Silt accumulations were recorded in the local sewer at Lister Street during the site investigation; this siltation was identified as reducing hydraulic capacity and contributing to poor sewer performance during the reported flood events. [PAG,MUL] (p36)",3 properties (p35),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5689076,-0.0970275,"<div id=""html_e282d3769d88b743413c88764338fb16"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Henry Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding caused by surcharging of the main sewer which backed up out of highway and footway gullies in the alleyway; sewers connect to the Haycroft Drain which was surcharging due to intense rainfall. [MUL] (p35)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 properties [MUL] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Henry Street, Grimsby",England,Internal flooding caused by surcharging of the main sewer which backed up out of highway and footway gullies in the alleyway; sewers connect to the Haycroft Drain which was surcharging due to intense rainfall. [MUL] (p35),11 properties [MUL] (p35),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5691948,-0.0976501,"<div id=""html_a10e6f354eab1806b2bddc44b9f383dd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>James Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the sewer system in James Street were inaccessible due to seized manhole covers. James Street forms an indirect connection into the Haycroft Drain and the condition/connections of its sewers contributed to sewer surcharge/backups that led to internal flooding at nearby low&ndash;lying streets (Lawrence Street and Henry Street). [MUL] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"James Street, Grimsby",England,Parts of the sewer system in James Street were inaccessible due to seized manhole covers. James Street forms an indirect connection into the Haycroft Drain and the condition/connections of its sewers contributed to sewer surcharge/backups that led to internal flooding at nearby low–lying streets (Lawrence Street and Henry Street). [MUL] (p35),,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.567138,-0.0894519,"<div id=""html_0207973a61afbc8204d3dc1da386ed5b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fildes Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater containing raw sewage entered properties (front and, in three cases, rear). Water surcharged from manholes and drains in gardens and from highway gullies at the front, flowing across the footpath into properties. Flooding affected the majority of the ground floors. [MUL] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The sewer serving Fildes Street exhibited deterioration and was prone to siltation/slack gradients; the system surcharged during intense rainfall. An obstruction at a junction to the rear of a number of properties was found restricting sewer discharge and was removed; high&ndash;pressure jetting was recommended and partly completed in the Fildes Street area. [MUL] (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 3 properties [MUL] (p39)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Fildes Street, Grimsby",England,"Floodwater containing raw sewage entered properties (front and, in three cases, rear). Water surcharged from manholes and drains in gardens and from highway gullies at the front, flowing across the footpath into properties. Flooding affected the majority of the ground floors. [MUL] (p39)
The sewer serving Fildes Street exhibited deterioration and was prone to siltation/slack gradients; the system surcharged during intense rainfall. An obstruction at a junction to the rear of a number of properties was found restricting sewer discharge and was removed; high–pressure jetting was recommended and partly completed in the Fildes Street area. [MUL] (p40)",4 properties; 3 properties [MUL] (p39),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5670635,-0.0885793,"<div id=""html_a92c79f6a8dcd3f894e05b32b96ae8d8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Somersby Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in recommended high&ndash;pressure jetting to clear the public sewer network after surcharging during intense rainfall; identified as an area requiring sewer maintenance to reduce flood risk. [MUL] (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Somersby Street, Grimsby",England,Included in recommended high–pressure jetting to clear the public sewer network after surcharging during intense rainfall; identified as an area requiring sewer maintenance to reduce flood risk. [MUL] (p40),,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5555989,-0.0889784,"<div id=""html_b6661c49e26808d644caebe67e329220"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Welholme Avenue, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from Welholme Road entered Welholme Avenue and contributed to flooding at the lower&ndash;lying Close; two properties overlooking Welholme Avenue required property&ndash;level flood measures (flood boards and pumping) to avoid internal flooding. A manhole design prevented safe access during the event. (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Welholme Avenue, Grimsby",England,Surface water runoff from Welholme Road entered Welholme Avenue and contributed to flooding at the lower–lying Close; two properties overlooking Welholme Avenue required property–level flood measures (flood boards and pumping) to avoid internal flooding. A manhole design prevented safe access during the event. (p44),2 properties (p44),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5582916,-0.0899106,"<div id=""html_756be266ed342893168f83ed945e3719"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Le Burghdike Close, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in the lower&ndash;lying Close caused by a surcharging main sewer, worsened by surface water runoff entering from Welholme Road; two affected properties had flood boards in place and a pump was used to prevent internal flooding. (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Le Burghdike Close, Grimsby",England,"Flooding occurred in the lower–lying Close caused by a surcharging main sewer, worsened by surface water runoff entering from Welholme Road; two affected properties had flood boards in place and a pump was used to prevent internal flooding. (p44)",2 properties (p44),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.559608,-0.0731916,"<div id=""html_903ec717103fe7973900102fe7534ed2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Welholme Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed down Welholme Road into Welholme Avenue and contributed to flooding at the Close; the trunk sewer in Welholme Road was surcharging and was the source of extensive flooding observed nearby (including Peoples Park). (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Welholme Road, Grimsby",England,Surface water runoff flowed down Welholme Road into Welholme Avenue and contributed to flooding at the Close; the trunk sewer in Welholme Road was surcharging and was the source of extensive flooding observed nearby (including Peoples Park). (p44),,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5595495,-0.0832514,"<div id=""html_3faf864fe1b70b008fd4a02edc33b2b3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ainslie Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater containing raw sewage entered properties from the front (flowing from the road and surcharging from main sewers via highway gullies) and from the rear (surcharging from public sewer systems behind properties). Water entered through door openings, air bricks and defective brickwork, causing internal flooding of homes. [MUL] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers in Ainslie Street exhibited siltation and grease accumulations, increasing the chance they could be overwhelmed by intense rainfall during the 2014 events. Regular high&ndash;pressure jetting is undertaken in Ainslie Street (to the Wintringham Road junction). The report also suggests using large storage pipes in the adjacent Ainslie Street Recreation Ground to mitigate storm flows that currently discharge via Riby Street pumping station. [MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area around Ainslie Street experienced surface water / urban flooding as part of the summer 2014 flood incidents; storm flows from the Ainslie Street / Wintringham Road area were routed to the Riby Street pumping station. Anglian Water have proposed using large storage pipes in the adjacent recreation ground as a potential mitigation measure. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties internally flooded on 20th July 2014; 7 properties internally flooded on 10th August 2014 [MUL] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Ainslie Street, Grimsby",England,"Floodwater containing raw sewage entered properties from the front (flowing from the road and surcharging from main sewers via highway gullies) and from the rear (surcharging from public sewer systems behind properties). Water entered through door openings, air bricks and defective brickwork, causing internal flooding of homes. [MUL] (p47)
Sewers in Ainslie Street exhibited siltation and grease accumulations, increasing the chance they could be overwhelmed by intense rainfall during the 2014 events. Regular high–pressure jetting is undertaken in Ainslie Street (to the Wintringham Road junction). The report also suggests using large storage pipes in the adjacent Ainslie Street Recreation Ground to mitigate storm flows that currently discharge via Riby Street pumping station. [MUL] (p49)
Area around Ainslie Street experienced surface water / urban flooding as part of the summer 2014 flood incidents; storm flows from the Ainslie Street / Wintringham Road area were routed to the Riby Street pumping station. Anglian Water have proposed using large storage pipes in the adjacent recreation ground as a potential mitigation measure. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p50)",4 properties internally flooded on 20th July 2014; 7 properties internally flooded on 10th August 2014 [MUL] (p47),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5606261,-0.0814575,"<div id=""html_771c2b560af3e82cdb939b9f875f04a0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wintringham Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater containing raw sewage entered the property from the front (via surcharging sewers through highway gullies flowing across the footway) and from the rear garden; in the second event the front of the property nearly breached. Internal flooding was limited in one case by the inner door threshold. [MUL] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers in the Wintringham Road area were subject to siltation and grease buildup and were at risk of being overwhelmed by intense rainfall during the 2014 events. Anglian Water undertakes regular high&ndash;pressure jetting in Wintringham Road (at least to the Ainslie Street junction) as part of measures to manage this risk. [MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wintringham Road area is shown alongside Ainslie Street within the mapped area of surface water flood risk and is described as a source of storm flows draining to Riby Street pumping station during the summer 2014 flood events. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded on 20th July 2014 [MUL] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Wintringham Road, Grimsby",England,"Floodwater containing raw sewage entered the property from the front (via surcharging sewers through highway gullies flowing across the footway) and from the rear garden; in the second event the front of the property nearly breached. Internal flooding was limited in one case by the inner door threshold. [MUL] (p47)
Sewers in the Wintringham Road area were subject to siltation and grease buildup and were at risk of being overwhelmed by intense rainfall during the 2014 events. Anglian Water undertakes regular high–pressure jetting in Wintringham Road (at least to the Ainslie Street junction) as part of measures to manage this risk. [MUL] (p49)
Wintringham Road area is shown alongside Ainslie Street within the mapped area of surface water flood risk and is described as a source of storm flows draining to Riby Street pumping station during the summer 2014 flood events. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p50)",1 property internally flooded on 20th July 2014 [MUL] (p47),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5627541,-0.083,"<div id=""html_083d04deb9dc41244d5b5d7a5848c8b3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Doughty Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the entrance where water surcharged out of a manhole and flooded the underpass (Doughty Road subway). Pumped water recirculated into the sewer network because the pumping station outfall connected to the surcharged sewer, preventing effective lowering of water levels. [MUL] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm/storm&ndash;drain flows from the Doughty Road area contributed to surcharging of the sewer at the Doughty Road/Moss Road junction, producing surcharged flows that discharged into the adjacent subway. [MUL] (p53)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Doughty Road, Grimsby",England,"Flooding occurred at the entrance where water surcharged out of a manhole and flooded the underpass (Doughty Road subway). Pumped water recirculated into the sewer network because the pumping station outfall connected to the surcharged sewer, preventing effective lowering of water levels. [MUL] (p51)
Storm/storm–drain flows from the Doughty Road area contributed to surcharging of the sewer at the Doughty Road/Moss Road junction, producing surcharged flows that discharged into the adjacent subway. [MUL] (p53)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5630865,-0.0818335,"<div id=""html_3adc6e879858fc0abca8a22b603471bd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Moss Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A low&ndash;lying manhole in the subway area adjacent to the depot (near the Doughty Road / Moss Road area) acted as the primary release point when the main sewer surcharged, discharging flood water into the subway. [MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A low&ndash;lying manhole at the Doughty Road/Moss Road junction was surcharging, increasing flows into the subway; measures at this junction are proposed to reduce discharge into the subway. [MUL] (p53)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Moss Road, Grimsby",England,"A low–lying manhole in the subway area adjacent to the depot (near the Doughty Road / Moss Road area) acted as the primary release point when the main sewer surcharged, discharging flood water into the subway. [MUL] (p52)
A low–lying manhole at the Doughty Road/Moss Road junction was surcharging, increasing flows into the subway; measures at this junction are proposed to reduce discharge into the subway. [MUL] (p53)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5674397,-0.0781889,"<div id=""html_c3a29d2f5f5d5f0c9a860e1a526daca3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ellis Way, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Ellis Way underpass made the highway impassable and affected access between Peaks Parkway/Grimsby Town Centre and the Freeman Street/Asda area; contributing sewer issues included high siltation levels, a defective connection and surfaced&ndash;over manholes. [MUL] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The combined sewer serving Ellis Way had high siltation levels and a defective connection obscuring the pipework, contributing to surcharge of the receiving sewer and causing flooding of the Ellis Way underpass when the pumping station cut out during surcharging. [PAG,MUL] (p57)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>600mm [MUL] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Ellis Way, Grimsby",England,"Flooding in the Ellis Way underpass made the highway impassable and affected access between Peaks Parkway/Grimsby Town Centre and the Freeman Street/Asda area; contributing sewer issues included high siltation levels, a defective connection and surfaced–over manholes. [MUL] (p56)
The combined sewer serving Ellis Way had high siltation levels and a defective connection obscuring the pipework, contributing to surcharge of the receiving sewer and causing flooding of the Ellis Way underpass when the pumping station cut out during surcharging. [PAG,MUL] (p57)",600mm [MUL] (p56),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5604578,-0.0787648,"<div id=""html_e2b08ea346e69341bd91554c5722a538"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Peaks Parkway, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Section of the A16 underpass became impassable to traffic for a short period due to inundation. (p56)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>600mm (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Peaks Parkway, Grimsby",England,Section of the A16 underpass became impassable to traffic for a short period due to inundation. (p56),600mm (p56),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5649364,-0.0765582,"<div id=""html_d34c2447a90dc7edc7293466f94cc575"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willingham Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive surface water flooding of the road in front of properties; one property had external flooding (water through the front door caused by bow waves from cars) and three properties experienced internal flooding with water entering via brickwork around the front bay window and via the cavity dividing wall. These properties had previously been internally flooded in 2007 and had concrete floors installed to replace timber floors. [PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The manhole prevented access into the public sewerage system, indicating lack of maintenance and limiting the ability to de&ndash;silt and clear the sewer; this access failure contributed to sewer system performance problems during flood events. [MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The public sewer in Willingham Street is noted as requiring a replacement manhole for maintenance access and a full cleaning/CCTV survey to address siltation and the risk of the sewer being overwhelmed by intense rainfall; measures are recommended to reduce sewer surcharging/overflow. [PAG,MUL] (p62)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property externally flooded on 20th July 2014; 3 properties internally flooded on 10th August 2014 [PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Willingham Street, Grimsby",England,"Extensive surface water flooding of the road in front of properties; one property had external flooding (water through the front door caused by bow waves from cars) and three properties experienced internal flooding with water entering via brickwork around the front bay window and via the cavity dividing wall. These properties had previously been internally flooded in 2007 and had concrete floors installed to replace timber floors. [PAG,MUL] (p60)
The manhole prevented access into the public sewerage system, indicating lack of maintenance and limiting the ability to de–silt and clear the sewer; this access failure contributed to sewer system performance problems during flood events. [MUL] (p61)
The public sewer in Willingham Street is noted as requiring a replacement manhole for maintenance access and a full cleaning/CCTV survey to address siltation and the risk of the sewer being overwhelmed by intense rainfall; measures are recommended to reduce sewer surcharging/overflow. [PAG,MUL] (p62)","1 property externally flooded on 20th July 2014; 3 properties internally flooded on 10th August 2014 [PAG,MUL] (p60)",GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5624175,-0.0777295,"<div id=""html_637214b064e7c47bf08e9e2d47f10a30"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Catherine Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the large storage yard at the B&Q premises ran onto the public highway in Catherine Street and flowed towards Willingham Street; the large surface water culvert in Catherine Street had silt build&ndash;ups requiring jetting and adjacent road gullies were inspected (no blockages found). [PAG,MUL] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Public highway experienced significant surface water run&ndash;off from the adjacent B&Q storage yard which ran onto Catherine Street and flowed downhill towards Willingham Street, causing highway flooding and risk of surcharging of the surface water drainage system. [PAG,MUL] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The adjacent surface&ndash;water culvert/system was at risk of surcharging; the report recommends full de&ndash;silting of the adjacent surface water system to help prevent surcharging and local flooding. [MUL] (p62)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Catherine Street, Grimsby",England,"Surface water runoff from the large storage yard at the B&Q premises ran onto the public highway in Catherine Street and flowed towards Willingham Street; the large surface water culvert in Catherine Street had silt build–ups requiring jetting and adjacent road gullies were inspected (no blockages found). [PAG,MUL] (p60)
Public highway experienced significant surface water run–off from the adjacent B&Q storage yard which ran onto Catherine Street and flowed downhill towards Willingham Street, causing highway flooding and risk of surcharging of the surface water drainage system. [PAG,MUL] (p61)
The adjacent surface–water culvert/system was at risk of surcharging; the report recommends full de–silting of the adjacent surface water system to help prevent surcharging and local flooding. [MUL] (p62)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5726884,-0.0787459,"<div id=""html_7f7b1233418b2c522478abff6cfa004e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>King Edward Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Commercial property on King Edward Street experienced internal drainage surcharging that backed up through manholes and caused flooding into ground&ndash;floor business premises; floodwater contained raw sewerage from toilet/drain connections. [MUL] (p64)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"King Edward Street, Grimsby",England,Commercial property on King Edward Street experienced internal drainage surcharging that backed up through manholes and caused flooding into ground–floor business premises; floodwater contained raw sewerage from toilet/drain connections. [MUL] (p64),,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.572109,-0.0632702,"<div id=""html_87ed91d32ae8e0f6ad2e202308d3378f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Weelsby Street, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The main combined sewer surcharged and backed up out of highway gullies onto the carriageway and to the front of a property; water from the highway entered the property through the front door causing flooding of the porch. Siltation in the adjacent public sewer was noted as a contributing factor. (p67)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; silt levels up to 30% in adjacent public sewer (p67)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Weelsby Street, Grimsby",England,The main combined sewer surcharged and backed up out of highway gullies onto the carriageway and to the front of a property; water from the highway entered the property through the front door causing flooding of the porch. Siltation in the adjacent public sewer was noted as a contributing factor. (p67),1 property; silt levels up to 30% in adjacent public sewer (p67),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5257757,-0.0951808,"<div id=""html_48e9fd385a77bc65d70f8decf1b67cd2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Torbay Drive, Waltham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water surcharged from highway gullies and manholes in the road and ran down the driveway into the property; water entered the property through the front door and through the conservatory. Investigation found surface water sewers from Torbay Drive through to Boundary Road heavily silted, contributing to surcharge. (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging of the surface water sewer contributed to flooding in Torbay Drive; residents experienced water running down their driveways and have installed demountable flood boards across driveways to prevent ingress. Jetting and manhole improvements are expected to reduce flood risk. [MUL] (p71)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded; siltation levels at 50% in places (p70)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Torbay Drive, Waltham",England,"Flood water surcharged from highway gullies and manholes in the road and ran down the driveway into the property; water entered the property through the front door and through the conservatory. Investigation found surface water sewers from Torbay Drive through to Boundary Road heavily silted, contributing to surcharge. (p70)
Surcharging of the surface water sewer contributed to flooding in Torbay Drive; residents experienced water running down their driveways and have installed demountable flood boards across driveways to prevent ingress. Jetting and manhole improvements are expected to reduce flood risk. [MUL] (p71)",1 property internally flooded; siltation levels at 50% in places (p70),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5493645,-0.1360374,"<div id=""html_e09cd5662a03cd3cce0fa3cbb71bf1a1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Boundary Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The downstream manhole in Boundary Road had reduced capacity due to siltation and obstructions, which exacerbated surcharging of the surface water sewer and contributed to flooding. [MUL] (p71)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Boundary Road, Grimsby",England,"The downstream manhole in Boundary Road had reduced capacity due to siltation and obstructions, which exacerbated surcharging of the surface water sewer and contributed to flooding. [MUL] (p71)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5140888,-0.1077691,"<div id=""html_b85e15541513b1550e2a5856bd641400"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Salisbury Avenue, Waltham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A foul sewer manhole was surcharging; floodwater emerged from the manhole in the grassed area, ran down a driveway, around a house and flooded the back garden and utility room of an adjacent property. (p73)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property affected (p73)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Salisbury Avenue, Waltham",England,"A foul sewer manhole was surcharging; floodwater emerged from the manhole in the grassed area, ran down a driveway, around a house and flooded the back garden and utility room of an adjacent property. (p73)",1 property affected (p73),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5129165,-0.1051076,"<div id=""html_21dd6d2c103f7eab2e8f15340a99a81b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cotswold Drive, Waltham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An upstream foul manhole in Cotswold Drive was observed to have a large volume of water entering through the brickwork; this was identified as the source of water likely from a water mains leak that exacerbated surcharging of the foul sewer and downstream flooding. (p73)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Cotswold Drive, Waltham",England,An upstream foul manhole in Cotswold Drive was observed to have a large volume of water entering through the brickwork; this was identified as the source of water likely from a water mains leak that exacerbated surcharging of the foul sewer and downstream flooding. (p73),,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.53351567380273,-0.019407086590605173,"<div id=""html_bcd1ba2019ed866254d7c05b72c8b3ca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Parker Road, Humberston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties caused by surface water sewers surcharging and water rising through highway gullies at the lowest part of the carriageway, flowing down driveways into houses; ACO perimeter drains at affected houses connected to the same surcharging surface water sewer and were unable to drain away. (p76)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties internally flooded (p76)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Parker Road, Humberston",England,"Internal flooding of properties caused by surface water sewers surcharging and water rising through highway gullies at the lowest part of the carriageway, flowing down driveways into houses; ACO perimeter drains at affected houses connected to the same surcharging surface water sewer and were unable to drain away. (p76)",2 properties internally flooded (p76),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5335553,-0.0162357,"<div id=""html_ce208da67f32b7cb0bdcc23eb9b39d2a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hewson Road, Humberston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property due to surface water sewers surcharging and coming up through highway gullies, flowing over the footpath and into properties; residents reported toilets and sinks backing up (indicative of cross&ndash;connections). (p76)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded (p76)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Hewson Road, Humberston",England,"Internal flooding of a property due to surface water sewers surcharging and coming up through highway gullies, flowing over the footpath and into properties; residents reported toilets and sinks backing up (indicative of cross–connections). (p76)",1 property internally flooded (p76),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.535466,-0.0240258,"<div id=""html_7fd0549d4ae2c48d4a848556bf42bb07"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Sea Lane, Humberston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water collected on a private road, ran through a property garden and entered a property via airbricks. Contributed to flooding by displaced joint and root ingress in the main surface water sewer and structural defects in a smaller surface water sewer; lack of drainage and blocked gullies on the private road exacerbated the event. (p79)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded; 1 property externally flooded (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"North Sea Lane, Humberston",England,"Surface water collected on a private road, ran through a property garden and entered a property via airbricks. Contributed to flooding by displaced joint and root ingress in the main surface water sewer and structural defects in a smaller surface water sewer; lack of drainage and blocked gullies on the private road exacerbated the event. (p79)",1 property internally flooded; 1 property externally flooded (p79),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.51404611741972,-0.09726446265533742,"<div id=""html_3496e2fc65aecfa3fe3e41cd3dbb2c81"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cheesemans Close, Waltham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater surcharged from a domestic driveway gully (incorrectly connected to the foul sewer), flooding the front and rear gardens of two properties and entering the conservatory of the internally flooded property; residents diverted water into highway gullies which connect to the surface water sewer discharging to Buck Beck. (p82)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded; 1 property externally flooded (p82)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Cheesemans Close, Waltham",England,"Floodwater surcharged from a domestic driveway gully (incorrectly connected to the foul sewer), flooding the front and rear gardens of two properties and entering the conservatory of the internally flooded property; residents diverted water into highway gullies which connect to the surface water sewer discharging to Buck Beck. (p82)",1 property internally flooded; 1 property externally flooded (p82),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5570217,-0.0755718,"<div id=""html_1ef27266d8f08173c8c2311538d326ca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hainton Avenue, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging of the combined main sewer caused sewage to back up out of domestic gullies at the rear of a property, leading to internal flooding through a rear door into the utility area; local low topography and runoff from surrounding gardens/paved surfaces exacerbated ingress and containment of floodwater within the lower utility area. (p85)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p85)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Hainton Avenue, Grimsby",England,"Surcharging of the combined main sewer caused sewage to back up out of domestic gullies at the rear of a property, leading to internal flooding through a rear door into the utility area; local low topography and runoff from surrounding gardens/paved surfaces exacerbated ingress and containment of floodwater within the lower utility area. (p85)",1 property (p85),GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5734517,-0.1232484,"<div id=""html_54a0a3bef3773c2c9ad076b999accece"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ravenscar Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding to a property (floodwater entered via surcharging of the main sewer which backed up out of domestic gullies to the front and rear); the site's low spot and surrounding drainage towards the property exacerbated flooding. The public highway at this location also experienced extensive flooding due to sewers surcharging. [PAG,MUL] (p88)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 30% of the combined sewer obscured by fat and grease; 40% obstruction at the sewer outlet [PAG,MUL] (p88)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Ravenscar Road, Grimsby",England,"External flooding to a property (floodwater entered via surcharging of the main sewer which backed up out of domestic gullies to the front and rear); the site's low spot and surrounding drainage towards the property exacerbated flooding. The public highway at this location also experienced extensive flooding due to sewers surcharging. [PAG,MUL] (p88)","1 property; 30% of the combined sewer obscured by fat and grease; 40% obstruction at the sewer outlet [PAG,MUL] (p88)",GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5720789,-0.1218479,"<div id=""html_a0dfd4f5d17b604f4c1b5d33cf34dbb9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Service Road, Grimsby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Public highway (Service Road 26) suffered extensive flooding when the main sewer surcharged and backed up out of highway gullies; the low&ndash;lying nature of the highway allowed gullies to surcharge. [MUL] (p88)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Service Road, Grimsby",England,Public highway (Service Road 26) suffered extensive flooding when the main sewer surcharged and backed up out of highway gullies; the low–lying nature of the highway allowed gullies to surcharge. [MUL] (p88),,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5368034,-0.093681,"<div id=""html_b6dce39a9092c839d3b8bb58566b1c04"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North East Lincolnshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties on Henry Street, Lawrence Street and Lister Street in Grimsby, resulting in internal property flooding. [IMG,MUL] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in Grimsby (Area 14, Willingham Street) occurred within North East Lincolnshire: 1 property externally flooded on 20 July 2014 and 3 properties internally flooded on 10 August 2014; the report indicates additional external flooding likely affected other properties in the area due to topography. [IMG,MUL] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of one residential property at Torbay Drive, Scartho: a small difference in levels allowed surface water to enter one property but not neighbouring properties; engagement on property&ndash;level flood protection recommended to neighbours at risk. [IMG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report records a real flooding incident within North East Lincolnshire: one property on Salisbury Avenue, Waltham was flooded when water surcharged from a manhole in the adjacent grassed area, ran down the driveway, flooded the back garden and entered the side utility room. [IMG] (p72)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A property on Hainton Avenue, Grimsby (within North East Lincolnshire) was internally flooded when the main sewer surcharged and backed up out of domestic gullies to the rear of the property; floodwater entered through a rear door into the utility area and was contained there due to the lower level of that room and the property's topographical low spot. Surrounding gardens and paved surfaces draining toward the property exacerbated the event. [IMG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>North East Lincolnshire Council's Gully Cleansing Department visited all affected areas within a month to check and clear highway gullies; gullies were found to be operating in a satisfactory condition. A site investigation including sewer inspections was undertaken within four months. [MUL] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The map and report cover parts of the borough (North East Lincolnshire) and identify mapped surface water flood risk including a concentrated high&ndash;risk cluster (Cromwell Road area). The report also links intense summer storm rainfall to the observed flooding and describes borough&ndash;level mitigation actions (e.g. installing rain gauges). [IMG,MUL] (p91)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 properties [IMG,MUL] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 3 properties [IMG,MUL] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [IMG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [IMG] (p72)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [IMG] (p84)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,North East Lincolnshire,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties on Henry Street, Lawrence Street and Lister Street in Grimsby, resulting in internal property flooding. [IMG,MUL] (p34)
Flooding in Grimsby (Area 14, Willingham Street) occurred within North East Lincolnshire: 1 property externally flooded on 20 July 2014 and 3 properties internally flooded on 10 August 2014; the report indicates additional external flooding likely affected other properties in the area due to topography. [IMG,MUL] (p59)
Internal flooding of one residential property at Torbay Drive, Scartho: a small difference in levels allowed surface water to enter one property but not neighbouring properties; engagement on property–level flood protection recommended to neighbours at risk. [IMG] (p69)
The report records a real flooding incident within North East Lincolnshire: one property on Salisbury Avenue, Waltham was flooded when water surcharged from a manhole in the adjacent grassed area, ran down the driveway, flooded the back garden and entered the side utility room. [IMG] (p72)
A property on Hainton Avenue, Grimsby (within North East Lincolnshire) was internally flooded when the main sewer surcharged and backed up out of domestic gullies to the rear of the property; floodwater entered through a rear door into the utility area and was contained there due to the lower level of that room and the property's topographical low spot. Surrounding gardens and paved surfaces draining toward the property exacerbated the event. [IMG] (p84)
North East Lincolnshire Council's Gully Cleansing Department visited all affected areas within a month to check and clear highway gullies; gullies were found to be operating in a satisfactory condition. A site investigation including sewer inspections was undertaken within four months. [MUL] (p88)
The map and report cover parts of the borough (North East Lincolnshire) and identify mapped surface water flood risk including a concentrated high–risk cluster (Cromwell Road area). The report also links intense summer storm rainfall to the observed flooding and describes borough–level mitigation actions (e.g. installing rain gauges). [IMG,MUL] (p91)","16 properties [IMG,MUL] (p34)
