GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5647796,-0.1108212,"<div id=""html_747c7e0c443f5f125d5d884e2f56ee2c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yarborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the main wards where Anglian Water carried out high&ndash;power jetting maintenance of public sewers following the 2014 flooding to address siltation and reduce surcharging. (p93)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Yarborough,England,Identified as one of the main wards where Anglian Water carried out high–power jetting maintenance of public sewers following the 2014 flooding to address siltation and reduce surcharging. (p93),,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-LIN,2,2014-08-08,53.5735508,-0.0957245,"<div id=""html_1ea03804422a9fb347e696bc2a6cbaca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Marsh</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the 2014 flooding, Anglian Water carried out high&ndash;power jetting maintenance on lengths of public sewer in this ward to address siltation and reduce surcharging. (p93)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,West Marsh,England,"Following the 2014 flooding, Anglian Water carried out high–power jetting maintenance on lengths of public sewer in this ward to address siltation and reduce surcharging. (p93)",,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc.html,GB-LIN_2_2014-08-08_etc,,,
GB-BKM,25,2020-12-23,51.9904191,-1.046658,"<div id=""html_31b5cd62647987cf5bb516f26e523307"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Royal Oak</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement of The Royal Oak pub flooded during the event. (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property (The Royal Oak) flooded; basement flooded (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Royal Oak,England,Basement of The Royal Oak pub flooded during the event. (p38),1 commercial property (The Royal Oak) flooded; basement flooded (p38),GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23,Tingewick flooding 23 Dec 2020,Tingewick Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/tingewick-report-dec-2020.pdf
GB-BKM,25,2020-12-23,51.9902727,-1.0507299,"<div id=""html_18b8c2347020db2cf446053e1b6e6009"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Townsend Pond</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The embanked pond at the western end of the village overtopped on 23 December 2020 (figures in the report show flooding at 13:40 and further overtopping in the evening), discharging excess water via its outlet pipe into the downstream culvert and contributing to flooding in the village. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Townsend Pond overtopped (annotated as occurring at approximately 17:00 on 23 December 2020). The pond is embanked at its eastern end and discharges via a 300mm pipe into a 600mm culvert; the culvert was surcharged and its capacity exceeded during the event, contributing to downstream overland flow and flooding in the village. [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Townsend Pond overtopped: water rose above the 300mm outlet pipe (visible by 13:40) and, by approximately 17:00, overtopped the overflow structure and the top of the embankment. The overtopping released additional water down Main Street, triggering a second rapid rise in water levels and causing internal flooding of nearby properties. Water levels fell rapidly later that night and flooding had dissipated by 02:00 on 24 December. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Townsend Pond overtopped at approximately 17:00; water overtopped the overflow structure and the top of the embankment and additional water flowed down Main Street, coinciding with a second wave of rainfall and causing rapid rises in water levels that led to internal flooding of nearby properties. As pond levels fell below the embankment later in the event the culverted watercourse allowed the remaining floodwater on the road to drain relatively rapidly, with flooding largely dissipating by about 23:00 and no remaining flooding after 02:00 on 24 December. [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A modelled scenario doubling the pond's storage capacity would not have prevented the culvert surcharging during the 23rd December 2020 event because flows from the west were too large. The pond could potentially overtop in extreme events and increased storage might worsen downstream impacts. Management changes (maintaining a clear small channel to the outlet pipe to reduce silt/debris) were agreed. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 23 December 2020 event pond water rose above the 300mm outlet pipe and exceeded the overflow route into the 600mm culvert. This caused water to flow over the top of the embankment and down Main Street, contributing to downstream inundation. (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300mm pipe; 600mm culvert [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>doubled storage capacity (scenario tested) (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300mm outlet pipe (overtopped); 600mm culvert (overflowed) (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Townsend Pond,England,"The embanked pond at the western end of the village overtopped on 23 December 2020 (figures in the report show flooding at 13:40 and further overtopping in the evening), discharging excess water via its outlet pipe into the downstream culvert and contributing to flooding in the village. [PAG,IMG] (p15)
Townsend Pond overtopped (annotated as occurring at approximately 17:00 on 23 December 2020). The pond is embanked at its eastern end and discharges via a 300mm pipe into a 600mm culvert; the culvert was surcharged and its capacity exceeded during the event, contributing to downstream overland flow and flooding in the village. [PAG] (p34)
Townsend Pond overtopped: water rose above the 300mm outlet pipe (visible by 13:40) and, by approximately 17:00, overtopped the overflow structure and the top of the embankment. The overtopping released additional water down Main Street, triggering a second rapid rise in water levels and causing internal flooding of nearby properties. Water levels fell rapidly later that night and flooding had dissipated by 02:00 on 24 December. (p36)
Townsend Pond overtopped at approximately 17:00; water overtopped the overflow structure and the top of the embankment and additional water flowed down Main Street, coinciding with a second wave of rainfall and causing rapid rises in water levels that led to internal flooding of nearby properties. As pond levels fell below the embankment later in the event the culverted watercourse allowed the remaining floodwater on the road to drain relatively rapidly, with flooding largely dissipating by about 23:00 and no remaining flooding after 02:00 on 24 December. [IMG,PAG] (p37)
A modelled scenario doubling the pond's storage capacity would not have prevented the culvert surcharging during the 23rd December 2020 event because flows from the west were too large. The pond could potentially overtop in extreme events and increased storage might worsen downstream impacts. Management changes (maintaining a clear small channel to the outlet pipe to reduce silt/debris) were agreed. (p44)
During the 23 December 2020 event pond water rose above the 300mm outlet pipe and exceeded the overflow route into the 600mm culvert. This caused water to flow over the top of the embankment and down Main Street, contributing to downstream inundation. (p50)","300mm pipe; 600mm culvert [PAG] (p34)
doubled storage capacity (scenario tested) (p44)
300mm outlet pipe (overtopped); 600mm culvert (overflowed) (p50)",GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23,Tingewick flooding 23 Dec 2020,Tingewick Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/tingewick-report-dec-2020.pdf
GB-BKM,25,2020-12-23,51.990463,-1.0433428,"<div id=""html_d9bbcdda9462b2a812e745de4dd60f7b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Main Street, Tingewick</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A 600 mm culvert conveying discharge from Townsend Pond runs beneath the entire length of Main Street; during the 23 December 2020 event the pond overtopped and routed flows into this culvert and the village surface water system, making the culvert and highway corridor a pathway for floodwater. [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main Street is shown as having a high risk of surface water flooding and flood extents increase downstream of the Main Street culvert where the watercourse becomes open channel. Properties along Main Street experienced flooding during the December 2020 event, with reports that water rose through floors and that shallow groundwater/wells were present in rear gardens (south side of Main Street), suggesting both surface&ndash;water and groundwater pathways affected properties. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Manholes surcharged, causing water to flood onto the highway and into gardens. Sandbags were delivered to Main Street and an additional road closure at Main Street was requested by Thames Valley Police during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Received delivered sandbags as part of the flood response, indicating direct exposure to flooding risk during the event. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 600mm culvert running under the entire length of Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded. Several manholes along Main Street surcharged, blocked gullies prevented surface water draining to the sewer system, and surface water flow along Main Street led to internal property flooding adjacent to the Education Facility and other properties. [PAG,IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies on Main Street (which connect to the culverted watercourse) were reported blocked during the event and, because the culvert was overwhelmed on 23 December 2020, the gullies could not drain freely. Water was observed close to the top of manholes in the highway and flows ran down the street from the western end toward the eastern end during the afternoon. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters pooled and flowed along Main Street after additional surface water from adjoining downhill roads (including West Well Lane) and after Townsend Pond overtopped. From about 17:00 the overtopping wave caused rapidly rising water that entered properties via front doors and, in some cases, through floors, causing internal flooding. Flooding dissipated later that night and had cleared by 02:00 on 24 December. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant highway flooding along Main Street; worst affected areas towards the western end of Main Street with deeper external flood depths. The highway flooding led to a road closure being ordered. [PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted watercourse beneath Main Street was found mostly in good condition but had a vegetation blockage (35% obstruction) at its downstream end discharging to the open watercourse; this partial blockage is likely to have slowed discharge and exacerbated flooding during the 23rd December 2020 event. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred around Main Street and West Well Lane during the 23 December 2020 event. The West Well Lane surface water culvert had collapsed prior to the event, causing water to back up; the Main Street culvert (600mm diameter) was generally in good condition. The West Well Lane culvert was later restored and blockages/roots removed. [MUL] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surface water culvert beneath Main Street surcharged during the 23rd December 2020 event, causing flooding along the street. Modelling indicates that doubling culvert capacity and removing blockages would have reduced flood depths in that event but would not have completely prevented surcharging and consequent flooding; however the increased capacity would prevent surcharging in lower&ndash;order events up to the 1 in 20 annual chance event. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low property thresholds and several redundant dropped kerbs on Main Street provided flow routes for floodwater, exacerbating internal property flooding; raising kerb heights and removing/raising dropped kerbs is recommended to retain water in the highway and reduce property&ndash;level flooding. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed along Main Street from the western end during the afternoon of 23 December 2020 as culverts and drainage surcharged. Water from surcharged manholes and the overtopped pond embankment flowed down Main Street, causing pooling and leading to internal flooding of adjacent properties (water entering via rear doors and ground&ndash;floor rooms). The 600mm culvert beneath Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded. (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant highway flooding along Main Street leading to a road closure being imposed by the Highway Authority. (p51)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>external flood depths 150 &ndash; 600mm; worst affected western end with some properties reporting internal depths up to 300 &ndash; 600mm; road closure implemented within 2&ndash;3 hours [PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35% blockage; 1 Grade 3 defect; 5 Grade 4 defects; 0 Grade 5 defects (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>600mm culvert (surcharged and capacity exceeded); internal flood depths typically 75&ndash;200mm with some 300&ndash;600mm; external depths typically 150&ndash;600mm (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Main Street, Tingewick",England,"A 600 mm culvert conveying discharge from Townsend Pond runs beneath the entire length of Main Street; during the 23 December 2020 event the pond overtopped and routed flows into this culvert and the village surface water system, making the culvert and highway corridor a pathway for floodwater. [PAG,IMG] (p17)
Main Street is shown as having a high risk of surface water flooding and flood extents increase downstream of the Main Street culvert where the watercourse becomes open channel. Properties along Main Street experienced flooding during the December 2020 event, with reports that water rose through floors and that shallow groundwater/wells were present in rear gardens (south side of Main Street), suggesting both surface–water and groundwater pathways affected properties. [PAG] (p23)
Manholes surcharged, causing water to flood onto the highway and into gardens. Sandbags were delivered to Main Street and an additional road closure at Main Street was requested by Thames Valley Police during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p32)
Received delivered sandbags as part of the flood response, indicating direct exposure to flooding risk during the event. (p33)
The 600mm culvert running under the entire length of Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded. Several manholes along Main Street surcharged, blocked gullies prevented surface water draining to the sewer system, and surface water flow along Main Street led to internal property flooding adjacent to the Education Facility and other properties. [PAG,IMG] (p34)
Highway gullies on Main Street (which connect to the culverted watercourse) were reported blocked during the event and, because the culvert was overwhelmed on 23 December 2020, the gullies could not drain freely. Water was observed close to the top of manholes in the highway and flows ran down the street from the western end toward the eastern end during the afternoon. (p35)
Floodwaters pooled and flowed along Main Street after additional surface water from adjoining downhill roads (including West Well Lane) and after Townsend Pond overtopped. From about 17:00 the overtopping wave caused rapidly rising water that entered properties via front doors and, in some cases, through floors, causing internal flooding. Flooding dissipated later that night and had cleared by 02:00 on 24 December. [PAG] (p36)
Significant highway flooding along Main Street; worst affected areas towards the western end of Main Street with deeper external flood depths. The highway flooding led to a road closure being ordered. [PAG] (p38)
The culverted watercourse beneath Main Street was found mostly in good condition but had a vegetation blockage (35% obstruction) at its downstream end discharging to the open watercourse; this partial blockage is likely to have slowed discharge and exacerbated flooding during the 23rd December 2020 event. (p39)
Surface water flooding occurred around Main Street and West Well Lane during the 23 December 2020 event. The West Well Lane surface water culvert had collapsed prior to the event, causing water to back up; the Main Street culvert (600mm diameter) was generally in good condition. The West Well Lane culvert was later restored and blockages/roots removed. [MUL] (p41)
The surface water culvert beneath Main Street surcharged during the 23rd December 2020 event, causing flooding along the street. Modelling indicates that doubling culvert capacity and removing blockages would have reduced flood depths in that event but would not have completely prevented surcharging and consequent flooding; however the increased capacity would prevent surcharging in lower–order events up to the 1 in 20 annual chance event. (p43)
Low property thresholds and several redundant dropped kerbs on Main Street provided flow routes for floodwater, exacerbating internal property flooding; raising kerb heights and removing/raising dropped kerbs is recommended to retain water in the highway and reduce property–level flooding. (p49)
Floodwater flowed along Main Street from the western end during the afternoon of 23 December 2020 as culverts and drainage surcharged. Water from surcharged manholes and the overtopped pond embankment flowed down Main Street, causing pooling and leading to internal flooding of adjacent properties (water entering via rear doors and ground–floor rooms). The 600mm culvert beneath Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded. (p50)
Significant highway flooding along Main Street leading to a road closure being imposed by the Highway Authority. (p51)","external flood depths 150 – 600mm; worst affected western end with some properties reporting internal depths up to 300 – 600mm; road closure implemented within 2–3 hours [PAG] (p38)
35% blockage; 1 Grade 3 defect; 5 Grade 4 defects; 0 Grade 5 defects (p39)
600mm culvert (surcharged and capacity exceeded); internal flood depths typically 75–200mm with some 300–600mm; external depths typically 150–600mm (p50)",GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23,Tingewick flooding 23 Dec 2020,Tingewick Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/tingewick-report-dec-2020.pdf
GB-BKM,25,2020-12-23,51.9896356,-1.0483218,"<div id=""html_a305d179f217e58e18863855f0a6ae58"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Well Lane, Tingewick</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down West Well Lane during the event; the surface water pipe below West Well Lane had collapsed causing a blockage that contributed to local flooding and overland flow. [PAG,IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Initial reports (around 13:00 on 23 December 2020) describe flooding from manholes in rear gardens of properties at the western end of Main Street and West Well Lane due to private drainage backing up from the surcharged culvert and surface water sewer. A collapse and blockage of the surface water sewer on West Well Lane (just upstream of the junction with Main Street) exacerbated flooding, and the street's downhill slope sent additional surface water flows toward Main Street. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surface water sewer along West Well Lane had a significant partial obstruction (masonry bricks and root growth) at its downstream end where it connects into the Main Street culvert; this obstruction was included in the baseline model to replicate the conditions during the 23rd December 2020 flooding and would have restricted flow, contributing to flooding. The loose bricks and root growth were subsequently removed and the sewer confirmed structurally sound. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred around Main Street and West Well Lane during the 23 December 2020 event. The West Well Lane surface water culvert had collapsed prior to the event, causing water to back up; the Main Street culvert (600mm diameter) was generally in good condition. The West Well Lane culvert was later restored and blockages/roots removed. [MUL] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Private drainage connections surcharged, causing water to flood out from manholes and back up into rear gardens of properties along the western end of Main Street and West Well Lane. The surface water sewer along West Well Lane had collapsed (subsequently repaired), which likely exacerbated local flooding and contributed to internal and external flooding of nearby properties. (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 20 properties; 1 commercial property; 19 residential properties; 6 properties with entire ground&ndash;floor flooded; internal flood depths typically 75&ndash;200 mm (some 300&ndash;600 mm); external depths typically 150&ndash;600 mm (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"West Well Lane, Tingewick",England,"Surface water flowed down West Well Lane during the event; the surface water pipe below West Well Lane had collapsed causing a blockage that contributed to local flooding and overland flow. [PAG,IMG] (p34)
Initial reports (around 13:00 on 23 December 2020) describe flooding from manholes in rear gardens of properties at the western end of Main Street and West Well Lane due to private drainage backing up from the surcharged culvert and surface water sewer. A collapse and blockage of the surface water sewer on West Well Lane (just upstream of the junction with Main Street) exacerbated flooding, and the street's downhill slope sent additional surface water flows toward Main Street. (p35)
The surface water sewer along West Well Lane had a significant partial obstruction (masonry bricks and root growth) at its downstream end where it connects into the Main Street culvert; this obstruction was included in the baseline model to replicate the conditions during the 23rd December 2020 flooding and would have restricted flow, contributing to flooding. The loose bricks and root growth were subsequently removed and the sewer confirmed structurally sound. (p39)
Surface water flooding occurred around Main Street and West Well Lane during the 23 December 2020 event. The West Well Lane surface water culvert had collapsed prior to the event, causing water to back up; the Main Street culvert (600mm diameter) was generally in good condition. The West Well Lane culvert was later restored and blockages/roots removed. [MUL] (p41)
Private drainage connections surcharged, causing water to flood out from manholes and back up into rear gardens of properties along the western end of Main Street and West Well Lane. The surface water sewer along West Well Lane had collapsed (subsequently repaired), which likely exacerbated local flooding and contributed to internal and external flooding of nearby properties. (p50)",at least 20 properties; 1 commercial property; 19 residential properties; 6 properties with entire ground–floor flooded; internal flood depths typically 75–200 mm (some 300–600 mm); external depths typically 150–600 mm (p50),GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23,Tingewick flooding 23 Dec 2020,Tingewick Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/tingewick-report-dec-2020.pdf
GB-BKM,25,2020-12-23,51.98981818082121,-1.0445848690069097,"<div id=""html_07600f0617c446df2cb0765af12e984c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tingewick</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties within the village. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to residential properties (including basements) within the village; caused significant local impacts and triggered a Section 19 investigation by the Lead Local Flood Authority. [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village was affected by flooding during the reported event; the investigation collected mapping, historic flood datasets and sewer CCTV to understand the causes and impacts on the village. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Townsend Pond overtopped on 23 December 2020 (photographic evidence cited at 13:40 and later the same evening), resulting in overflow from the pond. The pond discharges via a 300mm pipe into a 600mm culvert that runs beneath Main Street; overtopping of the pond was observed during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Townsend Pond within the village flooded and overtopped on 23 December 2020; the pond discharges via a 300 mm pipe into a 600 mm culvert that conveys water under Main Street and acted as a pathway for surface water flooding through the village. [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Townsend Pond at the western end of the village overtopped on 23 December 2020 (photographs referenced as Figure 8&ndash;2 and Figure 8&ndash;3). The pond discharges via a 300mm pipe into a culvert under Main Street, which is described in the report as part of the drainage route connected to the pond. [PAG,IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 23 December 2020 event properties in Tingewick experienced internal flooding with numerous reports of water rising through floors. Groundwater was identified as being shallow in large areas of the village (within 0.025&ndash;0.5 m and 0.5&ndash;5 m of the surface in places) and likely contributed to flooding alongside surface water from local watercourses and pond overtopping. [PAG,IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tingewick experienced repeated surface&ndash;water flooding events over time, resulting in internal flooding to multiple properties (April 1998, July 2007, 2014). The investigation documents flooding associated with the 23 December 2020 event in the village (including documented pond flooding/overtopping on 23 December 2020). [PAG,IMG,TAB,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village was flooded on 23 December 2020; the event prompted the establishment of the Tingewick Flood Response Team and purchase of response equipment (pumps, hoses, dehumidifiers and sandbags) funded by a Buckinghamshire Council grant to support future responses. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 23 December 2020 intense rainfall over a saturated catchment produced a rapid catchment response; radar and gauge data show substantial rainfall (radar total ~48 mm; local gauge record nearby up to 52 mm) falling on soils with very low moisture deficit (0&ndash;10 mm), which led to rapid runoff and surface&ndash;water/flash flooding across the Tingewick catchment. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the village on 23 December 2020 from heavy rainfall and rapid catchment response on already&ndash;saturated soils. Residents were affected and received assistance from Buckinghamshire Fire and Rescue Service and Transport for Buckinghamshire. A hydraulic model was used to recreate flood levels and extents; modelled flows implied an approximate return period of over 50 years. [TAB,PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water emerged through manholes and flooded the highway and gardens; internal property flooding occurred (water rising through floors) affecting multiple properties; roads were closed during the event and remaining flood water receded overnight into 24 December. [TAB] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a significant area of flooding; roads flooded and communities were cut off; local residents used pumps and sandbags to try to protect properties; the Parish Council formed a Tingewick Emergency Response Team and supplied sandbags, pumps and manhole keys for future events; a house fire occurred in Tingewick caused by electrical damage to a boiler directly resulting from the flooding. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on an already saturated catchment caused the ordinary watercourse to rise rapidly and widespread overland flow through the village. A culvert running under Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded, several manholes along Main Street surcharged, blocked gullies and a collapsed surface water pipe (below West Well Lane) exacerbated flow. Surface water followed Main Street and West Well Lane, leading to internal property flooding (including properties adjacent to the Education Facility). [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ordinary watercourse through the village rose rapidly and Townsend Pond and the culverted watercourse were overwhelmed on 23 December 2020. The culvert under Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded, preventing the pond and surface water drainage systems from draining. This led to surcharging of private drainage, manhole flooding in rear gardens, pooling of water in gardens and flows along Main Street from the western toward the eastern end of the village. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of Townsend Pond led to floodwater flowing down Main Street and extensive internal and external flooding across the village. At least 20 properties were flooded internally (including one commercial property, The Royal Oak, and 19 residential properties). Many residents were forced into temporary accommodation, reported insurance claims and financial burden, and experienced mental&ndash;health impacts and stress. Internal flooding entered properties through front doors and in some cases through floors; floodwater depths were worst towards the western end of Main Street. Flooding began after pond overtopping around 17:00 and dissipated rapidly from about 23:00, with no remaining flooding after 02:00 on 24 December as the pond level fell and the culverted watercourse drained the road. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal and external flooding across the village: extensive internal flooding of properties (including whole&ndash;ground&ndash;floor inundation in worst cases), prolonged drying times for homes, damage to carpets/flooring/furniture, one property&#8217;s boiler damaged by flooding which subsequently caused a house fire. At least some households were displaced or lived on upper floors while repairs occurred. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted watercourse through Tingewick (along Main Street) and connecting surface water sewer were obstructed during the observed flood event, which slowed discharge to the downstream open watercourse. A 35% vegetation blockage was identified at the downstream end of the culvert and the downstream connection from the West Well Lane surface water sewer into the culvert was partially blocked by masonry bricks and root growth; these conditions were reproduced in the hydraulic baseline model for the 23 December 2020 flood. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected the village on 23 December 2020; the surface water drainage system was overloaded (model baseline calibrated to reported flood depths for that date). A collapsed culvert on West Well Lane prior to the event caused backing up of water that contributed to the flooding. [PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the ordinary watercourse affected the village; modelling of the 23 December 2020 event considered an upstream storage area to the west of the village which, in the model, operates online and captures water from the ordinary watercourse as a mitigation measure. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surface water/ordinary watercourse culvert through the village was at capacity and surcharged during the 23rd December 2020 event, causing flooding in the village. Modelled results indicate the culvert was overloaded above a 1 in 10 annual chance event and surcharging occurred; very large upstream storage (estimated 15,000&ndash;20,000 m3 for the December 2020 event, ~6,000 m3 for a 1% AEP design event) would have been required to reduce flows. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ordinary watercourse culvert upstream of the village was overloaded in the 23 December 2020 event, producing flood flows into the village. Floodwater entered many properties through doors and floors; a number of affected properties had suspended timber floors and low&ndash;level airbricks which contributed to internal entry. [PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the December 2020 event water entered many properties in Tingewick via multiple points, including doors and through floors; many affected properties had suspended timber floors and ground&ndash;level airbricks which contributed to internal ingress and property flooding. (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding on 23 December 2020; the severity was noted to be increased by blockage of highway gullies leading to surface water accumulation and added pressure on the village surface water culvert and receiving watercourse. Balancing ponds associated with the Tingewick Bypass were reviewed and concluded not to have been a significant factor in the village flooding (eastern pond discharges downstream of the village; the western pond was designed with capacity and an outfall limiting discharge). [TAB] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced rapid flooding on 23 December 2020. The ordinary watercourse rose rapidly and a 600mm culvert beneath Main Street was surcharged, while private drainage connections and manholes surcharged causing water to flood out into rear gardens and back up into properties. At least 20 properties flooded internally (including residential and one commercial property) and a further two properties experienced external or outbuilding flooding. Internal flooding entered mainly via rear doors; many ground&ndash;floor rooms flooded and six properties reported the entire ground floor flooded. One property suffered a fire due to a boiler damaged by floodwater. (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding affected properties in the village: at least 20 properties flooded internally (including one commercial property, The Royal Oak). Internal flooding was extensive&mdash;at least six properties reported flooding to the entire ground floor and the remainder had flooding to individual rooms on the ground floor. Internal flood depths were typically 75&ndash;200 mm (a small number 300&ndash;600 mm). External flood depths were typically 150&ndash;600 mm. One property suffered a boiler&ndash;related fire caused by flood damage. Highway flooding was significant and mental&ndash;health impacts and community concern about future flooding were reported. [TAB] (p51)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 20 properties (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 20 properties [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least three properties; at least 11 properties; at least 6 properties [PAG,IMG,TAB,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 20 properties; 1 commercial property; 19 residential properties; at least 6 properties with whole ground floor flooded; internal flood depths typically 75&ndash;200 mm (some 300&ndash;600 mm); external flood depths typically 150&ndash;600 mm (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 20 properties flooded internally; 1 commercial property and 19 residential properties; at least six properties reported flooding to the entire ground floor; internal flood depths typically 75 &ndash; 200mm (some 300 &ndash; 600mm); external flood depths typically 150 &ndash; 600mm; internal flooding durations from a few hours to approximately 10 or 11 hours; at least four households moved into temporary accommodation; at least one household still repairing damage by May 2021 (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35% blockage (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15&ndash;20,000m3 of storage; 6,000m3 of storage (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15,000&ndash;20,000 m3; 6,000 m3 [PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 20 properties; 2 properties (external/outbuildings); 1 commercial property (The Royal Oak); 19 residential properties; 6 properties (entire ground floor flooded); internal flood depths 75&ndash;200mm (typical) with a small number 300&ndash;600mm; external depths typically 150&ndash;600mm (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 20 properties; 1 commercial property (The Royal Oak); 19 residential properties; at least 6 properties reporting flooding to the entire ground floor [TAB] (p51)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Tingewick,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties within the village. (p4)
Internal flooding to residential properties (including basements) within the village; caused significant local impacts and triggered a Section 19 investigation by the Lead Local Flood Authority. [MUL] (p12)
The village was affected by flooding during the reported event; the investigation collected mapping, historic flood datasets and sewer CCTV to understand the causes and impacts on the village. (p13)
Townsend Pond overtopped on 23 December 2020 (photographic evidence cited at 13:40 and later the same evening), resulting in overflow from the pond. The pond discharges via a 300mm pipe into a 600mm culvert that runs beneath Main Street; overtopping of the pond was observed during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p15)
Townsend Pond within the village flooded and overtopped on 23 December 2020; the pond discharges via a 300 mm pipe into a 600 mm culvert that conveys water under Main Street and acted as a pathway for surface water flooding through the village. [PAG,IMG] (p17)
Townsend Pond at the western end of the village overtopped on 23 December 2020 (photographs referenced as Figure 8–2 and Figure 8–3). The pond discharges via a 300mm pipe into a culvert under Main Street, which is described in the report as part of the drainage route connected to the pond. [PAG,IMG] (p19)
During the 23 December 2020 event properties in Tingewick experienced internal flooding with numerous reports of water rising through floors. Groundwater was identified as being shallow in large areas of the village (within 0.025–0.5 m and 0.5–5 m of the surface in places) and likely contributed to flooding alongside surface water from local watercourses and pond overtopping. [PAG,IMG] (p23)
Tingewick experienced repeated surface–water flooding events over time, resulting in internal flooding to multiple properties (April 1998, July 2007, 2014). The investigation documents flooding associated with the 23 December 2020 event in the village (including documented pond flooding/overtopping on 23 December 2020). [PAG,IMG,TAB,MUL] (p24)
The village was flooded on 23 December 2020; the event prompted the establishment of the Tingewick Flood Response Team and purchase of response equipment (pumps, hoses, dehumidifiers and sandbags) funded by a Buckinghamshire Council grant to support future responses. (p29)
On 23 December 2020 intense rainfall over a saturated catchment produced a rapid catchment response; radar and gauge data show substantial rainfall (radar total ~48 mm; local gauge record nearby up to 52 mm) falling on soils with very low moisture deficit (0–10 mm), which led to rapid runoff and surface–water/flash flooding across the Tingewick catchment. [PAG] (p30)
Flooding in the village on 23 December 2020 from heavy rainfall and rapid catchment response on already–saturated soils. Residents were affected and received assistance from Buckinghamshire Fire and Rescue Service and Transport for Buckinghamshire. A hydraulic model was used to recreate flood levels and extents; modelled flows implied an approximate return period of over 50 years. [TAB,PAG] (p31)
Flood water emerged through manholes and flooded the highway and gardens; internal property flooding occurred (water rising through floors) affecting multiple properties; roads were closed during the event and remaining flood water receded overnight into 24 December. [TAB] (p32)
Identified as a significant area of flooding; roads flooded and communities were cut off; local residents used pumps and sandbags to try to protect properties; the Parish Council formed a Tingewick Emergency Response Team and supplied sandbags, pumps and manhole keys for future events; a house fire occurred in Tingewick caused by electrical damage to a boiler directly resulting from the flooding. (p33)
Intense rainfall on an already saturated catchment caused the ordinary watercourse to rise rapidly and widespread overland flow through the village. A culvert running under Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded, several manholes along Main Street surcharged, blocked gullies and a collapsed surface water pipe (below West Well Lane) exacerbated flow. Surface water followed Main Street and West Well Lane, leading to internal property flooding (including properties adjacent to the Education Facility). [PAG] (p34)
The ordinary watercourse through the village rose rapidly and Townsend Pond and the culverted watercourse were overwhelmed on 23 December 2020. The culvert under Main Street was surcharged and its capacity exceeded, preventing the pond and surface water drainage systems from draining. This led to surcharging of private drainage, manhole flooding in rear gardens, pooling of water in gardens and flows along Main Street from the western toward the eastern end of the village. (p35)
Overtopping of Townsend Pond led to floodwater flowing down Main Street and extensive internal and external flooding across the village. At least 20 properties were flooded internally (including one commercial property, The Royal Oak, and 19 residential properties). Many residents were forced into temporary accommodation, reported insurance claims and financial burden, and experienced mental–health impacts and stress. Internal flooding entered properties through front doors and in some cases through floors; floodwater depths were worst towards the western end of Main Street. Flooding began after pond overtopping around 17:00 and dissipated rapidly from about 23:00, with no remaining flooding after 02:00 on 24 December as the pond level fell and the culverted watercourse drained the road. (p37)
Internal and external flooding across the village: extensive internal flooding of properties (including whole–ground–floor inundation in worst cases), prolonged drying times for homes, damage to carpets/flooring/furniture, one property’s boiler damaged by flooding which subsequently caused a house fire. At least some households were displaced or lived on upper floors while repairs occurred. (p38)
The culverted watercourse through Tingewick (along Main Street) and connecting surface water sewer were obstructed during the observed flood event, which slowed discharge to the downstream open watercourse. A 35% vegetation blockage was identified at the downstream end of the culvert and the downstream connection from the West Well Lane surface water sewer into the culvert was partially blocked by masonry bricks and root growth; these conditions were reproduced in the hydraulic baseline model for the 23 December 2020 flood. (p39)
Surface water flooding affected the village on 23 December 2020; the surface water drainage system was overloaded (model baseline calibrated to reported flood depths for that date). A collapsed culvert on West Well Lane prior to the event caused backing up of water that contributed to the flooding. [PAG] (p41)
Flooding from the ordinary watercourse affected the village; modelling of the 23 December 2020 event considered an upstream storage area to the west of the village which, in the model, operates online and captures water from the ordinary watercourse as a mitigation measure. (p42)
The surface water/ordinary watercourse culvert through the village was at capacity and surcharged during the 23rd December 2020 event, causing flooding in the village. Modelled results indicate the culvert was overloaded above a 1 in 10 annual chance event and surcharging occurred; very large upstream storage (estimated 15,000–20,000 m3 for the December 2020 event, ~6,000 m3 for a 1% AEP design event) would have been required to reduce flows. (p44)
The ordinary watercourse culvert upstream of the village was overloaded in the 23 December 2020 event, producing flood flows into the village. Floodwater entered many properties through doors and floors; a number of affected properties had suspended timber floors and low–level airbricks which contributed to internal entry. [PAG] (p45)
During the December 2020 event water entered many properties in Tingewick via multiple points, including doors and through floors; many affected properties had suspended timber floors and ground–level airbricks which contributed to internal ingress and property flooding. (p46)
The village experienced flooding on 23 December 2020; the severity was noted to be increased by blockage of highway gullies leading to surface water accumulation and added pressure on the village surface water culvert and receiving watercourse. Balancing ponds associated with the Tingewick Bypass were reviewed and concluded not to have been a significant factor in the village flooding (eastern pond discharges downstream of the village; the western pond was designed with capacity and an outfall limiting discharge). [TAB] (p48)
The village experienced rapid flooding on 23 December 2020. The ordinary watercourse rose rapidly and a 600mm culvert beneath Main Street was surcharged, while private drainage connections and manholes surcharged causing water to flood out into rear gardens and back up into properties. At least 20 properties flooded internally (including residential and one commercial property) and a further two properties experienced external or outbuilding flooding. Internal flooding entered mainly via rear doors; many ground–floor rooms flooded and six properties reported the entire ground floor flooded. One property suffered a fire due to a boiler damaged by floodwater. (p50)
Internal flooding affected properties in the village: at least 20 properties flooded internally (including one commercial property, The Royal Oak). Internal flooding was extensive—at least six properties reported flooding to the entire ground floor and the remainder had flooding to individual rooms on the ground floor. Internal flood depths were typically 75–200 mm (a small number 300–600 mm). External flood depths were typically 150–600 mm. One property suffered a boiler–related fire caused by flood damage. Highway flooding was significant and mental–health impacts and community concern about future flooding were reported. [TAB] (p51)","at least 20 properties (p4)
at least 20 properties [MUL] (p12)
at least three properties; at least 11 properties; at least 6 properties [PAG,IMG,TAB,MUL] (p24)
at least 20 properties; 1 commercial property; 19 residential properties; at least 6 properties with whole ground floor flooded; internal flood depths typically 75–200 mm (some 300–600 mm); external flood depths typically 150–600 mm (p37)
at least 20 properties flooded internally; 1 commercial property and 19 residential properties; at least six properties reported flooding to the entire ground floor; internal flood depths typically 75 – 200mm (some 300 – 600mm); external flood depths typically 150 – 600mm; internal flooding durations from a few hours to approximately 10 or 11 hours; at least four households moved into temporary accommodation; at least one household still repairing damage by May 2021 (p38)
35% blockage (p39)
15–20,000m3 of storage; 6,000m3 of storage (p44)
15,000–20,000 m3; 6,000 m3 [PAG] (p45)
at least 20 properties; 2 properties (external/outbuildings); 1 commercial property (The Royal Oak); 19 residential properties; 6 properties (entire ground floor flooded); internal flood depths 75–200mm (typical) with a small number 300–600mm; external depths typically 150–600mm (p50)
at least 20 properties; 1 commercial property (The Royal Oak); 19 residential properties; at least 6 properties reporting flooding to the entire ground floor [TAB] (p51)",GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23,Tingewick flooding 23 Dec 2020,Tingewick Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/tingewick-report-dec-2020.pdf
GB-BKM,25,2020-12-23,51.998318,-0.9786725,"<div id=""html_4580061fd02528b15c9041e5cdaf2549"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckingham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced road closures during the flood event; nearby main roads (including the A422 and A421) were impassable in places, contributing to communities being cut off. [PAG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckingham,England,"Experienced road closures during the flood event; nearby main roads (including the A422 and A421) were impassable in places, contributing to communities being cut off. [PAG] (p33)",,GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_25_2020-12-23,Tingewick flooding 23 Dec 2020,Tingewick Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/tingewick-report-dec-2020.pdf
GB-BKM,7,2014-02-07,51.57011948079867,-0.5350591632053233,"<div id=""html_d13f021bc5ccbf5e868e2121a0ee2fce"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>M25 at Gerrards Cross</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding inundated the motorway, forcing major traffic disruption and lane closures. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The M25 was flooded when nearby silted ditches failed to convey surface water away from the area. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the M25 motorway near Gerrards Cross, prompting a Section 19 investigation because the incident met the threshold for motorways/trunk roads (significant disruption). [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on the M25 caused motorway closure that met the investigation threshold for major roads (closure of 2 hours or more), disrupting the motorway. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from overwhelmed drainage and the Fulmer Lane embankment flowed onto the M25 causing lane flooding and lane closures during the event. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water conveyance away from the M25 was impeded by silted ditches; the Highways Agency reported lane closures on the M25 during the flood event. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The M25 was flooded during the event, causing lane closures (timings/details of closures not provided in the report). [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the M25 was flooded due to the heavy rainfall. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,M25 at Gerrards Cross,England,"Surface water flooding inundated the motorway, forcing major traffic disruption and lane closures. [PAG] (p4)
The M25 was flooded when nearby silted ditches failed to convey surface water away from the area. (p5)
Flooding affected the M25 motorway near Gerrards Cross, prompting a Section 19 investigation because the incident met the threshold for motorways/trunk roads (significant disruption). [PAG] (p6)
Flooding on the M25 caused motorway closure that met the investigation threshold for major roads (closure of 2 hours or more), disrupting the motorway. [PAG] (p7)
Surface water from overwhelmed drainage and the Fulmer Lane embankment flowed onto the M25 causing lane flooding and lane closures during the event. (p13)
Surface water conveyance away from the M25 was impeded by silted ditches; the Highways Agency reported lane closures on the M25 during the flood event. [PAG] (p14)
The M25 was flooded during the event, causing lane closures (timings/details of closures not provided in the report). [PAG] (p15)
Part of the M25 was flooded due to the heavy rainfall. (p18)",,GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07.html,GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07,"Fulmer Lane, Denham flooding 07 Feb 2014","M25 and Fulmer Lane, Gerrards Cross, February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/fulmer-lane-garrards-cross/Feb/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,7,2014-02-07,51.5674493,-0.5423257,"<div id=""html_e60bc8e8b585daed7a42d1f6de57a587"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fulmer Lane, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Standing/pooled water in vegetation and a small stream/drainage channel adjacent to the lane; temporary barrier (white cylindrical sandbag&ndash;like object) placed along the stream bank and partially submerged, documenting flooding of the channel and bank. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Silted roadside ditches and overwhelmed road drainage meant surface water could not be conveyed away, contributing to flooding in the area and to flooding of the M25. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ditches along Fulmer Lane were silted and therefore unable to convey surface water away from the area, contributing to flooding of the nearby M25. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Fulmer Lane in Gerrards Cross (just west of the M25), prompting a Section 19 investigation into the incident. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Fulmer Lane in Gerrards Cross and led to road closure; the site is the focus of the flood investigation. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road closure shown on the location map (red dotted line) associated with the incident; indicates the lane was made impassable and closed during the event. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water built up to the west of Fulmer Lane and flowed over Fulmer Lane, then down the embankment onto the M25; flood maps (1 in 100 and 1 in 1000) show depths and the flow path. A road closure is marked on the location map. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from a saturated catchment caused surface water to run straight onto the road and into the remaining ditch capacity, producing road flooding and placing nearby built infrastructure at risk. Flood&ndash;depth maps (EA, 2013) for the Fulmer Lane area predict surface water depths across the road and adjacent low&ndash;lying areas. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall (night of 6&ndash;7 Feb) combined with heavily silted ditches overwhelmed the drainage system so surface water did not convey along the ditches. Water flowed down the embankment onto the M25 and Fulmer Lane itself flooded; Fulmer Lane was closed during the event for maintenance/ditch works by Transport for Buckinghamshire and the Highways Agency. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ditches on Fulmer Lane were heavily silted which contributed to reduced conveyance of surface water away from the M25; Fulmer Lane was closed during the flood event for maintenance works to the ditches. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fulmer Lane was flooded due to heavy rainfall and heavily silted ditches along both sides of the road; road flooding occurred. [TAB] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 hours or more (road closure) (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Fulmer Lane, Denham",England,"Standing/pooled water in vegetation and a small stream/drainage channel adjacent to the lane; temporary barrier (white cylindrical sandbag–like object) placed along the stream bank and partially submerged, documenting flooding of the channel and bank. (p1)
Silted roadside ditches and overwhelmed road drainage meant surface water could not be conveyed away, contributing to flooding in the area and to flooding of the M25. [PAG] (p4)
Ditches along Fulmer Lane were silted and therefore unable to convey surface water away from the area, contributing to flooding of the nearby M25. (p5)
Flooding occurred on Fulmer Lane in Gerrards Cross (just west of the M25), prompting a Section 19 investigation into the incident. [PAG] (p6)
Flooding affected Fulmer Lane in Gerrards Cross and led to road closure; the site is the focus of the flood investigation. (p7)
Road closure shown on the location map (red dotted line) associated with the incident; indicates the lane was made impassable and closed during the event. [PAG] (p8)
Surface water built up to the west of Fulmer Lane and flowed over Fulmer Lane, then down the embankment onto the M25; flood maps (1 in 100 and 1 in 1000) show depths and the flow path. A road closure is marked on the location map. [PAG] (p9)
Runoff from a saturated catchment caused surface water to run straight onto the road and into the remaining ditch capacity, producing road flooding and placing nearby built infrastructure at risk. Flood–depth maps (EA, 2013) for the Fulmer Lane area predict surface water depths across the road and adjacent low–lying areas. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
Heavy rainfall (night of 6–7 Feb) combined with heavily silted ditches overwhelmed the drainage system so surface water did not convey along the ditches. Water flowed down the embankment onto the M25 and Fulmer Lane itself flooded; Fulmer Lane was closed during the event for maintenance/ditch works by Transport for Buckinghamshire and the Highways Agency. [PAG] (p13)
Ditches on Fulmer Lane were heavily silted which contributed to reduced conveyance of surface water away from the M25; Fulmer Lane was closed during the flood event for maintenance works to the ditches. [PAG] (p14)
Fulmer Lane was flooded due to heavy rainfall and heavily silted ditches along both sides of the road; road flooding occurred. [TAB] (p18)",2 hours or more (road closure) (p7),GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07.html,GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07,"Fulmer Lane, Denham flooding 07 Feb 2014","M25 and Fulmer Lane, Gerrards Cross, February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/fulmer-lane-garrards-cross/Feb/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,7,2014-02-07,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_c6e71468c46ff78aedebe8abbd6a642d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;level flood investigation covers flooding at locations within the county (notably M25 and Fulmer Lane in Gerrards Cross) where standing water in vegetation/stream channels and temporary bank protection were photographed on 7 February 2014. [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"County–level flood investigation covers flooding at locations within the county (notably M25 and Fulmer Lane in Gerrards Cross) where standing water in vegetation/stream channels and temporary bank protection were photographed on 7 February 2014. [IMG,PAG] (p1)",,GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07.html,GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07,"Fulmer Lane, Denham flooding 07 Feb 2014","M25 and Fulmer Lane, Gerrards Cross, February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/fulmer-lane-garrards-cross/Feb/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,7,2014-02-07,51.5878131,-0.5537126,"<div id=""html_8d6dd5d25b15884be1613986fb063333"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gerrards Cross</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area around Fulmer Lane (in Gerrards Cross) experienced standing water/stream flooding in February 2014, evidenced by photographs showing pooled water in vegetation and a temporary bank barrier. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood event occurred in Gerrards Cross, affecting Fulmer Lane and the M25 there; saturated catchment and overwhelmed drainage led to localised surface water flooding. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected locations within Gerrards Cross (notably Fulmer Lane adjacent to the M25), triggering a Section 19 investigation by the LLFA. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local flooding impacted the Gerrards Cross area (specifically Fulmer Lane) and contributed to road closures in the locality. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fulmer Lane (in Gerrards Cross) was closed during the flood event for maintenance to ditches to address surface water conveyance issues. (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 hours or more (road closure) [PAG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Gerrards Cross,England,"Area around Fulmer Lane (in Gerrards Cross) experienced standing water/stream flooding in February 2014, evidenced by photographs showing pooled water in vegetation and a temporary bank barrier. (p1)
The flood event occurred in Gerrards Cross, affecting Fulmer Lane and the M25 there; saturated catchment and overwhelmed drainage led to localised surface water flooding. (p4)
Flooding affected locations within Gerrards Cross (notably Fulmer Lane adjacent to the M25), triggering a Section 19 investigation by the LLFA. [PAG] (p6)
Local flooding impacted the Gerrards Cross area (specifically Fulmer Lane) and contributed to road closures in the locality. [PAG] (p7)
Fulmer Lane (in Gerrards Cross) was closed during the flood event for maintenance to ditches to address surface water conveyance issues. (p14)",2 hours or more (road closure) [PAG] (p7),GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07.html,GB-BKM_7_2014-02-07,"Fulmer Lane, Denham flooding 07 Feb 2014","M25 and Fulmer Lane, Gerrards Cross, February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/fulmer-lane-garrards-cross/Feb/2014.pdf
GB-CAM,26,2020-12-23,52.1278302,-0.4250206,"<div id=""html_226ff75d54a9657098d6a8d2aafa6a73"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Great Ouse</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Closure of the sluice door at Webbs Hole prevents watercourses discharging into the River Great Ouse, which can exacerbate surface&ndash;water flooding in Swavesey. (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,River Great Ouse,England,"Closure of the sluice door at Webbs Hole prevents watercourses discharging into the River Great Ouse, which can exacerbate surface–water flooding in Swavesey. (p4)",,GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23,Swavesey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Swavesey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Swavesey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,26,2020-12-23,52.29368,-0.0021362,"<div id=""html_053895af34017c7e83e0d6798419c2d4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Middle Watch, Swavesey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was reported at this location; identified as one of three key risk areas within Swavesey. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooding was identified in the southern corridor of the village along the main north&ndash;south road, with outlined affected zones adjacent to and along this road. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked highway gullies caused surface water to pond on the road and eventually enter residential properties through the floor cavity and front door. Properties also experienced foul sewer flooding, likely from surface water ingress to the foul system or high groundwater during prolonged rainfall. [TAB] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area was reported flooded during the December 2020 event; RMAs and the LLFA attended to investigate and perform walkovers. Actions taken/required related to blocked highway gullies and foul sewer blockages which contributed to flooding. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected Middle Watch during the 23 December 2020 event; the location is recorded in Table 3 as having surface water flooding and RMA operational issues. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties and gardens; maximum depth c.80mm [TAB] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Middle Watch, Swavesey",England,"Internal property flooding was reported at this location; identified as one of three key risk areas within Swavesey. (p7)
Road flooding was identified in the southern corridor of the village along the main north–south road, with outlined affected zones adjacent to and along this road. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
Blocked highway gullies caused surface water to pond on the road and eventually enter residential properties through the floor cavity and front door. Properties also experienced foul sewer flooding, likely from surface water ingress to the foul system or high groundwater during prolonged rainfall. [TAB] (p9)
Area was reported flooded during the December 2020 event; RMAs and the LLFA attended to investigate and perform walkovers. Actions taken/required related to blocked highway gullies and foul sewer blockages which contributed to flooding. [TAB] (p11)
Surface water flooding affected Middle Watch during the 23 December 2020 event; the location is recorded in Table 3 as having surface water flooding and RMA operational issues. [TAB] (p15)",2 residential properties and gardens; maximum depth c.80mm [TAB] (p9),GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23,Swavesey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Swavesey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Swavesey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,26,2020-12-23,52.2915354,-0.0005568,"<div id=""html_2a1a7dc2edd3137b061af60a9f589377"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ramper Road, Swavesey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was reported at this location; identified as one of three key risk areas within Swavesey. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and foul sewer flooding occurred; sewers and surface water drains have been reported to overflow into residential gardens. The sewerage undertaker attended the area to remove blockages that caused flooding. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two residential properties were flooded internally (depth unspecified). The gardens and outbuildings of an additional five properties were inundated with floodwater. Occupants did not have to leave their premises. [TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooding location investigated by RMAs/LLFA; follow&ndash;up actions included consideration of highway gully maintenance and sewer network capacity and removal of foul sewer blockages to address flooding. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected Ramper Road during the 23 December 2020 event; Table 3 records RMA operational issues alongside surface water flooding for this location. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; gardens and outbuildings of an additional 5 properties [TAB] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Ramper Road, Swavesey",England,"Internal property flooding was reported at this location; identified as one of three key risk areas within Swavesey. (p7)
Surface water and foul sewer flooding occurred; sewers and surface water drains have been reported to overflow into residential gardens. The sewerage undertaker attended the area to remove blockages that caused flooding. (p9)
Two residential properties were flooded internally (depth unspecified). The gardens and outbuildings of an additional five properties were inundated with floodwater. Occupants did not have to leave their premises. [TAB] (p10)
Reported flooding location investigated by RMAs/LLFA; follow–up actions included consideration of highway gully maintenance and sewer network capacity and removal of foul sewer blockages to address flooding. [TAB] (p11)
Surface water flooding affected Ramper Road during the 23 December 2020 event; Table 3 records RMA operational issues alongside surface water flooding for this location. [TAB] (p15)",2 residential properties; gardens and outbuildings of an additional 5 properties [TAB] (p10),GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23,Swavesey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Swavesey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Swavesey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,26,2020-12-23,52.2899765,-0.0025505,"<div id=""html_faf924158cc144ad1db04bba40de8a6a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Boxworth End, Swavesey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was reported at this location; identified as one of three key risk areas within Swavesey. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding and foul sewer flooding occurred: five residential properties were flooded internally (depth unspecified) and the gardens and outbuildings of an additional five properties were inundated. Roads and footpaths were regularly inundated; gullies were ineffective at conveying water away, and properties on the western side experienced high&ndash;velocity surface water runoff from adjacent agricultural fields. Sewers in gardens backed up during sustained rainfall, causing foul sewer flooding at the rear of properties. [TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooding location investigated by RMAs/LLFA; actions included inspections, gully cleansing arrangements and removal of foul sewer blockages to mitigate flooding impacts. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected Boxworth End during the 23 December 2020 event; it is listed in Table 3 with RMA operational issues and surface water flooding. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 residential properties; gardens and outbuildings of an additional 5 properties [TAB] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Boxworth End, Swavesey",England,"Internal property flooding was reported at this location; identified as one of three key risk areas within Swavesey. (p7)
Surface water flooding and foul sewer flooding occurred: five residential properties were flooded internally (depth unspecified) and the gardens and outbuildings of an additional five properties were inundated. Roads and footpaths were regularly inundated; gullies were ineffective at conveying water away, and properties on the western side experienced high–velocity surface water runoff from adjacent agricultural fields. Sewers in gardens backed up during sustained rainfall, causing foul sewer flooding at the rear of properties. [TAB] (p10)
Reported flooding location investigated by RMAs/LLFA; actions included inspections, gully cleansing arrangements and removal of foul sewer blockages to mitigate flooding impacts. [TAB] (p11)
Surface water flooding affected Boxworth End during the 23 December 2020 event; it is listed in Table 3 with RMA operational issues and surface water flooding. [TAB] (p15)",5 residential properties; gardens and outbuildings of an additional 5 properties [TAB] (p10),GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23,Swavesey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Swavesey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Swavesey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,26,2020-12-23,52.4617238,0.6975642,"<div id=""html_c341f05087050d6fca8215dde203e2bb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Anglia</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged above&ndash;average rainfall and saturated ground across East Anglia produced very high runoff and significant surface&ndash;water flooding, contributing to the Swavesey flooding event. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020, on very wet and saturated catchments, led to significant localised flooding in Swavesey including internal property flooding and inundation of roads and gardens. [IMG] (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 residential properties [IMG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,East Anglia,England,"Prolonged above–average rainfall and saturated ground across East Anglia produced very high runoff and significant surface–water flooding, contributing to the Swavesey flooding event. (p4)
Surface water runoff from intense rainfall on 23rd December 2020, on very wet and saturated catchments, led to significant localised flooding in Swavesey including internal property flooding and inundation of roads and gardens. [IMG] (p7)",5 residential properties [IMG] (p7),GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23,Swavesey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Swavesey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Swavesey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,26,2020-12-23,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_05c1eac9bdbfe28a8bdf8921488a6bcb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The county experienced much above&ndash;average rainfall leading to saturated ground and high runoff that contributed to surface water flooding in Swavesey on 23 December 2020. [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant surface&ndash;water&ndash;driven flooding affected Swavesey on 23rd December 2020. Flood reports indicate internal flooding of residential properties, and additional flooding of roads and gardens. Three local risk areas where internal property flooding was reported are identified as Middle Watch, Ramper Road and Boxworth End. [IMG] (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 flood reports; 5 residential properties [IMG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"The county experienced much above–average rainfall leading to saturated ground and high runoff that contributed to surface water flooding in Swavesey on 23 December 2020. [IMG] (p5)
Significant surface–water–driven flooding affected Swavesey on 23rd December 2020. Flood reports indicate internal flooding of residential properties, and additional flooding of roads and gardens. Three local risk areas where internal property flooding was reported are identified as Middle Watch, Ramper Road and Boxworth End. [IMG] (p7)",5 flood reports; 5 residential properties [IMG] (p7),GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_26_2020-12-23,Swavesey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Swavesey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Swavesey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,26,2020-12-23,52.3007486,-0.0052338,"<div id=""html_effb17b040cb73659d0bfad871e4edca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swavesey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant surface&ndash;water flooding occurred in Swavesey on 23 December 2020. The intense short&ndash;duration rainfall on that day, combined with a prolonged period of above&ndash;average rainfall that saturated the ground (very high water table / low SMD), produced very high runoff and significant flooding. Flooding is worsened by generally low land levels and can be exacerbated if the Webbs Hole sluice is closed, preventing discharge to the River Great Ouse. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Five residential properties were flooded internally; additional flooding occurred in some roads and gardens. The LLFA received five flood reports and identified three key risk areas within the village. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties (internal property flooding reported by LLFA); additional flooding of some roads and gardens. Three key risk areas were identified where internal property flooding was reported: Middle Watch, Ramper Road and Boxworth End. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced reported flooding during the December 2020 event; LLFA and RMAs carried out site walkovers, residents known to have flooded were contacted, gully cleansing was ordered and Anglian Water attended to remove foul sewer blockages; discussions opened about highway gully maintenance and sewer network capacity. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village of Swavesey experienced surface water flooding (noted at Middle Watch, Ramper Road and Boxworth End) and had sewer&ndash;related issues requiring removal of blockages and capacity discussions; actions are ongoing to maintain drainage and reduce future flooding. (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 residential properties; 5 flood reports (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 residential properties; 5 flood reports [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Swavesey,England,"Significant surface–water flooding occurred in Swavesey on 23 December 2020. The intense short–duration rainfall on that day, combined with a prolonged period of above–average rainfall that saturated the ground (very high water table / low SMD), produced very high runoff and significant flooding. Flooding is worsened by generally low land levels and can be exacerbated if the Webbs Hole sluice is closed, preventing discharge to the River Great Ouse. (p4)
Five residential properties were flooded internally; additional flooding occurred in some roads and gardens. The LLFA received five flood reports and identified three key risk areas within the village. (p7)
Internal flooding of residential properties (internal property flooding reported by LLFA); additional flooding of some roads and gardens. Three key risk areas were identified where internal property flooding was reported: Middle Watch, Ramper Road and Boxworth End. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
The village experienced reported flooding during the December 2020 event; LLFA and RMAs carried out site walkovers, residents known to have flooded were contacted, gully cleansing was ordered and Anglian Water attended to remove foul sewer blockages; discussions opened about highway gully maintenance and sewer network capacity. [TAB] (p11)
The village of Swavesey experienced surface water flooding (noted at Middle Watch, Ramper Road and Boxworth End) and had sewer–related issues requiring removal of blockages and capacity discussions; actions are ongoing to maintain drainage and reduce future flooding. (p15)","5 residential properties; 5 flood reports (p7)
