GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7225877,-1.8948148,"<div id=""html_08783fdbc3153366b4aca5bb903ba29d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wessex Way, Bournemouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooding on Wessex Way during the 18th August 2011 event caused traffic problems and contributed to police diversions; it was cited as a location with operational problems on the day. [TAB] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole","Wessex Way, Bournemouth",England,Road flooding on Wessex Way during the 18th August 2011 event caused traffic problems and contributed to police diversions; it was cited as a location with operational problems on the day. [TAB] (p33),,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7595513,-1.9187862,"<div id=""html_f6c4c647e1e831bfc083b18c68c1277a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nutley Way, Bournemouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Nutley Way was reported to have flooded badly during the 18th August 2011 event (and had also flooded in September 2008), indicating it is a known high&ndash;risk location for road/highway flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole","Nutley Way, Bournemouth",England,"Nutley Way was reported to have flooded badly during the 18th August 2011 event (and had also flooded in September 2008), indicating it is a known high–risk location for road/highway flooding. [TAB,MUL] (p33)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7377165,-1.8193808,"<div id=""html_50c5f105337f1077ac4b8ad06a9d34b9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Meon Road, Bournemouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a flooding 'hot spot' during the 18 August 2011 event; operatives concentrated clean&ndash;up and recovery work there (including BBC and Wessex Water staff). Highway and internal flooding were reported in hotspot areas and clean&ndash;up operations were carried out on site. [TAB] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Gardens was identified as a 'hot spot' during cleanup operations following the event; operatives concentrated work there to clear flood impacts. The combined location (Lower Gardens and Meon Road) was also listed for future remedial schemes. (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole","Meon Road, Bournemouth",England,"Identified as a flooding 'hot spot' during the 18 August 2011 event; operatives concentrated clean–up and recovery work there (including BBC and Wessex Water staff). Highway and internal flooding were reported in hotspot areas and clean–up operations were carried out on site. [TAB] (p33)
Lower Gardens was identified as a 'hot spot' during cleanup operations following the event; operatives concentrated work there to clear flood impacts. The combined location (Lower Gardens and Meon Road) was also listed for future remedial schemes. (p34)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7167307,-1.8770821,"<div id=""html_fe44ed5f4ba8e318dbe9362d8c13d115"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Exeter Road, Bournemouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Closed due to flood water during the event, contributing to traffic disruption (the Pier Approach Flyover and Exeter Road were closed and traffic had to be rerouted). [TAB] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road closure due to flood water during the event, causing traffic to be diverted (example given of traffic being turned around at Priory Hill because Exeter Road and the Pier Approach Flyover were closed). Cleanup and response work were required in the area. [PAG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole","Exeter Road, Bournemouth",England,"Closed due to flood water during the event, contributing to traffic disruption (the Pier Approach Flyover and Exeter Road were closed and traffic had to be rerouted). [TAB] (p33)
Road closure due to flood water during the event, causing traffic to be diverted (example given of traffic being turned around at Priory Hill because Exeter Road and the Pier Approach Flyover were closed). Cleanup and response work were required in the area. [PAG] (p34)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7476133,-1.8275233,"<div id=""html_8efc1e21ccb1e3c32ddd3e46c82a0d18"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cooper Dean Roundabout</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>By about 11:00 a.m. the Cooper Dean Roundabout was affected by flooding as Castle Lane (East and West) was closed, impassable or grid locked, and access to/from Bournemouth Hospital via the roundabout was blocked. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Cooper Dean Roundabout,England,"By about 11:00 a.m. the Cooper Dean Roundabout was affected by flooding as Castle Lane (East and West) was closed, impassable or grid locked, and access to/from Bournemouth Hospital via the roundabout was blocked. (p12)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7201514,-1.8799118,"<div id=""html_819817de620c578421084e0a6a6a133b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bournemouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy surface&ndash;water flooding inundated urban streets and a public park, with water covering road surfaces and reaching up to the top of a park fence. Traffic was disrupted with buses and other vehicles driving through deep water; street furniture and shopfront frontages were partially submerged and debris was floating in the floodwater. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense convective rainfall on 18 August 2011 caused widespread surface water/urban flooding across the borough: significant property damage in localised areas, considerable disruption to the local transport system, and some residents were unable to return to their properties for at least six months while homes dried out. A major clean&ndash;up operation was required after the event. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A significant, highly localised storm centred on the Town Centre produced intense rainfall (epicentre moved N/NE over Winton and Charminster) that caused surface water/urban flooding across Bournemouth; the borough was affected more severely than neighbouring Poole and Weymouth. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread heavy rain produced surface&ndash;water/urban flooding across Bournemouth: roads were gridlocked or impassable (e.g. Wallisdown Road A3049 between Boundary Road and the University; Castle Lane East and West; access to Bournemouth Hospital via the Cooper Dean Roundabout), and emergency services and the council received very large numbers of flood&ndash;related calls. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid&ndash;onset surface water/urban flooding across the town: major roads (e.g. Castle Lane A3060 east and west, Wessex Way A338, Pier Approach Flyover) were closed, impassable or gridlocked (access to/from Bournemouth Hospital via Cooper Dean Roundabout affected). The sewerage system was overwhelmed causing manholes to surcharge foul sewage, manhole covers to be blown off, and road surfaces to lift; highway gullies blocked with debris. A small retaining wall collapsed in Exeter Road. Flooding to properties occurred within about 15 minutes of the heavy rainfall and subsided quickly. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Very localised but intense rainfall on 18th August 2011 caused significant local surface water flooding across Bournemouth; the town's urban nature and topography increased its vulnerability to this type of flooding. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple urban areas within Bournemouth were flooded during the storm; the council identified five significant localised areas for further study and assessed its own properties to identify high&ndash;risk properties for potential protection measures. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The coastal fringe shows clusters of external flood points, indicating the coastline and immediate coastal urban area experienced flooding on 18th August 2011. [IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding on 18th August 2011 concentrated in low&ndash;lying coastal and estuarine areas and along river valleys. Flooded locations clustered near the coast and river mouths, with events scattered across multiple administrative areas. [IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The intense, short&ndash;lived storm on 18 August 2011 caused widespread surface water/urban flooding: lower gardens, Boscombe Chine (emergency access for the Air Show) and other areas were affected; roads were closed (including Copper Dean Roundabout); contaminated public open spaces required fencing off; large clean&ndash;up effort enabled the Air Show to open the next day; Ask Bournemouth received nearly 1,000 calls in a short period. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 18 August 2011 intense surface&ndash;water/urban runoff flooding caused internal flooding of properties across Bournemouth. Council records cited about 250 properties flooded internally during the event; localised flooding also led to road closures (for example Castle Lane West), restricting access to the hospital. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 18 August 2011 the area experienced localised surface&ndash;water/urban flooding that caused internal flooding of domestic properties and disrupted access (Castle Lane West was closed restricting access to the hospital). Local response and cleanup were required. (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>270 reported or observed incidents of flooding; at least 157 properties flooded internally [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 calls to Wessex Water; over 80 incidents logged by Bournemouth BC; about 800 additional calls reported [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>nearly 1000 calls (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>250 properties [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>250 properties flooded internally (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Bournemouth,England,"Heavy surface–water flooding inundated urban streets and a public park, with water covering road surfaces and reaching up to the top of a park fence. Traffic was disrupted with buses and other vehicles driving through deep water; street furniture and shopfront frontages were partially submerged and debris was floating in the floodwater. (p1)
Intense convective rainfall on 18 August 2011 caused widespread surface water/urban flooding across the borough: significant property damage in localised areas, considerable disruption to the local transport system, and some residents were unable to return to their properties for at least six months while homes dried out. A major clean–up operation was required after the event. [PAG] (p3)
A significant, highly localised storm centred on the Town Centre produced intense rainfall (epicentre moved N/NE over Winton and Charminster) that caused surface water/urban flooding across Bournemouth; the borough was affected more severely than neighbouring Poole and Weymouth. (p5)
Widespread heavy rain produced surface–water/urban flooding across Bournemouth: roads were gridlocked or impassable (e.g. Wallisdown Road A3049 between Boundary Road and the University; Castle Lane East and West; access to Bournemouth Hospital via the Cooper Dean Roundabout), and emergency services and the council received very large numbers of flood–related calls. [PAG] (p12)
Rapid–onset surface water/urban flooding across the town: major roads (e.g. Castle Lane A3060 east and west, Wessex Way A338, Pier Approach Flyover) were closed, impassable or gridlocked (access to/from Bournemouth Hospital via Cooper Dean Roundabout affected). The sewerage system was overwhelmed causing manholes to surcharge foul sewage, manhole covers to be blown off, and road surfaces to lift; highway gullies blocked with debris. A small retaining wall collapsed in Exeter Road. Flooding to properties occurred within about 15 minutes of the heavy rainfall and subsided quickly. [PAG] (p13)
Very localised but intense rainfall on 18th August 2011 caused significant local surface water flooding across Bournemouth; the town's urban nature and topography increased its vulnerability to this type of flooding. (p17)
Multiple urban areas within Bournemouth were flooded during the storm; the council identified five significant localised areas for further study and assessed its own properties to identify high–risk properties for potential protection measures. (p18)
The coastal fringe shows clusters of external flood points, indicating the coastline and immediate coastal urban area experienced flooding on 18th August 2011. [IMG] (p26)
Widespread flooding on 18th August 2011 concentrated in low–lying coastal and estuarine areas and along river valleys. Flooded locations clustered near the coast and river mouths, with events scattered across multiple administrative areas. [IMG] (p27)
The intense, short–lived storm on 18 August 2011 caused widespread surface water/urban flooding: lower gardens, Boscombe Chine (emergency access for the Air Show) and other areas were affected; roads were closed (including Copper Dean Roundabout); contaminated public open spaces required fencing off; large clean–up effort enabled the Air Show to open the next day; Ask Bournemouth received nearly 1,000 calls in a short period. (p31)
On 18 August 2011 intense surface–water/urban runoff flooding caused internal flooding of properties across Bournemouth. Council records cited about 250 properties flooded internally during the event; localised flooding also led to road closures (for example Castle Lane West), restricting access to the hospital. [TAB] (p37)
On 18 August 2011 the area experienced localised surface–water/urban flooding that caused internal flooding of domestic properties and disrupted access (Castle Lane West was closed restricting access to the hospital). Local response and cleanup were required. (p38)","270 reported or observed incidents of flooding; at least 157 properties flooded internally [PAG] (p3)
33 calls to Wessex Water; over 80 incidents logged by Bournemouth BC; about 800 additional calls reported [PAG] (p12)
nearly 1000 calls (p31)
250 properties [TAB] (p37)
250 properties flooded internally (p38)",GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.71942676988921,-1.8803193025254896,"<div id=""html_197ccc20d73763a9432329fc95c1eb5e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Town Centre</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The storm was initially focused on the Town Centre on 18 August 2011, producing intense localised surface water flooding that contributed to borough&ndash;wide disruption including impacts on transport. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The storm was centred on the Town Centre and produced the most intense, localised rainfall, triggering surface water/urban flooding in the immediate area. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy, short&ndash;duration rainfall caused flooding in the town centre; many town&ndash;centre roads became impassable or were closed, including parts of the Wessex Way and the Pier Approach Flyover, producing widespread gridlock. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Town Centre,England,"The storm was initially focused on the Town Centre on 18 August 2011, producing intense localised surface water flooding that contributed to borough–wide disruption including impacts on transport. (p3)
The storm was centred on the Town Centre and produced the most intense, localised rainfall, triggering surface water/urban flooding in the immediate area. (p5)
Heavy, short–duration rainfall caused flooding in the town centre; many town–centre roads became impassable or were closed, including parts of the Wessex Way and the Pier Approach Flyover, producing widespread gridlock. (p12)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.716917044392346,-1.9761308319391357,"<div id=""html_185b0c8ed90fb6a2363abae913e57132"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Poole</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred in parts of Poole and Weymouth as reported by the Environment Agency; these areas were affected by flooding on the same day but were not impacted as severely as Bournemouth. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 18 August 2011 intense, localised surface water flooding caused internal flooding of residential and commercial premises in the Poole/Bournemouth area. The event led to road disruption (including the closure of Castle Lane West, restricting access to the hospital though alternative routes existed) and required external assistance and assessment visits by the Environment Agency. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 18 August 2011 the area experienced localised surface&ndash;water/urban flooding that caused internal flooding of domestic properties and disrupted access (Castle Lane West was closed restricting access to the hospital). Local response and cleanup were required. (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>250 properties flooded internally; 2 Environment Agency teams deployed to Poole and Bournemouth; 50&ndash;60 calls to the Environment Agency national call centre from the Bournemouth/Poole area [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>250 properties flooded internally (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Poole,England,"Surface water flooding occurred in parts of Poole and Weymouth as reported by the Environment Agency; these areas were affected by flooding on the same day but were not impacted as severely as Bournemouth. (p5)
On 18 August 2011 intense, localised surface water flooding caused internal flooding of residential and commercial premises in the Poole/Bournemouth area. The event led to road disruption (including the closure of Castle Lane West, restricting access to the hospital though alternative routes existed) and required external assistance and assessment visits by the Environment Agency. [TAB] (p37)
On 18 August 2011 the area experienced localised surface–water/urban flooding that caused internal flooding of domestic properties and disrupted access (Castle Lane West was closed restricting access to the hospital). Local response and cleanup were required. (p38)","250 properties flooded internally; 2 Environment Agency teams deployed to Poole and Bournemouth; 50–60 calls to the Environment Agency national call centre from the Bournemouth/Poole area [TAB] (p37)
250 properties flooded internally (p38)",GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.609627,-2.4543424,"<div id=""html_696a5053e62bf6baf0b3824b64c6329c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Weymouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred in parts of Poole and Weymouth as reported by the Environment Agency; these areas were affected by flooding on the same day but were not impacted as severely as Bournemouth. (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Weymouth,England,Surface water flooding occurred in parts of Poole and Weymouth as reported by the Environment Agency; these areas were affected by flooding on the same day but were not impacted as severely as Bournemouth. (p5),,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7448046,-1.8545975,"<div id=""html_97768fa17d05d82e83b52f106cd10eb1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Queens Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Queens Park flooded during the storm, and the area was identified as one of the five significant localised areas selected for further study because of the number of properties affected (emphasis on residential properties). (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Queens Park,England,"Parts of Queens Park flooded during the storm, and the area was identified as one of the five significant localised areas selected for further study because of the number of properties affected (emphasis on residential properties). (p18)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7589502,-1.8606272,"<div id=""html_4dfb1d0c376a5fe680e43d5ec24ef446"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Muscliff</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A part of Muscliffe, including Castle Lane West, experienced flooding during the storm and was identified as one of the five significant localised areas for further study based on the number of properties affected (residential emphasis). (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Muscliff,England,"A part of Muscliffe, including Castle Lane West, experienced flooding during the storm and was identified as one of the five significant localised areas for further study based on the number of properties affected (residential emphasis). (p18)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7627915,-1.9231484,"<div id=""html_060cbff995b5e50056ea173372faeb83"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Howe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An area of West Howe was identified as one of the localised areas that flooded; residential properties were among those affected by surface water / urban flooding. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",West Howe,England,An area of West Howe was identified as one of the localised areas that flooded; residential properties were among those affected by surface water / urban flooding. (p18),,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.7428875,-1.8780686,"<div id=""html_ac538895caf2323bc46617714141105a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Winton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area of heaviest rainfall moved north/north&ndash;east through parts of Charminster and Winton on 18 August 2011, causing localised surface water flooding that formed part of the wider borough&ndash;wide flooding and associated property damage and transport disruption. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two localised areas in Winton were identified among the five areas that flooded; residential properties were a focus of the impact and potential mitigation. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Winton,England,"The area of heaviest rainfall moved north/north–east through parts of Charminster and Winton on 18 August 2011, causing localised surface water flooding that formed part of the wider borough–wide flooding and associated property damage and transport disruption. (p3)
Two localised areas in Winton were identified among the five areas that flooded; residential properties were a focus of the impact and potential mitigation. (p18)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BCP,3,2011-08-18,50.738358,-1.864345,"<div id=""html_df9412b35e8e8132c0bedbf850f56938"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Charminster</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area of heaviest rainfall moved north/north&ndash;east through parts of Charminster and Winton on 18 August 2011, causing localised surface water flooding that formed part of the wider borough&ndash;wide flooding and associated property damage and transport disruption. (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>","Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole",Charminster,England,"The area of heaviest rainfall moved north/north–east through parts of Charminster and Winton on 18 August 2011, causing localised surface water flooding that formed part of the wider borough–wide flooding and associated property damage and transport disruption. (p3)",,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18.html,GB-BCP_3_2011-08-18,Bournemouth flooding 18 Aug 2011,Flooding in Bournemouth 18th August 2011: Report by 2011 Flooding Task and Finish Group,"011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/011/2011/Bournemouth, Christchurch and Poole/Flooding-in-Bournemouth-18th-August-2011-Flooding-Task-and-Finish-Group-Report-Final.pdf"
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7611558,-1.0593943,"<div id=""html_32be9ed4e77b308148f5093d8313c7d6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ickford School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewage and floodwater were observed emerging from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school, exposing the school frontage to contaminated floodwater during the October 2020 event. [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Ickford School,England,"Sewage and floodwater were observed emerging from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school, exposing the school frontage to contaminated floodwater during the October 2020 event. [PAG,IMG] (p17)",,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7533589,-0.9824202,"<div id=""html_eb5cf5a732eb3c162965a9faacff8635"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thame Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels upstream at Thame Bridge were very high during the event and rising river levels contributed to backing up water into the local watercourse and increasing flood risk to Ickford. (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thame Bridge,England,River levels upstream at Thame Bridge were very high during the event and rising river levels contributed to backing up water into the local watercourse and increasing flood risk to Ickford. (p55),,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7609337,-1.0637598,"<div id=""html_0a6304ee32256f38ee518f4f530e0965"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Road, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected roads at the Church Road / Worminghall Road / Sheldon Road junction; Thames Water operated a tanker pumping from the foul sewer at this junction. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sheldon Road experienced surface water flooding with flood water and sewage emerging from a manhole outside the school (photograph dated October 2020). Church Road is shown/reported with drainage/ditch capacity exceeded (photograph dated January 2021) and is identified in the report as a location at risk and affected by drainage exceedance. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Church Road has been directly affected by flooding: Winter 2001 the backed&ndash;up ditch (linked to high River Thame levels) flooded properties on Church Road; during 02&ndash;04 Oct 2020 the channel/drainage at Church Road exceeded capacity and overtopped onto the road; in January 2021 the drainage ditch overtopped onto Church Road and surface water flowed from the Worminghall Road junction. [PAG,TAB,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage ditch at Church Road overtopped onto the road; surface water flowed down from the Worminghall Road junction due to exceedance of channel capacity. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Capacity of the Church Road roadside ditch was exceeded leading to overland flow; sewage and flood water emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school (photographed October 2020). CCTV and investigations were carried out in the sewer network nearby. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A foul&ndash;sewer monitor at the far west end of Church Road recorded high levels; inspections and further CCTV work were carried out in the network linked to Church Road as part of the sewer&ndash;flooding investigation. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames Water deployed a tanker to pump water from the foul sewer at the Church Road/Worminghall Road/Sheldon Road junction; groundwater entered at least one nearby property (sandbags were supplied) and internal flooding later receded. [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed along Church Road and along Sheldon Road; the highway drainage was overwhelmed in these locations, leading to flooding, ponding and instances of drainage surcharge from gullies and manholes. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewage was observed bubbling out of manholes at Church Road and combining with surface water; Thames Water stationed a tanker at the Church Road junction to remove water from the foul system for an extended period during the October 2020 event. A resident reported a private manhole lifting during heavy rain, indicating flooding ingress to private pipework. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A private manhole at the Church Road junction was observed lifted during heavy rain, allowing surface water and/or sewage to enter and escape the foul network; a tanker operated at the Church Road junction to remove foul sewage for an extended period, disrupting the immediate area. [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Church Road was flooded; the Thames Water pumping station on Church Road was overwhelmed and the foul sewer system surcharged and spilled onto Church Road (and Sheldon Road). At least one property on Church Road was unable to flush downstairs toilets during the event. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The foul sewer in Ickford became overloaded and surcharged during the 02 Oct 2020 &ndash; 04 Oct 2020 flood. A sewer level monitor had been installed at Church Road (the far west end) but was not working during the event. Subsequent CCTV (Spring 2022) found surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface water misconnections entering the foul sewer, all contributing to the surcharging of the foul system. (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer is likely to surcharge on Sheldon Road and Church Road, causing sewer flooding and reducing the effectiveness of surface&ndash;water drainage measures; highway runoff can also overwhelm gullies, increasing local road flooding (explicitly cited for Church Road). (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Channel capacity was exceeded and river water overtopped onto Church Road; floodwaters flowed from the Worminghall Road / Ickford Road junction toward Church Road. The foul and surface water sewer systems along Church Road were overwhelmed during the event. (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Church Road, Ickford",England,"Flooding affected roads at the Church Road / Worminghall Road / Sheldon Road junction; Thames Water operated a tanker pumping from the foul sewer at this junction. (p6)
Sheldon Road experienced surface water flooding with flood water and sewage emerging from a manhole outside the school (photograph dated October 2020). Church Road is shown/reported with drainage/ditch capacity exceeded (photograph dated January 2021) and is identified in the report as a location at risk and affected by drainage exceedance. [PAG] (p23)
Church Road has been directly affected by flooding: Winter 2001 the backed–up ditch (linked to high River Thame levels) flooded properties on Church Road; during 02–04 Oct 2020 the channel/drainage at Church Road exceeded capacity and overtopped onto the road; in January 2021 the drainage ditch overtopped onto Church Road and surface water flowed from the Worminghall Road junction. [PAG,TAB,MUL] (p24)
Drainage ditch at Church Road overtopped onto the road; surface water flowed down from the Worminghall Road junction due to exceedance of channel capacity. (p25)
Capacity of the Church Road roadside ditch was exceeded leading to overland flow; sewage and flood water emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school (photographed October 2020). CCTV and investigations were carried out in the sewer network nearby. [PAG] (p30)
A foul–sewer monitor at the far west end of Church Road recorded high levels; inspections and further CCTV work were carried out in the network linked to Church Road as part of the sewer–flooding investigation. (p31)
Thames Water deployed a tanker to pump water from the foul sewer at the Church Road/Worminghall Road/Sheldon Road junction; groundwater entered at least one nearby property (sandbags were supplied) and internal flooding later receded. [TAB] (p35)
Surface water flowed along Church Road and along Sheldon Road; the highway drainage was overwhelmed in these locations, leading to flooding, ponding and instances of drainage surcharge from gullies and manholes. (p39)
Sewage was observed bubbling out of manholes at Church Road and combining with surface water; Thames Water stationed a tanker at the Church Road junction to remove water from the foul system for an extended period during the October 2020 event. A resident reported a private manhole lifting during heavy rain, indicating flooding ingress to private pipework. (p41)
A private manhole at the Church Road junction was observed lifted during heavy rain, allowing surface water and/or sewage to enter and escape the foul network; a tanker operated at the Church Road junction to remove foul sewage for an extended period, disrupting the immediate area. [PAG] (p42)
Church Road was flooded; the Thames Water pumping station on Church Road was overwhelmed and the foul sewer system surcharged and spilled onto Church Road (and Sheldon Road). At least one property on Church Road was unable to flush downstairs toilets during the event. (p43)
The foul sewer in Ickford became overloaded and surcharged during the 02 Oct 2020 – 04 Oct 2020 flood. A sewer level monitor had been installed at Church Road (the far west end) but was not working during the event. Subsequent CCTV (Spring 2022) found surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface water misconnections entering the foul sewer, all contributing to the surcharging of the foul system. (p46)
Foul sewer is likely to surcharge on Sheldon Road and Church Road, causing sewer flooding and reducing the effectiveness of surface–water drainage measures; highway runoff can also overwhelm gullies, increasing local road flooding (explicitly cited for Church Road). (p48)
Channel capacity was exceeded and river water overtopped onto Church Road; floodwaters flowed from the Worminghall Road / Ickford Road junction toward Church Road. The foul and surface water sewer systems along Church Road were overwhelmed during the event. (p55)","1 property [TAB] (p35)
1 property (p43)",GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7632585,-1.0606173,"<div id=""html_ff3804d8c27d422cc8493496611d212e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Worminghall Road, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected roads at the Church Road / Worminghall Road / Sheldon Road junction; this location was the focus of sewer pumping operations during the event. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Worminghall Road during the October 2020 event (photographs and map annotations show inundation and specific flooding at the junction with Ickford Road), blocking or disrupting the road and contributing to potential isolation of the village. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding observed on Worminghall Road during the October 2020 event. Photographs in the report (October 2020) show standing/tidal surface water on Worminghall Road (including views north towards the Rising Sun) and flooding at the junction with Ickford Road. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding made the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road impassable to regular vehicles. Sheldon Road experienced surface water together with foul/sewer flooding (sewage emerging from manholes) during the October 2020 heavy rainfall event. [PAG,TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road inundation from fluvial overflow and surface water; recorded flooding along Worminghall Road and Golders Close. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Worminghall Road was inundated during the October 2020 event (photographs show flooding looking north toward local landmarks and flooding at the Worminghall Road / Ickford Road junction). Thames Water recorded high sewer levels in the network at Worminghall Road, installed monitoring there as part of an investigation, and later upgraded manhole covers after the October 2020 incident. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A foul&ndash;sewer monitor was installed at Worminghall Road and recorded high sewage levels as part of Thames Water's investigation, indicating sewer flooding/overload in the local network and prompting further inspections. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames Water operated a tanker at the Church Road/Worminghall Road/Sheldon Road junction to remove water from the foul sewer; groundwater entered at least one nearby property (sandbags were provided) and internal floodwater later receded. [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Worminghall Road was flooded by surface water during the October 2020 event, including flooding around the junction with Ickford Road that rendered the junction impassable for regular vehicles. [PAG,IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High groundwater in the floodplain caused groundwater ponding in nearby fields (to the west of Worminghall Road). There is an unconfirmed report that groundwater came up through the floor of a property on Worminghall Road. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down and ponded along Worminghall Road; the lower part flooded (reported to flood before river water reached the northern junction). Highway drainage was overwhelmed and gullies surcharged, producing road flooding and hazardous conditions. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down and ponded along Worminghall Road; the highway drainage system was overwhelmed and water ponded above kerb level. Surface water backed up through the drainage network and surcharged from gullies and manholes, with sewage observed combining with surface water in nearby locations. Fluvial floodwater also reached the junction at the northern end of the road during the event. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sheldon Road was flooded; foul sewer system surcharged and spilled onto Sheldon Road (combined sewer/sewer impacts reported). (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The foul sewer in Ickford became overloaded and surcharged during the 02 Oct 2020 &ndash; 04 Oct 2020 flood. A sewer level monitor had been installed at Worminghall Road but was not working during the event. Subsequent CCTV (Spring 2022) found surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface water misconnections entering the foul sewer, all contributing to the surcharging of the foul system. (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River water can reach and inundate Worminghall Road in larger flood events, overwhelming small&ndash;scale SuDS (e.g. raingardens) and causing highway flooding. (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down Worminghall Road and ponded; the lower part reportedly flooded from surface water before river floodwater reached the junction. Floodwaters from surrounding brooks inundated the Worminghall Road and Ickford Road junction and then flowed south along Worminghall Road toward Church Road. Foul and surface water sewers were overwhelmed in the area with sewage observed bubbling. (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 cm deep [PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20cm deep [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded internally (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Worminghall Road, Ickford",England,"Flooding affected roads at the Church Road / Worminghall Road / Sheldon Road junction; this location was the focus of sewer pumping operations during the event. (p6)
Flooding occurred on Worminghall Road during the October 2020 event (photographs and map annotations show inundation and specific flooding at the junction with Ickford Road), blocking or disrupting the road and contributing to potential isolation of the village. [PAG] (p22)
Surface water flooding observed on Worminghall Road during the October 2020 event. Photographs in the report (October 2020) show standing/tidal surface water on Worminghall Road (including views north towards the Rising Sun) and flooding at the junction with Ickford Road. [PAG] (p23)
Surface water flooding made the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road impassable to regular vehicles. Sheldon Road experienced surface water together with foul/sewer flooding (sewage emerging from manholes) during the October 2020 heavy rainfall event. [PAG,TAB] (p24)
Road inundation from fluvial overflow and surface water; recorded flooding along Worminghall Road and Golders Close. (p25)
Worminghall Road was inundated during the October 2020 event (photographs show flooding looking north toward local landmarks and flooding at the Worminghall Road / Ickford Road junction). Thames Water recorded high sewer levels in the network at Worminghall Road, installed monitoring there as part of an investigation, and later upgraded manhole covers after the October 2020 incident. [PAG] (p30)
A foul–sewer monitor was installed at Worminghall Road and recorded high sewage levels as part of Thames Water's investigation, indicating sewer flooding/overload in the local network and prompting further inspections. (p31)
Thames Water operated a tanker at the Church Road/Worminghall Road/Sheldon Road junction to remove water from the foul sewer; groundwater entered at least one nearby property (sandbags were provided) and internal floodwater later receded. [TAB] (p35)
Worminghall Road was flooded by surface water during the October 2020 event, including flooding around the junction with Ickford Road that rendered the junction impassable for regular vehicles. [PAG,IMG] (p36)
High groundwater in the floodplain caused groundwater ponding in nearby fields (to the west of Worminghall Road). There is an unconfirmed report that groundwater came up through the floor of a property on Worminghall Road. (p37)
Surface water flowed down and ponded along Worminghall Road; the lower part flooded (reported to flood before river water reached the northern junction). Highway drainage was overwhelmed and gullies surcharged, producing road flooding and hazardous conditions. [PAG] (p39)
Surface water flowed down and ponded along Worminghall Road; the highway drainage system was overwhelmed and water ponded above kerb level. Surface water backed up through the drainage network and surcharged from gullies and manholes, with sewage observed combining with surface water in nearby locations. Fluvial floodwater also reached the junction at the northern end of the road during the event. [PAG] (p40)
Sheldon Road was flooded; foul sewer system surcharged and spilled onto Sheldon Road (combined sewer/sewer impacts reported). (p43)
The foul sewer in Ickford became overloaded and surcharged during the 02 Oct 2020 – 04 Oct 2020 flood. A sewer level monitor had been installed at Worminghall Road but was not working during the event. Subsequent CCTV (Spring 2022) found surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface water misconnections entering the foul sewer, all contributing to the surcharging of the foul system. (p46)
River water can reach and inundate Worminghall Road in larger flood events, overwhelming small–scale SuDS (e.g. raingardens) and causing highway flooding. (p48)
Surface water flowed down Worminghall Road and ponded; the lower part reportedly flooded from surface water before river floodwater reached the junction. Floodwaters from surrounding brooks inundated the Worminghall Road and Ickford Road junction and then flowed south along Worminghall Road toward Church Road. Foul and surface water sewers were overwhelmed in the area with sewage observed bubbling. (p55)","1 property [TAB] (p35)
20 cm deep [PAG] (p39)
20cm deep [PAG] (p40)
1 property flooded internally (p55)",GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7601697,-1.0588577,"<div id=""html_f51796a3676c0e9ad1f2b12396b1a64f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sheldon Road, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected roads at the Church Road / Worminghall Road / Sheldon Road junction; Thames Water conducted extended tanker pumping from the foul sewer at this junction. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater and sewage emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road, causing contaminated surface flooding on the road (photo dated October 2020). [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water and sewage were observed emerging from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school (photograph taken during October 2020), indicating combined sewer/sewer surcharge and surface water flooding at this location. [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater and sewage emerged from a manhole outside the school (photo taken October 2020), indicating combined sewer overflow and surface&ndash;water inundation on Sheldon Road that would affect the highway and immediate surroundings. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sheldon Road experienced surface water flooding with flood water and sewage emerging from a manhole outside the school (photograph dated October 2020). Church Road is shown/reported with drainage/ditch capacity exceeded (photograph dated January 2021) and is identified in the report as a location at risk and affected by drainage exceedance. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding made the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road impassable to regular vehicles. Sheldon Road experienced surface water together with foul/sewer flooding (sewage emerging from manholes) during the October 2020 heavy rainfall event. [PAG,TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding along Worminghall Road and Sheldon Road; surface water (and foul) flooding on Sheldon Road with water in places exceeding the kerb height. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Capacity of the Church Road roadside ditch was exceeded leading to overland flow; sewage and flood water emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school (photographed October 2020). CCTV and investigations were carried out in the sewer network nearby. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Additional CCTV inspections identified clear water sources in the foul&ndash;sewer network extending to Sheldon Road, implicating the area in the sewer&ndash;flooding issue. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames Water pumped water from the foul sewer at the junction with Church Road and Worminghall Road; groundwater entered at least one nearby property (sandbags were delivered) and internal water receded later on 04/10/2020. [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During 2&ndash;4 Oct 2020 Sheldon Road experienced surface water flooding and foul/sewer flooding; sewage emerged from a manhole outside the school and surface water affected the road and nearby properties. [PAG,IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High groundwater to the south of Sheldon Road was identified as one of the main sources of flooding; groundwater flooding is thought to have caused one property to flood internally during the event. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed along Church Road and along Sheldon Road; the highway drainage was overwhelmed in these locations, leading to flooding, ponding and instances of drainage surcharge from gullies and manholes. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Both Church Road and Sheldon Road experienced combined sewer and surface water issues in October 2020, with sewage bubbling from manholes and mixing with surface runoff along these roads. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water and foul sewage emerged from a manhole outside the school, causing roadway inundation and standing contaminated water over the carriageway and manhole cover. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sheldon Road was flooded and access was cut off in places; the foul sewer system surcharged and spilled onto Sheldon Road during the event. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>CCTV survey covering the route from Bulls Lane to Sheldon Road identified surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and misconnections entering the foul sewer; these flows are likely contributors to foul&ndash;system surcharging observed during the October 2020 event. (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer is likely to surcharge on Sheldon Road and Church Road, causing sewer flooding and reducing the effectiveness of surface&ndash;water drainage measures; highway runoff can also overwhelm gullies, increasing local road flooding (explicitly cited for Church Road). (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Sheldon Road flooded internally (residents report initial flooding from rising groundwater at ~04:00 on 4 October 2020, receding by ~20:00 the same day). Surface water ponded/flowed along Sheldon Road and foul/surface sewers were overwhelmed with sewage bubbling and combining with floodwater. (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (unable to flush downstairs toilets reported) (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded internally (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Sheldon Road, Ickford",England,"Flooding affected roads at the Church Road / Worminghall Road / Sheldon Road junction; Thames Water conducted extended tanker pumping from the foul sewer at this junction. (p6)
