1 property flooded internally (p55)",GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7588511,-1.0554371,"<div id=""html_06a73a62bca5cfbe24f4330f922c207c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Ickford, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The pond at the junction of Sheldon Road and Little Ickford discharges via a culvert and re&ndash;emerges as an ordinary watercourse to the south of the village before joining the River Thame; during the October 2020 event nearby ordinary watercourses and ditches exceeded capacity, contributing to local surface water flooding and sewer surcharging (Sheldon Road manhole surcharging and other local road/ditch flooding). [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ickford experienced surface water flooding during 2&ndash;4 October 2020 affecting local access routes and streets (including Worminghall Road, Church Road and Sheldon Road) and with local ditches/channels exceeding capacity in places. Little Ickford is shown on groundwater flood mapping as being at high risk, but no specific internal flooding of Little Ickford during the October 2020 event is explicitly recorded in the report. [PAG,IMG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water inundation and local groundwater/ditch ponding caused road flooding (e.g. Worminghall Road and junctions) and capacity exceedance of the Church Road drainage ditch; sewage and floodwater emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school. Thames Water recorded high sewer levels and subsequently installed monitors and replaced manhole covers after the October 2020 incident. [PAG,IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed down the road towards Little Ickford during the October 2020 event, indicating roads/access in the Little Ickford area were affected by pluvial flooding. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Little Ickford and contributed to access being cut off on roads in and out of the village for several days in places. (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Little Ickford, Ickford",England,"The pond at the junction of Sheldon Road and Little Ickford discharges via a culvert and re–emerges as an ordinary watercourse to the south of the village before joining the River Thame; during the October 2020 event nearby ordinary watercourses and ditches exceeded capacity, contributing to local surface water flooding and sewer surcharging (Sheldon Road manhole surcharging and other local road/ditch flooding). [PAG,IMG] (p17)
Ickford experienced surface water flooding during 2–4 October 2020 affecting local access routes and streets (including Worminghall Road, Church Road and Sheldon Road) and with local ditches/channels exceeding capacity in places. Little Ickford is shown on groundwater flood mapping as being at high risk, but no specific internal flooding of Little Ickford during the October 2020 event is explicitly recorded in the report. [PAG,IMG] (p24)
Surface water inundation and local groundwater/ditch ponding caused road flooding (e.g. Worminghall Road and junctions) and capacity exceedance of the Church Road drainage ditch; sewage and floodwater emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school. Thames Water recorded high sewer levels and subsequently installed monitors and replaced manhole covers after the October 2020 incident. [PAG,IMG] (p30)
Surface water runoff flowed down the road towards Little Ickford during the October 2020 event, indicating roads/access in the Little Ickford area were affected by pluvial flooding. (p39)
Flooding occurred at Little Ickford and contributed to access being cut off on roads in and out of the village for several days in places. (p43)",,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.767732,-1.0611828,"<div id=""html_2aba61886ac77e89a537447c7a3f3983"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ickford Road, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the junction with Worminghall Road (photo caption: flooding at the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road) causing inundation of the carriageway and interrupting the main access route into the village. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the junction made the junction impassable for regular vehicles. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding at the junction of Ickford Road and Worminghall Road during 2&ndash;4 Oct 2020 made the junction impassable for regular vehicles. There is potential fluvial contribution from Worminghall/Peppershill Brooks but the exact extent of fluvial flooding at the junction during the October 2020 event is not known. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the Lappingford/Worminghall and Pepperhill Brooks flowed across fields to the junction at Worminghall Road and Ickford Road; some of this water then flowed down Worminghall Road towards the village. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ickford Road was flooded, contributing to routes in and out of the village being cut off for several days in some locations. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from surrounding brooks inundated the Worminghall Road / Ickford Road junction; floodwaters then flowed south along Worminghall Road towards Church Road. The lower part of Worminghall Road flooded from surface water before river floodwater reached the junction. Foul and surface water sewers along the roads were overwhelmed, with sewage observed bubbling from manholes and combining with surface water. (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Ickford Road, Ickford",England,"Flooding occurred at the junction with Worminghall Road (photo caption: flooding at the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road) causing inundation of the carriageway and interrupting the main access route into the village. [PAG] (p22)
Flooding at the junction made the junction impassable for regular vehicles. (p25)
Surface water flooding at the junction of Ickford Road and Worminghall Road during 2–4 Oct 2020 made the junction impassable for regular vehicles. There is potential fluvial contribution from Worminghall/Peppershill Brooks but the exact extent of fluvial flooding at the junction during the October 2020 event is not known. [PAG] (p36)
Floodwater from the Lappingford/Worminghall and Pepperhill Brooks flowed across fields to the junction at Worminghall Road and Ickford Road; some of this water then flowed down Worminghall Road towards the village. [PAG] (p39)
Ickford Road was flooded, contributing to routes in and out of the village being cut off for several days in some locations. (p43)
Floodwater from surrounding brooks inundated the Worminghall Road / Ickford Road junction; floodwaters then flowed south along Worminghall Road towards Church Road. The lower part of Worminghall Road flooded from surface water before river floodwater reached the junction. Foul and surface water sewers along the roads were overwhelmed, with sewage observed bubbling from manholes and combining with surface water. (p55)",,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7616874,-1.06006,"<div id=""html_33fb49a4fce51215c6aab0adb8b3d899"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Golders Close, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road inundation from fluvial overflow and surface water; recorded flooding along Worminghall Road and Golders Close. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed and ponded on Golders Close during the event. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed and ponded along Golders Close during the event, inundating the road and verges and impeding access. [PAG,IMG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Golders Close was flooded, contributing to access and egress being cut off for several days in locations. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ponded on Golders Close during the event. (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Golders Close, Ickford",England,"Road inundation from fluvial overflow and surface water; recorded flooding along Worminghall Road and Golders Close. (p25)
Surface water flowed and ponded on Golders Close during the event. (p39)
Surface water flowed and ponded along Golders Close during the event, inundating the road and verges and impeding access. [PAG,IMG] (p40)
Golders Close was flooded, contributing to access and egress being cut off for several days in locations. (p43)
Surface water ponded on Golders Close during the event. (p55)",,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7535045,-1.0614796,"<div id=""html_1f37d92d80a6d135336bfdb961a77e44"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridge Road, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of a sewer monitor (Bridge Road junction) that recorded high levels; subject to lift&ndash;and&ndash;look inspections and additional CCTV to identify clear water sources in the sewer network. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A monitor at the Bridge Road junction recorded high foul&ndash;sewer levels and lift&ndash;and&ndash;look inspections progressed to additional CCTV along Bridge Road and Bulls Lane to identify clear water ingress points in the sewer network. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridge Street (sic) was flooded, contributing to access and egress to the village being cut off for several days in places. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridge Road (monitor junction and section surveyed) experienced sewer&ndash;system impacts during the Oct 2020 event: a sewer level monitor at the Bridge Road junction was installed but was not working during the event, and later CCTV surveys along Bridge Road identified surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and misconnections entering the foul sewer and contributing to surcharging. [PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer in the Bridge Road area was surcharged during the October 2020 event due to surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface&ndash;water misconnections entering the foul system. A sewer level monitor at the Bridge Road junction had been installed before the event but was not working during the incident, leaving a lack of monitored data for the occurrence. [PAG] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Bridge Road, Ickford",England,"Location of a sewer monitor (Bridge Road junction) that recorded high levels; subject to lift–and–look inspections and additional CCTV to identify clear water sources in the sewer network. [PAG] (p30)
A monitor at the Bridge Road junction recorded high foul–sewer levels and lift–and–look inspections progressed to additional CCTV along Bridge Road and Bulls Lane to identify clear water ingress points in the sewer network. (p31)
Bridge Street (sic) was flooded, contributing to access and egress to the village being cut off for several days in places. (p43)
Bridge Road (monitor junction and section surveyed) experienced sewer–system impacts during the Oct 2020 event: a sewer level monitor at the Bridge Road junction was installed but was not working during the event, and later CCTV surveys along Bridge Road identified surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and misconnections entering the foul sewer and contributing to surcharging. [PAG] (p46)
Foul sewer in the Bridge Road area was surcharged during the October 2020 event due to surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface–water misconnections entering the foul system. A sewer level monitor at the Bridge Road junction had been installed before the event but was not working during the incident, leaving a lack of monitored data for the occurrence. [PAG] (p47)",,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.7591384,-1.0542366,"<div id=""html_e2946c3486d6596b63dd8cfbd7e48d7a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bulls Lane, Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A monitor at the Bridge Road junction recorded high foul&ndash;sewer levels and lift&ndash;and&ndash;look inspections progressed to additional CCTV along Bridge Road and Bulls Lane to identify clear water ingress points in the sewer network. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Post&ndash;event CCTV survey along Bulls Lane (extending to Sheldon Road) identified multiple sources of flows entering the foul sewer&mdash;surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and misconnections&mdash;which are likely to have contributed to surcharging of the foul system in the October 2020 event. (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Bulls Lane, Ickford",England,"A monitor at the Bridge Road junction recorded high foul–sewer levels and lift–and–look inspections progressed to additional CCTV along Bridge Road and Bulls Lane to identify clear water ingress points in the sewer network. (p31)
Post–event CCTV survey along Bulls Lane (extending to Sheldon Road) identified multiple sources of flows entering the foul sewer—surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and misconnections—which are likely to have contributed to surcharging of the foul system in the October 2020 event. (p47)",,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_d100ce6d235733e81f560944b9d7dab5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Buckinghamshire Highways issued sandbags to a property in Ickford as part of the incident response to flooding. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Ickford Road and Worminghall Road flooded in October 2020, rendering the junction impassable to regular vehicles (this junction also flooded in January 2020). [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"Buckinghamshire Highways issued sandbags to a property in Ickford as part of the incident response to flooding. (p6)
The junction of Ickford Road and Worminghall Road flooded in October 2020, rendering the junction impassable to regular vehicles (this junction also flooded in January 2020). [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p36)",1 property (p6),GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02.html,GB-BKM_23_2020-10-02,Ickford flooding 02 Oct 2020 - 04 Oct 2020,Ickford Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Ickford/Flood/Investigation/Oct/2020.pdf
GB-BKM,23,2020-10-02,51.76107251093747,-1.0597228035543595,"<div id=""html_1726b62bd19c6f93c1a600bf4661b3ac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ickford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to at least one property during the October 2020 flood event; repeated flooding of highways/roads causing disruption to access and egress (recorded floods to roads in June 2008, January 2020 and January 2021). [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to at least one property, garden flooding, roads flooded causing problems with access and egress, overwhelmed sewer system and associated mental health impacts on residents. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 02 Oct 2020 &ndash; 04 Oct 2020 event Ickford experienced flooding. Properties suffered internal flooding; groundwater ingress into the foul sewer network contributed to sewer flooding and reduced sewer capacity; surface water and exceedance of channels/highway drainage flooded roads and affected access and egress. These impacts drove recommendations for sewer monitoring/CCTV, a groundwater&ndash;impacted system management plan, property flood resilience, and community flood preparedness. [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Between 02 Oct 2020 and 04 Oct 2020 the village experienced flooding that caused internal flooding to at least one residential property. Several properties experienced external flooding. Highways in the village flooded, causing disruption to access and egress; the village has a recorded history of highway flooding (at least four events in the previous three years). (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the village located on the edge of the River Thame floodplain; properties were flooded and roads were subject to closure during the event. Photographs, observations and flooded property information were collected as part of the event record. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the October 2020 event, surface water and groundwater ponding occurred in and around Ickford; a manhole on Sheldon Road surcharged, with flood water and sewage emerging outside the school; the capacity of the Church Road ditch was exceeded causing overtopping along Church Road; flooding occurred at the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road; waterlogging affected the Worminghall Road development site (42 Worminghall Road). These impacts affected highways, drainage assets (culverts/ditches) and local properties/sites. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced surface water flooding and sewer&ndash;related incidents during the October 2020 event: highway gullies and surface water pipes routed large runoff toward Church Road (low&ndash;lying area) and there were recorded incidents of flooded roads (e.g. Worminghall Road) and sewage emerging from a manhole on Sheldon Road. Thames Water&#8217;s foul sewer system and pumping at Ickford Pumping Station were identified as potential contributors requiring investigation. [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding associated with intense rainfall and runoff that affected low&ndash;lying areas (notably the southern part of the village). Flooding included surface water inundation and failures/overloads within drainage and sewer systems (images and timeline in the report reference flooding in Ickford during October 2020). [PAG,IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Access routes into the village flooded during the event (roads via Worminghall Road/Ickford Road, Sheldon Road/Ickford Road and Bridge Road are shown as flood&ndash;prone), creating risk of the village becoming isolated and affecting movement to/from the settlement. [PAG,IMG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected key access routes into Ickford (Worminghall Road, Church Road, Sheldon Road) during the 2&ndash;4 October 2020 event. Flooding on these roads (including sewage emergence on Sheldon Road) reduced normal access and threatened village connectivity. [PAG,IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During 02 Oct 2020 &ndash; 04 Oct 2020 heavy rainfall caused surface water flooding in and around Ickford: the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road was flooded and made impassable to regular vehicles; surface water and foul flooding occurred along Sheldon Road; the drainage channel at Church Road exceeded capacity and overtopped. [PAG,IMG,TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected parts of the village located on the River Thame floodplain during the event; local ditches and small watercourses contributed to localised inundation of the village edges. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 2&ndash;4 October 2020 led to surface&ndash;water inundation across the village and surcharge of the foul sewer network. Sewage and floodwater were observed emerging from manholes (e.g. Sheldon Road) and highway flooding occurred on roads through the village. Thames Water subsequently installed sewer monitors and later replaced vulnerable manhole covers following the October 2020 incident. [PAG,IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall from 02 Oct 2020 to 04 Oct 2020 caused widespread local flooding in Ickford. Surface water and urban flooding occurred, with Church Road ditch capacity exceeded leading to flooding along Church Road; groundwater ponding was observed in the field west of Worminghall Road; flooding occurred at the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road; Worminghall Road flooded (including areas near the Rising Sun and the Deanfield development site); and sewage and floodwater emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school. At least one property on Church Road installed property flood resilience measures (bunds, pumps and barriers). [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, localised rainfall over the Ickford catchment during 2&ndash;4 Oct 2020 produced a very large rainfall input to the catchment (HYRAD 81 mm over the 58&ndash;hour event; pronounced peak at 20:45 on 3 Oct) and is the meteorological driver for the flood investigated. The rainfall was highly localised and of high intensity (red/pink on HYRAD indicating &gt;32 mm/hr at the time of peak), contributing to rainfall&ndash;driven (surface water/flash) flooding across the catchment. [IMG,TAB] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the October 2&ndash;4, 2020 event, intense rainfall caused surface water flooding across the village and high groundwater was observed in large areas. Localised sewer/foul flooding also occurred in parts of the village (e.g. Sheldon Road). Nuisance/highway flooding is reported regularly in the village. [PAG,IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall together with rising river levels and high groundwater caused flooding across the village and surrounding fields. Groundwater ponding was observed (notably west of Worminghall Road and south of Sheldon Road). Groundwater to the south of Sheldon Road is cited as a main source of internal flooding for one property; there is an unconfirmed report of groundwater entering a property on Worminghall Road. Flooding was largely contained to agricultural land in places but resulted in at least one internally flooded property. [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater (surface&ndash;water and river&ndash;derived) moved into and through the village via roads such as Worminghall Road, causing road flooding, ponding and access impacts within the settlement. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall caused widespread surface water flooding across the village, with highway drainage overwhelmed in multiple locations and surface water ponding on roads (Worminghall Road, Church Road, Sheldon Road). Large areas of the village reported high groundwater levels and groundwater emergence during the October 2020 event. [PAG,IMG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced sewer&ndash;related flooding in October 2020: surcharging foul sewers released sewage that combined with surface water across roads where there is no separate surface water system, leading to localised road and drainage flooding. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 2&ndash;4 Oct 2020 event the foul sewer system in Ickford became overloaded and was surcharging because of multiple inflows (surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and misconnections), contributing to local flooding and sewer performance failure. [PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During 02 Oct 2020 &ndash; 04 Oct 2020 the local foul sewer became overloaded and surcharged. Surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface&ndash;water misconnections were identified as entering the foul sewer and contributing to sewer surcharging. The condition and unknown operating rate of Ickford Pumping Station was identified as linked to local flood risk. Thames Water's pre&ndash;installed sewer level monitors were not working during the event, limiting monitoring of the surcharge. (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) and surface water inundation affected properties and could ingress into foul sewers via private manholes and gullies; access and egress issues for vulnerable residents during the flood; damage/loss to household belongings and property prompted community resilience actions (sandbagging, advice on reducing ingress, development of a Flood Action Group and Flood Plan). (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding in October 2020. The report identifies fluvial flooding in the lower reaches around Ickford influenced by the very flat floodplain and River Thame water levels, and also notes problems with surface water and sewer flooding in the village that would not be mitigated by upstream NFM measures. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial floodwaters surrounded the village during the event, severely restricting access and egress for residents and emergency services; residents also reported foul flooding on Church Lane caused by groundwater ingress into the sewer network. (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced surface water flooding on roads, river flooding from surrounding watercourses and floodplains, and groundwater flooding in alluvial gravels. High water levels and backing up from the River Thame caused channels to exceed capacity and overtop onto roads; fields adjacent to Ickford were inundated. Highways flooding caused disruption to access and egress. (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in Ickford during 02 Oct 2020 &ndash; 04 Oct 2020 included fluvial (main river) and surface water inundation, groundwater ingress into the foul sewer network leading to sewer flooding and reduced sewer capacity, and internal property flooding and issues with access/egress. [TAB,PAG] (p56)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 1 property; at least 4 events in the last 3 years [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 4 events (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least one property on Church Road [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four events reported by residents in the last three years [PAG,IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded internally; at least 4 flooding events in the last three years (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Ickford,England,"Internal flooding to at least one property during the October 2020 flood event; repeated flooding of highways/roads causing disruption to access and egress (recorded floods to roads in June 2008, January 2020 and January 2021). [MUL] (p5)
Internal flooding to at least one property, garden flooding, roads flooded causing problems with access and egress, overwhelmed sewer system and associated mental health impacts on residents. (p6)
During the 02 Oct 2020 – 04 Oct 2020 event Ickford experienced flooding. Properties suffered internal flooding; groundwater ingress into the foul sewer network contributed to sewer flooding and reduced sewer capacity; surface water and exceedance of channels/highway drainage flooded roads and affected access and egress. These impacts drove recommendations for sewer monitoring/CCTV, a groundwater–impacted system management plan, property flood resilience, and community flood preparedness. [TAB] (p7)
Between 02 Oct 2020 and 04 Oct 2020 the village experienced flooding that caused internal flooding to at least one residential property. Several properties experienced external flooding. Highways in the village flooded, causing disruption to access and egress; the village has a recorded history of highway flooding (at least four events in the previous three years). (p13)
Flooding affected the village located on the edge of the River Thame floodplain; properties were flooded and roads were subject to closure during the event. Photographs, observations and flooded property information were collected as part of the event record. (p14)
During the October 2020 event, surface water and groundwater ponding occurred in and around Ickford; a manhole on Sheldon Road surcharged, with flood water and sewage emerging outside the school; the capacity of the Church Road ditch was exceeded causing overtopping along Church Road; flooding occurred at the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road; waterlogging affected the Worminghall Road development site (42 Worminghall Road). These impacts affected highways, drainage assets (culverts/ditches) and local properties/sites. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p17)
The village experienced surface water flooding and sewer–related incidents during the October 2020 event: highway gullies and surface water pipes routed large runoff toward Church Road (low–lying area) and there were recorded incidents of flooded roads (e.g. Worminghall Road) and sewage emerging from a manhole on Sheldon Road. Thames Water’s foul sewer system and pumping at Ickford Pumping Station were identified as potential contributors requiring investigation. [PAG,IMG] (p20)
The village experienced flooding associated with intense rainfall and runoff that affected low–lying areas (notably the southern part of the village). Flooding included surface water inundation and failures/overloads within drainage and sewer systems (images and timeline in the report reference flooding in Ickford during October 2020). [PAG,IMG] (p21)
Access routes into the village flooded during the event (roads via Worminghall Road/Ickford Road, Sheldon Road/Ickford Road and Bridge Road are shown as flood–prone), creating risk of the village becoming isolated and affecting movement to/from the settlement. [PAG,IMG] (p22)
Surface water flooding affected key access routes into Ickford (Worminghall Road, Church Road, Sheldon Road) during the 2–4 October 2020 event. Flooding on these roads (including sewage emergence on Sheldon Road) reduced normal access and threatened village connectivity. [PAG,IMG] (p23)
During 02 Oct 2020 – 04 Oct 2020 heavy rainfall caused surface water flooding in and around Ickford: the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road was flooded and made impassable to regular vehicles; surface water and foul flooding occurred along Sheldon Road; the drainage channel at Church Road exceeded capacity and overtopped. [PAG,IMG,TAB] (p24)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected parts of the village located on the River Thame floodplain during the event; local ditches and small watercourses contributed to localised inundation of the village edges. (p29)
Intense rainfall on 2–4 October 2020 led to surface–water inundation across the village and surcharge of the foul sewer network. Sewage and floodwater were observed emerging from manholes (e.g. Sheldon Road) and highway flooding occurred on roads through the village. Thames Water subsequently installed sewer monitors and later replaced vulnerable manhole covers following the October 2020 incident. [PAG,IMG] (p30)
Intense rainfall from 02 Oct 2020 to 04 Oct 2020 caused widespread local flooding in Ickford. Surface water and urban flooding occurred, with Church Road ditch capacity exceeded leading to flooding along Church Road; groundwater ponding was observed in the field west of Worminghall Road; flooding occurred at the junction of Worminghall Road and Ickford Road; Worminghall Road flooded (including areas near the Rising Sun and the Deanfield development site); and sewage and floodwater emerged from a manhole on Sheldon Road outside the school. At least one property on Church Road installed property flood resilience measures (bunds, pumps and barriers). [PAG] (p32)
Intense, localised rainfall over the Ickford catchment during 2–4 Oct 2020 produced a very large rainfall input to the catchment (HYRAD 81 mm over the 58–hour event; pronounced peak at 20:45 on 3 Oct) and is the meteorological driver for the flood investigated. The rainfall was highly localised and of high intensity (red/pink on HYRAD indicating >32 mm/hr at the time of peak), contributing to rainfall–driven (surface water/flash) flooding across the catchment. [IMG,TAB] (p33)
During the October 2–4, 2020 event, intense rainfall caused surface water flooding across the village and high groundwater was observed in large areas. Localised sewer/foul flooding also occurred in parts of the village (e.g. Sheldon Road). Nuisance/highway flooding is reported regularly in the village. [PAG,IMG] (p36)
Intense rainfall together with rising river levels and high groundwater caused flooding across the village and surrounding fields. Groundwater ponding was observed (notably west of Worminghall Road and south of Sheldon Road). Groundwater to the south of Sheldon Road is cited as a main source of internal flooding for one property; there is an unconfirmed report of groundwater entering a property on Worminghall Road. Flooding was largely contained to agricultural land in places but resulted in at least one internally flooded property. [PAG] (p37)
Floodwater (surface–water and river–derived) moved into and through the village via roads such as Worminghall Road, causing road flooding, ponding and access impacts within the settlement. [PAG] (p39)
Intense rainfall caused widespread surface water flooding across the village, with highway drainage overwhelmed in multiple locations and surface water ponding on roads (Worminghall Road, Church Road, Sheldon Road). Large areas of the village reported high groundwater levels and groundwater emergence during the October 2020 event. [PAG,IMG] (p40)
The village experienced sewer–related flooding in October 2020: surcharging foul sewers released sewage that combined with surface water across roads where there is no separate surface water system, leading to localised road and drainage flooding. (p41)
During the 2–4 Oct 2020 event the foul sewer system in Ickford became overloaded and was surcharging because of multiple inflows (surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and misconnections), contributing to local flooding and sewer performance failure. [PAG] (p46)
During 02 Oct 2020 – 04 Oct 2020 the local foul sewer became overloaded and surcharged. Surface water inundation, groundwater infiltration and surface–water misconnections were identified as entering the foul sewer and contributing to sewer surcharging. The condition and unknown operating rate of Ickford Pumping Station was identified as linked to local flood risk. Thames Water's pre–installed sewer level monitors were not working during the event, limiting monitoring of the surcharge. (p47)
Fluvial (river) and surface water inundation affected properties and could ingress into foul sewers via private manholes and gullies; access and egress issues for vulnerable residents during the flood; damage/loss to household belongings and property prompted community resilience actions (sandbagging, advice on reducing ingress, development of a Flood Action Group and Flood Plan). (p49)
The village experienced flooding in October 2020. The report identifies fluvial flooding in the lower reaches around Ickford influenced by the very flat floodplain and River Thame water levels, and also notes problems with surface water and sewer flooding in the village that would not be mitigated by upstream NFM measures. (p52)
Fluvial floodwaters surrounded the village during the event, severely restricting access and egress for residents and emergency services; residents also reported foul flooding on Church Lane caused by groundwater ingress into the sewer network. (p53)
The village experienced surface water flooding on roads, river flooding from surrounding watercourses and floodplains, and groundwater flooding in alluvial gravels. High water levels and backing up from the River Thame caused channels to exceed capacity and overtop onto roads; fields adjacent to Ickford were inundated. Highways flooding caused disruption to access and egress. (p55)
Flooding in Ickford during 02 Oct 2020 – 04 Oct 2020 included fluvial (main river) and surface water inundation, groundwater ingress into the foul sewer network leading to sewer flooding and reduced sewer capacity, and internal property flooding and issues with access/egress. [TAB,PAG] (p56)","at least 1 property; at least 4 events in the last 3 years [MUL] (p5)
