GB-HEF,23,2020-02-16,52.1860199,-2.7861744,"<div id=""html_45301e15143f1ac586b3337dc1220e96"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Honeylake Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a likely primary fluvial source for properties in/adjacent to Flood Zone 3 in Ivington that experienced internal flooding during the February 2020 event. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding occurred in Honeylake Brook due to backwater effects from the River Lugg; this produced flood risk to nearby properties (report recommends Property Level Resilience measures). (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 1 property also flooded in 2019 (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Honeylake Brook,England,"Identified as a likely primary fluvial source for properties in/adjacent to Flood Zone 3 in Ivington that experienced internal flooding during the February 2020 event. (p2)
Fluvial flooding occurred in Honeylake Brook due to backwater effects from the River Lugg; this produced flood risk to nearby properties (report recommends Property Level Resilience measures). (p3)",4 properties; 1 property also flooded in 2019 (p2),GB-HEF_23_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_23_2020-02-16,Ivington flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Ivington,055/Herefordshire County/22/Ivington.pdf
GB-HEF,23,2020-02-16,52.2355219,-2.7449348,"<div id=""html_2fd1f6f2426ce485cb1c35fdb0166b3b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Lugg</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Backwater effects from the River Lugg caused fluvial flooding in the Honeylake Brook. (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,River Lugg,England,Backwater effects from the River Lugg caused fluvial flooding in the Honeylake Brook. (p3),,GB-HEF_23_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_23_2020-02-16,Ivington flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Ivington,055/Herefordshire County/22/Ivington.pdf
GB-HEF,23,2020-02-16,52.2056106,-2.7700457,"<div id=""html_3d248653820857161ad78714b05a3811"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ivington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Village area adjacent to the river corridor is shown as being within or adjacent to Flood Zone 3 on the Environment Agency planning map, indicating exposure to river (fluvial) flooding. The mapping and report context link the settlement to the key flood event for the area. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding recorded in the village during February 2020: four properties flooded internally; one respondent reported internal depths of 6 inches to 1 ft lasting less than one day. Ivington C of E Primary School was recorded as impacted during the event. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 1 property also flooded in 2019 [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Ivington,England,"Village area adjacent to the river corridor is shown as being within or adjacent to Flood Zone 3 on the Environment Agency planning map, indicating exposure to river (fluvial) flooding. The mapping and report context link the settlement to the key flood event for the area. [IMG] (p1)
Internal flooding recorded in the village during February 2020: four properties flooded internally; one respondent reported internal depths of 6 inches to 1 ft lasting less than one day. Ivington C of E Primary School was recorded as impacted during the event. [TAB] (p2)",4 properties; 1 property also flooded in 2019 [TAB] (p2),GB-HEF_23_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_23_2020-02-16,Ivington flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Ivington,055/Herefordshire County/22/Ivington.pdf
GB-NNH,3,2016-03-09,52.4812301,-0.4894626,"<div id=""html_4a52faef2aec6e7e873dd667b63555ae"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lyveden Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lyveden Brook was implicated in the event: ordinary watercourse flooding from the brook contributed to flooding of residential areas near its confluence with the River Nene. Upstream of Stoke Doyle Road a severe narrowing at a large tree, a length of barbed wire across the channel and a build&ndash;up of debris were observed, restricting flow and likely exacerbating flooding. Parts of the western developed area drain to public sewers that discharge to the brook, increasing flow into the channel during the event. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On the morning of 9th March 2016 the depth of water in the brook increased by approximately two metres above usual, causing the banks to be overtopped and water to flow across rear gardens of properties on the north and east edge of the Warren Bridge residential estate. Water levels in gardens and the brook subsided in the late afternoon after the rain stopped. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels in the brook rose during the event and contributed to higher garden and upstream water depths. Debris (barbed wire) trapped upstream reduced conveyance and caused the channel to become deeper upstream; the narrow, twisting channel downstream limited flow away to the River Nene, contributing to raised water levels. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Shown with evidence of high water levels; produced overbank and overland flow routes that contributed to schematic flood areas and inundation of nearby rear gardens and properties. Narrowing of the channel and a barbed&ndash;wire obstruction were identified as localised pinch points that increased water levels and localised flooding. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A barbed&ndash;wire fence crossing the brook trapped debris after high flows, creating an obstruction that impeded water flow and contributed to flooding of adjacent banks and nearby gardens/properties. [IMG] (p46)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 70 properties flooded internally; over 200 reports of flooding (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two metres deeper than usual (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Lyveden Brook,England,"Lyveden Brook was implicated in the event: ordinary watercourse flooding from the brook contributed to flooding of residential areas near its confluence with the River Nene. Upstream of Stoke Doyle Road a severe narrowing at a large tree, a length of barbed wire across the channel and a build–up of debris were observed, restricting flow and likely exacerbating flooding. Parts of the western developed area drain to public sewers that discharge to the brook, increasing flow into the channel during the event. (p10)
On the morning of 9th March 2016 the depth of water in the brook increased by approximately two metres above usual, causing the banks to be overtopped and water to flow across rear gardens of properties on the north and east edge of the Warren Bridge residential estate. Water levels in gardens and the brook subsided in the late afternoon after the rain stopped. (p13)
Water levels in the brook rose during the event and contributed to higher garden and upstream water depths. Debris (barbed wire) trapped upstream reduced conveyance and caused the channel to become deeper upstream; the narrow, twisting channel downstream limited flow away to the River Nene, contributing to raised water levels. (p14)
Shown with evidence of high water levels; produced overbank and overland flow routes that contributed to schematic flood areas and inundation of nearby rear gardens and properties. Narrowing of the channel and a barbed–wire obstruction were identified as localised pinch points that increased water levels and localised flooding. [IMG] (p33)
A barbed–wire fence crossing the brook trapped debris after high flows, creating an obstruction that impeded water flow and contributed to flooding of adjacent banks and nearby gardens/properties. [IMG] (p46)","over 70 properties flooded internally; over 200 reports of flooding (p10)
two metres deeper than usual (p13)",GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09,Oundle flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22328/FIR-Oundle/Rev03/161116.pdf
GB-NNH,3,2016-03-09,52.4812271,-0.5088718,"<div id=""html_3a4baa6ee4b0be82f99d5eefbf0aaecf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Biggin Fish Pond</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marked as a key location and part of the main overland water&ndash;flow routes during the flood incident, acting as a local pond/collection point contributing to downstream overland flow. [IMG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Biggin Fish Pond,England,"Marked as a key location and part of the main overland water–flow routes during the flood incident, acting as a local pond/collection point contributing to downstream overland flow. [IMG] (p33)",,GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09,Oundle flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22328/FIR-Oundle/Rev03/161116.pdf
GB-NNH,3,2016-03-09,52.4802145,-0.4788803,"<div id=""html_142279930405f8a0a96d57b16bdb8b8f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Warren Bridge, Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property at Warren Bridge due to heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment; ordinary watercourses could not convey flows and excess surface water flowed to low points around private property, entering the building. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property at Warren Bridge; the property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years, triggering a formal investigation. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the overtopped brook affected properties on Warren Bridge on 9th March 2016: 1st property had garden flooded with water coming to within approximately one metre (75mm height difference) from the garage floor; 2nd property had garden flooded, 75&ndash;100mm depth of water in an adjoining garage, seepage of water into the house and water flowed into airbricks; 3rd property experienced flooding of an adjoining garage; 4th property had garden flooded with no impact on the house. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused ordinary watercourses to be unable to convey flows, producing excess surface water that flowed over ground to low points and caused flooding at Warren Bridge. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear garden of a private property was heavily inundated; muddy brown floodwater covered most of the garden and partially submerged a garden bench, potted plants and other garden features, indicating significant local flooding of the property. [IMG] (p45)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 75&ndash;100mm depth in garage; 75mm height difference (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Warren Bridge, Oundle",England,"Internal flooding of a property at Warren Bridge due to heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment; ordinary watercourses could not convey flows and excess surface water flowed to low points around private property, entering the building. (p7)
Internal flooding of a property at Warren Bridge; the property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years, triggering a formal investigation. [MUL] (p9)
Flooding from the overtopped brook affected properties on Warren Bridge on 9th March 2016: 1st property had garden flooded with water coming to within approximately one metre (75mm height difference) from the garage floor; 2nd property had garden flooded, 75–100mm depth of water in an adjoining garage, seepage of water into the house and water flowed into airbricks; 3rd property experienced flooding of an adjoining garage; 4th property had garden flooded with no impact on the house. (p13)
Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused ordinary watercourses to be unable to convey flows, producing excess surface water that flowed over ground to low points and caused flooding at Warren Bridge. (p16)
