4 properties; 75–100mm depth in garage; 75mm height difference (p13)",GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09,Oundle flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22328/FIR-Oundle/Rev03/161116.pdf
GB-NNH,3,2016-03-09,52.4756564,-0.4875469,"<div id=""html_af996eb7bc1c02fc84978e5d5253539f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stoke Doyle Road, Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A severe narrowing of the watercourse and a build&ndash;up of debris (including a section of barbed wire fencing across the watercourse) was observed upstream of Stoke Doyle Road; these obstructions were noted during flood investigations and can contribute to ordinary watercourse flooding. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood&ndash;investigation observations recorded a severe narrowing of Lyveden Brook immediately upstream of Stoke Doyle Road at a large tree, together with a section of barbed&ndash;wire fencing across the channel and a build&ndash;up of debris; downstream of the road the channel is narrow, shallow and meandering. These channel obstructions and constrictions were noted during flood investigations and are relevant to river flooding in the vicinity of Stoke Doyle Road. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Garden flood to a level 25 mm below the threshold of the dwelling; water flowed into airbricks. An industrial pump was used all day to remove water; resident reported that without pumping the house would have flooded internally. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property garden flooded to a depth 25 mm below the internal threshold; floodwater flowed into airbricks. An industrial pump was used all day to remove water; resident reported that without pumping the house would have flooded internally. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert/bridge under Stoke Doyle Road was at full capacity with water reportedly reaching the soffit, causing water to become deeper upstream and flow over the road east of the culvert/bridge. A property on Stoke Doyle Road had flood water in its garden that reached 25 mm below the threshold level, flowed into airbricks; an industrial pump was used all day to remove water and the resident reported the house would have flooded internally without it. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A narrow, twisting watercourse downstream of Stoke Doyle Road constrained flow toward the River Nene during the 9 March 2016 event, preventing water from flowing away quickly and contributing to local drainage/flooding issues. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bridge/culvert on Stoke Doyle Road was the subject of initial investigations into its type and capacity because of concerns about its ability to convey flood flows during the event. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A bridge/culvert at Stoke Doyle Road (star 05) is shown interacting with flow; the crossing/culvert is identified as a critical location where constriction could cause localised flooding of the carriageway and adjacent areas. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 mm below threshold level (garden) on 09/03/16; industrial pump used all day. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 25 mm below threshold level [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 mm below threshold level [PAG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Stoke Doyle Road, Oundle",England,"A severe narrowing of the watercourse and a build–up of debris (including a section of barbed wire fencing across the watercourse) was observed upstream of Stoke Doyle Road; these obstructions were noted during flood investigations and can contribute to ordinary watercourse flooding. [PAG] (p10)
Flood–investigation observations recorded a severe narrowing of Lyveden Brook immediately upstream of Stoke Doyle Road at a large tree, together with a section of barbed–wire fencing across the channel and a build–up of debris; downstream of the road the channel is narrow, shallow and meandering. These channel obstructions and constrictions were noted during flood investigations and are relevant to river flooding in the vicinity of Stoke Doyle Road. (p11)
Garden flood to a level 25 mm below the threshold of the dwelling; water flowed into airbricks. An industrial pump was used all day to remove water; resident reported that without pumping the house would have flooded internally. [TAB] (p12)
Property garden flooded to a depth 25 mm below the internal threshold; floodwater flowed into airbricks. An industrial pump was used all day to remove water; resident reported that without pumping the house would have flooded internally. [PAG] (p13)
The culvert/bridge under Stoke Doyle Road was at full capacity with water reportedly reaching the soffit, causing water to become deeper upstream and flow over the road east of the culvert/bridge. A property on Stoke Doyle Road had flood water in its garden that reached 25 mm below the threshold level, flowed into airbricks; an industrial pump was used all day to remove water and the resident reported the house would have flooded internally without it. [PAG] (p14)
A narrow, twisting watercourse downstream of Stoke Doyle Road constrained flow toward the River Nene during the 9 March 2016 event, preventing water from flowing away quickly and contributing to local drainage/flooding issues. [PAG] (p15)
The bridge/culvert on Stoke Doyle Road was the subject of initial investigations into its type and capacity because of concerns about its ability to convey flood flows during the event. (p16)
A bridge/culvert at Stoke Doyle Road (star 05) is shown interacting with flow; the crossing/culvert is identified as a critical location where constriction could cause localised flooding of the carriageway and adjacent areas. [IMG] (p33)","25 mm below threshold level (garden) on 09/03/16; industrial pump used all day. [TAB] (p12)
1 property; 25 mm below threshold level [PAG] (p13)
25 mm below threshold level [PAG] (p14)",GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_3_2016-03-09,Oundle flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22328/FIR-Oundle/Rev03/161116.pdf
GB-NNH,3,2016-03-09,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_02abd1c6992632fcd9453572102fe039"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wide&ndash;area impacts from heavy rainfall on 9 March 2016 across Northamptonshire: ordinary watercourse flooding, surface water runoff and agricultural runoff led to flows entering low points and properties, resulting in internal flooding and access/service disruption in the county. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding on 9 March 2016 produced internal flooding and numerous reports across the county due to a combination of agricultural runoff, surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse flooding. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 9th March 2016 the county experienced widespread flooding from a combination of agricultural runoff, surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse (river) flooding. Over 70 properties were reported flooded internally and there were over 200 reports of flooding across the county. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 70 properties; over 200 reports of flooding (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 70 properties; over 200 reports of flooding [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 70 properties flooded internally; over 200 reports of flooding [PAG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"Wide–area impacts from heavy rainfall on 9 March 2016 across Northamptonshire: ordinary watercourse flooding, surface water runoff and agricultural runoff led to flows entering low points and properties, resulting in internal flooding and access/service disruption in the county. (p7)
