three locations [TAB,PAG] (p15)",GB-DOR_5_2016-09-16.html,GB-DOR_5_2016-09-16,Wimborne Minster flooding 16 Sep 2016,Flood Investigation Report Wimborne Town Centre Storm of 16th September 2016,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/Sept 2016 Wimborne Town Centre Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-NNH,5,2016-03-09,52.382199299999996,-0.7081929963418551,"<div id=""html_96bc1fb2027381b23c9c47258e9bba7c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wicksteed Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Where the River Ise passes into Wicksteed Park two partially fallen trees were recorded across the river, creating a damming effect that would have contributed to higher upstream river levels; deposited flood arisings were evident on adjacent banks. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 partially fallen trees (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Wicksteed Park,England,"Where the River Ise passes into Wicksteed Park two partially fallen trees were recorded across the river, creating a damming effect that would have contributed to higher upstream river levels; deposited flood arisings were evident on adjacent banks. (p12)",2 partially fallen trees (p12),GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09,Kettering flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Community Building, Ise Lodge, Kettering, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22333/FIR/Ise-Lodge/Kettering/Rev-03/161116.pdf
GB-NNH,5,2016-03-09,52.433157,-0.7574993,"<div id=""html_992e230038c41facb12d05247b6e51d2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Ise</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ise, the main drainage conduit flowing adjacent to the community building, was associated with fluvial flooding that affected the nearby community building (the building is located in Flood Zone 3). Surface water public sewers from neighbouring residential estates discharge into the River Ise at five outfall locations to the north of the flooded building, forming part of the flood pathway. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Ise flood water flowed around the front and rear of the community building at Ise Lodge, ponding at the property (external sports facilities were completely submerged) and entering the rear store where water reached the rear walls; deposited arisings were evident on adjacent banks. Partially fallen trees in the channel likely produced a damming effect and contributed to raised upstream levels. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ise received high volumes of water from the saturated catchment, overwhelmed its channel in the vicinity of Ise Lodge and flooded onto the adjacent floodplain. Fallen trees downstream and restrictions at Barton Road bridge were noted as possible contributors to raised upstream water levels. Large amounts of deposited arisings were evident on the adjacent banks. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater in the River Ise carried and deposited debris along the channel and banks; flow interacted with in&ndash;channel obstructions (trees) leading to accumulation of debris downstream. [PAG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Downstream reach of the River Ise experienced flooding that deposited debris on banks and in drainage features. Flood flows were impeded by a second tree obstruction on the main channel, exacerbating local high water levels. Floodwater also accumulated into a downstream storm water outlet/pond located below a flooded building and collected around a concrete culvert fitted with a metal grate, indicating overstressing of local drainage infrastructure. (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Obstructions (fallen trees / debris) were present on the downstream reaches of the River Ise and contributed to blocked flow that led to flooding of nearby property; the river downstream is explicitly cited in the report pages describing flood impacts. [PAG] (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 outfall locations [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 300mm ponded depth (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,River Ise,England,"The River Ise, the main drainage conduit flowing adjacent to the community building, was associated with fluvial flooding that affected the nearby community building (the building is located in Flood Zone 3). Surface water public sewers from neighbouring residential estates discharge into the River Ise at five outfall locations to the north of the flooded building, forming part of the flood pathway. [PAG] (p10)
River Ise flood water flowed around the front and rear of the community building at Ise Lodge, ponding at the property (external sports facilities were completely submerged) and entering the rear store where water reached the rear walls; deposited arisings were evident on adjacent banks. Partially fallen trees in the channel likely produced a damming effect and contributed to raised upstream levels. (p12)
The River Ise received high volumes of water from the saturated catchment, overwhelmed its channel in the vicinity of Ise Lodge and flooded onto the adjacent floodplain. Fallen trees downstream and restrictions at Barton Road bridge were noted as possible contributors to raised upstream water levels. Large amounts of deposited arisings were evident on the adjacent banks. [PAG] (p13)
Floodwater in the River Ise carried and deposited debris along the channel and banks; flow interacted with in–channel obstructions (trees) leading to accumulation of debris downstream. [PAG] (p51)
Downstream reach of the River Ise experienced flooding that deposited debris on banks and in drainage features. Flood flows were impeded by a second tree obstruction on the main channel, exacerbating local high water levels. Floodwater also accumulated into a downstream storm water outlet/pond located below a flooded building and collected around a concrete culvert fitted with a metal grate, indicating overstressing of local drainage infrastructure. (p53)
Obstructions (fallen trees / debris) were present on the downstream reaches of the River Ise and contributed to blocked flow that led to flooding of nearby property; the river downstream is explicitly cited in the report pages describing flood impacts. [PAG] (p54)","5 outfall locations [PAG] (p10)
up to 300mm ponded depth (p12)",GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09,Kettering flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Community Building, Ise Lodge, Kettering, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22333/FIR/Ise-Lodge/Kettering/Rev-03/161116.pdf
GB-NNH,5,2016-03-09,52.3902387,-0.7036172,"<div id=""html_6108f2259e49a9a5964a89fb3241259d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Durness Close, Ise Lodge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface runoff was reported from Durness Close and flowed across the adjacent car parking area; this runoff may have contributed to increased flood volumes affecting the nearby River Ise and nearby properties. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported surface water runoff from Durness Close may have contributed to the volume of water present around the Ise Lodge community building during the river flooding event. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Durness Close, Ise Lodge",England,"Surface runoff was reported from Durness Close and flowed across the adjacent car parking area; this runoff may have contributed to increased flood volumes affecting the nearby River Ise and nearby properties. (p12)
Reported surface water runoff from Durness Close may have contributed to the volume of water present around the Ise Lodge community building during the river flooding event. (p13)",,GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09,Kettering flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Community Building, Ise Lodge, Kettering, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22333/FIR/Ise-Lodge/Kettering/Rev-03/161116.pdf
GB-NNH,5,2016-03-09,52.3896718,-0.7034314,"<div id=""html_9cd6514aeca10166cf1c62d12eadd91c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grantown Close, Ise Lodge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface runoff was reported from Grantown Close and flowed across the adjacent car parking area; this runoff may have contributed to increased flood volumes affecting the nearby River Ise and nearby properties. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported surface water runoff occurred from Grantown Close, contributing additional runoff to the area around the community building at Ise Lodge. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Grantown Close, Ise Lodge",England,"Surface runoff was reported from Grantown Close and flowed across the adjacent car parking area; this runoff may have contributed to increased flood volumes affecting the nearby River Ise and nearby properties. (p12)
