Flooding occurred at the community building at Ise Lodge in Kettering when the River Ise overflowed onto the adjacent floodplain following heavy rainfall over a saturated catchment. (p13)",1 property (p7),GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_5_2016-03-09,Kettering flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Community Building, Ise Lodge, Kettering, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22333/FIR/Ise-Lodge/Kettering/Rev-03/161116.pdf
GB-HEF,21,2020-02-16,52.3021846,-2.7397881,"<div id=""html_642a614e94fe1b8fea816b6290147ee5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kings Road, Orleton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and blocked gullies caused surface&ndash;water flooding on the road; respondents recorded problems with surface water on King's Road during the flood event. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,"Kings Road, Orleton",England,Surface water runoff and blocked gullies caused surface–water flooding on the road; respondents recorded problems with surface water on King's Road during the flood event. (p2),,GB-HEF_21_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_21_2020-02-16,Orleton flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Orleton,055/Herefordshire County/20/Orleton.pdf
GB-HEF,21,2020-02-16,52.2998812,-2.7510955,"<div id=""html_bf90665dd409b1c9740058fcbce08a37"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Millbrook Way, Orleton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from Orleton Brook broke out of its channel and emerged onto the highway, draining along Millbrook Way as an ephemeral watercourse; respondents recorded surface&ndash;water flooding on Millbrook Way during the event. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,"Millbrook Way, Orleton",England,"Floodwater from Orleton Brook broke out of its channel and emerged onto the highway, draining along Millbrook Way as an ephemeral watercourse; respondents recorded surface–water flooding on Millbrook Way during the event. (p2)",,GB-HEF_21_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_21_2020-02-16,Orleton flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Orleton,055/Herefordshire County/20/Orleton.pdf
GB-HEF,21,2020-02-16,52.299060394317,-2.7478081076398513,"<div id=""html_1342e2462c6ea94f22d7b651c63abb57"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Orleton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding reported to residential properties on the west side of the village and one further downstream; internal flood depths ranged from less than 6 inches up to 1 ft, with duration typically less than one day. [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple domestic properties experienced internal flooding (depths reported from under 6 inches up to 1 ft) with typical durations under 1 day. Surface&ndash;water flooding occurred on roads (notably B4362, King's Road and Millbrook Way). A basement at a property on the B4362 flooded for 6&ndash;7 days. Some flooded properties lie within or adjacent to Flood Zone 3, indicating fluvial influence; respondents also reported surface&ndash;water runoff and blocked gullies as sources. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 1 property [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties on the west side internally flooded; a further downstream property; 6 internally flooded properties related to the key flood event; 8 internally flooded during the most acute flood; 1 basement flooded (6&ndash;7 days) (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Orleton,England,"Internal flooding reported to residential properties on the west side of the village and one further downstream; internal flood depths ranged from less than 6 inches up to 1 ft, with duration typically less than one day. [IMG,PAG] (p1)
Multiple domestic properties experienced internal flooding (depths reported from under 6 inches up to 1 ft) with typical durations under 1 day. Surface–water flooding occurred on roads (notably B4362, King's Road and Millbrook Way). A basement at a property on the B4362 flooded for 6–7 days. Some flooded properties lie within or adjacent to Flood Zone 3, indicating fluvial influence; respondents also reported surface–water runoff and blocked gullies as sources. (p2)","4 properties; 1 property [IMG,PAG] (p1)
4 properties on the west side internally flooded; a further downstream property; 6 internally flooded properties related to the key flood event; 8 internally flooded during the most acute flood; 1 basement flooded (6–7 days) (p2)",GB-HEF_21_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_21_2020-02-16,Orleton flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Orleton,055/Herefordshire County/20/Orleton.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.94125,-1.4712486,"<div id=""html_1b022bd53087df4c93fd34c285d58ce9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Derby Rugby Club</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters from the overtopping River Derwent / Folly Brook inundated the rugby club grounds. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 8 November 2019 the access road, adjacent fences and playing fields at the Derby Rugby Football Grounds were inundated and rendered inaccessible; road surface and surrounding field areas were submerged, indicating significant disruption to site access and use. Nearby Haslams Lane experienced flood depths in excess of 1 m and its flood gate was closed at approximately 14:00 on 8 November and reopened the following morning. [PAG,IMG] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths in excess of 1 m along Haslams Lane [PAG,IMG] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Derby Rugby Club,England,"Floodwaters from the overtopping River Derwent / Folly Brook inundated the rugby club grounds. (p22)
On 8 November 2019 the access road, adjacent fences and playing fields at the Derby Rugby Football Grounds were inundated and rendered inaccessible; road surface and surrounding field areas were submerged, indicating significant disruption to site access and use. Nearby Haslams Lane experienced flood depths in excess of 1 m and its flood gate was closed at approximately 14:00 on 8 November and reopened the following morning. [PAG,IMG] (p24)","flood depths in excess of 1 m along Haslams Lane [PAG,IMG] (p24)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9371959,-1.4743197,"<div id=""html_b91b1a1b7bd6ab4c0468c8859f3cf727"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Darley Fields</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Darley Fields, an area identified as active floodplain and not protected by defences, was inundated as flood flows spread from the River Derwent / Folly Brook. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Darley Fields,England,"Darley Fields, an area identified as active floodplain and not protected by defences, was inundated as flood flows spread from the River Derwent / Folly Brook. (p22)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.94355125,-1.4747185395431328,"<div id=""html_e231c3f7d165afce1fe2af5cc046e5a7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Darley Abbey Mills</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Site flood defences were not overtopped but experienced seepage; fluvial water seeping through defences (and groundwater seeping up through floors) caused internal flooding in commercial premises and disrupted business access. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded flood outlines surround Abbey Mills; although defences were not overtopped, seepage through defences and through property floors caused internal flooding of commercial premises and business disruption due to loss of access. [PAG,IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seepage through the flood defences occurred at Darley Abbey Mills during the 8&ndash;9 November flood. (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 1 commercial property internally flooded; internal flood depth approximately 150 mm (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property; internal flooding up to 150 mm [PAG,IMG] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Darley Abbey Mills,England,"Site flood defences were not overtopped but experienced seepage; fluvial water seeping through defences (and groundwater seeping up through floors) caused internal flooding in commercial premises and disrupted business access. (p25)
Recorded flood outlines surround Abbey Mills; although defences were not overtopped, seepage through defences and through property floors caused internal flooding of commercial premises and business disruption due to loss of access. [PAG,IMG] (p26)
Seepage through the flood defences occurred at Darley Abbey Mills during the 8–9 November flood. (p43)","at least 1 commercial property internally flooded; internal flood depth approximately 150 mm (p25)
1 commercial property; internal flooding up to 150 mm [PAG,IMG] (p26)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.92419,-1.4719355,"<div id=""html_d573e0aacf890a5a48074d48a477d7de"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Exeter House</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood bank located in front of Exeter House was overtopped as river levels rose; overtopping contributed to flood water flowing onto Exeter Place and Meadow Road. Derby City Council deployed sandbags along the top of the flood bank and at the junction with the flood wall to contain flood waters and prevent further overtopping. [PAG,IMG] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Exeter House,England,"The flood bank located in front of Exeter House was overtopped as river levels rose; overtopping contributed to flood water flowing onto Exeter Place and Meadow Road. Derby City Council deployed sandbags along the top of the flood bank and at the junction with the flood wall to contain flood waters and prevent further overtopping. [PAG,IMG] (p32)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.925380303065204,-1.4681276674454808,"<div id=""html_701098084aa997f3e09a1a7722afe70e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cardinal House</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The combined drainage system for Cardinal House has a hydraulic connection into the surface water system; when the surface water system backed up due to high river levels the combined system also backed up. Gullies in Cardinal House flooded as a result, with recorded flood outlines matching the area's surface&ndash;water flood risk. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Backing up of the connected surface water and combined drainage caused water to flood out of the yard gully into the below&ndash;ground Western Power sub&ndash;station at Cardinal House. Flood water rose internally within the sub&ndash;station and caused failure of switchgear; the circuit failed at 21:56 on 8 Nov leading to widespread blackouts across Derby. Power restoration was completed in stages with the final stage at 23:25 on 8 Nov; restoration was delayed by flooded/closed roads elsewhere. [PAG,IMG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the yard gully led to water entering the underground sub&ndash;station and caused switchgear failure; owners subsequently installed a non&ndash;return valve on the yard gully and a pump inside the sub&ndash;station, and Western Power raised the switchgear level. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected the area around Cardinal House due to backing up of fluvial flows into the surface water drainage network, prompting consideration of non&ndash;return valves/pumping to allow discharge of flows. [TAB] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>greater than 130 mm (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths greater than 130mm [PAG,IMG] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Cardinal House,England,"The combined drainage system for Cardinal House has a hydraulic connection into the surface water system; when the surface water system backed up due to high river levels the combined system also backed up. Gullies in Cardinal House flooded as a result, with recorded flood outlines matching the area's surface–water flood risk. (p36)
Backing up of the connected surface water and combined drainage caused water to flood out of the yard gully into the below–ground Western Power sub–station at Cardinal House. Flood water rose internally within the sub–station and caused failure of switchgear; the circuit failed at 21:56 on 8 Nov leading to widespread blackouts across Derby. Power restoration was completed in stages with the final stage at 23:25 on 8 Nov; restoration was delayed by flooded/closed roads elsewhere. [PAG,IMG] (p37)
Flooding of the yard gully led to water entering the underground sub–station and caused switchgear failure; owners subsequently installed a non–return valve on the yard gully and a pump inside the sub–station, and Western Power raised the switchgear level. (p38)
Surface water flooding affected the area around Cardinal House due to backing up of fluvial flows into the surface water drainage network, prompting consideration of non–return valves/pumping to allow discharge of flows. [TAB] (p44)","greater than 130 mm (p36)
flood depths greater than 130mm [PAG,IMG] (p37)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9529937,-1.4489864,"<div id=""html_6a088de9fd2c9b09ce3b07564022c009"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dam Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dam Brook, an ordinary watercourse in Derby, was part of the local drainage network associated with the flood event of 08 Nov 2019 &ndash; 09 Nov 2019 and is reported alongside other ordinary watercourses and privately owned ditches that were affected. It likely contributed to localised runoff/overland flow and drainage connectivity during the event (some privately owned ditches connecting into the brook were affected). There is insufficient detail to state precise overtopping locations or property&ndash;level inundation. (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Dam Brook,England,"Dam Brook, an ordinary watercourse in Derby, was part of the local drainage network associated with the flood event of 08 Nov 2019 – 09 Nov 2019 and is reported alongside other ordinary watercourses and privately owned ditches that were affected. It likely contributed to localised runoff/overland flow and drainage connectivity during the event (some privately owned ditches connecting into the brook were affected). There is insufficient detail to state precise overtopping locations or property–level inundation. (p9)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9212617,-1.4761491,"<div id=""html_2fecf706bec564892a4929aa5f99cd69"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding at multiple commercial properties and one residential property near the city border; disruption to critical infrastructure including closure of main roads, failure of a pumping station, and flooding of an electricity sub&ndash;station resulting in blackouts. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred across the city within twelve investigation sites (many located adjacent to the River Derwent), affecting urban areas and infrastructure and prompting this Section 19 investigation. [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected multiple sites across the city during the investigated event; the majority of the twelve sites considered are located close to the River Derwent and its tributary/ordinary watercourse network. Privately owned ditches connecting to the river were reported as being affected during the flood event. [PAG,IMG,TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood events occurred in Derby on 8 November 2019 driven by high river flows from the River Derwent following intense rainfall over the river's headwaters. [PAG,IMG,TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Derwent peak flows propagated downstream to Derby. The River Derwent at St Mary's Bridge recorded a record high peak flow during the event, producing very high river levels that were associated with the flood incident in Derby on 8 November 2019. [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 8&ndash;9 November 2019 fluvial flooding from the River Derwent propagated downstream into Derby. Exceptionally high river levels overtopped and/or caused seepage through some flood defences, produced groundwater flooding in topographically low areas, and led to the sewer network backing up/surcharging from river water ingress to outfalls. These combined mechanisms produced the recorded flooding across the city; DCiC mapped flood outlines and managed response. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on the A61 at Pektron Island (within the Derby urban area) caused significant highway inundation and required response from Derby City Council. [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Derby (central area) experienced river&ndash;driven flooding impacts during the event: seepage through partially constructed/unsealed sheet pile flood defences at the River Derwent bank led to water being pumped from the construction site back to the river; pump failure allowed floodwater to flow onto adjacent streets (notably City Road) where sandbags were deployed to protect properties. [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread impacts across the city including flooded and closed roads that delayed power restoration and blackouts caused by flood&ndash;induced substation failure. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the surface water drainage system caused backing&ndash;up at a Western Power underground sub&ndash;station yard gully, resulting in internal flooding of the sub&ndash;station, failure of the switchgear and a circuit failure at 21:56 on 8 Nov that produced blackouts across large areas of Derby. Power was restored in stages, with the final stage completed at 23:25 on 8 Nov; restoration was delayed in places due to flooded/closed roads. [IMG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties across the city; significant flooding on several main highways causing road closures; disruption to the electricity supply network causing a blackout in Derby. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Derby experienced flooding during 8&ndash;9 November 2019 from the River Derwent and from surface water/runoff. Multiple locations were affected: Haslams Lane acted as an active floodplain, Sowter Road functioned as an exceedance channel, and City Road flooded due to incomplete flood defences. Surface water network connectivity and outfalls (including into the River Derwent) contributed to local inundation. [PAG] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 sites; one residential property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>highest peak on record across an 85&ndash;year period; estimated 75&ndash;95 year return period (ESS analysis); OCOR review estimated 60&ndash;95 year return period (report range 50&ndash;150 years) [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>water depths of approximately 500mm [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>depths up to 50mm; two pumps used (4&#8220; and 6""), 6&#8221; pump failed [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 7 commercial properties; one residential property; five locations (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Derby,England,"Internal flooding at multiple commercial properties and one residential property near the city border; disruption to critical infrastructure including closure of main roads, failure of a pumping station, and flooding of an electricity sub–station resulting in blackouts. (p7)
Flooding occurred across the city within twelve investigation sites (many located adjacent to the River Derwent), affecting urban areas and infrastructure and prompting this Section 19 investigation. [IMG,PAG] (p9)
Flooding affected multiple sites across the city during the investigated event; the majority of the twelve sites considered are located close to the River Derwent and its tributary/ordinary watercourse network. Privately owned ditches connecting to the river were reported as being affected during the flood event. [PAG,IMG,TAB] (p10)
Flood events occurred in Derby on 8 November 2019 driven by high river flows from the River Derwent following intense rainfall over the river's headwaters. [PAG,IMG,TAB] (p16)
River Derwent peak flows propagated downstream to Derby. The River Derwent at St Mary's Bridge recorded a record high peak flow during the event, producing very high river levels that were associated with the flood incident in Derby on 8 November 2019. [PAG,IMG] (p17)
On 8–9 November 2019 fluvial flooding from the River Derwent propagated downstream into Derby. Exceptionally high river levels overtopped and/or caused seepage through some flood defences, produced groundwater flooding in topographically low areas, and led to the sewer network backing up/surcharging from river water ingress to outfalls. These combined mechanisms produced the recorded flooding across the city; DCiC mapped flood outlines and managed response. [PAG] (p20)
Flooding on the A61 at Pektron Island (within the Derby urban area) caused significant highway inundation and required response from Derby City Council. [IMG,PAG] (p21)
Parts of Derby (central area) experienced river–driven flooding impacts during the event: seepage through partially constructed/unsealed sheet pile flood defences at the River Derwent bank led to water being pumped from the construction site back to the river; pump failure allowed floodwater to flow onto adjacent streets (notably City Road) where sandbags were deployed to protect properties. [PAG,IMG] (p28)
Widespread impacts across the city including flooded and closed roads that delayed power restoration and blackouts caused by flood–induced substation failure. (p38)
Flooding of the surface water drainage system caused backing–up at a Western Power underground sub–station yard gully, resulting in internal flooding of the sub–station, failure of the switchgear and a circuit failure at 21:56 on 8 Nov that produced blackouts across large areas of Derby. Power was restored in stages, with the final stage completed at 23:25 on 8 Nov; restoration was delayed in places due to flooded/closed roads. [IMG] (p39)
Internal flooding of properties across the city; significant flooding on several main highways causing road closures; disruption to the electricity supply network causing a blackout in Derby. (p43)
Derby experienced flooding during 8–9 November 2019 from the River Derwent and from surface water/runoff. Multiple locations were affected: Haslams Lane acted as an active floodplain, Sowter Road functioned as an exceedance channel, and City Road flooded due to incomplete flood defences. Surface water network connectivity and outfalls (including into the River Derwent) contributed to local inundation. [PAG] (p44)","12 sites; one residential property (p7)
highest peak on record across an 85–year period; estimated 75–95 year return period (ESS analysis); OCOR review estimated 60–95 year return period (report range 50–150 years) [PAG] (p20)
water depths of approximately 500mm [IMG,PAG] (p21)
depths up to 50mm; two pumps used (4“ and 6""), 6” pump failed [PAG,IMG] (p28)
approximately 7 commercial properties; one residential property; five locations (p43)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9199308,-1.4518518,"<div id=""html_0b695a49e20a8ec03de17fefa523cfb6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chaddesden Sidings</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water drainage outfall behind Chaddesden Sidings and the railway underpass were identified as affected during the 8&ndash;9 November 2019 event. Actions recommended include investigating a non&ndash;return valve or pumping station on the surface water outfall, reinstating a flood defence bund around the railway underpass as a temporary measure, and investigating a former culvert beneath the railway. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from multiple sources inundated Chaddesden Sidings. The River Derwent overtopped its banks and flooded the flood plain south of the railway; an underpass allowed that floodwater into Chaddesden Sidings. Additional sources included failure of the Downing Road pumping station and the STW surface water sewer backing up. The A52 and Chequers Road were flooded and closed, and commercial properties along Chequers Road experienced internal flooding. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Chaddesden Sidings experienced flooding from multiple sources: fluvial flooding from the River Derwent overtopping the banks and flowing through an underpass, flooding through a missing/eroded flood defence bund, and backflow from the surface water sewer and railway culvert. Flooding affected the A52 and Chequers Road and caused internal flooding to commercial properties (Chequers Road) up to 1.5 m. [PAG,IMG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred north of the railway when high river levels caused flows to back up through a previously unknown railway culvert connected into the surface water drainage and the STW surface water sewer; water flowed back from the river into the culvert and flooded into Chaddesden Sidings. Foul manholes in the area are known to surcharge and foul water flooding is believed to have occurred. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood defences at Chaddesden Siding were overtopped during the 8&ndash;9 November flood event. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred as fluvial flows backed up into the surface water network behind Chaddesden Sidings, causing discharge blockage and inundation; this led to recommended investigation of the railway culvert function and the potential reinstatement of a flood&ndash;defence bund around the underpass. [TAB] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths up to 1.5 m; A52 closed from 21:45 on 08 Nov 2019 (reopened 09 Nov 2019); Chequers Road closed at 07:41 on 09 Nov 2019 (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding depths up to 1.5 m to commercial properties (Chequers Road area) [PAG,IMG] (p39)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Chaddesden Sidings,England,"Surface water drainage outfall behind Chaddesden Sidings and the railway underpass were identified as affected during the 8–9 November 2019 event. Actions recommended include investigating a non–return valve or pumping station on the surface water outfall, reinstating a flood defence bund around the railway underpass as a temporary measure, and investigating a former culvert beneath the railway. (p7)
Flooding from multiple sources inundated Chaddesden Sidings. The River Derwent overtopped its banks and flooded the flood plain south of the railway; an underpass allowed that floodwater into Chaddesden Sidings. Additional sources included failure of the Downing Road pumping station and the STW surface water sewer backing up. The A52 and Chequers Road were flooded and closed, and commercial properties along Chequers Road experienced internal flooding. (p38)
Chaddesden Sidings experienced flooding from multiple sources: fluvial flooding from the River Derwent overtopping the banks and flowing through an underpass, flooding through a missing/eroded flood defence bund, and backflow from the surface water sewer and railway culvert. Flooding affected the A52 and Chequers Road and caused internal flooding to commercial properties (Chequers Road) up to 1.5 m. [PAG,IMG] (p39)
Flooding occurred north of the railway when high river levels caused flows to back up through a previously unknown railway culvert connected into the surface water drainage and the STW surface water sewer; water flowed back from the river into the culvert and flooded into Chaddesden Sidings. Foul manholes in the area are known to surcharge and foul water flooding is believed to have occurred. (p40)
Flood defences at Chaddesden Siding were overtopped during the 8–9 November flood event. (p43)
Surface water flooding occurred as fluvial flows backed up into the surface water network behind Chaddesden Sidings, causing discharge blockage and inundation; this led to recommended investigation of the railway culvert function and the potential reinstatement of a flood–defence bund around the underpass. [TAB] (p44)","flood depths up to 1.5 m; A52 closed from 21:45 on 08 Nov 2019 (reopened 09 Nov 2019); Chequers Road closed at 07:41 on 09 Nov 2019 (p38)
internal flooding depths up to 1.5 m to commercial properties (Chequers Road area) [PAG,IMG] (p39)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,53.1395212,-1.5590102,"<div id=""html_a9a07f344466a4b7bc40745be406a0db"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Derwent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy, persistent rainfall over the Derwent headwaters on 6&ndash;7 Nov 2019 produced high river response in the River Derwent, which contributed to flood events affecting downstream areas including Derby on 8 November 2019. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall over the Derwent catchment generated a strong river response that propagated downstream and contributed to the high river flows observed at downstream gauges and into Derby. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river flows in the River Derwent and its tributary (Folly Brook) overtopped banks. Fluvial water entered a low&ndash;gradient, poorly maintained ditch and then the highway drainage outfall (which lacked a non&ndash;return valve), allowing river water to backflow into gullies and emerge at the A61 Pektron Island roundabout causing highway flooding. Flood flows from the overtopping also followed Folly Brook onto Haslams Lane, then onto the Rugby Club grounds and Darley Fields. The Pektron Island area was submerged and the roundabout required temporary closure. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river flows caused the River Derwent to overtop its banks; fluvial water entered a nearby low&ndash;fall, poorly maintained ditch, forcing ditch levels above the highway drainage outfall and driving floodwater into the highway drainage network. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the River Derwent is implicated in seepage through flood defences around Darley Abbey Mills and is represented by the recorded flood outline that follows the river, contributing to internal flooding and groundwater emergence at nearby properties. [PAG,IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels led to seepage through unsealed joints in partially constructed sheet pile flood defences on the riverbank (behind Old Tomlinson Yard). The seepage required pumping of water from the construction site back to the River Derwent and contributed to surface water reaching City Road when a pump failed. [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Derwent overtopped its banks (south of Causey Bridge) and sent floodwaters into exceedance channels and across adjacent urban areas, contributing to flows that reached Sowter Road and collected in the Sowter Road underpass. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rising levels in the River Derwent caused overtopping of riverbanks and seepage through the riverside flood wall. These failure modes produced substantial flooding in the Exeter Place / Meadow Road area (seepage and overtopping), contributed to flooding along Meadow Lane (overtopping near the footbridge), and prompted local mitigation (sandbags, penstock operation elsewhere). [PAG,IMG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Derwent overtopped its banks at the footbridge, constricting flows and causing fluvial flooding onto Meadow Lane. Overtopping flooded the road and led to internal flooding of nearby commercial properties and the evacuation of one commercial property. [PAG,IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Derwent overtopped its banks (starting by the footbridge, which acted as a constriction), causing fluvial flooding of the Meadow Lane industrial area (flooding of the road and internal flooding of commercial properties). High river levels also caused the surface water sewer to back up, which led to manhole/gully flooding and inundation of highway locations away from the river (including Pentagon Island and Eastgate gullies). [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Derwent overtopped its banks during the event, flooding the floodplain to the south of the railway tracks. Overtopping and high river levels contributed to flood water flowing into Chaddesden Sidings (via an underpass and via backing up into connected drainage features). [PAG,IMG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Derwent overtopped its banks and flooded the floodplain south of the railway, allowing water to flow into Chaddesden Sidings via an underpass (the protective flood bund was absent/eroded). High river levels also caused river flows to back up into a railway culvert and the connected surface water sewer, contributing to flooding north of the railway. Elevated river levels prevented outfalls (including a flap&ndash;valved ditch outfall) from discharging: the ditch filled and overtopped, flooding an adjacent commercial car park, and a separate surface water outfall was prevented from discharging causing pluvial flows to back up and flood one commercial property on Raynesway. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Elevated river levels in the River Derwent during the event prevented connected outfalls (flap valves) and surface water systems from discharging. This led to ditch filling and overtopping into an adjacent car park, backflow along surface water pipes causing internal flooding of a commercial property, and contributed to flows backing up through a railway culvert and surface water sewer into areas north of the railway (contributing to flooding and pump station inundation reported by STW). [PAG,IMG] (p41)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20&ndash;25 year return period event recorded at Derwent Reservoir (upper catchment) [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>500 mm water depth at Pektron Roundabout; Pektron Island underwater by 21:45 on 8 Nov 2019 [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>depths up to 50mm (reported within the construction site and along City Road); two pumps used (4&#8220; and 6""), 6&#8221; pump failure recorded [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths exceeding 2 m on Meadow Road; seepage&ndash;related flooding in Darwin Place up to 50 mm; approx. 150&ndash;200 mm flood depths near the bridge on Meadow Lane; four commercial properties internally flooded (Meadow Lane) [PAG,IMG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four commercial properties; one commercial property evacuated; 150mm to 200mm flood depth near the bridge (by 14:30 on 8 Nov 2019) [PAG,IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths of approximately 150mm to 200mm near the bridge by 14:30 on 08 Nov 2019; internal flooding at four commercial properties; one commercial property evacuated [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding depths up to 1.5 m (commercial properties on Chequers Road); car park flooding to approx 300 mm [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximate maximum depth of 300mm (in adjacent car park); one commercial property internally flooded; one pumping station became flooded [PAG,IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,River Derwent,England,"Heavy, persistent rainfall over the Derwent headwaters on 6–7 Nov 2019 produced high river response in the River Derwent, which contributed to flood events affecting downstream areas including Derby on 8 November 2019. [PAG] (p16)
Heavy rainfall over the Derwent catchment generated a strong river response that propagated downstream and contributed to the high river flows observed at downstream gauges and into Derby. [PAG] (p17)
High river flows in the River Derwent and its tributary (Folly Brook) overtopped banks. Fluvial water entered a low–gradient, poorly maintained ditch and then the highway drainage outfall (which lacked a non–return valve), allowing river water to backflow into gullies and emerge at the A61 Pektron Island roundabout causing highway flooding. Flood flows from the overtopping also followed Folly Brook onto Haslams Lane, then onto the Rugby Club grounds and Darley Fields. The Pektron Island area was submerged and the roundabout required temporary closure. [PAG] (p21)
High river flows caused the River Derwent to overtop its banks; fluvial water entered a nearby low–fall, poorly maintained ditch, forcing ditch levels above the highway drainage outfall and driving floodwater into the highway drainage network. [PAG] (p22)
Fluvial flooding from the River Derwent is implicated in seepage through flood defences around Darley Abbey Mills and is represented by the recorded flood outline that follows the river, contributing to internal flooding and groundwater emergence at nearby properties. [PAG,IMG] (p26)
High river levels led to seepage through unsealed joints in partially constructed sheet pile flood defences on the riverbank (behind Old Tomlinson Yard). The seepage required pumping of water from the construction site back to the River Derwent and contributed to surface water reaching City Road when a pump failed. [PAG,IMG] (p28)
The River Derwent overtopped its banks (south of Causey Bridge) and sent floodwaters into exceedance channels and across adjacent urban areas, contributing to flows that reached Sowter Road and collected in the Sowter Road underpass. [IMG] (p29)
Rising levels in the River Derwent caused overtopping of riverbanks and seepage through the riverside flood wall. These failure modes produced substantial flooding in the Exeter Place / Meadow Road area (seepage and overtopping), contributed to flooding along Meadow Lane (overtopping near the footbridge), and prompted local mitigation (sandbags, penstock operation elsewhere). [PAG,IMG] (p32)
The River Derwent overtopped its banks at the footbridge, constricting flows and causing fluvial flooding onto Meadow Lane. Overtopping flooded the road and led to internal flooding of nearby commercial properties and the evacuation of one commercial property. [PAG,IMG] (p34)
The River Derwent overtopped its banks (starting by the footbridge, which acted as a constriction), causing fluvial flooding of the Meadow Lane industrial area (flooding of the road and internal flooding of commercial properties). High river levels also caused the surface water sewer to back up, which led to manhole/gully flooding and inundation of highway locations away from the river (including Pentagon Island and Eastgate gullies). [PAG] (p35)
The River Derwent overtopped its banks during the event, flooding the floodplain to the south of the railway tracks. Overtopping and high river levels contributed to flood water flowing into Chaddesden Sidings (via an underpass and via backing up into connected drainage features). [PAG,IMG] (p39)
The River Derwent overtopped its banks and flooded the floodplain south of the railway, allowing water to flow into Chaddesden Sidings via an underpass (the protective flood bund was absent/eroded). High river levels also caused river flows to back up into a railway culvert and the connected surface water sewer, contributing to flooding north of the railway. Elevated river levels prevented outfalls (including a flap–valved ditch outfall) from discharging: the ditch filled and overtopped, flooding an adjacent commercial car park, and a separate surface water outfall was prevented from discharging causing pluvial flows to back up and flood one commercial property on Raynesway. [PAG] (p40)
Elevated river levels in the River Derwent during the event prevented connected outfalls (flap valves) and surface water systems from discharging. This led to ditch filling and overtopping into an adjacent car park, backflow along surface water pipes causing internal flooding of a commercial property, and contributed to flows backing up through a railway culvert and surface water sewer into areas north of the railway (contributing to flooding and pump station inundation reported by STW). [PAG,IMG] (p41)","20–25 year return period event recorded at Derwent Reservoir (upper catchment) [PAG] (p17)
500 mm water depth at Pektron Roundabout; Pektron Island underwater by 21:45 on 8 Nov 2019 [PAG] (p21)
depths up to 50mm (reported within the construction site and along City Road); two pumps used (4“ and 6""), 6” pump failure recorded [PAG,IMG] (p28)
flood depths exceeding 2 m on Meadow Road; seepage–related flooding in Darwin Place up to 50 mm; approx. 150–200 mm flood depths near the bridge on Meadow Lane; four commercial properties internally flooded (Meadow Lane) [PAG,IMG] (p32)
four commercial properties; one commercial property evacuated; 150mm to 200mm flood depth near the bridge (by 14:30 on 8 Nov 2019) [PAG,IMG] (p34)
flood depths of approximately 150mm to 200mm near the bridge by 14:30 on 08 Nov 2019; internal flooding at four commercial properties; one commercial property evacuated [PAG] (p35)
internal flooding depths up to 1.5 m (commercial properties on Chequers Road); car park flooding to approx 300 mm [PAG] (p40)
approximate maximum depth of 300mm (in adjacent car park); one commercial property internally flooded; one pumping station became flooded [PAG,IMG] (p41)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.94819680667783,-1.4595766423594412,"<div id=""html_30d605b9b6fa7746b8c043b56524c5dd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pektron Roundabout</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pektron Roundabout was significantly flooded when fluvial water re&ndash;entered the highway drainage from an adjacent ditch (which lacks a non&ndash;return valve), causing gullies to discharge and the roundabout to be closed. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding related to local watercourses affected the A61 at/near Pektron Roundabout during the 8&ndash;9 Nov 2019 event; recommended follow&ndash;up actions include investigation of watercourse connections and inspection/maintenance of flood defences due to observed issues. [TAB] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>water depths of approximately 500mm (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Pektron Roundabout,England,"The Pektron Roundabout was significantly flooded when fluvial water re–entered the highway drainage from an adjacent ditch (which lacks a non–return valve), causing gullies to discharge and the roundabout to be closed. (p21)
Flooding related to local watercourses affected the A61 at/near Pektron Roundabout during the 8–9 Nov 2019 event; recommended follow–up actions include investigation of watercourse connections and inspection/maintenance of flood defences due to observed issues. [TAB] (p44)",water depths of approximately 500mm (p21),GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9272181,-1.475871,"<div id=""html_dc48ea98af59888c29078ca81d92ae3c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Mary's Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Marys Bridge acted as a constriction on river flow during the event; exceedance channel routing and overtopping were used to bypass the constriction so flows could re&ndash;enter the channel south of the bridge, altering local flow paths and contributing to water collecting in the underpass. [PAG,IMG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,St Mary's Bridge,England,"St Marys Bridge acted as a constriction on river flow during the event; exceedance channel routing and overtopping were used to bypass the constriction so flows could re–enter the channel south of the bridge, altering local flow paths and contributing to water collecting in the underpass. [PAG,IMG] (p30)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9266659,-1.4752046,"<div id=""html_63b79ac3706a8f51c0818c0ee1486821"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Causey Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The exceedance channel south of Causey Bridge conveyed flood water during the event (photographed at 13:14 on 8 Nov 2019), showing active flow in the channel toward the bridge as part of the flood routing. [PAG,IMG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Causey Bridge,England,"The exceedance channel south of Causey Bridge conveyed flood water during the event (photographed at 13:14 on 8 Nov 2019), showing active flow in the channel toward the bridge as part of the flood routing. [PAG,IMG] (p30)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9248702,-1.4616072,"<div id=""html_69b13ca0ba61fe7b17758269f1c90606"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pentagon Island</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Back&ndash;up of the surface water drainage system caused highway drainage manholes and gullies to flood at low spots, inundating the southern section of Pentagon Island. The A52 slip road (Nottingham&ndash;bound to St Alkmunds Way) was closed from 23:20 on 8 Nov and reopened at 04:26 on 9 Nov after the water had cleared. [PAG,IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roundabout/roadway closed due to flooding (A52 slip road Nottingham&ndash;bound to St Alkmunds Way). Closed at 23:20 on 08/11/2019 and reopened once clear of water at 04:26 on 09/11/2019. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred when fluvial flows backed up into the surface water drainage network, overwhelming the drainage outfalls and causing inundation at/around Pentagon Island; actions recommended (non&ndash;return valves, pumping) to prevent backing up. [TAB] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four commercial properties; one commercial property evacuated; flood depths of approximately 150mm to 200mm near the bridge [IMG] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Pentagon Island,England,"Back–up of the surface water drainage system caused highway drainage manholes and gullies to flood at low spots, inundating the southern section of Pentagon Island. The A52 slip road (Nottingham–bound to St Alkmunds Way) was closed from 23:20 on 8 Nov and reopened at 04:26 on 9 Nov after the water had cleared. [PAG,IMG] (p35)
Roundabout/roadway closed due to flooding (A52 slip road Nottingham–bound to St Alkmunds Way). Closed at 23:20 on 08/11/2019 and reopened once clear of water at 04:26 on 09/11/2019. [PAG] (p36)
Surface water flooding occurred when fluvial flows backed up into the surface water drainage network, overwhelming the drainage outfalls and causing inundation at/around Pentagon Island; actions recommended (non–return valves, pumping) to prevent backing up. [TAB] (p44)",four commercial properties; one commercial property evacuated; flood depths of approximately 150mm to 200mm near the bridge [IMG] (p35),GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9582487,-1.4674399,"<div id=""html_e97d5e29e16e6226b7c154e68d8ccfc4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ford Lane, Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Derwent overtopped its banks causing fluvial flooding on Ford Lane; one residential property was flooded internally over most of the ground floor (resident had to be rescued via an upstairs window), the nearby railway track was closed with trains reversing back to Derby Station, and caravans in an adjacent caravan park were reported flooded. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was experienced on Ford Lane during the event; the area sits within Derbyshire County Council boundaries but response was managed by Derby City Council. [IMG] (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property [PAG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Ford Lane, Derby",England,"River Derwent overtopped its banks causing fluvial flooding on Ford Lane; one residential property was flooded internally over most of the ground floor (resident had to be rescued via an upstairs window), the nearby railway track was closed with trains reversing back to Derby Station, and caravans in an adjacent caravan park were reported flooded. [PAG] (p20)
Flooding was experienced on Ford Lane during the event; the area sits within Derbyshire County Council boundaries but response was managed by Derby City Council. [IMG] (p21)",1 residential property [PAG] (p20),GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.943468323030274,-1.475287642972216,"<div id=""html_6761ebeecf887f9de2f9c163eee99e69"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Haslams Lane, Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood flows from overtopping of the River Derwent and its tributary Folly Brook spread onto Haslams Lane; highway drainage also received fluvial backflow from a backed&ndash;up ditch (no non&ndash;return valve) causing gullies to discharge and the highway to flood. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Derwent and its tributary Folly Brook overtopped their banks and flood flows followed the line of Folly Brook onto Haslams Lane, inundating the lane (the area was the unprotected active floodplain) and contributing to flooding of adjacent Rugby Club grounds and Darley Fields. [PAG,IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood gate on Haslams Lane was closed during the event to prevent floodwater entering the lane; floodwater was visible beyond the gate and the gate remained closed overnight, reopening the following morning. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Haslams Lane, an area designed as an active floodplain, inundated during 8&ndash;9 November 2019 and performed as intended; no specific remedial actions were recommended as a result of the event. (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths in excess of 1 m along Haslams Lane [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths in excess of 1 m along Haslams Lane [PAG,IMG] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Haslams Lane, Derby",England,"Flood flows from overtopping of the River Derwent and its tributary Folly Brook spread onto Haslams Lane; highway drainage also received fluvial backflow from a backed–up ditch (no non–return valve) causing gullies to discharge and the highway to flood. [PAG] (p22)
River Derwent and its tributary Folly Brook overtopped their banks and flood flows followed the line of Folly Brook onto Haslams Lane, inundating the lane (the area was the unprotected active floodplain) and contributing to flooding of adjacent Rugby Club grounds and Darley Fields. [PAG,IMG] (p23)
A flood gate on Haslams Lane was closed during the event to prevent floodwater entering the lane; floodwater was visible beyond the gate and the gate remained closed overnight, reopening the following morning. [IMG] (p25)
Haslams Lane, an area designed as an active floodplain, inundated during 8–9 November 2019 and performed as intended; no specific remedial actions were recommended as a result of the event. (p44)","flood depths in excess of 1 m along Haslams Lane [PAG] (p22)
flood depths in excess of 1 m along Haslams Lane [PAG,IMG] (p23)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9440717,-1.4560772,"<div id=""html_f8620a5738e5de0c984f42dd4ea74bab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mansfield Road, Little Chester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Partially constructed sheet pile walls on the east bank near Mansfield Road had unsealed joints that leaked (seepage) during the event; this seepage was documented and required pumping to manage water at the construction site. [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Mansfield Road, Little Chester",England,"Partially constructed sheet pile walls on the east bank near Mansfield Road had unsealed joints that leaked (seepage) during the event; this seepage was documented and required pumping to manage water at the construction site. [PAG,IMG] (p28)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9321409,-1.4756489,"<div id=""html_69be74c212ce5e39938f1aaddaba84a1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>City Road, Little Chester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seepage through unsealed sheet piles at a nearby construction site (OCOR scheme behind Old Tomlinson Yard) allowed river water into the site. Two pumps (4&#8220; and 6"") were used to return water to the River Derwent; failure of the 6&#8221; pump resulted in flood water flowing onto City Road. Sandbags were deployed along City Road to protect residential and commercial properties; no internal flooding was reported. [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from a failed 6"" pump at a construction site flowed onto City Road. Sandbags were deployed along the road to protect residential and commercial properties, and no internal flooding was reported. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>City Road flooded during 8&ndash;9 November 2019 due to incomplete flood defences. The defences have subsequently been completed to prevent recurrence via the same mechanism. (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4&#8220; pump; 6&#8221; pump (failed); flood depths up to 50 mm [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flooding depths up to 50 mm on City Road and within the construction site [IMG] (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"City Road, Little Chester",England,"Seepage through unsealed sheet piles at a nearby construction site (OCOR scheme behind Old Tomlinson Yard) allowed river water into the site. Two pumps (4“ and 6"") were used to return water to the River Derwent; failure of the 6” pump resulted in flood water flowing onto City Road. Sandbags were deployed along City Road to protect residential and commercial properties; no internal flooding was reported. [PAG,IMG] (p28)
Floodwater from a failed 6"" pump at a construction site flowed onto City Road. Sandbags were deployed along the road to protect residential and commercial properties, and no internal flooding was reported. [IMG] (p29)
City Road flooded during 8–9 November 2019 due to incomplete flood defences. The defences have subsequently been completed to prevent recurrence via the same mechanism. (p44)","4“ pump; 6” pump (failed); flood depths up to 50 mm [PAG,IMG] (p28)
flooding depths up to 50 mm on City Road and within the construction site [IMG] (p29)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9272142,-1.4764567,"<div id=""html_ce344af024fbb09f72b4e3683140ad55"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sowter Road, Little Chester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters overtopped the River Derwent banks and flowed along the exceedance channel onto Sowter Road; flows collected and were stored at a low spot in the Sowter Road underpass, demonstrating the underpass filled during the event. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood waters entered the exceedance channel along Sowter Road during high river levels and collected in the underpass area. A manhole under the underpass was connected to a penstock that was shut to prevent flows entering the sewer network, allowing water to attenuate on the road; flood walls and gates functioned and no internal property flooding was reported. After river levels fell the penstock was gradually reopened to drain the attenuated water into the drainage system. [PAG,IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood waters entered the Sowter Road exceedance channel (from the south of St Marys Bridge) and collected in the underpass. A manhole penstock under the underpass was shut to prevent flows entering the sewer and to allow water to attenuate on the road; flood walls and gates functioned and no internal property flooding was reported. The exceedance channel levels south of the bridge were later altered to match the design. [PAG,IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sowter Road, designed to act as an exceedance channel, flooded during November 2019 and conveyed flows as intended; no specific additional actions were recommended for this location. (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Sowter Road, Little Chester",England,"Floodwaters overtopped the River Derwent banks and flowed along the exceedance channel onto Sowter Road; flows collected and were stored at a low spot in the Sowter Road underpass, demonstrating the underpass filled during the event. [IMG] (p29)
Flood waters entered the exceedance channel along Sowter Road during high river levels and collected in the underpass area. A manhole under the underpass was connected to a penstock that was shut to prevent flows entering the sewer network, allowing water to attenuate on the road; flood walls and gates functioned and no internal property flooding was reported. After river levels fell the penstock was gradually reopened to drain the attenuated water into the drainage system. [PAG,IMG] (p30)
Flood waters entered the Sowter Road exceedance channel (from the south of St Marys Bridge) and collected in the underpass. A manhole penstock under the underpass was shut to prevent flows entering the sewer and to allow water to attenuate on the road; flood walls and gates functioned and no internal property flooding was reported. The exceedance channel levels south of the bridge were later altered to match the design. [PAG,IMG] (p31)
Sowter Road, designed to act as an exceedance channel, flooded during November 2019 and conveyed flows as intended; no specific additional actions were recommended for this location. (p44)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9237573,-1.4707932,"<div id=""html_424fa45ccf397260523f422532fe35ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Meadow Road, Little Chester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seepage through the flood wall along the riverside footpath resulted in flooding on Meadow Road, with significant inundation in the lowest spot. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seepage through the riverside flood wall and overtopping of the adjacent flood bank resulted in substantial flooding of Meadow Road; water accumulated on the road surface and submerged street signs and poles. Sandbags were used nearby to limit further overtopping; no internal property flooding was reported for properties on Meadow Road. [PAG,IMG] (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths exceeding 2 m in the lowest spot [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths exceeding 2 m in the lowest spot on Meadow Road [PAG,IMG] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Meadow Road, Little Chester",England,"Seepage through the flood wall along the riverside footpath resulted in flooding on Meadow Road, with significant inundation in the lowest spot. [PAG] (p31)
