Exeter Place experienced seepage through flood defences and overtopping of flood defences during the 8–9 November flood. (p43)
River flooding during 8–9 Nov 2019 affected flood defences along the riverbank at Exeter Place; missing coping on the flood wall was identified and reinstalling it was recommended. [TAB] (p44)","flood depths exceeding 2m; flood depths of up to 50mm [PAG,IMG] (p32)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.922009433985004,-1.466079099290674,"<div id=""html_0624db2a6f3a124168ceac156af23ad0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Meadow Lane, Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River overtopped its banks near the footbridge, causing floodwater to flow onto the road and into adjacent commercial properties. The road experienced flooding prior to the peak with flood depths recorded near the bridge. One commercial property was evacuated and four commercial properties experienced internal flooding during the event. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Derwent overtopped its banks (starting by the footbridge), causing flooding of Meadow Lane. The overtopping flooded the road and resulted in internal flooding of commercial premises adjacent to the river. One commercial property was evacuated during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths of approximately 150mm to 200mm; 1 commercial property evacuated; 4 commercial properties internally flooded [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one commercial property evacuated; four commercial properties internally flooded; flood depths of approximately 150 mm to 200 mm near the bridge [PAG,IMG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Meadow Lane, Derby",England,"River overtopped its banks near the footbridge, causing floodwater to flow onto the road and into adjacent commercial properties. The road experienced flooding prior to the peak with flood depths recorded near the bridge. One commercial property was evacuated and four commercial properties experienced internal flooding during the event. [PAG] (p33)
River Derwent overtopped its banks (starting by the footbridge), causing flooding of Meadow Lane. The overtopping flooded the road and resulted in internal flooding of commercial premises adjacent to the river. One commercial property was evacuated during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p34)","flood depths of approximately 150mm to 200mm; 1 commercial property evacuated; 4 commercial properties internally flooded [PAG] (p33)
one commercial property evacuated; four commercial properties internally flooded; flood depths of approximately 150 mm to 200 mm near the bridge [PAG,IMG] (p34)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9245935,-1.4611024,"<div id=""html_2e953d6aa6fb9e8f974f21c6feb39c6a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eastgate, Derby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Eastgate underpass flooded (underpass reported flooded by 14:40 on 8 November) as highway drainage gullies at low points flooded due to back&ndash;up in the connected surface water drainage network caused by high river levels. [IMG,PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area around Eastgate (including low points such as the Eastgate underpass and adjacent gullies) flooded as surface water drainage backed up; area identified as high risk and experienced inundation during the event. (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Eastgate, Derby",England,"The Eastgate underpass flooded (underpass reported flooded by 14:40 on 8 November) as highway drainage gullies at low points flooded due to back–up in the connected surface water drainage network caused by high river levels. [IMG,PAG] (p35)
Area around Eastgate (including low points such as the Eastgate underpass and adjacent gullies) flooded as surface water drainage backed up; area identified as high risk and experienced inundation during the event. (p36)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9260717,-1.4507692,"<div id=""html_fffa4beebbf8b484b639dd1189c8066d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nottingham Road, Chaddesden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies on Nottingham Road flooded as the combined/surface water system backed up due to high river levels; the Nottingham Road underpass is identified as a low point that flooded during the event. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The combined drainage system backed up (via a hydraulic connection to the surface water network) causing gullies along Nottingham Road to flood and the underpass on Nottingham Road to be inundated (photographed flooded at 17:47 on 8 Nov). Recorded flood outlines in the area match surface&ndash;water flood&ndash;prone zones. [PAG,IMG] (p37)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>greater than 130 mm (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths greater than 130mm [PAG,IMG] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Nottingham Road, Chaddesden",England,"Gullies on Nottingham Road flooded as the combined/surface water system backed up due to high river levels; the Nottingham Road underpass is identified as a low point that flooded during the event. (p36)
The combined drainage system backed up (via a hydraulic connection to the surface water network) causing gullies along Nottingham Road to flood and the underpass on Nottingham Road to be inundated (photographed flooded at 17:47 on 8 Nov). Recorded flood outlines in the area match surface–water flood–prone zones. [PAG,IMG] (p37)","greater than 130 mm (p36)
flood depths greater than 130mm [PAG,IMG] (p37)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9253714,-1.4680884316105876,"<div id=""html_5770300f779bcd7569991fd6749de2e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cardinal Square, Little Chester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The below&ndash;ground sub&ndash;station in Cardinal Square, a low point in the topography, experienced yard gully flooding when the connected surface water/combined system backed up; recorded flood depths exceeded 130 mm. (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>greater than 130 mm (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Cardinal Square, Little Chester",England,"The below–ground sub–station in Cardinal Square, a low point in the topography, experienced yard gully flooding when the connected surface water/combined system backed up; recorded flood depths exceeded 130 mm. (p36)",greater than 130 mm (p36),GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9232053,-1.4538819,"<div id=""html_a6a833da988863171637ca94d99df7c7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chequers Road, Pride Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding inundated the carriageway and caused internal flooding to several commercial properties along Chequers Road, with flood depths up to 1.5 m; the road was closed at 07:41 on 09 Nov 2019 due to flooding within the carriageway. (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.5 m (maximum internal flood depth) (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Chequers Road, Pride Park",England,"Flooding inundated the carriageway and caused internal flooding to several commercial properties along Chequers Road, with flood depths up to 1.5 m; the road was closed at 07:41 on 09 Nov 2019 due to flooding within the carriageway. (p38)",1.5 m (maximum internal flood depth) (p38),GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.921215,-1.4504511,"<div id=""html_4c3a4ca90e2f6ac7dc5a3897f1ffcfe9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Downing Road, Pride Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Downing Road pumping station failed during the event and is identified as one of three sources of flooding into Chaddesden Sidings, contributing to road closures and internal flooding of nearby properties. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Downing Road pumping station failed during the event and is identified as one of the three sources contributing to flooding in the Chaddesden Sidings area. The combined effects (pumping station failure, river overtopping and sewer backing) resulted in flooding of the A52 and Chequers Road and internal flooding to commercial properties along Chequers Road. [PAG,IMG] (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding depths up to 1.5 m to commercial properties (Chequers Road area) [PAG,IMG] (p39)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Downing Road, Pride Park",England,"The Downing Road pumping station failed during the event and is identified as one of three sources of flooding into Chaddesden Sidings, contributing to road closures and internal flooding of nearby properties. (p38)
The Downing Road pumping station failed during the event and is identified as one of the three sources contributing to flooding in the Chaddesden Sidings area. The combined effects (pumping station failure, river overtopping and sewer backing) resulted in flooding of the A52 and Chequers Road and internal flooding to commercial properties along Chequers Road. [PAG,IMG] (p39)","internal flooding depths up to 1.5 m to commercial properties (Chequers Road area) [PAG,IMG] (p39)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.902492,-1.4245402,"<div id=""html_822385e09779d7ffb83335d1f6e33a5d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Raynesway, Alvaston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding adjacent to Raynesway occurred where a drainage ditch and a surface water outfall could not discharge to the River Derwent because of high river levels; this led to overtopping into an adjacent commercial car park (approx. 300mm) and backup of surface water causing internal flooding to one commercial property via a flooded internal drain. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels on the River Derwent prevented the ditch outfall flap valve from opening, causing the adjacent ditch to fill and overtop its banks and flood into a commercial property's car park (up to approx. 300 mm). Separately, a surface water outfall north of the ditch was unable to discharge due to high river levels, causing pluvial/surface water to back up the pipe and result in internal flooding of one commercial property via a drain within the property. [PAG,IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pluvial (surface water) flooding risk affecting the surface water drainage/ditches in the Raynesway area during the 8&ndash;9 November 2019 event; recommended routine inspection and maintenance of ditches and investigation of upstream storage to mitigate pluvial flooding when river levels are high. [TAB] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one commercial property; car park flooded to approx. 300mm [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximate maximum depth of 300mm; one commercial property internally flooded [PAG,IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Raynesway, Alvaston",England,"Flooding adjacent to Raynesway occurred where a drainage ditch and a surface water outfall could not discharge to the River Derwent because of high river levels; this led to overtopping into an adjacent commercial car park (approx. 300mm) and backup of surface water causing internal flooding to one commercial property via a flooded internal drain. [PAG] (p40)
High river levels on the River Derwent prevented the ditch outfall flap valve from opening, causing the adjacent ditch to fill and overtop its banks and flood into a commercial property's car park (up to approx. 300 mm). Separately, a surface water outfall north of the ditch was unable to discharge due to high river levels, causing pluvial/surface water to back up the pipe and result in internal flooding of one commercial property via a drain within the property. [PAG,IMG] (p41)
Pluvial (surface water) flooding risk affecting the surface water drainage/ditches in the Raynesway area during the 8–9 November 2019 event; recommended routine inspection and maintenance of ditches and investigation of upstream storage to mitigate pluvial flooding when river levels are high. [TAB] (p44)","one commercial property; car park flooded to approx. 300mm [PAG] (p40)
approximate maximum depth of 300mm; one commercial property internally flooded [PAG,IMG] (p41)",GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,53.118504,-1.556618,"<div id=""html_313470ba6a0b08edff8c21596c1b1727"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Derbyshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred across Derbyshire in November 2019 (investigated as flooding between 7 and 20 November 2019); exceptional rainfall and saturated soils contributed to flooding impacts on communities and infrastructure during this period. [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Derbyshire (notably Matlock) were affected by intense rainfall and subsequent surface water and fluvial flooding on 8&ndash;9 November 2019, producing widespread flooding including internal flooding of residential and commercial properties. [PAG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Derbyshire,England,"Flooding occurred across Derbyshire in November 2019 (investigated as flooding between 7 and 20 November 2019); exceptional rainfall and saturated soils contributed to flooding impacts on communities and infrastructure during this period. [MUL] (p12)
Parts of Derbyshire (notably Matlock) were affected by intense rainfall and subsequent surface water and fluvial flooding on 8–9 November 2019, producing widespread flooding including internal flooding of residential and commercial properties. [PAG] (p20)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9266862278223,-1.4763586564388622,"<div id=""html_dd4af7e5de09ad96b1e9af499917875f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sowter Road Underpass</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The underpass was inundated with river flood water (photo evidence at 22:57 on 8 Nov 2019) with water covering the road and reaching the base of the bridge, rendering the road impassable and storing attenuated flood water beneath the underpass until river levels reduced and the penstock was reopened. [PAG,IMG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Sowter Road Underpass,England,"The underpass was inundated with river flood water (photo evidence at 22:57 on 8 Nov 2019) with water covering the road and reaching the base of the bridge, rendering the road impassable and storing attenuated flood water beneath the underpass until river levels reduced and the penstock was reopened. [PAG,IMG] (p30)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,53.1374514,-1.5544694,"<div id=""html_a1fa9275451f24c8b002f986612496fe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Matlock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall and catchment runoff caused surface water and fluvial flows that exceeded drainage and river capacities in Matlock, resulting in widespread flooding including internal flooding at both residential and commercial properties. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Matlock,England,"Intense rainfall and catchment runoff caused surface water and fluvial flows that exceeded drainage and river capacities in Matlock, resulting in widespread flooding including internal flooding at both residential and commercial properties. (p20)",,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-DER,1,2019-11-08,52.9294476,-1.440326,"<div id=""html_50ea060d9546727d50b5467f9d163aa2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chaddesden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodplain inundation from the River Derwent overtopping affected areas south of the railway and allowed water to pass into the Chaddesden Sidings area via an underpass; this led to local road closures (A52 and Chequers Road) and property flooding in the adjacent Sidings. (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths up to 1.5 m; A52 closed from 21:45 on 08 Nov 2019 (reopened 09 Nov 2019); Chequers Road closed at 07:41 on 09 Nov 2019 (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Chaddesden,England,Floodplain inundation from the River Derwent overtopping affected areas south of the railway and allowed water to pass into the Chaddesden Sidings area via an underpass; this led to local road closures (A52 and Chequers Road) and property flooding in the adjacent Sidings. (p38),flood depths up to 1.5 m; A52 closed from 21:45 on 08 Nov 2019 (reopened 09 Nov 2019); Chequers Road closed at 07:41 on 09 Nov 2019 (p38),GB-DER_1_2019-11-08.html,GB-DER_1_2019-11-08,Derby flooding 08 Nov 2019 - 09 Nov 2019,Investigation Report into November 2019 Derwent Floods in Derby City,033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/2022-09-15-derby-section-19-report-p03.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.336748211544034,-0.0735907022351177,"<div id=""html_7f4286c75c4bec22b036760c481cba4d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sanderstead Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Unspecified flooding reported at 28&ndash;30 Sanderstead Court; London Fire Brigade attended. [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Sanderstead Court,England,Unspecified flooding reported at 28–30 Sanderstead Court; London Fire Brigade attended. [TAB] (p12),,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.401742850000005,-0.1080078200583821,"<div id=""html_2491f4fc0434f10a86f9c65c7f74aefc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thornton Heath Recreation Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater pooled at the footpath entrance into the recreation ground, causing shallow surface water that could impede access from the adjacent road/footpath. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Thornton Heath Recreation Ground,England,"Floodwater pooled at the footpath entrance into the recreation ground, causing shallow surface water that could impede access from the adjacent road/footpath. [PAG,IMG] (p15)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.339275,-0.1172902,"<div id=""html_23eeef91be5dce4775c1ad93716ad9d6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Cross</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pedestrian underpass and approaching footpaths flooded after the highway drainage network at the roundabout was overwhelmed and surcharged; underpass flooding was exacerbated by failure of the pumping system. [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Purley Cross area (central intersection and surrounding roads) experienced surface water flooding during the event; area identified as a topographic depression where surface water flowed overland when drainage capacity was exceeded, contributing to widespread urban flooding and travel disruption around the junction. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High intensity rainfall exceeded gully and drain capacity causing overland surface water flows. The downstream sewer network backed up, surcharged and flooded to significant depths in low&ndash;lying areas. The pedestrian underpass flooded when its pump failed. Transport for London drainage assets on the A22/A23 and the pedestrian underpass were among the most affected. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water exceeded highway drainage capacity and flowed overland causing flooding at Purley Cross including the underpass; pumps and tanker operations were used to remove floodwater, pump control panel was at risk/submerged and sandbags were placed, cleanup of silt/debris undertaken and pump repairs/upgrade works planned. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Suffered surface&ndash;water flooding on 24 August 2015; drainage engineers removed flood water by tanker and repaired pumps as part of the immediate response. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage network upstream and downstream of the pump station did not have sufficient capacity to manage the rainfall; TfL&ndash;operated pumps at the pedestrian underpass failed due to flooding of the pump control box, exacerbating flooding at this location. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximate underpass flood depth 2.4 m (reported as ~1 ft (0.3 m) below underside of bridge with 2.7 m clearance) [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley Cross,England,"Pedestrian underpass and approaching footpaths flooded after the highway drainage network at the roundabout was overwhelmed and surcharged; underpass flooding was exacerbated by failure of the pumping system. [TAB,PAG] (p12)
The Purley Cross area (central intersection and surrounding roads) experienced surface water flooding during the event; area identified as a topographic depression where surface water flowed overland when drainage capacity was exceeded, contributing to widespread urban flooding and travel disruption around the junction. [IMG] (p13)
High intensity rainfall exceeded gully and drain capacity causing overland surface water flows. The downstream sewer network backed up, surcharged and flooded to significant depths in low–lying areas. The pedestrian underpass flooded when its pump failed. Transport for London drainage assets on the A22/A23 and the pedestrian underpass were among the most affected. [PAG] (p14)
Surface water exceeded highway drainage capacity and flowed overland causing flooding at Purley Cross including the underpass; pumps and tanker operations were used to remove floodwater, pump control panel was at risk/submerged and sandbags were placed, cleanup of silt/debris undertaken and pump repairs/upgrade works planned. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p17)
Suffered surface–water flooding on 24 August 2015; drainage engineers removed flood water by tanker and repaired pumps as part of the immediate response. (p18)
Drainage network upstream and downstream of the pump station did not have sufficient capacity to manage the rainfall; TfL–operated pumps at the pedestrian underpass failed due to flooding of the pump control box, exacerbating flooding at this location. [PAG] (p19)","approximate underpass flood depth 2.4 m (reported as ~1 ft (0.3 m) below underside of bridge with 2.7 m clearance) [TAB,PAG] (p12)",GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3550556,-0.0643103753173489,"<div id=""html_08a07968e179f680c12e40e8eb2df941"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe surface water flooding in August 2015 affected multiple locations across the Borough, impacting commercial and residential properties and major transport routes. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface water flooding across the borough in August 2015; a significant number of calls were placed to emergency services by the public about risks to properties. The report identifies pump failure and surcharge of surface water sewers as contributing factors increasing flood risk in affected sites. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough has experienced high&ndash;intensity surface water / flash flood events. On 24 August 2015 the Borough received a short, high&ndash;intensity rainfall event across Croydon that produced a high risk of surface water flooding. Separately, the most significant recent recorded surface water flood affecting the Borough occurred on 20 July 2007 when intense rainfall caused flash floods across numerous locations in the Borough; Purley town centre experienced significant flooding to property and the transport network. [MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Short, intense rainfall on 24 August 2015 caused significant surface&ndash;water flooding across the Borough. High rainfall intensity exceeded the capacity of road gullies and drain gratings, producing runoff that ponded in low&ndash;lying areas. The road drainage network was put under pressure, resulting in lifting of sewer manhole covers. Pump failure at the Purley Cross Underpass caused flooding within the underpass and led to backing up of the sewer system. [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface&ndash;water and urban flooding across the borough during the August 2015 event: highway drainage was overwhelmed, drainage systems surcharged and manhole covers lifted, causing rapid onset of surface&ndash;water flooding at multiple locations (including roundabouts, underpasses and roads). [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, high&ndash;intensity rainfall across the urbanised borough overwhelmed highway drainage and sewer networks causing widespread surface water flooding and sewer surcharge. Pump failure in a pedestrian underpass caused additional local ponding. Impacts included five commercial properties flooded at Purley Oaks Road; temporary closure of roads and pedestrian access (including access to Purley Oaks train station); nuisance flooding of roads and footpaths borough&ndash;wide; and some external residential property flooding (garages and driveways) reported by the London Fire Brigade. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the intense August 2015 rainfall the borough experienced multiple localised surface&ndash;water flooding incidents: nuisance flooding of roads and footpaths, pooling on residential streets (photographed at Harewood Gardens and Warren Road), some external residential property flooding (garages and driveways), and surcharge/backing&ndash;up of highway drainage where gullies and drains were blocked. [IMG,PAG] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 commercial properties [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Croydon,England,"Severe surface water flooding in August 2015 affected multiple locations across the Borough, impacting commercial and residential properties and major transport routes. (p6)
Widespread surface water flooding across the borough in August 2015; a significant number of calls were placed to emergency services by the public about risks to properties. The report identifies pump failure and surcharge of surface water sewers as contributing factors increasing flood risk in affected sites. [PAG] (p7)
The borough has experienced high–intensity surface water / flash flood events. On 24 August 2015 the Borough received a short, high–intensity rainfall event across Croydon that produced a high risk of surface water flooding. Separately, the most significant recent recorded surface water flood affecting the Borough occurred on 20 July 2007 when intense rainfall caused flash floods across numerous locations in the Borough; Purley town centre experienced significant flooding to property and the transport network. [MUL] (p10)
Short, intense rainfall on 24 August 2015 caused significant surface–water flooding across the Borough. High rainfall intensity exceeded the capacity of road gullies and drain gratings, producing runoff that ponded in low–lying areas. The road drainage network was put under pressure, resulting in lifting of sewer manhole covers. Pump failure at the Purley Cross Underpass caused flooding within the underpass and led to backing up of the sewer system. [TAB,PAG] (p11)
Widespread surface–water and urban flooding across the borough during the August 2015 event: highway drainage was overwhelmed, drainage systems surcharged and manhole covers lifted, causing rapid onset of surface–water flooding at multiple locations (including roundabouts, underpasses and roads). [TAB,PAG] (p12)
Intense, high–intensity rainfall across the urbanised borough overwhelmed highway drainage and sewer networks causing widespread surface water flooding and sewer surcharge. Pump failure in a pedestrian underpass caused additional local ponding. Impacts included five commercial properties flooded at Purley Oaks Road; temporary closure of roads and pedestrian access (including access to Purley Oaks train station); nuisance flooding of roads and footpaths borough–wide; and some external residential property flooding (garages and driveways) reported by the London Fire Brigade. [IMG,PAG] (p15)
During the intense August 2015 rainfall the borough experienced multiple localised surface–water flooding incidents: nuisance flooding of roads and footpaths, pooling on residential streets (photographed at Harewood Gardens and Warren Road), some external residential property flooding (garages and driveways), and surcharge/backing–up of highway drainage where gullies and drains were blocked. [IMG,PAG] (p16)","5 commercial properties [IMG,PAG] (p15)",GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3574645,-0.1736269,"<div id=""html_83878fc18087132169edadd657785913"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sutton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wandle was reported by the Environment Agency to have burst its banks in some locations during the 24 August 2015 event. The river flows from an open section in Wandle Park into a culvert and then into the London Borough of Sutton, and the reported bank bursts (outside Croydon) indicate high fluvial levels and local fluvial flooding in areas downstream. [TAB] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Sutton,England,"The River Wandle was reported by the Environment Agency to have burst its banks in some locations during the 24 August 2015 event. The river flows from an open section in Wandle Park into a culvert and then into the London Borough of Sutton, and the reported bank bursts (outside Croydon) indicate high fluvial levels and local fluvial flooding in areas downstream. [TAB] (p11)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.34705497308258,-0.09902922880819734,"<div id=""html_5cb35fe82907b2242f5ad3112decd08a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Oaks Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High intensity rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage so a manhole at the bottom of the road surcharged. Floodwater entered commercial properties and covered the road and pavements, requiring temporary closure of the road and pedestrian access to Purley Oaks rail station. [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage mechanisms at Purley Oaks (including the underpass pump and interaction with the sewer network at Purley Oaks station) were affected during the 24 August 2015 surface&ndash;water floods; TfL proposed relocating the underpass pump control cabinet after it was at risk of submersion. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Purley Oaks station prompted recommendations for further engagement with the sewer company (TWUL) to review drainage mechanisms and interaction with the sewer network and to review soakaway outfall pipe size to enhance drainage capacity. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 commercial properties; 1 manhole surcharged [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley Oaks Station,England,"High intensity rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage so a manhole at the bottom of the road surcharged. Floodwater entered commercial properties and covered the road and pavements, requiring temporary closure of the road and pedestrian access to Purley Oaks rail station. [IMG,PAG] (p14)
Drainage mechanisms at Purley Oaks (including the underpass pump and interaction with the sewer network at Purley Oaks station) were affected during the 24 August 2015 surface–water floods; TfL proposed relocating the underpass pump control cabinet after it was at risk of submersion. (p18)
Flooding at Purley Oaks station prompted recommendations for further engagement with the sewer company (TWUL) to review drainage mechanisms and interaction with the sewer network and to review soakaway outfall pipe size to enhance drainage capacity. [PAG] (p19)","5 commercial properties; 1 manhole surcharged [IMG,PAG] (p14)",GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.41648,-0.1806258,"<div id=""html_2c6092d1b3f698f106ea9c6079eaba4a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Wandle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported by the Environment Agency to have burst its banks in some locations during the 24 August 2015 event, indicating the river was at high levels and contributing to flooding in the catchment (the reported bank bursts were outside the London Borough of Croydon). [TAB] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,River Wandle,England,"Reported by the Environment Agency to have burst its banks in some locations during the 24 August 2015 event, indicating the river was at high levels and contributing to flooding in the catchment (the reported bank bursts were outside the London Borough of Croydon). [TAB] (p11)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3437642,-0.0922807,"<div id=""html_0fdb48d9d9585af4ca813f74cef5b00f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Oaks Road, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Subject to severe surface water flooding during torrential rain in August 2015, causing localised transport route disruption. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple commercial properties were inundated by surface water flooding. The report suggests a pump failure and surcharge of surface water sewers may have contributed to flooding at this location. [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Manhole surcharge occurred and five commercial properties were flooded on the station approach (Purley Oaks Road) during the event. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High&ndash;intensity rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage network; a manhole at the bottom of the road surcharged and surface water flowed overland into the road and shopfronts, flooding commercial properties and blocking access to the railway station (road and pedestrian access temporarily closed by the London Fire Brigade). [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, high&ndash;intensity rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage causing a manhole at the bottom of the road to surcharge; surface water flowed overland and flooded commercial premises and pedestrian areas, blocking access to the station. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced surface&ndash;water flooding on 24 August 2015; the highway drainage network was investigated afterwards, with CCTV surveys and a detailed drainage investigation carried out. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five properties (commercial) flooded [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 commercial properties; 1 manhole [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five commercial properties [PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Purley Oaks Road, Croydon",England,"Subject to severe surface water flooding during torrential rain in August 2015, causing localised transport route disruption. (p6)
Multiple commercial properties were inundated by surface water flooding. The report suggests a pump failure and surcharge of surface water sewers may have contributed to flooding at this location. [TAB] (p7)
Manhole surcharge occurred and five commercial properties were flooded on the station approach (Purley Oaks Road) during the event. [TAB] (p12)
High–intensity rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage network; a manhole at the bottom of the road surcharged and surface water flowed overland into the road and shopfronts, flooding commercial properties and blocking access to the railway station (road and pedestrian access temporarily closed by the London Fire Brigade). [IMG,PAG] (p14)
Intense, high–intensity rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage causing a manhole at the bottom of the road to surcharge; surface water flowed overland and flooded commercial premises and pedestrian areas, blocking access to the station. [PAG] (p15)
Experienced surface–water flooding on 24 August 2015; the highway drainage network was investigated afterwards, with CCTV surveys and a detailed drainage investigation carried out. (p18)","five properties (commercial) flooded [TAB] (p12)
