GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3169288,-0.1327789,"<div id=""html_8c97fe872ba87a32f829121c728752bf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ullswater Crescent, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway flooding at the junction of Marlpit Lane and Ullswater Crescent during the August 2015 event, causing the junction roadway to flood. [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Ullswater Crescent, Croydon",England,"Highway flooding at the junction of Marlpit Lane and Ullswater Crescent during the August 2015 event, causing the junction roadway to flood. [TAB] (p12)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3371837,-0.1158413,"<div id=""html_cf71a87cd4a4e567a5661a043fa4d62f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Road, Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the approaching roads and surrounding road network, with photographs showing the northern section of the roundabout looking towards Purley Road inundated and vehicles driving through floodwater; contributed to travel disruption. [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Purley Road, Purley",England,"Flooding affected the approaching roads and surrounding road network, with photographs showing the northern section of the roundabout looking towards Purley Road inundated and vehicles driving through floodwater; contributed to travel disruption. [IMG] (p13)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.33766666433047,-0.11694467799549156,"<div id=""html_89404e44db9d03df2fc1cdcaace194c8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Underpass</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced severe surface water flooding during torrential rain in August 2015; contributed to transport disruption (underpass flooding) and localised impacts on infrastructure. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pump failure at the Purley Cross Underpass during the event led to flooding of the underpass and caused backing up of the sewer system; short, intense rainfall also put pressure on the road drainage network leading to lifting of sewer manhole covers. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The underpass was inundated with surface water and required pumping and gully&ndash;suctioning to remove floodwater and silt; the pump control panel was submerged and later sandbagged. Repairs/alterations to the underpass pump were planned following the event. [TAB,MUL] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley Underpass,England,"Experienced severe surface water flooding during torrential rain in August 2015; contributed to transport disruption (underpass flooding) and localised impacts on infrastructure. (p6)
Pump failure at the Purley Cross Underpass during the event led to flooding of the underpass and caused backing up of the sewer system; short, intense rainfall also put pressure on the road drainage network leading to lifting of sewer manhole covers. [TAB] (p11)
The underpass was inundated with surface water and required pumping and gully–suctioning to remove floodwater and silt; the pump control panel was submerged and later sandbagged. Repairs/alterations to the underpass pump were planned following the event. [TAB,MUL] (p17)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.322754,-0.1037854,"<div id=""html_376c8c908c1f7f8f66344c24d8dc4cc0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced localised highway flooding from surface water during the torrential rain event in August 2015, contributing to disruption on local roads. (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Kenley,England,"Experienced localised highway flooding from surface water during the torrential rain event in August 2015, contributing to disruption on local roads. (p6)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3385156,-0.115893,"<div id=""html_609429febc76dd4ead2959080e11e843"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced localised highway and surface water flooding during the torrential rain in August 2015, contributing to transport and property impacts noted in the report. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations in Purley experienced flooding during August 2015: highway flooding on the northern and western sides of the roundabout; manhole surcharge on Purley Oaks Road (Station Approach) with subsequent flooding of commercial premises; residential flooding reported at Brancaster Lane (including garage flooding at 46 Brancaster Lane); highway flooding on Sunnydene Road and multiple&ndash;source flooding (manhole surcharge, sewer and highway) on Old Lodge Lane. [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage at Purley Cross roundabout causing the drainage system to surcharge and lift manhole covers. Surface water flooded across the roundabout and the pedestrian underpass beneath the roundabout became rapidly inundated. The underpass flooding was exacerbated by a pumping system failure. Flooding on surrounding roads (including northern and western sections of the roundabout) caused travel disruption. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface&ndash;water flooding was reported at multiple locations in Purley (examples in the report include Lower Barn Road and Warren Road), causing road and footpath inundation and instances of external residential flooding (garages/driveways) where reported. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface water flooding from high intensity rainfall that exceeded highway drainage capacity. Reported impacts include nuisance flooding of roads and footpaths, and some external residential property flooding (garages and driveways). Flooding was exacerbated in locations by blocked gullies and drainage bottlenecks (notably issues identified on Purley Oaks Road). [IMG,PAG] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five properties (commercial) flooded (Purley Oaks Road) [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximate depth of 2.4m in the pedestrian underpass [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley,England,"Experienced localised highway and surface water flooding during the torrential rain in August 2015, contributing to transport and property impacts noted in the report. (p6)
Multiple locations in Purley experienced flooding during August 2015: highway flooding on the northern and western sides of the roundabout; manhole surcharge on Purley Oaks Road (Station Approach) with subsequent flooding of commercial premises; residential flooding reported at Brancaster Lane (including garage flooding at 46 Brancaster Lane); highway flooding on Sunnydene Road and multiple–source flooding (manhole surcharge, sewer and highway) on Old Lodge Lane. [TAB,PAG] (p12)
Heavy rainfall overwhelmed the highway drainage at Purley Cross roundabout causing the drainage system to surcharge and lift manhole covers. Surface water flooded across the roundabout and the pedestrian underpass beneath the roundabout became rapidly inundated. The underpass flooding was exacerbated by a pumping system failure. Flooding on surrounding roads (including northern and western sections of the roundabout) caused travel disruption. [IMG] (p13)
Localised surface–water flooding was reported at multiple locations in Purley (examples in the report include Lower Barn Road and Warren Road), causing road and footpath inundation and instances of external residential flooding (garages/driveways) where reported. [IMG,PAG] (p15)
Localised surface water flooding from high intensity rainfall that exceeded highway drainage capacity. Reported impacts include nuisance flooding of roads and footpaths, and some external residential property flooding (garages and driveways). Flooding was exacerbated in locations by blocked gullies and drainage bottlenecks (notably issues identified on Purley Oaks Road). [IMG,PAG] (p16)","five properties (commercial) flooded (Purley Oaks Road) [TAB,PAG] (p12)
approximate depth of 2.4m in the pedestrian underpass [IMG] (p13)",GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3199716,-0.1393506,"<div id=""html_c6fe7d784ca778be43be35ec2d59b3bb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coulsdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced localised highway and surface water flooding during the torrential rain in August 2015, contributing to transport disruption and localised incidents. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood reports across Coulsdon during August 2015, including highway flooding at junctions (e.g. Brantwood Road & Brighton Road), highway flooding at 67 Harewood Gardens, and unspecified flooding at 28&ndash;30 Sanderstead Court (attended by LFB). [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Coulsdon,England,"Experienced localised highway and surface water flooding during the torrential rain in August 2015, contributing to transport disruption and localised incidents. (p6)
Multiple flood reports across Coulsdon during August 2015, including highway flooding at junctions (e.g. Brantwood Road & Brighton Road), highway flooding at 67 Harewood Gardens, and unspecified flooding at 28–30 Sanderstead Court (attended by LFB). [TAB] (p12)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.3425414,-0.0162924,"<div id=""html_8a7888a47cffa95447e27b118bfe8f7b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>New Addington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced localised highway and surface water flooding during the torrential rain in August 2015, contributing to localised incidents and disruption. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Footpath flooding at the junction of Melfort Avenue and Carew Road caused by blocked gully (pedestrian route affected). [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,New Addington,England,"Experienced localised highway and surface water flooding during the torrential rain in August 2015, contributing to localised incidents and disruption. (p6)
Footpath flooding at the junction of Melfort Avenue and Carew Road caused by blocked gully (pedestrian route affected). [TAB] (p12)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-CRY,3,2015-08-01,51.39911,-0.0986059,"<div id=""html_169c18e18242600cb74d8539b6383cd8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thornton Heath</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area&ndash;level flooding during August 2015 including unspecified flooding at 5 Holmes Place and highway flooding at 12 Bensham Manor Road (blocked gully reported). [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface water flooding occurred at a junction in Thornton Heath (Melfort Avenue/footpath), producing shallow pooling on road and pavement and affecting pedestrian access in the area. [IMG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Thornton Heath,England,"Area–level flooding during August 2015 including unspecified flooding at 5 Holmes Place and highway flooding at 12 Bensham Manor Road (blocked gully reported). [TAB] (p12)
Localised surface water flooding occurred at a junction in Thornton Heath (Melfort Avenue/footpath), producing shallow pooling on road and pavement and affecting pedestrian access in the area. [IMG] (p15)",,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01.html,GB-CRY_3_2015-08-01,Croydon flooding August 2015,Croydon Flood Investigation Report – 24th August 2015,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/flood-investigation-august15.pdf
GB-KEN,7,2021-08-01,51.20647415219595,0.6513327817030702,"<div id=""html_0544c773d117c8321d08e2419c9d0841"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pye Corner</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Three properties at Pye Corner reported flooding; available information does not state whether flooding was internal or external. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Pye Corner,England,Three properties at Pye Corner reported flooding; available information does not state whether flooding was internal or external. [TAB] (p2),3 properties [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_7_2021-08-01.html,GB-KEN_7_2021-08-01,Ulcombe flooding 01 Aug 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Ulcombe,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Ulcombe-on-1-August-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,7,2021-08-01,51.2110514,0.6443637,"<div id=""html_2f29b206cd23bf9abc70c8ed0e658de5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Street, Ulcombe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Parish Council reported 27 properties flooded during the flood event. Internal flooding at properties on The Street was reported up to a depth of 15 cm. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>27 properties; 15 cm internal flood depth [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"The Street, Ulcombe",England,The Parish Council reported 27 properties flooded during the flood event. Internal flooding at properties on The Street was reported up to a depth of 15 cm. [TAB] (p2),27 properties; 15 cm internal flood depth [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_7_2021-08-01.html,GB-KEN_7_2021-08-01,Ulcombe flooding 01 Aug 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Ulcombe,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Ulcombe-on-1-August-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,7,2021-08-01,51.2110061,0.6444271,"<div id=""html_773504989cf665a35727fab75325ada5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ulcombe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage ditches, local watercourses and parts of the local sewer network were unable to cope with intense, localised rainfall, resulting in property flooding across Ulcombe and the surrounding area. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Survey responses and reports indicate properties within the village were flooded. Of the survey responses, five properties reported internal flooding and one reported external flooding. Within Ulcombe, The Street had 27 properties reported flooded (internal flooding reported up to 15 cm) and Pye Corner had 3 properties reported flooded. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 27 properties (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties internal; 1 property external; 27 properties; 3 properties; 15 cm internal flood depth (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Ulcombe,England,"Drainage ditches, local watercourses and parts of the local sewer network were unable to cope with intense, localised rainfall, resulting in property flooding across Ulcombe and the surrounding area. (p1)
Survey responses and reports indicate properties within the village were flooded. Of the survey responses, five properties reported internal flooding and one reported external flooding. Within Ulcombe, The Street had 27 properties reported flooded (internal flooding reported up to 15 cm) and Pye Corner had 3 properties reported flooded. (p2)","approximately 27 properties (p1)
5 properties internal; 1 property external; 27 properties; 3 properties; 15 cm internal flood depth (p2)",GB-KEN_7_2021-08-01.html,GB-KEN_7_2021-08-01,Ulcombe flooding 01 Aug 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Ulcombe,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Ulcombe-on-1-August-2021.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.682309,-1.1236769,"<div id=""html_eb1b55e620171cd3acf9748705dc848f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brading Marshes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The WLMP contains proposals to change water levels in the Brading Marshes which could have exacerbated flood risk to Brading; the hydraulic modelling underpinning the WLMP is reported as unclear and possibly insufficient to assess flood risk impacts. [TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Modelled flood water reached 0.65 m OD at the Brading Marshes (left bank) under the base&ndash;case, WLMP and mitigation scenarios (no change relative to the base case). [TAB,PAG] (p67)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Brading Marshes,England,"The WLMP contains proposals to change water levels in the Brading Marshes which could have exacerbated flood risk to Brading; the hydraulic modelling underpinning the WLMP is reported as unclear and possibly insufficient to assess flood risk impacts. [TAB] (p10)
Modelled flood water reached 0.65 m OD at the Brading Marshes (left bank) under the base–case, WLMP and mitigation scenarios (no change relative to the base case). [TAB,PAG] (p67)",,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.6684414,-1.212803,"<div id=""html_870483ea08b1ef6d90355ba4f677dbb4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Yar</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Yar and its lower reaches / tributaries are associated with flood risk in Brading. A small tributary/drainage channel that flows towards the Yar is linked to the flooding event that affected Nicholas Close on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,River Yar,England,"The River Yar and its lower reaches / tributaries are associated with flood risk in Brading. A small tributary/drainage channel that flows towards the Yar is linked to the flooding event that affected Nicholas Close on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p15)",,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.6720958,-1.1455718,"<div id=""html_f30160388feed0d28698e8fdd7b996a0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nicholas Close, Brading</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant pooling outside a property on Nicholas Close; flooding of the adjacent garden; high water levels in the nearby stream east of Nicholas Close. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties flooded; at least six properties were reported flooded during the event under investigation. Flooding at Nicholas Close has occurred on multiple occasions and is strongly influenced by elevated river levels and downstream/tidal controls. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The wastewater pumping station at Nicholas Close is specifically recommended for a review of condition and capacity as part of the Section 19 investigation following recurrent flooding in Brading, indicating the pumping station's relevance to the local flooding issues. [TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Repeated internal flooding of residential properties on Nicholas Close. [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recurrent flooding of a residential road; properties on Nicholas Close were inundated during the October 2023 event (part of Storm Babet). [PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on 25 October 2023 mainly affected the residential road Nicholas Close. A small tributary/drainage channel flows through the gardens of Nicholas Close towards the River Yar; the channel is culverted where it meets the railway northeast of the road. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on 25 October 2023 inundated the residential road and gardens of Nicholas Close as a small tributary/drainage channel that flows through the gardens overtopped. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in Nicholas Close on 25 October 2023. The flooding was strongly influenced by elevated river levels within a low&ndash;lying, flat catchment; downstream controls and tidal effects (tide locking) are noted as influential on upstream river levels, contributing to inundation. Multiple agencies responded to the event. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Repeated flooding of properties and gardens; residents had to move out of a flooded property for the majority of the year (Jan 2023); contaminated flood water was present and Southern Water deployed tankers to remove contaminated flood water on 25 Oct 2023. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties and gardens flooded; contaminated flood water was removed by Southern Water tankers; at least one household had to move out of a flooded property for the majority of the year. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Channel exceedance of the ordinary watercourse east of the residential area combined with surface water runoff from Morton Common and Morton Road caused pooling at the junction with Morton Road and flow down Nicholas Close, resulting in inundation of the residential area and significant flooding. EA officers and an incident response contractor carried out welfare checks and inspected for blockages (25 Oct&ndash;16 Nov 2023); no visible blockages were located. A silted Network Rail culvert (EW1&ndash;28) was noted as potentially exacerbating impacts. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water pooled at the junction with Morton Road and flowed down Nicholas Close. The culverted ordinary watercourse through gardens of Nicholas Close reached high water levels and overflowed into surrounding gardens. Blockage of Network Rail culvert EW1&ndash;28 by silt (last cleared three years prior) may have exacerbated the flooding and played a role in the event. [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from a culverted watercourse overflowed into surrounding gardens and properties. Six properties were affected on 25 October 2023, with at least two suffering internal flooding that forced residents to vacate. One property had garden, driveway and house submerged. Some residents (including an elderly couple) were displaced and required extensive repair works; furniture and personal possessions were destroyed. [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Southern Water pumping station at Nicholas Close flooded and caused sewage to leak into surrounding properties. [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Photograph and mapping show significant pooling/outside property inundation on Nicholas Close (street and property frontage flooded), indicating local surface water/urban flooding and contributing to disruption and economic impacts. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant standing water pooled across the front/yard of a residential property, with water up to the building foundation and garden features (flower pots, planters) partially submerged; a hose is present indicating active water removal efforts. [IMG,PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hydraulic modelling (base&ndash;case and WLMP scenarios) predicts flooding at Nos. 5 and 6 Nicholas Close. The WLMP scenario would slightly increase flood duration at Nicholas Close (notably increasing duration at No.5); the proposed mitigation scenario reduces the flooded area and reduces flood duration at No.5. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater in the area contained foul sewage; residents reported toilets not functioning during flood events. The nearby Network Rail culvert (EW1&ndash;28) adjacent to Nicholas Close has been reported silted and uncleared for around three years and could increase flood risk to properties if it becomes blocked. The condition and capacity of the Nicholas Close foul pumping station has been questioned and may be allowing sewage to enter floodwater. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding; the report concludes the flooding at Nicholas Close is unlikely to be solely linked to extreme rainfall and that antecedent conditions or other factors are likely drivers of the flooding. (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 6 properties [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Six properties; at least two properties internal flooding [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties; 2 properties with internal flooding; 1 property with garden, driveway and house submerged; 1 household displaced for at least 6 months [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (No.5 and No.6); duration change at No.5 described (increased under WLMP, reduced under mitigation) [IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,"Nicholas Close, Brading",England,"Significant pooling outside a property on Nicholas Close; flooding of the adjacent garden; high water levels in the nearby stream east of Nicholas Close. (p7)
Residential properties flooded; at least six properties were reported flooded during the event under investigation. Flooding at Nicholas Close has occurred on multiple occasions and is strongly influenced by elevated river levels and downstream/tidal controls. [MUL] (p9)
The wastewater pumping station at Nicholas Close is specifically recommended for a review of condition and capacity as part of the Section 19 investigation following recurrent flooding in Brading, indicating the pumping station's relevance to the local flooding issues. [TAB] (p10)
Repeated internal flooding of residential properties on Nicholas Close. [PAG,MUL] (p11)
Recurrent flooding of a residential road; properties on Nicholas Close were inundated during the October 2023 event (part of Storm Babet). [PAG,MUL] (p12)
Flooding on 25 October 2023 mainly affected the residential road Nicholas Close. A small tributary/drainage channel flows through the gardens of Nicholas Close towards the River Yar; the channel is culverted where it meets the railway northeast of the road. [IMG,PAG] (p15)
Flooding on 25 October 2023 inundated the residential road and gardens of Nicholas Close as a small tributary/drainage channel that flows through the gardens overtopped. [PAG] (p16)
Flooding occurred in Nicholas Close on 25 October 2023. The flooding was strongly influenced by elevated river levels within a low–lying, flat catchment; downstream controls and tidal effects (tide locking) are noted as influential on upstream river levels, contributing to inundation. Multiple agencies responded to the event. (p29)
Repeated flooding of properties and gardens; residents had to move out of a flooded property for the majority of the year (Jan 2023); contaminated flood water was present and Southern Water deployed tankers to remove contaminated flood water on 25 Oct 2023. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p30)
Properties and gardens flooded; contaminated flood water was removed by Southern Water tankers; at least one household had to move out of a flooded property for the majority of the year. [PAG] (p31)
Channel exceedance of the ordinary watercourse east of the residential area combined with surface water runoff from Morton Common and Morton Road caused pooling at the junction with Morton Road and flow down Nicholas Close, resulting in inundation of the residential area and significant flooding. EA officers and an incident response contractor carried out welfare checks and inspected for blockages (25 Oct–16 Nov 2023); no visible blockages were located. A silted Network Rail culvert (EW1–28) was noted as potentially exacerbating impacts. [PAG] (p33)
Surface water pooled at the junction with Morton Road and flowed down Nicholas Close. The culverted ordinary watercourse through gardens of Nicholas Close reached high water levels and overflowed into surrounding gardens. Blockage of Network Rail culvert EW1–28 by silt (last cleared three years prior) may have exacerbated the flooding and played a role in the event. [PAG] (p34)
Flooding from a culverted watercourse overflowed into surrounding gardens and properties. Six properties were affected on 25 October 2023, with at least two suffering internal flooding that forced residents to vacate. One property had garden, driveway and house submerged. Some residents (including an elderly couple) were displaced and required extensive repair works; furniture and personal possessions were destroyed. [PAG] (p35)
Southern Water pumping station at Nicholas Close flooded and caused sewage to leak into surrounding properties. [PAG] (p37)
Photograph and mapping show significant pooling/outside property inundation on Nicholas Close (street and property frontage flooded), indicating local surface water/urban flooding and contributing to disruption and economic impacts. [IMG,PAG] (p38)
Significant standing water pooled across the front/yard of a residential property, with water up to the building foundation and garden features (flower pots, planters) partially submerged; a hose is present indicating active water removal efforts. [IMG,PAG] (p39)
Hydraulic modelling (base–case and WLMP scenarios) predicts flooding at Nos. 5 and 6 Nicholas Close. The WLMP scenario would slightly increase flood duration at Nicholas Close (notably increasing duration at No.5); the proposed mitigation scenario reduces the flooded area and reduces flood duration at No.5. [IMG] (p41)
Floodwater in the area contained foul sewage; residents reported toilets not functioning during flood events. The nearby Network Rail culvert (EW1–28) adjacent to Nicholas Close has been reported silted and uncleared for around three years and could increase flood risk to properties if it becomes blocked. The condition and capacity of the Nicholas Close foul pumping station has been questioned and may be allowing sewage to enter floodwater. (p49)
Experienced flooding; the report concludes the flooding at Nicholas Close is unlikely to be solely linked to extreme rainfall and that antecedent conditions or other factors are likely drivers of the flooding. (p50)","at least 6 properties [MUL] (p9)
6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p11)
at least 6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p12)
Six properties; at least two properties internal flooding [PAG] (p34)
6 properties; 2 properties with internal flooding; 1 property with garden, driveway and house submerged; 1 household displaced for at least 6 months [PAG] (p35)
2 properties (No.5 and No.6); duration change at No.5 described (increased under WLMP, reduced under mitigation) [IMG] (p41)",GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.6677572,-1.1506248,"<div id=""html_3d41c056cd1b6c5c58947538a59b5cf6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Morton Common, Brading</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Repeat flooding along the A3055 at Morton Common causing disruption to a major transport link to Sandown and resulting in road closures. [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred along Morton Common during the October 2023 event. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was recorded at the A3055 (Morton Common) as shown on the map (red star). The location is in a low&ndash;lying area and the event affected the road alignment at Morton Common. [IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in Morton Common on 25 October 2023. The inundation was strongly influenced by elevated river levels in a very low&ndash;lying, flat catchment; downstream controls and tidal influences (tide locking) are identified as highly influential on upstream river levels, contributing to the flooding and its persistence. Multiple agencies responded to the flood event. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reports of flooding affected the A3055 at Morton Common and caused road closures on multiple occasions; transport infrastructure was disrupted. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused closures of the A3055 at Morton Common. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Morton Common (A3055) was reported closed due to flooding. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed from Morton Common towards the ordinary watercourse east of Nicholas Close, contributing to channel exceedance and the inundation of the residential area during the flood event. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A3055 at Morton Common has flooded repeatedly, causing significant disruption and economic impacts due to road closures on multiple dates. [MUL] (p37)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 reports of flooding and road closures of A3055 at Morton Common. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 road closures [MUL] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,"Morton Common, Brading",England,"Repeat flooding along the A3055 at Morton Common causing disruption to a major transport link to Sandown and resulting in road closures. [MUL] (p11)
Flooding occurred along Morton Common during the October 2023 event. [PAG] (p12)
Flooding was recorded at the A3055 (Morton Common) as shown on the map (red star). The location is in a low–lying area and the event affected the road alignment at Morton Common. [IMG] (p15)
Flooding occurred in Morton Common on 25 October 2023. The inundation was strongly influenced by elevated river levels in a very low–lying, flat catchment; downstream controls and tidal influences (tide locking) are identified as highly influential on upstream river levels, contributing to the flooding and its persistence. Multiple agencies responded to the flood event. (p29)
Reports of flooding affected the A3055 at Morton Common and caused road closures on multiple occasions; transport infrastructure was disrupted. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p30)
Flooding caused closures of the A3055 at Morton Common. [PAG] (p31)
Morton Common (A3055) was reported closed due to flooding. (p32)
Surface water flowed from Morton Common towards the ordinary watercourse east of Nicholas Close, contributing to channel exceedance and the inundation of the residential area during the flood event. (p33)
The A3055 at Morton Common has flooded repeatedly, causing significant disruption and economic impacts due to road closures on multiple dates. [MUL] (p37)","6 reports of flooding and road closures of A3055 at Morton Common. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p30)
6 road closures [MUL] (p37)",GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.6708316,-1.1476613,"<div id=""html_eba17681f29d9f65c4a2c070e9ca6da8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Morton Road, Brading</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Repeat flooding along the A3055 at Morton Road causing disruption to a major transport link to Sandown and resulting in road closures. [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water pooled at the junction with Morton Road before flowing down Nicholas Close, contributing to channel exceedance and subsequent inundation of the residential area during the flood event. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,"Morton Road, Brading",England,"Repeat flooding along the A3055 at Morton Road causing disruption to a major transport link to Sandown and resulting in road closures. [MUL] (p11)
Surface water pooled at the junction with Morton Road before flowing down Nicholas Close, contributing to channel exceedance and subsequent inundation of the residential area during the flood event. (p33)",,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.67108245,-1.3328042802764226,"<div id=""html_4514cf832457886bc03db9a500ce92fb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Isle of Wight</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding on the Isle of Wight during October&ndash;November 2023, including at least six properties in Nicholas Close, Brading; these events were investigated as part of the Section 19 report. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread road closures around the Island were reported due to flooding on 25 October 2023. (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Isle of Wight,England,"Property flooding on the Isle of Wight during October–November 2023, including at least six properties in Nicholas Close, Brading; these events were investigated as part of the Section 19 report. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
Widespread road closures around the Island were reported due to flooding on 25 October 2023. (p32)","at least 6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p9)",GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.68121,-1.142232,"<div id=""html_82b3f3d72841feb346ec0c1c93891616"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brading</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding in the town, including at least six residential properties in Nicholas Close on 25 October 2023; frequent highway flooding affecting the A3055 at Morton Common with multiple road closures over the past year. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recurrent flooding in the town, including internal flooding of residential properties in Nicholas Close and disruption to the A3055 transport link (Morton Common / Morton Road) causing road closures. [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the town were affected during the Storm Babet floods, notably Nicholas Close (recurrent property flooding) and Morton Common which flooded on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on 25 October 2023 mainly affected Nicholas Close, a residential road in Brading. A small tributary/drainage channel that flows through gardens towards the River Yar is associated with the incident (the channel is culverted where it meets the railway). [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Brading were affected by flooding, notably Nicholas Close where residential properties and gardens were inundated on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Brading Marshes were inundated during a period of significant flooding; floodwaters affected the marshland and extend into low&ndash;lying parts of Brading as shown in Figure 2&ndash;2 and the flood map. [IMG,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A3055 in Brading flooded on multiple occasions, resulting in road closures on the listed dates. [PAG,MUL] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recurrent flooding at Brading is reported; the report assessed a range of past flood events and analysed the storm event on 24 and 25 October 2023 that affected the area. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recurrent flooding in Brading connected with multiple historical rainfall events. Antecedent conditions (soil saturation) and tide locking in the Brading Marshes are identified as likely contributors to flooding. The event on 25 October 2023 is described as extreme and infrequent (FEH estimate ~1 in 322) and is linked to flooding in the Brading area. [PAG,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Long history of flooding in the town area, including repeated reports of flooding affecting Nicholas Close (properties and gardens) and repeated flood reports/road closures at the A3055 at Morton Common. [TAB,MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Garden and property flooding in Nicholas Close (residents had to move out of a flooded property for the majority of the year); road flooding led to closure of the A3055 at Morton Common; contaminated flood water was removed from Nicholas Close by Southern Water. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on Morton Common (A3055) within Brading resulted in the road being closed. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Text notes tide locking from the Brading Marshes may have contributed to the flooding by causing higher water levels/backwater effects; this contributed to the flooding mechanisms affecting Nicholas Close and surrounding areas and to wider disruption and economic impacts. [PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties. (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 6 properties; 5 recorded flood events in Brading since November 2000 [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 6 properties [IMG,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 road closure incidents [PAG,MUL] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 6 properties (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Brading,England,"Property flooding in the town, including at least six residential properties in Nicholas Close on 25 October 2023; frequent highway flooding affecting the A3055 at Morton Common with multiple road closures over the past year. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
Recurrent flooding in the town, including internal flooding of residential properties in Nicholas Close and disruption to the A3055 transport link (Morton Common / Morton Road) causing road closures. [PAG,MUL] (p11)
Parts of the town were affected during the Storm Babet floods, notably Nicholas Close (recurrent property flooding) and Morton Common which flooded on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p12)
Flooding on 25 October 2023 mainly affected Nicholas Close, a residential road in Brading. A small tributary/drainage channel that flows through gardens towards the River Yar is associated with the incident (the channel is culverted where it meets the railway). [IMG,PAG] (p15)
Parts of Brading were affected by flooding, notably Nicholas Close where residential properties and gardens were inundated on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p16)
Brading Marshes were inundated during a period of significant flooding; floodwaters affected the marshland and extend into low–lying parts of Brading as shown in Figure 2–2 and the flood map. [IMG,PAG] (p17)
The A3055 in Brading flooded on multiple occasions, resulting in road closures on the listed dates. [PAG,MUL] (p22)
Recurrent flooding at Brading is reported; the report assessed a range of past flood events and analysed the storm event on 24 and 25 October 2023 that affected the area. [PAG] (p25)
Recurrent flooding in Brading connected with multiple historical rainfall events. Antecedent conditions (soil saturation) and tide locking in the Brading Marshes are identified as likely contributors to flooding. The event on 25 October 2023 is described as extreme and infrequent (FEH estimate ~1 in 322) and is linked to flooding in the Brading area. [PAG,MUL] (p26)
Long history of flooding in the town area, including repeated reports of flooding affecting Nicholas Close (properties and gardens) and repeated flood reports/road closures at the A3055 at Morton Common. [TAB,MUL] (p30)
Garden and property flooding in Nicholas Close (residents had to move out of a flooded property for the majority of the year); road flooding led to closure of the A3055 at Morton Common; contaminated flood water was removed from Nicholas Close by Southern Water. [PAG] (p31)
Flooding on Morton Common (A3055) within Brading resulted in the road being closed. (p32)
Text notes tide locking from the Brading Marshes may have contributed to the flooding by causing higher water levels/backwater effects; this contributed to the flooding mechanisms affecting Nicholas Close and surrounding areas and to wider disruption and economic impacts. [PAG] (p38)
Internal flooding of residential properties. (p50)","at least 6 properties; 5 recorded flood events in Brading since November 2000 [PAG,MUL] (p9)
6 properties [PAG,MUL] (p11)
at least 6 properties [IMG,PAG] (p12)
6 road closure incidents [PAG,MUL] (p22)
at least 6 properties (p50)",GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.674091145822096,-1.1457988995675068,"<div id=""html_fbf40bd29554a847f7cf51796a9700e9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Morton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred along Morton Common during the October 2023 event; community survey responses indicate Morton Common flooded on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map legend and image description mark flooding at the A3055 (Morton Common) within the Morton area (red star). This indicates inundation occurred at the A3055 / Morton Common location during the event, affecting the road and adjacent low&ndash;lying land in the Morton area. [IMG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Morton,England,"Flooding occurred along Morton Common during the October 2023 event; community survey responses indicate Morton Common flooded on 25 October 2023. [IMG,PAG] (p12)
Map legend and image description mark flooding at the A3055 (Morton Common) within the Morton area (red star). This indicates inundation occurred at the A3055 / Morton Common location during the event, affecting the road and adjacent low–lying land in the Morton area. [IMG] (p15)",,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.6644696,-1.1338788,"<div id=""html_23c162f38a39df0f54680cd19dfa4bc1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yaverland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodplain water levels rose during the event (peak around 1.5 m OD) but did not exceed the residential property threshold levels for Yaverland properties, so no residential property flooding was recorded. [IMG,PAG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Modelled floodwater reached levels of 1.54 m OD (base case), 1.55 m OD (WLMP) and 1.54 m OD (mitigation) at the Yaverland assessment point. [TAB,PAG] (p67)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Yaverland,England,"Floodplain water levels rose during the event (peak around 1.5 m OD) but did not exceed the residential property threshold levels for Yaverland properties, so no residential property flooding was recorded. [IMG,PAG] (p66)
Modelled floodwater reached levels of 1.54 m OD (base case), 1.55 m OD (WLMP) and 1.54 m OD (mitigation) at the Yaverland assessment point. [TAB,PAG] (p67)",,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,1,2023-10-25,50.664448,-1.1493748,"<div id=""html_5f7bd08b785f4b6671469180a4ada440"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sandown Wastewater Treatment Works</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Received contaminated flood water transferred from Nicholas Close as part of the response (Southern Water deployed tankers to remove and transfer contaminated flood water to the works). (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Sandown Wastewater Treatment Works,England,Received contaminated flood water transferred from Nicholas Close as part of the response (Southern Water deployed tankers to remove and transfer contaminated flood water to the works). (p31),,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_1_2023-10-25,Brading flooding 25 Oct 2023,Brading Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Brading Oct 2024.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.441582321443995,-0.10692611855447572,"<div id=""html_f42768cd520e08752b2df20827423b66"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ramsey Golf and Bowls Club</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding occurred on the golf course; the Town Council and Middle Level Commissioners highlighted flooding at the golf course and noted properties have reported flooding in this location in the past (south of Hollow Lane). Flooding in this locality may affect the wider catchment and contribute to property flooding nearby. [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Ramsey Golf and Bowls Club,England,Fluvial flooding occurred on the golf course; the Town Council and Middle Level Commissioners highlighted flooding at the golf course and noted properties have reported flooding in this location in the past (south of Hollow Lane). Flooding in this locality may affect the wider catchment and contribute to property flooding nearby. [TAB] (p12),,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.443919,-0.121826,"<div id=""html_4de0881d149dcbe456eb65bf22edce36"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Slade Dyke</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding caused internal flooding in the Slade Dyke area, including Field Road and Slade Close; emergency pumping was used to protect properties. [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Open section of the dyke exceeded channel capacity; blockages (fly&ndash;tipped waste, security/trash screen blockage and wild&ndash;seeded vegetation) reduced channel storage and caused water to flow overland following the culverted watercourse. This overland flow routed water along Field Road and Slade Close and was the source of downstream property flooding. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Open section exceeded channel capacity due to high flows, culvert/screen blockage (from large volumes of fly&ndash;tipped material and vegetation) and inadequate conveyance, leading to overland flow along the culverted route and inundation of properties on Field Road and Slade Close; Fire Service attended and pumped to protect some properties. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Obstructions at the Slade Dyke trash screen and issues with vegetation, debris and siltation are reducing discharge efficiency into the culvert, causing drainage issues that contribute to local flooding risk and require riparian maintenance. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fly tipping in Slade Dyke exacerbated the rapid&ndash;runoff surface water flooding, worsening flooding impacts in Ramsey. (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 46 properties [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Approximately 46 properties internally flooded in the Slade Dyke area (area total). [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Slade Dyke,England,"Surface water flooding caused internal flooding in the Slade Dyke area, including Field Road and Slade Close; emergency pumping was used to protect properties. [PAG,IMG] (p13)
Open section of the dyke exceeded channel capacity; blockages (fly–tipped waste, security/trash screen blockage and wild–seeded vegetation) reduced channel storage and caused water to flow overland following the culverted watercourse. This overland flow routed water along Field Road and Slade Close and was the source of downstream property flooding. (p14)
Open section exceeded channel capacity due to high flows, culvert/screen blockage (from large volumes of fly–tipped material and vegetation) and inadequate conveyance, leading to overland flow along the culverted route and inundation of properties on Field Road and Slade Close; Fire Service attended and pumped to protect some properties. [PAG,IMG] (p15)
Obstructions at the Slade Dyke trash screen and issues with vegetation, debris and siltation are reducing discharge efficiency into the culvert, causing drainage issues that contribute to local flooding risk and require riparian maintenance. [PAG] (p21)
Fly tipping in Slade Dyke exacerbated the rapid–runoff surface water flooding, worsening flooding impacts in Ramsey. (p28)","approximately 46 properties [PAG,IMG] (p13)
Approximately 46 properties internally flooded in the Slade Dyke area (area total). [PAG,IMG] (p15)",GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.4511137,-0.1084597,"<div id=""html_c468a447d1bea7076f554e689b23ad2b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Lode</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Some flooding occurred within the fluvial flood plain of High Lode when the channel exceeded capacity, contributing to local inundation. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High Lode experienced prolonged high river levels that caused fluvial flooding and prevented ordinary watercourses, highway gullies and surface water sewers from discharging efficiently into the river, exacerbating surface water flooding in areas north and south of Ramsey. [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted section between Field Road and High Lode has limited capacity; high water levels in High Lode reduce discharge efficiency and, together with vegetation, debris and siltation, contribute to drainage problems and local flooding risk. (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,High Lode,England,"Some flooding occurred within the fluvial flood plain of High Lode when the channel exceeded capacity, contributing to local inundation. (p9)
High Lode experienced prolonged high river levels that caused fluvial flooding and prevented ordinary watercourses, highway gullies and surface water sewers from discharging efficiently into the river, exacerbating surface water flooding in areas north and south of Ramsey. [PAG,IMG] (p17)
The culverted section between Field Road and High Lode has limited capacity; high water levels in High Lode reduce discharge efficiency and, together with vegetation, debris and siltation, contribute to drainage problems and local flooding risk. (p21)",,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.3979263,-0.1131182,"<div id=""html_7e8941a7b19a789d758fc29456b04e88"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bury Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bury Brook is mapped as Flood Zone 3 adjacent to the flooded High Street/Lion Yard. A throttle point exists where Bury Brook passes into the Great Whyte Tunnels culvert through Ramsey; the culvert entrance is judged inadequate to receive the volume and velocity of flows during an exceptionally high rainfall event, increasing flood risk to upstream and nearby properties (some of which reported internal flooding on 23 December 2020). (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Bury Brook,England,"Bury Brook is mapped as Flood Zone 3 adjacent to the flooded High Street/Lion Yard. A throttle point exists where Bury Brook passes into the Great Whyte Tunnels culvert through Ramsey; the culvert entrance is judged inadequate to receive the volume and velocity of flows during an exceptionally high rainfall event, increasing flood risk to upstream and nearby properties (some of which reported internal flooding on 23 December 2020). (p16)",,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.4509134,-0.1133938,"<div id=""html_41c22782b0474e889c4ce63e0f969128"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Slade Close, Ramsey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties experienced internal flooding and gardens were flooded. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported; external flooding affecting gardens. [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding caused internal flooding to properties in the Slade Dyke area; Fire Service attended and pumped to protect some properties. [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland surface water flowed when the open section of Slade Dyke exceeded capacity and water followed the culverted watercourse along Slade Close. Properties were internally flooded and gardens/outbuildings inundated; Fire Service attended and were able to protect some properties by pumping. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Open section of Slade Dyke exceeded channel capacity and a blocked culvert/security screen caused water to flow overland along the culverted watercourse into Field Road and Slade Close. Resulting surface water and fluvial/overland flooding caused internal flooding of properties, inundation of gardens and outbuildings, and displacement of residents; Fire Service attended and pumped to protect some properties. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>c.25 properties [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Internal &ndash; c.46 properties (combined Slade Close c.25; Field Road c.19) [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 46 properties (area total); properties internally up to a depth of c.30cm; gardens and outbuildings up to a depth of c.60cm; 3 residents displaced for up to 1 month; 1 resident displaced for longer than 1 month [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 46 properties; Field Road: properties internally up to c.30cm, gardens/outbuildings up to c.90cm; Slade Close: properties internally up to c.30cm, gardens/outbuildings up to c.60cm; displacement reports: 2 residents (Field Road) &gt;1 month; 3 residents (Slade Close) up to 1 month; 1 resident (Slade Close) &gt;1 month. [PAG,IMG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Slade Close, Ramsey",England,"Properties experienced internal flooding and gardens were flooded. [TAB] (p11)
Internal property flooding reported; external flooding affecting gardens. [TAB,PAG] (p12)
