GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.4475395,-0.1073302,"<div id=""html_ab3c661eebc79292b31ab12f74cd753d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lion Yard, Ramsey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding affected properties (external flooding reported). [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lion Yard (near the flooded section of High Street) experienced internal property flooding reported in the same event on 23 December 2020; area is close to Bury Brook and the culvert entrance which increases flood risk. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Lion Yard, Ramsey",England,"External flooding affected properties (external flooding reported). [TAB] (p12)
Lion Yard (near the flooded section of High Street) experienced internal property flooding reported in the same event on 23 December 2020; area is close to Bury Brook and the culvert entrance which increases flood risk. (p16)",,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.4536185,-0.1113472,"<div id=""html_d1ba685f9923838fc32982d899cf7eec"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Great Whyte, Ramsey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert (Great Whyte Tunnels) acted as a throttle point for Bury Brook as it passes into the culvert through Ramsey, increasing upstream flood risk. The culvert entrance was inadequate to accept the volume and velocity of flows during an exceptionally high rainfall event, contributing to internal property flooding on High Street and Lion Yard. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert at Great Whyte Tunnels is a throttle point for flows (Bury Brook) and during an exceptionally high rainfall event the culvert entrance is inadequate to receive the volume and velocity of flows, increasing the risk of upstream surcharge and internal property flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Great Whyte, Ramsey",England,"The culvert (Great Whyte Tunnels) acted as a throttle point for Bury Brook as it passes into the culvert through Ramsey, increasing upstream flood risk. The culvert entrance was inadequate to accept the volume and velocity of flows during an exceptionally high rainfall event, contributing to internal property flooding on High Street and Lion Yard. (p16)
The culvert at Great Whyte Tunnels is a throttle point for flows (Bury Brook) and during an exceptionally high rainfall event the culvert entrance is inadequate to receive the volume and velocity of flows, increasing the risk of upstream surcharge and internal property flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p17)",,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.4617238,0.6975642,"<div id=""html_e21326ecf283462fd42be2ae13e783ce"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Anglia</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A prolonged period of higher&ndash;than&ndash;average rainfall (including December 2020) led to saturated ground and a high water&ndash;table across East Anglia, overwhelming surface water drainage systems and contributing to surface water flooding and elevated groundwater levels that affected the region. [PAG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,East Anglia,England,"A prolonged period of higher–than–average rainfall (including December 2020) led to saturated ground and a high water–table across East Anglia, overwhelming surface water drainage systems and contributing to surface water flooding and elevated groundwater levels that affected the region. [PAG] (p9)",,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,20,2020-12-23,52.4494068,-0.1021136,"<div id=""html_39b1b4be4ebe7b3aa9ce794932fbeb71"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ramsey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Large areas of Ramsey (north and south) are at risk from fluvial flooding from High Lode; surface water flooding also affects many areas of the town. During the extreme rainfall event on 23 December 2020 the already&ndash;saturated catchment caused flooding in and upstream of the Ramsey area, placing significant pressure on drainage systems and reducing the ability of watercourses, gullies and sewers to discharge. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall on 23 December 2020 on an already saturated catchment put significant pressure on drainage systems in and upstream of the Ramsey area. Surface water drainage systems were unable to convey water, causing excess surface water to flow over ground and flood properties in areas at risk of surface water flooding; high groundwater also entered property basements. Some flooding occurred within the nearby fluvial flood plain where channel capacity was exceeded. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local community reported multiple flood incidents to the LLFA: internal and external property flooding across the town, including internal property flooding of residences and flooding of gardens, garages and outbuildings. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two key flood risk areas within the town (Slade Dyke inc. Field Road and Slade Close; High Street) experienced flooding, resulting in internal and external property flooding across the town. [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant surface water flooding caused by rapid runoff from prolonged higher&ndash;than&ndash;average autumn rainfall followed by intense rainfall on 23 December 2020; flooding was exacerbated by fly tipping in Slade Dyke. The event prompted actions including highway drainage improvements, completion of new drainage systems, and upholding riparian maintenance responsibilities; Risk Management Authorities and the LLFA committed to ongoing projects and support for Land Drainage Consent applications. (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>26 flood reports; 11 properties reported flooded internally (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>c.52 properties [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Ramsey,England,"Large areas of Ramsey (north and south) are at risk from fluvial flooding from High Lode; surface water flooding also affects many areas of the town. During the extreme rainfall event on 23 December 2020 the already–saturated catchment caused flooding in and upstream of the Ramsey area, placing significant pressure on drainage systems and reducing the ability of watercourses, gullies and sewers to discharge. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
Extreme rainfall on 23 December 2020 on an already saturated catchment put significant pressure on drainage systems in and upstream of the Ramsey area. Surface water drainage systems were unable to convey water, causing excess surface water to flow over ground and flood properties in areas at risk of surface water flooding; high groundwater also entered property basements. Some flooding occurred within the nearby fluvial flood plain where channel capacity was exceeded. (p9)
Local community reported multiple flood incidents to the LLFA: internal and external property flooding across the town, including internal property flooding of residences and flooding of gardens, garages and outbuildings. (p11)
Two key flood risk areas within the town (Slade Dyke inc. Field Road and Slade Close; High Street) experienced flooding, resulting in internal and external property flooding across the town. [PAG,IMG] (p13)
Significant surface water flooding caused by rapid runoff from prolonged higher–than–average autumn rainfall followed by intense rainfall on 23 December 2020; flooding was exacerbated by fly tipping in Slade Dyke. The event prompted actions including highway drainage improvements, completion of new drainage systems, and upholding riparian maintenance responsibilities; Risk Management Authorities and the LLFA committed to ongoing projects and support for Land Drainage Consent applications. (p28)","26 flood reports; 11 properties reported flooded internally (p11)
c.52 properties [PAG,IMG] (p13)",GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_20_2020-12-23,Ramsey flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Ramsey,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Ramsey-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-NNH,36,2013-10-28,52.4812301,-0.4894626,"<div id=""html_235a63eb497423f467025f374eaf3e95"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lyveden Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of Lyveden Brook during the incident caused high risk of internal flooding to residential properties; damage and loss occurred due to the depth and velocity of the overtopping water. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inspection during the flood investigation found a severe narrowing of the watercourse upstream of Stoke Doyle Road with a section of barbed wire across the channel and a build&ndash;up of debris, indicating obstruction and elevated flow conditions; downstream reaches were described as narrow and shallow and the brook flows to the River Nene. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Depth and flow of water from Lyveden Brook caused flooding that affected people and property during the event; listed as the cause of various incidents of flooding on 28/10/2013. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Banks overtopped; reported to be over one metre higher than normal and flowing strongly. High water from the brook caused high&ndash;velocity overland flow across garden boundaries at Warren Bridge, led to significant damage to outbuildings, came close to internally flooding a residential property, and high levels upstream affected sports facilities (closure). (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An excessive flow in Lyveden Brook caused raised water levels and strong flows that overtopped banks and affected adjoining property; reductions in the cross&ndash;sectional area of the watercourse are identified as a factor in the high velocity and depth of flow. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reductions to the cross&ndash;sectional area of the watercourse and man&ndash;made flow controls upstream and downstream of Warren Bridge increased flow velocity and water depth, contributing to flooding at this location; these factors were identified for further investigation by the responsible owners. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping / insufficient channel capacity from Lyveden Brook contributed to flooding in the town catchment, prompting recommendations to reinstate historic drainage (re&ndash;excavate ditches, repair/replace piped/culvert sections, dredge and trim watercourses, replace structures and clear vegetation) to reduce future flood risk. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lyveden Brook overtopped its banks and discharged muddy floodwater into adjacent private gardens and yards, submerging vegetation and enabling water to reach house bases. [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lyveden Brook was swollen and overflowed its banks, producing muddy, high&ndash;velocity flow that submerged the bases of trees and shrubs and inundated the rear of a nearby property and gardens. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lyveden Brook overflowed its banks, inundating adjacent vegetation and land; trees and shrubs were partially submerged and water encroached onto the embankment. [IMG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels observed along Lyveden Brook at the rear of Warren Bridge. Evidence of elevated flows and deposits of debris at a public sewer outfall opposite Warren Bridge. [IMG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels were observed at a public sewer outfall opposite Warren Bridge, with deposits of debris indicating the brook reached elevated levels and overtopped or reached infrastructure margins. [IMG,PAG] (p51)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over one metre higher than normal; approximately two days (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Lyveden Brook,England,"Overtopping of Lyveden Brook during the incident caused high risk of internal flooding to residential properties; damage and loss occurred due to the depth and velocity of the overtopping water. [PAG] (p9)
Inspection during the flood investigation found a severe narrowing of the watercourse upstream of Stoke Doyle Road with a section of barbed wire across the channel and a build–up of debris, indicating obstruction and elevated flow conditions; downstream reaches were described as narrow and shallow and the brook flows to the River Nene. [PAG] (p10)
Depth and flow of water from Lyveden Brook caused flooding that affected people and property during the event; listed as the cause of various incidents of flooding on 28/10/2013. [PAG] (p11)
Banks overtopped; reported to be over one metre higher than normal and flowing strongly. High water from the brook caused high–velocity overland flow across garden boundaries at Warren Bridge, led to significant damage to outbuildings, came close to internally flooding a residential property, and high levels upstream affected sports facilities (closure). (p12)
An excessive flow in Lyveden Brook caused raised water levels and strong flows that overtopped banks and affected adjoining property; reductions in the cross–sectional area of the watercourse are identified as a factor in the high velocity and depth of flow. (p17)
Reductions to the cross–sectional area of the watercourse and man–made flow controls upstream and downstream of Warren Bridge increased flow velocity and water depth, contributing to flooding at this location; these factors were identified for further investigation by the responsible owners. (p18)
Overtopping / insufficient channel capacity from Lyveden Brook contributed to flooding in the town catchment, prompting recommendations to reinstate historic drainage (re–excavate ditches, repair/replace piped/culvert sections, dredge and trim watercourses, replace structures and clear vegetation) to reduce future flood risk. [PAG] (p19)
Lyveden Brook overtopped its banks and discharged muddy floodwater into adjacent private gardens and yards, submerging vegetation and enabling water to reach house bases. [IMG,PAG] (p44)
Lyveden Brook was swollen and overflowed its banks, producing muddy, high–velocity flow that submerged the bases of trees and shrubs and inundated the rear of a nearby property and gardens. [IMG,PAG] (p45)
Lyveden Brook overflowed its banks, inundating adjacent vegetation and land; trees and shrubs were partially submerged and water encroached onto the embankment. [IMG] (p47)
High water levels observed along Lyveden Brook at the rear of Warren Bridge. Evidence of elevated flows and deposits of debris at a public sewer outfall opposite Warren Bridge. [IMG] (p49)
High water levels were observed at a public sewer outfall opposite Warren Bridge, with deposits of debris indicating the brook reached elevated levels and overtopped or reached infrastructure margins. [IMG,PAG] (p51)",over one metre higher than normal; approximately two days (p12),GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28.html,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28,Oundle flooding 28 Oct 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 28th October 2013",090/North Northamptonshire/Oundle-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,36,2013-10-28,52.4812271,-0.5088718,"<div id=""html_1d947627888d7befdb3b519c41d2cec6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Biggin Fish Pond</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the flood investigation the inflow to the silt pond was observed to be at a high level and there was evidence that water levels had been higher, with debris deposited around the pond banks; the main lake discharges via a weir and spillway when levels exceed the weir capacity and some historic outlet structures east of the lake were found disused or non&ndash;operational. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a feature to inspect after the 28 October 2013 flood incident: the Environment Agency was asked to review inspection and supervision under the Reservoirs Act 1975 and to consider an immediate inspection to assess current flow controls, water and silt levels, and any recent changes to sluice/overflow arrangements that could have affected downstream flows. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The pond spilled over its grassy spillway/overflow and discharged water via the main dam/weir into Lyveden Brook. Photographs show the spillway overtopping but the embankment appeared vegetated and without obvious erosion. [IMG,PAG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Elevated water levels in the upstream silt pond caused pond water to encroach onto and partially flood a woodland/dirt path alongside the pond. Photographs show the waterline reaching the path and high water at the point where a watercourse enters the upstream silt pond (stone culvert/bridge). [IMG,PAG] (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Biggin Fish Pond,England,"During the flood investigation the inflow to the silt pond was observed to be at a high level and there was evidence that water levels had been higher, with debris deposited around the pond banks; the main lake discharges via a weir and spillway when levels exceed the weir capacity and some historic outlet structures east of the lake were found disused or non–operational. (p10)
Identified as a feature to inspect after the 28 October 2013 flood incident: the Environment Agency was asked to review inspection and supervision under the Reservoirs Act 1975 and to consider an immediate inspection to assess current flow controls, water and silt levels, and any recent changes to sluice/overflow arrangements that could have affected downstream flows. (p20)
The pond spilled over its grassy spillway/overflow and discharged water via the main dam/weir into Lyveden Brook. Photographs show the spillway overtopping but the embankment appeared vegetated and without obvious erosion. [IMG,PAG] (p53)
Elevated water levels in the upstream silt pond caused pond water to encroach onto and partially flood a woodland/dirt path alongside the pond. Photographs show the waterline reaching the path and high water at the point where a watercourse enters the upstream silt pond (stone culvert/bridge). [IMG,PAG] (p58)",,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28.html,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28,Oundle flooding 28 Oct 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 28th October 2013",090/North Northamptonshire/Oundle-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,36,2013-10-28,52.56628789856925,-0.2027211766370756,"<div id=""html_ec21dd696e45361fa6548d90f5e8ee7b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Nene</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Elevated water levels in the River Nene in the vicinity of the incident were relevant to the flood event and could affect flows from other watercourses into the river, potentially contributing to downstream flooding. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,River Nene,England,"Elevated water levels in the River Nene in the vicinity of the incident were relevant to the flood event and could affect flows from other watercourses into the river, potentially contributing to downstream flooding. (p20)",,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28.html,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28,Oundle flooding 28 Oct 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 28th October 2013",090/North Northamptonshire/Oundle-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,36,2013-10-28,52.4802145,-0.4788803,"<div id=""html_25919c114c197ca94fe0044280ff8a7a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Warren Bridge, Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of gardens at Warren Bridge and significant damage to outbuildings at a residential property (impacts recorded for the 28/10/2013 event). The location also experienced garden flooding on 04/11/2012 and 22/12/2012, and internal flooding of at least one residential property in Spring 1998. [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear gardens became flooded; varying risk to dwellings depending on floor level. High&ndash;velocity flow across garden boundaries caused significant damage to outbuildings and nearly internally flooded a residential property. Land opposite the rear gardens was also flooded. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Man&ndash;made controls of the natural flow upstream and downstream of Warren Bridge, and reductions in the watercourse cross&ndash;sectional area in that area, are identified as contributing factors to the high velocity and depth of water during the flood. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Muddy floodwater pooled up to the house wall at the property by Warren Bridge and partially entered the adjoining garage, affecting access and property safety. [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear garden and patio inundated by floodwater (Lyveden Brook adjacent); outdoor furniture and potted plants partially submerged; a summer house was moved/rotated on its footings and the patio area was submerged by muddy water. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from Lyveden Brook overtopped nearby banks and inundated rear gardens adjacent to Warren Bridge; water reached up to the base of houses and garden areas, moving/rotating a summer house on its footings and submerging garden structures. [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area at the rear of a property adjacent to Warren Bridge was inundated when Lyveden Brook overflowed; high&ndash;velocity floodwater flowed from a neighbouring garden into the property's rear/passageway, threatening structures and submerging vegetation. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High&ndash;velocity, turbulent muddy water flowed from a neighbouring property's garden into the rear of a property at Warren Bridge, passing forcefully through a wooden fence and inundating the rear area and narrow passage. [PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High&ndash;velocity flow of floodwater entered the rear garden from neighbouring property; garden and grounds were inundated and floodwater reached and surrounded a small outbuilding at the rear of the property. [IMG,PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels recorded at the rear of Warren Bridge; nearby public sewer outfall opposite the bridge showed evidence of high water and deposits of debris. [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Public sewer outfall opposite Warren Bridge was nearly submerged (water level near top of brick structure) with deposits of debris; Lyveden Brook at the rear of Warren Bridge also showed evidence of high water levels and debris deposits. [IMG,PAG] (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property (damage to outbuildings on 28/10/2013); 1+ residential property (internal flooding in Spring 1998) [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Warren Bridge, Oundle",England,"Flooding of gardens at Warren Bridge and significant damage to outbuildings at a residential property (impacts recorded for the 28/10/2013 event). The location also experienced garden flooding on 04/11/2012 and 22/12/2012, and internal flooding of at least one residential property in Spring 1998. [TAB,MUL] (p11)
Rear gardens became flooded; varying risk to dwellings depending on floor level. High–velocity flow across garden boundaries caused significant damage to outbuildings and nearly internally flooded a residential property. Land opposite the rear gardens was also flooded. (p12)
Man–made controls of the natural flow upstream and downstream of Warren Bridge, and reductions in the watercourse cross–sectional area in that area, are identified as contributing factors to the high velocity and depth of water during the flood. (p17)
Muddy floodwater pooled up to the house wall at the property by Warren Bridge and partially entered the adjoining garage, affecting access and property safety. [IMG,PAG] (p42)
Rear garden and patio inundated by floodwater (Lyveden Brook adjacent); outdoor furniture and potted plants partially submerged; a summer house was moved/rotated on its footings and the patio area was submerged by muddy water. [IMG,PAG] (p43)
Floodwater from Lyveden Brook overtopped nearby banks and inundated rear gardens adjacent to Warren Bridge; water reached up to the base of houses and garden areas, moving/rotating a summer house on its footings and submerging garden structures. [IMG,PAG] (p44)
Area at the rear of a property adjacent to Warren Bridge was inundated when Lyveden Brook overflowed; high–velocity floodwater flowed from a neighbouring garden into the property's rear/passageway, threatening structures and submerging vegetation. [IMG,PAG] (p45)
High–velocity, turbulent muddy water flowed from a neighbouring property's garden into the rear of a property at Warren Bridge, passing forcefully through a wooden fence and inundating the rear area and narrow passage. [PAG] (p46)
High–velocity flow of floodwater entered the rear garden from neighbouring property; garden and grounds were inundated and floodwater reached and surrounded a small outbuilding at the rear of the property. [IMG,PAG] (p47)
High water levels recorded at the rear of Warren Bridge; nearby public sewer outfall opposite the bridge showed evidence of high water and deposits of debris. [IMG,PAG] (p49)
Public sewer outfall opposite Warren Bridge was nearly submerged (water level near top of brick structure) with deposits of debris; Lyveden Brook at the rear of Warren Bridge also showed evidence of high water levels and debris deposits. [IMG,PAG] (p50)","1 residential property (damage to outbuildings on 28/10/2013); 1+ residential property (internal flooding in Spring 1998) [TAB,MUL] (p11)",GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28.html,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28,Oundle flooding 28 Oct 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 28th October 2013",090/North Northamptonshire/Oundle-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,36,2013-10-28,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_4666a0a555d85cae4a902007e925b05c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A formal Section 19 flood investigation was carried out by the LLFA into a flood incident in Oundle (within Northamptonshire) on 28 October 2013. The incident was caused by high flows in an Ordinary Watercourse leading to overtopping of its banks and overflow affecting private property. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme flooding occurred in the town of Oundle (within Northamptonshire). The flooding was described as extreme in nature and extent and prompted the Lead Local Flood Authority to carry out a formal Section 19 investigation despite the county thresholds for a formal investigation not being formally met. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"A formal Section 19 flood investigation was carried out by the LLFA into a flood incident in Oundle (within Northamptonshire) on 28 October 2013. The incident was caused by high flows in an Ordinary Watercourse leading to overtopping of its banks and overflow affecting private property. (p6)
Extreme flooding occurred in the town of Oundle (within Northamptonshire). The flooding was described as extreme in nature and extent and prompted the Lead Local Flood Authority to carry out a formal Section 19 investigation despite the county thresholds for a formal investigation not being formally met. (p8)",,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28.html,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28,Oundle flooding 28 Oct 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 28th October 2013",090/North Northamptonshire/Oundle-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,36,2013-10-28,52.480678744170966,-0.46597423959156464,"<div id=""html_a89ae6b6153a47be454ecdc6ecd607ee"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels and strong flows in an Ordinary Watercourse caused the watercourse banks to overtop, with overflow affecting private property in Oundle. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels and strong flows in an ordinary watercourse caused the watercourse banks to overtop and overflow into private property; private properties were affected by floodwater. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties suffered internal flooding; damage and loss were caused by the depth and velocity of water overtopping the Ordinary Watercourse (Lyveden Brook). (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 28th October 2013 depth and flow from Lyveden Brook caused flooding in Oundle: gardens at Warren Bridge were flooded and there was significant damage to outbuildings at a residential property. Road drainage (gullies/kerb offlets) on Benefield Road north of Lyveden Brook were observed blocked with silt and debris, contributing to local surface&ndash;water issues. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Various incidents of flooding on 28 October 2013 affected people and property as a result of depth and flow from Lyveden Brook. Rear gardens and land were flooded; sports facilities were affected and closed for approximately two days. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Lyveden Brook affected local sports facilities in Oundle, causing the facilities to close. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excessive flow in Lyveden Brook caused water levels to rise and strong flows to overtop the brook banks, affecting adjoining property in Oundle. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the town on 28th October 2013 was driven by high&ndash;velocity, deep flows in Lyveden Brook associated with reductions to the watercourse cross&ndash;sectional area and man&ndash;made controls near Warren Bridge, causing river flooding of parts of Oundle. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property (outbuildings significantly damaged) (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately two days [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>closure of sports facilities for approximately two days [PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Oundle,England,"High water levels and strong flows in an Ordinary Watercourse caused the watercourse banks to overtop, with overflow affecting private property in Oundle. [PAG] (p6)
High water levels and strong flows in an ordinary watercourse caused the watercourse banks to overtop and overflow into private property; private properties were affected by floodwater. [PAG] (p7)
Residential properties suffered internal flooding; damage and loss were caused by the depth and velocity of water overtopping the Ordinary Watercourse (Lyveden Brook). (p9)
On 28th October 2013 depth and flow from Lyveden Brook caused flooding in Oundle: gardens at Warren Bridge were flooded and there was significant damage to outbuildings at a residential property. Road drainage (gullies/kerb offlets) on Benefield Road north of Lyveden Brook were observed blocked with silt and debris, contributing to local surface–water issues. (p11)
Various incidents of flooding on 28 October 2013 affected people and property as a result of depth and flow from Lyveden Brook. Rear gardens and land were flooded; sports facilities were affected and closed for approximately two days. [PAG] (p12)
High water levels in Lyveden Brook affected local sports facilities in Oundle, causing the facilities to close. [PAG] (p13)
Excessive flow in Lyveden Brook caused water levels to rise and strong flows to overtop the brook banks, affecting adjoining property in Oundle. (p17)
Flooding in the town on 28th October 2013 was driven by high–velocity, deep flows in Lyveden Brook associated with reductions to the watercourse cross–sectional area and man–made controls near Warren Bridge, causing river flooding of parts of Oundle. (p18)","1 residential property (outbuildings significantly damaged) (p11)
approximately two days [PAG] (p12)
closure of sports facilities for approximately two days [PAG] (p13)",GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28.html,GB-NNH_36_2013-10-28,Oundle flooding 28 Oct 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Warren Bridge, Oundle, 28th October 2013",090/North Northamptonshire/Oundle-FIR.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.817448,-1.6048001,"<div id=""html_f5a334cefa721cb2fd034c559010a9e7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kirkstall Bridge Shopping Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the street and forecourt in front of the shopping park, inundating the commercial area and making access to businesses impossible/unsafe. [IMG] (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Kirkstall Bridge Shopping Park,England,"Floodwater covered the street and forecourt in front of the shopping park, inundating the commercial area and making access to businesses impossible/unsafe. [IMG] (p50)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8070829,-1.5942463,"<div id=""html_18e75b02e5a8abaac9f87bebafb22610"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>City Golf Course</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Located on the River Aire floodplain and shown on the incident distribution map as receiving flood inundation (smaller number of incidents compared with central Kirkstall areas). [IMG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The golf course lies within the mapped flood&ndash;affected zone adjacent to the River Aire and was inundated as part of the area flooding along the river corridor (amenity/area flooding). [IMG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The City Golf Course is shown on the map within the area affected by overtopping flows from the River Aire, indicating inundation by fluvial floodwaters during the event. [IMG] (p64)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,City Golf Course,England,"Located on the River Aire floodplain and shown on the incident distribution map as receiving flood inundation (smaller number of incidents compared with central Kirkstall areas). [IMG] (p54)
The golf course lies within the mapped flood–affected zone adjacent to the River Aire and was inundated as part of the area flooding along the river corridor (amenity/area flooding). [IMG] (p61)
The City Golf Course is shown on the map within the area affected by overtopping flows from the River Aire, indicating inundation by fluvial floodwaters during the event. [IMG] (p64)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8160124,-1.6067194,"<div id=""html_cd2010ec8909eb1da2cb915fdbec11db"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Bridge Inn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bridge Inn was directly flooded by water from the overtopping River Aire, resulting in internal flooding of the building and associated property damage to the building fabric, fixtures and contents. (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,The Bridge Inn,England,"The Bridge Inn was directly flooded by water from the overtopping River Aire, resulting in internal flooding of the building and associated property damage to the building fabric, fixtures and contents. (p55)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.788624,-1.5307949,"<div id=""html_7793128f9e1c96ef335d9f78852554af"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Discovery Centre</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Clusters of internal and external property flooding were recorded immediately adjacent to the Discovery Centre during the event; the area around the Discovery Centre was one of the highest&ndash;density locations of flooded properties, indicating properties in the immediate vicinity experienced flooding impacts. [IMG] (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Discovery Centre,England,"Clusters of internal and external property flooding were recorded immediately adjacent to the Discovery Centre during the event; the area around the Discovery Centre was one of the highest–density locations of flooded properties, indicating properties in the immediate vicinity experienced flooding impacts. [IMG] (p69)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7762231,-1.5040427,"<div id=""html_96e3c8257bd79a2243c6a34b6665fb32"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thwaite Watermill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the Aire Calder Navigation canal (surcharged after the Knostrop by&ndash;wash embankment collapse at Knostrop Lock) flooded the low&ndash;lying Thwaite Mill Industrial Museum. (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Thwaite Watermill,England,Overtopping of the Aire Calder Navigation canal (surcharged after the Knostrop by–wash embankment collapse at Knostrop Lock) flooded the low–lying Thwaite Mill Industrial Museum. (p79),,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9257831,-1.391306,"<div id=""html_871ac9c2ed7c1eeaadee366dbcc184a2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wetherby Sports Association</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sports facility grounds and courts were inundated by the River Wharfe overtopping its channel. Courts and playing surfaces were submerged, fencing damaged or collapsed, mud and debris deposited across courts, and the facility rendered unusable. [IMG] (p125)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths locally in excess of 2 m; internal property flood depths of 800 mm were recorded in Wetherby (general area impacts) [IMG] (p125)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Wetherby Sports Association,England,"Sports facility grounds and courts were inundated by the River Wharfe overtopping its channel. Courts and playing surfaces were submerged, fencing damaged or collapsed, mud and debris deposited across courts, and the facility rendered unusable. [IMG] (p125)",flood depths locally in excess of 2 m; internal property flood depths of 800 mm were recorded in Wetherby (general area impacts) [IMG] (p125),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9268512,-1.3927023,"<div id=""html_e27d060b86e9beddb8fb512c3d35782c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wetherby Ings</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodplain (Wetherby Ings) was inundated when the River Wharfe overtopped its banks; public amenity areas, a stone archway and trees were submerged and observers gathered at the edge of the flood. [IMG] (p125)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Wetherby Ings,England,"Floodplain (Wetherby Ings) was inundated when the River Wharfe overtopped its banks; public amenity areas, a stone archway and trees were submerged and observers gathered at the edge of the flood. [IMG] (p125)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.80259064466948,-1.5826245718376455,"<div id=""html_4f6c65e00107b8148db6f18ceea77016"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Armley Mills Industrial Museum</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>2015 river flood reached a height above the museum window and signage; exterior wall carries a marked flood level for 2015 significantly higher than the 1866 level, indicating major inundation at the building during the 2015 event. [IMG] (p216)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two recorded flood events (1866 and 2015) [IMG] (p216)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Armley Mills Industrial Museum,England,"2015 river flood reached a height above the museum window and signage; exterior wall carries a marked flood level for 2015 significantly higher than the 1866 level, indicating major inundation at the building during the 2015 event. [IMG] (p216)",two recorded flood events (1866 and 2015) [IMG] (p216),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7959382,-1.5534691,"<div id=""html_b100588edabe2da7e0cabf227eca7af6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Point</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters from the River Aire bursting its banks on 27 December 2015 ran down nearby streets (Kirkstall Road, Wellington Street) and into Northern Street, inundating adjacent streets and access routes around the West Point high&ndash;rise accommodation. [PAG] (p241)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement car park was entered by floodwater (CCTV shows water coming into the basement at 01:37). Water subsequently filled level &ndash;2 of the car park. Out&ndash;of&ndash;hours staff were notified and emergency services were later contacted about the severity of the flooding. [PAG] (p242)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,West Point,England,"Floodwaters from the River Aire bursting its banks on 27 December 2015 ran down nearby streets (Kirkstall Road, Wellington Street) and into Northern Street, inundating adjacent streets and access routes around the West Point high–rise accommodation. [PAG] (p241)
Basement car park was entered by floodwater (CCTV shows water coming into the basement at 01:37). Water subsequently filled level –2 of the car park. Out–of–hours staff were notified and emergency services were later contacted about the severity of the flooding. [PAG] (p242)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8040608,-1.5788002,"<div id=""html_f9946d995727163c76932dd44932fe8f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kirkstall Industrial Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Business/industrial area inundated by deep floodwater: vehicles partially submerged and pedestrian access obstructed. Flooding is consistent with urban/surface&ndash;water inundation exacerbated by high river levels and fluvial backwater that constrained surface water discharge. [IMG] (p59)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Kirkstall Industrial Park,England,Business/industrial area inundated by deep floodwater: vehicles partially submerged and pedestrian access obstructed. Flooding is consistent with urban/surface–water inundation exacerbated by high river levels and fluvial backwater that constrained surface water discharge. [IMG] (p59),,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7870485,-1.5320749,"<div id=""html_82bf2e78a1af0d7bc7476a2229352925"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hunslet Trading Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (River Aire) flooding inundated the estate during the event, causing internal and external flooding of commercial properties; listed among the worst&ndash;affected areas. (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying properties at Hunslet Trading Estate were flooded when the Aire Calder Navigation canal and adjacent River Aire exceeded capacity; internal flooding was verified inside buildings. (p71)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1350 mm depth of internal flooding (p71)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Hunslet Trading Estate,England,"Fluvial (River Aire) flooding inundated the estate during the event, causing internal and external flooding of commercial properties; listed among the worst–affected areas. (p68)
Low–lying properties at Hunslet Trading Estate were flooded when the Aire Calder Navigation canal and adjacent River Aire exceeded capacity; internal flooding was verified inside buildings. (p71)",1350 mm depth of internal flooding (p71),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.77172843163662,-1.4976376736869037,"<div id=""html_30d344a82bdd36c4f6440bcb8a399c02"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Haigh Park Road Industrial Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flooding on 27 December 2015 inundated the industrial estate road, with floodwater covering the entire road and reaching approximately waist height for a person, disrupting business operations and affecting industrial property. [IMG,MUL] (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial (River Aire) flooding inundated commercial/industrial premises in this estate; area identified among the worst affected in Stourton. (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the surcharged Aire and Calder Navigation canal overtopped and flowed into the low&ndash;lying industrial estate, flooding properties and units within the estate. [IMG,PAG] (p78)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the Aire Calder Navigation canal overtopping (after the Knostrop by&ndash;wash embankment collapse) inundated low&ndash;lying areas of Haigh Park Road Industrial Estate. [PAG] (p79)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>36 internal commercial floods; 4 external commercial floods; 40 properties total (Stourton) (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>surge height 1.305m; canal swollen by over 1300mm [IMG,PAG] (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Haigh Park Road Industrial Estate,England,"Severe flooding on 27 December 2015 inundated the industrial estate road, with floodwater covering the entire road and reaching approximately waist height for a person, disrupting business operations and affecting industrial property. [IMG,MUL] (p73)
Extensive fluvial (River Aire) flooding inundated commercial/industrial premises in this estate; area identified among the worst affected in Stourton. (p75)
Floodwater from the surcharged Aire and Calder Navigation canal overtopped and flowed into the low–lying industrial estate, flooding properties and units within the estate. [IMG,PAG] (p78)
Flooding from the Aire Calder Navigation canal overtopping (after the Knostrop by–wash embankment collapse) inundated low–lying areas of Haigh Park Road Industrial Estate. [PAG] (p79)","36 internal commercial floods; 4 external commercial floods; 40 properties total (Stourton) (p75)
surge height 1.305m; canal swollen by over 1300mm [IMG,PAG] (p78)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,54.7023545,-3.2765753,"<div id=""html_9d983f08e6fc45528eeef56cf5a3288c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>UK</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Many locations in the north and northwest recorded highest&ndash;ever river flood levels on the Rivers Aire, Wharfe and Calder. The River Aire at Armley recorded its highest&ndash;ever flow on 27 December 2015, producing flood levels in some locations approximately 1.2 m higher than the previous record (autumn 2000). The event was estimated to exceed a 0.5% (1 in 200) AEP based on river flow. [PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,UK,England,"Many locations in the north and northwest recorded highest–ever river flood levels on the Rivers Aire, Wharfe and Calder. The River Aire at Armley recorded its highest–ever flow on 27 December 2015, producing flood levels in some locations approximately 1.2 m higher than the previous record (autumn 2000). The event was estimated to exceed a 0.5% (1 in 200) AEP based on river flow. [PAG,MUL] (p24)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7859472,-1.5384486,"<div id=""html_e8fc0840c98c00e8984f19424f283b48"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pottery Field</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding (internal and external) affected properties adjacent to the river corridor at and immediately north of Pottery Field; incidents cluster within the Flood Zone 3 extent and form one of the largest concentrations of flooded properties in the mapped area. [IMG] (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>206&ndash;592 properties [IMG] (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Pottery Field,England,Property flooding (internal and external) affected properties adjacent to the river corridor at and immediately north of Pottery Field; incidents cluster within the Flood Zone 3 extent and form one of the largest concentrations of flooded properties in the mapped area. [IMG] (p69),206–592 properties [IMG] (p69),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7354024,-1.3564686,"<div id=""html_a61edbde9e5f4f9ccd8fc205d7ab4931"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Allerton Ings</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Allerton Ings functions as a flood storage area designed to hold water back during high flows; during record high river levels the Ings would have stored and routed floodwater via designed spillways to reduce flood impact on properties. [IMG] (p89)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Functioned as natural floodplain storage during the event, holding water at times of high River Calder flow and helping to reduce downstream flood levels as part of the washlands system. (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Allerton Ings at Allerton Bywater filled as a washland during the extreme flood of 25 Dec 2015 &ndash; 29 Dec 2015, acting as temporary storage and contributing to reduced flooding of Allerton Bywater and downstream areas. (p99)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Allerton Ings came into operation as floodplain storage during fluvial flooding, inundating the low&ndash;lying area and contributing to inundation of Barnsdale Road and nearby areas (acting under River Aire flows and when backed up by River Calder). [PAG] (p101)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1289 properties saved (modelled estimate for combined effect of St Aidan's and associated washlands between Woodlesford and Castleford) (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1289 properties (p99)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Allerton Ings,England,"Allerton Ings functions as a flood storage area designed to hold water back during high flows; during record high river levels the Ings would have stored and routed floodwater via designed spillways to reduce flood impact on properties. [IMG] (p89)
Functioned as natural floodplain storage during the event, holding water at times of high River Calder flow and helping to reduce downstream flood levels as part of the washlands system. (p91)
Allerton Ings at Allerton Bywater filled as a washland during the extreme flood of 25 Dec 2015 – 29 Dec 2015, acting as temporary storage and contributing to reduced flooding of Allerton Bywater and downstream areas. (p99)
Allerton Ings came into operation as floodplain storage during fluvial flooding, inundating the low–lying area and contributing to inundation of Barnsdale Road and nearby areas (acting under River Aire flows and when backed up by River Calder). [PAG] (p101)","1289 properties saved (modelled estimate for combined effect of St Aidan's and associated washlands between Woodlesford and Castleford) (p91)
1289 properties (p99)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9160968,-1.4015126,"<div id=""html_1f15d7d987584764eed7cfeb7dd93ff8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Linton Ings</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Isolated cases of internal property flooding were reported and extensive garden flooding occurred in the Linton Ings area due to overbank flows from the River Wharfe. (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Wharfe caused clusters of internal and external property flooding adjacent to Linton Ings. The map shows the largest number of flooded properties occurring in grid squares next to Linton Ings and the nearby sports field, indicating heavy riverside inundation of properties and land. [IMG] (p124)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Linton Ings,England,"Isolated cases of internal property flooding were reported and extensive garden flooding occurred in the Linton Ings area due to overbank flows from the River Wharfe. (p117)
Flooding from the River Wharfe caused clusters of internal and external property flooding adjacent to Linton Ings. The map shows the largest number of flooded properties occurring in grid squares next to Linton Ings and the nearby sports field, indicating heavy riverside inundation of properties and land. [IMG] (p124)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7974185,-1.5437941,"<div id=""html_107a431445b6590232ec85359f0d5b6a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Leeds Metropolitan District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread inundation of urban areas: streets, parking lots and building perimeters in both residential neighborhoods and industrial/commercial zones, with visible flooding around a church/spired building; transportation routes and access within the photographed area were disrupted. [MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The city was hit by devastating, unprecedented flooding causing considerable damage across Leeds, affecting road networks, power supplies and residents' properties and possessions and prompting major recovery efforts. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Unprecedented flooding across the Leeds district (25&ndash;29 December 2015) had devastating consequences for residents, businesses and communities; some homeowners/tenants remained unable to return while repair works continued, and the flooding had a major impact on businesses and jobs with some businesses moving operations out of the area and out of the UK. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the district during the event causing significant disruption to residents, businesses and communities; major transport links and critical infrastructure were flooded or disrupted (including A65 Kirkstall Road, Linton Bridge, Network Rail lines at Kirkstall, Northern Power Grid substations and IT/data centres). [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds city (located on the River Aire and affected by the River Calder and tributaries) experienced river and urban flooding during Storm Eva; Leeds city centre and neighbouring areas were particularly vulnerable and suffered severe impacts including inundation of communities and disruption to transport and key sites. High&ndash;rise and multi&ndash;occupancy buildings in the city suffered indirect impacts (stranded cars in basements, disabled lifts, power outages) that caused severe inconvenience and loss to residents and businesses. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the metropolitan district: 4,712 reported flooding incidents (internal and external), including 2,826 affected by internal flooding; property damage to homes and businesses, some buildings left empty or undergoing remediation; indirect impacts to high&ndash;rise/multiple&ndash;occupancy buildings (e.g. cars stranded in basement car parks, lifts incapacitated, power outages); businesses ruined and many homeowners displaced into temporary accommodation. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flooding affected the wider Leeds district. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds city centre and other parts of the city were affected by fluvial flooding driven by Main Rivers (notably the River Aire) during the event. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas adjacent to the River Aire in Leeds City Centre were affected by fluvial flooding during the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event; Leeds City Centre is listed as an Aire&ndash;focused sub&ndash;area in the Section 19 report. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by the Boxing Day floods (downstream impacts from extreme rainfall and saturated ground); included among communities impacted by the Boxing Day/late&ndash;December 2015 flooding. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Metropolitan district extensively impacted by flooding attributed predominantly to the Rivers Aire and Wharfe. Impacts included internal and external flooding of properties, indirect impacts to high&ndash;rise multi&ndash;occupancy buildings (e.g. basement car parks flooded leaving cars stranded, access/utility failures such as lifts incapacitated and power outages), and buildings remaining empty or undergoing remediation. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Verified property flooding across the 13 study areas in the Leeds Metropolitan District: 2,826 properties suffered internal flooding and 1,886 properties suffered external flooding. Table 3a summarizes impacts by use: total flooded 673 properties, 2,212 properties affected, 483 severely affected, total properties impacted 3,368. Breakdowns include residential total 2,683 (298 flooded, 1,902 affected, 483 severely affected), houses total 367 (242 flooded, 125 affected), flats total 2,316 (56 flooded, 1,777 affected, 483 severely affected), commercial total 678 (371 flooded, 307 affected), other (churches, allotments, sports clubs) total 7 (4 flooded, 3 affected). [TAB] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Leeds City Centre experienced internal residential flooding and both external residential and internal/external commercial flooding as recorded in the study. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding from the afternoon of 26 December into 27 December 2015 causing widespread disruption; major transport links were flooded (including the A65 Kirkstall / Wellington Street); critical infrastructure including electricity substations and IT, communications and data centres were affected; surface water drainage could not discharge to the river leading to surcharge/back pressures and area flooding which subsided once river levels dropped. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial (river) flooding and constrained surface water drainage caused widespread internal and external flooding of residential and commercial properties; deep 'area flooding' occurred when surface water could not discharge to the river, and subsided only when river levels dropped. Roads and rail exits were cut off, trapping office workers in the city centre. [TAB,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread property flooding across the city centre during Storm Eva caused major damage to internal fixtures, fittings, building fabric, vehicles, lifts/elevators and utility supplies; flooding incidents were concentrated along the River Aire and in Flood Zone 3, with major disruption and lengthy refurbishment required for affected properties. [IMG,PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the River Aire overtopping its channel across central Leeds. River overtopping produced overland flow routes and directly flooded multiple locations in the city centre (examples listed in the report): Little Neville Street (Direct Line Group offices and nearby high&ndash;rise apartments), Bridge End/Meadow Lane (offices, Navigation Walk, Dock Street), Turlow Court (residential areas), Neptune Street (residential and commercial properties), Clarence Road (Leeds Assisted Living, nearby commercial properties and Armouries Drive) and low&ndash;lying properties along Sovereign Street. The event rendered offices and residential properties inaccessible and caused significant property inundation. [IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads acted as pathways for fluvial exceedance flows from remote river escapes upstream of the city centre; these aggregated flows caused urban flooding across the centre and contributed to property and road flooding. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial exceedance flows from the River Aire overtopping in Kirkstall flowed southeast into Leeds city centre, inundating large parts of the central area and flooding thousands of properties, with people&#8217;s lives put at risk. [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds City Centre was inundated by the fluvial exceedance flow from the River Aire, flooding streets and thousands of properties and placing life at risk. [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds city centre experienced widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers overtopping due to exceeded channel capacity; high river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage systems, preventing free discharge and causing backing up through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage. Specific flooded locations in the city centre are referenced (e.g. Northern St flooded 28/12 09:43; Wellington Place shown flooded in social media image). [PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial flooding from the River Aire (flood zone 3) caused widespread disruption across Leeds districts (notably Kirkstall and Burley), flooding major transport links and critical infrastructure and leading to property flooding. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Electrical supply disruption associated with the storm/flood event: loss of electricity supply to 27,000 properties from 20:11 on 26 December 2015 linked to Northern Power Grid sub&ndash;station issues reported in the context of the event. [IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding concentrated along the River Aire within the Leeds extent &mdash; notably Kirkstall &mdash; resulting in numerous property flood incidents, inundation of transport infrastructure (Network Rail tracks) and damage to commercial premises and residences. [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its banks and breached private flood defences in Kirkstall, causing overland fluvial flow into floodplains and infrastructure. Floodwaters spilled onto the Network Rail line and directly inundated The Bridge Inn, Leeds rugby academy facility, Morrison's Supermarket car park, St Ann's Mills and low&ndash;lying properties between the river and Kirkstall Road; the Kirkstall Bridge retail area (Bridge Road) was also heavily flooded, with vehicles and buildings submerged and major property damage to internal fixtures, fittings, furniture, vehicles and utility supplies reported. [IMG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the Leeds district (notably Kirkstall) were affected by river flooding when the River Aire overtopped banks and breached private defences, causing inundation of local infrastructure and properties as described for Kirkstall. [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread fluvial flooding from Main Rivers across the Leeds area during the event, with river channels overtopping and high river levels surcharging surface water drainage systems causing backup through gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds city centre and districts experienced major fluvial and area flooding; high river levels limited surface water drainage causing area flooding and indirect impacts to high&ndash;rise/multi&ndash;occupancy accommodation and commercial premises. [PAG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Central Leeds experienced concentrated flood incidents along the River Aire during the event, with internal property flooding the dominant incident type and clusters of incidents (including Burley and Botany Bay) shown on the incident distribution map. [IMG,PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Aggregated flood flow from the River Aire travelled downstream into Leeds city centre, inundating streets and properties. Major property damage is described (internal flooding, damage to fixtures/fittings, vehicles, lifts and utilities) and the map shows the highest concentrations of flooded properties in the city centre. [IMG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Aggregated flood flow from the River Aire proceeded into Leeds city centre, flooding streets and areas in its path. (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Aggregated flood flows from the River Aire moved rapidly into Leeds city centre, flooding roads, industrial and commercial properties along the main flow path (notably around the A65 Kirkstall Road corridor) as documented in aerial imagery. [IMG,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers overtopped due to heavy rainfall on saturated catchments; high river levels surcharged surface water drainage systems causing backup through road gullies, manholes and private drains. Nine severe flood warnings were in force across Leeds on 26 December 2015. (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Clusters of property flooding occurred within Leeds during the event, particularly along the river corridor north of Pottery Field and near the Discovery Centre; incidents are concentrated inside the Flood Zone 3 area. [IMG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) overtopping and associated overland flows affected the Hunslet / Thwaite Gate area of Leeds. Surface water flowed from an adjacent car park onto George Mann Way and flooded the street, extending to George Mann Road (photographs dated 26 Dec 2015 at 23:15 and 23:30). Hunslet Trading Estate experienced verified internal flooding to a depth of 1350 mm. [IMG] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread fluvial flooding across Leeds from main rivers that overtopped due to record river rises; high river levels also surcharged surface water systems, causing backup through gullies, manholes and private drainage and increasing urban inundation. (p71)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flood incidents during the event, concentrated in Flood Zone 3 along the River Aire with clusters of property flooding across the mapped area. [IMG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the City of Leeds (the Stourton area) experienced flooding from river overtopping and canal surcharge that inundated industrial premises and infrastructure within the Leeds administrative area. [IMG] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread fluvial flooding from Main Rivers overtopped channel capacity across the city. High river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage, causing backup through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage systems and resulting in urban inundation. Severe flood warnings were in force. (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded major river flooding during the same events in December 2015 that affected nearby settlements; high river levels in Leeds are referenced as the major flooding incident which disrupted areas upstream/downstream. [PAG] (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Woodlesford area of Leeds experienced extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3 from the River Aire. High river levels caused backwater effects that rendered surface&ndash;water drainage inoperable, contributing to localised flooding until river levels fell. [IMG,PAG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Leeds area experienced river and canal&ndash;related flooding during the event; properties were flooded along the River Aire and adjacent waterways, with incidents concentrated along river and canal corridors in the Leeds area. The report records multiple severe flood warnings affecting the Leeds inner urban area during the event period. [IMG,PAG] (p85)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire overtopped into adjacent floodplains and inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation; the canal then exceeded capacity (surcharged/overtopped) and flooded low&ndash;lying properties in the Woodlesford area of Leeds. [IMG] (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in the Methley and Mickletown area experienced internal and external flooding. Flooding incidents were clustered adjacent to watercourses and within high&ndash;risk flood zones; worst affected streets were Mill Lane, Lower Mickletown, Wilson's Row and Green Lane. [IMG] (p93)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas within the Leeds district (notably Allerton Bywater and surrounding low&ndash;lying settlements) experienced fluvial/river flooding during the event, with internal and external property flooding concentrated close to the River Aire floodplain. [IMG] (p102)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial flooding from the River Wharfe within Flood Zone 3 affecting Otley (part of Leeds). Flooding caused surface water drainage to be inoperable (backwater/surcharge effects) until river levels subsided. Properties and streets were inundated; worst&ndash;affected streets and areas included A660, A659, Old Pool Road, Moor Drive, Meagill Rise, Weston Drive, Newall Carr Road, St Richards Road, Wrenbeck Drive, Farnley Lane, Billams Hill, Bridge Avenue, Old Cattle Market site and the Wharfe flood plains. [IMG] (p107)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incidents during the event occurred within the Leeds Metropolitan District boundary (Otley is inside Leeds), with mapped Flood Zone 3 areas and property flooding shown in the Otley area. [IMG] (p108)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Collingham area of the Leeds district resulted from River Wharfe overtopping and breaching defences, causing property and garden flooding and infrastructure damage (notably Linton Bridge). [IMG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Administrative area containing the Thorp Arch and Boston Spa flood incidents; fluvial flooding from the River Wharfe inundated parts of the Leeds district covered in the report. [IMG] (p131)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding during Storm Eva (26&ndash;27 Dec 2015) caused extensive damage across the city, triggered a citywide Strategic Recovery Plan and recovery actions by Leeds City Council, and led to commitments for further investment in river flood alleviation (notably the River Aire Flood Alleviation Scheme). [PAG] (p136)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds experienced widespread river flooding during Storm Eva (26&ndash;27 December 2015), described as devastation not seen since 1866; flooding extent closely matched EA flood zones and the absence of formal defences along the River Aire left the city at significant risk. [TAB,PAG] (p137)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds experienced widespread flooding from rivers overtopping their channels; areas of the city suffered significant damage and remain at risk in the absence of formal defences along the River Aire. [PAG] (p138)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Communities in Leeds were impacted by flooding associated with Storm Eva &mdash; Otley is cited as being significantly affected and Linton Bridge (a listed structure) was badly damaged; repair works are underway and funding/mitigation measures are being pursued to protect communities along the River Wharfe. [PAG] (p139)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding (from the River Aire) prevented safe access to the Kirkstall electrical compound until 28 Dec; electrical repair work began 29 Dec after waters cleared. Floods caused road closures and bridge collapses or instability that hampered restoration; many customers were displaced. Extensive post&ndash;flood inspections and recovery work were carried out in the Leeds area. (p227)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the Kirkstall C bulk supply point on the evening of 26 December 2015 caused RTU (Remote Terminal Unit) water damage and communication failure, contributed to EHV/HV faults on local substations and triggered customer power interruptions; the power cuts at this time coincided with very high call volumes and abandoned calls to the supplier. [IMG] (p232)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Urban flooding affected parts of Leeds during the event, including severe inundation on Kirkstall Road that disrupted transport and required emergency response. [IMG] (p237)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 27 December 2015 the River Aire burst its banks; floodwater ran down Kirkstall Road onto Wellington Street and into Northern Street, which was already flooded. (p241)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties and businesses in Leeds were flooded; recovery and resilience work has been required, including Council&ndash;administered grants and targeted interventions to support those unable to return home and businesses not yet fully operational. [PAG] (p285)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding affected residents, businesses and local infrastructure across the city; recovery and support actions were implemented for those impacted and some householders remain unable to return to their homes. (p287)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread residential flooding response and recovery activity organised by the council and partners: upfront payments to owners, targeting of flooded properties by letter and officer visits, support for group residential schemes, council tax relief for properties vacated due to flooding, grant funding and community support activity across the city. (p289)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with Storm Eva in late December 2015 affected businesses and properties across Leeds. Recovery actions included business support, property resilience measures, business rate relief and grant funding to support affected businesses. (p290)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds as a city experienced business and property impacts from the Storm Eva floods; businesses in the city received grant assistance and business rate relief as part of the recovery effort, and there has been at least one high&ndash;profile business closure linked to the event. (p291)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread business disruption and longer&ndash;term economic impacts across floodplain employment areas: business closures, downgraded presences/relocations, reduced investment confidence and difficulties obtaining flood insurance. [PAG] (p292)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding associated with Storm Eva in late December 2015 affected the city, prompting immediate recovery activity and the commissioning of a catchment&ndash;wide feasibility study (River Aire Flood Alleviation Scheme). Flood impacts required infrastructure repairs (for example Linton Bridge) and targeted communications to affected businesses and residents. [PAG] (p294)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding during Storm Eva that affected residents and businesses, prompted community engagement and recovery actions, and led to infrastructure impacts such as repairs and alternative travel arrangements around Linton Bridge. [PAG] (p295)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm Eva flooding in Leeds on 26 December 2015 flooded industrial/office premises occupied by businesses (example: Company A). Three of four industrial/office units were flooded, leading to loss of two months' production and significant damage to essential production machinery, work in progress and business stock. The business submitted a multi&ndash;million pound insurance claim and faced a large increase in future flood insurance costs (reported as a 460% premium increase), prompting consideration of relocation out of the floodplain. (p310)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>27,000 properties [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4,712 properties; 2,826 properties with internal flooding; 3,368 properties reported under the DCLG EVA scheme (finalised June 2016) [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4,712 incidents; 2,826 affected by internal flooding [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3368 properties; 4712 properties; 2826 properties (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2,826 properties (internal flooding); 1,886 properties (external flooding); 673 properties flooded; 2,212 affected; 483 severely affected; 3,368 total; residential total 2,683; houses total 367; flats total 2,316; commercial total 678; other total 7; residential flooded 298; residential affected 1,902; residential severely affected 483; houses flooded 242; houses affected 125; flats flooded 56; flats affected 1,777; flats severely affected 483; commercial flooded 371; commercial affected 307; other flooded 4; other affected 3 [TAB] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1938 internal residential; 566 external residential; 296 internal commercial; 55 external commercial; total 2855. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>27,000 properties (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1,938 internal residential properties; 566 external residential properties; 296 internal commercial properties; 55 external commercial properties; 2,855 properties total affected in Leeds City Centre; internal flood depths 1 m to in excess of 2 m; direct economic impact £60.3 million; ~27,000 office workers trapped [TAB,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>thousands of properties [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>thousands of properties [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>96 properties flooded in Kirkstall; loss of electricity supply to 27,000 properties (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>27,000 properties [IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Multiple properties flooded across Kirkstall; largest 250m grid squares fall in the 206&ndash;592 incidents bin (map legend ranges) [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>122&ndash;395 properties (largest incident clusters mapped near the city centre) [IMG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>206&ndash;592 properties (largest 250m grid&ndash;square cluster shown on Figure 80) [IMG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1350 mm depth of internal flooding [IMG] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Largest cluster shown as 206&ndash;592 properties in a 250m grid square (map legend and distribution figures) [IMG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 internal residential flooding; 4 external residential flooding; 0 internal commercial flooding; 1 external commercial flooding; Woodlesford total 6 properties [IMG,PAG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 internal residential; 32 external residential; 0 internal commercial; 0 external commercial; total 36 properties [IMG] (p93)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 internal residential properties; 14 external residential properties; 5 internal commercial properties; 1 external commercial property; 74 properties in total [IMG] (p107)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flood depths in some cases in excess of 2 m; south supporting pier settlement 200 mm; 4 miles vehicle diversion; estimated repair cost £4.3 million [IMG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>verified internal flood depths 500mm to 1500mm (within affected localities) [IMG] (p131)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 EHV/HV incidents recorded at the Kirkstall site; 219 'at risk' substation inspections (Calder Valley and Leeds combined); 18 substations required replacement; 14 required maintenance; 49 required cleaning; 3,285 properties inspected in the West Yorkshire service recovery programme; 284 service termination cut&ndash;outs replaced; 190 services renewed; 53 properties disconnected on safety grounds (later reconnected) (p227)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 RTU communication failure at Kirkstall due to flood water damage (evening 26 Dec 2015); 1,540 calls abandoned between 20:00 and 23:45 on 26 Dec 2015 when power cuts began affecting customers [IMG] (p232)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>673 properties flooded; 2212 properties affected; 483 properties severely affected; 3368 properties in total affected; 2683 residential properties affected (242 houses flooded; 56 flats flooded); 371 commercial properties flooded; 4 other properties flooded (p287)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>£105,562 of council tax relief offered; 7 properties still receiving relief as of Oct 1; Leeds Community Foundation raised approximately £300,000 and given/committed just under £260,000; 25 organisations funded; 47 individual homeowner grants of £250 or £500; proposed support for 8 city&ndash;centre flat complexes (£160,000); proposed utility relocation in 26 properties on Kirkstall Road (£130,000) (p289)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 Leeds businesses included in 43 LEP Flood Recovery Grant approvals; council provided over £1.1m rates relief to affected Leeds businesses; one high&ndash;profile business closure (Thyssen Krupp) reported (p291)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Leeds Metropolitan District,England,"Widespread inundation of urban areas: streets, parking lots and building perimeters in both residential neighborhoods and industrial/commercial zones, with visible flooding around a church/spired building; transportation routes and access within the photographed area were disrupted. [MUL] (p1)
The city was hit by devastating, unprecedented flooding causing considerable damage across Leeds, affecting road networks, power supplies and residents' properties and possessions and prompting major recovery efforts. (p4)
Unprecedented flooding across the Leeds district (25–29 December 2015) had devastating consequences for residents, businesses and communities; some homeowners/tenants remained unable to return while repair works continued, and the flooding had a major impact on businesses and jobs with some businesses moving operations out of the area and out of the UK. [PAG] (p7)
Widespread flooding across the district during the event causing significant disruption to residents, businesses and communities; major transport links and critical infrastructure were flooded or disrupted (including A65 Kirkstall Road, Linton Bridge, Network Rail lines at Kirkstall, Northern Power Grid substations and IT/data centres). [PAG] (p8)
Leeds city (located on the River Aire and affected by the River Calder and tributaries) experienced river and urban flooding during Storm Eva; Leeds city centre and neighbouring areas were particularly vulnerable and suffered severe impacts including inundation of communities and disruption to transport and key sites. High–rise and multi–occupancy buildings in the city suffered indirect impacts (stranded cars in basements, disabled lifts, power outages) that caused severe inconvenience and loss to residents and businesses. [PAG] (p9)
Widespread flooding across the metropolitan district: 4,712 reported flooding incidents (internal and external), including 2,826 affected by internal flooding; property damage to homes and businesses, some buildings left empty or undergoing remediation; indirect impacts to high–rise/multiple–occupancy buildings (e.g. cars stranded in basement car parks, lifts incapacitated, power outages); businesses ruined and many homeowners displaced into temporary accommodation. [PAG] (p10)
Severe flooding affected the wider Leeds district. (p11)
Leeds city centre and other parts of the city were affected by fluvial flooding driven by Main Rivers (notably the River Aire) during the event. [PAG] (p16)
Areas adjacent to the River Aire in Leeds City Centre were affected by fluvial flooding during the 25–29 Dec 2015 event; Leeds City Centre is listed as an Aire–focused sub–area in the Section 19 report. [PAG] (p17)
Affected by the Boxing Day floods (downstream impacts from extreme rainfall and saturated ground); included among communities impacted by the Boxing Day/late–December 2015 flooding. (p25)
Metropolitan district extensively impacted by flooding attributed predominantly to the Rivers Aire and Wharfe. Impacts included internal and external flooding of properties, indirect impacts to high–rise multi–occupancy buildings (e.g. basement car parks flooded leaving cars stranded, access/utility failures such as lifts incapacitated and power outages), and buildings remaining empty or undergoing remediation. (p27)
Verified property flooding across the 13 study areas in the Leeds Metropolitan District: 2,826 properties suffered internal flooding and 1,886 properties suffered external flooding. Table 3a summarizes impacts by use: total flooded 673 properties, 2,212 properties affected, 483 severely affected, total properties impacted 3,368. Breakdowns include residential total 2,683 (298 flooded, 1,902 affected, 483 severely affected), houses total 367 (242 flooded, 125 affected), flats total 2,316 (56 flooded, 1,777 affected, 483 severely affected), commercial total 678 (371 flooded, 307 affected), other (churches, allotments, sports clubs) total 7 (4 flooded, 3 affected). [TAB] (p28)
Properties in Leeds City Centre experienced internal residential flooding and both external residential and internal/external commercial flooding as recorded in the study. [TAB] (p29)
Significant flooding from the afternoon of 26 December into 27 December 2015 causing widespread disruption; major transport links were flooded (including the A65 Kirkstall / Wellington Street); critical infrastructure including electricity substations and IT, communications and data centres were affected; surface water drainage could not discharge to the river leading to surcharge/back pressures and area flooding which subsided once river levels dropped. (p32)
Extensive fluvial (river) flooding and constrained surface water drainage caused widespread internal and external flooding of residential and commercial properties; deep 'area flooding' occurred when surface water could not discharge to the river, and subsided only when river levels dropped. Roads and rail exits were cut off, trapping office workers in the city centre. [TAB,PAG] (p33)
Widespread property flooding across the city centre during Storm Eva caused major damage to internal fixtures, fittings, building fabric, vehicles, lifts/elevators and utility supplies; flooding incidents were concentrated along the River Aire and in Flood Zone 3, with major disruption and lengthy refurbishment required for affected properties. [IMG,PAG] (p35)
Fluvial flooding from the River Aire overtopping its channel across central Leeds. River overtopping produced overland flow routes and directly flooded multiple locations in the city centre (examples listed in the report): Little Neville Street (Direct Line Group offices and nearby high–rise apartments), Bridge End/Meadow Lane (offices, Navigation Walk, Dock Street), Turlow Court (residential areas), Neptune Street (residential and commercial properties), Clarence Road (Leeds Assisted Living, nearby commercial properties and Armouries Drive) and low–lying properties along Sovereign Street. The event rendered offices and residential properties inaccessible and caused significant property inundation. [IMG] (p36)
Roads acted as pathways for fluvial exceedance flows from remote river escapes upstream of the city centre; these aggregated flows caused urban flooding across the centre and contributed to property and road flooding. (p40)
Fluvial exceedance flows from the River Aire overtopping in Kirkstall flowed southeast into Leeds city centre, inundating large parts of the central area and flooding thousands of properties, with people’s lives put at risk. [IMG,PAG] (p41)
Leeds City Centre was inundated by the fluvial exceedance flow from the River Aire, flooding streets and thousands of properties and placing life at risk. [PAG] (p42)
Leeds city centre experienced widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers overtopping due to exceeded channel capacity; high river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage systems, preventing free discharge and causing backing up through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage. Specific flooded locations in the city centre are referenced (e.g. Northern St flooded 28/12 09:43; Wellington Place shown flooded in social media image). [PAG] (p43)
Extensive fluvial flooding from the River Aire (flood zone 3) caused widespread disruption across Leeds districts (notably Kirkstall and Burley), flooding major transport links and critical infrastructure and leading to property flooding. (p52)
Electrical supply disruption associated with the storm/flood event: loss of electricity supply to 27,000 properties from 20:11 on 26 December 2015 linked to Northern Power Grid sub–station issues reported in the context of the event. [IMG] (p53)
Flooding concentrated along the River Aire within the Leeds extent — notably Kirkstall — resulting in numerous property flood incidents, inundation of transport infrastructure (Network Rail tracks) and damage to commercial premises and residences. [IMG,PAG] (p54)
The River Aire overtopped its banks and breached private flood defences in Kirkstall, causing overland fluvial flow into floodplains and infrastructure. Floodwaters spilled onto the Network Rail line and directly inundated The Bridge Inn, Leeds rugby academy facility, Morrison's Supermarket car park, St Ann's Mills and low–lying properties between the river and Kirkstall Road; the Kirkstall Bridge retail area (Bridge Road) was also heavily flooded, with vehicles and buildings submerged and major property damage to internal fixtures, fittings, furniture, vehicles and utility supplies reported. [IMG] (p55)
Parts of the Leeds district (notably Kirkstall) were affected by river flooding when the River Aire overtopped banks and breached private defences, causing inundation of local infrastructure and properties as described for Kirkstall. [IMG,PAG] (p56)
Widespread fluvial flooding from Main Rivers across the Leeds area during the event, with river channels overtopping and high river levels surcharging surface water drainage systems causing backup through gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p57)
Leeds city centre and districts experienced major fluvial and area flooding; high river levels limited surface water drainage causing area flooding and indirect impacts to high–rise/multi–occupancy accommodation and commercial premises. [PAG] (p60)
Central Leeds experienced concentrated flood incidents along the River Aire during the event, with internal property flooding the dominant incident type and clusters of incidents (including Burley and Botany Bay) shown on the incident distribution map. [IMG,PAG] (p61)
Aggregated flood flow from the River Aire travelled downstream into Leeds city centre, inundating streets and properties. Major property damage is described (internal flooding, damage to fixtures/fittings, vehicles, lifts and utilities) and the map shows the highest concentrations of flooded properties in the city centre. [IMG] (p62)
Aggregated flood flow from the River Aire proceeded into Leeds city centre, flooding streets and areas in its path. (p63)
Aggregated flood flows from the River Aire moved rapidly into Leeds city centre, flooding roads, industrial and commercial properties along the main flow path (notably around the A65 Kirkstall Road corridor) as documented in aerial imagery. [IMG,PAG] (p64)
Widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers overtopped due to heavy rainfall on saturated catchments; high river levels surcharged surface water drainage systems causing backup through road gullies, manholes and private drains. Nine severe flood warnings were in force across Leeds on 26 December 2015. (p65)
Clusters of property flooding occurred within Leeds during the event, particularly along the river corridor north of Pottery Field and near the Discovery Centre; incidents are concentrated inside the Flood Zone 3 area. [IMG] (p69)
Fluvial (river) overtopping and associated overland flows affected the Hunslet / Thwaite Gate area of Leeds. Surface water flowed from an adjacent car park onto George Mann Way and flooded the street, extending to George Mann Road (photographs dated 26 Dec 2015 at 23:15 and 23:30). Hunslet Trading Estate experienced verified internal flooding to a depth of 1350 mm. [IMG] (p70)
Widespread fluvial flooding across Leeds from main rivers that overtopped due to record river rises; high river levels also surcharged surface water systems, causing backup through gullies, manholes and private drainage and increasing urban inundation. (p71)
Widespread flood incidents during the event, concentrated in Flood Zone 3 along the River Aire with clusters of property flooding across the mapped area. [IMG] (p76)
Part of the City of Leeds (the Stourton area) experienced flooding from river overtopping and canal surcharge that inundated industrial premises and infrastructure within the Leeds administrative area. [IMG] (p77)
Widespread fluvial flooding from Main Rivers overtopped channel capacity across the city. High river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage, causing backup through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage systems and resulting in urban inundation. Severe flood warnings were in force. (p81)
Recorded major river flooding during the same events in December 2015 that affected nearby settlements; high river levels in Leeds are referenced as the major flooding incident which disrupted areas upstream/downstream. [PAG] (p83)
The Woodlesford area of Leeds experienced extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3 from the River Aire. High river levels caused backwater effects that rendered surface–water drainage inoperable, contributing to localised flooding until river levels fell. [IMG,PAG] (p84)
The Leeds area experienced river and canal–related flooding during the event; properties were flooded along the River Aire and adjacent waterways, with incidents concentrated along river and canal corridors in the Leeds area. The report records multiple severe flood warnings affecting the Leeds inner urban area during the event period. [IMG,PAG] (p85)
River Aire overtopped into adjacent floodplains and inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation; the canal then exceeded capacity (surcharged/overtopped) and flooded low–lying properties in the Woodlesford area of Leeds. [IMG] (p86)
Residential properties in the Methley and Mickletown area experienced internal and external flooding. Flooding incidents were clustered adjacent to watercourses and within high–risk flood zones; worst affected streets were Mill Lane, Lower Mickletown, Wilson's Row and Green Lane. [IMG] (p93)
Areas within the Leeds district (notably Allerton Bywater and surrounding low–lying settlements) experienced fluvial/river flooding during the event, with internal and external property flooding concentrated close to the River Aire floodplain. [IMG] (p102)
Extensive fluvial flooding from the River Wharfe within Flood Zone 3 affecting Otley (part of Leeds). Flooding caused surface water drainage to be inoperable (backwater/surcharge effects) until river levels subsided. Properties and streets were inundated; worst–affected streets and areas included A660, A659, Old Pool Road, Moor Drive, Meagill Rise, Weston Drive, Newall Carr Road, St Richards Road, Wrenbeck Drive, Farnley Lane, Billams Hill, Bridge Avenue, Old Cattle Market site and the Wharfe flood plains. [IMG] (p107)
Flood incidents during the event occurred within the Leeds Metropolitan District boundary (Otley is inside Leeds), with mapped Flood Zone 3 areas and property flooding shown in the Otley area. [IMG] (p108)
Flooding in the Collingham area of the Leeds district resulted from River Wharfe overtopping and breaching defences, causing property and garden flooding and infrastructure damage (notably Linton Bridge). [IMG] (p117)
Administrative area containing the Thorp Arch and Boston Spa flood incidents; fluvial flooding from the River Wharfe inundated parts of the Leeds district covered in the report. [IMG] (p131)
Widespread flooding during Storm Eva (26–27 Dec 2015) caused extensive damage across the city, triggered a citywide Strategic Recovery Plan and recovery actions by Leeds City Council, and led to commitments for further investment in river flood alleviation (notably the River Aire Flood Alleviation Scheme). [PAG] (p136)
Leeds experienced widespread river flooding during Storm Eva (26–27 December 2015), described as devastation not seen since 1866; flooding extent closely matched EA flood zones and the absence of formal defences along the River Aire left the city at significant risk. [TAB,PAG] (p137)
Leeds experienced widespread flooding from rivers overtopping their channels; areas of the city suffered significant damage and remain at risk in the absence of formal defences along the River Aire. [PAG] (p138)
Communities in Leeds were impacted by flooding associated with Storm Eva — Otley is cited as being significantly affected and Linton Bridge (a listed structure) was badly damaged; repair works are underway and funding/mitigation measures are being pursued to protect communities along the River Wharfe. [PAG] (p139)
Flooding (from the River Aire) prevented safe access to the Kirkstall electrical compound until 28 Dec; electrical repair work began 29 Dec after waters cleared. Floods caused road closures and bridge collapses or instability that hampered restoration; many customers were displaced. Extensive post–flood inspections and recovery work were carried out in the Leeds area. (p227)
Flooding at the Kirkstall C bulk supply point on the evening of 26 December 2015 caused RTU (Remote Terminal Unit) water damage and communication failure, contributed to EHV/HV faults on local substations and triggered customer power interruptions; the power cuts at this time coincided with very high call volumes and abandoned calls to the supplier. [IMG] (p232)
Urban flooding affected parts of Leeds during the event, including severe inundation on Kirkstall Road that disrupted transport and required emergency response. [IMG] (p237)
On 27 December 2015 the River Aire burst its banks; floodwater ran down Kirkstall Road onto Wellington Street and into Northern Street, which was already flooded. (p241)
Residential properties and businesses in Leeds were flooded; recovery and resilience work has been required, including Council–administered grants and targeted interventions to support those unable to return home and businesses not yet fully operational. [PAG] (p285)
Widespread flooding affected residents, businesses and local infrastructure across the city; recovery and support actions were implemented for those impacted and some householders remain unable to return to their homes. (p287)
Widespread residential flooding response and recovery activity organised by the council and partners: upfront payments to owners, targeting of flooded properties by letter and officer visits, support for group residential schemes, council tax relief for properties vacated due to flooding, grant funding and community support activity across the city. (p289)
Flooding associated with Storm Eva in late December 2015 affected businesses and properties across Leeds. Recovery actions included business support, property resilience measures, business rate relief and grant funding to support affected businesses. (p290)
Leeds as a city experienced business and property impacts from the Storm Eva floods; businesses in the city received grant assistance and business rate relief as part of the recovery effort, and there has been at least one high–profile business closure linked to the event. (p291)
Widespread business disruption and longer–term economic impacts across floodplain employment areas: business closures, downgraded presences/relocations, reduced investment confidence and difficulties obtaining flood insurance. [PAG] (p292)
River flooding associated with Storm Eva in late December 2015 affected the city, prompting immediate recovery activity and the commissioning of a catchment–wide feasibility study (River Aire Flood Alleviation Scheme). Flood impacts required infrastructure repairs (for example Linton Bridge) and targeted communications to affected businesses and residents. [PAG] (p294)
Experienced flooding during Storm Eva that affected residents and businesses, prompted community engagement and recovery actions, and led to infrastructure impacts such as repairs and alternative travel arrangements around Linton Bridge. [PAG] (p295)
Storm Eva flooding in Leeds on 26 December 2015 flooded industrial/office premises occupied by businesses (example: Company A). Three of four industrial/office units were flooded, leading to loss of two months' production and significant damage to essential production machinery, work in progress and business stock. The business submitted a multi–million pound insurance claim and faced a large increase in future flood insurance costs (reported as a 460% premium increase), prompting consideration of relocation out of the floodplain. (p310)","27,000 properties [PAG] (p8)
