£105,562 of council tax relief offered; 7 properties still receiving relief as of Oct 1; Leeds Community Foundation raised approximately £300,000 and given/committed just under £260,000; 25 organisations funded; 47 individual homeowner grants of £250 or £500; proposed support for 8 city–centre flat complexes (£160,000); proposed utility relocation in 26 properties on Kirkstall Road (£130,000) (p289)
14 Leeds businesses included in 43 LEP Flood Recovery Grant approvals; council provided over £1.1m rates relief to affected Leeds businesses; one high–profile business closure (Thyssen Krupp) reported (p291)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7944229,-1.7519186,"<div id=""html_9df89613866c671e99b402be588620ea"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bradford Metropolitan District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>EHV/HV substations in the Bradford area were recorded as affected by EHV/HV faults during the event; these faults were associated with flooding and contributed to the regional power interruptions and asset flooding recorded by Northern Powergrid. [IMG] (p232)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to the Northern Powergrid (Yorkshire) totals of 62 flooded assets and 49 HV incidents (event&ndash;wide figures) [IMG] (p232)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Bradford Metropolitan District,England,EHV/HV substations in the Bradford area were recorded as affected by EHV/HV faults during the event; these faults were associated with flooding and contributed to the regional power interruptions and asset flooding recorded by Northern Powergrid. [IMG] (p232),Contributed to the Northern Powergrid (Yorkshire) totals of 62 flooded assets and 49 HV incidents (event–wide figures) [IMG] (p232),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.80551782987439,-1.5797277861268815,"<div id=""html_a5a68112be72db3df974da7b45f50d3b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>A65 at Armley Mills</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire flooding produced deep standing water on the A65 through the Armley Mills area, submerging the road surface and urban infrastructure and rendering the road impassable at the time shown. [IMG] (p62)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,A65 at Armley Mills,England,"River Aire flooding produced deep standing water on the A65 through the Armley Mills area, submerging the road surface and urban infrastructure and rendering the road impassable at the time shown. [IMG] (p62)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.753027,-1.3954201,"<div id=""html_dfea8f6521a6e8e8cb254d7e7dfa4767"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>RSPB St Aidan's</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive inundation of St Aidan&#8217;s former opencast and adjacent washlands used as temporary flood storage; industrial facilities, tanks and a marina were shown submerged in aerial imagery. [IMG] (p90)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The St Aidan's temporary storage reservoir and spillways were tested by the extreme flood (first time since completion). Its operation, together with associated washlands, reduced downstream flood severity (modelled ~500 mm reduction at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown) and almost certainly prevented severe inundation of properties between Woodlesford and Castleford. [PAG] (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The St Aidan's temporary storage reservoir and washlands were tested and operated during the extreme flood, reducing peak river flood levels and preventing severe inundation; modelled to reduce a 1% AEP flood by around 500mm at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown, and its operation is credited with almost certainly preventing severe flooding of many properties in the corridor between Woodlesford and Castleford. [PAG] (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The former opencast area acted as a temporary storage reservoir during the extreme flood event, filling with floodwater and reducing downstream flood levels. The St Aidan spillways and reservoir were tested for the first time and together with associated washlands substantially reduced inundation downstream between Woodlesford and Castleford. [IMG,PAG] (p99)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Aidan's washland flood storage areas were inundated by overtopping from the River Aire and River Calder, receiving floodwater and forming part of the flow routes that led to flooding of isolated properties (e.g. on Barnsdale Road). (p103)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 500 mm reduction in flood level at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown; 1289 properties saved (modelled estimate for combined effect of St Aidan's and associated washlands between Woodlesford and Castleford) [PAG] (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1289 properties saved; 500mm reduction in flood level at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown [PAG] (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 million cubic metres capacity; models indicate up to 1289 properties saved (combined with associated washlands) [IMG,PAG] (p99)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,RSPB St Aidan's,England,"Extensive inundation of St Aidan’s former opencast and adjacent washlands used as temporary flood storage; industrial facilities, tanks and a marina were shown submerged in aerial imagery. [IMG] (p90)
The St Aidan's temporary storage reservoir and spillways were tested by the extreme flood (first time since completion). Its operation, together with associated washlands, reduced downstream flood severity (modelled ~500 mm reduction at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown) and almost certainly prevented severe inundation of properties between Woodlesford and Castleford. [PAG] (p91)
The St Aidan's temporary storage reservoir and washlands were tested and operated during the extreme flood, reducing peak river flood levels and preventing severe inundation; modelled to reduce a 1% AEP flood by around 500mm at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown, and its operation is credited with almost certainly preventing severe flooding of many properties in the corridor between Woodlesford and Castleford. [PAG] (p92)
The former opencast area acted as a temporary storage reservoir during the extreme flood event, filling with floodwater and reducing downstream flood levels. The St Aidan spillways and reservoir were tested for the first time and together with associated washlands substantially reduced inundation downstream between Woodlesford and Castleford. [IMG,PAG] (p99)
St Aidan's washland flood storage areas were inundated by overtopping from the River Aire and River Calder, receiving floodwater and forming part of the flow routes that led to flooding of isolated properties (e.g. on Barnsdale Road). (p103)","around 500 mm reduction in flood level at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown; 1289 properties saved (modelled estimate for combined effect of St Aidan's and associated washlands between Woodlesford and Castleford) [PAG] (p91)
1289 properties saved; 500mm reduction in flood level at Allerton Bywater, Castleford, Methley and Mickletown [PAG] (p92)
7 million cubic metres capacity; models indicate up to 1289 properties saved (combined with associated washlands) [IMG,PAG] (p99)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8072519,-1.5871044,"<div id=""html_d26c5bf8925c86df231456d1f8f9d50a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>ASDA</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Shopfronts including ASDA on Kirkstall Road are shown submerged by floodwater as the overtopped River Aire&#8217;s overland flows covered the road and immediate frontage, indicating inundation of commercial premises and immediate access disruption. [IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,ASDA,England,"Shopfronts including ASDA on Kirkstall Road are shown submerged by floodwater as the overtopped River Aire’s overland flows covered the road and immediate frontage, indicating inundation of commercial premises and immediate access disruption. [IMG] (p41)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8283777,-1.6390362,"<div id=""html_37bde2897f3cd596f58726416b9cb1d1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>New flood cells in Leeds City Centre were not complete at the time of the December 2015 floods, so the scheme did not provide protection during the event and its effectiveness was neutralised for the purposes of this report. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Advance works (low&ndash;level embankments) provided protection to Woodlesford during the event, protecting residential properties from river flooding. (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flooding from Storm Eva highlighted that there were no formal defences along the River Aire and the city remained at risk. Phase 1 of the Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme is being progressed to provide protection for areas south of the railway station; Phase 2 work is being developed to identify options for the whole River Aire catchment from Malham Tarn. The scheme is being promoted to reduce the likelihood of a repeat of the river flooding experienced during Storm Eva. [TAB] (p137)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,The Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme,England,"New flood cells in Leeds City Centre were not complete at the time of the December 2015 floods, so the scheme did not provide protection during the event and its effectiveness was neutralised for the purposes of this report. (p43)
Advance works (low–level embankments) provided protection to Woodlesford during the event, protecting residential properties from river flooding. (p84)
The flooding from Storm Eva highlighted that there were no formal defences along the River Aire and the city remained at risk. Phase 1 of the Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme is being progressed to provide protection for areas south of the railway station; Phase 2 work is being developed to identify options for the whole River Aire catchment from Malham Tarn. The scheme is being promoted to reduce the likelihood of a repeat of the river flooding experienced during Storm Eva. [TAB] (p137)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7677971,-1.5034571,"<div id=""html_6ee31118883e890b30952079cfdb2274"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stourton Junction</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incidents were recorded at and around Stourton Junction during the event; the area experienced internal and external property flooding in adjacent industrial/transport premises within the Flood Zone 3 corridor beside the River Aire. [IMG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overbank flows and mapped flood routes passed through/near the junction, indicating inundation or flooding of adjacent railway infrastructure and surrounding land during the event. [IMG] (p77)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Stourton Junction,England,"Flood incidents were recorded at and around Stourton Junction during the event; the area experienced internal and external property flooding in adjacent industrial/transport premises within the Flood Zone 3 corridor beside the River Aire. [IMG] (p76)
Overbank flows and mapped flood routes passed through/near the junction, indicating inundation or flooding of adjacent railway infrastructure and surrounding land during the event. [IMG] (p77)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.91145987418847,-1.3132872853260913,"<div id=""html_32ef359b17bd9cbcc6371e46702bac57"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thorp Arch Trading Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the river corridor shown to have experienced fluvial flooding as the River Wharfe came out of channel; incidents nearby include internal flooding and flooding to highways/amenity areas affecting the trading estate vicinity. [IMG] (p131)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flood depths 500mm to 1500mm [IMG] (p131)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Thorp Arch Trading Estate,England,Part of the river corridor shown to have experienced fluvial flooding as the River Wharfe came out of channel; incidents nearby include internal flooding and flooding to highways/amenity areas affecting the trading estate vicinity. [IMG] (p131),internal flood depths 500mm to 1500mm [IMG] (p131),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9113525,-1.7028841,"<div id=""html_1b04238dc47ba0812d1f04da6f74ff8e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kell Beck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runs along the west side of Otley and then into a culverted system; the report notes Kell Beck may contribute to flooding of properties on the left bank when the River Wharfe overtops, contributing to flood impacts in the Otley area during the December 2015 events. (p105)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kell Beck outfall (just upstream of Otley Bridge) became ineffective during record&ndash;high River Wharfe levels; fluvial backwater and surcharge prevented gravity drainage, causing localised flooding where the outfall and connected surface water systems could not discharge to the river. (p106)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Kell Beck,England,"Runs along the west side of Otley and then into a culverted system; the report notes Kell Beck may contribute to flooding of properties on the left bank when the River Wharfe overtops, contributing to flood impacts in the Otley area during the December 2015 events. (p105)
The Kell Beck outfall (just upstream of Otley Bridge) became ineffective during record–high River Wharfe levels; fluvial backwater and surcharge prevented gravity drainage, causing localised flooding where the outfall and connected surface water systems could not discharge to the river. (p106)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9056119,-1.4160136,"<div id=""html_49247e72948bb138f85054ac8795ce5d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Collingham Beck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Collingham Beck, a steep rapid&ndash;response watercourse, rose to high levels during the event and contributed surcharge/back&ndash;pressure to surface water systems and localised flooding in Collingham. [PAG] (p113)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Collingham Beck caused flooding in 2007 (which led to construction of an EA floodwall). During the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event, high beck levels contributed to fluvial backwater and surcharge on surface water systems, worsening localised flooding. [MUL] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Collingham Beck overtopped/produced overland flows that led to extensive and deep garden flooding adjacent to Beck Lane and Church Lane. [IMG] (p117)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Collingham Beck,England,"Collingham Beck, a steep rapid–response watercourse, rose to high levels during the event and contributed surcharge/back–pressure to surface water systems and localised flooding in Collingham. [PAG] (p113)
Collingham Beck caused flooding in 2007 (which led to construction of an EA floodwall). During the 25–29 Dec 2015 event, high beck levels contributed to fluvial backwater and surcharge on surface water systems, worsening localised flooding. [MUL] (p114)
Collingham Beck overtopped/produced overland flows that led to extensive and deep garden flooding adjacent to Beck Lane and Church Lane. [IMG] (p117)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7833129,-1.5193082,"<div id=""html_cdf5ab7ba36cb9df5ae0f4f6eb2873ea"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Knowsthorpe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A dense cluster of internal property flooding was concentrated north of the river near the Knowsthorpe area during Storm Eva; properties in this area experienced internal flooding consistent with the mapped flood extent. [IMG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map of the event shows overland fluvial flows and flood extents affecting Knowsthorpe, with flood incidents concentrated along the river corridor and into built&ndash;up areas. [IMG] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A severe flood warning was issued for Knowsthorpe (indicating imminent flooding risk from main rivers); area was affected by high river levels and surcharging of drainage during the event. (p81)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Included within grid squares of the mapped distribution, contributing to the highest&ndash;density category (206&ndash;592 properties in the largest grid squares) [IMG] (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Knowsthorpe,England,"A dense cluster of internal property flooding was concentrated north of the river near the Knowsthorpe area during Storm Eva; properties in this area experienced internal flooding consistent with the mapped flood extent. [IMG] (p69)
Map of the event shows overland fluvial flows and flood extents affecting Knowsthorpe, with flood incidents concentrated along the river corridor and into built–up areas. [IMG] (p70)
A severe flood warning was issued for Knowsthorpe (indicating imminent flooding risk from main rivers); area was affected by high river levels and surcharging of drainage during the event. (p81)","Included within grid squares of the mapped distribution, contributing to the highest–density category (206–592 properties in the largest grid squares) [IMG] (p69)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7684422,-1.4230156,"<div id=""html_e2675d7ff58c4eb08b2d04ec2524ff83"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swillington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the River Aire and inundation from the adjacent Aire & Calder Navigation overtopping caused inundation of low&ndash;lying land and properties in the Swillington area. [IMG] (p85)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from a nearby canal breach (near Swillington Bridge) affected adjacent land and infrastructure in the Swillington area. [IMG] (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Swillington washlands inundated and acted as temporary flood storage during high River Aire flows, interacting with St Aidan's to hold water and reduce downstream peaks. (p90)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Swillington washlands (natural floodplain) filled with excess River Aire flows during the extreme flood of 25 Dec 2015 &ndash; 29 Dec 2015, acting as temporary storage and reducing downstream inundation; it interacted with St Aidan's storage to limit flooding of properties downstream (part of the system modelled to have saved properties). [PAG] (p99)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1289 properties [PAG] (p99)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Swillington,England,"Fluvial flooding from the River Aire and inundation from the adjacent Aire & Calder Navigation overtopping caused inundation of low–lying land and properties in the Swillington area. [IMG] (p85)
Floodwater from a nearby canal breach (near Swillington Bridge) affected adjacent land and infrastructure in the Swillington area. [IMG] (p86)
The Swillington washlands inundated and acted as temporary flood storage during high River Aire flows, interacting with St Aidan's to hold water and reduce downstream peaks. (p90)
The Swillington washlands (natural floodplain) filled with excess River Aire flows during the extreme flood of 25 Dec 2015 – 29 Dec 2015, acting as temporary storage and reducing downstream inundation; it interacted with St Aidan's storage to limit flooding of properties downstream (part of the system modelled to have saved properties). [PAG] (p99)",1289 properties [PAG] (p99),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7394457,-1.3847636,"<div id=""html_e5bb7cf6c2c1b5413428dac1f184499e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Mickletown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Standing floodwater covered a residential street, water encroaching from the adjacent water body (river) onto the road and pavements, restricting access and evidencing inundation of highway on 28/12/2015. [IMG] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding incidents occurred on Lower Mickletown during the event; identified among the worst&ndash;affected streets with internal and/or external residential flooding near watercourses. (p93)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Mickletown (outlying area) experienced fewer flooding incidents as part of the wider fluvial event; flood incidents were present but smaller in number and extent compared with central Mickletown. [IMG] (p94)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded flood incidents during 25 Dec 2015 &ndash; 29 Dec 2015 in the surrounding area (map scope and incident distribution include Lower Mickletown within the affected area near the River Aire/floodplain). [IMG] (p102)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Lower Mickletown,England,"Standing floodwater covered a residential street, water encroaching from the adjacent water body (river) onto the road and pavements, restricting access and evidencing inundation of highway on 28/12/2015. [IMG] (p88)
Property flooding incidents occurred on Lower Mickletown during the event; identified among the worst–affected streets with internal and/or external residential flooding near watercourses. (p93)
Lower Mickletown (outlying area) experienced fewer flooding incidents as part of the wider fluvial event; flood incidents were present but smaller in number and extent compared with central Mickletown. [IMG] (p94)
Recorded flood incidents during 25 Dec 2015 – 29 Dec 2015 in the surrounding area (map scope and incident distribution include Lower Mickletown within the affected area near the River Aire/floodplain). [IMG] (p102)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7313734,-1.3940495,"<div id=""html_17609e444337dc9cc190d1595b4bcf37"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Windmill Moor</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map shading and flow&ndash;route arrows show flood extents and overland fluvial flow reaching low&ndash;lying areas including Windmill Moor, indicating inundation of fields/adjacent land and possible impacts to nearby properties/paths. [IMG] (p94)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Windmill Moor,England,"Map shading and flow–route arrows show flood extents and overland fluvial flow reaching low–lying areas including Windmill Moor, indicating inundation of fields/adjacent land and possible impacts to nearby properties/paths. [IMG] (p94)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7584914,-1.3397191,"<div id=""html_dcdf8a8420bdaf90184675efa0fc17b6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ledston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents recorded during 25 Dec 2015 &ndash; 29 Dec 2015, with clusters of incidents shown close to the river/floodplain within Flood Zone 3 (map indicates Ledston had flood incident clusters associated with the event). [IMG] (p102)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Ledston,England,"Flooding incidents recorded during 25 Dec 2015 – 29 Dec 2015, with clusters of incidents shown close to the river/floodplain within Flood Zone 3 (map indicates Ledston had flood incident clusters associated with the event). [IMG] (p102)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8034294,-1.5829169,"<div id=""html_78488edd03aa3e6936321f103ce725c1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Aire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Further rainfall over Christmas and Boxing Day produced the highest river levels ever seen in many places along the River Aire catchment, contributing to widespread flooding of communities, homes and businesses. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high flows in the River Aire (at Armley) produced very high flood levels that were the main cause of widespread flooding; water also came out of channel approximately 0.8 miles upstream of the Armley flow gauge and flowed down Kirkstall Road towards the city centre. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire is identified as a dominant feature and a principal cause of flooding to Leeds and adjacent communities. Its steep profile, rapid response to rainfall and many tributaries contributed to river flooding of Leeds city centre and surrounding areas during Storm Eva. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Source of deep, fast&ndash;flowing flood water that rapidly inundated streets (including Kirkstall Road and Wellington Street) and carried floodwater over a distance into the heart of the city; formal and informal defences were on occasion overtopped or bypassed. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding originated from the River Aire (a Main River) and is identified in the report as a source of Main River flooding affecting Leeds; the report references flood alleviation measures (River Aire Flood Alleviation Scheme). [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the District's Main Rivers and a major characteristic of Leeds City Centre; the report states the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 flooding was almost exclusively a Main River fluvial event, implicating the River Aire as a principal source of fluvial flooding that affected communities along its corridor. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A Main River fluvial event on 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 along the River Aire caused flooding of areas adjacent to the river and contributed to widespread inundation across Leeds MDC (including multiple Aire&ndash;focused sub&ndash;areas). [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Photographs and captions document River Aire flooding in December 2015 in the Leeds area, showing urban streets, industrial/commercial areas and city streets submerged (water reaching street furniture and the base of buildings). [IMG,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow in the River Aire at Armley on 27 December 2015 was the highest ever recorded, producing flood levels approximately 1.2 m higher than the previous record from autumn 2000. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows from the River Aire's source (Malham Cove) following extreme rainfall contributed to downstream flooding; this was a precursor to the Boxing Day floods that affected communities including Leeds. [MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified in the report as one of the predominant causes of property flooding in Leeds and nearby areas during the event; contributed to the internal and external property flooding counts reported for the metropolitan district. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in proximity to the River Aire affected properties across multiple study areas. Reported impacts by area include Leeds City Centre (1938 internal residential; 566 external residential; 296 internal commercial; 55 external commercial), Newlay (5 internal residential; 4 external residential; 1 internal commercial), Kirkstall (0 internal residential; 45 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 15 external commercial), Burley (8 internal residential; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial; 16 external commercial), Thwaite Gate and Hunslet (60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial), Stourton (0 internal residential; 0 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 4 external commercial), Woodlesford (1 internal residential; 4 external residential; 0 internal commercial; 1 external commercial), Methley and Mickletown (4 internal residential; 32 external residential), and Allerton Bywater (2 internal residential; 2 external residential; 7 internal commercial). [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High/record river levels, steep profile and rapid response to rainfall contributed to flooding in Leeds; elevated river levels prevented surface water drainage systems from discharging and caused fluvial backwater effects that led to surcharge of surface water systems and extensive flooding. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high river levels in the River Aire produced extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3, caused fluvial backwater effects that prevented free discharge from the surface water drainage system and led to surcharge/back pressures that produced additional area and surface&ndash;water flooding in the city centre. [TAB] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire and its tributaries are shown as the principal loci of flooding, with incidents highly concentrated along the river and largest clusters of flooded properties occurring adjacent to its banks, driving widespread property inundation in central Leeds and riverside neighbourhoods. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Came out of channel and overtopped its banks at multiple locations in Leeds City Centre (including Little Neville Street, Bridge End/Meadow Lane, Turlow Court, Neptune Street, Clarence Road and Sovereign Street), causing direct flooding of offices, residential properties, assisted living and other commercial areas. [IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its channel in multiple locations in Leeds City Centre, causing primary fluvial flooding that directly inundated Little Neville Street (flooding Direct Line Group offices and nearby high&ndash;rise apartments), Bridge End/Meadow Lane (offices, Navigation Walk and Dock Street), Turlow Court (residential areas), Neptune Street (residential and commercial properties), Clarence Road (Leeds Assisted Living, nearby commercial properties and Armouries Drive) and low&ndash;lying properties along Sovereign Street. [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the River Aire caused flooding of adjacent urban streets and properties (images and captions link overtopping to inundation at Dock Street, Neptune Street, Bridge End and Meadow Lane). [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its banks (overtopping at Meadow Lane) and raised river levels so high that surface water drainage could not discharge freely for long periods (lasting in some areas for days). These high river levels produced backwater effects and surcharge/back pressures on surface water and sewer systems, which in turn caused flooding of Northern Street, East Street, Sovereign Street, Concordia Street and properties along Whitehall Road as a precursor to later overland fluvial flows. Sewer surcharge related to the high river levels caused roads to become impassable (example: East Street reported impassable after surcharge). [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Aire levels caused sewer surcharge at downstream locations (notably East Street) and contributed to overland fluvial exceedance flows and urban flooding in Leeds, making roads impassable in places. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped upstream in Kirkstall and produced overland fluvial exceedance flows that travelled approximately 2.7 km (over a mile) downstream into Leeds city centre, causing widespread inundation and putting life at risk. [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped about 2.7 km upstream in Kirkstall, sending a south&ndash;easterly fluvial exceedance flow into Leeds City Centre that flooded properties along its path and put life at risk. [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid runoff and river rise led to the River Aire overtopping its channel capacity, producing widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage and caused backup into gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial flooding from the River Aire (within flood zone 3) which caused property flooding and disruption in adjacent districts (including Kirkstall and Burley). [TAB] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped/caused flooding along its Kirkstall corridor during 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015, producing internal and external flooding of built properties (mud and debris evident) and severe internal water ingress in some buildings. [IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopped its banks and breached private flood defences in Kirkstall, spilling onto floodplains and directly flooding areas including The Bridge Inn, Leeds rugby academy facility, Morrison's supermarket car park, St Ann's Mills and low&ndash;lying properties between the river and Kirkstall Road. [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its banks and breached private flood defence assets, spilling into floodplains and onto land in Kirkstall. Floodwaters inundated the Network Rail line and directly flooded The Bridge Inn, Leeds rugby academy facility, Morrison's Supermarket car park, St Ann's Mills and low&ndash;lying properties between the river and Kirkstall Road. [IMG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high river levels on the River Aire produced fluvial backwater that constrained free discharge from the surface water drainage system and caused surcharge/back pressures, which in turn led to extensive surface water/urban flooding in Burley and nearby areas. (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3; record high river levels constrained free discharge from surface water systems, caused fluvial backwater and surcharge that contributed to widespread area and property flooding in adjacent districts (e.g., Burley). [TAB] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overbank flooding from the River Aire produced the majority of recorded incidents along the river corridor, causing internal flooding to nearby properties (notably in Burley) and area/amenity flooding along the floodplain. [IMG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its banks at multiple locations, breached informal private flood defences and spilled onto adjacent roads (including the A65), producing aggregated flood flows that travelled rapidly downstream into Leeds city centre and caused widespread property flooding and damage. [IMG,PAG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its channel and banks, breached private informal flood defences and spilled onto the A65 Kirkstall Road (opposite Woodside View). Flood flows travelled downstream into side roads and then continued rapidly into Leeds city centre, inundating streets and properties along the route. [IMG,PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its banks at several locations, breached private informal flood defence assets, and spilled onto the A65 Kirkstall Road (at the meander opposite Woodside View) and downstream side roads; these overtopping flows aggregated and proceeded into Leeds city centre, flooding areas along the flow path. [IMG,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high River Aire levels caused fluvial backwater effects that rendered surface water drainage systems draining toward the river inoperable and generated surcharge/back pressures that contributed to localised flooding in adjacent low&ndash;lying areas (notably Thwaite Gate and Hunslet). (p67)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high river levels caused extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3. High river levels produced backwater/surcharge effects that rendered surface water drainage (including the nearby pumping station) inoperable until levels fell, contributing to localised flooding; when river levels dropped gravity discharge resumed and local flooding subsided. [PAG] (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high flows in the River Aire led to swollen adjacent waterways and overtopping of channels, producing widespread fluvial flooding and contributing to the inundation of low&ndash;lying areas and properties. (p71)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main River fluvial flooding: the River Aire overtopped and exceeded channel capacity, causing widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage, preventing free discharge and causing backups through gullies, manholes and private drainage. [PAG] (p72)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high River Aire levels during 25 Dec 2015 &ndash; 29 Dec 2015 caused localised flooding influence in low&ndash;lying areas of Stourton. Elevated river levels rendered surface&ndash;water drainage systems discharging to the river (including the syphon beneath the canal and the nearby pumping station) inoperable until river levels subsided. [PAG] (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high river levels produced fluvial backwater effects that prevented gravity discharge of local surface water systems, contributing to localised flooding in low&ndash;lying areas. [IMG] (p74)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire produced extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3 that inundated Stourton and caused property flooding (commercial and external commercial) in the area. [TAB] (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire and its watercourses are shown as the primary focus of mapped flood zones and are spatially associated with overbanking/flow routes that led to internal and external property flooding in adjacent Stourton. [IMG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped footpaths and contributed overland fluvial flows into the adjacent industrial estate; its interaction with the canal and overtopping routes is mapped as a primary source of inundation. [IMG] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Structural collapse of the Knostrop by&ndash;wash embankment allowed River Aire waters to enter the canal system, surcharging the Aire and Calder Navigation and contributing to overtopping and downstream flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p78)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire waters breached the by&ndash;wash embankment at Knostrop and inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation canal, contributing to overtopping and flooding of nearby land and properties. [IMG,PAG] (p79)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire overtopped/flowed through the breached Knostrop Lock structure, inundating the adjacent canal and causing significant water flow into the canal system. [IMG] (p80)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high River Aire levels during 25 Dec 2015 &ndash; 29 Dec 2015 caused fluvial backwater that rendered surface water drainage systems draining to the river inoperable, produced surcharge/back pressures in surface water systems and led to localised flooding in Woodlesford; standing floodwater inundated industrial units and signage (photograph dated 27 Dec 2015). (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high river levels on the River Aire caused extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3, rendered surface water drainage systems inoperable (backwater effects and surcharge), and produced localised flooding until river levels subsided. [TAB,PAG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river overtopped its channel, spilling onto adjacent floodplains and driving the inundation of the Aire & Calder Navigation; this fluvial overflow was described as the primary cause of flooding that led to low&ndash;lying properties and waterside areas being flooded around Woodlesford/Oulton/Swillington. [IMG,PAG] (p85)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire came out of its channel, overtopping into adjacent floodplains. This overtopping inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation canal and contributed to downstream canal surcharging, breaches and flooding of nearby low&ndash;lying properties and land. [IMG] (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high River Aire levels in late December 2015 caused conventional surface water drainage systems draining toward the river to become inoperable, contributing to localised fluvial flooding in low&ndash;lying areas. Photographic evidence documents standing floodwater on a residential street in Lower Mickletown on 28 December 2015, with water encroaching from the river side onto the road and affecting accessibility. [PAG,MUL] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high River Aire levels generated fluvial backwater and made surface water drainage systems discharging to the river inoperable, causing localised flooding and surcharge/back&ndash;pressure of surface water systems in low&ndash;lying areas. [PAG] (p90)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire produced extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3 during the event, contributing to inundation of adjacent settlements (including areas referenced such as Methley, Mickletown and Castleford). [TAB] (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its banks and contributed to fluvial inundation of the adjacent Mickletown Ings flood storage area; overtopping and overland flows from the river drove floodwaters into low&ndash;lying adjacent areas and contributed to property flooding on the fringes of the flood washland. [IMG,PAG] (p94)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire spills into Allerton Ings and causes flooding of the Barnsdale Road area; it is identified as the source of significant historical flooding affecting Allerton Bywater and contributed to the December 2015 inundation (Station Road submerged). (p97)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire was the primary influence for extensive fluvial flooding in flood zone 3, driving inundation of Allerton Ings, Barnsdale Road and properties in Allerton Bywater during the event. [TAB] (p101)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its channel and flowed into St Aidan's and associated washland flood storage areas; this overtopping and release of water contributed to inundation that flooded isolated properties along Barnsdale Road in Allerton Bywater. [IMG,PAG] (p102)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire came out of channel and overtopped into St Aidan's and associated washland flood storage areas, driving overland flows and contributing to inundation of adjacent low&ndash;lying land and property flooding (including along Barnsdale Road). [IMG] (p103)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire overtopped its channel during Storm Eva (26&ndash;27 December 2015), driving river flooding across Leeds; the report states there are no formal flood defences along the River Aire, leaving the city exposed to that flooding source. [TAB,PAG] (p137)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main river (River Aire) came out of channel during the event, causing flooding across Leeds; there were no formal flood defences along the River Aire so the city was particularly affected and remains at risk until defences are provided. (p138)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire levels caused flooding that inundated the Kirkstall compound, prevented safe site access until 28 Dec 2015 and delayed electrical repair works until flood waters were cleared on 29 Dec 2015. (p227)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river burst its banks on 27.12.15 and was the source of floodwater that ran down Kirkstall Road, onto Wellington Street and into Northern Street, initiating the urban/basement flooding described. (p242)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the river banks and adjacent premises in Kirkstall led to volunteer and corporate clean&ndash;ups of the River Aire's banks and contributed to commercial premises being flooded. (p290)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the River Aire affected businesses along its banks in Kirkstall, Hunslet and Stourton; the river&ndash;related flooding caused visual amenity damage to premises and contributed to reduced trade and inward investment for those flood&ndash;affected businesses. (p292)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 1.2m higher than the previous record; approximately 0.8 miles (out&ndash;of&ndash;channel flow origin) [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 1.2 m higher than the previous record; in excess of 0.5% (1 in 200) AEP (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Leeds City Centre: 1938 internal residential; 566 external residential; 296 internal commercial; 55 external commercial (total 2855). Newlay: 5 internal residential; 4 external residential; 1 internal commercial (total 10). Kirkstall: 0 internal residential; 45 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 15 external commercial (total 96). Burley: 8 internal residential; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial; 16 external commercial (total 303). Thwaite Gate and Hunslet: 60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial (total 1170). Stourton: 0 internal residential; 0 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 4 external commercial (total 40). Woodlesford: 1 internal residential; 4 external residential; 0 internal commercial; 1 external commercial (total 6). Methley and Mickletown: 4 internal residential; 32 external residential (total 36). Allerton Bywater: 2 internal residential; 2 external residential; 7 internal commercial (total 11). [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2,855 properties in Leeds City Centre reported flood&ndash;affected during the event; internal flood depths 1 m to in excess of 2 m; direct economic impact £60.3 million [TAB] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Map analysis notes largest incident clusters located along the river and in Flood Zone 3; grid&ndash;square incident categories include ranges up to 205&ndash;392 properties. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>thousands of properties [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>thousands of properties [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>96 properties flooded in Kirkstall; flood depths up to 1500mm (reported in Kirkstall) [TAB] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>96 properties; up to 1500mm internal flood depths [IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to flooding that affected at least 303 properties in Burley. [TAB] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 303 properties (in Burley); internal flood depths 840 mm to 1540 mm [IMG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>60 internal residential properties; 1,051 external residential properties; 49 internal commercial properties; 10 external commercial properties; total 1,170 properties flooded (Thwaite Gate and Hunslet) [PAG] (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>36 internal commercial properties; 4 external commercial properties; 40 properties total [TAB] (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 206 properties flooded in a central Mickletown grid square (map category 206&ndash;592) [IMG,PAG] (p94)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to 11 properties flooded in Allerton Bywater; contributed to Barnsdale Road being modelled ~1.5 m under water in a 1 in 5 year event [TAB] (p101)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,River Aire,England,"Further rainfall over Christmas and Boxing Day produced the highest river levels ever seen in many places along the River Aire catchment, contributing to widespread flooding of communities, homes and businesses. (p7)
Record high flows in the River Aire (at Armley) produced very high flood levels that were the main cause of widespread flooding; water also came out of channel approximately 0.8 miles upstream of the Armley flow gauge and flowed down Kirkstall Road towards the city centre. [PAG] (p8)
The River Aire is identified as a dominant feature and a principal cause of flooding to Leeds and adjacent communities. Its steep profile, rapid response to rainfall and many tributaries contributed to river flooding of Leeds city centre and surrounding areas during Storm Eva. [PAG] (p9)
Source of deep, fast–flowing flood water that rapidly inundated streets (including Kirkstall Road and Wellington Street) and carried floodwater over a distance into the heart of the city; formal and informal defences were on occasion overtopped or bypassed. (p10)
Flooding originated from the River Aire (a Main River) and is identified in the report as a source of Main River flooding affecting Leeds; the report references flood alleviation measures (River Aire Flood Alleviation Scheme). [PAG] (p12)
Identified as one of the District's Main Rivers and a major characteristic of Leeds City Centre; the report states the 25–29 Dec 2015 flooding was almost exclusively a Main River fluvial event, implicating the River Aire as a principal source of fluvial flooding that affected communities along its corridor. [PAG] (p16)
A Main River fluvial event on 25–29 Dec 2015 along the River Aire caused flooding of areas adjacent to the river and contributed to widespread inundation across Leeds MDC (including multiple Aire–focused sub–areas). [IMG] (p17)
Photographs and captions document River Aire flooding in December 2015 in the Leeds area, showing urban streets, industrial/commercial areas and city streets submerged (water reaching street furniture and the base of buildings). [IMG,PAG] (p20)
Flow in the River Aire at Armley on 27 December 2015 was the highest ever recorded, producing flood levels approximately 1.2 m higher than the previous record from autumn 2000. (p24)
High flows from the River Aire's source (Malham Cove) following extreme rainfall contributed to downstream flooding; this was a precursor to the Boxing Day floods that affected communities including Leeds. [MUL] (p25)
Identified in the report as one of the predominant causes of property flooding in Leeds and nearby areas during the event; contributed to the internal and external property flooding counts reported for the metropolitan district. (p27)
Flooding in proximity to the River Aire affected properties across multiple study areas. Reported impacts by area include Leeds City Centre (1938 internal residential; 566 external residential; 296 internal commercial; 55 external commercial), Newlay (5 internal residential; 4 external residential; 1 internal commercial), Kirkstall (0 internal residential; 45 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 15 external commercial), Burley (8 internal residential; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial; 16 external commercial), Thwaite Gate and Hunslet (60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial), Stourton (0 internal residential; 0 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 4 external commercial), Woodlesford (1 internal residential; 4 external residential; 0 internal commercial; 1 external commercial), Methley and Mickletown (4 internal residential; 32 external residential), and Allerton Bywater (2 internal residential; 2 external residential; 7 internal commercial). [TAB] (p29)
High/record river levels, steep profile and rapid response to rainfall contributed to flooding in Leeds; elevated river levels prevented surface water drainage systems from discharging and caused fluvial backwater effects that led to surcharge of surface water systems and extensive flooding. [PAG] (p32)
Record–high river levels in the River Aire produced extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3, caused fluvial backwater effects that prevented free discharge from the surface water drainage system and led to surcharge/back pressures that produced additional area and surface–water flooding in the city centre. [TAB] (p33)
The River Aire and its tributaries are shown as the principal loci of flooding, with incidents highly concentrated along the river and largest clusters of flooded properties occurring adjacent to its banks, driving widespread property inundation in central Leeds and riverside neighbourhoods. [IMG] (p35)
Came out of channel and overtopped its banks at multiple locations in Leeds City Centre (including Little Neville Street, Bridge End/Meadow Lane, Turlow Court, Neptune Street, Clarence Road and Sovereign Street), causing direct flooding of offices, residential properties, assisted living and other commercial areas. [IMG] (p36)
The River Aire overtopped its channel in multiple locations in Leeds City Centre, causing primary fluvial flooding that directly inundated Little Neville Street (flooding Direct Line Group offices and nearby high–rise apartments), Bridge End/Meadow Lane (offices, Navigation Walk and Dock Street), Turlow Court (residential areas), Neptune Street (residential and commercial properties), Clarence Road (Leeds Assisted Living, nearby commercial properties and Armouries Drive) and low–lying properties along Sovereign Street. [IMG,PAG] (p37)
Overtopping of the River Aire caused flooding of adjacent urban streets and properties (images and captions link overtopping to inundation at Dock Street, Neptune Street, Bridge End and Meadow Lane). [IMG,PAG] (p38)
The River Aire overtopped its banks (overtopping at Meadow Lane) and raised river levels so high that surface water drainage could not discharge freely for long periods (lasting in some areas for days). These high river levels produced backwater effects and surcharge/back pressures on surface water and sewer systems, which in turn caused flooding of Northern Street, East Street, Sovereign Street, Concordia Street and properties along Whitehall Road as a precursor to later overland fluvial flows. Sewer surcharge related to the high river levels caused roads to become impassable (example: East Street reported impassable after surcharge). [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p39)
High River Aire levels caused sewer surcharge at downstream locations (notably East Street) and contributed to overland fluvial exceedance flows and urban flooding in Leeds, making roads impassable in places. [PAG] (p40)
The River Aire overtopped upstream in Kirkstall and produced overland fluvial exceedance flows that travelled approximately 2.7 km (over a mile) downstream into Leeds city centre, causing widespread inundation and putting life at risk. [IMG,PAG] (p41)
The River Aire overtopped about 2.7 km upstream in Kirkstall, sending a south–easterly fluvial exceedance flow into Leeds City Centre that flooded properties along its path and put life at risk. [PAG] (p42)
Rapid runoff and river rise led to the River Aire overtopping its channel capacity, producing widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage and caused backup into gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p43)
Extensive fluvial flooding from the River Aire (within flood zone 3) which caused property flooding and disruption in adjacent districts (including Kirkstall and Burley). [TAB] (p52)
The River Aire overtopped/caused flooding along its Kirkstall corridor during 25–29 Dec 2015, producing internal and external flooding of built properties (mud and debris evident) and severe internal water ingress in some buildings. [IMG] (p53)
Overtopped its banks and breached private flood defences in Kirkstall, spilling onto floodplains and directly flooding areas including The Bridge Inn, Leeds rugby academy facility, Morrison's supermarket car park, St Ann's Mills and low–lying properties between the river and Kirkstall Road. [IMG,PAG] (p54)
The River Aire overtopped its banks and breached private flood defence assets, spilling into floodplains and onto land in Kirkstall. Floodwaters inundated the Network Rail line and directly flooded The Bridge Inn, Leeds rugby academy facility, Morrison's Supermarket car park, St Ann's Mills and low–lying properties between the river and Kirkstall Road. [IMG] (p55)
Record–high river levels on the River Aire produced fluvial backwater that constrained free discharge from the surface water drainage system and caused surcharge/back pressures, which in turn led to extensive surface water/urban flooding in Burley and nearby areas. (p59)
Experienced extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3; record high river levels constrained free discharge from surface water systems, caused fluvial backwater and surcharge that contributed to widespread area and property flooding in adjacent districts (e.g., Burley). [TAB] (p60)
Overbank flooding from the River Aire produced the majority of recorded incidents along the river corridor, causing internal flooding to nearby properties (notably in Burley) and area/amenity flooding along the floodplain. [IMG] (p61)
The River Aire overtopped its banks at multiple locations, breached informal private flood defences and spilled onto adjacent roads (including the A65), producing aggregated flood flows that travelled rapidly downstream into Leeds city centre and caused widespread property flooding and damage. [IMG,PAG] (p62)
The River Aire overtopped its channel and banks, breached private informal flood defences and spilled onto the A65 Kirkstall Road (opposite Woodside View). Flood flows travelled downstream into side roads and then continued rapidly into Leeds city centre, inundating streets and properties along the route. [IMG,PAG] (p63)
The River Aire overtopped its banks at several locations, breached private informal flood defence assets, and spilled onto the A65 Kirkstall Road (at the meander opposite Woodside View) and downstream side roads; these overtopping flows aggregated and proceeded into Leeds city centre, flooding areas along the flow path. [IMG,PAG] (p64)
Record high River Aire levels caused fluvial backwater effects that rendered surface water drainage systems draining toward the river inoperable and generated surcharge/back pressures that contributed to localised flooding in adjacent low–lying areas (notably Thwaite Gate and Hunslet). (p67)
Record high river levels caused extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3. High river levels produced backwater/surcharge effects that rendered surface water drainage (including the nearby pumping station) inoperable until levels fell, contributing to localised flooding; when river levels dropped gravity discharge resumed and local flooding subsided. [PAG] (p68)
Record high flows in the River Aire led to swollen adjacent waterways and overtopping of channels, producing widespread fluvial flooding and contributing to the inundation of low–lying areas and properties. (p71)
Main River fluvial flooding: the River Aire overtopped and exceeded channel capacity, causing widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage, preventing free discharge and causing backups through gullies, manholes and private drainage. [PAG] (p72)
Record high River Aire levels during 25 Dec 2015 – 29 Dec 2015 caused localised flooding influence in low–lying areas of Stourton. Elevated river levels rendered surface–water drainage systems discharging to the river (including the syphon beneath the canal and the nearby pumping station) inoperable until river levels subsided. [PAG] (p73)
Record high river levels produced fluvial backwater effects that prevented gravity discharge of local surface water systems, contributing to localised flooding in low–lying areas. [IMG] (p74)
The River Aire produced extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3 that inundated Stourton and caused property flooding (commercial and external commercial) in the area. [TAB] (p75)
The River Aire and its watercourses are shown as the primary focus of mapped flood zones and are spatially associated with overbanking/flow routes that led to internal and external property flooding in adjacent Stourton. [IMG] (p76)
The River Aire overtopped footpaths and contributed overland fluvial flows into the adjacent industrial estate; its interaction with the canal and overtopping routes is mapped as a primary source of inundation. [IMG] (p77)
Structural collapse of the Knostrop by–wash embankment allowed River Aire waters to enter the canal system, surcharging the Aire and Calder Navigation and contributing to overtopping and downstream flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p78)
River Aire waters breached the by–wash embankment at Knostrop and inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation canal, contributing to overtopping and flooding of nearby land and properties. [IMG,PAG] (p79)
River Aire overtopped/flowed through the breached Knostrop Lock structure, inundating the adjacent canal and causing significant water flow into the canal system. [IMG] (p80)
Record high River Aire levels during 25 Dec 2015 – 29 Dec 2015 caused fluvial backwater that rendered surface water drainage systems draining to the river inoperable, produced surcharge/back pressures in surface water systems and led to localised flooding in Woodlesford; standing floodwater inundated industrial units and signage (photograph dated 27 Dec 2015). (p83)
Record–high river levels on the River Aire caused extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3, rendered surface water drainage systems inoperable (backwater effects and surcharge), and produced localised flooding until river levels subsided. [TAB,PAG] (p84)
The river overtopped its channel, spilling onto adjacent floodplains and driving the inundation of the Aire & Calder Navigation; this fluvial overflow was described as the primary cause of flooding that led to low–lying properties and waterside areas being flooded around Woodlesford/Oulton/Swillington. [IMG,PAG] (p85)
The River Aire came out of its channel, overtopping into adjacent floodplains. This overtopping inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation canal and contributed to downstream canal surcharging, breaches and flooding of nearby low–lying properties and land. [IMG] (p86)
Record–high River Aire levels in late December 2015 caused conventional surface water drainage systems draining toward the river to become inoperable, contributing to localised fluvial flooding in low–lying areas. Photographic evidence documents standing floodwater on a residential street in Lower Mickletown on 28 December 2015, with water encroaching from the river side onto the road and affecting accessibility. [PAG,MUL] (p88)
Record–high River Aire levels generated fluvial backwater and made surface water drainage systems discharging to the river inoperable, causing localised flooding and surcharge/back–pressure of surface water systems in low–lying areas. [PAG] (p90)
The River Aire produced extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3 during the event, contributing to inundation of adjacent settlements (including areas referenced such as Methley, Mickletown and Castleford). [TAB] (p92)
The River Aire overtopped its banks and contributed to fluvial inundation of the adjacent Mickletown Ings flood storage area; overtopping and overland flows from the river drove floodwaters into low–lying adjacent areas and contributed to property flooding on the fringes of the flood washland. [IMG,PAG] (p94)
The River Aire spills into Allerton Ings and causes flooding of the Barnsdale Road area; it is identified as the source of significant historical flooding affecting Allerton Bywater and contributed to the December 2015 inundation (Station Road submerged). (p97)
The River Aire was the primary influence for extensive fluvial flooding in flood zone 3, driving inundation of Allerton Ings, Barnsdale Road and properties in Allerton Bywater during the event. [TAB] (p101)
The River Aire overtopped its channel and flowed into St Aidan's and associated washland flood storage areas; this overtopping and release of water contributed to inundation that flooded isolated properties along Barnsdale Road in Allerton Bywater. [IMG,PAG] (p102)
The River Aire came out of channel and overtopped into St Aidan's and associated washland flood storage areas, driving overland flows and contributing to inundation of adjacent low–lying land and property flooding (including along Barnsdale Road). [IMG] (p103)
The River Aire overtopped its channel during Storm Eva (26–27 December 2015), driving river flooding across Leeds; the report states there are no formal flood defences along the River Aire, leaving the city exposed to that flooding source. [TAB,PAG] (p137)
Main river (River Aire) came out of channel during the event, causing flooding across Leeds; there were no formal flood defences along the River Aire so the city was particularly affected and remains at risk until defences are provided. (p138)
River Aire levels caused flooding that inundated the Kirkstall compound, prevented safe site access until 28 Dec 2015 and delayed electrical repair works until flood waters were cleared on 29 Dec 2015. (p227)
The river burst its banks on 27.12.15 and was the source of floodwater that ran down Kirkstall Road, onto Wellington Street and into Northern Street, initiating the urban/basement flooding described. (p242)
Flooding of the river banks and adjacent premises in Kirkstall led to volunteer and corporate clean–ups of the River Aire's banks and contributed to commercial premises being flooded. (p290)
Flooding of the River Aire affected businesses along its banks in Kirkstall, Hunslet and Stourton; the river–related flooding caused visual amenity damage to premises and contributed to reduced trade and inward investment for those flood–affected businesses. (p292)","approximately 1.2m higher than the previous record; approximately 0.8 miles (out–of–channel flow origin) [PAG] (p8)
approximately 1.2 m higher than the previous record; in excess of 0.5% (1 in 200) AEP (p24)
Leeds City Centre: 1938 internal residential; 566 external residential; 296 internal commercial; 55 external commercial (total 2855). Newlay: 5 internal residential; 4 external residential; 1 internal commercial (total 10). Kirkstall: 0 internal residential; 45 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 15 external commercial (total 96). Burley: 8 internal residential; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial; 16 external commercial (total 303). Thwaite Gate and Hunslet: 60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial (total 1170). Stourton: 0 internal residential; 0 external residential; 36 internal commercial; 4 external commercial (total 40). Woodlesford: 1 internal residential; 4 external residential; 0 internal commercial; 1 external commercial (total 6). Methley and Mickletown: 4 internal residential; 32 external residential (total 36). Allerton Bywater: 2 internal residential; 2 external residential; 7 internal commercial (total 11). [TAB] (p29)
2,855 properties in Leeds City Centre reported flood–affected during the event; internal flood depths 1 m to in excess of 2 m; direct economic impact £60.3 million [TAB] (p33)
Map analysis notes largest incident clusters located along the river and in Flood Zone 3; grid–square incident categories include ranges up to 205–392 properties. [IMG] (p35)
thousands of properties [IMG,PAG] (p41)
thousands of properties [PAG] (p42)
96 properties flooded in Kirkstall; flood depths up to 1500mm (reported in Kirkstall) [TAB] (p52)
96 properties; up to 1500mm internal flood depths [IMG] (p53)
Contributed to flooding that affected at least 303 properties in Burley. [TAB] (p60)
at least 303 properties (in Burley); internal flood depths 840 mm to 1540 mm [IMG] (p61)
60 internal residential properties; 1,051 external residential properties; 49 internal commercial properties; 10 external commercial properties; total 1,170 properties flooded (Thwaite Gate and Hunslet) [PAG] (p68)
36 internal commercial properties; 4 external commercial properties; 40 properties total [TAB] (p75)
over 206 properties flooded in a central Mickletown grid square (map category 206–592) [IMG,PAG] (p94)
Contributed to 11 properties flooded in Allerton Bywater; contributed to Barnsdale Road being modelled ~1.5 m under water in a 1 in 5 year event [TAB] (p101)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.6884889,-1.8393557,"<div id=""html_fe7edf78c43219798ae74c106ed75d27"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Calder</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highest river levels ever seen in many places of the Calder catchment contributed to devastating effects on local communities, businesses, individual homes and critical/local infrastructure. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leeds experiences flooding from the River Calder; the river contributed to flooding in the district during the 25&ndash;29 December 2015 event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as a Main River forming the district's southern boundary and therefore part of the main river network implicated in the fluvial flooding during the event; linked hydrologically to the River Aire at the confluence near Castleford. [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment saturation and further heavy rainfall led to almost instant runoff and rapid river rise in the Calder catchment, resulting in widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers. High river levels from the Calder contributed to overtopping and surcharging of conventional surface water drainage systems in Leeds, preventing free discharge and causing backups through road gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated ground and rapid runoff; river levels rose rapidly with many stations recording highest levels ever, resulting in Main River overtopping and widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage and backed up through gullies, manholes and private drains. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Calder catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated ground and rapid runoff; main&ndash;river flooding occurred with channels overtopping, causing widespread fluvial flooding and surcharging of surface water drainage systems that backed up through road gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Calder catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall on already saturated ground during 25&ndash;29 December 2015, producing almost&ndash;instant runoff and rapid river rises that caused fluvial flooding in Burley. Channels were overtopped, contributing to widespread river flooding and surcharging of surface water drainage (backing up via road gullies, manholes and private drains). (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Calder catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 leading to saturated ground and very rapid runoff. Record/high river flows contributed to overtopping and widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers, which also surcharged surface water drainage and caused backing up through gullies and manholes. (p71)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment experienced heavy prolonged rainfall before and over 25&ndash;29 December causing saturated ground and very rapid runoff; resulted in river levels reaching record highs and widespread fluvial flooding from the Calder overtopping channels and surcharging surface water systems in the catchment. (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Calder catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 that left soils saturated and contributed to rapid river rises and periodic flooding in the catchment, increasing flood risk during the 25&ndash;29 Dec event. [IMG] (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high River Calder levels in late December 2015 contributed to localised flooding in low&ndash;lying areas between the Aire and Calder by rendering surface water drainage inoperable and producing fluvial backwater effects that caused surcharge of unprotected surface water systems. Historically, the River Calder caused severe flooding at Methley in 1960, with homes reported to have been under up to 4 metres of water. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high River Calder levels contributed to fluvial backwater effects, which would have caused localised flooding in low&ndash;lying areas and prevented surface water drainage systems from discharging to the river. [PAG] (p90)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows on the River Calder caused washlands (including Allerton Ings and Lowther Washlands) to fill and act as temporary storage during the extreme flood, driving the need for the storage function. (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Calder contributed to the extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3 during the event, alongside the River Aire. [TAB] (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Calder overtopped and, together with the River Aire, discharged into Mickletown Ings flood storage areas; this contributed to overland flows that inundated adjacent low&ndash;lying land and led to isolated property flooding on the extreme fringes of the washland. [IMG] (p94)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River came out of channel and overtopped into adjacent Mickletown Ings flood storage areas, contributing to inundation of floodplain and flooding of isolated properties on the extreme fringes of the washland. (p95)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Washlands on the River Calder at Whitwood Mere acted as temporary storage during the extreme event, holding floodwater as part of the downstream flood management system. (p99)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the River Calder forced water into and back up the River Aire at the confluence, which contributed to Allerton Ings operating and caused flooding of the Barnsdale Road/Allerton Bywater area. [TAB] (p101)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Calder overtopped into St Aidan's and the washland flood storage areas, contributing to overland flood flows and flooding of low&ndash;lying areas and properties near the rivers. [IMG] (p103)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Calder catchment experienced prolonged heavy rainfall in November&ndash;December 2015 contributing to saturated catchments and rapid runoff during the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event, leading to rapid river rises and fluvial flooding where Main Rivers overtopped their channels; surface water drainage surcharge also occurred. (p111)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Calder catchment experienced prolonged November&ndash;December rainfall leading to saturated ground; further heavy rainfall over Christmas caused rapid runoff and fast river rise in the catchment, contributing to record high river levels and downstream flooding. (p119)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Calder catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated catchments and contributed to widespread fluvial flooding and overtopping of main rivers. (p127)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Calder catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to the event, producing saturated ground and periodic flooding; this saturation contributed to rapid river rises and flooding during the Christmas period. (p133)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>homes under up to 4 metres of water [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 206 properties flooded in a central Mickletown grid square (map category 206&ndash;592) [IMG] (p94)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to 11 properties flooded in Allerton Bywater (p101)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,River Calder,England,"Highest river levels ever seen in many places of the Calder catchment contributed to devastating effects on local communities, businesses, individual homes and critical/local infrastructure. (p7)
Leeds experiences flooding from the River Calder; the river contributed to flooding in the district during the 25–29 December 2015 event. (p8)
Described as a Main River forming the district's southern boundary and therefore part of the main river network implicated in the fluvial flooding during the event; linked hydrologically to the River Aire at the confluence near Castleford. [IMG] (p17)
Catchment saturation and further heavy rainfall led to almost instant runoff and rapid river rise in the Calder catchment, resulting in widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers. High river levels from the Calder contributed to overtopping and surcharging of conventional surface water drainage systems in Leeds, preventing free discharge and causing backups through road gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p43)
Catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated ground and rapid runoff; river levels rose rapidly with many stations recording highest levels ever, resulting in Main River overtopping and widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage and backed up through gullies, manholes and private drains. (p49)
River Calder catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated ground and rapid runoff; main–river flooding occurred with channels overtopping, causing widespread fluvial flooding and surcharging of surface water drainage systems that backed up through road gullies, manholes and private drainage. (p57)
The River Calder catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall on already saturated ground during 25–29 December 2015, producing almost–instant runoff and rapid river rises that caused fluvial flooding in Burley. Channels were overtopped, contributing to widespread river flooding and surcharging of surface water drainage (backing up via road gullies, manholes and private drains). (p65)
The Calder catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25–29 Dec 2015 leading to saturated ground and very rapid runoff. Record/high river flows contributed to overtopping and widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers, which also surcharged surface water drainage and caused backing up through gullies and manholes. (p71)
Catchment experienced heavy prolonged rainfall before and over 25–29 December causing saturated ground and very rapid runoff; resulted in river levels reaching record highs and widespread fluvial flooding from the Calder overtopping channels and surcharging surface water systems in the catchment. (p81)
The Calder catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25–29 Dec 2015 that left soils saturated and contributed to rapid river rises and periodic flooding in the catchment, increasing flood risk during the 25–29 Dec event. [IMG] (p86)
Record–high River Calder levels in late December 2015 contributed to localised flooding in low–lying areas between the Aire and Calder by rendering surface water drainage inoperable and producing fluvial backwater effects that caused surcharge of unprotected surface water systems. Historically, the River Calder caused severe flooding at Methley in 1960, with homes reported to have been under up to 4 metres of water. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p88)
Record–high River Calder levels contributed to fluvial backwater effects, which would have caused localised flooding in low–lying areas and prevented surface water drainage systems from discharging to the river. [PAG] (p90)
High flows on the River Calder caused washlands (including Allerton Ings and Lowther Washlands) to fill and act as temporary storage during the extreme flood, driving the need for the storage function. (p91)
The River Calder contributed to the extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3 during the event, alongside the River Aire. [TAB] (p92)
The River Calder overtopped and, together with the River Aire, discharged into Mickletown Ings flood storage areas; this contributed to overland flows that inundated adjacent low–lying land and led to isolated property flooding on the extreme fringes of the washland. [IMG] (p94)
River came out of channel and overtopped into adjacent Mickletown Ings flood storage areas, contributing to inundation of floodplain and flooding of isolated properties on the extreme fringes of the washland. (p95)
Washlands on the River Calder at Whitwood Mere acted as temporary storage during the extreme event, holding floodwater as part of the downstream flood management system. (p99)
High flows in the River Calder forced water into and back up the River Aire at the confluence, which contributed to Allerton Ings operating and caused flooding of the Barnsdale Road/Allerton Bywater area. [TAB] (p101)
The River Calder overtopped into St Aidan's and the washland flood storage areas, contributing to overland flood flows and flooding of low–lying areas and properties near the rivers. [IMG] (p103)
The River Calder catchment experienced prolonged heavy rainfall in November–December 2015 contributing to saturated catchments and rapid runoff during the 25–29 Dec 2015 event, leading to rapid river rises and fluvial flooding where Main Rivers overtopped their channels; surface water drainage surcharge also occurred. (p111)
The Calder catchment experienced prolonged November–December rainfall leading to saturated ground; further heavy rainfall over Christmas caused rapid runoff and fast river rise in the catchment, contributing to record high river levels and downstream flooding. (p119)
The Calder catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated catchments and contributed to widespread fluvial flooding and overtopping of main rivers. (p127)
The River Calder catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to the event, producing saturated ground and periodic flooding; this saturation contributed to rapid river rises and flooding during the Christmas period. (p133)","homes under up to 4 metres of water [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p88)
over 206 properties flooded in a central Mickletown grid square (map category 206–592) [IMG] (p94)
Contributed to 11 properties flooded in Allerton Bywater (p101)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.910881,-1.6910531,"<div id=""html_99a0a266cc0e8b67a502e6e41fafe536"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Wharfe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highest river levels in parts of the Wharfe catchment contributed to devastating effects on local communities, businesses, homes and local infrastructure. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Wharfe was identified as one of the predominant causes of flooding across the metropolitan district of Leeds during the Storm Eva event, contributing to widespread inundation of properties and urban impacts (including internal flooding, disruption to access and utilities, stranded vehicles in basement car parks and incapacitated lifts in multi&ndash;occupancy/high&ndash;rise buildings). [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contributed to widespread and significant flooding across the Leeds district; flow routes from the river caused overtopping or bypassing of defences and widespread flooding of properties. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe is one of the Main Rivers and contributed to fluvial flooding that affected settlements along its corridor (notably Otley and Wetherby). [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the main rivers in the district and as contributing to the main river fluvial flooding during the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event; the Wharfe corridor flooding affected adjacent settlements (for example Collingham and Thorpe Arch). [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named alongside the Rivers Aire as a predominant source of the property flooding reported in the Leeds metropolitan district for the event. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Wharfe catchment experienced prolonged rainfall and saturation prior to the event, and further heavy rainfall over Christmas caused rapid river rises and widespread fluvial flooding. High river levels associated with the Wharfe contributed to surcharging of surface water drainage in the urban area, backing up through gullies, manholes and private drainage systems. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated ground and rapid runoff; river levels rose rapidly with many stations recording highest levels ever, resulting in Main River overtopping and widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage and backed up through gullies, manholes and private drains. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Wharfe catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall on saturated ground leading to rapid runoff and river levels that overtopped channels; this caused widespread fluvial flooding and contributed to surcharging of surface water drainage, resulting in backups through gullies, manholes and private drains. (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe catchment, following heavy and prolonged rainfall on saturated ground during 25&ndash;29 December 2015, produced rapid river rises and contributed to fluvial flooding in Burley. High river levels led to overtopping of channels and exacerbated surface water drainage surcharging and backup. (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Wharfe catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25&ndash;29 December 2015, contributing to saturated catchments and rapid river rises. This contributed to widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers that overtopped their channels, surcharged surface water drainage (backing up through gullies and manholes) and exacerbated flooding in downstream areas such as Thwaite Gate, Hunslet and Hunslet Trading Estate. (p71)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment experienced heavy prolonged rainfall prior to and over the 25&ndash;29 December event, producing saturated ground and rapid runoff; contributed to very high river levels and overtopping leading to fluvial flooding that exacerbated surface water surcharge in the area. (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Wharfe catchment had also experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015, producing saturated catchments and contributing to rapid river rises and flooding during the event. (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the River Wharfe was the main source of floodwater that inundated adjacent flood plains, old gravel pits and contributed to property flooding in Otley; tributaries (Kell Beck and Holbeck) also contributed locally. (p105)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe overtopped its banks during the event and was identified as the main source of floodwater that inundated flood plains and old gravel pits adjacent to the river. Overtopping and fluvial backwater effects caused inundation of left&ndash;bank properties and prevented surface water systems discharging to the river (rendering them inoperable), contributing to localised flooding and surcharge at outfalls such as Kell Beck. [PAG] (p106)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high river levels in the River Wharfe caused extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3 in Otley. High river levels created backwater and surcharge effects that rendered surface water drainage systems draining to the river inoperable (including the Kell Beck outfall upstream of Otley Bridge), contributing to localised flooding across the town and resulting in internal and external property flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p107)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe came out of channel, overtopping and inundating adjacent floodplains, amenity areas and low&ndash;lying properties. Overtopping led to flow into gravel pits and across fields, flooding the old cattle market site, crossing the B6451 Farnley Lane and isolating homes with deep, fast&ndash;flowing water; floodwaters rejoined the river at the Holbeck tributary downstream of the weir. [PAG] (p109)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe came out of channel, overtopping and inundating adjacent flood plains, low&ndash;lying properties and amenity areas; flows filled nearby gravel pits and produced overland flow across fields into built&ndash;up areas. (p110)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe experienced its highest flow levels on record in December 2015. A mid&ndash;catchment intense rainfall event (Storm Eva/Boxing Day) produced very rapid runoff, producing a 1 in 100 year event in the Lower Wharfe that overtopped channels and caused flooding across communities within the Leeds boundary (including Otley). Flooding exceeded drainage design standards and surcharged surface water systems. (p111)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high river levels in the River Wharfe produced fluvial backwater effects that contributed to localised flooding in Collingham (rendering drainage systems and pumping stations inoperable and causing surcharge into surface water systems). [PAG] (p113)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high river levels on the River Wharfe produced extensive fluvial flooding and fluvial backwater effects that rendered surface water drainage and pumping stations inoperable, contributing to localised flooding in low&ndash;lying areas until levels dropped. [TAB,PAG] (p115)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe (referred to as River Wharf in figures) came out of channel and breached local flood defences in Collingham, overtopping embankments and inundating adjacent flood plains, low&ndash;lying properties and amenity areas; embankment breach events allowed flow onto residential streets. [IMG,PAG] (p116)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe came out of its channel, overtopped and breached flood defences, inundating adjacent floodplains, low&ndash;lying properties, gardens and amenity areas in Collingham (including flow routes into streets and across fields); contributed to isolated internal property flooding at Linton Ings and Church Lane and caused major damage to Linton Bridge. [IMG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment experienced heavy rainfall on already saturated ground causing almost instant runoff and rapid river rise; river level stations recorded their highest levels ever and the rapid rise contributed to flooding in the catchment. (p119)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopped its banks in Wetherby, causing floodwater to submerge riverside structures and inundate the riverbank area. [IMG] (p122)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high river levels in the River Wharfe caused extensive fluvial flooding (flood zone 3) that inundated low&ndash;lying areas in Wetherby and rendered surface water drainage systems inoperable; fluvial backwater produced surcharge/back pressure on surface water systems, contributing to localised flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe overtopped/rose and is shown as the primary source correlated with the distribution of internal and external property flooding along its banks in Wetherby and downstream areas; riverside properties and nearby floodplain (sports fields, Linton Ings) were inundated. [IMG] (p124)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river came out of its channel, overtopped its banks and inundated adjacent flood plains, low&ndash;lying properties and amenity areas; this fluvial (river) flooding led to internal property flooding and wide overland flow routes through central Wetherby. [IMG] (p126)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Wharfe overtopped its channel and inundated adjacent flood plains, low&ndash;lying properties and amenity areas; extensive fluvial flooding occurred across the catchment. [PAG] (p127)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe overtopped its banks on 26 December 2015 and caused inundation of adjacent residential areas and land at Thorp Arch (flooding of roads, gardens and lower parts of houses). [IMG] (p129)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high river levels in the River Wharfe produced extensive fluvial flooding (flood zone 3) and backwater effects that rendered surface water drainage inoperable and caused surcharge/backpressure into drainage systems, contributing to localised inundation of adjacent communities (Thorp Arch and Boston Spa). [TAB,PAG] (p130)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe came out of its channel, overtopping and inundating adjacent flood plains and low&ndash;lying properties, causing internal inundation of residential buildings and widespread surface inundation of streets and gardens. [PAG] (p133)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Wharfe affected communities along its course during Storm Eva; associated damage included significant impacts to Otley and severe damage to Linton Bridge. [TAB] (p137)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Wharfe affected communities along its course during Storm Eva, contributing to significant local impacts including damage at Linton Bridge and substantial effects in Otley. [PAG] (p138)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flooding in December 2015 that caused damage to Linton Bridge and triggered stabilisation works. (p255)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4712 flooding incidents across the metropolitan district of Leeds (final DEFRA figure, Sept 2016, combined); 2826 properties affected by internal flooding; 3368 properties reported to central Government under the DCLG EVA scheme (June 2016) [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total [TAB,PAG] (p107)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths up to 1000&ndash;2000 mm (reported range) [PAG] (p109)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths thought to be up to 1000 mm to 2000 mm (p110)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flood depths in some properties in excess of 2 m [IMG,PAG] (p116)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flood depths in some cases in excess of 2 m; south supporting pier settlement 200 mm; 4 miles vehicle diversion [IMG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 riverside pavilion partially submerged [IMG] (p122)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>26 properties flooded in Wetherby (see Wetherby totals) [TAB,PAG] (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Incidents per 250m grid square recorded in the map legend: 2&ndash;17; 18&ndash;46; 47&ndash;121; 122&ndash;205; 206&ndash;392 (graduated circles showing number of flooded properties/multi&ndash;occupancy incidents) [IMG] (p124)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths in excess of 2 m; internal property flooding depths of 800 mm; 1 flood warning covering Wetherby; 66 properties considered at risk (44 registered to receive EA warnings) [IMG] (p126)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths in excess of 2m; internal property flooding depth of 800mm [PAG] (p127)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>32 properties flooded in Thorp Arch and Boston Spa total (7 internal residential; 25 external residential; 0 internal commercial; 0 external commercial). [TAB,PAG] (p130)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flood depths 500mm to 1500mm [PAG] (p133)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,River Wharfe,England,"Highest river levels in parts of the Wharfe catchment contributed to devastating effects on local communities, businesses, homes and local infrastructure. (p7)
Flooding from the River Wharfe was identified as one of the predominant causes of flooding across the metropolitan district of Leeds during the Storm Eva event, contributing to widespread inundation of properties and urban impacts (including internal flooding, disruption to access and utilities, stranded vehicles in basement car parks and incapacitated lifts in multi–occupancy/high–rise buildings). [PAG] (p9)
Contributed to widespread and significant flooding across the Leeds district; flow routes from the river caused overtopping or bypassing of defences and widespread flooding of properties. (p10)
The River Wharfe is one of the Main Rivers and contributed to fluvial flooding that affected settlements along its corridor (notably Otley and Wetherby). [PAG] (p16)
Identified as one of the main rivers in the district and as contributing to the main river fluvial flooding during the 25–29 Dec 2015 event; the Wharfe corridor flooding affected adjacent settlements (for example Collingham and Thorpe Arch). [IMG] (p17)
Named alongside the Rivers Aire as a predominant source of the property flooding reported in the Leeds metropolitan district for the event. (p27)
The Wharfe catchment experienced prolonged rainfall and saturation prior to the event, and further heavy rainfall over Christmas caused rapid river rises and widespread fluvial flooding. High river levels associated with the Wharfe contributed to surcharging of surface water drainage in the urban area, backing up through gullies, manholes and private drainage systems. (p43)
Catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall leading to saturated ground and rapid runoff; river levels rose rapidly with many stations recording highest levels ever, resulting in Main River overtopping and widespread fluvial flooding that surcharged surface water drainage and backed up through gullies, manholes and private drains. (p49)
River Wharfe catchment experienced heavy, prolonged rainfall on saturated ground leading to rapid runoff and river levels that overtopped channels; this caused widespread fluvial flooding and contributed to surcharging of surface water drainage, resulting in backups through gullies, manholes and private drains. (p57)
The River Wharfe catchment, following heavy and prolonged rainfall on saturated ground during 25–29 December 2015, produced rapid river rises and contributed to fluvial flooding in Burley. High river levels led to overtopping of channels and exacerbated surface water drainage surcharging and backup. (p65)
The Wharfe catchment experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25–29 December 2015, contributing to saturated catchments and rapid river rises. This contributed to widespread fluvial flooding from main rivers that overtopped their channels, surcharged surface water drainage (backing up through gullies and manholes) and exacerbated flooding in downstream areas such as Thwaite Gate, Hunslet and Hunslet Trading Estate. (p71)
Catchment experienced heavy prolonged rainfall prior to and over the 25–29 December event, producing saturated ground and rapid runoff; contributed to very high river levels and overtopping leading to fluvial flooding that exacerbated surface water surcharge in the area. (p81)
The Wharfe catchment had also experienced heavy and prolonged rainfall prior to and during 25–29 Dec 2015, producing saturated catchments and contributing to rapid river rises and flooding during the event. (p86)
Overtopping of the River Wharfe was the main source of floodwater that inundated adjacent flood plains, old gravel pits and contributed to property flooding in Otley; tributaries (Kell Beck and Holbeck) also contributed locally. (p105)
The River Wharfe overtopped its banks during the event and was identified as the main source of floodwater that inundated flood plains and old gravel pits adjacent to the river. Overtopping and fluvial backwater effects caused inundation of left–bank properties and prevented surface water systems discharging to the river (rendering them inoperable), contributing to localised flooding and surcharge at outfalls such as Kell Beck. [PAG] (p106)
Record high river levels in the River Wharfe caused extensive fluvial flooding within flood zone 3 in Otley. High river levels created backwater and surcharge effects that rendered surface water drainage systems draining to the river inoperable (including the Kell Beck outfall upstream of Otley Bridge), contributing to localised flooding across the town and resulting in internal and external property flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p107)
The River Wharfe came out of channel, overtopping and inundating adjacent floodplains, amenity areas and low–lying properties. Overtopping led to flow into gravel pits and across fields, flooding the old cattle market site, crossing the B6451 Farnley Lane and isolating homes with deep, fast–flowing water; floodwaters rejoined the river at the Holbeck tributary downstream of the weir. [PAG] (p109)
The River Wharfe came out of channel, overtopping and inundating adjacent flood plains, low–lying properties and amenity areas; flows filled nearby gravel pits and produced overland flow across fields into built–up areas. (p110)
The River Wharfe experienced its highest flow levels on record in December 2015. A mid–catchment intense rainfall event (Storm Eva/Boxing Day) produced very rapid runoff, producing a 1 in 100 year event in the Lower Wharfe that overtopped channels and caused flooding across communities within the Leeds boundary (including Otley). Flooding exceeded drainage design standards and surcharged surface water systems. (p111)
Record high river levels in the River Wharfe produced fluvial backwater effects that contributed to localised flooding in Collingham (rendering drainage systems and pumping stations inoperable and causing surcharge into surface water systems). [PAG] (p113)
Record high river levels on the River Wharfe produced extensive fluvial flooding and fluvial backwater effects that rendered surface water drainage and pumping stations inoperable, contributing to localised flooding in low–lying areas until levels dropped. [TAB,PAG] (p115)
The River Wharfe (referred to as River Wharf in figures) came out of channel and breached local flood defences in Collingham, overtopping embankments and inundating adjacent flood plains, low–lying properties and amenity areas; embankment breach events allowed flow onto residential streets. [IMG,PAG] (p116)
The River Wharfe came out of its channel, overtopped and breached flood defences, inundating adjacent floodplains, low–lying properties, gardens and amenity areas in Collingham (including flow routes into streets and across fields); contributed to isolated internal property flooding at Linton Ings and Church Lane and caused major damage to Linton Bridge. [IMG] (p117)
Catchment experienced heavy rainfall on already saturated ground causing almost instant runoff and rapid river rise; river level stations recorded their highest levels ever and the rapid rise contributed to flooding in the catchment. (p119)
Overtopped its banks in Wetherby, causing floodwater to submerge riverside structures and inundate the riverbank area. [IMG] (p122)
Record high river levels in the River Wharfe caused extensive fluvial flooding (flood zone 3) that inundated low–lying areas in Wetherby and rendered surface water drainage systems inoperable; fluvial backwater produced surcharge/back pressure on surface water systems, contributing to localised flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p123)
The River Wharfe overtopped/rose and is shown as the primary source correlated with the distribution of internal and external property flooding along its banks in Wetherby and downstream areas; riverside properties and nearby floodplain (sports fields, Linton Ings) were inundated. [IMG] (p124)
The river came out of its channel, overtopped its banks and inundated adjacent flood plains, low–lying properties and amenity areas; this fluvial (river) flooding led to internal property flooding and wide overland flow routes through central Wetherby. [IMG] (p126)
River Wharfe overtopped its channel and inundated adjacent flood plains, low–lying properties and amenity areas; extensive fluvial flooding occurred across the catchment. [PAG] (p127)
The River Wharfe overtopped its banks on 26 December 2015 and caused inundation of adjacent residential areas and land at Thorp Arch (flooding of roads, gardens and lower parts of houses). [IMG] (p129)
Record high river levels in the River Wharfe produced extensive fluvial flooding (flood zone 3) and backwater effects that rendered surface water drainage inoperable and caused surcharge/backpressure into drainage systems, contributing to localised inundation of adjacent communities (Thorp Arch and Boston Spa). [TAB,PAG] (p130)
The River Wharfe came out of its channel, overtopping and inundating adjacent flood plains and low–lying properties, causing internal inundation of residential buildings and widespread surface inundation of streets and gardens. [PAG] (p133)
Flooding from the River Wharfe affected communities along its course during Storm Eva; associated damage included significant impacts to Otley and severe damage to Linton Bridge. [TAB] (p137)
Flooding from the River Wharfe affected communities along its course during Storm Eva, contributing to significant local impacts including damage at Linton Bridge and substantial effects in Otley. [PAG] (p138)
Severe flooding in December 2015 that caused damage to Linton Bridge and triggered stabilisation works. (p255)","4712 flooding incidents across the metropolitan district of Leeds (final DEFRA figure, Sept 2016, combined); 2826 properties affected by internal flooding; 3368 properties reported to central Government under the DCLG EVA scheme (June 2016) [PAG] (p9)
54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total [TAB,PAG] (p107)
flood depths up to 1000–2000 mm (reported range) [PAG] (p109)
flood depths thought to be up to 1000 mm to 2000 mm (p110)
internal flood depths in some properties in excess of 2 m [IMG,PAG] (p116)
internal flood depths in some cases in excess of 2 m; south supporting pier settlement 200 mm; 4 miles vehicle diversion [IMG] (p117)
1 riverside pavilion partially submerged [IMG] (p122)
26 properties flooded in Wetherby (see Wetherby totals) [TAB,PAG] (p123)
Incidents per 250m grid square recorded in the map legend: 2–17; 18–46; 47–121; 122–205; 206–392 (graduated circles showing number of flooded properties/multi–occupancy incidents) [IMG] (p124)
flood depths in excess of 2 m; internal property flooding depths of 800 mm; 1 flood warning covering Wetherby; 66 properties considered at risk (44 registered to receive EA warnings) [IMG] (p126)
flood depths in excess of 2m; internal property flooding depth of 800mm [PAG] (p127)
32 properties flooded in Thorp Arch and Boston Spa total (7 internal residential; 25 external residential; 0 internal commercial; 0 external commercial). [TAB,PAG] (p130)
internal flood depths 500mm to 1500mm [PAG] (p133)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8157377,-1.6074046,"<div id=""html_9c0084fbe1b070d8a040693e0941407c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kirkstall Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Near Kirkstall Bridge an un&ndash;flapped surface water outfall and a manhole were overtopped/surcharged because river level was higher than the manhole cover. This caused sewer overload and escape of surcharged water onto the surrounding area and directed flow from the bridge area toward downstream locations. [IMG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kirkstall Bridge retail area was inundated by floodwater; vehicles were partially submerged, roads were impassable and retail/outdoor seating areas were flooded, causing direct damage to businesses and street&ndash;level assets. [IMG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels overtopped/overwhelmed defences at Kirkstall Bridge, causing flooding that affected the safe and efficient operation of the railway. Flooding occurred when the river reached 2.8m on 26 and 27 December 2015 and contributed to repeated operational disruption in late 2015. (p211)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Kirkstall Bridge,England,"Near Kirkstall Bridge an un–flapped surface water outfall and a manhole were overtopped/surcharged because river level was higher than the manhole cover. This caused sewer overload and escape of surcharged water onto the surrounding area and directed flow from the bridge area toward downstream locations. [IMG] (p51)
The Kirkstall Bridge retail area was inundated by floodwater; vehicles were partially submerged, roads were impassable and retail/outdoor seating areas were flooded, causing direct damage to businesses and street–level assets. [IMG] (p55)
River levels overtopped/overwhelmed defences at Kirkstall Bridge, causing flooding that affected the safe and efficient operation of the railway. Flooding occurred when the river reached 2.8m on 26 and 27 December 2015 and contributed to repeated operational disruption in late 2015. (p211)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8048393,-1.5830439,"<div id=""html_8d16a626673801280eb3fc665705f4a2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cardigan Fields Leisure Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named as being within the Burley severe flood warning issued at 10:16 on 26 December 2015 (area described between Viaduct Road and City Gate). (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Cardigan Fields Leisure Park,England,Named as being within the Burley severe flood warning issued at 10:16 on 26 December 2015 (area described between Viaduct Road and City Gate). (p65),,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7582298,-1.4398012,"<div id=""html_e0d86b0ac66f962bba4faa7858e3d053"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swillington Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area around Swillington Bridge was one of the largest clusters of flooded properties caused by River Aire overtopping and canal surcharge; low&ndash;lying properties in proximity were inundated. [IMG] (p85)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A canal breach occurred at this location allowing floodwater to escape from the Aire & Calder Navigation and spread onto adjacent land/infrastructure. [IMG] (p86)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Swillington Bridge,England,"Area around Swillington Bridge was one of the largest clusters of flooded properties caused by River Aire overtopping and canal surcharge; low–lying properties in proximity were inundated. [IMG] (p85)
A canal breach occurred at this location allowing floodwater to escape from the Aire & Calder Navigation and spread onto adjacent land/infrastructure. [IMG] (p86)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.908296,-1.4129191,"<div id=""html_5b9bb56d7e7729dbd0619621dc344619"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Collingham Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial flooding occurred on the River Wharfe with clusters of flood incidents concentrated along the river close to Collingham Bridge; this contributed to property flooding in central Collingham. [IMG] (p115)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Collingham Bridge,England,Extensive fluvial flooding occurred on the River Wharfe with clusters of flood incidents concentrated along the river close to Collingham Bridge; this contributed to property flooding in central Collingham. [IMG] (p115),,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9265242,-1.3860832,"<div id=""html_2af9e21f4877d1c663bce907b0e55983"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wetherby Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping occurred at multiple points along the River Wharfe, especially near footpaths and infrastructure close to the riverbank; the bridge area is identified on the map among affected infrastructure and is likely to have experienced overtopping or adjacent inundation and access disruption. [IMG] (p126)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Wetherby Bridge,England,"Overtopping occurred at multiple points along the River Wharfe, especially near footpaths and infrastructure close to the riverbank; the bridge area is identified on the map among affected infrastructure and is likely to have experienced overtopping or adjacent inundation and access disruption. [IMG] (p126)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8849895,-1.2599117,"<div id=""html_43de71d84f4b6c88a27764a7d216579b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tadcaster Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Central span of a historic stone bridge collapsed; structural failure with debris in the river requiring emergency response and assessment. [IMG] (p237)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Tadcaster Bridge,England,Central span of a historic stone bridge collapsed; structural failure with debris in the river requiring emergency response and assessment. [IMG] (p237),,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9062291,-1.3439259,"<div id=""html_c36784228a8215f784a47afb2891e429"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thorp Arch Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding damaged a path adjacent to Thorp Arch Bridge; the parish council arranged repairs as a post&ndash;flood recovery action. (p289)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Thorp Arch Bridge,England,Flooding damaged a path adjacent to Thorp Arch Bridge; the parish council arranged repairs as a post–flood recovery action. (p289),,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8050269,-1.5782123,"<div id=""html_9b90bd8b26367b98494bf93f7fb2031f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kirkstall Road, Burley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Aire/channels flowed out approximately 0.8 miles upstream of Armley flow gauge and travelled down Kirkstall Road toward the city centre; listed as a major transport route that was flooded and disrupted during the event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid inundation with deep, fast&ndash;flowing flood water from the River Aire into the city centre; presented a danger to life and caused significant disruption to the area. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of the Liberty Park complex which experienced internal flooding (flooded power room and reception), loss of mains power and deployment of temporary generators; surrounding courtyard areas were inundated. [MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A65 (Kirkstall Road) became inundated as fluvial exceedance flows from the overtopped River Aire flowed southeast into the city; surfacing water covered the entire road carriageway with visible current and turbulence, submerging road signs and disrupting transport. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kirkstall Road is shown completely submerged under floodwater, rendering the road impassable as floodwaters look to extend towards the city centre. [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A65 Kirkstall Road (a major route into the city) was flooded, causing major transport disruption. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying properties between the River Aire and Kirkstall Road were inundated when the river overtopped and breached private defences; properties experienced internal flooding and damage to fixtures, fittings, building fabric and vehicles. (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire spilled onto the A65 Kirkstall Road at the meander opposite Woodside View; the A65 was shown flooded in photographic and aerial evidence (road submerged, water encroaching into adjacent properties and streets). [IMG,PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kirkstall Road experienced inundation where River Aire overtopping spilled onto the road corridor and onto several side roads downstream, contributing to the flow path into Leeds city centre. [IMG,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe urban/ surface water flooding at night with the road inundated; a vehicle was partially submerged and at least one person was wading through knee&ndash;deep water, causing transport disruption and safety risk in the commercial area. [IMG] (p237)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Aire ran down Kirkstall Road and flowed towards Wellington Street, contributing to rising water levels and downstream flooding of city&ndash;centre streets and West Point. (p242)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement utilities in flooded properties were affected; a proposal was progressed to move utilities out of basements for 26 properties on Kirkstall Road to reduce future flood impacts. (p288)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of a high&ndash;profile business (Thyssen Krupp) that closed as a result of the flooding. (p292)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>564 student rooms; 7 kitchens; 5 temporary generators; 5 elevators out of commission [MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>26 properties; £130,000 (proposed cost for utilities work) (p288)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Kirkstall Road, Burley",England,"Floodwater from the River Aire/channels flowed out approximately 0.8 miles upstream of Armley flow gauge and travelled down Kirkstall Road toward the city centre; listed as a major transport route that was flooded and disrupted during the event. (p8)
Rapid inundation with deep, fast–flowing flood water from the River Aire into the city centre; presented a danger to life and caused significant disruption to the area. (p10)
Location of the Liberty Park complex which experienced internal flooding (flooded power room and reception), loss of mains power and deployment of temporary generators; surrounding courtyard areas were inundated. [MUL] (p35)
The A65 (Kirkstall Road) became inundated as fluvial exceedance flows from the overtopped River Aire flowed southeast into the city; surfacing water covered the entire road carriageway with visible current and turbulence, submerging road signs and disrupting transport. [IMG] (p41)
Kirkstall Road is shown completely submerged under floodwater, rendering the road impassable as floodwaters look to extend towards the city centre. [IMG] (p42)
The A65 Kirkstall Road (a major route into the city) was flooded, causing major transport disruption. (p52)
Low–lying properties between the River Aire and Kirkstall Road were inundated when the river overtopped and breached private defences; properties experienced internal flooding and damage to fixtures, fittings, building fabric and vehicles. (p55)
The River Aire spilled onto the A65 Kirkstall Road at the meander opposite Woodside View; the A65 was shown flooded in photographic and aerial evidence (road submerged, water encroaching into adjacent properties and streets). [IMG,PAG] (p63)
Kirkstall Road experienced inundation where River Aire overtopping spilled onto the road corridor and onto several side roads downstream, contributing to the flow path into Leeds city centre. [IMG,PAG] (p64)
Severe urban/ surface water flooding at night with the road inundated; a vehicle was partially submerged and at least one person was wading through knee–deep water, causing transport disruption and safety risk in the commercial area. [IMG] (p237)
Floodwater from the River Aire ran down Kirkstall Road and flowed towards Wellington Street, contributing to rising water levels and downstream flooding of city–centre streets and West Point. (p242)
Basement utilities in flooded properties were affected; a proposal was progressed to move utilities out of basements for 26 properties on Kirkstall Road to reduce future flood impacts. (p288)
Location of a high–profile business (Thyssen Krupp) that closed as a result of the flooding. (p292)","564 student rooms; 7 kitchens; 5 temporary generators; 5 elevators out of commission [MUL] (p35)
26 properties; £130,000 (proposed cost for utilities work) (p288)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7974731,-1.7532149,"<div id=""html_cbdaa7b0fbf6b93dc0e309f39c205658"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bradford Forster Square, Bradford Metropolitan District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Kirkstall affected the Leeds to Bradford Forster Square rail line, causing disruption to services. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Bradford Forster Square, Bradford Metropolitan District",England,"Flooding at Kirkstall affected the Leeds to Bradford Forster Square rail line, causing disruption to services. (p8)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7964147,-1.5523095,"<div id=""html_255f3924d44fc08215b8df0be98ece29"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wellington Street, Holbeck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid inundation with deep, fast&ndash;flowing flood water from the River Aire into the city centre; presented a danger to life and caused significant disruption to the area. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of this major transport route into the city caused widespread disruption to transport during the main event. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flooding inundated Wellington Street; an emergency/highway maintenance vehicle and workers in high&ndash;visibility clothing are shown operating in the floodwater, indicating active response and significant street inundation. [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater rose on Wellington Street and flowed towards the traffic lights and into Northern Street, contributing to the ingress of water into nearby properties and basement areas. (p242)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Wellington Street, Holbeck",England,"Rapid inundation with deep, fast–flowing flood water from the River Aire into the city centre; presented a danger to life and caused significant disruption to the area. (p10)
Flooding of this major transport route into the city caused widespread disruption to transport during the main event. (p32)
Severe flooding inundated Wellington Street; an emergency/highway maintenance vehicle and workers in high–visibility clothing are shown operating in the floodwater, indicating active response and significant street inundation. [IMG] (p42)
Floodwater rose on Wellington Street and flowed towards the traffic lights and into Northern Street, contributing to the ingress of water into nearby properties and basement areas. (p242)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9100812,-1.697358,"<div id=""html_8aedefde3315095ba5ab611d6123e488"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Billams Hill, Newall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area (Billams Hill) was inundated by River Wharfe flooding during the Nov/Dec 2015 events; streets and adjacent properties were submerged as shown in photographs of the Billams Hill / Farnley Lane area. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Billams Hill (area) was inundated during the December 2015 floods; photographs referenced in the report are of the Billams Hill / Farnley Lane area showing streets and nearby properties submerged by floodwater from the River Wharfe and associated surface water. (p106)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Billams Hill was affected by river flooding from the Wharfe and associated surface water drainage surcharge/backflow, contributing to localised property flooding within Otley. (p107)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Street entirely submerged by floodwater; road surface covered and access/traffic disrupted; people observed on&ndash;scene. Photograph timestamp 26 Dec (16:27) shows inundation of the full carriageway. [IMG] (p108)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood route crossed the B6451 at Billams Hill, causing the road to be closed for several hours as fluvial floodwater passed through the area. (p110)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Billams Hill, Newall",England,"Area (Billams Hill) was inundated by River Wharfe flooding during the Nov/Dec 2015 events; streets and adjacent properties were submerged as shown in photographs of 