GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9127997,-1.6974513,"<div id=""html_8f4a17742489bbb484c6ab760056fea1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Newall Carr Road, Newall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Wharfe inundated low&ndash;lying streets in Otley; high river levels rendered surface water drainage inoperable and caused surcharge/backflow at outfalls, leading to localised flooding on Newall Carr Road and contributing to residential/commercial property flooding in the town. (p107)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Newall Carr Road, Newall",England,"Floodwater from the River Wharfe inundated low–lying streets in Otley; high river levels rendered surface water drainage inoperable and caused surcharge/backflow at outfalls, leading to localised flooding on Newall Carr Road and contributing to residential/commercial property flooding in the town. (p107)",54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9161935,-1.7056281,"<div id=""html_71af97f8f7cb11889bbb67eb3fab15e4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Richards Road, Otley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Wharfe and surcharge of surface water drainage inundated St Richards Road, contributing to localised internal and external property flooding within Otley. (p107)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"St Richards Road, Otley",England,"Floodwater from the River Wharfe and surcharge of surface water drainage inundated St Richards Road, contributing to localised internal and external property flooding within Otley. (p107)",54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9162872,-1.6889272,"<div id=""html_b4474d4d8b78c243066d3d8cc0fbf31d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wrenbeck Drive, Newall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wrenbeck Drive was affected by fluvial flooding from the River Wharfe and by localised surface water flooding where drainage could not discharge to the river, contributing to property inundation in Otley. (p107)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Wrenbeck Drive, Newall",England,"Wrenbeck Drive was affected by fluvial flooding from the River Wharfe and by localised surface water flooding where drainage could not discharge to the river, contributing to property inundation in Otley. (p107)",54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9095355,-1.6947419,"<div id=""html_c5b591ce78afc40e394f1fa113e09f9d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridge Avenue, Newall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridge Avenue, adjacent to the River Wharfe floodplain, was inundated by fluvial flooding and experienced surface water drainage issues due to high river levels, contributing to the property flooding totals reported for Otley. (p107)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridge Avenue community was affected by floods (November and December 2015); local residents are working to improve and implement flood defences and to put in place a Flood Day Action Plan, with support from LCC and the Environment Agency. [MUL] (p290)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Bridge Avenue, Newall",England,"Bridge Avenue, adjacent to the River Wharfe floodplain, was inundated by fluvial flooding and experienced surface water drainage issues due to high river levels, contributing to the property flooding totals reported for Otley. (p107)
Bridge Avenue community was affected by floods (November and December 2015); local residents are working to improve and implement flood defences and to put in place a Flood Day Action Plan, with support from LCC and the Environment Agency. [MUL] (p290)",54 internal residential; 14 external residential; 5 internal commercial; 1 external commercial; 74 properties total (Otley aggregate across listed streets) (p107),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9099947,-1.41703,"<div id=""html_447cd28fd173aea849cc402bfd437f3e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Avenue, Linton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential street flooded with water covering the road and encroaching onto front gardens and house entrances; photographic evidence dated 27 Dec 2015 shows the Avenue submerged during the event. The local pumping station(s) associated with The Avenue were rendered inoperable while river levels remained high. [IMG,PAG] (p113)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record&ndash;high River Wharfe levels during the event rendered surface&ndash;water drainage and the pumping station(s) at The Avenue inoperable until river levels fell, contributing to localised flooding of low&ndash;lying areas that drain towards the river. [IMG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood defences constructed around The Avenue (1995) provide a 1 in 50 year standard of protection; during the event high river/beck levels and resulting backwater effects rendered the surface water pumping station at The Avenue inoperable and contributed to localised flooding until river levels subsided. [PAG] (p115)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Avenue flood embankment overtopped and then breached on 26 Dec, allowing river water to flow onto the residential street. The street became impassable by foot/vehicle (people were shown using a small boat) and properties/gardens were inundated. [IMG,PAG] (p116)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Defences overtopped causing floodwater to enter the street and properties; 14 properties were flooded and floodwater remained in the street until it was pumped out by the Fire Service. (p119)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local flood defences at The Avenue were overtopped by the extreme river levels, producing flooding of the street and surrounding area. (p120)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties [IMG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties (protected by the Avenue flood defence) [PAG] (p115)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 properties; 3 days (p119)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"The Avenue, Linton",England,"Residential street flooded with water covering the road and encroaching onto front gardens and house entrances; photographic evidence dated 27 Dec 2015 shows the Avenue submerged during the event. The local pumping station(s) associated with The Avenue were rendered inoperable while river levels remained high. [IMG,PAG] (p113)
Record–high River Wharfe levels during the event rendered surface–water drainage and the pumping station(s) at The Avenue inoperable until river levels fell, contributing to localised flooding of low–lying areas that drain towards the river. [IMG] (p114)
Flood defences constructed around The Avenue (1995) provide a 1 in 50 year standard of protection; during the event high river/beck levels and resulting backwater effects rendered the surface water pumping station at The Avenue inoperable and contributed to localised flooding until river levels subsided. [PAG] (p115)
The Avenue flood embankment overtopped and then breached on 26 Dec, allowing river water to flow onto the residential street. The street became impassable by foot/vehicle (people were shown using a small boat) and properties/gardens were inundated. [IMG,PAG] (p116)
Defences overtopped causing floodwater to enter the street and properties; 14 properties were flooded and floodwater remained in the street until it was pumped out by the Fire Service. (p119)
Local flood defences at The Avenue were overtopped by the extreme river levels, producing flooding of the street and surrounding area. (p120)","12 properties [IMG] (p114)
12 properties (protected by the Avenue flood defence) [PAG] (p115)
14 properties; 3 days (p119)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9068064,-1.4136345,"<div id=""html_6b47b2f9d1ae67ccea127a62df9f18bd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Millbeck Green, Linton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mill Beck Green had privately&ndash;built embankments (1970s&ndash;80s) and, following flooding in 2007, the Environment Agency constructed a wall extension; the defence now protects properties in the Mill Beck Green area. The report notes high beck and river levels during the event that could cause localised flooding; it is therefore plausible Mill Beck Green was at risk from these high beck levels during the event (the text does not definitively state whether the EA wall was overtopped in 2015). (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Anecdotal reports indicate Millbeck Green narrowly escaped internal property flooding, but gardens in proximity to homes experienced extensive flooding from overland/river flows. (p117)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 40 properties (p114)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Millbeck Green, Linton",England,"Mill Beck Green had privately–built embankments (1970s–80s) and, following flooding in 2007, the Environment Agency constructed a wall extension; the defence now protects properties in the Mill Beck Green area. The report notes high beck and river levels during the event that could cause localised flooding; it is therefore plausible Mill Beck Green was at risk from these high beck levels during the event (the text does not definitively state whether the EA wall was overtopped in 2015). (p114)
Anecdotal reports indicate Millbeck Green narrowly escaped internal property flooding, but gardens in proximity to homes experienced extensive flooding from overland/river flows. (p117)",approximately 40 properties (p114),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9093853,-1.409884,"<div id=""html_50fa18ee0c08a88325fa9344b6ffa8db"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Lane, Linton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Church Lane experienced extensive and deep garden flooding adjacent to Collingham Beck; isolated internal property flooding was also reported in the area. [IMG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive and deep garden flooding adjacent to Collingham Beck, with water extending up to house walls and submerging gardens (photographed 26 December 2015). [IMG,PAG] (p118)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,"Church Lane, Linton",England,"Church Lane experienced extensive and deep garden flooding adjacent to Collingham Beck; isolated internal property flooding was also reported in the area. [IMG] (p117)
