1 in 100 year event (1% AEP) affecting the Lower River Wharfe; highest recorded flood levels [TAB,PAG] (p134)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.82631093745671,-1.6377097995891179,"<div id=""html_993d8658df5423240c1bdb3470c911d3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Newlay</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Listed as one of the worst&ndash;affected sub&ndash;areas (Aire, Newlay) in the Leeds investigation; experienced fluvial/main&ndash;river related flooding during the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event that affected communities and properties. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Newlay experienced internal and external residential flooding and internal commercial flooding according to the table. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire came out of channel, overtopped and inundated adjacent residential properties in Newlay, producing internal flooding and flood damage to properties. Verified internal flood depths vary from 800 mm to 1100 mm. The local flood warning area covers 47 properties considered to be at risk. [PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>For Newlay (Horsforth) the source table identifies Fluvial (Main River) flooding. Main River overtopping occurred as channel capacity was exceeded; high river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage preventing free discharge and causing backup through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage systems. [TAB] (p49)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 internal residential; 4 external residential; 1 internal commercial; 0 external commercial; total 10. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>47 properties; 7 properties registered to receive flood warnings; internal flood depths 800 mm to 1100 mm [PAG] (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Newlay,England,"Listed as one of the worst–affected sub–areas (Aire, Newlay) in the Leeds investigation; experienced fluvial/main–river related flooding during the 25–29 Dec 2015 event that affected communities and properties. (p16)
Newlay experienced internal and external residential flooding and internal commercial flooding according to the table. [TAB] (p29)
The River Aire came out of channel, overtopped and inundated adjacent residential properties in Newlay, producing internal flooding and flood damage to properties. Verified internal flood depths vary from 800 mm to 1100 mm. The local flood warning area covers 47 properties considered to be at risk. [PAG] (p48)
For Newlay (Horsforth) the source table identifies Fluvial (Main River) flooding. Main River overtopping occurred as channel capacity was exceeded; high river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage preventing free discharge and causing backup through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage systems. [TAB] (p49)","5 internal residential; 4 external residential; 1 internal commercial; 0 external commercial; total 10. [TAB] (p29)
47 properties; 7 properties registered to receive flood warnings; internal flood depths 800 mm to 1100 mm [PAG] (p48)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.808968860768886,-1.5794676961494223,"<div id=""html_fffa5b85d8aa3094aa7466876c0b82f2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Burley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Listed as a worst&ndash;affected sub&ndash;area (Aire, Burley); subject to fluvial/main&ndash;river flooding during the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event that affected properties and urban areas. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Burley sustained internal residential flooding and substantial internal commercial flooding as reported. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding and disruption in the Burley district associated with the River Aire flood event, including impacts to transport links and critical infrastructure. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High (record) River Aire levels prevented free discharge from the surface water drainage system and produced fluvial backwater. This caused surcharge/back pressure in surface water systems and led to extensive flooding in the area; use of flap valves was noted as a possible mitigant. (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial (River Aire) flooding and associated surface&ndash;water/area flooding caused internal and external flooding of residential and commercial properties; constrained surface water drainage due to record high river levels and fluvial backwater/surcharge increased area flooding; verified internal flood depths of 840mm to 1540mm. [TAB,PAG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive internal property flooding: at least 303 properties in Burley were flood&ndash;affected during the event, including indirect impacts to high&ndash;rise accommodation. Verified internal flood depths recorded in properties ranged from 840 mm to 1,540 mm; area flooding remote to properties is reported to have been even deeper. [IMG,PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire overtopped and breached informal private flood defences in Burley, spilling onto the A65 (Kirkstall Road) and adjacent side roads. This caused internal and external property flooding with major damage to fixtures, fittings, furniture, building fabric, vehicles, lifts/elevators and utility supplies; properties required months of refurbishment. [IMG,PAG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding caused by the River Aire overtopping and breaching informal private defences, resulting in inundation across Burley; floodwaters spilled onto the A65 (Kirkstall Road) and into side streets, with aggregated flow continuing towards Leeds city centre. [PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Burley was affected by overtopping flows from the River Aire; flood flow routes and overtopped fluvial flows are shown concentrated in the Burley corridor, and a severe flood warning was issued for the area (Cardigan Fields Leisure Park and Kirkstall Works between Viaduct Road and City Gate). [IMG,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Burley was the subject of a severe flood warning issued at 10:16 on 26 December 2015; the report lists local sources of flooding as fluvial (main river and ordinary watercourse), surface water (including on the highway), groundwater and sewer flooding. [PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main&ndash;river (fluvial) flooding affected the Burley area during the December 2015 event; Burley is identified as having been impacted by fluvial (main river) flooding. [TAB] (p66)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 internal residential; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial; 16 external commercial; total 303. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>303 properties total; 8 internal residential properties; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial properties; 16 external commercial properties; verified internal flood depths 840mm to 1540mm. [TAB,PAG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>303 properties; internal flood depths 840 mm, 970 mm, 1540 mm [IMG,PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1&ndash;18 properties (mapped as a smaller incident cluster) [IMG,PAG] (p62)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Burley,England,"Listed as a worst–affected sub–area (Aire, Burley); subject to fluvial/main–river flooding during the 25–29 Dec 2015 event that affected properties and urban areas. (p16)
Burley sustained internal residential flooding and substantial internal commercial flooding as reported. [TAB] (p29)
Widespread flooding and disruption in the Burley district associated with the River Aire flood event, including impacts to transport links and critical infrastructure. (p52)
High (record) River Aire levels prevented free discharge from the surface water drainage system and produced fluvial backwater. This caused surcharge/back pressure in surface water systems and led to extensive flooding in the area; use of flap valves was noted as a possible mitigant. (p59)
Extensive fluvial (River Aire) flooding and associated surface–water/area flooding caused internal and external flooding of residential and commercial properties; constrained surface water drainage due to record high river levels and fluvial backwater/surcharge increased area flooding; verified internal flood depths of 840mm to 1540mm. [TAB,PAG] (p60)
Extensive internal property flooding: at least 303 properties in Burley were flood–affected during the event, including indirect impacts to high–rise accommodation. Verified internal flood depths recorded in properties ranged from 840 mm to 1,540 mm; area flooding remote to properties is reported to have been even deeper. [IMG,PAG] (p61)
River Aire overtopped and breached informal private flood defences in Burley, spilling onto the A65 (Kirkstall Road) and adjacent side roads. This caused internal and external property flooding with major damage to fixtures, fittings, furniture, building fabric, vehicles, lifts/elevators and utility supplies; properties required months of refurbishment. [IMG,PAG] (p62)
Fluvial flooding caused by the River Aire overtopping and breaching informal private defences, resulting in inundation across Burley; floodwaters spilled onto the A65 (Kirkstall Road) and into side streets, with aggregated flow continuing towards Leeds city centre. [PAG] (p63)
Burley was affected by overtopping flows from the River Aire; flood flow routes and overtopped fluvial flows are shown concentrated in the Burley corridor, and a severe flood warning was issued for the area (Cardigan Fields Leisure Park and Kirkstall Works between Viaduct Road and City Gate). [IMG,PAG] (p64)
Burley was the subject of a severe flood warning issued at 10:16 on 26 December 2015; the report lists local sources of flooding as fluvial (main river and ordinary watercourse), surface water (including on the highway), groundwater and sewer flooding. [PAG] (p65)
Main–river (fluvial) flooding affected the Burley area during the December 2015 event; Burley is identified as having been impacted by fluvial (main river) flooding. [TAB] (p66)","8 internal residential; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial; 16 external commercial; total 303. [TAB] (p29)
303 properties total; 8 internal residential properties; 0 external residential; 279 internal commercial properties; 16 external commercial properties; verified internal flood depths 840mm to 1540mm. [TAB,PAG] (p60)
303 properties; internal flood depths 840 mm, 970 mm, 1540 mm [IMG,PAG] (p61)
1–18 properties (mapped as a smaller incident cluster) [IMG,PAG] (p62)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7781924,-1.51951,"<div id=""html_9dab509358c4659780fc8de667234111"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thwaite Gate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the sub&ndash;area 'Aire, Thwaite Gate and Hunslet' identified as among the worst affected; experienced fluvial/main&ndash;river related flooding during 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 impacting communities and properties. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thwaite Gate (presented together with Hunslet) experienced large numbers of external residential flood incidents plus internal residential and commercial flooding. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding occurred in low&ndash;lying areas influenced by record high River Aire levels. Surface water drainage systems draining toward the river (including the nearby pumping station) were rendered inoperable until river levels subsided; surface water systems may also have suffered surcharge/backpressure contributing to localised flooding. Flooding subsided once river levels dropped and gravity discharge was restored. [PAG] (p67)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial flooding causing internal and external residential and commercial property flooding. Record&ndash;high River Aire levels produced backwater/surcharge on surface water drainage (and nearby pumping station), rendering systems inoperable until river levels fell; flooding subsided once gravity discharge was restored. [PAG] (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal and external property flooding within the mapped flood extent during the event; incidents clustered adjacent to the river and within the Flood Zone 3 floodplain. [IMG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in Thwaite Gate was driven primarily by fluvial sources with overland flow routes mapped (figure 83), causing inundation along the identified flow paths. [PAG] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding affected Thwaite Gate as part of the main flow routes in the area; rivers overtopped and high river levels surcharged drainage systems causing floodwater to inundate low&ndash;lying areas. [PAG] (p71)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from Main Rivers (River Aire) overtopped channel capacity in the Thwaite Gate area, exacerbating surface water drainage surcharge and causing backups into road gullies, manholes and private drainage systems. [TAB] (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial; total 1170. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial; 1170 total properties (these totals are for Thwaite Gate and Hunslet combined) [PAG] (p68)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Thwaite Gate,England,"Part of the sub–area 'Aire, Thwaite Gate and Hunslet' identified as among the worst affected; experienced fluvial/main–river related flooding during 25–29 Dec 2015 impacting communities and properties. (p16)
Thwaite Gate (presented together with Hunslet) experienced large numbers of external residential flood incidents plus internal residential and commercial flooding. [TAB] (p29)
Localised flooding occurred in low–lying areas influenced by record high River Aire levels. Surface water drainage systems draining toward the river (including the nearby pumping station) were rendered inoperable until river levels subsided; surface water systems may also have suffered surcharge/backpressure contributing to localised flooding. Flooding subsided once river levels dropped and gravity discharge was restored. [PAG] (p67)
Extensive fluvial flooding causing internal and external residential and commercial property flooding. Record–high River Aire levels produced backwater/surcharge on surface water drainage (and nearby pumping station), rendering systems inoperable until river levels fell; flooding subsided once gravity discharge was restored. [PAG] (p68)
Internal and external property flooding within the mapped flood extent during the event; incidents clustered adjacent to the river and within the Flood Zone 3 floodplain. [IMG] (p69)
Flooding in Thwaite Gate was driven primarily by fluvial sources with overland flow routes mapped (figure 83), causing inundation along the identified flow paths. [PAG] (p70)
Fluvial flooding affected Thwaite Gate as part of the main flow routes in the area; rivers overtopped and high river levels surcharged drainage systems causing floodwater to inundate low–lying areas. [PAG] (p71)
Fluvial flooding from Main Rivers (River Aire) overtopped channel capacity in the Thwaite Gate area, exacerbating surface water drainage surcharge and causing backups into road gullies, manholes and private drainage systems. [TAB] (p72)","60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial; total 1170. [TAB] (p29)
60 internal residential; 1051 external residential; 49 internal commercial; 10 external commercial; 1170 total properties (these totals are for Thwaite Gate and Hunslet combined) [PAG] (p68)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.772493735859314,-1.5098103159310874,"<div id=""html_35206f81ecab711ba6edd3a9e7c8f494"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stourton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the district sub&ndash;areas affected by the main river fluvial flood event of 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected commercial properties and surroundings with recorded internal and external commercial flooding incidents in Stourton. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding occurred in low&ndash;lying areas due to record high River Aire levels. Surface water drainage systems discharging to the river, including the syphon beneath the canal and the nearby pumping station, were rendered inoperable until river levels subsided. [PAG] (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying areas under the influence of the River Aire experienced localised flooding; surface water drainage systems draining to the river (including the syphon and pumping station) were rendered inoperable until river levels dropped and gravity discharge resumed. [IMG] (p74)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial flooding (from the River Aire) within Flood Zone 3 caused property flooding in Stourton: commercial premises flooded internally and externally; high&ndash;rise and multi&ndash;occupancy accommodation were noted as affected where present. [TAB,PAG] (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal and external property flooding concentrated within Flood Zone 3 close to the River Aire; flood flow routes mapped through the area and incidents clustered in central Stourton affecting industrial and commercial zones. [IMG,PAG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Industrial estate and multiple buildings were inundated by floodwaters routed from the River Aire and the adjacent canal (canal surcharge/overflow). Flood flow routes and wrack lines are mapped across the estate; water reached entrances and partially submerged modular structures and yards, disrupting operations. [IMG] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding across Stourton resulting from the River Aire and the surcharged Aire & Calder Navigation Canal. Floodwaters flowed into low&ndash;lying areas and properties in Stourton, following the canal overtopping and breach at Knostrop. (p79)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Stourton was included in a severe flood warning and was affected by river overtopping and surcharging of surface water drainage during the flood event. [PAG] (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (main river) identified as the source of flooding affecting Stourton during the 25 Dec 2015&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event. [TAB] (p82)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of the Knostrop by&ndash;wash embankment collapse (at Knostrop Lock); the failure led to surcharging of the Aire & Calder Navigation and contributed to overtopping and local inundation. (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected businesses along parts of the River Aire in Stourton, causing visual amenity damage and contributing to reduced trade and inward investment for those businesses. (p292)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0 internal residential flooding; 0 external residential flooding; 36 internal commercial flooding; 4 external commercial flooding; total 40 incidents [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>36 internal commercial properties; 4 external commercial properties; 40 properties total [TAB,PAG] (p75)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Incidents per 250m grid squares include categories up to 206&ndash;592 properties in the largest grid square; other grid&ndash;square ranges shown: 1&ndash;12, 18&ndash;46, 47&ndash;121, 122&ndash;205 [IMG,PAG] (p76)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>wrack line/recorded flood levels on buildings 21.617 m, 21.630 m, 21.635 m, 21.644 m, 21.667 m [IMG] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 structural embankment collapse (p86)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Stourton,England,"Identified as one of the district sub–areas affected by the main river fluvial flood event of 25–29 Dec 2015. (p16)
Flooding affected commercial properties and surroundings with recorded internal and external commercial flooding incidents in Stourton. [TAB] (p29)
Localised flooding occurred in low–lying areas due to record high River Aire levels. Surface water drainage systems discharging to the river, including the syphon beneath the canal and the nearby pumping station, were rendered inoperable until river levels subsided. [PAG] (p73)
Low–lying areas under the influence of the River Aire experienced localised flooding; surface water drainage systems draining to the river (including the syphon and pumping station) were rendered inoperable until river levels dropped and gravity discharge resumed. [IMG] (p74)
Extensive fluvial flooding (from the River Aire) within Flood Zone 3 caused property flooding in Stourton: commercial premises flooded internally and externally; high–rise and multi–occupancy accommodation were noted as affected where present. [TAB,PAG] (p75)
Internal and external property flooding concentrated within Flood Zone 3 close to the River Aire; flood flow routes mapped through the area and incidents clustered in central Stourton affecting industrial and commercial zones. [IMG,PAG] (p76)
Industrial estate and multiple buildings were inundated by floodwaters routed from the River Aire and the adjacent canal (canal surcharge/overflow). Flood flow routes and wrack lines are mapped across the estate; water reached entrances and partially submerged modular structures and yards, disrupting operations. [IMG] (p77)
Fluvial flooding across Stourton resulting from the River Aire and the surcharged Aire & Calder Navigation Canal. Floodwaters flowed into low–lying areas and properties in Stourton, following the canal overtopping and breach at Knostrop. (p79)
Stourton was included in a severe flood warning and was affected by river overtopping and surcharging of surface water drainage during the flood event. [PAG] (p81)
Fluvial (main river) identified as the source of flooding affecting Stourton during the 25 Dec 2015–29 Dec 2015 event. [TAB] (p82)
Location of the Knostrop by–wash embankment collapse (at Knostrop Lock); the failure led to surcharging of the Aire & Calder Navigation and contributed to overtopping and local inundation. (p86)
Flooding affected businesses along parts of the River Aire in Stourton, causing visual amenity damage and contributing to reduced trade and inward investment for those businesses. (p292)","0 internal residential flooding; 0 external residential flooding; 36 internal commercial flooding; 4 external commercial flooding; total 40 incidents [TAB] (p29)
36 internal commercial properties; 4 external commercial properties; 40 properties total [TAB,PAG] (p75)
Incidents per 250m grid squares include categories up to 206–592 properties in the largest grid square; other grid–square ranges shown: 1–12, 18–46, 47–121, 122–205 [IMG,PAG] (p76)
