1289 properties [PAG] (p99)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.75629964225097,-1.4503907004593726,"<div id=""html_7ad12363143b5defec5e66310ad71323"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woodlesford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected residential properties and some commercial/outbuilding areas in Woodlesford. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying areas were inundated by standing floodwater caused by record&ndash;high River Aire levels and fluvial backwater; surface water drainage systems draining to the river were rendered inoperable until river levels fell. Industrial units, buildings and storage tanks were submerged and Trans Pennine Trail signage and recreational routes were inundated (photograph dated 27 December 2015 shows submerged industrial area). The recently completed low&ndash;level embankment flood defences provided protection to residential properties during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3 of the River Aire affected Woodlesford. Flooding included internal residential flooding, external residential flooding and external commercial flooding; high&ndash;rise and multi&ndash;occupancy accommodation were also affected. [IMG,TAB] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire overtopped into adjacent floodplains and inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation; the canal also surcharged and overtopped (exacerbated by upstream structural collapse at Knostrop), leading to flooding of low&ndash;lying properties and commercial units in Woodlesford. Photographs (27 Dec) show flooded commercial units and The Locks residential area; transport was affected in vicinity. [IMG,PAG] (p85)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in Woodlesford was caused primarily by the River Aire overtopping into adjacent floodplains and inundating the Aire & Calder Navigation canal, which then exceeded its channel capacity and overtopped. Canal breaches and river overtopping routes spread floodwater across low&ndash;lying areas, affecting farmland, infrastructure and low&ndash;lying properties in the Woodlesford area. [IMG] (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main River (fluvial) flooding overtopped channel capacity during the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event. High river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage, preventing discharge and causing back&ndash;up through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage systems in Woodlesford. [TAB] (p87)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 internal residential flooding; 4 external residential flooding; 0 internal commercial flooding; 1 external commercial flooding; total 6 incidents [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 internal residential flooding; 4 external residential flooding; 0 internal commercial flooding; 1 external commercial flooding; Woodlesford total 6 properties flooded [IMG,TAB] (p84)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Woodlesford,England,"Flooding affected residential properties and some commercial/outbuilding areas in Woodlesford. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
Low–lying areas were inundated by standing floodwater caused by record–high River Aire levels and fluvial backwater; surface water drainage systems draining to the river were rendered inoperable until river levels fell. Industrial units, buildings and storage tanks were submerged and Trans Pennine Trail signage and recreational routes were inundated (photograph dated 27 December 2015 shows submerged industrial area). The recently completed low–level embankment flood defences provided protection to residential properties during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p83)
Extensive fluvial flooding within Flood Zone 3 of the River Aire affected Woodlesford. Flooding included internal residential flooding, external residential flooding and external commercial flooding; high–rise and multi–occupancy accommodation were also affected. [IMG,TAB] (p84)
River Aire overtopped into adjacent floodplains and inundated the Aire & Calder Navigation; the canal also surcharged and overtopped (exacerbated by upstream structural collapse at Knostrop), leading to flooding of low–lying properties and commercial units in Woodlesford. Photographs (27 Dec) show flooded commercial units and The Locks residential area; transport was affected in vicinity. [IMG,PAG] (p85)
Flooding in Woodlesford was caused primarily by the River Aire overtopping into adjacent floodplains and inundating the Aire & Calder Navigation canal, which then exceeded its channel capacity and overtopped. Canal breaches and river overtopping routes spread floodwater across low–lying areas, affecting farmland, infrastructure and low–lying properties in the Woodlesford area. [IMG] (p86)
Main River (fluvial) flooding overtopped channel capacity during the 25–29 Dec 2015 event. High river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage, preventing discharge and causing back–up through road gullies, manhole chambers and private drainage systems in Woodlesford. [TAB] (p87)","1 internal residential flooding; 4 external residential flooding; 0 internal commercial flooding; 1 external commercial flooding; total 6 incidents [TAB,PAG] (p29)
1 internal residential flooding; 4 external residential flooding; 0 internal commercial flooding; 1 external commercial flooding; Woodlesford total 6 properties flooded [IMG,TAB] (p84)",GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.783322001404755,-1.5702734329612564,"<div id=""html_89865626024278095c59b32c4c7f401e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holbeck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map and analysis show clusters of property&ndash;flooding incidents in Holbeck concentrated along the River Aire and areas designated Flood Zone 3 during Storm Eva. This indicates multi&ndash;occupancy and residential properties in Holbeck experienced internal and external flooding leading to property damage (fixtures/fittings, vehicles, utilities) and disruption consistent with city&ndash;centre impacts described in the report. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Holbeck is identified on the map as falling within the area affected by overland fluvial flows from the River Aire overtopping; floodwaters entered urban areas along the corridor, inundating properties and infrastructure in its path. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runs southwards through Newall, is culverted under Farnley Lane and joins the River Wharfe; the culverted channel is identified as a potential contributor to flooding of properties and to the inundation observed at Farnley Lane (photographed 26 December 2015). [MUL] (p105)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters rejoined the River Wharfe at the Holbeck tributary downstream of the weir, forming part of the overland flood route. (p110)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Holbeck,England,"Map and analysis show clusters of property–flooding incidents in Holbeck concentrated along the River Aire and areas designated Flood Zone 3 during Storm Eva. This indicates multi–occupancy and residential properties in Holbeck experienced internal and external flooding leading to property damage (fixtures/fittings, vehicles, utilities) and disruption consistent with city–centre impacts described in the report. [IMG] (p35)
Holbeck is identified on the map as falling within the area affected by overland fluvial flows from the River Aire overtopping; floodwaters entered urban areas along the corridor, inundating properties and infrastructure in its path. [IMG] (p41)
Runs southwards through Newall, is culverted under Farnley Lane and joins the River Wharfe; the culverted channel is identified as a potential contributor to flooding of properties and to the inundation observed at Farnley Lane (photographed 26 December 2015). [MUL] (p105)
Floodwaters rejoined the River Wharfe at the Holbeck tributary downstream of the weir, forming part of the overland flood route. (p110)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7939576,-1.5770966,"<div id=""html_060b4311795da3617a7b68597499be39"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>New Wortley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping flows concentrated between Burley and New Wortley, with New Wortley identified on the map as one of the areas most affected by river flooding. [IMG] (p64)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,New Wortley,England,"Overtopping flows concentrated between Burley and New Wortley, with New Wortley identified on the map as one of the areas most affected by river flooding. [IMG] (p64)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.78941386267931,-1.5191391482443863,"<div id=""html_58ced0b2bbe50672cacad3515ffdf965"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cross Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding (internal and external) occurred along the river corridor through the Cross Green area, with incidents aggregated into 250m grid squares overlapping the Flood Zone 3 extent. [IMG] (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>47&ndash;121 properties [IMG] (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Cross Green,England,"Property flooding (internal and external) occurred along the river corridor through the Cross Green area, with incidents aggregated into 250m grid squares overlapping the Flood Zone 3 extent. [IMG] (p69)",47–121 properties [IMG] (p69),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.83120993214061,-1.3435067748332545,"<div id=""html_5e8d63d2b4b46f0e79c17e8e6b37a90f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Aberford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A642 (Aberford / Wakefield Road) is listed among the worst affected streets/areas, indicating flooding impacts along this route in the Aberford/Wakefield Road area. (p84)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Aberford,England,"The A642 (Aberford / Wakefield Road) is listed among the worst affected streets/areas, indicating flooding impacts along this route in the Aberford/Wakefield Road area. (p84)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.7492696,-1.4541201,"<div id=""html_e0f7c892f8f4c04a10325b9bf28499d2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oulton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map and analysis show Oulton located within the mapped area where the River Aire flood extent and incident clusters are shown. This indicates Oulton&#8217;s low&ndash;lying areas adjacent to the river were likely inundated by fluvial (river) flooding and influenced by backwater/surcharge effects from the River Aire during the event. [IMG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The incident distribution map and image metadata include Oulton/Oulton Hall Hotel within the geographic extent of flooding along the River Aire and canal corridors, indicating properties/areas near Oulton were within the flood extent during the event (inference from map imagery rather than detailed textual description). [IMG] (p85)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Oulton,England,"Map and analysis show Oulton located within the mapped area where the River Aire flood extent and incident clusters are shown. This indicates Oulton’s low–lying areas adjacent to the river were likely inundated by fluvial (river) flooding and influenced by backwater/surcharge effects from the River Aire during the event. [IMG] (p84)
The incident distribution map and image metadata include Oulton/Oulton Hall Hotel within the geographic extent of flooding along the River Aire and canal corridors, indicating properties/areas near Oulton were within the flood extent during the event (inference from map imagery rather than detailed textual description). [IMG] (p85)",,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.722353,-1.3512543,"<div id=""html_e4d02972ed57b3e0071e4d2319565c75"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Castleford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Was at risk of severe inundation during the extreme flood but benefited from the operation of St Aidan's and the associated washlands, which modelled to reduce flood levels by about 500 mm and helped prevent widespread severe flooding. [PAG] (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Castleford was within the area of extensive fluvial flooding associated with the River Aire and Calder; operation of St Aidan's reduced peak flood levels by about 500mm at Castleford, helping to prevent severe inundation of properties. [PAG] (p92)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 500 mm reduction in flood level at Castleford; 1289 properties saved (modelled estimate for combined effect of St Aidan's and associated washlands between Woodlesford and Castleford) [PAG] (p91)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Castleford,England,"Was at risk of severe inundation during the extreme flood but benefited from the operation of St Aidan's and the associated washlands, which modelled to reduce flood levels by about 500 mm and helped prevent widespread severe flooding. [PAG] (p91)
Castleford was within the area of extensive fluvial flooding associated with the River Aire and Calder; operation of St Aidan's reduced peak flood levels by about 500mm at Castleford, helping to prevent severe inundation of properties. [PAG] (p92)",around 500 mm reduction in flood level at Castleford; 1289 properties saved (modelled estimate for combined effect of St Aidan's and associated washlands between Woodlesford and Castleford) [PAG] (p91),GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.9139502,-1.6913845,"<div id=""html_c91e34a9bb4b7875193d44309e4abc42"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Newall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area north of the River Wharfe that was affected by the Otley flooding in Nov/Dec 2015; flooding at Farnley Lane (in Newall) was photographed on 26 December 2015 and tributary/catchment flows (Holbeck culvert) are noted as contributing to property flooding. [MUL] (p105)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Newall,England,Area north of the River Wharfe that was affected by the Otley flooding in Nov/Dec 2015; flooding at Farnley Lane (in Newall) was photographed on 26 December 2015 and tributary/catchment flows (Holbeck culvert) are noted as contributing to property flooding. [MUL] (p105),,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25.html,GB-LDS_1_2015-12-25,Leeds Metropolitan District flooding 25 Dec 2015 - 29 Dec 2015,"Leeds Strategic Flood Investigation Report: Storm Eva Flood Event, 25-29 Dec 2015",070/Leeds/Section 19 Report Appendix 1 060217.pdf
GB-LDS,1,2015-12-25,53.8971657,-1.5914638,"<div id=""html_9cd33ff44b20024b05bf9b5fa96dd492"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Arthington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by the Lower River Wharfe flood on Boxing Day (part of the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event) when the river reached its highest recorded levels, causing fluvial flooding that exceeded drainage design standards and inundated the community. (p111)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced highest&ndash;ever river levels on Boxing Day causing Main River overtopping and fluvial flooding; high river levels also surcharged surface water drainage leading to backup into road gullies and drainage assets and increased flood depths in the community. (p120)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the Lower River Wharfe overtopped the channel and inundated adjacent low&ndash;lying properties and amenity areas; communities experienced the highest recorded river levels with rivers surcharging drainage and causing internal property flooding. (p127)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On Boxing Day during the 25&ndash;29 Dec 2015 event the Lower River Wharfe reached its highest recorded levels, causing fluvial flooding that affected Arthington. Main river overtopping and very high river levels surcharged conventional surface water drainage, backing up via gullies and manholes and resulting in inundation of the community and drainage systems that exceeded design standards. (p134)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths in some cases in excess of 2m; internal property flooding depths of 800mm recorded (p127)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 in 100 year event (1% AEP) affecting the Lower River Wharfe; highest recorded flood levels (p134)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leeds,Arthington,England,"Affected by the Lower River Wharfe flood on Boxing Day (part of the 25–29 Dec 2015 event) when the river reached its highest recorded levels, causing fluvial flooding that exceeded drainage design standards and inundated the community. (p111)
Experienced highest–ever river levels on Boxing Day causing Main River overtopping and fluvial flooding; high river levels also surcharged surface water drainage leading to backup into road gullies and drainage assets and increased flood depths in the community. (p120)
Fluvial flooding from the Lower River Wharfe overtopped the channel and inundated adjacent low–lying properties and amenity areas; communities experienced the highest recorded river levels with rivers surcharging drainage and causing internal property flooding. (p127)
