GB-LAN,22,2019-07-27,53.8509186,-2.2208391405389625,"<div id=""html_d97c2a61149aa0f72dcd7d4b90a363df"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Barrowford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm and flood problems impacted safe travel, with standing water on roads reported and a tree blown across the road contributing to unsafe travel conditions. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Barrowford,England,"Storm and flood problems impacted safe travel, with standing water on roads reported and a tree blown across the road contributing to unsafe travel conditions. (p16)",,GB-LAN_22_2019-07-27.html,GB-LAN_22_2019-07-27,Lancashire flooding 27 Jul 2019 - 01 Aug 2019,27 July – 1 August 2019 Flooding In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation Report,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-july-august-2019-flooding.pdf
GB-LAN,22,2019-07-27,53.73814964937003,-2.965901693843432,"<div id=""html_9edaf597ffb54efc298417d01b4fd806"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lytham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on 28 July 2019 affected the Royal Lytham area, causing delays to the Senior Open Championship. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lytham,England,"Heavy rainfall on 28 July 2019 affected the Royal Lytham area, causing delays to the Senior Open Championship. (p17)",,GB-LAN_22_2019-07-27.html,GB-LAN_22_2019-07-27,Lancashire flooding 27 Jul 2019 - 01 Aug 2019,27 July – 1 August 2019 Flooding In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation Report,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-july-august-2019-flooding.pdf
GB-DBY,7,2023-10-18,53.02109393868759,-1.3273159380402457,"<div id=""html_22878090b475f436de442c63198d3600"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>D and D Transport</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater overtopped the River Erewash bank behind the DandD Transport car park, flowed across the car park and onto Cromford Road, contributing to downstream inundation of streets and properties. (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,D and D Transport,England,"Floodwater overtopped the River Erewash bank behind the DandD Transport car park, flowed across the car park and onto Cromford Road, contributing to downstream inundation of streets and properties. (p29)",,GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-7-trent-and-lower-erewash-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,7,2023-10-18,52.9138669,-1.3304161,"<div id=""html_918b8f54ee5f7ebde4e776f55fd5af55"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Golden Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Received flow from watercourses (Golden Stream and a drainage ditch) draining the west of Breaston during Storm Babet; acted as the downstream receptor conveying floodwater towards the River Erewash and onwards through Long Eaton. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding during the event was concentrated near local watercourses including Golden Brook; properties in the nearby Long Eaton community were internally flooded while flooding extents were adjacent to or near the course of Golden Brook. [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ordinary watercourse located about 700 m south of the flooded community and mapped in Flood Zone 3; identified in the report as part of the high&ndash;risk watercourse network adjacent to the flooded area and could have contributed to the local flood risk during Storm Babet (role inferred from mapping and proximity). [PAG,IMG] (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 residential properties in Long Eaton suffered internal flooding (community total) [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Golden Brook,England,"Received flow from watercourses (Golden Stream and a drainage ditch) draining the west of Breaston during Storm Babet; acted as the downstream receptor conveying floodwater towards the River Erewash and onwards through Long Eaton. (p3)
Flooding during the event was concentrated near local watercourses including Golden Brook; properties in the nearby Long Eaton community were internally flooded while flooding extents were adjacent to or near the course of Golden Brook. [PAG,IMG] (p20)
Ordinary watercourse located about 700 m south of the flooded community and mapped in Flood Zone 3; identified in the report as part of the high–risk watercourse network adjacent to the flooded area and could have contributed to the local flood risk during Storm Babet (role inferred from mapping and proximity). [PAG,IMG] (p21)","102 residential properties in Long Eaton suffered internal flooding (community total) [PAG,IMG] (p20)",GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-7-trent-and-lower-erewash-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,7,2023-10-18,52.995808,-1.3017821,"<div id=""html_bd28b198f15990c4b07e601b89a474e8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Erewash</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash and its channel network are described as part of the sub&ndash;catchment hydrological response during Storm Babet (flowing south through multiple towns into Attenborough Nature Reserve). (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Shorter and earlier river responses to heavy rainfall were recorded upstream (near Pinxton and Eastwood) and later, longer responses downstream (near Sandiacre). River Erewash produced very high flows/levels in response to Storm Babet, with stations recording some of the highest&ndash;ranked flows or levels on record. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Erewash inundation adjacent to Ilkeston contributed to internal flooding of nearby properties during Storm Babet. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding/adjacent river levels contributed to internal flooding of properties in Ilkeston; the community is on the river's floodplain and proximity to the river is linked to clusters of flooded homes. [PAG,IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Erewash flooding during Storm Babet/October 2023 caused inundation of adjacent residential properties in the Ilkeston floodplain, specifically properties on Digby Street, Station Street and Wentworth Street. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the River Erewash (conveyed via the canal) caused flooding in the Ilkeston community; the river was the source of fluvial flood water that inundated properties. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash overtopped its banks and drove fluvial flooding (with the canal acting as a conduit), contributing to flooding across Ilkeston including internal flooding of homes and non&ndash;residential properties; railway lines and the station also flooded. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contributed to internal flooding of the Sandiacre community during Storm Babet; residential and some commercial properties between the River Erewash (east) and the Erewash Valley Canal (west) were flooded. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Erewash flooding affected the Sandiacre community (community on its floodplain); contributed to internal flooding of residential and commercial properties during the storm. Historic floods of similar extent are recorded for the river. [PAG,MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping/inundation from the River Erewash caused internal flooding of residential properties and some commercial premises in the Sandiacre community. Properties that flooded during Storm Babet had previously flooded from the River Erewash; the community lies on the Erewash floodplain and many flooded properties are within Flood Zone 2. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the adjacent Sandiacre community; river flooding from the River Erewash caused the internal property flooding reported during the event. Historic similar flood extents recorded in 1947, 1957, 1960, 1977 and 1978. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash overtopped its banks and flowed into the Erewash Canal north of Gas Street; this overtopping initiated the flow path that led to widespread property flooding in the community. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river overtopped its banks, flowed into the Erewash Canal north of Gas Street and caused overtopping of defence walls (west and east banks) opposite Megavaux. Overflowing led to flood water flowing westward and south across Station Road, flooding the industrial park, railyard and residential/business areas; the railway became a flow path and was closed. [PAG,IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash overflowed, causing flooding to an industrial estate and a railyard south of Station Road; the Environment Agency issued a Flood Alert (19 Oct 2023) and a Flood Warning for the River Erewash at Sandiacre (21 Oct 2023). (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main river adjacent to the flooded Long Eaton community; flooding from the River Erewash inundated residential properties in Long Eaton during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels on the River Erewash prompted a Flood Alert (19 Oct 2023) and a Flood Warning (21 Oct 2023) and contributed to internal flooding in the Long Eaton community. [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash overflowed and conveyed floodwater into the Long Eaton community, entering via Doncaster Road/Doncaster Grove and causing internal flooding to houses. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash overflowed and sent flow routes into the Long Eaton community (initially joining Doncaster Road), causing gardens and ground floors of properties to flood and leading to internal flooding across the community. [PAG,IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The community (Langley Mill) sits on the floodplain of the River Erewash and was flooded during the event, causing inundation of residential terraced homes and non&ndash;residential properties (restaurants, shops, depot yard, self&ndash;storage). Critical infrastructure lies adjacent (Midlands Railway Line, A608 Derby Road, A610) and a Severn Trent pumping station and Millhay Heanor STW lie approximately 400m and 900m downstream respectively of the flooded area. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash is the main watercourse running through the mapped area and is implicated in river&ndash;flooding interactions with the urban area; culverted sections and tributaries run close to streets and buildings that experienced flooding. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Erewash is identified as the primary watercourse along which the highest flood risk (Flood Zone 3) is concentrated. Properties in Langley Mill flooded internally during Storm Babet; many of those flooded properties fall within the river flood zones shown adjacent to the River Erewash and Erewash Canal, indicating river flooding from the River Erewash impacted the community. [PAG,IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Primary source of flooding in the Langley Mill community. The river overtopped (first identified at a 90&deg; bend south of the Crabapple Drive allotments) and drove floodwater into the Erewash Canal (which acted as a conduit) and, in some locations, interacted with flows from Nether Green Brook to cause internal flooding of properties on Cromford Road, Dean Street, Queen Street, Station Road, Derby Road, Boundary Lane and Enterprise Way. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash overtopped its west embankment at a 90&ndash;degree bend south of the Crabapple Drive allotments. Overtopping and subsequent flow across industrial yards and the DandD Transport car park routed floodwater onto Cromford Road and caused internal flooding of properties on adjacent streets. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash overtopped its banks in multiple locations (including behind DandD Transport car park and at the Derby Road bridge), sending water across the car park onto Cromford Road and contributing directly to internal and external flooding of nearby streets and properties. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced exceptionally high flows that overtopped banks and infrastructure, contributing to flooding across Langley Mill and adjacent roads. Wrack marks show peak water reached 59.9mAOD; the river produced record gauge levels at Shipley Gate, reflecting the primary source of the observed inundation. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Erewash affected the Ironville floodplain and contributed to internal flooding of properties in Adelaide Walk during Storm Babet. [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Primary source of the event: the Erewash overflowed its west bank upstream of the Midland Railway Line and the excess water flowed across adjacent land onto Nottingham Lane and into the Ironville community, causing internal flooding of homes. [PAG,IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflowed its west bank upstream of the Midland Railway Line, causing water to flow over fields and onto Nottingham Lane, through the railway underpass and southwards across recreational playing fields into residential areas. The river overflow was the primary source of internal flooding that affected properties on Adelaide Walk, Regent Court and Deepdale. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash was the focus of Flood Alerts and a Flood Warning during the event and contributed to local flooding in Pinxton. The Environment Agency issued a Flood Alert for the River Erewash (Derbyshire and Nottinghamshire) on 19 Oct 2023 at 01:13 and a Flood Warning for the River Erewash at Ironville on 20 Oct 2023 at 11:45. Flooding associated with the river (driven by Storm Babet) led to internal flooding of residential properties in the south&ndash;west corner of Pinxton and emergency evacuations by Derbyshire Fire and Rescue using boats. [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash caused river flooding affecting the Pinxton community during Storm Babet; the Environment Agency historic record also shows previous river flooding in this location (1965, 1977, 2007). [IMG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Erewash reached a peak gauge level of 2.65 metres (the highest recorded at this station), contributing to local flooding by preventing sewer discharge and allowing floodwater to enter Pinxton Wharf. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>53 residential and 14 non&ndash;residential properties (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>53 residential properties; 14 non&ndash;residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>53 homes [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>53 homes flooded internally; 14 non&ndash;residential properties reported flooding [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>129 properties [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>129 properties [PAG,MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>129 properties flooded (community total attributable to river overtopping and subsequent canal conveyance) [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>104 homes; 25 businesses (129 homes and businesses total) [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 residential properties [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 properties [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 homes [PAG,IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>21 residential properties; 29 non&ndash;residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>28 homes; 35 non&ndash;residential properties [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>59.9mAOD peak wrack mark on both banks; bank heights approx 55mAOD (west) and 58mAOD (east); 1m floodwater depths reported in multiple locations [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>maximum gauge level 3.25m recorded at Shipley Gate; wrack level 59.9mAOD; floodwater depths approximately 1m reported in multiple locations [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 residential properties; 0.9m internal depth at one property in Regent Court; up to 0.5m depth at properties within the Adelaide Park area (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 Flood Alert; 1 Flood Warning; 22 properties [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2.65 metres [PAG] (p39)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Erewash,England,"The River Erewash and its channel network are described as part of the sub–catchment hydrological response during Storm Babet (flowing south through multiple towns into Attenborough Nature Reserve). (p1)
Shorter and earlier river responses to heavy rainfall were recorded upstream (near Pinxton and Eastwood) and later, longer responses downstream (near Sandiacre). River Erewash produced very high flows/levels in response to Storm Babet, with stations recording some of the highest–ranked flows or levels on record. (p3)
River Erewash inundation adjacent to Ilkeston contributed to internal flooding of nearby properties during Storm Babet. (p8)
River flooding/adjacent river levels contributed to internal flooding of properties in Ilkeston; the community is on the river's floodplain and proximity to the river is linked to clusters of flooded homes. [PAG,IMG] (p9)
River Erewash flooding during Storm Babet/October 2023 caused inundation of adjacent residential properties in the Ilkeston floodplain, specifically properties on Digby Street, Station Street and Wentworth Street. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Fluvial flooding from the River Erewash (conveyed via the canal) caused flooding in the Ilkeston community; the river was the source of fluvial flood water that inundated properties. [PAG] (p11)
The River Erewash overtopped its banks and drove fluvial flooding (with the canal acting as a conduit), contributing to flooding across Ilkeston including internal flooding of homes and non–residential properties; railway lines and the station also flooded. [IMG] (p12)
Contributed to internal flooding of the Sandiacre community during Storm Babet; residential and some commercial properties between the River Erewash (east) and the Erewash Valley Canal (west) were flooded. [IMG] (p13)
River Erewash flooding affected the Sandiacre community (community on its floodplain); contributed to internal flooding of residential and commercial properties during the storm. Historic floods of similar extent are recorded for the river. [PAG,MUL] (p14)
Overtopping/inundation from the River Erewash caused internal flooding of residential properties and some commercial premises in the Sandiacre community. Properties that flooded during Storm Babet had previously flooded from the River Erewash; the community lies on the Erewash floodplain and many flooded properties are within Flood Zone 2. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p15)
Flooding of the adjacent Sandiacre community; river flooding from the River Erewash caused the internal property flooding reported during the event. Historic similar flood extents recorded in 1947, 1957, 1960, 1977 and 1978. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p16)
The River Erewash overtopped its banks and flowed into the Erewash Canal north of Gas Street; this overtopping initiated the flow path that led to widespread property flooding in the community. [PAG] (p17)
The river overtopped its banks, flowed into the Erewash Canal north of Gas Street and caused overtopping of defence walls (west and east banks) opposite Megavaux. Overflowing led to flood water flowing westward and south across Station Road, flooding the industrial park, railyard and residential/business areas; the railway became a flow path and was closed. [PAG,IMG] (p18)
The River Erewash overflowed, causing flooding to an industrial estate and a railyard south of Station Road; the Environment Agency issued a Flood Alert (19 Oct 2023) and a Flood Warning for the River Erewash at Sandiacre (21 Oct 2023). (p19)
Main river adjacent to the flooded Long Eaton community; flooding from the River Erewash inundated residential properties in Long Eaton during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p20)
River levels on the River Erewash prompted a Flood Alert (19 Oct 2023) and a Flood Warning (21 Oct 2023) and contributed to internal flooding in the Long Eaton community. [IMG,PAG] (p21)
The River Erewash overflowed and conveyed floodwater into the Long Eaton community, entering via Doncaster Road/Doncaster Grove and causing internal flooding to houses. [PAG] (p22)
The River Erewash overflowed and sent flow routes into the Long Eaton community (initially joining Doncaster Road), causing gardens and ground floors of properties to flood and leading to internal flooding across the community. [PAG,IMG] (p23)
The community (Langley Mill) sits on the floodplain of the River Erewash and was flooded during the event, causing inundation of residential terraced homes and non–residential properties (restaurants, shops, depot yard, self–storage). Critical infrastructure lies adjacent (Midlands Railway Line, A608 Derby Road, A610) and a Severn Trent pumping station and Millhay Heanor STW lie approximately 400m and 900m downstream respectively of the flooded area. (p24)
The River Erewash is the main watercourse running through the mapped area and is implicated in river–flooding interactions with the urban area; culverted sections and tributaries run close to streets and buildings that experienced flooding. [IMG] (p25)
River Erewash is identified as the primary watercourse along which the highest flood risk (Flood Zone 3) is concentrated. Properties in Langley Mill flooded internally during Storm Babet; many of those flooded properties fall within the river flood zones shown adjacent to the River Erewash and Erewash Canal, indicating river flooding from the River Erewash impacted the community. [PAG,IMG] (p26)
Primary source of flooding in the Langley Mill community. The river overtopped (first identified at a 90° bend south of the Crabapple Drive allotments) and drove floodwater into the Erewash Canal (which acted as a conduit) and, in some locations, interacted with flows from Nether Green Brook to cause internal flooding of properties on Cromford Road, Dean Street, Queen Street, Station Road, Derby Road, Boundary Lane and Enterprise Way. [PAG] (p28)
The River Erewash overtopped its west embankment at a 90–degree bend south of the Crabapple Drive allotments. Overtopping and subsequent flow across industrial yards and the DandD Transport car park routed floodwater onto Cromford Road and caused internal flooding of properties on adjacent streets. [IMG,PAG] (p29)
The River Erewash overtopped its banks in multiple locations (including behind DandD Transport car park and at the Derby Road bridge), sending water across the car park onto Cromford Road and contributing directly to internal and external flooding of nearby streets and properties. [PAG] (p30)
Experienced exceptionally high flows that overtopped banks and infrastructure, contributing to flooding across Langley Mill and adjacent roads. Wrack marks show peak water reached 59.9mAOD; the river produced record gauge levels at Shipley Gate, reflecting the primary source of the observed inundation. [PAG] (p31)
Flooding from the River Erewash affected the Ironville floodplain and contributed to internal flooding of properties in Adelaide Walk during Storm Babet. [IMG,PAG] (p33)
Primary source of the event: the Erewash overflowed its west bank upstream of the Midland Railway Line and the excess water flowed across adjacent land onto Nottingham Lane and into the Ironville community, causing internal flooding of homes. [PAG,IMG] (p34)
Overflowed its west bank upstream of the Midland Railway Line, causing water to flow over fields and onto Nottingham Lane, through the railway underpass and southwards across recreational playing fields into residential areas. The river overflow was the primary source of internal flooding that affected properties on Adelaide Walk, Regent Court and Deepdale. (p35)
The River Erewash was the focus of Flood Alerts and a Flood Warning during the event and contributed to local flooding in Pinxton. The Environment Agency issued a Flood Alert for the River Erewash (Derbyshire and Nottinghamshire) on 19 Oct 2023 at 01:13 and a Flood Warning for the River Erewash at Ironville on 20 Oct 2023 at 11:45. Flooding associated with the river (driven by Storm Babet) led to internal flooding of residential properties in the south–west corner of Pinxton and emergency evacuations by Derbyshire Fire and Rescue using boats. [IMG,PAG] (p36)
The River Erewash caused river flooding affecting the Pinxton community during Storm Babet; the Environment Agency historic record also shows previous river flooding in this location (1965, 1977, 2007). [IMG] (p37)
The River Erewash reached a peak gauge level of 2.65 metres (the highest recorded at this station), contributing to local flooding by preventing sewer discharge and allowing floodwater to enter Pinxton Wharf. [PAG] (p39)","53 residential and 14 non–residential properties (p8)
