GB-DBY,7,2023-10-18,52.8577218,-1.3910666,"<div id=""html_a7c6b073da40e3b15b7b90397db43cdd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yates Avenue, Aston-On-Trent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Eight residential properties on Yates Avenue suffered internal flooding. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopped ditch/runoff from farmland west of Weston Road flowed across Weston Road and reached Yates Avenue, causing internal flooding of homes. [IMG] (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 homes [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties [IMG] (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"Yates Avenue, Aston-On-Trent",England,"Eight residential properties on Yates Avenue suffered internal flooding. [IMG] (p41)
Overtopped ditch/runoff from farmland west of Weston Road flowed across Weston Road and reached Yates Avenue, causing internal flooding of homes. [IMG] (p43)","8 homes [IMG] (p41)
8 properties [IMG] (p43)",GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-7-trent-and-lower-erewash-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,7,2023-10-18,52.9380048,-1.3513172,"<div id=""html_39fc349525d0aae2e5c4d74c7240aefb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Erewash</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Shorter and earlier river responses to the heavy rainfall were recorded upstream on the River Erewash (e.g. Pinxton, Eastwood). Gauging stations at Pinxton, Sandiacre and near Eastwood produced the highest&ndash;ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding within the Erewash administrative area (Ilkeston) resulted in internal flooding of homes and businesses; local authority response included sandbag distribution, empty and filled sandbag collection, and removal of flood&ndash;damaged household items by the borough council. [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 gauging stations (Pinxton, Sandiacre, near Eastwood) produced highest&ndash;ranked flows/levels on record; shortest lag time 13 hours (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>53 residential properties; 14 non&ndash;residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Erewash,England,"Shorter and earlier river responses to the heavy rainfall were recorded upstream on the River Erewash (e.g. Pinxton, Eastwood). Gauging stations at Pinxton, Sandiacre and near Eastwood produced the highest–ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3)
Flooding within the Erewash administrative area (Ilkeston) resulted in internal flooding of homes and businesses; local authority response included sandbag distribution, empty and filled sandbag collection, and removal of flood–damaged household items by the borough council. [IMG,PAG] (p9)","3 gauging stations (Pinxton, Sandiacre, near Eastwood) produced highest–ranked flows/levels on record; shortest lag time 13 hours (p3)
53 residential properties; 14 non–residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p9)",GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_7_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-7-trent-and-lower-erewash-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,7,2023-10-18,53.118504,-1.556618,"<div id=""html_7fb7a66b0083fc5496aecb0942aa70bc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Derbyshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within Derbyshire, Storm Babet's intense rainfall led to river and surface&ndash;water flooding in parts of the county. The report records internal flooding of properties in Breaston (west of the community) adjacent to Golden Stream and a drainage ditch, and flooding affected local transport infrastructure (the Midlands Main Line railway). [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with Storm Babet affected communities in Derbyshire including internal flooding of properties in Breaston. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in the county during Storm Babet: in Ilkeston, properties adjacent to the River Erewash experienced internal flooding from river&ndash;related inundation; in Breaston (Festival Avenue) surface water and sewer surcharge (blocked drains and a surcharging manhole) caused runoff to reach driveways on a downslope and led to internal flooding of homes. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in Ilkeston (within Derbyshire) during the event resulted in internal flooding to residential and non&ndash;residential properties in the community. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding in the Long Eaton community (within Derbyshire) caused internal flooding of residential properties and affected local infrastructure near the River Erewash floodplain. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties in the Long Eaton area (Erewash borough) caused by the River Erewash overflowing and flow routes entering the community via Doncaster Grove/Doncaster Road. Reported impacts include gardens and ground floors flooded, whole ground&ndash;floor internal flooding at a property on Margaret Avenue, and floodwater rising through floors at properties on Denacre Avenue and George Avenue. Derbyshire County Council recorded local water depths of 0.45 m to 0.60 m on Nottingham Road under the railway bridge near Long Eaton Kwik Fit. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties within the county (the studied community) experienced internal flooding during Storm Babet; the record of previous flooding in the community is also reported (see quant). Specific streets in the community have high surface&ndash;water flood risk, and some properties have flooded historically. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in Langley Mill after the River Erewash overtopped and floodwater transferred via the Erewash Canal and contributions from Nether Green Brook. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Erewash in Ironville resulted in internal flooding of residential properties on Adelaide Walk, Regent Court and Deepdale, flooding of Nottingham Lane, Victoria Street and the B6016, and external stock affected at a business depot to the south of the recreational fields. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Derbyshire County Council visited the area and observed evidence of sewer surcharge (manhole covers lifted), indicating sewer network flooding/surcharge and surface water run&ndash;off contributed to internal flooding of residential properties. [PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff accumulated at a local low point north of the railway line causing pooling and flooding on streets in the community (notably Alexandra Terrace and York Terrace). Derbyshire County Council engaged in post&ndash;event investigations with Severn Trent into drainage issues related to this surface water flooding. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Communities within the county experienced internal property flooding associated with Storm Babet. Nine residential properties in Aston&ndash;on&ndash;Trent were internally flooded. Local response included removal of effluent from the adopted highway, collection of flood&ndash;damaged items and offers of temporary accommodation; Derbyshire County Council worked with Severn Trent Water to investigate drainage and residents were provided PFR scheme claim forms. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>49 residential properties; 2 non&ndash;residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>49 homes flooded in Breaston (within Derbyshire) [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>53 residential properties; 14 non&ndash;residential properties; one surcharging manhole (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>53 homes; 14 non&ndash;residential properties [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 residential properties [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>102 homes; Recreation Street 1; Devonshire Avenue 4; Harlaxton Drive 1; Carrfield Avenue 2; George Avenue 2; Norfolk Road 23; Doncaster Grove 6; Florence Avenue 10; Landsdown Grove 17; Denacre Avenue 5; Conway Street 7; Nottingham Road 17; Mayfield Grove 4; Margaret Avenue 3; reported local water depths 0.45 m to 0.60 m on Nottingham Road [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 properties previously flooded (3 residential; 12 non&ndash;residential); 21 residential and 29 non&ndash;residential properties reported flooded internally during Storm Babet (see Storm Babet entry) [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>28 homes; 35 non&ndash;residential properties [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 residential properties; 1 property (Regent Court) with internal flood depth of 0.9m; properties in Adelaide Park experienced up to 0.5m of floodwater. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 residential properties in Aston&ndash;on&ndash;Trent; 1 home on Weston Road; 8 homes on Yates Avenue [IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Derbyshire,England,"Within Derbyshire, Storm Babet's intense rainfall led to river and surface–water flooding in parts of the county. The report records internal flooding of properties in Breaston (west of the community) adjacent to Golden Stream and a drainage ditch, and flooding affected local transport infrastructure (the Midlands Main Line railway). [PAG,IMG] (p4)
Flooding associated with Storm Babet affected communities in Derbyshire including internal flooding of properties in Breaston. [PAG] (p6)
Internal flooding of properties in the county during Storm Babet: in Ilkeston, properties adjacent to the River Erewash experienced internal flooding from river–related inundation; in Breaston (Festival Avenue) surface water and sewer surcharge (blocked drains and a surcharging manhole) caused runoff to reach driveways on a downslope and led to internal flooding of homes. (p8)
Flooding in Ilkeston (within Derbyshire) during the event resulted in internal flooding to residential and non–residential properties in the community. [PAG] (p11)
River flooding in the Long Eaton community (within Derbyshire) caused internal flooding of residential properties and affected local infrastructure near the River Erewash floodplain. [PAG] (p21)
Internal flooding of residential properties in the Long Eaton area (Erewash borough) caused by the River Erewash overflowing and flow routes entering the community via Doncaster Grove/Doncaster Road. Reported impacts include gardens and ground floors flooded, whole ground–floor internal flooding at a property on Margaret Avenue, and floodwater rising through floors at properties on Denacre Avenue and George Avenue. Derbyshire County Council recorded local water depths of 0.45 m to 0.60 m on Nottingham Road under the railway bridge near Long Eaton Kwik Fit. [PAG] (p23)
Properties within the county (the studied community) experienced internal flooding during Storm Babet; the record of previous flooding in the community is also reported (see quant). Specific streets in the community have high surface–water flood risk, and some properties have flooded historically. [IMG] (p25)
Internal flooding of properties in Langley Mill after the River Erewash overtopped and floodwater transferred via the Erewash Canal and contributions from Nether Green Brook. [PAG] (p28)
Flooding from the River Erewash in Ironville resulted in internal flooding of residential properties on Adelaide Walk, Regent Court and Deepdale, flooding of Nottingham Lane, Victoria Street and the B6016, and external stock affected at a business depot to the south of the recreational fields. (p35)
Derbyshire County Council visited the area and observed evidence of sewer surcharge (manhole covers lifted), indicating sewer network flooding/surcharge and surface water run–off contributed to internal flooding of residential properties. [PAG] (p38)
Surface water runoff accumulated at a local low point north of the railway line causing pooling and flooding on streets in the community (notably Alexandra Terrace and York Terrace). Derbyshire County Council engaged in post–event investigations with Severn Trent into drainage issues related to this surface water flooding. [PAG] (p40)
Communities within the county experienced internal property flooding associated with Storm Babet. Nine residential properties in Aston–on–Trent were internally flooded. Local response included removal of effluent from the adopted highway, collection of flood–damaged items and offers of temporary accommodation; Derbyshire County Council worked with Severn Trent Water to investigate drainage and residents were provided PFR scheme claim forms. [IMG] (p41)","49 residential properties; 2 non–residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p4)
49 homes flooded in Breaston (within Derbyshire) [PAG] (p6)
53 residential properties; 14 non–residential properties; one surcharging manhole (p8)
53 homes; 14 non–residential properties [PAG] (p11)
102 residential properties [PAG] (p21)
102 homes; Recreation Street 1; Devonshire Avenue 4; Harlaxton Drive 1; Carrfield Avenue 2; George Avenue 2; Norfolk Road 23; Doncaster Grove 6; Florence Avenue 10; Landsdown Grove 17; Denacre Avenue 5; Conway Street 7; Nottingham Road 17; Mayfield Grove 4; Margaret Avenue 3; reported local water depths 0.45 m to 0.60 m on Nottingham Road [PAG] (p23)
