GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.5996252,-2.4669597,"<div id=""html_0546361fa9c8be223b9365bc2435be8c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lydwell Close, Weymouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties on the street. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,"Lydwell Close, Weymouth",England,Internal flooding of properties on the street. [TAB] (p24),1 property [TAB] (p24),GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.74737687263831,-2.4192394577688887,"<div id=""html_93e30d18d41046eec448598d69085564"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dorset</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Many parts of Dorset experienced the worst flooding for over ten years during the event (6&ndash;13 July 2012). Intense torrential rain caused flash flooding in rapid&ndash;response catchments, larger catchments were affected as floodwaters passed through rivers, and rapid rises in groundwater levels caused groundwater flooding to properties and roads. The rainfall triggered landslips (Eype, Lyme Regis, Beaminster Tunnel) and led to road damage and communities being cut off. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe disruption was occurring across Dorset on 7 July 2012; the River Bride was placed on a Severe Flood Warning and the Flood Guidance Statement indicated a HIGH flood risk for Dorset as impacts were occurring. There was continuing rainfall with ongoing risk of surface water and river flooding in parts of the county. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>By 15:00 on 7 July severe disruption was occurring across Dorset as the flood risk was assessed HIGH; the River Bride was on a Severe Flood Warning. Continued episodes of river and surface&ndash;water flooding caused repeated periods of MEDIUM risk (notably 9 July) with significant disruption expected; groundwater flooding began to be noted from 10 July and contributed to ongoing low&ndash;level flooding into mid&ndash;July. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on already&ndash;saturated ground (6&ndash;7 July) produced rapid rises in river levels, raised groundwater and widespread ponding of surface water across many parts of the county. [IMG,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, slow&ndash;moving occluded frontal rainfall on 6&ndash;8 July 2012 produced runoff and caused flooding in parts of Dorset (particularly West Dorset). Rain gauges located within the flooded areas were used to assess the rarity/return period of the rainfall totals. [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Across Dorset approximately 1,700 properties received fluvial and groundwater flood alerts and warnings during the July 2012 event. [TAB,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Over 70 communities across the county were affected by flooding; multiple hub locations held flood clinics and agencies engaged with affected residents to record impacts and provide support. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;level map shows internal flooding incidents across Dorset with the most severe clusters in southern and southwestern parts (notably around Weymouth, Dorchester and Bridport). Darker&ndash;blue zones on the map indicate high incidence of internal (internal&ndash;property) flooding affecting communities and properties within the county. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 40 properties flooded along the South Winterbourne; three fatalities; 12m increase in groundwater levels (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 1700 properties [TAB,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 70 communities; over 300 residents (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Dorset,England,"Many parts of Dorset experienced the worst flooding for over ten years during the event (6–13 July 2012). Intense torrential rain caused flash flooding in rapid–response catchments, larger catchments were affected as floodwaters passed through rivers, and rapid rises in groundwater levels caused groundwater flooding to properties and roads. The rainfall triggered landslips (Eype, Lyme Regis, Beaminster Tunnel) and led to road damage and communities being cut off. (p2)
Severe disruption was occurring across Dorset on 7 July 2012; the River Bride was placed on a Severe Flood Warning and the Flood Guidance Statement indicated a HIGH flood risk for Dorset as impacts were occurring. There was continuing rainfall with ongoing risk of surface water and river flooding in parts of the county. [PAG] (p8)
By 15:00 on 7 July severe disruption was occurring across Dorset as the flood risk was assessed HIGH; the River Bride was on a Severe Flood Warning. Continued episodes of river and surface–water flooding caused repeated periods of MEDIUM risk (notably 9 July) with significant disruption expected; groundwater flooding began to be noted from 10 July and contributed to ongoing low–level flooding into mid–July. [PAG] (p9)
Intense rainfall on already–saturated ground (6–7 July) produced rapid rises in river levels, raised groundwater and widespread ponding of surface water across many parts of the county. [IMG,PAG] (p12)
Intense, slow–moving occluded frontal rainfall on 6–8 July 2012 produced runoff and caused flooding in parts of Dorset (particularly West Dorset). Rain gauges located within the flooded areas were used to assess the rarity/return period of the rainfall totals. [IMG,PAG] (p13)
Across Dorset approximately 1,700 properties received fluvial and groundwater flood alerts and warnings during the July 2012 event. [TAB,PAG] (p16)
Over 70 communities across the county were affected by flooding; multiple hub locations held flood clinics and agencies engaged with affected residents to record impacts and provide support. (p17)
County–level map shows internal flooding incidents across Dorset with the most severe clusters in southern and southwestern parts (notably around Weymouth, Dorchester and Bridport). Darker–blue zones on the map indicate high incidence of internal (internal–property) flooding affecting communities and properties within the county. [IMG] (p25)","more than 40 properties flooded along the South Winterbourne; three fatalities; 12m increase in groundwater levels (p2)
approximately 1700 properties [TAB,PAG] (p16)
over 70 communities; over 300 residents (p17)",GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.7568771,-3.2215643,"<div id=""html_4a988c8fed802f1464990f6756cdbe0b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Devon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy and prolonged rainfall on the weekend of 6&ndash;7 July 2012 affected parts of East Devon as part of the same event that produced local flooding across the area. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,East Devon,England,Heavy and prolonged rainfall on the weekend of 6–7 July 2012 affected parts of East Devon as part of the same event that produced local flooding across the area. (p2),,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.796837,-2.3447323,"<div id=""html_d740b640824c8da56587745b8708c228"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dorset</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties experienced internal flooding up to 200 mm and external flooding up to 600 mm. Water within property sewer inspection chambers rose close to overtopping and sewer levels were reported to drop suddenly for no explained reason. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>200 mm internal flooding; 600 mm external flooding [PAG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Dorset,England,Residential properties experienced internal flooding up to 200 mm and external flooding up to 600 mm. Water within property sewer inspection chambers rose close to overtopping and sewer levels were reported to drop suddenly for no explained reason. [PAG] (p20),200 mm internal flooding; 600 mm external flooding [PAG] (p20),GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.704212797378865,-2.7257633386213076,"<div id=""html_4407dd488d48875d976d28bcb234be05"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Burton Bradstock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the public road, reaching about halfway up vehicle wheels and partially submerging cars; emergency services (police and recovery vehicle) were on scene and normal traffic was disrupted, indicating road closure and vehicle recovery operations. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from hills to the north flowed into the village, inundating highway drainage and causing internal flooding in the village centre (Middle Street, Shadrach) and disruption on the B3157 coast road; sewerage system was generally overwhelmed. Highway flood depths up to 1200mm were reported. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding impacted the community, prompting the need for a flood alleviation scheme and funding application to reduce future flood impacts. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties recorded in Appendix 1; water entered buildings resulting in internal property flooding. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties [TAB] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Burton Bradstock,England,"Floodwater covered the public road, reaching about halfway up vehicle wheels and partially submerging cars; emergency services (police and recovery vehicle) were on scene and normal traffic was disrupted, indicating road closure and vehicle recovery operations. [IMG] (p1)
Surface water from hills to the north flowed into the village, inundating highway drainage and causing internal flooding in the village centre (Middle Street, Shadrach) and disruption on the B3157 coast road; sewerage system was generally overwhelmed. Highway flood depths up to 1200mm were reported. (p18)
Flooding impacted the community, prompting the need for a flood alleviation scheme and funding application to reduce future flood impacts. [TAB] (p22)
Internal flooding of properties recorded in Appendix 1; water entered buildings resulting in internal property flooding. [TAB] (p24)","10 properties (p18)
10 properties [TAB] (p24)",GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.7201514,-1.8799118,"<div id=""html_d0c8aca727484e8704a7faaa39f9138d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bournemouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels on the Lower Stour rose, an emergency response plan was activated and residents were evacuated from a low&ndash;lying community (Iford Home Park) on the outskirts of Bournemouth. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>80 properties evacuated (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Bournemouth,England,"River levels on the Lower Stour rose, an emergency response plan was activated and residents were evacuated from a low–lying community (Iford Home Park) on the outskirts of Bournemouth. (p2)",80 properties evacuated (p2),GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.714402500000006,-2.5603476556571554,"<div id=""html_a3d4e380b87119ad5d810cbc731cf753"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Winterbourne Abbas</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along the South Winterbourne (which flows through Winterbourne Abbas) were flooded by surface runoff and rising groundwater; high groundwater levels also led to road closures. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of a combined impact across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown where 42 properties experienced internal flooding. Flooding was initially from surface water (local watercourse unable to cope) but the main impact was groundwater entering properties directly, producing extensive flooding lasting up to two weeks. The A35 trunk road was closed and traffic diversions exacerbated flooding impacts locally. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by flooding associated with the South Winterbourne; included within recommended consultant review of flooding mechanisms and actions to reduce flood impact on the community. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties with water entering buildings. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 40 properties (across the South Winterbourne through Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties (across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown); internal depths up to 150 mm; external depths up to 500 mm; flooding duration up to two weeks (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 properties [TAB] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Winterbourne Abbas,England,"Properties along the South Winterbourne (which flows through Winterbourne Abbas) were flooded by surface runoff and rising groundwater; high groundwater levels also led to road closures. [MUL] (p2)
Part of a combined impact across Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown where 42 properties experienced internal flooding. Flooding was initially from surface water (local watercourse unable to cope) but the main impact was groundwater entering properties directly, producing extensive flooding lasting up to two weeks. The A35 trunk road was closed and traffic diversions exacerbated flooding impacts locally. (p19)
Affected by flooding associated with the South Winterbourne; included within recommended consultant review of flooding mechanisms and actions to reduce flood impact on the community. [TAB] (p22)
Internal flooding of residential properties with water entering buildings. [TAB] (p24)","more than 40 properties (across the South Winterbourne through Winterbourne Abbas, Winterbourne Steepleton and Martinstown) [MUL] (p2)
