GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.713207,-2.4371228,"<div id=""html_8f1da9639a69c21bace2b9908838a9b4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dorchester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dorchester is shown in the imagery as receiving moderate to high 24&ndash;hour rainfall accumulations during 6&ndash;7 July 2012; these intense rainfall totals formed part of the county&ndash;wide event that produced localised surface water and urban flooding between 06 Jul 2012 and 13 Jul 2012. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded very high rainfall totals on 6&ndash;7 July 2012 (map shows ~111 mm). The intense rain fell onto already saturated ground across West Dorset producing rapid rises in groundwater and ponding of surface water &mdash; conditions that caused surface&ndash;water flooding and localised inundation in towns across West Dorset including Dorchester. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dorchester lies within the western area that recorded high rainfall totals during 6&ndash;7 July 2012; the intense precipitation contributed to rapid runoff and surface water / flash flooding impacts in West Dorset including Dorchester. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency Flood Warning issued for the Upper Frome at Dorchester (07/07/2012), indicating river flooding risk in Dorchester during the event period. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dorchester was affected by flooding associated with the Mill Stream during 6&ndash;13 July 2012; the report recommends reviewing Mill Stream hydrology, modelling and options for improvement to address these flood impacts. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties across the town, causing inundation of internal rooms and flood&ndash;related damage. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Noted cluster of internal flooding incidents in and around Dorchester on the county map, indicating internal property flooding occurred there. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>13 properties [TAB] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Dorchester,England,"Dorchester is shown in the imagery as receiving moderate to high 24–hour rainfall accumulations during 6–7 July 2012; these intense rainfall totals formed part of the county–wide event that produced localised surface water and urban flooding between 06 Jul 2012 and 13 Jul 2012. [IMG] (p11)
Recorded very high rainfall totals on 6–7 July 2012 (map shows ~111 mm). The intense rain fell onto already saturated ground across West Dorset producing rapid rises in groundwater and ponding of surface water — conditions that caused surface–water flooding and localised inundation in towns across West Dorset including Dorchester. [IMG] (p12)
Dorchester lies within the western area that recorded high rainfall totals during 6–7 July 2012; the intense precipitation contributed to rapid runoff and surface water / flash flooding impacts in West Dorset including Dorchester. [IMG] (p13)
Environment Agency Flood Warning issued for the Upper Frome at Dorchester (07/07/2012), indicating river flooding risk in Dorchester during the event period. [TAB] (p16)
Dorchester was affected by flooding associated with the Mill Stream during 6–13 July 2012; the report recommends reviewing Mill Stream hydrology, modelling and options for improvement to address these flood impacts. [TAB] (p22)
Internal flooding of properties across the town, causing inundation of internal rooms and flood–related damage. [TAB] (p24)
Noted cluster of internal flooding incidents in and around Dorchester on the county map, indicating internal property flooding occurred there. [IMG] (p25)",13 properties [TAB] (p24),GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.733784,-2.7589004,"<div id=""html_a57eb0fd6a636d714a1888fe4ddd1e9a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridport</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridport sits on the River Brit (mentioned in the report as rising sharply). Heavy rainfall on 6&ndash;7 July 2012 on already saturated ground produced rapid river level rises (River Brit) and ponding of surface water, causing river&ndash;related flooding and localised surface&ndash;water inundation in Bridport and its vicinity. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridport area received some of the highest rainfall totals during 6&ndash;7 July 2012; the intense rainfall generated rapid runoff in West Dorset and contributed to surface water / flash flooding in the Bridport area. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding overwhelmed local drainage causing internal flooding of properties; highway drainage could not discharge into rivers due to high river levels. Vehicle&ndash;generated waves worsened impacts despite main river flood defences preventing river flooding. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bridport flood alleviation scheme and surface water drainage were identified for review following flooding during 6&ndash;13 July 2012, indicating the town experienced river and surface water flood impacts and that defence performance and surface water interaction required assessment. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in the community. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Notable cluster of internal flooding incidents near Bridport, indicating internal property flooding affected the town. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties; internal flood depths in excess of 100mm; external depths up to 400mm (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties [TAB] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Bridport,England,"Bridport sits on the River Brit (mentioned in the report as rising sharply). Heavy rainfall on 6–7 July 2012 on already saturated ground produced rapid river level rises (River Brit) and ponding of surface water, causing river–related flooding and localised surface–water inundation in Bridport and its vicinity. [IMG] (p12)
Bridport area received some of the highest rainfall totals during 6–7 July 2012; the intense rainfall generated rapid runoff in West Dorset and contributed to surface water / flash flooding in the Bridport area. [IMG] (p13)
Surface water flooding overwhelmed local drainage causing internal flooding of properties; highway drainage could not discharge into rivers due to high river levels. Vehicle–generated waves worsened impacts despite main river flood defences preventing river flooding. (p18)
The Bridport flood alleviation scheme and surface water drainage were identified for review following flooding during 6–13 July 2012, indicating the town experienced river and surface water flood impacts and that defence performance and surface water interaction required assessment. [TAB] (p22)
