GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.609627,-2.4543424,"<div id=""html_b6cbd2181d49250585a70a2fa49caab0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Weymouth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Weymouth recorded very high rainfall during 6&ndash;7 July 2012 (map shows ~97 mm). The intense, prolonged rainfall on saturated ground caused rapid rises in groundwater and widespread ponding of surface water across West Dorset, producing localised surface&ndash;water flooding and inundation in coastal and low&ndash;lying urban areas including Weymouth. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Weymouth was in the area covered by Flood Warnings for the River Wey (Upwey to Weymouth), indicating river flooding risk to parts of Weymouth during the event. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in The Park District (part of Weymouth) included internal property flooding and near&ndash;overtopping of sewer inspection chambers; combined sewer system drainage failure contributed to impacts that required pumping to treatment works. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Weymouth required investigation for combined sewerage and surface water network problems (including Park District and Lanehouse Rocks Road), indicating sewer and surface water flooding, infiltration to the sewer network and local drainage pinch points. [TAB,PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Weymouth contains Lanehouse Rocks Road, where combined sewer and surface water drainage problems caused flooding during the event and triggered investigation and remedial recommendations. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties causing inundation of internal rooms and associated damage. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cluster of internal property flooding incidents concentrated in and around Weymouth; several urban neighbourhoods (e.g. Upwey and Broadwey, Littlemoor, Westham, Wey Valley, Melcombe Regis and Westham sub&ndash;areas) are shown as the most severely affected (darkest blue) on the map. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties (internal flooding in The Park District, Weymouth); internal depths up to 200mm; external depths up to 600mm (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Weymouth,England,"Weymouth recorded very high rainfall during 6–7 July 2012 (map shows ~97 mm). The intense, prolonged rainfall on saturated ground caused rapid rises in groundwater and widespread ponding of surface water across West Dorset, producing localised surface–water flooding and inundation in coastal and low–lying urban areas including Weymouth. [IMG] (p12)
Weymouth was in the area covered by Flood Warnings for the River Wey (Upwey to Weymouth), indicating river flooding risk to parts of Weymouth during the event. [TAB] (p16)
Flooding in The Park District (part of Weymouth) included internal property flooding and near–overtopping of sewer inspection chambers; combined sewer system drainage failure contributed to impacts that required pumping to treatment works. (p19)
