GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.6138596,-2.4733657,"<div id=""html_ebcee1176bb2fc5373747afe859261bc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Westham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Westham (and its sub&ndash;areas listed in the legend) is identified among the darkest&ndash;blue zones on the map, indicating concentrated internal flooding incidents and a high incidence of internal&ndash;property flooding in this part of Weymouth. [IMG] (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Westham,England,"Westham (and its sub–areas listed in the legend) is identified among the darkest–blue zones on the map, indicating concentrated internal flooding incidents and a high incidence of internal–property flooding in this part of Weymouth. [IMG] (p25)",,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.6234832,-2.4555217,"<div id=""html_dddce183205ddb0dd0fd1a9d4be295ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Melcombe Regis</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cluster of severe internal (urban) flooding in the Melcombe Regis area of Weymouth, identified on the map as one of the highest&ndash;incidence flood zones. [IMG] (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Melcombe Regis,England,"Cluster of severe internal (urban) flooding in the Melcombe Regis area of Weymouth, identified on the map as one of the highest–incidence flood zones. [IMG] (p25)",,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,4,2012-07-06,50.9486876,-2.5197693,"<div id=""html_658ea782d8d80c774f9eafa1a291779b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sherborne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sherborne is shown on the map within areas of internal flooding incidents, indicating some internal property flooding occurred there (not identified as a major cluster). [IMG] (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Sherborne,England,"Sherborne is shown on the map within areas of internal flooding incidents, indicating some internal property flooding occurred there (not identified as a major cluster). [IMG] (p25)",,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06.html,GB-DOR_4_2012-07-06,Dorset flooding 06 Jul 2012 - 13 Jul 2012,Dorset County Council July 2012 Flood Investigation Report,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/July 2012 Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-HEF,7,2020-02-16,52.098089,-2.8077579,"<div id=""html_0b4d0f0364daa89ec3e7de925fcd4b77"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Credenhill Park Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An ephemeral watercourse originating at Credenhill Park Wood contributed runoff that caused surface&ndash;water flooding which flowed onto the main road and towards nearby properties. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Credenhill Park Wood,England,An ephemeral watercourse originating at Credenhill Park Wood contributed runoff that caused surface–water flooding which flowed onto the main road and towards nearby properties. (p2),,GB-HEF_7_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_7_2020-02-16,Credenhill flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Credenhill,055/Herefordshire County/06/Credenhill.pdf
GB-HEF,7,2020-02-16,52.0625321,-2.7362102,"<div id=""html_c6bcc81897fd9f866c1086e1a9743110"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yazor Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Yazor Brook (an ordinary watercourse and tributary of the River Wye) is identified in the report as the relevant watercourse for the catchment; its flooding is associated with the February 2020 fluvial/surface&ndash;water flooding that led to internal flooding of properties in Credenhill. [PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflow and breaching of Yazor Brook or its upstream tributaries caused fluvial flooding of properties; at least one report states the brook breached its banks, encircled a house and came up through the drains. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflow/peak flows in the Yazor Brook caused flooding to properties downstream of the inlet weir; flows were routed into the FAS culvert which discharges to the River Wye. (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties [PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Yazor Brook,England,"Yazor Brook (an ordinary watercourse and tributary of the River Wye) is identified in the report as the relevant watercourse for the catchment; its flooding is associated with the February 2020 fluvial/surface–water flooding that led to internal flooding of properties in Credenhill. [PAG] (p1)
Overflow and breaching of Yazor Brook or its upstream tributaries caused fluvial flooding of properties; at least one report states the brook breached its banks, encircled a house and came up through the drains. [MUL] (p2)
Overflow/peak flows in the Yazor Brook caused flooding to properties downstream of the inlet weir; flows were routed into the FAS culvert which discharges to the River Wye. (p3)","10 properties [PAG] (p1)
4 properties [MUL] (p2)",GB-HEF_7_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_7_2020-02-16,Credenhill flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Credenhill,055/Herefordshire County/06/Credenhill.pdf
GB-HEF,7,2020-02-16,52.0880024,-2.8043354,"<div id=""html_5ad246fa2f3b290f681fc81ae1ca63c6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Credenhill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ten properties recorded internal flooding in February 2020. Flood depths inside ranged from less than 6 inches to 2 ft; some outbuildings were affected with depths up to 3 ft. Flooding persisted up to 1 day internally and up to 7 days in external areas. [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ten properties in Credenhill suffered internal flooding in February 2020 with depths reported from less than 6 inches to 2 ft; some outbuildings were affected with depths up to 3 ft. Surface water flooding affected five properties from three sources (an ephemeral watercourse originating at Credenhill Park Wood, direct runoff from fields south of the A480, and runoff impacting a low&ndash;lying property). A further four properties were flooded from Yazor Brook or its upstream tributaries. Drains/gullies were reported at capacity, with reports of overflowing ditches, blocked culverts/bridges and at least one report of groundwater/spring involvement. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties [IMG,PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties internally flooded; 5 properties from surface water sources; 4 properties from Yazor Brook/tributaries; 3 listed properties located within the Flood Zone; internal depths less than 6 inches to 2 ft; outbuilding depths up to 3 ft; internal flooding lasted up to 1 day and external flooding up to 7 days [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Credenhill,England,"Ten properties recorded internal flooding in February 2020. Flood depths inside ranged from less than 6 inches to 2 ft; some outbuildings were affected with depths up to 3 ft. Flooding persisted up to 1 day internally and up to 7 days in external areas. [IMG,PAG] (p1)
Ten properties in Credenhill suffered internal flooding in February 2020 with depths reported from less than 6 inches to 2 ft; some outbuildings were affected with depths up to 3 ft. Surface water flooding affected five properties from three sources (an ephemeral watercourse originating at Credenhill Park Wood, direct runoff from fields south of the A480, and runoff impacting a low–lying property). A further four properties were flooded from Yazor Brook or its upstream tributaries. Drains/gullies were reported at capacity, with reports of overflowing ditches, blocked culverts/bridges and at least one report of groundwater/spring involvement. [MUL] (p2)","10 properties [IMG,PAG] (p1)
10 properties internally flooded; 5 properties from surface water sources; 4 properties from Yazor Brook/tributaries; 3 listed properties located within the Flood Zone; internal depths less than 6 inches to 2 ft; outbuilding depths up to 3 ft; internal flooding lasted up to 1 day and external flooding up to 7 days [MUL] (p2)",GB-HEF_7_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_7_2020-02-16,Credenhill flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Credenhill,055/Herefordshire County/06/Credenhill.pdf
GB-KEN,18,2014-11-09,51.1105808,0.8573825,"<div id=""html_0b9db3d9c17441a410d01da19588cfac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Steeds Close, Kingsnorth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two residential properties were internally inundated and adjacent gardens flooded. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two residential properties and their gardens flooded following periods of heavy rainfall; Figure 2 and accompanying photographs document water pooling against the houses during the January event and emergency responders attending the scene. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Steeds Close were flooded when the adjacent watercourse was overwhelmed and water flowed onto the lowest lying land, inundating some properties. [TAB,MUL] (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Steeds Close, Kingsnorth",England,"Two residential properties were internally inundated and adjacent gardens flooded. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)
Two residential properties and their gardens flooded following periods of heavy rainfall; Figure 2 and accompanying photographs document water pooling against the houses during the January event and emergency responders attending the scene. [MUL] (p2)
Properties in Steeds Close were flooded when the adjacent watercourse was overwhelmed and water flowed onto the lowest lying land, inundating some properties. [TAB,MUL] (p7)","2 properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)
2 properties [MUL] (p2)",GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc.html,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc,Kingsnorth flooding 09 Nov 2014 and Kingsnorth flooding 08 Jan 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Steeds Close, Kingsnorth, Ashford",063/Kent/Steeds-Close-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf
GB-KEN,18,2014-11-09,51.1129676,0.8657456,"<div id=""html_476786eee5a005a9731bb507240ce15e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bond Lane, Kingsnorth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roadside ditch to the rear of 3 Bond Lane was filled with muddy water; evidence of surface water accumulation in the ditch associated with local flooding. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and muddy runoff inundated Bond Lane at the junction with Steeds Lane, covering the road and adjacent verge and reaching the base of the Bond Lane street sign; road surface was waterlogged and posed a hazard to vehicles and pedestrians. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert connecting the ditches on either side of Bond Lane has been assessed and is thought to have collapsed. The report states this is unlikely to have had a significant impact on properties but could cause or exacerbate highway flooding in the area during the investigated events. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert connecting the ditches either side of Bond Lane was assessed and is thought to have collapsed; this collapse could cause or exacerbate highway flooding (though it was assessed as unlikely to have had a significant impact on properties). [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Bond Lane, Kingsnorth",England,"Roadside ditch to the rear of 3 Bond Lane was filled with muddy water; evidence of surface water accumulation in the ditch associated with local flooding. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)
Surface water and muddy runoff inundated Bond Lane at the junction with Steeds Lane, covering the road and adjacent verge and reaching the base of the Bond Lane street sign; road surface was waterlogged and posed a hazard to vehicles and pedestrians. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)
The culvert connecting the ditches on either side of Bond Lane has been assessed and is thought to have collapsed. The report states this is unlikely to have had a significant impact on properties but could cause or exacerbate highway flooding in the area during the investigated events. [MUL] (p6)
The culvert connecting the ditches either side of Bond Lane was assessed and is thought to have collapsed; this collapse could cause or exacerbate highway flooding (though it was assessed as unlikely to have had a significant impact on properties). [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p7)",,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc.html,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc,Kingsnorth flooding 09 Nov 2014 and Kingsnorth flooding 08 Jan 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Steeds Close, Kingsnorth, Ashford",063/Kent/Steeds-Close-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf
GB-KEN,18,2014-11-09,51.1098928,0.8605616,"<div id=""html_59872685b026b9a61248b71ad81645e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Steeds Lane, Kingsnorth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Junction of Steeds Lane and Bond Lane was heavily flooded, with water covering the entire width of the road and extending onto adjacent grassy areas; a culvert beneath Steeds Lane is visible at the site. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at the junction with Bond Lane; muddy water covered the junction and adjacent verges. A culvert beneath Steeds Lane is noted at the junction (visible behind railings) in the imagery associated with the flooded scene. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One half of the twin&ndash;culvert beneath Steeds Lane is believed to be obstructed; this is reported as unlikely to have had a significant impact on properties but could cause or exacerbate highway flooding. Additionally, groundwater emergence to the south of Steeds Lane indicates a high water table in the wider area, which contributed to flood conditions. [IMG,MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One half of the twin culvert beneath Steeds Lane was observed to be partially obstructed; this obstruction could cause or exacerbate highway flooding in the area. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Steeds Lane, Kingsnorth",England,"Junction of Steeds Lane and Bond Lane was heavily flooded, with water covering the entire width of the road and extending onto adjacent grassy areas; a culvert beneath Steeds Lane is visible at the site. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)
Surface water flooding occurred at the junction with Bond Lane; muddy water covered the junction and adjacent verges. A culvert beneath Steeds Lane is noted at the junction (visible behind railings) in the imagery associated with the flooded scene. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)
One half of the twin–culvert beneath Steeds Lane is believed to be obstructed; this is reported as unlikely to have had a significant impact on properties but could cause or exacerbate highway flooding. Additionally, groundwater emergence to the south of Steeds Lane indicates a high water table in the wider area, which contributed to flood conditions. [IMG,MUL] (p6)
One half of the twin culvert beneath Steeds Lane was observed to be partially obstructed; this obstruction could cause or exacerbate highway flooding in the area. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p7)",,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc.html,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc,Kingsnorth flooding 09 Nov 2014 and Kingsnorth flooding 08 Jan 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Steeds Close, Kingsnorth, Ashford",063/Kent/Steeds-Close-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf
GB-KEN,18,2014-11-09,51.1250463,0.8620645,"<div id=""html_76270f74ef761f41ac7ae5826188e373"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ashford Road, Kingsnorth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert beneath Ashford Road was observed to be partially obstructed by silt and a nearby chamber (where the watercourse changes direction into the culvert) was also partially obstructed. These obstructions reduced free flow away from the lower&ndash;lying land and were identified as contributory factors to the surface water flooding that affected the area in the two investigated events. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert beneath Ashford Road at the western end of the watercourse and the adjacent chamber (where the watercourse changes direction into the culvert) were observed to be partially obstructed by silt, reducing free flow and contributing to the watercourse becoming overwhelmed and flooding nearby low&ndash;lying land. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Ashford Road, Kingsnorth",England,"The culvert beneath Ashford Road was observed to be partially obstructed by silt and a nearby chamber (where the watercourse changes direction into the culvert) was also partially obstructed. These obstructions reduced free flow away from the lower–lying land and were identified as contributory factors to the surface water flooding that affected the area in the two investigated events. [MUL] (p6)
The culvert beneath Ashford Road at the western end of the watercourse and the adjacent chamber (where the watercourse changes direction into the culvert) were observed to be partially obstructed by silt, reducing free flow and contributing to the watercourse becoming overwhelmed and flooding nearby low–lying land. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p7)",,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc.html,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc,Kingsnorth flooding 09 Nov 2014 and Kingsnorth flooding 08 Jan 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Steeds Close, Kingsnorth, Ashford",063/Kent/Steeds-Close-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf
GB-KEN,18,2014-11-09,51.119331372509066,0.8585780032486546,"<div id=""html_554c4c74eceefc501f1fc4604a91e60d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kingsnorth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Steeds Close in Kingsnorth led to internal inundation of two properties and flooding of gardens. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in Kingsnorth at Steeds Close where two properties and gardens were inundated during the heavy&ndash;rainfall events; photographic evidence and media reports document the January event. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Kingsnorth,England,"Flooding at Steeds Close in Kingsnorth led to internal inundation of two properties and flooding of gardens. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)
Flooding occurred in Kingsnorth at Steeds Close where two properties and gardens were inundated during the heavy–rainfall events; photographic evidence and media reports document the January event. [MUL] (p2)","2 properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)
2 properties [MUL] (p2)",GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc.html,GB-KEN_18_2014-11-09_etc,Kingsnorth flooding 09 Nov 2014 and Kingsnorth flooding 08 Jan 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Steeds Close, Kingsnorth, Ashford",063/Kent/Steeds-Close-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4141772,-1.9028817,"<div id=""html_d29af18260e88479e37236559fa8e7c6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brandwood Cemetery</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from Brandwood Cemetery overwhelmed cemetery drainage and flowed into the rear of nearby properties, contributing to internal property flooding and garden flooding in the area. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the cemetery overwhelmed cemetery drainage and flowed into the rear of nearby properties following the natural topography. Post&ndash;event site walkover found some cemetery drainage to be blocked. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from the cemetery drainage was overwhelmed by the storm and flowed out of the cemetery onto Broad Lane, contributing to overland flow down the local road network. (p101)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Brandwood Cemetery,England,"Surface water runoff from Brandwood Cemetery overwhelmed cemetery drainage and flowed into the rear of nearby properties, contributing to internal property flooding and garden flooding in the area. [PAG] (p40)
Surface water runoff from the cemetery overwhelmed cemetery drainage and flowed into the rear of nearby properties following the natural topography. Post–event site walkover found some cemetery drainage to be blocked. (p41)
Runoff from the cemetery drainage was overwhelmed by the storm and flowed out of the cemetery onto Broad Lane, contributing to overland flow down the local road network. (p101)","5 properties [PAG] (p40)
3 properties (p41)",GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.41994789653116,-1.9096763082686525,"<div id=""html_e11784e8ec7d0206b544d9972723a15d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dawberry Fields</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse capacity was exceeded during the storm, the channel overtopped and flooded across surrounding land, sending flow into the rear of properties on Brandwood Park Road; blockage of a trash screen may have exacerbated the overtopping. (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Dawberry Fields,England,"The watercourse capacity was exceeded during the storm, the channel overtopped and flooded across surrounding land, sending flow into the rear of properties on Brandwood Park Road; blockage of a trash screen may have exacerbated the overtopping. (p43)",,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4511425,-1.9545301,"<div id=""html_402a38152d9558bf3f896002222cab5d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grove Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from Grove Park overtopped a flood bund and flowed into lower&ndash;lying residential streets, contributing to internal property flooding and flooding of gardens, footpaths, public open space and the highway. [IMG,PAG] (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm runoff from Grove Park overtopped a flood bund and directed floodwater towards lower&ndash;lying properties, initiating flooding that moved into Mill Farm Close. (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties [IMG,PAG] (p68)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Grove Park,England,"Runoff from Grove Park overtopped a flood bund and flowed into lower–lying residential streets, contributing to internal property flooding and flooding of gardens, footpaths, public open space and the highway. [IMG,PAG] (p68)
Storm runoff from Grove Park overtopped a flood bund and directed floodwater towards lower–lying properties, initiating flooding that moved into Mill Farm Close. (p69)","8 properties [IMG,PAG] (p68)",GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.557941,-1.8965023,"<div id=""html_0b844d652139c4b078d4fd6c3c5b7f7c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sundridge Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from the adjacent school car park exceeded the capacity of local drainage and, following natural topography, flooded into lower&ndash;lying property. (p85)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Sundridge Primary School,England,"Runoff from the adjacent school car park exceeded the capacity of local drainage and, following natural topography, flooded into lower–lying property. (p85)",,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4440464,-1.9204327,"<div id=""html_7e289673d7778e0306c507994738ba57"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Selly Park South</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff during the storm caused flooding in the Selly Park / Selly Park South area, including at least one internal property on Fashoda Road and flooding of nearby gardens and highway. (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Selly Park South,England,"Surface water runoff during the storm caused flooding in the Selly Park / Selly Park South area, including at least one internal property on Fashoda Road and flooding of nearby gardens and highway. (p34)",1 property (p34),GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.419357,-1.8982536,"<div id=""html_b10e98070b24e42e9f8e016ef535b4bb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brandwood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Three incidents of internal property flooding occurred on Brandwood Park Road within the Brandwood area; gardens and the highway were also flooded. Flooding was driven by overtopping of the Dawberry Fields watercourse (river overflow) combined with surface water runoff and highway drainage/sewer&ndash;related flows. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water runoff travelling along the highway network ponded in a local low spot causing flooding in the Brandwood/Kings Heath area; properties and gardens were impacted and highways flooded as runoff followed natural topography and highway drainage routes. (p46)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (combined across Newick Grove and Bryndale Avenue) (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Brandwood,England,"Three incidents of internal property flooding occurred on Brandwood Park Road within the Brandwood area; gardens and the highway were also flooded. Flooding was driven by overtopping of the Dawberry Fields watercourse (river overflow) combined with surface water runoff and highway drainage/sewer–related flows. (p42)
Surface–water runoff travelling along the highway network ponded in a local low spot causing flooding in the Brandwood/Kings Heath area; properties and gardens were impacted and highways flooded as runoff followed natural topography and highway drainage routes. (p46)","3 properties (p42)
2 properties (combined across Newick Grove and Bryndale Avenue) (p46)",GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4816814,-1.9482807,"<div id=""html_a2b0f6ef31dd8ec40e3700b3a99f982c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Edgbaston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The North Edgbaston area (specifically Wadhurst Road) was affected by surface water flooding on 27 May 2018: 11 internal property flood incidents occurred, and highway and garden flooding were reported. The event was driven by runoff from an adjacent field under development into sports pitches which followed topography and routed water toward the rear of the properties. (p82)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 incidents of internal property flooding (p82)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,North Edgbaston,England,"The North Edgbaston area (specifically Wadhurst Road) was affected by surface water flooding on 27 May 2018: 11 internal property flood incidents occurred, and highway and garden flooding were reported. The event was driven by runoff from an adjacent field under development into sports pitches which followed topography and routed water toward the rear of the properties. (p82)",11 incidents of internal property flooding (p82),GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.5595582,-1.863424,"<div id=""html_65172218039cde6a022d75e10336dc07"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sutton Vesey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the storm on 27th May 2018 five incidents of internal property flooding were reported within the Sutton Vesey area (specifically Boldmere Road and Wakefield Close). Two of the affected properties were commercial. Flooding of the highway was also reported. Runoff from Boldmere Road, Gate Lane, Wakefield Road and the surrounding catchment overwhelmed the surface water sewers and produced the flow route shown in the report map. (p98)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (2 commercial) (p98)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Sutton Vesey,England,"During the storm on 27th May 2018 five incidents of internal property flooding were reported within the Sutton Vesey area (specifically Boldmere Road and Wakefield Close). Two of the affected properties were commercial. Flooding of the highway was also reported. Runoff from Boldmere Road, Gate Lane, Wakefield Road and the surrounding catchment overwhelmed the surface water sewers and produced the flow route shown in the report map. (p98)",5 properties (2 commercial) (p98),GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4459562,-1.9577666,"<div id=""html_757889c0f7be553473b2864654d0716e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bourn Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the storm on 27 May 2018 the Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and breached, sending floodwater across Pershore Road and along road corridors into adjacent residential areas. This caused internal flooding to properties, flooding of gardens and inundation of the highway; flood flows followed mapped routes from the breach into side streets. (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Bourn Brook,England,"During the storm on 27 May 2018 the Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and breached, sending floodwater across Pershore Road and along road corridors into adjacent residential areas. This caused internal flooding to properties, flooding of gardens and inundation of the highway; flood flows followed mapped routes from the breach into side streets. (p32)",,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.5589793,-1.8356318,"<div id=""html_9a51e7c1e5d54022e8f4a21d7c521577"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wyndley Pool</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A breach at Wyndley Pool initiated a flood flow route; floodwater moved from the breach along roads (notably toward Wyndley Lane), contributing to flooding of downstream properties and infrastructure. [IMG] (p96)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Wyndley Pool,England,"A breach at Wyndley Pool initiated a flood flow route; floodwater moved from the breach along roads (notably toward Wyndley Lane), contributing to flooding of downstream properties and infrastructure. [IMG] (p96)",,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.5187481,-1.8199825,"<div id=""html_d35763f0f4c2fd8459687fb2c7437898"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Erdington Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the storm the channel capacity was exceeded and Erdington Brook overtopped its banks; the overtopping flow flooded adjacent properties from the rear at Quinton (Amersham Close). (p87)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p87)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Erdington Brook,England,During the storm the channel capacity was exceeded and Erdington Brook overtopped its banks; the overtopping flow flooded adjacent properties from the rear at Quinton (Amersham Close). (p87),2 properties (p87),GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4796992,-1.9026911,"<div id=""html_35e2f4661af5cf095d06eb1b7ce952db"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Birmingham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 27 May 2018 storm produced short, intense, highly localised heavy rainfall that led to numerous reported flooding incidents across the city, including internal property flooding, garden flooding and highway flooding. Birmingham City Council received 448 resident flood survey responses and investigations were carried out in 41 areas to determine causes and propose actions. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding of highways, open spaces and properties across Birmingham. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the city following the storm on 27 May 2018: waterlogged gardens, impassable roads, water outflowing from highway gullies, rivers breaching banks, manholes and sewers surcharging and internal property flooding. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two distinct zones within the city were affected by flooding on 27 May 2018: a larger south&ndash;west area (including Northfield, Selly Oak and Edgbaston) and a smaller north&ndash;east area (parts of Sutton Coldfield). Flooding followed exceptional, highly localised rainfall (up to 90 mm recorded in the south of the city). [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from the storm on 27th May 2018 followed local topography and the highway network, overtopping kerbs and ponding outside properties; water built up and flowed towards properties lower than road level, flooding properties on Hubert Road in Bournbrook and Selly Park. [TAB] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from the surrounding catchment followed local topography across the area, producing surface&ndash;water/urban flooding that flowed through Saxelby Close, along Manningford Road and into Bicknell Croft, affecting properties and the highway network. [TAB] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the storm on 27 May 2018, runoff from surrounding public open space followed the natural topography toward the rear of properties; existing drainage adjacent to properties was overwhelmed, water levels rose and entered properties from the rear. In the Druids Heath and Monyhull &ndash; Sherston Covert area this resulted in internal flooding of properties. [TAB] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in Harts Green Brook and Bourn Brook exceeded channel capacity and overtopped into adjacent property. Flooding was exacerbated by blockages of Harts Green Brook from debris and fallen trees and by silt restrictions in Bourn Brook. Local works were carried out (site walkover, removal of blockages) and a community event held. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>448 responses; 180 incidents of internal flooding; 41 areas [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [TAB] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties [TAB] (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Birmingham,England,"The 27 May 2018 storm produced short, intense, highly localised heavy rainfall that led to numerous reported flooding incidents across the city, including internal property flooding, garden flooding and highway flooding. Birmingham City Council received 448 resident flood survey responses and investigations were carried out in 41 areas to determine causes and propose actions. [PAG] (p5)
Widespread flooding of highways, open spaces and properties across Birmingham. (p6)
Widespread flooding across the city following the storm on 27 May 2018: waterlogged gardens, impassable roads, water outflowing from highway gullies, rivers breaching banks, manholes and sewers surcharging and internal property flooding. (p7)
Two distinct zones within the city were affected by flooding on 27 May 2018: a larger south–west area (including Northfield, Selly Oak and Edgbaston) and a smaller north–east area (parts of Sutton Coldfield). Flooding followed exceptional, highly localised rainfall (up to 90 mm recorded in the south of the city). [IMG,PAG] (p8)
Runoff from the storm on 27th May 2018 followed local topography and the highway network, overtopping kerbs and ponding outside properties; water built up and flowed towards properties lower than road level, flooding properties on Hubert Road in Bournbrook and Selly Park. [TAB] (p27)
Runoff from the surrounding catchment followed local topography across the area, producing surface–water/urban flooding that flowed through Saxelby Close, along Manningford Road and into Bicknell Croft, affecting properties and the highway network. [TAB] (p51)
During the storm on 27 May 2018, runoff from surrounding public open space followed the natural topography toward the rear of properties; existing drainage adjacent to properties was overwhelmed, water levels rose and entered properties from the rear. In the Druids Heath and Monyhull – Sherston Covert area this resulted in internal flooding of properties. [TAB] (p55)
Flows in Harts Green Brook and Bourn Brook exceeded channel capacity and overtopped into adjacent property. Flooding was exacerbated by blockages of Harts Green Brook from debris and fallen trees and by silt restrictions in Bourn Brook. Local works were carried out (site walkover, removal of blockages) and a community event held. [TAB] (p65)","448 responses; 180 incidents of internal flooding; 41 areas [PAG] (p5)
1 property [TAB] (p27)
2 properties [TAB] (p55)
4 properties [TAB] (p65)",GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.42190057460374,-1.9254579664685425,"<div id=""html_520d216d6149bf31099766980c8c6fc8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cotteridge Brook at Stirchley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Elevated water level in the brook prevented the surface water sewer and gullies from discharging into it, contributing to highway overtopping and property flooding to the rear of properties. (p93)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,Cotteridge Brook at Stirchley,England,"Elevated water level in the brook prevented the surface water sewer and gullies from discharging into it, contributing to highway overtopping and property flooding to the rear of properties. (p93)",,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.420124616999644,-1.856655520987268,"<div id=""html_ef769793d69e55afd0b463123c386c0f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Cole at Yardley Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels in the River Cole prevented runoff from dispersing via highway gullies and contributed to ponding at Brookwood Close; blockages/debris in the channel may have increased river levels and exacerbated flooding; blockages were later removed by Birmingham City Council. (p63)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,River Cole at Yardley Wood,England,High river levels in the River Cole prevented runoff from dispersing via highway gullies and contributed to ponding at Brookwood Close; blockages/debris in the channel may have increased river levels and exacerbated flooding; blockages were later removed by Birmingham City Council. (p63),,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4315269,-1.9170747,"<div id=""html_b3b1a5c6f1b6b39a7253f5f4abdf7ad8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Rea</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Received flood water when Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and breached; wall removal at the end of Sir Johns Road and dropped kerbs allowed flood water into the River Rea. Floodwaters also flowed across Pershore Road into the front of properties on Riverside Drive and surface water runoff was routed into nearby streets (St John's Road, Fourth Avenue, Third Avenue). (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,River Rea,England,"Received flood water when Bourn Brook was overwhelmed and breached; wall removal at the end of Sir Johns Road and dropped kerbs allowed flood water into the River Rea. Floodwaters also flowed across Pershore Road into the front of properties on Riverside Drive and surface water runoff was routed into nearby streets (St John's Road, Fourth Avenue, Third Avenue). (p33)",1 property (p33),GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4681051,-1.8289785,"<div id=""html_244ab5aaddc92739d728808590593802"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Cole</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flow exceeded channel capacity, overtopped the channel and flooded across surrounding land and highways into properties (floodwaters routed from the River Cole into the local area, including Stirchley &ndash; Dell Road). (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm flows exceeded the channel capacity; flow overtopped the River Cole, flooding across surrounding land into properties. Flood levels were worsened because part of the floodplain was occupied by artificially raised ground composed of urban debris, knitted vegetation and earth. (p105)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p91)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,River Cole,England,"River flow exceeded channel capacity, overtopped the channel and flooded across surrounding land and highways into properties (floodwaters routed from the River Cole into the local area, including Stirchley – Dell Road). (p91)
Storm flows exceeded the channel capacity; flow overtopped the River Cole, flooding across surrounding land into properties. Flood levels were worsened because part of the floodplain was occupied by artificially raised ground composed of urban debris, knitted vegetation and earth. (p105)",1 property (p91),GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
GB-BIR,2,2018-05-27,52.4315494,-1.8258531,"<div id=""html_04f71d26cef5099553e2d77a47874807"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Broom Hall Crescent, Birmingham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property experienced internal flooding; highways and gardens in the area were also flooded as runoff ponded after following the highway network. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from the storm on 27th May 2018 followed the highway network and ponded in the low spot in Broom Hall Crescent. Water levels built up because it could not discharge into the drainage system quickly enough; bow waves from passing vehicles caused water to overtop kerbs and flood into properties that lie lower than the road level. (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Birmingham,"Broom Hall Crescent, Birmingham",England,"One property experienced internal flooding; highways and gardens in the area were also flooded as runoff ponded after following the highway network. (p24)
Runoff from the storm on 27th May 2018 followed the highway network and ponded in the low spot in Broom Hall Crescent. Water levels built up because it could not discharge into the drainage system quickly enough; bow waves from passing vehicles caused water to overtop kerbs and flood into properties that lie lower than the road level. (p25)",1 property (p24),GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27.html,GB-BIR_2_2018-05-27,Birmingham flooding 27 May 2018,"May 2018 Flooding: Flood and Water Management Act, Section 19 Investigation",007/Birmingham/007/2018/Birmingham/Flooding/Section/19/Investigation///May/2018//2.0/.pdf
