GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.8673441,-3.0202744,"<div id=""html_3c7eee3716f931d0bb0922131454a3cf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Green Oak Place, Thornton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Greenoak Place during the intense rainfall flood event impacting Blackpool on 22 November 2017; part of the wider event affecting the area. [TAB] (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300 properties [TAB] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,"Green Oak Place, Thornton",England,Flooding occurred at Greenoak Place during the intense rainfall flood event impacting Blackpool on 22 November 2017; part of the wider event affecting the area. [TAB] (p3),300 properties [TAB] (p3),GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.8625903,-3.025887,"<div id=""html_76d9c6a8695f51235097d988e77f2cd1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wroxton Close, Bispham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Wroxton Close during the intense rainfall flood event impacting Blackpool on 22 November 2017; part of the wider event affecting the area. [TAB] (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300 properties [TAB] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,"Wroxton Close, Bispham",England,Flooding occurred at Wroxton Close during the intense rainfall flood event impacting Blackpool on 22 November 2017; part of the wider event affecting the area. [TAB] (p3),300 properties [TAB] (p3),GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.8080686,-3.0555857,"<div id=""html_541c19d9804bd664fee7985e3fce213a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Manchester Square, Blackpool</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location where the Fylde Tunnel system starts; the tunnel collection/storage serving Manchester Square was at or near full capacity as part of the system response to the heavy rainfall. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced spills/pumping activity during the storm; pumped intermittently to sea as the system was full in order to manage storm&ndash;water. [TAB,MUL] (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17.25 21 Nov &ndash; 16.00 22 Nov (intermittent); 600 minutes of 1&ndash;3 pumps operating [TAB,MUL] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,"Manchester Square, Blackpool",England,"Location where the Fylde Tunnel system starts; the tunnel collection/storage serving Manchester Square was at or near full capacity as part of the system response to the heavy rainfall. (p5)
Experienced spills/pumping activity during the storm; pumped intermittently to sea as the system was full in order to manage storm–water. [TAB,MUL] (p6)","17.25 21 Nov – 16.00 22 Nov (intermittent); 600 minutes of 1–3 pumps operating [TAB,MUL] (p6)",GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.867293,-3.0346897,"<div id=""html_3a8ceddb99d6b89752e4983552274ca0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Drive, Anchorsholme</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in the East Anchorsholme area that was impassable on 22nd November 2017 due to flooding across the highway; some areas also reported loss of electricity. (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 500mm depth across the highway (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,"North Drive, Anchorsholme",England,Included in the East Anchorsholme area that was impassable on 22nd November 2017 due to flooding across the highway; some areas also reported loss of electricity. (p7),approx. 500mm depth across the highway (p7),GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.86117,-2.5650887,"<div id=""html_68c8a6454baba082fff7afaf2f653239"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancashire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface&ndash;water flooding reported across the Blackpool and Lancashire area on 22 November 2017, affecting multiple streets and local communities. (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 300 properties (Blackpool and Lancashire) (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,Lancashire,England,"Widespread surface–water flooding reported across the Blackpool and Lancashire area on 22 November 2017, affecting multiple streets and local communities. (p3)",approximately 300 properties (Blackpool and Lancashire) (p3),GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.8696556,-3.0321975,"<div id=""html_cfd1a2a1f86d567cd592cd0c0148f13e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Anchorsholme</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Anchorsholme area caused a number of power failures. [TAB] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Anchorsholme Academy was reported to have been subject to flooding on 22nd November 2017. The Anchorsholme area was affected by flooding when highway drainage and the sewer network were overwhelmed; United Utilities recorded stormwater systems (tanks and the Fylde Tunnel sewer) already at full capacity before the storm. [IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Anchorsholme Pumping Station pumps were called into duty sequentially as tunnel levels rose; the station reached capacity during the extreme rainfall on the night of 22nd November 2017 and spill data for the week was produced. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property&ndash;level flooding was reported in Anchorsholme following the event; council officers visited affected properties after the 22 November 2017 flood. [TAB,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewage system was full to capacity and ground conditions saturated; sewer surcharge and surface water led to property flooding and highway inundation. East Anchorsholme (area bounded by Warren Drive, Sevenoaks Drive, Wood Green Drive, North Drive and Snowhills Crescent) was impassable with water approximately 500 mm deep across the highway. Some areas reported loss of electricity. Water receded for affected properties on the morning of 23rd November 2017. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The performance of the Anchorsholme pumping station was questioned as a possible reason for flooding at Mossom Lane; United Utilities reported the pumping station was operational and pumps were operating intermittently on the evening of 22nd November. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 pumps at Anchorsholme Pumping Station (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>500 mm highway depth in East Anchorsholme (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,Anchorsholme,England,"Flooding in the Anchorsholme area caused a number of power failures. [TAB] (p3)
Anchorsholme Academy was reported to have been subject to flooding on 22nd November 2017. The Anchorsholme area was affected by flooding when highway drainage and the sewer network were overwhelmed; United Utilities recorded stormwater systems (tanks and the Fylde Tunnel sewer) already at full capacity before the storm. [IMG] (p4)
Anchorsholme Pumping Station pumps were called into duty sequentially as tunnel levels rose; the station reached capacity during the extreme rainfall on the night of 22nd November 2017 and spill data for the week was produced. (p5)
Property–level flooding was reported in Anchorsholme following the event; council officers visited affected properties after the 22 November 2017 flood. [TAB,PAG] (p6)
Sewage system was full to capacity and ground conditions saturated; sewer surcharge and surface water led to property flooding and highway inundation. East Anchorsholme (area bounded by Warren Drive, Sevenoaks Drive, Wood Green Drive, North Drive and Snowhills Crescent) was impassable with water approximately 500 mm deep across the highway. Some areas reported loss of electricity. Water receded for affected properties on the morning of 23rd November 2017. (p7)
The performance of the Anchorsholme pumping station was questioned as a possible reason for flooding at Mossom Lane; United Utilities reported the pumping station was operational and pumps were operating intermittently on the evening of 22nd November. (p8)","5 pumps at Anchorsholme Pumping Station (p5)
500 mm highway depth in East Anchorsholme (p7)",GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.851856,-3.039379,"<div id=""html_d8fe87e10579cc3dd75993b7ce1312eb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bispham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Plan A (Bispham) states the exceptional storm on 22 Nov 2017 overwhelmed highway drainage and sewers in the Bispham area, causing flooding during the event. [IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property&ndash;level flooding was reported in the Norbreck and Bispham areas; council officers visited affected properties following the 22 November 2017 event. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Council officers attended but were unable to alleviate water; ground conditions were saturated and sewage systems were full to capacity during the event, contributing to flooding. (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,Bispham,England,"Plan A (Bispham) states the exceptional storm on 22 Nov 2017 overwhelmed highway drainage and sewers in the Bispham area, causing flooding during the event. [IMG] (p4)
Property–level flooding was reported in the Norbreck and Bispham areas; council officers visited affected properties following the 22 November 2017 event. (p6)
Council officers attended but were unable to alleviate water; ground conditions were saturated and sewage systems were full to capacity during the event, contributing to flooding. (p7)",,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.9224847,-3.0329340355325565,"<div id=""html_14644bb9eeb651577b6eb0ce5e21b9e3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fleetwood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fylde Tunnel discharges at Fleetwood wastewater treatment works (WwTW); the WwTW and associated tunnel/storage were at full capacity during the rainfall event and the rain gauge at Fleetwood WwTW recorded the heavy rainfall. (p5)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>200 million litres storage capacity (system total) (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,Fleetwood,England,Fylde Tunnel discharges at Fleetwood wastewater treatment works (WwTW); the WwTW and associated tunnel/storage were at full capacity during the rainfall event and the rain gauge at Fleetwood WwTW recorded the heavy rainfall. (p5),200 million litres storage capacity (system total) (p5),GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.8578946,-3.0493697,"<div id=""html_ec94e45913b40ad6fb98b325b2ae2b55"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Norbreck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property&ndash;level flooding was reported in the Norbreck and Bispham areas; council officers visited affected properties following the 22 November 2017 event. (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,Norbreck,England,Property–level flooding was reported in the Norbreck and Bispham areas; council officers visited affected properties following the 22 November 2017 event. (p6),,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BPL,1,2017-11-22,53.868415248032555,-3.0246776679351663,"<div id=""html_84b97c5b22d7bc22104cee3e01b7c1ac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Anchorsholme</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Largest area recorded with flooding; area bounded by Warren Drive, Sevenoaks Drive, Wood Green Drive, North Drive and Snowhills Crescent was impassable due to water across the highway; some areas reported loss of electricity. Sewage systems were full to capacity in the Anchorsholme catchment and sewer surcharge contributed to flooding. (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 500mm deep across the highway (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Blackpool,East Anchorsholme,England,"Largest area recorded with flooding; area bounded by Warren Drive, Sevenoaks Drive, Wood Green Drive, North Drive and Snowhills Crescent was impassable due to water across the highway; some areas reported loss of electricity. Sewage systems were full to capacity in the Anchorsholme catchment and sewer surcharge contributed to flooding. (p7)",approximately 500mm deep across the highway (p7),GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22.html,GB-BPL_1_2017-11-22,Blackpool flooding 22 Nov 2017,Section 19 Report: Investigation into flooding on 22nd November 2017,009/Blackpool/009/2017/Blackpool/Blackpool November 2017 FINAL.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.596420489415756,-0.9293689718400323,"<div id=""html_73dbb029b546fa16587fa095fc91f31a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stonor Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The observation borehole at Stonor Park recorded unprecedentedly high groundwater levels in the Chalk aquifer. Levels started rising in December 2013, had reached new highs by the New Year and continued to rise through January, peaking in early 2014; this represented exceptional groundwater rise feeding southwards to the River Thames and River Wye. [PAG,IMG] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Stonor Park,England,"The observation borehole at Stonor Park recorded unprecedentedly high groundwater levels in the Chalk aquifer. Levels started rising in December 2013, had reached new highs by the New Year and continued to rise through January, peaking in early 2014; this represented exceptional groundwater rise feeding southwards to the River Thames and River Wye. [PAG,IMG] (p19)",,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.61726984402831,-0.7321594820691124,"<div id=""html_8a57057f145728f81c8de56a2a53059e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wycombe District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wycombe District SFRA records multiple fluvial and drainage&ndash;related flooding incidents affecting areas within the district (including Bourne End). The winter December 2013&ndash;February 2014 was unusually wet (notably 23 Dec 2013 and 6 Feb 2014), contributing to flooding in Bourne End and the district during the January&ndash;February 2014 event. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Wycombe District,England,"Wycombe District SFRA records multiple fluvial and drainage–related flooding incidents affecting areas within the district (including Bourne End). The winter December 2013–February 2014 was unusually wet (notably 23 Dec 2013 and 6 Feb 2014), contributing to flooding in Bourne End and the district during the January–February 2014 event. [TAB,PAG] (p17)",,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.61063486564572,-0.5955778826581319,"<div id=""html_a5c8db2a7a1c2e51ec6cd63e6b65d965"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thames Valley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Declared the Thames&ndash;wide flooding as a major incident and convened Strategic Coordination Group meetings twice daily to coordinate partner agencies and support response efforts; the group liaised with Government and the Ministry of Defence during the incident. (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thames Valley,England,Declared the Thames–wide flooding as a major incident and convened Strategic Coordination Group meetings twice daily to coordinate partner agencies and support response efforts; the group liaised with Government and the Ministry of Defence during the incident. (p25),,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.5623537,-0.7059295,"<div id=""html_ed259946f5b2680b540ab94b27db3711"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cookham Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Access to Cookham Bridge was inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the winter floods. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ferry Lane (A4094) flooded and prevented access to Cookham Bridge; road closures affected access routes. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Cookham Bridge,England,"Access to Cookham Bridge was inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the winter floods. (p23)
Ferry Lane (A4094) flooded and prevented access to Cookham Bridge; road closures affected access routes. [TAB,MUL] (p26)",,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.5707035,-0.7079005,"<div id=""html_77b80e02353326c826b44d8ded60f88b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hedsor Road, Bourne End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the winter floods. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater affected homes along Hedsor Road (Riversdale); an officer attended to assess the situation and provide advice to residents. [TAB] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Hedsor Road, Bourne End",England,"Inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the winter floods. (p23)
Floodwater affected homes along Hedsor Road (Riversdale); an officer attended to assess the situation and provide advice to residents. [TAB] (p26)",,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.5663068,-0.7098666,"<div id=""html_55d1cf74e35a33c42b1c2f3341923624"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Riversdale, Bourne End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding reported along A4094 Ferry Lane caused by heavy persistent rainfall, inadequate drains and low freeboard of the River Thames; many houses around Riversdale and Ferry Lane are built on stilts as a resilience measure against repeated flooding, reducing the number of reported property flood incidents. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties were inundated by River Thames flood waters; some properties are elevated on stilts and had individual flood barriers/other measures. Residents used two aluminium boats to move from their properties to higher ground via the private access road. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Riversdale and the adjacent Ferry Lane were inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the January&ndash;February 2014 floods. Properties in the vicinity were affected by rising river water; residents used two aluminium boats to move to higher ground and some properties in the area are elevated on stilts and have flood barriers. Local records indicate internal flooding of properties in the Riversdale area. [IMG,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water was reported at property thresholds in Abbotsbrook (inspection on 6 January); Riversdale private road flooded on 8 January (initially no properties reported flooded); by 14 February about 20 properties were reported flooded, mostly centred around Abbotsbrook and Riversdale. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [IMG,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 20 properties flooded (Abbotsbrook and Riversdale) [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Riversdale, Bourne End",England,"Flooding reported along A4094 Ferry Lane caused by heavy persistent rainfall, inadequate drains and low freeboard of the River Thames; many houses around Riversdale and Ferry Lane are built on stilts as a resilience measure against repeated flooding, reducing the number of reported property flood incidents. (p17)
Properties were inundated by River Thames flood waters; some properties are elevated on stilts and had individual flood barriers/other measures. Residents used two aluminium boats to move from their properties to higher ground via the private access road. (p23)
Riversdale and the adjacent Ferry Lane were inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the January–February 2014 floods. Properties in the vicinity were affected by rising river water; residents used two aluminium boats to move to higher ground and some properties in the area are elevated on stilts and have flood barriers. Local records indicate internal flooding of properties in the Riversdale area. [IMG,PAG] (p24)
Water was reported at property thresholds in Abbotsbrook (inspection on 6 January); Riversdale private road flooded on 8 January (initially no properties reported flooded); by 14 February about 20 properties were reported flooded, mostly centred around Abbotsbrook and Riversdale. [TAB,MUL] (p26)","2 properties [IMG,PAG] (p24)
approximately 20 properties flooded (Abbotsbrook and Riversdale) [TAB,MUL] (p26)",GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.5793415,-0.7241472,"<div id=""html_138d8146703cb3d2e6de594a4aadc22e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Upper Thames Way, Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the January&ndash;February 2014 winter event. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Upper Thames Way, Buckinghamshire",England,Inundated by flood waters from the River Thames during the January–February 2014 winter event. (p23),,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.579679,-0.7168799,"<div id=""html_d95632381337dd66b0656be6c28d9da7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Drive, Little Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying land along The Drive was inundated by Abbots Brook; only gardens were reported flooded (no reports of internal property flooding). [PAG] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"The Drive, Little Marlow",England,Low–lying land along The Drive was inundated by Abbots Brook; only gardens were reported flooded (no reports of internal property flooding). [PAG] (p24),,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.58074006960954,-0.7064994356552246,"<div id=""html_3f6621ad363870eb41f76534e20a8a98"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>New Road, Bourne End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cores End Road was closed due to flooding and 100 sandbags were requested for deployment at the junction between New Road and Highfield Road to protect the area. [TAB] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"New Road, Bourne End",England,Cores End Road was closed due to flooding and 100 sandbags were requested for deployment at the junction between New Road and Highfield Road to protect the area. [TAB] (p26),,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.5798089,-0.7085159,"<div id=""html_e773fec70de70b1472a3c96816714fb3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Highfield Road, Bourne End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cores End Road was closed due to flooding and 100 sandbags were requested for deployment at the junction between New Road and Highfield Road to protect the area. [TAB] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Highfield Road, Bourne End",England,Cores End Road was closed due to flooding and 100 sandbags were requested for deployment at the junction between New Road and Highfield Road to protect the area. [TAB] (p26),,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_0558c8288db2f106f4fb17e7cce5e274"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Buckinghamshire (notably the Bourne End area) were shown to be vulnerable to fluvial flooding from the River Thames and River Wye and to surface water flooding along transport routes where drainage could not cope. Groundwater maps indicate large areas (including Bourne End) would have groundwater at or within 2 m of the surface in a winter similar to 2000/01. [IMG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Buckinghamshire (the Bourne End study area) experienced river flooding during January&ndash;February 2014 when the River Thames peaked (around 10 January and again around 10 February), causing properties to flood and roads to remain impassable until 21 February 2014. [PAG,IMG] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 20 properties inundated; 2 properties flooded internally [PAG,IMG] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"Parts of Buckinghamshire (notably the Bourne End area) were shown to be vulnerable to fluvial flooding from the River Thames and River Wye and to surface water flooding along transport routes where drainage could not cope. Groundwater maps indicate large areas (including Bourne End) would have groundwater at or within 2 m of the surface in a winter similar to 2000/01. [IMG] (p14)
Parts of Buckinghamshire (the Bourne End study area) experienced river flooding during January–February 2014 when the River Thames peaked (around 10 January and again around 10 February), causing properties to flood and roads to remain impassable until 21 February 2014. [PAG,IMG] (p24)","approximately 20 properties inundated; 2 properties flooded internally [PAG,IMG] (p24)",GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.560097,-0.7081509,"<div id=""html_6e691c295cf6d6fddd82fddf9f0cba97"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cookham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Thames levels at Cookham rose to a higher peak between 9&ndash;11 February 2014 (following an earlier peak in January), indicating elevated river conditions at Cookham that contributed to constrained discharge of tributaries upstream. [PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Cookham,England,"River Thames levels at Cookham rose to a higher peak between 9–11 February 2014 (following an earlier peak in January), indicating elevated river conditions at Cookham that contributed to constrained discharge of tributaries upstream. [PAG,MUL] (p20)",,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-BKM,4,2014-01-01,51.5808407,-0.718079,"<div id=""html_a711b7c8f492727887991b8147686227"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Abbotsbrook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas centred around Abbotsbrook and Riversdale were inundated during January&ndash;February 2014. Riversdale and nearby roads were inundated by flood waters from the River Thames; emergency works (culvert inspection, blockage removal), sandbagging and pumping ('Supersucker') were used to manage floodwaters. Some properties are elevated on stilts and had flood barriers in place. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced raised water levels with water at property thresholds and was cited as one of the centres of residential flooding during the event. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties flooded internally; approximately 20 properties inundated (Abbotsbrook and Riversdale) [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 20 properties flooded (area centred around Abbotsbrook and Riversdale) [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Abbotsbrook,England,"Areas centred around Abbotsbrook and Riversdale were inundated during January–February 2014. Riversdale and nearby roads were inundated by flood waters from the River Thames; emergency works (culvert inspection, blockage removal), sandbagging and pumping ('Supersucker') were used to manage floodwaters. Some properties are elevated on stilts and had flood barriers in place. [PAG] (p24)
Area experienced raised water levels with water at property thresholds and was cited as one of the centres of residential flooding during the event. [TAB,MUL] (p26)","2 properties flooded internally; approximately 20 properties inundated (Abbotsbrook and Riversdale) [PAG] (p24)
approximately 20 properties flooded (area centred around Abbotsbrook and Riversdale) [TAB,MUL] (p26)",GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_4_2014-01-01,Bourne End flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bourne End, January and February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bourne-end-jan-feb-2014.pdf
GB-WNH,6,2013-08-05,52.56628789856925,-0.2027211766370756,"<div id=""html_8e3d83a0ac803d33b04e36726f0543ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Nene</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Nene sits within Flood Zone 3 adjacent to Bedford Road; the flood hazard associated with the river area encompassed Bedford Road on 5th August 2013, during which the A428 Bedford Road was temporarily closed because of the depth of flood water and manhole covers being lifted. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Elevated water levels in the River Nene in the vicinity of the incident during the 5th August 2013 event may have prevented discharge from drainage systems and contributed to local flooding impacts. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A surface water outfall discharging into the River Nene was heavily clogged with debris (metal grate and organic/plastic material), partially blocking flow. The blockage would have impeded drainage into the Nene and increased the risk of upstream surface&ndash;water flooding during the flood investigation (documented following the 5 August 2013 event). [IMG] (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,River Nene,England,"The River Nene sits within Flood Zone 3 adjacent to Bedford Road; the flood hazard associated with the river area encompassed Bedford Road on 5th August 2013, during which the A428 Bedford Road was temporarily closed because of the depth of flood water and manhole covers being lifted. (p9)
Elevated water levels in the River Nene in the vicinity of the incident during the 5th August 2013 event may have prevented discharge from drainage systems and contributed to local flooding impacts. (p20)
A surface water outfall discharging into the River Nene was heavily clogged with debris (metal grate and organic/plastic material), partially blocking flow. The blockage would have impeded drainage into the Nene and increased the risk of upstream surface–water flooding during the flood investigation (documented following the 5 August 2013 event). [IMG] (p43)",,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05.html,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05,Northampton flooding 05 Aug 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Bedford Road, Northampton, 5th August 2013","090/West Northamptonshire/Bedford Road, Northampton - August 2013.pdf"
GB-WNH,6,2013-08-05,52.2329997,-0.8848447,"<div id=""html_d9de3869480e72112ec542ca79aa3a4a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bedford Road, Northampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration rainfall caused existing drainage to back up and overtop at ground level, resulting in surface water flooding of Bedford Road; the flood affected Bedford Road, a critical arterial route for Northampton. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration rainfall overwhelmed local drainage; drainage systems backed up and overtopped at ground level, causing flooding of the carriageway on Bedford Road, disrupting this critical arterial route and prompting a formal Section 19 investigation. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A428 Bedford Road was temporarily closed to traffic due to the depth of flood water and manhole covers being lifted; the closure affected a critical arterial route connecting Northampton town centre to the A45. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Standing water ponded in the carriageway (reaching a depth higher than the kerb lines so verges and footways were flooded), manhole covers were lifted by excessive volumes in the sewerage system, and the road was closed to through traffic causing delays, congestion and traffic diversions. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bedford Road was flooded by intense short&ndash;duration rainfall; existing drainage systems were unable to cope, became surcharged and ejected water back to the surface, causing surface water to flow into areas and drainage systems that could not manage the volume. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carriageway and road gullies on Bedford Road were liable to overflow during extreme rainfall, contributing to surface water flooding and increasing flood risk to lower&ndash;lying ground and properties; recommendation to allow controlled overflow routes to lower ground (e.g. grip drains, lowered verges). (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the extreme rainfall on 5th August 2013 the carriageway and road gullies on Bedford Road overtopped, causing surface water to flow across the road. Measures were recommended to allow overflow of road gullies to lower ground (e.g. grip drains, lowering verges/footways) to reduce repeat overtopping. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the junction of Bedford Road and Cliftonville Road; Main River Nene overflowed into the adjacent flood channel south of Bedford Road. Flood pressures caused manhole covers to be lifted at the junction. [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the junction of Bedford Road and Cliftonville Road; manhole covers were lifted (surcharging) and the junction was inundated. (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the west end of Bedford Road and at the junction with Cliftonville Road; surface water outfall to the River Nene is located at the west end south of the car park. Manhole covers were lifted, indicating sewer surcharge and local surface&ndash;water inundation. Low spots in the vertical alignment of Bedford Road contributed to ponding. [IMG,PAG] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,"Bedford Road, Northampton",England,"Intense short–duration rainfall caused existing drainage to back up and overtop at ground level, resulting in surface water flooding of Bedford Road; the flood affected Bedford Road, a critical arterial route for Northampton. [PAG] (p6)
Intense short–duration rainfall overwhelmed local drainage; drainage systems backed up and overtopped at ground level, causing flooding of the carriageway on Bedford Road, disrupting this critical arterial route and prompting a formal Section 19 investigation. [PAG] (p7)
The A428 Bedford Road was temporarily closed to traffic due to the depth of flood water and manhole covers being lifted; the closure affected a critical arterial route connecting Northampton town centre to the A45. [PAG] (p9)
Standing water ponded in the carriageway (reaching a depth higher than the kerb lines so verges and footways were flooded), manhole covers were lifted by excessive volumes in the sewerage system, and the road was closed to through traffic causing delays, congestion and traffic diversions. (p12)
Bedford Road was flooded by intense short–duration rainfall; existing drainage systems were unable to cope, became surcharged and ejected water back to the surface, causing surface water to flow into areas and drainage systems that could not manage the volume. (p17)
Carriageway and road gullies on Bedford Road were liable to overflow during extreme rainfall, contributing to surface water flooding and increasing flood risk to lower–lying ground and properties; recommendation to allow controlled overflow routes to lower ground (e.g. grip drains, lowered verges). (p19)
During the extreme rainfall on 5th August 2013 the carriageway and road gullies on Bedford Road overtopped, causing surface water to flow across the road. Measures were recommended to allow overflow of road gullies to lower ground (e.g. grip drains, lowering verges/footways) to reduce repeat overtopping. [PAG] (p20)
Flooding occurred at the junction of Bedford Road and Cliftonville Road; Main River Nene overflowed into the adjacent flood channel south of Bedford Road. Flood pressures caused manhole covers to be lifted at the junction. [IMG,PAG] (p44)
Flooding occurred at the junction of Bedford Road and Cliftonville Road; manhole covers were lifted (surcharging) and the junction was inundated. (p45)
Flooding occurred at the west end of Bedford Road and at the junction with Cliftonville Road; surface water outfall to the River Nene is located at the west end south of the car park. Manhole covers were lifted, indicating sewer surcharge and local surface–water inundation. Low spots in the vertical alignment of Bedford Road contributed to ponding. [IMG,PAG] (p46)",,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05.html,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05,Northampton flooding 05 Aug 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Bedford Road, Northampton, 5th August 2013","090/West Northamptonshire/Bedford Road, Northampton - August 2013.pdf"
GB-WNH,6,2013-08-05,52.2353234,-0.8791172,"<div id=""html_0154e7727c1a88a72602df28196e4144"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cliftonville Road, Northampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water inundation affected Cliftonville Road and the adjacent verge/footpath area; nearby lower&ndash;lying land adjacent to the skate park formed part of the flow pathway toward Bedford Road. [IMG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the junction with Bedford Road with lifted manhole covers reported; photograph views from Cliftonville Road show the area affected by surface&ndash;water/ponding and sewer surcharge at the junction. [PAG] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,"Cliftonville Road, Northampton",England,"Surface water inundation affected Cliftonville Road and the adjacent verge/footpath area; nearby lower–lying land adjacent to the skate park formed part of the flow pathway toward Bedford Road. [IMG] (p45)
Flooding at the junction with Bedford Road with lifted manhole covers reported; photograph views from Cliftonville Road show the area affected by surface–water/ponding and sewer surcharge at the junction. [PAG] (p46)",,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05.html,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05,Northampton flooding 05 Aug 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Bedford Road, Northampton, 5th August 2013","090/West Northamptonshire/Bedford Road, Northampton - August 2013.pdf"
GB-WNH,6,2013-08-05,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_5f56a285c1c2cb830e6fdce2b9206bc4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall overwhelmed existing drainage on Bedford Road, Northampton. Water flowed onto the road and into local drainage systems that were unable to manage the volume, causing flooding of the highway and surrounding drainage assets. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"Intense, short–duration rainfall overwhelmed existing drainage on Bedford Road, Northampton. Water flowed onto the road and into local drainage systems that were unable to manage the volume, causing flooding of the highway and surrounding drainage assets. (p16)",,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05.html,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05,Northampton flooding 05 Aug 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Bedford Road, Northampton, 5th August 2013","090/West Northamptonshire/Bedford Road, Northampton - August 2013.pdf"
GB-WNH,6,2013-08-05,52.2381355,-0.8963907,"<div id=""html_a80796923352fa1e60c0f8d1abcb73b2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from intense rainfall caused drainage systems to back up and overtop at ground level on Bedford Road, which is a critical arterial route for the town. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood incident on Bedford Road within Northampton, caused by intense short&ndash;duration rainfall and overwhelmed drainage, affected a key arterial route in the town and led to a formal investigation. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A primary arterial route within Northampton (Bedford Road) was temporarily closed due to flood water depth and lifted manhole covers, disrupting traffic circulation in the town. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration rainfall across Northampton caused existing drainage systems to be unable to cope; systems were surcharged and ejected water to the surface. High levels in the receiving watercourse may have restricted discharge rates from drainage systems, exacerbating urban/surface water flooding. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",West Northamptonshire,Northampton,England,"Flooding from intense rainfall caused drainage systems to back up and overtop at ground level on Bedford Road, which is a critical arterial route for the town. [PAG] (p6)
The flood incident on Bedford Road within Northampton, caused by intense short–duration rainfall and overwhelmed drainage, affected a key arterial route in the town and led to a formal investigation. [PAG] (p7)
A primary arterial route within Northampton (Bedford Road) was temporarily closed due to flood water depth and lifted manhole covers, disrupting traffic circulation in the town. (p9)
Intense short–duration rainfall across Northampton caused existing drainage systems to be unable to cope; systems were surcharged and ejected water to the surface. High levels in the receiving watercourse may have restricted discharge rates from drainage systems, exacerbating urban/surface water flooding. (p17)",,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05.html,GB-WNH_6_2013-08-05,Northampton flooding 05 Aug 2013,"Flood Investigation Report: Bedford Road, Northampton, 5th August 2013","090/West Northamptonshire/Bedford Road, Northampton - August 2013.pdf"
