GB-CAM,17,2020-12-01,52.5391238,0.085445,"<div id=""html_6759837382fe920febfed02f6a2abf2a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Avenue, March</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External surface&ndash;water flooding reported on the street; no internal flooding of properties was reported (area falls outside remit of internal&ndash;flood investigation). (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"The Avenue, March",England,External surface–water flooding reported on the street; no internal flooding of properties was reported (area falls outside remit of internal–flood investigation). (p13),,GB-CAM_17_2020-12-01.html,GB-CAM_17_2020-12-01,March flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: March,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/March-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,17,2020-12-01,52.5628401,0.0101577,"<div id=""html_f3275aa2687e14fed1039f51773fc304"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fenland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contains the town of March which frequently experiences flooding with considerable internal flooding historically; large areas of March are shown to be at significant risk from surface water, and EA mapping also shows flood risk associated with the old course River Nene affecting the area. [MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Fenland,England,"Contains the town of March which frequently experiences flooding with considerable internal flooding historically; large areas of March are shown to be at significant risk from surface water, and EA mapping also shows flood risk associated with the old course River Nene affecting the area. [MUL] (p4)",,GB-CAM_17_2020-12-01.html,GB-CAM_17_2020-12-01,March flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: March,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/March-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-CAM,17,2020-12-01,52.4617238,0.6975642,"<div id=""html_2790d35410f722a0abb71fa403f3d53e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Anglia</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on an already saturated catchment (notably on 23 December 2020) overwhelmed surface water drainage. Excess surface water flowed over ground into the public sewer network, causing the sewer system to surcharge and flood. Saturated ground and a high water&ndash;table exacerbated runoff and the inability of drainage to convey water. [PAG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,East Anglia,England,"Intense rainfall on an already saturated catchment (notably on 23 December 2020) overwhelmed surface water drainage. Excess surface water flowed over ground into the public sewer network, causing the sewer system to surcharge and flood. Saturated ground and a high water–table exacerbated runoff and the inability of drainage to convey water. [PAG] (p6)",,GB-CAM_17_2020-12-01.html,GB-CAM_17_2020-12-01,March flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: March,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/March-December-2020-Flood-Investigation-Report.pdf
GB-NNH,11,2017-12-27,52.32362159750876,-0.47958390760853803,"<div id=""html_78ec96c8e50603d350b9f8f49eb84792"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hargrave</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incident on 27 December 2017 at the B645 east of Hargrave where surface water drainage systems were unable to convey rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow to low points around private property. Internal flooding of one dwelling has occurred (on more than one occasion within the last five years). (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property located adjacent to the B645 east of Hargrave; internal flooding has been experienced on more than one occasion in the last five years, triggering a formal investigation. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recommendations are made to undertake regular highway drainage cleansing throughout Hargrave to manage highway runoff and reduce flood risk to properties and the highway network. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy sleety snow and rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be unable to collect and convey rainwater. Excess surface water flowed over ground following localised topography to low points around private properties; an affected private property was located in a high risk area for surface water flooding. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one dwelling (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Hargrave,England,"Flood incident on 27 December 2017 at the B645 east of Hargrave where surface water drainage systems were unable to convey rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow to low points around private property. Internal flooding of one dwelling has occurred (on more than one occasion within the last five years). (p7)
Internal flooding of a property located adjacent to the B645 east of Hargrave; internal flooding has been experienced on more than one occasion in the last five years, triggering a formal investigation. [PAG] (p9)
Recommendations are made to undertake regular highway drainage cleansing throughout Hargrave to manage highway runoff and reduce flood risk to properties and the highway network. (p16)
Heavy sleety snow and rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be unable to collect and convey rainwater. Excess surface water flowed over ground following localised topography to low points around private properties; an affected private property was located in a high risk area for surface water flooding. (p18)","one dwelling (p7)
one property [PAG] (p9)",GB-NNH_11_2017-12-27.html,GB-NNH_11_2017-12-27,Northamptonshire flooding 27 Dec 2017,"Flood Investigation Report: B645 East of Hargrave, 2017-12-27",090/North Northamptonshire/28647/FIR-B645-east-of-Hargrave/Rev-04/301018.pdf
GB-NNH,6,2016-03-09,52.4562208,-0.6097755,"<div id=""html_e9cc1b312b09ed4e41c32f45047070e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harper's Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ordinary watercourse flooding from Harpers Brook contributed to the local flood event that affected Lowick, combining with agricultural and surface water runoff to cause internal flooding of properties in the village. (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Over 70 properties flooded internally; over 200 reports of flooding (impacts in Lowick linked to Harpers Brook's flooding) (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Harper's Brook,England,"Ordinary watercourse flooding from Harpers Brook contributed to the local flood event that affected Lowick, combining with agricultural and surface water runoff to cause internal flooding of properties in the village. (p10)",Over 70 properties flooded internally; over 200 reports of flooding (impacts in Lowick linked to Harpers Brook's flooding) (p10),GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09,Lowick flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Main Street, Lowick, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22520/FIR-Lowick/Rev03/200916.pdf
GB-NNH,6,2016-03-09,52.41606807987691,-0.5632303461423048,"<div id=""html_77f54028b13d6f37f79d9121b6deeb58"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Main Street, Lowick</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a residential property occurred at Main Street; this property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the five years prior to the investigation. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main Street lies in the valley and localised catchment of Harpers Brook and was affected by surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse flooding associated with the event; properties in the Main Street area were among those flooded. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water overland flows travelled through the access tunnel beneath the A6116 to a cut&ndash;off ditch behind a stable; the ditch filled and spilled. Internal flooding of one residential property occurred on 09/03/2016 (all ground&ndash;floor rooms flooded) and there was minor ingress of surface water to the rear of a second residential property. Historical incidents include internal flooding of one property (kitchen and another room) at Easter 1998 and internal flooding of one property in late 2012/early 2013 (whole floor replaced). [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down an access road to the rear gardens of two properties on Main Street. A second flow route crossed the rear garden of a third property, submerging the patio and then entering the house, causing internal flooding. The flow through the access tunnel beneath the A6116 was reported as very strong. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be overwhelmed so excess surface water flowed over ground and followed natural contours to low points around private properties on Main Street; properties at a low point in the valley were vulnerable to overland flow, exacerbated by A6116 embankments which channelled a concentrated flow through the access tunnel. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties and recent developments on Main Street experienced surface water flooding and/or had ineffective surface water drainage, prompting a recommendation to review planning policies, flood risk assessments and drainage designs and to contact developers to address the flooding and drainage failures. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main Street lies within the mapped geographic extent of the 9 March 2016 incident and was subject to overland flow routed from surrounding fields via the mapped drainage network (pipes/culverts and a cut&ndash;off ditch). [IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ran along the front of a property on Main Street and flowed through to the rear gardens of neighbouring properties, causing flooding of those rear gardens. [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed across the paved driveway/access route in front of a property on Main Street, travelling along depressions and slope toward the rear gardens of neighbouring properties. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property (Easter 1998); 1 residential property (late 2012/early 2013); 1 residential property (09/03/2016 &ndash; all ground floor rooms flooded); minor ingress to rear of a second residential property (09/03/2016) [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two properties; one property (patio submerged and water entered house) [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two properties [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (front) [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Main Street, Lowick",England,"Internal flooding of a residential property occurred at Main Street; this property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the five years prior to the investigation. (p9)
Main Street lies in the valley and localised catchment of Harpers Brook and was affected by surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse flooding associated with the event; properties in the Main Street area were among those flooded. (p10)
Surface–water overland flows travelled through the access tunnel beneath the A6116 to a cut–off ditch behind a stable; the ditch filled and spilled. Internal flooding of one residential property occurred on 09/03/2016 (all ground–floor rooms flooded) and there was minor ingress of surface water to the rear of a second residential property. Historical incidents include internal flooding of one property (kitchen and another room) at Easter 1998 and internal flooding of one property in late 2012/early 2013 (whole floor replaced). [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
Surface water flowed down an access road to the rear gardens of two properties on Main Street. A second flow route crossed the rear garden of a third property, submerging the patio and then entering the house, causing internal flooding. The flow through the access tunnel beneath the A6116 was reported as very strong. [PAG] (p12)
Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be overwhelmed so excess surface water flowed over ground and followed natural contours to low points around private properties on Main Street; properties at a low point in the valley were vulnerable to overland flow, exacerbated by A6116 embankments which channelled a concentrated flow through the access tunnel. (p13)
Properties and recent developments on Main Street experienced surface water flooding and/or had ineffective surface water drainage, prompting a recommendation to review planning policies, flood risk assessments and drainage designs and to contact developers to address the flooding and drainage failures. (p22)
Main Street lies within the mapped geographic extent of the 9 March 2016 incident and was subject to overland flow routed from surrounding fields via the mapped drainage network (pipes/culverts and a cut–off ditch). [IMG] (p30)
Surface water ran along the front of a property on Main Street and flowed through to the rear gardens of neighbouring properties, causing flooding of those rear gardens. [IMG,PAG] (p44)
Surface water flowed across the paved driveway/access route in front of a property on Main Street, travelling along depressions and slope toward the rear gardens of neighbouring properties. [IMG,PAG] (p45)","1 property (p9)
1 residential property (Easter 1998); 1 residential property (late 2012/early 2013); 1 residential property (09/03/2016 – all ground floor rooms flooded); minor ingress to rear of a second residential property (09/03/2016) [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
two properties; one property (patio submerged and water entered house) [PAG] (p12)
two properties [IMG,PAG] (p44)
1 property (front) [IMG,PAG] (p45)",GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09,Lowick flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Main Street, Lowick, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22520/FIR-Lowick/Rev03/200916.pdf
GB-NNH,6,2016-03-09,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_3109f5e3d83038af2e8ac2b49a8ee654"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall (approx. 30mm in 12 hours) on saturated ground led to agricultural runoff, surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse flooding across the county, resulting in multiple internal flood reports and widespread surface water impacts. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 9th March 2016 heavy rainfall produced runoff and ordinary watercourse flooding across the county, resulting in widespread reports of flooding and internal flooding of properties. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 70 properties; over 200 reports of flooding (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 70 properties internally flooded; over 200 reports of flooding [PAG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"Heavy rainfall (approx. 30mm in 12 hours) on saturated ground led to agricultural runoff, surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse flooding across the county, resulting in multiple internal flood reports and widespread surface water impacts. (p7)
On 9th March 2016 heavy rainfall produced runoff and ordinary watercourse flooding across the county, resulting in widespread reports of flooding and internal flooding of properties. [PAG] (p9)","over 70 properties; over 200 reports of flooding (p7)
over 70 properties internally flooded; over 200 reports of flooding [PAG] (p9)",GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09,Lowick flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Main Street, Lowick, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22520/FIR-Lowick/Rev03/200916.pdf
GB-NNH,6,2016-03-09,52.41672735909132,-0.5651347148490603,"<div id=""html_d80095d49b33a0e29a80211c79393c4b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lowick</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident on 9th March 2016 affected Main Street in Lowick, resulting in internal flooding of one property. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties and widespread local flooding from a combination of agricultural runoff, surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse (Harpers Brook) flooding. Main Street is within the affected valley and properties there and elsewhere in the village were impacted. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused local drainage systems to be unable to collect and convey rainwater, producing excess surface water that flowed overland to low points around private properties in Lowick; concentrated flows were formed by the A6116 embankments. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from surrounding fields was routed toward and into the village during the incident (map inset shows the flood extent). Flow was channelled along mapped main routes and controlled/modified by three key drainage features (Q1, Q2, Q3), pipe/culvert sections and a cut&ndash;off ditch. [IMG] (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Over 70 properties flooded internally; over 200 reports of flooding (total reported incidents) (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three critical drainage points (Q1, Q2, Q3) [IMG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Lowick,England,"A flood incident on 9th March 2016 affected Main Street in Lowick, resulting in internal flooding of one property. [PAG] (p9)
Internal flooding of residential properties and widespread local flooding from a combination of agricultural runoff, surface water runoff and ordinary watercourse (Harpers Brook) flooding. Main Street is within the affected valley and properties there and elsewhere in the village were impacted. (p10)
Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused local drainage systems to be unable to collect and convey rainwater, producing excess surface water that flowed overland to low points around private properties in Lowick; concentrated flows were formed by the A6116 embankments. [PAG] (p13)
Overland flow from surrounding fields was routed toward and into the village during the incident (map inset shows the flood extent). Flow was channelled along mapped main routes and controlled/modified by three key drainage features (Q1, Q2, Q3), pipe/culvert sections and a cut–off ditch. [IMG] (p30)","1 property [PAG] (p9)
Over 70 properties flooded internally; over 200 reports of flooding (total reported incidents) (p10)
three critical drainage points (Q1, Q2, Q3) [IMG] (p30)",GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_6_2016-03-09,Lowick flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Main Street, Lowick, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22520/FIR-Lowick/Rev03/200916.pdf
GB-CAM,6,2014-08-08,52.20826144094562,-0.023243533980603078,"<div id=""html_a667f0b59f91a9e958a235e43962af3f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Caldecote</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Various locations in Caldecote experienced flooding from multiple sources, specifically drains, local ditches and surface water runoff during the incident. [TAB,MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An extreme short&ndash;duration rainfall event overwhelmed drainage and watercourses causing flooding across the village: 12 properties were reported flooded internally; the road network was inundated; local drains surcharged and watercourses overtopped; 17 residents were evacuated from a residential care home. [IMG] (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties; 17 residents [IMG] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Caldecote,England,"Various locations in Caldecote experienced flooding from multiple sources, specifically drains, local ditches and surface water runoff during the incident. [TAB,MUL] (p2)
An extreme short–duration rainfall event overwhelmed drainage and watercourses causing flooding across the village: 12 properties were reported flooded internally; the road network was inundated; local drains surcharged and watercourses overtopped; 17 residents were evacuated from a residential care home. [IMG] (p3)",12 properties; 17 residents [IMG] (p3),GB-CAM_6_2014-08-08.html,GB-CAM_6_2014-08-08,Caldecote flooding 08 Aug 2014 - 09 Aug 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Caldecote,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Flood/Investigation/Report/Caldecote.pdf
GB-HEF,6,2020-02-16,52.083333,-2.75,"<div id=""html_abeb5ee8629282aeb230a714d87ecc88"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Herefordshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties north of Staunton on Wye caused by fluvial (river/tributary) flooding from the Letton Lake tributary / River Wye. Properties experienced depths typically 1 ft to 5 ft (outbuildings &gt;6 ft). Flooding persisted for between 1 and 4 days. One property had water reach the top of the stairs, trapping residents for c.24 hours until rescued by the fire service. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties (February 16th 2020); 3 properties (October 2019); 2 properties flooded in both events; outbuildings &gt;6 ft [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Herefordshire,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties north of Staunton on Wye caused by fluvial (river/tributary) flooding from the Letton Lake tributary / River Wye. Properties experienced depths typically 1 ft to 5 ft (outbuildings >6 ft). Flooding persisted for between 1 and 4 days. One property had water reach the top of the stairs, trapping residents for c.24 hours until rescued by the fire service. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)","12 properties (February 16th 2020); 3 properties (October 2019); 2 properties flooded in both events; outbuildings >6 ft [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p1)",GB-HEF_6_2020-02-16_etc.html,GB-HEF_6_2020-02-16_etc,,,
GB-HEF,6,2020-02-16,52.0993001,-2.9166867,"<div id=""html_d817d6e85f313e8cce88acff1177ae9b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Staunton-On-Wye</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties north of Staunton on Wye during fluvial events. Depths typically 1 ft to 5 ft (outbuildings recorded &gt;6 ft). Flooding durations reported 1&ndash;4 days. One household reported water reaching top of stairs and occupants trapped for 24 hours until rescued by fire service. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties north of Staunton on Wye experienced internal flooding caused by overflow of the River Wye and/or its tributary. Flooding depths at affected properties were typically 1ft to 5ft (with outbuildings &gt;6ft); respondents reported flood durations of 1 to 4 days; one property had residents trapped for 24 hours and rescued by the fire service; some properties reported historic flooding (1998, 2000, 2002, and ~20 years ago). [MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties (February 2020); 3 properties (October 2019); 2 properties flooded in both events [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties internally flooded; 3 properties in October 2019; 2 properties flooded in both events; one property trapped occupants for 24 hours; depths 1ft to 5ft; &gt;6ft in outbuildings; durations 1 to 4 days [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Staunton-On-Wye,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties north of Staunton on Wye during fluvial events. Depths typically 1 ft to 5 ft (outbuildings recorded >6 ft). Flooding durations reported 1–4 days. One household reported water reaching top of stairs and occupants trapped for 24 hours until rescued by fire service. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)
Properties north of Staunton on Wye experienced internal flooding caused by overflow of the River Wye and/or its tributary. Flooding depths at affected properties were typically 1ft to 5ft (with outbuildings >6ft); respondents reported flood durations of 1 to 4 days; one property had residents trapped for 24 hours and rescued by the fire service; some properties reported historic flooding (1998, 2000, 2002, and ~20 years ago). [MUL] (p2)","12 properties (February 2020); 3 properties (October 2019); 2 properties flooded in both events [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p1)
12 properties internally flooded; 3 properties in October 2019; 2 properties flooded in both events; one property trapped occupants for 24 hours; depths 1ft to 5ft; >6ft in outbuildings; durations 1 to 4 days [MUL] (p2)",GB-HEF_6_2020-02-16_etc.html,GB-HEF_6_2020-02-16_etc,,,
GB-HEF,6,2020-02-16,52.112663,-2.9722197,"<div id=""html_0d4564d41831eb022d0e1908a863ccb2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Letton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A438 flooded at Letton (strategic highway network impact reported in the table). (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Letton,England,A438 flooded at Letton (strategic highway network impact reported in the table). (p2),,GB-HEF_6_2020-02-16_etc.html,GB-HEF_6_2020-02-16_etc,,,
GB-IOW,3,2023-10-25,50.7218922,-1.1589552,"<div id=""html_04836326d8c0708d8da78824227afc94"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Monktonmead Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a source of flooding contributing to the October 25, 2023 event in Ryde that led to internal property flooding. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Monktonmead Brook is the main river through the study area toward The Solent and is a focus of investigation (including the operation of the Monktonmead Flood Alleviation Scheme) in relation to the causes of the 25 October 2023 flooding in Ryde. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Monktonmead Brook was a primary watercourse considered in the Section 19 addendum for the Ryde flooding of 25 October 2023 (Storm Babet); the report and follow&ndash;up investigation (including review of the Monktonmead Flood Alleviation Scheme) identify the brook as a key source/pathway for flooding that affected Monktonmead/Simeon Street and wider parts of Ryde. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Monktonmead Brook rose to threshold levels, impeding surface water drainage and contributing to flooding: flows pushed out of manholes and gullies towards lower&ndash;lying areas, triggered alarms and led to EA issuing Flood Alert/Warning and directing installation of drop boards at Simeon Recreation Ground. [TAB,MUL] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rivers in the catchment were rising rapidly and substantial volumes of water flowed down the catchment during the event, contributing to flooding in the area. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Monktonmead Brook is identified on the map as the main watercourse where flood risk is concentrated; flowpaths and Flood Zone shading run along the brook and are associated with internal property flooding nearby. The brook acted as a source/pathway for flooding into adjacent low&ndash;lying areas in the Simeon Street area. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial floodwater exceeded the channel capacity (left bank), contributing to flooding that filled the Monktonmead Flood Alleviation Scheme at Simeon Recreation Ground. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported cases of flooding were mostly in the vicinity of Monktonmead Brook; the brook's vicinity (and likely overflow) was a primary location for flooding during the event, contributing to local property flooding around the brook. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 25 October 2023 fluvial floodwater overtopped Monktonmead Brook and flowed into the Simeon Recreation Ground, contributing to flooding in Ryde. [IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As the main watercourse in the catchment, the brook would have collected runoff from tributaries during the intense 18&ndash;hour rainfall event and conveyed raised flows northward toward downstream areas, increasing flood risk to downstream urban locations. [IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River level reached 1.9mAOD, which impeded surface water drainage into Monktonmead Brook. This contributed to the drainage network overloading and flows emerging from manholes and gullies. (p37)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>107 properties (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Monktonmead Brook,England,"Identified as a source of flooding contributing to the October 25, 2023 event in Ryde that led to internal property flooding. (p7)
The Monktonmead Brook is the main river through the study area toward The Solent and is a focus of investigation (including the operation of the Monktonmead Flood Alleviation Scheme) in relation to the causes of the 25 October 2023 flooding in Ryde. (p9)
Monktonmead Brook was a primary watercourse considered in the Section 19 addendum for the Ryde flooding of 25 October 2023 (Storm Babet); the report and follow–up investigation (including review of the Monktonmead Flood Alleviation Scheme) identify the brook as a key source/pathway for flooding that affected Monktonmead/Simeon Street and wider parts of Ryde. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Monktonmead Brook rose to threshold levels, impeding surface water drainage and contributing to flooding: flows pushed out of manholes and gullies towards lower–lying areas, triggered alarms and led to EA issuing Flood Alert/Warning and directing installation of drop boards at Simeon Recreation Ground. [TAB,MUL] (p18)
Rivers in the catchment were rising rapidly and substantial volumes of water flowed down the catchment during the event, contributing to flooding in the area. (p19)
Monktonmead Brook is identified on the map as the main watercourse where flood risk is concentrated; flowpaths and Flood Zone shading run along the brook and are associated with internal property flooding nearby. The brook acted as a source/pathway for flooding into adjacent low–lying areas in the Simeon Street area. [IMG] (p20)
Fluvial floodwater exceeded the channel capacity (left bank), contributing to flooding that filled the Monktonmead Flood Alleviation Scheme at Simeon Recreation Ground. (p21)
Reported cases of flooding were mostly in the vicinity of Monktonmead Brook; the brook's vicinity (and likely overflow) was a primary location for flooding during the event, contributing to local property flooding around the brook. (p25)
On 25 October 2023 fluvial floodwater overtopped Monktonmead Brook and flowed into the Simeon Recreation Ground, contributing to flooding in Ryde. [IMG] (p31)
As the main watercourse in the catchment, the brook would have collected runoff from tributaries during the intense 18–hour rainfall event and conveyed raised flows northward toward downstream areas, increasing flood risk to downstream urban locations. [IMG] (p34)
River level reached 1.9mAOD, which impeded surface water drainage into Monktonmead Brook. This contributed to the drainage network overloading and flows emerging from manholes and gullies. (p37)",107 properties (p7),GB-IOW_3_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_3_2023-10-25,Ryde flooding 25 Oct 2023,Monktonmead Section 19 – 2024 Addendum,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Monktonmead addendum Sept 2024 inc App A.pdf
GB-IOW,3,2023-10-25,50.6855525,-1.1870178,"<div id=""html_b20734e6da08656b957a6394123316a4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ashey Down</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location at the brook's source that was within the catchment which received intense rainfall during the 18&ndash;hour event, contributing runoff into Monktonmead Brook. [IMG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Ashey Down,England,"Location at the brook's source that was within the catchment which received intense rainfall during the 18–hour event, contributing runoff into Monktonmead Brook. [IMG] (p34)",,GB-IOW_3_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_3_2023-10-25,Ryde flooding 25 Oct 2023,Monktonmead Section 19 – 2024 Addendum,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation Monktonmead addendum Sept 2024 inc App A.pdf
GB-IOW,3,2023-10-25,50.7298757,-1.1525686,"<div id=""html_99a3c92efce8fcb720375131afaa74ad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Simeon Street, Ryde</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Simeon Street area was disproportionately impacted during the 25 October 2023 flooding in Ryde (Storm Babet) and is the focus of further detail in this addendum. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Simeon Street was disproportionately impacted by flooding during the 25 October 2023 Storm Babet event; the addendum focuses on this area to better understand causes and impacts. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flowpaths directed water from north and central areas toward low&ndash;lying parts of the Simeon Street area, causing internal property flooding clustered in the central and northern parts of the mapped area; demountable and permanent flood walls provided some local protection. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed from the recreation ground along Simeon Street, contributing to property flooding of roads neighbouring Simeon Recreation Ground. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Majority of the flooding took place in the vicinity of Simeon Street; the street and surrounding properties were inundated, causing inundation of residential properties and disruption to access routes. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flood risk in the vicinity of Simeon Street was identified in connection with the 25 October 2023 event and recommended for further investigation to understand effects on nearby properties and drainage. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,"Simeon Street, Ryde",England,"Simeon Street area was disproportionately impacted during the 25 October 2023 flooding in Ryde (Storm Babet) and is the focus of further detail in this addendum. (p9)
