GB-KIR,1,2012-01-03,53.7181095,-1.6544516,"<div id=""html_736806001f588167475c2e69fa78441e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Enfield Close, Carlinghow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to the property's basement kitchen. Floodwater entered the property from a disused coal store adjacent to the basement steps through a point source in the coal store wall approximately 2 m below ground level. Investigation concluded no known drainage system contributed and the water is presumed to have come from raised groundwater. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement of 6 Enfield Close (a back&ndash;to&ndash;back late 19th century property) flooded when water issued from an adjacent unused underground coal store and entered the basement/kitchen. Floodwater filled the basement to a depth of 75 mm before receding. Flooring, kitchen units and white goods were destroyed. The resident used two small pumps to limit the flooding. A neighbouring property (4 Enfield Close) reported water entering its basement. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement/kitchen of 6 Enfield Close was inundated when water issued from an adjacent unused underground coal store through a side wall into the cellar; the basement filled to a depth of 75 mm before receding. Flooring, kitchen units and white appliances were destroyed; the resident used two small pumps to limit flooding. Neighbouring 4 Enfield Close reported water entering its basement. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The resident at 6 Enfield Close reported multiple recent incidents where water threatened a basement room; council records show nearby incidents including a flooded cellar (June 2008). During a council inspection water was observed entering the foot of the external stairwell. [MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The property at 6 Enfield Close experienced flooding (basement/house) on the event. Floodwater appeared uncontaminated with foul sewage. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>75 mm flood depth; 2 small pumps used; 1 neighbouring property (4 Enfield Close) reported water ingress [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Basement filled to 75 mm; two small pumps used; 1 property (6 Enfield Close) sustained internal damage [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 flooded cellar (June 2008) [MUL] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (6 Enfield Close) (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kirklees,"Enfield Close, Carlinghow",England,"Internal flooding to the property's basement kitchen. Floodwater entered the property from a disused coal store adjacent to the basement steps through a point source in the coal store wall approximately 2 m below ground level. Investigation concluded no known drainage system contributed and the water is presumed to have come from raised groundwater. (p3)
Basement of 6 Enfield Close (a back–to–back late 19th century property) flooded when water issued from an adjacent unused underground coal store and entered the basement/kitchen. Floodwater filled the basement to a depth of 75 mm before receding. Flooring, kitchen units and white goods were destroyed. The resident used two small pumps to limit the flooding. A neighbouring property (4 Enfield Close) reported water entering its basement. [PAG] (p5)
Basement/kitchen of 6 Enfield Close was inundated when water issued from an adjacent unused underground coal store through a side wall into the cellar; the basement filled to a depth of 75 mm before receding. Flooring, kitchen units and white appliances were destroyed; the resident used two small pumps to limit flooding. Neighbouring 4 Enfield Close reported water entering its basement. [PAG] (p6)
The resident at 6 Enfield Close reported multiple recent incidents where water threatened a basement room; council records show nearby incidents including a flooded cellar (June 2008). During a council inspection water was observed entering the foot of the external stairwell. [MUL] (p10)
The property at 6 Enfield Close experienced flooding (basement/house) on the event. Floodwater appeared uncontaminated with foul sewage. (p12)","75 mm flood depth; 2 small pumps used; 1 neighbouring property (4 Enfield Close) reported water ingress [PAG] (p5)
Basement filled to 75 mm; two small pumps used; 1 property (6 Enfield Close) sustained internal damage [PAG] (p6)
1 flooded cellar (June 2008) [MUL] (p10)
1 property (6 Enfield Close) (p12)",GB-KIR_1_2012-01-03.html,GB-KIR_1_2012-01-03,Batley flooding 03 Jan 2012 - 05 Jan 2012,"Kirklees Council Flood Investigation 6 Enfield Close, Batley",066/Kirklees/230112EnfieldCloseBatley.pdf
GB-KIR,1,2012-01-03,53.716564,-1.6363856,"<div id=""html_4989522a5fc9876d95393feb0743f670"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Batley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood investigation concerns an event at Enfield Close within Batley. The excerpt does not provide details on impacts to the wider town or other locations in Batley. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kirklees,Batley,England,The flood investigation concerns an event at Enfield Close within Batley. The excerpt does not provide details on impacts to the wider town or other locations in Batley. (p1),,GB-KIR_1_2012-01-03.html,GB-KIR_1_2012-01-03,Batley flooding 03 Jan 2012 - 05 Jan 2012,"Kirklees Council Flood Investigation 6 Enfield Close, Batley",066/Kirklees/230112EnfieldCloseBatley.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.3185152,-0.2146521,"<div id=""html_86292916074a89e04a2f4c73a12b6194"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brampton Park Golf Club</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incident cluster located adjacent to Brampton Golf CP was identified as one of three areas where internal property flooding occurred during the event (map circle 2 / 'The Fairways'). [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from a saturated catchment caused a tributary and an on&ndash;channel pond to exceed capacity on 23rd December 2020; floodwaters travelled north, inundating operational buildings of the golf club and the golf club car park and contributing to flooding of nearby residential properties. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding prompted the club (and landowners/operators) to renew inlet and outlet culverts for an on&ndash;channel pond, perform channel maintenance on sections of the watercourse on club land, and engage in discussions about groundworks to reduce future flood risk; unconsented works by a private landowner were also identified during the post&ndash;flood investigations. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the adjacent riparian watercourse affected the golf course (including The Fairways area and an on&ndash;channel pond). Works undertaken in response included renewal of inlet and outlet culverts for the on&ndash;channel pond, channel maintenance on sections of the watercourse on club land, and planning of groundworks and riparian maintenance to reduce future inundation risk. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Brampton Park Golf Club,England,"Flood incident cluster located adjacent to Brampton Golf CP was identified as one of three areas where internal property flooding occurred during the event (map circle 2 / 'The Fairways'). [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Surface water runoff from a saturated catchment caused a tributary and an on–channel pond to exceed capacity on 23rd December 2020; floodwaters travelled north, inundating operational buildings of the golf club and the golf club car park and contributing to flooding of nearby residential properties. (p12)
Flooding prompted the club (and landowners/operators) to renew inlet and outlet culverts for an on–channel pond, perform channel maintenance on sections of the watercourse on club land, and engage in discussions about groundworks to reduce future flood risk; unconsented works by a private landowner were also identified during the post–flood investigations. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Flooding associated with the adjacent riparian watercourse affected the golf course (including The Fairways area and an on–channel pond). Works undertaken in response included renewal of inlet and outlet culverts for the on–channel pond, channel maintenance on sections of the watercourse on club land, and planning of groundworks and riparian maintenance to reduce future inundation risk. (p14)",,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.311586,-0.225585,"<div id=""html_48601ef50fb80d910b4ac5d0fd65e9e6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stirling Farm</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incident cluster located near Stirling Farm was identified as one of three areas where internal property flooding occurred during the event; properties in this area are part of the cluster labelled 'The Fairways' on the report map. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Stirling Farm,England,"Flood incident cluster located near Stirling Farm was identified as one of three areas where internal property flooding occurred during the event; properties in this area are part of the cluster labelled 'The Fairways' on the report map. [PAG,IMG] (p10)",,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.3752877,-0.2666856,"<div id=""html_e0aee5f60166868abf51cd78f9ebbd09"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alconbury Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Alconbury Brook experienced a significant increase in flow which caused the downstream drainage system to back up and contributed to surface water flooding at Thrapston Road; its high flows also meant floodwaters were held upstream and discharged over a longer period. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Alconbury Brook contributed to flooding affecting the north of the village (Thrapston Road area). Measures to alleviate flooding at Alconbury Brook are being explored by the Alconbury Brook Flood Partnership to reduce this flood risk. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Alconbury Brook,England,"Alconbury Brook experienced a significant increase in flow which caused the downstream drainage system to back up and contributed to surface water flooding at Thrapston Road; its high flows also meant floodwaters were held upstream and discharged over a longer period. (p11)
Alconbury Brook contributed to flooding affecting the north of the village (Thrapston Road area). Measures to alleviate flooding at Alconbury Brook are being explored by the Alconbury Brook Flood Partnership to reduce this flood risk. (p18)",,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.32953378197989,-0.23925863058026167,"<div id=""html_1f2f86a6b82665db4ef13381ef41a802"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>A141 at Brampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident cluster is shown north&ndash;west of Brampton near the A141 (map circle 1); the report identifies that one of the three circled areas experienced internal property flooding during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,A141 at Brampton,England,"A flood incident cluster is shown north–west of Brampton near the A141 (map circle 1); the report identifies that one of the three circled areas experienced internal property flooding during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p10)",,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.3287782,-0.2374131,"<div id=""html_d5be9e0a30277815c8dc5fc71f48b175"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thrapston Road, Brampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of three key risk areas where internal property flooding was reported during the event; included among the most significantly impacted areas. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two residential properties, outbuildings, gardens, land and fields were flooded (surface water ponding exacerbated by backing up of the drainage system caused by high flows in Alconbury Brook). Residents and businesses were forced to evacuate properties for up to one month. Floodwaters held upstream at Alconbury prolonged discharge through this area. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from Alconbury Brook affected the north of the village in the Thrapston Road area; Risk Management Authority operational issues were identified and measures are being explored to reduce future risk. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected Thrapston Road and the report records associated RMA operational issues at this location. [TAB] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; c.500mm depth; up to 1 month evacuation [TAB] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Thrapston Road, Brampton",England,"Identified as one of three key risk areas where internal property flooding was reported during the event; included among the most significantly impacted areas. [PAG] (p9)
Two residential properties, outbuildings, gardens, land and fields were flooded (surface water ponding exacerbated by backing up of the drainage system caused by high flows in Alconbury Brook). Residents and businesses were forced to evacuate properties for up to one month. Floodwaters held upstream at Alconbury prolonged discharge through this area. [TAB] (p11)
Flooding from Alconbury Brook affected the north of the village in the Thrapston Road area; Risk Management Authority operational issues were identified and measures are being explored to reduce future risk. [PAG] (p18)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected Thrapston Road and the report records associated RMA operational issues at this location. [TAB] (p19)",2 residential properties; c.500mm depth; up to 1 month evacuation [TAB] (p11),GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.3225546,-0.2343997,"<div id=""html_a3116491eda01731e574c588fd24751b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Park Road, Brampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of three key risk areas where internal property flooding was reported during the event; grouped among the most significantly impacted areas. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding inundated one residential property on 23rd December 2020; the property was flooded internally and its garden and outbuildings were inundated, and residents were forced to vacate. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding inundated one residential property, its garden and outbuildings. Local drainage was overwhelmed by exceptional runoff and concurrent high flows in the River Great Ouse. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Park Road area experienced increased flood risk during the event; the LLFA is working with Highways England to determine whether the new A14 has contributed to that increased flood risk and RMA operational issues were noted. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected Park Road; the report also notes RMA operational issues associated with this location. [TAB] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property; c.150mm depth; residents vacated for 5 days [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Park Road, Brampton",England,"Identified as one of three key risk areas where internal property flooding was reported during the event; grouped among the most significantly impacted areas. [PAG] (p9)
Surface water flooding inundated one residential property on 23rd December 2020; the property was flooded internally and its garden and outbuildings were inundated, and residents were forced to vacate. [TAB] (p12)
Surface water flooding inundated one residential property, its garden and outbuildings. Local drainage was overwhelmed by exceptional runoff and concurrent high flows in the River Great Ouse. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
The Park Road area experienced increased flood risk during the event; the LLFA is working with Highways England to determine whether the new A14 has contributed to that increased flood risk and RMA operational issues were noted. [PAG] (p18)
Surface water flooding affected Park Road; the report also notes RMA operational issues associated with this location. [TAB] (p19)","1 residential property; c.150mm depth; residents vacated for 5 days [TAB] (p12)
1 residential property [TAB,PAG] (p13)",GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.4617238,0.6975642,"<div id=""html_15a27a06e2eb4bc15b0fc63118232c85"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Anglia</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface&ndash;water flooding resulting from intense afternoon rainfall on 23rd December 2020 combined with very wet antecedent conditions (high water table, saturated ground) that produced very high runoff across the region. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 23rd December 2020 East Anglia experienced intense heavy rainfall on saturated ground (very low soil moisture deficit), producing very high runoff and significant surface&ndash;water flooding across the region. These conditions contributed to localized flooding incidents such as those reported in Brampton. [PAG,IMG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,East Anglia,England,"Widespread surface–water flooding resulting from intense afternoon rainfall on 23rd December 2020 combined with very wet antecedent conditions (high water table, saturated ground) that produced very high runoff across the region. [PAG] (p7)
On 23rd December 2020 East Anglia experienced intense heavy rainfall on saturated ground (very low soil moisture deficit), producing very high runoff and significant surface–water flooding across the region. These conditions contributed to localized flooding incidents such as those reported in Brampton. [PAG,IMG] (p9)",,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.32161590655935,-0.2238513563624762,"<div id=""html_e9df4cab55b884debf629a5e30409ddf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brampton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding from surface water sources caused by intense short&ndash;duration rainfall on already saturated ground, producing very high runoff that led to flooding in Brampton. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant surface&ndash;water/urban flooding across the area; 24 flood reports were received by the LLFA; roads and gardens were flooded; internal flooding to residential properties occurred. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area&ndash;wide flooding in Brampton with 24 flood reports to the LLFA. Internal flooding affected residential properties (roads, gardens and some public/green land were also flooded). Thrapston Road area: two residential properties, industrial outbuildings, gardens, land and fields flooded to a maximum depth of approximately 500 mm; residents and businesses were evacuated for up to one month. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas around Brampton experienced surface water flooding due to exceptional runoff (soil capacity vastly exceeded) and concurrent high flows entering the River Great Ouse from other watercourses, which exceeded channel capacity. The LLFA carried out site walkovers and met residents known to have flooded as part of the investigation. [TAB,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in the village, particularly the south of Brampton where nearby watercourses are thought to have contributed to flooding; maintenance actions have been taken but further work is required to ensure watercourses convey water away from the village effectively. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>24 flood reports; 9 residential properties flooded internally [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>24 flood reports; 9 residential properties flooded internally; 2 residential properties (Thrapston Road); up to 1 month evacuations; max depth c.500 mm [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Brampton,England,"Significant flooding from surface water sources caused by intense short–duration rainfall on already saturated ground, producing very high runoff that led to flooding in Brampton. (p7)
Significant surface–water/urban flooding across the area; 24 flood reports were received by the LLFA; roads and gardens were flooded; internal flooding to residential properties occurred. [PAG] (p9)
Area–wide flooding in Brampton with 24 flood reports to the LLFA. Internal flooding affected residential properties (roads, gardens and some public/green land were also flooded). Thrapston Road area: two residential properties, industrial outbuildings, gardens, land and fields flooded to a maximum depth of approximately 500 mm; residents and businesses were evacuated for up to one month. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Areas around Brampton experienced surface water flooding due to exceptional runoff (soil capacity vastly exceeded) and concurrent high flows entering the River Great Ouse from other watercourses, which exceeded channel capacity. The LLFA carried out site walkovers and met residents known to have flooded as part of the investigation. [TAB,PAG] (p13)
Flooding occurred in the village, particularly the south of Brampton where nearby watercourses are thought to have contributed to flooding; maintenance actions have been taken but further work is required to ensure watercourses convey water away from the village effectively. (p18)","24 flood reports; 9 residential properties flooded internally [PAG] (p9)
24 flood reports; 9 residential properties flooded internally; 2 residential properties (Thrapston Road); up to 1 month evacuations; max depth c.500 mm [PAG,IMG] (p10)",GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,13,2020-12-23,52.368714542527975,-0.25833707792256316,"<div id=""html_1a61176b454538ddd1f6b8c110c4d698"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alconbury</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters were held upstream at Alconbury, which prolonged discharge through downstream areas (including Thrapston Road) and contributed to extended flooding impacts. (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Alconbury,England,"Floodwaters were held upstream at Alconbury, which prolonged discharge through downstream areas (including Thrapston Road) and contributed to extended flooding impacts. (p11)",,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_13_2020-12-23,Brampton flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Brampton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Brampton-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.13944432952492,0.7259324912478369,"<div id=""html_2a4a09c139f77646b8e0d7cdc9045fdd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sunnyside Farm</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A field pond north of Sunnyside (sic) overtopped during the July 2021 flood, producing overland flows that reached the Sunnyside pond; the pond bank was subsequently raised to reduce overtopping and overland flows (Sunnyside Farm?). (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Sunnyside Farm,England,"A field pond north of Sunnyside (sic) overtopped during the July 2021 flood, producing overland flows that reached the Sunnyside pond; the pond bank was subsequently raised to reduce overtopping and overland flows (Sunnyside Farm?). (p12)",,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1235296,0.7455538,"<div id=""html_a6344bc0c030046b3f653cb4da6c0e46"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>A28 at Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow pathways across agricultural land north of the A28 connected into the drainage network, contributing to flows through culverts (underneath Forge Hill) and the wider drainage response that led to overtopping and flooding of adjacent properties (Ashford Road/A28 area). [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A28 at the junction with Forge Hill is described as a low point where surface water ponds; the Bethersden Stream passes under the carriageway in a culvert at this location, affecting carriageway drainage and causing ponding during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Forge Hill and Ashford Road (A28) is a known low spot where surface water ponds; during the 25 July 2021 event high flows bypassed the culvert beneath Forge Hill, contributing to ponding and local road flooding at this junction. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carriageway flooding of the A28 occurred as local surface water drainage was overwhelmed; subsequent highway drainage repairs were undertaken. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,A28 at Bethersden,England,"Overland flow pathways across agricultural land north of the A28 connected into the drainage network, contributing to flows through culverts (underneath Forge Hill) and the wider drainage response that led to overtopping and flooding of adjacent properties (Ashford Road/A28 area). [IMG] (p9)
The A28 at the junction with Forge Hill is described as a low point where surface water ponds; the Bethersden Stream passes under the carriageway in a culvert at this location, affecting carriageway drainage and causing ponding during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
The junction of Forge Hill and Ashford Road (A28) is a known low spot where surface water ponds; during the 25 July 2021 event high flows bypassed the culvert beneath Forge Hill, contributing to ponding and local road flooding at this junction. [IMG] (p11)
Carriageway flooding of the A28 occurred as local surface water drainage was overwhelmed; subsequent highway drainage repairs were undertaken. (p14)",,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1653225,0.6190017,"<div id=""html_f8336d829e2fb75e524ac5fcfb69d089"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Beult</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An unnamed tributary of the River Beult experienced rapidly rising levels which contributed to local highway drainage surcharging, culvert surcharging and flooding of properties in the area. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the River Beult and its tributaries contributed to flooding of lower&ndash;lying areas in and around Bethersden, corresponding with properties affected during the 25 July 2021 event. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the extreme rainfall on 25 July 2021 the River Beult showed a steep rise in levels (evidenced at the nearest gauge at Smarden), beginning on the evening of 25 July and peaking in the early hours of 26 July. This rapid rise indicates a fluvial response to the heavy rainfall and contributed to fluvial flooding in the catchment. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the River Beult produced reported flood incidents in and around Bethersden, with flood locations clustered along the river corridor and at nearby road junctions, causing local inundation and disruption to roads and areas of the village. [IMG] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Approximately 33 properties flooded (22 internally; 11 externally). (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,River Beult,England,"An unnamed tributary of the River Beult experienced rapidly rising levels which contributed to local highway drainage surcharging, culvert surcharging and flooding of properties in the area. (p1)
Fluvial flooding from the River Beult and its tributaries contributed to flooding of lower–lying areas in and around Bethersden, corresponding with properties affected during the 25 July 2021 event. (p4)
Following the extreme rainfall on 25 July 2021 the River Beult showed a steep rise in levels (evidenced at the nearest gauge at Smarden), beginning on the evening of 25 July and peaking in the early hours of 26 July. This rapid rise indicates a fluvial response to the heavy rainfall and contributed to fluvial flooding in the catchment. [MUL] (p5)
Flooding associated with the River Beult produced reported flood incidents in and around Bethersden, with flood locations clustered along the river corridor and at nearby road junctions, causing local inundation and disruption to roads and areas of the village. [IMG] (p15)",Approximately 33 properties flooded (22 internally; 11 externally). (p1),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1289294,0.7534495,"<div id=""html_f165a4e22917e59201ab369d0e132227"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Hill, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert located under Church Hill surcharged due to the volume of water within the river channels, contributing to local drainage surcharge and flooding issues. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties with shallow flood depths reported inside properties. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts beneath Church Hill surcharged during the flood, causing floodwater to flow onto the adjacent road during the 25 July 2021 event. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert beneath Church Hill surcharged during the flood event and floodwater flowed onto the adjacent road. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 10 cm &ndash; 30 cm of floodwater within properties [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Church Hill, Bethersden",England,"The culvert located under Church Hill surcharged due to the volume of water within the river channels, contributing to local drainage surcharge and flooding issues. (p1)
Internal flooding of residential properties with shallow flood depths reported inside properties. [TAB] (p2)
Culverts beneath Church Hill surcharged during the flood, causing floodwater to flow onto the adjacent road during the 25 July 2021 event. (p11)
Culvert beneath Church Hill surcharged during the flood event and floodwater flowed onto the adjacent road. [PAG] (p12)",4 properties; 10 cm – 30 cm of floodwater within properties [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1313529,0.7516678,"<div id=""html_0681236219693ed181b11cf65966721c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Norton Lane, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert located under Norton Lane surcharged because of the high volume of water in the river channels, contributing to local drainage surcharge and flooding. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding of a property; garden inundated. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts beneath Norton Lane surcharged and floodwater flowed onto the adjacent roads during the 25 July 2021 event. The downstream section of watercourse downstream of Norton Lane (maintained by Bethersden Parish Council) performed well and did not contribute to upstream problems. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert beneath Norton Lane surcharged during the 25 July 2021 flood, with floodwater flowing onto the adjacent road. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 100 cm of floodwater within the garden [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Norton Lane, Bethersden",England,"The culvert located under Norton Lane surcharged because of the high volume of water in the river channels, contributing to local drainage surcharge and flooding. (p1)
External flooding of a property; garden inundated. [TAB] (p2)
Culverts beneath Norton Lane surcharged and floodwater flowed onto the adjacent roads during the 25 July 2021 event. The downstream section of watercourse downstream of Norton Lane (maintained by Bethersden Parish Council) performed well and did not contribute to upstream problems. [PAG] (p11)
The culvert beneath Norton Lane surcharged during the 25 July 2021 flood, with floodwater flowing onto the adjacent road. [PAG] (p12)",1 property; 100 cm of floodwater within the garden [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.12821,0.7519141,"<div id=""html_f5dde6c420c0ed0bcc6b5e6e5ffcf933"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chester Avenue, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding affecting properties. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bethersden Stream flows as an open channel between the rear gardens of properties on Chester Avenue (and Forgefield); local restrictions to the channel (sheds/fences) during the 25 July 2021 high flows could have reduced conveyance and contributed to water leaving the channel and flooding adjacent properties. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Chester Avenue, Bethersden",England,"External flooding affecting properties. [TAB] (p2)
The Bethersden Stream flows as an open channel between the rear gardens of properties on Chester Avenue (and Forgefield); local restrictions to the channel (sheds/fences) during the 25 July 2021 high flows could have reduced conveyance and contributed to water leaving the channel and flooding adjacent properties. (p11)",3 properties [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.126083930077016,0.7593833699180583,"<div id=""html_b4b0a0073bfeb518bef3163a2de00b81"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ashford Road, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of multiple properties with shallow flood depths; some properties also reported external flooding. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along Ashford Road were flooded when the adjacent land drainage ditch overtopped during the 25 July 2021 intense rainfall; KFRS attended to pump out floodwater from multiple properties. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the land drainage ditch and associated overland flows led to flooding of properties along Ashford Road; Kent Fire and Rescue Service attended on 25 July 2021 to pump out floodwater from multiple properties. [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Ashford Road (A28) and Forge Hill is a low spot where surface water ponds; the Bethersden Stream flows through a culvert beneath this junction and then into an open channel behind nearby properties, contributing to local flooding and ponding at the road junction. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 675 mm culvert beneath the junction of Forge Hill and Ashford Road (A28) was overwhelmed/bypassed by high flows on 25 July 2021 and the junction is a known low spot where surface water ponds, contributing to local flooding and ponding at the road junction. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the 25 July 2021 flood, KCC Highways reviewed the surface water drainage infrastructure for Ashford Road (including CCTV surveys) to understand condition and connectivity as part of the post&ndash;flood response. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 properties internally flooded; 2 properties externally flooded; 5 cm &ndash; 15 cm of floodwater within properties [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Ashford Road, Bethersden",England,"Internal flooding of multiple properties with shallow flood depths; some properties also reported external flooding. [TAB] (p2)
Properties along Ashford Road were flooded when the adjacent land drainage ditch overtopped during the 25 July 2021 intense rainfall; KFRS attended to pump out floodwater from multiple properties. [PAG] (p8)
Overtopping of the land drainage ditch and associated overland flows led to flooding of properties along Ashford Road; Kent Fire and Rescue Service attended on 25 July 2021 to pump out floodwater from multiple properties. [IMG,PAG] (p9)
The junction of Ashford Road (A28) and Forge Hill is a low spot where surface water ponds; the Bethersden Stream flows through a culvert beneath this junction and then into an open channel behind nearby properties, contributing to local flooding and ponding at the road junction. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
The 675 mm culvert beneath the junction of Forge Hill and Ashford Road (A28) was overwhelmed/bypassed by high flows on 25 July 2021 and the junction is a known low spot where surface water ponds, contributing to local flooding and ponding at the road junction. [IMG] (p11)
Following the 25 July 2021 flood, KCC Highways reviewed the surface water drainage infrastructure for Ashford Road (including CCTV surveys) to understand condition and connectivity as part of the post–flood response. (p12)",12 properties internally flooded; 2 properties externally flooded; 5 cm – 15 cm of floodwater within properties [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1267837,0.7664553,"<div id=""html_7e875d444c99f1d94f6894d19c19b298"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kiln Lane, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property with deep floodwater reported inside. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The land drainage ditch alongside Kiln Lane was diverted away from Bethersden so that overland flows are directed south alongside Kiln Lane, cross the carriageway via a culvert and continue into fields to the east &mdash; changing the overland flow pathway and drainage connectivity during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; approximately 90 cm of floodwater within the property [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Kiln Lane, Bethersden",England,"Internal flooding of a property with deep floodwater reported inside. [TAB] (p2)
The land drainage ditch alongside Kiln Lane was diverted away from Bethersden so that overland flows are directed south alongside Kiln Lane, cross the carriageway via a culvert and continue into fields to the east — changing the overland flow pathway and drainage connectivity during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)",1 property; approximately 90 cm of floodwater within the property [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1279608,0.7540173,"<div id=""html_c06b07b73da73ca955f4c85665b28fc0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Forgefield, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties experienced internal and external flooding: internal inundation to living spaces and gardens, with one property only avoiding internal flooding by use of sandbags. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bethersden Stream flows as an open channel between the rear gardens of properties on Forgefield (and Chester Avenue); during the 25 July 2021 event high flows (and local channel restrictions from sheds/fences) affected flow conveyance and may have led to water leaving the channel and causing flooding to adjacent properties. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties flooded internally; 3 properties flooded externally; reports of approximately 10 cm of floodwater within properties; 1 property avoided internal flooding by using sandbags. [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Forgefield, Bethersden",England,"Properties experienced internal and external flooding: internal inundation to living spaces and gardens, with one property only avoiding internal flooding by use of sandbags. [TAB] (p2)
The Bethersden Stream flows as an open channel between the rear gardens of properties on Forgefield (and Chester Avenue); during the 25 July 2021 event high flows (and local channel restrictions from sheds/fences) affected flow conveyance and may have led to water leaving the channel and causing flooding to adjacent properties. (p11)",4 properties flooded internally; 3 properties flooded externally; reports of approximately 10 cm of floodwater within properties; 1 property avoided internal flooding by using sandbags. [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1276575,0.7577963,"<div id=""html_94ed22f3079cc37b6f1bceb3cc22ef93"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Forge Hill, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property with floodwater entering the building to a significant depth. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bethersden Stream passes via a 675 mm culvert beneath the junction of Forge Hill and Ashford Road (A28); the junction is a local low spot where surface water is known to pond. The culvert and junction geometry contributed to local ponding and conveyance of stream flows through the junction during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert beneath Forge Hill was being bypassed by high river flows on 25 July 2021. The Forge Hill/A28 junction is a known low spot where surface water ponds; during the event properties on Forge Hill were flooded and Kent Fire & Rescue Service pumped floodwater from inside properties into the Bethersden Stream, which may have increased downstream flooding. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert and surrounding drainage located at The Street and Forge Hill junction were cleaned and pipe repairs were undertaken (March 2022) as part of remedial works following the 25 July 2021 flood. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded internally; reports of 20 cm of floodwater within the property. [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Forge Hill, Bethersden",England,"Internal flooding of a property with floodwater entering the building to a significant depth. [TAB] (p2)
The Bethersden Stream passes via a 675 mm culvert beneath the junction of Forge Hill and Ashford Road (A28); the junction is a local low spot where surface water is known to pond. The culvert and junction geometry contributed to local ponding and conveyance of stream flows through the junction during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
The culvert beneath Forge Hill was being bypassed by high river flows on 25 July 2021. The Forge Hill/A28 junction is a known low spot where surface water ponds; during the event properties on Forge Hill were flooded and Kent Fire & Rescue Service pumped floodwater from inside properties into the Bethersden Stream, which may have increased downstream flooding. [IMG] (p11)
The culvert and surrounding drainage located at The Street and Forge Hill junction were cleaned and pipe repairs were undertaken (March 2022) as part of remedial works following the 25 July 2021 flood. (p12)",1 property flooded internally; reports of 20 cm of floodwater within the property. [TAB] (p2),GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.1292949,0.7567037,"<div id=""html_f1b0de65392b740b30d1e7cdeef4d0ad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Street, Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding affected properties (water in gardens/driveways/external areas). [TAB,PAG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding to properties on The Street. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 25 July 2021 event a culvert (beneath Union Chapel) was reported collapsed; floodwater surcharged and flooded surrounding properties on The Street and KFRS pumped floodwater from a property. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties flooded externally. [TAB,PAG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"The Street, Bethersden",England,"External flooding affected properties (water in gardens/driveways/external areas). [TAB,PAG] (p2)
External flooding to properties on The Street. (p3)
During the 25 July 2021 event a culvert (beneath Union Chapel) was reported collapsed; floodwater surcharged and flooded surrounding properties on The Street and KFRS pumped floodwater from a property. (p12)","2 properties flooded externally. [TAB,PAG] (p2)
2 properties (p3)",GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,5,2021-07-25,51.12805764822621,0.7533061547698318,"<div id=""html_6f8e3cbc5a2e6f32914feac55ecaec5e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bethersden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 25 July 2021 caused local river levels (Bethersden Stream and an unnamed tributary of the River Beult) to rise quickly, surcharging highway surface water drainage and culverts (under Church Hill and Norton Lane). Sewer network was reported unable to cope in isolated areas. Approximately 33 properties were flooded (22 internally, 11 externally). (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected properties within the village: at least one property was flooded internally (reported ~90 cm of floodwater) and other properties experienced external flooding (two properties on The Street). [IMG,PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower&ndash;lying areas of the village associated with the two tributaries experienced surface&ndash;water and tributary/river flooding; the high&ndash;risk areas on surface&ndash;water mapping largely correspond to the properties affected in the 25 July 2021 event. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, localized rainfall on 25 July 2021 led to flooding in Bethersden, with flooding reported to start at approximately 15:00 as a result of the extreme hourly rainfall. The local Environment Agency rain gauge is located in Bethersden and recorded the extreme event used to characterise the flood. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 25 July 2021 overwhelmed drainage and watercourses, causing main&ndash;river overtopping and surface water runoff that flooded properties in the village. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows from land to the south overwhelmed the rear land drainage ditch south of Bailey Fields, which overtopped and led to flooding of properties along Ashford Road. Debris was reported near drainage pipes south of Bailey Fields. Kent Fire and Rescue Service attended on 25 July 2021 to pump out floodwater from multiple properties. Subsequent KCC Highways surveys (2022) identified defects in the surface water drainage system (deformed/partially collapsed pipes and root masses) that would have reduced system capacity. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 25 July 2021 culverts (including beneath Church Hill and Norton Lane) surcharged and sent floodwater onto adjacent roads; an unnamed tributary and collapsed culvert beneath Union Chapel caused surcharge and flooding of surrounding properties. Emergency responders (KCC, KFRS) attended and pump and clearance operations were undertaken. Subsequent actions included raising a pond bank north of Sunnyside and remedial culvert/pipe cleaning and repairs. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 25 July 2021 overwhelmed local surface water drainage systems and local watercourses, causing internal flooding to properties and carriageway flooding of local roads in the village. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 25 July 2021 intense rainfall overwhelmed local surface water drainage and local watercourses, causing flooding to properties and roads. High water levels in receiving watercourses prevented the drainage network from discharging. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents were reported across the village, with clusters of inundation near the village centre and along main roads. Flooding locations on the map are concentrated close to local watercourses and at road junctions, indicating inundation of roads and adjacent areas. [IMG] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 33 properties; 22 properties internal flooding; 11 properties external flooding (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 2 properties [IMG,PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties; 30 properties (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 properties [PAG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Bethersden,England,"Intense rainfall on 25 July 2021 caused local river levels (Bethersden Stream and an unnamed tributary of the River Beult) to rise quickly, surcharging highway surface water drainage and culverts (under Church Hill and Norton Lane). Sewer network was reported unable to cope in isolated areas. Approximately 33 properties were flooded (22 internally, 11 externally). (p1)
Flooding affected properties within the village: at least one property was flooded internally (reported ~90 cm of floodwater) and other properties experienced external flooding (two properties on The Street). [IMG,PAG] (p3)
Lower–lying areas of the village associated with the two tributaries experienced surface–water and tributary/river flooding; the high–risk areas on surface–water mapping largely correspond to the properties affected in the 25 July 2021 event. (p4)
Intense, localized rainfall on 25 July 2021 led to flooding in Bethersden, with flooding reported to start at approximately 15:00 as a result of the extreme hourly rainfall. The local Environment Agency rain gauge is located in Bethersden and recorded the extreme event used to characterise the flood. (p5)
Intense rainfall on 25 July 2021 overwhelmed drainage and watercourses, causing main–river overtopping and surface water runoff that flooded properties in the village. (p8)
Overland flows from land to the south overwhelmed the rear land drainage ditch south of Bailey Fields, which overtopped and led to flooding of properties along Ashford Road. Debris was reported near drainage pipes south of Bailey Fields. Kent Fire and Rescue Service attended on 25 July 2021 to pump out floodwater from multiple properties. Subsequent KCC Highways surveys (2022) identified defects in the surface water drainage system (deformed/partially collapsed pipes and root masses) that would have reduced system capacity. (p9)
On 25 July 2021 culverts (including beneath Church Hill and Norton Lane) surcharged and sent floodwater onto adjacent roads; an unnamed tributary and collapsed culvert beneath Union Chapel caused surcharge and flooding of surrounding properties. Emergency responders (KCC, KFRS) attended and pump and clearance operations were undertaken. Subsequent actions included raising a pond bank north of Sunnyside and remedial culvert/pipe cleaning and repairs. [PAG] (p12)
Intense rainfall on 25 July 2021 overwhelmed local surface water drainage systems and local watercourses, causing internal flooding to properties and carriageway flooding of local roads in the village. [PAG] (p13)
On 25 July 2021 intense rainfall overwhelmed local surface water drainage and local watercourses, causing flooding to properties and roads. High water levels in receiving watercourses prevented the drainage network from discharging. [PAG] (p14)
Flooding incidents were reported across the village, with clusters of inundation near the village centre and along main roads. Flooding locations on the map are concentrated close to local watercourses and at road junctions, indicating inundation of roads and adjacent areas. [IMG] (p15)","approximately 33 properties; 22 properties internal flooding; 11 properties external flooding (p1)
1 property; 2 properties [IMG,PAG] (p3)
5 properties; 30 properties (p8)
33 properties [PAG] (p13)
33 properties [PAG] (p14)",GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25.html,GB-KEN_5_2021-07-25,Bethersden flooding 25 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Bethersden,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Bethersden-on-25-July-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.258458899999994,0.26984495092441824,"<div id=""html_fe557908a3265d0ceae294961c8d8a01"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ightham Mote</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of the property occurred due to run&ndash;off from adjacent fields exceeding the capacity of drains and local watercourses. (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Ightham Mote,England,Internal flooding of the property occurred due to run–off from adjacent fields exceeding the capacity of drains and local watercourses. (p25),,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.2923841,0.2959146,"<div id=""html_2bca6165a07815372e49fd991b2780f0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Borough Green Road, Ightham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Borough Green Road (referred to here as Green Road) during the event; the street was one of the areas named as being affected by flooding. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties in the area of Borough Green Road (referred to here as Green Road) adjacent to the Busty Stream during the flood reported on 25 June 2016. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The full width of the carriageway on Borough Green Road (referred to here as Green Road) was flooded by flowing water above kerb height; flows ran down the road and entered properties internally via doorways and airbricks. Flows continued eastwards where some water re&ndash;entered the watercourse via driveway entrances. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood&ndash;water exceedance flow routed around Borough Green Road during the event, consistent with the updated Flood Map for Surface Water. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Borough Green Road, Ightham",England,"Flooding affected Borough Green Road (referred to here as Green Road) during the event; the street was one of the areas named as being affected by flooding. (p1)
Internal flooding to properties in the area of Borough Green Road (referred to here as Green Road) adjacent to the Busty Stream during the flood reported on 25 June 2016. (p4)
The full width of the carriageway on Borough Green Road (referred to here as Green Road) was flooded by flowing water above kerb height; flows ran down the road and entered properties internally via doorways and airbricks. Flows continued eastwards where some water re–entered the watercourse via driveway entrances. (p14)
Flood–water exceedance flow routed around Borough Green Road during the event, consistent with the updated Flood Map for Surface Water. (p20)",,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.2874911,0.2874215,"<div id=""html_7af534d32af68390d1f3f1f7c5f4e87b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Busty Lane, Ightham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Busty Lane; it is named in the report as one of the areas impacted by the flood event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties in the area of Busty Lane during the flood reported on 25 June 2016. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>KCC records show high flows in the watercourse caused flooding at the eastern end of Busty Lane in Sep 2009, Nov 2013 and Jan 2014. Anecdotal evidence from residents also links wet winters (notably 2000 and 2003) to high flows and flooding affecting external areas of properties alongside the watercourse. The lane lies within the area of flooding considered by this report. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows from the Busty Stream travelled down Busty Lane, coming out of bank near the bend by Elim Cottages and the culvert under Ezel Villas. High flows could not drain downstream through the culvert quickly enough, causing significant ponding/backing up of water in the lower part of Busty Lane and adjacent areas. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water flowed out of the watercourse bank through and around properties adjacent to the watercourse, through front and rear gardens and onto Borough Green Road. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As follow&ndash;up maintenance after the flooding, the community and parish council cleared excess vegetation from the banks and removed excess silt from the bed of the watercourse along Busty Lane. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A small build&ndash;up of silt was noted within the watercourse at the end of Busty Lane, deposited during the flooding event; the depth was limited and considered not significant. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood exceedance flows passed around Busty Lane (and Borough Green Road); residents report historical flooding at the end of Busty Lane and external areas of property have flooded in wet conditions. During the 25th June 2016 event the exceedance route was close to that predicted and upstream ponding reached about 2 metres. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A culvert at Busty Lane was implicated in the flooding (risk of blockages by debris/standing water); report recommends installing a trash screen and inspecting/maintaining the culvert to reduce blockage&ndash;related flooding. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>maximum depth of flood water approximately 2 metres (as illustrated around a parked van) (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>less than 150mm silt depth (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 metres deep (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Busty Lane, Ightham",England,"Flooding affected Busty Lane; it is named in the report as one of the areas impacted by the flood event. (p1)
Internal flooding to properties in the area of Busty Lane during the flood reported on 25 June 2016. (p4)
KCC records show high flows in the watercourse caused flooding at the eastern end of Busty Lane in Sep 2009, Nov 2013 and Jan 2014. Anecdotal evidence from residents also links wet winters (notably 2000 and 2003) to high flows and flooding affecting external areas of properties alongside the watercourse. The lane lies within the area of flooding considered by this report. [MUL] (p6)
Flows from the Busty Stream travelled down Busty Lane, coming out of bank near the bend by Elim Cottages and the culvert under Ezel Villas. High flows could not drain downstream through the culvert quickly enough, causing significant ponding/backing up of water in the lower part of Busty Lane and adjacent areas. (p13)
Water flowed out of the watercourse bank through and around properties adjacent to the watercourse, through front and rear gardens and onto Borough Green Road. (p14)
As follow–up maintenance after the flooding, the community and parish council cleared excess vegetation from the banks and removed excess silt from the bed of the watercourse along Busty Lane. (p16)
A small build–up of silt was noted within the watercourse at the end of Busty Lane, deposited during the flooding event; the depth was limited and considered not significant. (p19)
Flood exceedance flows passed around Busty Lane (and Borough Green Road); residents report historical flooding at the end of Busty Lane and external areas of property have flooded in wet conditions. During the 25th June 2016 event the exceedance route was close to that predicted and upstream ponding reached about 2 metres. (p20)
A culvert at Busty Lane was implicated in the flooding (risk of blockages by debris/standing water); report recommends installing a trash screen and inspecting/maintaining the culvert to reduce blockage–related flooding. [TAB] (p26)","maximum depth of flood water approximately 2 metres (as illustrated around a parked van) (p13)
less than 150mm silt depth (p19)
2 metres deep (p20)",GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.2880106,0.2893648,"<div id=""html_6e03131957e32a2340e422588a17026b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Durlings Orchard, Ightham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert serving Durling's Orchard was inspected after the flood: overall structurally sound but two timber&ndash;roofed areas noted; minor debris (sticks/branches) and silt deposits were present and are likely to have been deposited during the flood; a small localised collapse at the downstream end of the culvert was repaired following the event. [TAB] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two areas (minor debris) [TAB] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Durlings Orchard, Ightham",England,Culvert serving Durling's Orchard was inspected after the flood: overall structurally sound but two timber–roofed areas noted; minor debris (sticks/branches) and silt deposits were present and are likely to have been deposited during the flood; a small localised collapse at the downstream end of the culvert was repaired following the event. [TAB] (p19),two areas (minor debris) [TAB] (p19),GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.289651,0.2868657,"<div id=""html_b3820a417f8499f9c6545abfb09da9d2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fen Pond Road, Ightham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred adjacent to Fen Pond where the Busty Stream flows through the pond and crosses the road in a culvert. High flows are assumed to have exceeded the culvert capacity, causing water to flow over the road; there are unconfirmed reports that adjacent properties were flooded. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Fen Pond Road, Ightham",England,"Flooding occurred adjacent to Fen Pond where the Busty Stream flows through the pond and crosses the road in a culvert. High flows are assumed to have exceeded the culvert capacity, causing water to flow over the road; there are unconfirmed reports that adjacent properties were flooded. (p23)",,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.2868359,0.3000877,"<div id=""html_1f5f30b0e1c2214f28e3bfe5d2cc069f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thong Lane, Borough Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Out&ndash;of&ndash;bank flows from the adjacent watercourse (part of the River Bourne) ran onto the road; the carriageway was flooded to a depth that made it impassable except to high vehicles and 4x4s; the culvert under the bridge abutment was blocked with mud and silt. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Thong Lane, Borough Green",England,Out–of–bank flows from the adjacent watercourse (part of the River Bourne) ran onto the road; the carriageway was flooded to a depth that made it impassable except to high vehicles and 4x4s; the culvert under the bridge abutment was blocked with mud and silt. (p24),,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.2642908,0.2695242,"<div id=""html_268aaf3605414e1d290784598d8dbedf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mote Road, Ivy Hatch</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Run&ndash;off from adjacent fields exceeded the capacity of highway drains and watercourses, depositing a significant amount of mud, gravel and debris across the road and filling drainage gullies and pipework. KCC Highways arranged debris clearance and carried out maintenance and repair of the highway drainage system under a road closure; drainage works were completed by November 2016. (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Mote Road, Ivy Hatch",England,"Run–off from adjacent fields exceeded the capacity of highway drains and watercourses, depositing a significant amount of mud, gravel and debris across the road and filling drainage gullies and pipework. KCC Highways arranged debris clearance and carried out maintenance and repair of the highway drainage system under a road closure; drainage works were completed by November 2016. (p25)",,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.253523211364325,0.15885961792210257,"<div id=""html_6a434dc914ebbecd4b73c729f3b086ab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sevenoaks</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High numbers of reports of highway flooding were received for the Sevenoaks area on 25 June 2016; incidents were attended by Kent County Council Highways duty officers. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Sevenoaks,England,High numbers of reports of highway flooding were received for the Sevenoaks area on 25 June 2016; incidents were attended by Kent County Council Highways duty officers. (p23),,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.27943030200367,0.27986803170056684,"<div id=""html_9ad8b19c7b3885a2d6fcab47ba107042"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ightham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village of Ightham experienced flooding affecting areas including Borough Green Road and Busty Lane during the reported flood event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding within the village reported on 25 June 2016 that caused internal flooding to properties in the areas of Busty Lane and Borough Green Road adjacent to the Busty Stream. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported downstream of Ightham along the River Bourne as high flows exceeded the watercourse capacity, causing inundation downstream of the village. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ightham experienced flooding associated with the intense, highly variable rainfall on 25 June 2016. Rain&ndash;gauge records from nearby sites were used in the post&ndash;event analysis to assess how rainfall across the Busty Stream catchment contributed to flooding at Ightham. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fen Pond Road, located upstream of Ightham within the Busty Stream catchment, experienced flooding on 25 June 2016 when high flows exceeded a culvert; this was reported to KCC by Ightham Parish Council and may have resulted in flooding affecting properties in the vicinity. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the village caused by the Busty Stream and associated culvert issues, resulting in internal flooding of properties and accumulation of silt and debris in the watercourse. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties and residents in the community experienced flooding; the area has been identified as having suffered flood impacts and for follow&ndash;up community resilience support. (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Ightham,England,"The village of Ightham experienced flooding affecting areas including Borough Green Road and Busty Lane during the reported flood event. (p1)
Significant flooding within the village reported on 25 June 2016 that caused internal flooding to properties in the areas of Busty Lane and Borough Green Road adjacent to the Busty Stream. (p4)
Flooding was reported downstream of Ightham along the River Bourne as high flows exceeded the watercourse capacity, causing inundation downstream of the village. (p15)
Ightham experienced flooding associated with the intense, highly variable rainfall on 25 June 2016. Rain–gauge records from nearby sites were used in the post–event analysis to assess how rainfall across the Busty Stream catchment contributed to flooding at Ightham. (p17)
Fen Pond Road, located upstream of Ightham within the Busty Stream catchment, experienced flooding on 25 June 2016 when high flows exceeded a culvert; this was reported to KCC by Ightham Parish Council and may have resulted in flooding affecting properties in the vicinity. (p23)
Flooding in the village caused by the Busty Stream and associated culvert issues, resulting in internal flooding of properties and accumulation of silt and debris in the watercourse. (p26)
Residential properties and residents in the community experienced flooding; the area has been identified as having suffered flood impacts and for follow–up community resilience support. (p27)",,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.3826754,0.3158355498459994,"<div id=""html_c21f832be016dcdd5461c41a0c609439"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hartley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface water flooding occurred in areas around Hartley during the heavy rainfall between 11:30 and 15:00 on 25th June 2016. [IMG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Hartley,England,Localised surface water flooding occurred in areas around Hartley during the heavy rainfall between 11:30 and 15:00 on 25th June 2016. [IMG] (p18),,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-KEN,10,2016-06-25,51.4422972,0.3696206,"<div id=""html_7a87e6134f3b4b650674cbdccb00b2d9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gravesend</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Gravesend area received high numbers of reports of highway flooding on 25 June 2016; these were attended by KCC Highways duty officers. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Gravesend,England,The Gravesend area received high numbers of reports of highway flooding on 25 June 2016; these were attended by KCC Highways duty officers. (p23),,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25.html,GB-KEN_10_2016-06-25,Ightham flooding 25 Jun 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Borough Green Road and Busty Lane, Ightham, on the 25th June 2016",063/Kent/Igtham-S.19-Flood-Report-Final.pdf
GB-HEF,28,2019-10-01,52.0379102,-2.7281176,"<div id=""html_5c3b5e412da00b1d9fa9d4a920e3d6a5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hereford Academy</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Three properties adjacent to the Academy flooded when private surface&ndash;water gullies serving the properties blocked, or when the surface&ndash;water drainage network (into which the roofs' downpipes discharge) surcharged during the storm. [PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties [PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Hereford Academy,England,"Three properties adjacent to the Academy flooded when private surface–water gullies serving the properties blocked, or when the surface–water drainage network (into which the roofs' downpipes discharge) surcharged during the storm. [PAG,MUL] (p3)","three properties [PAG,MUL] (p3)",GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc.html,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc,Hereford flooding October 2019 and Hereford flooding February 2020,"Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Hereford (Newton Farm, Redhill)",055/Herefordshire County/27/Hereford/Newton/Redhill.pdf
GB-HEF,28,2019-10-01,52.04170747710633,-2.7360774973892603,"<div id=""html_3a2381513a229f4ce888bd9347193540"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Newton Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flows from Newton Brook inundated properties, causing internal flooding. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Newton Brook,England,"Fluvial flows from Newton Brook inundated properties, causing internal flooding. [MUL] (p2)",2 properties [MUL] (p2),GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc.html,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc,Hereford flooding October 2019 and Hereford flooding February 2020,"Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Hereford (Newton Farm, Redhill)",055/Herefordshire County/27/Hereford/Newton/Redhill.pdf
GB-HEF,28,2019-10-01,52.0439157,-2.7299112,"<div id=""html_7ad34090d52ee611d209ae6f96231cb2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Belmont Road, Hereford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A resident reported flooding caused by an overflowing drainage system, describing 'water and sewerage' entering the property (indicative of sewer/system surcharge contributing to internal flooding). [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,"Belmont Road, Hereford",England,"A resident reported flooding caused by an overflowing drainage system, describing 'water and sewerage' entering the property (indicative of sewer/system surcharge contributing to internal flooding). [MUL] (p2)",,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc.html,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc,Hereford flooding October 2019 and Hereford flooding February 2020,"Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Hereford (Newton Farm, Redhill)",055/Herefordshire County/27/Hereford/Newton/Redhill.pdf
GB-HEF,28,2019-10-01,52.0412241,-2.7297841,"<div id=""html_bffd3f3fa4dec8add023b3e39822cc3a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Charlton Avenue, Hereford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected properties on Charlton Avenue; a resident reported flooding having come from The Great Western Way (flow route). [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,"Charlton Avenue, Hereford",England,Surface water flooding affected properties on Charlton Avenue; a resident reported flooding having come from The Great Western Way (flow route). [MUL] (p2),,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc.html,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc,Hereford flooding October 2019 and Hereford flooding February 2020,"Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Hereford (Newton Farm, Redhill)",055/Herefordshire County/27/Hereford/Newton/Redhill.pdf
GB-HEF,28,2019-10-01,52.0365377,-2.7280524,"<div id=""html_86b5625dd1af8ac6db8547bb932536d5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Falstaff Road, Hereford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water entered properties associated with a blocked gully; the report supports surface water flooding being primary with possible contribution from sewer/surcharging. [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,"Falstaff Road, Hereford",England,Surface water entered properties associated with a blocked gully; the report supports surface water flooding being primary with possible contribution from sewer/surcharging. [MUL] (p2),,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc.html,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc,Hereford flooding October 2019 and Hereford flooding February 2020,"Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Hereford (Newton Farm, Redhill)",055/Herefordshire County/27/Hereford/Newton/Redhill.pdf
GB-HEF,28,2019-10-01,52.0513692,-2.7267635,"<div id=""html_4da6367d47e09b689dd26ed4b288ca03"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Great Western Way</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Acted as a flow route for surface water that inundated nearby properties; a property off Great Western Way was reported at risk and residents reported flood flows originating from this path/route. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property at risk [MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Great Western Way,England,Acted as a flow route for surface water that inundated nearby properties; a property off Great Western Way was reported at risk and residents reported flood flows originating from this path/route. [MUL] (p2),1 property at risk [MUL] (p2),GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc.html,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc,Hereford flooding October 2019 and Hereford flooding February 2020,"Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Hereford (Newton Farm, Redhill)",055/Herefordshire County/27/Hereford/Newton/Redhill.pdf
GB-HEF,28,2019-10-01,52.0553813,-2.7151735,"<div id=""html_364140ad538bd9d551ad09c166ce2d18"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hereford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Hereford area experienced fluvial flooding from Newton Brook (two properties flooded) and surface water flooding at other local locations (reports from Charlton Avenue, Great Western Way, Falstaff Road); properties near Hereford Academy and a property off Great Western Way were affected or at risk due to topography and drainage issues. [TAB,MUL] (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties (internally flooded); 2 properties [TAB,MUL] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Hereford,England,"The Hereford area experienced fluvial flooding from Newton Brook (two properties flooded) and surface water flooding at other local locations (reports from Charlton Avenue, Great Western Way, Falstaff Road); properties near Hereford Academy and a property off Great Western Way were affected or at risk due to topography and drainage issues. [TAB,MUL] (p2)","6 properties (internally flooded); 2 properties [TAB,MUL] (p2)",GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc.html,GB-HEF_28_2019-10-01_etc,Hereford flooding October 2019 and Hereford flooding February 2020,"Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Hereford (Newton Farm, Redhill)",055/Herefordshire County/27/Hereford/Newton/Redhill.pdf
GB-KHL,1,2012-08-25,53.78632,-0.32134384,"<div id=""html_af7a45d1e885611e391e339149b260c6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Point Shopping Centre</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A commercial unit at the shopping centre was flooded due to the extreme localized rainfall, causing internal flooding of the premises. [TAB] (p5)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 commercial property [TAB] (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,North Point Shopping Centre,England,"A commercial unit at the shopping centre was flooded due to the extreme localized rainfall, causing internal flooding of the premises. [TAB] (p5)",1 commercial property [TAB] (p5),GB-KHL_1_2012-08-25.html,GB-KHL_1_2012-08-25,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 25 Aug 2012,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: City Wide Flash Flooding – August 2012,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/FloodInvestigationReportHeavyRainAugust2012.pdf"
GB-KHL,1,2012-08-25,53.7435722,-0.3394758,"<div id=""html_735c1380696e33fb025171b1b9586292"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kingston Upon Hull</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>City&ndash;wide flash flooding in August 2012 caused extensive urban street flooding across Hull. Roads had standing water deep enough to force vehicles to drive through floodwater and left parked cars partially submerged; floodwater contained foam and debris and disrupted transport and streetscape. [PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread highway flooding across the city and surface water inundation; drainage infrastructure was overwhelmed in many areas leading to flooding before drains could convey flows. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across Hull were flooded on 25th August 2012 (Table 1 lists numerous street&ndash;level incidents and the map shows a cluster of flooded points within the urban area). [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple streets within the city were flooded on 25th August 2012. Flooding affected Stockholm Road (full length), Copenhagen Road, Sutton Road, Garrick Close, Corona Drive, Ullswater Close, Lowgate, Malham Avenue, Kendal Way, Hawkshead Green, Foster Street, Princes Avenue and Calvert Lane. Flooding was associated with the local drainage system being overwhelmed, producing surface&ndash;water/urban flood impacts on the streets listed. [IMG,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the city. In 2007 drains, ditches and watercourses were overwhelmed causing flooding across large areas of Hull; flood flows exceeded drainage capacity. On 25th August 2012 an intense, localised storm caused surface water flooding concentrated in a central belt of the city, with roads affected, sandbagging and pump operations deployed by council and fire crews. [PAG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, highly localised rainfall on 25th August 2012 caused surface water (pluvial) flooding across Hull; residents and operating authorities were unprepared, prompting investigations and planned remediation measures. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>21 properties (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 1300 businesses; 8600 residential properties; 19 residential properties; 2 commercial properties [PAG,MUL] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Kingston Upon Hull,England,"City–wide flash flooding in August 2012 caused extensive urban street flooding across Hull. Roads had standing water deep enough to force vehicles to drive through floodwater and left parked cars partially submerged; floodwater contained foam and debris and disrupted transport and streetscape. [PAG] (p1)
Widespread highway flooding across the city and surface water inundation; drainage infrastructure was overwhelmed in many areas leading to flooding before drains could convey flows. (p4)
Multiple locations across Hull were flooded on 25th August 2012 (Table 1 lists numerous street–level incidents and the map shows a cluster of flooded points within the urban area). [PAG] (p6)
Multiple streets within the city were flooded on 25th August 2012. Flooding affected Stockholm Road (full length), Copenhagen Road, Sutton Road, Garrick Close, Corona Drive, Ullswater Close, Lowgate, Malham Avenue, Kendal Way, Hawkshead Green, Foster Street, Princes Avenue and Calvert Lane. Flooding was associated with the local drainage system being overwhelmed, producing surface–water/urban flood impacts on the streets listed. [IMG,PAG] (p7)
Widespread flooding across the city. In 2007 drains, ditches and watercourses were overwhelmed causing flooding across large areas of Hull; flood flows exceeded drainage capacity. On 25th August 2012 an intense, localised storm caused surface water flooding concentrated in a central belt of the city, with roads affected, sandbagging and pump operations deployed by council and fire crews. [PAG,MUL] (p8)
Intense, highly localised rainfall on 25th August 2012 caused surface water (pluvial) flooding across Hull; residents and operating authorities were unprepared, prompting investigations and planned remediation measures. [TAB] (p17)","21 properties (p4)
more than 1300 businesses; 8600 residential properties; 19 residential properties; 2 commercial properties [PAG,MUL] (p8)",GB-KHL_1_2012-08-25.html,GB-KHL_1_2012-08-25,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 25 Aug 2012,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: City Wide Flash Flooding – August 2012,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/FloodInvestigationReportHeavyRainAugust2012.pdf"
GB-KHL,1,2012-08-25,53.7474574,-0.2875034,"<div id=""html_7afcfe88972a5c4932c129aaeed48d32"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Hull Pumping Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>East Hull Northern trunk sewer and East Hull pumping station were involved in operational response during the 25 August 2012 event. The East Hull pumping station operated following standard telemetry triggers; an intermittent failure of one pump was recorded and was rectified quickly on each occasion. Inspections of branch and trunk sewers found no indications of issues on the branch sewers; some trunk/combined sewer inspections identified minor silt and fat blockages elsewhere that required cleaning. [PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,East Hull Pumping Station,England,East Hull Northern trunk sewer and East Hull pumping station were involved in operational response during the 25 August 2012 event. The East Hull pumping station operated following standard telemetry triggers; an intermittent failure of one pump was recorded and was rectified quickly on each occasion. Inspections of branch and trunk sewers found no indications of issues on the branch sewers; some trunk/combined sewer inspections identified minor silt and fat blockages elsewhere that required cleaning. [PAG] (p13),,GB-KHL_1_2012-08-25.html,GB-KHL_1_2012-08-25,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 25 Aug 2012,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: City Wide Flash Flooding – August 2012,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/FloodInvestigationReportHeavyRainAugust2012.pdf"
GB-KHL,1,2012-08-25,53.7938212,-0.3166075,"<div id=""html_e5ce775caffbc2480b0f3623c30e8b15"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Noddle Hill Way, Kingston Upon Hull</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, localized extreme rainfall caused surface water flooding on Noddle Hill Way at the junction with Kestrel Avenue resulting in inundation of properties and road flooding. [TAB] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding affected Noddle Hill Way during the intense storm of 25 August 2012; sandbags were requested for this location and council sandbag supplies were dispatched. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced surface water (pluvial) flooding of the road (and in some cases nearby properties); residents observed a drawdown in water levels as downstream pumping operations began. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported highway flooding at a low point on Noddle Hill Way with a mapped flood flow route; HCC is developing capital drainage and storage measures and a new connection to the surface water sewer to manage future events. [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Noddle Hill Way was identified for flood risk management scheme development by the council in response to surface water flooding experienced during the event, indicating it was impacted by the 25 August surface water/pluvial incident. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, localised rainfall overwhelmed highway drainage at a low spot in the road causing roadway/footpath ponding and flooding; prompted council&ndash;led works to increase highway drainage provision at the low spot. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at Noddle Hill Way during the intense, localised rainfall; the report recommends delivery of a surface water flood alleviation scheme at this location as a remediation measure following the 25th August 2012 event. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties [TAB] (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,"Noddle Hill Way, Kingston Upon Hull",England,"Intense, localized extreme rainfall caused surface water flooding on Noddle Hill Way at the junction with Kestrel Avenue resulting in inundation of properties and road flooding. [TAB] (p5)
