Subject to investigations for reported flooding caused by obstructed watercourse/culvert and silt/vegetation build–up; remedial actions included riparian clearance and planned maintenance to reduce future blockages. [IMG] (p18)",flood depths approximately 20–45cm across the area; residents displaced for 6–8 months [TAB] (p14),GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc.html,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc,Huntingdonshire flooding 23 Dec 2020 and Huntingdonshire flooding 14 Jan 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Offord Cluny & Offord D’arcy,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Offords-Winter-2020-21-Flood-Investigation-inc.-Clarification.pdf
GB-CAM,18,2020-12-23,52.2866607,-0.2096311,"<div id=""html_dd0f1cba4022d99b217515d1dcdeaeb9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Latin Close, Offord Cluny</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in the southern cluster of streets where internal property flooding was reported; ingress of water into properties persisted for days. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in the Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street (South) cluster where internal property flooding was reported. Properties in this southern cluster experienced ingress that did not recede for a number of days and some residents experienced prolonged loss of foul sewerage service. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties and gardens in Latin Close were inundated by surface water flows resulting from upstream blockage and overland flow. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in the Parkway/Elm Drive/Latin Close/New Road/High Street (South) investigation: experienced or was at risk from local flooding due to obstructed watercourse (silt/vegetation and fence obstruction); subject to inspections and riparian remediation actions. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths approximately 20&ndash;45cm across the area; residents displaced for 6&ndash;8 months [TAB] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Latin Close, Offord Cluny",England,"Included in the southern cluster of streets where internal property flooding was reported; ingress of water into properties persisted for days. [PAG] (p11)
Included in the Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street (South) cluster where internal property flooding was reported. Properties in this southern cluster experienced ingress that did not recede for a number of days and some residents experienced prolonged loss of foul sewerage service. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Properties and gardens in Latin Close were inundated by surface water flows resulting from upstream blockage and overland flow. [TAB] (p14)
Included in the Parkway/Elm Drive/Latin Close/New Road/High Street (South) investigation: experienced or was at risk from local flooding due to obstructed watercourse (silt/vegetation and fence obstruction); subject to inspections and riparian remediation actions. [IMG] (p18)",flood depths approximately 20–45cm across the area; residents displaced for 6–8 months [TAB] (p14),GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc.html,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc,Huntingdonshire flooding 23 Dec 2020 and Huntingdonshire flooding 14 Jan 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Offord Cluny & Offord D’arcy,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Offords-Winter-2020-21-Flood-Investigation-inc.-Clarification.pdf
GB-CAM,18,2020-12-23,52.2872146,-0.2055952,"<div id=""html_0be0c2333b8780c4b1d538a490835c7b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>New Road, Offord Cluny</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named as part of the Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street (South) grouping where internal property flooding occurred; affected properties experienced prolonged water ingress. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included within the southern cluster (Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street South) where internal and external property flooding incidents were reported. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on New Road were flooded due to surcharging sewer systems (sewer flooding), causing internal and external property inundation. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooding linked to adjacent watercourse obstruction and silt/vegetation accumulation; investigations conducted by RMA/LLFA with actions including vegetation clearance, removal of the obstructing fence and planned maintenance (gully cleansing and de&ndash;silting by Network Rail). [IMG] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths approximately 20&ndash;45cm across the area; residents displaced for 6&ndash;8 months [TAB] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"New Road, Offord Cluny",England,"Named as part of the Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street (South) grouping where internal property flooding occurred; affected properties experienced prolonged water ingress. [PAG] (p11)
Included within the southern cluster (Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street South) where internal and external property flooding incidents were reported. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Properties on New Road were flooded due to surcharging sewer systems (sewer flooding), causing internal and external property inundation. [TAB] (p14)
Reported flooding linked to adjacent watercourse obstruction and silt/vegetation accumulation; investigations conducted by RMA/LLFA with actions including vegetation clearance, removal of the obstructing fence and planned maintenance (gully cleansing and de–silting by Network Rail). [IMG] (p18)",flood depths approximately 20–45cm across the area; residents displaced for 6–8 months [TAB] (p14),GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc.html,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc,Huntingdonshire flooding 23 Dec 2020 and Huntingdonshire flooding 14 Jan 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Offord Cluny & Offord D’arcy,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Offords-Winter-2020-21-Flood-Investigation-inc.-Clarification.pdf
GB-CAM,18,2020-12-23,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_a93df50628606a837e115395fb8ddbc6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding affected The Offords (Offord Cluny and Offord D'arcy) due to very high runoff from intense rainfall on 23 December 2020 and re&ndash;flooding on 14 January 2021. Flooding included surface water and groundwater inundation followed by fluvial flooding; the local authority received 22 flood reports from the community across Offord Cluny and Offord D'arcy. [IMG,MUL] (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 flood reports [IMG,MUL] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"Significant flooding affected The Offords (Offord Cluny and Offord D'arcy) due to very high runoff from intense rainfall on 23 December 2020 and re–flooding on 14 January 2021. Flooding included surface water and groundwater inundation followed by fluvial flooding; the local authority received 22 flood reports from the community across Offord Cluny and Offord D'arcy. [IMG,MUL] (p10)","22 flood reports [IMG,MUL] (p10)",GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc.html,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc,Huntingdonshire flooding 23 Dec 2020 and Huntingdonshire flooding 14 Jan 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Offord Cluny & Offord D’arcy,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Offords-Winter-2020-21-Flood-Investigation-inc.-Clarification.pdf
GB-CAM,18,2020-12-23,52.371044,-0.2235779,"<div id=""html_d1a2f3b7b0b157c7c589682cb9677f1a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Huntingdonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flooding across the district on 23rd December 2020 that prompted multi&ndash;agency response, community meetings and follow&ndash;up inspections. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Huntingdonshire,England,"Severe flooding across the district on 23rd December 2020 that prompted multi–agency response, community meetings and follow–up inspections. (p15)",,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc.html,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc,Huntingdonshire flooding 23 Dec 2020 and Huntingdonshire flooding 14 Jan 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Offord Cluny & Offord D’arcy,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Offords-Winter-2020-21-Flood-Investigation-inc.-Clarification.pdf
GB-CAM,18,2020-12-23,52.287903,-0.2130961,"<div id=""html_742566ffae2e99f45e28a951beccc012"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Offord Cluny</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As part of The Offords, Offord Cluny was within the area reported to have experienced significant flooding on 23 December 2020 and on 14 January 2021. The report and accompanying surface&ndash;water risk map show built&ndash;up areas (including parts of Offord Cluny) overlap medium/high surface&ndash;water risk zones, but the report does not specify which particular streets or properties in Offord Cluny were flooded. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the settlement with internal and external property flooding in multiple areas. Three key risk areas with internal property flooding were identified (High Street North; Station Lane; Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street South). Ingress into properties did not recede for a number of days; some properties lost foul sewerage provision for about three weeks. Significant and prolonged flooding occurred along the B1043 High Street to the north, causing a full road closure. At least eight properties were rendered uninhabitable for between one and eight months. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive surface&ndash;water flooding at High Street (North) low spot and adjacent wetland overtopping, causing inundation of the B1043 carriageway, gardens and properties to the east and low rear spots. Flood depths reached approximately 45 cm; highway was closed for approximately 3 weeks; Anglian Water sewer system was impacted leaving some residents without foul sewerage for about 3 weeks. Ingress into properties did not recede for days and at least some properties were made uninhabitable for extended periods (see combined figures). [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the 23rd December 2020 event inundated properties in The Offords; residents were reported to have to leave their homes for around 6&ndash;8 months. Investigations identified a substantially blocked Network Rail railway culvert on High Street (North) as potentially critical to local flood risk and maintenance actions were confirmed. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rural road inundated with standing floodwater (photo Jan 2021); road surface damaged and scheduled for replacement after prolonged flooding and high groundwater; a Network Rail culvert in the vicinity was found to be substantially blocked and identified as potentially critical to local flood risk; some properties experienced loss of foul sewerage service associated with ingress to the sewer system. [IMG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooding prompted LLFA investigations and downstream inspections; highway response included cleansing of highway gullies across The Offords and increasing gully maintenance frequency (response to local flooding and drainage issues). [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding and sewer&ndash;system impacts from excessive rainfall: highway gullies across The Offords required cleansing and gully maintenance frequency was increased; numerous properties were left without foul sewerage for an extended period, prompting response actions. [MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding associated with the ordinary watercourse and blocked/undersized culverts on 23 December 2020. Post&ndash;event actions recorded include significant riparian maintenance and clearance of the ordinary watercourse and culverts under the railway to reduce future risk, and Parish Council work to prepare a Local Flood Plan. (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 flood reports, 29 properties flooded internally, 31 properties flooded externally, 8 properties uninhabitable, road closure lasting approximately 3 weeks (Offord Cluny and Offord d'Arcy) (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>45 cm; 3 weeks; 3 weeks; 8 properties (uninhabitable, at least) [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Offord Cluny,England,"As part of The Offords, Offord Cluny was within the area reported to have experienced significant flooding on 23 December 2020 and on 14 January 2021. The report and accompanying surface–water risk map show built–up areas (including parts of Offord Cluny) overlap medium/high surface–water risk zones, but the report does not specify which particular streets or properties in Offord Cluny were flooded. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p7)
Widespread flooding across the settlement with internal and external property flooding in multiple areas. Three key risk areas with internal property flooding were identified (High Street North; Station Lane; Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street South). Ingress into properties did not recede for a number of days; some properties lost foul sewerage provision for about three weeks. Significant and prolonged flooding occurred along the B1043 High Street to the north, causing a full road closure. At least eight properties were rendered uninhabitable for between one and eight months. (p11)
Extensive surface–water flooding at High Street (North) low spot and adjacent wetland overtopping, causing inundation of the B1043 carriageway, gardens and properties to the east and low rear spots. Flood depths reached approximately 45 cm; highway was closed for approximately 3 weeks; Anglian Water sewer system was impacted leaving some residents without foul sewerage for about 3 weeks. Ingress into properties did not recede for days and at least some properties were made uninhabitable for extended periods (see combined figures). [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Flooding associated with the 23rd December 2020 event inundated properties in The Offords; residents were reported to have to leave their homes for around 6–8 months. Investigations identified a substantially blocked Network Rail railway culvert on High Street (North) as potentially critical to local flood risk and maintenance actions were confirmed. (p15)
Rural road inundated with standing floodwater (photo Jan 2021); road surface damaged and scheduled for replacement after prolonged flooding and high groundwater; a Network Rail culvert in the vicinity was found to be substantially blocked and identified as potentially critical to local flood risk; some properties experienced loss of foul sewerage service associated with ingress to the sewer system. [IMG,MUL] (p16)
Reported flooding prompted LLFA investigations and downstream inspections; highway response included cleansing of highway gullies across The Offords and increasing gully maintenance frequency (response to local flooding and drainage issues). [TAB] (p17)
Experienced flooding and sewer–system impacts from excessive rainfall: highway gullies across The Offords required cleansing and gully maintenance frequency was increased; numerous properties were left without foul sewerage for an extended period, prompting response actions. [MUL] (p19)
The village experienced flooding associated with the ordinary watercourse and blocked/undersized culverts on 23 December 2020. Post–event actions recorded include significant riparian maintenance and clearance of the ordinary watercourse and culverts under the railway to reduce future risk, and Parish Council work to prepare a Local Flood Plan. (p25)","22 flood reports, 29 properties flooded internally, 31 properties flooded externally, 8 properties uninhabitable, road closure lasting approximately 3 weeks (Offord Cluny and Offord d'Arcy) (p11)
45 cm; 3 weeks; 3 weeks; 8 properties (uninhabitable, at least) [PAG,IMG] (p12)",GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc.html,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc,Huntingdonshire flooding 23 Dec 2020 and Huntingdonshire flooding 14 Jan 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Offord Cluny & Offord D’arcy,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Offords-Winter-2020-21-Flood-Investigation-inc.-Clarification.pdf
GB-CAM,18,2020-12-23,52.2822224,-0.2136807,"<div id=""html_d8307221da6a3f79f807f3f07ae86045"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Offord D'Arcy</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding occurred in the Offords settlement (which includes Offord D'Arcy) as a result of intense rainfall and existing ground conditions; flooding was exacerbated by high River Great Ouse levels and restricted discharge from an ordinary contributory watercourse. Large areas of the Offords are shown at significant risk from surface water flooding and parts of the built environment are in medium&ndash;high risk zones. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the settlement with internal and external property flooding in multiple areas. Three key risk areas with internal property flooding were identified (High Street North; Station Lane; Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street South). Ingress into properties did not recede for a number of days; some properties lost foul sewerage provision for about three weeks. Significant and prolonged flooding occurred along the B1043 High Street to the north, causing a full road closure. At least eight properties were rendered uninhabitable for between one and eight months. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area&ndash;wide reporting to the LLFA shows multiple flood incidents across the villages: 22 community flood reports were received; properties experienced internal and external flooding; ingress did not recede for days, some properties were uninhabitable and there were prolonged sewer and highway impacts. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the 23rd December 2020 event inundated properties in The Offords; residents were reported to have to leave their homes for around 6&ndash;8 months. Investigations identified a substantially blocked Network Rail railway culvert on High Street (North) as potentially critical to local flood risk and maintenance actions were confirmed. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rural road inundated with standing floodwater (photo Jan 2021); road surface damaged and scheduled for replacement after prolonged flooding and high groundwater; a Network Rail culvert in the vicinity was found to be substantially blocked and identified as potentially critical to local flood risk; some properties experienced loss of foul sewerage service associated with ingress to the sewer system. [IMG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooding prompted LLFA investigations and downstream inspections; highway response included cleansing of highway gullies across The Offords and increasing gully maintenance frequency (response to local flooding and drainage issues). [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding and sewer&ndash;system impacts from excessive rainfall: highway gullies across The Offords required cleansing and gully maintenance frequency was increased; numerous properties were left without foul sewerage for an extended period, prompting response actions. [MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding associated with the ordinary watercourse and blocked/undersized culverts on 23 December 2020. Post&ndash;event actions recorded include significant riparian maintenance and clearance of the ordinary watercourse and culverts under the railway to reduce future risk, and Parish Council work to prepare a Local Flood Plan. (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 flood reports, 29 properties flooded internally, 31 properties flooded externally, 8 properties uninhabitable, road closure lasting approximately 3 weeks (Offord Cluny and Offord d'Arcy) (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 flood reports; 29 properties reported flooded internally; 31 properties reported flooded externally; at least 8 properties uninhabitable for 1&ndash;8 months; full road closure lasting approximately 3 weeks; loss of foul sewerage provision for approximately 3 weeks (Offord Cluny and Offord d'Arcy) [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Offord D'Arcy,England,"Significant flooding occurred in the Offords settlement (which includes Offord D'Arcy) as a result of intense rainfall and existing ground conditions; flooding was exacerbated by high River Great Ouse levels and restricted discharge from an ordinary contributory watercourse. Large areas of the Offords are shown at significant risk from surface water flooding and parts of the built environment are in medium–high risk zones. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p7)
Widespread flooding across the settlement with internal and external property flooding in multiple areas. Three key risk areas with internal property flooding were identified (High Street North; Station Lane; Parkway/Elm Close/Latin Close/New Road/High Street South). Ingress into properties did not recede for a number of days; some properties lost foul sewerage provision for about three weeks. Significant and prolonged flooding occurred along the B1043 High Street to the north, causing a full road closure. At least eight properties were rendered uninhabitable for between one and eight months. (p11)
Area–wide reporting to the LLFA shows multiple flood incidents across the villages: 22 community flood reports were received; properties experienced internal and external flooding; ingress did not recede for days, some properties were uninhabitable and there were prolonged sewer and highway impacts. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Flooding associated with the 23rd December 2020 event inundated properties in The Offords; residents were reported to have to leave their homes for around 6–8 months. Investigations identified a substantially blocked Network Rail railway culvert on High Street (North) as potentially critical to local flood risk and maintenance actions were confirmed. (p15)
Rural road inundated with standing floodwater (photo Jan 2021); road surface damaged and scheduled for replacement after prolonged flooding and high groundwater; a Network Rail culvert in the vicinity was found to be substantially blocked and identified as potentially critical to local flood risk; some properties experienced loss of foul sewerage service associated with ingress to the sewer system. [IMG,MUL] (p16)
Reported flooding prompted LLFA investigations and downstream inspections; highway response included cleansing of highway gullies across The Offords and increasing gully maintenance frequency (response to local flooding and drainage issues). [TAB] (p17)
Experienced flooding and sewer–system impacts from excessive rainfall: highway gullies across The Offords required cleansing and gully maintenance frequency was increased; numerous properties were left without foul sewerage for an extended period, prompting response actions. [MUL] (p19)
The village experienced flooding associated with the ordinary watercourse and blocked/undersized culverts on 23 December 2020. Post–event actions recorded include significant riparian maintenance and clearance of the ordinary watercourse and culverts under the railway to reduce future risk, and Parish Council work to prepare a Local Flood Plan. (p25)","22 flood reports, 29 properties flooded internally, 31 properties flooded externally, 8 properties uninhabitable, road closure lasting approximately 3 weeks (Offord Cluny and Offord d'Arcy) (p11)
22 flood reports; 29 properties reported flooded internally; 31 properties reported flooded externally; at least 8 properties uninhabitable for 1–8 months; full road closure lasting approximately 3 weeks; loss of foul sewerage provision for approximately 3 weeks (Offord Cluny and Offord d'Arcy) [PAG,IMG] (p12)",GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc.html,GB-CAM_18_2020-12-23_etc,Huntingdonshire flooding 23 Dec 2020 and Huntingdonshire flooding 14 Jan 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Offord Cluny & Offord D’arcy,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Offords-Winter-2020-21-Flood-Investigation-inc.-Clarification.pdf
GB-NNH,24,2020-08-16,52.5020476,-0.7609373,"<div id=""html_167fd04698c80810cc0984a1ffbdfb03"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Manor House</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A large area of surface water ponding was evident over the westbound carriageway immediately in front of the Manor House (observed during the site visit). Blocked highway gullies were present nearby. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water emerged from beneath the driveway, ran down the driveway and entered the property via two back doors and the basement access; the whole ground floor and basement were flooded internally; standing floodwater remained around the property for 4&ndash;6 weeks. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A buried watercourse through the grounds of the Manor House is considered highly likely to have an underground blockage that forced water to the surface, causing it to flow rapidly down the driveway. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 road gullies blocked (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded internally; internal flood depth 4 inches; external flood depth up to 0.5 m; standing water 4&ndash;6 weeks (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Manor House,England,"A large area of surface water ponding was evident over the westbound carriageway immediately in front of the Manor House (observed during the site visit). Blocked highway gullies were present nearby. (p15)
Surface water emerged from beneath the driveway, ran down the driveway and entered the property via two back doors and the basement access; the whole ground floor and basement were flooded internally; standing floodwater remained around the property for 4–6 weeks. (p16)
A buried watercourse through the grounds of the Manor House is considered highly likely to have an underground blockage that forced water to the surface, causing it to flow rapidly down the driveway. (p19)","2 road gullies blocked (p15)
1 property flooded internally; internal flood depth 4 inches; external flood depth up to 0.5 m; standing water 4–6 weeks (p16)",GB-NNH_24_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_24_2020-08-16,Middleton flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Main Street, Middleton, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Main Street, Middleton.pdf"
