GB-DER,4,2014-07-19,52.9277763,-1.3914742,"<div id=""html_f5e671a2f41237c839adec07b7dc51cc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Huntley Avenue, Spondon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the urban catchment feeding the surface water sewer and outfall to the unnamed tributary; implicated in the surface water/urban flooding described in the report. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Huntley Avenue, Spondon",England,Part of the urban catchment feeding the surface water sewer and outfall to the unnamed tributary; implicated in the surface water/urban flooding described in the report. (p8),,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19.html,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19,Spondon flooding 19 Jul 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Sandringham Drive, Spondon 19th July 2014",033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Sandringham-Drive-Spondon-S19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,4,2014-07-19,52.9251035,-1.3910414,"<div id=""html_48a0801a2a0d468f983bfa2b88570456"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Windsor Drive, Spondon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drains to the same surface water sewer/outfall into the unnamed tributary and is therefore part of the area affected by surface water/urban flooding when the system is overloaded. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Windsor Drive, Spondon",England,Drains to the same surface water sewer/outfall into the unnamed tributary and is therefore part of the area affected by surface water/urban flooding when the system is overloaded. (p8),,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19.html,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19,Spondon flooding 19 Jul 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Sandringham Drive, Spondon 19th July 2014",033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Sandringham-Drive-Spondon-S19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,4,2014-07-19,52.9219996,-1.3921391,"<div id=""html_58190ac3eb2ead0bbe3623ca2b57fc18"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hampton Close, Spondon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by surface water flooding from the urban catchment draining to the surface water sewer and its outfall to an unnamed tributary of the River Derwent, causing inundation of the road area. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Hampton Close, Spondon",England,"Affected by surface water flooding from the urban catchment draining to the surface water sewer and its outfall to an unnamed tributary of the River Derwent, causing inundation of the road area. (p8)",,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19.html,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19,Spondon flooding 19 Jul 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Sandringham Drive, Spondon 19th July 2014",033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Sandringham-Drive-Spondon-S19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,4,2014-07-19,52.9227253,-1.393481,"<div id=""html_fd9b30c08f8797fb4a7aeeb4f4ef3df1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Beaumaris Court, Spondon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by surface water flooding from the urban catchment relying on the surface water sewer and its outfall to the unnamed tributary of the River Derwent, causing inundation at the court during the event. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Beaumaris Court, Spondon",England,"Affected by surface water flooding from the urban catchment relying on the surface water sewer and its outfall to the unnamed tributary of the River Derwent, causing inundation at the court during the event. (p8)",,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19.html,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19,Spondon flooding 19 Jul 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Sandringham Drive, Spondon 19th July 2014",033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Sandringham-Drive-Spondon-S19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,4,2014-07-19,52.9233938,-1.3963915,"<div id=""html_cb308706e92895a7f1b88b0769637f89"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dale Road, Spondon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A section of Dale Road formed part of the surface water sewer catchment and received runoff via highway drains that outfall to a ditch / unnamed tributary of the River Derwent. During the intense rainfall event on 19 July 2014 the surface water drainage on and around Dale Road conveyed runoff to the outfall, contributing to the surface water flooding that affected local roads. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,"Dale Road, Spondon",England,"A section of Dale Road formed part of the surface water sewer catchment and received runoff via highway drains that outfall to a ditch / unnamed tributary of the River Derwent. During the intense rainfall event on 19 July 2014 the surface water drainage on and around Dale Road conveyed runoff to the outfall, contributing to the surface water flooding that affected local roads. [PAG,IMG] (p8)",,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19.html,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19,Spondon flooding 19 Jul 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Sandringham Drive, Spondon 19th July 2014",033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Sandringham-Drive-Spondon-S19-Report.pdf
GB-DER,4,2014-07-19,52.9205217,-1.4057218,"<div id=""html_dddebe078590c14484efdf538cab921b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Spondon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding across the area on 19 July 2014, including internal property flooding in multiple locations and concentrated severe flooding in the Sandringham Drive area. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Up to 17 residential properties were internally flooded in the Spondon area (properties on Sandringham Drive, Caernarvon Close, Holyrood Close and Harlech Close experienced internal flooding). (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Many areas of Spondon were affected by the flooding event on 19 July 2014, including internal property flooding. The City Council identified a significant cluster of reported internal and external property and highway flooding incidents on Sandringham Drive, Caernarvon Close, Holyrood Close and Harlech Close. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced an extremely localised, short&ndash;duration intense storm (around 15:30 on 19 July 2014) that produced very high rainfall rates centred on and just southwest of Spondon. Because watercourse levels were not expected to be high, any flood impacts associated with this event would be due to rapid, localised surface&ndash;water/urban flooding rather than river overtopping. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred across Spondon during the intense localised storm, with local drainage overwhelmed and rapid runoff accumulating in low spots leading to property flooding in parts of the area. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An intense thunderstorm struck Spondon on the afternoon of 19 July 2014, producing extreme/localised rainfall that caused surface water flooding conditions in the area. (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 18 properties (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 17 residential properties (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derby,Spondon,England,"Surface water flooding across the area on 19 July 2014, including internal property flooding in multiple locations and concentrated severe flooding in the Sandringham Drive area. (p4)
Up to 17 residential properties were internally flooded in the Spondon area (properties on Sandringham Drive, Caernarvon Close, Holyrood Close and Harlech Close experienced internal flooding). (p5)
Many areas of Spondon were affected by the flooding event on 19 July 2014, including internal property flooding. The City Council identified a significant cluster of reported internal and external property and highway flooding incidents on Sandringham Drive, Caernarvon Close, Holyrood Close and Harlech Close. [IMG] (p6)
Experienced an extremely localised, short–duration intense storm (around 15:30 on 19 July 2014) that produced very high rainfall rates centred on and just southwest of Spondon. Because watercourse levels were not expected to be high, any flood impacts associated with this event would be due to rapid, localised surface–water/urban flooding rather than river overtopping. [IMG] (p12)
Surface water flooding occurred across Spondon during the intense localised storm, with local drainage overwhelmed and rapid runoff accumulating in low spots leading to property flooding in parts of the area. (p14)
An intense thunderstorm struck Spondon on the afternoon of 19 July 2014, producing extreme/localised rainfall that caused surface water flooding conditions in the area. (p16)","up to 18 properties (p4)
up to 17 residential properties (p5)",GB-DER_4_2014-07-19.html,GB-DER_4_2014-07-19,Spondon flooding 19 Jul 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Sandringham Drive, Spondon 19th July 2014",033/Derby/033/2014/Derby/Sandringham-Drive-Spondon-S19-Report.pdf
GB-NLN,2,2016-11-21,53.5894797,-0.701847,"<div id=""html_3bda0e713bdceea5a3c2d3ab14a8f800"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Doncaster Road, Gunness</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of rear&ndash;garden&ndash;connected systems led to internal flooding of dwellings on Doncaster Road after heavy rainfall combined with a temporary blockage in a foul sewer in rear gardens. (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Lincolnshire,"Doncaster Road, Gunness",England,Internal flooding of rear–garden–connected systems led to internal flooding of dwellings on Doncaster Road after heavy rainfall combined with a temporary blockage in a foul sewer in rear gardens. (p1),5 properties (p1),GB-NLN_2_2016-11-21.html,GB-NLN_2_2016-11-21,Gunness flooding 21 Nov 2016,"Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Doncaster Road, Gunness",086/North Lincolnshire/S19-Report-Doncaster-Road-Gunness.pdf
GB-NLN,2,2016-11-21,53.594097,-0.7283643,"<div id=""html_05e8b786add7fa9c825d27afbc03938d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gunness</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in the village (properties on Doncaster Road) caused by heavy rainfall combined with a temporary blockage in a foul sewer in rear gardens. (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Lincolnshire,Gunness,England,Internal flooding of properties in the village (properties on Doncaster Road) caused by heavy rainfall combined with a temporary blockage in a foul sewer in rear gardens. (p1),5 properties (p1),GB-NLN_2_2016-11-21.html,GB-NLN_2_2016-11-21,Gunness flooding 21 Nov 2016,"Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Doncaster Road, Gunness",086/North Lincolnshire/S19-Report-Doncaster-Road-Gunness.pdf
GB-NNH,13,2015-08-01,52.382254,-0.6928544,"<div id=""html_7ab61368dd1c738a7ff0d7a13963ddb0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Latimer Arts College</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sheets of surface water flowed over the asphalt courts towards Grosvenor Way; deposits of silt were present on the asphalt courts. Dished concrete channel drainage on the south boundary of the hockey courts had deposits of silt, leaves and concrete slabs, reducing capability for water to flow to underground systems and risking silt/debris being carried into pipework and causing blockages. [MUL] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Latimer Arts College,England,"Sheets of surface water flowed over the asphalt courts towards Grosvenor Way; deposits of silt were present on the asphalt courts. Dished concrete channel drainage on the south boundary of the hockey courts had deposits of silt, leaves and concrete slabs, reducing capability for water to flow to underground systems and risking silt/debris being carried into pipework and causing blockages. [MUL] (p12)",,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01.html,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01,Barton Seagrave flooding August 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave, August 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Barton-Seagrave-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,13,2015-08-01,52.38026,-0.6932222,"<div id=""html_bc9ae8452fe33d7026f7fa2cc0b1ac29"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Barton Seagrave Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff was witnessed originating from the school and flowing over ground towards the north round turning area at Grosvenor Way. [MUL] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Barton Seagrave Primary School,England,Surface water runoff was witnessed originating from the school and flowing over ground towards the north round turning area at Grosvenor Way. [MUL] (p12),,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01.html,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01,Barton Seagrave flooding August 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave, August 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Barton-Seagrave-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,13,2015-08-01,52.3628438,-0.6856391,"<div id=""html_df5ea8b0daccb23e68d6b0a4da8cb335"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Burton Latimer Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Deposits of sticks, litter and other debris were present at the northwest corner of the playing field; these appear to be deposits from a concentrated flow of surface water from higher ground. [MUL] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Burton Latimer Primary School,England,"Deposits of sticks, litter and other debris were present at the northwest corner of the playing field; these appear to be deposits from a concentrated flow of surface water from higher ground. [MUL] (p12)",,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01.html,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01,Barton Seagrave flooding August 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave, August 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Barton-Seagrave-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,13,2015-08-01,52.378741,-0.6938852,"<div id=""html_5e558c61f395af35cae97a144ba790b6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall produced excess surface water that flowed over ground following natural contours to low points on the public highway and around private property; existing drainage on higher ground could not collect and convey the runoff, resulting in internal flooding of private property adjacent to the highway. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall caused existing higher&ndash;ground drainage to be unable to collect and carry rainwater; excess surface water flowed over ground following natural contours to low points on the public highway and around private property, resulting in internal flooding of a property on Grosvenor Way. Flooding has occurred on more than one occasion in the five years prior to the investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a residential property at the north end of Grosvenor Way; repeated internal flooding incidents led to a formal investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a residential property on Grosvenor Way during heavy rainfall. Residents reported surface water runoff from the highway, private areas of Grosvenor Way, Latimer Community Arts College and Barton Seagrave Primary School flowing over the ground to the round turning area at the north of Grosvenor Way. Extent of internal flooding in August 2015 was mitigated due to alterations to the layout of rooms within the dwelling. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road gullies and the public surface water sewer could not drain the volume of water; water levels rose in the carriageway forming ponding over the entire road and footway on the north side of the round turning area. Ponding became deep enough to spill over the back of the footway towards a residential property. Surface water entered the front door on the east elevation of a residential property and flowed through the house affecting multiple rooms; water also flowed around the north of the house into the rear garden and up to external walls on the north elevation. [PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from higher ground and private property ponded on Grosvenor Way when highway drainage and public sewers could not cope. Ponding increased in depth until water flowed over the footway and entered a dwelling (via the front door on the east elevation), flowed through the house affecting multiple rooms, and also flowed around the north of the house into the rear garden up to the north external walls. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents reported flow of surface water from the hockey pitches behind the fence onto the round turning area and adjacent footpath on Grosvenor Way; images document the area where runoff has reached the path/carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave",England,"Heavy rainfall produced excess surface water that flowed over ground following natural contours to low points on the public highway and around private property; existing drainage on higher ground could not collect and convey the runoff, resulting in internal flooding of private property adjacent to the highway. [PAG] (p6)
Heavy rainfall caused existing higher–ground drainage to be unable to collect and carry rainwater; excess surface water flowed over ground following natural contours to low points on the public highway and around private property, resulting in internal flooding of a property on Grosvenor Way. Flooding has occurred on more than one occasion in the five years prior to the investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p7)
Internal flooding of a residential property at the north end of Grosvenor Way; repeated internal flooding incidents led to a formal investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
Internal flooding of a residential property on Grosvenor Way during heavy rainfall. Residents reported surface water runoff from the highway, private areas of Grosvenor Way, Latimer Community Arts College and Barton Seagrave Primary School flowing over the ground to the round turning area at the north of Grosvenor Way. Extent of internal flooding in August 2015 was mitigated due to alterations to the layout of rooms within the dwelling. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
Road gullies and the public surface water sewer could not drain the volume of water; water levels rose in the carriageway forming ponding over the entire road and footway on the north side of the round turning area. Ponding became deep enough to spill over the back of the footway towards a residential property. Surface water entered the front door on the east elevation of a residential property and flowed through the house affecting multiple rooms; water also flowed around the north of the house into the rear garden and up to external walls on the north elevation. [PAG,MUL] (p12)
Surface water from higher ground and private property ponded on Grosvenor Way when highway drainage and public sewers could not cope. Ponding increased in depth until water flowed over the footway and entered a dwelling (via the front door on the east elevation), flowed through the house affecting multiple rooms, and also flowed around the north of the house into the rear garden up to the north external walls. [PAG] (p13)
Residents reported flow of surface water from the hockey pitches behind the fence onto the round turning area and adjacent footpath on Grosvenor Way; images document the area where runoff has reached the path/carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p36)","1 property [PAG] (p6)
1 property [PAG,MUL] (p7)
1 property [PAG,MUL] (p9)",GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01.html,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01,Barton Seagrave flooding August 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave, August 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Barton-Seagrave-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,13,2015-08-01,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_301e5374cc3902db390e58be196b10b7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village of Barton Seagrave experienced a flood incident at Grosvenor Way which resulted in internal flooding of a property and led to a formal investigation by the LLFA. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,The village of Barton Seagrave experienced a flood incident at Grosvenor Way which resulted in internal flooding of a property and led to a formal investigation by the LLFA. (p8),one property (p8),GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01.html,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01,Barton Seagrave flooding August 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave, August 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Barton-Seagrave-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,13,2015-08-01,52.3783814,-0.6925942,"<div id=""html_f2dc8acf67de13863c84c3332b662939"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Barton Seagrave</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Grosvenor Way in Barton Seagrave experienced flooding and was the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council (LLFA) flood investigation report. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced heavy&ndash;rainfall&ndash;driven surface water runoff that flowed to low points on the public highway and around private property, causing internal flooding of at least one property in the settlement. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of the flood incident at Grosvenor Way: heavy rainfall and ineffective drainage on higher ground caused surface water to run to low points on the public highway and around private property, leading to internal flooding of at least one property within the village. [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contains Grosvenor Way where internal flooding of a residential property occurred; the affected area is shown to be at high risk of surface water flooding. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents and properties are described as being at risk of flooding; residents are advised to take protective actions such as implementing property&ndash;level protection and moving valuable items to higher ground. (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Barton Seagrave,England,"Grosvenor Way in Barton Seagrave experienced flooding and was the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council (LLFA) flood investigation report. (p2)
Area experienced heavy–rainfall–driven surface water runoff that flowed to low points on the public highway and around private property, causing internal flooding of at least one property in the settlement. [PAG] (p6)
Location of the flood incident at Grosvenor Way: heavy rainfall and ineffective drainage on higher ground caused surface water to run to low points on the public highway and around private property, leading to internal flooding of at least one property within the village. [PAG,MUL] (p7)
Contains Grosvenor Way where internal flooding of a residential property occurred; the affected area is shown to be at high risk of surface water flooding. [MUL] (p9)
Residents and properties are described as being at risk of flooding; residents are advised to take protective actions such as implementing property–level protection and moving valuable items to higher ground. (p14)","1 property [PAG] (p6)
1 property [PAG,MUL] (p7)
1 property [MUL] (p9)",GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01.html,GB-NNH_13_2015-08-01,Barton Seagrave flooding August 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: Grosvenor Way, Barton Seagrave, August 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Barton-Seagrave-FIR.pdf
