GB-LAN,25,2023-07-01,53.64704799368739,-2.6735276397097056,"<div id=""html_7e825dbcd1eb3286e17ed998ea114acd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chorley District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Chorley area (which contains Eccleston and Tincklers Lane) was affected by the July 2023 flooding. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Chorley District,England,The Chorley area (which contains Eccleston and Tincklers Lane) was affected by the July 2023 flooding. (p1),,GB-LAN_25_2023-07-01.html,GB-LAN_25_2023-07-01,Eccleston flooding 01 Jul 2023 - 31 Jul 2023,"July 2023 Flooding In Lancashire: Tincklers Lane, Eccleston (Chorley)",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-tincklers-lane-eccleston-july-2023.pdf
GB-LAN,25,2023-07-01,53.6505717,-2.7256101,"<div id=""html_f1234270e316cde20dbbd33f45a6fd36"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eccleston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Eccleston (Chorley) was impacted by the July 2023 flood event. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A house in Eccleston was flooded on two occasions when local surface water drainage networks were overwhelmed. Local highway drainage assets in the vicinity showed anomalies (new, unregistered gullies). Floodwater at the property contained foul contamination, though sewer monitoring indicated the local sewer did not surcharge and United Utilities found no sewer bursts or reports of sewer flooding attributable to the network. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 home flooded on 2 occasions; 2 anomalies identified with local surface water drainage assets; 1 new gully installed opposite the driveway; 3 new gullies installed adjacent to the highway [MUL] (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Eccleston,England,"Eccleston (Chorley) was impacted by the July 2023 flood event. (p1)
A house in Eccleston was flooded on two occasions when local surface water drainage networks were overwhelmed. Local highway drainage assets in the vicinity showed anomalies (new, unregistered gullies). Floodwater at the property contained foul contamination, though sewer monitoring indicated the local sewer did not surcharge and United Utilities found no sewer bursts or reports of sewer flooding attributable to the network. [MUL] (p5)",1 home flooded on 2 occasions; 2 anomalies identified with local surface water drainage assets; 1 new gully installed opposite the driveway; 3 new gullies installed adjacent to the highway [MUL] (p5),GB-LAN_25_2023-07-01.html,GB-LAN_25_2023-07-01,Eccleston flooding 01 Jul 2023 - 31 Jul 2023,"July 2023 Flooding In Lancashire: Tincklers Lane, Eccleston (Chorley)",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-tincklers-lane-eccleston-july-2023.pdf
GB-HEF,2,2020-02-01,52.1310877,-2.7605585,"<div id=""html_f10e1fedf5dda3c62c0ee7dbf600a9b8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wellington Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wellington Brook is the main watercourse through the settlement and is shown on mapping where Flood Zone 3 follows the river corridor; it is designated an Ordinary Watercourse under Herefordshire Council and is associated with the flood risk in the town. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the Wellington Brook was identified as the source of flooding for many properties in the village; almost every flooded property was within the brook's flood zone. There were multiple accounts of the brook or stream overflowing and some reports of blocked bridge/culvert or blocked gullies contributing to runoff and ponding. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flood water spilled onto the brook's right&ndash;hand bank where the bank is low; adjacent low&ndash;lying road conveyed floodwater and highway gullies and piped outfalls were submerged. Following the flooding, channel maintenance work was carried out on the length of Wellington Brook downstream of the A49. (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>seven accounts of an overflowing river or stream; four accounts of a blocked bridge or culvert; six reports of surface water runoff; six respondents noted a blocked gulley (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Wellington Brook,England,"Wellington Brook is the main watercourse through the settlement and is shown on mapping where Flood Zone 3 follows the river corridor; it is designated an Ordinary Watercourse under Herefordshire Council and is associated with the flood risk in the town. [IMG] (p1)
Fluvial flooding from the Wellington Brook was identified as the source of flooding for many properties in the village; almost every flooded property was within the brook's flood zone. There were multiple accounts of the brook or stream overflowing and some reports of blocked bridge/culvert or blocked gullies contributing to runoff and ponding. (p2)
Fluvial flood water spilled onto the brook's right–hand bank where the bank is low; adjacent low–lying road conveyed floodwater and highway gullies and piped outfalls were submerged. Following the flooding, channel maintenance work was carried out on the length of Wellington Brook downstream of the A49. (p3)",seven accounts of an overflowing river or stream; four accounts of a blocked bridge or culvert; six reports of surface water runoff; six respondents noted a blocked gulley (p2),GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01.html,GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01,Wellington flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Wellington,055/Herefordshire County/01/Wellington.pdf
GB-HEF,2,2020-02-01,52.2355219,-2.7449348,"<div id=""html_3da8bd047cc9969c186be0da1468753f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Lugg</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A backwater effect from the River Lugg contributed to the flooding in Wellington during the fluvial event. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,River Lugg,England,A backwater effect from the River Lugg contributed to the flooding in Wellington during the fluvial event. (p2),,GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01.html,GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01,Wellington flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Wellington,055/Herefordshire County/01/Wellington.pdf
GB-HEF,2,2020-02-01,52.131565,-2.7503213,"<div id=""html_bce1065e90af3c4f9a39b367526fd3ad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mill Lane, Wellington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The investigation maps show Mill Lane within the Environment Agency flood zones that follow the main river through Wellington, so the street was exposed to river (main&ndash;river) flooding associated with the key event and therefore at risk of inundation during that event. [IMG] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,"Mill Lane, Wellington",England,"The investigation maps show Mill Lane within the Environment Agency flood zones that follow the main river through Wellington, so the street was exposed to river (main–river) flooding associated with the key event and therefore at risk of inundation during that event. [IMG] (p1)",,GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01.html,GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01,Wellington flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Wellington,055/Herefordshire County/01/Wellington.pdf
GB-HEF,2,2020-02-01,52.128436787879465,-2.7480458491079505,"<div id=""html_c4daadd5060bec33df0f4a268ec5ba27"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wellington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the settlement fall within Environment Agency Flood Zone 3 along the main river corridor; areas adjacent to the river are identified at highest risk and some areas are shown as benefiting from flood defences. Wellington Primary School is shown outside the highest&ndash;risk zone. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of domestic properties across the village during the February 2020 fluvial event, caused primarily by Wellington Brook with additional surface water runoff and backwater from the River Lugg. Flood depths at ground floor ranged from under 6 inches up to 2 ft; some basements recorded depths greater than 6 ft. Flood durations ranged from less than 1 day up to 2 days. Two properties are recorded as flooding from surface water due to a bridge deck being higher than the road causing ponding when gullies blocked. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the February 2020 flood event internal flooding was reported across the village: road drainage and gullies were submerged so roads conveyed floodwater; a highway closure was implemented. Flooding depths reached up to 1 ft at ground&ndash;floor level and up to 6 ft in basements. Historic floods are recorded (one property in 1998; eight properties in 2007). (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>36 properties (questionnaire returns); 51 properties (Parish Council records); two properties (surface water due to bridge deck) (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>36 properties; depths up to 1 ft at ground level; basements up to 6 ft; 1 property (1998); 8 properties (2007) (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Wellington,England,"Parts of the settlement fall within Environment Agency Flood Zone 3 along the main river corridor; areas adjacent to the river are identified at highest risk and some areas are shown as benefiting from flood defences. Wellington Primary School is shown outside the highest–risk zone. [IMG] (p1)
Internal flooding of domestic properties across the village during the February 2020 fluvial event, caused primarily by Wellington Brook with additional surface water runoff and backwater from the River Lugg. Flood depths at ground floor ranged from under 6 inches up to 2 ft; some basements recorded depths greater than 6 ft. Flood durations ranged from less than 1 day up to 2 days. Two properties are recorded as flooding from surface water due to a bridge deck being higher than the road causing ponding when gullies blocked. (p2)
During the February 2020 flood event internal flooding was reported across the village: road drainage and gullies were submerged so roads conveyed floodwater; a highway closure was implemented. Flooding depths reached up to 1 ft at ground–floor level and up to 6 ft in basements. Historic floods are recorded (one property in 1998; eight properties in 2007). (p3)","36 properties (questionnaire returns); 51 properties (Parish Council records); two properties (surface water due to bridge deck) (p2)
36 properties; depths up to 1 ft at ground level; basements up to 6 ft; 1 property (1998); 8 properties (2007) (p3)",GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01.html,GB-HEF_2_2020-02-01,Wellington flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Wellington,055/Herefordshire County/01/Wellington.pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4038921,-0.2181221,"<div id=""html_e6d642f4ce310fc6c1f15a9f22e05baa"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Joseph Hood Recreation Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sir Joseph Hood (labelled as Joseph Hood) is within Hotspot 4 and experienced surface water/urban flooding during the extreme rainfall events, with reports of internal, external and highway flooding in the hotspot area due to sewer surcharge and surface water runoff. [IMG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Listed as part of Hotspot 4 ('MARINA AVENUE and SIR JOSEPH HOOD') impacted during the 25 July rainfall event, indicating flooding occurred on this street. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sir Joseph Hood area (labelled Joseph Hood) is part of the Hotspot 4 cluster where multiple internal, external and highways flooding reports occurred during the 25th July 2021 event. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at Sir Joseph Hood during the reported events; modelled maximum Hydromaster return period 1 in 2 to 1 in 5 yr (25 July 2021). [TAB] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sir Joseph Hood (part of Hotspot 4) experienced surface water flooding: flooding was limited to external areas of properties on 12th July 2021 and was substantially worse on 25th July 2021. [PAG,MUL] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding affected a pavilion within the park (described as a residential property within the pavilion). Much of the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial playing fields were flooded, particularly in areas closer to the river line to the west. (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding affected one residential property located within the pavilion inside Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Park; external areas of properties and much of the playing fields were inundated, particularly in areas closer to the river line to the west. [PAG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water collected on the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields and flowed northwards toward the Sir Joseph Hood pavilion, contributing to surface water flooding risk at the pavilion area and toward Marina Avenue during the 25 July 2021 rainfall event. Gully maintenance at the pavilion area was last recorded as cleaned in December 2020 and not again until October 2022, indicating they were not on a high&ndash;priority cleansing schedule prior to the event. [IMG,PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding occurred within the hotspot that includes the park during the event on 25th July 2021. Areas immediately adjacent to the pavilion were modelled at high risk of surface water flooding (EA RoFSW) and incident mapping shows flood incidents clustered adjacent to the Sports/Leisure Centre and pavilion area. (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Fields and adjacent rear external areas of residential properties (within the Sir Joseph Hood area) lie in Flood Zone 3 and were within the hotspot where fluvial flooding occurred; therefore these areas experienced fluvial flooding during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial floodwaters from Beverley Brook overflowed channel banks and entered the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields; local topography caused areas of standing floodwater to remain after the event peak. [TAB,PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields received fluvial floodwaters that overflowed from Beverley Brook and retained standing floodwater after the event peak due to topography. Poor pitch drainage increased surface water runoff during the heavy rainfall, contributing to floodwater collecting within the playing fields and flowing towards Marina Avenue. (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields ran onto Marina Avenue; a blocked outlet and frequently blocked gullies were identified as compounding local flooding. (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 residential property (pavilion) (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one residential property [PAG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (internal flooding adjacent to the playing fields) (p66)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Joseph Hood Recreation Ground,England,"Sir Joseph Hood (labelled as Joseph Hood) is within Hotspot 4 and experienced surface water/urban flooding during the extreme rainfall events, with reports of internal, external and highway flooding in the hotspot area due to sewer surcharge and surface water runoff. [IMG,MUL] (p4)
Listed as part of Hotspot 4 ('MARINA AVENUE and SIR JOSEPH HOOD') impacted during the 25 July rainfall event, indicating flooding occurred on this street. (p8)
Sir Joseph Hood area (labelled Joseph Hood) is part of the Hotspot 4 cluster where multiple internal, external and highways flooding reports occurred during the 25th July 2021 event. [IMG] (p29)
Surface water flooding occurred at Sir Joseph Hood during the reported events; modelled maximum Hydromaster return period 1 in 2 to 1 in 5 yr (25 July 2021). [TAB] (p32)
Sir Joseph Hood (part of Hotspot 4) experienced surface water flooding: flooding was limited to external areas of properties on 12th July 2021 and was substantially worse on 25th July 2021. [PAG,MUL] (p58)
Internal flooding affected a pavilion within the park (described as a residential property within the pavilion). Much of the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial playing fields were flooded, particularly in areas closer to the river line to the west. (p59)
Internal flooding affected one residential property located within the pavilion inside Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Park; external areas of properties and much of the playing fields were inundated, particularly in areas closer to the river line to the west. [PAG] (p60)
Surface water collected on the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields and flowed northwards toward the Sir Joseph Hood pavilion, contributing to surface water flooding risk at the pavilion area and toward Marina Avenue during the 25 July 2021 rainfall event. Gully maintenance at the pavilion area was last recorded as cleaned in December 2020 and not again until October 2022, indicating they were not on a high–priority cleansing schedule prior to the event. [IMG,PAG] (p61)
Fluvial flooding occurred within the hotspot that includes the park during the event on 25th July 2021. Areas immediately adjacent to the pavilion were modelled at high risk of surface water flooding (EA RoFSW) and incident mapping shows flood incidents clustered adjacent to the Sports/Leisure Centre and pavilion area. (p62)
The Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Fields and adjacent rear external areas of residential properties (within the Sir Joseph Hood area) lie in Flood Zone 3 and were within the hotspot where fluvial flooding occurred; therefore these areas experienced fluvial flooding during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p63)
Fluvial floodwaters from Beverley Brook overflowed channel banks and entered the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields; local topography caused areas of standing floodwater to remain after the event peak. [TAB,PAG] (p65)
Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields received fluvial floodwaters that overflowed from Beverley Brook and retained standing floodwater after the event peak due to topography. Poor pitch drainage increased surface water runoff during the heavy rainfall, contributing to floodwater collecting within the playing fields and flowing towards Marina Avenue. (p66)
Surface water runoff from the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields ran onto Marina Avenue; a blocked outlet and frequently blocked gullies were identified as compounding local flooding. (p72)","1 residential property (pavilion) (p59)
one residential property [PAG] (p60)
1 property (internal flooding adjacent to the playing fields) (p66)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.38407324314741,-0.22288289159351118,"<div id=""html_bcdf18c23e0736b15e7cb6687a978fcd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lynmouth Gardens</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified to be expanded/used as a flood storage area between Garth Road and Lynmouth Avenue to limit the risk of fluvial flooding and manage surface runoff in the locality. (p72)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Lynmouth Gardens,England,Identified to be expanded/used as a flood storage area between Garth Road and Lynmouth Avenue to limit the risk of fluvial flooding and manage surface runoff in the locality. (p72),,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.432678600281825,-0.22407725661071165,"<div id=""html_27e6db2c1f6ac806191891980cfa178d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Village Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Village ward lies within Hotspot 1 (Raynes Park) and experienced surface&ndash;water/urban flooding on 12th July 2021, with internal, external and highway flooding reported across the hotspot that affected access routes and properties within the ward. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Village Ward,England,"The Village ward lies within Hotspot 1 (Raynes Park) and experienced surface–water/urban flooding on 12th July 2021, with internal, external and highway flooding reported across the hotspot that affected access routes and properties within the ward. (p33)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.41891266184527,-0.2152285762079745,"<div id=""html_4ed13b35eec99b036f802ec67349d448"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hillside Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hillside ward is within the Raynes Park hotspot which was impacted by surface&ndash;water/urban flooding on 12th July 2021; the hotspot experienced internal and external property flooding and highway flooding that affected access routes within the ward. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Hillside Ward,England,Hillside ward is within the Raynes Park hotspot which was impacted by surface–water/urban flooding on 12th July 2021; the hotspot experienced internal and external property flooding and highway flooding that affected access routes within the ward. (p33),,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.41499274173446,-0.21041370263625875,"<div id=""html_633558af7776290b0a62f1b3d5c33d86"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dundonald Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dundonald ward falls within the Raynes Park hotspot; on 12th July 2021 the hotspot experienced surface&ndash;water/urban flooding including internal and external property inundation and highway flooding that impacted routes through the ward. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Dundonald Ward,England,Dundonald ward falls within the Raynes Park hotspot; on 12th July 2021 the hotspot experienced surface–water/urban flooding including internal and external property inundation and highway flooding that impacted routes through the ward. (p33),,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.39991886030812,-0.21253423147239492,"<div id=""html_c7a0a672673c103cd9d10748a2890c66"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cannon Hill Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas within Cannon Hill ward (including Cannon Hill Lane and Northway) experienced flooding during the rainfall event on 12th July 2021; extent was limited. (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Cannon Hill Ward,England,Areas within Cannon Hill ward (including Cannon Hill Lane and Northway) experienced flooding during the rainfall event on 12th July 2021; extent was limited. (p58),,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.39629146123445,-0.1930615621556989,"<div id=""html_8e56e0827dce08959b2d17f574d00119"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Helier Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Elsrick Avenue in the St Helier ward was reported as an impacted location that experienced flooding during the rainfall event on 12th July 2021. (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,St Helier Ward,England,Elsrick Avenue in the St Helier ward was reported as an impacted location that experienced flooding during the rainfall event on 12th July 2021. (p58),,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.41128536582493,-0.17363580263642106,"<div id=""html_659841d9a4814707ee0a038a47d0f545"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lavender Fields Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contains Hawthorne Avenue, which was reported as an additional location that experienced limited flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event. (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Lavender Fields Ward,England,"Contains Hawthorne Avenue, which was reported as an additional location that experienced limited flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event. (p69)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4037983,-0.2433798,"<div id=""html_1c8c3b75c25a9876528569cd4953c756"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pyl Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels resulted in backflow and a capacity issue: outfalls located along the river channel were surcharged and unable to discharge increased surface water runoff, contributing to local flooding and sewer/network capacity problems. [MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pyl Brook is identified as a source of flooding affecting Hotspots 3 and 5; the EA may have had direct responsibilities for these flood impacts. [MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted Pyl Brook behind properties on West Barnes Lane and the Westway was identified as causing flooding on those roads when river levels are high; the culvert was surveyed (January 2021) and lateral sewer connections/blockages were implicated in the flooding. [TAB] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted section of the Pyl Brook (designated as a main river) beneath Westway/West Barnes Lane area contributed to fluvial flood risk; properties along Westway, West Barnes Lane and Linkway lie within Flood Zone 3 above the culvert and highway/external area flooding occurred in this locality during the 12th July 2021 rainfall event. [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels in Pyl Brook during the 25th July 2021 storm reduced the capacity of the local sewer network (which is directly connected to the brook), contributing to sewer surcharges and flooding of adjacent roads. (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one garage outbuilding [PAG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Pyl Brook,England,"High river levels resulted in backflow and a capacity issue: outfalls located along the river channel were surcharged and unable to discharge increased surface water runoff, contributing to local flooding and sewer/network capacity problems. [MUL] (p4)
Pyl Brook is identified as a source of flooding affecting Hotspots 3 and 5; the EA may have had direct responsibilities for these flood impacts. [MUL] (p13)
The culverted Pyl Brook behind properties on West Barnes Lane and the Westway was identified as causing flooding on those roads when river levels are high; the culvert was surveyed (January 2021) and lateral sewer connections/blockages were implicated in the flooding. [TAB] (p20)
The culverted section of the Pyl Brook (designated as a main river) beneath Westway/West Barnes Lane area contributed to fluvial flood risk; properties along Westway, West Barnes Lane and Linkway lie within Flood Zone 3 above the culvert and highway/external area flooding occurred in this locality during the 12th July 2021 rainfall event. [PAG] (p56)
High river levels in Pyl Brook during the 25th July 2021 storm reduced the capacity of the local sewer network (which is directly connected to the brook), contributing to sewer surcharges and flooding of adjacent roads. (p69)",one garage outbuilding [PAG] (p56),GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.5073219,-0.1276474,"<div id=""html_903eb2d7d2e9bff706cb613430df3940"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London Region</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall on 12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021 caused widespread surface&ndash;water and urban flooding across London. Sewer networks were overwhelmed (surface water entering the surface water sewer network exceeded capacity), causing surcharging and backflow at outfalls in river channels; high river levels (notably Beverley Brook) exceeded channel capacity and caused fluvial (river) flooding to some locations within the borough of Merton. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of London experienced surface water / urban flooding during the Summer 2021 storms. In the London Borough of Merton several locations were flooded during the July 2021 events (Apostles area, Coombe Lane, Abbott Avenue, West Barnes Lane and Burlington Road). [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 25th July 2021 rainfall event produced substantial flooding across London, of which Merton was one affected borough. [IMG,PAG] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 locations [PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,London Region,England,"Extreme rainfall on 12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021 caused widespread surface–water and urban flooding across London. Sewer networks were overwhelmed (surface water entering the surface water sewer network exceeded capacity), causing surcharging and backflow at outfalls in river channels; high river levels (notably Beverley Brook) exceeded channel capacity and caused fluvial (river) flooding to some locations within the borough of Merton. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p4)
Parts of London experienced surface water / urban flooding during the Summer 2021 storms. In the London Borough of Merton several locations were flooded during the July 2021 events (Apostles area, Coombe Lane, Abbott Avenue, West Barnes Lane and Burlington Road). [PAG] (p15)
The 25th July 2021 rainfall event produced substantial flooding across London, of which Merton was one affected borough. [IMG,PAG] (p22)",5 locations [PAG] (p15),GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.37907248674538,-0.0978613186618963,"<div id=""html_50358afae6042dbcd6f3bdc2592835d7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London Borough of Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Substantial flooding occurred in the borough during the 25 July 2021 rainfall event: the southern half of the borough was primarily affected, with highways and both internal and external areas of properties flooded. Flood reports were concentrated in two hotspots in the south&ndash;west of the borough. [IMG] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35 reports; 2 incidents (reported to social media); 2 hotspots [IMG] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,London Borough of Croydon,England,"Substantial flooding occurred in the borough during the 25 July 2021 rainfall event: the southern half of the borough was primarily affected, with highways and both internal and external areas of properties flooded. Flood reports were concentrated in two hotspots in the south–west of the borough. [IMG] (p22)",35 reports; 2 incidents (reported to social media); 2 hotspots [IMG] (p22),GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.408966,-0.23054,"<div id=""html_153b61d0c8fa79a4ef35ec29d45a2e50"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Raynes Park Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surface&ndash;water sewer network at/around Raynes Park Station was implicated in flooding issues; TWUL undertook high&ndash;pressure jet cleaning and cleared silt from the surface water sewer network at the station as part of post&ndash;2021 works. [TAB,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at the railway underpass/adjacent station area; internal and external flooding incidents are shown clustering near the station and highway flooding is reported nearby in the hotspot. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the event, the surface water sewer network at Raynes Park Station required high&ndash;pressure jet cleaning and gully clearance as part of remediation for flooding&ndash;related issues. [TAB] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Raynes Park Station,England,"The surface–water sewer network at/around Raynes Park Station was implicated in flooding issues; TWUL undertook high–pressure jet cleaning and cleared silt from the surface water sewer network at the station as part of post–2021 works. [TAB,MUL] (p19)
Surface water flooding occurred at the railway underpass/adjacent station area; internal and external flooding incidents are shown clustering near the station and highway flooding is reported nearby in the hotspot. (p40)
Following the event, the surface water sewer network at Raynes Park Station required high–pressure jet cleaning and gully clearance as part of remediation for flooding–related issues. [TAB] (p46)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.432801,-0.2067157,"<div id=""html_cfc0b277da984a86bc3a427c3f44c59f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Home Park Road, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Designated Hotspot 2 on the 12 July 2021 map with clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding during the extreme rainfall event; one of the primary concentrations of reported incidents used to prioritise flood risk management. [IMG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The main surface water sewer line running below the railway behind Home Park Road suffered defects or capacity problems associated with the extreme rainfall, prompting investigations and repair actions; highway gullies on Home Park Road were also identified for priority cleansing to reduce surface&ndash;water flooding. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Designated as Hotspot 2 on the 12 July 2021 map with clustered flood reports (internal, external and highways flooding present within the hotspot area). [PAG,IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water/urban flooding affected Home Park Road during the 12 July 2021 event (Hydromaster 1 in 2 to 1 in 5 yr recorded in the report). [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highways flooding with much of Home Park Road covered by several inches of floodwater; external flooding to front and rear gardens of multiple properties; internal flooding of ground floors and basements of two residential properties (basement floodwaters reached door&ndash;handle height in one property); a surface water sewer running below Home Park Road near the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed. [IMG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Eastern portion of Home Park Road is a local low point where surface water collected and flowed through gaps between properties into rear gardens and towards the railway line; flood extents from surface water extended into the boundaries of some properties and impacted the carriageway along the eastern ~100m of the road. [PAG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Approximately 100 m stretch of the road (from the junction with Kenilworth Avenue to the junction with Vineyard Hill Road) is at high risk of surface water flooding, with high&ndash;risk extent primarily limited to the carriageway but extending into some property boundaries. A larger area is at medium risk with estimated flood extent reaching onto adjacent roads. Local surface water and combined sewers converge at the eastern end of Home Park Road; during extreme rainfall these sewers could surcharge, rendering gullies unable to take additional runoff and causing localised flooding. The surface water sewer main is constrained under the railway, likely preventing effective conveyance of high flows away from Home Park Road. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer network surcharged during extreme rainfall, causing gullies to be unable to take additional runoff and resulting in internal, external and highways flooding incidents along Home Park Road. The surface water sewer main is constrained under the railway which likely prevented effective conveyance of high flows away from the road. [IMG,MUL] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12th July 2021 rainfall event the surface water sewer system at Home Park Road was unable to cope with increased flows, with flows collecting at the eastern end of Home Park Road. The surface water sewer along Home Park Road at the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed during the event. Blocked gullies outside properties at the primary affected location on the eastern end of Home Park Road exacerbated runoff and worsened the flooding impacts; the main line below the railway is restricted, causing the system to be at high risk of surcharging during extreme rainfall. [TAB,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surface water sewer at Home Park Road was unable to cope during the 12 July 2021 rainfall: flows collected at the eastern end of Home Park Road, surface runoff accumulated in the low point and flowed through gaps into rear gardens toward the railway, and gullies outside properties were blocked, worsening flooding. The surface water sewer at the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed during the event, reducing the sewer system's ability to manage increased flows. (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flows and an overloaded/defective surface&ndash;water sewer (including the main sewer running below the railway) caused flooding and sewer surcharging on Home Park Road; gullies were surcharging and the sewer defects/capacity constraints contributed to flooding and risk to ground&ndash;floor and basement areas. [MUL] (p71)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; 4 instances of sewer flooding in postcode SW19 7 [IMG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 instances of sewer flooding reported to TWUL in the Home Park Road postcode area [IMG,MUL] (p53)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Home Park Road, London Borough of Merton",England,"Designated Hotspot 2 on the 12 July 2021 map with clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding during the extreme rainfall event; one of the primary concentrations of reported incidents used to prioritise flood risk management. [IMG] (p3)
The main surface water sewer line running below the railway behind Home Park Road suffered defects or capacity problems associated with the extreme rainfall, prompting investigations and repair actions; highway gullies on Home Park Road were also identified for priority cleansing to reduce surface–water flooding. [MUL] (p5)
Designated as Hotspot 2 on the 12 July 2021 map with clustered flood reports (internal, external and highways flooding present within the hotspot area). [PAG,IMG] (p29)
Surface–water/urban flooding affected Home Park Road during the 12 July 2021 event (Hydromaster 1 in 2 to 1 in 5 yr recorded in the report). [TAB] (p31)
Highways flooding with much of Home Park Road covered by several inches of floodwater; external flooding to front and rear gardens of multiple properties; internal flooding of ground floors and basements of two residential properties (basement floodwaters reached door–handle height in one property); a surface water sewer running below Home Park Road near the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed. [IMG] (p49)
Eastern portion of Home Park Road is a local low point where surface water collected and flowed through gaps between properties into rear gardens and towards the railway line; flood extents from surface water extended into the boundaries of some properties and impacted the carriageway along the eastern ~100m of the road. [PAG] (p51)
Approximately 100 m stretch of the road (from the junction with Kenilworth Avenue to the junction with Vineyard Hill Road) is at high risk of surface water flooding, with high–risk extent primarily limited to the carriageway but extending into some property boundaries. A larger area is at medium risk with estimated flood extent reaching onto adjacent roads. Local surface water and combined sewers converge at the eastern end of Home Park Road; during extreme rainfall these sewers could surcharge, rendering gullies unable to take additional runoff and causing localised flooding. The surface water sewer main is constrained under the railway, likely preventing effective conveyance of high flows away from Home Park Road. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)
Sewer network surcharged during extreme rainfall, causing gullies to be unable to take additional runoff and resulting in internal, external and highways flooding incidents along Home Park Road. The surface water sewer main is constrained under the railway which likely prevented effective conveyance of high flows away from the road. [IMG,MUL] (p53)
During the 12th July 2021 rainfall event the surface water sewer system at Home Park Road was unable to cope with increased flows, with flows collecting at the eastern end of Home Park Road. The surface water sewer along Home Park Road at the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed during the event. Blocked gullies outside properties at the primary affected location on the eastern end of Home Park Road exacerbated runoff and worsened the flooding impacts; the main line below the railway is restricted, causing the system to be at high risk of surcharging during extreme rainfall. [TAB,PAG] (p54)
The surface water sewer at Home Park Road was unable to cope during the 12 July 2021 rainfall: flows collected at the eastern end of Home Park Road, surface runoff accumulated in the low point and flowed through gaps into rear gardens toward the railway, and gullies outside properties were blocked, worsening flooding. The surface water sewer at the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed during the event, reducing the sewer system's ability to manage increased flows. (p55)
Surface–water flows and an overloaded/defective surface–water sewer (including the main sewer running below the railway) caused flooding and sewer surcharging on Home Park Road; gullies were surcharging and the sewer defects/capacity constraints contributed to flooding and risk to ground–floor and basement areas. [MUL] (p71)","2 residential properties; 4 instances of sewer flooding in postcode SW19 7 [IMG] (p49)
4 instances of sewer flooding reported to TWUL in the Home Park Road postcode area [IMG,MUL] (p53)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.3934831,-0.238597,"<div id=""html_b03a8138b8d5b751f07ddee781644f60"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marina Avenue, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marina Avenue is within Hotspot 4 where extreme rainfall and exceeded sewer capacity produced surface water runoff and urban flooding; reported incidents include internal, external and highway flooding clustered in this hotspot. [IMG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marina Avenue is part of the Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood hotspot where clustered internal, external and highways flood reports were recorded during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event. [PAG,IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at Marina Avenue during the reported events; modelled maximum Hydromaster return period 1 in 2 to 1 in 5 yr. [TAB,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marina Avenue (part of Hotspot 4) experienced surface water flooding: on 12th July 2021 flooding was limited to external areas of properties; on 25th July 2021 the location experienced substantial flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 25th July 2021 surface water/urban flooding caused internal flooding to two residential properties on Marina Avenue, flooding to external areas of properties, and extensive highway flooding (approximately 30 m of carriageway and 65 m of footway). [IMG] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway and property flooding along Marina Avenue during the 25 July 2021 event: internal flooding to two residential properties; external flooding to property external areas; extensive highway flooding affecting the carriageway and footway. [PAG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the lower&ndash;elevation Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields flowed northwards and collected on Marina Avenue (flat topography). The western half of Marina Avenue and rear external areas of properties along the street were at medium risk of surface water flooding during the 25 July 2021 rainfall event; internal areas of properties were not classed at medium/high risk but are adjacent to medium&ndash;high risk zones. Gullies on Marina Avenue are not included in the Council's current cleansing programme (historical cleansing priority unavailable). [IMG,PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents (internal, external and highways) are reported on the map clustered along Marina Avenue during the event; rear external areas of properties on the south side of Marina Avenue are in Flood Zone 3 and the street and its properties are in Flood Zone 2. Merton Council confirmed fluvial flooding occurred within this hotspot on 25th July 2021. [IMG,PAG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected Marina Avenue during the event; properties and the road are within EA Flood Zone 2/3 and experienced external, internal and highways flooding in locations adjacent to Beverley Brook. Merton Council confirmed fluvial flooding occurred within this hotspot on 25th July 2021. [IMG,PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents were recorded along Marina Avenue including internal property flooding and highway (road) flooding; the western half of Marina Avenue is described as an area that could be affected by sewer&ndash;related flooding during extreme rainfall, and coloured dots marking incidents are shown along Marina Avenue on the hotspot map. [IMG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial floodwaters from Beverley Brook overflowed the channel banks and caused flooding to the Marina Avenue area. [TAB,PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marina Avenue was flooded when Beverley Brook overflowed and when surface water runoff from the nearby playing fields collected and flowed onto the street during the 25th July 2021 event. Properties immediately adjacent to Marina Avenue were at risk of expansion of surface water onto them; one property undergoing renovations experienced internal flooding and had lowered ground&ndash;floor thresholds, increasing its vulnerability to floodwater. (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from adjacent sports pitches and blocked outlets at Sir Joseph Hood caused runoff and flooding onto Marina Avenue; potential compounding from a main river culvert beneath the railway was identified and recommended for survey to address river/culvert&ndash;related contributions to flooding. [MUL] (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; 30m of road; 65m of footway [IMG] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; approximately 30m of road; approximately 65m of footway [PAG] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (internal flooding, undergoing renovations with lowered thresholds) (p66)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Marina Avenue, London Borough of Merton",England,"Marina Avenue is within Hotspot 4 where extreme rainfall and exceeded sewer capacity produced surface water runoff and urban flooding; reported incidents include internal, external and highway flooding clustered in this hotspot. [IMG,MUL] (p4)
Marina Avenue is part of the Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood hotspot where clustered internal, external and highways flood reports were recorded during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event. [PAG,IMG] (p29)
Surface water flooding occurred at Marina Avenue during the reported events; modelled maximum Hydromaster return period 1 in 2 to 1 in 5 yr. [TAB,MUL] (p32)
Marina Avenue (part of Hotspot 4) experienced surface water flooding: on 12th July 2021 flooding was limited to external areas of properties; on 25th July 2021 the location experienced substantial flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p58)
On 25th July 2021 surface water/urban flooding caused internal flooding to two residential properties on Marina Avenue, flooding to external areas of properties, and extensive highway flooding (approximately 30 m of carriageway and 65 m of footway). [IMG] (p59)
Highway and property flooding along Marina Avenue during the 25 July 2021 event: internal flooding to two residential properties; external flooding to property external areas; extensive highway flooding affecting the carriageway and footway. [PAG] (p60)
Surface water runoff from the lower–elevation Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields flowed northwards and collected on Marina Avenue (flat topography). The western half of Marina Avenue and rear external areas of properties along the street were at medium risk of surface water flooding during the 25 July 2021 rainfall event; internal areas of properties were not classed at medium/high risk but are adjacent to medium–high risk zones. Gullies on Marina Avenue are not included in the Council's current cleansing programme (historical cleansing priority unavailable). [IMG,PAG] (p61)
Flooding incidents (internal, external and highways) are reported on the map clustered along Marina Avenue during the event; rear external areas of properties on the south side of Marina Avenue are in Flood Zone 3 and the street and its properties are in Flood Zone 2. Merton Council confirmed fluvial flooding occurred within this hotspot on 25th July 2021. [IMG,PAG] (p62)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected Marina Avenue during the event; properties and the road are within EA Flood Zone 2/3 and experienced external, internal and highways flooding in locations adjacent to Beverley Brook. Merton Council confirmed fluvial flooding occurred within this hotspot on 25th July 2021. [IMG,PAG] (p63)
Flooding incidents were recorded along Marina Avenue including internal property flooding and highway (road) flooding; the western half of Marina Avenue is described as an area that could be affected by sewer–related flooding during extreme rainfall, and coloured dots marking incidents are shown along Marina Avenue on the hotspot map. [IMG] (p64)
Fluvial floodwaters from Beverley Brook overflowed the channel banks and caused flooding to the Marina Avenue area. [TAB,PAG] (p65)
Marina Avenue was flooded when Beverley Brook overflowed and when surface water runoff from the nearby playing fields collected and flowed onto the street during the 25th July 2021 event. Properties immediately adjacent to Marina Avenue were at risk of expansion of surface water onto them; one property undergoing renovations experienced internal flooding and had lowered ground–floor thresholds, increasing its vulnerability to floodwater. (p66)
Surface water runoff from adjacent sports pitches and blocked outlets at Sir Joseph Hood caused runoff and flooding onto Marina Avenue; potential compounding from a main river culvert beneath the railway was identified and recommended for survey to address river/culvert–related contributions to flooding. [MUL] (p72)","2 residential properties; 30m of road; 65m of footway [IMG] (p59)
2 residential properties; approximately 30m of road; approximately 65m of footway [PAG] (p60)
1 property (internal flooding, undergoing renovations with lowered thresholds) (p66)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4095154,-0.2329622,"<div id=""html_ba9d3fff96069a8e1a6829bbc576ef3e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coombe Lane, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and extreme rainfall caused sewer capacity to be exceeded across hotspots, affecting Coombe Lane (Hotspot 1) with surface water entering the drainage/sewer network and contributing to local sewer capacity issues and flooding. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Lane was reported as an area affected by flooding (listed in the report's summary of previous Section 19 (2018) and as part of Hotspot 1 &mdash; Raynes Park &mdash; for the 12 July 2021 event). Local drainage was overwhelmed during the heavy rainfall, resulting in flooding of the street. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at a local low point (including at Raynes Park railway bridge) caused by collection of water from multiple sewers and high surface runoff; drainage discharged to a pump station but sewer capacity was exceeded so the area flooded. [PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration rainfall on 25th July 2021 produced surface water flooding in the western part of the borough. The Coombe Lane West Hydromaster gauge recorded the highest west&ndash;borough intensity during the 25 July event and is identified in the report as associated with the rainfall that caused flooding in the area. [IMG,PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Lane West gauge recorded one of the highest short&ndash;duration rainfall peaks during 25th July 2021; the location was a Hydromaster hotspot within a cluster of surface water flood reports and was associated with surface&ndash;water/flash flooding during the 25th July 2021 event. [IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Lane area (including Raynes Park railway bridge) experienced surface water flooding during the 12th July 2021 event. [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Most severe flooding occurred along Coombe Lane on 12 July 2021; floodwaters were deepest running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass. Internal flooding affected properties on Coombe Lane and commercial premises near Raynes Park railway station; highway flooding led to closure of access to Coombe Lane, impacting emergency access. [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters were deepest along Coombe Lane running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass. Internal flooding affected properties along Coombe Lane (commercial properties near Raynes Park railway station were internally flooded) and external areas of properties were inundated; highway flooding caused road closures and loss of access. [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe highway flooding with deepest floodwaters running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass; road closed to emergency vehicles, contributing to loss of access routes and meeting 'risk to residential property' and 'risk to commercial property' Section 19 thresholds. [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Lane was identified as one of the primary affected locations in the July 2021 hotspot where surface water sewers flow and converge toward Beverley Brook; the local surface water sewer network was implicated in the hotspot flooding and highway drainage (gullies) condition is noted in relation to the July 2021 flood event. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Coombe Lane area experienced clusters of flood incidents (internal, external and highways) during the July 2021 event. Highway gullies in the Coombe Lane area were part of the council's high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and had been cleaned in June 2021, but gullies below the railway had last been cleaned in March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flood, indicating parts of the local drainage/highway system were implicated in the surface water/highway flooding observed. [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flows routed downhill collected in local low points along Coombe Lane and the railway underpass, producing flooding at these low points due to steep local topography and the blocking effect of the raised railway embankment. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred along Coombe Lane during the 12th July 2021 event, with internal and external flooding incidents clustering along the corridor close to Raynes Park station and associated highway inundation near the railway underpass. [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding impacted highway drainage and the drainage network at Coombe Lane, triggering assessment of the surface water sewer and the local pump station, reconstruction/upsizing and repair of highway drainage, CCTV inspection of drains and gully inspections to address blockages and faults. [TAB] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The foul sewer network surcharged and combined sewer overflows discharged onto Coombe Lane during the 12th July 2021 event, contributing to sewer flooding and road access issues/closures. [TAB,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Many converging surface water sewers and topographic low points caused gullies to surcharge and sewer flooding during the 12 July 2021 event; surface water runoff accumulated at the low point below the railway and may have entered sewers via misconnections; a resident reported distortion of a swimming pool lining attributable to groundwater pressure. (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Lane area experienced flooding during the extreme rainfall events and was identified within the Raynes Park hotspot as requiring additional resilience measures and SuDS to reduce surface water and sewer flood risk. [MUL] (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>postcode area SW20 0: 3 instances of sewer flooding; postcode area SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding (these postcode areas cover most/all of the Coombe Lane area) [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 road closure [TAB] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (swimming pool lining distorted) (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Coombe Lane, London Borough of Merton",England,"Surface water runoff and extreme rainfall caused sewer capacity to be exceeded across hotspots, affecting Coombe Lane (Hotspot 1) with surface water entering the drainage/sewer network and contributing to local sewer capacity issues and flooding. [MUL] (p5)
Coombe Lane was reported as an area affected by flooding (listed in the report's summary of previous Section 19 (2018) and as part of Hotspot 1 — Raynes Park — for the 12 July 2021 event). Local drainage was overwhelmed during the heavy rainfall, resulting in flooding of the street. [MUL] (p7)
Flooding at a local low point (including at Raynes Park railway bridge) caused by collection of water from multiple sewers and high surface runoff; drainage discharged to a pump station but sewer capacity was exceeded so the area flooded. [PAG,MUL] (p16)
Intense short–duration rainfall on 25th July 2021 produced surface water flooding in the western part of the borough. The Coombe Lane West Hydromaster gauge recorded the highest west–borough intensity during the 25 July event and is identified in the report as associated with the rainfall that caused flooding in the area. [IMG,PAG] (p26)
Coombe Lane West gauge recorded one of the highest short–duration rainfall peaks during 25th July 2021; the location was a Hydromaster hotspot within a cluster of surface water flood reports and was associated with surface–water/flash flooding during the 25th July 2021 event. [IMG] (p28)
Coombe Lane area (including Raynes Park railway bridge) experienced surface water flooding during the 12th July 2021 event. [TAB] (p31)
Most severe flooding occurred along Coombe Lane on 12 July 2021; floodwaters were deepest running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass. Internal flooding affected properties on Coombe Lane and commercial premises near Raynes Park railway station; highway flooding led to closure of access to Coombe Lane, impacting emergency access. [IMG,PAG] (p33)
Floodwaters were deepest along Coombe Lane running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass. Internal flooding affected properties along Coombe Lane (commercial properties near Raynes Park railway station were internally flooded) and external areas of properties were inundated; highway flooding caused road closures and loss of access. [PAG] (p34)
Severe highway flooding with deepest floodwaters running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass; road closed to emergency vehicles, contributing to loss of access routes and meeting 'risk to residential property' and 'risk to commercial property' Section 19 thresholds. [PAG] (p35)
Coombe Lane was identified as one of the primary affected locations in the July 2021 hotspot where surface water sewers flow and converge toward Beverley Brook; the local surface water sewer network was implicated in the hotspot flooding and highway drainage (gullies) condition is noted in relation to the July 2021 flood event. (p36)
The Coombe Lane area experienced clusters of flood incidents (internal, external and highways) during the July 2021 event. Highway gullies in the Coombe Lane area were part of the council's high–priority cleansing programme and had been cleaned in June 2021, but gullies below the railway had last been cleaned in March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flood, indicating parts of the local drainage/highway system were implicated in the surface water/highway flooding observed. [IMG,PAG] (p37)
Surface water flows routed downhill collected in local low points along Coombe Lane and the railway underpass, producing flooding at these low points due to steep local topography and the blocking effect of the raised railway embankment. (p39)
Surface water flooding occurred along Coombe Lane during the 12th July 2021 event, with internal and external flooding incidents clustering along the corridor close to Raynes Park station and associated highway inundation near the railway underpass. [IMG,PAG] (p40)
Surface water and sewer–related flooding impacted highway drainage and the drainage network at Coombe Lane, triggering assessment of the surface water sewer and the local pump station, reconstruction/upsizing and repair of highway drainage, CCTV inspection of drains and gully inspections to address blockages and faults. [TAB] (p45)
The foul sewer network surcharged and combined sewer overflows discharged onto Coombe Lane during the 12th July 2021 event, contributing to sewer flooding and road access issues/closures. [TAB,PAG] (p46)
Many converging surface water sewers and topographic low points caused gullies to surcharge and sewer flooding during the 12 July 2021 event; surface water runoff accumulated at the low point below the railway and may have entered sewers via misconnections; a resident reported distortion of a swimming pool lining attributable to groundwater pressure. (p47)
Coombe Lane area experienced flooding during the extreme rainfall events and was identified within the Raynes Park hotspot as requiring additional resilience measures and SuDS to reduce surface water and sewer flood risk. [MUL] (p70)","2 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p33)
postcode area SW20 0: 3 instances of sewer flooding; postcode area SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding (these postcode areas cover most/all of the Coombe Lane area) [PAG] (p34)
1 road closure [TAB] (p46)
1 property (swimming pool lining distorted) (p47)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.41139,-0.2206129,"<div id=""html_495c3aa984ac83abc2b7581d503ed358"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Abbott Avenue, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme surface water runoff exceeded sewer capacity affecting Abbott Avenue (Hotspot 1); the report references attenuation tank and pumping station operation/monitoring as responses to sewer capacity issues during extreme rainfall events. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded flooding during the borough's historical incidents and again during the July 2021 events (surface&ndash;water/sewer&ndash;related urban flooding). [PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the area receiving foul sewer flow potentially increased by misconnections from recent private developments upstream of a pumping station; investigations were recommended to identify misconnections that may contribute surface water into the foul sewer system draining towards Abbott Avenue. [TAB] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Abbott Avenue experienced flooding during the July 2021 events; the report records flooding at Abbott Avenue on 12th July 2021 (Hydromaster return period 1 in 5 to 1 in 10 yr; TWUL FEH13 1 in 393.2 yr). [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway flooding resulted in closure of access to Abbott Avenue on 12 July 2021, impacting routes and emergency access. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway flooding led to closure of access along Abbott Avenue by the police, impacting access routes for emergency services. Properties in the area were affected by sewer&ndash;related flooding instances recorded in the local postcode. [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Access was closed during the event, inhibiting emergency vehicle access and meeting the 'risk to transport' Section 19 threshold. [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the July 2021 flood event (25th July 2021) Abbott Avenue experienced surface&ndash;water (urban) flooding. Highway gullies in Abbott Avenue had not been cleaned since March 2020 prior to the event; this lack of recent gully cleansing was noted in the report and may have contributed to drainage blockage and the local flooding. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway and external flooding incidents are marked on the local drainage map for Abbott Avenue. Highway gullies on Abbott Avenue had not been cleaned since March 2020 (despite earlier inclusion in the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme), which is noted as a possible contributory factor to blockage and subsequent surface water/highway flooding during the July 2021 flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Abbott Avenue experienced flooding issues leading to its addition to the high priority gully cleansing programme and to network investigations and foul sewer cleaning. [TAB] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12th July 2021 extreme rainfall event the continued operation/discharge from the nearby pump station increased flows onto Abbott Avenue, causing gully surcharging and local sewer flooding at the street. [TAB,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12 July 2021 extreme rainfall event the continuing operation of the Abbott Avenue pumping station increased flow onto Abbott Avenue, leading to gully surcharging and localised flooding of the street. (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding linked to sewer system behaviour during extreme rainfall: continued operation of the Abbott Avenue pumping station can increase flow into the road's sewer network, leading to gully surcharging and localized flooding (with potential for property backflow). [MUL] (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>postcode area SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding [PAG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Abbott Avenue, Raynes Park",England,"Extreme surface water runoff exceeded sewer capacity affecting Abbott Avenue (Hotspot 1); the report references attenuation tank and pumping station operation/monitoring as responses to sewer capacity issues during extreme rainfall events. [MUL] (p5)
Recorded flooding during the borough's historical incidents and again during the July 2021 events (surface–water/sewer–related urban flooding). [PAG,MUL] (p16)
Identified as the area receiving foul sewer flow potentially increased by misconnections from recent private developments upstream of a pumping station; investigations were recommended to identify misconnections that may contribute surface water into the foul sewer system draining towards Abbott Avenue. [TAB] (p20)
Abbott Avenue experienced flooding during the July 2021 events; the report records flooding at Abbott Avenue on 12th July 2021 (Hydromaster return period 1 in 5 to 1 in 10 yr; TWUL FEH13 1 in 393.2 yr). [TAB] (p31)
Highway flooding resulted in closure of access to Abbott Avenue on 12 July 2021, impacting routes and emergency access. [PAG] (p33)
Highway flooding led to closure of access along Abbott Avenue by the police, impacting access routes for emergency services. Properties in the area were affected by sewer–related flooding instances recorded in the local postcode. [PAG] (p34)
Access was closed during the event, inhibiting emergency vehicle access and meeting the 'risk to transport' Section 19 threshold. [PAG] (p35)
During the July 2021 flood event (25th July 2021) Abbott Avenue experienced surface–water (urban) flooding. Highway gullies in Abbott Avenue had not been cleaned since March 2020 prior to the event; this lack of recent gully cleansing was noted in the report and may have contributed to drainage blockage and the local flooding. (p37)
Highway and external flooding incidents are marked on the local drainage map for Abbott Avenue. Highway gullies on Abbott Avenue had not been cleaned since March 2020 (despite earlier inclusion in the high–priority cleansing programme), which is noted as a possible contributory factor to blockage and subsequent surface water/highway flooding during the July 2021 flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p38)
Abbott Avenue experienced flooding issues leading to its addition to the high priority gully cleansing programme and to network investigations and foul sewer cleaning. [TAB] (p45)
During the 12th July 2021 extreme rainfall event the continued operation/discharge from the nearby pump station increased flows onto Abbott Avenue, causing gully surcharging and local sewer flooding at the street. [TAB,PAG] (p46)
During the 12 July 2021 extreme rainfall event the continuing operation of the Abbott Avenue pumping station increased flow onto Abbott Avenue, leading to gully surcharging and localised flooding of the street. (p47)
Experienced flooding linked to sewer system behaviour during extreme rainfall: continued operation of the Abbott Avenue pumping station can increase flow into the road's sewer network, leading to gully surcharging and localized flooding (with potential for property backflow). [MUL] (p70)",postcode area SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding [PAG] (p34),GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4088503,-0.229751,"<div id=""html_16724fa2fc2249730b00305523ab5a1f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Approach Road, Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The pumping station below the railway at Approach Road was affected by sewer capacity issues caused by extreme surface water ingress during the July 2021 rainfall events; it was upgraded after the events and is subject to routine monitoring. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The railway underpass at Approach Road was closed due to highway flooding on 12th July 2021, cutting an access route and affecting emergency service access. (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Underpass became impassable due to deep floodwater; vehicles were partially submerged and immobilised, presenting a hazard and inhibiting road access for emergency services. [IMG,PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The railway underpass at Approach Road was closed during the event due to flood waters limiting road access (closure by the Metropolitan Police Service during the 12th July 2021 event). [TAB] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The upgraded pumping station below the railway at Approach Road was implicated in local flood performance issues; monitoring was recommended to ensure optimal operation to prevent sewer surcharging and local flooding at the low point beneath the railway. [MUL] (p71)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Approach Road, Cottenham Park",England,"The pumping station below the railway at Approach Road was affected by sewer capacity issues caused by extreme surface water ingress during the July 2021 rainfall events; it was upgraded after the events and is subject to routine monitoring. [MUL] (p5)
The railway underpass at Approach Road was closed due to highway flooding on 12th July 2021, cutting an access route and affecting emergency service access. (p34)
Underpass became impassable due to deep floodwater; vehicles were partially submerged and immobilised, presenting a hazard and inhibiting road access for emergency services. [IMG,PAG] (p35)
The railway underpass at Approach Road was closed during the event due to flood waters limiting road access (closure by the Metropolitan Police Service during the 12th July 2021 event). [TAB] (p46)
The upgraded pumping station below the railway at Approach Road was implicated in local flood performance issues; monitoring was recommended to ensure optimal operation to prevent sewer surcharging and local flooding at the low point beneath the railway. [MUL] (p71)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.413675,-0.2216815,"<div id=""html_f18fcf7efb57d050e39b328481ee549d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Downs Road, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer network capacity along Lower Downs Road was exceeded during the extreme rainfall events, prompting recommendations to investigate and upgrade sewer capacity to address flooding risk. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Downs Road was affected by flooding on 12th July 2021 (reported together with Worple Road). The Hydromaster return period for the event is 1 in 10 to 1 in 20 yr; TWUL FEH13 1 in 177.86 yr. [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The railway underpass at Lower Downs Road was flooded and closed during the 12 July 2021 event, contributing to loss of access and impacting emergency routes; nearby property basements (Worple Road junction area) were also affected. [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Lower Downs Road railway underpass was inundated on 12th July 2021 with deep floodwater (photo evidence) that reached wheel&ndash;axle depths on a vehicle; the underpass/highway was closed by the MPS, restricting access and impeding emergency services. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Railway underpass and road access were closed due to flooding, restricting emergency vehicle access; the location lies within postcode SW20 8 which had recorded sewer flooding during the event. [PAG,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water sewers from adjacent streets converge onto Lower Downs Road and flow toward the railway underpass; highway gullies on Lower Downs Road were part of the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme in 2018/19&ndash;2019/20 but had last been routinely cleaned in March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flooding. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flows were directed into the local low point at the Lower Downs Road railway underpass (compounded by the raised railway embankment), resulting in collection of runoff and recorded highway/external flooding incidents within the hotspot. Map16 data indicate gullies on Lower Downs Road had last been routinely cleaned in March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows were routed along Worple Road into Lower Downs Road during the 12th July 2021 event, collecting at the local low point at the Lower Downs Road railway underpass; the raised railway embankment redirected flows into this low point, causing accumulation and highway/underpass flooding. [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Downs Road was targeted for gully cleansing and added to a high&ndash;priority gully cleansing programme as a risk management action related to surface water/flooding (gullies cleaned in early and late 2017, late 2018, early 2019 and early 2020). [TAB] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced gullies surcharging toward the topographic low below the railway; the railway underpass at Lower Downs Road was closed due to flood waters. [TAB,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from higher elevations flowed towards the low point along Lower Downs Road below the railway, compounding an over&ndash;capacity sewer network and causing gully surcharging during the 12 July 2021 event; recommendations noted upgrading surface water sewer capacity to limit future gully surcharging. [PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gully surcharging and inadequate surface water sewer capacity resulted in surface water collecting at the local low point below the railway; upgrades to the surface water sewer and increased gully cleansing were recommended to limit future roadway flooding. [MUL] (p71)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding [PAG,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 railway underpass closure [TAB,PAG] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Lower Downs Road, Raynes Park",England,"Sewer network capacity along Lower Downs Road was exceeded during the extreme rainfall events, prompting recommendations to investigate and upgrade sewer capacity to address flooding risk. [MUL] (p5)
Lower Downs Road was affected by flooding on 12th July 2021 (reported together with Worple Road). The Hydromaster return period for the event is 1 in 10 to 1 in 20 yr; TWUL FEH13 1 in 177.86 yr. [TAB] (p31)
The railway underpass at Lower Downs Road was flooded and closed during the 12 July 2021 event, contributing to loss of access and impacting emergency routes; nearby property basements (Worple Road junction area) were also affected. [IMG,PAG] (p33)
The Lower Downs Road railway underpass was inundated on 12th July 2021 with deep floodwater (photo evidence) that reached wheel–axle depths on a vehicle; the underpass/highway was closed by the MPS, restricting access and impeding emergency services. [IMG,PAG] (p34)
Railway underpass and road access were closed due to flooding, restricting emergency vehicle access; the location lies within postcode SW20 8 which had recorded sewer flooding during the event. [PAG,MUL] (p35)
Surface water sewers from adjacent streets converge onto Lower Downs Road and flow toward the railway underpass; highway gullies on Lower Downs Road were part of the high–priority cleansing programme in 2018/19–2019/20 but had last been routinely cleaned in March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flooding. (p38)
Surface water flows were directed into the local low point at the Lower Downs Road railway underpass (compounded by the raised railway embankment), resulting in collection of runoff and recorded highway/external flooding incidents within the hotspot. Map16 data indicate gullies on Lower Downs Road had last been routinely cleaned in March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p39)
Flows were routed along Worple Road into Lower Downs Road during the 12th July 2021 event, collecting at the local low point at the Lower Downs Road railway underpass; the raised railway embankment redirected flows into this low point, causing accumulation and highway/underpass flooding. [PAG] (p40)
Lower Downs Road was targeted for gully cleansing and added to a high–priority gully cleansing programme as a risk management action related to surface water/flooding (gullies cleaned in early and late 2017, late 2018, early 2019 and early 2020). [TAB] (p45)
Area experienced gullies surcharging toward the topographic low below the railway; the railway underpass at Lower Downs Road was closed due to flood waters. [TAB,PAG] (p46)
Surface water runoff from higher elevations flowed towards the low point along Lower Downs Road below the railway, compounding an over–capacity sewer network and causing gully surcharging during the 12 July 2021 event; recommendations noted upgrading surface water sewer capacity to limit future gully surcharging. [PAG] (p47)
Gully surcharging and inadequate surface water sewer capacity resulted in surface water collecting at the local low point below the railway; upgrades to the surface water sewer and increased gully cleansing were recommended to limit future roadway flooding. [MUL] (p71)","SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding [PAG,MUL] (p35)
1 railway underpass closure [TAB,PAG] (p46)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.400709,-0.233575,"<div id=""html_f63c11457061a7309e0bb39c18ba1b8f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Westway, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The main surface water sewer at Westway was overloaded/insufficient during the rainfall events; report recommends upsizing the main surface water sewer and increasing inspection frequency to reduce future surface&ndash;water/sewer flooding at this location. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported in the report as a flooded location investigated (non&ndash;Section 19 investigation) under Hotspot 3 (West Barnes) associated with the 12 July rainfall event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Westway when river levels rose due to the culverted Pyl Brook and its lateral connection with the sewer network; blockages of the surface water line into the culvert (caused by a private extension) were identified as contributing to the flooding. [TAB] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway flooding and flooding of external areas of properties occurred along Westway during the 12th July 2021 event; gullies on Westway had been included in a high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and most recently cleaned in Sept 2020. Access for emergency vehicles remained possible. [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The main surface&ndash;water sewer at Westway exhibited capacity and backflow issues contributing to flooding; proposed actions include increased inspection frequency and enlarging the sewer to limit backflows and increase capacity. [MUL] (p71)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Westway, London Borough of Merton",England,"The main surface water sewer at Westway was overloaded/insufficient during the rainfall events; report recommends upsizing the main surface water sewer and increasing inspection frequency to reduce future surface–water/sewer flooding at this location. [MUL] (p6)
