GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.3994765,-0.2437999,"<div id=""html_95958968c78c3bb20910af2efe193457"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Shannon Corner, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Shannon Corner Junction is mentioned (as part of Burlington Road) in the previous Section 19 summary and in non&ndash;section 19 investigations under Hotspot 3 (West Barnes) for the 12 July 2021 event, indicating the junction experienced flooding during the heavy rainfall when local drainage was overwhelmed. [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a flooded junction (Shannon Corner) and investigated (non&ndash;Section 19) as part of Hotspot 3 in the 12 July 2021 event. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Burlington Road and Shannon Corner junction experienced surface water flooding during the 12th July 2021 and 25th July 2021 events. [TAB,MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Junction area (Burlington Road and Shannon Corner) identified as primarily affected in the 12th July 2021 event; experienced highway/external area flooding. (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12th July 2021 event flooding was constrained to highways at the eastern end of Burlington Road adjacent to the Shannon Corner roundabout. The likely mechanism was sewer surcharge/backflow caused by limited gradient and heavy siltation of local surface water sewers; surface water flooding was also a contributory factor. Access for emergency vehicles remained possible. [PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Shannon Corner junction (including the roundabout) is part of the area that flooded during the 12th July 2021 event; flooding was likely linked to silted and limited&ndash;gradient surface water sewers causing surcharges/backflows, with high surface&ndash;water flood risk contributing. [PAG] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction experienced flooding during the extreme rainfall events; flooding was more substantial on 12th July 2021 with fewer reports on 25th July 2021. [MUL] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and poor infiltration at the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction and within the Shannon Corner roundabout contributed to flooding risk; SuDS (permeable paving) is recommended to improve infiltration and reduce runoff that causes flooding and highway impacts. [MUL] (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three previous rainfall incidents [PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three previous rainfall incidents [PAG] (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Shannon Corner, London Borough of Merton",England,"Shannon Corner Junction is mentioned (as part of Burlington Road) in the previous Section 19 summary and in non–section 19 investigations under Hotspot 3 (West Barnes) for the 12 July 2021 event, indicating the junction experienced flooding during the heavy rainfall when local drainage was overwhelmed. [PAG,MUL] (p7)
Identified as a flooded junction (Shannon Corner) and investigated (non–Section 19) as part of Hotspot 3 in the 12 July 2021 event. [PAG] (p8)
Burlington Road and Shannon Corner junction experienced surface water flooding during the 12th July 2021 and 25th July 2021 events. [TAB,MUL] (p31)
Junction area (Burlington Road and Shannon Corner) identified as primarily affected in the 12th July 2021 event; experienced highway/external area flooding. (p56)
During the 12th July 2021 event flooding was constrained to highways at the eastern end of Burlington Road adjacent to the Shannon Corner roundabout. The likely mechanism was sewer surcharge/backflow caused by limited gradient and heavy siltation of local surface water sewers; surface water flooding was also a contributory factor. Access for emergency vehicles remained possible. [PAG] (p57)
The Shannon Corner junction (including the roundabout) is part of the area that flooded during the 12th July 2021 event; flooding was likely linked to silted and limited–gradient surface water sewers causing surcharges/backflows, with high surface–water flood risk contributing. [PAG] (p58)
The Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction experienced flooding during the extreme rainfall events; flooding was more substantial on 12th July 2021 with fewer reports on 25th July 2021. [MUL] (p69)
Surface water runoff and poor infiltration at the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction and within the Shannon Corner roundabout contributed to flooding risk; SuDS (permeable paving) is recommended to improve infiltration and reduce runoff that causes flooding and highway impacts. [MUL] (p72)","three previous rainfall incidents [PAG] (p57)
three previous rainfall incidents [PAG] (p58)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.3833774,-0.2239164,"<div id=""html_b18ee892371e0b8232edf9b76e57fe15"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Garth Road, Lower Morden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road and adjacent properties affected by flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event (listed as a non&ndash;Section 19 flooded location under Hotspot 5 &ndash; Lower Morden). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred at Garth Road during the reported events; modelled maximum Hydromaster return period 1 in 2 to 1 in 5 yr (25 July 2021). [TAB] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooded highways and external property areas along Garth Road. One instance of internal flooding occurred to an extension within a residential property at the southern end of Garth Road. [PAG] (p67)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul&ndash;water surcharges from the surface&ndash;water/sewer network on Garth Road occurred during the 25th July 2021 storm and flowed down to surrounding roads due to local topography; the event triggered recommendations for increased inspection and clearing of the surface&ndash;water sewer on Garth Road. (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging and backflow of the surface water/sewer network along Garth Road leading to flooding risk to the highway and nearby properties; increased inspection and clearing of the surface water sewer network was recommended to limit surcharges. [MUL] (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one internal property extension flooded [PAG] (p67)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Garth Road, Lower Morden",England,"Road and adjacent properties affected by flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event (listed as a non–Section 19 flooded location under Hotspot 5 – Lower Morden). (p8)
