GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4098622,-0.2281921,"<div id=""html_054a5f71755bc8edc9927015849769e8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pepys Road, Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pepys Road forms part of the converging surface water sewer network (including a 1120mm combined runoff conduit along Pepys Road toward Coombe Lane) and was within the Coombe Lane hotspot affected by the July 2021 surface water/sewer&ndash;related flooding. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pepys Road is identified as a road with high surface water flood risk adjacent to the Coombe Lane/Raynes Park corridor and was affected by surface water flooding during the hotspot event. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Council requested funding for SuDS features proposed along Pepys Road as a response to flooding at the Raynes Park hotspot. [TAB] (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Pepys Road, Cottenham Park",England,"Pepys Road forms part of the converging surface water sewer network (including a 1120mm combined runoff conduit along Pepys Road toward Coombe Lane) and was within the Coombe Lane hotspot affected by the July 2021 surface water/sewer–related flooding. (p36)
Pepys Road is identified as a road with high surface water flood risk adjacent to the Coombe Lane/Raynes Park corridor and was affected by surface water flooding during the hotspot event. (p40)
Council requested funding for SuDS features proposed along Pepys Road as a response to flooding at the Raynes Park hotspot. [TAB] (p45)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4121557,-0.2311322,"<div id=""html_d606517e0eb270e2e269e339d66473f8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lambton Road, Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lambton Road lies on flow paths of local surface water sewers that drain toward Coombe Lane and was part of the Coombe Lane hotspot area affected by the July 2021 surface water flooding, with sewer pipes from Lambton Road joining the network that was stressed during the event. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Lambton Road were within the high&ndash;risk surface water flood area associated with the Coombe Lane/Raynes Park hotspot and were affected by surface water flows during the hotspot event. (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Lambton Road, Cottenham Park",England,"Lambton Road lies on flow paths of local surface water sewers that drain toward Coombe Lane and was part of the Coombe Lane hotspot area affected by the July 2021 surface water flooding, with sewer pipes from Lambton Road joining the network that was stressed during the event. (p36)
Parts of Lambton Road were within the high–risk surface water flood area associated with the Coombe Lane/Raynes Park hotspot and were affected by surface water flows during the hotspot event. (p40)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4102003,-0.2266002,"<div id=""html_d6b61df1b8061dcf1988fa5646f84033"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wyke Road, Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration July 2021 storm produced high runoff into the local surface water sewer network that runs along Wyke Road; converging surface water pipes and nearby gullies (some last cleaned March 2020 below the railway) were part of the drainage system implicated in the July 2021 flooding, causing surface water/sewer flooding along Wyke Road. (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Wyke Road, Cottenham Park",England,"Intense short–duration July 2021 storm produced high runoff into the local surface water sewer network that runs along Wyke Road; converging surface water pipes and nearby gullies (some last cleaned March 2020 below the railway) were part of the drainage system implicated in the July 2021 flooding, causing surface water/sewer flooding along Wyke Road. (p36)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.414624,-0.2202144,"<div id=""html_ef15076c27dc2ab86cd7c596303b7dd3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Worple Road, Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Worple Road was affected by flooding on 12th July 2021 (grouped with Lower Downs Road in the report). The Hydromaster return period for the event is given as 1 in 10 to 1 in 20 yr; TWUL FEH13 1 in 177.86 yr. [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement areas of properties along Worple Road near the junction with Lower Downs Road were internally flooded during the 12 July 2021 event; external flooding also affected property exteriors and highway access was disrupted. [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement areas of properties along Worple Road near the junction with Lower Downs Road were flooded. Highway flooding also contributed to closures and impacted access in the locality. [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road closures due to highway/underpass flooding that inhibited access for emergency vehicles; part of the area sits within postcode SW20 8 which experienced sewer flooding during the event. [PAG,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water pipes from Worple Road converge with other local surface water sewers at the Worple Road / Pepys Road junction; these sewer flows were implicated in the Coombe Lane hotspot flooding during the July 2021 event, resulting in surface water/sewer flooding affecting the Worple Road area. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local surface water sewer network along Worple Road conveys flows eastwards; highway gullies on Worple Road were on Merton&#8217;s high&ndash;priority cleansing programme and were last recorded as cleaned in June 2021 prior to the July 2021 flooding. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from higher ground concentrated onto Worple Road causing highway flooding within the Raynes Park hotspot. Map16 gully&ndash;sensor data show Worple Road gullies were on Merton Council's high&ndash;priority cleansing programme but were last cleaned in June 2021 prior to the July 2021 flooding incident. [IMG,PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Worple Road is identified as part of the corridor receiving overland flows from higher ground; parts of Worple Road are shown as high&ndash;risk for surface water flooding and flows were directed toward local low points (contributing to flooding at the hotspot). [PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Council requested funding for SuDS features proposed along Worple Road as a response to flooding at the Raynes Park hotspot. [TAB] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along Worple Road (near the junction with Lower Downs Road) were impacted by sewer backflows caused by overflows in the combined sewer network; the road was also closed during the flood. [TAB,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along Worple Road near the junction with Lower Downs Road were impacted by sewer backflows caused by overflows within the combined sewer network during the 12 July 2021 event; gullies also surcharged in the area. (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall increased flows into the Worple Road sewer network and manholes, contributing to surcharging and local flood risk; monitoring and upstream storage were recommended to keep extreme flows away from nearby roads and properties. [MUL] (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>postcode area SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding [PAG,MUL] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Worple Road, Raynes Park",England,"Worple Road was affected by 