GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.42544863785698,-0.20253296090838302,"<div id=""html_974931818d70e31a5e36077c780121bd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rostrevor Road, Wimbledon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported as one of the additional locations that experienced flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event; flooding reports were few and extent was not sufficient to trigger a full Section 19 investigation. (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Rostrevor Road, Wimbledon",England,Reported as one of the additional locations that experienced flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event; flooding reports were few and extent was not sufficient to trigger a full Section 19 investigation. (p69),,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4087094,-0.1767347,"<div id=""html_ab8c2ee38491f581f958b01e1a573cf3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hawthorne Avenue, London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified in the report as an additional location that experienced flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event; the number of reports and extent were limited and did not meet the threshold for a full Section 19 investigation. (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,"Hawthorne Avenue, London Borough of Merton",England,Identified in the report as an additional location that experienced flooding during the 25th July 2021 rainfall event; the number of reports and extent were limited and did not meet the threshold for a full Section 19 investigation. (p69),,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.41086985,-0.18809708858824303,"<div id=""html_7cc7f4b70dc249c4d09c596dec998d63"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London Borough of Merton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding concentrated in western and south&ndash;western parts of Merton during the extreme rainfall events; the report identifies clustered hotspots (e.g. Raynes Park, Home Park Road, West Barnes) where internal, external and highways flooding were recorded. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the borough during extreme rainfall on 12 and 25 July 2021. Sewer capacity was exceeded as large volumes of surface water entered the surface water network; high river levels (Pyl Brook and Beverley Brook) caused backflow, surcharged outfalls and fluvial flooding in some locations. Local factors (topography, gully blockages, sewer configuration) compounded impacts. [PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall caused widespread surface water flooding and sewer capacity exceedance within the borough; high river levels on Beverley Brook caused fluvial flooding in parts of the borough and outfalls were surcharged leading to backflow issues. [PAG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced multiple surface water / sewer&ndash;related flooding hotspots during the July rainfall events, prompting borough&ndash;wide recommendations (sewer capacity upgrades, SuDS retrofits, increased gully cleansing and inspection programmes) to reduce recurrence. [PAG,MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations within the borough are identified as hotspots and affected locations in the report for the rainfall events of 12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021. [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Merton borough area experienced flooding associated with the 12th July 2021 and 25th July 2021 heavy rainfall events, with multiple hotspots and non&ndash;Section 19 flooded locations documented. [PAG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two significant flood events in the borough produced a total of 112 reports of flooding; nine properties were internally flooded; one highway incident impeded road access to emergency vehicles across five roads; reports originated from five hotspot locations (four in the western half of the borough and one in the north&ndash;east). [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Beverley Brook is reported to run adjacent to Hotspot 4 and is identified in the report as potentially contributing to flooding at that hotspot during the investigated events. [PAG,MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood incidents affected the borough: three events in June 2016 and July and August 2017 reached the Section 19 investigation threshold, and several locations that had previously flooded were affected again during the July 2021 storms. Named locations affected in July 2021 include the Apostles area, Coombe Lane, Abbott Avenue, West Barnes Lane and Burlington Road. [PAG,MUL] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across the borough experienced surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding during the recorded incidents; these reached the Section 19 investigation threshold and prompted borough&ndash;level investigation and follow&ndash;up. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced surface water runoff overloading foul sewers that caused sewage flooding onto streets and into homes in the borough during the 2021 events; borough&ndash;level activity with partners (TWUL and Merton Council) has since delivered clearance, inspections and pump upgrades to reduce recurrence. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding on 12 July 2021 causing substantial disruption to roads across the borough, temporarily limiting road access to Category One responder vehicles and private vehicles; flooding reported to highways and to internal and external areas of properties. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface&ndash;water/urban flooding across the borough on 12 July 2021: substantial disruption to roads (temporary limitation of access to Category One responder vehicles and private vehicles in some locations); highways and both internal and external areas of properties reported flooded. Flooding was concentrated primarily in four hotspots across the west and north&ndash;east of the borough. [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 25th July 2021 caused surface&ndash;water/urban flooding across the borough, primarily affecting the southern half. Highways and both internal and external areas of properties were flooded. Most reports were concentrated in two hotspots in the south&ndash;western corner of the borough. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense July 2021 rainfall led to surface&ndash;water / urban flooding across the borough; the two events (12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021) prompted monitoring and assessment using EA and Hydromaster rain gauges. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall on 12th July and 25th July led to surface water / urban flooding across the borough. On 25th July the report states the rainfall that caused flooding entered the northern areas by about 13:50 and reached the southern areas by about 14:00. The 12th July event produced highly variable, intense downpours (most intense in the north&ndash;west) that contributed to borough&ndash;wide surface water flooding. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced clustered surface water/urban flooding during the intense rainfall on 12th and 25th July 2021, with multiple streets reporting flooding and a number of internal property floodings. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread reports of internal, external and highways flooding across the borough during the two July 2021 rainfall events; reports were clustered into hotspots and 14 distinct flooded locations were recorded enabling this Section 19 investigation. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during the storm events, triggering Section 19 investigation criteria. The report distinguishes 14 different locations across seven hydrological catchments and five hotspots where flood reports were concentrated. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The administrative area experienced multiple surface&ndash;water/urban flood hotspots during the 2021 summer storm events identified in the report. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 12th July 2021 parts of the London Borough of Merton (Hotspot 1: Raynes Park) experienced surface water flooding that caused internal flooding to properties (including two residential properties), commercial property flooding along Coombe Lane, basement flooding on Worple Road and highway flooding leading to road and underpass closures which affected emergency access. [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 12 July 2021 a flooding hotspot in Merton caused internal flooding to properties (residential and commercial), basement flooding on Worple Road, severe highway flooding in underpasses (e.g., Lower Downs Road underpass) and closures that disrupted emergency access. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced an intense July 2021 rainfall hotspot that caused surface water / urban flooding across the hotspot area; local drainage networks were stressed and the event is treated as a primary affected hotspot in the borough&ndash;level investigation. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Merton Council&#8217;s gully&ndash;cleansing programme was changed after a 2016 flooding incident; Map16 data supplied by the council show gullies in Abbott Avenue and other local roads were on the high&ndash;priority cleansing programme in 2018/19&ndash;2019/20. Map16 also shows Abbott Avenue gullies had not been cleaned since March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas within the borough (Raynes Park, Abbott Avenue, Coombe Lane) experienced surface water/sewer flooding, triggering borough&ndash;led investigations, cleansing and infrastructure works. [TAB,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough hotspot experienced sewer surcharging with blown manhole covers, combined sewer overflow discharge and foul water within property boundaries; intense rainfall&ndash;driven surface water runoff flowed to low points (including Coombe Lane and below the railway in Raynes Park) compounding sewer flooding; groundwater pressure distorted a swimming pool lining at a Coombe Lane property. (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple surface water and sewer flooding incidents occurred within the borough during the reported events, including sewer overflow flooding to Dupont Road and Clifton Park Avenue, highways flooding, external garden flooding and internal flooding of residential properties; hotspot impacts included a partially collapsed surface water sewer at Home Park Road. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A stretch of Home Park Road experienced surface&ndash;water flooding concentrated on the carriageway; flood extent extended into the boundaries of some adjacent residential properties and a larger area was at medium risk with estimated flood extent onto some adjacent roads. [PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12th July 2021 rainfall event the surface water sewer system at Home Park Road was unable to cope, with flows collecting at the eastern end of Home Park Road. The surface water sewer partially collapsed at the junction with Dora Road and blocked gullies outside properties exacerbated runoff, worsening local flooding impacts. [TAB,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The hotspot within the London Borough of Merton experienced sewer flooding (primary) and surface water flooding (secondary) during the 12 July 2021 rainfall event, with reports to Merton Council about overwhelmed sewers and surface runoff concentrating at Home Park Road. [PAG] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during the 12th July 2021 rainfall event; some locations (including Hotspot 4: Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood) experienced more substantial flooding on 25th July 2021. [MUL] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within the London Borough of Merton, Hotspot 4 (Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood) in West Barnes experienced surface water/urban flooding on 25th July 2021 that caused internal flooding to a total of three residential properties (two on Marina Avenue and one within the pavilion) and extensive highway flooding along Marina Avenue; one instance of sewer flooding was recorded in postcode KT3 6. [IMG,PAG] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the borough were affected by fluvial flooding during the event on 25th July 2021; Merton Council confirmed fluvial flooding occurred within the Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood hotspot. [IMG,PAG] (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A fluvial (river) flood incident occurred within a hotspot in the London Borough of Merton (Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood area) on 25th July 2021; Merton Council confirmed flooding within this hotspot. [IMG,PAG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood hotspot within the London Borough of Merton experienced flooding incidents (internal and highways) during the 25 July 2021 event, concentrated along Marina Avenue and near the Sports/Leisure Centre. [IMG,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from Beverley Brook overflowed channel banks and entered the borough hotspot on 25th July 2021, causing flooding to the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields and Marina Avenue. Surface water runoff due to poor drainage in the playing fields contributed to floodwater collecting and spreading onto Marina Avenue. Within the borough, the Lower Morden hotspot experienced highways and external&ndash;area flooding (with one residential extension internally flooded on Garth Road). [PAG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall events on 12th July 2021 and 25th July 2021 caused borough&ndash;wide flooding: numerous surface water and sewer flooding incidents, some river&ndash;influenced impacts (high Pyl Brook levels reduced sewer capacity) and road closures that disrupted emergency response. [MUL] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred across the borough during the 12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021 extreme rainfall events, producing multiple reports, incidents of internal flooding and localized road closures (including in Raynes Park). The borough was identified as primarily at risk of sewer and surface water flooding, with parts additionally at high risk of fluvial and groundwater flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced concentrated surface&ndash;water and sewer flooding at identified hotspots (Raynes Park, Home Park Road, West Barnes, Burlington Road / Shannon Corner). Specific impacts included sewer surcharge and property backflows at Abbott Avenue (western end), overflow/flows from the Worple Road CSO entering manholes, gully surcharging on Lower Downs Road at the low point below the railway, capacity and defect&ndash;related flooding of the main surface&ndash;water sewer under the railway behind Home Park Road, and backflows/insufficient capacity on the Westway surface&ndash;water sewer. [PAG,MUL] (p71)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple hotspots across the borough (including West Barnes, Burlington Road / Shannon Corner, Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood, and Lower Morden) experienced surface&ndash;water flooding, sewer surcharges/backflows and fluvial flooding risks; the council and RMAs were given recommendations to improve drainage, clear blocked outlets and introduce SuDS and flood storage to reduce future impacts. [PAG,MUL] (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Clusters of reports concentrated within three hotspot areas on the 12 July 2021 map; five borough&ndash;wide hotspots were selected for analysis in the investigation [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>112 reports of flooding; nine internally flooded properties; one highway incident; five roads; five hotspot locations [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 locations; 9 locations met the Section 19 threshold; 5 named locations affected (Apostles area, Coombe Lane, Abbott Avenue, West Barnes Lane, Burlington Road) [PAG,MUL] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 flooding incidents; 14 locations affected; 9 locations met Section 19 threshold [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>65 reports of flooding; 10 incidents reported to social media [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>65 reports of flooding to Merton Council; 10 further incidents reported on social media [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35 reports of flooding to Merton Council; 2 further incidents reported on social media [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>65 resident flood reports across 35 streets (12th July 2021); 35 resident flood reports across 21 streets (25th July 2021); 9 internal flooding incidents (12th July 2021); 4 internal flooding incidents (25th July 2021) [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 different locations; seven hydrological catchments; five hotspots [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 different locations; seven hydrological catchments; five hotspots [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>14 primary flooded locations [PAG,MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>postcode area SW20 0: 3 instances of sewer flooding; postcode area SW20 8: 5 instances of sewer flooding [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (Coombe Lane swimming pool) (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; 4 instances of sewer flooding in postcode SW19 7 [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 collapsed surface water sewer; 1 flooded property [TAB,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 residential properties; 30m of road; 65m of footway; 1 instance of sewer flooding [IMG,PAG] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (internal flooding adjacent to Sir Joseph Hood area); 1 property extension (internal flooding, Garth Road) [PAG] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>75 reports; 37 reports; 9 internal flooding incidents; 4 internal flooding incidents [MUL] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>75 reports; 37 reports; nine incidents of internal flooding; four incidents of internal flooding [PAG,MUL] (p70)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,London Borough of Merton,England,"Significant flooding concentrated in western and south–western parts of Merton during the extreme rainfall events; the report identifies clustered hotspots (e.g. Raynes Park, Home Park Road, West Barnes) where internal, external and highways flooding were recorded. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)
Widespread flooding across the borough during extreme rainfall on 12 and 25 July 2021. Sewer capacity was exceeded as large volumes of surface water entered the surface water network; high river levels (Pyl Brook and Beverley Brook) caused backflow, surcharged outfalls and fluvial flooding in some locations. Local factors (topography, gully blockages, sewer configuration) compounded impacts. [PAG,MUL] (p4)
Extreme rainfall caused widespread surface water flooding and sewer capacity exceedance within the borough; high river levels on Beverley Brook caused fluvial flooding in parts of the borough and outfalls were surcharged leading to backflow issues. [PAG,MUL] (p5)
The borough experienced multiple surface water / sewer–related flooding hotspots during the July rainfall events, prompting borough–wide recommendations (sewer capacity upgrades, SuDS retrofits, increased gully cleansing and inspection programmes) to reduce recurrence. [PAG,MUL] (p6)
Multiple locations within the borough are identified as hotspots and affected locations in the report for the rainfall events of 12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021. [PAG,MUL] (p7)
The Merton borough area experienced flooding associated with the 12th July 2021 and 25th July 2021 heavy rainfall events, with multiple hotspots and non–Section 19 flooded locations documented. [PAG,MUL] (p8)
Two significant flood events in the borough produced a total of 112 reports of flooding; nine properties were internally flooded; one highway incident impeded road access to emergency vehicles across five roads; reports originated from five hotspot locations (four in the western half of the borough and one in the north–east). [MUL] (p12)
Beverley Brook is reported to run adjacent to Hotspot 4 and is identified in the report as potentially contributing to flooding at that hotspot during the investigated events. [PAG,MUL] (p13)
Multiple flood incidents affected the borough: three events in June 2016 and July and August 2017 reached the Section 19 investigation threshold, and several locations that had previously flooded were affected again during the July 2021 storms. Named locations affected in July 2021 include the Apostles area, Coombe Lane, Abbott Avenue, West Barnes Lane and Burlington Road. [PAG,MUL] (p15)
Multiple locations across the borough experienced surface water and sewer–related flooding during the recorded incidents; these reached the Section 19 investigation threshold and prompted borough–level investigation and follow–up. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p16)
The borough experienced surface water runoff overloading foul sewers that caused sewage flooding onto streets and into homes in the borough during the 2021 events; borough–level activity with partners (TWUL and Merton Council) has since delivered clearance, inspections and pump upgrades to reduce recurrence. [PAG] (p19)
Widespread flooding on 12 July 2021 causing substantial disruption to roads across the borough, temporarily limiting road access to Category One responder vehicles and private vehicles; flooding reported to highways and to internal and external areas of properties. [PAG] (p20)
Widespread surface–water/urban flooding across the borough on 12 July 2021: substantial disruption to roads (temporary limitation of access to Category One responder vehicles and private vehicles in some locations); highways and both internal and external areas of properties reported flooded. Flooding was concentrated primarily in four hotspots across the west and north–east of the borough. [IMG,PAG] (p21)
Intense rainfall on 25th July 2021 caused surface–water/urban flooding across the borough, primarily affecting the southern half. Highways and both internal and external areas of properties were flooded. Most reports were concentrated in two hotspots in the south–western corner of the borough. [PAG] (p22)
Intense July 2021 rainfall led to surface–water / urban flooding across the borough; the two events (12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021) prompted monitoring and assessment using EA and Hydromaster rain gauges. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p24)
Intense, short–duration rainfall on 12th July and 25th July led to surface water / urban flooding across the borough. On 25th July the report states the rainfall that caused flooding entered the northern areas by about 13:50 and reached the southern areas by about 14:00. The 12th July event produced highly variable, intense downpours (most intense in the north–west) that contributed to borough–wide surface water flooding. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p26)
The borough experienced clustered surface water/urban flooding during the intense rainfall on 12th and 25th July 2021, with multiple streets reporting flooding and a number of internal property floodings. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p28)
Widespread reports of internal, external and highways flooding across the borough during the two July 2021 rainfall events; reports were clustered into hotspots and 14 distinct flooded locations were recorded enabling this Section 19 investigation. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p29)
Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during the storm events, triggering Section 19 investigation criteria. The report distinguishes 14 different locations across seven hydrological catchments and five hotspots where flood reports were concentrated. [PAG] (p30)
The administrative area experienced multiple surface–water/urban flood hotspots during the 2021 summer storm events identified in the report. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p31)
On 12th July 2021 parts of the London Borough of Merton (Hotspot 1: Raynes Park) experienced surface water flooding that caused internal flooding to properties (including two residential properties), commercial property flooding along Coombe Lane, basement flooding on Worple Road and highway flooding leading to road and underpass closures which affected emergency access. [IMG,PAG] (p33)
On 12 July 2021 a flooding hotspot in Merton caused internal flooding to properties (residential and commercial), basement flooding on Worple Road, severe highway flooding in underpasses (e.g., Lower Downs Road underpass) and closures that disrupted emergency access. [IMG,PAG] (p34)
The borough experienced an intense July 2021 rainfall hotspot that caused surface water / urban flooding across the hotspot area; local drainage networks were stressed and the event is treated as a primary affected hotspot in the borough–level investigation. [PAG] (p36)
Merton Council’s gully–cleansing programme was changed after a 2016 flooding incident; Map16 data supplied by the council show gullies in Abbott Avenue and other local roads were on the high–priority cleansing programme in 2018/19–2019/20. Map16 also shows Abbott Avenue gullies had not been cleaned since March 2020 prior to the July 2021 flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p38)
Areas within the borough (Raynes Park, Abbott Avenue, Coombe Lane) experienced surface water/sewer flooding, triggering borough–led investigations, cleansing and infrastructure works. [TAB,PAG] (p45)
The borough hotspot experienced sewer surcharging with blown manhole covers, combined sewer overflow discharge and foul water within property boundaries; intense rainfall–driven surface water runoff flowed to low points (including Coombe Lane and below the railway in Raynes Park) compounding sewer flooding; groundwater pressure distorted a swimming pool lining at a Coombe Lane property. (p47)
Multiple surface water and sewer flooding incidents occurred within the borough during the reported events, including sewer overflow flooding to Dupont Road and Clifton Park Avenue, highways flooding, external garden flooding and internal flooding of residential properties; hotspot impacts included a partially collapsed surface water sewer at Home Park Road. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p49)
A stretch of Home Park Road experienced surface–water flooding concentrated on the carriageway; flood extent extended into the boundaries of some adjacent residential properties and a larger area was at medium risk with estimated flood extent onto some adjacent roads. [PAG,MUL] (p52)
During the 12th July 2021 rainfall event the surface water sewer system at Home Park Road was unable to cope, with flows collecting at the eastern end of Home Park Road. The surface water sewer partially collapsed at the junction with Dora Road and blocked gullies outside properties exacerbated runoff, worsening local flooding impacts. [TAB,PAG] (p54)
The hotspot within the London Borough of Merton experienced sewer flooding (primary) and surface water flooding (secondary) during the 12 July 2021 rainfall event, with reports to Merton Council about overwhelmed sewers and surface runoff concentrating at Home Park Road. [PAG] (p55)
Multiple locations across the borough experienced flooding during the 12th July 2021 rainfall event; some locations (including Hotspot 4: Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood) experienced more substantial flooding on 25th July 2021. [MUL] (p58)
Within the London Borough of Merton, Hotspot 4 (Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood) in West Barnes experienced surface water/urban flooding on 25th July 2021 that caused internal flooding to a total of three residential properties (two on Marina Avenue and one within the pavilion) and extensive highway flooding along Marina Avenue; one instance of sewer flooding was recorded in postcode KT3 6. [IMG,PAG] (p59)
Parts of the borough were affected by fluvial flooding during the event on 25th July 2021; Merton Council confirmed fluvial flooding occurred within the Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood hotspot. [IMG,PAG] (p62)
A fluvial (river) flood incident occurred within a hotspot in the London Borough of Merton (Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood area) on 25th July 2021; Merton Council confirmed flooding within this hotspot. [IMG,PAG] (p63)
The Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood hotspot within the London Borough of Merton experienced flooding incidents (internal and highways) during the 25 July 2021 event, concentrated along Marina Avenue and near the Sports/Leisure Centre. [IMG,PAG] (p64)
Fluvial flooding from Beverley Brook overflowed channel banks and entered the borough hotspot on 25th July 2021, causing flooding to the Sir Joseph Hood Memorial Playing Fields and Marina Avenue. Surface water runoff due to poor drainage in the playing fields contributed to floodwater collecting and spreading onto Marina Avenue. Within the borough, the Lower Morden hotspot experienced highways and external–area flooding (with one residential extension internally flooded on Garth Road). [PAG] (p66)
Extreme rainfall events on 12th July 2021 and 25th July 2021 caused borough–wide flooding: numerous surface water and sewer flooding incidents, some river–influenced impacts (high Pyl Brook levels reduced sewer capacity) and road closures that disrupted emergency response. [MUL] (p69)
Flooding occurred across the borough during the 12 July 2021 and 25 July 2021 extreme rainfall events, producing multiple reports, incidents of internal flooding and localized road closures (including in Raynes Park). The borough was identified as primarily at risk of sewer and surface water flooding, with parts additionally at high risk of fluvial and groundwater flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p70)
The borough experienced concentrated surface–water and sewer flooding at identified hotspots (Raynes Park, Home Park Road, West Barnes, Burlington Road / Shannon Corner). Specific impacts included sewer surcharge and property backflows at Abbott Avenue (western end), overflow/flows from the Worple Road CSO entering manholes, gully surcharging on Lower Downs Road at the low point below the railway, capacity and defect–related flooding of the main surface–water sewer under the railway behind Home Park Road, and backflows/insufficient capacity on the Westway surface–water sewer. [PAG,MUL] (p71)
Multiple hotspots across the borough (including West Barnes, Burlington Road / Shannon Corner, Marina Avenue / Sir Joseph Hood, and Lower Morden) experienced surface–water flooding, sewer surcharges/backflows and fluvial flooding risks; the council and RMAs were given recommendations to improve drainage, clear blocked outlets and introduce SuDS and flood storage to reduce future impacts. [PAG,MUL] (p72)","Clusters of reports concentrated within three hotspot areas on the 12 July 2021 map; five borough–wide hotspots were selected for analysis in the investigation [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p3)
