75 reports; 37 reports; nine incidents of internal flooding; four incidents of internal flooding [PAG,MUL] (p70)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.40819685319756,-0.22832760780359473,"<div id=""html_eae2c869ca05680fda132072aa0d82cb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Raynes Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Designated Hotspot 1 on the 12 July 2021 map with clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding during the extreme rainfall event; identified as a concentrated area of flood reports indicating local vulnerability likely related to topography, drainage or infrastructure. [IMG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the July 2021 flooding, a pumping station in Raynes Park was upgraded and is subject to monitoring&mdash;implying the area experienced sewer capacity issues and required asset upgrades due to flooding. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Raynes Park town centre experienced surface&ndash;water&ndash;related and sewage flooding during the 2021 events; subsequent joint works (gully clearance, CCTV surveys and pump upgrades) have been implemented and have since prevented flooding to the town centre and underneath the railway bridge following the 2021 incidents. [TAB,MUL] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Raynes Park appears in the map cells showing high return periods (50&ndash;100 years and above) during the 12 July 2021 event; the intense rainfall and the borough&ndash;wide flooding response imply Raynes Park likely experienced localised surface&ndash;water/urban flooding as part of the broader disruption (including road access limitations and multiple borough reports of flooding). [IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding concentrated within the Raynes Park hotspot (map for 12 July 2021 shows many of the borough's reported incidents were located here). [PAG,IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Coombe Lane area including the Raynes Park railway bridge during the July 2021 events. The report records this location as flooded on 12th July 2021 (Hydromaster return period 1 in 50 to 1 in 100 yr; TWUL FEH13 1 in 393.2 yr). [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 12th July 2021 intense rainfall caused surface water/urban flooding across the Raynes Park hotspot. Internal flooding occurred to two residential properties on Coombe Lane and Richmond Road; several commercial units along Coombe Lane near Raynes Park railway station were internally flooded; basement areas of properties on Worple Road near the junction with Lower Downs Road were flooded; external areas of properties were inundated; highway flooding led to closures of Coombe Lane, Abbott Avenue, Worple Road and the railway underpasses at Lower Downs Road and Approach Road. The deepest flooding was along Coombe Lane adjacent to the railway embankment and within the underpass. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hotspot area experienced combined sewer and surface water flooding that led to internal flooding of residential and commercial properties, road closures (including railway underpasses and access routes), and disruption to emergency access. Coombe Lane experienced the deepest floodwaters, running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Raynes Park is identified as Hotspot 1 on the map with clustering of internal, external and highways flooding incidents along main drainage corridors during the July 2021 event. Flood incidents were shown near key infrastructure (education, medical, library), indicating the area experienced surface water/urban flood impacts. [IMG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows from higher ground collected in central Raynes Park against the raised railway embankment, causing concentrated surface&ndash;water and highway flooding in the town centre and local low points (including the railway underpass). The map shows internal, external and highway flooding incidents clustered within the hotspot boundary in the Worple Road / Lower Downs Road area. [IMG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area around Raynes Park (including the railway underpass by Raynes Park Railway Station and adjacent streets) experienced surface water flooding during the 12th July 2021 event, with recorded internal/external incidents clustering near the station and along Coombe Lane. [IMG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water/sewer flooding affected the Raynes Park town centre area leading to installation of 4G sensors across most of the town centre to monitor blocked drains and silt/water levels, and funding proposals for SuDS measures on nearby roads to reduce future surface water impacts. [TAB] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water sewer network at Raynes Park Station required high&ndash;pressure jet cleaning after the event; surface water overloading and sewer surcharging occurred in the area contributing to local flooding and gully surcharging that discharged onto downstream streets. [TAB] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Central Raynes Park low points (below the railway bridge) experienced surface water runoff and surcharging of the sewer network during the 12 July 2021 rainfall, creating areas of floodwater and contributing to property impacts in the hotspot. (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12 July 2021 event, access to several roads across Raynes Park was closed, disrupting emergency responder movements. The area has high susceptibility to groundwater emergence and sewer&ndash;related issues (including gully surcharging related to pumping station operation) that contributed to localised flooding. (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties [IMG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Raynes Park,England,"Designated Hotspot 1 on the 12 July 2021 map with clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding during the extreme rainfall event; identified as a concentrated area of flood reports indicating local vulnerability likely related to topography, drainage or infrastructure. [IMG] (p3)
Following the July 2021 flooding, a pumping station in Raynes Park was upgraded and is subject to monitoring—implying the area experienced sewer capacity issues and required asset upgrades due to flooding. [MUL] (p5)
Raynes Park town centre experienced surface–water–related and sewage flooding during the 2021 events; subsequent joint works (gully clearance, CCTV surveys and pump upgrades) have been implemented and have since prevented flooding to the town centre and underneath the railway bridge following the 2021 incidents. [TAB,MUL] (p19)
Raynes Park appears in the map cells showing high return periods (50–100 years and above) during the 12 July 2021 event; the intense rainfall and the borough–wide flooding response imply Raynes Park likely experienced localised surface–water/urban flooding as part of the broader disruption (including road access limitations and multiple borough reports of flooding). [IMG] (p21)
Clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding concentrated within the Raynes Park hotspot (map for 12 July 2021 shows many of the borough's reported incidents were located here). [PAG,IMG] (p29)
Flooding in the Coombe Lane area including the Raynes Park railway bridge during the July 2021 events. The report records this location as flooded on 12th July 2021 (Hydromaster return period 1 in 50 to 1 in 100 yr; TWUL FEH13 1 in 393.2 yr). [TAB] (p31)
On 12th July 2021 intense rainfall caused surface water/urban flooding across the Raynes Park hotspot. Internal flooding occurred to two residential properties on Coombe Lane and Richmond Road; several commercial units along Coombe Lane near Raynes Park railway station were internally flooded; basement areas of properties on Worple Road near the junction with Lower Downs Road were flooded; external areas of properties were inundated; highway flooding led to closures of Coombe Lane, Abbott Avenue, Worple Road and the railway underpasses at Lower Downs Road and Approach Road. The deepest flooding was along Coombe Lane adjacent to the railway embankment and within the underpass. [IMG] (p33)
Hotspot area experienced combined sewer and surface water flooding that led to internal flooding of residential and commercial properties, road closures (including railway underpasses and access routes), and disruption to emergency access. Coombe Lane experienced the deepest floodwaters, running parallel to the railway embankment and within the underpass. [IMG] (p35)
Raynes Park is identified as Hotspot 1 on the map with clustering of internal, external and highways flooding incidents along main drainage corridors during the July 2021 event. Flood incidents were shown near key infrastructure (education, medical, library), indicating the area experienced surface water/urban flood impacts. [IMG] (p37)
Flows from higher ground collected in central Raynes Park against the raised railway embankment, causing concentrated surface–water and highway flooding in the town centre and local low points (including the railway underpass). The map shows internal, external and highway flooding incidents clustered within the hotspot boundary in the Worple Road / Lower Downs Road area. [IMG] (p39)
The area around Raynes Park (including the railway underpass by Raynes Park Railway Station and adjacent streets) experienced surface water flooding during the 12th July 2021 event, with recorded internal/external incidents clustering near the station and along Coombe Lane. [IMG] (p40)
Surface water/sewer flooding affected the Raynes Park town centre area leading to installation of 4G sensors across most of the town centre to monitor blocked drains and silt/water levels, and funding proposals for SuDS measures on nearby roads to reduce future surface water impacts. [TAB] (p45)
Surface water sewer network at Raynes Park Station required high–pressure jet cleaning after the event; surface water overloading and sewer surcharging occurred in the area contributing to local flooding and gully surcharging that discharged onto downstream streets. [TAB] (p46)
Central Raynes Park low points (below the railway bridge) experienced surface water runoff and surcharging of the sewer network during the 12 July 2021 rainfall, creating areas of floodwater and contributing to property impacts in the hotspot. (p47)
During the 12 July 2021 event, access to several roads across Raynes Park was closed, disrupting emergency responder movements. The area has high susceptibility to groundwater emergence and sewer–related issues (including gully surcharging related to pumping station operation) that contributed to localised flooding. (p70)",2 residential properties [IMG] (p33),GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.3977576,-0.2383655,"<div id=""html_989a61c7a93c188de185f00f716f1d65"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Barnes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Designated as Hotspot 3 on the 12 July 2021 map with multiple reported incidents of internal, external and highways flooding concentrated in the area; identified as a localised area of elevated flood risk and prioritised for further investigation and management. [IMG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as Hotspot 3: localised flooding issues linked to constrained surface water sewer capacity and surface water runoff; recommendations include increasing sewer size at Westway, higher&ndash;frequency inspections, SuDS retrofits along West Barnes Lane/Westway/Burlington Road&ndash;Shannon Corner, and increased gully cleansing. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report&#8217;s rainfall analysis shows West Barnes lying within west/south areas that experienced elevated return periods (approximately 50&ndash;100 years) on 12 July 2021. That extreme rainfall is associated with surface water/urban flooding across western LB Merton and with temporary road access disruption noted in the report. [IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Clustered reports of internal (property) and external flooding and some highways flooding concentrated in the West Barnes hotspot during the 12th July 2021 intense rainfall event. [PAG,IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred across the West Barnes hotspot during the 12th July 2021 rainfall event, affecting highways and external areas of properties; primarily&ndash;affected locations included Westway, West Barnes Lane, and the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction. One residential property's garage outbuilding experienced internal flooding (not involving foul water). [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Hotspot located within West Barnes (Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood) experienced surface water/urban flooding on 25th July 2021 causing internal flooding to two residential properties on Marina Avenue and one residential property within the Sir Joseph Hood pavilion, plus extensive highway flooding on Marina Avenue; a sewer&ndash;flooding incident was recorded within postcode KT3 6 in the hotspot area. (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The West Barnes area experienced flooding associated with the two extreme rainfall events; specific roads in the area saw few reports on 25th July 2021 but more substantial flooding on 12th July 2021. [MUL] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface runoff and constrained surface&ndash;water sewer capacity led to flooding issues in the West Barnes area, with risk of sewer backflows; recommendations include increased inspection and upsizing of the main surface&ndash;water sewer and introduction of highway SuDS to reduce runoff. [MUL] (p71)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 garage outbuilding [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 residential properties; 30m of road; 65m of footway; 1 instance of sewer flooding (p59)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,West Barnes,England,"Designated as Hotspot 3 on the 12 July 2021 map with multiple reported incidents of internal, external and highways flooding concentrated in the area; identified as a localised area of elevated flood risk and prioritised for further investigation and management. [IMG] (p3)
Identified as Hotspot 3: localised flooding issues linked to constrained surface water sewer capacity and surface water runoff; recommendations include increasing sewer size at Westway, higher–frequency inspections, SuDS retrofits along West Barnes Lane/Westway/Burlington Road–Shannon Corner, and increased gully cleansing. [MUL] (p6)
The report’s rainfall analysis shows West Barnes lying within west/south areas that experienced elevated return periods (approximately 50–100 years) on 12 July 2021. That extreme rainfall is associated with surface water/urban flooding across western LB Merton and with temporary road access disruption noted in the report. [IMG] (p21)
Clustered reports of internal (property) and external flooding and some highways flooding concentrated in the West Barnes hotspot during the 12th July 2021 intense rainfall event. [PAG,IMG] (p29)
Flooding occurred across the West Barnes hotspot during the 12th July 2021 rainfall event, affecting highways and external areas of properties; primarily–affected locations included Westway, West Barnes Lane, and the Burlington Road / Shannon Corner junction. One residential property's garage outbuilding experienced internal flooding (not involving foul water). [PAG] (p56)
The Hotspot located within West Barnes (Marina Avenue & Sir Joseph Hood) experienced surface water/urban flooding on 25th July 2021 causing internal flooding to two residential properties on Marina Avenue and one residential property within the Sir Joseph Hood pavilion, plus extensive highway flooding on Marina Avenue; a sewer–flooding incident was recorded within postcode KT3 6 in the hotspot area. (p59)
The West Barnes area experienced flooding associated with the two extreme rainfall events; specific roads in the area saw few reports on 25th July 2021 but more substantial flooding on 12th July 2021. [MUL] (p69)
Surface runoff and constrained surface–water sewer capacity led to flooding issues in the West Barnes area, with risk of sewer backflows; recommendations include increased inspection and upsizing of the main surface–water sewer and introduction of highway SuDS to reduce runoff. [MUL] (p71)","1 garage outbuilding [PAG] (p56)
3 residential properties; 30m of road; 65m of footway; 1 instance of sewer flooding (p59)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.3897736,-0.2163635,"<div id=""html_33c600918ebef58650c268284f50fe6f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Morden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Morden is identified as Hotspot 5 and was a concentration of reported incidents on 25 July 2021; the area experienced surface water runoff and associated internal, external and highway flooding as sewer capacity was exceeded during the extreme rainfall events. [IMG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Morden is shown on the rainfall&ndash;return&ndash;period map within the south/west portion of the borough that experienced elevated return periods on 12 July 2021 and therefore likely experienced surface&ndash;water/urban flooding associated with that event, contributing to road disruption and reports of flooding to highways and to internal and external areas of properties. This assessment is inferred from borough&ndash;wide descriptions and map intensity rather than incident&ndash;level detail for Lower Morden specifically. [IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding within the Lower Morden hotspot; multiple properties/roads in the southwest of the borough were affected during the event. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highways and external areas were flooded at Lynmouth Avenue, Kingsbridge Road and Garth Road (locations 11&ndash;13); one residential property extension at the southern end of Garth Road experienced internal flooding. Access for emergency vehicles across the hotspot remained possible. (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 25th July 2021 storm event a dislodged manhole cover was reported on Lower Morden Lane; foul water surcharged from Garth Road flowed down to roads in this area due to local topography and sewer surcharges occurred within the local roads. High Pyl Brook levels likely reduced local sewer&ndash;network capacity during the event. (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as Hotspot 5 and affected by the July 2021 extreme rainfall events; parts of the area experienced flood impacts from fluvial sources (Beverley Brook / Pyl Brook risk), and from surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding with water collecting in local low spots. [PAG,MUL] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area (Garth Road / Lynmouth Avenue) experienced sewer surcharges and was identified at risk of fluvial flooding; recommendations include expanding local flood storage (Lynmouth Gardens), SuDS retrofits to properties and highways, and increased sewer inspection/clearing to limit surcharges and surface runoff. (p72)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>65 reports of flooding received by Merton Council across LB Merton; 10 further incidents reported via social media (borough&ndash;wide) [IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internally flooded; 4 locations (locations 11&ndash;14) identified as primarily affected (p66)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Lower Morden,England,"Lower Morden is identified as Hotspot 5 and was a concentration of reported incidents on 25 July 2021; the area experienced surface water runoff and associated internal, external and highway flooding as sewer capacity was exceeded during the extreme rainfall events. [IMG,MUL] (p4)
Lower Morden is shown on the rainfall–return–period map within the south/west portion of the borough that experienced elevated return periods on 12 July 2021 and therefore likely experienced surface–water/urban flooding associated with that event, contributing to road disruption and reports of flooding to highways and to internal and external areas of properties. This assessment is inferred from borough–wide descriptions and map intensity rather than incident–level detail for Lower Morden specifically. [IMG] (p21)
Clustered reports of internal, external and highways flooding within the Lower Morden hotspot; multiple properties/roads in the southwest of the borough were affected during the event. [IMG] (p29)
Highways and external areas were flooded at Lynmouth Avenue, Kingsbridge Road and Garth Road (locations 11–13); one residential property extension at the southern end of Garth Road experienced internal flooding. Access for emergency vehicles across the hotspot remained possible. (p66)
During the 25th July 2021 storm event a dislodged manhole cover was reported on Lower Morden Lane; foul water surcharged from Garth Road flowed down to roads in this area due to local topography and sewer surcharges occurred within the local roads. High Pyl Brook levels likely reduced local sewer–network capacity during the event. (p69)
Identified as Hotspot 5 and affected by the July 2021 extreme rainfall events; parts of the area experienced flood impacts from fluvial sources (Beverley Brook / Pyl Brook risk), and from surface water and sewer–related flooding with water collecting in local low spots. [PAG,MUL] (p70)
The area (Garth Road / Lynmouth Avenue) experienced sewer surcharges and was identified at risk of fluvial flooding; recommendations include expanding local flood storage (Lynmouth Gardens), SuDS retrofits to properties and highways, and increased sewer inspection/clearing to limit surcharges and surface runoff. (p72)","65 reports of flooding received by Merton Council across LB Merton; 10 further incidents reported via social media (borough–wide) [IMG] (p21)
1 property internally flooded; 4 locations (locations 11–14) identified as primarily affected (p66)",GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.41902299447477,-0.2175629037404358,"<div id=""html_686234351dd0f40131cd221b5fb19e42"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wimbledon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hydromaster and FEH13 analysis for 12 July 2021 show the highest 1&ndash;hour rainfall return periods concentrated in the northwest of the borough, including Wimbledon Village. That extreme rainfall on 12th July 2021 contributed to the borough&ndash;wide surface&ndash;water/urban flooding event which caused substantial disruption to roads and produced multiple reports of internal and external property flooding across Merton. The source text does not provide site&ndash;level counts for Wimbledon specifically, but identifies Wimbledon Village as a location of very high rainfall intensity during the event. [IMG] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Wimbledon,England,"Hydromaster and FEH13 analysis for 12 July 2021 show the highest 1–hour rainfall return periods concentrated in the northwest of the borough, including Wimbledon Village. That extreme rainfall on 12th July 2021 contributed to the borough–wide surface–water/urban flooding event which caused substantial disruption to roads and produced multiple reports of internal and external property flooding across Merton. The source text does not provide site–level counts for Wimbledon specifically, but identifies Wimbledon Village as a location of very high rainfall intensity during the event. [IMG] (p21)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.394180555174394,-0.19425702910207002,"<div id=""html_73d0defa2fec3a64beb721c7bcd01414"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Morden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 12 July 2021 intense rainfall event produced very high local return periods across the borough's west/southwest; Morden is identified on the event map among areas with high return periods and is therefore likely to have experienced localised surface&ndash;water/urban flooding associated with that event. Borough&ndash;wide the event caused road access limitations to responder and private vehicles and produced numerous reports of flooding to highways and properties. [IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by the two intense July 2021 rainfall events that impacted the London Borough of Merton; experienced rainfall&ndash;driven urban / surface&ndash;water flooding during these events as assessed using EA and Hydromaster rain gauges. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Morden,England,"The 12 July 2021 intense rainfall event produced very high local return periods across the borough's west/southwest; Morden is identified on the event map among areas with high return periods and is therefore likely to have experienced localised surface–water/urban flooding associated with that event. Borough–wide the event caused road access limitations to responder and private vehicles and produced numerous reports of flooding to highways and properties. [IMG] (p21)
Affected by the two intense July 2021 rainfall events that impacted the London Borough of Merton; experienced rainfall–driven urban / surface–water flooding during these events as assessed using EA and Hydromaster rain gauges. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p24)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4089922,-0.2226123,"<div id=""html_55838b9c49ec38bd1a510b0c11667b3a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Apostles</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External areas of properties and highway surfaces experienced flooding; however, access for emergency vehicles was not inhibited and impacts did not meet the Section 19 threshold for internal property or transport risk. [MUL] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,The Apostles,England,"External areas of properties and highway surfaces experienced flooding; however, access for emergency vehicles was not inhibited and impacts did not meet the Section 19 threshold for internal property or transport risk. [MUL] (p35)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4143002,-0.2360857,"<div id=""html_97b3c5bd37cb7298904ce3d0c4879390"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cottenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cottenham Park is shown on the map as an area where flood incidents (internal, external and highways) cluster along drainage corridors during the July 2021 event, indicating local impacts to properties, highways and nearby infrastructure from surface water/urban flooding. [IMG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Modelled and observed surface&ndash;water (RoFSW) flooding concentrated along the watercourse running through Cottenham Park. The map and report link internal and external flooding incidents to the high&ndash;risk RoFSW zones in this area; highway flooding is also correlated nearby (close to Raynes Park station and Coombe Lane). [IMG] (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Cottenham Park,England,"Cottenham Park is shown on the map as an area where flood incidents (internal, external and highways) cluster along drainage corridors during the July 2021 event, indicating local impacts to properties, highways and nearby infrastructure from surface water/urban flooding. [IMG] (p37)
Modelled and observed surface–water (RoFSW) flooding concentrated along the watercourse running through Cottenham Park. The map and report link internal and external flooding incidents to the high–risk RoFSW zones in this area; highway flooding is also correlated nearby (close to Raynes Park station and Coombe Lane). [IMG] (p40)",,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc.html,GB-MRT_1_2021-07-12_etc,London Borough of Merton flooding 12 Jul 2021 and London Borough of Merton flooding 25 Jul 2021,Merton Section 19 Flood Risk Investigation Report,079/Merton/Merton S19 Report v1.3 (inc DGF Obs).pdf
GB-MRT,1,2021-07-12,51.4351625,-0.1964558,"<div id=""html_2257d61a17ed651bb8b84ad303767ae1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wimbledon Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hotspot within the ward (Home Park Road) experienced highways flooding (road covered by several inches of water), external flooding to front and rear gardens of several properties, and internal flooding of ground floors and basements of two residential properties (basement floodwater reached up to door handles in one property). A surface water sewer running below Home Park Road near the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed. Thames Water records show four instances of sewer flooding in postcode area SW19 7 (which includes this location). (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cluster of flooding incidents along Home Park Road adjacent to Wimbledon Park station: highways flooding with much of the road covered by several inches of floodwater; external flooding to front and rear gardens of several properties; internal flooding of ground floors and basements to two residential properties (basement floodwaters reached up to the door handles in one property); partial collapse of a surface water sewer beneath Home Park Road near the junction with Dora Road. [IMG] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Home Park Road hotspot within Wimbledon Park experienced clustered internal, external and highways flooding linked to sewer surcharge during extreme rainfall; areas of the hotspot also overlap zones of heightened groundwater susceptibility although groundwater was not identified as causing the incident. [IMG,MUL] (p53)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties; 4 instances of sewer flooding (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two residential properties; four instances of sewer flooding within postcode area SW19 7 [IMG] (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Merton,Wimbledon Park,England,"Hotspot within the ward (Home Park Road) experienced highways flooding (road covered by several inches of water), external flooding to front and rear gardens of several properties, and internal flooding of ground floors and basements of two residential properties (basement floodwater reached up to door handles in one property). A surface water sewer running below Home Park Road near the junction with Dora Road partially collapsed. Thames Water records show four instances of sewer flooding in postcode area SW19 7 (which includes this location). (p49)
Cluster of flooding incidents along Home Park Road adjacent to Wimbledon Park station: highways flooding with much of the road covered by several inches of floodwater; external flooding to front and rear gardens of several properties; internal flooding of ground floors and basements to two residential properties (basement floodwaters reached up to the door handles in one property); partial collapse of a surface water sewer beneath Home Park Road near the junction with Dora Road. [IMG] (p50)
