GB-KTT,1,2019-02-01,51.4144735,-0.2662496,"<div id=""html_3314c11bf4b14d4e84a8ae88c68939b5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coombe Hill Infant School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flooding incidents reported at the school; the site has experienced an ongoing flooding problem over several months. The site is potentially at risk of groundwater&ndash;influenced flooding via throughflow and flooding from artificial sources; sub&ndash;surface flows associated with the Coombe Conduit beneath the school may affect flood behaviour. [MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of the school building reported on multiple occasions; these repeated internal flood reports triggered a Section 19 flood investigation by the local authority. [IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The site is located in a topographic low and experienced springs/groundwater emergence on the school site. Large volumes of water appeared on the Adventure Playground mulch, with flows increasing and earth being washed away beneath the playground surface, creating hollows on the school grounds. [IMG,PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Site is in a topographical low; runoff from the main road flowed towards the school buildings (north of the site). Large volumes of water appeared on the Adventure Playground play&ndash;area mulch, causing the mulch to increase in size and the flow of water to increase quickly. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flooding issue at the school playground was reported. The Local Lead Flood Authority (Kingston) conducted a site visit in February 2019 and concluded a land and structural survey was needed. A further LLFA site visit was carried out in November 2019 where previous flooding issues and the potential for SuDS on the site were discussed. Thames Water assessed sewer sources and concluded the likelihood of the flooding coming from sewer sources was low. [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding of the school building(s) reported in a written submission; flooding is attributed to groundwater&ndash;related impacts (Chapter 3.1.5) and not to public or private sewer failure. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood risk investigation was triggered by multiple reported flood incidents at Coombe Hill Infant School. Anecdotal evidence from the school's site manager indicated the flooding did not require a response from a Category One Responder. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Repeated groundwater&ndash;influenced flooding at the school; the site experienced a flooding problem over a period of several months. Investigation concluded the site is at risk of groundwater throughflow flooding, with risk exacerbated by steep topographic differences, a change from permeable upstream geology (Black Park Gravel Member) to London Clay at the site, and building work that may have altered subsurface flows associated with the Coombe Conduit. [MUL] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 reported flooding incidents [MUL] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Thames,Coombe Hill Infant School,England,"Multiple flooding incidents reported at the school; the site has experienced an ongoing flooding problem over several months. The site is potentially at risk of groundwater–influenced flooding via throughflow and flooding from artificial sources; sub–surface flows associated with the Coombe Conduit beneath the school may affect flood behaviour. [MUL] (p3)
Internal flooding of the school building reported on multiple occasions; these repeated internal flood reports triggered a Section 19 flood investigation by the local authority. [IMG] (p8)
The site is located in a topographic low and experienced springs/groundwater emergence on the school site. Large volumes of water appeared on the Adventure Playground mulch, with flows increasing and earth being washed away beneath the playground surface, creating hollows on the school grounds. [IMG,PAG] (p10)
Site is in a topographical low; runoff from the main road flowed towards the school buildings (north of the site). Large volumes of water appeared on the Adventure Playground play–area mulch, causing the mulch to increase in size and the flow of water to increase quickly. (p18)
A flooding issue at the school playground was reported. The Local Lead Flood Authority (Kingston) conducted a site visit in February 2019 and concluded a land and structural survey was needed. A further LLFA site visit was carried out in November 2019 where previous flooding issues and the potential for SuDS on the site were discussed. Thames Water assessed sewer sources and concluded the likelihood of the flooding coming from sewer sources was low. [PAG,MUL] (p21)
Internal property flooding of the school building(s) reported in a written submission; flooding is attributed to groundwater–related impacts (Chapter 3.1.5) and not to public or private sewer failure. (p22)
The flood risk investigation was triggered by multiple reported flood incidents at Coombe Hill Infant School. Anecdotal evidence from the school's site manager indicated the flooding did not require a response from a Category One Responder. [PAG] (p23)
Repeated groundwater–influenced flooding at the school; the site experienced a flooding problem over a period of several months. Investigation concluded the site is at risk of groundwater throughflow flooding, with risk exacerbated by steep topographic differences, a change from permeable upstream geology (Black Park Gravel Member) to London Clay at the site, and building work that may have altered subsurface flows associated with the Coombe Conduit. [MUL] (p24)",2 reported flooding incidents [MUL] (p24),GB-KTT_1_2019-02-01.html,GB-KTT_1_2019-02-01,Kingston Upon Thames flooding February 2019,Coombe Hill Infant School: Flood Risk Investigation,065/Kingston upon Thames/Coombe/Hill/Infant/School/v1.2.pdf
GB-KTT,1,2019-02-01,51.41700390150547,-0.26074482774517044,"<div id=""html_547bb2e8ddfb0acb1eb121010d0f0594"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coombe Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coombe Hill Infant School (located in Coombe Hill) experienced repeated groundwater&ndash;influenced flooding incidents; the school reported flooding over a period of several months and this triggered a formal flood risk investigation. [MUL] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two reported incidents (2007; February 2019) [MUL] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Thames,Coombe Hill,England,Coombe Hill Infant School (located in Coombe Hill) experienced repeated groundwater–influenced flooding incidents; the school reported flooding over a period of several months and this triggered a formal flood risk investigation. [MUL] (p24),two reported incidents (2007; February 2019) [MUL] (p24),GB-KTT_1_2019-02-01.html,GB-KTT_1_2019-02-01,Kingston Upon Thames flooding February 2019,Coombe Hill Infant School: Flood Risk Investigation,065/Kingston upon Thames/Coombe/Hill/Infant/School/v1.2.pdf
GB-KTT,1,2019-02-01,51.4147083,-0.2686688,"<div id=""html_4e6db1f90eeeb9d8858e30dd8c9d7f43"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coombe Conduit</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Coombe Conduit runs diagonally across the site and passes adjacent to the proposed building extension. It is identified in the report as potentially placing the site at greater risk of flooding and is implicated in the flooding experienced at the site. [IMG,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sub&ndash;surface flows associated with the Coombe Conduit may have been altered by building work and are identified as a factor that exacerbated groundwater&ndash;influenced flooding at Coombe Hill Infant School. [MUL] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two reported incidents (2007; February 2019) [MUL] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Thames,Coombe Conduit,England,"The Coombe Conduit runs diagonally across the site and passes adjacent to the proposed building extension. It is identified in the report as potentially placing the site at greater risk of flooding and is implicated in the flooding experienced at the site. [IMG,PAG] (p16)
Sub–surface flows associated with the Coombe Conduit may have been altered by building work and are identified as a factor that exacerbated groundwater–influenced flooding at Coombe Hill Infant School. [MUL] (p24)",two reported incidents (2007; February 2019) [MUL] (p24),GB-KTT_1_2019-02-01.html,GB-KTT_1_2019-02-01,Kingston Upon Thames flooding February 2019,Coombe Hill Infant School: Flood Risk Investigation,065/Kingston upon Thames/Coombe/Hill/Infant/School/v1.2.pdf
GB-CON,4,2017-07-18,50.0270211,-5.0969544,"<div id=""html_4e38a9a50bddb6fe773f370ce741da1e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Croft</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At 'The Croft' on North Corner Stream the 18th July 2017 intense three&ndash;hour rainfall produced rapid overland runoff and overwhelmed the watercourse. The flow changed direction and large boulders were moved, ripping out the gravity sewer. (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,The Croft,England,"At 'The Croft' on North Corner Stream the 18th July 2017 intense three–hour rainfall produced rapid overland runoff and overwhelmed the watercourse. The flow changed direction and large boulders were moved, ripping out the gravity sewer. (p42)",,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18.html,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18,Coverack flooding 18 Jul 2017,Flood Investigation Reports 2017,027/Cornwall/027/2017/Cornwall/flood-investigation-reports-2017.pdf
GB-CON,4,2017-07-18,50.02223096192254,-5.094894304761497,"<div id=""html_6a9dc0e31c13e3ca01ea3bbbde6e35b2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sunny Corner</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Landslip undermined buildings and caused structural damage to the footpath and cottages below; repair works were carried out in response to the event (in partnership with the Environment Agency). (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,Sunny Corner,England,Landslip undermined buildings and caused structural damage to the footpath and cottages below; repair works were carried out in response to the event (in partnership with the Environment Agency). (p12),,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18.html,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18,Coverack flooding 18 Jul 2017,Flood Investigation Reports 2017,027/Cornwall/027/2017/Cornwall/flood-investigation-reports-2017.pdf
GB-CON,4,2017-07-18,50.028082436361665,-5.095842457029834,"<div id=""html_730d06a2e0453b78e667a85e1b72d5ac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Corner</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water/flash flooding affected the North Corner area during the storm; flooding was observed to commence at 15:45 and cease at 17:15 on 18/07/2017. [TAB] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sudden surge of water came down onto the road, initiating flooding at North Corner; flooding later ceased. (p51)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,North Corner,England,"Surface–water/flash flooding affected the North Corner area during the storm; flooding was observed to commence at 15:45 and cease at 17:15 on 18/07/2017. [TAB] (p50)
