over 50 properties affected (across the incident) (p33)
1 property flooded (p49)",GB-CON_4_2017-07-18.html,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18,Coverack flooding 18 Jul 2017,Flood Investigation Reports 2017,027/Cornwall/027/2017/Cornwall/flood-investigation-reports-2017.pdf
GB-CON,4,2017-07-18,50.0562982,-5.0661298,"<div id=""html_28e1c70b9ced7f046ec95d3d79e27bda"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Porthoustock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by the significant and unprecedented flooding associated with the 18th July 2017 event in the Coverack area. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Porthoustock was affected by the 18th July 2017 flash flooding. The intense rainfall led to rapid catchment flows causing property and infrastructure damage, channel erosion and blockages from transported rocks, sediment and debris, and community disruption requiring emergency and recovery work. (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,Porthoustock,England,"Affected by the significant and unprecedented flooding associated with the 18th July 2017 event in the Coverack area. [PAG] (p4)
Porthoustock was affected by the 18th July 2017 flash flooding. The intense rainfall led to rapid catchment flows causing property and infrastructure damage, channel erosion and blockages from transported rocks, sediment and debris, and community disruption requiring emergency and recovery work. (p5)",,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18.html,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18,Coverack flooding 18 Jul 2017,Flood Investigation Reports 2017,027/Cornwall/027/2017/Cornwall/flood-investigation-reports-2017.pdf
GB-CON,4,2017-07-18,50.03054591337257,-5.092165014276627,"<div id=""html_2ce6860eb7533b65c0afc67eb5eb114b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coverack Wastewater Treatment Works</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood flows associated with the stream event caused damage to the Waste Water Treatment works' access road and were connected with the ripping out of a gravity sewer. (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cornwall,Coverack Wastewater Treatment Works,England,Flood flows associated with the stream event caused damage to the Waste Water Treatment works' access road and were connected with the ripping out of a gravity sewer. (p11),,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18.html,GB-CON_4_2017-07-18,Coverack flooding 18 Jul 2017,Flood Investigation Reports 2017,027/Cornwall/027/2017/Cornwall/flood-investigation-reports-2017.pdf
GB-CAM,19,2020-12-04,52.3801715,-0.0932279,"<div id=""html_0c6f581faae44bf5276f39f97166145a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grove Park, Old Hurst</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater significantly affected external premises (gardens) and caused internal flooding to residential properties in the Grove Park area. [TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred where three watercourses converge at the eastern corner of Grove Park; ditches exceeded channel capacity and contributed to overland flows toward nearby properties. A culvert under Grove Park was observed (April and September 2021) and was clear of large debris in Sept 2021 but further desilting was identified as required. One watercourse had been altered without Land Drainage Consent; three small pipes installed within that watercourse were removed after the flood. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties, gardens and outbuildings with floodwater reaching about 30 cm depth; at least one resident was displaced from their home. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the winter 2020 flooding the Grove Park area experienced watercourse&ndash;related flooding. The LLFA undertook walkovers of reported flooded locations, inspected topography and relevant features, and met residents and landowners known to have flooded. The LLFA also met riparian landowners on site to confirm required watercourse management and the need for Ordinary Watercourse Land Drainage Consent and planned to write to riparian owners to clarify responsibilities under the Land Drainage Act (1991). [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties with internal property flooding [TAB] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two residential properties; maximum internal flood depth of c.30cm; residents unable to return for 5&ndash;10 days; three small pipes removed [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>c.30 cm depth; 1 resident displaced for 5&ndash;10 days [TAB] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Grove Park, Old Hurst",England,"Floodwater significantly affected external premises (gardens) and caused internal flooding to residential properties in the Grove Park area. [TAB] (p10)
Surface water flooding occurred where three watercourses converge at the eastern corner of Grove Park; ditches exceeded channel capacity and contributed to overland flows toward nearby properties. A culvert under Grove Park was observed (April and September 2021) and was clear of large debris in Sept 2021 but further desilting was identified as required. One watercourse had been altered without Land Drainage Consent; three small pipes installed within that watercourse were removed after the flood. [IMG] (p12)
Internal flooding of properties, gardens and outbuildings with floodwater reaching about 30 cm depth; at least one resident was displaced from their home. [TAB] (p13)
During the winter 2020 flooding the Grove Park area experienced watercourse–related flooding. The LLFA undertook walkovers of reported flooded locations, inspected topography and relevant features, and met residents and landowners known to have flooded. The LLFA also met riparian landowners on site to confirm required watercourse management and the need for Ordinary Watercourse Land Drainage Consent and planned to write to riparian owners to clarify responsibilities under the Land Drainage Act (1991). [TAB] (p17)","2 properties with internal property flooding [TAB] (p10)
two residential properties; maximum internal flood depth of c.30cm; residents unable to return for 5–10 days; three small pipes removed [IMG] (p12)
c.30 cm depth; 1 resident displaced for 5–10 days [TAB] (p13)",GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc.html,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc,Old Hurst flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Old Hurst,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Old-Hurst-Winter-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
