GB-CAM,19,2020-12-04,52.3729228,-0.0928501,"<div id=""html_d4d28a022ea2b8abd92998a38c3cab70"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Ives Road, Old Hurst</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The highways authority carried out an extensive CCTV survey of surface water and highways drains on St Ives Road at the LLFA's request following the flooding. Two sections of pipe across the surveyed roads were identified as requiring maintenance to improve conveyance after upstream ditch clearance. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"St Ives Road, Old Hurst",England,The highways authority carried out an extensive CCTV survey of surface water and highways drains on St Ives Road at the LLFA's request following the flooding. Two sections of pipe across the surveyed roads were identified as requiring maintenance to improve conveyance after upstream ditch clearance. (p15),,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc.html,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc,Old Hurst flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Old Hurst,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Old-Hurst-Winter-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,19,2020-12-04,52.3750156,-0.0933613,"<div id=""html_82d6fa2706f6ff003f5eecda1751877e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancaster Close, Old Hurst</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lancaster Road (sic) was included in the extensive CCTV survey of surface water and highways drains carried out after the winter 2020 flooding; the inspections identified pipe sections needing maintenance to improve conveyance following ditch clearance upstream. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Lancaster Close, Old Hurst",England,Lancaster Road (sic) was included in the extensive CCTV survey of surface water and highways drains carried out after the winter 2020 flooding; the inspections identified pipe sections needing maintenance to improve conveyance following ditch clearance upstream. (p15),,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc.html,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc,Old Hurst flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Old Hurst,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Old-Hurst-Winter-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,19,2020-12-04,52.4617238,0.6975642,"<div id=""html_92dcdc7fead7510c906e6a84240dde14"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Anglia</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The region experienced widespread surface&ndash;water flooding associated with very wet conditions in December 2020 and an intense rainfall event on 23rd December 2020; saturated soils and high runoff contributed to significant surface&ndash;water flooding across the area (including recorded events on 4th and 23rd December). [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,East Anglia,England,"The region experienced widespread surface–water flooding associated with very wet conditions in December 2020 and an intense rainfall event on 23rd December 2020; saturated soils and high runoff contributed to significant surface–water flooding across the area (including recorded events on 4th and 23rd December). [PAG,MUL] (p7)",,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc.html,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc,Old Hurst flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Old Hurst,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Old-Hurst-Winter-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,19,2020-12-04,52.37618721635427,-0.0938999599840953,"<div id=""html_62c5c017f37d5b78abc554ce04b6ea42"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Old Hurst</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localized inundation of low points in the village (one to the north and one to the south) from surface water. To the north, the confluence of two unnamed ordinary watercourses adjacent to residential properties can flood when runoff exceeds bank capacity. To the south, runoff from arable land can overwhelm surface water ditches and flow toward residential properties. Flooding has affected buildings: Cambridge Fire and Rescue Service recorded flooding of properties to the north in February 2014, and the northern location flooded again during the Winter 2020 event. [MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding affected Old Hurst during intense heavy rainfall on 23rd December 2020. Saturated ground and a high water table following a very wet autumn and December produced very high runoff and resulted in significant surface&ndash;water flooding in and around the village. Surface&ndash;water flooding had also been reported to the LLFA for areas to the south of the village on 4th December 2020. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An area within/adjacent to Old Hurst flooded in winter 2020. A ditch to the south and east of the flooded area had been poorly maintained for many years and is expected to have contributed to the flood risk; LLFA intervention and follow&ndash;up maintenance (including removal of a previously blocked piped access crossing) were carried out to improve conveyance. [IMG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant overland surface water flooding across saturated farmland and from ditches which exceeded channel capacity during December 2020; groundwater and fluvial (river) flooding were also identified in village areas. The LLFA has engaged riparian owners and is taking/considering maintenance and enforcement actions to reduce future risk. [MUL] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 domestic properties; 1 commercial property [MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Old Hurst,England,"Localized inundation of low points in the village (one to the north and one to the south) from surface water. To the north, the confluence of two unnamed ordinary watercourses adjacent to residential properties can flood when runoff exceeds bank capacity. To the south, runoff from arable land can overwhelm surface water ditches and flow toward residential properties. Flooding has affected buildings: Cambridge Fire and Rescue Service recorded flooding of properties to the north in February 2014, and the northern location flooded again during the Winter 2020 event. [MUL] (p4)
Surface–water flooding affected Old Hurst during intense heavy rainfall on 23rd December 2020. Saturated ground and a high water table following a very wet autumn and December produced very high runoff and resulted in significant surface–water flooding in and around the village. Surface–water flooding had also been reported to the LLFA for areas to the south of the village on 4th December 2020. [MUL] (p7)
An area within/adjacent to Old Hurst flooded in winter 2020. A ditch to the south and east of the flooded area had been poorly maintained for many years and is expected to have contributed to the flood risk; LLFA intervention and follow–up maintenance (including removal of a previously blocked piped access crossing) were carried out to improve conveyance. [IMG] (p15)
Significant overland surface water flooding across saturated farmland and from ditches which exceeded channel capacity during December 2020; groundwater and fluvial (river) flooding were also identified in village areas. The LLFA has engaged riparian owners and is taking/considering maintenance and enforcement actions to reduce future risk. [MUL] (p22)",2 domestic properties; 1 commercial property [MUL] (p4),GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc.html,GB-CAM_19_2020-12-04_etc,Old Hurst flooding December 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Old Hurst,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Old-Hurst-Winter-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.2456855,-1.6148936,"<div id=""html_c3f2a70fe0eaa401acce3df8add5ab43"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Baslow Village Hall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inundated when floodwater from Bar Brook (which exceeded capacity and overtopped its banks) converged with surface&ndash;water runoff from Cock Hill, causing the Village Hall to be flooded. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Baslow Village Hall,England,"Inundated when floodwater from Bar Brook (which exceeded capacity and overtopped its banks) converged with surface–water runoff from Cock Hill, causing the Village Hall to be flooded. (p15)",,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.01656066360479,-1.432246702458056,"<div id=""html_890fb480767a9fc6868f5e79734d0beb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Denby Institute</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Channel exceedance of Bottle Brook North on its right bank at the Denby Institute and Recreation Ground caused internal flooding of buildings on the site, explicitly including The Bowling Green Sports Pavilion and the Denby Institute building. (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 non&ndash;residential properties internally flooded (The Bowling Green Sports Pavilion; Denby Institute building) (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Denby Institute,England,"Channel exceedance of Bottle Brook North on its right bank at the Denby Institute and Recreation Ground caused internal flooding of buildings on the site, explicitly including The Bowling Green Sports Pavilion and the Denby Institute building. (p27)",2 non–residential properties internally flooded (The Bowling Green Sports Pavilion; Denby Institute building) (p27),GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,52.9872203,-1.4999681,"<div id=""html_6aa2ff93492388faf24150c52c45c588"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Duffield Meadows Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internally flooded as one of the non&ndash;residential properties affected in the community. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The school (a non&ndash;residential property located off Park Road) experienced internal flooding. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Suffered internal flooding primarily from the overflow of ordinary watercourses west of the school and south of the River Ecclesbourne, with additional overland surface water routes from the south contributing. The school was temporarily closed and pupils were relocated to temporary classrooms for several months while flooded classrooms were repaired. (p46)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 school (Duffield Meadows Primary School); part of 3 non&ndash;residential properties flooded (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 non&ndash;residential property (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Duffield Meadows Primary School,England,"Internally flooded as one of the non–residential properties affected in the community. (p40)
