Part of 17 homes and 3 commercial holiday lets (these 20 properties were affected collectively across Millbridge, Trickett Close and Mill Court) [IMG] (p54)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.34306074100874,-1.7772135676225116,"<div id=""html_41e8ad6b8ab99cd44c2e2036ae89950c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Island</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of homes and commercial premises as a result of rapid flows from Peakshole Water and associated surface water runoff. (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 homes and 2 commercial properties flooded internally (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,The Island,England,Internal flooding of homes and commercial premises as a result of rapid flows from Peakshole Water and associated surface water runoff. (p54),4 homes and 2 commercial properties flooded internally (p54),GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,52.9652481,-1.464655,"<div id=""html_626802cf0e723d777f7be0fb97fcc675"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bottle Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook recorded shorter and earlier river responses to the heavy rainfall and its gauging stations produced some of the highest&ndash;ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook overtopped/caused fluvial flooding in Little Eaton that resulted in internal flooding of nearby residences on both banks. [IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook overtopped/increased flow during the event and caused internal flooding to nearby residential properties on the west side (around Croft End) and to a property on the east side of the brook in Little Eaton. [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook is the local watercourse on whose floodplain the affected properties lie; a flood alert for the 'Bottle Brook in Derbyshire' Flood Alert Area was issued on 19 October 2023 and properties on its floodplain experienced internal flooding during the event period (noting the Smithy Houses gauge did not reach the flood&ndash;warning threshold). [PAG,IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels in Bottle Brook during the event contributed to flooding impacts in adjacent communities; elevated levels would have closed a flap valve preventing ingress to the surface water network and were associated with internal flooding of nearby properties (including eight homes in the local community and 17 properties in Lower Kilburn during Storm Babet). [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels in Bottle Brook contributed to flooding in Lower Kilburn, closed a flap valve on a surface water outfall and would have prevented discharge back into the brook, impacting the connected surface water network. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook floods its floodplain and has caused river flooding of Lower Kilburn; historical flood incidents are recorded (1977, 2007) and the Environment Agency assessment following Storm Babet indicates recent flood activity associated with the brook that has affected nearby properties. [PAG,IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flows in Bottle Brook inundated Gypsy Bridge to the north of Lower Kilburn and then spread southwards, contributing to flooding in Lower Kilburn (combining with surface water from the east). (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflow/overflowing along the Bottle Brook (including culverted sections) was closely associated with internal flooding of properties in Denby Bottles during Storm Babet. [IMG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook overflowed and caused flooding of the Denby Bottles community; the Environment Agency historic map and report link Bottle Brook to the internal flooding of properties during the storm. [PAG,IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook caused river flooding that led to internal flooding of properties in Denby Bottles during Storm Babet. The Environment Agency's historic flood map identifies Bottle Brook as having flooded Denby Bottles in the past. [PAG,IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook North and Bottle Brook East overtopped their channels and caused fluvial flooding in the Denby Bottles area. Exceedance of Bottle Brook North at two upstream locations led to floodwater spilling onto Derby Road and flowing south along the road; a further channel exceedance and right&ndash;bank exceedance at the Denby Institute and Recreation Ground caused water to enter non&ndash;residential buildings. Floodwater entered properties from both front (via Derby Road) and rear. [IMG,PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bottle Brook North exceeded its channel at two upstream locations and further downstream, spilling onto Derby Road and using Derby Road as a secondary flow route. Floodwater entered properties on Derby Road via their fronts and also from the rear due to downstream channel exceedance. Properties further south experienced greater depths. Channel exceedance on the right bank at Denby Institute and Recreation Ground caused internal flooding of The Bowling Green Sports Pavilion and the Denby Institute and Recreation Ground building. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 residential properties; 7 homes west of the brook; 1 home east of the brook [IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 residential properties; 1 property (east side of Bottle Brook) [PAG,IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties; 17 properties [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 residential properties [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35 properties internally flooded in Denby Bottles (see Denby Bottles entry) [IMG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two channel exceedances upstream; 35 properties internally flooded (total across Denby Bottles) [IMG,PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>28 homes; 3 businesses; 2 buildings; flood depths between 0.1m and 1m [PAG] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Bottle Brook,England,"Bottle Brook recorded shorter and earlier river responses to the heavy rainfall and its gauging stations produced some of the highest–ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3)
Bottle Brook overtopped/caused fluvial flooding in Little Eaton that resulted in internal flooding of nearby residences on both banks. [IMG] (p16)
Bottle Brook overtopped/increased flow during the event and caused internal flooding to nearby residential properties on the west side (around Croft End) and to a property on the east side of the brook in Little Eaton. [PAG,IMG] (p17)
Bottle Brook is the local watercourse on whose floodplain the affected properties lie; a flood alert for the 'Bottle Brook in Derbyshire' Flood Alert Area was issued on 19 October 2023 and properties on its floodplain experienced internal flooding during the event period (noting the Smithy Houses gauge did not reach the flood–warning threshold). [PAG,IMG] (p18)
High river levels in Bottle Brook during the event contributed to flooding impacts in adjacent communities; elevated levels would have closed a flap valve preventing ingress to the surface water network and were associated with internal flooding of nearby properties (including eight homes in the local community and 17 properties in Lower Kilburn during Storm Babet). [PAG] (p19)
High river levels in Bottle Brook contributed to flooding in Lower Kilburn, closed a flap valve on a surface water outfall and would have prevented discharge back into the brook, impacting the connected surface water network. [PAG] (p20)
Bottle Brook floods its floodplain and has caused river flooding of Lower Kilburn; historical flood incidents are recorded (1977, 2007) and the Environment Agency assessment following Storm Babet indicates recent flood activity associated with the brook that has affected nearby properties. [PAG,IMG] (p21)
River flows in Bottle Brook inundated Gypsy Bridge to the north of Lower Kilburn and then spread southwards, contributing to flooding in Lower Kilburn (combining with surface water from the east). (p22)
Overflow/overflowing along the Bottle Brook (including culverted sections) was closely associated with internal flooding of properties in Denby Bottles during Storm Babet. [IMG] (p24)
Bottle Brook overflowed and caused flooding of the Denby Bottles community; the Environment Agency historic map and report link Bottle Brook to the internal flooding of properties during the storm. [PAG,IMG] (p25)
Bottle Brook caused river flooding that led to internal flooding of properties in Denby Bottles during Storm Babet. The Environment Agency's historic flood map identifies Bottle Brook as having flooded Denby Bottles in the past. [PAG,IMG] (p26)
Bottle Brook North and Bottle Brook East overtopped their channels and caused fluvial flooding in the Denby Bottles area. Exceedance of Bottle Brook North at two upstream locations led to floodwater spilling onto Derby Road and flowing south along the road; a further channel exceedance and right–bank exceedance at the Denby Institute and Recreation Ground caused water to enter non–residential buildings. Floodwater entered properties from both front (via Derby Road) and rear. [IMG,PAG] (p27)
Bottle Brook North exceeded its channel at two upstream locations and further downstream, spilling onto Derby Road and using Derby Road as a secondary flow route. Floodwater entered properties on Derby Road via their fronts and also from the rear due to downstream channel exceedance. Properties further south experienced greater depths. Channel exceedance on the right bank at Denby Institute and Recreation Ground caused internal flooding of The Bowling Green Sports Pavilion and the Denby Institute and Recreation Ground building. [PAG] (p28)","8 residential properties; 7 homes west of the brook; 1 home east of the brook [IMG] (p16)
