two channel exceedances upstream; 35 properties internally flooded (total across Denby Bottles) [IMG,PAG] (p27)
28 homes; 3 businesses; 2 buildings; flood depths between 0.1m and 1m [PAG] (p28)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.2610241,-1.5934361,"<div id=""html_19cdec48d3e94a3270de48b1fb7f63ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bar Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in Bar Brook (a tributary of the River Derwent) overtopped or surged onto its floodplain during Storm Babet, causing internal flooding of residential properties on Cock Hill and Church Lane and of non&ndash;residential properties in Nether End in Baslow East. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bar Brook ran through the centre of the Baslow East area and caused river flooding concentrated along its banks, resulting in adjacent properties and nearby roads being flooded during the event. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bar Brook exceeded its channel capacity and overflowed its banks, causing river flooding that inundated residential properties in the Baslow east community. [PAG,IMG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bar Brook exceeded its capacity and overflowed its banks, sending floodwater southwest into Nether End and Baslow East and contributing to flooding of properties, Nether End car park and Baslow Village Hall. [IMG] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 residential properties; 11 non&ndash;residential properties; 4 homes on Cock Hill; 5 homes on Church Lane [IMG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Bar Brook,England,"Flows in Bar Brook (a tributary of the River Derwent) overtopped or surged onto its floodplain during Storm Babet, causing internal flooding of residential properties on Cock Hill and Church Lane and of non–residential properties in Nether End in Baslow East. [IMG] (p12)
Bar Brook ran through the centre of the Baslow East area and caused river flooding concentrated along its banks, resulting in adjacent properties and nearby roads being flooded during the event. [IMG] (p13)
Bar Brook exceeded its channel capacity and overflowed its banks, causing river flooding that inundated residential properties in the Baslow east community. [PAG,IMG] (p14)
Bar Brook exceeded its capacity and overflowed its banks, sending floodwater southwest into Nether End and Baslow East and contributing to flooding of properties, Nether End car park and Baslow Village Hall. [IMG] (p15)",9 residential properties; 11 non–residential properties; 4 homes on Cock Hill; 5 homes on Church Lane [IMG] (p12),GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,52.9529937,-1.4489864,"<div id=""html_980cdbc948240f60230fc4d68790b040"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dam Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dam Brook reached capacity and overflowed its banks, contributing to internal flooding of residential properties in Breadsall during Storm Babet. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dam Brook (an ordinary watercourse) reached capacity and overtopped its banks during Storm Babet, causing river/ordinary&ndash;watercourse flooding that led to internal flooding of adjacent homes (notably the three homes on Refectory Lane) and contributed to local inundation in Breadsall. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood Zone 2 and Flood Alert areas are concentrated along Dam Brook; river flooding extent along Dam Brook affected nearby properties (near Rectory Lane, Church Lane, Croft Lane and Brookfields Drive). Dam Brook also limited drainage outfall during heavy rainfall, contributing to surface water ponding and internal property flooding in Breadsall. [PAG,IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dam Brook is identified as a primary receptor that drainage networks in the community must discharge to; during heavy rainfall the local drainage was unable to discharge to Dam Brook (and Boosemoor Brook), contributing to surface water flooding concentrated along the watercourse (notably near Rectory Lane and Church Lane). [PAG,IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dam Brook overtopped its banks on 20 Oct 2023 (first overtopping ~08:30 then again around 10:20), causing Brookside Road to flood to about 0.5 m in low spots and contributing to sewer surcharge and internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dam Brook is identified as the watercourse associated with local flood risk; Derbyshire County Council is progressing studies of natural flood management measures in response to flooding in the area (e.g., leaky dams, floodplain reconnection, bunding, woodland creation). (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>six residential properties (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 homes [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties (community total: 2 Brookside Road; 1 Moor Road; 3 Rectory Lane); 0.5 m depth (road flooding in low spots) [PAG] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Dam Brook,England,"Dam Brook reached capacity and overflowed its banks, contributing to internal flooding of residential properties in Breadsall during Storm Babet. (p28)
Dam Brook (an ordinary watercourse) reached capacity and overtopped its banks during Storm Babet, causing river/ordinary–watercourse flooding that led to internal flooding of adjacent homes (notably the three homes on Refectory Lane) and contributed to local inundation in Breadsall. [IMG,PAG] (p29)
Flood Zone 2 and Flood Alert areas are concentrated along Dam Brook; river flooding extent along Dam Brook affected nearby properties (near Rectory Lane, Church Lane, Croft Lane and Brookfields Drive). Dam Brook also limited drainage outfall during heavy rainfall, contributing to surface water ponding and internal property flooding in Breadsall. [PAG,IMG] (p30)
Dam Brook is identified as a primary receptor that drainage networks in the community must discharge to; during heavy rainfall the local drainage was unable to discharge to Dam Brook (and Boosemoor Brook), contributing to surface water flooding concentrated along the watercourse (notably near Rectory Lane and Church Lane). [PAG,IMG] (p31)
Dam Brook overtopped its banks on 20 Oct 2023 (first overtopping ~08:30 then again around 10:20), causing Brookside Road to flood to about 0.5 m in low spots and contributing to sewer surcharge and internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG] (p32)
Dam Brook is identified as the watercourse associated with local flood risk; Derbyshire County Council is progressing studies of natural flood management measures in response to flooding in the area (e.g., leaky dams, floodplain reconnection, bunding, woodland creation). (p33)","six residential properties (p28)
3 homes [IMG,PAG] (p29)
6 properties (community total: 2 Brookside Road; 1 Moor Road; 3 Rectory Lane); 0.5 m depth (road flooding in low spots) [PAG] (p32)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,52.9558868,-1.45408,"<div id=""html_319a20cca0fab7aedf1dd5f407302eea"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Boosemoor Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Although it does not have adjacent Flood Zone 2 in the map figure, Boosemoor Brook is identified as a receiving watercourse that drainage networks could not discharge to during heavy rainfall, contributing to surface water flooding and internal property flooding in Breadsall. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 18&ndash;21 Oct 2023 storm period the brook acted as a local flood receptor; drainage networks were unable to discharge to the brook during heavy rainfall, concentrating high (>3.3% AEP) surface water flood risk along its corridor and contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties in Breadsall. [PAG,IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Boosemoor Brook was identified alongside Dam Brook as a primary source of the community flooding that led to internal flooding of residential properties on 20 Oct 2023. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties (community total: 2 Brookside Road; 1 Moor Road; 3 Rectory Lane) [PAG] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Boosemoor Brook,England,"Although it does not have adjacent Flood Zone 2 in the map figure, Boosemoor Brook is identified as a receiving watercourse that drainage networks could not discharge to during heavy rainfall, contributing to surface water flooding and internal property flooding in Breadsall. [PAG] (p30)
During the 18–21 Oct 2023 storm period the brook acted as a local flood receptor; drainage networks were unable to discharge to the brook during heavy rainfall, concentrating high (>3.3% AEP) surface water flood risk along its corridor and contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties in Breadsall. [PAG,IMG] (p31)
Boosemoor Brook was identified alongside Dam Brook as a primary source of the community flooding that led to internal flooding of residential properties on 20 Oct 2023. [PAG] (p32)",6 properties (community total: 2 Brookside Road; 1 Moor Road; 3 Rectory Lane) [PAG] (p32),GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,52.9234033,-1.3608037,"<div id=""html_d9f678761b0b24ce749af6272421ebe8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ock Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping/surcharge from Ock Brook resulted in internal flooding to nearby residential properties in Ockbrook during the storm; homes close to the watercourse (which passes under Cole Lane and south of Collier Lane) were flooded internally. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ock Brook, an ordinary watercourse flowing through the village and culverted under Collier Lane, was associated with the internal flooding of nearby residential properties during Storm Babet. The culverted section beneath Collier Lane is highlighted as a critical location linked to the flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert blockage and raised water levels in Ock Brook caused the brook to overflow its banks; floodwater travelled westward along Cole Lane and Collier Lane and resulted in internal flooding of nearby residential properties. [PAG,IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert under Cole Lane was partially blocked causing water levels in Ock Brook to rise upstream; Ock Brook overflowed its banks and produced out&ndash;of&ndash;bank flows that sent floodwater westward along Cole Lane and Collier Lane, contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 residential properties (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 homes; 4 properties on Cole Lane; 4 properties on Collier Lane [PAG,IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>eight properties; four properties on Cole Lane; four properties on Collier Lane [PAG] (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Ock Brook,England,"Overtopping/surcharge from Ock Brook resulted in internal flooding to nearby residential properties in Ockbrook during the storm; homes close to the watercourse (which passes under Cole Lane and south of Collier Lane) were flooded internally. (p33)
Ock Brook, an ordinary watercourse flowing through the village and culverted under Collier Lane, was associated with the internal flooding of nearby residential properties during Storm Babet. The culverted section beneath Collier Lane is highlighted as a critical location linked to the flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p34)
Culvert blockage and raised water levels in Ock Brook caused the brook to overflow its banks; floodwater travelled westward along Cole Lane and Collier Lane and resulted in internal flooding of nearby residential properties. [PAG,IMG] (p35)
The culvert under Cole Lane was partially blocked causing water levels in Ock Brook to rise upstream; Ock Brook overflowed its banks and produced out–of–bank flows that sent floodwater westward along Cole Lane and Collier Lane, contributing to internal flooding of nearby properties. [PAG] (p36)","8 residential properties (p33)
8 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p34)
8 homes; 4 properties on Cole Lane; 4 properties on Collier Lane [PAG,IMG] (p35)
eight properties; four properties on Cole Lane; four properties on Collier Lane [PAG] (p36)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.2782256,-1.6694874,"<div id=""html_a0d9afe92d19c12e42621bd1bbc2d3e0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dale Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with Dale Brook led to internal flooding of properties in the village of Stoney Middleton; ten properties suffered internal flooding (seven residential and three non&ndash;residential). (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dale Brook is the main watercourse through the village and the community is within its floodplain; flooding associated with Dale Brook contributed to internal flooding of nearby properties in Stoney Middleton. [PAG,IMG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dale Brook, a shallow watercourse with culverted sections and upstream trash screens, exceeded culvert capacity during the event and flowed out of bank, causing internal flooding to nearby properties and contributing to inundation of The Nook and The Dale; CCTV showed culvert capacity was exceeded though screens/culverts were not blocked. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>ten properties; seven residential; three non&ndash;residential (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties [PAG,IMG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 culverted sections; 4 debris screens; 7 homes; 3 non&ndash;residential properties [PAG] (p39)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Dale Brook,England,"Flooding associated with Dale Brook led to internal flooding of properties in the village of Stoney Middleton; ten properties suffered internal flooding (seven residential and three non–residential). (p36)
Dale Brook is the main watercourse through the village and the community is within its floodplain; flooding associated with Dale Brook contributed to internal flooding of nearby properties in Stoney Middleton. [PAG,IMG] (p37)
Dale Brook, a shallow watercourse with culverted sections and upstream trash screens, exceeded culvert capacity during the event and flowed out of bank, causing internal flooding to nearby properties and contributing to inundation of The Nook and The Dale; CCTV showed culvert capacity was exceeded though screens/culverts were not blocked. [PAG] (p39)","ten properties; seven residential; three non–residential (p36)
10 properties [PAG,IMG] (p37)
4 culverted sections; 4 debris screens; 7 homes; 3 non–residential properties [PAG] (p39)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.3349686,-1.7349183,"<div id=""html_c197e3f3c29030f6873b52c3e4ef05c6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bradwell Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bradwell Brook flowed through the village and was associated with concentrated flooding along and near the watercourse; this led to internal flooding of residential and non&ndash;residential properties in Bradwell. [IMG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bradwell Brook is identified as a source of flooding for the village and is linked to flood alerts/warnings for the area; flooding from the brook has contributed to the community flood incidents described. [IMG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from Bradwell Brook contributed to property flooding in the village during Storm Babet; the brook flows through the centre of Bradwell and is associated with flood zones and the 'Bradwell Brook at Bradwell' flood warning. Culverts and a debris screen on the brook are noted in the area. [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bradwell Brook exceeded its capacity and overtopped at Church Street bridge, contributing to flooding of Church Street and Soft Water Lane. High flows combined with converging surface water to flood onto adjacent streets. A culvert under a property built over the brook may have been restricted by silt/debris, exacerbating local flooding. [IMG] (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>26 residential properties and 3 non&ndash;residential properties internally flooded [IMG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to flooding that internally flooded 29 properties on 20 Oct 2023 [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 houses on Soft Water Lane; 1 property built over Bradwell Brook [IMG] (p50)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Bradwell Brook,England,"Bradwell Brook flowed through the village and was associated with concentrated flooding along and near the watercourse; this led to internal flooding of residential and non–residential properties in Bradwell. [IMG] (p47)
Bradwell Brook is identified as a source of flooding for the village and is linked to flood alerts/warnings for the area; flooding from the brook has contributed to the community flood incidents described. [IMG] (p48)
Flooding from Bradwell Brook contributed to property flooding in the village during Storm Babet; the brook flows through the centre of Bradwell and is associated with flood zones and the 'Bradwell Brook at Bradwell' flood warning. Culverts and a debris screen on the brook are noted in the area. [IMG,PAG] (p49)
Bradwell Brook exceeded its capacity and overtopped at Church Street bridge, contributing to flooding of Church Street and Soft Water Lane. High flows combined with converging surface water to flood onto adjacent streets. A culvert under a property built over the brook may have been restricted by silt/debris, exacerbating local flooding. [IMG] (p50)","26 residential properties and 3 non–residential properties internally flooded [IMG] (p47)
Contributed to flooding that internally flooded 29 properties on 20 Oct 2023 [IMG,PAG] (p49)
2 houses on Soft Water Lane; 1 property built over Bradwell Brook [IMG] (p50)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.3440905,-1.7594935,"<div id=""html_29440f90cb7968e1879387450aee38e3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Peakshole Water</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Peakshole Water overtopped/triggered river flooding affecting properties on its floodplain in Castleton; properties that flooded during the event have also been flooded by Peakshole Water in previous years. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the watercourse expected to cause flooding; high river levels from Peakshole Water contributed to flooding and prompted alerts and the use of a flap valve to prevent backing up into the surface water drainage network. [IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the main source of flooding &mdash; rapidly rose during intense rainfall (fed by the cave system/Peak Cavern) and overtopped its banks, flooding adjacent streets (for example onto The Stones, and causing flooding at Eades Fold and Vicarage Lane) and properties. [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reached capacity and overflowed its banks; flooded over the right bank onto The Stones and was the source of flooding to nearby streets and properties (Eades Fold, Vicarage Lane, Mill Court, Millbridge and Tricketts Close). [PAG] (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>24 residential properties; 5 non&ndash;residential properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Peakshole Water,England,"Peakshole Water overtopped/triggered river flooding affecting properties on its floodplain in Castleton; properties that flooded during the event have also been flooded by Peakshole Water in previous years. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)
Identified as the watercourse expected to cause flooding; high river levels from Peakshole Water contributed to flooding and prompted alerts and the use of a flap valve to prevent backing up into the surface water drainage network. [IMG] (p53)
Identified as the main source of flooding — rapidly rose during intense rainfall (fed by the cave system/Peak Cavern) and overtopped its banks, flooding adjacent streets (for example onto The Stones, and causing flooding at Eades Fold and Vicarage Lane) and properties. [IMG,PAG] (p54)
Reached capacity and overflowed its banks; flooded over the right bank onto The Stones and was the source of flooding to nearby streets and properties (Eades Fold, Vicarage Lane, Mill Court, Millbridge and Tricketts Close). [PAG] (p55)","24 residential properties; 5 non–residential properties [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p52)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.1447012,-1.4216889,"<div id=""html_6221ecc19c722047104566ebf4ece510"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Smithy Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding adjacent to Smithy Brook during Storm Babet affected a compact residential area in Clay Cross; homes close to the watercourse flooded internally when the event caused the local floodplain to inundate. [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Smithy Brook flows southwards through Clay Cross and its floodplain underlies part of the community; Flood Zones 2 and 3 follow the brook and are concentrated along it, indicating the brook caused river flooding affecting adjacent residential properties and Meadow Farm Pond. [IMG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse is implicated in the local flooding that affected adjacent residential properties during the event; responsibility for its maintenance sits with riparian owners. (p58)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>45 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Smithy Brook,England,"Flooding adjacent to Smithy Brook during Storm Babet affected a compact residential area in Clay Cross; homes close to the watercourse flooded internally when the event caused the local floodplain to inundate. [IMG,PAG] (p56)
Smithy Brook flows southwards through Clay Cross and its floodplain underlies part of the community; Flood Zones 2 and 3 follow the brook and are concentrated along it, indicating the brook caused river flooding affecting adjacent residential properties and Meadow Farm Pond. [IMG] (p57)
The watercourse is implicated in the local flooding that affected adjacent residential properties during the event; responsibility for its maintenance sits with riparian owners. (p58)","45 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p56)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.3425821,-1.7790276,"<div id=""html_af48becc036a67191ae3f201d622a291"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Peak Cavern</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall produced large volumes of water through the cave system south of the village that fed Peakshole Water, contributing to the rapid rise in river levels and downstream flooding. (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Peak Cavern,England,"Intense rainfall produced large volumes of water through the cave system south of the village that fed Peakshole Water, contributing to the rapid rise in river levels and downstream flooding. (p54)",,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.064622931103074,-1.4679803933977134,"<div id=""html_8cf874a423a247ca5a38bd26c81f3fc0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>A610 at Ridgeway</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The stretch of the A610 (locally called Riversdale and Ripley Road) is the focus of the internally flooded properties in Ridgeway; multiple homes along this road experienced internal flooding during the event. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sixteen residential properties along the A610 (locally Riversdale / Ripley Road) in Ridgeway suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet. [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on the east side of Riversdale experienced river flooding when the River Amber exceeded its channel and overtopped its banks; internal flooding entered homes via doorways (reported depths reached 0.9m). The local sewage pumping station was overwhelmed and the sewer network backed up, causing foul flooding from drains around properties. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The A610 was flooded and was shut by Police and the Fire and Rescue Service. Before it was closed, HGVs drove through the flood water creating waves that exacerbated flooding into people's homes adjacent to the road. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 residential properties (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 homes internally flooded; 4 homes flooded externally; internal depths up to 0.9m [PAG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,A610 at Ridgeway,England,"The stretch of the A610 (locally called Riversdale and Ripley Road) is the focus of the internally flooded properties in Ridgeway; multiple homes along this road experienced internal flooding during the event. (p7)
Sixteen residential properties along the A610 (locally Riversdale / Ripley Road) in Ridgeway suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet. [PAG,IMG] (p8)
Residential properties on the east side of Riversdale experienced river flooding when the River Amber exceeded its channel and overtopped its banks; internal flooding entered homes via doorways (reported depths reached 0.9m). The local sewage pumping station was overwhelmed and the sewer network backed up, causing foul flooding from drains around properties. [PAG] (p10)
The A610 was flooded and was shut by Police and the Fire and Rescue Service. Before it was closed, HGVs drove through the flood water creating waves that exacerbated flooding into people's homes adjacent to the road. (p12)","16 residential properties (p7)
16 residential properties [PAG,IMG] (p8)
16 homes internally flooded; 4 homes flooded externally; internal depths up to 0.9m [PAG] (p10)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.315826,-1.727034,"<div id=""html_af201ae4fdca94b2b3b997b2fc1707af"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Peak District National Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Baslow village (within the Peak District National Park) experienced internal flooding from flows on Bar Brook during Storm Babet, affecting residential and non&ndash;residential properties in Baslow East. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 residential properties; 11 non&ndash;residential properties (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Peak District National Park,England,"Baslow village (within the Peak District National Park) experienced internal flooding from flows on Bar Brook during Storm Babet, affecting residential and non–residential properties in Baslow East. (p12)",9 residential properties; 11 non–residential properties (p12),GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.1395212,-1.5590102,"<div id=""html_58921c71f0bb25221c6dababf3ed3e35"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Derwent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Derwent experienced later and longer flow responses to Storm Babet with record/highest&ndash;ranked flows or levels at downstream stations (notably St Mary's Bridge in Derby). Upstream locations (Yorkshire Bridge, Mytham Bridge) did not record high&ndash;ranking flows because Ladybower Reservoir was intentionally drawn down by Severn Trent prior to the event to create storage capacity. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels on the River Derwent during the storm hindered the River Amber's ability to discharge into it, causing the Amber to back up, rise and contribute to overtopping of Amber banks that flooded local properties. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency issued a Flood Warning for the River Derwent at Baslow and Bubnell on 20 October; floodwater from Bar Brook converged towards the River Derwent, increasing river levels and flood risk in the Baslow area. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A Flood Warning was issued for the River Derwent at Baslow and Bubnell on 20 Oct 2023 at 15:44. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood Warning was issued for the River Derwent at Baslow and Bubnell on 20 October, and Flood Alerts covered the Upper River Derwent and tributaries, indicating elevated river levels and flood risk to communities along the Derwent. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood warning for the River Derwent at Little Eaton was issued on 21 October 2023 as part of the incident; this formed part of the local flood risk during the same period. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower Kilburn sits in the Derwent catchment and experienced internal property flooding during the event; high flows in tributary Bottle Brook (within the Derwent catchment) contributed to property flooding. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Derwent (Lower Derwent) formed part of the flood alert/warning area for the event and contributed to local flood risk around Duffield as described in flood warning coverage. [IMG] (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>27 hours lag time between peak rainfall and peak flow at St. Mary's Bridge; 'highest ranked flows or levels on record' recorded at St. Mary's Bridge (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 residential properties (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 flooded properties were within flood alert/warning areas that include the 'Lower Derwent' Flood Alert Area [IMG] (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Derwent,England,"River Derwent experienced later and longer flow responses to Storm Babet with record/highest–ranked flows or levels at downstream stations (notably St Mary's Bridge in Derby). Upstream locations (Yorkshire Bridge, Mytham Bridge) did not record high–ranking flows because Ladybower Reservoir was intentionally drawn down by Severn Trent prior to the event to create storage capacity. (p3)
High river levels on the River Derwent during the storm hindered the River Amber's ability to discharge into it, causing the Amber to back up, rise and contribute to overtopping of Amber banks that flooded local properties. (p10)
Environment Agency issued a Flood Warning for the River Derwent at Baslow and Bubnell on 20 October; floodwater from Bar Brook converged towards the River Derwent, increasing river levels and flood risk in the Baslow area. [IMG,PAG] (p15)
A Flood Warning was issued for the River Derwent at Baslow and Bubnell on 20 Oct 2023 at 15:44. (p16)
Flood Warning was issued for the River Derwent at Baslow and Bubnell on 20 October, and Flood Alerts covered the Upper River Derwent and tributaries, indicating elevated river levels and flood risk to communities along the Derwent. [PAG] (p17)
A flood warning for the River Derwent at Little Eaton was issued on 21 October 2023 as part of the incident; this formed part of the local flood risk during the same period. [PAG] (p18)
Lower Kilburn sits in the Derwent catchment and experienced internal property flooding during the event; high flows in tributary Bottle Brook (within the Derwent catchment) contributed to property flooding. (p19)
The River Derwent (Lower Derwent) formed part of the flood alert/warning area for the event and contributed to local flood risk around Duffield as described in flood warning coverage. [IMG] (p43)","27 hours lag time between peak rainfall and peak flow at St. Mary's Bridge; 'highest ranked flows or levels on record' recorded at St. Mary's Bridge (p3)
17 residential properties (p19)
33 flooded properties were within flood alert/warning areas that include the 'Lower Derwent' Flood Alert Area [IMG] (p43)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.0164935,-1.5451459,"<div id=""html_9782aa96df404120b91f147691387e84"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Ecclesbourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Downstream reaches of the River Ecclesbourne recorded shorter and earlier responses to the heavy rainfall during Storm Babet. Gauging stations on the River Ecclesbourne produced some of the highest&ndash;ranked flows or levels on record in response to the event. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding on the Ecclesbourne floodplain was associated with internal flooding of properties in Duffield; flooded homes are concentrated adjacent to the watercourse (notably around Ecclesbourne Avenue and Village Court). [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ecclesbourne flows through the Duffield floodplain and is identified as a main river contributing to flooding of adjacent properties; flood maps show Flood Zone 3/2 adjacent to the river and the report records past and recent flooding in Duffield linked to the watercourse. [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of properties in Duffield associated with the River Ecclesbourne during the event; the river is the subject of flood alerts/warnings and has historic flood defence walls (constructed 1970&ndash;1975) intended to provide ~1% AEP standard of protection. The river&#8217;s condition (conveyance) was a focus of maintenance activity immediately prior to the event. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ecclesbourne exceeded its capacity and overflowed its banks and formal defences in several locations through Duffield, overtopping left banks and defences, flowing overland via the riverside footpath, across Duck Island bridge, and directly causing internal flooding to numerous properties and flooding of roads and gardens. [PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Raised flows/overflow from the River Ecclesbourne contributed to flooding of adjacent low&ndash;lying areas in Duffield, including overflow of ordinary watercourses near the school and flood flows toward Village Court and nearby locations. [IMG] (p46)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 hours lag time (shortest lag at Duffield on the River Ecclesbourne); 'highest ranked flows or levels on record' at River Ecclesbourne gauging stations (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>34 properties internally flooded in Duffield (31 residential; 3 non&ndash;residential including Duffield Meadows Primary School); map annotations show 5 homes flooded on Ecclesbourne Avenue and 19 homes flooded on Village Court [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>30 properties in Flood Zone 3; 3 properties in Flood Zone 2; 1 property in Flood Zone 1 (Duffield, per Flood Map for Planning) [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>33 flooded properties (within flood alert/warning areas) [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>31 homes internally flooded; 3 non&ndash;residential properties internally flooded [PAG] (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Ecclesbourne,England,"Downstream reaches of the River Ecclesbourne recorded shorter and earlier responses to the heavy rainfall during Storm Babet. Gauging stations on the River Ecclesbourne produced some of the highest–ranked flows or levels on record in response to the event. (p3)
River flooding on the Ecclesbourne floodplain was associated with internal flooding of properties in Duffield; flooded homes are concentrated adjacent to the watercourse (notably around Ecclesbourne Avenue and Village Court). [IMG] (p41)
The River Ecclesbourne flows through the Duffield floodplain and is identified as a main river contributing to flooding of adjacent properties; flood maps show Flood Zone 3/2 adjacent to the river and the report records past and recent flooding in Duffield linked to the watercourse. [IMG] (p42)
Flooding of properties in Duffield associated with the River Ecclesbourne during the event; the river is the subject of flood alerts/warnings and has historic flood defence walls (constructed 1970–1975) intended to provide ~1% AEP standard of protection. The river’s condition (conveyance) was a focus of maintenance activity immediately prior to the event. [IMG,PAG] (p43)
The River Ecclesbourne exceeded its capacity and overflowed its banks and formal defences in several locations through Duffield, overtopping left banks and defences, flowing overland via the riverside footpath, across Duck Island bridge, and directly causing internal flooding to numerous properties and flooding of roads and gardens. [PAG] (p44)
Raised flows/overflow from the River Ecclesbourne contributed to flooding of adjacent low–lying areas in Duffield, including overflow of ordinary watercourses near the school and flood flows toward Village Court and nearby locations. [IMG] (p46)","7 hours lag time (shortest lag at Duffield on the River Ecclesbourne); 'highest ranked flows or levels on record' at River Ecclesbourne gauging stations (p3)
34 properties internally flooded in Duffield (31 residential; 3 non–residential including Duffield Meadows Primary School); map annotations show 5 homes flooded on Ecclesbourne Avenue and 19 homes flooded on Village Court [IMG] (p41)
30 properties in Flood Zone 3; 3 properties in Flood Zone 2; 1 property in Flood Zone 1 (Duffield, per Flood Map for Planning) [IMG] (p42)
33 flooded properties (within flood alert/warning areas) [IMG,PAG] (p43)
31 homes internally flooded; 3 non–residential properties internally flooded [PAG] (p44)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.147494,-1.4583957,"<div id=""html_e3fbe11333e77460f1b7abaf95601d3a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Amber</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows caused the River Amber to exceed channel capacity and overtop its banks; backwater from the River Derwent hindered discharge, causing levels to rise and contributing to flooding of adjacent properties. Peak level recorded upstream (Wingfield Park gauge) reached 2.97 m on 20 Oct 2023. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding associated with the River Amber prompted a Flood Alert (19 Oct) and a Flood Warning for Ambergate (20 Oct). The warning was issued shortly (approximately 20 minutes) before flooding began; the river&ndash;related flooding contributed to rapid onset inundation of properties in the area, with internal water levels observed up to 0.9 m in at least some homes. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 homes internally flooded; 4 homes externally flooded; 3 homes flooded on Drovers Way (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.9 m internal flooding depth (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Amber,England,"High flows caused the River Amber to exceed channel capacity and overtop its banks; backwater from the River Derwent hindered discharge, causing levels to rise and contributing to flooding of adjacent properties. Peak level recorded upstream (Wingfield Park gauge) reached 2.97 m on 20 Oct 2023. (p10)
Fluvial flooding associated with the River Amber prompted a Flood Alert (19 Oct) and a Flood Warning for Ambergate (20 Oct). The warning was issued shortly (approximately 20 minutes) before flooding began; the river–related flooding contributed to rapid onset inundation of properties in the area, with internal water levels observed up to 0.9 m in at least some homes. (p11)","16 homes internally flooded; 4 homes externally flooded; 3 homes flooded on Drovers Way (p10)
0.9 m internal flooding depth (p11)",GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,52.9272181,-1.475871,"<div id=""html_0a962e8375367773c305568aa58800e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Mary's Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Mary's Bridge on the River Derwent experienced the longest lag time between peak rainfall and peak flow (27 hours) and produced one of the highest&ndash;ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,St Mary's Bridge,England,St Mary's Bridge on the River Derwent experienced the longest lag time between peak rainfall and peak flow (27 hours) and produced one of the highest–ranked flows or levels on record in response to Storm Babet. (p3),,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.245324784960566,-1.6147126878805032,"<div id=""html_692e3e44c73b9c20c32a2d48fb95ef18"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nether End Car Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooded when Bar Brook exceeded its channel capacity and overflowed its banks; combined with surface&ndash;water runoff from Cock Hill (including flows from springs and road&ndash;erosion&ndash;related flows), this led to inundation of the car park. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Nether End Car Park,England,"Flooded when Bar Brook exceeded its channel capacity and overflowed its banks; combined with surface–water runoff from Cock Hill (including flows from springs and road–erosion–related flows), this led to inundation of the car park. (p15)",,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_3_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-3-derwent-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,53.1216063,-1.4103066,"<div id=""html_a438abc2337bf6e4325f3a51a08eaad8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lilac Way, Shirland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties on Lilac Way adjacent to a small culverted ordinary watercourse running north&ndash;south; flooding occurred in the shallow valley next to the culvert and affected homes. [IMG,PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties on the west side of Lilac Way where gardens back onto a partly culverted ordinary watercourse. Six properties suffered internal flooding; three of these properties were also flooded externally. [PAG,IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A small, culverted ordinary watercourse running parallel to Lilac Way had limited capacity and a small, poorly designed inlet that became restricted. During Storm Babet water bypassed the culvert, flowed through rear gardens of properties on the west side of Lilac Way and entered homes through doorways. Flood depths in doorways reached about 0.6 m. Siltation and debris in a downstream chamber impeded flow and was subsequently removed. [PAG,IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert flow at Lilac Way was impeded by partial siltation and debris in an access chamber which exacerbated local flooding; residents attempted to remove debris during the event; Fire and Rescue pumped water and silt from the area; the chamber was later de&ndash;silted and cleared. (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 residential properties [IMG,PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties internally flooded; 3 properties externally flooded [PAG,IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>six properties flooded internally; three properties flooded externally; flood depths reached 0.6 m [PAG,IMG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"Lilac Way, Shirland",England,"Internal flooding of residential properties on Lilac Way adjacent to a small culverted ordinary watercourse running north–south; flooding occurred in the shallow valley next to the culvert and affected homes. [IMG,PAG] (p4)
Internal flooding of residential properties on the west side of Lilac Way where gardens back onto a partly culverted ordinary watercourse. Six properties suffered internal flooding; three of these properties were also flooded externally. [PAG,IMG] (p5)
