GB-DBY,3,2023-10-18,52.9523568,-1.4457258,"<div id=""html_4488f77adb006c5e557a86d0bdea6b25"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brookside Road, Breadsall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties associated with brooks reaching capacity and overflowing during Storm Babet; locations are near local watercourses. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A property on Brookside Road suffered internal flooding during the 18&ndash;21 Oct 2023 event; the internal flood is attributed to surface water/runoff and drainage unable to discharge to local watercourses. The affected property had previously installed a demountable flood gate following earlier flood events. [PAG,IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dam Brook overtopped and flooded Brookside Road on 20 Oct 2023, flooding low spots to about 0.5 m. The road remained flooded for several hours; sewer network surcharge then caused internal flooding of properties. A property with a demountable driveway barrier was initially protected from river water but flooded internally due to drainage/sewer surcharge. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage surcharge / sewer&ndash;related flooding investigated beneath Brookside Road after the event; Severn Trent attended (20&ndash;21 Oct 2023) to check property drainage connections to the main sewer following flood reports. (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 homes flooded internally [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG,IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties; 0.5 m depth (road flood in low spots) [PAG] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"Brookside Road, Breadsall",England,"Internal flooding of residential properties associated with brooks reaching capacity and overflowing during Storm Babet; locations are near local watercourses. [IMG] (p29)
