GB-DOR,2,2016-02-07,50.796837,-2.3447323,"<div id=""html_758fa759d0b7be538636b305580586a0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dorset</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village of Plush experienced a flash flood on 7 Feb 2016 in which flows from the north (including Plush Brook overtopping) and surface water from surrounding lanes/roads converged on the village, causing internal flooding of properties and blocking highway gullies with mud and scalping. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The county containing the study area and the communities identified as affected; the report describes actions for Dorset Council (LLFA) to work with EA, district councils, Wessex Water and local communities following flooding associated with the event. [IMG] (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties [IMG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Dorset,England,"The village of Plush experienced a flash flood on 7 Feb 2016 in which flows from the north (including Plush Brook overtopping) and surface water from surrounding lanes/roads converged on the village, causing internal flooding of properties and blocking highway gullies with mud and scalping. [IMG] (p11)
The county containing the study area and the communities identified as affected; the report describes actions for Dorset Council (LLFA) to work with EA, district councils, Wessex Water and local communities following flooding associated with the event. [IMG] (p21)",7 properties [IMG] (p11),GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07.html,GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07,Piddle Valley flooding 07 Feb 2016 - 08 Feb 2016,Flood Investigation Report Piddle Valley 7th - 8th February 2016,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/Feb 2016 Piddle Valley Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,2,2016-02-07,50.7711529,-2.410609,"<div id=""html_58d4eece7c2f0554c01f38bd0deefa0a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Piddlehinton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Piddlehinton is noted as being prone to flooding from surface water run&ndash;off, fluvial flows (River Piddle) and raised groundwater. High groundwater can infiltrate the sewer network causing sewer overflows that exacerbate flooding in Piddlehinton. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties caused by surface water, raised groundwater levels and main river flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in the village (approx. 03:30&ndash;04:30 on 08/02/2016) with Main River and surface water inundation causing internal flooding to properties. [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from West Lane did not enter the FAS inlet and flowed onto nearby roads within Piddlehinton, requiring residents to divert water with sandbags and highlighting a drainage inlet issue at West Lane. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Piddlehinton experienced surface water flooding issues (notably on Rectory Road and at West Lane), prompting hydraulic modelling and drainage improvement actions. [TAB] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [TAB,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Piddlehinton,England,"Piddlehinton is noted as being prone to flooding from surface water run–off, fluvial flows (River Piddle) and raised groundwater. High groundwater can infiltrate the sewer network causing sewer overflows that exacerbate flooding in Piddlehinton. [IMG] (p6)
Internal flooding of residential properties caused by surface water, raised groundwater levels and main river flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p8)
Flooding occurred in the village (approx. 03:30–04:30 on 08/02/2016) with Main River and surface water inundation causing internal flooding to properties. [IMG,PAG] (p15)
Surface water from West Lane did not enter the FAS inlet and flowed onto nearby roads within Piddlehinton, requiring residents to divert water with sandbags and highlighting a drainage inlet issue at West Lane. (p16)
Piddlehinton experienced surface water flooding issues (notably on Rectory Road and at West Lane), prompting hydraulic modelling and drainage improvement actions. [TAB] (p19)","2 properties [TAB,PAG] (p8)
