GB-DOR,2,2016-02-07,50.798449745419475,-2.4213293540754264,"<div id=""html_ec3818ab62761cd2951e32791d652ee2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Piddletrenthide</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties caused by a combination of surface water, high groundwater and main river (River Piddle) flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised intense rainfall and adjacent&ndash;field surface water run&ndash;off combined with the River Piddle overtopping upstream of Church Lane bridge, causing internal flooding to properties; the Church Lane bridge was identified as a possible hydraulic restriction to flow. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Piddle overtopped upstream of Church Lane causing internal flooding of properties. Church Lane also experienced sewage flooding when ground and surface water inundated sewer pipes. Post&ndash;event inspections by Wessex Water followed to locate sewer defects and inform remedial actions. [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>These communities were subject to remedial works and reviews after flooding: WDDC jetted through the Flood Alleviation Scheme in Egypt (Piddletrenthide); a new sandbag store was set up in Plush and sandbag stores replenished in Piddletrenthide; DCC Highways reviewed highway drainage infrastructure in affected areas within Plush and Piddletrenthide and identified locations requiring further works. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties [TAB,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties; 1200m of sewer inspected; 4 lengths require sealing [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Piddletrenthide,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties caused by a combination of surface water, high groundwater and main river (River Piddle) flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p8)
Localised intense rainfall and adjacent–field surface water run–off combined with the River Piddle overtopping upstream of Church Lane bridge, causing internal flooding to properties; the Church Lane bridge was identified as a possible hydraulic restriction to flow. [IMG] (p12)
River Piddle overtopped upstream of Church Lane causing internal flooding of properties. Church Lane also experienced sewage flooding when ground and surface water inundated sewer pipes. Post–event inspections by Wessex Water followed to locate sewer defects and inform remedial actions. [IMG,PAG] (p13)
These communities were subject to remedial works and reviews after flooding: WDDC jetted through the Flood Alleviation Scheme in Egypt (Piddletrenthide); a new sandbag store was set up in Plush and sandbag stores replenished in Piddletrenthide; DCC Highways reviewed highway drainage infrastructure in affected areas within Plush and Piddletrenthide and identified locations requiring further works. [TAB] (p17)","4 properties [TAB,PAG] (p8)
three properties [IMG] (p12)
three properties; 1200m of sewer inspected; 4 lengths require sealing [IMG,PAG] (p13)",GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07.html,GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07,Piddle Valley flooding 07 Feb 2016 - 08 Feb 2016,Flood Investigation Report Piddle Valley 7th - 8th February 2016,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/Feb 2016 Piddle Valley Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,2,2016-02-07,50.82215,-2.4291797,"<div id=""html_384bea86f2fe4f1b0f609fe60a5201bd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alton Pancras</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential property(ies) due to surface water. [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property flooded internally due to surface water runoff from an adjacent field during the event; the incident occurred at approximately 21:30 on 7 February. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Run&ndash;off from fields contributed to surface water flooding in the locality; recommendations include working with landowners to reduce runoff and explore options to reduce surface&ndash;water flood risk. [TAB] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Alton Pancras,England,"Internal flooding of residential property(ies) due to surface water. [TAB] (p8)
One property flooded internally due to surface water runoff from an adjacent field during the event; the incident occurred at approximately 21:30 on 7 February. (p16)
Run–off from fields contributed to surface water flooding in the locality; recommendations include working with landowners to reduce runoff and explore options to reduce surface–water flood risk. [TAB] (p19)","1 property [TAB] (p8)
one property (p16)",GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07.html,GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07,Piddle Valley flooding 07 Feb 2016 - 08 Feb 2016,Flood Investigation Report Piddle Valley 7th - 8th February 2016,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/Feb 2016 Piddle Valley Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-DOR,2,2016-02-07,50.818316,-2.4066152,"<div id=""html_d848102f78607f39a377d3f31966a8db"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Plush</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On the evening of 7 Feb 2016 a flash flood from intense surface&ndash;water run&ndash;off and Plush Brook overtopping caused internal flooding of properties in the village; the brook rose rapidly and flowed out of bank, converging with additional run&ndash;off. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced a flash/fluvial and surface water flood on the evening of 7 February 2016. Flows from the north and converging surface water routes caused internal flooding to properties; residents reported a 'roar of rushing water' prior to inundation. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>These communities were subject to remedial works and reviews after flooding: WDDC jetted through the Flood Alleviation Scheme in Egypt (Piddletrenthide); a new sandbag store was set up in Plush and sandbag stores replenished in Piddletrenthide; DCC Highways reviewed highway drainage infrastructure in affected areas within Plush and Piddletrenthide and identified locations requiring further works. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Plush was affected by surface&ndash;water/runoff and watercourse issues during the event. Actions implemented included setting up a new sandbag store in Plush, DCC Highways reviewing highway drainage in Plush, and recommendations to keep Plush Brook channel clear of obstructions and to investigate diversion of surface water away from the village centre. [TAB,PAG] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties flooded internally on 07 Feb 2016; 3 properties initially flooded by the brook; brook rose by approximately 1.5m (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties flooded internally [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Dorset,Plush,England,"On the evening of 7 Feb 2016 a flash flood from intense surface–water run–off and Plush Brook overtopping caused internal flooding of properties in the village; the brook rose rapidly and flowed out of bank, converging with additional run–off. (p10)
The village experienced a flash/fluvial and surface water flood on the evening of 7 February 2016. Flows from the north and converging surface water routes caused internal flooding to properties; residents reported a 'roar of rushing water' prior to inundation. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
These communities were subject to remedial works and reviews after flooding: WDDC jetted through the Flood Alleviation Scheme in Egypt (Piddletrenthide); a new sandbag store was set up in Plush and sandbag stores replenished in Piddletrenthide; DCC Highways reviewed highway drainage infrastructure in affected areas within Plush and Piddletrenthide and identified locations requiring further works. [TAB] (p17)
Plush was affected by surface–water/runoff and watercourse issues during the event. Actions implemented included setting up a new sandbag store in Plush, DCC Highways reviewing highway drainage in Plush, and recommendations to keep Plush Brook channel clear of obstructions and to investigate diversion of surface water away from the village centre. [TAB,PAG] (p18)","7 properties flooded internally on 07 Feb 2016; 3 properties initially flooded by the brook; brook rose by approximately 1.5m (p10)
7 properties flooded internally [IMG,PAG] (p11)",GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07.html,GB-DOR_2_2016-02-07,Piddle Valley flooding 07 Feb 2016 - 08 Feb 2016,Flood Investigation Report Piddle Valley 7th - 8th February 2016,037/Dorset/037/2020/Dorset/Feb 2016 Piddle Valley Flood Investigation Report.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,52.01170948298128,-0.9302632957565421,"<div id=""html_ca08995613995485f968ea69beda8372"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thornborough Mill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the river (Great Ouse) was reported in the Thornborough Mill area and receded the following evening. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the 23 December 2020 flood event, landowners in the Thornborough Mill area worked together to reinstate the ditch alongside the solar farm to re&ndash;establish channelled land drainage. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in the Thornborough Mill area later in the evening of 23 December 2020, associated with high flows on the Great Ouse; an Environment Agency warning for the Great Ouse was issued that evening. [TAB,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Thornborough Mill area started to flood from surface runoff in the late evening of 23/12/2020; residents deployed resilience measures and sandbags that night. The area then started to flood from the main river (Great Ouse) at about 03:30 on 24/12/2020. Flood waters at the Mill receded by late evening on 24/12/2020. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area flooded first from surface runoff (late evening) and later from the main river; residents at Thornborough Mill deployed resilience measures and sandbags; flood later receded. [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mill area was flooded during the event; flood water later receded at the mill. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels on the River Great Ouse led to overtopping of the south bank at Thornborough Mill; surface water flowed south from the river into the floodplain, affecting properties immediately south of the river. [IMG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Great Ouse levels overtopped the south bank at Thornborough Mill; surface water from fields to the south was also observed flowing onto the site. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two properties reported internal flooding across ground floors; one property experienced flood depths up to 14cm and subsequently installed resilience measures including raised floor levels. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Great Ouse overtopped its banks at Thornborough Mill during the 23rd December 2020 event, causing flooding at the mill location and adjacent areas. A large left&ndash;bank floodplain exists but raised ground at the bank restricted flow from the channel to the floodplain, likely concentrating floodwater at the mill. A weir immediately upstream controls water levels and is noted in relation to the event. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Thornborough Mill area was inundated by the December 2020 flood event. [PAG] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Subject to flooding from the Great Ouse during the extreme December 2020 event; the catchment and river flows at Thornborough Mill were directly discussed in relation to the December 2020 flood magnitude. [PAG,IMG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows were observed in the Thornborough Mill area, contributed to by a blocked ditch below the solar farm at Thornborough Grounds. (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Great Ouse levels caused floodwater to overtop the south bank near Thornborough Mill, with surface water observed flowing off the adjoining fields onto the area. [PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Thornborough Mill catchment on the Great Ouse experienced elevated flows in response to the 23rd December 2020 rainfall (soils already saturated), with an approximate flow return period of 10&ndash;30 years. This increased flood risk at Thornborough Mill and underpinned recommendations for measures such as natural flood management and property flood resilience. [PAG] (p65)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 400 mm depth [PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties; 14cm (ground&ndash;floor flood depth) (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thornborough Mill,England,"Flooding from the river (Great Ouse) was reported in the Thornborough Mill area and receded the following evening. (p6)
Following the 23 December 2020 flood event, landowners in the Thornborough Mill area worked together to reinstate the ditch alongside the solar farm to re–establish channelled land drainage. (p30)
Flooding occurred in the Thornborough Mill area later in the evening of 23 December 2020, associated with high flows on the Great Ouse; an Environment Agency warning for the Great Ouse was issued that evening. [TAB,PAG] (p33)
The Thornborough Mill area started to flood from surface runoff in the late evening of 23/12/2020; residents deployed resilience measures and sandbags that night. The area then started to flood from the main river (Great Ouse) at about 03:30 on 24/12/2020. Flood waters at the Mill receded by late evening on 24/12/2020. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p34)
Area flooded first from surface runoff (late evening) and later from the main river; residents at Thornborough Mill deployed resilience measures and sandbags; flood later receded. [PAG] (p35)
Mill area was flooded during the event; flood water later receded at the mill. (p36)
High river levels on the River Great Ouse led to overtopping of the south bank at Thornborough Mill; surface water flowed south from the river into the floodplain, affecting properties immediately south of the river. [IMG] (p38)
High River Great Ouse levels overtopped the south bank at Thornborough Mill; surface water from fields to the south was also observed flowing onto the site. [PAG] (p39)
Two properties reported internal flooding across ground floors; one property experienced flood depths up to 14cm and subsequently installed resilience measures including raised floor levels. (p41)
The Great Ouse overtopped its banks at Thornborough Mill during the 23rd December 2020 event, causing flooding at the mill location and adjacent areas. A large left–bank floodplain exists but raised ground at the bank restricted flow from the channel to the floodplain, likely concentrating floodwater at the mill. A weir immediately upstream controls water levels and is noted in relation to the event. (p49)
The Thornborough Mill area was inundated by the December 2020 flood event. [PAG] (p50)
Subject to flooding from the Great Ouse during the extreme December 2020 event; the catchment and river flows at Thornborough Mill were directly discussed in relation to the December 2020 flood magnitude. [PAG,IMG] (p51)
Overland flows were observed in the Thornborough Mill area, contributed to by a blocked ditch below the solar farm at Thornborough Grounds. (p62)
High River Great Ouse levels caused floodwater to overtop the south bank near Thornborough Mill, with surface water observed flowing off the adjoining fields onto the area. [PAG] (p64)
The Thornborough Mill catchment on the Great Ouse experienced elevated flows in response to the 23rd December 2020 rainfall (soils already saturated), with an approximate flow return period of 10–30 years. This increased flood risk at Thornborough Mill and underpinned recommendations for measures such as natural flood management and property flood resilience. [PAG] (p65)","approximately 400 mm depth [PAG] (p35)
2 properties; 14cm (ground–floor flood depth) (p41)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.996635990340835,-0.9186163260386571,"<div id=""html_b574eb8411957b114a415b85736c3d6f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thornborough Village Hall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road outside the village hall (Church Lane) flooded, making it impassable in places. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The pond adjacent to the Village Hall was overtopped by Tonne Brook, causing flooding at/near the Village Hall area. [PAG] (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thornborough Village Hall,England,"Road outside the village hall (Church Lane) flooded, making it impassable in places. (p35)
The pond adjacent to the Village Hall was overtopped by Tonne Brook, causing flooding at/near the Village Hall area. [PAG] (p36)",,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.9949995,-0.9263237,"<div id=""html_aa285487985c3863bfec94b04f8a4e77"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stonelands Farm</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area surrounding Stonelands Farm generated overland surface water flows that ran off towards the village and contributed substantial runoff into nearby streets and properties. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fields in the area surrounding Stonelands Farm produced overland surface water flows during the 23 December 2020 event; these contributed significant volumes of runoff that flowed towards and impacted the rear of properties on Lower End and High Street. (p64)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Stonelands Farm,England,"The area surrounding Stonelands Farm generated overland surface water flows that ran off towards the village and contributed substantial runoff into nearby streets and properties. (p39)
Fields in the area surrounding Stonelands Farm produced overland surface water flows during the 23 December 2020 event; these contributed significant volumes of runoff that flowed towards and impacted the rear of properties on Lower End and High Street. (p64)",,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,52.0008063,-0.938927,"<div id=""html_7ca195d03499dbf3b214901a782a0fa0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thornborough Grounds</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A blocked ditch below the solar farm at Thornborough Grounds was identified as a contributor to the overland flows observed in the Thornborough Mill area during the event. (p62)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thornborough Grounds,England,A blocked ditch below the solar farm at Thornborough Grounds was identified as a contributor to the overland flows observed in the Thornborough Mill area during the event. (p62),,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.998123908630134,-0.9186427083143276,"<div id=""html_c64573431dca1c4050bc32f34f017cb7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Back Street Ford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooding was seen at the ford on Back Street with water levels anecdotally reaching greater than 6 ft; water level was noted as above the 6 ft marker at the ford. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ford at Back Street was overtopped by Thorn Brook, causing inundation at that crossing/location. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ford and adjacent footpath on Back Street were flooded from Thorn Brook, with water pooling on the road and reaching the second railing. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Ford on Back Street was flooded during the event; when both this Ford and Lower End were flooded, residents who live between these two points were cut off until the flood waters receded. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Vegetation in the narrow channel downstream of the Ford contributed to exacerbation of flooding on nearby Back Street by restricting conveyance. (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Back Street Ford (Thorn Brook) produced concentrated overland flows along Back Street; in the Back Street/Lower End area at least 13 cars became stranded and flooded requiring Fire Brigade attendance. (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>water levels above 6ft marker; greater than 6ft (anecdotal) (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 ft 2 in (flood board reading at the Back Street ford) (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>13 cars (p64)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Back Street Ford,England,"Road flooding was seen at the ford on Back Street with water levels anecdotally reaching greater than 6 ft; water level was noted as above the 6 ft marker at the ford. (p24)
The ford at Back Street was overtopped by Thorn Brook, causing inundation at that crossing/location. [PAG] (p36)
The ford and adjacent footpath on Back Street were flooded from Thorn Brook, with water pooling on the road and reaching the second railing. (p37)
The Ford on Back Street was flooded during the event; when both this Ford and Lower End were flooded, residents who live between these two points were cut off until the flood waters receded. (p42)
Vegetation in the narrow channel downstream of the Ford contributed to exacerbation of flooding on nearby Back Street by restricting conveyance. (p48)
Flooding at Back Street Ford (Thorn Brook) produced concentrated overland flows along Back Street; in the Back Street/Lower End area at least 13 cars became stranded and flooded requiring Fire Brigade attendance. (p64)","water levels above 6ft marker; greater than 6ft (anecdotal) (p24)
5 ft 2 in (flood board reading at the Back Street ford) (p37)
13 cars (p64)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,52.1278302,-0.4250206,"<div id=""html_2278752b0f0b87d1cf73831f604db2df"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Great Ouse</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Great Ouse experienced high flows contributing to flooding at Thornborough Mill later in the evening of 23 December 2020; an Environment Agency flood warning for the Great Ouse was issued at 18:35 referencing the area. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The south bank of the River Great Ouse at Thornborough Mill was overtopped, allowing river water to flow southward across the floodplain and into properties immediately south of the river. [IMG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced an extreme river flood event during December 2020 that affected flows at Thornborough Mill; the December 2020 flood magnitude on the Great Ouse at Thornborough Mill was described as 'too extreme' in the report and is the primary river flooding influence in the Thornborough Mill area. [PAG] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Great Ouse (affecting the Thornborough Mill catchment) experienced elevated flows in response to the 23rd December 2020 rainfall and saturated soils, contributing to raised river levels and flood risk at Thornborough Mill; this influenced recommendations for catchment and riparian measures. (p65)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Great Ouse,England,"The Great Ouse experienced high flows contributing to flooding at Thornborough Mill later in the evening of 23 December 2020; an Environment Agency flood warning for the Great Ouse was issued at 18:35 referencing the area. [PAG] (p33)
The south bank of the River Great Ouse at Thornborough Mill was overtopped, allowing river water to flow southward across the floodplain and into properties immediately south of the river. [IMG] (p38)
Experienced an extreme river flood event during December 2020 that affected flows at Thornborough Mill; the December 2020 flood magnitude on the Great Ouse at Thornborough Mill was described as 'too extreme' in the report and is the primary river flooding influence in the Thornborough Mill area. [PAG] (p51)
The Great Ouse (affecting the Thornborough Mill catchment) experienced elevated flows in response to the 23rd December 2020 rainfall and saturated soils, contributing to raised river levels and flood risk at Thornborough Mill; this influenced recommendations for catchment and riparian measures. (p65)",,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.99881914734554,-0.924389080465892,"<div id=""html_70b9471b2e92c9c04ae5ca5d83c1de2e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower End Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bridge at Lower End Road became impassable due to flooding. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The location where the watercourse passes under Lower End Bridge was identified in maintenance responsibility notes and was adjacent to channel clearance works carried out after the 23rd December 2020 flood; the IDB cleared upstream sections to increase capacity and removed a concrete in&ndash;channel weir on the upstream face of Hatchet Leys Bridge. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower End Bridge became impassable due to flood water; road impassable and flood water at the bridge reached approximately 400 mm depth (observed 23/12/2020). [TAB] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater reached road level at the bridge, making the road impassable. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cowerde Brook overtopped the bridge and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. Flooding began at around 16:00 and is shown in Figure 8&ndash;2. (p37)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>400 mm depth at Lower End Bridge [TAB] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 400 mm depth (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Lower End Bridge,England,"The bridge at Lower End Road became impassable due to flooding. (p6)
The location where the watercourse passes under Lower End Bridge was identified in maintenance responsibility notes and was adjacent to channel clearance works carried out after the 23rd December 2020 flood; the IDB cleared upstream sections to increase capacity and removed a concrete in–channel weir on the upstream face of Hatchet Leys Bridge. (p29)
Lower End Bridge became impassable due to flood water; road impassable and flood water at the bridge reached approximately 400 mm depth (observed 23/12/2020). [TAB] (p34)
Floodwater reached road level at the bridge, making the road impassable. (p35)
Cowerde Brook overtopped the bridge and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. Flooding began at around 16:00 and is shown in Figure 8–2. (p37)","400 mm depth at Lower End Bridge [TAB] (p34)
approximately 400 mm depth (p35)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.99902235004813,-0.9247592033582557,"<div id=""html_0d3e18e3ea3a77ba63be962cf5aec638"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hatchet Leys Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the 23 December 2020 flood the IDB removed a concrete in&ndash;channel weir on the upstream face of Hatchet Leys Bridge and cleared the stream between the bridges to increase conveyance and reduce blockages. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridge/road became impassable due to flood water; depth at Lower End Bridge reached approximately 400 mm making the road impassable. [TAB] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cowerde Brook overtopped the bridge and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. Flooding began at around 16:00 and is shown in Figure 8&ndash;2. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflow at Hatchet Leys Bridge caused floodwater onto Hatchet Leys Lane; a property flooded internally with one room inundated to about 10 cm, fast&ndash;flowing water breached raised ground outside the front door, and pumping measures were deployed to limit further ingress. Flooding at the bridge was also worsened by converging overland flows from Back Street and High Street. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water backed up immediately downstream of Hatchet Leys bridge due to blockages in the upstream channel/IDB drain, contributing to local flooding at the bridge location. (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Concrete single&ndash;span bridge with a downstream livestock gate that can trap debris and contribute to blockage; during the 23rd December 2020 event water pooled upstream of the bridge, leading to the watercourse overtopping and floodwater spilling onto nearby fields, roads and properties. (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bridge and associated livestock gate/constriction caused water to back up; recommendations include removing the livestock gate and investigating a bypass around the bridge to allow floodwater to flow onto downstream fields to reduce upstream backing. [TAB] (p60)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>400 mm depth [TAB] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one room flooded; approximately 10cm depth (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Hatchet Leys Bridge,England,"Following the 23 December 2020 flood the IDB removed a concrete in–channel weir on the upstream face of Hatchet Leys Bridge and cleared the stream between the bridges to increase conveyance and reduce blockages. (p30)
Bridge/road became impassable due to flood water; depth at Lower End Bridge reached approximately 400 mm making the road impassable. [TAB] (p34)
Cowerde Brook overtopped the bridge and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. Flooding began at around 16:00 and is shown in Figure 8–2. (p37)
Overflow at Hatchet Leys Bridge caused floodwater onto Hatchet Leys Lane; a property flooded internally with one room inundated to about 10 cm, fast–flowing water breached raised ground outside the front door, and pumping measures were deployed to limit further ingress. Flooding at the bridge was also worsened by converging overland flows from Back Street and High Street. (p40)
Water backed up immediately downstream of Hatchet Leys bridge due to blockages in the upstream channel/IDB drain, contributing to local flooding at the bridge location. (p48)
Concrete single–span bridge with a downstream livestock gate that can trap debris and contribute to blockage; during the 23rd December 2020 event water pooled upstream of the bridge, leading to the watercourse overtopping and floodwater spilling onto nearby fields, roads and properties. (p59)
The bridge and associated livestock gate/constriction caused water to back up; recommendations include removing the livestock gate and investigating a bypass around the bridge to allow floodwater to flow onto downstream fields to reduce upstream backing. [TAB] (p60)","400 mm depth [TAB] (p34)
one room flooded; approximately 10cm depth (p40)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.9971062,-0.9201385,"<div id=""html_61565bff7c9ae8d793ac49a6ac0f8395"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Thornborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tonne Brook was observed beginning to overtop at the High Street, causing water to reach and flow along the street (photo evidence from the event). A small watercourse is culverted under the High Street and was a pathway for flows. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>After overtopping of the River Great Ouse, ordinary watercourses overtopped and floodwater on High Street flooded the green to approximately 6 ft in depth. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the street during the event; residents on High Street deployed resilience measures and sandbags, and Transport for Buckinghamshire implemented road closures affecting High Street. [TAB,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents deployed resilience measures and sandbags to protect properties from floodwater. (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tonne Brook began to overtop at High Street (at the pond next to the Village Hall and downstream pond outlet), with photographic evidence of overtopping during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water from the surcharging culvert flowed onto High Street and combined with surface water (exacerbated by blocked drains), resulting in flooding of the street. [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert under High Street surcharged, backing up water that flowed onto the road; this combined with surface water from blocked drains. Tonne Brook began to overtop adjacent to High Street, with floodwater encroaching onto the grassy area, footpath and carriageway. [PAG,IMG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland surface water flows from saturated fields (notably from the area around Stonelands Farm) contributed significant amounts of water towards the rear of properties on Lower End and High Street. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A channel developed on High Street flowing northwards from the spring at Location 9, directing floodwater towards the village. A property near the village hall flooded internally when muddy water entered via a surcharged manhole, reaching approximately 15&ndash;20cm in two rooms. The main village High Street experienced significant flooding between about 19:00 and 20:00 that lasted roughly five hours, causing residents to wade to their cars. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two properties flooded externally; at one property floodwater reached the front&ndash;door sill, and at another floodwaters from Tonne Brook flowed through the rear garden via a diversion flow path (residents reported this garden floods regularly; a sluice is fitted at the gate). (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding was reported at properties (from field runoff) and flooding occurred near the village hall on High Street during the event. [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Playing fields and footpaths on the floodplain off the High Street (sports fields and adjacent footpaths) were flooded; these floodings did not present a significant hazard to residents. [PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tonne Brook began to overtop onto High Street on 23rd December 2020, resulting in water flowing onto the street (reported and photographed in the investigation). [PAG,IMG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from farmland to the south of the village caused external flooding around properties off High Street. No internal flooding was reported, largely because homeowners had carried out resilience works to route water around properties. (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding of High Street after a village&ndash;hall meeting forced residents to wade to their cars; the flooding began between 19:00 and 20:00 and caused major disruption. (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 6ft in depth (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two rooms; 15&ndash;20cm; approximately five hours (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties; 1 property (flood reached front&ndash;door sill) (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [PAG] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"High Street, Thornborough",England,"Tonne Brook was observed beginning to overtop at the High Street, causing water to reach and flow along the street (photo evidence from the event). A small watercourse is culverted under the High Street and was a pathway for flows. [PAG] (p22)
After overtopping of the River Great Ouse, ordinary watercourses overtopped and floodwater on High Street flooded the green to approximately 6 ft in depth. (p24)
Flooding affected the street during the event; residents on High Street deployed resilience measures and sandbags, and Transport for Buckinghamshire implemented road closures affecting High Street. [TAB,PAG] (p34)
Residents deployed resilience measures and sandbags to protect properties from floodwater. (p35)
Tonne Brook began to overtop at High Street (at the pond next to the Village Hall and downstream pond outlet), with photographic evidence of overtopping during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p36)
Water from the surcharging culvert flowed onto High Street and combined with surface water (exacerbated by blocked drains), resulting in flooding of the street. [PAG] (p37)
The culvert under High Street surcharged, backing up water that flowed onto the road; this combined with surface water from blocked drains. Tonne Brook began to overtop adjacent to High Street, with floodwater encroaching onto the grassy area, footpath and carriageway. [PAG,IMG] (p38)
Overland surface water flows from saturated fields (notably from the area around Stonelands Farm) contributed significant amounts of water towards the rear of properties on Lower End and High Street. (p39)
A channel developed on High Street flowing northwards from the spring at Location 9, directing floodwater towards the village. A property near the village hall flooded internally when muddy water entered via a surcharged manhole, reaching approximately 15–20cm in two rooms. The main village High Street experienced significant flooding between about 19:00 and 20:00 that lasted roughly five hours, causing residents to wade to their cars. (p40)
Two properties flooded externally; at one property floodwater reached the front–door sill, and at another floodwaters from Tonne Brook flowed through the rear garden via a diversion flow path (residents reported this garden floods regularly; a sluice is fitted at the gate). (p41)
External flooding was reported at properties (from field runoff) and flooding occurred near the village hall on High Street during the event. [PAG] (p42)
Playing fields and footpaths on the floodplain off the High Street (sports fields and adjacent footpaths) were flooded; these floodings did not present a significant hazard to residents. [PAG] (p43)
Tonne Brook began to overtop onto High Street on 23rd December 2020, resulting in water flowing onto the street (reported and photographed in the investigation). [PAG,IMG] (p46)
Overland flow from farmland to the south of the village caused external flooding around properties off High Street. No internal flooding was reported, largely because homeowners had carried out resilience works to route water around properties. (p62)
Significant flooding of High Street after a village–hall meeting forced residents to wade to their cars; the flooding began between 19:00 and 20:00 and caused major disruption. (p64)","approximately 6ft in depth (p24)
two rooms; 15–20cm; approximately five hours (p40)
2 properties; 1 property (flood reached front–door sill) (p41)
2 properties [PAG] (p42)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.9988998,-0.9204107,"<div id=""html_4feeea16f64e109cb70c31d98c9b1feb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Back Street, Thornborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from agricultural fields accumulated on Back Street, producing surface&ndash;water pooling/flow on the roadway during the event (photo evidence). [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater on Back Street flooded the green to around 6 ft; road flooding was reported at the ford on Back Street with water levels noted above the 6 ft marker and anecdotal reports of depths greater than 6 ft. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cowerde Brook overtopped at Hatchet Leys Lane (around 16:00 on 23 December 2020) and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Back Street was flooded: the ford and footpath were inundated and floodwater pooled on the road. Areas of Back Street were submerged by water overflowing from local brooks (including Cowerde Brook). [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water was observed flowing off fields onto Back Street; an overflowing drainage ditch along the highway verge exacerbated flooding and a channel formed that flowed along the street towards Lower End. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A defined channel developed from converging surface water from fields to the north and flowed along Back Street towards Lower End. Flooding at the junction of Back Street and Lower End stranded and flooded numerous vehicles, creating a hazardous situation that required emergency response. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred along Back Street (including the ford) during the 23 December 2020 event, contributing to local road inundation and isolating residents between the flooded points until waters receded. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Watercourse overtopped at Back Street, causing flooding of the Back Street area. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was exacerbated on Back Street due to vegetation in the narrow channel downstream of the Ford; channel condition contributed to increased local flood risk though a relief route exists. [TAB] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from fields to the north of Back Street was reported to flow out onto the road and down to the junction with Lower End, contributing to overland flows affecting the highway and local area. (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe road flooding at the junction of Back Street and Lower End caused at least 13 cars to become stranded and flooded, creating a hazardous situation that required emergency response. (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 6ft in depth; water level above 6ft marker; greater than 6ft (anecdotal) (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 13 cars (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Back Street, Thornborough",England,"Overland flow from agricultural fields accumulated on Back Street, producing surface–water pooling/flow on the roadway during the event (photo evidence). [PAG] (p22)
Floodwater on Back Street flooded the green to around 6 ft; road flooding was reported at the ford on Back Street with water levels noted above the 6 ft marker and anecdotal reports of depths greater than 6 ft. (p24)
Cowerde Brook overtopped at Hatchet Leys Lane (around 16:00 on 23 December 2020) and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. [PAG] (p36)
Back Street was flooded: the ford and footpath were inundated and floodwater pooled on the road. Areas of Back Street were submerged by water overflowing from local brooks (including Cowerde Brook). [PAG] (p37)
Surface water was observed flowing off fields onto Back Street; an overflowing drainage ditch along the highway verge exacerbated flooding and a channel formed that flowed along the street towards Lower End. (p39)
A defined channel developed from converging surface water from fields to the north and flowed along Back Street towards Lower End. Flooding at the junction of Back Street and Lower End stranded and flooded numerous vehicles, creating a hazardous situation that required emergency response. (p40)
Flooding occurred along Back Street (including the ford) during the 23 December 2020 event, contributing to local road inundation and isolating residents between the flooded points until waters receded. (p42)
Watercourse overtopped at Back Street, causing flooding of the Back Street area. (p43)
Flooding was exacerbated on Back Street due to vegetation in the narrow channel downstream of the Ford; channel condition contributed to increased local flood risk though a relief route exists. [TAB] (p48)
Runoff from fields to the north of Back Street was reported to flow out onto the road and down to the junction with Lower End, contributing to overland flows affecting the highway and local area. (p62)
Severe road flooding at the junction of Back Street and Lower End caused at least 13 cars to become stranded and flooded, creating a hazardous situation that required emergency response. (p64)","approximately 6ft in depth; water level above 6ft marker; greater than 6ft (anecdotal) (p24)
at least 13 cars (p40)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.9961812,-0.9165076,"<div id=""html_001b482d01e4e89786131a462c9789b6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Green, Thornborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the Green near the Two Brewers pub during the event. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Footpaths located within the floodplain were flooded (inundation of pedestrian routes across the floodplain). [TAB] (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"The Green, Thornborough",England,"Flooding affected the Green near the Two Brewers pub during the event. (p42)
Footpaths located within the floodplain were flooded (inundation of pedestrian routes across the floodplain). [TAB] (p43)",,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.9927301,-0.9172068,"<div id=""html_5de6744afdf6768cd6a61b82fea244bd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridge Street, Thornborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding observed in a property, with water coming up through floorboards. [TAB] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tonne Brook overtopped at Bridge Street (location 15), causing flooding of the street and adjacent areas. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of Tonne Brook occurred at Bridge Street, with floodwater reaching the first white railing of the bridge. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents reported sewage backing up into toilets (sewer flooding) and observers noted overland flows pouring off fields onto Bridge Street (surface water reaching the road). (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows from surrounding fields and overflows from the Tonne Brook converged on Bridge Street. Floodwater ran down the street, encroaching on the front of one property; on the far side of the bridge flows reportedly reached depths of about 30 cm as they ran into the village. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred along Bridge Street causing external flooding to properties (water reaching door thresholds) and contributing to road inundation in the village. [PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred along Bridge Street during the event. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Watercourse overtopped at Bridge Street, resulting in overtopping&ndash;related flooding at this location. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridge Street acted as the only possible relief flow route for the Tonne Brook where overtopping of the bridge deck occurred once in&ndash;channel levels backed up; the restricted floodplain (houses on banks) forced flows to use this route, causing floodwater to pass over the bridge and threaten adjoining properties. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was exacerbated on Bridge Street because the narrow, engineered channel provides no relief flow routes, increasing flood depths/impact on the street. [TAB] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 23 December 2020 Bridge Street experienced surface water flowing onto the road (including water pouring off adjacent fields) and sewer&ndash;related impacts: residents reported sewer backup with water entering toilets. The ordinary watercourse at Bridge Street was also recorded as overtopping during the event. (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 30 cm deep (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties; 18cm (reported maximum depth at door thresholds along Bridge Street) [PAG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Bridge Street, Thornborough",England,"Internal flooding observed in a property, with water coming up through floorboards. [TAB] (p34)
Tonne Brook overtopped at Bridge Street (location 15), causing flooding of the street and adjacent areas. [PAG] (p36)
Overtopping of Tonne Brook occurred at Bridge Street, with floodwater reaching the first white railing of the bridge. (p37)
Residents reported sewage backing up into toilets (sewer flooding) and observers noted overland flows pouring off fields onto Bridge Street (surface water reaching the road). (p39)
Overland flows from surrounding fields and overflows from the Tonne Brook converged on Bridge Street. Floodwater ran down the street, encroaching on the front of one property; on the far side of the bridge flows reportedly reached depths of about 30 cm as they ran into the village. (p40)
Flooding occurred along Bridge Street causing external flooding to properties (water reaching door thresholds) and contributing to road inundation in the village. [PAG] (p41)
Flooding occurred along Bridge Street during the event. (p42)
Watercourse overtopped at Bridge Street, resulting in overtopping–related flooding at this location. (p43)
Bridge Street acted as the only possible relief flow route for the Tonne Brook where overtopping of the bridge deck occurred once in–channel levels backed up; the restricted floodplain (houses on banks) forced flows to use this route, causing floodwater to pass over the bridge and threaten adjoining properties. (p44)
Flooding was exacerbated on Bridge Street because the narrow, engineered channel provides no relief flow routes, increasing flood depths/impact on the street. [TAB] (p48)
On 23 December 2020 Bridge Street experienced surface water flowing onto the road (including water pouring off adjacent fields) and sewer–related impacts: residents reported sewer backup with water entering toilets. The ordinary watercourse at Bridge Street was also recorded as overtopping during the event. (p64)","1 property; 30 cm deep (p40)
6 properties; 18cm (reported maximum depth at door thresholds along Bridge Street) [PAG] (p41)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.9989568,-0.9249728,"<div id=""html_e791aaa6c16e78f97532d201609fb3d2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hatchet Leys Lane, Thornborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooding with vehicles stuck; Fire and Rescue attended and the road was shut for safety. [TAB] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bridge at Hatchet Leys Lane was overtopped and the lane/adjacent road was submerged by overflow from Cowerde Brook (flooding began at around 16:00). [PAG,IMG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A property on Hatchet Leys Lane flooded from overflow of Cowerde Brook at Hatchet Leys Bridge; one room flooded to approximately 10cm depth. Fast&ndash;flowing water breached raised land outside the front door, though further internal flooding was limited by use of a sump pump and other measures. Flooding was exacerbated by converging overland flows from Back Street and High Street. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River/watercourse overtopped at the location adjacent to Hatchet Leys Lane, causing localised flooding of the lane/adjacent land. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate siltation of the Cowerde Brook between the bridges at Lower End and Hatchet Leys End produced low&ndash;velocity flows and reduced conveyance in this reach, contributing to local flooding risk during the event. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway gullies were reported blocked or overwhelmed during the event, producing surface water flow paths at the road edges; this roadside surface water contributed to internal flooding of a property and caused localised roadside inundation. [TAB] (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Hatchet Leys Lane bridge area experienced overtopping (from Cowerde Brook), causing road flooding and contributing to roads being unpassable in the village. (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>13 vehicles; one person [TAB] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one room; approximately 10cm (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p60)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Hatchet Leys Lane, Thornborough",England,"Road flooding with vehicles stuck; Fire and Rescue attended and the road was shut for safety. [TAB] (p34)
The bridge at Hatchet Leys Lane was overtopped and the lane/adjacent road was submerged by overflow from Cowerde Brook (flooding began at around 16:00). [PAG,IMG] (p36)
A property on Hatchet Leys Lane flooded from overflow of Cowerde Brook at Hatchet Leys Bridge; one room flooded to approximately 10cm depth. Fast–flowing water breached raised land outside the front door, though further internal flooding was limited by use of a sump pump and other measures. Flooding was exacerbated by converging overland flows from Back Street and High Street. (p40)
River/watercourse overtopped at the location adjacent to Hatchet Leys Lane, causing localised flooding of the lane/adjacent land. (p43)
