at least five residential properties; at least seven overtopping locations; at least 13 cars; approximately five hours [PAG] (p64)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,51.9980626,-0.9236183,"<div id=""html_e5ae63da9f3530a2a1661739fcc20130"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding made the bridge on Lower End Road impassable during the event. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lower End is repeatedly reported as being flooded: in 1902 the whole village (including Lower End) was flooded; in the September 1935 storm many homes at Lower End were invaded and houses near the brook were commonly flooded, with floodwater sweeping over a 6 ft wall and entering houses (reports describe water entering properties to depths around 1 ft). Photographic evidence in the report shows flooding at Lower End Bridge and Hatchet Leys Bridge on 23 December 2020. [PAG,TAB,MUL] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Houses at Lower End near the brook were flooded; many homes were invaded when the brook burst its banks and water entered houses up to 1 foot deep in some properties. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The reach of watercourse passing under the Lower End bridge was the focus of post&ndash;flood clearance works carried out after the 23rd December 2020 event (IDB cleared upstream channel to increase capacity). (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cowerde Brook overtopped at Hatchet Leys Lane (around 16:00 on 23 December 2020) and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. [PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland surface water flows from saturated fields (notably from the area around Stonelands Farm) contributed significant amounts of water towards the rear of properties on Lower End and High Street. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Converging overland flows and overflows from nearby watercourses reached Lower End. Flow paths from College Farm discharged onto the road at Lower End and then flowed northwards; the junction with Back Street experienced stranded vehicles. Surface flows and overtopping combined here to exacerbate flooding. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Field runoff converging from fields to the south caused external flooding at properties on Lower End and High Street; roads were flooded (notably Lower End), the Fire and Rescue Service were called to rescue cars stranded in floodwater, and when Lower End and Back Street Ford were both flooded residents between those points were cut off until waters receded. (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding reported at properties from field runoff converging from fields to the south. The road along Lower End was flooded and the Fire and Rescue Service were called to rescue cars stranded in the floodwater. When Lower End and the Ford on Back Street were both flooded during the event, residents between these points were cut off until waters receded. [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Watercourse overtopped at Lower End, causing flooding of the Lower End area. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate siltation of the Cowerde Brook between the bridges at Lower End and Hatchet Leys End produced low&ndash;velocity flows and reduced conveyance in this reach, contributing to local flooding risk during the event. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows/runoff from surrounding fields flowed down to the junction with Lower End, indicating local road flooding and contribution to flood pathways in this area during the event. (p62)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe road flooding at the junction of Back Street and Lower End caused at least 13 cars to become stranded and flooded, creating a hazardous situation that required emergency response. (p64)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>water entered houses to a depth of 1 foot; 17 or 18 houses; many homes [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 13 cars (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (Lower End and High Street) (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [PAG] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Lower End,England,"Flooding made the bridge on Lower End Road impassable during the event. (p6)
Lower End is repeatedly reported as being flooded: in 1902 the whole village (including Lower End) was flooded; in the September 1935 storm many homes at Lower End were invaded and houses near the brook were commonly flooded, with floodwater sweeping over a 6 ft wall and entering houses (reports describe water entering properties to depths around 1 ft). Photographic evidence in the report shows flooding at Lower End Bridge and Hatchet Leys Bridge on 23 December 2020. [PAG,TAB,MUL] (p23)
Houses at Lower End near the brook were flooded; many homes were invaded when the brook burst its banks and water entered houses up to 1 foot deep in some properties. [PAG] (p24)
The reach of watercourse passing under the Lower End bridge was the focus of post–flood clearance works carried out after the 23rd December 2020 event (IDB cleared upstream channel to increase capacity). (p29)
Cowerde Brook overtopped at Hatchet Leys Lane (around 16:00 on 23 December 2020) and spilled into the road, submerging areas of Back Street and Lower End. [PAG] (p36)
Overland surface water flows from saturated fields (notably from the area around Stonelands Farm) contributed significant amounts of water towards the rear of properties on Lower End and High Street. (p39)
Converging overland flows and overflows from nearby watercourses reached Lower End. Flow paths from College Farm discharged onto the road at Lower End and then flowed northwards; the junction with Back Street experienced stranded vehicles. Surface flows and overtopping combined here to exacerbate flooding. (p40)
Field runoff converging from fields to the south caused external flooding at properties on Lower End and High Street; roads were flooded (notably Lower End), the Fire and Rescue Service were called to rescue cars stranded in floodwater, and when Lower End and Back Street Ford were both flooded residents between those points were cut off until waters receded. (p41)
External flooding reported at properties from field runoff converging from fields to the south. The road along Lower End was flooded and the Fire and Rescue Service were called to rescue cars stranded in the floodwater. When Lower End and the Ford on Back Street were both flooded during the event, residents between these points were cut off until waters receded. [PAG] (p42)
Watercourse overtopped at Lower End, causing flooding of the Lower End area. (p43)
Moderate siltation of the Cowerde Brook between the bridges at Lower End and Hatchet Leys End produced low–velocity flows and reduced conveyance in this reach, contributing to local flooding risk during the event. (p44)
Overland flows/runoff from surrounding fields flowed down to the junction with Lower End, indicating local road flooding and contribution to flood pathways in this area during the event. (p62)
Severe road flooding at the junction of Back Street and Lower End caused at least 13 cars to become stranded and flooded, creating a hazardous situation that required emergency response. (p64)","water entered houses to a depth of 1 foot; 17 or 18 houses; many homes [PAG] (p24)
at least 13 cars (p40)
2 properties (Lower End and High Street) (p41)
2 properties [PAG] (p42)",GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,52.0154955,-0.9053801,"<div id=""html_4443b95fb2c37be19dda3ef2b4eee25f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thornton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding during the 23 December 2020 event. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thornton,England,Experienced flooding during the 23 December 2020 event. (p14),,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,20,2020-12-23,52.0336802,-0.9402602,"<div id=""html_a719f19298dc4383dcdb79253b040ec5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Leckhampstead</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding during the 23 December 2020 event. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Leckhampstead,England,Experienced flooding during the 23 December 2020 event. (p14),,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23.html,GB-BKM_20_2020-12-23,Thornborough flooding 23 Dec 2020,Thornborough Section 19 Flood Investigation: Final Technical Report,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2020/Buckinghamshire County/Thornborough/Section/19/Flood/Investigation/July.pdf
GB-BKM,15,2013-12-01,51.5122422,-0.5495416,"<div id=""html_5f3b10c07bed281c8a5aec5567539690"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Orchards Residential Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged above&ndash;average rainfall caused groundwater levels to rise, contributing to the overwhelming of drainage and resulting localised flooding. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected mainly the south of The Orchards residential park during the December 2013&ndash;February 2014 event. Flooding resulted from a combination of high watercourse (fluvial) levels, elevated groundwater and surface water ponding which inundated the southern part of the park. [PAG,IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The southern area of the residential park flooded during the December 2013&ndash;February 2014 event. Surface water flooding observed on the maps corresponds to flooding actually experienced; high watercourse (river) levels and high groundwater levels, together with surface runoff, combined to cause inundation of the park. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The combined drainage system became inundated (full to capacity) due to groundwater infiltration and excess runoff; it backed up and discharged via gullies, causing contaminated floodwater to pond around the residential park and reducing system capacity. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Orchards Residential Park experienced flooding when the combined drainage system could not cope with extreme, prolonged rainfall. Floodwater (contaminated with sewerage) covered roads, gardens and footpaths, making access very difficult and creating environmental and public health risks. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive external flooding across The Orchards Residential Park driven by extreme, prolonged rainfall. The combined drainage system backed up and discharged via gullies; floodwater was contaminated with sewage and covered roads, gardens and footpaths, restricting access and creating environmental and public health risk. No properties were flooded internally. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0 properties internally affected (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,The Orchards Residential Park,England,"Prolonged above–average rainfall caused groundwater levels to rise, contributing to the overwhelming of drainage and resulting localised flooding. (p4)
Surface water flooding affected mainly the south of The Orchards residential park during the December 2013–February 2014 event. Flooding resulted from a combination of high watercourse (fluvial) levels, elevated groundwater and surface water ponding which inundated the southern part of the park. [PAG,IMG] (p9)
The southern area of the residential park flooded during the December 2013–February 2014 event. Surface water flooding observed on the maps corresponds to flooding actually experienced; high watercourse (river) levels and high groundwater levels, together with surface runoff, combined to cause inundation of the park. [PAG] (p10)
The combined drainage system became inundated (full to capacity) due to groundwater infiltration and excess runoff; it backed up and discharged via gullies, causing contaminated floodwater to pond around the residential park and reducing system capacity. [PAG] (p12)
The Orchards Residential Park experienced flooding when the combined drainage system could not cope with extreme, prolonged rainfall. Floodwater (contaminated with sewerage) covered roads, gardens and footpaths, making access very difficult and creating environmental and public health risks. [TAB] (p16)
Extensive external flooding across The Orchards Residential Park driven by extreme, prolonged rainfall. The combined drainage system backed up and discharged via gullies; floodwater was contaminated with sewage and covered roads, gardens and footpaths, restricting access and creating environmental and public health risk. No properties were flooded internally. (p17)",0 properties internally affected (p17),GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01,Wexham flooding December 2013 to February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: The Orchards Residential Park, Wexham December 2013 – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/the-orchards-wexham.pdf
GB-BKM,15,2013-12-01,51.5385835,-0.4967758,"<div id=""html_5b714cce15822d4c326686c247223b43"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Colne Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Orchards Residential Park experienced a prolonged period of flooding from December 2013 to February 2014, with severe flooding in early February 2014. Floodwater ponded around the park after the combined drainage system backed up and discharged via gullies; the ponded water was contaminated with sewerage, posing environmental and public health risks. Due to the height of the homes there were no records of internal flooding. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Colne Brook,England,"The Orchards Residential Park experienced a prolonged period of flooding from December 2013 to February 2014, with severe flooding in early February 2014. Floodwater ponded around the park after the combined drainage system backed up and discharged via gullies; the ponded water was contaminated with sewerage, posing environmental and public health risks. Due to the height of the homes there were no records of internal flooding. (p12)",,GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01,Wexham flooding December 2013 to February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: The Orchards Residential Park, Wexham December 2013 – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/the-orchards-wexham.pdf
GB-BKM,15,2013-12-01,51.5314718,-0.5518437,"<div id=""html_a98f4ad19f47d13aec4f3f8c2e8d37d8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wexham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An increased volume of surface water contributed to overwhelming the private drainage and causing localised flooding on site. (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Wexham,England,An increased volume of surface water contributed to overwhelming the private drainage and causing localised flooding on site. (p4),,GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01,Wexham flooding December 2013 to February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: The Orchards Residential Park, Wexham December 2013 – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/the-orchards-wexham.pdf
GB-BKM,15,2013-12-01,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_f9ccf17679f05a970ca3e27c7f29ccc8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the December 2013&ndash;February 2014 event the Colne Brook tributaries were flowing at full capacity due to prolonged heavy rainfall; following the flood event log debris was observed within the watercourse though no large blockage was reported. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames Water carried out extra pumping at the pumping station to relieve the sewer/system, an action thought to have caused the rapid receding of accumulated flood water during the event. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"During the December 2013–February 2014 event the Colne Brook tributaries were flowing at full capacity due to prolonged heavy rainfall; following the flood event log debris was observed within the watercourse though no large blockage was reported. [PAG] (p11)
Thames Water carried out extra pumping at the pumping station to relieve the sewer/system, an action thought to have caused the rapid receding of accumulated flood water during the event. (p14)",,GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01.html,GB-BKM_15_2013-12-01,Wexham flooding December 2013 to February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: The Orchards Residential Park, Wexham December 2013 – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/the-orchards-wexham.pdf
