GB-NNH,1,2023-06-18,52.3914945,-0.7166333,"<div id=""html_7902d618ef66b90bb8ccf4c6ff7a0b06"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wallis Road, Kettering</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties were flooded on Wallis Road. [TAB] (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties [TAB] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Wallis Road, Kettering",England,Residential properties were flooded on Wallis Road. [TAB] (p6),4 properties [TAB] (p6),GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc.html,GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc,,,
GB-NNH,1,2023-06-18,52.4185781,-0.7278625,"<div id=""html_e391b920a5d4c0403077012c1509053c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rockingham Paddocks, Kettering</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties served off a shared driveway; carriageway slopes toward Rockingham Road and runoff reached properties downstream/north of Rockingham Paddocks. Two upstream road gullies were observed partially blocked and contributed to runoff not being drained, leading to repeated internal property flooding and triggering a Section 19 investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [PAG,MUL] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Rockingham Paddocks, Kettering",England,"Internal flooding of residential properties served off a shared driveway; carriageway slopes toward Rockingham Road and runoff reached properties downstream/north of Rockingham Paddocks. Two upstream road gullies were observed partially blocked and contributed to runoff not being drained, leading to repeated internal property flooding and triggering a Section 19 investigation. [PAG,MUL] (p20)","2 properties [PAG,MUL] (p20)",GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc.html,GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc,,,
GB-NNH,1,2023-06-18,52.4822863,-0.4956477,"<div id=""html_f37f49da2236db2f9a5316971315b1f4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Benefield Road, Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property on two occasions within a two&ndash;year period; the repeated internal flooding prompted a Section 19 flood investigation to be commissioned. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During intense rainfall, rainwater ponded on Benefield Road in front of a dwelling, overtopped a retaining wall upstand (or was splashed over by vehicles), ponded on the lower footway and entered the affected property through the door and wall, causing internal flooding. A similar event occurred in July 2020 with more extensive damage. [MUL] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two internal flooding incidents at the property [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 flood incidents (July 2020; 18/06/2023) [MUL] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Benefield Road, Oundle",England,"Internal flooding of a property on two occasions within a two–year period; the repeated internal flooding prompted a Section 19 flood investigation to be commissioned. [PAG] (p22)
During intense rainfall, rainwater ponded on Benefield Road in front of a dwelling, overtopped a retaining wall upstand (or was splashed over by vehicles), ponded on the lower footway and entered the affected property through the door and wall, causing internal flooding. A similar event occurred in July 2020 with more extensive damage. [MUL] (p23)","two internal flooding incidents at the property [PAG] (p22)
2 flood incidents (July 2020; 18/06/2023) [MUL] (p23)",GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc.html,GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc,,,
GB-NNH,1,2023-06-18,52.3994233,-0.728004,"<div id=""html_aa8eab7e2dd01fd3e82b03822a0069e3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kettering</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in the Rockingham Paddocks area of Kettering experienced internal flooding on multiple occasions, prompting a formal Section 19 flood investigation. [MUL] (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [MUL] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Kettering,England,"Residential properties in the Rockingham Paddocks area of Kettering experienced internal flooding on multiple occasions, prompting a formal Section 19 flood investigation. [MUL] (p20)",2 properties [MUL] (p20),GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc.html,GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc,,,
GB-NNH,1,2023-06-18,52.480678744170966,-0.46597423959156464,"<div id=""html_142f73ac7f3918ec1e3d6a40491608df"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface&ndash;water flooding on Benefield Road resulted in ponding and overtopping of a wall upstand, leading to internal flooding of at least one dwelling; the same mechanism produced a similar, larger&ndash;impact event in July 2020. [MUL] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 flood incidents (July 2020; 18/06/2023) [MUL] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Oundle,England,"Localised surface–water flooding on Benefield Road resulted in ponding and overtopping of a wall upstand, leading to internal flooding of at least one dwelling; the same mechanism produced a similar, larger–impact event in July 2020. [MUL] (p23)",2 flood incidents (July 2020; 18/06/2023) [MUL] (p23),GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc.html,GB-NNH_1_2023-06-18_etc,,,
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9785241,-0.0253413,"<div id=""html_f02cd286570a51a0d065b434846d58f6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Botolph's Church</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The historic St Botolph's Church (the 'Boston Stump') was damaged as part of the impacts from the coastal surge and associated flooding/overtopping during the event. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater pooled around and partially submerged the base of the Boston Stump (St Botolph's Church), reaching several inches up the stone walls. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Botolph&#8217;s Church is shown within/adjacent to the BOS1A/B flood warning areas; since the reported flood extent closely matched those FWAs on 5 December 2013, the church area was likely exposed to inundation or flood&ndash;related disruption from overtopping/breach risk. (p35)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 770 properties (total in BOS1A and BOS1B) (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,St Botolph's Church,England,"The historic St Botolph's Church (the 'Boston Stump') was damaged as part of the impacts from the coastal surge and associated flooding/overtopping during the event. (p4)
Floodwater pooled around and partially submerged the base of the Boston Stump (St Botolph's Church), reaching several inches up the stone walls. [IMG] (p13)
St Botolph’s Church is shown within/adjacent to the BOS1A/B flood warning areas; since the reported flood extent closely matched those FWAs on 5 December 2013, the church area was likely exposed to inundation or flood–related disruption from overtopping/breach risk. (p35)",approx. 770 properties (total in BOS1A and BOS1B) (p35),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.0963304,0.3220058,"<div id=""html_74ad88d71447db38a2f74b565156f17a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre was damaged by flooding during the coastal surge. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Defence overtopping caused flooding and damage to the Gibraltar Point visitor centre. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre,England,"Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre was damaged by flooding during the coastal surge. (p4)
Defence overtopping caused flooding and damage to the Gibraltar Point visitor centre. (p13)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.99156205,-0.009981582258731664,"<div id=""html_7d70a19f302ac5c1fb9bab5ea57e0e26"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pilgrim Hospital</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The hospital was cited as being under threat from the flood event and therefore at risk from inundation or flood impacts. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Was the subject of contingency discussions to protect hospital operations and services in case of flooding from the predicted surge and coastal overtopping. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>In anticipation of the tidal surge and flood risk the hospital cancelled elective and outpatient activity for 6 December; held mutual aid discussions with Nottinghamshire trusts regarding potential relocation of patients; undertook evacuation planning (including vertical evacuation and alternative power supply); ambulance diversions were temporarily in place and then lifted following consultation. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as at risk of flooding; required business continuity planning for flooding affecting the hospital and its A&E, and operational liaison (e.g. police access through cordons) to maintain staff and ambulance access. (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Pilgrim Hospital,England,"The hospital was cited as being under threat from the flood event and therefore at risk from inundation or flood impacts. (p5)
Was the subject of contingency discussions to protect hospital operations and services in case of flooding from the predicted surge and coastal overtopping. (p16)
In anticipation of the tidal surge and flood risk the hospital cancelled elective and outpatient activity for 6 December; held mutual aid discussions with Nottinghamshire trusts regarding potential relocation of patients; undertook evacuation planning (including vertical evacuation and alternative power supply); ambulance diversions were temporarily in place and then lifted following consultation. (p22)
Identified as at risk of flooding; required business continuity planning for flooding affecting the hospital and its A&E, and operational liaison (e.g. police access through cordons) to maintain staff and ambulance access. (p40)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9391975,0.0671494,"<div id=""html_030be6a74e13cbaf0b30d6ffc4b8827f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>HMP North Sea Camp</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The prison was identified as being threatened by the flood event and at risk from flood impacts. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as being under threat from the coastal flood event; protective actions for infrastructure were required as part of the multi&ndash;agency response. (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,HMP North Sea Camp,England,"The prison was identified as being threatened by the flood event and at risk from flood impacts. (p5)
Identified as being under threat from the coastal flood event; protective actions for infrastructure were required as part of the multi–agency response. (p43)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.141949499999996,0.34379555882352747,"<div id=""html_ddc42d7dacc9805440e88c305e96384b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Clock Tower</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater extended up to the Clock Tower (landmark in Skegness), indicating inundation of the immediate seafront area. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Clock Tower,England,"Floodwater extended up to the Clock Tower (landmark in Skegness), indicating inundation of the immediate seafront area. (p22)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9852652,-0.025935,"<div id=""html_2ada1d057641f8eb2e984eb9cc2c24b5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Boston Fire Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Threatened by a high&ndash;tide surge and potential breaches to defences on 5 December 2013. The threat prompted relocation of the Boston 'surge task force' / joint Bronze to Fen Road Depot and contributed to evacuation activity and disruption to fire station operations during the event. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Boston Fire Station,England,Threatened by a high–tide surge and potential breaches to defences on 5 December 2013. The threat prompted relocation of the Boston 'surge task force' / joint Bronze to Fen Road Depot and contributed to evacuation activity and disruption to fire station operations during the event. (p22),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9014341,-0.6089289,"<div id=""html_a837dcef2a71eccfa3b2009aeff1ecc5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Prince William of Gloucester Barracks</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prince William of Gloucester Barracks was closed for evacuation purposes because of the flood/tide risk and later returned to normal use. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Prince William of Gloucester Barracks,England,Prince William of Gloucester Barracks was closed for evacuation purposes because of the flood/tide risk and later returned to normal use. (p26),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.4733156,0.1501702,"<div id=""html_477e022c3255e7922bddca80dae914ec"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Donna Nook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Preventive action during the event led seals to seek refuge on higher ground in the dunes; reported seal mortalities were low. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Donna Nook,England,Preventive action during the event led seals to seek refuge on higher ground in the dunes; reported seal mortalities were low. (p13),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.1022872,0.3172214,"<div id=""html_4b2f16f16f66b5d6f9023310c83a3716"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Burgh Sluice</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood walls at Burgh Sluice overtopped, causing damage to the compound and local inundation. [MUL] (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Burgh Sluice,England,"Flood walls at Burgh Sluice overtopped, causing damage to the compound and local inundation. [MUL] (p31)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,56.0027,2.8144672,"<div id=""html_a6ee9434d9edcfabbf29673a08345bdc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Sea</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A significant storm surge moved along the North Sea coast, coinciding with high tide and driving coastal flooding and overtopping along the east coast of England. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Surge ~2 metres above predicted high water; surge reached 5.2 metres at Immingham (70 cm higher than 1953) [PAG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,North Sea,England,"A significant storm surge moved along the North Sea coast, coinciding with high tide and driving coastal flooding and overtopping along the east coast of England. [PAG] (p12)",Surge ~2 metres above predicted high water; surge reached 5.2 metres at Immingham (70 cm higher than 1953) [PAG] (p12),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.5278051,0.0267549,"<div id=""html_e47a4afe7918688b10fe4791b34f0fed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tetney Marsh</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Breach of flood defences at Tetney Marsh during the surge, resulting in inundation of agricultural land. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one of 4 breach locations; breaches collectively inundated an estimated 1,700 hectares of agricultural land (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Tetney Marsh,England,"Breach of flood defences at Tetney Marsh during the surge, resulting in inundation of agricultural land. (p12)","one of 4 breach locations; breaches collectively inundated an estimated 1,700 hectares of agricultural land (p12)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9483421,0.0036583,"<div id=""html_16ff1ec25e7083672665380892728b8f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wyberton Marsh</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Secondary defence breach at Wyberton Marsh (same area as the waste management site). Defences possibly further compromised by shipping movements; prompted police&ndash;led repairs (aggregate collection), a request for military assistance and precautionary/localised evacuation planning to contain flooding and minimise risk to life. (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1,800 residential properties identified as potentially 'at risk' (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Wyberton Marsh,England,"Secondary defence breach at Wyberton Marsh (same area as the waste management site). Defences possibly further compromised by shipping movements; prompted police–led repairs (aggregate collection), a request for military assistance and precautionary/localised evacuation planning to contain flooding and minimise risk to life. (p27)","1,800 residential properties identified as potentially 'at risk' (p27)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.0957994,0.3261359,"<div id=""html_12592cb8f17d89d8124e084087b9bf42"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gibraltar Point</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Breach of flood defences at Gibraltar Point (noted at Bull Dog Bank) during the surge, causing inundation of agricultural land. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Damage to the natural environment at Gibraltar Point; defence overtopping and flood damage to Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre were confirmed by Lincolnshire Wildlife Trust. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Breaches at the nearby privately owned Jubilee Bank led to inundation of adjacent agricultural land near Gibraltar Point. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Defence overtopping and flood damage to Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre; coastal flooding affected facilities at Gibraltar Point. [MUL] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant damage to the natural environment and Wildlife Trust properties; concerns that local flood defences had been compromised, prompting plans to deploy temporary demountable flood defences; nearby flood walls (Burgh Sluice) were overtopped causing damage to a compound. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The early December surge created threats to coastal defences at Gibraltar Point; these were assessed and managed by the EA and contingency planning was implemented to strengthen weakened defences ahead of subsequent high tides. (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one of 4 breach locations; breaches collectively inundated an estimated 1,700 hectares of agricultural land (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>500 acres of agricultural land flooded (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Gibraltar Point,England,"Breach of flood defences at Gibraltar Point (noted at Bull Dog Bank) during the surge, causing inundation of agricultural land. (p12)
Damage to the natural environment at Gibraltar Point; defence overtopping and flood damage to Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre were confirmed by Lincolnshire Wildlife Trust. (p13)
Breaches at the nearby privately owned Jubilee Bank led to inundation of adjacent agricultural land near Gibraltar Point. (p27)
Defence overtopping and flood damage to Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre; coastal flooding affected facilities at Gibraltar Point. [MUL] (p31)
Significant damage to the natural environment and Wildlife Trust properties; concerns that local flood defences had been compromised, prompting plans to deploy temporary demountable flood defences; nearby flood walls (Burgh Sluice) were overtopped causing damage to a compound. [PAG] (p32)
The early December surge created threats to coastal defences at Gibraltar Point; these were assessed and managed by the EA and contingency planning was implemented to strengthen weakened defences ahead of subsequent high tides. (p47)","one of 4 breach locations; breaches collectively inundated an estimated 1,700 hectares of agricultural land (p12)
500 acres of agricultural land flooded (p27)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.4719692,-0.7839006,"<div id=""html_7fccb4746a0deadba1deba2bd976b488"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wildsworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads reported flooded and described as passable with care. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Wildsworth,England,Roads reported flooded and described as passable with care. (p23),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.8770582,-1.3062493,"<div id=""html_ab90f9ef9c8f01f83b5d7a949b94c6f1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Trent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as a tidal&ndash;locked river subject to flooding from coastal surges: flooding within the river channel and accumulations of seawater on adjacent low&ndash;lying land due to overtopping of sea defences, with associated risk of damage to defences and potential breaches. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Information indicated potential tidal flooding from the River Trent affecting nearby communities (for example East Ferry), prompting local preparedness and sandbag deployment. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal flooding from the River Trent caused flooding that affected the village of Susworth, resulting in residential properties being flooded. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Caused 'cross&ndash;border' flood threats during the event, prompting a requirement for the LRF to ensure effective liaison and communication with the EA over these threats. (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 households (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,River Trent,England,"Described as a tidal–locked river subject to flooding from coastal surges: flooding within the river channel and accumulations of seawater on adjacent low–lying land due to overtopping of sea defences, with associated risk of damage to defences and potential breaches. (p7)
Information indicated potential tidal flooding from the River Trent affecting nearby communities (for example East Ferry), prompting local preparedness and sandbag deployment. (p21)
Tidal flooding from the River Trent caused flooding that affected the village of Susworth, resulting in residential properties being flooded. (p38)
Caused 'cross–border' flood threats during the event, prompting a requirement for the LRF to ensure effective liaison and communication with the EA over these threats. (p50)",4 households (p38),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9448212,0.018233,"<div id=""html_6d358e6c1b6b7502003dbe4376dff7bc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Haven</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as a tidal&ndash;locked river subject to flooding from coastal surges: flooding within the river channel and accumulations of seawater on adjacent low&ndash;lying land due to overtopping of sea defences, with associated risk of damage to defences and potential breaches. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A prolonged positive tidal surge was predicted to push up the tidal River Haven, creating conditions for overtopping and spray along the open coastline and placing pressure on local defences during the high tides on 5&ndash;6 December 2013. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Burst its banks and overtopped into adjacent urban streets in Boston, flooding neighbouring streets and nearby properties; overtopping occurred with water level reported at 6.1 m; damage to the top 50 cm of a 10 metres low floodwall. [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Apparent breach of the River Haven and failure of associated defences contributing to flood threat for adjacent areas (linked to secondary defence concerns at Wyberton Marsh). This breach presented risk to residential areas at the next high tide. (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>top 50 cm of a 10 metres low floodwall; approx. 350 properties [IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1,800 residential properties identified as potentially 'at risk' (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,River Haven,England,"Described as a tidal–locked river subject to flooding from coastal surges: flooding within the river channel and accumulations of seawater on adjacent low–lying land due to overtopping of sea defences, with associated risk of damage to defences and potential breaches. (p7)
A prolonged positive tidal surge was predicted to push up the tidal River Haven, creating conditions for overtopping and spray along the open coastline and placing pressure on local defences during the high tides on 5–6 December 2013. (p17)
Burst its banks and overtopped into adjacent urban streets in Boston, flooding neighbouring streets and nearby properties; overtopping occurred with water level reported at 6.1 m; damage to the top 50 cm of a 10 metres low floodwall. [IMG] (p25)
Apparent breach of the River Haven and failure of associated defences contributing to flood threat for adjacent areas (linked to secondary defence concerns at Wyberton Marsh). This breach presented risk to residential areas at the next high tide. (p27)","top 50 cm of a 10 metres low floodwall; approx. 350 properties [IMG] (p25)
1,800 residential properties identified as potentially 'at risk' (p27)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.7124015,-0.3652257,"<div id=""html_07081b8bd23b607865ff62934bf8ed8e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Humber</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Defences were overtopped and there was flood damage to visitor facilities: Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre. Preventive action at Donna Nook allowed seals to seek refuge on higher ground in the dunes; reported seal mortalities were low. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The tidal surge posed a potential flooding threat around the south bank of the Humber where defences had been damaged; partners monitored high tides and issued guidance/alerts though high tides later passed without further incident. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,River Humber,England,"Defences were overtopped and there was flood damage to visitor facilities: Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre. Preventive action at Donna Nook allowed seals to seek refuge on higher ground in the dunes; reported seal mortalities were low. (p14)
The tidal surge posed a potential flooding threat around the south bank of the Humber where defences had been damaged; partners monitored high tides and issued guidance/alerts though high tides later passed without further incident. (p33)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9747017,-0.0238988,"<div id=""html_acc444d237b90fda00e40587324db22b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Haven Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Serves as the downstream boundary of FWA BOS1A; waterside properties between Town Bridge and Haven Bridge (BOS1A) were inundated during the tidal surge on 5th December 2013. (p35)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>part of the approx. 770 properties combined in BOS1A and BOS1B (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Haven Bridge,England,Serves as the downstream boundary of FWA BOS1A; waterside properties between Town Bridge and Haven Bridge (BOS1A) were inundated during the tidal surge on 5th December 2013. (p35),part of the approx. 770 properties combined in BOS1A and BOS1B (p35),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9777459,-0.024807,"<div id=""html_b0f1d771bb114d708d857487f4539efd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Street, Boston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Site of reported flooding that prompted the first FRS call and subsequent rescue activity. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Church Street, Boston",England,Site of reported flooding that prompted the first FRS call and subsequent rescue activity. (p22),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9551715,-0.0089288,"<div id=""html_d7c2b0bcf3dbc57f1176b16d963d2ab8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Slippery Gowt Lane, Boston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water entered business premises on Slippery Gowt Lane during the first critical high tide, causing inundation of the street and premises. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Erosion of flood defences at Slippery Gowt Lane (Wyberton) with repairs underway to reinstate defences prior to the next high tides. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood surge in early December compromised the flood defences at Slippery Gowt Lane; the weakened defences were assessed and managed by the EA and contingency measures were put in place to strengthen them ahead of subsequent high tides. (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Slippery Gowt Lane, Boston",England,"Water entered business premises on Slippery Gowt Lane during the first critical high tide, causing inundation of the street and premises. (p23)
Erosion of flood defences at Slippery Gowt Lane (Wyberton) with repairs underway to reinstate defences prior to the next high tides. (p30)
Flood surge in early December compromised the flood defences at Slippery Gowt Lane; the weakened defences were assessed and managed by the EA and contingency measures were put in place to strengthen them ahead of subsequent high tides. (p47)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9642649,-0.0721753,"<div id=""html_f3d6b25cc801b8c52b58e9048b891ef9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fen Road, Boston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lincolnshire Fire and Rescue deployed pumping operations in the Fen Road area to remove floodwater and protect properties/streets. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Fen Road, Boston",England,Lincolnshire Fire and Rescue deployed pumping operations in the Fen Road area to remove floodwater and protect properties/streets. (p24),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9738215,-0.0241045,"<div id=""html_7b3aed628d029d5815361c06c184e438"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Whitehorse Lane, Boston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>EA identified concerns that flood defences in the White Horse Lane area were potentially compromised by the December surge; plans were made to deploy temporary demountable flood defences at the site. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood surge in early December compromised the flood defences at White Horse Lane; these threats were assessed and managed by the EA and actions were taken to strengthen weakened defences prior to subsequent high tides. (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,"Whitehorse Lane, Boston",England,"EA identified concerns that flood defences in the White Horse Lane area were potentially compromised by the December surge; plans were made to deploy temporary demountable flood defences at the site. (p32)
Flood surge in early December compromised the flood defences at White Horse Lane; these threats were assessed and managed by the EA and actions were taken to strengthen weakened defences prior to subsequent high tides. (p47)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.53102,-1.2649062,"<div id=""html_1801f588bbd57ab57f5c607a67fbf8a5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>England</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A deepening pressure system combined with high tides and strong north&ndash;westerly winds generated a coastal surge on 5 December 2013 along the east coast of England, resulting in overtopping and breaches of sea defences and inundation of properties in the affected area. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The coastal surge on 5 December 2013 affected the east coast of England, resulting in overtopping and breaches of defences, inundation of land, damage to infrastructure and flooding of properties in the county. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flood warnings were issued across England as part of the wider event; warnings were concentrated along coastal areas and major river valleys, prompting large&ndash;scale warning messaging to homes and businesses. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood Guidance System (FGS) raised the alert to Amber for the north&ndash;east coast of England, indicating a medium likelihood of significant coastal/tidal flooding impacts and prompting multi&ndash;agency warnings and preparedness at a national/coastal scale. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 720 residential and commercial properties inundated; 3 casualties (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18&ndash;20 km of flood defences overtopped; four breach locations; 1,700 hectares of agricultural land inundated; 6,072 residential properties flooded; 121 businesses flooded; approximately 350 tonnes of flood&ndash;contaminated waste collected; £8.1m damage to infrastructure (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>71 severe flood warnings (total for England & Wales); more than 160,000 warning messages sent directly to homes and businesses [IMG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,England,England,"A deepening pressure system combined with high tides and strong north–westerly winds generated a coastal surge on 5 December 2013 along the east coast of England, resulting in overtopping and breaches of sea defences and inundation of properties in the affected area. (p2)
The coastal surge on 5 December 2013 affected the east coast of England, resulting in overtopping and breaches of defences, inundation of land, damage to infrastructure and flooding of properties in the county. (p4)
Severe flood warnings were issued across England as part of the wider event; warnings were concentrated along coastal areas and major river valleys, prompting large–scale warning messaging to homes and businesses. [IMG] (p6)
Flood Guidance System (FGS) raised the alert to Amber for the north–east coast of England, indicating a medium likelihood of significant coastal/tidal flooding impacts and prompting multi–agency warnings and preparedness at a national/coastal scale. (p18)","more than 720 residential and commercial properties inundated; 3 casualties (p2)
18–20 km of flood defences overtopped; four breach locations; 1,700 hectares of agricultural land inundated; 6,072 residential properties flooded; 121 businesses flooded; approximately 350 tonnes of flood–contaminated waste collected; £8.1m damage to infrastructure (p4)
71 severe flood warnings (total for England & Wales); more than 160,000 warning messages sent directly to homes and businesses [IMG] (p6)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.1823034,-0.2031209,"<div id=""html_aa0bdbb8b354720ddb30c37423d14d3f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lincolnshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A forecast&ndash;led coastal surge event in early December 2013 led to a large multi&ndash;agency emergency response in Lincolnshire; the surge caused overtopping and breaches of defences, evacuations, rescues of people isolated by floodwaters, and inundation of properties. [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;wide sea defences were severely tested protecting homes, businesses and 220,000 hectares; widespread standing water was managed by drainage and high&ndash;volume pumping; Boston (within the county) suffered the largest number of flooded residential properties. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The event required multi&ndash;agency coordination in Lincolnshire: reliance on mutual aid and national specialist assets, prolonged deployments of responders, and targeted welfare, evacuation and medical support for vulnerable people. Recommendations were made to the Local Resilience Forum to improve planning, information&ndash;sharing and integration of community volunteers. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal surge and associated high tides/onshore winds can cause overtopping and defence breaches affecting Lincolnshire: mapped flood depth, velocity and extent have been developed for defence overtopping and defence breach scenarios. Reasonable worst&ndash;case (multiple breaches) impacts include large numbers of properties, infrastructure and casualties; overtopping&ndash;only scenarios (1:200 and 1:1000 annual chance tide levels) give quantified property and premise impacts. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood defences were overtopped along 18&ndash;20 km of coastline, causing large areas of scour (e.g. Mablethorpe), four breach locations at Boston Haven, Gibraltar Point (Bull Dog Bank), Tetney Marsh and Friskney, inundation of extensive agricultural land, damage to industrial/commercial sites and local infrastructure, failure of ~90 m of floodwall at Skegness, and damage to pumps at Black Sluice Gate. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Defence overtopping and flood damage to coastal visitor centres: Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre. Preventive action at Donna Nook allowed seals to seek refuge on higher ground in the dunes; reported seal mortalities were low. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lincolnshire was placed under an anticipated Amber flood guidance level for the coastal/ tidal surge on 5 December 2013; multi&ndash;agency contingency planning, pre&ndash;deployment of assets and precautionary SCG arrangements were implemented across the county in response to the threat of spray, overtopping and a predicted River Haven surge. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The county faced a medium overall risk of significant coastal/tidal flooding (FGS raised to 'Amber'); Flood Alerts were issued for the Lincolnshire coastline, coastal floodgates were patrolled/closed and contingency planning and escalation arrangements (including opening the County Emergency Centre) were activated for the predicted high tides on 5&ndash;6 December 2013. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency flood warnings ('be prepared') issued for waterside properties in and around Boston (waterside properties between Town Bridge and Haven Bridge, Grand Sluice and the Docks) and selected coastal areas from Gibraltar Point to Sutton Bridge; Boston identified as the focus for high tide periods; multi&ndash;agency evacuation planning authorised and surge task forces established (Boston and Louth) to respond to coastal flooding risk. [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County subject to an elevated flood threat (FGS 'Amber'); tactical coordination included evacuation planning and multi&ndash;agency flood response. Internal Drainage Boards activated emergency pumping arrangements; an evacuation hub and forward base were identified (Princess Royal Sports Arena) and voluntary sector and health partners mobilised to support potential evacuations. Fire & Rescue Service identified flood rescue capacity to support large&ndash;scale rescues. [IMG,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;scale response to a coastal/tidal surge on 5 December 2013: evacuation planning for vulnerable areas (including planning for up to 600 properties), deployment of empty and filled sandbags to coastal towns (Skegness, Mablethorpe and strategic coastal locations), cancellation of shipping movements on The Haven, requests for Military Assistance to Civil Authorities to access PWOG for evacuation, mutual aid mobilised (police and FRS) and Highways dealing with storm/wind damage (fallen trees). [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area subject to highest (Red) coastal flood threat; large&ndash;scale evacuation planning and mobilisation across the county including establishment of surge task forces, opening of strategic evacuation centres, and deployment of rescue assets. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood impacts across the county: areas in Boston adjacent to the river were flooded mainly due to overtopping; damage to a low floodwall; River Haven burst its banks flooding neighbouring streets; evacuees transported from assembly points; impacts on water pumping stations and road closures; emergency services deployed including mutual aid requests and IDB pumping equipment sent to assist. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;wide impacts from the surge and breaches included a 40m breach at Slippery Gowt affecting local waste sites, flooding of agricultural land near Gibraltar Point, large&ndash;scale evacuation activity around Boston, and breaches/concerns at River Haven and secondary defences at Wyberton Marsh. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal flooding and damage to flood defences with ongoing pumping operations to remove standing water; community impact and flood&ndash;extent assessments carried out; door&ndash;to&ndash;door welfare checks and housing support provided to displaced or affected households; debris and property damage observed in affected towns (including Boston). (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced a tidal surge on 5 December 2013 that led to multi&ndash;agency response and coordinated recovery. Impacts included damage and weaknesses to coastal/tidal flood defences (notably around Boston), and effects on households, businesses and infrastructure prompting structured recovery and investigation. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Lincolnshire coastline was hit by a coastal surge at high tide on the evening of 5th December 2013. (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lincolnshire's coastline was affected by the tidal surge at high tide on 5th December 2013; county&ndash;level flood risk assessments rose from Low to High within 12 hours before the surge, concentrating flood impacts on the Boston FWAs which flooded to extents matching mapped areas. [IMG,PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the tidal Trent and the coastal surge affected parts of the county; specifically, flooding impacted households in Susworth (West Lindsey), and the developing tidal/river flooding prompted multi&ndash;agency response and precautionary evacuation planning. [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Communities within Lincolnshire were affected by flooding from the tidal River Trent (example: Susworth). (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;level escalation and multi&ndash;agency response to a coastal surge threat: FGS level increased to Amber, strategic and tactical coordination structures activated, military liaison and voluntary sector coordination activated, and county command support functions mobilised at the County Emergency Centre. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>East Coast coastal flooding on 5 Dec 2013 severely tested sea defences; defences protected large areas but some properties and businesses flooded. Multi&ndash;agency response implemented including pre&ndash;deployment, evacuation and flood rescue. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced a coastal surge on 5 December 2013 that required a coordinated multi&ndash;agency evacuation and response. Recovery planning was disrupted by the threat of secondary flooding from the Wyberton breach. (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced a major coastal surge on 5th December 2013 that produced coastal/tidal flooding across the county and provided a significant test of multi&ndash;agency response and recovery capabilities; highlighted need for ongoing investment in defences and resilience. (p49)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 720 residential and commercial properties inundated; 3 casualties [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>103,000 homes and businesses; 220,000 hectares of land (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>80,500 properties at risk (reasonable worse&ndash;case, multiple breaches); 3 hospitals; 29 schools; 32 care homes; 1 prison; 20 fatalities; 300 casualties; 50,000 evacuees; Local infrastructure: 6 fire/police/ambulance stations; 176km of road; 30 bridges; 11.8km of rail; 6 major hazard sites; 32 electricity sub&ndash;stations; 63 water pumping; 15 sewage works; Overtopping&ndash;only 1:200: 4,383 residential properties at risk (of which approximately 900 &gt;0.5m flood depth); 229 commercial premises; 85 'socially vulnerable' premises; Total 4,697 properties; Overtopping&ndash;only 1:1000: 9,129 residential properties at risk; 431 commercial premises; 122 'socially vulnerable' premises; Total 9,682 properties (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18&ndash;20 km of flood defences overtopped; 4 breach locations; 1,700 hectares inundated; 100+ industrial/commercial sites damaged; 90 metres of floodwall failed; 3 of the 5 main pumps damaged (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>30,300 properties [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>600 properties; 6,000 properties; 38,000 properties; 15% of affected households; 240 rescues an hour [IMG,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 600 properties; 8 weather/wind related incidents; 5 mobile Beat Duty Units (25 staff each) &mdash; 3 deployed to Boston, 2 deployed to Louth [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 350 properties flooded; approximately 220 evacuees transported; 1 casualty (asthma attack); damage to the top 50 cm of a 10 metres low floodwall; Two IDB high&ndash;volume pumps deployed to Boston (unable to operate due to insufficient hose lengths/couplings). (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>40m breach at Slippery Gowt; 500 acres of agricultural land flooded; 263 assisted evacuees (all from Boston area); 1,800 residential properties identified as potentially at risk (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>529 residential properties; 50% uninsured; 114 families in need of alternative accommodation; 300 tonnes of waste collected; approx. 90 metre of floodwall failed at Skegness; 3 of the 5 main pumps at Black Sluice Gate damaged (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 770 properties across BOS1A and BOS1B [IMG,PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 households [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 households (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>103,000 homes and businesses protected; 220,200 hectares of land protected; 720+ households and businesses suffered flooding; 203 people evacuated; 30,300 people received flood warnings direct from the EA (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Lincolnshire,England,"A forecast–led coastal surge event in early December 2013 led to a large multi–agency emergency response in Lincolnshire; the surge caused overtopping and breaches of defences, evacuations, rescues of people isolated by floodwaters, and inundation of properties. [IMG] (p2)
County–wide sea defences were severely tested protecting homes, businesses and 220,000 hectares; widespread standing water was managed by drainage and high–volume pumping; Boston (within the county) suffered the largest number of flooded residential properties. (p5)
The event required multi–agency coordination in Lincolnshire: reliance on mutual aid and national specialist assets, prolonged deployments of responders, and targeted welfare, evacuation and medical support for vulnerable people. Recommendations were made to the Local Resilience Forum to improve planning, information–sharing and integration of community volunteers. (p6)
Coastal surge and associated high tides/onshore winds can cause overtopping and defence breaches affecting Lincolnshire: mapped flood depth, velocity and extent have been developed for defence overtopping and defence breach scenarios. Reasonable worst–case (multiple breaches) impacts include large numbers of properties, infrastructure and casualties; overtopping–only scenarios (1:200 and 1:1000 annual chance tide levels) give quantified property and premise impacts. (p7)
Flood defences were overtopped along 18–20 km of coastline, causing large areas of scour (e.g. Mablethorpe), four breach locations at Boston Haven, Gibraltar Point (Bull Dog Bank), Tetney Marsh and Friskney, inundation of extensive agricultural land, damage to industrial/commercial sites and local infrastructure, failure of ~90 m of floodwall at Skegness, and damage to pumps at Black Sluice Gate. (p12)
Defence overtopping and flood damage to coastal visitor centres: Gibraltar Point Visitor Centre and the Wash Study Centre. Preventive action at Donna Nook allowed seals to seek refuge on higher ground in the dunes; reported seal mortalities were low. [PAG] (p14)
Lincolnshire was placed under an anticipated Amber flood guidance level for the coastal/ tidal surge on 5 December 2013; multi–agency contingency planning, pre–deployment of assets and precautionary SCG arrangements were implemented across the county in response to the threat of spray, overtopping and a predicted River Haven surge. [PAG] (p16)
The county faced a medium overall risk of significant coastal/tidal flooding (FGS raised to 'Amber'); Flood Alerts were issued for the Lincolnshire coastline, coastal floodgates were patrolled/closed and contingency planning and escalation arrangements (including opening the County Emergency Centre) were activated for the predicted high tides on 5–6 December 2013. [PAG] (p17)
Environment Agency flood warnings ('be prepared') issued for waterside properties in and around Boston (waterside properties between Town Bridge and Haven Bridge, Grand Sluice and the Docks) and selected coastal areas from Gibraltar Point to Sutton Bridge; Boston identified as the focus for high tide periods; multi–agency evacuation planning authorised and surge task forces established (Boston and Louth) to respond to coastal flooding risk. [IMG,PAG] (p18)
County subject to an elevated flood threat (FGS 'Amber'); tactical coordination included evacuation planning and multi–agency flood response. Internal Drainage Boards activated emergency pumping arrangements; an evacuation hub and forward base were identified (Princess Royal Sports Arena) and voluntary sector and health partners mobilised to support potential evacuations. Fire & Rescue Service identified flood rescue capacity to support large–scale rescues. [IMG,PAG] (p19)
County–scale response to a coastal/tidal surge on 5 December 2013: evacuation planning for vulnerable areas (including planning for up to 600 properties), deployment of empty and filled sandbags to coastal towns (Skegness, Mablethorpe and strategic coastal locations), cancellation of shipping movements on The Haven, requests for Military Assistance to Civil Authorities to access PWOG for evacuation, mutual aid mobilised (police and FRS) and Highways dealing with storm/wind damage (fallen trees). [PAG] (p20)
Area subject to highest (Red) coastal flood threat; large–scale evacuation planning and mobilisation across the county including establishment of surge task forces, opening of strategic evacuation centres, and deployment of rescue assets. (p21)
Multiple flood impacts across the county: areas in Boston adjacent to the river were flooded mainly due to overtopping; damage to a low floodwall; River Haven burst its banks flooding neighbouring streets; evacuees transported from assembly points; impacts on water pumping stations and road closures; emergency services deployed including mutual aid requests and IDB pumping equipment sent to assist. (p24)
County–wide impacts from the surge and breaches included a 40m breach at Slippery Gowt affecting local waste sites, flooding of agricultural land near Gibraltar Point, large–scale evacuation activity around Boston, and breaches/concerns at River Haven and secondary defences at Wyberton Marsh. (p27)
Coastal flooding and damage to flood defences with ongoing pumping operations to remove standing water; community impact and flood–extent assessments carried out; door–to–door welfare checks and housing support provided to displaced or affected households; debris and property damage observed in affected towns (including Boston). (p29)
Experienced a tidal surge on 5 December 2013 that led to multi–agency response and coordinated recovery. Impacts included damage and weaknesses to coastal/tidal flood defences (notably around Boston), and effects on households, businesses and infrastructure prompting structured recovery and investigation. (p33)
The Lincolnshire coastline was hit by a coastal surge at high tide on the evening of 5th December 2013. (p34)
Lincolnshire's coastline was affected by the tidal surge at high tide on 5th December 2013; county–level flood risk assessments rose from Low to High within 12 hours before the surge, concentrating flood impacts on the Boston FWAs which flooded to extents matching mapped areas. [IMG,PAG] (p35)
Flooding associated with the tidal Trent and the coastal surge affected parts of the county; specifically, flooding impacted households in Susworth (West Lindsey), and the developing tidal/river flooding prompted multi–agency response and precautionary evacuation planning. [PAG] (p37)
Communities within Lincolnshire were affected by flooding from the tidal River Trent (example: Susworth). (p38)
County–level escalation and multi–agency response to a coastal surge threat: FGS level increased to Amber, strategic and tactical coordination structures activated, military liaison and voluntary sector coordination activated, and county command support functions mobilised at the County Emergency Centre. [PAG] (p39)
East Coast coastal flooding on 5 Dec 2013 severely tested sea defences; defences protected large areas but some properties and businesses flooded. Multi–agency response implemented including pre–deployment, evacuation and flood rescue. (p43)
Experienced a coastal surge on 5 December 2013 that required a coordinated multi–agency evacuation and response. Recovery planning was disrupted by the threat of secondary flooding from the Wyberton breach. (p45)
Experienced a major coastal surge on 5th December 2013 that produced coastal/tidal flooding across the county and provided a significant test of multi–agency response and recovery capabilities; highlighted need for ongoing investment in defences and resilience. (p49)","more than 720 residential and commercial properties inundated; 3 casualties [IMG] (p2)
103,000 homes and businesses; 220,000 hectares of land (p5)
80,500 properties at risk (reasonable worse–case, multiple breaches); 3 hospitals; 29 schools; 32 care homes; 1 prison; 20 fatalities; 300 casualties; 50,000 evacuees; Local infrastructure: 6 fire/police/ambulance stations; 176km of road; 30 bridges; 11.8km of rail; 6 major hazard sites; 32 electricity sub–stations; 63 water pumping; 15 sewage works; Overtopping–only 1:200: 4,383 residential properties at risk (of which approximately 900 >0.5m flood depth); 229 commercial premises; 85 'socially vulnerable' premises; Total 4,697 properties; Overtopping–only 1:1000: 9,129 residential properties at risk; 431 commercial premises; 122 'socially vulnerable' premises; Total 9,682 properties (p7)
