GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,51.2070749,0.7210362,"<div id=""html_f07312f6d0c8c450f876476deac1fcc1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kent experienced extreme tidal levels during the surge, with the highest recorded surge at Sheerness contributing to coastal flooding impacts in the area. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Highest surge 3.02 metres at Sheerness (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Kent,England,"Kent experienced extreme tidal levels during the surge, with the highest recorded surge at Sheerness contributing to coastal flooding impacts in the area. (p12)",Highest surge 3.02 metres at Sheerness (p12),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.2680103,0.0127758,"<div id=""html_eddc8ceb7e7dc4b33f14e4097f5eae04"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Lindsey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal/tidal flooding of the East Lindsey coastline prompted evacuation of vulnerable residents and movement of evacuees to reception centres (Meridian Centre, Louth); multi&ndash;agency surge tasking and large&ndash;scale transport/evacuation arrangements were put in place. (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,East Lindsey,England,"Coastal/tidal flooding of the East Lindsey coastline prompted evacuation of vulnerable residents and movement of evacuees to reception centres (Meridian Centre, Louth); multi–agency surge tasking and large–scale transport/evacuation arrangements were put in place. (p21)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,55.25,-2.000559,"<div id=""html_93e2c33e877961e8bce4eb930ad09744"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northumberland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported tidal water levels peaked 20 cm above predicted levels at high tide, indicating an elevated tidal/ coastal flood risk during the event. (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 cm above predicted levels (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Northumberland,England,"Reported tidal water levels peaked 20 cm above predicted levels at high tide, indicating an elevated tidal/ coastal flood risk during the event. (p21)",20 cm above predicted levels (p21),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9689077,-0.01827557709134635,"<div id=""html_bc48139a935b0f4b19c9dbdd00e15872"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Port of Boston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The port was threatened by floodwaters; protecting infrastructure included managing defences and addressing threats to the port. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cancelled shipping movements on The Haven during the evening high tide period (6&ndash;9pm) because of the tidal/coastal flood risk. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Port managed continuing speed restrictions at high tides following the flooding event; shipping operations were affected and subsequently resumed. (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Port of Boston,England,"The port was threatened by floodwaters; protecting infrastructure included managing defences and addressing threats to the port. (p5)
Cancelled shipping movements on The Haven during the evening high tide period (6–9pm) because of the tidal/coastal flood risk. (p20)
Port managed continuing speed restrictions at high tides following the flooding event; shipping operations were affected and subsequently resumed. (p28)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9689077,-0.0182756,"<div id=""html_5ccf0b3f83794266ce0a732e62e33fbf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Boston Dock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The docks lie within the BOS1A/B warning footprint and the report states the actual flooding closely matched those areas; the docks therefore experienced inundation, overtopping and increased breach risk during the tidal surge on 5 December 2013. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 770 properties (total in BOS1A and BOS1B) [IMG] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Boston Dock,England,"The docks lie within the BOS1A/B warning footprint and the report states the actual flooding closely matched those areas; the docks therefore experienced inundation, overtopping and increased breach risk during the tidal surge on 5 December 2013. [IMG] (p35)",approx. 770 properties (total in BOS1A and BOS1B) [IMG] (p35),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.97704,-0.0239836,"<div id=""html_6c8a8b20c0224346eb3297adaeb2bb69"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Boston</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced flooding during the coastal surge; the majority of the 6,072 residential properties flooded in the county were located in Boston. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive flooding of residential properties (largest number of flooded homes in the county); standing water was dispersed within 48 hours by the drainage system and high&ndash;volume pumping; evacuations of at&ndash;risk properties (including assisted evacuation of people) and a structured community recovery were required. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties and local streets were flooded; the majority of the 607 residential properties flooded across the county were located in Boston (together with Trusthorpe and Susworth). Businesses in the town were also affected as part of the county&ndash;wide business flooding. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency reported pre&ndash;identified seepage through flood defences in Boston. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal flooding threat to Boston: concerns about seepage through ground and defences, Flood Alerts issued for the coastline, and contingency planning for possible overtopping or breach of defences. The Environment Agency identified approximately 500 properties at risk from seepage under the forecast scenario and used a worse&ndash;case breach model to identify a further 18,000 properties potentially at risk. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Designated focus for high tide periods; evacuation planning authorised by the Strategic Coordinating Group; flood warnings ('be prepared') issued covering waterside areas including Grand Sluice and the Docks; multi&ndash;agency surge task force established to respond at Boston. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Evacuation of known vulnerable people in a predicted flood zone; multi&ndash;agency surge task force established; Princess Royal Sports Arena opened as an evacuation processing hub; FRS deployed 6 type B flood rescue boat teams plus support rafts and 7 fire appliances to Boston; Nottinghamshire Underwater Search Unit (13 staff and boat) deployed to Boston; EMAS deployed a HART to Boston. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Higher&ndash;than&ndash;expected tidal surge prompted EA advice to prepare for possible breaches to defences at the point of high tide; multi&ndash;agency evacuation process was fully under&ndash;way; FRS received the first call about flooding on Church Street and experienced a dramatic rise in calls lasting about 3 hours; FRS (supported by EMAS HART) carried out rescues and assisted residents isolated by floodwater; the surge task force/bronze relocated operations (Fen Road Depot) due to flood threat to Boston Fire station; evacuees were re&ndash;routed and a temporary alternative evacuation centre was used at Stickney. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tide surge caused water to overtop barriers and flood urban streets and waterside residential properties; water entered business premises on Slippery Gowt Lane; emergency responders (police and FRS) were unable to reach some addresses due to depth and velocity of floodwater; NPAS helicopter deployed to provide overhead visual intelligence. [IMG,PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Haven burst its banks and adjacent areas flooded mainly due to overtopping. Damage to the top 50 cm of a 10&ndash;metre low floodwall. Road closures and pumping operations (Fen Road). Approximately 220 evacuees transported from Princess Royal Sports Arena to alternative centres; one casualty (asthma attack) recorded. Two IDB high&ndash;volume pumps deployed but unable to operate due to insufficient hose lengths/couplings. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Boston adjacent to the River Haven were inundated when the river overtopped its banks. Flooding is reported across neighbouring streets; the top 50 cm of a 10 metre low floodwall was damaged. The river/bridge disruption led to road bridge closure during the morning high tide, evacuees were relocated to a consolidated centre, and emergency services were deployed to respond. [IMG,PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding that led to mass evacuation and recovery operations; flood response included pumping of floodwater, local recovery meetings and coordination. Local infrastructure impacts included breaches of defences affecting waste facilities and power failures. [IMG,PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Boston area led to an evacuation operation and support for affected residents; vulnerable residents relocated into care homes and casualties reported from the previous evening's flooding. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents were evacuated as a precaution and 20 evacuees from Boston sought alternative accommodation; home inspections and overnight hotel accommodation were arranged, with liaison between SCG, Boston Borough Council and health partners; port operations subject to speed restrictions at high tides. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal/ tidal flooding on 5th December 2013 inundated residential streets in Boston; extensive household property damage and large volumes of damaged furniture and debris placed along streets; community impact and flood extent assessments and door&ndash;to&ndash;door welfare checks conducted; waste collection and recovery operations mounted. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood defence failures and erosion led to widespread flooding of residential areas; garden wall collapse; clear&ndash;up and waste disposal operations required; some schools damaged or closed (Boston Grammar). [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sea&ndash;defence failures in Boston including a garden wall collapse and erosion of defences at Slippery Gowt Lane (Wyberton). Emergency response teams visited properties, waste&ndash;clearance and hygiene advice operations mobilised, and schools/local services disrupted (Boston Grammar remained closed); damage assessments and repair works initiated. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties flooded across the borough from the December surge events; large&ndash;scale recovery activity including alternative accommodation and waste clearance; damage to natural environment and agricultural land; concerns over compromised local flood defences (White Horse Lane area) and deployment of temporary demountable defences. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Boston was a focal area for the surge: previously damaged defences and identified weaknesses led to flood guidance and alerts, recovery activity and a multi&ndash;agency response. Localised tidal flood risk around Boston prompted assessment, revisits to properties and a formal recovery/de&ndash;brief process. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Central Boston's river corridor and adjacent central/southern urban areas experienced flooding during the tidal surge on 5th December 2013; the mapped flooded zones corresponded to the FWAs and included waterside properties and nearby transport/civic infrastructure. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local Resilience Forum deployed its main response efforts in Boston during the event based on EA predictions; partners used mapping to pre&ndash;identify vulnerable premises and assets (including care facilities and other infrastructure) and to prioritise preventive evacuation and protection of those premises. [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Declaration of an Emergency at 11:05 on 5th December 2013 when probable impacts of the surge became clear; evacuation orders for 'at risk' areas in Boston were initiated (a delay in initiating the evacuation decision on the afternoon of 5th December required a one-off whole-room announcement to resolve confusion); business continuity planning for flooding affecting Boston Pilgrim Hospital and its A&amp;E were scoped and managed; mutual aid and local coordination for flood response were managed by Boston Borough Council. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the town experienced coastal flooding that affected households and businesses and required preventive evacuations of properties at immediate risk. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hosted the initial evacuation hub at the Princess Royal Sports Arena to receive and assess evacuees as part of the flood evacuation operation. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal surge flooding led to evacuation of residents, homes being flooded and disrupted safe access. Evacuees were transported to view their flooded homes the following Friday; alternative accommodation (mainly hotel based) was provided where requested. Initial community recovery was started on the morning of 6th December but was deflected by the threat of secondary flooding from the Wyberton breach. [PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas and properties in Boston were identified as flooded following the coastal surge on 5th December 2013. Immediate priorities included assessment of critical structures (notably flood defences), clean up and salvage, detailed analysis and identification of flooded properties. Police&ndash;led street surveys and door&ndash;knocking with occupant questionnaires were used to establish priorities for assistance to affected households and businesses. (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The early December surge and subsequent flooding caused homes and businesses in Boston to flood, created immediate waste&ndash;management challenges (exacerbated by damage to the town's only waste disposal site), required door&ndash;to&ndash;door surveys and a large recovery operation to support affected households and businesses. [PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Boston experienced flooding and subsequent recovery activity; learning from Boston's recovery was proposed as a case study and used to inform a countywide Flood Victim Impact Assessment questionnaire and the Flood Victim Pack. (p51)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>majority of 6,072 residential properties (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>203 people (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>607 residential properties flooded (total in county; majority in Boston, Trusthorpe and Susworth); 121 businesses flooded (county total) [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>500 properties; 18,000 properties (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>30,300 properties (EA flood warnings issued overall); 1 surge task force at Boston (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 50 flood&ndash;related incident calls [IMG,PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>350 properties; top 50 cm of a 10 metres low floodwall; 220 evacuees; 1 casualty; 2 IDB high&ndash;volume pumps [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 350 properties flooded; top 50 cm of a 10 metres low floodwall damaged [IMG,PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>263 assisted evacuees; 40 vulnerable persons relocated into care homes; 3 casualties (not serious); 322 properties affected by power failures; 40m breach of defences [IMG,PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>263 assisted evacuees; 40 vulnerable persons relocated into care homes; 3 non&ndash;serious casualties [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 evacuees (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>400 properties confirmed flooded; more than 700 properties visited; 2 defence failures; more than 300 white goods collected; 1 school (Boston Grammar) remained closed [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>700 properties visited; 400 properties confirmed flooded [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>529 residential properties flooded; 114 families in need of alternative accommodation; 300 tonnes of waste collected [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 770 properties across BOS1A and BOS1B [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>203 people evacuated; 720+ households and businesses flooded (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Boston,England,"The town experienced flooding during the coastal surge; the majority of the 6,072 residential properties flooded in the county were located in Boston. (p4)
Extensive flooding of residential properties (largest number of flooded homes in the county); standing water was dispersed within 48 hours by the drainage system and high–volume pumping; evacuations of at–risk properties (including assisted evacuation of people) and a structured community recovery were required. (p5)
Residential properties and local streets were flooded; the majority of the 607 residential properties flooded across the county were located in Boston (together with Trusthorpe and Susworth). Businesses in the town were also affected as part of the county–wide business flooding. [IMG] (p13)
Environment Agency reported pre–identified seepage through flood defences in Boston. (p15)
Tidal flooding threat to Boston: concerns about seepage through ground and defences, Flood Alerts issued for the coastline, and contingency planning for possible overtopping or breach of defences. The Environment Agency identified approximately 500 properties at risk from seepage under the forecast scenario and used a worse–case breach model to identify a further 18,000 properties potentially at risk. (p17)
Designated focus for high tide periods; evacuation planning authorised by the Strategic Coordinating Group; flood warnings ('be prepared') issued covering waterside areas including Grand Sluice and the Docks; multi–agency surge task force established to respond at Boston. (p18)
Evacuation of known vulnerable people in a predicted flood zone; multi–agency surge task force established; Princess Royal Sports Arena opened as an evacuation processing hub; FRS deployed 6 type B flood rescue boat teams plus support rafts and 7 fire appliances to Boston; Nottinghamshire Underwater Search Unit (13 staff and boat) deployed to Boston; EMAS deployed a HART to Boston. (p21)
Higher–than–expected tidal surge prompted EA advice to prepare for possible breaches to defences at the point of high tide; multi–agency evacuation process was fully under–way; FRS received the first call about flooding on Church Street and experienced a dramatic rise in calls lasting about 3 hours; FRS (supported by EMAS HART) carried out rescues and assisted residents isolated by floodwater; the surge task force/bronze relocated operations (Fen Road Depot) due to flood threat to Boston Fire station; evacuees were re–routed and a temporary alternative evacuation centre was used at Stickney. [PAG] (p22)
High tide surge caused water to overtop barriers and flood urban streets and waterside residential properties; water entered business premises on Slippery Gowt Lane; emergency responders (police and FRS) were unable to reach some addresses due to depth and velocity of floodwater; NPAS helicopter deployed to provide overhead visual intelligence. [IMG,PAG] (p23)
River Haven burst its banks and adjacent areas flooded mainly due to overtopping. Damage to the top 50 cm of a 10–metre low floodwall. Road closures and pumping operations (Fen Road). Approximately 220 evacuees transported from Princess Royal Sports Arena to alternative centres; one casualty (asthma attack) recorded. Two IDB high–volume pumps deployed but unable to operate due to insufficient hose lengths/couplings. [PAG] (p24)
Areas of Boston adjacent to the River Haven were inundated when the river overtopped its banks. Flooding is reported across neighbouring streets; the top 50 cm of a 10 metre low floodwall was damaged. The river/bridge disruption led to road bridge closure during the morning high tide, evacuees were relocated to a consolidated centre, and emergency services were deployed to respond. [IMG,PAG] (p25)
Experienced flooding that led to mass evacuation and recovery operations; flood response included pumping of floodwater, local recovery meetings and coordination. Local infrastructure impacts included breaches of defences affecting waste facilities and power failures. [IMG,PAG] (p26)
Flooding in the Boston area led to an evacuation operation and support for affected residents; vulnerable residents relocated into care homes and casualties reported from the previous evening's flooding. [PAG] (p27)
Residents were evacuated as a precaution and 20 evacuees from Boston sought alternative accommodation; home inspections and overnight hotel accommodation were arranged, with liaison between SCG, Boston Borough Council and health partners; port operations subject to speed restrictions at high tides. (p28)
Coastal/ tidal flooding on 5th December 2013 inundated residential streets in Boston; extensive household property damage and large volumes of damaged furniture and debris placed along streets; community impact and flood extent assessments and door–to–door welfare checks conducted; waste collection and recovery operations mounted. [IMG,PAG] (p29)
Flood defence failures and erosion led to widespread flooding of residential areas; garden wall collapse; clear–up and waste disposal operations required; some schools damaged or closed (Boston Grammar). [PAG] (p30)
Sea–defence failures in Boston including a garden wall collapse and erosion of defences at Slippery Gowt Lane (Wyberton). Emergency response teams visited properties, waste–clearance and hygiene advice operations mobilised, and schools/local services disrupted (Boston Grammar remained closed); damage assessments and repair works initiated. [PAG] (p31)
Residential properties flooded across the borough from the December surge events; large–scale recovery activity including alternative accommodation and waste clearance; damage to natural environment and agricultural land; concerns over compromised local flood defences (White Horse Lane area) and deployment of temporary demountable defences. [PAG] (p32)
Boston was a focal area for the surge: previously damaged defences and identified weaknesses led to flood guidance and alerts, recovery activity and a multi–agency response. Localised tidal flood risk around Boston prompted assessment, revisits to properties and a formal recovery/de–brief process. (p33)
Central Boston's river corridor and adjacent central/southern urban areas experienced flooding during the tidal surge on 5th December 2013; the mapped flooded zones corresponded to the FWAs and included waterside properties and nearby transport/civic infrastructure. [IMG] (p35)
Local Resilience Forum deployed its main response efforts in Boston during the event based on EA predictions; partners used mapping to pre–identify vulnerable premises and assets (including care facilities and other infrastructure) and to prioritise preventive evacuation and protection of those premises. [IMG,PAG] (p36)
Declaration of an Emergency at 11:05 on 5th December 2013 when probable impacts of the surge became clear; evacuation orders for 'at risk' areas in Boston were initiated (a delay in initiating the evacuation decision on the afternoon of 5th December required a one-off whole-room announcement to resolve confusion); business continuity planning for flooding affecting Boston Pilgrim Hospital and its A&E were scoped and managed; mutual aid and local coordination for flood response were managed by Boston Borough Council. (p40)
Parts of the town experienced coastal flooding that affected households and businesses and required preventive evacuations of properties at immediate risk. (p43)
Hosted the initial evacuation hub at the Princess Royal Sports Arena to receive and assess evacuees as part of the flood evacuation operation. (p44)
Coastal surge flooding led to evacuation of residents, homes being flooded and disrupted safe access. Evacuees were transported to view their flooded homes the following Friday; alternative accommodation (mainly hotel based) was provided where requested. Initial community recovery was started on the morning of 6th December but was deflected by the threat of secondary flooding from the Wyberton breach. [PAG] (p45)
Areas and properties in Boston were identified as flooded following the coastal surge on 5th December 2013. Immediate priorities included assessment of critical structures (notably flood defences), clean up and salvage, detailed analysis and identification of flooded properties. Police–led street surveys and door–knocking with occupant questionnaires were used to establish priorities for assistance to affected households and businesses. (p46)
The early December surge and subsequent flooding caused homes and businesses in Boston to flood, created immediate waste–management challenges (exacerbated by damage to the town's only waste disposal site), required door–to–door surveys and a large recovery operation to support affected households and businesses. [PAG] (p47)
Boston experienced flooding and subsequent recovery activity; learning from Boston's recovery was proposed as a case study and used to inform a countywide Flood Victim Impact Assessment questionnaire and the Flood Victim Pack. (p51)","majority of 6,072 residential properties (p4)
203 people (p5)
607 residential properties flooded (total in county; majority in Boston, Trusthorpe and Susworth); 121 businesses flooded (county total) [IMG] (p13)
