529 residential properties flooded; 114 families in need of alternative accommodation; 300 tonnes of waste collected [PAG] (p32)
approx. 770 properties across BOS1A and BOS1B [IMG] (p35)
203 people evacuated; 720+ households and businesses flooded (p43)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.3408271,0.2606772,"<div id=""html_b4b065d8164b26195d6cc909b08c1188"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mablethorpe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of flood defences during the tidal surge caused a large area of scour at Mablethorpe. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>East Lindsey District Council deployed empty sandbags to Mablethorpe (and sent filled sandbags to strategic coastal locations stored on vehicles) in anticipation of coastal flooding. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal flooding prompted deployments to assist with road closures, possible evacuations and warning/informing activities. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was confirmed between Huttoft and Mablethorpe on the day of the event. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater damage was assessed as limited in the area between Trusthorpe and Mablethorpe; no further assistance required. (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18&ndash;20 km of flood defences overtopped (regional total) (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Mablethorpe,England,"Overtopping of flood defences during the tidal surge caused a large area of scour at Mablethorpe. (p12)
East Lindsey District Council deployed empty sandbags to Mablethorpe (and sent filled sandbags to strategic coastal locations stored on vehicles) in anticipation of coastal flooding. (p20)
Coastal flooding prompted deployments to assist with road closures, possible evacuations and warning/informing activities. (p22)
Flooding was confirmed between Huttoft and Mablethorpe on the day of the event. (p27)
Floodwater damage was assessed as limited in the area between Trusthorpe and Mablethorpe; no further assistance required. (p28)",18–20 km of flood defences overtopped (regional total) (p12),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.0508513,0.22889676323794406,"<div id=""html_2a5b9df9dcb22e31c0cb5b110de4bc8b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Friskney</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Breach of flood defences at Friskney during the tidal surge, contributing to inundation of agricultural land. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one of 4 breach locations; breaches collectively inundated an estimated 1,700 hectares of agricultural land (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Friskney,England,"Breach of flood defences at Friskney during the tidal surge, contributing to inundation of agricultural land. (p12)","one of 4 breach locations; breaches collectively inundated an estimated 1,700 hectares of agricultural land (p12)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.616957,-0.210646,"<div id=""html_a6b207e8d80d60f19c5d5210e1d15d3f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Immingham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surge at the monitoring point nearest Lincolnshire reached 5.2 metres on 5 December 2013 (higher than forecast and 70 cm higher than 1953), contributing to extreme tidal levels and local flood risk. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5.2 metres; 70 cm higher than 1953 (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Immingham,England,"The surge at the monitoring point nearest Lincolnshire reached 5.2 metres on 5 December 2013 (higher than forecast and 70 cm higher than 1953), contributing to extreme tidal levels and local flood risk. (p12)",5.2 metres; 70 cm higher than 1953 (p12),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.75387,0.3954774,"<div id=""html_ab0f862cbdbd6033aeec70e3b335527e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>King's Lynn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded the highest absolute water levels during the event, with total levels reaching 6.02 metres. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6.02 metres (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,King's Lynn,England,"Recorded the highest absolute water levels during the event, with total levels reaching 6.02 metres. (p12)",6.02 metres (p12),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,51.44005763476694,0.7633759360650039,"<div id=""html_4fabb1f6de5611bf842ca9c406c514e8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sheerness</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded the highest surge of the event (3.02 metres). (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3.02 metres (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Sheerness,England,Recorded the highest surge of the event (3.02 metres). (p12),3.02 metres (p12),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.14375,0.3428958,"<div id=""html_71ba7d6b41bab8371cb17c5a7f15f8fe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Skegness</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Approximately 90 metres of the floodwall between the pier and the lifeboat station failed during the surge, breaching defences and causing local coastal inundation. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>East Lindsey District Council deployed empty sandbags to Skegness (and sent filled sandbags to strategic coastal locations stored on vehicles) in anticipation of coastal flooding. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal flooding along the seafront; floodwater extended up to the Clock Tower. Police deployed to assist with road closures, potential evacuations and public safety on the seafront; public ignoring safety requests created additional safety concerns. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Damage confirmed to the seafront and to kiosks as a result of coastal flooding. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Around 90 metres of floodwall (between the pier and lifeboat station) failed during the December surge, representing a coastal defence failure and associated coastal flooding risk/damage. (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>90 metres of floodwall (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx. 90 metre of floodwall (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Skegness,England,"Approximately 90 metres of the floodwall between the pier and the lifeboat station failed during the surge, breaching defences and causing local coastal inundation. (p12)
East Lindsey District Council deployed empty sandbags to Skegness (and sent filled sandbags to strategic coastal locations stored on vehicles) in anticipation of coastal flooding. (p20)
Coastal flooding along the seafront; floodwater extended up to the Clock Tower. Police deployed to assist with road closures, potential evacuations and public safety on the seafront; public ignoring safety requests created additional safety concerns. (p22)
Damage confirmed to the seafront and to kiosks as a result of coastal flooding. (p29)
Around 90 metres of floodwall (between the pier and lifeboat station) failed during the December surge, representing a coastal defence failure and associated coastal flooding risk/damage. (p32)","90 metres of floodwall (p12)
approx. 90 metre of floodwall (p32)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.326783,0.2714389,"<div id=""html_26b7507cec978827e641bab2444d9ce3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Trusthorpe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in the village were flooded; Trusthorpe was named as one of the locations (with Boston and Susworth) that accounted for the majority of the 607 residential properties flooded in the county. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected residential properties; residents self&ndash;evacuated to a local pub and no partner assistance was required. (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>607 residential properties flooded (total in county; majority in Boston, Trusthorpe and Susworth) (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Trusthorpe,England,"Residential properties in the village were flooded; Trusthorpe was named as one of the locations (with Boston and Susworth) that accounted for the majority of the 607 residential properties flooded in the county. (p13)
Flooding affected residential properties; residents self–evacuated to a local pub and no partner assistance was required. (p28)","607 residential properties flooded (total in county; majority in Boston, Trusthorpe and Susworth) (p13)
9 properties (p28)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.5102932,-0.7412945,"<div id=""html_d2b063f0008f339d26dcc58413152482"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Susworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties were flooded; Susworth was identified alongside Boston and Trusthorpe as accounting for the majority of the 607 residential properties flooded in the county. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads were reported flooded (passable with care). A single property (occupied by elderly residents) experienced internal flooding and local resources were dispatched to assist; EA staff were door&ndash;knocking in the area. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential flooding affecting households in the village. (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>607 residential properties flooded (total in county; majority in Boston, Trusthorpe and Susworth) (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 households (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Susworth,England,"Residential properties were flooded; Susworth was identified alongside Boston and Trusthorpe as accounting for the majority of the 607 residential properties flooded in the county. (p13)
Roads were reported flooded (passable with care). A single property (occupied by elderly residents) experienced internal flooding and local resources were dispatched to assist; EA staff were door–knocking in the area. (p23)
Residential flooding affecting households in the village. (p38)","607 residential properties flooded (total in county; majority in Boston, Trusthorpe and Susworth) (p13)
1 property (p23)
4 households (p38)",GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.367516,-0.0022381,"<div id=""html_18473fd9a623e2783b98126033d869e1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Louth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Allocated as the location for a multi&ndash;agency surge task force and identified as a forward base to respond to any developments along the coastline during the high&ndash;tide threat. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multi&ndash;agency 'surge task force' established at Louth; FRS deployed 2 type B flood rescue boat teams plus support rafts to Louth; mutual&ndash;aid police BDUs (2 mobile BDUs, 25 staff each) deployed to Louth; Meridian Centre in Louth opened for evacuees from the East Lindsey coastline. (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Louth,England,"Allocated as the location for a multi–agency surge task force and identified as a forward base to respond to any developments along the coastline during the high–tide threat. (p18)
Multi–agency 'surge task force' established at Louth; FRS deployed 2 type B flood rescue boat teams plus support rafts to Louth; mutual–aid police BDUs (2 mobile BDUs, 25 staff each) deployed to Louth; Meridian Centre in Louth opened for evacuees from the East Lindsey coastline. (p21)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.4882836,-0.7734562,"<div id=""html_455e304c1f72d68bd6f42aa1b32c6d56"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Ferry</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Community in East Ferry was identified as at risk from tidal flooding from the River Trent and was provided with sandbags for community use by local authorities. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads reported flooded and described as passable with care. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,East Ferry,England,"Community in East Ferry was identified as at risk from tidal flooding from the River Trent and was provided with sandbags for community use by local authorities. (p21)
Roads reported flooded and described as passable with care. (p23)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.223394074790804,0.3379215555948892,"<div id=""html_07ee3bb887d4569779ea78979f08ed07"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chapel St Leonards</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Coastal flooding led to responder deployments to assist with road closures, potential evacuations and public warnings. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Chapel St Leonards,England,"Coastal flooding led to responder deployments to assist with road closures, potential evacuations and public warnings. (p22)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.264597017368295,0.2674240307704543,"<div id=""html_46f78744b504151e9fc6978dc4b491a3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Huttoft</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was confirmed between Huttoft and Mablethorpe on the day of the event. (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Huttoft,England,Flooding was confirmed between Huttoft and Mablethorpe on the day of the event. (p27),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9544795,-0.0411564,"<div id=""html_42db46a115d2788aabda47da1cb0b858"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wyberton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Breach of defences prompted confirmation of MOD assistance to attempt secondary defence repair; limited precautionary evacuation requested; immediate repairs to damaged defences and pumping operations to remove standing water undertaken; high tide passed without further significant flooding. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A breach in defences believed to have occurred on 5 December 2013 created a potential risk of secondary flooding; the breach was not immediately identified and this threat diverted partner resources, prompting a precautionary evacuation and authorisation of defence repairs. [MUL] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A breach at Wyberton created a threat of secondary flooding during the event on 5th December 2013; this threat diverted recovery planning and delayed declaring the emergency over until the breach threat had been dealt with. (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding damaged the town's only waste disposal site, including a collapsed access road; repairs and mutual aid were used to restore operations. (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 residents evacuated (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Wyberton,England,"Breach of defences prompted confirmation of MOD assistance to attempt secondary defence repair; limited precautionary evacuation requested; immediate repairs to damaged defences and pumping operations to remove standing water undertaken; high tide passed without further significant flooding. (p28)
A breach in defences believed to have occurred on 5 December 2013 created a potential risk of secondary flooding; the breach was not immediately identified and this threat diverted partner resources, prompting a precautionary evacuation and authorisation of defence repairs. [MUL] (p36)
A breach at Wyberton created a threat of secondary flooding during the event on 5th December 2013; this threat diverted recovery planning and delayed declaring the emergency over until the breach threat had been dealt with. (p46)
Flooding damaged the town's only waste disposal site, including a collapsed access road; repairs and mutual aid were used to restore operations. (p47)",20 residents evacuated (p28),GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9668611,-0.0264224,"<div id=""html_bc54eeca5a94090f250e1de268392153"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Black Sluice Pumping Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contingencies were discussed for the pumping station to manage the predicted surge and potential impacts on drainage and water levels. (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Black Sluice Pumping Station,England,Contingencies were discussed for the pumping station to manage the predicted surge and potential impacts on drainage and water levels. (p16),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,53.73995960659919,-0.33077521308515384,"<div id=""html_b21f00cdfa1ffc77ae76fb2f0c422cc4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hull Tidal Barrier</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hull Flood Barrier was put into operation (closed) at 18:00 as part of the response to manage tidal flood risk. (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Hull Tidal Barrier,England,Hull Flood Barrier was put into operation (closed) at 18:00 as part of the response to manage tidal flood risk. (p21),,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-LIN,1,2013-12-05,52.9404064,0.0216931,"<div id=""html_5636c1fbf4eb0ee1128ed3fe4e7b5b4e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wyberton Marsh Pumping Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A sudden rise in the Black Sluice IDB dyke system was noted at the pumping station, indicating a potential breach of tidal banks; water levels at the station rose and pumping infrastructure was monitored and reported as impacted (not critical). (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lincolnshire,Wyberton Marsh Pumping Station,England,"A sudden rise in the Black Sluice IDB dyke system was noted at the pumping station, indicating a potential breach of tidal banks; water levels at the station rose and pumping infrastructure was monitored and reported as impacted (not critical). (p24)",,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05.html,GB-LIN_1_2013-12-05,Lincolnshire flooding 05 Dec 2013,The East Coast Tidal Surge: 5th December 2013,074/Lincolnshire County/East/Coast/Tidal/Surge/////5th/December/2013/report/PDFA.pdf
GB-CAM,1,2014-08-08,52.2524329,0.0195501,"<div id=""html_8ff1dbaf69c1165794592d6918e4b005"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bar Hill Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The school was flooded during the same extreme rainfall event. The report states the school was flooded but does not specify the exact source of flooding for this asset (likely surface water and/or surcharging drains given the overall causes). (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 school (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Bar Hill Primary School,England,The school was flooded during the same extreme rainfall event. The report states the school was flooded but does not specify the exact source of flooding for this asset (likely surface water and/or surcharging drains given the overall causes). (p3),1 school (p3),GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08.html,GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08,Bar Hill flooding 08 Aug 2014 - 09 Aug 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Bar Hill,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Bar/Hill/2014/Flood/investigation/Report.pdf
GB-CAM,1,2014-08-08,52.25131909020944,0.018458066824952817,"<div id=""html_6d61bed0b5f558d05f494b9371f5ec41"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Recreation Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The incident map shows a cluster of flood&ndash;affected locations in the central area around the Recreation Ground. This indicates the Recreation Ground and its immediate surrounds were inundated by surface water / urban flooding during the extreme rainfall, causing standing water on the grounds and likely short&ndash;term inundation and access disruption to the open space on 8th August 2014. [IMG] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Recreation Ground,England,"The incident map shows a cluster of flood–affected locations in the central area around the Recreation Ground. This indicates the Recreation Ground and its immediate surrounds were inundated by surface water / urban flooding during the extreme rainfall, causing standing water on the grounds and likely short–term inundation and access disruption to the open space on 8th August 2014. [IMG] (p3)",,GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08.html,GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08,Bar Hill flooding 08 Aug 2014 - 09 Aug 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Bar Hill,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Bar/Hill/2014/Flood/investigation/Report.pdf
GB-CAM,1,2014-08-08,52.2503527,0.0231618,"<div id=""html_a1301bb4ae807c25433f3bf72a00cf6a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oakington Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified by residents as the main source of flooding through Bar Hill during the August 2014 event; hydraulic modelling showed flooding from the brook is sensitive to summer weed growth and culvert blockages and recommended a maintenance regime and reminders to riparian owners. [TAB] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Oakington Brook,England,Identified by residents as the main source of flooding through Bar Hill during the August 2014 event; hydraulic modelling showed flooding from the brook is sensitive to summer weed growth and culvert blockages and recommended a maintenance regime and reminders to riparian owners. [TAB] (p4),,GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08.html,GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08,Bar Hill flooding 08 Aug 2014 - 09 Aug 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Bar Hill,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Bar/Hill/2014/Flood/investigation/Report.pdf
GB-CAM,1,2014-08-08,52.2511064,0.0183845,"<div id=""html_8fd82cb570cfe28eb5b8f5e006d29894"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bar Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected various locations within Bar Hill; residents reported flooding caused by multiple sources including surface water and drains. [TAB] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the village caused by an extreme short&ndash;duration rainfall event that overwhelmed drainage networks, inundated watercourses and the road network. Properties and local infrastructure flooded internally and externally. [IMG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding during the August 2014 extreme rainfall event; Oakington Brook flowing through the village was identified by residents as a main source of flooding, hydraulic modelling was commissioned to investigate mechanisms, and local infrastructure (infiltration basin outfall and watercourses) was cleared by the parish council; Anglian Water inspected and maintained sewer/network sections that drain to the infiltration basin. [TAB] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>79 properties (internally flooded); approximately 30 properties (external flooding) [IMG] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Bar Hill,England,"Flooding affected various locations within Bar Hill; residents reported flooding caused by multiple sources including surface water and drains. [TAB] (p2)
Widespread flooding across the village caused by an extreme short–duration rainfall event that overwhelmed drainage networks, inundated watercourses and the road network. Properties and local infrastructure flooded internally and externally. [IMG] (p3)
Experienced flooding during the August 2014 extreme rainfall event; Oakington Brook flowing through the village was identified by residents as a main source of flooding, hydraulic modelling was commissioned to investigate mechanisms, and local infrastructure (infiltration basin outfall and watercourses) was cleared by the parish council; Anglian Water inspected and maintained sewer/network sections that drain to the infiltration basin. [TAB] (p4)",79 properties (internally flooded); approximately 30 properties (external flooding) [IMG] (p3),GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08.html,GB-CAM_1_2014-08-08,Bar Hill flooding 08 Aug 2014 - 09 Aug 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Bar Hill,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2014/Cambridgeshire County/Bar/Hill/2014/Flood/investigation/Report.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.5533914,-0.8491936,"<div id=""html_7aec8e2a7389017261eb7399cc833af4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Westfield Farm</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fields to the west of the Thames (including the area around Westfield Farm) were inundated during the event, with flood depths in fields typically around 1 m according to the annotated map; agricultural land and farm access were affected. [IMG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Westfield Farm,England,"Fields to the west of the Thames (including the area around Westfield Farm) were inundated during the event, with flood depths in fields typically around 1 m according to the annotated map; agricultural land and farm access were affected. [IMG] (p18)",,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.61726984402831,-0.7321594820691124,"<div id=""html_1c49a85495ff8532a1576b4a94a02cfd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wycombe District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the Thames broke banks upstream and flowed across fields from the westerly direction into the Medmenham area, forming part of the overland flood route. (p27)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 properties; 10 properties; 2 homes; 2 occurrences (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Wycombe District,England,"Floodwater from the Thames broke banks upstream and flowed across fields from the westerly direction into the Medmenham area, forming part of the overland flood route. (p27)",23 properties; 10 properties; 2 homes; 2 occurrences (p27),GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.5542319,-0.8504841,"<div id=""html_4d7f4415e5b4489638bc3d9dd42e796c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Westfield</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged flooding of the back garden of one bungalow caused by locally rising groundwater (consistent with high groundwater in adjacent areas). (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (back garden of one bungalow) (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Westfield,England,Prolonged flooding of the back garden of one bungalow caused by locally rising groundwater (consistent with high groundwater in adjacent areas). (p16),1 property (back garden of one bungalow) (p16),GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.5575525,-0.781404,"<div id=""html_2cf5c67e43c575f3dfd5791e9ca4f962"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Thames</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames experienced very high flows with peaks in January and February 2014; these high main&ndash;river levels rose into the Medmenham area, coming up Ferry Lane and causing inundation of roads and houses and preventing drains from discharging. [MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames is identified as the dominant source of flooding in the area; river levels (constrained by locks and weirs at Mill End (Hambleden) upstream and Hurley downstream) led to inundation of the Medmenham floodplain. There are no flood defences along this stretch, contributing to flood impact on the village. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames is identified as the primary driver of flooding in the area; river levels dominate the mapped flood extent and the Thames (constrained by upstream and downstream locks/weirs) causes inundation of the Medmenham floodplain shown as Flood Zone 3. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels rose during the winter event; the Thames broke its banks west of Medmenham and water flowed across the floodplain. This breach initiated floodwater propagation up Ferry Lane, across land to the bridge near Abbey Cottages, and produced a dominant north&ndash;east flood flow toward wetlands adjacent to New Lock Lane, causing property flooding in the southern part of Ferry Lane. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Thames levels and bank overtopping caused local land drains to back up and break their banks; this led to flood water moving westwards, contributing to property flooding in west Medmenham and to flooding on the north of Ferry Lane (Common Moor and land south of the church) when the river broke its banks west of Ferry Lane. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames overtopped its banks during the January&ndash;February 2014 event and was the primary source of flooding that flowed inland across fields and into the Medmenham area, producing fast&ndash;flowing water across roads (e.g. Ferry Lane) and extensive inundation of low&ndash;lying land and properties. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Thames levels backed up into low&ndash;lying areas around Medmenham, overflowing into Ferry Lane and adjoining ditches and contributing to flooding of local properties and infrastructure (bridges were covered at times and water flowed into adjacent land). [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames rose and breached its banks (south of Hambleden Lock), sending water across fields toward the former Water Research Centre; flows met on Ferry Lane and caused the road to become impassable (except by boat or tractor) and inundated nearby houses. The January and February 2014 events produced prolonged high flows. [PAG,MUL] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames was at very high levels and breached its banks upstream during January&ndash;February 2014, sending floodwater across fields toward Medmenham and causing back&ndash;up in surface water channels, ditches and wetland areas which contributed to property flooding and restricted access. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the watercourse related to flooding in Medmenham; prompted consideration of capital works on the Thames to protect Medmenham and led to changes to flood warning arrangements. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Typically 1 m deep over fields; parts noted up to 1 m depth with localized inundation lasting up to 3 months (north&ndash;east area) [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 properties flooded [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Upstream Jan 2014 water level peak 29.61m; Upstream Feb 2014 water level peak 29.67m; Downstream Jan 2014 water level peak 29.1m; Downstream Feb 2014 water level peak 29.13m [PAG,MUL] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Thames,England,"The River Thames experienced very high flows with peaks in January and February 2014; these high main–river levels rose into the Medmenham area, coming up Ferry Lane and causing inundation of roads and houses and preventing drains from discharging. [MUL] (p4)
The River Thames is identified as the dominant source of flooding in the area; river levels (constrained by locks and weirs at Mill End (Hambleden) upstream and Hurley downstream) led to inundation of the Medmenham floodplain. There are no flood defences along this stretch, contributing to flood impact on the village. (p10)
The River Thames is identified as the primary driver of flooding in the area; river levels dominate the mapped flood extent and the Thames (constrained by upstream and downstream locks/weirs) causes inundation of the Medmenham floodplain shown as Flood Zone 3. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
River levels rose during the winter event; the Thames broke its banks west of Medmenham and water flowed across the floodplain. This breach initiated floodwater propagation up Ferry Lane, across land to the bridge near Abbey Cottages, and produced a dominant north–east flood flow toward wetlands adjacent to New Lock Lane, causing property flooding in the southern part of Ferry Lane. (p15)
High River Thames levels and bank overtopping caused local land drains to back up and break their banks; this led to flood water moving westwards, contributing to property flooding in west Medmenham and to flooding on the north of Ferry Lane (Common Moor and land south of the church) when the river broke its banks west of Ferry Lane. [PAG] (p16)
The River Thames overtopped its banks during the January–February 2014 event and was the primary source of flooding that flowed inland across fields and into the Medmenham area, producing fast–flowing water across roads (e.g. Ferry Lane) and extensive inundation of low–lying land and properties. [IMG] (p18)
River Thames levels backed up into low–lying areas around Medmenham, overflowing into Ferry Lane and adjoining ditches and contributing to flooding of local properties and infrastructure (bridges were covered at times and water flowed into adjacent land). [TAB] (p21)
The River Thames rose and breached its banks (south of Hambleden Lock), sending water across fields toward the former Water Research Centre; flows met on Ferry Lane and caused the road to become impassable (except by boat or tractor) and inundated nearby houses. The January and February 2014 events produced prolonged high flows. [PAG,MUL] (p23)
The River Thames was at very high levels and breached its banks upstream during January–February 2014, sending floodwater across fields toward Medmenham and causing back–up in surface water channels, ditches and wetland areas which contributed to property flooding and restricted access. (p27)
Identified as the watercourse related to flooding in Medmenham; prompted consideration of capital works on the Thames to protect Medmenham and led to changes to flood warning arrangements. [TAB] (p28)","Typically 1 m deep over fields; parts noted up to 1 m depth with localized inundation lasting up to 3 months (north–east area) [IMG] (p18)
15 properties flooded [TAB] (p21)
Upstream Jan 2014 water level peak 29.61m; Upstream Feb 2014 water level peak 29.67m; Downstream Jan 2014 water level peak 29.1m; Downstream Feb 2014 water level peak 29.13m [PAG,MUL] (p23)",GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.5501699,-0.8391862,"<div id=""html_047951130dbba3bff622c0131d36a39c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ferry Lane, Medmenham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Thames levels came up Ferry Lane, leading to inundation of the main route and contributing to flooding of roads and houses in the village; associated drainage (highways and field drains) could not discharge due to high river levels. [MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Thames levels rose and floodwater progressed up Ferry Lane from the slipway. When the Thames broke its banks west of Medmenham water travelled across the floodplain to the bridge near Abbey Cottages and then flowed northeast toward the wetlands adjacent to New Lock Lane. Properties typically affected are those south of the 'white bridge', notably Abbey Cottages and the entrance to Medmenham Abbey; once the Thames has broken its banks practically all properties south of Abbey Cottages can be impacted. Some raised properties were surrounded by floodwater. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>When the River Thames broke its banks west of Ferry Lane the combined floodwater exacerbated drainage backup and caused property flooding in the area. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ferry Lane experienced fast&ndash;flowing floodwater from the River Thames during the Jan&ndash;Feb 2014 event. Flood depths reached up to 80 cm there, making the road impassable and preventing many residents from accessing their houses. Bucks Fire & Rescue deemed the road and floodwater too dangerous to cross (even in 4x4 vehicles); a tractor taxi service was used to transport residents. Owners of some properties operated pumps to limit water ingress. [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooded with west&ndash;to&ndash;east flowing water, preventing pedestrian and vehicle access; many residents could not reach their houses and normal access was replaced by a 'tractor taxi'. Road and floodwater judged too dangerous to cross even in 4x4 vehicles. [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ferry Lane was inundated, restricting and limiting access to multiple properties and hampering response; flood depths on the lane and at property entrances were measured and caused water ingress to houses. [TAB,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Thames water backed up into Ferry Lane: lane flooded, water flowed rapidly along Ferry Lane and over the bridge, water reached as far as Southlands. Sandbags were delivered and rescue activity took place on Ferry Lane. Floodwater later drained into ditches and levels fell over the following days. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Repeated floodwater flowed up Ferry Lane from the River Thames and from adjacent higher ground, crossing and covering the bridge, flowing fast along the lane and across the road. At times water was &#8216;all the way up Ferry Lane&#8217; and drained later via roadside ditches; by later dates flow reduced to puddles and drainage. Residents recorded depths and sandbagging occurred nearby. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Thames rose and flowed up Ferry Lane, flooding the road and surrounding houses; the road was made impassable except by boat or tractor. After the peaks water was still coming up Ferry Lane (noted 21 February 2014) then reduced to very slight flow and began draining via the roadside ditch with puddles remaining (noted 22 February 2014). [TAB,PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the Thames flowed up Ferry Lane from the south and met flows from across the fields, flooding the road and houses; the road was made impassable except by boat or tractor and houses were flooded to significant depth. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater rose up Ferry Lane as the River Thames levels increased; Ferry Lane acted as a source/path for floodwater flowing into fields and properties and contributed to the backing up of drainage channels. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Measured river flood depth at the shallowest position in Ferry Lane (near the river): 27 cm on 11.1.2014 (corrected to 29 cm), indicating local inundation at the low point of the lane. [TAB] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood depths were recorded along Ferry Lane (datum: left gate post) on 12 January 2014. Recorded depths (measured; corrected by +13 cm where indicated) include: Monkshood 22 cm (35 cm corrected); Olmeda 43 cm (56 cm corrected); Abbey Drive 10 cm (23 cm corrected); Ferry Nab 35 cm (48 cm corrected); Abbey Lodge 45 cm (58 cm corrected); 1 Abbey Cottage 56 cm (69 cm corrected); Gate of Monks Cottage 58 cm (71 cm corrected); H sign on wall of Ferry Nab 36 cm (49 cm corrected); Base of 'beware river' traffic sign 22 cm (35 cm corrected). [TAB] (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Water depth up to 80 cm; map shows a circle labeled '18' at Ferry Lane (likely indicating 18 properties affected) [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>80 cm; 60 cm; two houses evacuated [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>60 cm; 5 inches; 18 inches; 3 foot; 4 inches; 8 inches; 1 inch; 5 mm [TAB,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 properties flooded at Medmenham; water fell by 4 inches and 5 inches (measured during recession) [MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>29cm (11.1.2014 corrected) [TAB] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Monkshood 22 cm (35 cm); Olmeda 43 cm (56 cm); Abbey Drive 10 cm (23 cm); Ferry Nab 35 cm (48 cm); Abbey Lodge 45 cm (58 cm); 1 Abbey Cottage 56 cm (69 cm); Gate of Monks Cottage 58 cm (71 cm); H sign on wall of Ferry Nab 36 cm (49 cm); Base of 'beware river' traffic sign 22 cm (35 cm) [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Ferry Lane, Medmenham",England,"High River Thames levels came up Ferry Lane, leading to inundation of the main route and contributing to flooding of roads and houses in the village; associated drainage (highways and field drains) could not discharge due to high river levels. [MUL] (p4)
River Thames levels rose and floodwater progressed up Ferry Lane from the slipway. When the Thames broke its banks west of Medmenham water travelled across the floodplain to the bridge near Abbey Cottages and then flowed northeast toward the wetlands adjacent to New Lock Lane. Properties typically affected are those south of the 'white bridge', notably Abbey Cottages and the entrance to Medmenham Abbey; once the Thames has broken its banks practically all properties south of Abbey Cottages can be impacted. Some raised properties were surrounded by floodwater. (p15)
When the River Thames broke its banks west of Ferry Lane the combined floodwater exacerbated drainage backup and caused property flooding in the area. (p16)
Ferry Lane experienced fast–flowing floodwater from the River Thames during the Jan–Feb 2014 event. Flood depths reached up to 80 cm there, making the road impassable and preventing many residents from accessing their houses. Bucks Fire & Rescue deemed the road and floodwater too dangerous to cross (even in 4x4 vehicles); a tractor taxi service was used to transport residents. Owners of some properties operated pumps to limit water ingress. [IMG,PAG] (p18)
Road flooded with west–to–east flowing water, preventing pedestrian and vehicle access; many residents could not reach their houses and normal access was replaced by a 'tractor taxi'. Road and floodwater judged too dangerous to cross even in 4x4 vehicles. [TAB,PAG] (p19)
Ferry Lane was inundated, restricting and limiting access to multiple properties and hampering response; flood depths on the lane and at property entrances were measured and caused water ingress to houses. [TAB,PAG] (p20)
River Thames water backed up into Ferry Lane: lane flooded, water flowed rapidly along Ferry Lane and over the bridge, water reached as far as Southlands. Sandbags were delivered and rescue activity took place on Ferry Lane. Floodwater later drained into ditches and levels fell over the following days. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p21)
Repeated floodwater flowed up Ferry Lane from the River Thames and from adjacent higher ground, crossing and covering the bridge, flowing fast along the lane and across the road. At times water was ‘all the way up Ferry Lane’ and drained later via roadside ditches; by later dates flow reduced to puddles and drainage. Residents recorded depths and sandbagging occurred nearby. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p22)
River Thames rose and flowed up Ferry Lane, flooding the road and surrounding houses; the road was made impassable except by boat or tractor. After the peaks water was still coming up Ferry Lane (noted 21 February 2014) then reduced to very slight flow and began draining via the roadside ditch with puddles remaining (noted 22 February 2014). [TAB,PAG] (p23)
Floodwater from the Thames flowed up Ferry Lane from the south and met flows from across the fields, flooding the road and houses; the road was made impassable except by boat or tractor and houses were flooded to significant depth. [PAG] (p24)
Floodwater rose up Ferry Lane as the River Thames levels increased; Ferry Lane acted as a source/path for floodwater flowing into fields and properties and contributed to the backing up of drainage channels. (p27)
Measured river flood depth at the shallowest position in Ferry Lane (near the river): 27 cm on 11.1.2014 (corrected to 29 cm), indicating local inundation at the low point of the lane. [TAB] (p33)
Flood depths were recorded along Ferry Lane (datum: left gate post) on 12 January 2014. Recorded depths (measured; corrected by +13 cm where indicated) include: Monkshood 22 cm (35 cm corrected); Olmeda 43 cm (56 cm corrected); Abbey Drive 10 cm (23 cm corrected); Ferry Nab 35 cm (48 cm corrected); Abbey Lodge 45 cm (58 cm corrected); 1 Abbey Cottage 56 cm (69 cm corrected); Gate of Monks Cottage 58 cm (71 cm corrected); H sign on wall of Ferry Nab 36 cm (49 cm corrected); Base of 'beware river' traffic sign 22 cm (35 cm corrected). [TAB] (p34)","Water depth up to 80 cm; map shows a circle labeled '18' at Ferry Lane (likely indicating 18 properties affected) [IMG,PAG] (p18)
80 cm; 60 cm; two houses evacuated [TAB,PAG] (p19)
60 cm; 5 inches; 18 inches; 3 foot; 4 inches; 8 inches; 1 inch; 5 mm [TAB,PAG] (p20)
15 properties flooded at Medmenham; water fell by 4 inches and 5 inches (measured during recession) [MUL] (p21)
29cm (11.1.2014 corrected) [TAB] (p33)
Monkshood 22 cm (35 cm); Olmeda 43 cm (56 cm); Abbey Drive 10 cm (23 cm); Ferry Nab 35 cm (48 cm); Abbey Lodge 45 cm (58 cm); 1 Abbey Cottage 56 cm (69 cm); Gate of Monks Cottage 58 cm (71 cm); H sign on wall of Ferry Nab 36 cm (49 cm); Base of 'beware river' traffic sign 22 cm (35 cm) [TAB] (p34)",GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_8ee6f166274b454f8ef0c25390b153ab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>When the River Thames rose, water flowed up Ferry Lane from the south flooding the road and houses. A second flow route occurred when the Thames broke its banks south of Hambleden Lock and flowed across fields towards and through the site of the former Water Research Centre. The two flow routes met on Ferry Lane, making the road impassable except by boat or tractor and flooding houses to a significant depth. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"When the River Thames rose, water flowed up Ferry Lane from the south flooding the road and houses. A second flow route occurred when the Thames broke its banks south of Hambleden Lock and flowed across fields towards and through the site of the former Water Research Centre. The two flow routes met on Ferry Lane, making the road impassable except by boat or tractor and flooding houses to a significant depth. (p24)",,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.5512466,-0.8087369,"<div id=""html_735aba2731765988d35ed6b8cf4f872c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hurley Lock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed downstream water level peaks are reported for the flood events: Jan 2014 and Feb 2014 peaks are recorded and compared with Jan 2003, showing 2014 levels were slightly lower than 2003 locally at Hurley. [MUL] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Jan 2014 water level peak 29.1 m; Feb 2014 water level peak 29.13 m; Jan 2003 water level peak 29.14 m [MUL] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Hurley Lock,England,"Observed downstream water level peaks are reported for the flood events: Jan 2014 and Feb 2014 peaks are recorded and compared with Jan 2003, showing 2014 levels were slightly lower than 2003 locally at Hurley. [MUL] (p23)",Jan 2014 water level peak 29.1 m; Feb 2014 water level peak 29.13 m; Jan 2003 water level peak 29.14 m [MUL] (p23),GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.5536495,-0.84037,"<div id=""html_99bc35098cb672c0c266b691495d4346"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Medmenham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced flooding when very high River Thames levels rose and came up the main route (Ferry Lane), inundating roads and flooding houses. High river levels also prevented highways and field drains from discharging. [MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident occurred in Medmenham during the January&ndash;February 2014 event, prompting an investigation. The village lies immediately adjacent to the River Thames with the main route (Ferry Lane) running down to the river; the settlement includes low&ndash;lying open areas, fields and drainage ditches alongside the Thames that are described in the investigation context as relevant to the flood incident. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village is on a natural floodplain south of the A4155 and was affected by flooding dominated by the River Thames. Flood Zone 3 / the 1&ndash;in&ndash;100&ndash;year model extent covers almost the entire village; low&ndash;lying land (~30&ndash;31 m AOD) and properties along Ferry Lane and near New Lock Lane lie within the floodplain and were at risk of inundation. There are no flood defences on this stretch of river, increasing exposure. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village lies on the natural Thames floodplain south of the A4155 and is shown as being within Flood Zone 3. Flooding in the area is dominated by the River Thames; low&ndash;lying parts of the village (notably south of the A4155 and along Ferry Lane, with additional properties near New Lock Lane) are at high risk of inundation. There are no flood defences along this stretch of river and river levels are controlled by nearby locks and weirs, contributing to flood risk. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames rose and broke its banks west of Medmenham, sending floodwater north&ndash;east across the floodplain into the village. Flooding progressed up Ferry Lane from the slipway, affected the eastern side of Medmenham Abbey and, once banks had breached further west, created a dominant NE flow that inundated wetlands and led to property flooding in the southern half of Ferry Lane (notably south of the white bridge and Abbey Cottages). Some raised properties were surrounded by water. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>When the River Thames was high the land drains backed up and broke their banks, sending floodwater westwards into the main part of Medmenham and inundating gardens and properties; the situation was worsened when the Thames breached its banks west of Ferry Lane. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Medmenham was impacted by river flooding in January and February 2014 (onset December 2013) with flood marks and resident reports indicating inundation. Local groundwater emergence also caused prolonged flooding of the back gardens of one bungalow. [PAG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Thames overtopping affected the Medmenham area in January&ndash;February 2014. Properties were inundated; most residents remained in their homes, some used pumps to limit ingress, and two houses were evacuated. Access to some houses was prevented by deep floodwater on local roads (notably Ferry Lane). A tractor taxi was used to move people because roads were unsafe. [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Thames and backed&ndash;up flows inundated gardens, drives and local roads. Gardens and drive were fully immersed to depths reported between 30 cm and 1 m (about 0.5 m average). A house flooded to a maximum depth of 20 cm in February 2014; other houses had no water inside in January 2014. The boat yard area was flooded (estimated inches depth). Water reached up Ferry Lane to Southlands and covered a bridge; water was reported just inside Abbey Gates and at the level of Weybrooks garage. Bucks Fire & Rescue Service recorded 15 properties flooded in Medmenham during the event. [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying areas in Medmenham were flooded when Thames water flowed up Ferry Lane from the south, flooding the road and houses. Ferry Lane and the road were made impassable (only passable by boat or tractor). During the February 2014 episode water was still coming up Ferry Lane (noted 21 Feb) then by 22 Feb was mostly draining via roadside ditches and puddles remained. [MUL] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Thames at very high levels caused flooding in Medmenham. 23 properties were affected, 10 experienced internal flooding and two homes were evacuated. Around half of properties were impacted either by internal flooding or restricted access; the disruption was extensive and occurred twice over the January&ndash;February 2014 events. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding associated with the River Thames that led to calls for improved emergency response, temporary defences, community flood planning and investigation of capital works to reduce future flood risk. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two flood events; one bungalow [PAG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 houses evacuated [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>30cm to 1m; 0.5m average; 20cm; 2&ndash;3 inches; 15 properties [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 properties; 10 internal flooding; 2 homes evacuated (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Medmenham,England,"The village experienced flooding when very high River Thames levels rose and came up the main route (Ferry Lane), inundating roads and flooding houses. High river levels also prevented highways and field drains from discharging. [MUL] (p4)
A flood incident occurred in Medmenham during the January–February 2014 event, prompting an investigation. The village lies immediately adjacent to the River Thames with the main route (Ferry Lane) running down to the river; the settlement includes low–lying open areas, fields and drainage ditches alongside the Thames that are described in the investigation context as relevant to the flood incident. [PAG] (p6)
The village is on a natural floodplain south of the A4155 and was affected by flooding dominated by the River Thames. Flood Zone 3 / the 1–in–100–year model extent covers almost the entire village; low–lying land (~30–31 m AOD) and properties along Ferry Lane and near New Lock Lane lie within the floodplain and were at risk of inundation. There are no flood defences on this stretch of river, increasing exposure. (p10)
The village lies on the natural Thames floodplain south of the A4155 and is shown as being within Flood Zone 3. Flooding in the area is dominated by the River Thames; low–lying parts of the village (notably south of the A4155 and along Ferry Lane, with additional properties near New Lock Lane) are at high risk of inundation. There are no flood defences along this stretch of river and river levels are controlled by nearby locks and weirs, contributing to flood risk. [PAG] (p12)
The River Thames rose and broke its banks west of Medmenham, sending floodwater north–east across the floodplain into the village. Flooding progressed up Ferry Lane from the slipway, affected the eastern side of Medmenham Abbey and, once banks had breached further west, created a dominant NE flow that inundated wetlands and led to property flooding in the southern half of Ferry Lane (notably south of the white bridge and Abbey Cottages). Some raised properties were surrounded by water. [PAG] (p15)
When the River Thames was high the land drains backed up and broke their banks, sending floodwater westwards into the main part of Medmenham and inundating gardens and properties; the situation was worsened when the Thames breached its banks west of Ferry Lane. (p16)
Medmenham was impacted by river flooding in January and February 2014 (onset December 2013) with flood marks and resident reports indicating inundation. Local groundwater emergence also caused prolonged flooding of the back gardens of one bungalow. [PAG,MUL] (p17)
Flooding from the River Thames overtopping affected the Medmenham area in January–February 2014. Properties were inundated; most residents remained in their homes, some used pumps to limit ingress, and two houses were evacuated. Access to some houses was prevented by deep floodwater on local roads (notably Ferry Lane). A tractor taxi was used to move people because roads were unsafe. [IMG,PAG] (p18)
Flooding from the River Thames and backed–up flows inundated gardens, drives and local roads. Gardens and drive were fully immersed to depths reported between 30 cm and 1 m (about 0.5 m average). A house flooded to a maximum depth of 20 cm in February 2014; other houses had no water inside in January 2014. The boat yard area was flooded (estimated inches depth). Water reached up Ferry Lane to Southlands and covered a bridge; water was reported just inside Abbey Gates and at the level of Weybrooks garage. Bucks Fire & Rescue Service recorded 15 properties flooded in Medmenham during the event. [TAB,MUL] (p21)
Low–lying areas in Medmenham were flooded when Thames water flowed up Ferry Lane from the south, flooding the road and houses. Ferry Lane and the road were made impassable (only passable by boat or tractor). During the February 2014 episode water was still coming up Ferry Lane (noted 21 Feb) then by 22 Feb was mostly draining via roadside ditches and puddles remained. [MUL] (p23)
The River Thames at very high levels caused flooding in Medmenham. 23 properties were affected, 10 experienced internal flooding and two homes were evacuated. Around half of properties were impacted either by internal flooding or restricted access; the disruption was extensive and occurred twice over the January–February 2014 events. (p27)
Experienced flooding associated with the River Thames that led to calls for improved emergency response, temporary defences, community flood planning and investigation of capital works to reduce future flood risk. [TAB] (p28)","two flood events; one bungalow [PAG,MUL] (p17)
2 houses evacuated [IMG,PAG] (p18)
30cm to 1m; 0.5m average; 20cm; 2–3 inches; 15 properties [TAB,MUL] (p21)
23 properties; 10 internal flooding; 2 homes evacuated (p27)",GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.545506,-0.8101712,"<div id=""html_b79381fcd0a49d9d362a848f2ab64cfd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hurley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying land around Hurley was flooded by the River Thames; residents reported water flowed up Ferry Lane faster than in 2003, roads (Ferry Lane) became impassable and houses were flooded to significant depth as two flow routes met on Ferry Lane. [TAB,MUL] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Hurley,England,"Low–lying land around Hurley was flooded by the River Thames; residents reported water flowed up Ferry Lane faster than in 2003, roads (Ferry Lane) became impassable and houses were flooded to significant depth as two flow routes met on Ferry Lane. [TAB,MUL] (p23)",,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-BKM,12,2025-01-01,51.57156365870574,-0.7767503540424273,"<div id=""html_cea607a80721adce8ae14b96d5e2057e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marlow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report notes higher population centres such as Marlow were impacted during the January and February 2014 flood events and received concentrated response resources (staff, sandbags, pumping), indicating Marlow experienced river&ndash;flood impacts during those events. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Marlow,England,"The report notes higher population centres such as Marlow were impacted during the January and February 2014 flood events and received concentrated response resources (staff, sandbags, pumping), indicating Marlow experienced river–flood impacts during those events. (p23)",,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01.html,GB-BKM_12_2025-01-01,Medmenham flooding January - February 2014,Medmenham January & February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-medmenham.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.265402,-0.7534214,"<div id=""html_2f15224a8d42c961cc22796194022263"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Square, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall overwhelmed local drainage so excess surface water flowed over ground and along the public highway and around private property; carriageway, kerbs and footways directed surface water toward vulnerable properties, causing internal flooding of property(ies) in The Square. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall and ineffective drainage caused excess surface water to flow over ground and along the public highway and around private property; local carriageway/kerb/footway levels directed surface water toward vulnerable properties, resulting in internal flooding of one property. [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water crossed the threshold of a building at The Square causing internal flooding of a property; the property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Square is a focal point for surface water flow and was affected by surface water flooding. Surface water sewers drain to The Square from High Street and West Street; blocked road gullies on approaches were seen during the Flood Incident Investigation. The carriageway is higher than the south footway and some building thresholds, leading to water reaching doorways; frontage channel drains have been installed to try to protect thresholds. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 16/09/15 surface water from High Street and West Way overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square; surface water entered the doorway of a property on The Square and flowed into several rooms, nearly reaching the electrical cupboard. The Square has also experienced external flooding of private property and repeated near&ndash;miss internal flooding requiring temporary barriers. [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water built up at the corner of Station Road and The Square, overtopped lowered kerbs and flowed into the doorway of a property at The Square, causing internal flooding that reached close to an electrical cupboard and required cleaning; the business was temporarily affected. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The arrangement of kerbs and levels meant surface water flowing off the carriageway could enter The Square, flow onto the footway and into doorways of properties during the 16th September 2015 event. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The street was flooded with muddy water overtopping the kerbs and flowing across the pavement. Debris (wooden planks, fencing) and a partially submerged construction cone were present; buildings adjacent to the square had sandbags and planks placed at entrances to reduce water ingress. [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kerb and footway arrangement at The Square with observed gully blockages and leaf accumulation recorded during the flood investigation, indicating reduced drainage capacity and association with surface water flooding at the Square and its junctions. [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG,MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately six times every year for the last five years (repeated kerb overtopping/near&ndash;miss at The Square) [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 25 mm depth of water (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"The Square, Earls Barton",England,"Moderate rainfall overwhelmed local drainage so excess surface water flowed over ground and along the public highway and around private property; carriageway, kerbs and footways directed surface water toward vulnerable properties, causing internal flooding of property(ies) in The Square. [PAG] (p6)
Moderate rainfall and ineffective drainage caused excess surface water to flow over ground and along the public highway and around private property; local carriageway/kerb/footway levels directed surface water toward vulnerable properties, resulting in internal flooding of one property. [PAG,MUL] (p7)
Water crossed the threshold of a building at The Square causing internal flooding of a property; the property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. [PAG] (p9)
The Square is a focal point for surface water flow and was affected by surface water flooding. Surface water sewers drain to The Square from High Street and West Street; blocked road gullies on approaches were seen during the Flood Incident Investigation. The carriageway is higher than the south footway and some building thresholds, leading to water reaching doorways; frontage channel drains have been installed to try to protect thresholds. [PAG] (p10)
On 16/09/15 surface water from High Street and West Way overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square; surface water entered the doorway of a property on The Square and flowed into several rooms, nearly reaching the electrical cupboard. The Square has also experienced external flooding of private property and repeated near–miss internal flooding requiring temporary barriers. [TAB,PAG] (p11)
Surface water built up at the corner of Station Road and The Square, overtopped lowered kerbs and flowed into the doorway of a property at The Square, causing internal flooding that reached close to an electrical cupboard and required cleaning; the business was temporarily affected. (p12)
The arrangement of kerbs and levels meant surface water flowing off the carriageway could enter The Square, flow onto the footway and into doorways of properties during the 16th September 2015 event. (p13)
The street was flooded with muddy water overtopping the kerbs and flowing across the pavement. Debris (wooden planks, fencing) and a partially submerged construction cone were present; buildings adjacent to the square had sandbags and planks placed at entrances to reduce water ingress. [PAG] (p34)
Kerb and footway arrangement at The Square with observed gully blockages and leaf accumulation recorded during the flood investigation, indicating reduced drainage capacity and association with surface water flooding at the Square and its junctions. [IMG,PAG] (p40)","one property [PAG] (p6)
one property [PAG,MUL] (p7)
one property [PAG] (p9)
approximately six times every year for the last five years (repeated kerb overtopping/near–miss at The Square) [TAB,PAG] (p11)
1 property; 25 mm depth of water (p12)",GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2683739,-0.7489795,"<div id=""html_71226471b58fa6b10a1119e161912968"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Road, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from the residential area of North Street (sic) forms part of the catchment flowing to The Square and contributed to the surface water flow that led to internal flooding of a property at The Square. (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"North Road, Earls Barton",England,Runoff from the residential area of North Street (sic) forms part of the catchment flowing to The Square and contributed to the surface water flow that led to internal flooding of a property at The Square. (p9),one property (p9),GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2691538,-0.7531519,"<div id=""html_969592eb8b22ec6de043f13e10cbf49b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Manor Road, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from Manor Road, as part of the catchment feeding The Square, contributed to the surface water flow that resulted in internal flooding of a property at The Square. (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Manor Road, Earls Barton",England,"Runoff from Manor Road, as part of the catchment feeding The Square, contributed to the surface water flow that resulted in internal flooding of a property at The Square. (p9)",one property (p9),GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2667899,-0.7554185,"<div id=""html_d69b3b04fa6db2080e929f635a1a1dd2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harrowick Lane, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contribution of runoff from Hardwick Lane (sic) (and West Street) helped create the flow meeting at The Square that led to internal flooding of a property at The Square. (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Harrowick Lane, Earls Barton",England,Contribution of runoff from Hardwick Lane (sic) (and West Street) helped create the flow meeting at The Square that led to internal flooding of a property at The Square. (p9),one property (p9),GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2646634,-0.7511289,"<div id=""html_fef234da9ad0c1df294daea901cb6433"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Broad Street, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Broad Street was identified as a low point receiving surface water flows and is shown on mapping as high risk. Several road gullies on Broad Street were observed blocked with leaves and silt during the Flood Incident Investigation, contributing to surface water ponding on the carriageway and the potential for water to reach lower footways and building thresholds. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed east along Broad Street to the low point opposite Churchill Road; water was reported flowing out of road gullies and overtopping kerbs at the low point, flooding the road and footways. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Broad Street were vulnerable to surface water flowing off the carriageway; during the 16th September 2015 event this water could flow onto the footway and into property doorways, causing flooding impacts. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water discharged from a kerb offlet gully and spread onto the pavement and road, causing surface water flooding on the south and north sides of Broad Street; pooling and flow across the carriageway and footway with muddy/discoloured runoff indicating drainage overflow and potential contamination and hazards to pedestrians and vehicles. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Footway flooding on the south side of Broad Street (opposite Churchill Road): multiple damp patches and pooling of water, with a visible puddle concentrated around a metal drain cover on the pavement. [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Footway flooding occurred on the south side of Broad Street (opposite Churchill Road). Roof drainage discharges onto a gully in the pavement which is blocked, causing water to accumulate on the footway and verge and contributing to flooding of the pedestrian area and adjacent carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one visible puddle [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Broad Street, Earls Barton",England,"Broad Street was identified as a low point receiving surface water flows and is shown on mapping as high risk. Several road gullies on Broad Street were observed blocked with leaves and silt during the Flood Incident Investigation, contributing to surface water ponding on the carriageway and the potential for water to reach lower footways and building thresholds. [PAG] (p10)
Surface water flowed east along Broad Street to the low point opposite Churchill Road; water was reported flowing out of road gullies and overtopping kerbs at the low point, flooding the road and footways. (p12)
Areas of Broad Street were vulnerable to surface water flowing off the carriageway; during the 16th September 2015 event this water could flow onto the footway and into property doorways, causing flooding impacts. (p13)
Water discharged from a kerb offlet gully and spread onto the pavement and road, causing surface water flooding on the south and north sides of Broad Street; pooling and flow across the carriageway and footway with muddy/discoloured runoff indicating drainage overflow and potential contamination and hazards to pedestrians and vehicles. [IMG,PAG] (p38)
Footway flooding on the south side of Broad Street (opposite Churchill Road): multiple damp patches and pooling of water, with a visible puddle concentrated around a metal drain cover on the pavement. [IMG,PAG] (p42)
Footway flooding occurred on the south side of Broad Street (opposite Churchill Road). Roof drainage discharges onto a gully in the pavement which is blocked, causing water to accumulate on the footway and verge and contributing to flooding of the pedestrian area and adjacent carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p43)","one visible puddle [IMG,PAG] (p42)",GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2670253,-0.7502767,"<div id=""html_4e8946dace03f3584e567b3f65f5df3b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water sewers from High Street flow towards The Square. Several road gullies on High Street were seen to be blocked with leaves and silt during the Flood Incident Investigation. Limited local drainage on private car parks and hardstandings allowed runoff to reach the public highway, increasing surface water flow toward the town centre. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed from High Street from north to south across the crossroads junction toward the corner of Station Road and Broad Street, contributing to accumulation at the low point. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked road gully adjacent to the kerb (partially blocked by fallen leaves) was documented during the flood incident investigation, reducing drainage capacity on the downhill approach to The Square and linked to surface water flooding impacting the roadway and junction. [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked road gully reduced drainage capacity, causing surface water to pond on the carriageway and contributing to highway flooding/run&ndash;off during the flood event. [PAG] (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"High Street, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water sewers from High Street flow towards The Square. Several road gullies on High Street were seen to be blocked with leaves and silt during the Flood Incident Investigation. Limited local drainage on private car parks and hardstandings allowed runoff to reach the public highway, increasing surface water flow toward the town centre. (p10)
Surface water runoff flowed from High Street from north to south across the crossroads junction toward the corner of Station Road and Broad Street, contributing to accumulation at the low point. (p12)
Blocked road gully adjacent to the kerb (partially blocked by fallen leaves) was documented during the flood incident investigation, reducing drainage capacity on the downhill approach to The Square and linked to surface water flooding impacting the roadway and junction. [IMG,PAG] (p40)
Blocked road gully reduced drainage capacity, causing surface water to pond on the carriageway and contributing to highway flooding/run–off during the flood event. [PAG] (p45)",,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2657751,-0.7550298,"<div id=""html_33b74bcba7eeb06f7b7ba6d75224a9ae"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Street, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from West Street flows into the centre of Earls Barton and towards The Square. Some sewers direct surface water from outside the catchment into the centre via West Street. Limited inspected areas showed private hardstandings and car parks runoff onto the highway, contributing to surface water conveyance into the town centre during the incident. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leaf fall accumulation at the kerb offlet gully was observed from West Street toward The Square, blocking the offlet and reducing drainage capacity; this condition was recorded as part of the flood incident investigation and is associated with localised surface water flooding on the street. [IMG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Leaf build&ndash;up blocking a kerb offlet gully and private paved areas with limited or unmaintained drainage led to surface water run&ndash;off onto the public highway, exacerbating highway flooding/run&ndash;off during the flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"West Street, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water from West Street flows into the centre of Earls Barton and towards The Square. Some sewers direct surface water from outside the catchment into the centre via West Street. Limited inspected areas showed private hardstandings and car parks runoff onto the highway, contributing to surface water conveyance into the town centre during the incident. (p10)
Leaf fall accumulation at the kerb offlet gully was observed from West Street toward The Square, blocking the offlet and reducing drainage capacity; this condition was recorded as part of the flood incident investigation and is associated with localised surface water flooding on the street. [IMG] (p40)
Leaf build–up blocking a kerb offlet gully and private paved areas with limited or unmaintained drainage led to surface water run–off onto the public highway, exacerbating highway flooding/run–off during the flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p45)",,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2562302,-0.759913,"<div id=""html_32131f5e318845eb8d414f212676b1ad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northampton Road, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water sewers from Northampton Road were reported to direct flow into the centre of Earls Barton, contributing to surface water entering the town centre drainage system during the incident. (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Northampton Road, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water sewers from Northampton Road were reported to direct flow into the centre of Earls Barton, contributing to surface water entering the town centre drainage system during the incident. (p10)",,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2524261,-0.7470122,"<div id=""html_b89a0faa7420186cd4ff16ea53cb6f56"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding closed Station Road for 48 hours (09/04/98). Kerb overtopping at the corner of Station Road and The Square led to highway flooding and contributed to surface water entering nearby properties during later events. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overtopped lowered kerbs at the pedestrian crossing at the corner with The Square, flooding the footway on the south side of the crossroads and contributing to water entering an adjacent property. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>road closed for 48 hours [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Station Road, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water flooding closed Station Road for 48 hours (09/04/98). Kerb overtopping at the corner of Station Road and The Square led to highway flooding and contributed to surface water entering nearby properties during later events. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
Surface water overtopped lowered kerbs at the pedestrian crossing at the corner with The Square, flooding the footway on the south side of the crossroads and contributing to water entering an adjacent property. (p12)","road closed for 48 hours [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)",GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2677619,-0.7563716,"<div id=""html_61c9d4bb0282efacd36b62cab37e9acd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Way, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from higher ground on West Way flowed along carriageways, overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square on 16/09/2015 and contributed to surface water entering a property at The Square (flowing to several rooms and nearly reaching the electrical cupboard). [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed from higher ground on West Way along the carriageway and over road gullies toward The Square and the crossroads at Station Road/Broad Street, contributing to flooding at downstream low points. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 times per year for 5 years; 1 property internally flooded [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"West Way, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water runoff from higher ground on West Way flowed along carriageways, overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square on 16/09/2015 and contributed to surface water entering a property at The Square (flowing to several rooms and nearly reaching the electrical cupboard). [TAB,PAG] (p11)
Surface water runoff flowed from higher ground on West Way along the carriageway and over road gullies toward The Square and the crossroads at Station Road/Broad Street, contributing to flooding at downstream low points. (p12)","6 times per year for 5 years; 1 property internally flooded [TAB,PAG] (p11)",GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.2665922,-0.7474644,"<div id=""html_adc6a20d42a3e8892f3dc01cd38854ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Churchill Road, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from Churchill Road joined flows on Broad Street and contributed to accumulation at the low point opposite Churchill Road where gullies discharged and footways/road were flooded. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Churchill Road, Earls Barton",England,Surface water runoff from Churchill Road joined flows on Broad Street and contributed to accumulation at the low point opposite Churchill Road where gullies discharged and footways/road were flooded. (p12),,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_418b062e553e3ca39f7f6ede80e27c8b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A formal Section 19 investigation was carried out by the Lead Local Flood Authority into a flood incident at The Square, Earls Barton. The incident involved internal flooding of property(ies) and met the County LLFA threshold for investigation. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"A formal Section 19 investigation was carried out by the Lead Local Flood Authority into a flood incident at The Square, Earls Barton. The incident involved internal flooding of property(ies) and met the County LLFA threshold for investigation. (p8)",one property (p8),GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-NNH,37,2015-09-16,52.263397,-0.7455269,"<div id=""html_b27150b4f45a803df027527e41887734"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named as the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation report (The Square, Earls Barton). No specific flood impacts are described in the provided excerpt. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall and ineffective drainage produced excess surface water that flowed to low points on the public highway and around private properties in Earls Barton (The Square), resulting in internal flooding of at least one property and repeated internal flooding incidents noted. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from moderate rainfall collected and flowed along natural contours to low points on the public highway at The Square, causing internal flooding of at least one property within Earls Barton. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village contained the flood incident at The Square where internal flooding of one property occurred. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town centre experienced surface water flooding during the investigated event. Blocked road gullies were observed during the Flood Incident Investigation which contributed to poor highway drainage. Runoff from private car parks and hardstandings on West Street and High Street flowed onto the public highway, increasing surface water conveyance into the centre. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall on 16th September 2015 produced surface water that could not be collected or conveyed by drainage. Surface water flowed from higher ground (private property and highway) following natural contours into low points of the village, causing flooding of low&ndash;lying areas and temporary impacts to properties and businesses. Highway drainage systems and public sewers were vulnerable to blockage and exceedance during the event. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Settlement experienced flooding and was the subject of a Section 19 flood investigation by NCC following the event on 16th September 2015. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced surface water flooding on 16th September 2015: highway drainage and runoff led to overtopping of kerbs and flow towards private properties, prompting reporting of incidents, community response planning and recommended drainage/asset improvements. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Earls Barton,England,"Named as the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation report (The Square, Earls Barton). No specific flood impacts are described in the provided excerpt. (p2)
Moderate rainfall and ineffective drainage produced excess surface water that flowed to low points on the public highway and around private properties in Earls Barton (The Square), resulting in internal flooding of at least one property and repeated internal flooding incidents noted. [PAG] (p6)
Surface water from moderate rainfall collected and flowed along natural contours to low points on the public highway at The Square, causing internal flooding of at least one property within Earls Barton. [PAG] (p7)
The village contained the flood incident at The Square where internal flooding of one property occurred. [PAG] (p9)
The town centre experienced surface water flooding during the investigated event. Blocked road gullies were observed during the Flood Incident Investigation which contributed to poor highway drainage. Runoff from private car parks and hardstandings on West Street and High Street flowed onto the public highway, increasing surface water conveyance into the centre. (p10)
Moderate rainfall on 16th September 2015 produced surface water that could not be collected or conveyed by drainage. Surface water flowed from higher ground (private property and highway) following natural contours into low points of the village, causing flooding of low–lying areas and temporary impacts to properties and businesses. Highway drainage systems and public sewers were vulnerable to blockage and exceedance during the event. [PAG] (p13)
Settlement experienced flooding and was the subject of a Section 19 flood investigation by NCC following the event on 16th September 2015. (p16)
The town experienced surface water flooding on 16th September 2015: highway drainage and runoff led to overtopping of kerbs and flow towards private properties, prompting reporting of incidents, community response planning and recommended drainage/asset improvements. (p20)","one property [PAG] (p6)
one property [PAG] (p7)
one property [PAG] (p9)",GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_37_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls Barton - September 2015.pdf
GB-HEF,20,2020-02-16,52.19713,-2.9304386,"<div id=""html_167f428f9418bd532a6fa33bc228ff34"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Curl Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Curl Brook is the main watercourse shown as producing river flooding; flood zones 3 and 2 follow the course of the brook, placing adjacent land and properties at the highest flood risk; areas benefiting from flood defences are limited and closely follow the brook. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River overflow from the Curl Brook caused internal flooding to properties in Lyonshall; site visit (June 2021) also confirmed previous episodes of flooding and foul (sewer) flooding associated with the brook. [IMG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a source of fluvial flood risk close to properties in the village. An upstream tributary of the Curl Brook was diverted (railway construction) and the channel gradient and routing are reported to allow water to drain onto the road and into the village; Herefordshire Council LLFA has proposed a feasibility study into the Curl Brook to address the fluvial flood risk. (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties reported river overflow from Curl Brook; internal flood depths reported as less than 6 inches [IMG,MUL] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Curl Brook,England,"Curl Brook is the main watercourse shown as producing river flooding; flood zones 3 and 2 follow the course of the brook, placing adjacent land and properties at the highest flood risk; areas benefiting from flood defences are limited and closely follow the brook. [IMG] (p1)
River overflow from the Curl Brook caused internal flooding to properties in Lyonshall; site visit (June 2021) also confirmed previous episodes of flooding and foul (sewer) flooding associated with the brook. [IMG,MUL] (p3)
Identified as a source of fluvial flood risk close to properties in the village. An upstream tributary of the Curl Brook was diverted (railway construction) and the channel gradient and routing are reported to allow water to drain onto the road and into the village; Herefordshire Council LLFA has proposed a feasibility study into the Curl Brook to address the fluvial flood risk. (p4)","2 properties reported river overflow from Curl Brook; internal flood depths reported as less than 6 inches [IMG,MUL] (p3)",GB-HEF_20_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_20_2020-02-16,Lyonshall flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Lyonshall,055/Herefordshire County/19/Lyonshall.pdf
GB-HEF,20,2020-02-16,52.1930032,-2.9435351,"<div id=""html_98a0cabe6ad76cadad4df1b4b454f852"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sour Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the Sour Brook caused property flooding in the parish. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two properties in the parish flooded from the Sour Brook during the February 16, 2020 flood event (internal flooding reported). [PAG] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties flooded from Sour Brook (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties [PAG] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Sour Brook,England,"Flooding from the Sour Brook caused property flooding in the parish. (p3)
Two properties in the parish flooded from the Sour Brook during the February 16, 2020 flood event (internal flooding reported). [PAG] (p4)","2 properties flooded from Sour Brook (p3)
2 properties [PAG] (p4)",GB-HEF_20_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_20_2020-02-16,Lyonshall flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Lyonshall,055/Herefordshire County/19/Lyonshall.pdf
GB-HEF,20,2020-02-16,52.1999644,-2.9796914,"<div id=""html_faa2854cad8d7ffb6ca1c78d7f91b578"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lyonshall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties adjacent to Curl Brook lie within Flood Zone 3 (highest risk); areas benefiting from flood defences are limited and closely follow the river, so properties near the brook are at significant risk of river flooding. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding occurred to properties in Lyonshall during the key event; flooding sources reported include river overflow from Curl Brook, blocked gullies/overland flows, and overflowing sewers/toilets (foul flooding). (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced internal flooding to residential properties (five properties internally flooded during the key flood event). Surface water runoff affected the highway with water draining onto the road and into the village; foul sewers surcharged during storms and there are reports of foul flooding on highways. No injuries or loss of life were reported. (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties recorded internal flooding (depths all less than 6 inches); 2 additional properties in the parish flooded from Sour Brook (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Lyonshall,England,"Properties adjacent to Curl Brook lie within Flood Zone 3 (highest risk); areas benefiting from flood defences are limited and closely follow the river, so properties near the brook are at significant risk of river flooding. [IMG] (p1)
Internal flooding occurred to properties in Lyonshall during the key event; flooding sources reported include river overflow from Curl Brook, blocked gullies/overland flows, and overflowing sewers/toilets (foul flooding). (p3)
The village experienced internal flooding to residential properties (five properties internally flooded during the key flood event). Surface water runoff affected the highway with water draining onto the road and into the village; foul sewers surcharged during storms and there are reports of foul flooding on highways. No injuries or loss of life were reported. (p4)","3 properties recorded internal flooding (depths all less than 6 inches); 2 additional properties in the parish flooded from Sour Brook (p3)
5 properties (p4)",GB-HEF_20_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_20_2020-02-16,Lyonshall flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Lyonshall,055/Herefordshire County/19/Lyonshall.pdf
GB-NNH,21,2020-08-16,52.2847067,-0.726483,"<div id=""html_99921a9a5405dec9e950a10c86ddc235"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swanspool Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows were directed toward Swanspool Brook but were restricted from entering the brook at the residential block perimeter, causing water to deepen locally and enter properties; elsewhere a strong flow from a car park discharged into Swanspool Brook. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water flowed downhill toward Swanspool Brook; low points in the catchment had no effective overland flow routes to divert excess surface water to Swanspool Brook, resulting in reliance on buried drainage systems which were overwhelmed during the event. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Swanspool Brook,England,"Flows were directed toward Swanspool Brook but were restricted from entering the brook at the residential block perimeter, causing water to deepen locally and enter properties; elsewhere a strong flow from a car park discharged into Swanspool Brook. (p17)
Excess surface water flowed downhill toward Swanspool Brook; low points in the catchment had no effective overland flow routes to divert excess surface water to Swanspool Brook, resulting in reliance on buried drainage systems which were overwhelmed during the event. (p19)",,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,21,2020-08-16,52.3009948,-0.684621,"<div id=""html_43edbdb1a392b19d52ed22a837907f69"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fields View, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location was the subject of a flood investigation by North Northamptonshire Council into a flooding incident affecting the area. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water from intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall flowed over ground to low points and entered buildings on Fields View, causing internal flooding of properties. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties on Fields View during the flood incident; part of a single event where internal flooding of more than five buildings in close proximity was experienced, triggering a formal investigation. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Very fast surface water flow moved south towards the residential block at the turning head; flow was diverted around both sides of the building toward Swanspool Brook and a strong wide flow moved through the car park of a nearby residential block. At the residential block at the south of Fields View water deepened to approximately 300mm and entered properties via doors. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (across Castle Street and Fields View) (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings in close proximity (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Fields View, Wellingborough",England,"Location was the subject of a flood investigation by North Northamptonshire Council into a flooding incident affecting the area. (p2)
Excess surface water from intense, short–duration rainfall flowed over ground to low points and entered buildings on Fields View, causing internal flooding of properties. (p6)
Internal flooding of properties on Fields View during the flood incident; part of a single event where internal flooding of more than five buildings in close proximity was experienced, triggering a formal investigation. (p8)
Very fast surface water flow moved south towards the residential block at the turning head; flow was diverted around both sides of the building toward Swanspool Brook and a strong wide flow moved through the car park of a nearby residential block. At the residential block at the south of Fields View water deepened to approximately 300mm and entered properties via doors. (p17)","more than five buildings (across Castle Street and Fields View) (p6)
more than five buildings in close proximity (p8)",GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,21,2020-08-16,52.30069,-0.6859694,"<div id=""html_d0258a3c2b779b17efaad07708ace703"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Castle Street, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water from intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall flowed over ground to low points and entered buildings on Castle Street, causing internal flooding of properties. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties on Castle Street during the flood incident; part of a single event where internal flooding of more than five buildings in close proximity was experienced, triggering a formal investigation. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Castle Street were affected by the rainfall and flooding event; public surface water sewers serving the affected residential properties are recorded with an outfall to Swanspool Brook. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Castle Street were affected by surface water/urban flooding during the documented flood incident. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water deepened at the low point of Castle Street near the school, flowed across the footway into school grounds, and strong flows from an adjacent car park and the Park View car park flowed toward the front of two residential properties at the lowest point, deepening outside front doors and entering dwellings despite flood doors; onset was rapid so removable barriers could not be installed. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage channel installed at the frontages of properties to manage water accumulation and protect property frontages; photo shows the linear drainage channel running between the paved walkway and parking area with no visible pooling or blockage at the time of imaging. [IMG] (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (across Castle Street and Fields View) (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings in close proximity (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 residential properties at the lowest point (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Castle Street, Wellingborough",England,"Excess surface water from intense, short–duration rainfall flowed over ground to low points and entered buildings on Castle Street, causing internal flooding of properties. (p6)
Internal flooding of properties on Castle Street during the flood incident; part of a single event where internal flooding of more than five buildings in close proximity was experienced, triggering a formal investigation. (p8)
Residential properties on Castle Street were affected by the rainfall and flooding event; public surface water sewers serving the affected residential properties are recorded with an outfall to Swanspool Brook. (p14)
Residential properties on Castle Street were affected by surface water/urban flooding during the documented flood incident. [PAG] (p15)
Surface water deepened at the low point of Castle Street near the school, flowed across the footway into school grounds, and strong flows from an adjacent car park and the Park View car park flowed toward the front of two residential properties at the lowest point, deepening outside front doors and entering dwellings despite flood doors; onset was rapid so removable barriers could not be installed. (p17)
Drainage channel installed at the frontages of properties to manage water accumulation and protect property frontages; photo shows the linear drainage channel running between the paved walkway and parking area with no visible pooling or blockage at the time of imaging. [IMG] (p36)","more than five buildings (across Castle Street and Fields View) (p6)
more than five buildings in close proximity (p8)
2 residential properties at the lowest point (p17)",GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,21,2020-08-16,52.30136,-0.6833182,"<div id=""html_841ef653ad0904616280e9c6e7a77c37"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Midland Road, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Strong surface water flows moved south along Midland Road onto Castle Street and Fields View after drainage systems were exceeded; the road acted as a conduit increasing flow velocity and reducing the effectiveness of drainage. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Midland Road, Wellingborough",England,Strong surface water flows moved south along Midland Road onto Castle Street and Fields View after drainage systems were exceeded; the road acted as a conduit increasing flow velocity and reducing the effectiveness of drainage. (p17),,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,21,2020-08-16,52.4175503,-0.6431175,"<div id=""html_e7c38f0b24b3bd5c79c961e84b2a00fc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in North Northamptonshire were affected by flooding. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,North Northamptonshire,England,Properties in North Northamptonshire were affected by flooding. (p26),,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,21,2020-08-16,52.30189,-0.6937309,"<div id=""html_b79d320abbbad2c16d4912ffb8321a5a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Wellingborough (town centre area) experienced flooding during the rainfall and flooding event; public foul and surface water sewers exist throughout the town centre and overland surface water flow from exceedance higher in the catchment is described as a mechanism affecting the area. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Various areas of Wellingborough were affected by flooding; Fire and Rescue received emergency calls from affected areas and attended properties and external areas where relatively deep flood water was held and was pumped to safe areas. Follow&ndash;up teams cleared debris, street surfaces and delivered sandbags to some properties. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced surface water flooding caused by intense short&ndash;duration rainfall. Surface water drainage systems across the catchment were unable to convey the rain; cumulative flows lower in the catchment were deep and fast&ndash;moving, occupying the full width of roads and footways. Surface water flowed to low points that have no effective overland relief, with reliance on buried drainage systems; some localised drainage points required maintenance. Properties in the area were recorded as being affected and details of affected properties were shared with the LLFA. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wellingborough is identified as part of a community resilience project because communities there are at risk of surface water flooding; the risk/flood incident prompted recommendations for drainage inspection, maintenance and community preparedness measures. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Wellingborough,England,"Parts of Wellingborough (town centre area) experienced flooding during the rainfall and flooding event; public foul and surface water sewers exist throughout the town centre and overland surface water flow from exceedance higher in the catchment is described as a mechanism affecting the area. (p14)
Various areas of Wellingborough were affected by flooding; Fire and Rescue received emergency calls from affected areas and attended properties and external areas where relatively deep flood water was held and was pumped to safe areas. Follow–up teams cleared debris, street surfaces and delivered sandbags to some properties. (p18)
Area experienced surface water flooding caused by intense short–duration rainfall. Surface water drainage systems across the catchment were unable to convey the rain; cumulative flows lower in the catchment were deep and fast–moving, occupying the full width of roads and footways. Surface water flowed to low points that have no effective overland relief, with reliance on buried drainage systems; some localised drainage points required maintenance. Properties in the area were recorded as being affected and details of affected properties were shared with the LLFA. (p19)
Wellingborough is identified as part of a community resilience project because communities there are at risk of surface water flooding; the risk/flood incident prompted recommendations for drainage inspection, maintenance and community preparedness measures. (p23)",,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_21_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Castle Street and Fields View, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,7,2016-03-09,52.3513573,-0.5468019,"<div id=""html_196776620b6004650f232400ca0869fb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brick Kiln Road, Raunds</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be unable to convey water. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around private property, resulting in internal flooding of a property at the Brick Kiln Road / North Street location. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property at Brick Kiln Road associated with the flood incident on 9 March 2016; the property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down the carriageway of Brick Kiln Road around the bend and onto North Street; Brick Kiln Road was recorded as flooded on the listed dates. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down the carriageway of Brick Kiln Road around the bend and onto North Street; runoff from a blocked ditch in the south verge (entering a pipe immediately north of Furnells Close) contributed significant additional surface water. During the investigation a flow of muddy brown water was observed entering road gullies on Brick Kiln Road from Enterprise Road, contributing to ponding at the low point on North Street. [PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage systems taking water away from Brick Kiln Road were not recently maintained and were unable to collect and convey rainwater on the saturated catchment. This caused drainage to overtop and excess surface water to flow to low points around properties, producing ponding in the carriageway at low points. Fully surcharged main drainage systems caused nearby domestic drainage to back up, which could lead to internal flooding of properties. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recent and ongoing developments on Brick Kiln Road were reviewed because their flood risk assessments and drainage designs may be ineffective, potentially contributing to surface water drainage problems and flood risk to nearby properties; ENC was advised to review and contact developers. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ditch and pipe systems on Brick Kiln Road (marked Q1) were involved in the incident plan. Overland surface&ndash;water flow routes and the schematic flooded area are shown concentrated along the main road, indicating flooding affected the road corridor and adjacent residential frontage during the incident. [IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered a significant portion of the roadway, leaving standing water and debris that made the road hazardous or impassable for vehicles and pedestrians. [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the full width of the roadway and partially submerged pavements and roadside areas, rendering the road impassable to most vehicles. The water encroached onto the frontage of adjacent properties and a parked vehicle was partially surrounded by water. [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the entire road surface and adjacent verge, obstructing transport access and inundating roadside infrastructure. A red post box was partially submerged and water reached the base of a streetlamp; floodwater extended into the surrounding area near fences and nearby properties. [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the entire road surface, reaching up to sidewalks and property boundaries and partially submerging houses and vegetation lining the street; the road was rendered impassable to vehicles and pedestrians and people were present conducting response or assessment activities. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the North Street/Brick Kiln Road area inundated the road surface, causing deep standing water that impeded vehicles (evidenced by a bus driving through high water), creating hazardous conditions and likely disrupting traffic on Brick Kiln Road. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Waterlogged field drainage north of Brick Kiln Road with a narrow flooded path and standing water; and muddy, partially flooded ground at the east end/bend where Brick Kiln Road meets North Street, showing localised surface&ndash;water flooding and poor drainage. [IMG,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Adjacent field drainage north of Brick Kiln Road was waterlogged with muddy standing water near the road and properties; surface water was present at the east end where the road meets North Street, contributing to poor drainage and saturated ground. [IMG,PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the east end/bend covered the road surface, impeding vehicle passage. Public transport was affected (a bus was observed approaching through the flooded area) and a car was parked off the road to avoid the water. Field drainage immediately north of Brick Kiln Road was carrying cloudy, sediment&ndash;laden water and vegetation was encroaching on the channel, potentially reducing drainage efficiency. [IMG,PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water on the carriageway/adjacent verge at the east end of Brick Kiln Road associated with the 9 March 2016 flood; nearby field drainage north of the road was carrying pale, sediment&ndash;laden runoff indicating overland flow into drainage channels. [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water accumulated and flowed along Brick Kiln Road, channelled along the road edge into a road gully; water flowed on and off the south&ndash;side footway at the access to a development, and muddy runoff collected in a ditch in the south verge, indicating localized surface&ndash;water flooding and drainage overload. [IMG,PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowing on and off the south&ndash;side footway at the access to a development; pooling and muddy conditions in the carriageway and verge (south verge ditch), impairing pedestrian access and creating a road hazard. Runoff was fed by water flowing from Enterprise Road. [IMG,PAG] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed on and off the footway on the south side at the access to a development. Surface water was reported flowing from Enterprise Road onto Brick Kiln Road. Debris in the ditch in the south verge (immediately north of Furnells Close) was restricting flow, and standing water was present at a brick culvert/ditch at the development access. [IMG,PAG] (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris in the ditch on the south verge restricted flow. Two inlets (a smaller inlet and a second, larger inlet) convey runoff from Brick Kiln Road and fields to the north into a watercourse east of Brick Kiln Road; overgrown vegetation and a partially broken concrete inlet contained water and mud, impeding conveyance into the channel and restricting drainage at the road edge. [IMG,PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Muddy surface water was present on the carriageway; roadside ditches and headwalls contained pooled water and vegetation encroachment that reduced channel capacity and contributed to road surface water. [IMG,PAG] (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked road gully on the south side at the junction with Enterprise Road leading to muddy surface water on the road and pavement; accumulation/flow of mud and surface water across the carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (internally flooded) (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Brick Kiln Road, Raunds",England,"Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be unable to convey water. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around private property, resulting in internal flooding of a property at the Brick Kiln Road / North Street location. (p7)
Internal flooding of a property at Brick Kiln Road associated with the flood incident on 9 March 2016; the property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p9)
Surface water flowed down the carriageway of Brick Kiln Road around the bend and onto North Street; Brick Kiln Road was recorded as flooded on the listed dates. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
Surface water flowed down the carriageway of Brick Kiln Road around the bend and onto North Street; runoff from a blocked ditch in the south verge (entering a pipe immediately north of Furnells Close) contributed significant additional surface water. During the investigation a flow of muddy brown water was observed entering road gullies on Brick Kiln Road from Enterprise Road, contributing to ponding at the low point on North Street. [PAG,MUL] (p12)
Drainage systems taking water away from Brick Kiln Road were not recently maintained and were unable to collect and convey rainwater on the saturated catchment. This caused drainage to overtop and excess surface water to flow to low points around properties, producing ponding in the carriageway at low points. Fully surcharged main drainage systems caused nearby domestic drainage to back up, which could lead to internal flooding of properties. (p13)
Recent and ongoing developments on Brick Kiln Road were reviewed because their flood risk assessments and drainage designs may be ineffective, potentially contributing to surface water drainage problems and flood risk to nearby properties; ENC was advised to review and contact developers. (p22)
Ditch and pipe systems on Brick Kiln Road (marked Q1) were involved in the incident plan. Overland surface–water flow routes and the schematic flooded area are shown concentrated along the main road, indicating flooding affected the road corridor and adjacent residential frontage during the incident. [IMG] (p30)
Floodwater covered a significant portion of the roadway, leaving standing water and debris that made the road hazardous or impassable for vehicles and pedestrians. [IMG,PAG] (p40)
Floodwater covered the full width of the roadway and partially submerged pavements and roadside areas, rendering the road impassable to most vehicles. The water encroached onto the frontage of adjacent properties and a parked vehicle was partially surrounded by water. [IMG,PAG] (p41)
Floodwater covered the entire road surface and adjacent verge, obstructing transport access and inundating roadside infrastructure. A red post box was partially submerged and water reached the base of a streetlamp; floodwater extended into the surrounding area near fences and nearby properties. [IMG,PAG] (p42)
Floodwater covered the entire road surface, reaching up to sidewalks and property boundaries and partially submerging houses and vegetation lining the street; the road was rendered impassable to vehicles and pedestrians and people were present conducting response or assessment activities. [IMG,PAG] (p43)
Flooding in the North Street/Brick Kiln Road area inundated the road surface, causing deep standing water that impeded vehicles (evidenced by a bus driving through high water), creating hazardous conditions and likely disrupting traffic on Brick Kiln Road. [IMG,PAG] (p45)
Waterlogged field drainage north of Brick Kiln Road with a narrow flooded path and standing water; and muddy, partially flooded ground at the east end/bend where Brick Kiln Road meets North Street, showing localised surface–water flooding and poor drainage. [IMG,PAG] (p46)
Adjacent field drainage north of Brick Kiln Road was waterlogged with muddy standing water near the road and properties; surface water was present at the east end where the road meets North Street, contributing to poor drainage and saturated ground. [IMG,PAG] (p47)
Flooding at the east end/bend covered the road surface, impeding vehicle passage. Public transport was affected (a bus was observed approaching through the flooded area) and a car was parked off the road to avoid the water. Field drainage immediately north of Brick Kiln Road was carrying cloudy, sediment–laden water and vegetation was encroaching on the channel, potentially reducing drainage efficiency. [IMG,PAG] (p48)
Surface water on the carriageway/adjacent verge at the east end of Brick Kiln Road associated with the 9 March 2016 flood; nearby field drainage north of the road was carrying pale, sediment–laden runoff indicating overland flow into drainage channels. [IMG,PAG] (p49)
Surface water accumulated and flowed along Brick Kiln Road, channelled along the road edge into a road gully; water flowed on and off the south–side footway at the access to a development, and muddy runoff collected in a ditch in the south verge, indicating localized surface–water flooding and drainage overload. [IMG,PAG] (p57)
Surface water runoff flowing on and off the south–side footway at the access to a development; pooling and muddy conditions in the carriageway and verge (south verge ditch), impairing pedestrian access and creating a road hazard. Runoff was fed by water flowing from Enterprise Road. [IMG,PAG] (p58)
Surface water flowed on and off the footway on the south side at the access to a development. Surface water was reported flowing from Enterprise Road onto Brick Kiln Road. Debris in the ditch in the south verge (immediately north of Furnells Close) was restricting flow, and standing water was present at a brick culvert/ditch at the development access. [IMG,PAG] (p59)
Debris in the ditch on the south verge restricted flow. Two inlets (a smaller inlet and a second, larger inlet) convey runoff from Brick Kiln Road and fields to the north into a watercourse east of Brick Kiln Road; overgrown vegetation and a partially broken concrete inlet contained water and mud, impeding conveyance into the channel and restricting drainage at the road edge. [IMG,PAG] (p61)
Muddy surface water was present on the carriageway; roadside ditches and headwalls contained pooled water and vegetation encroachment that reduced channel capacity and contributed to road surface water. [IMG,PAG] (p68)
Blocked road gully on the south side at the junction with Enterprise Road leading to muddy surface water on the road and pavement; accumulation/flow of mud and surface water across the carriageway. [IMG,PAG] (p69)","one property (internally flooded) (p7)
one property (p9)",GB-NNH_7_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_7_2016-03-09,Raunds flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Brick Kiln Road/North Street, Raunds, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22520/FIR-Raunds/Rev03/190916.pdf
GB-NNH,7,2016-03-09,52.34903,-0.5332655,"<div id=""html_dea72f22beb917242b5d8bf4b5779363"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Street, Raunds</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be unable to convey water. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around private property at the Brick Kiln Road / North Street location, resulting in internal flooding of a property. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named as part of the location of the flood incident on 9 March 2016; associated internal flooding at the Brick Kiln Road/North Street location (one property internally flooded, recurring incidents reported). (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a property on North Street. The internally flooded property has domestic drainage systems which discharge to main drainage in North Street. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential property on North Street experienced internal flooding (water entering properties). Surface water flowed from Brick Kiln Road onto North Street and developed into a pool at the low point on North Street; internal flooding damaged carpets on at least one occasion. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water collected at the low point on North Street forming a pool. The pool reached within 50mm of entering one property. Domestic drainage for a second property on North Street could not discharge to the main drainage, causing rainwater from the roof to back up and flood into that house (internal flooding). The Fire Service pumped water from the low point and the garden of the internally flooded property into the Main River (Hog Dyke). [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flooding that occurred in Brick Kiln Road/North Street was caused by heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment; drainage systems were unable to convey rainwater, leading to excess surface water flowing over ground to low points around private properties. Drainage at low points was fully surcharged, causing ponding in the carriageway and backups into domestic drainage systems with potential internal property flooding. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the roadway and extended up to property boundaries, encroaching on frontages and making the street impassable. [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Deep floodwater in the North Street/Brick Kiln Road area that partially submerged vehicle wheels and produced large waves as vehicles passed, creating hazardous driving conditions and likely disrupting local traffic. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Muddy, waterlogged ground with standing surface water at the bend at the top of North Street (the east end junction with Brick Kiln Road), indicating poor drainage and localised flooding that obstructs the area. [IMG,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and standing water accumulated on the road at the bend (top of North Street / east end of Brick Kiln Road), leaving the carriageway wet and muddy and creating hazardous driving/walking conditions. [IMG,PAG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the bend at the top of North Street (where it meets the east end of Brick Kiln Road) covered the carriageway and impeded vehicle movement; a bus was observed using/approaching the flooded road and a car was parked off the verge to avoid the water. [IMG,PAG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wet road surface and evidence of surface water at the bend at the top of North Street during the 9 March 2016 flood incident, showing surface water accumulation on the carriageway and adjacent grass verge. [IMG,PAG] (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road completely covered with floodwater that encroached onto sidewalks and front gardens; water entered residential properties causing internal flooding despite sandbags being in place, with household items and floors submerged and access along the street impeded. [IMG,MUL] (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During heavy rainfall the carriageway became wet and reflective with surface water accumulating and pooling at a low point beside the kerb. Water flowed along the road surface toward a road gully at the low point; the gully was shown collecting this flow but appeared at or near capacity, indicating local drainage overwhelmed by the rainfall. [IMG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Deposits of silt at a road gully on North Street, indicating surface&ndash;water flow and gully deposition affecting the carriageway/drainage. [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Deposits of silt were recorded around a road gully on North Street, obstructing the gully and contributing to the risk of localised surface&ndash;water flooding; recent foul&ndash;water drainage works have been carried out on North Street (post&ndash;event remediation/works noted). [IMG,PAG] (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (internally flooded) (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approx 750mm deep internal flooding [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>50mm; one property internally flooded; one additional property had drainage unable to discharge [PAG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"North Street, Raunds",England,"Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment caused drainage systems to be unable to convey water. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around private property at the Brick Kiln Road / North Street location, resulting in internal flooding of a property. (p7)
Named as part of the location of the flood incident on 9 March 2016; associated internal flooding at the Brick Kiln Road/North Street location (one property internally flooded, recurring incidents reported). (p9)
Internal flooding of a property on North Street. The internally flooded property has domestic drainage systems which discharge to main drainage in North Street. (p10)
Residential property on North Street experienced internal flooding (water entering properties). Surface water flowed from Brick Kiln Road onto North Street and developed into a pool at the low point on North Street; internal flooding damaged carpets on at least one occasion. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
Surface water collected at the low point on North Street forming a pool. The pool reached within 50mm of entering one property. Domestic drainage for a second property on North Street could not discharge to the main drainage, causing rainwater from the roof to back up and flood into that house (internal flooding). The Fire Service pumped water from the low point and the garden of the internally flooded property into the Main River (Hog Dyke). [PAG] (p12)
The flooding that occurred in Brick Kiln Road/North Street was caused by heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment; drainage systems were unable to convey rainwater, leading to excess surface water flowing over ground to low points around private properties. Drainage at low points was fully surcharged, causing ponding in the carriageway and backups into domestic drainage systems with potential internal property flooding. (p13)
Floodwater covered the roadway and extended up to property boundaries, encroaching on frontages and making the street impassable. [IMG,PAG] (p44)
Deep floodwater in the North Street/Brick Kiln Road area that partially submerged vehicle wheels and produced large waves as vehicles passed, creating hazardous driving conditions and likely disrupting local traffic. [IMG,PAG] (p45)
Muddy, waterlogged ground with standing surface water at the bend at the top of North Street (the east end junction with Brick Kiln Road), indicating poor drainage and localised flooding that obstructs the area. [IMG,PAG] (p46)
Surface water and standing water accumulated on the road at the bend (top of North Street / east end of Brick Kiln Road), leaving the carriageway wet and muddy and creating hazardous driving/walking conditions. [IMG,PAG] (p47)
Flooding at the bend at the top of North Street (where it meets the east end of Brick Kiln Road) covered the carriageway and impeded vehicle movement; a bus was observed using/approaching the flooded road and a car was parked off the verge to avoid the water. [IMG,PAG] (p48)
Wet road surface and evidence of surface water at the bend at the top of North Street during the 9 March 2016 flood incident, showing surface water accumulation on the carriageway and adjacent grass verge. [IMG,PAG] (p49)
Road completely covered with floodwater that encroached onto sidewalks and front gardens; water entered residential properties causing internal flooding despite sandbags being in place, with household items and floors submerged and access along the street impeded. [IMG,MUL] (p51)
During heavy rainfall the carriageway became wet and reflective with surface water accumulating and pooling at a low point beside the kerb. Water flowed along the road surface toward a road gully at the low point; the gully was shown collecting this flow but appeared at or near capacity, indicating local drainage overwhelmed by the rainfall. [IMG] (p56)
Deposits of silt at a road gully on North Street, indicating surface–water flow and gully deposition affecting the carriageway/drainage. [IMG,PAG] (p69)
Deposits of silt were recorded around a road gully on North Street, obstructing the gully and contributing to the risk of localised surface–water flooding; recent foul–water drainage works have been carried out on North Street (post–event remediation/works noted). [IMG,PAG] (p70)","one property (internally flooded) (p7)
one property (p9)
