GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.514955,-0.2029265,"<div id=""html_26401c7ec4a0fe7575da4ee69180944b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Colville Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The school was flooded internally by sewage/overland flows. [TAB] (p81)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 building (Colville Primary School) flooded internally [TAB] (p81)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,Colville Primary School,England,The school was flooded internally by sewage/overland flows. [TAB] (p81),1 building (Colville Primary School) flooded internally [TAB] (p81),GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5014375,-0.1942639299957558,"<div id=""html_de0cb57b5109004afe829621fd1f9f3b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kensington Central Library</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The library was flooded by sewage/ floodwater and was closed for several days. [TAB] (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The library was flooded and closed for several days following the intense rainfall and sewer surcharge. (p83)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 building [TAB] (p81)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,Kensington Central Library,England,"The library was flooded by sewage/ floodwater and was closed for several days. [TAB] (p81)
The library was flooded and closed for several days following the intense rainfall and sewer surcharge. (p83)",1 building [TAB] (p81),GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5142494,-0.2116908,"<div id=""html_9600a5cedd31bce94098aa573886a1ee"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Camelford Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Subject to overland flow and surface flooding caused by intense rainfall and sewer surcharge; formed part of a network of streets where flows synchronised and roads channelled water, increasing local flood depths. (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding reported at this location on 12th July 2021. Basement properties were affected by overland flows that collected at basement level and by sewer water backing up through toilets, sinks and drains into basements. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At least one property was flooded; the street is in an area of high hydrological connectivity (increasing susceptibility to surface water flooding). [TAB] (p75)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 1 property [TAB] (p75)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,Camelford Court,England,"Subject to overland flow and surface flooding caused by intense rainfall and sewer surcharge; formed part of a network of streets where flows synchronised and roads channelled water, increasing local flood depths. (p61)
Flooding reported at this location on 12th July 2021. Basement properties were affected by overland flows that collected at basement level and by sewer water backing up through toilets, sinks and drains into basements. [TAB] (p65)
At least one property was flooded; the street is in an area of high hydrological connectivity (increasing susceptibility to surface water flooding). [TAB] (p75)",at least 1 property [TAB] (p75),GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5073219,-0.1276474,"<div id=""html_3b23a851de61d853e1b8aaf21ade6d12"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>London</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and sewer flooding affected properties and local infrastructure across London boroughs, prompting multi&ndash;agency response and follow&ndash;up actions (reviews of Critical Drainage Areas, updates to the Multi&ndash;Agency Flood Plan, consideration of property&ndash;level resilience measures, gully cleaning frequency and sharing of sewer forecasts). [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 that led to surface&ndash;water/urban flooding in parts of the city (first reports of property flooding around 15:00, majority around 17:00). Emergency services and utilities were heavily engaged &mdash; London Fire Brigade took &gt;1,000 calls and Thames Water received nearly 4,000 calls (and ~7,000 online contacts) as customers sought help during the flooding. [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A pump at Hammersmith Pumping Station failed and remained offline throughout the event, reducing the station's maximum capacity by 3.2 m3/s, affecting pumping capacity during the flood. (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 caused surface water flooding and synchronized overland flows in dense urban streets; roads acted as channels conveying floodwater across Notting Hill and northern/central parts of the Royal Borough. Surcharged sewers produced fountains at manholes (reported at the junction of St Marks Road and Lancaster Road, and at Cornwall Crescent). Floodwater collected in basements and internal parts of properties, damaging interiors and business stock; Sloane Square Station experienced internal flooding. Heatmapping of flooded properties shows the highest concentrations in northern and central RBKC (Notting Hill area). [PAG,IMG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of London, including Kensington and Chelsea, experienced intense surface&ndash;water/flash flooding on 12 July 2021 that led to streets and properties being internally flooded and businesses damaged. (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 12 July 2021 numerous flooding incidents occurred across the city. London Fire Brigade and Thames Water deployed teams city&ndash;wide; London Fire Brigade declared a major incident and worked through the night to remove floodwater and provide resources such as pumps and sandbags. Emergency services were heavily tasked and resources were stretched across multiple boroughs. (p105)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>first flooding reports around 15:00; majority of reports around 17:00; more than 1,000 calls to London Fire Brigade; nearly 4,000 calls to Thames Water; nearly 7,000 online contacts to Thames Water [PAG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,London,England,"Surface water and sewer flooding affected properties and local infrastructure across London boroughs, prompting multi–agency response and follow–up actions (reviews of Critical Drainage Areas, updates to the Multi–Agency Flood Plan, consideration of property–level resilience measures, gully cleaning frequency and sharing of sewer forecasts). [TAB] (p7)
Experienced intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 that led to surface–water/urban flooding in parts of the city (first reports of property flooding around 15:00, majority around 17:00). Emergency services and utilities were heavily engaged — London Fire Brigade took >1,000 calls and Thames Water received nearly 4,000 calls (and ~7,000 online contacts) as customers sought help during the flooding. [PAG] (p56)
A pump at Hammersmith Pumping Station failed and remained offline throughout the event, reducing the station's maximum capacity by 3.2 m3/s, affecting pumping capacity during the flood. (p57)
Intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 caused surface water flooding and synchronized overland flows in dense urban streets; roads acted as channels conveying floodwater across Notting Hill and northern/central parts of the Royal Borough. Surcharged sewers produced fountains at manholes (reported at the junction of St Marks Road and Lancaster Road, and at Cornwall Crescent). Floodwater collected in basements and internal parts of properties, damaging interiors and business stock; Sloane Square Station experienced internal flooding. Heatmapping of flooded properties shows the highest concentrations in northern and central RBKC (Notting Hill area). [PAG,IMG] (p63)
Parts of London, including Kensington and Chelsea, experienced intense surface–water/flash flooding on 12 July 2021 that led to streets and properties being internally flooded and businesses damaged. (p73)
On 12 July 2021 numerous flooding incidents occurred across the city. London Fire Brigade and Thames Water deployed teams city–wide; London Fire Brigade declared a major incident and worked through the night to remove floodwater and provide resources such as pumps and sandbags. Emergency services were heavily tasked and resources were stretched across multiple boroughs. (p105)","first flooding reports around 15:00; majority of reports around 17:00; more than 1,000 calls to London Fire Brigade; nearly 4,000 calls to Thames Water; nearly 7,000 online contacts to Thames Water [PAG] (p56)",GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.478942,-0.1797153,"<div id=""html_a258da64c612ee2c09eaa5201c885e7c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lots Road Pumping Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Thames Water switched on pumps at the Lots Road pumping station to manage floodwater: three of seven pumps were switched on at 16:45 and the remaining pumps were running by 17:00; pumps operated to remove floodwater during the event. [TAB] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Delay in switching on pumps limited the only available discharge route while gravity outfalls were tide&ndash;locked, which significantly contributed to sewer surcharge and surface flooding across the borough. (p60)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,Lots Road Pumping Station,England,"Thames Water switched on pumps at the Lots Road pumping station to manage floodwater: three of seven pumps were switched on at 16:45 and the remaining pumps were running by 17:00; pumps operated to remove floodwater during the event. [TAB] (p57)
Delay in switching on pumps limited the only available discharge route while gravity outfalls were tide–locked, which significantly contributed to sewer surcharge and surface flooding across the borough. (p60)",,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5575525,-0.781404,"<div id=""html_acd42eba31be8da2803aafa30fcd0790"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Thames</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal conditions on the River Thames (Chelsea tide gauge) produced a high tide of 3.80 mAOD at 15:45 on 12 July 2021 which coincided with the intense rainfall that afternoon, acting as a compounding factor for urban/surface&ndash;water flood impacts in riverside and low&ndash;lying areas. (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A high tide at 15:45 on 12 July 2021 (3.80 mAOD at the Chelsea gauge) impeded gravity drainage from CSOs into the Thames, acting as a significant contributory factor that reduced outflow capacity and exacerbated sewer and surface water flooding across the borough. (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tide at the Chelsea gauge coincided with the intense rainfall on 12 July 2021; elevated tide levels likely impeded sewer drainage and exacerbated surface water and sewer flooding in RBKC. (p105)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,River Thames,England,"Tidal conditions on the River Thames (Chelsea tide gauge) produced a high tide of 3.80 mAOD at 15:45 on 12 July 2021 which coincided with the intense rainfall that afternoon, acting as a compounding factor for urban/surface–water flood impacts in riverside and low–lying areas. (p55)
A high tide at 15:45 on 12 July 2021 (3.80 mAOD at the Chelsea gauge) impeded gravity drainage from CSOs into the Thames, acting as a significant contributory factor that reduced outflow capacity and exacerbated sewer and surface water flooding across the borough. (p59)
High tide at the Chelsea gauge coincided with the intense rainfall on 12 July 2021; elevated tide levels likely impeded sewer drainage and exacerbated surface water and sewer flooding in RBKC. (p105)",,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5152296,-0.2124632,"<div id=""html_cce30e6dbf959ca1b9150dd9542730e2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Marks Close, Lancaster West Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding on 12 July 2021 with continuing impacts on residents observed during site visits. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Marks Close was reported flooded. Basement properties were affected by overland flows pooling at basement level and by sewer flooding that entered basements through internal drains and sanitary fittings. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ground&ndash;floor flooding at St Marks Care Leavers Centre; area has high hydrological connectivity causing fast flow between interlinked side&ndash;streets. [TAB] (p86)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties flooded internally; 7 care leavers relocated to temporary accommodation [TAB] (p86)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"St Marks Close, Lancaster West Estate",England,"Experienced flooding on 12 July 2021 with continuing impacts on residents observed during site visits. (p19)
St Marks Close was reported flooded. Basement properties were affected by overland flows pooling at basement level and by sewer flooding that entered basements through internal drains and sanitary fittings. [TAB] (p65)
Ground–floor flooding at St Marks Care Leavers Centre; area has high hydrological connectivity causing fast flow between interlinked side–streets. [TAB] (p86)",9 properties flooded internally; 7 care leavers relocated to temporary accommodation [TAB] (p86),GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.516808,-0.2098086,"<div id=""html_60fb05b8a937dace2a3a465004ada0b2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancaster Road, Lancaster West Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on 12 July 2021, affecting the local community and noted during Council site visits. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Street flooded during the 12 July 2021 event (surface water/urban flooding). Listed in RBKC gully cleaning programme as cleaned twice a year. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At the junction with St Marks Road, sewers surcharged and produced fountain&ndash;like discharges at manholes, ejecting water onto the carriageway and causing localized flooding and overland flow. [PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater entered properties through drains, toilets and sinks, causing internal flooding of properties/basement flats. [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding reported on Lancaster Road during the 12th July 2021 event: surface water inundation of the street and sewer backup through property drains, affecting the carriageway and adjacent properties (including potential basement inundation and internal drainage/backflow). [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharged drains and sewers produced a c.1 m high fountain at the intersection with St Marks Road, preventing vehicle movement; floodwater from streets and gardens then entered properties and basements. [TAB] (p79)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharged drains and sewers caused street flooding; a 1 m high fountain of water formed at the intersection with St Marks Road preventing vehicles from passing. Floodwater from streets and gardens entered properties; sewage flowed along the street (east to west). (p80)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 properties [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [TAB] (p79)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Lancaster Road, Lancaster West Estate",England,"Flooding occurred on 12 July 2021, affecting the local community and noted during Council site visits. (p19)
