GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5131568,-0.2138962,"<div id=""html_53db8faaa2b70f85641dc24fc21f3cca"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bomore Road, Lancaster West Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Impacted by flooding on 12 July 2021 with continuing effects on residents reported during site visits. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Bomore Road, Lancaster West Estate",England,Impacted by flooding on 12 July 2021 with continuing effects on residents reported during site visits. (p19),,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5142954,-0.2134153,"<div id=""html_00d6ea9d0e917c307900a63e75f9ffdc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Verity Close, Lancaster West Estate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on 12 July 2021 affected residents and community, as reported during Council site visits to worst affected areas. (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Verity Close, Lancaster West Estate",England,"Flooding on 12 July 2021 affected residents and community, as reported during Council site visits to worst affected areas. (p19)",,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5000757,-0.2099184,"<div id=""html_574d27571208e57676580e6ab2573226"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holland Villas Road, Kensington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the worst&ndash;affected streets during site visits; residents on Holland Villas Road reported flooding on 12 July 2021 with impacts to properties and ongoing community disruption. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded as flooded during the intense rainfall event on 12th July 2021; surface water inundation affected the street. [TAB] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported as part of the connected street network (extending from Royal Crescent) that experienced surface water flooding and sewer surcharging; acted as a channel for floodwater, contributing to synchronisation of flows and elevated water levels. (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Holland Villas Road experienced internal flooding due to sewer capacity being exceeded and backflow through property drains, allowing water to enter properties (including basement&ndash;level flats) via toilets, sinks and drains. [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooded on 12 July 2021; floodwater affected the street and was associated with overland flow accumulation at basement level and sewer backups entering properties. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement properties flooded by overland flows and by sewer surcharging; sewage rose from toilets and drains into basements, contaminating and staining walls and flooring which required replacement. [TAB] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater collecting in basements and ground&ndash;floor properties due to overland flows from surcharged sewers and overflowing drains; sewer flooding also reported coming up from internal drains/toilets, causing interior damage. (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers surcharged and manholes came up, causing heavy inundation; large volumes collected in basements and multiple properties were flooded internally, in some cases by more than a metre. Addisland Court was particularly badly affected with basements and ground floor inundated, car park flooded, building pumps overwhelmed and electrical/boiler services damaged, rendering flats uninhabitable and forcing temporary rehousing. [TAB] (p79)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drains and sewers were overloaded and (in one case) inadequate roof drainage led to very deep internal flooding of properties, including basement flooding. (p80)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded as having one of the largest maximum internal flood depths; inferred to have experienced internal flooding associated with sewer overflow events in the area. [TAB] (p85)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>13 properties [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>13 properties flooded; average internal depth 105 cm; maximum internal depth 300 cm; Addisland Court flood depths up to 3 m [TAB] (p79)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>maximum internal depth 100 cm; average internal depth 80 cm (p80)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Holland Villas Road, Kensington",England,"Identified as one of the worst–affected streets during site visits; residents on Holland Villas Road reported flooding on 12 July 2021 with impacts to properties and ongoing community disruption. (p19)
Recorded as flooded during the intense rainfall event on 12th July 2021; surface water inundation affected the street. [TAB] (p38)
Reported as part of the connected street network (extending from Royal Crescent) that experienced surface water flooding and sewer surcharging; acted as a channel for floodwater, contributing to synchronisation of flows and elevated water levels. (p61)
Properties on Holland Villas Road experienced internal flooding due to sewer capacity being exceeded and backflow through property drains, allowing water to enter properties (including basement–level flats) via toilets, sinks and drains. [TAB] (p64)
Reported flooded on 12 July 2021; floodwater affected the street and was associated with overland flow accumulation at basement level and sewer backups entering properties. [TAB] (p65)
Basement properties flooded by overland flows and by sewer surcharging; sewage rose from toilets and drains into basements, contaminating and staining walls and flooring which required replacement. [TAB] (p66)
Floodwater collecting in basements and ground–floor properties due to overland flows from surcharged sewers and overflowing drains; sewer flooding also reported coming up from internal drains/toilets, causing interior damage. (p68)
Sewers surcharged and manholes came up, causing heavy inundation; large volumes collected in basements and multiple properties were flooded internally, in some cases by more than a metre. Addisland Court was particularly badly affected with basements and ground floor inundated, car park flooded, building pumps overwhelmed and electrical/boiler services damaged, rendering flats uninhabitable and forcing temporary rehousing. [TAB] (p79)
Drains and sewers were overloaded and (in one case) inadequate roof drainage led to very deep internal flooding of properties, including basement flooding. (p80)
Recorded as having one of the largest maximum internal flood depths; inferred to have experienced internal flooding associated with sewer overflow events in the area. [TAB] (p85)","13 properties [TAB] (p64)
13 properties flooded; average internal depth 105 cm; maximum internal depth 300 cm; Addisland Court flood depths up to 3 m [TAB] (p79)
maximum internal depth 100 cm; average internal depth 80 cm (p80)",GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5047886,-0.2112234,"<div id=""html_fcc0bc7645174657f13df5f9bbfc6fc3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holland Park Gardens, Notting Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded during site visits as a worst&ndash;affected street where residents experienced flooding on 12 July 2021, with consequent impacts to homes and the local community. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Street was flooded during the 12 July 2021 event. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties recorded as flooded on Holland Park Gardens in the Thames Water database and RBKC questionnaire. [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported on the street during the 12 July 2021 event. Floodwater inundated the street and was associated with basement flooding where overland flows collected at basement level and where sewer backups forced water up through toilets, sinks and drains into properties. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water came up from overflowing drains and manholes and through external lightwell drains; floodwater originated at the junction with Holland Park Avenue and flowed into properties, primarily basements. [TAB] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater originated at the junction of Holland Park Gardens and Holland Park Avenue and entered properties via overflowing drains and manholes and through external lightwell drains; primarily basement flooding. (p78)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [TAB] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties flooded internally (across affected properties in this report); average internal depth 30 cm; maximum internal depth 30 cm (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Holland Park Gardens, Notting Hill",England,"Recorded during site visits as a worst–affected street where residents experienced flooding on 12 July 2021, with consequent impacts to homes and the local community. (p19)
