GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5070506,-0.2060285,"<div id=""html_10a57e46768e08a9e7a73880e885d35e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holland Park Avenue, Notting Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Visited by council officers as part of the worst&ndash;affected area; residents reported flooding on 12 July 2021 affecting properties and community life. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Street was flooded during the 12 July 2021 event. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding (pluvial and sewer&ndash;related) occurred on the avenue, shown in images of flooding at street level. (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12 July 2021 event the avenue was identified within the linked streets where intense rainfall and sewers surcharging produced overland flows. The road acted as a conveyance channel, increasing local water levels as flows from adjacent streets combined and moved through the area. (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties recorded as flooded on Holland Park Avenue in the Thames Water database and RBKC questionnaire. [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported on 12th July 2021. Basement properties were flooded by overland flows pooling at basement level and by sewer backup entering properties via toilets, sinks and drains. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement properties flooded by overland flows and by sewer surcharging; sewage rose from toilets and drains into basements, contaminating and staining walls and flooring which required replacement. [TAB] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer flooding/backflow entered properties via shower drains and toilets, producing internal flooding, unsanitary contamination and damage to bathroom areas and fittings. [PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer flooding rose through shower drains and toilets and caused internal flooding and property damage (Figure 9&ndash;7 top left). Flooding included sewage contamination that stained walls/flooring and required replacement, posing health risks. [PAG] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The street experienced sewer surcharging and surface water collecting downhill; residents experienced sewer flooding coming up from basement toilets, roof/ceiling water ingress, and water entering properties. [TAB] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drains overflowed as sewers surcharged, causing floodwater to flow along the street into properties. Reports include sewage rising from toilets in basement flats, water entering from roofs/ceilings in at least one case, and Holland Park Station being flooded; the street&#8217;s downhill position caused water to collect. (p78)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>21 properties [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one of 76 streets reported flooded [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>21 properties [TAB] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties flooded internally (across affected properties in this report); average internal depth 30 cm; maximum internal depth 30 cm (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Holland Park Avenue, Notting Hill",England,"Visited by council officers as part of the worst–affected area; residents reported flooding on 12 July 2021 affecting properties and community life. (p19)
Street was flooded during the 12 July 2021 event. [TAB] (p37)
