Sewer flooding/backflow entered properties via shower drains and toilets, producing internal flooding, unsanitary contamination and damage to bathroom areas and fittings. [PAG] (p69)
Sewer flooding rose through shower drains and toilets and caused internal flooding and property damage (Figure 9–7 top left). Flooding included sewage contamination that stained walls/flooring and required replacement, posing health risks. [PAG] (p70)
The street experienced sewer surcharging and surface water collecting downhill; residents experienced sewer flooding coming up from basement toilets, roof/ceiling water ingress, and water entering properties. [TAB] (p77)
Drains overflowed as sewers surcharged, causing floodwater to flow along the street into properties. Reports include sewage rising from toilets in basement flats, water entering from roofs/ceilings in at least one case, and Holland Park Station being flooded; the street’s downhill position caused water to collect. (p78)","21 properties [TAB] (p64)
