5 properties flooded internally (across affected properties in this report); average internal depth 30 cm; maximum internal depth 30 cm (p78)",GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5055471,-0.2128254,"<div id=""html_6efcf1e6dcdc48d3cd78856d4abf4a49"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Royal Crescent, Notting Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Included in site visits to worst&ndash;affected streets; residents at Royal Crescent reported flooding on 12 July 2021 with impacts to properties and ongoing effects on residents. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded as flooded during the intense rainfall event on 12th July 2021; surface water inundation affected the street. [TAB] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the area around Royal Crescent that experienced surface water flooding driven by intense rainfall and surcharged sewers; flows from neighbouring streets synchronised and the road network conveyed water, increasing local flood depths. (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties recorded as flooded on Royal Crescent in the Thames Water database and RBKC questionnaire. [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding reported on the street on 12 July 2021; properties (including basements) were affected by overland flows collecting at basement level and by sewer backup entering through drains, toilets and sinks. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement properties were flooded by overland flows that collected at basement level and by sewer flooding with sewage backflow from toilets and drains, causing staining and damage to walls and flooring requiring replacement of finishes. [TAB] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement and internal flooding at 4 Royal Crescent with water contaminated by sewage overflowing from manholes on the road and from toilets inside the property; basement flooding in some cases rose to the same level as the ground floor. (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced very large internal flood depths caused by overloaded drains and sewers; properties suffered internal and basement flooding. (p80)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Many properties were flooded internally: sewage backed up through toilets in basements and the majority were flooded by overflowing drains and manholes which collected in basements. One resident pumped out a very large volume; flooding persisted for about two hours before the water suddenly drained away. [TAB] (p84)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 properties [TAB] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (4 Royal Crescent) (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>maximum internal depth 100 cm; average internal depth 80 cm (p80)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 properties flooded internally; average internal depth 60 cm; maximum internal depth 300 cm; 130 m³ pumped out by one resident; internal flooding up to 3 m; flooding lasted ~2 hours for several residents [TAB] (p84)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Royal Crescent, Notting Hill",England,"Included in site visits to worst–affected streets; residents at Royal Crescent reported flooding on 12 July 2021 with impacts to properties and ongoing effects on residents. (p19)
