GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.4811502,-0.1832291,"<div id=""html_a4e52fd259e478e1869b4c02002d78c1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ashburnham Road, Kensington and Chelsea</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement properties were flooded by overland flows and by sewer flooding (sewage coming up from toilets and drains into basements at a similar level to the local sewer). Sewage caused staining to walls and flooring, requiring removal/replacement of finishes and fittings. [TAB] (p66)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Ashburnham Road, Kensington and Chelsea",England,"Basement properties were flooded by overland flows and by sewer flooding (sewage coming up from toilets and drains into basements at a similar level to the local sewer). Sewage caused staining to walls and flooring, requiring removal/replacement of finishes and fittings. [TAB] (p66)",,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.4916061,-0.2028821,"<div id=""html_87e25a915d17ffb77d794f0601c2551b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Cromwell Road, West Kensington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The vicinity of the junction with West Cromwell Road is reported to have several FLIPs; the sewers surcharged in the area causing water to flow onto the road and collect in basements near this junction. (p88)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"West Cromwell Road, West Kensington",England,The vicinity of the junction with West Cromwell Road is reported to have several FLIPs; the sewers surcharged in the area causing water to flow onto the road and collect in basements near this junction. (p88),,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5179545,-0.2181318,"<div id=""html_f7688ad19c40b33dd893af12abae5b52"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Helens Gardens, North Kensington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding reported on 12th July 2021 with significant water depths entering properties, indicating substantial internal inundation and associated damage risk. [IMG] (p90)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Average internal flood depth reported approx. 100 cm. [IMG] (p90)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"St Helens Gardens, North Kensington",England,"Internal flooding reported on 12th July 2021 with significant water depths entering properties, indicating substantial internal inundation and associated damage risk. [IMG] (p90)",Average internal flood depth reported approx. 100 cm. [IMG] (p90),GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5095154,-0.2007614,"<div id=""html_038486f51a35129983536eaa21e8a9af"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ladbroke Road, Notting Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties on Ladbroke Road with substantial inundation of internal spaces reported by residents; ground&ndash;floor areas were flooded during the 12 July 2021 event. [PAG,IMG] (p90)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>average internal flood depth 62 cm [PAG,IMG] (p90)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,"Ladbroke Road, Notting Hill",England,"Internal flooding of properties on Ladbroke Road with substantial inundation of internal spaces reported by residents; ground–floor areas were flooded during the 12 July 2021 event. [PAG,IMG] (p90)","average internal flood depth 62 cm [PAG,IMG] (p90)",GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.50379515,-0.20078938323179596,"<div id=""html_e8af9298fae2b5d77c957daf9270f573"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kensington and Chelsea</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall on 12 July 2021 caused internal flooding across the borough, resulting in flooded properties and overland flows that overwhelmed local drainage. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 12 July 2021 the borough experienced flooding that led to properties being flooded and road closures. Residents submitted photographs and reports of flooded properties which were used to document impacts. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Council officers undertook site visits to the worst&ndash;affected areas of the borough following flooding. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the intense rainfall event on 12 July 2021 the borough experienced combined sewer and surface water flooding. The combined sewer system reached capacity, causing sewers to surcharge and stormwater to overflow via manholes onto roads, with roads then conveying water downhill according to local topography. The report identifies sewer and surface water flooding as the main cause of flooding in the July 2021 event. [PAG,IMG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 12 July 2021 event, sewer and surface&ndash;water flooding occurred across the borough: combined sewers reached capacity and surcharged, causing overflows via manholes and overland flow along roads; sewer flow was pushed up into properties (including basement properties) through toilets, sinks and shower drains as sewers backed up. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 the borough experienced widespread surface water ponding and sewer surcharging. Sewers and drains reached capacity causing surcharges through manholes onto roads (roads acted as conveyance pathways) and floodwater entering properties, with basement properties particularly affected by backflow through toilets, sinks and shower drains. [PAG,IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basements of properties were flooded; reports of basement flooding in the borough following the event. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water (urban) flooding occurred during the intense rainfall event on 12 July 2021. The Tidal Thames defences were reported as operating normally during the event (no overtopping or breach of tidal defences). (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the borough experienced surface water / urban flooding during the 12 July 2021 intense rainfall event; streets within the borough were listed as having been flooded in the report. [TAB] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water / urban flooding during the event on 12 July 2021 caused inundation of multiple streets in the borough; the streets listed in Table 6&ndash;1 (Stafford Terrace, Holland Road, Queensdale Road, Warwick Road, Holland Villas Road and Royal Crescent) were flooded. [PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 12 July 2021 the borough experienced surface&ndash;water and sewer (urban) flooding: sewers surcharged, causing sewage and floodwater to back up into basements and via shower/toilet drains, manholes/fountains formed where sewers were surcharged, roads conveyed floodwater, floodwater collected in basements and internal flooding damaged business stock (including flooding inside Sloane Square station). [PAG,IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense convective rainfall on 12 July 2021 caused surface&ndash;water and sewer&ndash;related flooding across parts of the borough. The event prompted hydrological analysis (radar and gauge data) to quantify rainfall totals and understand the surface&ndash;water flooding response in the borough. [PAG,IMG] (p47)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An intense band of rain formed rapidly over central London and produced high rainfall totals over the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea between 15:00&ndash;18:00 on 12 July 2021. The heavy, concentrated rainfall caused surface&ndash;water/urban flooding in the borough and triggered the Section 19 flood investigation. [PAG,IMG] (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense convective rainfall on 12 July 2021 produced very high local rainfall totals across the borough (15:00&ndash;18:00), which led to surface water flooding across streets and public areas and caused sewer surcharge (fountains at manholes and internal sewer flooding) and internal flooding of properties and businesses. [PAG,IMG] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall on 12 July 2021 (starting around 15:00) produced surface water/urban flooding across the borough. Areas experienced highly variable return periods (southern parts <1 in 2 years; locations of heaviest rainfall in the borough up to around a 1 in 185‑year storm). A high Thames tide at Chelsea (15:45, 3.80mAOD) coincided with the heavy rainfall on 12 July. (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Interceptor and local sewers surcharged, causing flooding at street level; tide&ndash;locking impeded sewer discharge and increased manhole depths; operational failures at pumping stations contributed to borough&ndash;wide flooding. (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall and surcharged sewers produced overland flow across the borough. Roads acted as channels conveying floodwater (examples given: A3220, A40, Portobello Road). Surcharging sewers caused fountains at manholes (photographed at the junction of St Marks Road and Lancaster Road, and at Cornwall Crescent). High urban and hydrological connectivity synchronised flows across streets in the Notting Hill area and adjacent neighbourhoods&mdash;Portobello Road, Arundel Gardens, Elgin Crescent, Blenheim Crescent, Lower Clarendon Walk, St Marks Road, Camelford Court, Clarendon Road&mdash;and a wider corridor including Royal Crescent, Holland Villas Road, Bramley Road, St Anns Road, Stoneleigh Street, St Anns Villas, St James's Gardens, Norland Square, Queensdale Road, Princedale Road, Holland Park Avenue, Addison Road, Warwick Road, Napier Road, Edwardes Square, Cope Place and Scarsdale Villas, all of which contributed to and experienced surface water/sewer&ndash;driven flooding on 12 July 2021. [PAG,IMG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties recorded as flooded; floodwater entered properties via backflow through property drains into basement flats (through toilets, sinks and drains). [TAB,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding across the borough affected properties and basements: surface water and sewage&ndash;contaminated water entered properties (including basements and bathrooms), causing internal flooding, sewage contamination and damage to fixtures, finishes and fittings on multiple streets. [PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Council&ndash;owned properties were internally flooded with sewage and overland floodwater; sewage staining required replacement of walls and flooring and posed health risks. The Council incurred substantial costs responding and repairing flood&ndash;damaged properties and providing temporary accommodation. [PAG] (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, 76 streets were reported to have flooded on 12 July 2021; properties were internally flooded (receptors), business stock was damaged (e.g. Portobello Road), and three primary schools (Colville Primary, Thomas Jones Primary, Avondale Park Primary) experienced internal flooding. The borough&#8217;s records note that many flooded streets had at least one FLIP installed yet some properties still flooded. (p73)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread intense rainfall caused surface water and combined sewer systems to exceed capacity across the borough, leading to sewers surcharging, manhole overflows and multiple basement and ground&ndash;floor property inundations on several streets. [TAB] (p79)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread internal flooding across the borough during the intense rainfall on 12th July 2021. Multiple streets and properties experienced internal flooding; sewer surcharge and surface&ndash;water flow pathways contributed to internal property flooding and sewer&ndash;related issues. [PAG] (p90)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the borough on 12 July 2021: 76 streets were reported to have flooded, with internal flooding of properties (including basements), sewer surcharging (manhole fountains and sewage entering buildings) and roads acting as channels conveying floodwater. Businesses suffered internal flooding and stock damage (for example reported at Portobello Road) and Sloane Square Station experienced internal flooding. Many flooded streets had FLIPs installed but some of those properties still experienced internal flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced intense surface&ndash;water/urban flooding on 12 July 2021 that triggered the Section 19 investigation and ongoing engagement with Thames Water over sewer and asset performance; RBKC has been actively engaged with Thames Water's independent review on behalf of residents. (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water / urban flooding on 12 July 2021 produced very short warning lead times for the borough, affected properties and local emergency decision&ndash;making, and led to recommendations that the borough seeks funding for Property Flood Resilience (PFR) schemes, develops local surface&ndash;water forecasting, and coordinates with other Risk Management Authorities. [PAG] (p95)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough collected reports of internal property flooding following the 12 July 2021 event and ran the Section 19 investigation questionnaire; over 300 responses were submitted to the RBKC questionnaire. Initial reports of flooding within the borough were received by local teams during the event. (p96)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As the local authority, RBKC was required to update and consolidate three existing Multi&ndash;Agency Flood Plans into a single Multi&ndash;Agency Flood Plan, to review it annually and after events, and to introduce a training and exercising framework. RBKC is also tasked with supporting London&ndash;wide actions on rest centre standards and ensuring critical infrastructure sites (points of contact, contingency plans, protection resources and business continuity arrangements) are included in the MAFP. (p100)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area experienced intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 that produced overland flows and caused sewer surcharge, leading to internal property flooding (including basement inundation) and surface flooding of streets. Borough authorities undertook Section 19 investigation and coordinated response and recovery actions. (p105)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 340 properties (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>less than 10 reports of basement flooding (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>manhole depths increased by 0.5m; fountains at surcharged manholes rose to over 1m (p60)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [TAB,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>76 streets reported flooded; around 65% of flooded streets had at least one FLIP installed; highest average internal flood depth reported on a street ~140 cm. [PAG] (p90)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 76 streets; over 340 properties (p105)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,Kensington and Chelsea,England,"Extreme rainfall on 12 July 2021 caused internal flooding across the borough, resulting in flooded properties and overland flows that overwhelmed local drainage. (p5)
On 12 July 2021 the borough experienced flooding that led to properties being flooded and road closures. Residents submitted photographs and reports of flooded properties which were used to document impacts. (p16)
Council officers undertook site visits to the worst–affected areas of the borough following flooding. (p18)
During the intense rainfall event on 12 July 2021 the borough experienced combined sewer and surface water flooding. The combined sewer system reached capacity, causing sewers to surcharge and stormwater to overflow via manholes onto roads, with roads then conveying water downhill according to local topography. The report identifies sewer and surface water flooding as the main cause of flooding in the July 2021 event. [PAG,IMG] (p22)
During the 12 July 2021 event, sewer and surface–water flooding occurred across the borough: combined sewers reached capacity and surcharged, causing overflows via manholes and overland flow along roads; sewer flow was pushed up into properties (including basement properties) through toilets, sinks and shower drains as sewers backed up. (p23)
During the intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 the borough experienced widespread surface water ponding and sewer surcharging. Sewers and drains reached capacity causing surcharges through manholes onto roads (roads acted as conveyance pathways) and floodwater entering properties, with basement properties particularly affected by backflow through toilets, sinks and shower drains. [PAG,IMG] (p27)
Basements of properties were flooded; reports of basement flooding in the borough following the event. (p30)
Surface water (urban) flooding occurred during the intense rainfall event on 12 July 2021. The Tidal Thames defences were reported as operating normally during the event (no overtopping or breach of tidal defences). (p36)
Parts of the borough experienced surface water / urban flooding during the 12 July 2021 intense rainfall event; streets within the borough were listed as having been flooded in the report. [TAB] (p37)
Surface water / urban flooding during the event on 12 July 2021 caused inundation of multiple streets in the borough; the streets listed in Table 6–1 (Stafford Terrace, Holland Road, Queensdale Road, Warwick Road, Holland Villas Road and Royal Crescent) were flooded. [PAG] (p38)
On 12 July 2021 the borough experienced surface–water and sewer (urban) flooding: sewers surcharged, causing sewage and floodwater to back up into basements and via shower/toilet drains, manholes/fountains formed where sewers were surcharged, roads conveyed floodwater, floodwater collected in basements and internal flooding damaged business stock (including flooding inside Sloane Square station). [PAG,IMG] (p41)
Intense convective rainfall on 12 July 2021 caused surface–water and sewer–related flooding across parts of the borough. The event prompted hydrological analysis (radar and gauge data) to quantify rainfall totals and understand the surface–water flooding response in the borough. [PAG,IMG] (p47)
An intense band of rain formed rapidly over central London and produced high rainfall totals over the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea between 15:00–18:00 on 12 July 2021. The heavy, concentrated rainfall caused surface–water/urban flooding in the borough and triggered the Section 19 flood investigation. [PAG,IMG] (p52)
Intense convective rainfall on 12 July 2021 produced very high local rainfall totals across the borough (15:00–18:00), which led to surface water flooding across streets and public areas and caused sewer surcharge (fountains at manholes and internal sewer flooding) and internal flooding of properties and businesses. [PAG,IMG] (p53)
Intense, short–duration rainfall on 12 July 2021 (starting around 15:00) produced surface water/urban flooding across the borough. Areas experienced highly variable return periods (southern parts <1 in 2 years; locations of heaviest rainfall in the borough up to around a 1 in 185‑year storm). A high Thames tide at Chelsea (15:45, 3.80mAOD) coincided with the heavy rainfall on 12 July. (p55)
Interceptor and local sewers surcharged, causing flooding at street level; tide–locking impeded sewer discharge and increased manhole depths; operational failures at pumping stations contributed to borough–wide flooding. (p60)
Intense rainfall and surcharged sewers produced overland flow across the borough. Roads acted as channels conveying floodwater (examples given: A3220, A40, Portobello Road). Surcharging sewers caused fountains at manholes (photographed at the junction of St Marks Road and Lancaster Road, and at Cornwall Crescent). High urban and hydrological connectivity synchronised flows across streets in the Notting Hill area and adjacent neighbourhoods—Portobello Road, Arundel Gardens, Elgin Crescent, Blenheim Crescent, Lower Clarendon Walk, St Marks Road, Camelford Court, Clarendon Road—and a wider corridor including Royal Crescent, Holland Villas Road, Bramley Road, St Anns Road, Stoneleigh Street, St Anns Villas, St James's Gardens, Norland Square, Queensdale Road, Princedale Road, Holland Park Avenue, Addison Road, Warwick Road, Napier Road, Edwardes Square, Cope Place and Scarsdale Villas, all of which contributed to and experienced surface water/sewer–driven flooding on 12 July 2021. [PAG,IMG] (p63)
Properties recorded as flooded; floodwater entered properties via backflow through property drains into basement flats (through toilets, sinks and drains). [TAB,PAG] (p64)
Flooding across the borough affected properties and basements: surface water and sewage–contaminated water entered properties (including basements and bathrooms), causing internal flooding, sewage contamination and damage to fixtures, finishes and fittings on multiple streets. [PAG] (p69)
Council–owned properties were internally flooded with sewage and overland floodwater; sewage staining required replacement of walls and flooring and posed health risks. The Council incurred substantial costs responding and repairing flood–damaged properties and providing temporary accommodation. [PAG] (p70)
Within the Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea, 76 streets were reported to have flooded on 12 July 2021; properties were internally flooded (receptors), business stock was damaged (e.g. Portobello Road), and three primary schools (Colville Primary, Thomas Jones Primary, Avondale Park Primary) experienced internal flooding. The borough’s records note that many flooded streets had at least one FLIP installed yet some properties still flooded. (p73)
Widespread intense rainfall caused surface water and combined sewer systems to exceed capacity across the borough, leading to sewers surcharging, manhole overflows and multiple basement and ground–floor property inundations on several streets. [TAB] (p79)
Widespread internal flooding across the borough during the intense rainfall on 12th July 2021. Multiple streets and properties experienced internal flooding; sewer surcharge and surface–water flow pathways contributed to internal property flooding and sewer–related issues. [PAG] (p90)
Widespread flooding across the borough on 12 July 2021: 76 streets were reported to have flooded, with internal flooding of properties (including basements), sewer surcharging (manhole fountains and sewage entering buildings) and roads acting as channels conveying floodwater. Businesses suffered internal flooding and stock damage (for example reported at Portobello Road) and Sloane Square Station experienced internal flooding. Many flooded streets had FLIPs installed but some of those properties still experienced internal flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p91)
The borough experienced intense surface–water/urban flooding on 12 July 2021 that triggered the Section 19 investigation and ongoing engagement with Thames Water over sewer and asset performance; RBKC has been actively engaged with Thames Water's independent review on behalf of residents. (p92)
Surface water / urban flooding on 12 July 2021 produced very short warning lead times for the borough, affected properties and local emergency decision–making, and led to recommendations that the borough seeks funding for Property Flood Resilience (PFR) schemes, develops local surface–water forecasting, and coordinates with other Risk Management Authorities. [PAG] (p95)
The borough collected reports of internal property flooding following the 12 July 2021 event and ran the Section 19 investigation questionnaire; over 300 responses were submitted to the RBKC questionnaire. Initial reports of flooding within the borough were received by local teams during the event. (p96)
As the local authority, RBKC was required to update and consolidate three existing Multi–Agency Flood Plans into a single Multi–Agency Flood Plan, to review it annually and after events, and to introduce a training and exercising framework. RBKC is also tasked with supporting London–wide actions on rest centre standards and ensuring critical infrastructure sites (points of contact, contingency plans, protection resources and business continuity arrangements) are included in the MAFP. (p100)
The area experienced intense rainfall on 12 July 2021 that produced overland flows and caused sewer surcharge, leading to internal property flooding (including basement inundation) and surface flooding of streets. Borough authorities undertook Section 19 investigation and coordinated response and recovery actions. (p105)","at least 340 properties (p5)
less than 10 reports of basement flooding (p30)
manhole depths increased by 0.5m; fountains at surcharged manholes rose to over 1m (p60)
5 properties [TAB,PAG] (p64)
76 streets reported flooded; around 65% of flooded streets had at least one FLIP installed; highest average internal flood depth reported on a street ~140 cm. [PAG] (p90)
at least 76 streets; over 340 properties (p105)",GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.5109995,-0.2055267,"<div id=""html_b78674655b47bfb67cd09a9e4c24a849"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Notting Hill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced the heaviest rainfall during the 12 July 2021 storm; this extreme rainfall is described in the report as likely to have caused the observed flooding in the borough. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Radar&ndash;derived estimates show Notting Hill (radar grid square 152) received the heaviest rainfall in RBKC during the 12 July 2021 storm, an intensity estimated up to a 1&ndash;in&ndash;185&ndash;year event, producing intense short&ndash;duration runoff and surface water flooding in the areas that experienced the heaviest rainfall. (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall combined with surcharged sewers produced overland flow across the Notting Hill area; roads acted as channels conveying floodwater and sewers discharged at manholes, increasing local water levels and causing urban flooding. (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall and surcharging of sewers produced overland flow through the Notting Hill area. Roads (including Portobello Road and Ladbroke Grove) acted as channels conveying water, synchronising flows from neighbouring streets and increasing water levels. Specific streets in the Notting Hill area reported overland flow and property flooding: Arundel Gardens, Elgin Crescent, Blenheim Crescent, Lower Clarendon Walk, St Marks Road (junction with Lancaster Road reported manhole fountains), Camelford Court and Clarendon Road. Flooded properties are concentrated in this area as shown in the report heatmap. [IMG] (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding affected businesses on Portobello Road (photograph reproduced from My London shows internal flooding damaging stock). The area also shows a high concentration of FLIPs in the borough FLIP heatmap, indicating properties in this area were subject to flood risk and some properties with FLIPs suffered flooding during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Notting Hill experienced the heaviest rainfall during the storm on 12 July 2021 (the maximum local return period was estimated up to 1 in 185 years), contributing to local surface water and urban flooding across the area. (p105)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,Notting Hill,England,"Area experienced the heaviest rainfall during the 12 July 2021 storm; this extreme rainfall is described in the report as likely to have caused the observed flooding in the borough. (p5)
Radar–derived estimates show Notting Hill (radar grid square 152) received the heaviest rainfall in RBKC during the 12 July 2021 storm, an intensity estimated up to a 1–in–185–year event, producing intense short–duration runoff and surface water flooding in the areas that experienced the heaviest rainfall. (p55)
Intense rainfall combined with surcharged sewers produced overland flow across the Notting Hill area; roads acted as channels conveying floodwater and sewers discharged at manholes, increasing local water levels and causing urban flooding. (p61)
Intense rainfall and surcharging of sewers produced overland flow through the Notting Hill area. Roads (including Portobello Road and Ladbroke Grove) acted as channels conveying water, synchronising flows from neighbouring streets and increasing water levels. Specific streets in the Notting Hill area reported overland flow and property flooding: Arundel Gardens, Elgin Crescent, Blenheim Crescent, Lower Clarendon Walk, St Marks Road (junction with Lancaster Road reported manhole fountains), Camelford Court and Clarendon Road. Flooded properties are concentrated in this area as shown in the report heatmap. [IMG] (p63)
Internal flooding affected businesses on Portobello Road (photograph reproduced from My London shows internal flooding damaging stock). The area also shows a high concentration of FLIPs in the borough FLIP heatmap, indicating properties in this area were subject to flood risk and some properties with FLIPs suffered flooding during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p91)
Notting Hill experienced the heaviest rainfall during the storm on 12 July 2021 (the maximum local return period was estimated up to 1 in 185 years), contributing to local surface water and urban flooding across the area. (p105)",,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12.html,GB-KEC_1_2021-07-12,Kensington and Chelsea flooding 12 Jul 2021,Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation: 12 July 2021,062/Kensington and Chelsea/Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea Section 19 Flood Investigation 12 July 2021.pdf
GB-KEC,1,2021-07-12,51.50807896641321,-0.21089186367846494,"<div id=""html_50d08d547822d9e767e332b02f1f8d83"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holland Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Council officers carried out site visits and met residents from Holland Park who were affected by flooding on 12 July 2021; residents gave first&ndash;hand accounts and described continuing impacts on individuals and the community from floodwater inundation of properties and streets. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported on 12th July 2021. Basement properties were flooded by overland flows that collected at basement level and by sewer water backing up through toilets, sinks and drains into basements. [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drains overflowed as sewers surcharged, causing floodwater to flow along the street and into properties. [TAB] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple properties were flooded, particularly basement and lower&ndash;ground flats, due to surcharging local sewers and surface water entering properties. (p78)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one of 76 streets reported flooded [TAB] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p77)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties flooded internally (across affected properties in this report); average internal depth 30 cm; maximum internal depth 30 cm (p78)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kensington and Chelsea,Holland Park,England,"Council officers carried out site visits and met residents from Holland Park who were affected by flooding on 12 July 2021; residents gave first–hand accounts and described continuing impacts on individuals and the community from floodwater inundation of properties and streets. (p19)
