GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.7196271,-0.5359661,"<div id=""html_b62bfe771bef798dce9eaf65abcfb28f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hamer Close, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Hamer Close were reported flooded during the surface water flooding incident on 16 September 2016 and the street was included within the Section 19 investigation area following resident questionnaire responses. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the single respondent property; water entered from road and from manhole covers (sewer water) contributing to overland flow into the property. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>HCC's highways infiltration tank is located on Hamer Close; during the 16 September 2016 storm the local highway drainage and infiltration capacity in the Hamer area would have been insufficient, with gullies unable to cope and contributing to surface water running across the highway. [IMG] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>More than 5 properties flooded across the investigation area (including streets listed such as Hamer Close) (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded; 0 properties internally flooded (per questionnaire breakdown) [TAB] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Hamer Close, Bovingdon",England,"Properties on Hamer Close were reported flooded during the surface water flooding incident on 16 September 2016 and the street was included within the Section 19 investigation area following resident questionnaire responses. (p8)
Flooding affected the single respondent property; water entered from road and from manhole covers (sewer water) contributing to overland flow into the property. [TAB] (p13)
HCC's highways infiltration tank is located on Hamer Close; during the 16 September 2016 storm the local highway drainage and infiltration capacity in the Hamer area would have been insufficient, with gullies unable to cope and contributing to surface water running across the highway. [IMG] (p19)","More than 5 properties flooded across the investigation area (including streets listed such as Hamer Close) (p8)
1 property flooded; 0 properties internally flooded (per questionnaire breakdown) [TAB] (p13)",GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.71618017373075,-0.5491977941733924,"<div id=""html_ed9e04cb15be5362d5226c753fa1013c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ley Hill Road, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage on Leyhill Road was not operational during the 16 September 2016 event, contributing to impaired removal of surface water on the approach to Bovingdon from the south&ndash;west. (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Ley Hill Road, Bovingdon",England,"Highway drainage on Leyhill Road was not operational during the 16 September 2016 event, contributing to impaired removal of surface water on the approach to Bovingdon from the south–west. (p14)",,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.723194,-0.5324634,"<div id=""html_18d24df8d9e1e93f531830fc8a298908"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The foul water sewer surcharged on Bovingdon High Street during the 16 September 2016 event, causing sewer&ndash;related flooding on the High Street. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway drainage from the High Street discharges to Bovingdon Docks; limited highway drainage/infiltration capacity would have increased surface runoff entering the highway during the storm on 16 September 2016, contributing to local surface water flooding. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"High Street, Bovingdon",England,"The foul water sewer surcharged on Bovingdon High Street during the 16 September 2016 event, causing sewer–related flooding on the High Street. (p14)
Highway drainage from the High Street discharges to Bovingdon Docks; limited highway drainage/infiltration capacity would have increased surface runoff entering the highway during the storm on 16 September 2016, contributing to local surface water flooding. (p20)",,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.7187384,-0.5366693,"<div id=""html_526f150bda8cc7421a720fc73bb142b1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Farnham Close, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Farnham Close lies within a foul sewer catchment that is pumped to the wider network; the foul sewer network surcharged during the 16 September 2016 storm, with reports of foul water surcharging inside and outside properties in the area. [IMG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Farnham Close, Bovingdon",England,"Farnham Close lies within a foul sewer catchment that is pumped to the wider network; the foul sewer network surcharged during the 16 September 2016 storm, with reports of foul water surcharging inside and outside properties in the area. [IMG] (p20)",,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.7190835,-0.5361489,"<div id=""html_cb50d0feb7579b5a725a95bfb3a898ea"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Claverton Close, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer catchment serving the area is pumped out to the wider network; during and after the 16 September 2016 storm the foul sewer network surcharged, with reports of foul water surcharging inside and outside properties. Surface water ingress to the foul network (via cross&ndash;connections from roofs, driveways and patios) likely worsened the surcharging; the pumped arrangement for Farnham Close and Claverton meant reliance on pumping station discharge during the event. [IMG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Claverton Close, Bovingdon",England,"Foul sewer catchment serving the area is pumped out to the wider network; during and after the 16 September 2016 storm the foul sewer network surcharged, with reports of foul water surcharging inside and outside properties. Surface water ingress to the foul network (via cross–connections from roofs, driveways and patios) likely worsened the surcharging; the pumped arrangement for Farnham Close and Claverton meant reliance on pumping station discharge during the event. [IMG] (p20)",,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.7212591,-0.5384448,"<div id=""html_900035af1651b3a40cb7ef54c2081ef5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hyde Lane, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed surface water flooding occurred on and around Hyde Lane during the 16 September 2016 event. Intense rainfall exceeded the urban drainage capacity producing overland flows that drained along the corridor where Hyde Lane lies (heatmap hotspot). Local foul sewers and the main sewer line in the area were partially involved through surcharge or infiltration of surface water, which limited the ability of gullies and highway drainage to remove water. The Bovingdon Docks serving the highway drainage were full and overflowing, reducing discharge capacity and contributing to flooding impacts in this location. [IMG] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Hyde Lane, Bovingdon",England,"Observed surface water flooding occurred on and around Hyde Lane during the 16 September 2016 event. Intense rainfall exceeded the urban drainage capacity producing overland flows that drained along the corridor where Hyde Lane lies (heatmap hotspot). Local foul sewers and the main sewer line in the area were partially involved through surcharge or infiltration of surface water, which limited the ability of gullies and highway drainage to remove water. The Bovingdon Docks serving the highway drainage were full and overflowing, reducing discharge capacity and contributing to flooding impacts in this location. [IMG] (p23)",,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.7204727,-0.5294174,"<div id=""html_f4049bad0e1bdf4db99f5fd83ed7bef3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yew Tree Drive, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An interception trench and borehole to the rear of Yew Tree Drive were identified for condition investigation and for addition to the LLFA asset register after flooding on 16th September 2016, indicating local infiltration features were implicated in the event. [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An interception trench and borehole to the rear of Yew Tree Drive were identified for condition investigation and for addition to the LLFA asset register as part of the post&ndash;flood investigations. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The interception trench and deep bore soakaway on Yew Tree Drive were implicated in the investigation and their condition was to be inspected and the assets added to the county asset register due to performance concerns during the flood event. (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Yew Tree Drive, Bovingdon",England,"An interception trench and borehole to the rear of Yew Tree Drive were identified for condition investigation and for addition to the LLFA asset register after flooding on 16th September 2016, indicating local infiltration features were implicated in the event. [TAB] (p31)
An interception trench and borehole to the rear of Yew Tree Drive were identified for condition investigation and for addition to the LLFA asset register as part of the post–flood investigations. (p32)
The interception trench and deep bore soakaway on Yew Tree Drive were implicated in the investigation and their condition was to be inspected and the assets added to the county asset register due to performance concerns during the flood event. (p35)",,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.7202435,-0.5325633,"<div id=""html_9779217b4f7cbdd57e945bcc34b29aba"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nye Way, Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report identifies highway soakaways on Nye Way as old and recommends they be investigated and considered for improvement as part of actions to address surface water flooding following the 16th September 2016 Bovingdon flood. [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Soakaways on Nye Way were identified as old and should be investigated and considered for improvement to address surface water drainage shortfalls observed during the flood. (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,"Nye Way, Bovingdon",England,"The report identifies highway soakaways on Nye Way as old and recommends they be investigated and considered for improvement as part of actions to address surface water flooding following the 16th September 2016 Bovingdon flood. [TAB] (p31)
Soakaways on Nye Way were identified as old and should be investigated and considered for improvement to address surface water drainage shortfalls observed during the flood. (p32)",,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.840052299999996,-0.09785519224523963,"<div id=""html_f693e2db8f74966dfebb382f7115a4fe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hertfordshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A localized area within the county (in and around Bovingdon) experienced exceptionally intense rainfall causing predominantly overland surface water flows with secondary surcharging from sewers and highway drainage; this resulted in property flooding in the county. [TAB,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A localised area within Hertfordshire (Bovingdon) experienced exceptionally intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 leading to overland surface water flooding and sewer surcharge in the investigation area. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 caused flash flooding in Bovingdon (within Hertfordshire); the event generated flood reports to the LLFA and emergency services. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread heavy, localized rainfall on 16 September 2016 across Hertfordshire, with the highest totals concentrated in a small area (including the area around Bovingdon). This intense rainfall produced surface water / flash flooding impacts in the affected locality (including Bovingdon). [IMG,PAG] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 properties flooded; 14 flooded internally [TAB,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 properties; 14 properties flooded internally [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two incidents of flooding to dwellings (recorded by Thames Water Utilities Ltd) (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Hertfordshire,England,"A localized area within the county (in and around Bovingdon) experienced exceptionally intense rainfall causing predominantly overland surface water flows with secondary surcharging from sewers and highway drainage; this resulted in property flooding in the county. [TAB,PAG] (p6)
A localised area within Hertfordshire (Bovingdon) experienced exceptionally intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 leading to overland surface water flooding and sewer surcharge in the investigation area. [PAG] (p7)
Intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 caused flash flooding in Bovingdon (within Hertfordshire); the event generated flood reports to the LLFA and emergency services. (p12)
Widespread heavy, localized rainfall on 16 September 2016 across Hertfordshire, with the highest totals concentrated in a small area (including the area around Bovingdon). This intense rainfall produced surface water / flash flooding impacts in the affected locality (including Bovingdon). [IMG,PAG] (p15)","23 properties flooded; 14 flooded internally [TAB,PAG] (p6)
23 properties; 14 properties flooded internally [PAG] (p7)
two incidents of flooding to dwellings (recorded by Thames Water Utilities Ltd) (p12)",GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HRT,4,2016-09-16,51.72344,-0.5327056,"<div id=""html_88454382fcbb15a17c64012b7cc80623"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bovingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential streets were inundated with floodwater covering the road surface; a wooden lychgate/ shelter was partially surrounded by water; parked cars were partially submerged; nearby houses were affected by standing floodwater and access disruption. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Exceptionally intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 caused overland surface water flows and secondary surcharging of sewers and highway drainage in and around Bovingdon. Piped drainage was overwhelmed and localised flooding occurred across the investigation area. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred during the event on 16 September 2016; the incident led to properties in the village being inundated and prompted a Section 19 investigation to identify drainage asset issues and resilience measures. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Figure 3 and the investigation&ndash;area map identify a red&ndash;outlined area in central Bovingdon that coincides with the Environment Agency 'Risk of Flooding from Surface Water' (1&ndash;in&ndash;100&ndash;year) maximum extent and is labelled as including a flooded area. The investigation area runs west&ndash;east through central Bovingdon and crosses residential streets (including St. Norbert Road and Green Lane) and is adjacent to community facilities (library, education facility). The contributing surface water catchment directs flows west to east through this area. [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 16 September 2016 intense early&ndash;morning rainfall caused flash flooding in Bovingdon; the report states the heaviest rain fell between about 05:00 and 07:00 and corresponds with flood reports of surface&ndash;water inundation in the investigation area. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall (approx. 05:00&ndash;07:00 on 16 September 2016) caused flash flooding in the village; properties were flooded, Hertfordshire Fire and Rescue attended near the area known as 'Bovingdon Docks', and Thames Water recorded sewer&ndash;related flooding to dwellings. HCC sent questionnaires to properties identified as potentially affected. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localized intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 produced surface water flooding across Bovingdon (heatmap shows deepest at Eastnor and Green Lane). The foul water sewer also surcharged on Bovingdon High Street. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, localized rainfall between about 05:00 and 07:00 on 16 September 2016 produced surface water flooding in the Bovingdon investigation area, correlating with recorded flood reports and emergency call&ndash;outs. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced surface water flooding on 16th September 2016. Most properties rely on private plot&ndash;level soakaways and infiltration into the ground is very limited; soakaways are likely in poor condition or inefficient, reducing infiltration capacity. Highway drainage for the affected area drains to local soakaways/boreholes and to a large attenuation/infiltration tank (beneath the private car park between Eastnor and Hamer Close) &mdash; these drainage arrangements constrained runoff conveyance and contributed to surface water flooding during the event. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The highway drainage serving Bovingdon was described as running at or near design capacity and was overwhelmed by the rainfall on 16 September 2016; gullies, soakaways and the local attenuation/infiltration tank were unable to fully contain runoff, and surface water (including overland flow from private property) exceeded the drainage system causing surface&ndash;water/urban flooding across the investigation area. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 16 September 2016 intense rainfall exceeded the highway drainage capacity in Bovingdon. Highway gullies were unable to drain the large volumes of water and additional overland flow from private land, gardens and driveways overwhelmed the drainage network. Surface water flowed across the highway (notably High Street and Green Lane) and discharged to the Bovingdon Docks open water feature. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 16 September 2016 storm produced a large volume of surface water runoff that exceeded highway drainage capacity. Highway drainage from Green Lane and the High Street discharges to Bovingdon Docks (the only local open water feature); defects or silted soakaways would have reduced infiltration/storage and increased surface runoff entering the highway drainage. TWUL received reports during and after the event of foul sewer network surcharging causing foul water to discharge inside and outside properties; residents also raised concerns that a historical pipeline from the airfield may have discharged surface water toward Hamer Close during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Short&ndash;duration, intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 exceeded the urban drainage capacity and generated overland surface&ndash;water flows that followed topographic flow routes (matching the EA RoFfSW map). These flows drained towards low&ndash;lying properties in Bovingdon, causing flooding of properties and observable flood depths in the village. [IMG,PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 exceeded the capacity of the urban drainage network causing overland flows into low&ndash;lying properties; foul sewers partially surcharged contributing to flooding along the main sewer line; highway drainage (Bovingdon Docks) was full and overflowing, limiting discharge and exacerbating surface water on the highway. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Bovingdon experienced internal flooding and there was pressure on the downstream drainage network. The investigation notes reports of property flooding, limited knowledge of local surface water and highway drainage assets (including soakaways), and potential foul/surface sewer misconnections contributing to property flooding; options for catchment interception, improved drainage and flood detention were recommended to reduce risk. [TAB] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water/urban flooding affected the village during the 16 September 2016 event, prompting a Section 19 investigation. The investigation identified unknown condition of highways drainage (soakaways and infiltration features) and potential infiltration/mis&ndash;connections between surface water and foul sewers; four potential green areas within Bovingdon were identified as possible surface water attenuation sites to reduce future runoff. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area of investigation for a surface&ndash;water flood study; surface water flow routes and a RoFfSW (1 in 1000 yr extent) are shown as overlapping the investigation area. Four potential green areas in Bovingdon were identified to store runoff during heavy rain to reduce surface&ndash;water flooding and to slow release of flows. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding on 16th September 2016 affected the village and prompted investigation into local drainage assets (soakaways, sewers) and the ownership/condition of Bovingdon Docks as potential contributors to the flooding. [TAB] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village was the focus of the investigation following flooding; individual property owners were supported by the LLFA and recommendations were made to assess and upgrade local drainage (soakaways, interception features and sewer connections) to reduce flood risk. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 16 September 2016 surface water entered the foul sewer network and overwhelmed it, causing flooding in Bovingdon. Thames Water staff attended the village during the flooding and identified the flooding mechanism as surface water ingress overwhelming the foul network. (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 properties; 14 properties flooded internally (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 5 properties flooded; property flooded more than once in 10 year period [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two incidents of flooding to dwellings; 27 questionnaire responses returned (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hertfordshire,Bovingdon,England,"Residential streets were inundated with floodwater covering the road surface; a wooden lychgate/ shelter was partially surrounded by water; parked cars were partially submerged; nearby houses were affected by standing floodwater and access disruption. [IMG] (p1)
Exceptionally intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 caused overland surface water flows and secondary surcharging of sewers and highway drainage in and around Bovingdon. Piped drainage was overwhelmed and localised flooding occurred across the investigation area. (p7)
Surface water flooding occurred during the event on 16 September 2016; the incident led to properties in the village being inundated and prompted a Section 19 investigation to identify drainage asset issues and resilience measures. [PAG] (p8)
Figure 3 and the investigation–area map identify a red–outlined area in central Bovingdon that coincides with the Environment Agency 'Risk of Flooding from Surface Water' (1–in–100–year) maximum extent and is labelled as including a flooded area. The investigation area runs west–east through central Bovingdon and crosses residential streets (including St. Norbert Road and Green Lane) and is adjacent to community facilities (library, education facility). The contributing surface water catchment directs flows west to east through this area. [IMG,PAG] (p9)
On 16 September 2016 intense early–morning rainfall caused flash flooding in Bovingdon; the report states the heaviest rain fell between about 05:00 and 07:00 and corresponds with flood reports of surface–water inundation in the investigation area. [PAG] (p11)
Intense rainfall (approx. 05:00–07:00 on 16 September 2016) caused flash flooding in the village; properties were flooded, Hertfordshire Fire and Rescue attended near the area known as 'Bovingdon Docks', and Thames Water recorded sewer–related flooding to dwellings. HCC sent questionnaires to properties identified as potentially affected. (p12)
Localized intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 produced surface water flooding across Bovingdon (heatmap shows deepest at Eastnor and Green Lane). The foul water sewer also surcharged on Bovingdon High Street. (p14)
Intense, localized rainfall between about 05:00 and 07:00 on 16 September 2016 produced surface water flooding in the Bovingdon investigation area, correlating with recorded flood reports and emergency call–outs. [PAG] (p16)
The village experienced surface water flooding on 16th September 2016. Most properties rely on private plot–level soakaways and infiltration into the ground is very limited; soakaways are likely in poor condition or inefficient, reducing infiltration capacity. Highway drainage for the affected area drains to local soakaways/boreholes and to a large attenuation/infiltration tank (beneath the private car park between Eastnor and Hamer Close) — these drainage arrangements constrained runoff conveyance and contributed to surface water flooding during the event. [PAG] (p17)
The highway drainage serving Bovingdon was described as running at or near design capacity and was overwhelmed by the rainfall on 16 September 2016; gullies, soakaways and the local attenuation/infiltration tank were unable to fully contain runoff, and surface water (including overland flow from private property) exceeded the drainage system causing surface–water/urban flooding across the investigation area. [IMG] (p18)
On 16 September 2016 intense rainfall exceeded the highway drainage capacity in Bovingdon. Highway gullies were unable to drain the large volumes of water and additional overland flow from private land, gardens and driveways overwhelmed the drainage network. Surface water flowed across the highway (notably High Street and Green Lane) and discharged to the Bovingdon Docks open water feature. [PAG] (p19)
The 16 September 2016 storm produced a large volume of surface water runoff that exceeded highway drainage capacity. Highway drainage from Green Lane and the High Street discharges to Bovingdon Docks (the only local open water feature); defects or silted soakaways would have reduced infiltration/storage and increased surface runoff entering the highway drainage. TWUL received reports during and after the event of foul sewer network surcharging causing foul water to discharge inside and outside properties; residents also raised concerns that a historical pipeline from the airfield may have discharged surface water toward Hamer Close during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p20)
Short–duration, intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 exceeded the urban drainage capacity and generated overland surface–water flows that followed topographic flow routes (matching the EA RoFfSW map). These flows drained towards low–lying properties in Bovingdon, causing flooding of properties and observable flood depths in the village. [IMG,PAG] (p22)
Intense rainfall on 16 September 2016 exceeded the capacity of the urban drainage network causing overland flows into low–lying properties; foul sewers partially surcharged contributing to flooding along the main sewer line; highway drainage (Bovingdon Docks) was full and overflowing, limiting discharge and exacerbating surface water on the highway. [PAG] (p23)
Properties in Bovingdon experienced internal flooding and there was pressure on the downstream drainage network. The investigation notes reports of property flooding, limited knowledge of local surface water and highway drainage assets (including soakaways), and potential foul/surface sewer misconnections contributing to property flooding; options for catchment interception, improved drainage and flood detention were recommended to reduce risk. [TAB] (p27)
Surface water/urban flooding affected the village during the 16 September 2016 event, prompting a Section 19 investigation. The investigation identified unknown condition of highways drainage (soakaways and infiltration features) and potential infiltration/mis–connections between surface water and foul sewers; four potential green areas within Bovingdon were identified as possible surface water attenuation sites to reduce future runoff. [PAG] (p28)
Area of investigation for a surface–water flood study; surface water flow routes and a RoFfSW (1 in 1000 yr extent) are shown as overlapping the investigation area. Four potential green areas in Bovingdon were identified to store runoff during heavy rain to reduce surface–water flooding and to slow release of flows. [IMG] (p29)
Surface water flooding on 16th September 2016 affected the village and prompted investigation into local drainage assets (soakaways, sewers) and the ownership/condition of Bovingdon Docks as potential contributors to the flooding. [TAB] (p31)
The village was the focus of the investigation following flooding; individual property owners were supported by the LLFA and recommendations were made to assess and upgrade local drainage (soakaways, interception features and sewer connections) to reduce flood risk. (p32)
On 16 September 2016 surface water entered the foul sewer network and overwhelmed it, causing flooding in Bovingdon. Thames Water staff attended the village during the flooding and identified the flooding mechanism as surface water ingress overwhelming the foul network. (p34)","23 properties; 14 properties flooded internally (p7)
more than 5 properties flooded; property flooded more than once in 10 year period [PAG] (p8)
two incidents of flooding to dwellings; 27 questionnaire responses returned (p12)",GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16.html,GB-HRT_4_2016-09-16,Bovingdon flooding 16 Sep 2016,Hertfordshire County Council Section 19 Flood Investigation Report: Bovingdon,056/Hertfordshire/section-19-investigation-bovingdon-public.pdf
GB-HIL,1,2026-07-28,51.5029405,-0.4811768,"<div id=""html_837829b2e2cc0603d802ab24becb67de"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fray's River</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Environment Agency issued a flood alert for Fray's River in response to the heavy rainfall during the event. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named alongside the Lower River Colne in the Flood Alert for the area; contributed to elevated catchment river levels that influenced local drainage and flooding conditions. [TAB] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hillingdon,Fray's River,England,"The Environment Agency issued a flood alert for Fray's River in response to the heavy rainfall during the event. (p3)
Named alongside the Lower River Colne in the Flood Alert for the area; contributed to elevated catchment river levels that influenced local drainage and flooding conditions. [TAB] (p18)",,GB-HIL_1_2026-07-28.html,GB-HIL_1_2026-07-28,Hillingdon flooding 28 Jul 2026,Flood Investigation Report: July 28th 2014,057/Hillingdon/Flood/Investigation/Report/July28th/2014 2.pdf
GB-HIL,1,2026-07-28,51.5625936,-0.4369523,"<div id=""html_840d7417ac0f58b350da2bc40cb5e4f5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ickenham Stream</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from overwhelmed highway drains and the connected Thames Water surface water sewer discharged into/through the Ickenham Stream corridor, contributing to flood water that flowed across Glebe Avenue and entered nearby properties. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contractors prioritised and carried out clearance of the stream to restore and maintain flow following the flood event; clearance works were referenced in the report (scheduled previously for Nov/Dec 2014). (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 households [TAB] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hillingdon,Ickenham Stream,England,"Surface water from overwhelmed highway drains and the connected Thames Water surface water sewer discharged into/through the Ickenham Stream corridor, contributing to flood water that flowed across Glebe Avenue and entered nearby properties. [TAB] (p13)
Contractors prioritised and carried out clearance of the stream to restore and maintain flow following the flood event; clearance works were referenced in the report (scheduled previously for Nov/Dec 2014). (p14)",4 households [TAB] (p13),GB-HIL_1_2026-07-28.html,GB-HIL_1_2026-07-28,Hillingdon flooding 28 Jul 2026,Flood Investigation Report: July 28th 2014,057/Hillingdon/Flood/Investigation/Report/July28th/2014 2.pdf
GB-HIL,1,2026-07-28,51.532563,-0.453256,"<div id=""html_cbbc788ee430c777e233fd652e15174c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hillingdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Borough&ndash;wide impacts from the 28 July storm: roads and transport infrastructure flooded or closed; residents displaced and some placed in temporary accommodation. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Closure of the A40 and the resulting diversions caused tailbacks and traffic disruption across the borough. [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding overwhelmed local drains after heavy short&ndash;duration rainfall, causing flooding across access roads and into front/rear gardens and properties on Bedford Road, Clyfford Road and West End Road. The event lasted approximately 3 hours; approximately 23 houses were flooded and sandbags were issued to properties; some properties experienced internal flooding. The London Fire Brigade attended to assist residents. [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred within the borough at Rockingham Parade / The Lynch; resident reports and photographs document local impacts and the location lies within an Environment Agency Flood Zone. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected the local highway network within the borough (notably Cowley Road). The London Borough of Hillingdon undertook immediate response actions, including checking gullies to assist drainage and mitigate the event. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ponding on the highway at Blossom Way; road flooded and remained covered for approximately 3 hours because verge soakaways were full. The Council created a new soakaway in the verge to reduce the extent of future flooding. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced surface water/urban flooding during the 28 July 2026 heavy rainfall event leading to visible water on highways; the council carries out gully clearance maintenance and provided reporting routes to investigate and resolve blocked gullies or sewers causing the flooding. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local news coverage and photo reports documented flooding across parts of Hillingdon; streets and properties were reported as affected by floodwater. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 29 properties flooded internally; six cars stuck in floodwater (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 23 houses; duration approximately 3 hours; sandbags provided to 20&ndash;50 properties [TAB] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Hillingdon,Hillingdon,England,"Borough–wide impacts from the 28 July storm: roads and transport infrastructure flooded or closed; residents displaced and some placed in temporary accommodation. (p3)
Closure of the A40 and the resulting diversions caused tailbacks and traffic disruption across the borough. [TAB] (p7)
Surface water flooding overwhelmed local drains after heavy short–duration rainfall, causing flooding across access roads and into front/rear gardens and properties on Bedford Road, Clyfford Road and West End Road. The event lasted approximately 3 hours; approximately 23 houses were flooded and sandbags were issued to properties; some properties experienced internal flooding. The London Fire Brigade attended to assist residents. [TAB] (p8)
Flooding occurred within the borough at Rockingham Parade / The Lynch; resident reports and photographs document local impacts and the location lies within an Environment Agency Flood Zone. [TAB] (p16)
Surface water flooding affected the local highway network within the borough (notably Cowley Road). The London Borough of Hillingdon undertook immediate response actions, including checking gullies to assist drainage and mitigate the event. [IMG] (p18)
Surface water ponding on the highway at Blossom Way; road flooded and remained covered for approximately 3 hours because verge soakaways were full. The Council created a new soakaway in the verge to reduce the extent of future flooding. [IMG,TAB,MUL] (p20)
Experienced surface water/urban flooding during the 28 July 2026 heavy rainfall event leading to visible water on highways; the council carries out gully clearance maintenance and provided reporting routes to investigate and resolve blocked gullies or sewers causing the flooding. (p21)
Local news coverage and photo reports documented flooding across parts of Hillingdon; streets and properties were reported as affected by floodwater. [TAB] (p24)","approximately 29 properties flooded internally; six cars stuck in floodwater (p3)
approximately 23 houses; duration approximately 3 hours; sandbags provided to 20–50 properties [TAB] (p8)",GB-HIL_1_2026-07-28.html,GB-HIL_1_2026-07-28,Hillingdon flooding 28 Jul 2026,Flood Investigation Report: July 28th 2014,057/Hillingdon/Flood/Investigation/Report/July28th/2014 2.pdf
