Experienced flood incidents on the night of 7 June that required police and fire attendance; two vehicles were reported stranded and road debris was later identified and swept. (p11)
Heavy rainfall on 7 June caused flooding in Dunstable leading to closure of the A505 Church Street, internal flooding of commercial businesses on the A5 High Street South, road damage around sewer manholes, removal of abandoned vehicles and multi–agency emergency response. (p12)
Thirty properties in Dunstable were recorded as affected by flooding during the event; twenty of these suffered internal flooding (ten residential and ten commercial). Surface water flow paths ran north along High Street South through commercial properties, across Church Street and toward the Leisure Centre; low thresholds and basement entrances allowed ingress to properties (one property had a front flood gate but still experienced basement flooding). (p18)
Surface water flooding followed a flow path along High Street South through commercial properties, across Church Street and north toward the Leisure Centre, entering lower–lying properties. Surface water entered the public foul sewer causing overloading and resultant foul flooding beneath the busway. Property thresholds (including basement entrances) allowed ingress; one property with a fitted flood gate still experienced basement flooding, indicating other routes of ingress. [PAG] (p19)
Localised intense rainfall produced surface water flooding across these settlements. Surface water was the primary source of flooding during the event, with highway drainage and drainage networks overwhelmed leading to overland flows, surcharge of drainage systems and flooding of highways and properties (internal and external). (p25)
The 7 June 2016 rainfall produced predominantly surface water flooding across Dunstable. Topography channelled flows along High Street South into properties, highway drains were overwhelmed, vehicles worsened impacts by creating bow waves, and surface water entered and overloaded the foul public sewer causing foul flooding (notably under the busway). (p26)
These areas were affected by the flood incident, with surface water inundation causing localised flooding impacts to streets, drainage systems and properties; the report advises using its findings for planning decisions and reviewing flood assets. (p35)
Surface water / urban flooding affected parts of Dunstable during the event, overwhelming local drainage and prompting actions such as investigation of a pump station adjacent to the bus way, proposals for a Flood Response Plan for Church Street and maintenance of highway drainage on Church Street and Luton Road. [TAB,PAG] (p42)
