GB-KEN,6,2021-07-20,51.402112,0.1625548,"<div id=""html_8b34ffcd1411b96a73360630f413e6e1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woodview Road, St Mary's</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carriageway flooding reported; a noted depth of standing water on the carriageway was 20 cm. (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 cm (carriageway) (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Woodview Road, St Mary's",England,Carriageway flooding reported; a noted depth of standing water on the carriageway was 20 cm. (p30),20 cm (carriageway) (p30),GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc.html,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc,Swanley flooding 20 Jul 2021 and Swanley flooding 20 Oct 2021 - 21 Oct 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Swanley,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Swanley-on-20-July-and-20-to-21-October-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,6,2021-07-20,51.4042395,0.1899183,"<div id=""html_74edc841966463ead19ff4a8cb411e4b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Highland Hills, Swanley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carriageway flooding reported along the road. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Highland Hills, Swanley",England,Carriageway flooding reported along the road. (p30),,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc.html,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc,Swanley flooding 20 Jul 2021 and Swanley flooding 20 Oct 2021 - 21 Oct 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Swanley,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Swanley-on-20-July-and-20-to-21-October-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,6,2021-07-20,51.39750895,0.18022632679075817,"<div id=""html_fef2005fa311b73fee7f8e0e0d8244eb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swanley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in the town as a result of intense thunderstorms and heavy rainfall on 20th July 2021. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall caused large volumes of surface water runoff predominantly from the urban network leading to surcharging of gullies; there were also reports of overland flows from surrounding agricultural land to the east of Swanley. As a result, on 20 July 2021 approximately 53 properties (including two schools) were affected and 30 properties flooded internally. A later event on 20&ndash;21 October 2021 (thunderstorms/intense rainfall) produced similar surface water runoff and gully surcharging, with approximately 17 properties affected and 11 properties flooding internally. [MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple streets and properties across the town experienced surface water flooding on 20 July 2021, causing internal and external flooding to residences on named streets. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two intense periods of rainfall (around 13:00&ndash;14:30 and 16:30&ndash;17:30) produced surface water / flash flooding in Swanley. Resident reports of flooding were concentrated between about 17:00&ndash;18:00; the second intense storm (16:30&ndash;17:30) is consistent with the majority of these reports and radar suggests the storm was focused close to Swanley. Both intense periods are judged likely to have contributed to the flooding. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, highly localised rainfall on 20 July 2021 produced surface water and flash urban flooding across Swanley, inundating properties and streets and prompting the flood investigation response. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Urban / surface&ndash;water flooding affected Swanley during the event referenced in the report; intense convective storm runoff led to short&ndash;duration flooding across the town. [IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Swanley experienced surface water and urban flooding during the events; the report notes affected parts of Swanley and links the intense rainfall and runoff to flooding impacts in local streets and properties. [IMG,MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Swanley (notably St. Mary's Estate) experienced surface water runoff and flooding during the event, with drains surcharging and roads/alleyways inundated at low points. [IMG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Swanley (High Firs area and southern parts) experienced heavy surface water flooding during the intense rainfall on 20th July 2021. Overland flows from and across the A20 and overtopping of a balancing pond between the A20 and Pinks Hill contributed to flooding of nearby properties; principal routes including Goldsel Road experienced major disruption. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Swanley area (including High Firs Estate and nearby streets) experienced surface water flooding during the intense rainfall on 20th July 2021. Runoff from embankments and surcharged drains produced flooded roads and impacted residential streets and community infrastructure within the town. [IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 20th July 2021 caused surface water runoff and surcharge of drainage infrastructure across Swanley, with notable ponding and flow along roads in lower&ndash;lying areas (concentrated in the north&ndash;east of the town). [IMG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface water flooding concentrated in the Strawberry Fields/The Spinney area caused ponding on roads, surcharged drainage and internal flooding of properties where property thresholds were lower than the adjacent road. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Hextable area of Swanley experienced surface water/urban flooding on 20th July 2021: a section of College Road (between Egerton Avenue and the Post Office) was impassable to local traffic due to carriageway flooding; Stuart Close suffered significant flooding to the road and to properties; surface water runoff along Lower Road flowed into Laura Drive, and drainage infrastructure surcharged under the intense rainfall. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Swanley experienced surface water/urban flooding leading to requests for flood saxs and multiple reported incidents that generated emergency calls and follow&ndash;up gully and drainage clearance. [MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Short, intense rainfall on 20 July 2021 and 20&ndash;21 October 2021 overwhelmed local surface water drainage, causing flooding to properties across the town and disruption to local roads. [MUL] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple reported flood incidents were concentrated in the residential and urban streets of southern Swanley on 20th July 2021, with affected locations marked on the map. The incidents indicate localised inundation in the built&ndash;up area and disruption to streets and properties. [IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple reported flood incidents concentrated in the central and southern parts of Swanley, affecting residential areas and sections of major roads (locations marked as affected on the map). [IMG] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 53 properties including two schools affected (20 July 2021); 30 properties flooded internally (20 July 2021); approximately 17 properties affected (20&ndash;21 October 2021); 11 properties flooded internally (20&ndash;21 October 2021) [MUL] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 properties (sum of reported affected properties across named streets) (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>68 properties [MUL] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Swanley,England,"Flooding occurred in the town as a result of intense thunderstorms and heavy rainfall on 20th July 2021. (p1)
Intense rainfall caused large volumes of surface water runoff predominantly from the urban network leading to surcharging of gullies; there were also reports of overland flows from surrounding agricultural land to the east of Swanley. As a result, on 20 July 2021 approximately 53 properties (including two schools) were affected and 30 properties flooded internally. A later event on 20–21 October 2021 (thunderstorms/intense rainfall) produced similar surface water runoff and gully surcharging, with approximately 17 properties affected and 11 properties flooding internally. [MUL] (p2)
Multiple streets and properties across the town experienced surface water flooding on 20 July 2021, causing internal and external flooding to residences on named streets. (p5)
Two intense periods of rainfall (around 13:00–14:30 and 16:30–17:30) produced surface water / flash flooding in Swanley. Resident reports of flooding were concentrated between about 17:00–18:00; the second intense storm (16:30–17:30) is consistent with the majority of these reports and radar suggests the storm was focused close to Swanley. Both intense periods are judged likely to have contributed to the flooding. (p7)
Intense, highly localised rainfall on 20 July 2021 produced surface water and flash urban flooding across Swanley, inundating properties and streets and prompting the flood investigation response. (p9)
Urban / surface–water flooding affected Swanley during the event referenced in the report; intense convective storm runoff led to short–duration flooding across the town. [IMG] (p10)
Areas of Swanley experienced surface water and urban flooding during the events; the report notes affected parts of Swanley and links the intense rainfall and runoff to flooding impacts in local streets and properties. [IMG,MUL] (p13)
Parts of Swanley (notably St. Mary's Estate) experienced surface water runoff and flooding during the event, with drains surcharging and roads/alleyways inundated at low points. [IMG] (p14)
Swanley (High Firs area and southern parts) experienced heavy surface water flooding during the intense rainfall on 20th July 2021. Overland flows from and across the A20 and overtopping of a balancing pond between the A20 and Pinks Hill contributed to flooding of nearby properties; principal routes including Goldsel Road experienced major disruption. (p15)
The Swanley area (including High Firs Estate and nearby streets) experienced surface water flooding during the intense rainfall on 20th July 2021. Runoff from embankments and surcharged drains produced flooded roads and impacted residential streets and community infrastructure within the town. [IMG] (p16)
Intense rainfall on 20th July 2021 caused surface water runoff and surcharge of drainage infrastructure across Swanley, with notable ponding and flow along roads in lower–lying areas (concentrated in the north–east of the town). [IMG] (p17)
Localised surface water flooding concentrated in the Strawberry Fields/The Spinney area caused ponding on roads, surcharged drainage and internal flooding of properties where property thresholds were lower than the adjacent road. (p18)
The Hextable area of Swanley experienced surface water/urban flooding on 20th July 2021: a section of College Road (between Egerton Avenue and the Post Office) was impassable to local traffic due to carriageway flooding; Stuart Close suffered significant flooding to the road and to properties; surface water runoff along Lower Road flowed into Laura Drive, and drainage infrastructure surcharged under the intense rainfall. (p20)
Properties in Swanley experienced surface water/urban flooding leading to requests for flood saxs and multiple reported incidents that generated emergency calls and follow–up gully and drainage clearance. [MUL] (p21)
Short, intense rainfall on 20 July 2021 and 20–21 October 2021 overwhelmed local surface water drainage, causing flooding to properties across the town and disruption to local roads. [MUL] (p22)
Multiple reported flood incidents were concentrated in the residential and urban streets of southern Swanley on 20th July 2021, with affected locations marked on the map. The incidents indicate localised inundation in the built–up area and disruption to streets and properties. [IMG] (p23)
Multiple reported flood incidents concentrated in the central and southern parts of Swanley, affecting residential areas and sections of major roads (locations marked as affected on the map). [IMG] (p24)","approximately 53 properties including two schools affected (20 July 2021); 30 properties flooded internally (20 July 2021); approximately 17 properties affected (20–21 October 2021); 11 properties flooded internally (20–21 October 2021) [MUL] (p2)
16 properties (sum of reported affected properties across named streets) (p5)
68 properties [MUL] (p22)",GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc.html,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc,Swanley flooding 20 Jul 2021 and Swanley flooding 20 Oct 2021 - 21 Oct 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Swanley,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Swanley-on-20-July-and-20-to-21-October-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,6,2021-07-20,51.4109307,0.1779562,"<div id=""html_fa6fd041358f7ed2a50b418b7c97b230"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hextable</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows from surrounding agricultural land to the south&ndash;east of Hextable were reported as contributing to flooding during the intense rainfall event, indicating local surface water flooding impacts. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localized surface water flooding affected roads and residential areas; map marks Laura Drive and Stuart Close as flooded, with flow lines showing runoff along Laura Drive and from Stuart Close toward Victoria Hill Road; properties requested flood saxs. [IMG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the most affected areas where surface water flooding occurred after intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage; targeted for potential SuDS improvements by KCC Flood Water Management Team to improve flood resilience. [MUL] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flood incidents occurred in outlying areas such as Hextable on 20th July 2021, including incidents located near major road junctions according to the map. [IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Isolated reported flood incidents indicated in the Hextable area (marked as affected on the map). [IMG] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 roads (Laura Drive; Stuart Close) [IMG,MUL] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Hextable,England,"Overland flows from surrounding agricultural land to the south–east of Hextable were reported as contributing to flooding during the intense rainfall event, indicating local surface water flooding impacts. (p2)
Localized surface water flooding affected roads and residential areas; map marks Laura Drive and Stuart Close as flooded, with flow lines showing runoff along Laura Drive and from Stuart Close toward Victoria Hill Road; properties requested flood saxs. [IMG,MUL] (p21)
Identified as one of the most affected areas where surface water flooding occurred after intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage; targeted for potential SuDS improvements by KCC Flood Water Management Team to improve flood resilience. [MUL] (p22)
Reported flood incidents occurred in outlying areas such as Hextable on 20th July 2021, including incidents located near major road junctions according to the map. [IMG] (p23)
Isolated reported flood incidents indicated in the Hextable area (marked as affected on the map). [IMG] (p24)","2 roads (Laura Drive; Stuart Close) [IMG,MUL] (p21)",GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc.html,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc,Swanley flooding 20 Jul 2021 and Swanley flooding 20 Oct 2021 - 21 Oct 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Swanley,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Swanley-on-20-July-and-20-to-21-October-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,6,2021-07-20,51.399285045036706,0.1650441822664466,"<div id=""html_3df1fa8d62bb91cf137a71f2de7080a4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Mary's</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff flowed NE&ndash;SW along St Mary's Road, was funnelled through the alleyway between St Mary's Road and Hewitt Place, and continued along Hart Dyke Road causing surface water drains to become overwhelmed and surcharge. Floodwater moved towards lower topography at Almond Drive and Hibbs Close where significant flooding was reported. Runoff from higher ground (Oliver Road, West Harold Drive, Sermon Drive, Lavender Hill) exacerbated flows. Local drainage (gullies and soakaways) could not accommodate the rainfall volume and surcharged. [IMG] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 75 cm flood depth estimated along the alleyway between Oliver Road and Almond Drive [IMG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,St Mary's,England,"Surface water runoff flowed NE–SW along St Mary's Road, was funnelled through the alleyway between St Mary's Road and Hewitt Place, and continued along Hart Dyke Road causing surface water drains to become overwhelmed and surcharge. Floodwater moved towards lower topography at Almond Drive and Hibbs Close where significant flooding was reported. Runoff from higher ground (Oliver Road, West Harold Drive, Sermon Drive, Lavender Hill) exacerbated flows. Local drainage (gullies and soakaways) could not accommodate the rainfall volume and surcharged. [IMG] (p14)",approximately 75 cm flood depth estimated along the alleyway between Oliver Road and Almond Drive [IMG] (p14),GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc.html,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc,Swanley flooding 20 Jul 2021 and Swanley flooding 20 Oct 2021 - 21 Oct 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Swanley,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Swanley-on-20-July-and-20-to-21-October-2021.pdf
GB-KEN,6,2021-07-20,51.3904772,0.1769789,"<div id=""html_7c6d31cad761f8ddff0f63e2e6a579c5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Firs</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within High Firs Estate, floodwater flowed generally southward and converged toward the southern boundary during the intense rainfall on 20th July 2021. Multiple estate roads and intersections were flooded (map shows affected roads), with flow lines channeling water toward the A20 and causing road and localised property/amenity impacts. [IMG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in High Firs were affected by surface water runoff managed by the balancing pond located between the A20 and south Swanley; limited pond capacity contributed to impacts and the pond was later desilted and enlarged to increase capacity. (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,High Firs,England,"Within High Firs Estate, floodwater flowed generally southward and converged toward the southern boundary during the intense rainfall on 20th July 2021. Multiple estate roads and intersections were flooded (map shows affected roads), with flow lines channeling water toward the A20 and causing road and localised property/amenity impacts. [IMG] (p16)
Properties in High Firs were affected by surface water runoff managed by the balancing pond located between the A20 and south Swanley; limited pond capacity contributed to impacts and the pond was later desilted and enlarged to increase capacity. (p21)",,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc.html,GB-KEN_6_2021-07-20_etc,Swanley flooding 20 Jul 2021 and Swanley flooding 20 Oct 2021 - 21 Oct 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Swanley,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-Swanley-on-20-July-and-20-to-21-October-2021.pdf
GB-NNH,23,2020-12-23,52.4562208,-0.6097755,"<div id=""html_7ef1df064fd4308f573dbdfeaa9d9b65"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harper's Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An ordinary tributary of Harper's Brook that forms the western boundary of an affected property overflowed and caused internal flooding to that property. The tributary discharges to Harper's Brook through a headwall with a 300mm pipe and flap valve, which is described in connection with the flooding. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Harper's Brook was at or exceeding bank capacity in places and caused minor flooding to rear gardens behind properties, contributing limited flooding impacts separate from the culvert overtopping at Mill Hill. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Higher&ndash;than&ndash;normal water levels in the brook deepened downstream of the culvert, which likely closed the flap valve in the headwall, producing a surcharged outfall and limiting the culvert/watercourse's ability to discharge&mdash;contributing to local flooding. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Harper's Brook during the event may have restricted downstream flow and contributed to the culverted watercourse exceeding capacity, worsening flooding on Mill Hill and to nearby properties on 23rd December 2020. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties experienced limited rear&ndash;garden flooding from Harper's Brook (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Harper's Brook,England,"An ordinary tributary of Harper's Brook that forms the western boundary of an affected property overflowed and caused internal flooding to that property. The tributary discharges to Harper's Brook through a headwall with a 300mm pipe and flap valve, which is described in connection with the flooding. (p14)
Harper's Brook was at or exceeding bank capacity in places and caused minor flooding to rear gardens behind properties, contributing limited flooding impacts separate from the culvert overtopping at Mill Hill. (p16)
Higher–than–normal water levels in the brook deepened downstream of the culvert, which likely closed the flap valve in the headwall, producing a surcharged outfall and limiting the culvert/watercourse's ability to discharge—contributing to local flooding. (p17)
High water levels in Harper's Brook during the event may have restricted downstream flow and contributed to the culverted watercourse exceeding capacity, worsening flooding on Mill Hill and to nearby properties on 23rd December 2020. (p19)","1 property (p14)
2 properties experienced limited rear–garden flooding from Harper's Brook (p16)",GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23,Little Oakley flooding 23 Dec 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Oakley, 23 December 2020",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Little Oakley.pdf
GB-NNH,23,2020-12-23,52.2182267,-0.9432669,"<div id=""html_0a828dde6b0bfaafa8dd92deafbe36aa"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wootton Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff within the Wootton Brook catchment contributed to increased flows during the county&ndash;wide heavy rainfall event, increasing flood risk to Little Oakley and prompting a funding bid to test natural flood management measures to reduce flows into the village. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Wootton Brook,England,"Runoff within the Wootton Brook catchment contributed to increased flows during the county–wide heavy rainfall event, increasing flood risk to Little Oakley and prompting a funding bid to test natural flood management measures to reduce flows into the village. (p18)",,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23,Little Oakley flooding 23 Dec 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Oakley, 23 December 2020",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Little Oakley.pdf
GB-NNH,23,2020-12-23,52.4581788,-0.7212391,"<div id=""html_d6549f39ae606ac35be84982266d5d8d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mill Hill, Great Oakley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurring on the carriageway of Mill Hill near an affected property; this was associated with internal flooding to one property and flooding of external areas of other properties nearby. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carriageway flooding reported frequently near the affected property. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in the watercourse north of Mill Hill exceeded the capacity of the culvert beneath the road, causing the watercourse to overtop and flood the road. Floodwater depth on the carriageway increased and flowed toward an adjacent property, resulting in internal flooding of that property. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in the upstream watercourse exceeded the capacity of the culvert under Mill Hill, overtopped the watercourse and flooded the carriageway. As water depth increased on the road it flowed east toward properties causing internal flooding of one property and external flooding to an adjacent property (south) and two properties on the north side. Blocked highway gullies to the east likely increased ponding at the low spot near the watercourse. [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Standing water collected on the carriageway to depths that impeded use and reached nearby property. Highway drainage east of the property was blocked which likely increased flood volume at the low spot; culvert capacity was exceeded causing surcharged outfall and limiting discharge into the downstream watercourse. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water overtopped onto Mill Hill Road (sic) during the heavy rainfall on 23rd December 2020 and flowed to low points around residential properties, causing localised surface water flooding. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internal flooding; 1 adjacent property external flooding (south side); 2 properties external flooding (north side); carriageway standing water 400&ndash;500mm; water ~150&ndash;200mm around affected property [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>standing water depths of 400&ndash;500 mm on the carriageway; 100&ndash;200 mm around the affected property (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Mill Hill, Great Oakley",England,"Flooding occurring on the carriageway of Mill Hill near an affected property; this was associated with internal flooding to one property and flooding of external areas of other properties nearby. [PAG] (p10)
Carriageway flooding reported frequently near the affected property. [PAG] (p11)
Flows in the watercourse north of Mill Hill exceeded the capacity of the culvert beneath the road, causing the watercourse to overtop and flood the road. Floodwater depth on the carriageway increased and flowed toward an adjacent property, resulting in internal flooding of that property. [PAG] (p15)
Flows in the upstream watercourse exceeded the capacity of the culvert under Mill Hill, overtopped the watercourse and flooded the carriageway. As water depth increased on the road it flowed east toward properties causing internal flooding of one property and external flooding to an adjacent property (south) and two properties on the north side. Blocked highway gullies to the east likely increased ponding at the low spot near the watercourse. [PAG] (p16)
Standing water collected on the carriageway to depths that impeded use and reached nearby property. Highway drainage east of the property was blocked which likely increased flood volume at the low spot; culvert capacity was exceeded causing surcharged outfall and limiting discharge into the downstream watercourse. (p17)
Water overtopped onto Mill Hill Road (sic) during the heavy rainfall on 23rd December 2020 and flowed to low points around residential properties, causing localised surface water flooding. (p19)","1 property [PAG] (p10)
1 property [PAG] (p15)
1 property internal flooding; 1 adjacent property external flooding (south side); 2 properties external flooding (north side); carriageway standing water 400–500mm; water ~150–200mm around affected property [PAG] (p16)
standing water depths of 400–500 mm on the carriageway; 100–200 mm around the affected property (p17)",GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23,Little Oakley flooding 23 Dec 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Oakley, 23 December 2020",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Little Oakley.pdf
GB-NNH,23,2020-12-23,52.4667351,-0.6810123,"<div id=""html_903e2ac5c5a3d2dfc26ed5189bf67fab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Featherbed Lane, Little Oakley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ran down Featherbed Lane (a farm track ~300 m east) and flowed onto Mill Hill, bypassing blocked road gullies and contributing to the volume of water collecting at the low spot by the watercourse, increasing flooding on Mill Hill and toward properties. (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300 metres (distance from affected area referenced in report) (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Featherbed Lane, Little Oakley",England,"Surface water ran down Featherbed Lane (a farm track ~300 m east) and flowed onto Mill Hill, bypassing blocked road gullies and contributing to the volume of water collecting at the low spot by the watercourse, increasing flooding on Mill Hill and toward properties. (p16)",300 metres (distance from affected area referenced in report) (p16),GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23,Little Oakley flooding 23 Dec 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Oakley, 23 December 2020",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Little Oakley.pdf
GB-NNH,23,2020-12-23,52.4175503,-0.6431175,"<div id=""html_fb4e8230805ce475d18a391a1933310c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The local authority was involved in coordinating and directing responses after the surface water flooding event, including planning&ndash;team actions, highway drainage cleansing and inspections, and working with the LLFA and other parties to prepare and share drainage asset plans and public information. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,North Northamptonshire,England,"The local authority was involved in coordinating and directing responses after the surface water flooding event, including planning–team actions, highway drainage cleansing and inspections, and working with the LLFA and other parties to prepare and share drainage asset plans and public information. (p24)",,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23,Little Oakley flooding 23 Dec 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Oakley, 23 December 2020",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Little Oakley.pdf
GB-NNH,23,2020-12-23,52.461301,-0.688008,"<div id=""html_17fa6f6914fc8f2b44e4319963bc73f6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Oakley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dwellings to the east of Little Oakley experienced overland surface water flow when drainage systems were unable to cope; this led to water flowing to low points in and around properties and internal flooding of at least one building on multiple occasions. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding occurred to one building (this has happened on more than one occasion within the last five years). External flooding entered gardens/surrounding areas and restricted access to at least three other dwellings. [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding to the east of the village associated with a tributary of Harper's Brook; one property experienced internal flooding and external areas of other properties were flooded. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense heavy rainfall on 23rd December 2020 caused the culverted watercourse beneath Mill Hill to exceed capacity, leading to overtopping and surface water flowing along natural contours to low points around residential properties in Little Oakley. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one building; more than one occasion within the last five years (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 building; 3 dwellings [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Little Oakley,England,"Dwellings to the east of Little Oakley experienced overland surface water flow when drainage systems were unable to cope; this led to water flowing to low points in and around properties and internal flooding of at least one building on multiple occasions. (p6)
Internal flooding occurred to one building (this has happened on more than one occasion within the last five years). External flooding entered gardens/surrounding areas and restricted access to at least three other dwellings. [MUL] (p8)
Flooding to the east of the village associated with a tributary of Harper's Brook; one property experienced internal flooding and external areas of other properties were flooded. (p11)
Intense heavy rainfall on 23rd December 2020 caused the culverted watercourse beneath Mill Hill to exceed capacity, leading to overtopping and surface water flowing along natural contours to low points around residential properties in Little Oakley. (p19)","one building; more than one occasion within the last five years (p6)
1 building; 3 dwellings [MUL] (p8)
1 property (p11)",GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-NNH_23_2020-12-23,Little Oakley flooding 23 Dec 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Little Oakley, 23 December 2020",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Little Oakley.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.170582,0.3474234,"<div id=""html_909e91c96281d798f4159a439c2ef9d5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alder Stream</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels rose quickly and overtopped the riverbanks; this overtopping contributed to overbank and overland flow into Five Oak Green, causing flooding of properties. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid rise in flows in the Alder Stream during intense rainfall on 9 February 2020, causing localised flooding in the Five Oak Green catchment. Peak flow on the Alder Stream was reported earlier (approximately 18:30&ndash;19:30) than peaks recorded on nearby gauged rivers. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Alder Stream was the locus of fluvial flood risk in Five Oak Green; areas adjacent to the stream (including along Alders Road and through the centre of Five Oak Green) experienced flooding consistent with reported properties affected during Storm Ciara. Flows are noted as being well contained within the watercourse upstream of Capel, but surface water flow paths and local depressions also contributed to flooding in the village. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the Alder Stream overtopped banks during the flood event and contributed to flooding that affected the nearby settlement of Five Oak Green. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During Storm Ciara (9th February 2020) intense evening rainfall caused flows in Alder Stream to overtop its banks. Overtopping occurred upstream of the culvert under Five Oak Green Road; peak flows overtopped the channel and contributed to floodwater entering the locality while highway drainage and the local sewer network became overwhelmed and could not discharge to the stream. [IMG,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Alder Stream overtopped its banks upstream of the culvert beneath Five Oak Green Road (overtopping reported at approximately 20:00). The overtopping caused the local surface water drainage system to be unable to discharge to the watercourse, leading to surcharging of drains and surface water flooding of roads and properties in central Five Oak Green. [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopped its banks during Storm Ciara (9 Feb 2020), producing high river levels that contributed to local drainage systems surcharging and to flooding in downstream areas. (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 40 properties (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths up to approximately 30 cm internally (reported in main area of Five Oak Green) [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Alder Stream,England,"River levels rose quickly and overtopped the riverbanks; this overtopping contributed to overbank and overland flow into Five Oak Green, causing flooding of properties. (p1)
Rapid rise in flows in the Alder Stream during intense rainfall on 9 February 2020, causing localised flooding in the Five Oak Green catchment. Peak flow on the Alder Stream was reported earlier (approximately 18:30–19:30) than peaks recorded on nearby gauged rivers. (p5)
The Alder Stream was the locus of fluvial flood risk in Five Oak Green; areas adjacent to the stream (including along Alders Road and through the centre of Five Oak Green) experienced flooding consistent with reported properties affected during Storm Ciara. Flows are noted as being well contained within the watercourse upstream of Capel, but surface water flow paths and local depressions also contributed to flooding in the village. (p8)
High flows in the Alder Stream overtopped banks during the flood event and contributed to flooding that affected the nearby settlement of Five Oak Green. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
During Storm Ciara (9th February 2020) intense evening rainfall caused flows in Alder Stream to overtop its banks. Overtopping occurred upstream of the culvert under Five Oak Green Road; peak flows overtopped the channel and contributed to floodwater entering the locality while highway drainage and the local sewer network became overwhelmed and could not discharge to the stream. [IMG,PAG] (p12)
Alder Stream overtopped its banks upstream of the culvert beneath Five Oak Green Road (overtopping reported at approximately 20:00). The overtopping caused the local surface water drainage system to be unable to discharge to the watercourse, leading to surcharging of drains and surface water flooding of roads and properties in central Five Oak Green. [IMG,PAG] (p13)
Overtopped its banks during Storm Ciara (9 Feb 2020), producing high river levels that contributed to local drainage systems surcharging and to flooding in downstream areas. (p16)","approximately 40 properties (p1)
flood depths up to approximately 30 cm internally (reported in main area of Five Oak Green) [IMG,PAG] (p13)",GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.2513552,0.3138054,"<div id=""html_bee26842c91454838fd889afb3df8d98"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Bourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Bourne recorded a steep rise in flows on 9th February 2020 due to heavy rainfall associated with Storm Ciara; the gauge data show a pronounced peak in flow between 22:00 and 22:30. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow gauges recorded a steep rise in river flows during the storm, with a pronounced hydrological response in the River Bourne: flow rose sharply from the afternoon and reached a peak late in the evening. [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,River Bourne,England,"River Bourne recorded a steep rise in flows on 9th February 2020 due to heavy rainfall associated with Storm Ciara; the gauge data show a pronounced peak in flow between 22:00 and 22:30. [PAG] (p5)
Flow gauges recorded a steep rise in river flows during the storm, with a pronounced hydrological response in the River Bourne: flow rose sharply from the afternoon and reached a peak late in the evening. [IMG,PAG] (p6)",,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.1955172,0.0680555,"<div id=""html_28349d38a12df302a55ae40c97600c64"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Eden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency gauge data on the River Eden show a steep rise in river flows on 9th February 2020 as a result of the heavy rainfall from Storm Ciara. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow gauge data for the River Eden show increased river flows during Storm Ciara on 9 February 2020 (hydrological response to heavy rainfall). [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flow remained low and stable for most of 9 February 2020 then rose sharply in the late afternoon and evening during Storm Ciara, indicating a strong hydrological response to the storm. [IMG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,River Eden,England,"Environment Agency gauge data on the River Eden show a steep rise in river flows on 9th February 2020 as a result of the heavy rainfall from Storm Ciara. (p5)
Flow gauge data for the River Eden show increased river flows during Storm Ciara on 9 February 2020 (hydrological response to heavy rainfall). [IMG,PAG] (p6)
River flow remained low and stable for most of 9 February 2020 then rose sharply in the late afternoon and evening during Storm Ciara, indicating a strong hydrological response to the storm. [IMG] (p7)",,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.1653225,0.6190017,"<div id=""html_9183e731e121928c0c025b8a0113c346"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Beult</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gauge data on the River Beult show a large increase in flows on 9th February 2020 driven by the heavy rainfall during Storm Ciara, reflecting elevated river levels in the area. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flow was low and steady for most of 9 February 2020 then increased rapidly in the evening during Storm Ciara, showing a significant hydrological response to the storm. [IMG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,River Beult,England,"Gauge data on the River Beult show a large increase in flows on 9th February 2020 driven by the heavy rainfall during Storm Ciara, reflecting elevated river levels in the area. (p5)
River flow was low and steady for most of 9 February 2020 then increased rapidly in the evening during Storm Ciara, showing a significant hydrological response to the storm. [IMG] (p7)",,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.1762879,0.3386301,"<div id=""html_7ca57c3907b6398817ff7f9ed0a5e8b5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alders Road, Capel</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>9 properties flooded internally, including 1 commercial property; properties reported up to 30 cm of floodwater inside. [TAB] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas along Alders Road adjacent to the Alder Stream are within Flood Zone 3 and were part of the high&ndash;risk surface&ndash;water flow paths that experienced flooding during Storm Ciara; a ditch and overland flow to the south&ndash;west contributed to runoff into the Alder Stream, impacting the road corridor and nearby properties. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along Alders Road flooded from fluvial sources; floodwater entered the rear of properties causing internal flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along Alders Road were flooded from fluvial sources when a tributary of the Alder Stream and an adjacent ditch became overwhelmed. Floodwaters entered the rear of properties and reached internal depths of approximately 20 to 30 cm. [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm Ciara&ndash;induced runoff caused problems in highway drainage on Alders Road; KCC carried out gully cleansing, identified drainage defects and undertook ditch works with further investigation required. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties; 1 commercial property [TAB] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 to 30 cm internal flood depth [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 to 30 cm (internal flood depth) [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Alders Road, Capel",England,"9 properties flooded internally, including 1 commercial property; properties reported up to 30 cm of floodwater inside. [TAB] (p3)
Areas along Alders Road adjacent to the Alder Stream are within Flood Zone 3 and were part of the high–risk surface–water flow paths that experienced flooding during Storm Ciara; a ditch and overland flow to the south–west contributed to runoff into the Alder Stream, impacting the road corridor and nearby properties. (p8)
Properties along Alders Road flooded from fluvial sources; floodwater entered the rear of properties causing internal flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p13)
Properties along Alders Road were flooded from fluvial sources when a tributary of the Alder Stream and an adjacent ditch became overwhelmed. Floodwaters entered the rear of properties and reached internal depths of approximately 20 to 30 cm. [IMG,PAG] (p14)
Storm Ciara–induced runoff caused problems in highway drainage on Alders Road; KCC carried out gully cleansing, identified drainage defects and undertook ditch works with further investigation required. [TAB] (p15)","9 properties; 1 commercial property [TAB] (p3)
20 to 30 cm internal flood depth [IMG,PAG] (p13)
20 to 30 cm (internal flood depth) [IMG,PAG] (p14)",GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
