GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.189342,0.3702966,"<div id=""html_2da56192e5907fe09ec4bda3ec25dec8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Whetsted Road, Whetsted</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties experienced internal flooding, affecting both domestic and commercial premises. [TAB] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At the junction of Whetsted Road and Badsell Road surface water overwhelmed the highway drainage and surface water sewer; water ponded at a topographical low on the highway to a depth sufficient to enter property. Failure of the pumping station was reported to have contributed to flooding at this location. (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties flooded internally (1 domestic, 1 commercial) [TAB] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Whetsted Road, Whetsted",England,"Properties experienced internal flooding, affecting both domestic and commercial premises. [TAB] (p3)
At the junction of Whetsted Road and Badsell Road surface water overwhelmed the highway drainage and surface water sewer; water ponded at a topographical low on the highway to a depth sufficient to enter property. Failure of the pumping station was reported to have contributed to flooding at this location. (p14)","2 properties flooded internally (1 domestic, 1 commercial) [TAB] (p3)",GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.18284487700301,0.3530497274129059,"<div id=""html_e1d4b667856de52a8d71fc51513764a3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brookdene, Five Oak Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple properties were affected by flooding; one confirmed property experienced internal flooding. [TAB] (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 confirmed property [TAB] (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Brookdene, Five Oak Green",England,Multiple properties were affected by flooding; one confirmed property experienced internal flooding. [TAB] (p3),1 confirmed property [TAB] (p3),GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.1722938,0.3591578,"<div id=""html_de6c1ccfc297b2560e6188b3edec0709"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Colt's Hill, Capel</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along Alders Road (near Colt/Colt's Hill) were internally flooded when a tributary of the Alder Stream and an adjacent ditch became overwhelmed; floodwater entered the rear of properties. Environment Agency site visits support the tributary/ditch (not the main Alder Stream) as the cause. [IMG] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths of approximately 20 to 30 cm internally [IMG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Colt's Hill, Capel",England,Properties along Alders Road (near Colt/Colt's Hill) were internally flooded when a tributary of the Alder Stream and an adjacent ditch became overwhelmed; floodwater entered the rear of properties. Environment Agency site visits support the tributary/ditch (not the main Alder Stream) as the cause. [IMG] (p14),flood depths of approximately 20 to 30 cm internally [IMG] (p14),GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.1729253,0.3812515,"<div id=""html_974ecb063ab526ab16862882a78dd9a7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Badsell Road, Paddock Wood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Badsell Road were internally flooded with sewage after the nearby Larkfield pumping station stopped operating during power cuts caused by Storm Ciara; the pumping failure caused sewage to back up and flood nearby properties. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overwhelmed the highway drainage and surface water sewer at the junction with Whetsted Road; water accumulated and ponded at a topographical low point and reached sufficient depth to enter property. Failure of the Larkfield pumping station contributed to the highway flooding at this location. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Badsell Road, Paddock Wood",England,"Properties on Badsell Road were internally flooded with sewage after the nearby Larkfield pumping station stopped operating during power cuts caused by Storm Ciara; the pumping failure caused sewage to back up and flood nearby properties. (p14)
Surface water overwhelmed the highway drainage and surface water sewer at the junction with Whetsted Road; water accumulated and ponded at a topographical low point and reached sufficient depth to enter property. Failure of the Larkfield pumping station contributed to the highway flooding at this location. [TAB,PAG] (p15)",,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.2070749,0.7210362,"<div id=""html_322db5f2e5cc2e57371abe8a3413cced"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A particularly intense band of rain fell across Kent on 9 February 2020 causing rapid river level rises (Alder Stream overtopping), highway drains, ditches and the combined sewer network to be unable to cope, resulting in flooding to Five Oak Green and surrounding areas. (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 40 properties (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Kent,England,"A particularly intense band of rain fell across Kent on 9 February 2020 causing rapid river level rises (Alder Stream overtopping), highway drains, ditches and the combined sewer network to be unable to cope, resulting in flooding to Five Oak Green and surrounding areas. (p1)",approximately 40 properties (p1),GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.183888,0.3568368,"<div id=""html_7886d94d641571ba9a8d4d914efe5ea0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Five Oak Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A concentrated cluster of reported flood incidents (map shows majority clustered in and around Five Oak Green) &mdash; residents were surveyed in September 2020 to collate information about the flood extents and impacts in the area. [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding in Five Oak Green caused by rapid response of the Alder Stream to intense, locally exceptional rainfall during Storm Ciara. Flooding of this magnitude was restricted to Five Oak Green within the county. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During Storm Ciara the village experienced flooding driven by surface water runoff and fluvial overflow of the Alder Stream. Flood extents in the village matched high&ndash;risk areas on Environment Agency maps and locations of reported properties affected by the Storm Ciara event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Five Oak Green has experienced fluvial flooding associated with the River Medway. The report records multiple historic flood events affecting the settlement and indicates it was affected by the flood event on 9 February 2020. [IMG,PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The settlement experienced river (fluvial) flooding associated with the Alder Stream during the event; properties in the village were affected by overtopping flows. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 9th February 2020 (Storm Ciara) intense rainfall led to Alder Stream overtopping through the centre of Five Oak Green and, concurrently, highway drainage and the local sewer network were overwhelmed and unable to discharge into the stream. Properties located along Alders Road were affected by the resulting flooding. Local natural flood management measures in the catchment were operating but were limited in extent and not considered likely to have significantly reduced flooding at Five Oak Green. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water drainage in the main area of Five Oak Green surcharged and ponded because the watercourse overtopped, resulting in surface water flooding of local roads and internal flooding of properties (water entered properties and ponded around them). Fire and rescue attended multiple streets but could not pump properties because there was nowhere for surplus water to be discharged; the police implemented a road closure on Five Oak Road. [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding during Storm Ciara (9 Feb 2020). Highway drains and the local combined sewer network were unable to cope; the culvert beneath Five Oak Green may have contributed to the flooding (potential surcharging of the culvert). (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flood depths up to approximately 30 cm internally [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Five Oak Green,England,"A concentrated cluster of reported flood incidents (map shows majority clustered in and around Five Oak Green) — residents were surveyed in September 2020 to collate information about the flood extents and impacts in the area. [IMG] (p2)
Localised flooding in Five Oak Green caused by rapid response of the Alder Stream to intense, locally exceptional rainfall during Storm Ciara. Flooding of this magnitude was restricted to Five Oak Green within the county. [PAG] (p5)
During Storm Ciara the village experienced flooding driven by surface water runoff and fluvial overflow of the Alder Stream. Flood extents in the village matched high–risk areas on Environment Agency maps and locations of reported properties affected by the Storm Ciara event. (p8)
Five Oak Green has experienced fluvial flooding associated with the River Medway. The report records multiple historic flood events affecting the settlement and indicates it was affected by the flood event on 9 February 2020. [IMG,PAG] (p9)
The settlement experienced river (fluvial) flooding associated with the Alder Stream during the event; properties in the village were affected by overtopping flows. [IMG] (p11)
On 9th February 2020 (Storm Ciara) intense rainfall led to Alder Stream overtopping through the centre of Five Oak Green and, concurrently, highway drainage and the local sewer network were overwhelmed and unable to discharge into the stream. Properties located along Alders Road were affected by the resulting flooding. Local natural flood management measures in the catchment were operating but were limited in extent and not considered likely to have significantly reduced flooding at Five Oak Green. [PAG] (p12)
Surface water drainage in the main area of Five Oak Green surcharged and ponded because the watercourse overtopped, resulting in surface water flooding of local roads and internal flooding of properties (water entered properties and ponded around them). Fire and rescue attended multiple streets but could not pump properties because there was nowhere for surplus water to be discharged; the police implemented a road closure on Five Oak Road. [IMG,PAG] (p13)
Experienced flooding during Storm Ciara (9 Feb 2020). Highway drains and the local combined sewer network were unable to cope; the culvert beneath Five Oak Green may have contributed to the flooding (potential surcharging of the culvert). (p16)","flood depths up to approximately 30 cm internally [IMG,PAG] (p13)",GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-KEN,14,2020-02-09,51.1755296,0.3411531,"<div id=""html_cc082de11d9b82523f5904016ab542f5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Capel</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Several outlying reported flood incidents occurred in Capel and along connecting roads associated with the same storm event; these were included in the survey and map of reported incidents. [IMG] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Capel,England,Several outlying reported flood incidents occurred in Capel and along connecting roads associated with the same storm event; these were included in the survey and map of reported incidents. [IMG] (p2),,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09.html,GB-KEN_14_2020-02-09,Five Oak Green flooding 09 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Five Oak Green,063/Kent/Section-19-flooding-report-for-Five-Oak-Green.pdf
GB-NNH,30,2020-08-16,52.3904433,-0.7141689,"<div id=""html_2a16329ecf002000db70097296002c67"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kettering Rugby Club</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the rugby club grounds was reported to have flowed toward Waverley Road and contributed to overtopping of the kerb and subsequent flooding of properties on Waverley Road. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The low point at the junction with the Rugby Club entrance experienced overtopping; the manhole cover to the East Brook culvert just inside the Rugby Club entrance was surcharged and was discharging floodwater to surface. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The gully/ surface water connection at the Rugby Club entrance was not connected into the Anglian Water system (it abutted externally to the 300mm system) and had been damaged; this restriction prevented free discharge of surface water into the main sewer and contributed to drainage problems. The connection has since been repaired. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Rugby Club's surface water connection had been damaged (restricting discharge into the main surface water sewer) and the club entrance was included in the area where blocked gullies contributed to flooding; the damaged connection was investigated and subsequently repaired following coordination between AWS and Local Authorities. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharge flooding from an East Brook manhole at the Rugby Club entrance far exceeded system capacity, overwhelming the surface water drainage. The gully connection from the Rugby Club was not connected to a suitable discharge system and was unable to convey surface water effectively; overtopping occurred over dropped kerbs and the footpath at the entrance. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water reached the club entrance/driveway area; photographs show water on the paved entrance and nearby manhole covers, indicating the entrance was affected by the flooding and access was likely impeded. [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The vehicle entrance/manhole within the Rugby Club showed significant surface damage, cracking and vertical displacement around the manhole with water pooling on the surface, indicating inundation and structural damage to the entrance associated with drainage/culvert failure. [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300mm surface water system (adjacent) [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 gullies (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Kettering Rugby Club,England,"Surface water runoff from the rugby club grounds was reported to have flowed toward Waverley Road and contributed to overtopping of the kerb and subsequent flooding of properties on Waverley Road. (p17)
The low point at the junction with the Rugby Club entrance experienced overtopping; the manhole cover to the East Brook culvert just inside the Rugby Club entrance was surcharged and was discharging floodwater to surface. (p18)
The gully/ surface water connection at the Rugby Club entrance was not connected into the Anglian Water system (it abutted externally to the 300mm system) and had been damaged; this restriction prevented free discharge of surface water into the main sewer and contributed to drainage problems. The connection has since been repaired. [PAG] (p19)
The Rugby Club's surface water connection had been damaged (restricting discharge into the main surface water sewer) and the club entrance was included in the area where blocked gullies contributed to flooding; the damaged connection was investigated and subsequently repaired following coordination between AWS and Local Authorities. (p20)
Surcharge flooding from an East Brook manhole at the Rugby Club entrance far exceeded system capacity, overwhelming the surface water drainage. The gully connection from the Rugby Club was not connected to a suitable discharge system and was unable to convey surface water effectively; overtopping occurred over dropped kerbs and the footpath at the entrance. (p21)
Surface water reached the club entrance/driveway area; photographs show water on the paved entrance and nearby manhole covers, indicating the entrance was affected by the flooding and access was likely impeded. [IMG,PAG] (p36)
The vehicle entrance/manhole within the Rugby Club showed significant surface damage, cracking and vertical displacement around the manhole with water pooling on the surface, indicating inundation and structural damage to the entrance associated with drainage/culvert failure. [IMG,PAG] (p37)","300mm surface water system (adjacent) [PAG] (p19)
3 gullies (p20)",GB-NNH_30_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_30_2020-08-16,Kettering flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Waverley Road, Kettering, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Waverley Road, Kettering.pdf"
GB-NNH,30,2020-08-16,52.391895,-0.7143808,"<div id=""html_aa5a95335784932c19b1b4f3c1490c66"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Waverley Road, Kettering</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around properties on Waverley Road. This led to internal flooding of a garage that is integral to the main occupied building; the garage has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to a garage that is integral to the main occupied building; the garage has flooded on multiple occasions (including the incident that triggered this investigation). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Relief culvert under Waverley Road has unclear ownership; a 225mm diameter surface water public sewer runs beneath Waverley Road and was implicated in the incident when the adjacent 300mm pipe was severed and connected to East Brook, altering the drainage connection in the Waverley Road corridor. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from intense rainfall overwhelmed a road gully and exceeded the dropped kerb, flowing across the footpath and into an integral garage. One property on Waverley Road flooded internally and other properties experienced flooding to their rear gardens. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed over dropped kerbs and down through the integral garage at 52 Waverley Road, then into the rear garden where depth increased and water spread northwards into neighbouring rear gardens and properties. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties at the rear of Waverley Road were affected by the surcharging East Brook culvert; the severed/misconnected surface water sewer discharges directly into the culvert, and lids on the relief culvert have been observed to blow off on Waverley Road when the East Brook is surcharged. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The road flooded when intense heavy rainfall exceeded surface water drainage capacity. The East Brook culvert in Waverley Road surcharged and flooded out at locations where the culvert cross&ndash;sectional area reduced, causing manholes to surcharge and water to flow over ground into low points around nearby properties. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water was present on the carriageway and adjacent driveway as documented in photographs; the wet pavement at the street entrance indicates Waverley Road was affected by surface water/urban flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from a failed/blocked drainage route pooled at the road end and was associated with a collapsed area adjacent to inspection covers and a gully, indicating drainage failure and local inundation affecting the carriageway/entrance area. [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than one occasion in the last five years (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 garage (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>225mm diameter surface water public sewer; 225mm diameter foul water public sewer; 300mm diameter pipe severed [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property flooded internally (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 integral garage flooded (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300mm surface water sewer [PAG] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Waverley Road, Kettering",England,"Intense, short–duration rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around properties on Waverley Road. This led to internal flooding of a garage that is integral to the main occupied building; the garage has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p6)
Internal flooding to a garage that is integral to the main occupied building; the garage has flooded on multiple occasions (including the incident that triggered this investigation). (p8)
Relief culvert under Waverley Road has unclear ownership; a 225mm diameter surface water public sewer runs beneath Waverley Road and was implicated in the incident when the adjacent 300mm pipe was severed and connected to East Brook, altering the drainage connection in the Waverley Road corridor. [PAG] (p15)
Surface water from intense rainfall overwhelmed a road gully and exceeded the dropped kerb, flowing across the footpath and into an integral garage. One property on Waverley Road flooded internally and other properties experienced flooding to their rear gardens. (p17)
Surface water flowed over dropped kerbs and down through the integral garage at 52 Waverley Road, then into the rear garden where depth increased and water spread northwards into neighbouring rear gardens and properties. (p18)
Properties at the rear of Waverley Road were affected by the surcharging East Brook culvert; the severed/misconnected surface water sewer discharges directly into the culvert, and lids on the relief culvert have been observed to blow off on Waverley Road when the East Brook is surcharged. [PAG] (p19)
The road flooded when intense heavy rainfall exceeded surface water drainage capacity. The East Brook culvert in Waverley Road surcharged and flooded out at locations where the culvert cross–sectional area reduced, causing manholes to surcharge and water to flow over ground into low points around nearby properties. (p21)
Surface water was present on the carriageway and adjacent driveway as documented in photographs; the wet pavement at the street entrance indicates Waverley Road was affected by surface water/urban flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p36)
Surface water from a failed/blocked drainage route pooled at the road end and was associated with a collapsed area adjacent to inspection covers and a gully, indicating drainage failure and local inundation affecting the carriageway/entrance area. [IMG,PAG] (p37)","more than one occasion in the last five years (p6)
1 garage (p8)
225mm diameter surface water public sewer; 225mm diameter foul water public sewer; 300mm diameter pipe severed [PAG] (p15)
1 property flooded internally (p17)
1 integral garage flooded (p18)
300mm surface water sewer [PAG] (p19)",GB-NNH_30_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_30_2020-08-16,Kettering flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Waverley Road, Kettering, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Waverley Road, Kettering.pdf"
GB-NNH,30,2020-08-16,52.3994233,-0.728004,"<div id=""html_4b6b892051889afe3d1cff8bbc72db09"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kettering</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A surface water flood incident occurred in Kettering (at Waverley Road) on 16 August 2020 when intense heavy rainfall and ineffective surface water drainage caused excess water to flow to low points around properties, triggering a formal Section 19 investigation. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contained a flood incident on Waverley Road (16 August 2020) that caused internal flooding to an integral garage at a property on Waverley Road. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The flood&ndash;risk map for the urban area of Kettering shows a corridor of surface&ndash;water flood extent running through the town; a specific area is highlighted (red circle) where high/medium surface&ndash;water flood extent intersects residential streets and a playing field. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Kettering (notably Waverley Road) experienced surface water flooding when intense heavy rainfall overwhelmed local drainage systems, resulting in excess surface water flowing over ground to low points around properties. (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 garage (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Kettering,England,"A surface water flood incident occurred in Kettering (at Waverley Road) on 16 August 2020 when intense heavy rainfall and ineffective surface water drainage caused excess water to flow to low points around properties, triggering a formal Section 19 investigation. (p6)
Contained a flood incident on Waverley Road (16 August 2020) that caused internal flooding to an integral garage at a property on Waverley Road. (p8)
The flood–risk map for the urban area of Kettering shows a corridor of surface–water flood extent running through the town; a specific area is highlighted (red circle) where high/medium surface–water flood extent intersects residential streets and a playing field. [IMG] (p12)
Parts of Kettering (notably Waverley Road) experienced surface water flooding when intense heavy rainfall overwhelmed local drainage systems, resulting in excess surface water flowing over ground to low points around properties. (p21)",1 garage (p8),GB-NNH_30_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_30_2020-08-16,Kettering flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Waverley Road, Kettering, 16 August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood investigation report - Waverley Road, Kettering.pdf"
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,54.0145848,-2.8059849,"<div id=""html_1216a918a28a93869c7cf7066e621baa"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Burrow Beck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from Burrow Beck combined with other overtopping watercourses to produce high volumes of water that encroached into homes and outbuildings and obstructed highways in the Galgate/Ellel area. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Burrow Beck,England,Flooding from Burrow Beck combined with other overtopping watercourses to produce high volumes of water that encroached into homes and outbuildings and obstructed highways in the Galgate/Ellel area. (p13),,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,54.1044696,-2.9712714,"<div id=""html_d4e07a5bbfe8e2b31b2f5af904fbd495"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Morecambe Bay</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High tide in Morecambe Bay impeded run&ndash;off through westerly surface water drainage systems, restricting drainage outfalls to sea and contributing to surface water flooding that persisted into the daytime on 23 November until low tide allowed drainage to resume. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Morecambe Bay,England,"High tide in Morecambe Bay impeded run–off through westerly surface water drainage systems, restricting drainage outfalls to sea and contributing to surface water flooding that persisted into the daytime on 23 November until low tide allowed drainage to resume. (p12)",,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,53.8179442,-3.0509812,"<div id=""html_0a7b76219cc8f78736b22f2500429042"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Blackpool Unitary Authority</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage and caused extensive surface water flooding; sewer flooding occurred across the Blackpool area (United Utilities worked through the night to gain control). (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Landfall of the extreme rainfall on the Blackpool coast produced localised flooding. Flooding was associated with power outages (reported as a consequence of localised flooding), and interruptions to pumping operations; drainage outfalls to sea were impeded by a high tide so surface water flooding persisted into the daytime of 23 November. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Power supply issues caused intermittent interruptions to pumping operations along the Fylde coast; the effects of those interruptions were felt most significantly in Blackpool, contributing to flooding impacts in the coastal/urban area. [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>500 properties lost power (across Blackpool, Thornton&ndash;Cleveleys and Poulton) (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Blackpool Unitary Authority,England,"Intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage and caused extensive surface water flooding; sewer flooding occurred across the Blackpool area (United Utilities worked through the night to gain control). (p3)
Landfall of the extreme rainfall on the Blackpool coast produced localised flooding. Flooding was associated with power outages (reported as a consequence of localised flooding), and interruptions to pumping operations; drainage outfalls to sea were impeded by a high tide so surface water flooding persisted into the daytime of 23 November. (p12)
Power supply issues caused intermittent interruptions to pumping operations along the Fylde coast; the effects of those interruptions were felt most significantly in Blackpool, contributing to flooding impacts in the coastal/urban area. [IMG,PAG] (p13)","500 properties lost power (across Blackpool, Thornton–Cleveleys and Poulton) (p12)",GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,54.2230168,-2.6021945,"<div id=""html_cc6b43842046569131b336e72883e689"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Lune</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Lune overtopped and generated river flooding as the track of the heaviest rainfall reached the River Lune; this contributed to flooding in the Lune Valley impacting locations such as Caton and Halton. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,River Lune,England,The River Lune overtopped and generated river flooding as the track of the heaviest rainfall reached the River Lune; this contributed to flooding in the Lune Valley impacting locations such as Caton and Halton. (p12),,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,54.009267,-2.771252,"<div id=""html_463185c8b579d4cb5239d91a8baa5ad8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Conder</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Conder contributed to high volumes of water that encroached into homes and outbuildings and obstructed highways in the nearby Ellel and Galgate area. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,River Conder,England,Flooding from the River Conder contributed to high volumes of water that encroached into homes and outbuildings and obstructed highways in the nearby Ellel and Galgate area. (p13),,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,54.04133154198799,-2.7654051465975726,"<div id=""html_d524fe83da840199962d847dbd43c6c5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancaster District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The intense storm travelled to the north&ndash;easterly extent of Lancaster District; extreme downpours overwhelmed natural and constructed drainage, causing extensive surface water and river flooding. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Particularly damaging flooding affected Lancaster district when the extreme rainfall of 22/23 November 2017 overwhelmed drainage networks. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lancaster District,England,"The intense storm travelled to the north–easterly extent of Lancaster District; extreme downpours overwhelmed natural and constructed drainage, causing extensive surface water and river flooding. (p3)
Particularly damaging flooding affected Lancaster district when the extreme rainfall of 22/23 November 2017 overwhelmed drainage networks. (p18)",,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,53.793356,-2.894251,"<div id=""html_3877431a7e924f9fbf779a7e470af9af"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fylde</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Blackpool and Fylde coast received the landfall of the extreme rainfall; runoff into the Irish Sea/Morecambe Bay was impeded by a high tide, contributing to surface water flooding and prolonged drainage issues on the Fylde coast, and interruptions to pumping operations. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Power supply issues during the 22/23 November 2017 event caused intermittent interruptions to pumping operations along the Fylde coast, which affected drainage/pumping capacity and contributed to flooding problems in the coastal area (notably referenced with impacts in neighbouring Thornton&ndash;Cleveleys/Blackpool areas). [IMG,PAG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Fylde,England,"The Blackpool and Fylde coast received the landfall of the extreme rainfall; runoff into the Irish Sea/Morecambe Bay was impeded by a high tide, contributing to surface water flooding and prolonged drainage issues on the Fylde coast, and interruptions to pumping operations. (p12)
Power supply issues during the 22/23 November 2017 event caused intermittent interruptions to pumping operations along the Fylde coast, which affected drainage/pumping capacity and contributed to flooding problems in the coastal area (notably referenced with impacts in neighbouring Thornton–Cleveleys/Blackpool areas). [IMG,PAG] (p13)",,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,53.7271527,-2.7330066,"<div id=""html_c4cc81071adf32196a4064a400ed4a11"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>South Ribble</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The map and report indicate a small number of reported flooding incidents in the southern part of Lancashire, including South Ribble, on 22/23 November 2017. [IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,South Ribble,England,"The map and report indicate a small number of reported flooding incidents in the southern part of Lancashire, including South Ribble, on 22/23 November 2017. [IMG] (p13)",,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
GB-LAN,3,2017-11-22,53.89728410091331,-2.903724352506046,"<div id=""html_de3c19c37aed326fdf133b10ae28e588"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wyre</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant clustering of reported flooding around Wyre during the event on 22/23 November 2017. Extreme rainfall caused local watercourses to overtop banks (reported nearby in the Ellel/Galgate area and from the River Conder and Burrow Beck), producing high volumes of water that encroached into homes, outbuildings and obstructed highways in the affected area. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Particularly damaging flooding occurred across Wyre district when the 22/23 November 2017 extreme rainfall overwhelmed natural and man&ndash;made drainage networks. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Wyre,England,"Significant clustering of reported flooding around Wyre during the event on 22/23 November 2017. Extreme rainfall caused local watercourses to overtop banks (reported nearby in the Ellel/Galgate area and from the River Conder and Burrow Beck), producing high volumes of water that encroached into homes, outbuildings and obstructed highways in the affected area. [IMG] (p13)
Particularly damaging flooding occurred across Wyre district when the 22/23 November 2017 extreme rainfall overwhelmed natural and man–made drainage networks. (p18)",,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22.html,GB-LAN_3_2017-11-22,Lancashire flooding 22 Nov 2017 - 23 Nov 2017,22/23 November 2017 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/covering-report-nov-2017-section-19.pdf
