GB-NYK,2,2022-02-01,53.785097,-1.0990407,"<div id=""html_40101d184c5b6e0a0770f51de057b823"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Selby District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by significant flood events in February 2022; the flooding prompted a formal Section 19 Flood Investigation under the Flood and Water Management Act 2010. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant internal property flooding across the district: main area Tadcaster with the majority around Bridge Street and Commercial Street (many flooded properties were businesses) and additional internally flooded properties in Ulleskelf, Brotherton, Acaster Selby and Kirkby Wharfe. Flooding was from the River Wharfe, River Ouse (Acaster Selby) and the Aire. (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>63 properties internally flooded in Tadcaster; 12 internally flooded properties elsewhere in Selby District; estimated £400k of damages (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Selby District,England,"Affected by significant flood events in February 2022; the flooding prompted a formal Section 19 Flood Investigation under the Flood and Water Management Act 2010. (p1)
Significant internal property flooding across the district: main area Tadcaster with the majority around Bridge Street and Commercial Street (many flooded properties were businesses) and additional internally flooded properties in Ulleskelf, Brotherton, Acaster Selby and Kirkby Wharfe. Flooding was from the River Wharfe, River Ouse (Acaster Selby) and the Aire. (p10)",63 properties internally flooded in Tadcaster; 12 internally flooded properties elsewhere in Selby District; estimated £400k of damages (p10),GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01.html,GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding February 2022,Flood Investigation Report into the flooding in February 2022,089/North Yorkshire County/Flood and Water Management Act 2010 Section 19 Investigation.pdf
GB-NYK,2,2022-02-01,54.006741961160174,-1.5144704578826116,"<div id=""html_864200d7bc385a4f9f1cb02bf68cb2d2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harrogate District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by significant flood events in February 2022; the flooding prompted a formal Section 19 Flood Investigation under the Flood and Water Management Act 2010. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internally flooded properties across communities in the borough: Pateley Bridge (surface water), Summerbridge (from a nearby watercourse), Knaresborough and Ramsgill (flooding from the River Nidd affected properties and led to evacuations). (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following flood warnings on 19&ndash;21 February 2022, a number of properties within the district were put on standby for evacuation; Pateley Bridge experienced surface water flooding and Summerbridge had properties internally flooded from a nearby watercourse. (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 internally flooded properties in the Harrogate Borough Council area; 4 in Pateley Bridge; 3 in Summerbridge; 2 in Knaresborough (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Harrogate District,England,"Affected by significant flood events in February 2022; the flooding prompted a formal Section 19 Flood Investigation under the Flood and Water Management Act 2010. (p1)
Internally flooded properties across communities in the borough: Pateley Bridge (surface water), Summerbridge (from a nearby watercourse), Knaresborough and Ramsgill (flooding from the River Nidd affected properties and led to evacuations). (p10)
Following flood warnings on 19–21 February 2022, a number of properties within the district were put on standby for evacuation; Pateley Bridge experienced surface water flooding and Summerbridge had properties internally flooded from a nearby watercourse. (p15)",12 internally flooded properties in the Harrogate Borough Council area; 4 in Pateley Bridge; 3 in Summerbridge; 2 in Knaresborough (p10),GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01.html,GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding February 2022,Flood Investigation Report into the flooding in February 2022,089/North Yorkshire County/Flood and Water Management Act 2010 Section 19 Investigation.pdf
GB-NYK,2,2022-02-01,54.1345328,-1.4986285,"<div id=""html_5a8685c660e942b5cd52abcad5a28eb4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Yorkshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple communities across the county experienced internal flooding during the event; incidents were recorded in over nine separate communities with concentrated impacts in Tadcaster and Ulleskelf. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Communities in North Yorkshire were affected by prolonged and widespread rainfall (including Storm Franklin) that saturated catchments and gave rise to incidents of flooding. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents were recorded across over nine communities in North Yorkshire on 20&ndash;21 February 2022. The majority of the flooding occurred in Tadcaster (63 properties internally flooded) alongside six properties in Ulleskelf, with a further 15 internally flooded properties spread across the Nidd and Wharfe catchments. (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>84 properties internally flooded across the county (63 in Tadcaster + 6 in Ulleskelf + 15 across Nidd/Wharfe/Aire) [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>63 properties; 6 properties; 15 properties (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,North Yorkshire,England,"Multiple communities across the county experienced internal flooding during the event; incidents were recorded in over nine separate communities with concentrated impacts in Tadcaster and Ulleskelf. [PAG] (p8)
Communities in North Yorkshire were affected by prolonged and widespread rainfall (including Storm Franklin) that saturated catchments and gave rise to incidents of flooding. (p9)
Flooding incidents were recorded across over nine communities in North Yorkshire on 20–21 February 2022. The majority of the flooding occurred in Tadcaster (63 properties internally flooded) alongside six properties in Ulleskelf, with a further 15 internally flooded properties spread across the Nidd and Wharfe catchments. (p15)","84 properties internally flooded across the county (63 in Tadcaster + 6 in Ulleskelf + 15 across Nidd/Wharfe/Aire) [PAG] (p8)
63 properties; 6 properties; 15 properties (p15)",GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01.html,GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding February 2022,Flood Investigation Report into the flooding in February 2022,089/North Yorkshire County/Flood and Water Management Act 2010 Section 19 Investigation.pdf
GB-NYK,2,2022-02-01,53.9825271,-1.3852499932684972,"<div id=""html_d11eb0bce17ba9408441e9ed54905705"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yorkshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding incidents were recorded across multiple communities in Yorkshire (North Yorkshire), with the majority of the event impacts concentrated in Tadcaster; catchments across the region were saturated leading to very high river levels and internal property flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread and prolonged rainfall across Yorkshire produced some of the highest ranking river levels on record at many locations; catchment saturation contributed to river flooding across the region. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchments across Yorkshire saw elevated river levels during the incident, producing a large volume of flood warnings triggered at similar times and straining EA duty officers. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across Yorkshire during the 20&ndash;21 February 2022 event: very high river levels (driven by Storm Franklin) led to river flooding and breaches in locations such as Tadcaster and Ulleskelf; surface&ndash;water and pluvial flooding affected other communities. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Over nine separate communities recorded flooding; 63 properties internally flooded in Tadcaster; 6 properties in Ulleskelf; an additional 15 internally flooded properties across the Nidd, Wharfe and Aire catchments. [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>63 properties; 6 properties; 15 internally flooded properties [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Yorkshire,England,"Flooding incidents were recorded across multiple communities in Yorkshire (North Yorkshire), with the majority of the event impacts concentrated in Tadcaster; catchments across the region were saturated leading to very high river levels and internal property flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p8)
Widespread and prolonged rainfall across Yorkshire produced some of the highest ranking river levels on record at many locations; catchment saturation contributed to river flooding across the region. [PAG] (p10)
Catchments across Yorkshire saw elevated river levels during the incident, producing a large volume of flood warnings triggered at similar times and straining EA duty officers. (p14)
Widespread flooding across Yorkshire during the 20–21 February 2022 event: very high river levels (driven by Storm Franklin) led to river flooding and breaches in locations such as Tadcaster and Ulleskelf; surface–water and pluvial flooding affected other communities. [TAB] (p15)","Over nine separate communities recorded flooding; 63 properties internally flooded in Tadcaster; 6 properties in Ulleskelf; an additional 15 internally flooded properties across the Nidd, Wharfe and Aire catchments. [IMG,PAG] (p8)
63 properties; 6 properties; 15 internally flooded properties [TAB] (p15)",GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01.html,GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding February 2022,Flood Investigation Report into the flooding in February 2022,089/North Yorkshire County/Flood and Water Management Act 2010 Section 19 Investigation.pdf
GB-NYK,2,2022-02-01,52.53102,-1.2649062,"<div id=""html_c8dfa7fa7b6de707828765549f71b18b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>England</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The February 2022 storm sequence (including Storm Franklin) affected communities in England, producing prolonged rainfall that saturated catchments and resulted in river flooding in parts of England such as North Yorkshire. [IMG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,England,England,"The February 2022 storm sequence (including Storm Franklin) affected communities in England, producing prolonged rainfall that saturated catchments and resulted in river flooding in parts of England such as North Yorkshire. [IMG] (p8)",,GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01.html,GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding February 2022,Flood Investigation Report into the flooding in February 2022,089/North Yorkshire County/Flood and Water Management Act 2010 Section 19 Investigation.pdf
GB-NYK,2,2022-02-01,53.8852975,-1.2595975,"<div id=""html_b238b26384e83c665093838e3136db9b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tadcaster</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced flooding caused principally by the River Wharfe; this flood event prompted Section 19 investigations and local meetings with affected communities. [PAG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Community experienced flooding requiring a Flood Alleviation Scheme; surface water systems serving the location were implicated and must be accounted for in the FAS design. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread internal flooding of residential properties in the town caused by very high river levels (River Wharfe). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Wharfe inundated the town, with the majority of internal flooding concentrated around Bridge Street and Commercial Street; many flooded properties were businesses. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe overtopped and breached its banks at multiple locations through Tadcaster (both left and right banks), including points near the main bridge, a 90&deg; turn in the defence where a footpath crosses, the footbridge plinth downstream of the bridge, adjacent to Sainsbury's and near the Old Mill property; defences were overtopped/breached allowing floodwater to pass over the defences and reach adjacent areas and infrastructure. [IMG,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The February 2022 flood event led to EA action on warnings at Tadcaster; properties (for example on Bridge Street) experienced internal flooding despite some property flood resilience measures, and some residents/businesses used warnings to reduce losses by moving furniture/equipment enabling quicker re&ndash;opening of a number of businesses. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels breached defences in Tadcaster causing internal flooding of properties; overtopping of flood defences on the west bank contributed to flooding in the town. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tadcaster experienced major flooding (notably the 2015 event and flooding in February 2022) that led to internal property flooding, installation of property flood resilience measures, and programme&ndash;level responses including development of the Tadcaster Flood Alleviation Scheme and re&ndash;profiling of low spots on the River Wharfe to reduce breach risk. [TAB,MUL] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>63 properties internally flooded (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>63 properties internally flooded; estimated £400,000 of damages (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>63 properties [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>£11.5 million of funding [TAB,MUL] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Tadcaster,England,"The town experienced flooding caused principally by the River Wharfe; this flood event prompted Section 19 investigations and local meetings with affected communities. [PAG] (p2)
Community experienced flooding requiring a Flood Alleviation Scheme; surface water systems serving the location were implicated and must be accounted for in the FAS design. [PAG] (p3)
Widespread internal flooding of residential properties in the town caused by very high river levels (River Wharfe). (p8)
Flooding from the River Wharfe inundated the town, with the majority of internal flooding concentrated around Bridge Street and Commercial Street; many flooded properties were businesses. (p10)
The River Wharfe overtopped and breached its banks at multiple locations through Tadcaster (both left and right banks), including points near the main bridge, a 90° turn in the defence where a footpath crosses, the footbridge plinth downstream of the bridge, adjacent to Sainsbury's and near the Old Mill property; defences were overtopped/breached allowing floodwater to pass over the defences and reach adjacent areas and infrastructure. [IMG,PAG] (p12)
The February 2022 flood event led to EA action on warnings at Tadcaster; properties (for example on Bridge Street) experienced internal flooding despite some property flood resilience measures, and some residents/businesses used warnings to reduce losses by moving furniture/equipment enabling quicker re–opening of a number of businesses. (p13)
River levels breached defences in Tadcaster causing internal flooding of properties; overtopping of flood defences on the west bank contributed to flooding in the town. [TAB,PAG] (p15)
Tadcaster experienced major flooding (notably the 2015 event and flooding in February 2022) that led to internal property flooding, installation of property flood resilience measures, and programme–level responses including development of the Tadcaster Flood Alleviation Scheme and re–profiling of low spots on the River Wharfe to reduce breach risk. [TAB,MUL] (p16)","63 properties internally flooded (p8)
63 properties internally flooded; estimated £400,000 of damages (p10)
63 properties [TAB,PAG] (p15)
£11.5 million of funding [TAB,MUL] (p16)",GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01.html,GB-NYK_2_2022-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding February 2022,Flood Investigation Report into the flooding in February 2022,089/North Yorkshire County/Flood and Water Management Act 2010 Section 19 Investigation.pdf
GB-NYK,2,2022-02-01,53.853333,-1.2143782,"<div id=""html_caab5dc031ee2269185a5941c82f87eb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ulleskelf</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Ulleskelf have flooded internally; Environment Agency to review flood&ndash;risk assets and model, communicate with affected residents to investigate options for managing flood risk, review flood alert/warning timing, and recommend property&ndash;level resilience if funding or collective solutions are not available. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties in the village recorded during the event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding occurred at properties in Ulleskelf; specifically six properties were internally flooded at the location called West End. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood defence embankment to the rear of West End properties was breached/circumvented by high River Wharfe levels; Ulleskelf Ings (EA&ndash;managed water storage area) filled and flood extents mirrored Flood Zone 2; on Main Street water from the Ings surcharged up through drains (did not breach defences) and pumps were deployed to prevent internal property flooding. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Wharfe levels breached/circumvented the flood defence embankment to the rear of properties in West End, leading to property flooding; flood extents matched approximately Flood Zone 2. Water from the Ings surcharged through drains onto Main Street; pumps were deployed to stop internal flooding of properties. [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels breached the river in Ulleskelf causing internal flooding of properties. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Ulleskelf are reported to have flooded internally. [TAB,PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Ulleskelf have flooded internally; recommendation for the Environment Agency to review flood risk assets and models and to communicate with residents about options and potential contribution to solutions; advice to consider property flood resilience measures. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties internally flooded (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties internally flooded (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Ulleskelf,England,"Properties in Ulleskelf have flooded internally; Environment Agency to review flood–risk assets and model, communicate with affected residents to investigate options for managing flood risk, review flood alert/warning timing, and recommend property–level resilience if funding or collective solutions are not available. (p3)
