GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.3281139,0.432208,"<div id=""html_f8655681773c1d820aa87e4651837748"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Freelands Road, Snodland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway flooding reported on Freelands Road. [TAB] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Freelands Road, Snodland",England,Highway flooding reported on Freelands Road. [TAB] (p2),,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.3193509,0.4385067,"<div id=""html_4349436af4cf6d829658a644d3dd7d7b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brook Lane, Snodland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>CCTV survey of highway drainage located a damaged pipe (found during post&ndash;event investigations); the damaged pipe was repaired in May 2020 and further drainage works are required. (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Brook Lane, Snodland",England,CCTV survey of highway drainage located a damaged pipe (found during post–event investigations); the damaged pipe was repaired in May 2020 and further drainage works are required. (p10),,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.2717306,0.3566598,"<div id=""html_64cc4793deb2fcc8829d751fac844f2d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tonbridge and Malling</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Snodland, within Tonbridge and Malling, experienced surface&ndash;water flooding during the event; around 20 properties in Snodland were flooded (many internally). (p1)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 20 properties (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Tonbridge and Malling,England,"Snodland, within Tonbridge and Malling, experienced surface–water flooding during the event; around 20 properties in Snodland were flooded (many internally). (p1)",around 20 properties (p1),GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.2070749,0.7210362,"<div id=""html_a2eb95cc4c2a3fb447b5aa0d35197fec"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Very heavy rainfall occurred county&ndash;wide on 10 June 2019, with parts of the county particularly badly affected and flooding reported in places such as Snodland. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Kent,England,"Very heavy rainfall occurred county–wide on 10 June 2019, with parts of the county particularly badly affected and flooding reported in places such as Snodland. (p1)",,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.3307836506775,0.44540565893173256,"<div id=""html_a2ef6a9eeaeafe12b85be947c1002c25"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Snodland</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Around 20 properties were flooded by surface water; many of those were affected internally. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding across the town caused internal flooding to numerous properties, internal flooding at a retail depot, damage to vehicles at Lakeside, and internal flooding at St Katherine&#8217;s School (21 classrooms) which led to the school being closed for 2 days. Several roads experienced highway flooding. [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage network and watercourse infrastructure in Snodland were observed with blockages and reduced conveyance after the 10 June 2019 event: culvert inlets (e.g. at Paddlesworth Lake and Birling Road) had debris/litter lodged against security bars, a security screen at Saltings Way had significant debris accumulation, and standing water was observed in the open channel between Salting Road and the A228, indicating impaired drainage and local surface water pooling. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the town experienced surface water flooding on the routes shown by the Environment Agency surface water flood map; locations of flooded properties correspond to the watercourse route through the town. The town's impermeable urban surfaces and local drainage arrangements contributed to surface water run&ndash;off and flooding of properties. [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface water flooding following intense rainfall: overland flows from built areas drained toward the culverted watercourse and collected in topographical low points across the town. Deep surface water flooding occurred on Recreational Avenue, Rectory Lane and Saltings Road. Earlier blocked drainage contributed to road and external property flooding locally, and more widespread flooding developed after the intense hour of rain following 21:30. [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged and intense rainfall on saturated ground caused widespread surface&ndash;water and overland flow across Snodland. Drainage capacity was exceeded and water accumulated in topographical low points, flooding multiple roads (Pridmore Road, Robert Road, Birling Road, Recreational Avenue, Rectory Close Oxford Street, Saltings Road) and entering buildings. At the school on St Katherines Lane overland flow entered the buildings affecting classrooms; sections of flat roof collapsed and the private rising main surcharged within the main building causing extensive damage. KFRS attended internal flooding at a property on St Katherines Lane; KCC Highways deployed a tanker at Lakeside to control floodwater. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A heavy rainstorm (10 June 2019) produced extensive flooding from overland flows and overwhelmed drainage. Deep surface water collected at local low points and above the culverted watercourse, causing flooding to properties as water could not discharge quickly enough through the surface water sewer; floodwater drained away after the rainfall ceased. (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 20 properties (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>28 properties affected (aggregate); 21 classrooms affected at St Katherine&#8217;s School; 6 properties at Lakeside; multiple highway flooding incidents (roads named separately) [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 classrooms; one property; 6 inches (water depth reported at one property) (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Snodland,England,"Around 20 properties were flooded by surface water; many of those were affected internally. (p1)
Flooding across the town caused internal flooding to numerous properties, internal flooding at a retail depot, damage to vehicles at Lakeside, and internal flooding at St Katherine’s School (21 classrooms) which led to the school being closed for 2 days. Several roads experienced highway flooding. [IMG] (p2)
Drainage network and watercourse infrastructure in Snodland were observed with blockages and reduced conveyance after the 10 June 2019 event: culvert inlets (e.g. at Paddlesworth Lake and Birling Road) had debris/litter lodged against security bars, a security screen at Saltings Way had significant debris accumulation, and standing water was observed in the open channel between Salting Road and the A228, indicating impaired drainage and local surface water pooling. (p4)
Parts of the town experienced surface water flooding on the routes shown by the Environment Agency surface water flood map; locations of flooded properties correspond to the watercourse route through the town. The town's impermeable urban surfaces and local drainage arrangements contributed to surface water run–off and flooding of properties. [IMG] (p5)
Widespread surface water flooding following intense rainfall: overland flows from built areas drained toward the culverted watercourse and collected in topographical low points across the town. Deep surface water flooding occurred on Recreational Avenue, Rectory Lane and Saltings Road. Earlier blocked drainage contributed to road and external property flooding locally, and more widespread flooding developed after the intense hour of rain following 21:30. [TAB] (p8)
Prolonged and intense rainfall on saturated ground caused widespread surface–water and overland flow across Snodland. Drainage capacity was exceeded and water accumulated in topographical low points, flooding multiple roads (Pridmore Road, Robert Road, Birling Road, Recreational Avenue, Rectory Close Oxford Street, Saltings Road) and entering buildings. At the school on St Katherines Lane overland flow entered the buildings affecting classrooms; sections of flat roof collapsed and the private rising main surcharged within the main building causing extensive damage. KFRS attended internal flooding at a property on St Katherines Lane; KCC Highways deployed a tanker at Lakeside to control floodwater. (p9)
A heavy rainstorm (10 June 2019) produced extensive flooding from overland flows and overwhelmed drainage. Deep surface water collected at local low points and above the culverted watercourse, causing flooding to properties as water could not discharge quickly enough through the surface water sewer; floodwater drained away after the rainfall ceased. (p10)","around 20 properties (p1)
28 properties affected (aggregate); 21 classrooms affected at St Katherine’s School; 6 properties at Lakeside; multiple highway flooding incidents (roads named separately) [IMG] (p2)
18 classrooms; one property; 6 inches (water depth reported at one property) (p9)",GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.342944665244204,0.2627640552737047,"<div id=""html_11350957910798c0f69f3a4cefbb48cc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Kingsdown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as being 'particularly badly affected' by the very heavy rainfall associated with the 10 June 2019 flood event (specific local flood impacts not detailed in this excerpt). (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,West Kingsdown,England,Described as being 'particularly badly affected' by the very heavy rainfall associated with the 10 June 2019 flood event (specific local flood impacts not detailed in this excerpt). (p1),,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.3303854,0.3590695,"<div id=""html_d772aff438c554e592bb23781d729572"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Vigo Village</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as being 'particularly badly affected' by the very heavy rainfall associated with the 10 June 2019 flood event (specific local flood impacts not detailed in this excerpt). (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Vigo Village,England,Described as being 'particularly badly affected' by the very heavy rainfall associated with the 10 June 2019 flood event (specific local flood impacts not detailed in this excerpt). (p1),,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-KEN,17,2019-06-10,51.39750895,0.18022632679075817,"<div id=""html_75a5fc8a20907eeb6c76310fce92e93c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swanley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as being 'particularly badly affected' by the very heavy rainfall associated with the 10 June 2019 flood event (specific local flood impacts not detailed in this excerpt). (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Swanley,England,Described as being 'particularly badly affected' by the very heavy rainfall associated with the 10 June 2019 flood event (specific local flood impacts not detailed in this excerpt). (p1),,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10.html,GB-KEN_17_2019-06-10,Snodland flooding 10 Jun 2019,Flood Investigation Report: Snodland,063/Kent/Snodland-section-19-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5522239,-0.486831,"<div id=""html_13a0b2217d732cc09dce6aa7dfba3a49"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willow Carpets</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flood water had come through the bankside wall of the property into the shop and onto the road; the occupier was clearing up after internal flooding. [IMG] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Willow Carpets,England,Reported flood water had come through the bankside wall of the property into the shop and onto the road; the occupier was clearing up after internal flooding. [IMG] (p21),,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.556262276769814,-0.4852174873188524,"<div id=""html_93640a62d6bf55709d4e891b925fd983"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willowbank Island</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties and business premises in the Willowbank area caused by overflow/interaction of the River Colne channels (branches, weirs and sluices) around the residential area. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding in February 2014 caused by over&ndash;topping of river banks and of a low flood&ndash;defence bund; resulted in defined flow routes and inundation across the Willowbank and Oakside residential area. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River overtopping/outflow occurred near the gauging station weir at the northern end of Willowbank Island; water then flowed overland from that location into downstream Oakside areas. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rising river levels and a reported possible blockage at the southern end prompted response. Bucks Flood & Rescue Service opened the Allied Mills sluice at Kings Mill Cottage which resulted in upstream levels dropping. Debris on the access bridge indicated water reached over the soffit level. Sandbags were requested to protect low spots in the Willowbank recreation area. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding occurred in the Willowbank recreation area; site operative requested sandbags to protect two low spots and locations were passed to the response gang. Initial flooding in Willowbank was caused by surface water that could not drain away due to poorly maintained drainage channels and then exacerbated by high river levels. [TAB,PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Colne passes through New Denham and around Willowbank (classified as Main River); fluvial flood risk is identified and local open surface water ditches and piped systems (feeding from Alder Road, Willow Avenue and Hawthorn Drive) can back up and deliver water into properties. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area between Poplar Road and High Bridge is at high flood risk. Parts are protected by flood defences (designed to 1 in 100 year) which prevented internal flooding within the defended area, but defences did overtop at a location and caused external flooding of gardens. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Initial flooding in the Willowbank area was from surface water that could not flow away because of high river levels, leading to inundation of the area and contributing to onward fluvial flooding. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater entered private gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue; embankment vulnerability adjacent to the Willowbank residential area was identified and recommendations were made to raise/strengthen embankments. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A breach of the river right bank upstream of the gauging weir occurred and surface water drainage/piped systems in the Willowbank area were affected, causing flooding risk to properties and businesses (including on Oxford Road). The event prompted surveys of the breached bank, investigations of the River Colne modelling and flood warning accuracy, and actions relating to the surface water system (ownership, CCTV inspection) to address flood impacts. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Photographs taken in the Willowbank area document elevated river levels during the event, with water reaching the gauge board and coming close to the riverside footpath and nearby houses, indicating river flooding that threatened adjacent properties and vegetation. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported overland flow route from the River Colne near the gauging station at the north of Willowbank Island contributed to flow paths across the island area and toward Oakside; area associated with sandbagging activity affecting nearby Oakside properties. (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five or more properties; two or more business premises [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>numbers 25&ndash;29; numbers 42&ndash;44; at least 4 properties (Willowbank and Oakside) [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>water level 32.61mAOD (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Willowbank Island,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties and business premises in the Willowbank area caused by overflow/interaction of the River Colne channels (branches, weirs and sluices) around the residential area. [PAG] (p6)
Fluvial (river) flooding in February 2014 caused by over–topping of river banks and of a low flood–defence bund; resulted in defined flow routes and inundation across the Willowbank and Oakside residential area. [PAG] (p19)
River overtopping/outflow occurred near the gauging station weir at the northern end of Willowbank Island; water then flowed overland from that location into downstream Oakside areas. (p20)
Rising river levels and a reported possible blockage at the southern end prompted response. Bucks Flood & Rescue Service opened the Allied Mills sluice at Kings Mill Cottage which resulted in upstream levels dropping. Debris on the access bridge indicated water reached over the soffit level. Sandbags were requested to protect low spots in the Willowbank recreation area. (p22)
Surface–water flooding occurred in the Willowbank recreation area; site operative requested sandbags to protect two low spots and locations were passed to the response gang. Initial flooding in Willowbank was caused by surface water that could not drain away due to poorly maintained drainage channels and then exacerbated by high river levels. [TAB,PAG] (p23)
River Colne passes through New Denham and around Willowbank (classified as Main River); fluvial flood risk is identified and local open surface water ditches and piped systems (feeding from Alder Road, Willow Avenue and Hawthorn Drive) can back up and deliver water into properties. (p24)
The area between Poplar Road and High Bridge is at high flood risk. Parts are protected by flood defences (designed to 1 in 100 year) which prevented internal flooding within the defended area, but defences did overtop at a location and caused external flooding of gardens. (p27)
Initial flooding in the Willowbank area was from surface water that could not flow away because of high river levels, leading to inundation of the area and contributing to onward fluvial flooding. (p28)
Floodwater entered private gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue; embankment vulnerability adjacent to the Willowbank residential area was identified and recommendations were made to raise/strengthen embankments. [TAB] (p29)
A breach of the river right bank upstream of the gauging weir occurred and surface water drainage/piped systems in the Willowbank area were affected, causing flooding risk to properties and businesses (including on Oxford Road). The event prompted surveys of the breached bank, investigations of the River Colne modelling and flood warning accuracy, and actions relating to the surface water system (ownership, CCTV inspection) to address flood impacts. (p30)
Photographs taken in the Willowbank area document elevated river levels during the event, with water reaching the gauge board and coming close to the riverside footpath and nearby houses, indicating river flooding that threatened adjacent properties and vegetation. (p36)
Reported overland flow route from the River Colne near the gauging station at the north of Willowbank Island contributed to flow paths across the island area and toward Oakside; area associated with sandbagging activity affecting nearby Oakside properties. (p43)","five or more properties; two or more business premises [PAG] (p6)
numbers 25–29; numbers 42–44; at least 4 properties (Willowbank and Oakside) [PAG] (p20)
water level 32.61mAOD (p36)",GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.55256448782985,-0.4841792875393102,"<div id=""html_6c435f7dc1abfc2a1fb7f8a84d43a605"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>King's Island</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding and surface water ponding affected the island: residents sandbagged doors and garages on Willow Avenue, Willow Crescent West, Poplar Road and Alder Road; pumps were used at the northern end of the island to remove water; standing water accumulated at the northern end of the recreation ground after overtopping of the defence (flood kerbs exposed and topsoil eroded); the external area of Mill Stream Cottage (southern end of Willow Avenue) showed signs of both fluvial and foul sewer flooding. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road generally performed and prevented wider internal flooding of properties. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding prompted a requirement to determine and communicate the operating procedure for the King's Island sluice gates so the management company can operate them effectively to manage river discharge during floods. (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties; 16 Willow Avenue; 100&ndash;150mm topsoil erosion (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,King's Island,England,"Fluvial flooding and surface water ponding affected the island: residents sandbagged doors and garages on Willow Avenue, Willow Crescent West, Poplar Road and Alder Road; pumps were used at the northern end of the island to remove water; standing water accumulated at the northern end of the recreation ground after overtopping of the defence (flood kerbs exposed and topsoil eroded); the external area of Mill Stream Cottage (southern end of Willow Avenue) showed signs of both fluvial and foul sewer flooding. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road generally performed and prevented wider internal flooding of properties. (p18)
Flooding prompted a requirement to determine and communicate the operating procedure for the King's Island sluice gates so the management company can operate them effectively to manage river discharge during floods. (p30)",three properties; 16 Willow Avenue; 100–150mm topsoil erosion (p18),GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.6341785,-0.4172156,"<div id=""html_241d3370fdd6182c596a697d71dfd0f7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Colne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Branches and channels of the River Colne run through and around Willowbank (weirs, sluices and splits to the Grand Union canal); these channels were implicated in the flooding that caused internal flooding of properties and businesses in Willowbank. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River (Colne) reached elevated fluvial levels during the event; modelled flood level was approximately 1.278 m (between the 1 in 50&ndash;year and 1 in 100&ndash;year return periods). High river levels reduced the ability of local surface&ndash;water drains to discharge to the river, affecting drainage on Willowbank and adjacent areas; Environment Agency banks and the Willowbank weirs controlled and split flows around Willowbank island during the high flows. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the River Colne flowed around King's Island and fed water into the Grand Union Canal, contributing to elevated canal levels and downstream flooding; a sluice next to King's Island main gate was opened (to manage flows). (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Colne overflowed / conveyed floodwater into adjacent open land and along mapped flow routes, the water then flowed through car parks and along streets into properties on Oxford Road, the nearby pub and filling station. [IMG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Channel restrictions and lack of flood defences caused the river to back up through New Denham (around Willowbank), contributing to fluvial flooding and resulting in water backing up into properties via poor pipe overflow systems. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Colne was shown adjacent to a flood defence where flooding/interaction with the defence occurred; an overtopping location exposed the flood kerb and contributed to inundation of adjacent land. [IMG] (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five or more properties; two or more business premises (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.278 m; between 1 in 50&ndash;year and 1 in 100&ndash;year return period (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Colne,England,"Branches and channels of the River Colne run through and around Willowbank (weirs, sluices and splits to the Grand Union canal); these channels were implicated in the flooding that caused internal flooding of properties and businesses in Willowbank. (p6)
The River (Colne) reached elevated fluvial levels during the event; modelled flood level was approximately 1.278 m (between the 1 in 50–year and 1 in 100–year return periods). High river levels reduced the ability of local surface–water drains to discharge to the river, affecting drainage on Willowbank and adjacent areas; Environment Agency banks and the Willowbank weirs controlled and split flows around Willowbank island during the high flows. (p16)
High flows in the River Colne flowed around King's Island and fed water into the Grand Union Canal, contributing to elevated canal levels and downstream flooding; a sluice next to King's Island main gate was opened (to manage flows). (p18)
River Colne overflowed / conveyed floodwater into adjacent open land and along mapped flow routes, the water then flowed through car parks and along streets into properties on Oxford Road, the nearby pub and filling station. [IMG] (p21)
Channel restrictions and lack of flood defences caused the river to back up through New Denham (around Willowbank), contributing to fluvial flooding and resulting in water backing up into properties via poor pipe overflow systems. (p24)
River Colne was shown adjacent to a flood defence where flooding/interaction with the defence occurred; an overtopping location exposed the flood kerb and contributed to inundation of adjacent land. [IMG] (p40)","five or more properties; two or more business premises (p6)
1.278 m; between 1 in 50–year and 1 in 100–year return period (p16)",GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5549413,-0.4897928,"<div id=""html_25ad2312b88459744f26f0950ff4c704"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oxford Road, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Colne overtopped its banks and floodwater flowed into the area along Oxford Road; the channel overtopped and flowed into shops and houses on Oxford Road, causing inundation of properties and commercial premises. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Oxford Road was within the estimated fluvial flood extent in February 2014; floodwater reached the road as a result of over&ndash;topping of banks/low flood defence bunds shown on the map, causing flooding of the carriageway and exposure of adjacent properties to floodwater. [IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater travelled down through car parks to properties on Oxford Road, flooding the parade of shops and causing internal flooding to businesses (including the hair salon and carpet shop), and affecting the pub and filling station. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters followed flow routes into the parade of shops on Oxford Road, causing internal flooding to businesses (including the hair salon and Willow Carpets) and to nearby properties. [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear of Oxford Road properties experienced floodwater ingress; some downpipes and soil vent pipes were in poor repair and flood water may have come up through these pipes into properties. A request was made for 6 sandbags to fill a gap at the rear of 20A Oxford Road to prevent water coming from the River Colne. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The parade of shops along Oxford Road were flooded due to high river levels, channel restrictions and poor pipe overflow systems that caused water to back up into properties. A request was made for 6 sandbags to protect a gap at the rear of 20A Oxford Road. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties and businesses along Oxford Road. The parade of shops had two properties flooded internally. River/mill&ndash;channel restrictions and likely surcharged sewer/surface&ndash;water outlets and soil vent pipes caused backing up into properties. The area is in Flood Zone 3 and is unprotected by defences. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Business properties in the parade of shops along Oxford Road were flooded. Flooding was caused by backing up of flow due to surcharged and underwater soil vent pipes and surface&ndash;water outlets into the restricted mill channel/river, combined with high river (fluvial) levels; the area is in Flood Zone 3 and is undefended. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Business properties in the parade of shops along Oxford Road were flooded due to backing up of flows and poor pipe/overflow systems causing water to back up into properties. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed through a gap beside the hair salon at 20a Oxford Rd and into the parade of shops/pavement; the gap is documented as a route by which flood water entered the property area. (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 4 properties; 20a (hair salon) [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties flooded internally; 2 properties flooded internally (parade of shops) (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Oxford Road, Denham",England,"The River Colne overtopped its banks and floodwater flowed into the area along Oxford Road; the channel overtopped and flowed into shops and houses on Oxford Road, causing inundation of properties and commercial premises. (p4)
Oxford Road was within the estimated fluvial flood extent in February 2014; floodwater reached the road as a result of over–topping of banks/low flood defence bunds shown on the map, causing flooding of the carriageway and exposure of adjacent properties to floodwater. [IMG] (p19)
Floodwater travelled down through car parks to properties on Oxford Road, flooding the parade of shops and causing internal flooding to businesses (including the hair salon and carpet shop), and affecting the pub and filling station. [PAG] (p20)
Floodwaters followed flow routes into the parade of shops on Oxford Road, causing internal flooding to businesses (including the hair salon and Willow Carpets) and to nearby properties. [IMG,PAG] (p21)
Rear of Oxford Road properties experienced floodwater ingress; some downpipes and soil vent pipes were in poor repair and flood water may have come up through these pipes into properties. A request was made for 6 sandbags to fill a gap at the rear of 20A Oxford Road to prevent water coming from the River Colne. [TAB] (p22)
The parade of shops along Oxford Road were flooded due to high river levels, channel restrictions and poor pipe overflow systems that caused water to back up into properties. A request was made for 6 sandbags to protect a gap at the rear of 20A Oxford Road. [PAG] (p23)
Internal flooding of properties and businesses along Oxford Road. The parade of shops had two properties flooded internally. River/mill–channel restrictions and likely surcharged sewer/surface–water outlets and soil vent pipes caused backing up into properties. The area is in Flood Zone 3 and is unprotected by defences. (p27)
Business properties in the parade of shops along Oxford Road were flooded. Flooding was caused by backing up of flow due to surcharged and underwater soil vent pipes and surface–water outlets into the restricted mill channel/river, combined with high river (fluvial) levels; the area is in Flood Zone 3 and is undefended. [PAG] (p28)
Business properties in the parade of shops along Oxford Road were flooded due to backing up of flows and poor pipe/overflow systems causing water to back up into properties. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
Floodwater flowed through a gap beside the hair salon at 20a Oxford Rd and into the parade of shops/pavement; the gap is documented as a route by which flood water entered the property area. (p47)","at least 4 properties; 20a (hair salon) [PAG] (p20)
4 properties [IMG,PAG] (p21)
4 properties flooded internally; 2 properties flooded internally (parade of shops) (p27)",GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5540141,-0.4875305,"<div id=""html_b72515a0b335fbad4501a317448b27eb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oakside, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the overtopped River Colne flowed down a field into the area around Oakside and inundated nearby properties; houses on Oxford Road and shops at Oxford Road were flooded when the river channel overtopped and flowed into shops. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding in February 2014 caused by over&ndash;topping of river banks and of a low flood&ndash;defence bund; resulted in defined flow routes and inundation across the Willowbank and Oakside residential area. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overbank river water emerging upstream near the gauging station flowed overland through Oakside. No internal flooding reported for some properties, but water reached door thresholds at numbers 25&ndash;29; sandbagging was present around properties and along the riverbank; car parks and gardens adjacent to the properties were inundated. Floodwater from fields then flowed to Oxford Road causing internal flooding of properties and businesses. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial floodwater from overtopping of the right bank flowed into a field and into the residential area around Oakside, inundating properties and streets. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding of gardens and roads in both Willowbank and Oakside. In Oakside, properties were close to internal flooding and outbuilding flooding occurred. Flooding in Oakside resulted from a breach of the river bank upstream of the weir which allowed water to flow through fields into Oakside and then toward Oxford Road. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential area around Oakside was inundated when overtopped river water flowed from upstream (via the gauged weir location) into fields and the housing area, and flowed through onto Oxford Road. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbagging observed at Oakside residential properties (nos. 42&ndash;44); raised water level recorded at 2 Poplar Road; ponding and overland flow recorded in fields to the west indicating flow routes toward Oakside. [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties adjacent to the river experienced high water levels; properties Nos. 42&ndash;44 were sandbagged to protect against flood water and photographs show ponding and river encroachment onto the bank beside houses. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported overland flow routes and ponding directed into the residential area; water tracked across paved areas and grass toward buildings. Significant sandbagging was undertaken around properties. [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>numbers 25&ndash;29; number 25; numbers 42&ndash;44; at least 4 properties [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (nos. 42&ndash;44); 1 property (2 Poplar Rd) [PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (Nos. 42&ndash;44) sandbagged [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Oakside, Denham",England,"Floodwater from the overtopped River Colne flowed down a field into the area around Oakside and inundated nearby properties; houses on Oxford Road and shops at Oxford Road were flooded when the river channel overtopped and flowed into shops. (p4)
Fluvial (river) flooding in February 2014 caused by over–topping of river banks and of a low flood–defence bund; resulted in defined flow routes and inundation across the Willowbank and Oakside residential area. [PAG] (p19)
Overbank river water emerging upstream near the gauging station flowed overland through Oakside. No internal flooding reported for some properties, but water reached door thresholds at numbers 25–29; sandbagging was present around properties and along the riverbank; car parks and gardens adjacent to the properties were inundated. Floodwater from fields then flowed to Oxford Road causing internal flooding of properties and businesses. [PAG] (p20)
Fluvial floodwater from overtopping of the right bank flowed into a field and into the residential area around Oakside, inundating properties and streets. (p23)
External flooding of gardens and roads in both Willowbank and Oakside. In Oakside, properties were close to internal flooding and outbuilding flooding occurred. Flooding in Oakside resulted from a breach of the river bank upstream of the weir which allowed water to flow through fields into Oakside and then toward Oxford Road. (p27)
Residential area around Oakside was inundated when overtopped river water flowed from upstream (via the gauged weir location) into fields and the housing area, and flowed through onto Oxford Road. (p28)
Sandbagging observed at Oakside residential properties (nos. 42–44); raised water level recorded at 2 Poplar Road; ponding and overland flow recorded in fields to the west indicating flow routes toward Oakside. [PAG] (p42)
Residential properties adjacent to the river experienced high water levels; properties Nos. 42–44 were sandbagged to protect against flood water and photographs show ponding and river encroachment onto the bank beside houses. [IMG,PAG] (p43)
Reported overland flow routes and ponding directed into the residential area; water tracked across paved areas and grass toward buildings. Significant sandbagging was undertaken around properties. [IMG,PAG] (p44)","numbers 25–29; number 25; numbers 42–44; at least 4 properties [PAG] (p20)
3 properties (nos. 42–44); 1 property (2 Poplar Rd) [PAG] (p42)
3 properties (Nos. 42–44) sandbagged [IMG,PAG] (p43)",GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5563565,-0.4857477,"<div id=""html_029b1b26fe4a48012a077d6949d970f4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willow Crescent West, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flood water crossed the recreation ground into gardens on Willow Crescent West. Residents sandbagged doors and garages. There was evidence of pumping from three properties at the northern end of the island near the gauging station. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marked on the February 2014 flood map within the estimated fluvial flood extent; indicated to have been inundated by flood flows. [IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following breach of EA defences, fluvial floodwater flowed into gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue, flooding gardens and affecting adjacent residential properties. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding of gardens and roads occurred here when flood defences overtopped; residents used sandbags and pumping to protect properties. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial floodwater entered gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue after EA defences were breached at Grid Ref 504983 185326. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pumping operation at 79 Willow Cres W to remove floodwater; muddy ground and placement of hoses through a gate indicate residential garden/backyard inundation and active water removal. [IMG] (p37)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Willow Crescent West, Denham",England,"Fluvial flood water crossed the recreation ground into gardens on Willow Crescent West. Residents sandbagged doors and garages. There was evidence of pumping from three properties at the northern end of the island near the gauging station. (p17)
