External flooding of gardens and roads occurred here when flood defences overtopped; residents used sandbags and pumping to protect properties. (p27)
Fluvial floodwater entered gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue after EA defences were breached at Grid Ref 504983 185326. (p28)
Pumping operation at 79 Willow Cres W to remove floodwater; muddy ground and placement of hoses through a gate indicate residential garden/backyard inundation and active water removal. [IMG] (p37)",three properties (p17),GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5514606,-0.4861011,"<div id=""html_fe76eec3ab9837176b95625025da62f6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willow Avenue, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ditches and the piped surface water system affecting Willow Avenue filled. Foul sewers began to overflow as the local pumping station was overwhelmed and Thames Water tankered away waste. Sandbagging of doors and garages was observed along Willow Avenue. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Doors and garages sandbagged by residents; ongoing pumping used to protect properties. The resident of 16 Willow Avenue carried out regular pumping (every three hours) because groundwater was flooding their garden. Resident measures prevented internal flooding of several properties. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following breach of EA defences, fluvial floodwater flowed into gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue, flooding gardens and affecting adjacent residential properties. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding of gardens and roads occurred here when flood defences overtopped; residents used sandbags and pumping to protect properties. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial floodwater entered gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue after EA defences were breached at Grid Ref 504983 185326. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 9&ndash;15 Feb 2014 event water flowed into gardens on Willow Avenue (and Willow Crescent West). Flooding was associated with backing&ndash;up of piped/overflow systems causing water to enter garden areas and threaten properties. [TAB,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The piped surface&ndash;water system along Willow Avenue was affected by flooding; a CCTV survey was proposed from Alder Road south along Willow Avenue to Tudor Cottage to locate blockages or damage caused by the event. (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties; 16 Willow Avenue [PAG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Willow Avenue, Denham",England,"Surface water ditches and the piped surface water system affecting Willow Avenue filled. Foul sewers began to overflow as the local pumping station was overwhelmed and Thames Water tankered away waste. Sandbagging of doors and garages was observed along Willow Avenue. [PAG] (p17)
Doors and garages sandbagged by residents; ongoing pumping used to protect properties. The resident of 16 Willow Avenue carried out regular pumping (every three hours) because groundwater was flooding their garden. Resident measures prevented internal flooding of several properties. [PAG] (p18)
Following breach of EA defences, fluvial floodwater flowed into gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue, flooding gardens and affecting adjacent residential properties. (p23)
External flooding of gardens and roads occurred here when flood defences overtopped; residents used sandbags and pumping to protect properties. (p27)
Fluvial floodwater entered gardens on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue after EA defences were breached at Grid Ref 504983 185326. (p28)
During the 9–15 Feb 2014 event water flowed into gardens on Willow Avenue (and Willow Crescent West). Flooding was associated with backing–up of piped/overflow systems causing water to enter garden areas and threaten properties. [TAB,PAG] (p29)
The piped surface–water system along Willow Avenue was affected by flooding; a CCTV survey was proposed from Alder Road south along Willow Avenue to Tudor Cottage to locate blockages or damage caused by the event. (p30)",three properties; 16 Willow Avenue [PAG] (p18),GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5540537,-0.4854827,"<div id=""html_943c0a1a57ebfe08cf81bfd169357f33"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Poplar Road, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbagging of doors and garages was observed along Poplar Road and Alder Road. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road was reported to be in order (no breach noted) during the event. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbagging observed at doors and garages; residents deployed sandbags and pumps which prevented internal flooding of properties at the corners of Poplar Road and Willow Avenue. Pumping was carried out from properties at the northern end of the island. Estate surface water ditches and drains were not cleared, contributing to local flooding issues. Flood defence nearby (between Poplar Road and Alder Road) performed and reduced fluvial impacts. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels opposite Poplar Road were recorded high (gauge reading 32.61mAOD) and photographs show water close to the riverside path and fishing platform adjacent to Poplar Road, indicating river flooding impacted the riverside section of Poplar Road. [IMG] (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>32.61mAOD [IMG] (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Poplar Road, Denham",England,"Sandbagging of doors and garages was observed along Poplar Road and Alder Road. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road was reported to be in order (no breach noted) during the event. (p17)
Sandbagging observed at doors and garages; residents deployed sandbags and pumps which prevented internal flooding of properties at the corners of Poplar Road and Willow Avenue. Pumping was carried out from properties at the northern end of the island. Estate surface water ditches and drains were not cleared, contributing to local flooding issues. Flood defence nearby (between Poplar Road and Alder Road) performed and reduced fluvial impacts. (p18)
River levels opposite Poplar Road were recorded high (gauge reading 32.61mAOD) and photographs show water close to the riverside path and fishing platform adjacent to Poplar Road, indicating river flooding impacted the riverside section of Poplar Road. [IMG] (p36)",32.61mAOD [IMG] (p36),GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5531529,-0.4865644,"<div id=""html_59c2dc786070dc2630593982c6e23091"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Alder Road, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbagging of doors and garages was observed along Poplar Road and Alder Road. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road was reported to be in order (no breach noted) during the event. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbagging observed along Poplar Road and Alder Road. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road (rebuilt 2005/6) was reported to be all in order and performed well, preventing what residents judged would otherwise have been significant fluvial flooding to properties on the island. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Shown on the February 2014 flood map within the estimated fluvial flood extent adjacent to the watercourse, indicating local inundation of the street. [IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood&ndash;related concerns in the piped surface water system prompted a planned CCTV survey of the lower piped system from Alder Road south along Willow Avenue to Tudor Cottage and of the piped system along Hawthorn Drive to identify issues affecting drainage. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The rebuilt flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road experienced high river levels during the event; residents reported maximum water reaching just below the middle step of the fishing platform. At the footbridge by Alder Road the water reached a level measured as 11 courses of bricks from the top of the wall, demonstrating significant river flooding at these road&ndash;adjacent locations. (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Alder Road, Denham",England,"Sandbagging of doors and garages was observed along Poplar Road and Alder Road. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road was reported to be in order (no breach noted) during the event. (p17)
Sandbagging observed along Poplar Road and Alder Road. The flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road (rebuilt 2005/6) was reported to be all in order and performed well, preventing what residents judged would otherwise have been significant fluvial flooding to properties on the island. [PAG] (p18)
Shown on the February 2014 flood map within the estimated fluvial flood extent adjacent to the watercourse, indicating local inundation of the street. [IMG] (p19)
Flood–related concerns in the piped surface water system prompted a planned CCTV survey of the lower piped system from Alder Road south along Willow Avenue to Tudor Cottage and of the piped system along Hawthorn Drive to identify issues affecting drainage. (p30)
The rebuilt flood defence between Poplar Road and Alder Road experienced high river levels during the event; residents reported maximum water reaching just below the middle step of the fishing platform. At the footbridge by Alder Road the water reached a level measured as 11 courses of bricks from the top of the wall, demonstrating significant river flooding at these road–adjacent locations. (p36)",,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5534703,-0.4849928,"<div id=""html_e010dae3973e6773205cc9c4082dcb02"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hawthorn Drive, Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map annotation indicates surface&ndash;water and foul (sewer) flooding during the February 2014 event; the map notes the extent of these impacts is uncertain. [IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The piped surface&ndash;water system along Hawthorn Drive was targeted for CCTV inspection after the flood to identify issues and maintenance needs caused by the event. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Hawthorn Drive, Denham",England,"Map annotation indicates surface–water and foul (sewer) flooding during the February 2014 event; the map notes the extent of these impacts is uncertain. [IMG] (p19)
The piped surface–water system along Hawthorn Drive was targeted for CCTV inspection after the flood to identify issues and maintenance needs caused by the event. (p30)",,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_b282169fedb14965f6c8252b47affcb9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area within Buckinghamshire County (the Willowbank area of New Denham) experienced flooding that led to internal flooding of houses and businesses; this triggered a formal investigation because the flooding met the investigation threshold for internal flooding of properties/businesses within the county area. (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding of five or more properties; two or more business premises within 1km2 (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,The area within Buckinghamshire County (the Willowbank area of New Denham) experienced flooding that led to internal flooding of houses and businesses; this triggered a formal investigation because the flooding met the investigation threshold for internal flooding of properties/businesses within the county area. (p6),internal flooding of five or more properties; two or more business premises within 1km2 (p6),GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.609364,-0.498393,"<div id=""html_ca662fdeb692d3ad8f74e8a76a1b248a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grand Union Canal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A channel from the River Colne flows into and joins the Grand Union canal at the Willowbank area; these combined flows contributed to flooding of the Willowbank residential area, causing internal flooding of local houses and businesses within the Buckinghamshire County Council area. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Canal water levels rose during the flood; lock gates were reported closed but overtopped and sluice gates were discussed as having been opened, contributing to increased downstream flooding; water entering the canal system was reported to come from the River Colne. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding of five or more properties; two or more business premises within 1km2 (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Grand Union Canal,England,"A channel from the River Colne flows into and joins the Grand Union canal at the Willowbank area; these combined flows contributed to flooding of the Willowbank residential area, causing internal flooding of local houses and businesses within the Buckinghamshire County Council area. (p6)
Canal water levels rose during the flood; lock gates were reported closed but overtopped and sluice gates were discussed as having been opened, contributing to increased downstream flooding; water entering the canal system was reported to come from the River Colne. (p18)",internal flooding of five or more properties; two or more business premises within 1km2 (p6),GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.5532458,-0.4924888,"<div id=""html_f482f80d5ea3d4f76155119c20054f2a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>New Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following prolonged above&ndash;average rainfall ground was saturated and increased surface water entered highway drainage and main rivers. The River Colne was very high and overtopped its banks upstream of Willowbank; floodwater flowed across fields and into the area around Oakside and houses on Oxford Road. The river channel also overtopped alongside the shops on Oxford Road, with floodwater flowing into shops. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Willowbank residential area between two branches of the River Colne experienced internal flooding of properties; the Environment Agency surveyed the low bank upstream of Willowbank and contacted residents and the residents association to gather details and discuss options following the flood. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area&ndash;wide flooding event affecting the New Denham locality (including Willowbank) with internal flooding of properties and business premises. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow in the river/channel was backed up due to lack of flood defences and channel restrictions (overbuilds and access tracks), and poor pipe overflow systems caused water to back up into properties. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall and saturated ground led to surface water flooding in roads and gardens; high river levels and poorly maintained channels prevented discharge. A breach of the right river bank upstream of the weir sent water through fields into Oakside and on to Oxford Road, contributing to internal flooding of properties. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Garden areas on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue were inundated. The parade of shops on Oxford Road experienced internal flooding as business properties were flooded; the report attributes this to backing&ndash;up and poor pipe overflow systems allowing water into properties. [TAB] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties and residents were impacted by flooding, prompting distribution of guidance, offers to assist with Repair and Renewal Grant applications, requests to revisit River Colne modelling and to check flood warning accuracy, and investigations including a survey of a breached right bank upstream of the gauging weir. (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding of five or more properties [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five or more properties; two or more business premises [PAG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,New Denham,England,"Following prolonged above–average rainfall ground was saturated and increased surface water entered highway drainage and main rivers. The River Colne was very high and overtopped its banks upstream of Willowbank; floodwater flowed across fields and into the area around Oakside and houses on Oxford Road. The river channel also overtopped alongside the shops on Oxford Road, with floodwater flowing into shops. (p4)
The Willowbank residential area between two branches of the River Colne experienced internal flooding of properties; the Environment Agency surveyed the low bank upstream of Willowbank and contacted residents and the residents association to gather details and discuss options following the flood. [PAG] (p5)
Area–wide flooding event affecting the New Denham locality (including Willowbank) with internal flooding of properties and business premises. [PAG] (p6)
Flow in the river/channel was backed up due to lack of flood defences and channel restrictions (overbuilds and access tracks), and poor pipe overflow systems caused water to back up into properties. (p24)
Heavy rainfall and saturated ground led to surface water flooding in roads and gardens; high river levels and poorly maintained channels prevented discharge. A breach of the right river bank upstream of the weir sent water through fields into Oakside and on to Oxford Road, contributing to internal flooding of properties. (p27)
Garden areas on Willow Crescent West and Willow Avenue were inundated. The parade of shops on Oxford Road experienced internal flooding as business properties were flooded; the report attributes this to backing–up and poor pipe overflow systems allowing water into properties. [TAB] (p29)
Properties and residents were impacted by flooding, prompting distribution of guidance, offers to assist with Repair and Renewal Grant applications, requests to revisit River Colne modelling and to check flood warning accuracy, and investigations including a survey of a breached right bank upstream of the gauging weir. (p30)","internal flooding of five or more properties [PAG] (p5)
five or more properties; two or more business premises [PAG] (p6)",GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-BKM,14,2014-02-09,51.57299,-0.4998008,"<div id=""html_e053fe64079aeed867853fb37b2e500f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Denham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water reached close to properties in Denham; response gang reported it was roughly 18 inches from properties and planned to check a nearby culvert. [TAB] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>roughly 18 inches [TAB] (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Denham,England,Flood water reached close to properties in Denham; response gang reported it was roughly 18 inches from properties and planned to check a nearby culvert. [TAB] (p23),roughly 18 inches [TAB] (p23),GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09.html,GB-BKM_14_2014-02-09,New Denham flooding 09 Feb 2014 - 15 Feb 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Oxford Road New Denham February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-flood-investigation-new-denham-final-report.pdf
GB-LAN,6,2015-12-01,53.8611712,-2.5650945,"<div id=""html_4c872684c014d12422b84be6127fe11d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancashire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding affected the county in December 2015, prompting actions by local risk management authorities and the development of district&ndash;level reports to inform and support affected communities. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding during December 2015 caused very large impacts across the county, including property flooding; the county experienced the highest river flows and exceptionally wet conditions in that month. [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme storms and rainfall in November&ndash;December 2015 caused flooding throughout December that flooded properties and damaged private gardens, garages and out&ndash;buildings, business premises, agricultural property and critical public infrastructure including roads, bridges and retaining walls, water treatment plants, power and communications installations, and community buildings such as schools, village and town halls. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple communities across Lancashire experienced flooding as a result of two major storm events in December 2015; this prompted a countywide Section 19 investigation to identify affected communities and recommend actions, and led to follow&ndash;up stage&ndash;two reporting by the LLFA and other risk management authorities. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 2,500 properties flooded [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 2,500 properties (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lancashire,England,"Significant flooding affected the county in December 2015, prompting actions by local risk management authorities and the development of district–level reports to inform and support affected communities. [PAG] (p3)
Widespread flooding during December 2015 caused very large impacts across the county, including property flooding; the county experienced the highest river flows and exceptionally wet conditions in that month. [TAB] (p8)
Extreme storms and rainfall in November–December 2015 caused flooding throughout December that flooded properties and damaged private gardens, garages and out–buildings, business premises, agricultural property and critical public infrastructure including roads, bridges and retaining walls, water treatment plants, power and communications installations, and community buildings such as schools, village and town halls. (p9)
Multiple communities across Lancashire experienced flooding as a result of two major storm events in December 2015; this prompted a countywide Section 19 investigation to identify affected communities and recommend actions, and led to follow–up stage–two reporting by the LLFA and other risk management authorities. [PAG] (p10)","over 2,500 properties flooded [TAB] (p8)
over 2,500 properties (p9)",GB-LAN_6_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_6_2015-12-01,Hyndburn flooding December 2015,Hyndburn District Flood Report Recommended Actions,069/Lancashire County/hyndburn-final-report.pdf
GB-LAN,6,2015-12-01,53.9031969,-2.7762038183560396,"<div id=""html_01c465b99f40d107b12e019a99b3ccd0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Garstang</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood basins were activated in Garstang as part of the response to the December 2015 flood event to manage floodwaters. [TAB] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Garstang,England,Flood basins were activated in Garstang as part of the response to the December 2015 flood event to manage floodwaters. [TAB] (p8),,GB-LAN_6_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_6_2015-12-01,Hyndburn flooding December 2015,Hyndburn District Flood Report Recommended Actions,069/Lancashire County/hyndburn-final-report.pdf
