GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.7437027,-0.3680466,"<div id=""html_f2dd4abf3df49773869a79ed5d276de0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Anlaby Road, Kingston Upon Hull</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The trunk sewer in Anlaby Road was overwhelmed by intense rainfall on the upstream catchment, causing the local sewer system to be unable to discharge quickly enough and contributing to the flooding experienced at Anlaby Common. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,"Anlaby Road, Kingston Upon Hull",England,"The trunk sewer in Anlaby Road was overwhelmed by intense rainfall on the upstream catchment, causing the local sewer system to be unable to discharge quickly enough and contributing to the flooding experienced at Anlaby Common. (p13)",,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.7497438,-0.4182577,"<div id=""html_543fb44d1ecc657cb0e5ce593f9d2267"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Springhead Lane, Anlaby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The trunk sewer that runs down Springhead Lane into the Western Trunk was overwhelmed by intense rainfall, meaning local sewers could not discharge quickly enough and contributing to flooding at Anlaby Common. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,"Springhead Lane, Anlaby",England,"The trunk sewer that runs down Springhead Lane into the Western Trunk was overwhelmed by intense rainfall, meaning local sewers could not discharge quickly enough and contributing to flooding at Anlaby Common. (p13)",,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.88432508022958,-0.5364144843155703,"<div id=""html_964193b97c77eaabab9713c0bee11af3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Riding of Yorkshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in the Holderness Drain (pumps at East Hull Pumping Station were not operated) caused flooding of a business premises in the East Riding. [MUL] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 business premises [MUL] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,East Riding of Yorkshire,England,High water levels in the Holderness Drain (pumps at East Hull Pumping Station were not operated) caused flooding of a business premises in the East Riding. [MUL] (p13),1 business premises [MUL] (p13),GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.78376184526979,-0.3400888777748184,"<div id=""html_5054bb0a82a603a590bd5f047bb50f5f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sutton Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At least one property within Sutton Park (on Paxdale) experienced internal flooding during the heavy rain event on 10 November 2019. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water flowed from a higher footpath into a front garden with an impervious surface, causing slight internal flooding of the property via an air brick. (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Sutton Park,England,"At least one property within Sutton Park (on Paxdale) experienced internal flooding during the heavy rain event on 10 November 2019. (p4)
Water flowed from a higher footpath into a front garden with an impervious surface, causing slight internal flooding of the property via an air brick. (p7)",1 property (p4),GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.7416159,-0.315823,"<div id=""html_9132d28fc6bc98db29e30e1082b52a0c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Victoria Dock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Victoria Dock experienced internal flooding at least at one property (on Pilots Way) during the heavy rain event on 10 November 2019. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A report that water flowed down a slope in the garden to the back of the house; enquiries were unable to confirm whether flooding occurred or how much damage was caused. (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Victoria Dock,England,"Victoria Dock experienced internal flooding at least at one property (on Pilots Way) during the heavy rain event on 10 November 2019. (p4)
A report that water flowed down a slope in the garden to the back of the house; enquiries were unable to confirm whether flooding occurred or how much damage was caused. (p7)",1 property (p4),GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.74529067467464,-0.43207644471880285,"<div id=""html_a0bd6e96ae194bfc4eee390ae68aebf7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Anlaby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense upstream rainfall overwhelmed the trunk sewer network flowing west&ndash;east through the area; local sewer system could not discharge quickly enough into the trunk sewers, causing flooding at Anlaby Common. (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Anlaby,England,"Intense upstream rainfall overwhelmed the trunk sewer network flowing west–east through the area; local sewer system could not discharge quickly enough into the trunk sewers, causing flooding at Anlaby Common. (p13)",,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.7438625,-0.2351901,"<div id=""html_856b2af90cc678bd2e7af7c30d4fcea6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Salt End</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Old Fleet Drain at Salt End required on&ndash;site pumping and delayed bank clearance; a temporary sandbag wall was constructed on 15/11/2019 to provide a defence. [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Salt End,England,"High water levels in Old Fleet Drain at Salt End required on–site pumping and delayed bank clearance; a temporary sandbag wall was constructed on 15/11/2019 to provide a defence. [TAB,MUL] (p21)",,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.7769905,-0.2145499,"<div id=""html_4255391fa3f21930c89fc114516b58fd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Old Fleet Drain</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Fleet Drain over&ndash;topped on 27 October 2019 causing internal property flooding; it over&ndash;topped again following heavy rain on 10 November 2019 and remained at high levels for several days, with internal flooding only prevented by the use of pumps at strategic locations. [MUL] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tidal water can back up the drain when Lord's Clough tidal pointing doors are closed (tide locking), effectively damming fluvial flows and causing water to back up. Pumps were deployed to manage backed&ndash;up flows; high water levels delayed temporary works. A temporary sandbag wall was constructed once site works were possible. [TAB,MUL] (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Old Fleet Drain,England,"Old Fleet Drain over–topped on 27 October 2019 causing internal property flooding; it over–topped again following heavy rain on 10 November 2019 and remained at high levels for several days, with internal flooding only prevented by the use of pumps at strategic locations. [MUL] (p4)
Tidal water can back up the drain when Lord's Clough tidal pointing doors are closed (tide locking), effectively damming fluvial flows and causing water to back up. Pumps were deployed to manage backed–up flows; high water levels delayed temporary works. A temporary sandbag wall was constructed once site works were possible. [TAB,MUL] (p21)",5 properties [MUL] (p4),GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.8198048,-0.3215598,"<div id=""html_ba368dfd24a201f5c8d2b467f2248a5a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Holderness Drain</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high water levels on the Holderness Drain caused some flooding to property outside the city boundary. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Record high levels on the Holderness Drain during the event caused flooding to property outside of the Hull city boundary. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pumps at East Hull Pumping Station were not operated on 27 October 2019, causing high water levels in the Holderness Drain that resulted in flooding of a business premises in the East Riding. The text notes no properties were flooded in Hull in this event. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood alert was issued indicating a risk of flooding to Holderness Drain and adjacent areas. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Manager from Hull City Council attended for Holderness Drain during the high&ndash;tide flood response (monitored/managed alongside Bexhill Avenue). (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 business premises flooded; 0 properties flooded in Hull (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,Holderness Drain,England,"Record high water levels on the Holderness Drain caused some flooding to property outside the city boundary. (p7)
Record high levels on the Holderness Drain during the event caused flooding to property outside of the Hull city boundary. [PAG] (p8)
Pumps at East Hull Pumping Station were not operated on 27 October 2019, causing high water levels in the Holderness Drain that resulted in flooding of a business premises in the East Riding. The text notes no properties were flooded in Hull in this event. (p13)
A flood alert was issued indicating a risk of flooding to Holderness Drain and adjacent areas. (p16)
Manager from Hull City Council attended for Holderness Drain during the high–tide flood response (monitored/managed alongside Bexhill Avenue). (p21)",1 business premises flooded; 0 properties flooded in Hull (p13),GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-KHL,3,2019-10-27,53.7474574,-0.2875034,"<div id=""html_c4fa0b76c35c66318a10b24b8a8af88e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Hull Pumping Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 27 October the pumps at East Hull Pumping Station were not operated, resulting in high water levels in the Holderness Drain; this event caused flooding of a business premises in the East Riding (and that premises has flooded several times since). (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one business premises (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Hull,East Hull Pumping Station,England,"On 27 October the pumps at East Hull Pumping Station were not operated, resulting in high water levels in the Holderness Drain; this event caused flooding of a business premises in the East Riding (and that premises has flooded several times since). (p13)",one business premises (p13),GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc.html,GB-KHL_3_2019-10-27_etc,Kingston Upon Hull flooding 27 Oct 2019 and Kingston Upon Hull flooding 10 Nov 2019,Hull City Council Flood Investigation Report: Section 19 – Prolonged Rain October/November 2019,"064/Kingston upon Hull, City of/ProlongedRainOctoberNovember2019.pdf"
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6332453,-1.106592,"<div id=""html_cab53c88e44925f10e9ed15bd1d1f75a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Spinney Hill Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Evington Brook gauge located in Spinney Hills Park recorded a rapid rise to a peak around 17:15 on 22/06/2023, indicating the park area experienced elevated brook levels during the event. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>peak ≈ 1.2 m river level (gauge at park) [IMG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Spinney Hill Park,England,"The Evington Brook gauge located in Spinney Hills Park recorded a rapid rise to a peak around 17:15 on 22/06/2023, indicating the park area experienced elevated brook levels during the event. [IMG] (p33)",peak ≈ 1.2 m river level (gauge at park) [IMG] (p33),GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.634773649573084,-1.1296059467547577,"<div id=""html_d64bfb7b6e871b5a921f73a25e8a3132"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Castle Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported at multiple locations in Castle Ward (Lorne Road, Oxford Road, and the junction of Kingsley Street/Wordsworth Road). Surface water flow routes converge at the Kingsley/Wordsworth corner; Lorne Road is a low point receiving runoff from Victoria Park Road and has previous reports of flooding with possible sewer capacity exceedance; Oxford Road properties were flooded by surface water flowing from Montague Road and Edward Road into the rear of properties. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple streets within the Castle ward experienced surface water flooding: Lorne Road (low point of surface water flow from Victoria Park Road), Oxford Road (surface water flows from Montague Road and Edward Road), and the corner of Kingsley Street/Wordsworth Road where two surface water flow routes meet. [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Castle Ward,England,"Internal property flooding reported at multiple locations in Castle Ward (Lorne Road, Oxford Road, and the junction of Kingsley Street/Wordsworth Road). Surface water flow routes converge at the Kingsley/Wordsworth corner; Lorne Road is a low point receiving runoff from Victoria Park Road and has previous reports of flooding with possible sewer capacity exceedance; Oxford Road properties were flooded by surface water flowing from Montague Road and Edward Road into the rear of properties. [TAB] (p12)
Multiple streets within the Castle ward experienced surface water flooding: Lorne Road (low point of surface water flow from Victoria Park Road), Oxford Road (surface water flows from Montague Road and Edward Road), and the corner of Kingsley Street/Wordsworth Road where two surface water flow routes meet. [TAB] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.650109939447134,-1.070736468053334,"<div id=""html_32710315f9b5e3f60f4a3cda69301a90"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Humberstone and Hamilton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was reported within the ward during the event; multiple locations in the ward reported internal flooding associated with surface water routes and local watercourses. [TAB] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected multiple streets in the ward (St Marys Avenue, Abbots Road South, Tennis Court Drive, West Drive). Flood routes included flows along garage accesses adjacent to the Scraptoft Brook, overland flows following the public sewer line from Tennis Court Drive, and ponding in low spots before Humberstone Drive, causing flooding of garages, low&ndash;lying properties and surrounding areas. [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Humberstone and Hamilton,England,"Internal property flooding was reported within the ward during the event; multiple locations in the ward reported internal flooding associated with surface water routes and local watercourses. [TAB] (p13)
Surface water flooding affected multiple streets in the ward (St Marys Avenue, Abbots Road South, Tennis Court Drive, West Drive). Flood routes included flows along garage accesses adjacent to the Scraptoft Brook, overland flows following the public sewer line from Tennis Court Drive, and ponding in low spots before Humberstone Drive, causing flooding of garages, low–lying properties and surrounding areas. [TAB] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6383633273538,-1.0577319587922767,"<div id=""html_bc7246271b73e6485dcb758a02f5ae63"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thurncourt Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported in the ward (tables list multiple affected addresses). Flood sources recorded in the ward include surface water flow routes, combined/foul sewer discharge to rear gardens, and proximity to Bushby Brook. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple streets in the Thurncourt ward were affected by flooding on 22 June 2023: overland/surface water flow routes and nearby watercourses (e.g., Bushby Brook) impacted properties, and a combined sewer caused flooding into rear gardens on Uppingham Road. [TAB,PAG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Thurncourt Ward,England,"Internal property flooding reported in the ward (tables list multiple affected addresses). Flood sources recorded in the ward include surface water flow routes, combined/foul sewer discharge to rear gardens, and proximity to Bushby Brook. [TAB] (p17)
Multiple streets in the Thurncourt ward were affected by flooding on 22 June 2023: overland/surface water flow routes and nearby watercourses (e.g., Bushby Brook) impacted properties, and a combined sewer caused flooding into rear gardens on Uppingham Road. [TAB,PAG] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.667312552853495,-1.087155610526802,"<div id=""html_a1ddb019b1b558eaa13d60b9c6903cdd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Troon Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported within the ward: Wenlock Way (low area collecting surface water from a large carriageway area) and Barkby Road (surface water flows from the north and reported sewer operational issues contributing to flooding). [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ward contained flooded streets (Wenlock Way, Barkby Road) where surface water collected in low areas and flooded carriageways and adjacent properties during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Troon Ward,England,"Internal property flooding reported within the ward: Wenlock Way (low area collecting surface water from a large carriageway area) and Barkby Road (surface water flows from the north and reported sewer operational issues contributing to flooding). [TAB] (p17)
Ward contained flooded streets (Wenlock Way, Barkby Road) where surface water collected in low areas and flooded carriageways and adjacent properties during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.635822805479144,-1.1157143755900987,"<div id=""html_b80611935640e95e8651aacd814b0975"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wycliffe Ward</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported across the ward during the event (multiple properties experienced internal flooding). [TAB] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down connecting roads (Melbourne Road, Hasting Road, Cecil Road) towards Nedham Street and Humberstone Road, causing flooding within the Wycliffe area. [TAB,PAG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Wycliffe Ward,England,"Internal property flooding reported across the ward during the event (multiple properties experienced internal flooding). [TAB] (p18)
Surface water flowed down connecting roads (Melbourne Road, Hasting Road, Cecil Road) towards Nedham Street and Humberstone Road, causing flooding within the Wycliffe area. [TAB,PAG] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6383331,-1.0937954,"<div id=""html_2abf25a48b6f8650d25ee80e1aa0e21b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bushby Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported along the Bushby Brook (a tributary of the River Soar). (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows immediately to the rear of properties on Uppingham Road and Scraptoft Lane and is the main river adjacent to areas of surface water flood risk; acted as the receptor/adjacent watercourse in the reported flooding. During the event the brook level was reported to be within flood walls and at a relatively low level. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bushby Brook is shown as the main watercourse generating surface&ndash;water flooding in the Scraptoft Lane cluster; highest flood&ndash;risk areas are concentrated along and adjacent to the brook, with red&ndash;outlined Flood Impact Areas extending into neighbouring residential streets. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bushby Brook flows behind properties on Scraptoft Lane and forms the flood flow destination for overland surface water routes; its proximity caused flooding risk/impacts to rear gardens and properties adjacent to the brook. [TAB,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bushby Brook flows immediately to the rear of odd&ndash;numbered properties on Scraptoft Lane and formed a flood flow route behind those properties, contributing to surface water/flood flow into rear gardens and properties. (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Bushby Brook,England,"Flooding was reported along the Bushby Brook (a tributary of the River Soar). (p4)
Flows immediately to the rear of properties on Uppingham Road and Scraptoft Lane and is the main river adjacent to areas of surface water flood risk; acted as the receptor/adjacent watercourse in the reported flooding. During the event the brook level was reported to be within flood walls and at a relatively low level. [TAB] (p17)
Bushby Brook is shown as the main watercourse generating surface–water flooding in the Scraptoft Lane cluster; highest flood–risk areas are concentrated along and adjacent to the brook, with red–outlined Flood Impact Areas extending into neighbouring residential streets. [IMG] (p29)
Bushby Brook flows behind properties on Scraptoft Lane and forms the flood flow destination for overland surface water routes; its proximity caused flooding risk/impacts to rear gardens and properties adjacent to the brook. [TAB,PAG] (p34)
Bushby Brook flows immediately to the rear of odd–numbered properties on Scraptoft Lane and formed a flood flow route behind those properties, contributing to surface water/flood flow into rear gardens and properties. (p35)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6195362,-1.0647282,"<div id=""html_0435d8220b0fdd46121830c550fddd2c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Evington Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding was reported along the Evington Brooks (tributaries of the River Soar). (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the main river contributing to fluvial flooding and surface water flow routes; associated with internal property flooding on adjacent streets (e.g. Gwendolen Road, Mervyn Road, Evington Road) and provided flow paths along roads. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Evington Brook is shown as the watercourse along which surface water flowed and caused flood impact areas in the Staveley Road area; flow along the brook routed surface water north&ndash;to&ndash;south and spread into adjacent streets (notably near Hazelwood Road, Osmaston Road and Merlyn Road). [IMG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Evington Brook at Spinney Hills Park showed a rapid rise in water level in the late afternoon of 22/06/2023, peaking around 17:15 and then declining through the evening, consistent with a transient river&ndash;flood/flash response. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flow along Evington Brook produced low spots and flow routes that caused localised road and property flooding (noted adjacent to Gwendolen Road, Mervyn Road, Evington Drive, Linton Street and Evington Road), with road levels and nearby low spots exacerbating inundation. [TAB,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed along the flow path of the brook and contributed to flooding adjacent to the road and nearby properties; road level at nearby carriageway is a low spot relative to the brook, promoting inundation along the brook corridor. (p35)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>peak ≈ 1.2 m river level [IMG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Evington Brook,England,"Flooding was reported along the Evington Brooks (tributaries of the River Soar). (p4)
Identified as the main river contributing to fluvial flooding and surface water flow routes; associated with internal property flooding on adjacent streets (e.g. Gwendolen Road, Mervyn Road, Evington Road) and provided flow paths along roads. [TAB] (p16)
Evington Brook is shown as the watercourse along which surface water flowed and caused flood impact areas in the Staveley Road area; flow along the brook routed surface water north–to–south and spread into adjacent streets (notably near Hazelwood Road, Osmaston Road and Merlyn Road). [IMG] (p30)
Evington Brook at Spinney Hills Park showed a rapid rise in water level in the late afternoon of 22/06/2023, peaking around 17:15 and then declining through the evening, consistent with a transient river–flood/flash response. [IMG] (p33)
Surface–water flow along Evington Brook produced low spots and flow routes that caused localised road and property flooding (noted adjacent to Gwendolen Road, Mervyn Road, Evington Drive, Linton Street and Evington Road), with road levels and nearby low spots exacerbating inundation. [TAB,PAG] (p34)
Surface water flowed along the flow path of the brook and contributed to flooding adjacent to the road and nearby properties; road level at nearby carriageway is a low spot relative to the brook, promoting inundation along the brook corridor. (p35)",peak ≈ 1.2 m river level [IMG] (p33),GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6063713,-1.0959464,"<div id=""html_e96e7184d386cac8abeb13fac045e06e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hol Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hol Brook flooding (and its flood extent) generated surface water flow routes along nearby streets, contributing to internal property flooding in the area. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culverted channel of Hol Brook conveyed surface flows through housing (notably through Arreton Close), contributing to flooding where watercourse levels restricted the ability of public sewers and highway drainage to outfall effectively. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hol Brook is shown as the primary watercourse producing surface&ndash;water flooding; high and medium risk zones follow its path and Flood Impact Areas are outlined at several points along the brook where flooding affects adjacent residential streets and properties. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hol Brook acted as an ordinary watercourse and flow line that contributed to surface water flow routes and associated flooding to adjacent streets (notably Arreton Close and Knighton Church Road). [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Hol Brook,England,"Hol Brook flooding (and its flood extent) generated surface water flow routes along nearby streets, contributing to internal property flooding in the area. [TAB] (p14)
The culverted channel of Hol Brook conveyed surface flows through housing (notably through Arreton Close), contributing to flooding where watercourse levels restricted the ability of public sewers and highway drainage to outfall effectively. (p19)
Hol Brook is shown as the primary watercourse producing surface–water flooding; high and medium risk zones follow its path and Flood Impact Areas are outlined at several points along the brook where flooding affects adjacent residential streets and properties. [IMG] (p29)
Hol Brook acted as an ordinary watercourse and flow line that contributed to surface water flow routes and associated flooding to adjacent streets (notably Arreton Close and Knighton Church Road). [TAB] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.643649,-1.1185519,"<div id=""html_5062acd4d75cf9bca54947d307f19df6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willow Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Willow Brook experienced a sudden, short&ndash;lived surge in river level on the afternoon of 22/06/2023: a rapid rise to a peak then a gradual fall over the evening, indicating a transient river&ndash;flooding/flash response at Ash Street. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>peak ≈ 1.6 m river level [IMG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Willow Brook,England,"Willow Brook experienced a sudden, short–lived surge in river level on the afternoon of 22/06/2023: a rapid rise to a peak then a gradual fall over the evening, indicating a transient river–flooding/flash response at Ash Street. [IMG] (p33)",peak ≈ 1.6 m river level [IMG] (p33),GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6141085,-1.1398049,"<div id=""html_50094bdbf9db6dc4fd773ad6a6f9a674"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Saffron Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A pluvial low spot above Saffron Brook led to surface water flooding in the adjacent Welford Road area on 22nd June 2023. [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Saffron Brook,England,A pluvial low spot above Saffron Brook led to surface water flooding in the adjacent Welford Road area on 22nd June 2023. [TAB] (p34),,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6362,-1.1331969,"<div id=""html_104323c000fd355cec7798637277f1ce"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Leicester Unitary Authority</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall on 22 June 2023 caused surface water to flow across the ground and overwhelm drains and sewers, resulting in internal flooding of domestic properties. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties across a large area of the city were recorded as flooded during the storm covered by this report. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 22 June 2023 intense rainfall caused many sewers to surcharge in the south and east of the city. Evidence included displaced manhole/inspection covers. The event caused rapid overland flow to low areas, overwhelmed highway gullies and outfalls, and resulted in damage to roads, paving and significant damage to highway surfacing. [IMG,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface water and sewer surcharging across the city (particularly south and east) caused displaced manhole/inspection covers, roads and paving damage, flooded roads, and internal property flooding. Overland flows moved rapidly to low areas; emergency response and highway repairs were required. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A map of reported internal property flooding incidents shows distributions across the city with the highest concentration of reports in central and northeastern Leicester; several locations on the map are indicated as having affected more than six properties. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Public highways across the city experienced flooding related to the interaction between highway drainage and the public sewer network, prompting reporting to the water company and targeted maintenance actions. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>South and east areas of Leicester experienced flooding on 22nd June 2023: public highways were flooded and properties were flooded where rainfall exceeded the capacity of public sewer systems. [TAB] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of public highways across Leicester occurred on 22nd June 2023 when public sewers and highway drainage interacted, causing transport disruption and prompting investigations. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roads in Leicester experienced flooding on 22nd June 2023; this led to requests for sewer condition reports and localised modelling to identify capacity issues in drainage and sewer networks. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas in the South Knighton area along the Hol Brook were affected by flooding; flooding occurred along the watercourse and affected the local area. (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 80 domestic properties (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 flooding incidents; 95 personnel; 7 pumps (at 6 locations); over 60 properties visited [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than 6 properties at multiple locations [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Leicester Unitary Authority,England,"Intense rainfall on 22 June 2023 caused surface water to flow across the ground and overwhelm drains and sewers, resulting in internal flooding of domestic properties. (p4)
Properties across a large area of the city were recorded as flooded during the storm covered by this report. (p6)
On 22 June 2023 intense rainfall caused many sewers to surcharge in the south and east of the city. Evidence included displaced manhole/inspection covers. The event caused rapid overland flow to low areas, overwhelmed highway gullies and outfalls, and resulted in damage to roads, paving and significant damage to highway surfacing. [IMG,PAG] (p7)
Widespread surface water and sewer surcharging across the city (particularly south and east) caused displaced manhole/inspection covers, roads and paving damage, flooded roads, and internal property flooding. Overland flows moved rapidly to low areas; emergency response and highway repairs were required. [PAG] (p8)
A map of reported internal property flooding incidents shows distributions across the city with the highest concentration of reports in central and northeastern Leicester; several locations on the map are indicated as having affected more than six properties. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
Public highways across the city experienced flooding related to the interaction between highway drainage and the public sewer network, prompting reporting to the water company and targeted maintenance actions. [TAB] (p21)
South and east areas of Leicester experienced flooding on 22nd June 2023: public highways were flooded and properties were flooded where rainfall exceeded the capacity of public sewer systems. [TAB] (p23)
Flooding of public highways across Leicester occurred on 22nd June 2023 when public sewers and highway drainage interacted, causing transport disruption and prompting investigations. [PAG] (p24)
Roads in Leicester experienced flooding on 22nd June 2023; this led to requests for sewer condition reports and localised modelling to identify capacity issues in drainage and sewer networks. (p25)
Areas in the South Knighton area along the Hol Brook were affected by flooding; flooding occurred along the watercourse and affected the local area. (p28)","over 80 domestic properties (p4)
18 flooding incidents; 95 personnel; 7 pumps (at 6 locations); over 60 properties visited [PAG] (p8)
more than 6 properties at multiple locations [IMG,PAG] (p11)",GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
