In the Eyres Monsell ward, surface–water flow routes joined on Tamerton Road causing high surface–water flooding and inundation of properties on Tamerton Road. [TAB] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6072376,-1.1098891,"<div id=""html_ab2d7d624e1a2c7f1f5a082b4cea31ea"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Knighton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported in the ward; surface water flow routes following Hol Brook and pluvial low spots caused inundation of properties on roads listed in the ward. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flow routes followed the line of Hol Brook and along Knighton Church Road/Knighton Road, producing low&ndash;to&ndash;medium surface&ndash;water flood risk with overland flows toward Westernhay Road and localised roadway/property inundation in the Knighton area. [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Knighton,England,"Internal property flooding reported in the ward; surface water flow routes following Hol Brook and pluvial low spots caused inundation of properties on roads listed in the ward. [TAB] (p14)
Surface water flow routes followed the line of Hol Brook and along Knighton Church Road/Knighton Road, producing low–to–medium surface–water flood risk with overland flows toward Westernhay Road and localised roadway/property inundation in the Knighton area. [TAB] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6404528,-1.0969686,"<div id=""html_d9c0e75e062823869cb022626e2b6316"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Evington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported within the ward associated with surface water runoff and local low spots/flow routes. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area&ndash;level surface&ndash;water flooding in North Evington, with concentrated flood risk along The Littleway, Clumber Road and surrounding streets; Flood Impact Areas outlined on the map indicate parts of the neighbourhood were affected. [IMG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pluvial and surface&ndash;water flooding across multiple streets (Welford Road; Lotus/Lily/Halation Road area; The Littleway; Asfordby Street; The Portway; Clumber Road; Green Lane Road) &mdash; low spots, blocked flow routes and a pumping&ndash;station discharge to the Portway Brook contributed to properties being flooded. [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,North Evington,England,"Internal property flooding reported within the ward associated with surface water runoff and local low spots/flow routes. [TAB] (p15)
Area–level surface–water flooding in North Evington, with concentrated flood risk along The Littleway, Clumber Road and surrounding streets; Flood Impact Areas outlined on the map indicate parts of the neighbourhood were affected. [IMG] (p32)
Pluvial and surface–water flooding across multiple streets (Welford Road; Lotus/Lily/Halation Road area; The Littleway; Asfordby Street; The Portway; Clumber Road; Green Lane Road) — low spots, blocked flow routes and a pumping–station discharge to the Portway Brook contributed to properties being flooded. [TAB] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6196528,-1.1038861,"<div id=""html_d197e39297250bfdc060c7e3be601305"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stoneygate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area adjacent to Evington Brook experienced surface water flooding; roads (including Evington Road and Mervyn Road) were low spots/flow routes causing flooding of the local road network and properties. [TAB,PAG] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Stoneygate,England,"Area adjacent to Evington Brook experienced surface water flooding; roads (including Evington Road and Mervyn Road) were low spots/flow routes causing flooding of the local road network and properties. [TAB,PAG] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6647556,-1.1049633,"<div id=""html_f6db1f994f0f65c99e5baa95b9417351"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rushey Mead</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding reported in the ward (Canon Street), at a low spot location. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Low&ndash;lying areas (e.g. Halstead Street and Canon Street) formed pluvial/ surface water low spots leading to inundation of properties and localized flooding. [TAB] (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Rushey Mead,England,"Internal property flooding reported in the ward (Canon Street), at a low spot location. [TAB] (p16)
Low–lying areas (e.g. Halstead Street and Canon Street) formed pluvial/ surface water low spots leading to inundation of properties and localized flooding. [TAB] (p34)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-LCE,1,2023-06-22,52.6463523,-1.0799397,"<div id=""html_92cd1838fd4c5312b587ce4deda2f626"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Humberstone</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential parts of Humberstone adjacent to Portwey Brook were within high, medium and low surface&ndash;water flood risk zones; two main red&ndash;outlined flood impact areas are clustered beside the brook, with surface water flowing through streets into the brook and concentrating in those zones. [IMG] (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",Leicester,Humberstone,England,"Residential parts of Humberstone adjacent to Portwey Brook were within high, medium and low surface–water flood risk zones; two main red–outlined flood impact areas are clustered beside the brook, with surface water flowing through streets into the brook and concentrating in those zones. [IMG] (p31)",,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22.html,GB-LCE_1_2023-06-22,Leicester Unitary Authority flooding 22 Jun 2023,Flood Investigation Report of the Flood Event Thursday 22nd June 2023 (South and East Leicester City),071/Leicester/flood-investigation-report-22-06-2023-section-19/v2-0.pdf
GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.3015328,-0.6925923,"<div id=""html_7ff15441bf6a1ecb39ee3f21fa900c76"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Market Street, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration heavy rainfall caused surface water drainage systems to be overwhelmed, resulting in excess surface water flowing to low points and entering buildings on Market Street. Five buildings in close proximity experienced internal flooding (water crossed the threshold and entered the buildings). (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of buildings: water crossed thresholds and entered premises on Market Street during the incident. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from intense rainfall and runoff flowed along Market Street into premises. Multiple shops experienced internal flooding from front doors and entrances; water flowed through shops to rear egresses and pooled in central Market Street, preventing customer access and causing business disruption. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple commercial and residential premises on Market Street experienced internal and external flooding as surface water flowed into the footway and across ground floors. Water pooled in central Market Street (&#8216;held like a lake&#8217;) and flowed from the north and from the rear of the library. Customers were often unable to enter shops because of a river of water in the footway; vehicle bow waves increased depths inside properties. Emergency pumping (FandR) and manual removal were required; some businesses could not reopen for days or weeks. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Four properties on Market Street flooded internally. At a fifth property four people swept water away from the front door for several hours to prevent ingress. The road is relatively flat so water deepened along the entirety of the road and overtopped into Midland Road; external rear areas of properties are enclosed so water could not drain away effectively. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External areas to the rear of properties were enclosed so water could not drain away effectively, causing flood depths to increase along Market Street; the relatively flat road profile allowed surface water to deepen. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Blocked/inoperable slot drains and a visibly broken carriageway channel led to standing water and large puddles on the pavement and carriageway, causing drainage failure and creating hazards for pedestrians, cyclists and vehicles during the flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Inoperable slot drains and a broken carriageway channel on Market Street reduced drainage capacity and contributed to surface&ndash;water road flooding and local water accumulation during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five buildings (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five buildings (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Reported internal flood depths across shops: 50 mm; 75 mm; 100 mm; 50&ndash;100 mm; 300 mm; 200 mm; 900 mm (in one shop); loss of electricity for up to 48 hours; internet loss (one shop) until 22/09/20; some businesses unable to reopen for days to weeks (reopened dates reported 21/09/20 and 15/09/20). [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>900mm depth; 300mm depth; 50mm depth; 200mm depth; external 400mm / internal 25mm (for affected property); lost electricity for 48 hours; unable to reopen business until 15/09/2020; reopened 21/09/2020; FandR pumped at approx. 18:00 on 16/08/2020; one report of ~1 hour to recede (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; four people at a fifth property (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Market Street, Wellingborough",England,"Intense, short–duration heavy rainfall caused surface water drainage systems to be overwhelmed, resulting in excess surface water flowing to low points and entering buildings on Market Street. Five buildings in close proximity experienced internal flooding (water crossed the threshold and entered the buildings). (p6)
Internal flooding of buildings: water crossed thresholds and entered premises on Market Street during the incident. (p8)
Surface water from intense rainfall and runoff flowed along Market Street into premises. Multiple shops experienced internal flooding from front doors and entrances; water flowed through shops to rear egresses and pooled in central Market Street, preventing customer access and causing business disruption. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Multiple commercial and residential premises on Market Street experienced internal and external flooding as surface water flowed into the footway and across ground floors. Water pooled in central Market Street (‘held like a lake’) and flowed from the north and from the rear of the library. Customers were often unable to enter shops because of a river of water in the footway; vehicle bow waves increased depths inside properties. Emergency pumping (FandR) and manual removal were required; some businesses could not reopen for days or weeks. (p18)
Four properties on Market Street flooded internally. At a fifth property four people swept water away from the front door for several hours to prevent ingress. The road is relatively flat so water deepened along the entirety of the road and overtopped into Midland Road; external rear areas of properties are enclosed so water could not drain away effectively. (p20)
External areas to the rear of properties were enclosed so water could not drain away effectively, causing flood depths to increase along Market Street; the relatively flat road profile allowed surface water to deepen. [PAG] (p21)
Blocked/inoperable slot drains and a visibly broken carriageway channel led to standing water and large puddles on the pavement and carriageway, causing drainage failure and creating hazards for pedestrians, cyclists and vehicles during the flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p37)
Inoperable slot drains and a broken carriageway channel on Market Street reduced drainage capacity and contributed to surface–water road flooding and local water accumulation during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p38)","five buildings (p6)
five buildings (p8)
Reported internal flood depths across shops: 50 mm; 75 mm; 100 mm; 50–100 mm; 300 mm; 200 mm; 900 mm (in one shop); loss of electricity for up to 48 hours; internet loss (one shop) until 22/09/20; some businesses unable to reopen for days to weeks (reopened dates reported 21/09/20 and 15/09/20). [TAB,PAG] (p17)
900mm depth; 300mm depth; 50mm depth; 200mm depth; external 400mm / internal 25mm (for affected property); lost electricity for 48 hours; unable to reopen business until 15/09/2020; reopened 21/09/2020; FandR pumped at approx. 18:00 on 16/08/2020; one report of ~1 hour to recede (p18)
4 properties; four people at a fifth property (p20)",GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Market Street, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Market Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.302982,-0.69072,"<div id=""html_431b568a2e1c0614a759bbce5f06c8a3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridge Street, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Cambridge Street reported being flooded and were contacted as part of the investigation; flood report forms were hand&ndash;delivered and affected occupiers were visited by the investigating officer. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two StreetDoctor reports of blocked drains on Cambridge Street and observations of occasional gullies holding silt to surface level, impairing drainage during the flood event. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed from Cambridge Street toward Market Street, contributing to inundation of shop frontages; one respondent noted another property on Cambridge Street has historically flooded. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as the northern source area from which water flowed south down Glen Bank, contributing to the surface water runoff that caused property flooding at Glen Bank and Market Street. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Six properties on Cambridge Street flooded internally. Water flowed southeast through gaps between buildings toward Glen Bank and deepened due to relatively flat local levels. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Relatively flat areas of Cambridge Street caused surface water to deepen and acted as part of overland flow pathways contributing to flooding. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A blocked road gully on Cambridge Street (debris observed in the drain) impeded drainage, causing localised surface&ndash;water ponding at a low point and contributing to road flooding at the Cambridge/Market Street area during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two reports of blocked drains (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Cambridge Street, Wellingborough",England,"Properties on Cambridge Street reported being flooded and were contacted as part of the investigation; flood report forms were hand–delivered and affected occupiers were visited by the investigating officer. (p8)
Two StreetDoctor reports of blocked drains on Cambridge Street and observations of occasional gullies holding silt to surface level, impairing drainage during the flood event. (p15)
Floodwater flowed from Cambridge Street toward Market Street, contributing to inundation of shop frontages; one respondent noted another property on Cambridge Street has historically flooded. [TAB] (p17)
Described as the northern source area from which water flowed south down Glen Bank, contributing to the surface water runoff that caused property flooding at Glen Bank and Market Street. (p18)
Six properties on Cambridge Street flooded internally. Water flowed southeast through gaps between buildings toward Glen Bank and deepened due to relatively flat local levels. (p20)
Relatively flat areas of Cambridge Street caused surface water to deepen and acted as part of overland flow pathways contributing to flooding. (p21)
A blocked road gully on Cambridge Street (debris observed in the drain) impeded drainage, causing localised surface–water ponding at a low point and contributing to road flooding at the Cambridge/Market Street area during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p38)","two reports of blocked drains (p15)
6 properties (p20)",GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Market Street, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Market Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.30136,-0.6833182,"<div id=""html_5b0b6e556f42f14c198841fb44efbb91"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Midland Road, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Midland Road were identified in the vicinity of known flooding; flood report forms were hand&ndash;delivered and the investigating officer visited affected occupiers. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater caused interior flooding of properties on Midland Road; flooding of the carriageway and footway due to lack of hydraulic capacity in the highway drain; a combined sewer blockage caused internal flooding to a property on Midland Road (recorded 12/07/15). [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff and deepening surface water entered premises from front and back doors and filled rear yard and basement. Basement flooded to a depth of 1600mm; rear entrance/yard 25mm; kitchen 25mm; counter/service area 15mm. Majority of Midland Road was flooded; road was temporarily closed by police and Fire & Rescue. Fire & Rescue pumped flood water from the basement (pump&ndash;out took around 5 hours). Premises were affected for about 3 days before reopening. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Majority of Midland Road flooded by surface water runoff; frontline runoff entered the front door of a commercial/residential property and flooded external areas and streets. Road was temporarily closed by police. Floodwater prevented access to the property fuse box and emergency crews (FandR) pumped flood water from a basement (response activity). Blocked gully on Midland Road was reported prior to the event. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two properties on Midland Road flooded internally. Surface water reached Midland Road from Market Square and the rear of the library; Market Street overtopped into Midland Road. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Basement 1600mm; rear entrance/yard 25mm; kitchen 25mm; counter/service area 15mm; premises affected for 3 days; pump&ndash;out ~5 hours [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Basement flooded to 1600mm; Kitchen flooded to 25mm; Rear entrance/yard flooded to 25mm; Counter/service area flooded to 15mm (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Midland Road, Wellingborough",England,"Properties on Midland Road were identified in the vicinity of known flooding; flood report forms were hand–delivered and the investigating officer visited affected occupiers. (p8)
Groundwater caused interior flooding of properties on Midland Road; flooding of the carriageway and footway due to lack of hydraulic capacity in the highway drain; a combined sewer blockage caused internal flooding to a property on Midland Road (recorded 12/07/15). [TAB,MUL] (p11)
Runoff and deepening surface water entered premises from front and back doors and filled rear yard and basement. Basement flooded to a depth of 1600mm; rear entrance/yard 25mm; kitchen 25mm; counter/service area 15mm. Majority of Midland Road was flooded; road was temporarily closed by police and Fire & Rescue. Fire & Rescue pumped flood water from the basement (pump–out took around 5 hours). Premises were affected for about 3 days before reopening. [TAB] (p17)
Majority of Midland Road flooded by surface water runoff; frontline runoff entered the front door of a commercial/residential property and flooded external areas and streets. Road was temporarily closed by police. Floodwater prevented access to the property fuse box and emergency crews (FandR) pumped flood water from a basement (response activity). Blocked gully on Midland Road was reported prior to the event. (p19)
Two properties on Midland Road flooded internally. Surface water reached Midland Road from Market Square and the rear of the library; Market Street overtopped into Midland Road. (p20)","Basement 1600mm; rear entrance/yard 25mm; kitchen 25mm; counter/service area 15mm; premises affected for 3 days; pump–out ~5 hours [TAB] (p17)
Basement flooded to 1600mm; Kitchen flooded to 25mm; Rear entrance/yard flooded to 25mm; Counter/service area flooded to 15mm (p19)
2 properties (p20)",GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Market Street, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Market Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.301723,-0.6900522,"<div id=""html_e43a9aef618a08be32b5c19895942036"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Glenbank, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Glen Bank (sic) were contacted as being in the vicinity of known flooding; flood report forms were hand&ndash;delivered and affected occupiers were visited as part of the investigation. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies collecting surface water from commercial car parks north of Glen Bank (sic) were inoperable due to heavy silting across the gratings, reducing drainage capacity and contributing to surface water flooding during the 16th August 2020 event. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from a supermarket car park and flow down from Cambridge Street overtopped a boundary wall and flowed to the rear and front of properties. External flooding reached 400mm at the rear; water entered dwellings via air bricks causing internal flooding of 25mm affecting bathroom and kitchen. At the front low point water accumulated to about 500mm. Fire & Rescue attended and assisted with deep water. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff (reported from the supermarket car park at the northeast and from areas to the north) flowed down Glen Bank (sic) and accumulated at a low point in front of properties, entering buildings via doors and airbricks. Blocked gullies at the low point exacerbated accumulation. Fire and Rescue assisted with deep water. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Strong flow of surface water down Glen Bank (sic) caused rapid deepening of water at the low point (described as lake&ndash;like depth) and this rush/deepening caused floodwater to enter rear and side doors of the affected property and flooded external areas and streets. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Glen Bank (sic), being a low point, held and deepened surface water; water flowed to it via gaps between buildings from Cambridge Street and from commercial car parks which overtopped a retaining wall, causing localised inundation and standing water. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The alley is documented as a low point where surface water ponds/accumulates; drainage channel/grating is present at the low point and was noted as relevant to drainage performance (risk of water accumulation at the low spot). [IMG] (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>400mm external depth; 25mm internal depth; 500mm at front low point [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>accumulation reached 500mm at the front of a property; external 400mm / internal 25mm reported for affected property (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 gullies blocked (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Glenbank, Wellingborough",England,"Properties on Glen Bank (sic) were contacted as being in the vicinity of known flooding; flood report forms were hand–delivered and affected occupiers were visited as part of the investigation. (p8)
Gullies collecting surface water from commercial car parks north of Glen Bank (sic) were inoperable due to heavy silting across the gratings, reducing drainage capacity and contributing to surface water flooding during the 16th August 2020 event. (p16)
Surface water runoff from a supermarket car park and flow down from Cambridge Street overtopped a boundary wall and flowed to the rear and front of properties. External flooding reached 400mm at the rear; water entered dwellings via air bricks causing internal flooding of 25mm affecting bathroom and kitchen. At the front low point water accumulated to about 500mm. Fire & Rescue attended and assisted with deep water. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Runoff (reported from the supermarket car park at the northeast and from areas to the north) flowed down Glen Bank (sic) and accumulated at a low point in front of properties, entering buildings via doors and airbricks. Blocked gullies at the low point exacerbated accumulation. Fire and Rescue assisted with deep water. (p18)
Strong flow of surface water down Glen Bank (sic) caused rapid deepening of water at the low point (described as lake–like depth) and this rush/deepening caused floodwater to enter rear and side doors of the affected property and flooded external areas and streets. (p19)
Glen Bank (sic), being a low point, held and deepened surface water; water flowed to it via gaps between buildings from Cambridge Street and from commercial car parks which overtopped a retaining wall, causing localised inundation and standing water. (p20)
The alley is documented as a low point where surface water ponds/accumulates; drainage channel/grating is present at the low point and was noted as relevant to drainage performance (risk of water accumulation at the low spot). [IMG] (p40)","400mm external depth; 25mm internal depth; 500mm at front low point [TAB,PAG] (p17)
accumulation reached 500mm at the front of a property; external 400mm / internal 25mm reported for affected property (p18)
