GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.3054218,-0.6938794,"<div id=""html_f63d33e27a4f7eb1c1841e3feb022bd1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Park Road, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff and flow from Park Road contributed to floodwater reaching Market Street premises; water flowed from Park Road into front doors of shops on Market Street. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Strong flows of surface water from Park Road contributed to flooding that reached Market Street and Cambridge Street when drainage systems were exceeded. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Park Road, Wellingborough",England,"Runoff and flow from Park Road contributed to floodwater reaching Market Street premises; water flowed from Park Road into front doors of shops on Market Street. [TAB] (p17)
Strong flows of surface water from Park Road contributed to flooding that reached Market Street and Cambridge Street when drainage systems were exceeded. (p20)",,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Market Street, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Market Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.3029656,-0.6935226,"<div id=""html_4e461563ffa0b478defab6dd159f4885"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Street, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flows from Church Street contributed to flooding that reached Market Street and Cambridge Street when upstream drainage was exceeded. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Church Street, Wellingborough",England,Surface water flows from Church Street contributed to flooding that reached Market Street and Cambridge Street when upstream drainage was exceeded. (p20),,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Market Street, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Market Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.3019877,-0.6936322,"<div id=""html_033c65eb0dda6f4b52dbfa7cffeb414d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Market Square, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed from Market Square onto Market Street and Midland Road, contributing to deepening water on those streets as drainage was exceeded. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Market Square, Wellingborough",England,"Surface water flowed from Market Square onto Market Street and Midland Road, contributing to deepening water on those streets as drainage was exceeded. (p20)",,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Market Street, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Market Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,18,2020-08-16,52.30189,-0.6937309,"<div id=""html_ee7e62c2a57ae7d3e12662c735fd22e7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced intense heavy rainfall on 16 August 2020. Surface water drainage systems were unable to convey the rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around properties and leading to internal flooding of properties on Market Street. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of the flood incident on Market Street where internal flooding of multiple buildings occurred. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected parts of Wellingborough during the 16th August 2020 event. Private residential and commercial drainage in the area discharged to the surface water public sewer or unrecorded local systems (notably referenced in Market Street and Midland Road); no community&ndash; or property&ndash;level resilience facilities were reported to have been available to reduce surface water flood risk. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface water / urban flooding affected parts of the town (including Market Street and Glen Bank). Blocked storm drains and gullies were reported across the area; flows were reported as starting as far away as Broad Green and travelling into central streets, causing street ponding and property ingress. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>In this area of Wellingborough a total of 22 properties were reported to have flooded internally. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple areas of Wellingborough were affected by flooding: Fire and Rescue received emergency calls from areas of Wellingborough, crews attended properties and external areas where relatively deep flood water was held and pumped water to safe areas; follow&ndash;up clearing, rubbish collection and sandbag deliveries took place in the town centre and affected properties. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration rainfall caused surface water/flash flooding in parts of Wellingborough, inundating streets and properties. Local response teams (Wellingborough Norse and council staff) collected flood&ndash;related rubbish, sweepers cleared surfaces in the town centre, and sandbags were delivered to some properties. Drainage systems were cleared and flood debris removed by NH and AWS. The LLFA and Emergency Planning Team attended, liaised with risk management authorities and residents, recorded and mapped reports of flooding, and provided advice. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense heavy rainfall on 16th August 2020 overwhelmed surface water drainage across the catchment. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around properties; localised drainage systems were observed to be damaged or inoperable and some required maintenance/cleansing, reducing their ability to convey water. There are limited overland flow routes, so reliance on buried drainage contributed to flooding of properties and local infrastructure. No community&ndash; or property&ndash;level resilience measures were in place that could have been deployed. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five buildings (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five buildings (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 properties (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>82 separate formal and anecdotal reports of flooding across the county [PAG] (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Wellingborough,England,"The town experienced intense heavy rainfall on 16 August 2020. Surface water drainage systems were unable to convey the rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around properties and leading to internal flooding of properties on Market Street. (p6)
Location of the flood incident on Market Street where internal flooding of multiple buildings occurred. (p8)
Surface water flooding affected parts of Wellingborough during the 16th August 2020 event. Private residential and commercial drainage in the area discharged to the surface water public sewer or unrecorded local systems (notably referenced in Market Street and Midland Road); no community– or property–level resilience facilities were reported to have been available to reduce surface water flood risk. (p16)
Widespread surface water / urban flooding affected parts of the town (including Market Street and Glen Bank). Blocked storm drains and gullies were reported across the area; flows were reported as starting as far away as Broad Green and travelling into central streets, causing street ponding and property ingress. (p18)
In this area of Wellingborough a total of 22 properties were reported to have flooded internally. (p20)
Multiple areas of Wellingborough were affected by flooding: Fire and Rescue received emergency calls from areas of Wellingborough, crews attended properties and external areas where relatively deep flood water was held and pumped water to safe areas; follow–up clearing, rubbish collection and sandbag deliveries took place in the town centre and affected properties. (p21)
Intense short–duration rainfall caused surface water/flash flooding in parts of Wellingborough, inundating streets and properties. Local response teams (Wellingborough Norse and council staff) collected flood–related rubbish, sweepers cleared surfaces in the town centre, and sandbags were delivered to some properties. Drainage systems were cleared and flood debris removed by NH and AWS. The LLFA and Emergency Planning Team attended, liaised with risk management authorities and residents, recorded and mapped reports of flooding, and provided advice. [PAG] (p22)
Intense heavy rainfall on 16th August 2020 overwhelmed surface water drainage across the catchment. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around properties; localised drainage systems were observed to be damaged or inoperable and some required maintenance/cleansing, reducing their ability to convey water. There are limited overland flow routes, so reliance on buried drainage contributed to flooding of properties and local infrastructure. No community– or property–level resilience measures were in place that could have been deployed. [PAG] (p23)","five buildings (p6)
five buildings (p8)
22 properties (p20)
82 separate formal and anecdotal reports of flooding across the county [PAG] (p22)",GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_18_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Market Street, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Market Street, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.61730684214529,-2.157570431776049,"<div id=""html_4ebe4ec1e74855324e80982f9fc7f6be"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pool Hall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Pool Hall's ground&ndash;floor shopfront is inundated, with floodwater reaching its doors and windows and obstructing access. [IMG] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Pool Hall,England,"The Pool Hall's ground–floor shopfront is inundated, with floodwater reaching its doors and windows and obstructing access. [IMG] (p1)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.617365312136286,-2.157555679626517,"<div id=""html_7f0b43c2256f6fdd51bf30119fe249d4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grandma Mill's Traditional Diner</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Grandma Mill's Traditional Diner (ground&ndash;floor) is inundated at the shopfront level, with floodwater reaching up to doors and windows and interfering with access. [IMG] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Grandma Mill's Traditional Diner,England,"Grandma Mill's Traditional Diner (ground–floor) is inundated at the shopfront level, with floodwater reaching up to doors and windows and interfering with access. [IMG] (p1)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5604829,-2.3151923,"<div id=""html_ba98cf10f941969f8261a9172f1e7a14"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pioneer Mill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell at Pioneer Mills experienced exceptionally high peak levels during the Boxing Day event (26 December 2015), contributing to river flooding recorded at this station. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties at Pioneer Mill experienced internal flooding from main river flooding during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pioneer Mill is listed among areas affected by the 26 December 2015 flooding; river level at Pioneer Mills reached 5.33m (noted as 0.9m higher than any other reading in five years). [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell levels at Pioneer Mills rose to very high levels; the river gauge at Pioneer Mills was washed away at midday on 26th December 2015 and the site experienced significant flooding. [PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels were recorded at Pioneer Mill (downstream of Radcliffe and Redvales) during the event; flood&ndash;related sediment build&ndash;up was recorded and repairs to gauging&ndash;station equipment at Pioneer Mills were scheduled as part of the recovery programme. (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell overtopped, flooding the mill from three sides. Maximum flood depth above ground level 1.76m. Nineteen individual industrial units were flooded; businesses lost power for several weeks. Milltown Street footbridge (direct access) was damaged beyond repair. Post&ndash;event issues included security (break&ndash;ins), environmental health problems (waste and fly&ndash;tipping), and concerns about building structure. [IMG,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pioneer Mill was flooded when the River Irwell overtopped and inundated the mill on three sides. Flooding caused structural damage, widespread loss and contamination of stored goods, loss of power to businesses for weeks, damage to access (Milltown Street footbridge was destroyed), post&ndash;flood security problems (break&ndash;ins) and environmental health issues (waste and fly&ndash;tipping). [IMG,PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell overtopped at a meander and flooded the Mill from three sides. Maximum flood depth above ground was 1.76 m. Nineteen individual industrial units within the mill were flooded. Businesses lost power for several weeks. Flooding caused structural damage, created waste and fly&ndash;tipping that presented environmental health risks, and led Building Control to require demolition of some outlying, immediately dangerous sections. Security issues (break&ndash;ins) increased after the event. [PAG,MUL] (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell levels at Pioneer Mill on 26 December 2015 were recorded as extremely high (comparable to levels at Bury), contributing to local river flooding. (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>19 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5.33m river level (62.97m AOD) [PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>19 units flooded; 1.76m maximum flood depth above ground level [IMG,PAG] (p64)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>19 units flooded; 1.76 m maximum flood depth above ground; flooded from 3 sides [IMG,PAG] (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>19 units flooded; maximum flood depth 1.76 m [PAG,MUL] (p66)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Pioneer Mill,England,"The River Irwell at Pioneer Mills experienced exceptionally high peak levels during the Boxing Day event (26 December 2015), contributing to river flooding recorded at this station. (p16)
Properties at Pioneer Mill experienced internal flooding from main river flooding during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Pioneer Mill is listed among areas affected by the 26 December 2015 flooding; river level at Pioneer Mills reached 5.33m (noted as 0.9m higher than any other reading in five years). [PAG] (p56)
River Irwell levels at Pioneer Mills rose to very high levels; the river gauge at Pioneer Mills was washed away at midday on 26th December 2015 and the site experienced significant flooding. [PAG] (p57)
High river levels were recorded at Pioneer Mill (downstream of Radcliffe and Redvales) during the event; flood–related sediment build–up was recorded and repairs to gauging–station equipment at Pioneer Mills were scheduled as part of the recovery programme. (p63)
River Irwell overtopped, flooding the mill from three sides. Maximum flood depth above ground level 1.76m. Nineteen individual industrial units were flooded; businesses lost power for several weeks. Milltown Street footbridge (direct access) was damaged beyond repair. Post–event issues included security (break–ins), environmental health problems (waste and fly–tipping), and concerns about building structure. [IMG,PAG] (p64)
Pioneer Mill was flooded when the River Irwell overtopped and inundated the mill on three sides. Flooding caused structural damage, widespread loss and contamination of stored goods, loss of power to businesses for weeks, damage to access (Milltown Street footbridge was destroyed), post–flood security problems (break–ins) and environmental health issues (waste and fly–tipping). [IMG,PAG] (p65)
The River Irwell overtopped at a meander and flooded the Mill from three sides. Maximum flood depth above ground was 1.76 m. Nineteen individual industrial units within the mill were flooded. Businesses lost power for several weeks. Flooding caused structural damage, created waste and fly–tipping that presented environmental health risks, and led Building Control to require demolition of some outlying, immediately dangerous sections. Security issues (break–ins) increased after the event. [PAG,MUL] (p66)
River Irwell levels at Pioneer Mill on 26 December 2015 were recorded as extremely high (comparable to levels at Bury), contributing to local river flooding. (p70)","19 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)
