GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.617281923770925,-2.1536138427627107,"<div id=""html_c777f8efc84cb30cf030780ed78e9246"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rochdale Borough Council Office</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of Rochdale Council's town centre office affected computer servers, which hindered communications and coordination of flood recovery. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The council's new office (Number One Riverside) experienced internal flooding during the event; the building was later largely operational but the ground&ndash;floor library remained closed after the flood. (p121)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Number One Riverside (the Council Offices) flooded &mdash; the ground floor library was affected and computer servers in the cellar were flooded; repair work was required and the building was substantially operational from 4 January 2016 while the library remained closed at the time of the report. (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The recently built Council Offices at Number One Riverside in the town centre flooded: the ground&ndash;floor library was affected and computer servers in the cellar were flooded, complicating recovery. Repairs were underway and the building was substantially operational from 4 January 2016, though the ground&ndash;floor library remained closed. [IMG] (p124)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one council office building (Number One Riverside) [IMG] (p124)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Rochdale Borough Council Office,England,"Flooding of Rochdale Council's town centre office affected computer servers, which hindered communications and coordination of flood recovery. (p22)
The council's new office (Number One Riverside) experienced internal flooding during the event; the building was later largely operational but the ground–floor library remained closed after the flood. (p121)
Number One Riverside (the Council Offices) flooded — the ground floor library was affected and computer servers in the cellar were flooded; repair work was required and the building was substantially operational from 4 January 2016 while the library remained closed at the time of the report. (p123)
The recently built Council Offices at Number One Riverside in the town centre flooded: the ground–floor library was affected and computer servers in the cellar were flooded, complicating recovery. Repairs were underway and the building was substantially operational from 4 January 2016, though the ground–floor library remained closed. [IMG] (p124)",one council office building (Number One Riverside) [IMG] (p124),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.567008951691534,-2.299306016788804,"<div id=""html_b3334b569f6d9944e359052d7c2c8838"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bury Wastewater Treatment Works</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bury wastewater treatment works was flooded; a number of treatment processes were affected, reducing treatment effectiveness. A recovery plan was agreed with the Environment Agency to return the works to normal operation. The reduced treatment led to discharges that were more dilute than usual due to high volumes of storm water in the system. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The treatment works was damaged by the high river levels/flooding and, after the flood, discharged effluent to the river that did not meet full treatment standards for several weeks. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The treatment works flooded on Boxing Day and discharged treated sewage into the River Irwell. The discharge water did not meet full treatment standards for several weeks due to damage sustained on 26th December 2015. (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Bury Wastewater Treatment Works,England,"The Bury wastewater treatment works was flooded; a number of treatment processes were affected, reducing treatment effectiveness. A recovery plan was agreed with the Environment Agency to return the works to normal operation. The reduced treatment led to discharges that were more dilute than usual due to high volumes of storm water in the system. [PAG] (p24)
The treatment works was damaged by the high river levels/flooding and, after the flood, discharged effluent to the river that did not meet full treatment standards for several weeks. (p29)
The treatment works flooded on Boxing Day and discharged treated sewage into the River Irwell. The discharge water did not meet full treatment standards for several weeks due to damage sustained on 26th December 2015. (p58)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6447466,-2.3134127,"<div id=""html_acdea0f45d6be626588a3894a965370f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ramsbottom United Football</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The football (and cricket) grounds were flooded from the main river, resulting in inundation of playing fields and damage to adjacent river channel walls (investigations and repair works are being considered). (p73)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Ramsbottom United Football,England,"The football (and cricket) grounds were flooded from the main river, resulting in inundation of playing fields and damage to adjacent river channel walls (investigations and repair works are being considered). (p73)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.580930622823686,-2.041953564525023,"<div id=""html_b02c003c28b850baaf3b7e6f33c3d36a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Green Ash</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Roadway at Green Ash (Denshaw Road) was inundated, with water covering the road surface and partially obscuring lane markings, impeding traffic and access. [IMG,PAG] (p94)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater entered the residential property through the door, leaving the wooden floor wet and pooling inside; furnishings (black leather sofa and doormat) were exposed to water and at risk of damage. [IMG,PAG] (p95)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Green Ash,England,"Roadway at Green Ash (Denshaw Road) was inundated, with water covering the road surface and partially obscuring lane markings, impeding traffic and access. [IMG,PAG] (p94)
