GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.590114650000004,-2.536847989947367,"<div id=""html_a4e0c701223860c6f86b62b73780c2a0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chorley New Road Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the school flooded on 26 December 2015 when a manhole in the school car park blew its cover; the school is built over an under&ndash;sized, culverted main river (Nellies Clough) which contributed to the flooding. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The school flooded when a manhole in the school car park blew its cover; repair and refurbishment works were completed and rebuilding is being planned to address flood risk. (p53)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Chorley New Road Primary School,England,"Part of the school flooded on 26 December 2015 when a manhole in the school car park blew its cover; the school is built over an under–sized, culverted main river (Nellies Clough) which contributed to the flooding. (p52)
The school flooded when a manhole in the school car park blew its cover; repair and refurbishment works were completed and rebuilding is being planned to address flood risk. (p53)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.564445,-2.3043213,"<div id=""html_bd7476979a03949e2a51010718999b87"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Close Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by the Boxing Day 2015 flooding: surveyed and made safe by Council teams, debris cleared by volunteers and Council, included in a programme of repairs; recovery expected to take a long time. (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Close Park,England,"Affected by the Boxing Day 2015 flooding: surveyed and made safe by Council teams, debris cleared by volunteers and Council, included in a programme of repairs; recovery expected to take a long time. (p58)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6426383,-2.3119648,"<div id=""html_651404c693ee5066b084b7c5a8d22fb4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nuttall Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by the Boxing Day 2015 flooding: surveyed and made safe by Council teams and included in the programme of repairs; clearance and restoration work undertaken with volunteer support. (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River channel walls at Nuttall Park were damaged by the flood; the park was surveyed, closed and made safe as part of the immediate response. (p74)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Nuttall Park,England,"Affected by the Boxing Day 2015 flooding: surveyed and made safe by Council teams and included in the programme of repairs; clearance and restoration work undertaken with volunteer support. (p58)
River channel walls at Nuttall Park were damaged by the flood; the park was surveyed, closed and made safe as part of the immediate response. (p74)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5986927,-2.3019101,"<div id=""html_a207f96a720ff7d0263e185a4b51db3f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chamberhall Business Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Six industrial properties at Chamberhall were first flooded by surface water drains and then additionally by the River Irwell overtopping a boundary wall; floodwater seeped under the boundary wall and emerged behind it. Flooding inundated units to significant depths and debris was left across the area. [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Irwell on 26 December 2015: water seeped under a raised boundary wall and emerged from the ground behind it; properties at Chamberhall were identified as being most at risk and to be considered for flood resistance and resilience measures (including possible non&ndash;return valves where surface water drains outfall to the river). [PAG] (p70)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 industrial properties; flood depths 0.6 to 1.0 m [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Chamberhall Business Park,England,"Six industrial properties at Chamberhall were first flooded by surface water drains and then additionally by the River Irwell overtopping a boundary wall; floodwater seeped under the boundary wall and emerged behind it. Flooding inundated units to significant depths and debris was left across the area. [IMG,PAG] (p69)
Flooding from the River Irwell on 26 December 2015: water seeped under a raised boundary wall and emerged from the ground behind it; properties at Chamberhall were identified as being most at risk and to be considered for flood resistance and resilience measures (including possible non–return valves where surface water drains outfall to the river). [PAG] (p70)","6 industrial properties; flood depths 0.6 to 1.0 m [IMG,PAG] (p69)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6482539,-2.089229,"<div id=""html_be2174a36f03fafb34d182f78ae21042"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Greenvale Business Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Greenvale Business Park was flooded when the flood relief channel overtopped (the channel flows under the railway) and from overtopping of the River Roch, causing inundation of the business park. (p117)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Greenvale Business Park,England,"Greenvale Business Park was flooded when the flood relief channel overtopped (the channel flows under the railway) and from overtopping of the River Roch, causing inundation of the business park. (p117)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.504297904595575,-2.5048205948843973,"<div id=""html_a60f178fffd0e4cb678acd85b165e722"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lilford Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Lilford Park flood storage basin (Atherton Lake Brook) recorded a record high level during the event &mdash; basin/stream level reached 4.51 m, 1.3 m higher than any previous reading in its record &mdash; indicating the basin was heavily affected by the flood event. (p147)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels were recorded in the Lilford Park basin and brook during the event, contributing to local flooding in the Lilford Park area (including the car park and nearby properties). [IMG] (p155)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4.51 m; 1.3 m higher than previous record (p147)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>highest in 10 years; 1.3 m higher than previous highest [IMG] (p155)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Lilford Park,England,"The Lilford Park flood storage basin (Atherton Lake Brook) recorded a record high level during the event — basin/stream level reached 4.51 m, 1.3 m higher than any previous reading in its record — indicating the basin was heavily affected by the flood event. (p147)
High water levels were recorded in the Lilford Park basin and brook during the event, contributing to local flooding in the Lilford Park area (including the car park and nearby properties). [IMG] (p155)","4.51 m; 1.3 m higher than previous record (p147)
highest in 10 years; 1.3 m higher than previous highest [IMG] (p155)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.61727820375282,-2.157617370433649,"<div id=""html_a3efb4000944a4f9edf68aaf8b04cfc7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ladbrokes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Ladbrokes ground&ndash;floor shopfront is inundated with floodwater reaching up to its doors and windows, blocking access to the premises. [IMG] (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Ladbrokes,England,"The Ladbrokes ground–floor shopfront is inundated with floodwater reaching up to its doors and windows, blocking access to the premises. [IMG] (p1)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.615326,-2.128409,"<div id=""html_5628ccec9fb7f6a8a0cafdc59619f631"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Asda</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Asda superstore suffered internal flood damage when floodwater inundated the building. (p121)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flooded up to 50 mm deep (p121)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Asda,England,The Asda superstore suffered internal flood damage when floodwater inundated the building. (p121),flooded up to 50 mm deep (p121),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5742266,-2.0174296,"<div id=""html_781fb9ea98f3149bca45bdbd58e69d0f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hull Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hull Brook came out of its banks and caused flooding to adjacent gardens. (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in Hull Brook entering the River Tame at the confluence contributed to turbulent floodwaters and debris that inundated the bridge area and adjacent properties. [IMG] (p94)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Hull Brook,England,"Hull Brook came out of its banks and caused flooding to adjacent gardens. (p92)
High flows in Hull Brook entering the River Tame at the confluence contributed to turbulent floodwaters and debris that inundated the bridge area and adjacent properties. [IMG] (p94)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6260521,-2.1460457,"<div id=""html_0d04d82d6bd61be07ced5ac8e95c3683"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hey Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hey Brook (and Buckley Brook) contributed to the main river flooding: culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream of Foxholes Road/Red Lane, allowing floodwater onto Buckley Road and Park Lane. High brook levels led to cellar flooding from surface water/groundwater even where the brook did not overtop. [PAG] (p125)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream of Foxholes Road/Red Lane, allowing floodwater onto Buckley Road and Park Lane. Cellars close to Hey Brook flooded from surface water/groundwater when the brook reached very high levels although it did not overtop. [PAG] (p126)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hey Brook (main river and ordinary watercourse) caused flooding that resulted in 15 properties being internally flooded in the Bickershaw and Abram area. (p146)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hey Brook (main river) contributed to flooding: high river levels may have exacerbated surface water flooding, and possible fluvial flooding occurred where the brook exited its right bank at the rear of Browns Garage, causing inundation of nearby properties. (p152)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hey Brook (main river) overtopped/exited its right bank (noted at the rear of Browns Garage) causing fluvial flooding that contributed to property flooding and high water levels; high levels in the brook also prevented discharge of a non&ndash;main drain (Brookside Brook) causing surcharge and backup into nearby properties (Keats Way, Simpkin Street). [PAG] (p153)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Contributed to 35 properties flooded by main river (part of overall 36) [PAG] (p125)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 properties (p146)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Hey Brook,England,"Hey Brook (and Buckley Brook) contributed to the main river flooding: culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream of Foxholes Road/Red Lane, allowing floodwater onto Buckley Road and Park Lane. High brook levels led to cellar flooding from surface water/groundwater even where the brook did not overtop. [PAG] (p125)
Culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream of Foxholes Road/Red Lane, allowing floodwater onto Buckley Road and Park Lane. Cellars close to Hey Brook flooded from surface water/groundwater when the brook reached very high levels although it did not overtop. [PAG] (p126)
Hey Brook (main river and ordinary watercourse) caused flooding that resulted in 15 properties being internally flooded in the Bickershaw and Abram area. (p146)
Hey Brook (main river) contributed to flooding: high river levels may have exacerbated surface water flooding, and possible fluvial flooding occurred where the brook exited its right bank at the rear of Browns Garage, causing inundation of nearby properties. (p152)
Hey Brook (main river) overtopped/exited its right bank (noted at the rear of Browns Garage) causing fluvial flooding that contributed to property flooding and high water levels; high levels in the brook also prevented discharge of a non–main drain (Brookside Brook) causing surcharge and backup into nearby properties (Keats Way, Simpkin Street). [PAG] (p153)","Contributed to 35 properties flooded by main river (part of overall 36) [PAG] (p125)
15 properties (p146)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5278476,-2.5833219,"<div id=""html_b19f60ac2a785bca85465a02f91ec02d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Borsdane Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels spilled over the left bank of Borsdane Brook; floodwater flowed south&ndash;westerly from the channel into nearby streets (Platt Lane, Prescott Street, Aughton Street and Meadows Close) and then returned to Borsdane Brook. Properties were flooded as a result. (p161)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels spilled over the left bank of Borsdane Brook and flowed south&ndash;westerly along Platt Lane into Prescott Street, through Aughton Street and Meadows Close before returning into Borsdane Brook; other affected locations included Atherton Road and Lord Street. [PAG] (p162)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (p161)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [PAG] (p162)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Borsdane Brook,England,"High river levels spilled over the left bank of Borsdane Brook; floodwater flowed south–westerly from the channel into nearby streets (Platt Lane, Prescott Street, Aughton Street and Meadows Close) and then returned to Borsdane Brook. Properties were flooded as a result. (p161)
High river levels spilled over the left bank of Borsdane Brook and flowed south–westerly along Platt Lane into Prescott Street, through Aughton Street and Meadows Close before returning into Borsdane Brook; other affected locations included Atherton Road and Lord Street. [PAG] (p162)","5 properties (p161)
5 properties [PAG] (p162)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.4980205,-2.4979898,"<div id=""html_e8ef374b87439fa58430f735707f1d01"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lilford Park Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lilford Park Brook was the subject of an Environment Agency flood warning; the associated Lilford Park Basin gauge recorded record high water levels during the event. [PAG] (p156)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Lilford Park Brook,England,Lilford Park Brook was the subject of an Environment Agency flood warning; the associated Lilford Park Basin gauge recorded record high water levels during the event. [PAG] (p156),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5096091,-2.4780353,"<div id=""html_f73fe006e888dee7fee99591a3a47fe5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pen Leach Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood levels on Penleach Brook rose during the event and were managed by Bedford Pumping Station working with the Lilford Park flood storage basin to keep flood and flood&ndash;warning levels from being exceeded. (p155)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Pen Leach Brook,England,Flood levels on Penleach Brook rose during the event and were managed by Bedford Pumping Station working with the Lilford Park flood storage basin to keep flood and flood–warning levels from being exceeded. (p155),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.59580030621883,-2.3054224893129716,"<div id=""html_ed90cbac939bb4db4cbd0ec1911c19e3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bury Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main&ndash;river flooding (River Irwell) produced clustered property flooding near Bury Ground on Boxing Day 2015, as shown on the map and described in the report. [IMG] (p68)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties; 6 main river; 1 surface water (Woodhill Fold and Bury Ground) [IMG] (p68)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Bury Ground,England,"Main–river flooding (River Irwell) produced clustered property flooding near Bury Ground on Boxing Day 2015, as shown on the map and described in the report. [IMG] (p68)",7 properties; 6 main river; 1 surface water (Woodhill Fold and Bury Ground) [IMG] (p68),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.54494100778775,-2.3597638110248855,"<div id=""html_921c0bd8ec6ee39f67a5fbc97130b903"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ringley Bridges</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood flows displaced sand and gravel around Ringley Old Bridge and the nearby island, producing scour and sediment build&ndash;up that required post&ndash;flood actions (movement of sand, use of gravel to fill scour, and planned gravel removal as part of recovery). The arches and flow through the bridge openings were affected by sediment movement. [PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River channel and bridge openings experienced sediment deposition and scour requiring recovery works: sand on the river bank upstream and downstream of Ringley Old Bridge was moved to smooth flow through the arched bridge openings; gravel from the island downstream of Ringley Old Bridge was used to fill in scour on the right bank. (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Ringley Bridges,England,"Flood flows displaced sand and gravel around Ringley Old Bridge and the nearby island, producing scour and sediment build–up that required post–flood actions (movement of sand, use of gravel to fill scour, and planned gravel removal as part of recovery). The arches and flow through the bridge openings were affected by sediment movement. [PAG] (p44)
River channel and bridge openings experienced sediment deposition and scour requiring recovery works: sand on the river bank upstream and downstream of Ringley Old Bridge was moved to smooth flow through the arched bridge openings; gravel from the island downstream of Ringley Old Bridge was used to fill in scour on the right bank. (p45)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.615364,-2.155756,"<div id=""html_759d80013e346fed141fb301caeb0d94"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rochdale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Town&ndash;centre street and the ground floors of multiple businesses were inundated; water reached up to doors and windows of shopfronts and partially submerged street furniture (traffic lights and pedestrian posts), disrupting access to the commercial area. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe river flooding with water depths so great in parts of Rochdale that some residents were evacuated by boat; flooding described as the worst in living memory in some areas; rapid onset and subsequently began to recede by Boxing Day evening. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rochdale experienced widespread internal property flooding, predominantly from main river flooding with additional surface&ndash;water impacts across the town and vicinity. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected Rochdale (specifically Littleborough and Rochdale), inundating properties; a scheme has been proposed to assess options for reducing flood risk in the area. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive flooding in Rochdale led to major electricity supply impacts and direct asset damage: two sub&ndash;stations in Rochdale were damaged by floodwater. A control room at a Rochdale substation was inundated to about 1.5m, causing extensive equipment damage (repair estimate ~£7M). A hydroelectric plant adjacent to Rochdale bus station was damaged (~£80,000 repair). [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding closed the transport interchange (tram and bus terminus) in Rochdale town centre on Boxing Day (reopened the next day), disrupted tram and bus services, caused loss of power affecting many traffic signals, and flooded Rochdale Council's town centre office (affecting computer servers and communications). Rochdale wastewater works suffered flooding (damage described as minimal) and local pumping infrastructure was affected. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected local wastewater treatment (Rochdale WWTW suffered flooding, damage reported as minimal) and flooded Rochdale Council's town centre office, damaging computer servers and disrupting communications and coordination for recovery; a functional operations base was established at Rochdale Town Hall. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from main rivers (River Roch and Hey Brook) on 26 December 2015 led to direct flood warnings being issued to properties; a number of properties in Rochdale benefited from defences/operational protection. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the River Roch caused damage to flood walls and embankments in the Rochdale/Littleborough area. Minor repairs on walls/embankments are programmed. A flood wall completely failed at a residential care home next to the River Roch at Littleborough. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Roch flood defences (walls/embankments) were damaged and sandbagged as an immediate short&ndash;term measure. Wall rebuilding was included in an emergency repair programme; work started on site 14 March 2016 with completion due mid&ndash;June 2016. Minor repairs on other walls/embankments on the River Roch at Rochdale were also programmed. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rochdale Town Centre and its vicinity experienced flooding during the Boxing Day event, resulting in property flooding and local evacuations. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread internal flooding of properties across the borough from multiple sources (rivers, surface water, groundwater and sewers); extent of damage to habitable spaces and businesses being assessed; many cellar floods noted. [IMG,PAG] (p113)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the borough on 26 December 2015 from multiple sources (primarily rivers but also surface water, groundwater and sewers) causing internal flooding, infrastructure impacts and service disruption. [IMG,PAG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rochdale borough experienced significant impacts from the 26th December 2015 flooding: large&ndash;scale power loss and communications disruption beyond immediate flooded areas; flooding at Rochdale Wastewater Treatment Works causing some impacts to treatment; tram services were unable to reach the town centre and the new bus station sustained flood damage and power loss; a Strategic Command and Control Centre was established at Rochdale Town Hall and rest centres were opened with volunteer support; council&ndash;led recovery included payments, grants and ongoing support, plus infrastructure inspections, maintenance (de&ndash;silting, gully cleaning) and river works including de&ndash;culverting. [PAG] (p115)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in the River Roch exceeded the capacity of a culvert entrance next to the new bus station causing river water to flood the town centre. Floodwater flowed along Smith Street and South Parade and re&ndash;entered the river downstream. Most businesses on Smith Street, South Parade, The Butts and The Walk were flooded; the new Council office at Number One Riverside was flooded (ground&ndash;floor library remained closed after reopening); the town centre bus station and tram terminus sustained flood damage; power loss affected many homes and businesses; substantial cellar flooding affected business operations on South Parade and Yorkshire Street; two cellars and a yard at a piling contractor's depot flooded from the River Spodden near its confluence with the River Roch; downstream the Asda superstore, adjacent Curry's PC World and Sixth Form College suffered internal flood damage and St Mary's Gate subway was damaged. [IMG,PAG] (p120)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in Rochdale on 26 December 2015 inundated the recently built Council Offices at Number One Riverside; the ground floor library was flooded and computer servers in the cellar were affected. The flood extent was broadly similar to, or slightly beyond, Flood Zone 3. (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties adjacent to the River Roch flooded internally (water entered properties). Nearby electrical substations were flooded causing loss of power supply to properties. Allotments on Kellet Street were flooded. Some cellars flooded from groundwater seepage or surface water. (p126)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rochdale Metropolitan Borough Council led investigations into surface&ndash;water flooding in its area; raised river levels and surface&ndash;water runoff caused flooding that inundated properties and allowed floodwater to enter domestic and commercial premises within the borough. [IMG,PAG] (p131)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>288 properties (main river); 34 properties (surface water); 324 properties (total) [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>275 properties flooded [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18,550 properties without power in Rochdale; 2 sub&ndash;stations damaged; control room flooded to a depth of 1.5m [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>541 properties (River Roch in Rochdale) received flood warnings; 25 properties (Hey Brook) received flood warnings; 697 properties (River Roch at Littleborough) received flood warnings; 367 properties protected (by defences) [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one wall [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 properties (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>324 properties [IMG,PAG] (p113)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>324 properties flooded internally (excludes cellars unless habitable); Littleborough 174 properties (up to 1.2 m deep); Wardleworth and Hey Brook 36 properties; Rochdale town centre 54 properties (up to 1.5 m deep); Hooley Bridge 20 properties; power loss to 20,000 properties initially (reduced to 5,500 later on Boxing Day); some properties without power for up to 4 days [IMG,PAG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20,000 properties without power initially; 5,500 properties without power later on Boxing Day; some properties without power for up to 4 days; ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀; ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀; £500 Community Recovery payments available; Business Recovery Grant and Property Level Resilience Grant up to £5,000 per property [PAG] (p115)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 properties flooded; up to 1.5 metres flood depth measured at The Butts; two cellars; Asda flooded up to 50mm [IMG,PAG] (p120)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>541 properties (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>25 properties; up to 400mm (internal flood depth) (p126)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties [IMG,PAG] (p131)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Rochdale,England,"Town–centre street and the ground floors of multiple businesses were inundated; water reached up to doors and windows of shopfronts and partially submerged street furniture (traffic lights and pedestrian posts), disrupting access to the commercial area. [IMG] (p1)
Severe river flooding with water depths so great in parts of Rochdale that some residents were evacuated by boat; flooding described as the worst in living memory in some areas; rapid onset and subsequently began to recede by Boxing Day evening. (p13)
Rochdale experienced widespread internal property flooding, predominantly from main river flooding with additional surface–water impacts across the town and vicinity. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Flooding affected Rochdale (specifically Littleborough and Rochdale), inundating properties; a scheme has been proposed to assess options for reducing flood risk in the area. [IMG] (p20)
Extensive flooding in Rochdale led to major electricity supply impacts and direct asset damage: two sub–stations in Rochdale were damaged by floodwater. A control room at a Rochdale substation was inundated to about 1.5m, causing extensive equipment damage (repair estimate ~£7M). A hydroelectric plant adjacent to Rochdale bus station was damaged (~£80,000 repair). [TAB] (p21)
Flooding closed the transport interchange (tram and bus terminus) in Rochdale town centre on Boxing Day (reopened the next day), disrupted tram and bus services, caused loss of power affecting many traffic signals, and flooded Rochdale Council's town centre office (affecting computer servers and communications). Rochdale wastewater works suffered flooding (damage described as minimal) and local pumping infrastructure was affected. (p22)
Flooding affected local wastewater treatment (Rochdale WWTW suffered flooding, damage reported as minimal) and flooded Rochdale Council's town centre office, damaging computer servers and disrupting communications and coordination for recovery; a functional operations base was established at Rochdale Town Hall. [PAG] (p23)
Flooding from main rivers (River Roch and Hey Brook) on 26 December 2015 led to direct flood warnings being issued to properties; a number of properties in Rochdale benefited from defences/operational protection. [TAB] (p26)
Flooding of the River Roch caused damage to flood walls and embankments in the Rochdale/Littleborough area. Minor repairs on walls/embankments are programmed. A flood wall completely failed at a residential care home next to the River Roch at Littleborough. [PAG] (p27)
River Roch flood defences (walls/embankments) were damaged and sandbagged as an immediate short–term measure. Wall rebuilding was included in an emergency repair programme; work started on site 14 March 2016 with completion due mid–June 2016. Minor repairs on other walls/embankments on the River Roch at Rochdale were also programmed. (p28)
Rochdale Town Centre and its vicinity experienced flooding during the Boxing Day event, resulting in property flooding and local evacuations. (p29)
Widespread internal flooding of properties across the borough from multiple sources (rivers, surface water, groundwater and sewers); extent of damage to habitable spaces and businesses being assessed; many cellar floods noted. [IMG,PAG] (p113)
Widespread flooding across the borough on 26 December 2015 from multiple sources (primarily rivers but also surface water, groundwater and sewers) causing internal flooding, infrastructure impacts and service disruption. [IMG,PAG] (p114)
Rochdale borough experienced significant impacts from the 26th December 2015 flooding: large–scale power loss and communications disruption beyond immediate flooded areas; flooding at Rochdale Wastewater Treatment Works causing some impacts to treatment; tram services were unable to reach the town centre and the new bus station sustained flood damage and power loss; a Strategic Command and Control Centre was established at Rochdale Town Hall and rest centres were opened with volunteer support; council–led recovery included payments, grants and ongoing support, plus infrastructure inspections, maintenance (de–silting, gully cleaning) and river works including de–culverting. [PAG] (p115)
Flows in the River Roch exceeded the capacity of a culvert entrance next to the new bus station causing river water to flood the town centre. Floodwater flowed along Smith Street and South Parade and re–entered the river downstream. Most businesses on Smith Street, South Parade, The Butts and The Walk were flooded; the new Council office at Number One Riverside was flooded (ground–floor library remained closed after reopening); the town centre bus station and tram terminus sustained flood damage; power loss affected many homes and businesses; substantial cellar flooding affected business operations on South Parade and Yorkshire Street; two cellars and a yard at a piling contractor's depot flooded from the River Spodden near its confluence with the River Roch; downstream the Asda superstore, adjacent Curry's PC World and Sixth Form College suffered internal flood damage and St Mary's Gate subway was damaged. [IMG,PAG] (p120)
Flooding in Rochdale on 26 December 2015 inundated the recently built Council Offices at Number One Riverside; the ground floor library was flooded and computer servers in the cellar were affected. The flood extent was broadly similar to, or slightly beyond, Flood Zone 3. (p123)
Properties adjacent to the River Roch flooded internally (water entered properties). Nearby electrical substations were flooded causing loss of power supply to properties. Allotments on Kellet Street were flooded. Some cellars flooded from groundwater seepage or surface water. (p126)
Rochdale Metropolitan Borough Council led investigations into surface–water flooding in its area; raised river levels and surface–water runoff caused flooding that inundated properties and allowed floodwater to enter domestic and commercial premises within the borough. [IMG,PAG] (p131)","288 properties (main river); 34 properties (surface water); 324 properties (total) [TAB,PAG] (p17)
275 properties flooded [IMG] (p20)
18,550 properties without power in Rochdale; 2 sub–stations damaged; control room flooded to a depth of 1.5m [TAB] (p21)
541 properties (River Roch in Rochdale) received flood warnings; 25 properties (Hey Brook) received flood warnings; 697 properties (River Roch at Littleborough) received flood warnings; 367 properties protected (by defences) [TAB] (p26)
one wall [PAG] (p27)
54 properties (p29)
324 properties [IMG,PAG] (p113)
324 properties flooded internally (excludes cellars unless habitable); Littleborough 174 properties (up to 1.2 m deep); Wardleworth and Hey Brook 36 properties; Rochdale town centre 54 properties (up to 1.5 m deep); Hooley Bridge 20 properties; power loss to 20,000 properties initially (reduced to 5,500 later on Boxing Day); some properties without power for up to 4 days [IMG,PAG] (p114)
20,000 properties without power initially; 5,500 properties without power later on Boxing Day; some properties without power for up to 4 days; ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀; ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀; £500 Community Recovery payments available; Business Recovery Grant and Property Level Resilience Grant up to £5,000 per property [PAG] (p115)
