GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.4877463,-2.2891921,"<div id=""html_f25322c7454382bb5a3f49416e3f8d90"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Salford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe river flooding with water depths so great in parts of Salford that some residents were evacuated by boat; flooding described as the worst in living memory in some areas; rapid onset and subsequently began to recede by Boxing Day evening. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell reached an unprecedented peak at the Manchester Racecourse gauge in Salford, producing river flooding in the Salford area on Boxing Day 26 December 2015. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main&ndash;river flooding caused internal flooding to residential and other properties (ground&ndash;floor/internal flooding). 750 properties were flooded in Salford/Lower Broughton. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Delph and Salford experienced power outages caused by flooding; the report gives local property counts and the overall total for Greater Manchester outages. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe electrical damage to an Environment Agency river gauging station at Lower Broughton (in Salford) as a result of the flood, affecting local flood&ndash;warning data availability. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Erosion of the Manchester Ship Canal bank adjacent to a COMAH facility immediately downstream of Mode Wheel locks; site operator emptied oil tanks nearest the canal as a precaution to mitigate a potential environmental incident if undermining had occurred. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Salford was affected by the Boxing Day flooding; RMAs held three local drop&ndash;in events and carried out visual inspections of flood&ndash;risk assets in the aftermath. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Salford was one of the most heavily affected areas in the Boxing Day flood; around 750 properties flooded and homes were evacuated. The area suffered flood&ndash;related infrastructure damage and widespread disruption. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A larger area of properties in Salford (west of the main river, including parts of Broughton and Cheetham Hill) were shown flooded during the event. [IMG] (p82)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High rainfall caused the River Irwell to overtop raised defences and natural banks and exceed surface water network capacity. River levels at Salford reached a record 5.67m (29.83 AOD), contributing to flooding in the nearby Cheetham Hill area and prompting an Environment Agency investigation. [IMG] (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Irwell affected Salford with record river levels; a Flood Warning was issued for the Salford (Area B) reach. Properties flooded above ground floor are referenced as being within Flood Zone 2 and Flood Zone 3. [PAG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread impacts across Salford: around 750 properties in Lower Broughton were internally flooded; a further 164 properties were affected by power loss and access restrictions; Agecroft cemetery experienced flooding; internal properties accumulated silt and contamination; large amounts of debris were deposited along river reaches and at Media City requiring specialist barges for removal. (p139)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell levels at Salford rose sharply on 26 December 2015, exceeding warning thresholds; a Flood Warning was issued for the River Irwell (Area B) and river flooding caused inundation and flood impacts in Salford. Flood debris was surveyed after waters receded and used to support mapping of flood extents. [IMG,PAG] (p144)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels and flooding on 26th December 2015 affected areas of Salford (including Lower Broughton). Responses included opening rest centres, provision of sandbags to residents, structural assessments of bridges due to high river levels, large&ndash;scale waste collection and disposal and multi&ndash;day clean&ndash;up operations, mapping of flood extent, and protection measures for council apparatus at Salford Quays; property&ndash;level flood resilience measures and targeted funding were provided to affected parties. [PAG] (p145)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>peak river gauge level 5.668 m; peak flow 500.0 m³/s; Rank 1 (highest recorded); annual probability ~150 years [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>750 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>950 properties without power in Delph; 250 properties without power in Salford; 31,200 properties without power (Greater Manchester total) (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>750 properties (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 47 non&ndash;residential properties within the overall area affected; 27 non&ndash;residential properties flooded internally; river level 5.67m (record; 1.3m higher than any other reading in 75 years) [IMG] (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 750 properties; 164 properties (p139)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Salford,England,"Severe river flooding with water depths so great in parts of Salford that some residents were evacuated by boat; flooding described as the worst in living memory in some areas; rapid onset and subsequently began to recede by Boxing Day evening. (p13)
River Irwell reached an unprecedented peak at the Manchester Racecourse gauge in Salford, producing river flooding in the Salford area on Boxing Day 26 December 2015. [TAB] (p15)
Main–river flooding caused internal flooding to residential and other properties (ground–floor/internal flooding). 750 properties were flooded in Salford/Lower Broughton. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Delph and Salford experienced power outages caused by flooding; the report gives local property counts and the overall total for Greater Manchester outages. (p21)
Severe electrical damage to an Environment Agency river gauging station at Lower Broughton (in Salford) as a result of the flood, affecting local flood–warning data availability. (p22)
Erosion of the Manchester Ship Canal bank adjacent to a COMAH facility immediately downstream of Mode Wheel locks; site operator emptied oil tanks nearest the canal as a precaution to mitigate a potential environmental incident if undermining had occurred. [PAG] (p23)
Salford was affected by the Boxing Day flooding; RMAs held three local drop–in events and carried out visual inspections of flood–risk assets in the aftermath. (p27)
Salford was one of the most heavily affected areas in the Boxing Day flood; around 750 properties flooded and homes were evacuated. The area suffered flood–related infrastructure damage and widespread disruption. (p29)
A larger area of properties in Salford (west of the main river, including parts of Broughton and Cheetham Hill) were shown flooded during the event. [IMG] (p82)
High rainfall caused the River Irwell to overtop raised defences and natural banks and exceed surface water network capacity. River levels at Salford reached a record 5.67m (29.83 AOD), contributing to flooding in the nearby Cheetham Hill area and prompting an Environment Agency investigation. [IMG] (p83)
Flooding from the River Irwell affected Salford with record river levels; a Flood Warning was issued for the Salford (Area B) reach. Properties flooded above ground floor are referenced as being within Flood Zone 2 and Flood Zone 3. [PAG] (p84)
Widespread impacts across Salford: around 750 properties in Lower Broughton were internally flooded; a further 164 properties were affected by power loss and access restrictions; Agecroft cemetery experienced flooding; internal properties accumulated silt and contamination; large amounts of debris were deposited along river reaches and at Media City requiring specialist barges for removal. (p139)
River Irwell levels at Salford rose sharply on 26 December 2015, exceeding warning thresholds; a Flood Warning was issued for the River Irwell (Area B) and river flooding caused inundation and flood impacts in Salford. Flood debris was surveyed after waters receded and used to support mapping of flood extents. [IMG,PAG] (p144)
High river levels and flooding on 26th December 2015 affected areas of Salford (including Lower Broughton). Responses included opening rest centres, provision of sandbags to residents, structural assessments of bridges due to high river levels, large–scale waste collection and disposal and multi–day clean–up operations, mapping of flood extent, and protection measures for council apparatus at Salford Quays; property–level flood resilience measures and targeted funding were provided to affected parties. [PAG] (p145)","peak river gauge level 5.668 m; peak flow 500.0 m³/s; Rank 1 (highest recorded); annual probability ~150 years [TAB] (p15)
750 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)
950 properties without power in Delph; 250 properties without power in Salford; 31,200 properties without power (Greater Manchester total) (p21)
750 properties (p29)
up to 47 non–residential properties within the overall area affected; 27 non–residential properties flooded internally; river level 5.67m (record; 1.3m higher than any other reading in 75 years) [IMG] (p83)
around 750 properties; 164 properties (p139)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5927543,-2.2972827,"<div id=""html_e0a17264f796a71902827461302bed45"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bury</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River gauges in the Bury area recorded record or very high peak levels on Boxing Day (affecting locations within the Bury area), indicating river flooding in the Bury catchment. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive internal flooding to properties during the event; flooding was predominantly from main rivers with some surface&ndash;water and ordinary watercourse contribution. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two bridges in Bury (at Milltown Street and Lomax Street) that carried cables were destroyed by the floods; temporary supply restoration implemented and permanent repairs planned. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bury wastewater treatment works was flooded and several treatment processes were affected; additionally, standing water was reported on the M66 near Bury which temporarily closed a lane and the hard shoulder. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bury was affected by the Boxing Day flooding; RMAs held a local drop&ndash;in event and carried out visual inspections of flood&ndash;risk assets in the aftermath. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused damage to the wastewater treatment works leading to effluent being discharged to the river after the flood that did not meet full treatment standards for several weeks. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Across Bury borough a total of 804 properties were affected by the 26 December 2015 flood event; affected locations included Radcliffe and Redvales, Pioneer Mill, industrial properties near Bury town centre and other named settlements. [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected industrial properties close to Bury town centre and caused residential and commercial property flooding in the town; river levels at nearby gauges exceeded reference thresholds during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced widespread flooding on 26th December 2015: hundreds of residential and commercial properties and highways flooded (disrupting access), damage to drainage systems causing ongoing flooding, severe impacts to recreational grounds and parks, infrastructure failures (bridge collapses/damage), treated sewage discharge from the flooded wastewater works, and power outages affecting properties in the borough. (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the town: temporary road and bridge closures to protect significant bridges over the river; emergency sandbags were distributed; a rest centre was opened; clean&ndash;up operations (including free skips) were carried out; Bury wastewater treatment works was flooded and treatment processes were affected requiring a recovery plan with the Environment Agency; United Utilities reported sewer flooding of properties in the area. (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels at Bury reached extreme levels associated with the same event (Environment Agency estimated levels with an approximate 1 in 120&ndash;180 year likelihood), contributing to flooding in the local area and prompting review of the River Irwell at Pioneer Mills and Parkside Close Flood Warning Area and local recovery actions. (p66)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood impacts across Bury on 26 December 2015: six industrial properties at Chamberhall flooded (initially by surface&ndash;water drains, then by the River Irwell overtopping a boundary wall); all flooded units experienced inundation to depths of about 0.6&ndash;1.0 m. Residential properties in Redvales and Fairfield were also affected by surface&ndash;water runoff and by flooding related to culverted watercourses and blocked drains; one flood&ndash;proofed property flooded when water backed up through drains and leaked through walls. Ongoing investigations by Bury Council and the Environment Agency were recorded. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p68)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 26 December 2015 high River Irwell levels affected Bury (estimated as an unusually rare event), with properties in Flood Zones 2 and 3 flooded. A number of locations experienced surface water run&ndash;off and culvert&ndash;related flooding (notably the Lakeland Crescent area). Safety inspections were required on the day, several key structures were closed until detailed inspections could be completed, and residents were offered evacuation to a rest centre. (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Commercial properties within Bury Council's area (including the affected industrial/business park) were flooded; the event led to local community actions such as a Heap Bridge Residents Committee meeting regularly. (p81)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Pioneer Mills 5.331 m; Ramsbottom 3.385 m; Bury Grounds peak flow 284.0 m³/s and gauge 2.178 m; Rank 1 at listed stations [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>800 properties; 726 properties flooded by main river; 15 by ordinary watercourse; 59 by surface water [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 bridges destroyed (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>804 properties [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3,200 properties (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>19 properties flooded from sewers (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 industrial properties; depth 0.6&ndash;1.0 m [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p68)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Bury,England,"River gauges in the Bury area recorded record or very high peak levels on Boxing Day (affecting locations within the Bury area), indicating river flooding in the Bury catchment. [TAB,PAG] (p15)
Extensive internal flooding to properties during the event; flooding was predominantly from main rivers with some surface–water and ordinary watercourse contribution. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Two bridges in Bury (at Milltown Street and Lomax Street) that carried cables were destroyed by the floods; temporary supply restoration implemented and permanent repairs planned. (p21)
Bury wastewater treatment works was flooded and several treatment processes were affected; additionally, standing water was reported on the M66 near Bury which temporarily closed a lane and the hard shoulder. (p22)
Bury was affected by the Boxing Day flooding; RMAs held a local drop–in event and carried out visual inspections of flood–risk assets in the aftermath. (p27)
Flooding caused damage to the wastewater treatment works leading to effluent being discharged to the river after the flood that did not meet full treatment standards for several weeks. (p29)
Across Bury borough a total of 804 properties were affected by the 26 December 2015 flood event; affected locations included Radcliffe and Redvales, Pioneer Mill, industrial properties near Bury town centre and other named settlements. [IMG,PAG] (p56)
Flooding affected industrial properties close to Bury town centre and caused residential and commercial property flooding in the town; river levels at nearby gauges exceeded reference thresholds during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p57)
The borough experienced widespread flooding on 26th December 2015: hundreds of residential and commercial properties and highways flooded (disrupting access), damage to drainage systems causing ongoing flooding, severe impacts to recreational grounds and parks, infrastructure failures (bridge collapses/damage), treated sewage discharge from the flooded wastewater works, and power outages affecting properties in the borough. (p58)
Flooding affected the town: temporary road and bridge closures to protect significant bridges over the river; emergency sandbags were distributed; a rest centre was opened; clean–up operations (including free skips) were carried out; Bury wastewater treatment works was flooded and treatment processes were affected requiring a recovery plan with the Environment Agency; United Utilities reported sewer flooding of properties in the area. (p63)
River levels at Bury reached extreme levels associated with the same event (Environment Agency estimated levels with an approximate 1 in 120–180 year likelihood), contributing to flooding in the local area and prompting review of the River Irwell at Pioneer Mills and Parkside Close Flood Warning Area and local recovery actions. (p66)
Multiple flood impacts across Bury on 26 December 2015: six industrial properties at Chamberhall flooded (initially by surface–water drains, then by the River Irwell overtopping a boundary wall); all flooded units experienced inundation to depths of about 0.6–1.0 m. Residential properties in Redvales and Fairfield were also affected by surface–water runoff and by flooding related to culverted watercourses and blocked drains; one flood–proofed property flooded when water backed up through drains and leaked through walls. Ongoing investigations by Bury Council and the Environment Agency were recorded. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p68)
On 26 December 2015 high River Irwell levels affected Bury (estimated as an unusually rare event), with properties in Flood Zones 2 and 3 flooded. A number of locations experienced surface water run–off and culvert–related flooding (notably the Lakeland Crescent area). Safety inspections were required on the day, several key structures were closed until detailed inspections could be completed, and residents were offered evacuation to a rest centre. (p70)
Commercial properties within Bury Council's area (including the affected industrial/business park) were flooded; the event led to local community actions such as a Heap Bridge Residents Committee meeting regularly. (p81)","Pioneer Mills 5.331 m; Ramsbottom 3.385 m; Bury Grounds peak flow 284.0 m³/s and gauge 2.178 m; Rank 1 at listed stations [TAB,PAG] (p15)
800 properties; 726 properties flooded by main river; 15 by ordinary watercourse; 59 by surface water [TAB,PAG] (p17)
2 bridges destroyed (p21)
804 properties [IMG,PAG] (p56)
3,200 properties (p58)
19 properties flooded from sewers (p63)
6 industrial properties; depth 0.6–1.0 m [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p68)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.47949,-2.2451148,"<div id=""html_c9dd159b3a0144cd8fb055b83cf88e85"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Manchester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the River Irk affected the London Road / Medlock area of Manchester on 26 December 2015, causing internal flooding to properties (ground&ndash;floor/internal property flooding). [PAG] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties in Manchester caused by main river flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding (River Irk at Cheetham Hill) on 26 December 2015 led to direct flood warnings being issued and properties benefiting from protective measures. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map analysis identifies southern areas near Manchester as having very high concentrations of affected properties (dark blue grid squares) during the 26 Dec 2015 floods. [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A smaller cluster of properties in Manchester (east of the main river, near Strangeways) experienced flooding. [IMG] (p82)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>37 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>386 properties received flood warnings (River Irk at Cheetham Hill); 780 properties protected (by defences) [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>251&ndash;500 properties (per 1 km x 1 km grid square) [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Manchester,England,"Flooding associated with the River Irk affected the London Road / Medlock area of Manchester on 26 December 2015, causing internal flooding to properties (ground–floor/internal property flooding). [PAG] (p16)
Internal flooding to properties in Manchester caused by main river flooding. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
River flooding (River Irk at Cheetham Hill) on 26 December 2015 led to direct flood warnings being issued and properties benefiting from protective measures. [TAB] (p26)
Map analysis identifies southern areas near Manchester as having very high concentrations of affected properties (dark blue grid squares) during the 26 Dec 2015 floods. [IMG,PAG] (p56)
A smaller cluster of properties in Manchester (east of the main river, near Strangeways) experienced flooding. [IMG] (p82)","37 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)
386 properties received flood warnings (River Irk at Cheetham Hill); 780 properties protected (by defences) [TAB] (p26)
