GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5782863,-2.4300367,"<div id=""html_ea3666c61bdd778999b36b600a21859b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bolton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe river flooding with water depths so great in parts of Bolton that some residents were evacuated by boat; flooding described as the worst in living memory in some areas; rapid onset and subsequently began to recede by Boxing Day evening. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties' ground floors (including attached garages/outbuildings) caused by main river flooding, surface&ndash;water flooding, ordinary watercourse and sewer flooding. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused power outages in both Bolton and Littleborough and damaged at least one electrical sub&ndash;station in Littleborough. Temporary rerouting and overhead lines were used to restore supply where bridges carrying cables were affected. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency river gauging station at Prestolee (near Bolton, on the River Irwell) was completely destroyed by the flood; pumping stations at Prestolee were also affected. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused damage to debris screens around Bolton; these screens require repair following the floods. Risk Management Authorities visited the area and held two local drop&ndash;in events to engage the community after the flooding. [PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris screens around Bolton were damaged by the floods and require repair; operational recovery work on these screens was programmed to be completed by Autumn 2016. The Dean Brook culvert collapse occurred at Smithills near Bolton (reported after the flooding). (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties across the borough; properties experienced internal inundation from flooding on 26 December 2015. [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As part of recovery works upstream of Bolton Metropolitan Borough Council, occupiers of properties on Riverside Drive (a cul&ndash;de&ndash;sac) were temporarily trapped by floodwater (though not flooded); the area around Bolton was included in strategic assessments and flood&ndash;warning reviews after very high river levels were recorded. (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused damage to local transport and riverside infrastructure (bridges and footpaths), with areas closed for health and safety and inspections underway. (p53)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>116 properties total; 87 properties from main river; 4 properties from ordinary watercourse; 6 properties from sewer; 19 properties from surface water [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1,000 properties without power in Bolton; 1,000 properties without power in Littleborough; 1 sub&ndash;station damaged in Littleborough (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>116 properties [IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>67 properties; 1.6m higher (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>13 bridges; 300m of riverside footpaths (p53)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Bolton,England,"Severe river flooding with water depths so great in parts of Bolton that some residents were evacuated by boat; flooding described as the worst in living memory in some areas; rapid onset and subsequently began to recede by Boxing Day evening. (p13)
Internal flooding to properties' ground floors (including attached garages/outbuildings) caused by main river flooding, surface–water flooding, ordinary watercourse and sewer flooding. [TAB] (p17)
Flooding caused power outages in both Bolton and Littleborough and damaged at least one electrical sub–station in Littleborough. Temporary rerouting and overhead lines were used to restore supply where bridges carrying cables were affected. (p21)
Environment Agency river gauging station at Prestolee (near Bolton, on the River Irwell) was completely destroyed by the flood; pumping stations at Prestolee were also affected. (p22)
Flooding caused damage to debris screens around Bolton; these screens require repair following the floods. Risk Management Authorities visited the area and held two local drop–in events to engage the community after the flooding. [PAG] (p27)
Debris screens around Bolton were damaged by the floods and require repair; operational recovery work on these screens was programmed to be completed by Autumn 2016. The Dean Brook culvert collapse occurred at Smithills near Bolton (reported after the flooding). (p28)
Internal flooding of properties across the borough; properties experienced internal inundation from flooding on 26 December 2015. [IMG] (p41)
As part of recovery works upstream of Bolton Metropolitan Borough Council, occupiers of properties on Riverside Drive (a cul–de–sac) were temporarily trapped by floodwater (though not flooded); the area around Bolton was included in strategic assessments and flood–warning reviews after very high river levels were recorded. (p45)
Flooding caused damage to local transport and riverside infrastructure (bridges and footpaths), with areas closed for health and safety and inspections underway. (p53)","116 properties total; 87 properties from main river; 4 properties from ordinary watercourse; 6 properties from sewer; 19 properties from surface water [TAB] (p17)
1,000 properties without power in Bolton; 1,000 properties without power in Littleborough; 1 sub–station damaged in Littleborough (p21)
116 properties [IMG] (p41)
67 properties; 1.6m higher (p45)
13 bridges; 300m of riverside footpaths (p53)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5415797,-2.1147831,"<div id=""html_4d0ccd788c19e88a87ce5c08127aa900"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oldham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid river level rises on 26th December 2015 caused flooding in the area near Oldham (notably Uppermill and Delph were identified as affected). River levels rose and fell quickly during the event, contributing to local flooding and disrupting planned pre&ndash;emptive activities and communications. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties from main river and surface water (also reports of flooding from ordinary watercourses). [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Oldham was affected by the Boxing Day flooding; RMAs held a local drop&ndash;in event and carried out visual inspections of flood&ndash;risk assets following the event. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pickhill Brook culvert collapse occurred in Oldham after the 26 December 2015 flooding, creating a hole in a residential driveway above the failed culvert. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Across Oldham borough a total of 123 properties were internally flooded (statistics exclude cellars unless habitable). [IMG] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties caused by surface water where surface water culverts lacked capacity and road gullies were surcharging. [TAB,PAG] (p105)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>122 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 culvert collapse reported in Oldham (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>123 properties [IMG] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 properties [TAB,PAG] (p105)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Oldham,England,"Rapid river level rises on 26th December 2015 caused flooding in the area near Oldham (notably Uppermill and Delph were identified as affected). River levels rose and fell quickly during the event, contributing to local flooding and disrupting planned pre–emptive activities and communications. [TAB] (p14)
Internal flooding to properties from main river and surface water (also reports of flooding from ordinary watercourses). [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Oldham was affected by the Boxing Day flooding; RMAs held a local drop–in event and carried out visual inspections of flood–risk assets following the event. (p27)
Pickhill Brook culvert collapse occurred in Oldham after the 26 December 2015 flooding, creating a hole in a residential driveway above the failed culvert. (p28)
Across Oldham borough a total of 123 properties were internally flooded (statistics exclude cellars unless habitable). [IMG] (p88)
Internal flooding of residential properties caused by surface water where surface water culverts lacked capacity and road gullies were surcharging. [TAB,PAG] (p105)","122 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)
1 culvert collapse reported in Oldham (p28)
123 properties [IMG] (p88)
11 properties [TAB,PAG] (p105)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.4079,-2.160243,"<div id=""html_99cdb2cbee60b84a5e76dd7b744b9ee6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stockport</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on 26th December 2015 linked to rapidly rising river levels; Stockport is specifically noted as an exception in the Mersey catchment where flooding did occur during the event, with Boxing Day river levels among the highest recorded. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal (ground&ndash;floor) flooding of properties caused by sewer flooding. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties flooded (sewer) [TAB] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Stockport,England,"Flooding occurred on 26th December 2015 linked to rapidly rising river levels; Stockport is specifically noted as an exception in the Mersey catchment where flooding did occur during the event, with Boxing Day river levels among the highest recorded. (p14)
Internal (ground–floor) flooding of properties caused by sewer flooding. [TAB] (p17)",4 properties flooded (sewer) [TAB] (p17),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.455479260166264,-2.736902662451628,"<div id=""html_be00e50429fa17c41d42e3330afcd4d6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Helens</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred around St Helens on 26 December 2015, contributing to local flood impacts and the wider regional travel disruption and infrastructure effects described in the report. (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Around 2,250 properties flooded internally across Greater Manchester (figure applies to multiple affected areas including St Helens). (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,St Helens,England,"Flooding occurred around St Helens on 26 December 2015, contributing to local flood impacts and the wider regional travel disruption and infrastructure effects described in the report. (p29)","Around 2,250 properties flooded internally across Greater Manchester (figure applies to multiple affected areas including St Helens). (p29)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.39117518202302,-2.5967829867827383,"<div id=""html_6e41d4e5f7f46b1d2222fe5c33ca001f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Warrington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred around Warrington on 26 December 2015, contributing to local flood impacts and the wider travel disruption and infrastructure impacts documented after the event. (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Around 2,250 properties flooded internally across Greater Manchester (figure applies to multiple affected areas including Warrington). (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Warrington,England,"Flooding occurred around Warrington on 26 December 2015, contributing to local flood impacts and the wider travel disruption and infrastructure impacts documented after the event. (p29)","Around 2,250 properties flooded internally across Greater Manchester (figure applies to multiple affected areas including Warrington). (p29)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6437569,-2.0436566,"<div id=""html_b629ea98d66078d74ac7d43080a05484"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Blackstone Edge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows were reported off Blackstone Edge during the event and contributed significant surface water runoff into surrounding areas including Calderbook and Littleborough. (p118)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Blackstone Edge,England,Overland flows were reported off Blackstone Edge during the event and contributed significant surface water runoff into surrounding areas including Calderbook and Littleborough. (p118),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.65283894150306,-2.3145298952502227,"<div id=""html_bf740f0241a8f2916fc66332da475f66"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ramsbottom Level Crossing</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rail operations were disrupted: the East Lancashire Railway line was closed because of the flooding, affecting the level crossing and associated rail route. Road/rail closures were enforced around river crossings while levels were high; bridges were inspected and re&ndash;opened once river levels fell. [IMG] (p73)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Ramsbottom Level Crossing,England,"Rail operations were disrupted: the East Lancashire Railway line was closed because of the flooding, affecting the level crossing and associated rail route. Road/rail closures were enforced around river crossings while levels were high; bridges were inspected and re–opened once river levels fell. [IMG] (p73)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.594140016807536,-2.2993981173494524,"<div id=""html_bd119fccbbb6e9e5c57f6a66bdbc0dcc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Lancashire Railway</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The railway line was closed due to flooding during the event. (p72)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,East Lancashire Railway,England,The railway line was closed due to flooding during the event. (p72),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6091886,-2.185937,"<div id=""html_ebd3c4be6e34e4551dc19ac2014c16f0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Roch</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Roch (associated with River Roch monitoring locations) experienced very high river levels on 26 December 2015, contributing to river flooding in the town. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels in these catchments reached record highs, contributing to flooding across the catchments and exceeding defence design levels. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the River Roch during the 26 December 2015 event caused damage to flood defence walls and embankments at Rochdale and Littleborough, including the complete collapse of a wall beside a residential care home; minor repairs and rebuilding of affected walls were programmed. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Roch overtopped its right bank above a hydropower unit at the weir and flowed down into the industrial / J2 Business Park, causing flooding of commercial units and their accesses. Floodwater was fast, sudden, muddy and polluted. Debris accumulated in the channel and on structures (photo shows debris on a metal structure); a disused footbridge had its 3rd arch blocked and required clearance by riparian landowners. [IMG,PAG] (p80)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Roch overtopped its right bank above a hydropower unit at the weir and flowed down into an industrial/business park, causing fast, sudden, muddy and polluted flooding that inundated business units and blocked accesses (shutters and doors). Riparian debris blocking (including a blocked 3rd arch on a disused footbridge) was observed and cleared after the event. [PAG] (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Named as a primary source of flooding that caused internal property flooding across the borough, including in Rochdale town centre and Wardleworth. [PAG] (p113)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Roch rose to extreme levels on 26 December 2015 and was identified as a primary source of flooding across Rochdale borough, contributing to internal flooding of properties and record river levels at Blackford Bridge. [IMG,PAG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the River Roch contributed to flood risk between Littleborough and Rochdale town centre on 26th December 2015. This river flooding was a driver for subsequent works and investigations (Environment Agency proposals and a de&ndash;culverting project) to reduce future flood risk. (p115)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of raised defences and natural banks caused widespread flooding of nearby properties and damage to infrastructure. Contributed to flooding of Greenvale Business Park via overtopping and interaction with the flood relief channel. Infrastructure damage linked to the event included culvert damage at Calderbrook Road, Todmorden Road and Halifax Road, and gully blockage and damage to paths. [PAG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Roch at Littleborough reached record high levels during the event; high river flows caused surface water to pond against a defence wall (which subsequently collapsed) and prevented surface water from draining away, contributing to flooding in the area. A flood warning was issued for the River Roch at Littleborough. [PAG] (p118)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in the River Roch through Roch town centre exceeded the capacity of a culvert next to the new bus station, causing river water to flood the town centre. Floodwater travelled along Smith Street and South Parade and re&ndash;entered the river downstream; businesses on Smith Street, South Parade, The Butts and The Walk were flooded; Number One Riverside council office was flooded (ground&ndash;floor library remained closed after reopening); the town centre bus station and tram terminus sustained flood damage; power loss affected many homes and businesses; the town hall narrowly avoided flooding. Downstream, the Asda superstore, adjacent Curry's PC World and the Sixth Form College suffered internal flood damage and St Mary&#8217;s Gate subway was damaged. (p121)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river flows on the River Roch caused flooding in Rochdale; a formal flood warning area was active and the river gauge at Albert recorded the highest level in 22 years. (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Roch was the main river source for flooding in the area: properties close to the River Roch were flooded internally (to depths up to 400 mm) and a large portion of the reported flooded properties (35 of 36) were attributed to the main river (River Roch and Hey/Buckley Brook). Cellars adjacent to the River Roch flooded from surface water/groundwater when the brook reached high levels. (p125)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Very high river levels on 26 December 2015 (the gauge at Albert Royds Street recorded its highest level in 22 years) caused flooding of properties adjacent to the River Roch (internal flooding reported, cellars flooded from groundwater/surface water) and contributed to wider flood extent in Rochdale/Wardleworth. Local interventions (assessments, gravel removal and proposals to reduce risk) were initiated after the event. [PAG] (p126)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Roch (and its drains) caused rear yard drains of residential properties to fill and then river water entered properties. Flow was gradual at first then became fast; muddy, polluted water flowed into properties to depths of about 600 to 900 mm. Riparian debris (a blocked third arch of a disused footbridge) was identified and subsequently cleared by riparian landowners. (p128)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Exceptional flows in the River Roch on 26th December 2015 generated flooding in the Heap Bridge area and on Bury Road. Saturated ground increased runoff and contributed to the severity of flooding; nearby gauging (Rochdale Wastewater Treatment Works) recorded unusually high levels during the event. (p129)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels on the Roch rose to a very large event (highest gauge reading in 22 years at the gauge location) and caused flooding of properties (including 20 properties at Hooley Bridge Industrial Estate). [TAB] (p134)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels reached their highest in 22 years at Albert Royds Street (between Belfield and the town centre), producing significant river flooding that led to properties being flooded, including some properties outside Flood Zone 3. (p137)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>37 of 44 river level gauges in the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments recorded their highest ever levels (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one wall completely failed (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 business properties flooded; depths 0.3 m to 1.5 m (typical 0.5 m); one industrial unit where cars floated; 3rd arch blocked [IMG,PAG] (p80)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>324 properties flooded internally (borough total); Blackford Bridge level 3.362 m; 1.1 m higher than any previous reading [IMG,PAG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>158 properties flooded above ground floor level (shared with Greenvale Brook) [PAG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 690 properties [PAG] (p118)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>54 properties flooded; properties flooded up to 1.5 metres deep (measured at The Butts); Asda flooded up to 50 mm; two cellars flooded (p121)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>541 properties; highest gauge in 22 years; 400 mm higher than previous recorded high (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35 properties flooded due to main river (River Roch and Hey/Buckley/Buckley Brook); up to 400mm internal depth (p125)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties; 600 to 900 mm (p128)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 properties [TAB] (p134)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Roch,England,"Areas of Roch (associated with River Roch monitoring locations) experienced very high river levels on 26 December 2015, contributing to river flooding in the town. (p16)
River levels in these catchments reached record highs, contributing to flooding across the catchments and exceeding defence design levels. (p20)
High flows in the River Roch during the 26 December 2015 event caused damage to flood defence walls and embankments at Rochdale and Littleborough, including the complete collapse of a wall beside a residential care home; minor repairs and rebuilding of affected walls were programmed. (p27)
The River Roch overtopped its right bank above a hydropower unit at the weir and flowed down into the industrial / J2 Business Park, causing flooding of commercial units and their accesses. Floodwater was fast, sudden, muddy and polluted. Debris accumulated in the channel and on structures (photo shows debris on a metal structure); a disused footbridge had its 3rd arch blocked and required clearance by riparian landowners. [IMG,PAG] (p80)
The River Roch overtopped its right bank above a hydropower unit at the weir and flowed down into an industrial/business park, causing fast, sudden, muddy and polluted flooding that inundated business units and blocked accesses (shutters and doors). Riparian debris blocking (including a blocked 3rd arch on a disused footbridge) was observed and cleared after the event. [PAG] (p81)
Named as a primary source of flooding that caused internal property flooding across the borough, including in Rochdale town centre and Wardleworth. [PAG] (p113)
The River Roch rose to extreme levels on 26 December 2015 and was identified as a primary source of flooding across Rochdale borough, contributing to internal flooding of properties and record river levels at Blackford Bridge. [IMG,PAG] (p114)
Flooding of the River Roch contributed to flood risk between Littleborough and Rochdale town centre on 26th December 2015. This river flooding was a driver for subsequent works and investigations (Environment Agency proposals and a de–culverting project) to reduce future flood risk. (p115)
Overtopping of raised defences and natural banks caused widespread flooding of nearby properties and damage to infrastructure. Contributed to flooding of Greenvale Business Park via overtopping and interaction with the flood relief channel. Infrastructure damage linked to the event included culvert damage at Calderbrook Road, Todmorden Road and Halifax Road, and gully blockage and damage to paths. [PAG] (p117)
The River Roch at Littleborough reached record high levels during the event; high river flows caused surface water to pond against a defence wall (which subsequently collapsed) and prevented surface water from draining away, contributing to flooding in the area. A flood warning was issued for the River Roch at Littleborough. [PAG] (p118)
Flows in the River Roch through Roch town centre exceeded the capacity of a culvert next to the new bus station, causing river water to flood the town centre. Floodwater travelled along Smith Street and South Parade and re–entered the river downstream; businesses on Smith Street, South Parade, The Butts and The Walk were flooded; Number One Riverside council office was flooded (ground–floor library remained closed after reopening); the town centre bus station and tram terminus sustained flood damage; power loss affected many homes and businesses; the town hall narrowly avoided flooding. Downstream, the Asda superstore, adjacent Curry's PC World and the Sixth Form College suffered internal flood damage and St Mary’s Gate subway was damaged. (p121)
High river flows on the River Roch caused flooding in Rochdale; a formal flood warning area was active and the river gauge at Albert recorded the highest level in 22 years. (p123)
River Roch was the main river source for flooding in the area: properties close to the River Roch were flooded internally (to depths up to 400 mm) and a large portion of the reported flooded properties (35 of 36) were attributed to the main river (River Roch and Hey/Buckley Brook). Cellars adjacent to the River Roch flooded from surface water/groundwater when the brook reached high levels. (p125)
Very high river levels on 26 December 2015 (the gauge at Albert Royds Street recorded its highest level in 22 years) caused flooding of properties adjacent to the River Roch (internal flooding reported, cellars flooded from groundwater/surface water) and contributed to wider flood extent in Rochdale/Wardleworth. Local interventions (assessments, gravel removal and proposals to reduce risk) were initiated after the event. [PAG] (p126)
Flooding from the River Roch (and its drains) caused rear yard drains of residential properties to fill and then river water entered properties. Flow was gradual at first then became fast; muddy, polluted water flowed into properties to depths of about 600 to 900 mm. Riparian debris (a blocked third arch of a disused footbridge) was identified and subsequently cleared by riparian landowners. (p128)
Exceptional flows in the River Roch on 26th December 2015 generated flooding in the Heap Bridge area and on Bury Road. Saturated ground increased runoff and contributed to the severity of flooding; nearby gauging (Rochdale Wastewater Treatment Works) recorded unusually high levels during the event. (p129)
River levels on the Roch rose to a very large event (highest gauge reading in 22 years at the gauge location) and caused flooding of properties (including 20 properties at Hooley Bridge Industrial Estate). [TAB] (p134)
River levels reached their highest in 22 years at Albert Royds Street (between Belfield and the town centre), producing significant river flooding that led to properties being flooded, including some properties outside Flood Zone 3. (p137)","37 of 44 river level gauges in the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments recorded their highest ever levels (p20)
