GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5739664,-2.4499115,"<div id=""html_b536431536d79fd0ff2faa6d5c2867f2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Croal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels in these catchments reached record highs, contributing to flooding across the catchments and exceeding defence design levels. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Croal catchment recorded exceptionally high river levels and contributed to main&ndash;river flooding and associated damage to river&ndash;related assets and property flooding in the wider area. (p29)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>37 of 44 river level gauges in the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments recorded their highest ever levels (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Part of the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments where 37 of 44 gauges recorded their highest ever levels; contributed to the around 2,250 internally flooded properties across the catchments (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Croal,England,"River levels in these catchments reached record highs, contributing to flooding across the catchments and exceeding defence design levels. (p20)
The Croal catchment recorded exceptionally high river levels and contributed to main–river flooding and associated damage to river–related assets and property flooding in the wider area. (p29)","37 of 44 river level gauges in the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments recorded their highest ever levels (p20)
Part of the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments where 37 of 44 gauges recorded their highest ever levels; contributed to the around 2,250 internally flooded properties across the catchments (p29)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6023223,-2.2999892,"<div id=""html_fa465adc9452aaf02f943c616b8907cb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Irwell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels in the Upper Irwell rose rapidly in response to heavy, long&ndash;duration rainfall on saturated ground, producing fast&ndash;onset, widespread flooding across the Upper Irwell catchment and contributing to devastating flooding in Greater Manchester (depths in parts of the area were reported as so great that residents had to be evacuated by boat). [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 26th December 2015 river levels in the Irwell catchment rose very rapidly and reached exceptionally high peak levels (many sites recorded their highest&ndash;ever Boxing Day levels), producing fluvial flooding across downstream parts of the Irwell catchment in Greater Manchester and overwhelming planned pre&ndash;emptive actions and communications. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple gauging stations on the River Irwell across Greater Manchester recorded their highest&ndash;ever levels on Boxing Day, producing widespread river flooding along the Irwell corridor (examples include 6.330 m at Kearsley Ultrasonic and 5.668 m at Manchester Racecourse). [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell catchment experienced exceptionally high river levels contributing to widespread flooding across the catchment area; record gauge readings correlate with flooded properties in nearby areas. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell catchment was a primary focus of the heavy rainfall and recorded very high/record river levels, causing major river flooding, damage to river channel retaining walls, culverts and bridges, mobilisation of large volumes of gravel downstream, and contributing to extensive internal property flooding. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the River Irwell caused flooding in the local area; the river inundated Prestolee properties resulting in internal flooding of homes (see Prestolee impacts). [PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell rose to exceptionally high levels at Kearsley (6.33m, a 13&ndash;year record), with river level peaks driven by intense rainfall in the catchment. High river levels were associated with infrastructure damage including the collapse of a United Utilities pipe bridge at Kearsley and damage to Prestolee pumping station. [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell overtopped natural banks and caused river flooding that entered properties. Floodwaters from the Irwell internally flooded multiple properties and at least one business; floodwaters affected riverside streets and residential blocks in the local area. [PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell reached exceptionally high levels at multiple gauging stations and caused flooding that inundated properties (notably in Radcliffe and Redvales). Recorded high water levels coincided with property flooding in the Bury area. [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High River Irwell levels caused residential and commercial property flooding in the catchment; the Waterside Restaurant in Summerseat collapsed into the river causing partial collapse and closure of the associated masonry arch bridge; flood&ndash;related infrastructure failures (including the Lomax Street footbridge collapse, gas main rupture and sewage discharge) occurred along the river. [IMG,PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell reached extremely high levels (estimated rare event) and overtopped/breached banks causing flooding at Bury, Radcliffe and Redvales; flood sedimentation and damage to river monitoring equipment led to scheduled gravel removal and gauging&ndash;station repairs; flood warning areas (GM41/GM42) and thresholds were reviewed following the event. (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell overtopped its banks and caused flooding at Pioneer Mill, inundating the mill from three sides. (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell overtopped at Chamberhall, flooding adjacent roads and industrial properties; water also seeped under a raised boundary wall and emerged behind it, and debris and significant inundation were observed at Chamberhall. [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extremely high River Irwell levels on 26 December 2015 (reported at Bury and Pioneer Mill) caused river flooding &mdash; the raised wall delayed onset but was not a formal flood defence, and river water seeped beneath a boundary wall and emerged behind it. (p70)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Exceptionally high river levels caused main&ndash;river flooding on 26 December 2015, prevented surface water drainage outfalls, contributed to inundation of culverts and overland flows that combined with river flooding in low&ndash;lying areas; this contributed to property and business flooding and to damage at culverts (notably at Crow Lane/Bridge Street). (p72)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell reached extreme levels that overtopped/approached defences, caused flooding of adjacent open spaces (cricket and football grounds), damaged river channel walls at Nuttall Park, and damaged the weir and canoe pass just upstream of Peel Brow. Bridges were temporarily closed and inspected; gravel removal and channel repairs were part of recovery works. (p74)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels in the River Irwell overtopped raised defences and natural banks at Cheetham Hill and caused flooding which exceeded the capacity of the surface water network; non&ndash;residential properties in the area were inundated. [IMG] (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels at Salford reached a record high of 5.67 m (29.83 AOD), 1.3 m higher than any previous reading in 75 years; a Flood Warning was issued for the River Irwell (Salford Area B). [PAG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell overtopped raised defences and natural banks after high rainfall. Raised water levels (partly attributed to debris caught beneath Hough Lane footbridge) led to overtopping between Wallness Bridge and Hough Lane Bridge, causing flows into Lower Broughton, damage to river walls and walkways at the rear of the Soapworks site, erosion to a footbridge abutment in Clifton Country Park, and deposition of large amounts of debris along river banks and at Media City (requiring specialist removal). The overtopping and exceedance of the surface water network capacity resulted in widespread internal property flooding in Lower Broughton. [PAG] (p139)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in the River Irwell rose close to or onto its banks, producing fast&ndash;flowing, muddy water with debris and threatening nearby riverside areas and residential properties. The elevated river levels contributed to overtopping and inundation of adjacent roads and paths. [IMG] (p140)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell overtopped its banks, with river water breaching containment and inundating adjacent pedestrian areas (pavement and tree bases) at Heath Lane. [IMG,PAG] (p142)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded an extreme, record&ndash;high river level (5.67 m / 29.83 AOD), 1.3 m higher than any previous reading in 75 years, representing a rare (estimated 100&ndash;150 year) event and causing river flooding in the catchment that contributed to flooding downstream. [PAG] (p143)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell responded to intense rainfall with a large rise in level, peaking on 26 December 2015 above critical/warning thresholds; high flows triggered a Flood Warning for Area B and led to inundation (including of the Castle Irwell basin) and urban flood impacts around Salford. [IMG,PAG] (p144)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>peaks including 6.330 m (Kearsley Ultrasonic) and 5.668 m (Manchester Racecourse); flows up to 500.0 m³/s [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>37 of 44 river level gauges recorded their highest ever levels [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Particularly high levels in the Irwell catchment within the 37 of 44 gauges recording their highest ever levels; contributed to the total of around 2,250 properties flooded internally (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>57 homes (at Prestolee); flood depths up to 1.5 metres (at Prestolee) [PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6.33m river level; 1.6m above previous record [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>57 properties internally flooded; 1 business flooded [PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>671 properties; river levels recorded: 5.33 m at Pioneer Mills (Radcliffe), 3.39 m at Ramsbottom, 3.36 m at Blackford Bridge, 2.18 m at Bury Grounds [PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 restaurant collapsed; 1 footbridge collapsed [IMG,PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>River levels at Bury estimated 1 in 120 to 180 years; 0.6 to 0.8m general flood depth (Radcliffe and Redvales); maximum 1.45m at Parkside Close (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>flooded Pioneer Mill from 3 sides (p65)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 industrial properties [IMG,PAG] (p69)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>47 properties (p72)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>26 properties (p74)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>47 non&ndash;residential properties within the overall area affected; 27 non&ndash;residential properties flooded internally [IMG] (p83)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>river level of 5.67 m (29.83 AOD); 1.3 m higher than any other reading in 75 years; estimated likelihood once in 100 to 150 years [PAG] (p84)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 750 properties; 164 properties; three distinct flow routes [PAG] (p139)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5.67 m river level; 29.83 AOD; 1.3 m higher than previous maximum; 75 years of record; estimated return period 100&ndash;150 years [PAG] (p143)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Irwell,England,"River levels in the Upper Irwell rose rapidly in response to heavy, long–duration rainfall on saturated ground, producing fast–onset, widespread flooding across the Upper Irwell catchment and contributing to devastating flooding in Greater Manchester (depths in parts of the area were reported as so great that residents had to be evacuated by boat). [PAG] (p13)
On 26th December 2015 river levels in the Irwell catchment rose very rapidly and reached exceptionally high peak levels (many sites recorded their highest–ever Boxing Day levels), producing fluvial flooding across downstream parts of the Irwell catchment in Greater Manchester and overwhelming planned pre–emptive actions and communications. (p14)
Multiple gauging stations on the River Irwell across Greater Manchester recorded their highest–ever levels on Boxing Day, producing widespread river flooding along the Irwell corridor (examples include 6.330 m at Kearsley Ultrasonic and 5.668 m at Manchester Racecourse). [TAB,PAG] (p15)
River Irwell catchment experienced exceptionally high river levels contributing to widespread flooding across the catchment area; record gauge readings correlate with flooded properties in nearby areas. [IMG] (p20)
The River Irwell catchment was a primary focus of the heavy rainfall and recorded very high/record river levels, causing major river flooding, damage to river channel retaining walls, culverts and bridges, mobilisation of large volumes of gravel downstream, and contributing to extensive internal property flooding. (p29)
High flows in the River Irwell caused flooding in the local area; the river inundated Prestolee properties resulting in internal flooding of homes (see Prestolee impacts). [PAG] (p41)
The River Irwell rose to exceptionally high levels at Kearsley (6.33m, a 13–year record), with river level peaks driven by intense rainfall in the catchment. High river levels were associated with infrastructure damage including the collapse of a United Utilities pipe bridge at Kearsley and damage to Prestolee pumping station. [IMG] (p42)
The River Irwell overtopped natural banks and caused river flooding that entered properties. Floodwaters from the Irwell internally flooded multiple properties and at least one business; floodwaters affected riverside streets and residential blocks in the local area. [PAG] (p44)
The River Irwell reached exceptionally high levels at multiple gauging stations and caused flooding that inundated properties (notably in Radcliffe and Redvales). Recorded high water levels coincided with property flooding in the Bury area. [PAG] (p56)
High River Irwell levels caused residential and commercial property flooding in the catchment; the Waterside Restaurant in Summerseat collapsed into the river causing partial collapse and closure of the associated masonry arch bridge; flood–related infrastructure failures (including the Lomax Street footbridge collapse, gas main rupture and sewage discharge) occurred along the river. [IMG,PAG] (p57)
The River Irwell reached extremely high levels (estimated rare event) and overtopped/breached banks causing flooding at Bury, Radcliffe and Redvales; flood sedimentation and damage to river monitoring equipment led to scheduled gravel removal and gauging–station repairs; flood warning areas (GM41/GM42) and thresholds were reviewed following the event. (p63)
The River Irwell overtopped its banks and caused flooding at Pioneer Mill, inundating the mill from three sides. (p65)
The River Irwell overtopped at Chamberhall, flooding adjacent roads and industrial properties; water also seeped under a raised boundary wall and emerged behind it, and debris and significant inundation were observed at Chamberhall. [IMG,PAG] (p69)
Extremely high River Irwell levels on 26 December 2015 (reported at Bury and Pioneer Mill) caused river flooding — the raised wall delayed onset but was not a formal flood defence, and river water seeped beneath a boundary wall and emerged behind it. (p70)
Exceptionally high river levels caused main–river flooding on 26 December 2015, prevented surface water drainage outfalls, contributed to inundation of culverts and overland flows that combined with river flooding in low–lying areas; this contributed to property and business flooding and to damage at culverts (notably at Crow Lane/Bridge Street). (p72)
The River Irwell reached extreme levels that overtopped/approached defences, caused flooding of adjacent open spaces (cricket and football grounds), damaged river channel walls at Nuttall Park, and damaged the weir and canoe pass just upstream of Peel Brow. Bridges were temporarily closed and inspected; gravel removal and channel repairs were part of recovery works. (p74)
High river levels in the River Irwell overtopped raised defences and natural banks at Cheetham Hill and caused flooding which exceeded the capacity of the surface water network; non–residential properties in the area were inundated. [IMG] (p83)
River levels at Salford reached a record high of 5.67 m (29.83 AOD), 1.3 m higher than any previous reading in 75 years; a Flood Warning was issued for the River Irwell (Salford Area B). [PAG] (p84)
River Irwell overtopped raised defences and natural banks after high rainfall. Raised water levels (partly attributed to debris caught beneath Hough Lane footbridge) led to overtopping between Wallness Bridge and Hough Lane Bridge, causing flows into Lower Broughton, damage to river walls and walkways at the rear of the Soapworks site, erosion to a footbridge abutment in Clifton Country Park, and deposition of large amounts of debris along river banks and at Media City (requiring specialist removal). The overtopping and exceedance of the surface water network capacity resulted in widespread internal property flooding in Lower Broughton. [PAG] (p139)
High water levels in the River Irwell rose close to or onto its banks, producing fast–flowing, muddy water with debris and threatening nearby riverside areas and residential properties. The elevated river levels contributed to overtopping and inundation of adjacent roads and paths. [IMG] (p140)
River Irwell overtopped its banks, with river water breaching containment and inundating adjacent pedestrian areas (pavement and tree bases) at Heath Lane. [IMG,PAG] (p142)
Recorded an extreme, record–high river level (5.67 m / 29.83 AOD), 1.3 m higher than any previous reading in 75 years, representing a rare (estimated 100–150 year) event and causing river flooding in the catchment that contributed to flooding downstream. [PAG] (p143)
The River Irwell responded to intense rainfall with a large rise in level, peaking on 26 December 2015 above critical/warning thresholds; high flows triggered a Flood Warning for Area B and led to inundation (including of the Castle Irwell basin) and urban flood impacts around Salford. [IMG,PAG] (p144)","peaks including 6.330 m (Kearsley Ultrasonic) and 5.668 m (Manchester Racecourse); flows up to 500.0 m³/s [TAB,PAG] (p15)
37 of 44 river level gauges recorded their highest ever levels [IMG] (p20)
Particularly high levels in the Irwell catchment within the 37 of 44 gauges recording their highest ever levels; contributed to the total of around 2,250 properties flooded internally (p29)
57 homes (at Prestolee); flood depths up to 1.5 metres (at Prestolee) [PAG] (p41)
6.33m river level; 1.6m above previous record [IMG] (p42)
57 properties internally flooded; 1 business flooded [PAG] (p44)
671 properties; river levels recorded: 5.33 m at Pioneer Mills (Radcliffe), 3.39 m at Ramsbottom, 3.36 m at Blackford Bridge, 2.18 m at Bury Grounds [PAG] (p56)
1 restaurant collapsed; 1 footbridge collapsed [IMG,PAG] (p57)
River levels at Bury estimated 1 in 120 to 180 years; 0.6 to 0.8m general flood depth (Radcliffe and Redvales); maximum 1.45m at Parkside Close (p63)
