5.67 m river level; 29.83 AOD; 1.3 m higher than previous maximum; 75 years of record; estimated return period 100–150 years [PAG] (p143)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5489793,-2.0077622,"<div id=""html_e45c013b440b0432ebeb896b1f17385a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Tame</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Tame overtopped its natural banks causing internal flooding to properties; surface water and surcharging road gullies and ordinary watercourses also contributed to flooding across the area. (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Tame overflowed and caused river flooding that inundated riverside residential buildings; floodwater reached and covered the lower/ground&ndash;floor windows of a stone house adjacent to the river. [IMG] (p100)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows/overflow of the River Tame were described as adjacent to affected properties and are implicated in the flooding of the balcony and house at Wright Mill. [IMG,PAG] (p111)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties; 7 businesses; 35 residential properties; 16 properties (River Tame); 9 properties (ordinary watercourses); 17 properties (surface water) (p92)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Tame,England,"River Tame overtopped its natural banks causing internal flooding to properties; surface water and surcharging road gullies and ordinary watercourses also contributed to flooding across the area. (p92)
The River Tame overflowed and caused river flooding that inundated riverside residential buildings; floodwater reached and covered the lower/ground–floor windows of a stone house adjacent to the river. [IMG] (p100)
High flows/overflow of the River Tame were described as adjacent to affected properties and are implicated in the flooding of the balcony and house at Wright Mill. [IMG,PAG] (p111)",42 properties; 7 businesses; 35 residential properties; 16 properties (River Tame); 9 properties (ordinary watercourses); 17 properties (surface water) (p92),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6457771,-2.1810651,"<div id=""html_a7c2d2ade109f99eda96c8b63723218c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Spodden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Spodden was identified as one of the primary watercourses causing flooding in Rochdale borough, contributing to internal flooding of properties on 26 December 2015. (p113)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Spodden inundated two cellars and the yard at a piling contractor's depot near its confluence with the River Roch. (p121)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Spodden catchment experienced very high rainfall producing significant surface flows down moorland above Littleborough and elsewhere, contributing to the overall flood event. (p123)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two cellars (p121)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Spodden,England,"The River Spodden was identified as one of the primary watercourses causing flooding in Rochdale borough, contributing to internal flooding of properties on 26 December 2015. (p113)
Flooding from the River Spodden inundated two cellars and the yard at a piling contractor's depot near its confluence with the River Roch. (p121)
The River Spodden catchment experienced very high rainfall producing significant surface flows down moorland above Littleborough and elsewhere, contributing to the overall flood event. (p123)",two cellars (p121),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5973842,-2.1029209,"<div id=""html_2a7819c0ce5118cb1153879bb6555125"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Beal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant surface flows were observed in the River Beal catchment (moorland above Littleborough and other parts of the catchment) contributing to flooding conditions during the Boxing Day 2015 event. (p126)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels swelled beyond normal, causing floodwater to spill through gaps in the river wall and overtop/flow into adjacent cottage grounds and commercial premises; floodwater flowed down Lily Street onto Bridge Street and contributed to surface water pooling and property flooding. (p132)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Very high river levels in the River Beal at Station Road, Milnrow led to overtopping and were a primary cause of flooding to nearby infrastructure (including Firgrove Business Park). (p136)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Channel and culvert capacity were exceeded; flows overtopped the banks at the inlet of a culvert, causing flooding of adjoining units and yard areas at Firgrove Business Park. Ten industrial storage units at Firgrove Business Park were flooded. [PAG] (p137)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties; external flood depths 200&ndash;900mm; event estimated 1 in 50 to 100 year (p132)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 industrial storage units [PAG] (p137)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Beal,England,"Significant surface flows were observed in the River Beal catchment (moorland above Littleborough and other parts of the catchment) contributing to flooding conditions during the Boxing Day 2015 event. (p126)
River levels swelled beyond normal, causing floodwater to spill through gaps in the river wall and overtop/flow into adjacent cottage grounds and commercial premises; floodwater flowed down Lily Street onto Bridge Street and contributed to surface water pooling and property flooding. (p132)
Very high river levels in the River Beal at Station Road, Milnrow led to overtopping and were a primary cause of flooding to nearby infrastructure (including Firgrove Business Park). (p136)
Channel and culvert capacity were exceeded; flows overtopped the banks at the inlet of a culvert, causing flooding of adjoining units and yard areas at Firgrove Business Park. Ten industrial storage units at Firgrove Business Park were flooded. [PAG] (p137)","9 properties; external flood depths 200–900mm; event estimated 1 in 50 to 100 year (p132)
10 industrial storage units [PAG] (p137)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.57531,-2.7159472,"<div id=""html_c897acb64781f9c83b957bf93d6a9925"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Douglas</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Douglas (main river) contributed to internal flooding of properties in Standish. (p146)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Douglas rose rapidly and reached a record peak (2.55 m at Wigan), rising 1.4 m in the 2 hours 15 minutes before the peak on 26 December 2015. The high and fast&ndash;rising river level contributed to fluvial flooding that affected properties in the borough (Standish is specifically listed as flooded from the River Douglas). [IMG,PAG] (p147)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Environment Agency gauge at Wigan recorded exceptionally high river levels (the highest in 38 years), indicating extreme high river levels that contributed to local flooding impacts. (p159)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Environment Agency gauge on the River Douglas at Wigan recorded the highest river levels in 38 years of record (about 1 m higher than the previous highest level), indicating major river flooding at the time of the event. (p162)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties (p146)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties [IMG,PAG] (p147)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>highest in 38 years; 1 m higher than the previous highest level (p159)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>highest in 38 years; 1 m higher than previous highest levels (p162)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,River Douglas,England,"River Douglas (main river) contributed to internal flooding of properties in Standish. (p146)
River Douglas rose rapidly and reached a record peak (2.55 m at Wigan), rising 1.4 m in the 2 hours 15 minutes before the peak on 26 December 2015. The high and fast–rising river level contributed to fluvial flooding that affected properties in the borough (Standish is specifically listed as flooded from the River Douglas). [IMG,PAG] (p147)
The Environment Agency gauge at Wigan recorded exceptionally high river levels (the highest in 38 years), indicating extreme high river levels that contributed to local flooding impacts. (p159)
The Environment Agency gauge on the River Douglas at Wigan recorded the highest river levels in 38 years of record (about 1 m higher than the previous highest level), indicating major river flooding at the time of the event. (p162)","6 properties (p146)
6 properties [IMG,PAG] (p147)
highest in 38 years; 1 m higher than the previous highest level (p159)
highest in 38 years; 1 m higher than previous highest levels (p162)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.538845313386815,-2.2889024333185075,"<div id=""html_3898b922f1591dc1308c719db1178077"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Junction 17 M62</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Standing water on the M62 near the Prestwich/Whitefield (junction 17) closed lane 1 and the hard shoulder for a short period; Highways England maintenance crews cleared gullies and no full road closures were required. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Junction 17 M62,England,Standing water on the M62 near the Prestwich/Whitefield (junction 17) closed lane 1 and the hard shoulder for a short period; Highways England maintenance crews cleared gullies and no full road closures were required. (p22),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.57220572625561,-2.493729658899238,"<div id=""html_8c7cf4de1228c3118a0cad7d83395b71"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mill Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area around Mill Bridge experienced flooding, with a major cluster of flooded properties adjacent to the bridge and river crossing. [IMG] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Mill Bridge,England,"Area around Mill Bridge experienced flooding, with a major cluster of flooded properties adjacent to the bridge and river crossing. [IMG] (p46)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.4945871769253,-2.2701821906538076,"<div id=""html_402ff523ea38114c46b329ffd71855fc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wallness Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Left&ndash;bank flood defences were overtopped between Wallness Bridge and Hough Lane Bridge, producing three distinct flow routes into Lower Broughton that led to widespread internal flooding of properties and associated access/power impacts. (p139)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Wallness Bridge,England,"Left–bank flood defences were overtopped between Wallness Bridge and Hough Lane Bridge, producing three distinct flow routes into Lower Broughton that led to widespread internal flooding of properties and associated access/power impacts. (p139)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
