GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.62096155013149,-2.1514871524799264,"<div id=""html_aabb7f319aec9d438c42e7541d2afc49"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yorkshire Street, Rochdale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the Boxing Day flooding water was able to re&ndash;enter the river near the bottom of Yorkshire Street; this is reported to have reduced the duration of town&ndash;centre flooding. (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Businesses suffered substantial cellar flooding which affected operations; most businesses on Smith Street, South Parade, The Butts and The Walk were flooded. Flooding in the town centre (including this area) inundated premises and contributed to the ground floor library at Number One Riverside remaining closed for recovery. (p121)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 1.5 metres deep (measured at The Butts) (p121)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Yorkshire Street, Rochdale",England,"During the Boxing Day flooding water was able to re–enter the river near the bottom of Yorkshire Street; this is reported to have reduced the duration of town–centre flooding. (p114)
