River Roch level measured at the Royds Street/Albert gauge was the highest in 22 years, substantially exceeding previous peaks and indicating extreme river levels contributing to flooding in the area. (p126)","highest in 22 years; 400mm higher than the previous highest recorded in January 2008; 500mm higher than June 2012 (p123)
highest in 22 years; 400mm higher than the previous highest (Jan 2008); 500mm higher than June 2012 (p126)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.62982417208937,-2.144202720807851,"<div id=""html_e66704ec17e17eb751e4018748cfb983"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckley Road, Rochdale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream, allowing floodwater to flow onto Buckley Road and inundate the roadway. (p125)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Buckley Road, Rochdale",England,"Culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream, allowing floodwater to flow onto Buckley Road and inundate the roadway. (p125)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.61870699881645,-2.1587415470616955,"<div id=""html_8d1b41580e9fd4610e2af5c095da0352"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Park Lane, Rochdale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater reached Park Lane after culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream, resulting in inundation of the street. (p125)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Park Lane, Rochdale",England,"Floodwater reached Park Lane after culverts on Hey Brook overtopped upstream, resulting in inundation of the street. (p125)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.611977,-2.4283557,"<div id=""html_7775fd1e2b1f14a4c5bbc81e70037c3b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kellet Street, Bolton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Allotments on Kellet Street were flooded (also noted as having flooded in 2012). (p125)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Kellet Street, Bolton",England,Allotments on Kellet Street were flooded (also noted as having flooded in 2012). (p125),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5527051,-2.4051298,"<div id=""html_d38266bdb07a7ac3064b024473b4a413"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gower Street, Farnworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 26 December 2015 the area around Gower Street was affected by the flood: the Gower Street Youth Base was used as a rest centre, sand collection points and empty sandbags were provided, and properties in the vicinity were within the flooded extent. (p126)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Gower Street, Farnworth",England,"On 26 December 2015 the area around Gower Street was affected by the flood: the Gower Street Youth Base was used as a rest centre, sand collection points and empty sandbags were provided, and properties in the vicinity were within the flooded extent. (p126)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6206775,-2.1423166,"<div id=""html_0b194582f364acbc215287078c4a5e4a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Weedon Street, Wardleworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the vicinity of Weedon Street flooded on Boxing Day 2015; the report notes there were no raised defences on the River Roch in the vicinity of Gower Street and Weedon Street where properties flooded. (p126)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Weedon Street, Wardleworth",England,Properties in the vicinity of Weedon Street flooded on Boxing Day 2015; the report notes there were no raised defences on the River Roch in the vicinity of Gower Street and Weedon Street where properties flooded. (p126),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.6072982,-2.2087785,"<div id=""html_6549db7d935d7db5971cf198bb39f305"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bury Road, Heywood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential gardens and rear yards on Bury Road were inundated with muddy floodwater (documented from a first&ndash;floor window). Floodwater covered garden surfaces and encroached around outbuildings and planters; some properties experienced internal flooding from the river/drain surcharge to depths reported at about 600 to 900 mm. [PAG] (p128)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential and commercial properties on Bury Road were flooded on 26th December 2015. Gardens, fences and outbuildings were submerged and ground&ndash;level access to properties was compromised; floodwaters encroached into properties. Temporary lowering of ground levels associated with nearby stalled development appears to have influenced the route of floodwaters affecting these properties. [IMG,PAG] (p129)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties; 600 to 900 mm [PAG] (p128)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Bury Road, Heywood",England,"Residential gardens and rear yards on Bury Road were inundated with muddy floodwater (documented from a first–floor window). Floodwater covered garden surfaces and encroached around outbuildings and planters; some properties experienced internal flooding from the river/drain surcharge to depths reported at about 600 to 900 mm. [PAG] (p128)
Residential and commercial properties on Bury Road were flooded on 26th December 2015. Gardens, fences and outbuildings were submerged and ground–level access to properties was compromised; floodwaters encroached into properties. Temporary lowering of ground levels associated with nearby stalled development appears to have influenced the route of floodwaters affecting these properties. [IMG,PAG] (p129)",10 properties; 600 to 900 mm [PAG] (p128),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.591291514011445,-2.264647639701207,"<div id=""html_e97d9464dde54950bffb3307f507e960"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bury New Road, Heap Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Bury Road were flooded by floodwaters from the River Roch; ground&ndash;floor areas, gardens and fences were submerged and ground&ndash;level access was compromised. A stalled development with temporarily lowered ground levels appears to have influenced the route of floodwaters affecting these properties. (p129)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Bury New Road, Heap Bridge",England,"Properties on Bury Road were flooded by floodwaters from the River Roch; ground–floor areas, gardens and fences were submerged and ground–level access was compromised. A stalled development with temporarily lowered ground levels appears to have influenced the route of floodwaters affecting these properties. (p129)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.61125654237495,-2.114039788795328,"<div id=""html_e160b03338566f573f39536297338a4f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bridge Street, Milnrow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater seeped through gaps in the river wall adjoining properties on Bridge Street; floodwater flowed onto the street. Flood entered buildings adjacent to the river, including commercial premises via their back walls. (p132)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>external flood depths 200&ndash;900mm (p132)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Bridge Street, Milnrow",England,"Floodwater seeped through gaps in the river wall adjoining properties on Bridge Street; floodwater flowed onto the street. Flood entered buildings adjacent to the river, including commercial premises via their back walls. (p132)",external flood depths 200–900mm (p132),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.4966372,-2.6195968,"<div id=""html_3314daaaebb0e9f3631a3a22da2377ec"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lily Street, Wigan</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed down Lily Street and onto Bridge Street during the river&ndash;surge/surface&ndash;water flooding. (p132)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Lily Street, Wigan",England,Floodwater flowed down Lily Street and onto Bridge Street during the river–surge/surface–water flooding. (p132),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.611800924995954,-2.113130401692502,"<div id=""html_848f602c6829f990a6147d9da9108626"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Place, Milnrow</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as one of the other flooded locations, primarily affected by the River Beal and associated surface water. (p132)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"River Place, Milnrow",England,"Identified as one of the other flooded locations, primarily affected by the River Beal and associated surface water. (p132)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5145173,-2.5715381,"<div id=""html_c772ef156bbd6d8a2e16f5ff776442f9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bickershaw Lane, Hindley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood flow from Hey Brook passed where Bickershaw Lane meets Browns Service Centre, flowed under wagons and deposited silt on the road. (p150)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Bickershaw Lane, Hindley",England,"Flood flow from Hey Brook passed where Bickershaw Lane meets Browns Service Centre, flowed under wagons and deposited silt on the road. (p150)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.533418194144936,-2.5771442596485854,"<div id=""html_df0a386fab7a3fe080e3f4b985b75aff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Platt Lane, Hindley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from Borsdane Brook flowed along Platt Lane as part of the overtopping event, conveying water into adjacent streets and properties. (p161)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Platt Lane, Hindley",England,"Floodwater from Borsdane Brook flowed along Platt Lane as part of the overtopping event, conveying water into adjacent streets and properties. (p161)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5025785,-2.5019739,"<div id=""html_c5d206f3788a72aa68a588267480726d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eden Bank, Lilford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland surface&ndash;water flows passed through properties in Eden Bank, causing property flooding. (p155)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Eden Bank, Lilford",England,"Overland surface–water flows passed through properties in Eden Bank, causing property flooding. (p155)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5027689,-2.5012234,"<div id=""html_84380ca0ec439e9f55a81071f25a5755"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Elmridge, Lilford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the flooding, the Council obtained funding to improve road drainage on Elmridge (lowering the footpath and constructing a speed table) to manage initial surface water run&ndash;off and flood&ndash;basin spills downstream of the culvert under Elmridge. (p156)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Elmridge, Lilford",England,"Following the flooding, the Council obtained funding to improve road drainage on Elmridge (lowering the footpath and constructing a speed table) to manage initial surface water run–off and flood–basin spills downstream of the culvert under Elmridge. (p156)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5710428,-2.5949143,"<div id=""html_05e41b3684e7a797d77ba346041d11ab"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Saint David's Crescent, Haigh</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A culvert collapsed at this location during the flooding event and repairs were carried out. (p156)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"Saint David's Crescent, Haigh",England,A culvert collapsed at this location during the flooding event and repairs were carried out. (p156),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5707236,-2.5919314,"<div id=""html_96f072010a178cfb1961250132c1e81c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Elizabeth's Road, Aspull</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A culvert collapsed at this location during the flooding event and repairs were carried out. (p156)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,"St Elizabeth's Road, Aspull",England,A culvert collapsed at this location during the flooding event and repairs were carried out. (p156),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.47769049472794,-2.2435460795004105,"<div id=""html_5966037a8e9f1f6af0650317a20a0ebb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Greater Manchester</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across Greater Manchester resulted from intense, prolonged rainfall on saturated catchments. The event produced very high river levels (many rivers at their highest recorded levels) and caused significant impacts to communities, businesses, transport infrastructure and the environment. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding on 26 December 2015 that flooded homes and businesses across the area. Addresses of flooded homes and businesses were collected and verified; people claimed the £500 Community Recovery payment; road closures occurred and emergency services recorded call&ndash;outs and responses. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm Eva (Christmas Day and Boxing Day 2015) brought the heaviest rain over Pennine areas north of Manchester; soils were saturated beforehand and close to capacity, producing high runoff and elevated flood risk across the Greater Manchester area during the 25&ndash;26 December 2015 event. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread and rapid flooding occurred across Greater Manchester due to heavy rainfall on saturated ground. Rivers (notably the Irwell, Roch and Glaze) rose rapidly producing deep inundation in places; parts of Bolton, Salford and Rochdale experienced such depths that residents were evacuated by boat. A Major Incident was declared and recovery operations followed as levels receded by Boxing Day evening. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the county on 26 December 2015: many river gauges recorded their highest&ndash;ever levels (Rank 1) and major reaches of the Irwell catchment experienced extreme river levels and flows leading to river flooding of communities and infrastructure. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Greater Manchester area experienced widespread internal property flooding during the event; the report provides an overview map of flooding across the area. (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the metropolitan area caused internal flooding of properties, issued direct flood warnings to residents, and prompted investment and mitigation planning. [IMG,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread river and surface&ndash;water flooding across the Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments: 37 of 44 river&ndash;level gauges recorded their highest&ndash;ever levels. Flooding exceeded defence levels used for infrastructure resilience. Large&ndash;scale electricity supply loss across the conurbation (see local breakdowns); six electrical sub&ndash;stations were flooded or damaged, mobile generators were mobilised. Multiple bridges were damaged or displaced; some required extensive permanent repairs. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the region caused transport disruption (tram services suspended on several routes; major rail services disrupted and route checks required; road closures, diversions and impassable roads), damage to Environment Agency assets and river defences (including destruction/damage of river gauging stations), and flooding at wastewater treatment works and pumping stations which affected treatment processes and recovery operations. Local authority offices in town centres were flooded, affecting servers and slowing recovery coordination. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flood impacts across the county included inundation and operational disruption of wastewater treatment works (Bury flooded; Rochdale and Urmston affected), combined sewer overflow discharges and polluted runoff affecting water quality, mobilisation and deposition of large quantities of gravel and masonry rubble altering channels and increasing local flood or erosion risk, habitat damage and potential spread of invasive species, and local slope failures beside watercourses due to waterlogged ground. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A major incident was declared and a multi&ndash;agency response operated across the area. Rest centres were opened for displaced people. Significant repair works were required to highways, cycle paths, footpaths, river channels, retaining walls and drainage systems. An extensive cleanup operation collected and disposed of waste and debris. Electricity substations were inundated and defences overtopped causing power losses; Distribution Network Operators undertook emergency repairs. United Utilities saw a large increase in wastewater incidents and attended many sites; properties were flooded from sewers. A Recovery Coordinating Group was formed to manage recovery activity. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding from main rivers on 26 December 2015 affected the area; many properties received direct flood warnings and others benefited from defences and operational protection; some parts that flooded did not receive a flood warning and are subject to review. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding on 26 December 2015 affected multiple boroughs; Environment Agency and other RMAs carried out visual inspections of flood defence assets, recorded damage to assets and programmed emergency and minor repairs to walls and embankments in locations including Rochdale and Littleborough. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Largest recorded flood across the area with record high river levels in the Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments; widespread internal property flooding and damage to river channels, retaining walls, culverts and bridges; local wastewater treatment plant performance affected causing effluent discharge to rivers. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Greater Manchester experienced extreme rainfall and widespread flooding on Boxing Day that devastated many communities across the area; flooding mechanisms were complex with the majority caused by main rivers and further properties affected by surface water, ordinary watercourse and groundwater flooding. RMAs have continued work across the area on inspections, repairs and community engagement. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding affected communities across the metropolitan area; risk management authorities (RMAs) and responders worked together in incident response and recovery to support affected communities and have produced a joint flood investigation report with recommendations and next steps. [PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across Greater Manchester on 26th December 2015 that caused damage to infrastructure and local flood defences, flooding of communities across multiple boroughs and prompted multi&ndash;agency recovery, investigations and repair works (including collapsed river retaining walls, damaged channel walls and collapsed lengths of flood defence in specific locations). [TAB,PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding on 26 December 2015 affected communities across Greater Manchester (including Abram, Platt Bridge and Hindley). The event prompted an investigation and led to recommendations and planned actions to improve flood protection&mdash;specifically investigation of improvements at Hindley and consideration of natural measures to slow flows into Borsdane Brook upstream of Hindley&mdash;and coordinated work by Risk Management Authorities to prioritise and take forward measures subject to funding. (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Greater Manchester were affected by the 26 December 2015 floods; within Bury borough (part of Greater Manchester) a total of 804 properties were affected and map data show hotspots of high property impact in central Bury and southern areas near Prestwich and Manchester. [IMG] (p56)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2,200 properties flooded internally; 63 communities; 7 Local Authority districts; tens of thousands of properties disrupted by power loss and road closures (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Multiple gauging stations recorded Rank 1 (highest&ndash;ever) peaks; e.g. Irwell Vale peak flow 177.0 m³/s, Kearsley Ultrasonic peak flow 500.0 m³/s, Manchester Racecourse peak flow 500.0 m³/s (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 2,200 properties flooded internally (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Over 2,200 properties flooded internally; 5,718 properties received a direct flood warning; Over £22 million investment planned from 2015/16 to 2020/21 to protect approximately 6,000 households [IMG,PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>37 of 44 river level gauges; 31,200 properties without power; 6 sub&ndash;stations damaged; 30 mobile generators mobilised; 2 bridges destroyed [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 gauging stations destroyed; 1 gauging station with severe electrical damage (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 5,700 properties received a direct flood warning; United Utilities staff attended over 344 sites; 6 properties reported internal sewer flooding to United Utilities; 45 properties reported external flooding from sewers to United Utilities; Local Authorities reported a further 18 properties flooded internally from sewers [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5,718 properties received a direct flood warning; 2,454 properties benefited from protection (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>nearly 4,000 assets inspected; 24 formal flood defence assets recorded as having some damage; one flood wall completely failed (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>37 of 44 gauges in the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments recorded their highest ever levels; around 2,250 properties flooded internally across the affected area; approximately 2,450 properties were protected by defences; 25 properties reported internal sewer flooding; inspections of approximately 4,000 river assets/structures (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 4,000 assets/structures inspected across the area; Nearly £2 million allocated to repairing structures on main rivers and removing gravel; 36 affected communities visited within three days [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>804 properties; hotspots with 251&ndash;500 properties per 1 km x 1 km grid square (map legend indicated) [IMG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Greater Manchester,England,"Widespread flooding across Greater Manchester resulted from intense, prolonged rainfall on saturated catchments. The event produced very high river levels (many rivers at their highest recorded levels) and caused significant impacts to communities, businesses, transport infrastructure and the environment. (p6)
Widespread flooding on 26 December 2015 that flooded homes and businesses across the area. Addresses of flooded homes and businesses were collected and verified; people claimed the £500 Community Recovery payment; road closures occurred and emergency services recorded call–outs and responses. (p8)
Storm Eva (Christmas Day and Boxing Day 2015) brought the heaviest rain over Pennine areas north of Manchester; soils were saturated beforehand and close to capacity, producing high runoff and elevated flood risk across the Greater Manchester area during the 25–26 December 2015 event. [PAG] (p12)
Widespread and rapid flooding occurred across Greater Manchester due to heavy rainfall on saturated ground. Rivers (notably the Irwell, Roch and Glaze) rose rapidly producing deep inundation in places; parts of Bolton, Salford and Rochdale experienced such depths that residents were evacuated by boat. A Major Incident was declared and recovery operations followed as levels receded by Boxing Day evening. (p13)
Widespread flooding across the county on 26 December 2015: many river gauges recorded their highest–ever levels (Rank 1) and major reaches of the Irwell catchment experienced extreme river levels and flows leading to river flooding of communities and infrastructure. (p15)
The Greater Manchester area experienced widespread internal property flooding during the event; the report provides an overview map of flooding across the area. (p19)
Widespread flooding across the metropolitan area caused internal flooding of properties, issued direct flood warnings to residents, and prompted investment and mitigation planning. [IMG,PAG] (p20)
Widespread river and surface–water flooding across the Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments: 37 of 44 river–level gauges recorded their highest–ever levels. Flooding exceeded defence levels used for infrastructure resilience. Large–scale electricity supply loss across the conurbation (see local breakdowns); six electrical sub–stations were flooded or damaged, mobile generators were mobilised. Multiple bridges were damaged or displaced; some required extensive permanent repairs. [PAG] (p21)
Widespread flooding across the region caused transport disruption (tram services suspended on several routes; major rail services disrupted and route checks required; road closures, diversions and impassable roads), damage to Environment Agency assets and river defences (including destruction/damage of river gauging stations), and flooding at wastewater treatment works and pumping stations which affected treatment processes and recovery operations. Local authority offices in town centres were flooded, affecting servers and slowing recovery coordination. (p22)
Widespread flood impacts across the county included inundation and operational disruption of wastewater treatment works (Bury flooded; Rochdale and Urmston affected), combined sewer overflow discharges and polluted runoff affecting water quality, mobilisation and deposition of large quantities of gravel and masonry rubble altering channels and increasing local flood or erosion risk, habitat damage and potential spread of invasive species, and local slope failures beside watercourses due to waterlogged ground. (p23)
A major incident was declared and a multi–agency response operated across the area. Rest centres were opened for displaced people. Significant repair works were required to highways, cycle paths, footpaths, river channels, retaining walls and drainage systems. An extensive cleanup operation collected and disposed of waste and debris. Electricity substations were inundated and defences overtopped causing power losses; Distribution Network Operators undertook emergency repairs. United Utilities saw a large increase in wastewater incidents and attended many sites; properties were flooded from sewers. A Recovery Coordinating Group was formed to manage recovery activity. [PAG] (p25)
River flooding from main rivers on 26 December 2015 affected the area; many properties received direct flood warnings and others benefited from defences and operational protection; some parts that flooded did not receive a flood warning and are subject to review. (p26)
River flooding on 26 December 2015 affected multiple boroughs; Environment Agency and other RMAs carried out visual inspections of flood defence assets, recorded damage to assets and programmed emergency and minor repairs to walls and embankments in locations including Rochdale and Littleborough. (p27)
Largest recorded flood across the area with record high river levels in the Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments; widespread internal property flooding and damage to river channels, retaining walls, culverts and bridges; local wastewater treatment plant performance affected causing effluent discharge to rivers. (p29)
Greater Manchester experienced extreme rainfall and widespread flooding on Boxing Day that devastated many communities across the area; flooding mechanisms were complex with the majority caused by main rivers and further properties affected by surface water, ordinary watercourse and groundwater flooding. RMAs have continued work across the area on inspections, repairs and community engagement. [PAG] (p30)
Widespread flooding affected communities across the metropolitan area; risk management authorities (RMAs) and responders worked together in incident response and recovery to support affected communities and have produced a joint flood investigation report with recommendations and next steps. [PAG] (p31)
Widespread flooding across Greater Manchester on 26th December 2015 that caused damage to infrastructure and local flood defences, flooding of communities across multiple boroughs and prompted multi–agency recovery, investigations and repair works (including collapsed river retaining walls, damaged channel walls and collapsed lengths of flood defence in specific locations). [TAB,PAG] (p32)
Widespread flooding on 26 December 2015 affected communities across Greater Manchester (including Abram, Platt Bridge and Hindley). The event prompted an investigation and led to recommendations and planned actions to improve flood protection—specifically investigation of improvements at Hindley and consideration of natural measures to slow flows into Borsdane Brook upstream of Hindley—and coordinated work by Risk Management Authorities to prioritise and take forward measures subject to funding. (p37)
Parts of Greater Manchester were affected by the 26 December 2015 floods; within Bury borough (part of Greater Manchester) a total of 804 properties were affected and map data show hotspots of high property impact in central Bury and southern areas near Prestwich and Manchester. [IMG] (p56)","2,200 properties flooded internally; 63 communities; 7 Local Authority districts; tens of thousands of properties disrupted by power loss and road closures (p6)
Multiple gauging stations recorded Rank 1 (highest–ever) peaks; e.g. Irwell Vale peak flow 177.0 m³/s, Kearsley Ultrasonic peak flow 500.0 m³/s, Manchester Racecourse peak flow 500.0 m³/s (p15)
over 2,200 properties flooded internally (p19)
Over 2,200 properties flooded internally; 5,718 properties received a direct flood warning; Over £22 million investment planned from 2015/16 to 2020/21 to protect approximately 6,000 households [IMG,PAG] (p20)
37 of 44 river level gauges; 31,200 properties without power; 6 sub–stations damaged; 30 mobile generators mobilised; 2 bridges destroyed [PAG] (p21)
2 gauging stations destroyed; 1 gauging station with severe electrical damage (p23)
over 5,700 properties received a direct flood warning; United Utilities staff attended over 344 sites; 6 properties reported internal sewer flooding to United Utilities; 45 properties reported external flooding from sewers to United Utilities; Local Authorities reported a further 18 properties flooded internally from sewers [PAG] (p25)
5,718 properties received a direct flood warning; 2,454 properties benefited from protection (p26)
nearly 4,000 assets inspected; 24 formal flood defence assets recorded as having some damage; one flood wall completely failed (p27)
37 of 44 gauges in the River Irwell, Roch and Croal catchments recorded their highest ever levels; around 2,250 properties flooded internally across the affected area; approximately 2,450 properties were protected by defences; 25 properties reported internal sewer flooding; inspections of approximately 4,000 river assets/structures (p29)
approximately 4,000 assets/structures inspected across the area; Nearly £2 million allocated to repairing structures on main rivers and removing gravel; 36 affected communities visited within three days [PAG] (p30)
804 properties; hotspots with 251–500 properties per 1 km x 1 km grid square (map legend indicated) [IMG] (p56)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5737088,-2.492149,"<div id=""html_0e5f9de69d823c6c6c0e8cfd47bc384c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lostock Junction</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The largest cluster of flooded properties is indicated at the central river crossing and junction (Lostock Junction). [IMG] (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Lostock Junction,England,The largest cluster of flooded properties is indicated at the central river crossing and junction (Lostock Junction). [IMG] (p46),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.55622665353062,-2.326993826757757,"<div id=""html_1b986746e176cf730294623e9b4bac73"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lomax Street Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bridge was completely removed as part of repair work following flood damage; works have included complete removal and subsequent reconstruction/repairs with temporary reinstatement of utilities. (p59)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Lomax Street Bridge,England,Bridge was completely removed as part of repair work following flood damage; works have included complete removal and subsequent reconstruction/repairs with temporary reinstatement of utilities. (p59),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.4905944,-2.2594672,"<div id=""html_054fc45469479f09ba3225029982d18a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Broughton Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Broughton (including the Broughton Bridge area) were within the larger Salford&ndash;affected flood zone west of the main river, with properties shown flooded in that area. [IMG] (p82)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Broughton Bridge,England,"Parts of Broughton (including the Broughton Bridge area) were within the larger Salford–affected flood zone west of the main river, with properties shown flooded in that area. [IMG] (p82)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.4900331221043,-2.26831654276958,"<div id=""html_a4b09f3f23a12d7c6f14ff951c0586c6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hough Lane Footbridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Debris trapped against the footbridge increased the obstruction to flow during the event, which amplified upstream water levels and contributed to flooding at the nearby Broughton location. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Left&ndash;bank flood defences were overtopped between Wallness Bridge and Hough Lane Bridge, producing three distinct flow routes into Lower Broughton that led to widespread internal flooding of properties and associated access/power impacts. (p139)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Hough Lane Footbridge,England,"Debris trapped against the footbridge increased the obstruction to flow during the event, which amplified upstream water levels and contributed to flooding at the nearby Broughton location. (p23)
Left–bank flood defences were overtopped between Wallness Bridge and Hough Lane Bridge, producing three distinct flow routes into Lower Broughton that led to widespread internal flooding of properties and associated access/power impacts. (p139)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.3674704,-2.612316,"<div id=""html_86ebe70cafa34308c6745b3a77b9d202"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Manchester Ship Canal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the flooding the canal bank was eroded adjacent to a COMAH facility downstream of Mode Wheel locks in Salford; the site operator emptied oil tanks closest to the canal as a precaution against potential environmental release if undermined. Hydraulic modelling indicated upstream flood levels at nearby flooded locations were independent of canal levels. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Manchester Ship Canal,England,During the flooding the canal bank was eroded adjacent to a COMAH facility downstream of Mode Wheel locks in Salford; the site operator emptied oil tanks closest to the canal as a precaution against potential environmental release if undermined. Hydraulic modelling indicated upstream flood levels at nearby flooded locations were independent of canal levels. (p23),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5439356,-2.3587436,"<div id=""html_784ae75982f0bbfaa22ea84249059e43"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ringley Old Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sediment build&ndash;up and scour around the bridge: sand on the banks upstream/downstream and gravel from the island downstream were identified for movement/use to fill in scour on the right bank (looking downstream); modelling and potential gravel removal were planned to manage flow and reduce flood risk at the bridge. (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Ringley Old Bridge,England,Sediment build–up and scour around the bridge: sand on the banks upstream/downstream and gravel from the island downstream were identified for movement/use to fill in scour on the right bank (looking downstream); modelling and potential gravel removal were planned to manage flow and reduce flood risk at the bridge. (p45),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
