GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5967685,-2.2239715,"<div id=""html_aa07894d417adc06b15743491124a602"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Heywood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties in Heywood at specific bridge locations caused by main river flooding. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of Heywood (Hooley Bridge area) experienced river flooding from the River Roch, affecting properties including an industrial estate. [IMG] (p133)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties at Heap Bridge; 20 properties at Hooley Bridge (30 properties total) [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>20 properties [IMG] (p133)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Heywood,England,"Internal flooding to properties in Heywood at specific bridge locations caused by main river flooding. [TAB] (p17)
Part of Heywood (Hooley Bridge area) experienced river flooding from the River Roch, affecting properties including an industrial estate. [IMG] (p133)","10 properties at Heap Bridge; 20 properties at Hooley Bridge (30 properties total) [TAB] (p17)
20 properties [IMG] (p133)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.51240900654041,-2.281745077373086,"<div id=""html_7ae4bfc46667e9f08464e1c47d59fc75"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Broughton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive internal flooding to properties caused by main river flooding in the Lower Broughton area of Salford. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The area was listed among locations with significant flooding during the 26 December 2015 event; streets and properties were flooded and local authorities responded to incidents in the area. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties flooded internally as part of the December 2015 flooding; area included in the ~750 properties flooded across Lower Kersal, Lower Broughton and Cheetham Hill. Flood storage works were under construction to reduce future risk (protecting up to 1,900 homes/businesses). [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Environment Agency river gauging station at Lower Broughton suffered severe electrical damage, degrading the station that provides data used to trigger flood warnings for nearby properties. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Irwell overtopped raised defences and natural banks and high rainfall exceeded the capacity of the surface water network, causing properties in the Lower Broughton area to flood. Flooded properties are mapped on both the Salford (majority, west of the river) and Manchester (smaller contiguous zone east of the river) sides of the river. [IMG] (p138)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater entered the area via three flow routes after River Irwell overtopped, causing internal flooding of residential properties (including silt deposition inside homes), widespread disruption to access and power, and significant property damage and displacement. [IMG] (p139)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding deposited silt across the floor of at least one dwelling, disrupting living conditions. Streets in the area were inundated, with at least one vehicle becoming stuck and requiring multiple responders to push it free. [IMG] (p141)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered residential streets in the area (e.g., Lyra Place), impeding passage and requiring residents to wade through water; vehicles were partially submerged and residents were displaced/evacuated from properties. [IMG] (p143)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area experienced flooding prompting a separate detailed investigation by Salford City Council; United Utilities received reports of sewer flooding to external grounds in the Lower Broughton area. (p145)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>750 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Approximately 750 properties (across Lower Kersal, Lower Broughton and Cheetham Hill) [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>around 750 properties; 164 properties [IMG] (p139)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (p145)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Lower Broughton,England,"Extensive internal flooding to properties caused by main river flooding in the Lower Broughton area of Salford. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
The area was listed among locations with significant flooding during the 26 December 2015 event; streets and properties were flooded and local authorities responded to incidents in the area. (p18)
Properties flooded internally as part of the December 2015 flooding; area included in the ~750 properties flooded across Lower Kersal, Lower Broughton and Cheetham Hill. Flood storage works were under construction to reduce future risk (protecting up to 1,900 homes/businesses). [IMG] (p20)
Environment Agency river gauging station at Lower Broughton suffered severe electrical damage, degrading the station that provides data used to trigger flood warnings for nearby properties. (p22)
The River Irwell overtopped raised defences and natural banks and high rainfall exceeded the capacity of the surface water network, causing properties in the Lower Broughton area to flood. Flooded properties are mapped on both the Salford (majority, west of the river) and Manchester (smaller contiguous zone east of the river) sides of the river. [IMG] (p138)
Floodwater entered the area via three flow routes after River Irwell overtopped, causing internal flooding of residential properties (including silt deposition inside homes), widespread disruption to access and power, and significant property damage and displacement. [IMG] (p139)
Internal property flooding deposited silt across the floor of at least one dwelling, disrupting living conditions. Streets in the area were inundated, with at least one vehicle becoming stuck and requiring multiple responders to push it free. [IMG] (p141)
Floodwater covered residential streets in the area (e.g., Lyra Place), impeding passage and requiring residents to wade through water; vehicles were partially submerged and residents were displaced/evacuated from properties. [IMG] (p143)
Area experienced flooding prompting a separate detailed investigation by Salford City Council; United Utilities received reports of sewer flooding to external grounds in the Lower Broughton area. (p145)","750 properties [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Approximately 750 properties (across Lower Kersal, Lower Broughton and Cheetham Hill) [IMG] (p20)
around 750 properties; 164 properties [IMG] (p139)
2 properties (p145)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
