5 properties flooded; 2 properties reported as flooded from sewers (p162)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.54049790605651,-2.7070243056410797,"<div id=""html_c10db8c74828304a9dbe0a85418a1ea3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Orrell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties caused by sewer surcharge and surface water inundation. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (1 by sewer; 1 by surface water) [TAB] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Orrell,England,Internal flooding to properties caused by sewer surcharge and surface water inundation. [TAB] (p17),2 properties (1 by sewer; 1 by surface water) [TAB] (p17),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5293083,-2.6487934,"<div id=""html_7265fa04f5a2d3ae01a71207073da811"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Worsley Mesnes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal (ground&ndash;floor) flooding of properties caused by sewer surcharge and surface water runoff. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties due to sewer flooding in Worsley Mesnes. (p146)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties caused by sewer flooding (sewer failure or surcharge identified as a factor). [PAG] (p147)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties flooded: surface water flooding affected most properties and sewer flooding affected one property. Surface water flooding was compounded by high water levels in Smithy Brook (a tributary of the River Douglas). Flooded properties lie within Flood Zone 1. [IMG] (p158)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties flooded (1 from sewer flooding; 8 from surface water flooding) [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties (p146)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties [PAG] (p147)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties; 8 properties from surface water; 1 property from sewers [IMG] (p158)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Worsley Mesnes,England,"Internal (ground–floor) flooding of properties caused by sewer surcharge and surface water runoff. [TAB] (p17)
Internal flooding of properties due to sewer flooding in Worsley Mesnes. (p146)
Internal flooding of residential properties caused by sewer flooding (sewer failure or surcharge identified as a factor). [PAG] (p147)
Residential properties flooded: surface water flooding affected most properties and sewer flooding affected one property. Surface water flooding was compounded by high water levels in Smithy Brook (a tributary of the River Douglas). Flooded properties lie within Flood Zone 1. [IMG] (p158)","9 properties flooded (1 from sewer flooding; 8 from surface water flooding) [TAB] (p17)
9 properties (p146)
9 properties [PAG] (p147)
9 properties; 8 properties from surface water; 1 property from sewers [IMG] (p158)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5683549,-2.026164,"<div id=""html_fded03b75fbc48ff6e833670d2bace26"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Delph</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 26th December 2015 Delph was identified as an exception within the Mersey catchment where flooding did occur despite generally modest rainfall across the Mersey catchment. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties from main river, ordinary watercourse and surface water. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Delph and Salford experienced power outages caused by flooding; the report gives local property counts and the overall total for Greater Manchester outages. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Delph was one of the worst affected locations; 42 properties were internally flooded. River Tame at Delph rose in response to the rainfall. [PAG] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Tame at Delph rose sharply in response to intense rainfall on 26&ndash;27 December 2015; river level exceeded the bankful threshold and approached the flood threshold, producing a pronounced peak in river levels and elevated flood risk along the channel. [IMG] (p89)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Clusters of reported property flooding caused by the main river, ordinary watercourses and surface water. The largest main&ndash;river cluster southeast of Delph corresponded to 49&ndash;60 properties affected; additional main&ndash;river clusters of 25&ndash;36, 13&ndash;24 and 1&ndash;12 properties are shown along the river corridor. Ordinary watercourse flooding included a cluster of up to 4 properties near the southeast of Delph. Surface&ndash;water clusters include one of 8&ndash;9 properties northwest of Delph and smaller scattered clusters. Map points indicate clusters of reported property flooding (not exact property outlines). [IMG] (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding and local infrastructure impacts caused by the River Tame overtopping and by surcharging ordinary watercourses and surface water. Flooding was exacerbated where a bridge on High Street restricted flow, and where a culvert under industrial units on Delph New Road became restricted causing backup onto the road. Hull Brook also overtopped its banks causing garden flooding. Surface water from over&ndash;capacity road gullies affected the area. [IMG,PAG] (p92)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the confluence of Hull Brook and the River Tame inundated parts of Delph, with floodwaters surrounding buildings and industrial units; properties adjacent to the watercourse were threatened or flooded. [IMG,PAG] (p94)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village experienced significant flooding on 26 December 2015 with streets inundated (High Street/King Street), bridge/channel overtopping with debris marking the extent, and at least one property (Green Ash) suffering internal water ingress. [IMG,PAG] (p95)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Tame affected the centre of Delph (High Street / King Street toward Brookside Terrace); properties lying in Flood Zone 3 were flooded from the main river. [PAG] (p96)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>950 properties without power in Delph; 250 properties without power in Salford; 31,200 properties without power (Greater Manchester total) (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties [PAG] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>exceeded bankful (1.000 m) [IMG] (p89)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>49&ndash;60 properties; 25&ndash;36 properties; 13&ndash;24 properties; 1&ndash;12 properties; 4 properties; 8&ndash;9 properties [IMG] (p91)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>42 properties; 7 businesses; 35 residential properties; 16 properties flooded from the River Tame; 9 properties flooded from ordinary watercourses; 17 properties flooded due to surface water [IMG,PAG] (p92)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Delph,England,"On 26th December 2015 Delph was identified as an exception within the Mersey catchment where flooding did occur despite generally modest rainfall across the Mersey catchment. (p14)
Internal flooding to properties from main river, ordinary watercourse and surface water. [TAB] (p17)
Delph and Salford experienced power outages caused by flooding; the report gives local property counts and the overall total for Greater Manchester outages. (p21)
Delph was one of the worst affected locations; 42 properties were internally flooded. River Tame at Delph rose in response to the rainfall. [PAG] (p88)
The River Tame at Delph rose sharply in response to intense rainfall on 26–27 December 2015; river level exceeded the bankful threshold and approached the flood threshold, producing a pronounced peak in river levels and elevated flood risk along the channel. [IMG] (p89)
Clusters of reported property flooding caused by the main river, ordinary watercourses and surface water. The largest main–river cluster southeast of Delph corresponded to 49–60 properties affected; additional main–river clusters of 25–36, 13–24 and 1–12 properties are shown along the river corridor. Ordinary watercourse flooding included a cluster of up to 4 properties near the southeast of Delph. Surface–water clusters include one of 8–9 properties northwest of Delph and smaller scattered clusters. Map points indicate clusters of reported property flooding (not exact property outlines). [IMG] (p91)
Internal property flooding and local infrastructure impacts caused by the River Tame overtopping and by surcharging ordinary watercourses and surface water. Flooding was exacerbated where a bridge on High Street restricted flow, and where a culvert under industrial units on Delph New Road became restricted causing backup onto the road. Hull Brook also overtopped its banks causing garden flooding. Surface water from over–capacity road gullies affected the area. [IMG,PAG] (p92)
Flooding at the confluence of Hull Brook and the River Tame inundated parts of Delph, with floodwaters surrounding buildings and industrial units; properties adjacent to the watercourse were threatened or flooded. [IMG,PAG] (p94)
The village experienced significant flooding on 26 December 2015 with streets inundated (High Street/King Street), bridge/channel overtopping with debris marking the extent, and at least one property (Green Ash) suffering internal water ingress. [IMG,PAG] (p95)
Flooding from the River Tame affected the centre of Delph (High Street / King Street toward Brookside Terrace); properties lying in Flood Zone 3 were flooded from the main river. [PAG] (p96)","42 properties [TAB] (p17)
950 properties without power in Delph; 250 properties without power in Salford; 31,200 properties without power (Greater Manchester total) (p21)
42 properties [PAG] (p88)
exceeded bankful (1.000 m) [IMG] (p89)
49–60 properties; 25–36 properties; 13–24 properties; 1–12 properties; 4 properties; 8–9 properties [IMG] (p91)
42 properties; 7 businesses; 35 residential properties; 16 properties flooded from the River Tame; 9 properties flooded from ordinary watercourses; 17 properties flooded due to surface water [IMG,PAG] (p92)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5566119,-2.0165484,"<div id=""html_0dc35fe7c8e746f772d4797ab83a24bb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dobcross</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties caused by main river flooding. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Dobcross,England,Internal flooding to properties caused by main river flooding. [TAB] (p17),1 property [TAB] (p17),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
