GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.559338,-2.3261547,"<div id=""html_a9792cdf7ce71dbe9c8a329906d8bc41"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Radcliffe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main&ndash;river flooding resulted in internal flooding to properties in Radcliffe/Redvales. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the Radcliffe area, inundating domestic properties; a scheme has been proposed to assess options for reducing flood risk in the area. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Radcliffe experienced flood damage to electrical infrastructure and significant power outages: two sub&ndash;stations were damaged by floodwater and thousands of properties lost supply. [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused the collapse of a footbridge that carried a sewer and a gas main; the collapse ruptured the gas main and led to a gas&ndash;related incident. Gas supply was affected (low pressure) to many domestic properties and some non&ndash;domestic supplies were isolated. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Radcliffe (together with Redvales) was heavily affected by the Boxing Day flood, with many properties flooded and homes evacuated. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Radcliffe (with Redvales) was the most severely affected community in the borough: about 671 properties were flooded by the River Irwell. Maximum flood depth above ground level reached 1.4m, with typical depths of 0.6&ndash;0.8m. [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused the collapse of a footbridge carrying a sewer and gas main at Lomax Street; the gas main ruptured causing a fire and explosion and sewage was discharged into the River Irwell. Temporary measures by Bury Council later reopened the bridge to pedestrian traffic. [PAG] (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding affected residential and commercial properties and highways; footbridge failures (including Lomax Street and Milltown Street); treated sewage discharged into the River Irwell from flooded wastewater works; major disruption to services including loss of electricity supply at substations. [PAG] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flooding that affected residents and businesses (clusters of reported flooding); a flood extent outline was mapped, prompted a community drop&ndash;in and multi&ndash;agency working group, and led to proposals and a business case being developed for a flood defence and storage scheme. (p59)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding from the River Irwell and surface&ndash;water runoff affected Radcliffe: properties along Warth Road/Radcliffe Road, York Street/Dumers Lane, Morris Street and Riverside Drive were inundated; a footbridge at Lomax Street carrying a sewer and gas main collapsed and discharged sewage into the river; a further footbridge at Milltown Street was damaged beyond repair; businesses in the area were significantly impacted with some relocating outside the borough. [PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Radcliffe experienced overbank flooding with general flood depths around 0.6 to 0.8m; infrastructure was affected (a pipe bridge in Radcliffe is being replaced and a temporary pipe route installed); properties in the area lie in Flood Zone 2 and Flood Zone 3 and were among those flooded. (p63)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell overtopped and caused flooding affecting the Radcliffe area; the event prompted the Environment Agency to develop proposals for a flood defence scheme for the Radcliffe and Redvales area and to review flood warning thresholds for the River Irwell. (p66)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>670 properties in Radcliffe and Redvales combined [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Over 670 properties flooded [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3,200 properties without power; 2 sub&ndash;stations damaged [TAB] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>900 domestic properties; 2 non&ndash;domestic supplies (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>670 properties (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>671 properties [IMG,PAG] (p56)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3,200 properties [PAG] (p58)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>671 properties flooded; 900 domestic properties affected by low water pressure [PAG] (p61)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>0.6 to 0.8m flood depth (p63)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Radcliffe,England,"Main–river flooding resulted in internal flooding to properties in Radcliffe/Redvales. [TAB] (p17)
Flooding affected the Radcliffe area, inundating domestic properties; a scheme has been proposed to assess options for reducing flood risk in the area. [IMG] (p20)
Radcliffe experienced flood damage to electrical infrastructure and significant power outages: two sub–stations were damaged by floodwater and thousands of properties lost supply. [TAB] (p21)
Flooding caused the collapse of a footbridge that carried a sewer and a gas main; the collapse ruptured the gas main and led to a gas–related incident. Gas supply was affected (low pressure) to many domestic properties and some non–domestic supplies were isolated. (p22)
Radcliffe (together with Redvales) was heavily affected by the Boxing Day flood, with many properties flooded and homes evacuated. (p29)
Radcliffe (with Redvales) was the most severely affected community in the borough: about 671 properties were flooded by the River Irwell. Maximum flood depth above ground level reached 1.4m, with typical depths of 0.6–0.8m. [IMG,PAG] (p56)
Flooding caused the collapse of a footbridge carrying a sewer and gas main at Lomax Street; the gas main ruptured causing a fire and explosion and sewage was discharged into the River Irwell. Temporary measures by Bury Council later reopened the bridge to pedestrian traffic. [PAG] (p57)
Widespread flooding affected residential and commercial properties and highways; footbridge failures (including Lomax Street and Milltown Street); treated sewage discharged into the River Irwell from flooded wastewater works; major disruption to services including loss of electricity supply at substations. [PAG] (p58)
Experienced flooding that affected residents and businesses (clusters of reported flooding); a flood extent outline was mapped, prompted a community drop–in and multi–agency working group, and led to proposals and a business case being developed for a flood defence and storage scheme. (p59)
Widespread flooding from the River Irwell and surface–water runoff affected Radcliffe: properties along Warth Road/Radcliffe Road, York Street/Dumers Lane, Morris Street and Riverside Drive were inundated; a footbridge at Lomax Street carrying a sewer and gas main collapsed and discharged sewage into the river; a further footbridge at Milltown Street was damaged beyond repair; businesses in the area were significantly impacted with some relocating outside the borough. [PAG] (p61)
Radcliffe experienced overbank flooding with general flood depths around 0.6 to 0.8m; infrastructure was affected (a pipe bridge in Radcliffe is being replaced and a temporary pipe route installed); properties in the area lie in Flood Zone 2 and Flood Zone 3 and were among those flooded. (p63)
River Irwell overtopped and caused flooding affecting the Radcliffe area; the event prompted the Environment Agency to develop proposals for a flood defence scheme for the Radcliffe and Redvales area and to review flood warning thresholds for the River Irwell. (p66)","670 properties in Radcliffe and Redvales combined [TAB] (p17)
Over 670 properties flooded [IMG] (p20)
3,200 properties without power; 2 sub–stations damaged [TAB] (p21)
900 domestic properties; 2 non–domestic supplies (p22)
670 properties (p29)
671 properties [IMG,PAG] (p56)
3,200 properties [PAG] (p58)
671 properties flooded; 900 domestic properties affected by low water pressure [PAG] (p61)
0.6 to 0.8m flood depth (p63)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5830028,-2.3845554,"<div id=""html_98f253b37783c14455cf1cfb9e9feda0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Breightmet</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties caused by surface&ndash;water flooding. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ramsbottom and Breightmet are listed as affected by the 26 December 2015 floods; a River Irwell gauge at Ramsbottom reached 3.39m, a 10&ndash;year record high. (p56)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties; 4 properties [TAB] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Breightmet,England,"Internal flooding to properties caused by surface–water flooding. [TAB] (p17)
Ramsbottom and Breightmet are listed as affected by the 26 December 2015 floods; a River Irwell gauge at Ramsbottom reached 3.39m, a 10–year record high. (p56)",3 properties; 4 properties [TAB] (p17),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5617528,-2.4213409,"<div id=""html_f104810e25e7509b1f1dba0e95657b10"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Great Lever</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties caused by surface&ndash;water flooding. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [TAB] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Great Lever,England,Internal flooding to properties caused by surface–water flooding. [TAB] (p17),3 properties [TAB] (p17),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5727921,-2.494764,"<div id=""html_f3fda1d627b212aaeae27af1458c931e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lostock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties caused by main river flooding. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flooding cluster occurred at Lostock associated with Bessy Brook, causing internal flooding of properties in the area. [PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bessy Brook gauge at Lostock recorded a record high level of 1.44m during the event, indicating river&ndash;level flooding at Lostock. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Clusters of properties in the Lostock area were flooded, with impacts concentrated along the main river and canal corridors. [IMG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from Bessy Brook occurred when the long culvert beneath Sudbury Drive / Ashridge Close gardens, Regent Road and the railway embankment was overwhelmed. Debris at the culvert inlet exacerbated the flooding; properties were inundated above ground level (within Flood Zone 3). [MUL] (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.44m river level (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties; 16 properties (2002); 1 property (2007) [MUL] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Lostock,England,"Internal flooding to properties caused by main river flooding. [TAB] (p17)
A flooding cluster occurred at Lostock associated with Bessy Brook, causing internal flooding of properties in the area. [PAG] (p41)
