GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5475135,-2.0066494,"<div id=""html_002b80e62e4772d414974e8ead352fe9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Uppermill</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 26th December 2015 Uppermill at Saddleworth experienced flooding (called out as an exception in the Mersey catchment), with river levels rising sufficiently to cause flood impacts in that locality. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Uppermill experienced river flooding on 26 December 2015; the gauging station on the Tame recorded its highest&ndash;ever level (Rank 1) during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties from multiple sources including main river flooding and surface water, affecting the Uppermill area. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Uppermill experienced significant flooding with 35 properties internally flooded; river levels at Uppermill rose in response to the rainfall. [PAG] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Tame at Uppermill rose to a record level during the event: 1.242 m (156.79 m AOD), the highest reading in the station's 17&ndash;year record, with the peak driven by intense rainfall on and immediately after 26 December 2015. [IMG] (p89)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The EA gauge at Uppermill (downstream of Delph) recorded a river level of 1.24 m (156.79 AOD), the highest in its 17 years of recording; this level is assessed as having a 1 in 50 to 1 in 100 year likelihood and is associated with flooding of properties from the main river (FZ3). (p96)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Tame inundated the centre of Uppermill on 26 December 2015; map shows clustered main&ndash;river flood symbols in the village centre and along the river. United Utilities received three incidents of sewer flooding reported in the area on Boxing Day 2015. The Environment Agency led investigations into the river&ndash;sourced flooding. [IMG] (p97)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Riverside parts of Uppermill experienced significant flooding during the event; residential properties in the village had ground&ndash;floor inundation from the overflowing river. [IMG,PAG] (p100)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Uppermill experienced residential and street flooding (e.g. School Street and High Street) with water at doorsteps and ground&ndash;floor levels causing property water ingress and local disruption. [IMG,PAG] (p101)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The locality experienced extreme river levels and associated flooding that inundated streets and properties; infrastructure damage occurred including the collapse of a culvert on Pickhill Brook, prompting repair works and a replacement culvert scheme. [IMG,PAG] (p102)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak river gauge 1.242 m; peak flow 28.7 m³/s; annual probability indicated ≈ 1 in 100 years [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35 properties (12 main river; 2 ordinary watercourse; 21 surface water) [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>35 properties [PAG] (p88)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.242 m river level (156.79 m AOD); highest in 17 years [IMG] (p89)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.24 m (156.79 AOD); highest reading in 17 years; 1 in 50 to 1 in 100 year likelihood (p96)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 incidents of sewer flooding [IMG] (p97)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>river level 1.24m (highest reading in 18 years) [IMG,PAG] (p102)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Uppermill,England,"On 26th December 2015 Uppermill at Saddleworth experienced flooding (called out as an exception in the Mersey catchment), with river levels rising sufficiently to cause flood impacts in that locality. (p14)
Uppermill experienced river flooding on 26 December 2015; the gauging station on the Tame recorded its highest–ever level (Rank 1) during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p15)
Internal flooding to properties from multiple sources including main river flooding and surface water, affecting the Uppermill area. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Uppermill experienced significant flooding with 35 properties internally flooded; river levels at Uppermill rose in response to the rainfall. [PAG] (p88)
The River Tame at Uppermill rose to a record level during the event: 1.242 m (156.79 m AOD), the highest reading in the station's 17–year record, with the peak driven by intense rainfall on and immediately after 26 December 2015. [IMG] (p89)
The EA gauge at Uppermill (downstream of Delph) recorded a river level of 1.24 m (156.79 AOD), the highest in its 17 years of recording; this level is assessed as having a 1 in 50 to 1 in 100 year likelihood and is associated with flooding of properties from the main river (FZ3). (p96)
Flooding from the River Tame inundated the centre of Uppermill on 26 December 2015; map shows clustered main–river flood symbols in the village centre and along the river. United Utilities received three incidents of sewer flooding reported in the area on Boxing Day 2015. The Environment Agency led investigations into the river–sourced flooding. [IMG] (p97)
Riverside parts of Uppermill experienced significant flooding during the event; residential properties in the village had ground–floor inundation from the overflowing river. [IMG,PAG] (p100)
Parts of Uppermill experienced residential and street flooding (e.g. School Street and High Street) with water at doorsteps and ground–floor levels causing property water ingress and local disruption. [IMG,PAG] (p101)
The locality experienced extreme river levels and associated flooding that inundated streets and properties; infrastructure damage occurred including the collapse of a culvert on Pickhill Brook, prompting repair works and a replacement culvert scheme. [IMG,PAG] (p102)","Peak river gauge 1.242 m; peak flow 28.7 m³/s; annual probability indicated ≈ 1 in 100 years [TAB,PAG] (p15)
