GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.644282090435844,-2.094774727055187,"<div id=""html_de1555fab026a49159f620b1ab084091"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Littleborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main&ndash;river flooding caused internal flooding to properties in Littleborough (reported as part of the Rochdale borough impacts). (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the Littleborough and Rochdale area were flooded; a scheme was proposed to assess options for reducing flood risk. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused power outages in both Bolton and Littleborough and damaged at least one electrical sub&ndash;station in Littleborough. Temporary rerouting and overhead lines were used to restore supply where bridges carrying cables were affected. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the town; the Calder Brook flood basin prevented significant further property flooding. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties from rising rivers and also from surface water, groundwater seepage into cellars and sewer&ndash;related flooding. Flood depths reported up to 1.2 m in habitable spaces. [PAG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Littleborough was affected by the Boxing Day (26th December 2015) flooding. Community resilience work established prior to the event (Flood Resilience Community Pathfinder) and ongoing partnership with the National Flood Forum provided support after the floods; multi&ndash;agency drop&ndash;in sessions and ongoing support were delivered to those affected. (p115)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Main&ndash;river flooding from the River Roch and Greenvale Brook overtopped raised defences and natural banks across Littleborough. Flood depths were increased by surface water and flows from the Rochdale Canal and by a fully saturated catchment. Widespread property flooding above ground floor level, emergency response and community disruption occurred; a flood warning was issued. [PAG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Roch at Littleborough reached record&ndash;high levels causing overbanking; high river levels prevented surface water drainage leading to ponding and property flooding. A flood warning was issued and emergency measures (including sandbagging) were used. [IMG,PAG] (p118)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dramatic surface flows were observed down moorland above Littleborough during the intense rainfall, contributing to local flooding impacts in the area. (p123)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dramatic surface&ndash;water flows were recorded down the moorland above Littleborough during the 26 December 2015 event, producing significant runoff into the Upper Roch catchment and contributing to very high flows in the River Roch. (p126)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>175 properties (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>275 properties flooded across Littleborough and Rochdale [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1,000 properties without power in Bolton; 1,000 properties without power in Littleborough; 1 sub&ndash;station damaged in Littleborough (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>175 properties (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>174 properties (up to 1.2m deep) [PAG] (p114)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>158 properties flooded above ground floor level; 17 properties flooded from surface water; flood depths up to 1.2m at Garden Mews; flood warning issued to over 690 properties [PAG] (p117)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 690 properties [IMG,PAG] (p118)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Littleborough,England,"Main–river flooding caused internal flooding to properties in Littleborough (reported as part of the Rochdale borough impacts). (p17)
Properties in the Littleborough and Rochdale area were flooded; a scheme was proposed to assess options for reducing flood risk. [IMG] (p20)
Flooding caused power outages in both Bolton and Littleborough and damaged at least one electrical sub–station in Littleborough. Temporary rerouting and overhead lines were used to restore supply where bridges carrying cables were affected. (p21)
Flooding affected the town; the Calder Brook flood basin prevented significant further property flooding. (p29)
Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties from rising rivers and also from surface water, groundwater seepage into cellars and sewer–related flooding. Flood depths reported up to 1.2 m in habitable spaces. [PAG] (p114)
Littleborough was affected by the Boxing Day (26th December 2015) flooding. Community resilience work established prior to the event (Flood Resilience Community Pathfinder) and ongoing partnership with the National Flood Forum provided support after the floods; multi–agency drop–in sessions and ongoing support were delivered to those affected. (p115)
Main–river flooding from the River Roch and Greenvale Brook overtopped raised defences and natural banks across Littleborough. Flood depths were increased by surface water and flows from the Rochdale Canal and by a fully saturated catchment. Widespread property flooding above ground floor level, emergency response and community disruption occurred; a flood warning was issued. [PAG] (p117)
River Roch at Littleborough reached record–high levels causing overbanking; high river levels prevented surface water drainage leading to ponding and property flooding. A flood warning was issued and emergency measures (including sandbagging) were used. [IMG,PAG] (p118)
Dramatic surface flows were observed down moorland above Littleborough during the intense rainfall, contributing to local flooding impacts in the area. (p123)
Dramatic surface–water flows were recorded down the moorland above Littleborough during the 26 December 2015 event, producing significant runoff into the Upper Roch catchment and contributing to very high flows in the River Roch. (p126)","175 properties (p17)
275 properties flooded across Littleborough and Rochdale [IMG] (p20)
1,000 properties without power in Bolton; 1,000 properties without power in Littleborough; 1 sub–station damaged in Littleborough (p21)
175 properties (p29)
