GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5401383,-2.3779588,"<div id=""html_c06e7664ee1845ee411f1c79f74ff426"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kearsley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kearsley pumping station was affected by the flooding, compromising pumping infrastructure and operations. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Irwell reached a record high level at Kearsley (6.33m / 46.660m AOD), 1.6m higher than any previous reading in its records; this high river level is associated with the flooding in the nearby area (including Prestolee). [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River level reached 6.33m (46.660m AOD), a record high 1.6m above any previous reading in 13 years. Flooding&ndash;related damage included collapse of a United Utilities pipe bridge at Kearsley pumping station; a temporary pipe route was installed while the bridge is replaced. [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Environment Agency gauge on the River Irwell at Kearsley recorded an extreme river level during the event &mdash; the highest in 13 years of records &mdash; indicating exceptional river flooding in the locality. (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6.33m river level; 1.6m higher than previous record [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6.33m river level; 1.6m above previous record [IMG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Highest in 13 years of records; 1.6m higher than the previous highest levels; river level estimated as having a likelihood of occurring once in 100 to 150 years (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Kearsley,England,"Kearsley pumping station was affected by the flooding, compromising pumping infrastructure and operations. (p23)
River Irwell reached a record high level at Kearsley (6.33m / 46.660m AOD), 1.6m higher than any previous reading in its records; this high river level is associated with the flooding in the nearby area (including Prestolee). [IMG,PAG] (p41)
River level reached 6.33m (46.660m AOD), a record high 1.6m above any previous reading in 13 years. Flooding–related damage included collapse of a United Utilities pipe bridge at Kearsley pumping station; a temporary pipe route was installed while the bridge is replaced. [IMG] (p42)
The Environment Agency gauge on the River Irwell at Kearsley recorded an extreme river level during the event — the highest in 13 years of records — indicating exceptional river flooding in the locality. (p44)","6.33m river level; 1.6m higher than previous record [IMG,PAG] (p41)
6.33m river level; 1.6m above previous record [IMG] (p42)
Highest in 13 years of records; 1.6m higher than the previous highest levels; river level estimated as having a likelihood of occurring once in 100 to 150 years (p44)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.497929155532105,-2.5179949889890163,"<div id=""html_5563b855e222310494ba18b52054d6e2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Leigh</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Homes were evacuated by emergency services because of the flooding. (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Leigh,England,Homes were evacuated by emergency services because of the flooding. (p29),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.56837883751341,-2.291881662567234,"<div id=""html_5a08f424f4362add974af30149dc69ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Blackford Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River level at Blackford Bridge reached 3.36m (66.28m AOD), 1.1m higher than any other reading in its 64 years of records, corresponding to significant flooding at the bridge location. (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded an extreme river level during the event &mdash; the highest in the station record &mdash; indicating severe river flooding at that location on 26 December 2015. (p114)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3.36m river level (66.28m AOD) (p57)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Recorded level 3.362 m; 1.1 m higher than any other reading in its 64 years of records (p114)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Blackford Bridge,England,"River level at Blackford Bridge reached 3.36m (66.28m AOD), 1.1m higher than any other reading in its 64 years of records, corresponding to significant flooding at the bridge location. (p57)
Recorded an extreme river level during the event — the highest in the station record — indicating severe river flooding at that location on 26 December 2015. (p114)","3.36m river level (66.28m AOD) (p57)
Recorded level 3.362 m; 1.1 m higher than any other reading in its 64 years of records (p114)",GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5982902,-2.2680907,"<div id=""html_a7cb692e2aec3577f274d241743f291c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fairfield</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A number of properties were flooded on 26 December 2015 due to surface water run&ndash;off and issues with a culverted watercourse. (p69)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Fairfield,England,A number of properties were flooded on 26 December 2015 due to surface water run–off and issues with a culverted watercourse. (p69),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.56422555,-1.9999240666463873,"<div id=""html_884298ad51bf9160b09a8777190e263d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Saddleworth</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding inundated High Street in Uppermill and entered the interior of a church on High Street; streets were covered with water preventing normal use and community buildings were waterlogged. The Environment Agency recorded an exceptionally high river level at Uppermill (1.24m, highest in 18 years) and properties in Flood Zone 3 were flooded. (p102)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the Saddleworth area experienced internal property flooding due to surface water and overflow from ordinary watercourses; surface water flooding was driven by road gullies surcharging. Localised fluvial (ordinary watercourse) flooding also contributed to internal property inundation. (p109)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 properties (internal property flooding); 2 properties flooded from ordinary watercourses; 7 properties flooded from surface water (p109)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Saddleworth,England,"Flooding inundated High Street in Uppermill and entered the interior of a church on High Street; streets were covered with water preventing normal use and community buildings were waterlogged. The Environment Agency recorded an exceptionally high river level at Uppermill (1.24m, highest in 18 years) and properties in Flood Zone 3 were flooded. (p102)
Properties in the Saddleworth area experienced internal property flooding due to surface water and overflow from ordinary watercourses; surface water flooding was driven by road gullies surcharging. Localised fluvial (ordinary watercourse) flooding also contributed to internal property inundation. (p109)",2 properties (internal property flooding); 2 properties flooded from ordinary watercourses; 7 properties flooded from surface water (p109),GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.5891574,-2.2596753,"<div id=""html_b4b77a77db956264dc5e01f5c6f53851"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Heap Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heap Bridge experienced flooding from the River Roch on 26th December 2015; properties in the area (within Flood Zone 3) were inundated and floodwater extended into gardens and wooded areas, as shown in photographic evidence. [IMG,PAG] (p129)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Heap Bridge,England,"Heap Bridge experienced flooding from the River Roch on 26th December 2015; properties in the area (within Flood Zone 3) were inundated and floodwater extended into gardens and wooded areas, as shown in photographic evidence. [IMG,PAG] (p129)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.60863478169813,-2.1773687448788377,"<div id=""html_f612ade2cc56b74c3bac5a18308f6017"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rochdale Wastewater Treatment Works</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rochdale and Urmston (Davyhulme) wastewater treatment works suffered flooding; damage was reported as minimal but processes were affected. (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding impacted sewage treatment operations at the works. (p114)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Rochdale Wastewater Treatment Works,England,"Rochdale and Urmston (Davyhulme) wastewater treatment works suffered flooding; damage was reported as minimal but processes were affected. (p22)
Flooding impacted sewage treatment operations at the works. (p114)",,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-BOL,2,2015-12-26,53.465169528373025,-2.375129716857433,"<div id=""html_438b5faec8efb53de01940ec9ac9e786"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Davyhulme Wastewater Treatment Works</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rochdale and Urmston (Davyhulme) wastewater treatment works suffered flooding; damage was reported as minimal but processes were affected. (p22)</p></div></div></body></div>",Bolton,Davyhulme Wastewater Treatment Works,England,Rochdale and Urmston (Davyhulme) wastewater treatment works suffered flooding; damage was reported as minimal but processes were affected. (p22),,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26.html,GB-BOL_2_2015-12-26,Greater Manchester flooding 26 Dec 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Greater Manchester 26 December 2015,010/Bolton/010/2015/Bolton/Boxing/Day/2015///Flood/report.pdf
GB-LAN,20,2015-12-01,54.682888208302415,-2.7787770890036967,"<div id=""html_b699092b1f2fd0287f7fe85a1b124e00"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Coast Main Line</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A landslide during the December 2015 storms/flooding disrupted rail services on the West Coast Main Line; repairs were required before services could resume. (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,West Coast Main Line,England,A landslide during the December 2015 storms/flooding disrupted rail services on the West Coast Main Line; repairs were required before services could resume. (p25),,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01,Lancashire flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,December 2015 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-december-2015-floods.pdf
GB-LAN,20,2015-12-01,54.2230168,-2.6021945,"<div id=""html_a5999d9b24258a0b59a4584bdb0cf182"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Lune</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded the largest ever flows on an English river during the November&ndash;December 2015 events, representing an extreme river flood peak. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded exceptionally high flows in December 2015 relative to long&ndash;term averages; these elevated flows contributed to higher river levels and increased flood risk and impaired surface&ndash;water drainage capacity during the early&ndash;December storm period. [IMG] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,River Lune,England,"Recorded the largest ever flows on an English river during the November–December 2015 events, representing an extreme river flood peak. (p18)
Recorded exceptionally high flows in December 2015 relative to long–term averages; these elevated flows contributed to higher river levels and increased flood risk and impaired surface–water drainage capacity during the early–December storm period. [IMG] (p19)",,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01,Lancashire flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,December 2015 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-december-2015-floods.pdf
GB-LAN,20,2015-12-01,54.651764,-2.7356346,"<div id=""html_314718d15b4cecb34a33355c5b24e6b5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Eamont</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed exceptionally high river flows in December 2015 compared with long&ndash;term averages; contributed to elevated river levels that reduced the capacity of surface water systems to discharge during the Storm Desmond period (early December 2015), increasing local flood risk. [IMG] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,River Eamont,England,"Observed exceptionally high river flows in December 2015 compared with long–term averages; contributed to elevated river levels that reduced the capacity of surface water systems to discharge during the Storm Desmond period (early December 2015), increasing local flood risk. [IMG] (p19)",,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01,Lancashire flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,December 2015 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-december-2015-floods.pdf
GB-LAN,20,2015-12-01,53.910881,-1.6910531,"<div id=""html_f7ddb6da89d417b5545e1ef1ad1fdc63"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Wharfe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded exceptionally high flows in December 2015 versus long&ndash;term averages, contributing to elevated river levels and increased flood risk during the early&ndash;December storm period; this would have reduced the ability of surface&ndash;water systems to discharge locally. [IMG] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,River Wharfe,England,"Recorded exceptionally high flows in December 2015 versus long–term averages, contributing to elevated river levels and increased flood risk during the early–December storm period; this would have reduced the ability of surface–water systems to discharge locally. [IMG] (p19)",,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01,Lancashire flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,December 2015 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-december-2015-floods.pdf
GB-LAN,20,2015-12-01,52.6421055,-2.6353218,"<div id=""html_eb36f6eb2fb26d9324019774e52b7537"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Severn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Upper Severn showed exceptionally high river flows in December 2015 (well above the long&ndash;term average), indicating elevated flood risk and contributing to the high regional river levels recorded during the event. [IMG] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,River Severn,England,"The Upper Severn showed exceptionally high river flows in December 2015 (well above the long–term average), indicating elevated flood risk and contributing to the high regional river levels recorded during the event. [IMG] (p19)",,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_20_2015-12-01,Lancashire flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,December 2015 Floods In Lancashire: Section 19 Investigation,069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-december-2015-floods.pdf
GB-LAN,20,2015-12-01,53.86117,-2.5650887,"<div id=""html_58185cceff7109732616f98ce15245e5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancashire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme storms and rainfall in November&ndash;December 2015 caused flooding throughout December 2015 affecting communities across the county. Approximately 2,500 homes across 229 separate communities were flooded. Floods also damaged private gardens, garages and out&ndash;buildings, business premises, agricultural property and critical public infrastructure including roads, bridges, retaining walls, water treatment plants, power and communications installations, schools, village halls and town halls. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the county during December 2015 that required emergency response and recovery actions by Risk Management Authorities. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced widespread and extreme&ndash;magnitude flooding during December 2015 that affected infrastructure, commercial properties and the local economy, with a particular emphasis on internal flooding of residential properties. Investigations were required across many communities to account for flood mechanisms and remedial actions. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant and widespread flooding across the county from storm rainfall, affecting infrastructure, commercial properties and the local economy; many residential properties experienced internal flooding; highways and critical infrastructure were reported flooded in places. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extensive increases in river flows across the county in response to persistent heavy rainfall through November&ndash;December 2015; contributed to flood events investigated in the report and exceptional river flows recorded in the North West. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced fluvial (river) flooding with an additional surface water element during the event on 5/6 December 2015; river levels rose following the rainfall peak, reducing the ability of surface&ndash;water systems to discharge and resulting in internal flooding to domestic properties, mainly in the north of the county. [IMG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Northern Lancashire experienced fluvial (river) flooding with an additional surface&ndash;water element during the December 2015 events. Rivers were at capacity and overtopped, causing out&ndash;of&ndash;bank flow and backup/surcharge of surface drainage systems producing visible surface water on roads, gardens and causing internal flooding to domestic properties. [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rising river levels on 25&ndash;26 December caused river channels and receiving drains to become unable to discharge. Drainage systems surcharged (backed up) against elevated stream and river levels, causing flooding of properties. The majority of affected locations were caused by fluvial flooding or a combination of fluvial and surface water runoff. [IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;wide flooding during December 2015: 2,467 homes across 115 towns, villages and the city of Lancaster were flooded (7,500 people directly affected); 229 separate communities identified for further investigation; several major roads were flooded and not passable; bridges were closed due to concerns over structural integrity; roads closed due to collapse of retaining walls, landslips or deteriorated surfaces; disruption to rail services requiring repairs; electricity substations in Lancaster were flooded causing widespread power loss. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the December 2015 floods parts of Lancashire experienced widespread flooding: 2,467 homes across 115 towns, villages and the city of Lancaster were flooded (about 7,500 people directly affected); 229 communities/sub&ndash;communities were identified for further investigation; several major roads were flooded and impassable; bridges were closed over concerns about structural integrity; roads were also closed due to retaining wall collapse or landslips and required repairs before re&ndash;opening; rail disruption occurred when a landslide on the West Coast Main Line required repairs before services could resume; electricity substations were flooded, leaving 61,000 homes in Lancaster without power. [PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;wide flooding during December 2015 that prompted multi&ndash;agency response, investigations (Stage 2) and bids for flood&ndash;reduction funding; led to increased data collection and collaborative risk&ndash;management activity by local RMAs and the LLFA. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread December 2015 flooding affected communities across the county, prompting emergency response, investigations led by the LLFA and other RMAs, and some remedial works. Early investigations were disrupted by additional flooding later in the month; recovery and coordinated programmes of further investigation and mitigation were initiated. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Unprecedented December 2015 rainfall exceeded drainage and river capacities so significant volumes of water flowed above ground, damaging property and public assets and limiting travel, public services and business operations for many days. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During December 2015 prolonged heavy rainfall and saturated ground caused main rivers to flow out&ndash;of&ndash;bank (notably on 5&ndash;6 December) and surface water drainage systems to surcharge (notably on 25&ndash;26 December). Significant volumes of water flowed above ground, damaging property and public assets, restricting travel and limiting public services and business operations for many days, and causing many households to be displaced while repairs and restoration were carried out. (p31)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 2,500 homes; 229 separate communities (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Locations with between 51 and 344 properties flooded at the highest&ndash;impact sites (map legend category 51&ndash;344 properties). [IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2,467 homes; 115 towns, villages and the city of Lancaster; 7,500 people; 229 separate communities; 61,000 homes (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2,467 homes; 115 towns, villages and the city of Lancaster; 7,500 people; 229 separate communities and sub&ndash;communities; 61,000 homes without power; 1 landslide on the West Coast Main Line [PAG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>229 communities and sub&ndash;communities locations affected [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>229 individual locations investigated [PAG] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lancashire,England,"Extreme storms and rainfall in November–December 2015 caused flooding throughout December 2015 affecting communities across the county. Approximately 2,500 homes across 229 separate communities were flooded. Floods also damaged private gardens, garages and out–buildings, business premises, agricultural property and critical public infrastructure including roads, bridges, retaining walls, water treatment plants, power and communications installations, schools, village halls and town halls. (p3)
Widespread flooding across the county during December 2015 that required emergency response and recovery actions by Risk Management Authorities. (p7)
Experienced widespread and extreme–magnitude flooding during December 2015 that affected infrastructure, commercial properties and the local economy, with a particular emphasis on internal flooding of residential properties. Investigations were required across many communities to account for flood mechanisms and remedial actions. (p12)
Significant and widespread flooding across the county from storm rainfall, affecting infrastructure, commercial properties and the local economy; many residential properties experienced internal flooding; highways and critical infrastructure were reported flooded in places. (p13)
Extensive increases in river flows across the county in response to persistent heavy rainfall through November–December 2015; contributed to flood events investigated in the report and exceptional river flows recorded in the North West. [IMG] (p18)
Experienced fluvial (river) flooding with an additional surface water element during the event on 5/6 December 2015; river levels rose following the rainfall peak, reducing the ability of surface–water systems to discharge and resulting in internal flooding to domestic properties, mainly in the north of the county. [IMG] (p20)
Northern Lancashire experienced fluvial (river) flooding with an additional surface–water element during the December 2015 events. Rivers were at capacity and overtopped, causing out–of–bank flow and backup/surcharge of surface drainage systems producing visible surface water on roads, gardens and causing internal flooding to domestic properties. [IMG,PAG] (p21)
Rising river levels on 25–26 December caused river channels and receiving drains to become unable to discharge. Drainage systems surcharged (backed up) against elevated stream and river levels, causing flooding of properties. The majority of affected locations were caused by fluvial flooding or a combination of fluvial and surface water runoff. [IMG] (p23)
County–wide flooding during December 2015: 2,467 homes across 115 towns, villages and the city of Lancaster were flooded (7,500 people directly affected); 229 separate communities identified for further investigation; several major roads were flooded and not passable; bridges were closed due to concerns over structural integrity; roads closed due to collapse of retaining walls, landslips or deteriorated surfaces; disruption to rail services requiring repairs; electricity substations in Lancaster were flooded causing widespread power loss. (p25)
During the December 2015 floods parts of Lancashire experienced widespread flooding: 2,467 homes across 115 towns, villages and the city of Lancaster were flooded (about 7,500 people directly affected); 229 communities/sub–communities were identified for further investigation; several major roads were flooded and impassable; bridges were closed over concerns about structural integrity; roads were also closed due to retaining wall collapse or landslips and required repairs before re–opening; rail disruption occurred when a landslide on the West Coast Main Line required repairs before services could resume; electricity substations were flooded, leaving 61,000 homes in Lancaster without power. [PAG] (p26)
County–wide flooding during December 2015 that prompted multi–agency response, investigations (Stage 2) and bids for flood–reduction funding; led to increased data collection and collaborative risk–management activity by local RMAs and the LLFA. (p27)
Widespread December 2015 flooding affected communities across the county, prompting emergency response, investigations led by the LLFA and other RMAs, and some remedial works. Early investigations were disrupted by additional flooding later in the month; recovery and coordinated programmes of further investigation and mitigation were initiated. [PAG] (p28)
Unprecedented December 2015 rainfall exceeded drainage and river capacities so significant volumes of water flowed above ground, damaging property and public assets and limiting travel, public services and business operations for many days. [PAG] (p30)
During December 2015 prolonged heavy rainfall and saturated ground caused main rivers to flow out–of–bank (notably on 5–6 December) and surface water drainage systems to surcharge (notably on 25–26 December). Significant volumes of water flowed above ground, damaging property and public assets, restricting travel and limiting public services and business operations for many days, and causing many households to be displaced while repairs and restoration were carried out. (p31)","approximately 2,500 homes; 229 separate communities (p3)
Locations with between 51 and 344 properties flooded at the highest–impact sites (map legend category 51–344 properties). [IMG] (p23)
