The East Cowes area of the Isle of Wight experienced internal property flooding on 25 October 2023, causing property damage, displacement of residents, business closures and associated economic and health impacts. [IMG] (p35)",8 residential properties [TAB] (p30),GB-IOW_2_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_2_2023-10-25,East Cowes flooding 25 Oct 2023,East Cowes - Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation East Cowes Sept 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,2,2023-10-25,50.7587154,-1.289584,"<div id=""html_36d1f5cac99f1e0b2a9396339122992c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Red Funnel Terminal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas adjacent to the Red Funnel Ferry Terminal were among the worst&ndash;hit parts of East Cowes during the flood on 25 October 2023 and were affected again on the night of 8 April 2024; drainage proposals for proposed development at the terminal were reviewed to understand implications for flood risk to East Cowes. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas immediately adjacent to the ferry terminal were affected by surface water (pluvial) flooding during the October 2023 event; surface water runoff and inundation occurred on surrounding roads and low&ndash;lying areas near the terminal. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 properties (10 residential, 12 commercial) internally flooded in East Cowes during the event(s) affecting the terminal vicinity. [MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Red Funnel Terminal,England,"Areas adjacent to the Red Funnel Ferry Terminal were among the worst–hit parts of East Cowes during the flood on 25 October 2023 and were affected again on the night of 8 April 2024; drainage proposals for proposed development at the terminal were reviewed to understand implications for flood risk to East Cowes. [MUL] (p9)
Areas immediately adjacent to the ferry terminal were affected by surface water (pluvial) flooding during the October 2023 event; surface water runoff and inundation occurred on surrounding roads and low–lying areas near the terminal. (p18)","22 properties (10 residential, 12 commercial) internally flooded in East Cowes during the event(s) affecting the terminal vicinity. [MUL] (p9)",GB-IOW_2_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_2_2023-10-25,East Cowes flooding 25 Oct 2023,East Cowes - Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation East Cowes Sept 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,2,2023-10-25,50.759483,-1.2852106,"<div id=""html_8261f178a761a80fccf51803a943d200"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Cowes</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooding incidents occurred in East Cowes following the 25 October 2023 event; photographs in the report document flooding in the town. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties in the town centre and areas near the ferry terminal; investigation finds flooding was caused by a combination of extreme rainfall and tide locking. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties during the October 2023 event (and flooding again during the night of 8 April 2024) affecting areas in the vicinity of the ferry terminal; worst&ndash;hit areas from October correspond to areas affected in April 2024. [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in East Cowes such that a Section 19 investigation was carried out; the investigation area focused on the north&ndash;east tip of the island along the east bank of the River Medina and Medina Estuary. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of East Cowes experienced flooding on 25 October 2023; affected areas (for example Well Road, Castle Street and York Avenue) lie within the tidal extent of Flood Zones 2 and 3 indicating tidal inundation risk from the estuary/sea. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Narrow corridors and streets in East Cowes (particularly around the Ferry Terminal) are shown at high risk of surface water flooding; streets named in the report (Dover Road, Well Road/Castle Street, Marsh Road and Link Road) correspond to areas impacted in the October 2023 event. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water (pluvial) runoff concentrated into topographic low points and along the estuary shoreline, causing flooding in and around the Ferry Terminal area and central East Cowes. Roads identified as being impacted by the October 2023 event include Dover Road, Well Road, Castle Street, Marsh Road and Link Road; the Well Road roundabout and central discharge points for watercourses/surface water routes were also affected. [IMG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected by the storm on 24&ndash;25 October 2023; the majority of internal flooding incidents in the area occurred on 25 October 2023 associated with heavy rainfall and elevated river/tide levels. [MUL] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding on 25 October 2023 prompted East Cowes council and the fire service to contact Southern Water requesting the storm pumps at the Albany Road Pumping Station be switched on to alleviate capacity issues in the combined sewer network. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in East Cowes experienced internal flooding; sandbags were placed at property doors and on drains; storm pumps were deployed at Well Road roundabout to extract flood water; emergency services were called and a councillor attended flooded properties. [TAB] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported flooding incidents across the town centre, affecting streets and properties; main concentration in the northwest of the town and town centre vicinity. [IMG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the October 2023 flood event East Cowes experienced a combination of extreme rainfall and tide&ndash;locking/hydraulic overloading. The Sandown New Sewer Catchment (combined sewer system) experienced overload: floodwater overspilt and manhole covers burst; residents reported downstairs toilets bubbling; road drains were clogged, resulting in flooding of surrounding properties. Tide locking of discharge into the Medina Estuary contributed to overtopping/backing up of drainage. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of commercial buildings and residential dwellings (including properties on Castle Street, York Avenue, Church Path, Albany Road and Esplanade). Manholes in Castle Street, Well Road and Church Path surcharged, sending a significant volume of water onto roads and into properties. Residents were displaced to alternative accommodation (temporary or permanent), vulnerable residents unable to remain in their homes while repairs proceeded, loss of carpets, floorboards, furniture and ground&ndash;floor belongings, business closures with loss of income and insurance claims, and reported adverse effects on mental health (stress and anxiety). [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the town experienced significant surface water inundation during the event (photographed flooding on Church Path shows water extending up to building entrances and covering pedestrian routes). (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>East Cowes experienced flooding on 25 October 2023; local residents and a councillor provided assistance during the event, indicating community&ndash;level impacts and need for local flood resilience measures. (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected properties in East Cowes, with internal flooding of properties reported and uptake of Property Flood Resilience (PFR) measures at some residential properties. (p51)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water and urban flooding affected roads and the town centre, with floodwater reaching road surfaces and threatening property thresholds. Traffic driving through floodwater exacerbated the situation. The town centre drainage is influenced by tidal levels, increasing flood risk and contributing to surface water reaching properties. (p52)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to properties (residential and commercial); disruption to normal life with residents moved to alternative accommodation (temporary or permanent); loss of carpets, floorboards, furniture and ground&ndash;floor belongings; businesses closed with loss of income and insurance claims; emergency services overwhelmed; mental health impacts (anxiety, depression, loss of sleep). [MUL] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Community experienced flooding issues prompting recommendations to replace or improve the tidal outfall, increase surface water drainage capacity, and increase capacity of the Church Path watercourse to reduce future flood risk. (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 properties (10 residential, 12 commercial) [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 22 properties (10 residential, 12 commercial) [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 residential properties [TAB] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 properties (10 residential, 12 commercial) [MUL] (p53)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,East Cowes,England,"Reported flooding incidents occurred in East Cowes following the 25 October 2023 event; photographs in the report document flooding in the town. (p7)
Internal flooding to properties in the town centre and areas near the ferry terminal; investigation finds flooding was caused by a combination of extreme rainfall and tide locking. [MUL] (p9)
