Flooding on 25 October 2023 prompted East Cowes council and the fire service to contact Southern Water requesting the storm pumps at the Albany Road Pumping Station be switched on to alleviate capacity issues in the combined sewer network. (p29)
Residential properties in East Cowes experienced internal flooding; sandbags were placed at property doors and on drains; storm pumps were deployed at Well Road roundabout to extract flood water; emergency services were called and a councillor attended flooded properties. [TAB] (p30)
Reported flooding incidents across the town centre, affecting streets and properties; main concentration in the northwest of the town and town centre vicinity. [IMG] (p32)
During the October 2023 flood event East Cowes experienced a combination of extreme rainfall and tide–locking/hydraulic overloading. The Sandown New Sewer Catchment (combined sewer system) experienced overload: floodwater overspilt and manhole covers burst; residents reported downstairs toilets bubbling; road drains were clogged, resulting in flooding of surrounding properties. Tide locking of discharge into the Medina Estuary contributed to overtopping/backing up of drainage. (p33)
Internal flooding of commercial buildings and residential dwellings (including properties on Castle Street, York Avenue, Church Path, Albany Road and Esplanade). Manholes in Castle Street, Well Road and Church Path surcharged, sending a significant volume of water onto roads and into properties. Residents were displaced to alternative accommodation (temporary or permanent), vulnerable residents unable to remain in their homes while repairs proceeded, loss of carpets, floorboards, furniture and ground–floor belongings, business closures with loss of income and insurance claims, and reported adverse effects on mental health (stress and anxiety). [IMG] (p35)
Parts of the town experienced significant surface water inundation during the event (photographed flooding on Church Path shows water extending up to building entrances and covering pedestrian routes). (p37)
East Cowes experienced flooding on 25 October 2023; local residents and a councillor provided assistance during the event, indicating community–level impacts and need for local flood resilience measures. (p50)
Surface water flooding affected properties in East Cowes, with internal flooding of properties reported and uptake of Property Flood Resilience (PFR) measures at some residential properties. (p51)
Surface water and urban flooding affected roads and the town centre, with floodwater reaching road surfaces and threatening property thresholds. Traffic driving through floodwater exacerbated the situation. The town centre drainage is influenced by tidal levels, increasing flood risk and contributing to surface water reaching properties. (p52)
Internal flooding to properties (residential and commercial); disruption to normal life with residents moved to alternative accommodation (temporary or permanent); loss of carpets, floorboards, furniture and ground–floor belongings; businesses closed with loss of income and insurance claims; emergency services overwhelmed; mental health impacts (anxiety, depression, loss of sleep). [MUL] (p53)
Community experienced flooding issues prompting recommendations to replace or improve the tidal outfall, increase surface water drainage capacity, and increase capacity of the Church Path watercourse to reduce future flood risk. (p55)","22 properties (10 residential, 12 commercial) [MUL] (p9)
at least 22 properties (10 residential, 12 commercial) [MUL] (p11)
