GB-NNH,2,2016-03-09,52.4562208,-0.6097755,"<div id=""html_ec3d5f5801bcee6e9bdbd28a11ccb7fe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harper's Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High water levels in Harpers Brook caused overtopping onto local roads (notably Grafton Road and Sudborough Road) leading to highway flooding and internal flooding of properties. Harpers Brook flooding was the source of multiple incidents including internal flooding of properties and a community building, and repeated highway inundation that required sandbags and other temporary protection measures. [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water level rose (reported approximately six feet), overtopped banks and flowed onto roads and private property; a spillway channel came into partial use and the outflow contributed to deeper water on adjacent roads and properties. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels rose up against a building downstream of Grafton Road bridge, reaching two stone courses above the blue band of bricks on that building. [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of siltation were observed on Harpers Brook upstream of Grafton Road bridge following the flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p41)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three residential properties; community building; flooding 3 feet deep in community building; three properties on Sudborough Road; one property on Grafton Road [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately six feet rise in water level (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two stone courses above the blue band of bricks [IMG,PAG] (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two stone courses above the blue band of bricks [IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Harper's Brook,England,"High water levels in Harpers Brook caused overtopping onto local roads (notably Grafton Road and Sudborough Road) leading to highway flooding and internal flooding of properties. Harpers Brook flooding was the source of multiple incidents including internal flooding of properties and a community building, and repeated highway inundation that required sandbags and other temporary protection measures. [TAB,MUL] (p11)
Water level rose (reported approximately six feet), overtopped banks and flowed onto roads and private property; a spillway channel came into partial use and the outflow contributed to deeper water on adjacent roads and properties. (p12)
Water levels rose up against a building downstream of Grafton Road bridge, reaching two stone courses above the blue band of bricks on that building. [IMG,PAG] (p40)
Areas of siltation were observed on Harpers Brook upstream of Grafton Road bridge following the flood event. [IMG,PAG] (p41)","three residential properties; community building; flooding 3 feet deep in community building; three properties on Sudborough Road; one property on Grafton Road [TAB,MUL] (p11)
