GB-NNH,2,2016-03-09,52.442937,-0.5970523,"<div id=""html_eac733467c512c8a8de08a1fd8f77665"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sudborough Road, Brigstock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties on Sudborough Road caused by high water levels in Harpers Brook overtopping onto the road; bow waves from passing vehicles exacerbated flooding of properties. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water flowing from Harpers Brook onto Sudborough Road accumulated in low areas and deepened until it entered residential properties, causing internal flooding; one household installed a flood gate across their frontage (not tested). (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of ponding occurred on Sudborough Road, with standing water affecting the road and properties visible from Grafton Road bridge. [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of ponding/standing water formed on the carriageway adjacent to properties. Roadside channel drainage within the carriageway was partially filled with debris, reducing drainage capacity and contributing to the ponding; access to the frontage of properties on Sudborough Road was affected by the standing water. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of ponding observed on the carriageway; channel drainage systems cut into the carriageway were recorded (evidence of surface water ponding and drainage intervention on the road). [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Three properties [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties internally flooded (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Sudborough Road, Brigstock",England,"Internal flooding of properties on Sudborough Road caused by high water levels in Harpers Brook overtopping onto the road; bow waves from passing vehicles exacerbated flooding of properties. [TAB] (p11)
Water flowing from Harpers Brook onto Sudborough Road accumulated in low areas and deepened until it entered residential properties, causing internal flooding; one household installed a flood gate across their frontage (not tested). (p12)
Areas of ponding occurred on Sudborough Road, with standing water affecting the road and properties visible from Grafton Road bridge. [IMG,PAG] (p42)
Areas of ponding/standing water formed on the carriageway adjacent to properties. Roadside channel drainage within the carriageway was partially filled with debris, reducing drainage capacity and contributing to the ponding; access to the frontage of properties on Sudborough Road was affected by the standing water. [IMG,PAG] (p43)
Areas of ponding observed on the carriageway; channel drainage systems cut into the carriageway were recorded (evidence of surface water ponding and drainage intervention on the road). [IMG,PAG] (p44)","Three properties [TAB] (p11)
3 properties internally flooded (p12)",GB-NNH_2_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_2_2016-03-09,Brigstock flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Grafton Road, Brigstock, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22327/FIR-Brigstock/Rev03/151216.pdf
GB-NNH,2,2016-03-09,52.4577142,-0.610044,"<div id=""html_f5f95a71f2ad65f6bdd4837eeb03a606"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brigstock</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Subject of a Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation concerning flooding at Grafton Road. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment flowed to low points around private property in the village, resulting in internal flooding of at least one property on Grafton Road. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of a flood incident on Grafton Road in which a property was internally flooded; the property experienced repeated internal flooding events within the last five years. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flooding incident occurred in the village on 9th March 2016. The British Geological Survey dataset indicates the incident was in an area with potential for groundwater flooding to occur at surface level; affected residents within the village were interviewed about the event. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of the village experienced flooding from Harpers Brook and surface water. Recorded impacts include internal flooding of residential properties (multiple incidents), a community building flooded to about 3 feet deep, floodwater reaching near the High Street area, the cricket pitch flooded up to the pavilion, structural failure (walls) on Kennel Hill leading to further local flooding, and use of sandbags/air&ndash;brick covers to protect community and medical facilities. [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Harpers Brook rose and flowed over its banks onto roads and private property in Brigstock; surface water followed natural contours to low points around properties, entering residences and causing internal flooding in nearby homes. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment and high water levels in Harpers Brook overwhelmed drainage and watercourses, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around private properties and resulting in internal flooding. Bow waves from vehicles driving through floodwater exacerbated internal flooding to properties. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The map shows blue shaded flood zones overlapping the village centre; two red&ndash;circled locations on the main road lie adjacent to the primary water feature, indicating parts of Brigstock were within mapped flood areas and likely experienced surface&ndash;water flooding during the investigated event. [IMG] (p31)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three residential properties; community building; flooding 3 feet deep in community building; three properties on Sudborough Road; one property on Grafton Road [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties on Sudborough Road; 1 property on Grafton Road (internally flooded) [PAG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Brigstock,England,"Subject of a Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation concerning flooding at Grafton Road. (p2)
Surface water from heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment flowed to low points around private property in the village, resulting in internal flooding of at least one property on Grafton Road. [MUL] (p7)
Location of a flood incident on Grafton Road in which a property was internally flooded; the property experienced repeated internal flooding events within the last five years. [PAG] (p9)
A flooding incident occurred in the village on 9th March 2016. The British Geological Survey dataset indicates the incident was in an area with potential for groundwater flooding to occur at surface level; affected residents within the village were interviewed about the event. [PAG] (p10)
Areas of the village experienced flooding from Harpers Brook and surface water. Recorded impacts include internal flooding of residential properties (multiple incidents), a community building flooded to about 3 feet deep, floodwater reaching near the High Street area, the cricket pitch flooded up to the pavilion, structural failure (walls) on Kennel Hill leading to further local flooding, and use of sandbags/air–brick covers to protect community and medical facilities. [MUL] (p11)
Harpers Brook rose and flowed over its banks onto roads and private property in Brigstock; surface water followed natural contours to low points around properties, entering residences and causing internal flooding in nearby homes. [PAG] (p12)
Heavy rainfall on a saturated catchment and high water levels in Harpers Brook overwhelmed drainage and watercourses, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around private properties and resulting in internal flooding. Bow waves from vehicles driving through floodwater exacerbated internal flooding to properties. (p13)
The map shows blue shaded flood zones overlapping the village centre; two red–circled locations on the main road lie adjacent to the primary water feature, indicating parts of Brigstock were within mapped flood areas and likely experienced surface–water flooding during the investigated event. [IMG] (p31)","1 property [MUL] (p7)
1 property [PAG] (p9)
three residential properties; community building; flooding 3 feet deep in community building; three properties on Sudborough Road; one property on Grafton Road [MUL] (p11)
3 properties on Sudborough Road; 1 property on Grafton Road (internally flooded) [PAG] (p12)",GB-NNH_2_2016-03-09.html,GB-NNH_2_2016-03-09,Brigstock flooding 09 Mar 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Grafton Road, Brigstock, 9th March 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/22327/FIR-Brigstock/Rev03/151216.pdf
