0.15 m (depth of floodwater on the station track bed) [IMG] (p27)",GB-KEN_4_2015-08-24.html,GB-KEN_4_2015-08-24,Tunbridge Wells flooding 24 Aug 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Tunbridge Wells Area On 24th August 2015,063/Kent/Flooding-affecting-Tunbridge-Wells-investigation-24-August-2015.pdf
GB-KEN,4,2015-08-24,51.1281586,0.2389634,"<div id=""html_e6ceacd5935be2963d7d6447bdea2f3e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nevill Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall exceeded local drainage capacity and caused surface water to accumulate in low points and enter properties in the area. Surcharging of the combined sewer (reported at nearby Nevill Street) caused drains to back up into properties in the block including Nevill Park and Warwick Park. [MUL] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 reported flooding incidents (Nov 2013; Dec 2013; Feb 2014; Oct 2014; Aug 2015) [MUL] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Nevill Park,England,Intense rainfall exceeded local drainage capacity and caused surface water to accumulate in low points and enter properties in the area. Surcharging of the combined sewer (reported at nearby Nevill Street) caused drains to back up into properties in the block including Nevill Park and Warwick Park. [MUL] (p17),5 reported flooding incidents (Nov 2013; Dec 2013; Feb 2014; Oct 2014; Aug 2015) [MUL] (p17),GB-KEN_4_2015-08-24.html,GB-KEN_4_2015-08-24,Tunbridge Wells flooding 24 Aug 2015,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Tunbridge Wells Area On 24th August 2015,063/Kent/Flooding-affecting-Tunbridge-Wells-investigation-24-August-2015.pdf
GB-LAN,26,2021-01-13,53.9263985,-2.918886,"<div id=""html_5a5e7f0aae5c0f13d90e1981dab37cc7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Smallwood Hey Road, Pilling</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The public highway experienced blocked gullies and/or flooding; local surface water drainage was overwhelmed leading to flooding affecting nearby property. The highway had multiple recorded incidents of flooding&ndash;related problems. [PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15 recorded incidents; 1 home [PAG,MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,"Smallwood Hey Road, Pilling",England,"The public highway experienced blocked gullies and/or flooding; local surface water drainage was overwhelmed leading to flooding affecting nearby property. The highway had multiple recorded incidents of flooding–related problems. [PAG,MUL] (p4)","15 recorded incidents; 1 home [PAG,MUL] (p4)",GB-LAN_26_2021-01-13.html,GB-LAN_26_2021-01-13,Pilling flooding 13 Jan 2021,"2021 Flooding In Lancashire: West View, Pilling (Wyre)",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-west-view-pilling-2021.pdf
GB-LAN,26,2021-01-13,53.8611712,-2.5650945,"<div id=""html_f5d24211bdd22b900581aff3cb7acdaf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lancashire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall on 13 January 2021 overwhelmed local surface water drainage in the Pilling area, resulting in one home being flooded. The county council highways team also recorded and responded to flooding/blocked&ndash;gully incidents associated with the nearby public highway (Smallwood Hey Road). [MUL] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 home; 15 recorded incidents [MUL] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Lancashire,England,"Heavy rainfall on 13 January 2021 overwhelmed local surface water drainage in the Pilling area, resulting in one home being flooded. The county council highways team also recorded and responded to flooding/blocked–gully incidents associated with the nearby public highway (Smallwood Hey Road). [MUL] (p4)",1 home; 15 recorded incidents [MUL] (p4),GB-LAN_26_2021-01-13.html,GB-LAN_26_2021-01-13,Pilling flooding 13 Jan 2021,"2021 Flooding In Lancashire: West View, Pilling (Wyre)",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-west-view-pilling-2021.pdf
GB-LAN,26,2021-01-13,53.930207,-2.9088352,"<div id=""html_d1fb80e1e5a45c159d40a4dbce779d7c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pilling</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local surface water drainage networks were overwhelmed by heavy rainfall, and a residential property was flooded. (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 home (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Pilling,England,"Local surface water drainage networks were overwhelmed by heavy rainfall, and a residential property was flooded. (p4)",1 home (p4),GB-LAN_26_2021-01-13.html,GB-LAN_26_2021-01-13,Pilling flooding 13 Jan 2021,"2021 Flooding In Lancashire: West View, Pilling (Wyre)",069/Lancashire County/section-19-flood-investigation-report-west-view-pilling-2021.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.667735766681965,-0.6189785814342308,"<div id=""html_a9178ba917042a060838f431ebed6854"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Maltings</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Maltings culvert was identified as a possible restriction and under&ndash;capacity during high flows; its siphon design encourages debris build&ndash;up immediately downstream, contributing to high water levels. EA Operations attended to remove debris; a local resident entered the culvert to remove debris, placing themselves in danger. Two downstream weirs and collected silt further impeded flow. [MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert under The Maltings was partly restricted during the event, reducing conveyance and contributing to elevated local water levels and surface drainage not discharging freely into the river. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert located under The Maltings had its flow partly restricted, which prevented rapid discharge of river water, caused backing up of flow at the culvert and reduced the drainage system's ability to convey additional flows, contributing to local flooding. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert under The Maltings was partially restricted during high river levels, causing water to back up and impeding flow. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Investigation found a partial flow restriction within the culvert underneath The Maltings which contributed to High Street and garden flooding; removal of an obstruction and subsequent jetting of the culvert substantially restored flow and reduced water levels, ending most of the flooding affecting the High Street. [TAB,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert under The Maltings was partially restricted, causing water to back up and contributing to local flooding. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,The Maltings,England,"The Maltings culvert was identified as a possible restriction and under–capacity during high flows; its siphon design encourages debris build–up immediately downstream, contributing to high water levels. EA Operations attended to remove debris; a local resident entered the culvert to remove debris, placing themselves in danger. Two downstream weirs and collected silt further impeded flow. [MUL] (p14)
The culvert under The Maltings was partly restricted during the event, reducing conveyance and contributing to elevated local water levels and surface drainage not discharging freely into the river. [PAG] (p19)
The culvert located under The Maltings had its flow partly restricted, which prevented rapid discharge of river water, caused backing up of flow at the culvert and reduced the drainage system's ability to convey additional flows, contributing to local flooding. [PAG] (p20)
Culvert under The Maltings was partially restricted during high river levels, causing water to back up and impeding flow. (p23)
Investigation found a partial flow restriction within the culvert underneath The Maltings which contributed to High Street and garden flooding; removal of an obstruction and subsequent jetting of the culvert substantially restored flow and reduced water levels, ending most of the flooding affecting the High Street. [TAB,MUL] (p24)
The culvert under The Maltings was partially restricted, causing water to back up and contributing to local flooding. (p30)",,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.66386140077072,-0.6074233638836425,"<div id=""html_d064c5c2df164fcea7d5d1ef6a4c9d43"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Jaguar Garage</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A section of the riverbank downstream of the Jaguar garage collapsed and a fallen tree downstream of the garage further impeded river flow, worsening flooding. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A section of the riverbank downstream of the Jaguar garage collapsed during the high river conditions, contributing to the flood impacts. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Jaguar Garage,England,"A section of the riverbank downstream of the Jaguar garage collapsed and a fallen tree downstream of the garage further impeded river flow, worsening flooding. (p23)
A section of the riverbank downstream of the Jaguar garage collapsed during the high river conditions, contributing to the flood impacts. (p30)",,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.6480032,-0.5800363,"<div id=""html_c15675336bc67d5ad06cc82ee228dee2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Misbourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River levels were raised by prolonged rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater; a culvert flow restriction caused the River Misbourne to back up and a riverbank collapse occurred, which led to property flooding. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flow through Old Amersham was physically impeded: the culvert beneath The Maltings had restricted flow and a bank collapse occurred near the Jaguar garage where the channels rejoin, affecting the channel alignment and conveyance through the town. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within Old Amersham the river passes behind properties, through a series of culverts and splits into two channels east of the superstore before rejoining. A culvert beneath The Maltings had restricted flow (location A, SU 95599 97438) and there was a bank collapse near the Jaguar garage on the A355 (location B, SU 96533 96984). These issues are identified as key areas of concern along the River Misbourne through the town. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert flow restrictions and bank collapse were identified in Old Amersham where the Misbourne is culverted through the town; these issues contributed to fluvial flooding risks and affected areas where the channel passes under the highway and adjacent properties. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sections of the Misbourne in Old Amersham experienced culvert flow restriction and bank collapse that obstructed channel flow and contributed to local flooding of adjacent areas; the river is culverted through parts of the town and these failures affected flow conveyance. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Predicted surface&ndash;water flood depths and extents are concentrated along the river channel in the 1:100 and 1:1000 maps; the river corridor is associated with inundation of low&ndash;lying areas and the town centre and with property/basement flooding events recorded in winter 2000/01 and during the event described in this report. [PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Misbourne produced flows of river water that ran down the High Street, causing garden and property flooding in Old Amersham during multiple events (including winter 2000/1, March 2013, August 2013 and January&ndash;February 2014). [PAG,MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Misbourne experienced prolonged high water levels from November 2013 to mid&ndash;February 2014, with two particularly severe flooding events on/around 17 January and 06/07 February 2014. The high river levels and saturated catchment caused the river and highway drainage to be exceeded, contributed to surface and highway runoff, and a riverbank collapse occurred downstream of the Jaguar garage on 07 February 2014, allowing river water to flow into surrounding areas. Sedimentation and weirs downstream of The Maltings impeded flushing and exacerbated conveyance problems. [MUL] (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels in the River Misbourne, combined with saturated catchment and large surface runoff, exceeded channel and highway drainage capacity. Riverbank collapse allowed river water to flow into surrounding areas; backing up through drainage gullies contributed to flooding of High Street and nearby properties. A partial culvert restriction reduced conveyance until cleared by the EA. [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river and groundwater levels led to elevated Misbourne flows; a tampered/damaged flap valve allowed river water to back up and flow east along the High Street, and high river levels prevented return flow through downstream valves. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels and a partial flow restriction within a culvert beneath The Maltings caused flooding of gardens and High Street properties in Old Amersham. Removal of an obstruction to a flap valve on 18 January ended the bulk of High Street flooding. A pump was installed on 06 February and jetting of the restricted culvert (11&ndash;12 February) reduced water levels substantially (300 mm drop on 11 Feb and a further 300 mm on 12 Feb) and restored most flow through the culvert. Downstream bank collapse near the Jaguar garage required emergency bank restoration and a boom to protect against pollution. Sandbags and FloodSax were distributed to affected properties. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert flow restriction and obstructions on the River Misbourne caused flooding of Old Amersham High Street; removal of a flap valve obstruction ended the bulk of High Street flooding. A bank collapse downstream of the Jaguar garage required multi&ndash;agency repair and pollution control. A tree removal downstream lowered water levels and culvert jetting substantially reduced water levels and restored flow; EA planned repairs and later multi&ndash;agency meetings addressed culvert/weir removal to reduce future risk. [PAG,MUL] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Weirs and culvert flow restrictions on the Misbourne were identified as contributing to increased flood risk; a multi&ndash;agency meeting (2 July 2014) led to agreement in principle (mid&ndash;July 2014) between the Environment Agency and riparian owners to remove weirs to reduce flooding risk. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Misbourne overtopped/caused flooding in winter 2013/14 (January&ndash;February 2014), flooding properties in Old Amersham and prompting recovery actions. The event led to requirements for culvert and gully inspections, clearance of vegetation and debris, installation of trash screens on culverts, and coordination between RMAs and riparian owners to reduce blockages and restore flow. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 residential properties; 5 business premises (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Misbourne,England,"River levels were raised by prolonged rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater; a culvert flow restriction caused the River Misbourne to back up and a riverbank collapse occurred, which led to property flooding. (p4)
Flow through Old Amersham was physically impeded: the culvert beneath The Maltings had restricted flow and a bank collapse occurred near the Jaguar garage where the channels rejoin, affecting the channel alignment and conveyance through the town. [PAG] (p6)
Within Old Amersham the river passes behind properties, through a series of culverts and splits into two channels east of the superstore before rejoining. A culvert beneath The Maltings had restricted flow (location A, SU 95599 97438) and there was a bank collapse near the Jaguar garage on the A355 (location B, SU 96533 96984). These issues are identified as key areas of concern along the River Misbourne through the town. [PAG] (p8)
Culvert flow restrictions and bank collapse were identified in Old Amersham where the Misbourne is culverted through the town; these issues contributed to fluvial flooding risks and affected areas where the channel passes under the highway and adjacent properties. [PAG] (p9)
Sections of the Misbourne in Old Amersham experienced culvert flow restriction and bank collapse that obstructed channel flow and contributed to local flooding of adjacent areas; the river is culverted through parts of the town and these failures affected flow conveyance. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Predicted surface–water flood depths and extents are concentrated along the river channel in the 1:100 and 1:1000 maps; the river corridor is associated with inundation of low–lying areas and the town centre and with property/basement flooding events recorded in winter 2000/01 and during the event described in this report. [PAG,MUL] (p12)
The River Misbourne produced flows of river water that ran down the High Street, causing garden and property flooding in Old Amersham during multiple events (including winter 2000/1, March 2013, August 2013 and January–February 2014). [PAG,MUL] (p13)
The River Misbourne experienced prolonged high water levels from November 2013 to mid–February 2014, with two particularly severe flooding events on/around 17 January and 06/07 February 2014. The high river levels and saturated catchment caused the river and highway drainage to be exceeded, contributed to surface and highway runoff, and a riverbank collapse occurred downstream of the Jaguar garage on 07 February 2014, allowing river water to flow into surrounding areas. Sedimentation and weirs downstream of The Maltings impeded flushing and exacerbated conveyance problems. [MUL] (p20)
High river levels in the River Misbourne, combined with saturated catchment and large surface runoff, exceeded channel and highway drainage capacity. Riverbank collapse allowed river water to flow into surrounding areas; backing up through drainage gullies contributed to flooding of High Street and nearby properties. A partial culvert restriction reduced conveyance until cleared by the EA. [PAG,MUL] (p21)
High river and groundwater levels led to elevated Misbourne flows; a tampered/damaged flap valve allowed river water to back up and flow east along the High Street, and high river levels prevented return flow through downstream valves. (p23)
High river levels and a partial flow restriction within a culvert beneath The Maltings caused flooding of gardens and High Street properties in Old Amersham. Removal of an obstruction to a flap valve on 18 January ended the bulk of High Street flooding. A pump was installed on 06 February and jetting of the restricted culvert (11–12 February) reduced water levels substantially (300 mm drop on 11 Feb and a further 300 mm on 12 Feb) and restored most flow through the culvert. Downstream bank collapse near the Jaguar garage required emergency bank restoration and a boom to protect against pollution. Sandbags and FloodSax were distributed to affected properties. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)
Culvert flow restriction and obstructions on the River Misbourne caused flooding of Old Amersham High Street; removal of a flap valve obstruction ended the bulk of High Street flooding. A bank collapse downstream of the Jaguar garage required multi–agency repair and pollution control. A tree removal downstream lowered water levels and culvert jetting substantially reduced water levels and restored flow; EA planned repairs and later multi–agency meetings addressed culvert/weir removal to reduce future risk. [PAG,MUL] (p25)
Weirs and culvert flow restrictions on the Misbourne were identified as contributing to increased flood risk; a multi–agency meeting (2 July 2014) led to agreement in principle (mid–July 2014) between the Environment Agency and riparian owners to remove weirs to reduce flooding risk. [TAB] (p26)
The River Misbourne overtopped/caused flooding in winter 2013/14 (January–February 2014), flooding properties in Old Amersham and prompting recovery actions. The event led to requirements for culvert and gully inspections, clearance of vegetation and debris, installation of trash screens on culverts, and coordination between RMAs and riparian owners to reduce blockages and restore flow. [PAG] (p33)",4 residential properties; 5 business premises (p23),GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.6341785,-0.4172156,"<div id=""html_8e941dc31fb8438202eb30c67e878a56"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Colne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater across the Colne catchment was reported as 'exceptionally high' during the flooding period, and saturated soils (SMD = 0 in February 2014) meant continued rainfall produced widespread runoff and elevated river levels across the catchment; the EA treated the three&ndash;month period leading up to 08 March 2014 as a single event because of groundwater influence. [PAG,MUL] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Colne,England,"Groundwater across the Colne catchment was reported as 'exceptionally high' during the flooding period, and saturated soils (SMD = 0 in February 2014) meant continued rainfall produced widespread runoff and elevated river levels across the catchment; the EA treated the three–month period leading up to 08 March 2014 as a single event because of groundwater influence. [PAG,MUL] (p19)",,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.6736182,-0.6319411,"<div id=""html_fc9b2ce812db57f6bac1d80f98650221"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Old Amersham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A blockage of a drainage asset caused a secondary flow down the High Street, producing flooding of the street. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties along the High Street were inundated by flows of river water. Several properties flooded in winter 2000/1; at least one of those properties was flooded internally again during the January&ndash;February 2014 event. A garden backing onto the Misbourne flooded on three occasions prior to 2014 (including March 2013 and August 2013). Two businesses reported that barriers installed after 2000/1 protected their premises during the January&ndash;February 2014 event. [PAG,MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Garden of a household backing onto the River Misbourne was flooded; flows of river water ran down the High Street from west to east during flood events, matching the flows witnessed in the main report event. [PAG,MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High Street gullies designed to discharge to the River Misbourne were partly blocked with sediment and were largely unable to discharge at full capacity because of high water levels in the Misbourne, reducing highway drainage capacity and contributing to surface water/urban flooding of the highway and surrounding area. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On and after 17 January 2014 the High Street was flooded (road and above kerb line) when a flap valve was blocked, causing river water to back up through a nearby gully and flow eastward along the street; water entered basements (notably the Elephant and Castle pub). Parking and movement of temporary defences affected flow paths. The most severe event overnight 06&ndash;07 February 2014 caused internal flooding of properties along the High Street for several days. The primary cause included a partial culvert restriction under The Maltings; EA bypass pumping and culvert jetting (from 12 Feb 2014) removed the restriction and halted further flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water from the River Misbourne backed up and flowed eastward down the High Street; highway drainage was overwhelmed. Business activities were indirectly affected by impacts to parking and pavement use by customers. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The High Street experienced flooding driven by high river levels and a culvert/flap&ndash;valve obstruction; sandbags and FloodSax were distributed to protect at&ndash;risk properties; removal of the flap&ndash;valve obstruction on 18 January ended the bulk of the flooding; ATC cleared debris in a High Street property's garden and responders door&ndash;knocked to update residents. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High Street was flooded due to a culvert / flap&ndash;valve obstruction on the River Misbourne. Agencies removed the flap&ndash;valve obstruction which ended the bulk of the High Street flooding; pumps were installed to alleviate flooding; sandbags and FloodSax were distributed and residents door&ndash;knocked; debris in the garden of one High Street property was cleared. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water from the River Misbourne backed up (after tampering/damage to a flap valve) and flowed eastward down the High Street; high river levels then prevented return flow through downstream valves, prolonging flooding on the High Street. (p30)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least one property; two businesses; three occasions [PAG,MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (Elephant and Castle pub basement) [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>200 sandbags; one High Street property [PAG] (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"High Street, Old Amersham",England,"A blockage of a drainage asset caused a secondary flow down the High Street, producing flooding of the street. (p4)
Properties along the High Street were inundated by flows of river water. Several properties flooded in winter 2000/1; at least one of those properties was flooded internally again during the January–February 2014 event. A garden backing onto the Misbourne flooded on three occasions prior to 2014 (including March 2013 and August 2013). Two businesses reported that barriers installed after 2000/1 protected their premises during the January–February 2014 event. [PAG,MUL] (p13)
Garden of a household backing onto the River Misbourne was flooded; flows of river water ran down the High Street from west to east during flood events, matching the flows witnessed in the main report event. [PAG,MUL] (p14)
