GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.6691473,-0.6228362,"<div id=""html_459b52d23b3546bec281db60e6cb7802"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mill Lane, Old Amersham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The culvert at Mill Lane was included among the TfB culverts implicated in flow restrictions during the flood event and is identified for continued inspection and maintenance to reduce future flood risk. [TAB,PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culverts in the stretch from Mill Lane to the culvert under the A413 were implicated in the flooding response: they were identified for increased inspection and maintenance and for cleansing to remove blockages affecting highway drainage. Actions specified include inspections of culverts ≥900mm, jetting of drains and gullies and prioritised repair of detected flow restrictions to reduce recurrence. (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Mill Lane, Old Amersham",England,"The culvert at Mill Lane was included among the TfB culverts implicated in flow restrictions during the flood event and is identified for continued inspection and maintenance to reduce future flood risk. [TAB,PAG] (p31)
Culverts in the stretch from Mill Lane to the culvert under the A413 were implicated in the flooding response: they were identified for increased inspection and maintenance and for cleansing to remove blockages affecting highway drainage. Actions specified include inspections of culverts ≥900mm, jetting of drains and gullies and prioritised repair of detected flow restrictions to reduce recurrence. (p32)",,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_9605ac77a056800d49867337371607ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Amersham (in Buckinghamshire) experienced flooding during the January&ndash;February 2014 event; the report investigates that flooding and provides recommendations to Risk Management Agencies to prevent a repeat. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High river levels and a partial culvert flow restriction in Old Amersham caused flooding of the High Street and gardens; residents and businesses reported internal flooding of properties. Removal of an obstruction to a flap valve ended the bulk of High Street flooding. A bank collapsed downstream of the Jaguar garage and was restored in a multi&ndash;agency response. Emergency measures across the county included sandbag and FloodSax distribution, pump installation to alleviate culvert&ndash;caused flooding, culvert jetting to restore flow and multi&ndash;agency coordination. [TAB] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Amersham (within Buckinghamshire) experienced High Street flooding caused by a partial culvert flow restriction under The Maltings and an obstructed flap valve. Properties and gardens were at risk and received sandbags/FloodSax and door&ndash;to&ndash;door updates; a pump was installed to relieve flooding; a bank collapse downstream of the Jaguar garage was repaired (restoration work to the river bank); a storm drain overflow blockage near the superstore was cleared; jetting of the culvert reduced water levels and restored flow. (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"Old Amersham (in Buckinghamshire) experienced flooding during the January–February 2014 event; the report investigates that flooding and provides recommendations to Risk Management Agencies to prevent a repeat. [PAG] (p3)
High river levels and a partial culvert flow restriction in Old Amersham caused flooding of the High Street and gardens; residents and businesses reported internal flooding of properties. Removal of an obstruction to a flap valve ended the bulk of High Street flooding. A bank collapsed downstream of the Jaguar garage and was restored in a multi–agency response. Emergency measures across the county included sandbag and FloodSax distribution, pump installation to alleviate culvert–caused flooding, culvert jetting to restore flow and multi–agency coordination. [TAB] (p24)
Old Amersham (within Buckinghamshire) experienced High Street flooding caused by a partial culvert flow restriction under The Maltings and an obstructed flap valve. Properties and gardens were at risk and received sandbags/FloodSax and door–to–door updates; a pump was installed to relieve flooding; a bank collapse downstream of the Jaguar garage was repaired (restoration work to the river bank); a storm drain overflow blockage near the superstore was cleared; jetting of the culvert reduced water levels and restored flow. (p25)",,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.840052299999996,-0.09785519224523963,"<div id=""html_2b0323135a334356197893e8d030bd6e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hertfordshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hatfield Area Incident Room opened to respond to widespread flooding across the EA Hertfordshire and North London area; EA issued newsletters to residents informing of high river levels and investigations. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Hertfordshire,England,Hatfield Area Incident Room opened to respond to widespread flooding across the EA Hertfordshire and North London area; EA issued newsletters to residents informing of high river levels and investigations. (p24),,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.665451,-0.6170104,"<div id=""html_c414865d250d030e5ab131311dbeed5e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Old Amersham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Town experienced elevated river levels and backing up of the River Misbourne after prolonged above&ndash;average rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater levels. A culvert flow restriction caused the river to back up; a blockage of a drainage asset produced a secondary flow down the High Street; a riverbank collapse caused property flooding in the town. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of business premises and flooding of residential properties within the Old Amersham area. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Misbourne runs west&ndash;east through Old Amersham; flood&ndash;related issues were recorded within the town including a restricted culvert under The Maltings and a bank collapse near the Jaguar garage that affected the river channel behind houses on the northern side of the High Street and along the southern edge of Barn Meadow Recreation Ground. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Misbourne runs west&ndash;east through Old Amersham passing behind houses on the northern side of the High Street and along the southern edge of Barn Meadow. Within the town a culvert under The Maltings had a restricted flow (location A, SU 95599 97438) and there was a bank collapse near the Jaguar garage on the A355 (location B, SU 96533 96984). Highway gullies in Old Amersham connect to the surface water drainage system which discharges to the River Misbourne and the road drainage along the High Street flows into the river. [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Culvert flow restrictions and bank collapse locations were recorded in the town; highway gullies and the surface water drainage network (owned by TfB) discharge to the Misbourne, linking surface water drainage failure to local flooding in Old Amersham. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Locations within Old Amersham suffered culvert flow restriction and a bank collapse that obstructed flow through culverted sections beneath the town and contributed to local flooding; highway gullies along the High Street drain into the Misbourne and are connected to surface water drainage that influenced flood routing. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on the High Street were flooded; at least one property flooded internally during the event described in this report (January&ndash;February 2014). Basement flooding and flooding to the rear of St Mary's Church occurred in winter 2000/01. Two businesses reported that flood barriers installed after 2000/01 protected their premises during the later event. [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Misbourne overtopped/flowed through Old Amersham causing floodwaters to run down the High Street (west to east). Several properties on the High Street were flooded in the winter 2000/01 event (including basement flooding and flooding to the rear of St Mary&#8217;s Church). During the January&ndash;February 2014 event at least one property on the High Street was flooded internally again; two businesses reported their post&ndash;2000/01 flood barriers protected their premises during the 2014 event. A household with a garden backing onto the river reported garden flooding on three prior occasions, including March 2013 and August 2013, with similar river flows down the High Street as observed in the 2014 event. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Old Amersham experienced flooding associated with elevated river levels during peaks on 07&ndash;08 February 2014. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged flooding from November 2013 to mid&ndash;February 2014. Internal property flooding began around 17 January 2014 when a flap valve was lodged open by debris, causing river water to back up through a gully and flow down the High Street into basements (including the Elephant and Castle pub). The most severe event occurred overnight 06&ndash;07 February 2014, with several properties along the High Street flooded internally for days. A partial flow restriction in a culvert (under The Maltings) reduced conveyance; EA installed bypass pumping and jetted the culvert (12 February 2014) which stopped further flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Amersham experienced flooding caused by a partial flow restriction in a culvert beneath The Maltings and high river levels on the River Misbourne; EA and CDC investigated and responded (sandbag/FloodSax distribution, pump installation, jetting to clear culvert); a bank collapse downstream of the Jaguar garage was repaired as a multi&ndash;agency action and a tree removal lowered water levels downstream. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Amersham experienced flooding caused by a partial flow restriction in a culvert under The Maltings on the River Misbourne. EA and CDC distributed sandbags and FloodSax to properties, door&ndash;knocking and resident newsletters informed of culvert restrictions and repair plans. Jetting of the restricted culvert reduced water levels (300 mm drop on 11/02 and a further 300 mm drop on 12/02) and by 12/02 EA estimated ~98% of flow was passing through the culvert. Pumps were installed to relieve flooding and multi&ndash;agency coordination and flood surgeries were held. [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged high River Misbourne levels together with exceptionally high groundwater caused prolonged flooding in Old Amersham, including two severe flood events affecting the settlement. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Amersham residents and businesses were affected by flooding from the River Misbourne; recommendations include distributing guidance to adjacent residents, offering Repair and Renew grant support and business recovery support, and establishing community flood&ndash;action coordination and maintenance of riparian responsibilities. [TAB,PAG] (p31)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two or more business premises [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two locations (culvert flow restriction at The Maltings; bank collapse near Jaguar garage) [IMG,PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least one property; two businesses [IMG,PAG,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 1 property flooded internally; 2 businesses protected by barriers; garden flooded on 3 occasions (including March 2013 and August 2013) [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (Elephant and Castle pub basement) [PAG,MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>28 Hippo bags used to restore the river bank; tree removal lowered water levels by 100&ndash;150 mm; water level dropped ~300 mm on 11 February and a further ~300 mm on 12 February; 98% of flow restored through culvert after jetting [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>300 mm (drop on 11/02/2014); 300 mm (drop on 12/02/2014); 98% of flow through culvert after jetting; 200 sandbags [PAG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two severe flood events (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Old Amersham,England,"Town experienced elevated river levels and backing up of the River Misbourne after prolonged above–average rainfall and exceptionally high groundwater levels. A culvert flow restriction caused the river to back up; a blockage of a drainage asset produced a secondary flow down the High Street; a riverbank collapse caused property flooding in the town. (p4)
