GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.66688635,-0.624285834301358,"<div id=""html_8f6835811a329a8d14907bfd5860dd68"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Amersham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Amersham experienced property flooding during the high&ndash;flow peaks on 07&ndash;08 February 2014. Local river levels in Amersham are strongly influenced by culverts, natural restrictions and blockages, so the upstream Little Missenden gauge (≈2.6 km upstream) only provides an indication of the local high water conditions that led to property flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Old Amersham experienced a prolonged period of flooding (Nov 2013 to mid&ndash;Feb 2014) with particularly severe events on 17 Jan 2014 and 06&ndash;07 Feb 2014. Saturated catchment and heavy rainfall produced large runoff that, together with high River Misbourne levels, exceeded the capacity of the river and highway drainage. Local drainage assets were flow&ndash;restricted: the culvert under The Maltings was partly restricted; High Street gullies were partly blocked with sediment and largely unable to discharge because of high Misbourne levels; downstream weirs and sedimentation impeded flushing and contributed to backing up of water and reduced culvert outflow. A riverbank collapse on 07 Feb 2014 downstream of the Jaguar garage allowed river water to flow into surrounding areas. Overall the drainage infrastructure could not adequately convey additional surface and highway runoff. [MUL] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Amersham,England,"Old Amersham experienced property flooding during the high–flow peaks on 07–08 February 2014. Local river levels in Amersham are strongly influenced by culverts, natural restrictions and blockages, so the upstream Little Missenden gauge (≈2.6 km upstream) only provides an indication of the local high water conditions that led to property flooding. [PAG,MUL] (p18)
Old Amersham experienced a prolonged period of flooding (Nov 2013 to mid–Feb 2014) with particularly severe events on 17 Jan 2014 and 06–07 Feb 2014. Saturated catchment and heavy rainfall produced large runoff that, together with high River Misbourne levels, exceeded the capacity of the river and highway drainage. Local drainage assets were flow–restricted: the culvert under The Maltings was partly restricted; High Street gullies were partly blocked with sediment and largely unable to discharge because of high Misbourne levels; downstream weirs and sedimentation impeded flushing and contributed to backing up of water and reduced culvert outflow. A riverbank collapse on 07 Feb 2014 downstream of the Jaguar garage allowed river water to flow into surrounding areas. Overall the drainage infrastructure could not adequately convey additional surface and highway runoff. [MUL] (p20)",,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,1,2014-01-01,51.681848711830746,-0.6677507653552992,"<div id=""html_239276e6020417e40ceadee809edfc42"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Missenden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Telemetered river gauge at Little Missenden recorded exceptionally high river levels during the winter 2013&ndash;2014 event; the entire event is ranked number 1 of the top 10 recorded levels at this site. Peaks occurred on 07&ndash;08 February 2014 (coincident with much of the property flooding in the area) and the highest peak was recorded on 08 March 2014. [PAG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Little Missenden gauge recorded exceptionally high river levels across the three&ndash;month event to March 2014; the record for the gauge lists the whole period as the site&#8217;s number 1 top&ndash;10 level event. Peaks occurred on 07&ndash;08 February 2014 when much property flooding occurred locally, and the highest recorded peak at the gauge was on 08 March 2014. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>number 1 of the top 10 recorded levels at the gauge [PAG,MUL] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>459 mm; 443 mm [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Little Missenden,England,"Telemetered river gauge at Little Missenden recorded exceptionally high river levels during the winter 2013–2014 event; the entire event is ranked number 1 of the top 10 recorded levels at this site. Peaks occurred on 07–08 February 2014 (coincident with much of the property flooding in the area) and the highest peak was recorded on 08 March 2014. [PAG,MUL] (p17)
The Little Missenden gauge recorded exceptionally high river levels across the three–month event to March 2014; the record for the gauge lists the whole period as the site’s number 1 top–10 level event. Peaks occurred on 07–08 February 2014 when much property flooding occurred locally, and the highest recorded peak at the gauge was on 08 March 2014. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p19)","number 1 of the top 10 recorded levels at the gauge [PAG,MUL] (p17)
459 mm; 443 mm [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p19)",GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01.html,GB-BKM_1_2014-01-01,Old Amersham flooding January–February 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Old Amersham, Amersham January – February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/141017-old-amersham-s19-report/final2.pdf
GB-BKM,3,2013-12-24,51.78332268672904,-0.8297786104320022,"<div id=""html_96e34fac570d4f8af65af186342b3857"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Rookery</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Rookery area experienced the deepest surface&ndash;water inundation in the village during the event; map predictions (which the report states reflect the observed flooding) show the highest predicted depths concentrated near The Rookery and to the east of the village centre. [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,The Rookery,England,"The Rookery area experienced the deepest surface–water inundation in the village during the event; map predictions (which the report states reflect the observed flooding) show the highest predicted depths concentrated near The Rookery and to the east of the village centre. [PAG,IMG] (p11)",,GB-BKM_3_2013-12-24.html,GB-BKM_3_2013-12-24,Bishopstone flooding 24 Dec 2013 - 14 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bishopstone, Aylesbury 24th December 2013 – 14th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bishopstone-aylesbury/Feb/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,3,2013-12-24,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_1a964972a34c9885b3c77e1a2490ce44"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water flooding occurred in and around Bishopstone during 24 Dec 2013 &ndash; 14 Feb 2014, with the greatest flooding in the south&ndash;east of the village. Flood depths during the event matched the surface&ndash;water flood maps: most of the built&ndash;up area experienced shallow to moderate inundation, while deeper inundation was concentrated adjacent to streams, ponds and other low&ndash;lying watercourse corridors to the south and south&ndash;east of the village centre. [IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Buckinghamshire County Council (the LLFA) was contacted by residents who experienced internal property flooding and referred them to AVDC; BCC coordinated local flood risk management duties during the event. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"Surface–water flooding occurred in and around Bishopstone during 24 Dec 2013 – 14 Feb 2014, with the greatest flooding in the south–east of the village. Flood depths during the event matched the surface–water flood maps: most of the built–up area experienced shallow to moderate inundation, while deeper inundation was concentrated adjacent to streams, ponds and other low–lying watercourse corridors to the south and south–east of the village centre. [IMG] (p12)
Buckinghamshire County Council (the LLFA) was contacted by residents who experienced internal property flooding and referred them to AVDC; BCC coordinated local flood risk management duties during the event. (p18)",,GB-BKM_3_2013-12-24.html,GB-BKM_3_2013-12-24,Bishopstone flooding 24 Dec 2013 - 14 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bishopstone, Aylesbury 24th December 2013 – 14th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bishopstone-aylesbury/Feb/2014.pdf
GB-BKM,3,2013-12-24,51.78584831257912,-0.8352966833244703,"<div id=""html_b43ea21451fddc80d212c6a78be960da"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bishopstone</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged above&ndash;average rainfall saturated surrounding land and increased surface water flows into highway drainage and main rivers. Unmaintained rivers, undersized culverts and tight river bends caused rivers to overtop; floodwaters used the highway as a secondary flow route. (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Bishopstone,England,"Prolonged above–average rainfall saturated surrounding land and increased surface water flows into highway drainage and main rivers. Unmaintained rivers, undersized culverts and tight river bends caused rivers to overtop; floodwaters used the highway as a secondary flow route. (p4)",,GB-BKM_3_2013-12-24.html,GB-BKM_3_2013-12-24,Bishopstone flooding 24 Dec 2013 - 14 Feb 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Bishopstone, Aylesbury 24th December 2013 – 14th February 2014",018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/bishopstone-aylesbury/Feb/2014.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,53.014608442604576,-1.7380900132958323,"<div id=""html_54612b425d896ba73edfd3f4e9a23ea7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Oswald's Church Hall</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Oswald's Church Centre (hall) was internally flooded during Storm Babet; it is listed among the three non&ndash;residential properties that flooded in the community. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water affected the church hall area; channels were excavated through the top of the flood embankment to the west of the church hall to allow flood water to drain away. Excavations were carried out around 15:00 on 20 October 2023. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 non&ndash;residential property (part of 3 non&ndash;residential properties flooded) (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 cm depth (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,St Oswald's Church Hall,England,"Oswald's Church Centre (hall) was internally flooded during Storm Babet; it is listed among the three non–residential properties that flooded in the community. (p4)
Flood water affected the church hall area; channels were excavated through the top of the flood embankment to the west of the church hall to allow flood water to drain away. Excavations were carried out around 15:00 on 20 October 2023. (p8)","1 non–residential property (part of 3 non–residential properties flooded) (p4)
23 cm depth (p8)",GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
