Internally flooded after flood water overtopped banks adjacent to School Lane bridge and flowed southwards toward the hall. (p8)",1 non–residential property (part of 3 non–residential properties flooded) [PAG] (p4),GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,53.016269,-1.7323899,"<div id=""html_5da62c0516d45736f23f209313725629"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internally flooded during Storm Babet as one of the non&ndash;residential properties affected in the western part of Ashbourne on Dig Street; flooding was caused by the Henmore Brook / associated river flooding on the local floodplain. (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 non&ndash;residential property (Bridge Public House); part of 16 properties internally flooded in the community; part of 3 non&ndash;residential properties flooded (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,The Bridge,England,Internally flooded during Storm Babet as one of the non–residential properties affected in the western part of Ashbourne on Dig Street; flooding was caused by the Henmore Brook / associated river flooding on the local floodplain. (p4),1 non–residential property (Bridge Public House); part of 16 properties internally flooded in the community; part of 3 non–residential properties flooded (p4),GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,53.015750582324905,-1.7365591434184937,"<div id=""html_db1528c46279c2e7ee0b17c8341f14e4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Almshouses</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in the Almshouses complex on School Lane; the majority of the community's flooded homes were in this complex. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties within the Almshouses complex had floodwater enter the courtyard and then the properties through doorways after Henmore Brook overtopped adjacent defences; internal flooding depths reported up to 15 cm. The protective embankment did not overtop but was temporarily lowered after the peak to allow trapped water to drain. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>part of the 10 residential properties on School Lane that were internally flooded; internal flooding up to 15 cm [PAG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Almshouses,England,"Internal flooding of properties in the Almshouses complex on School Lane; the majority of the community's flooded homes were in this complex. (p4)
Properties within the Almshouses complex had floodwater enter the courtyard and then the properties through doorways after Henmore Brook overtopped adjacent defences; internal flooding depths reported up to 15 cm. The protective embankment did not overtop but was temporarily lowered after the peak to allow trapped water to drain. [PAG] (p7)",part of the 10 residential properties on School Lane that were internally flooded; internal flooding up to 15 cm [PAG] (p7),GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,53.0117523,-1.7417989,"<div id=""html_da32728150e02f6f8e3ddd60d2928154"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Henmore Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Responded rapidly to Storm Babet (short lag between peak rainfall and peak flow) and produced high flows that contributed to flooding in Ashbourne; peak flow during Storm Babet was the second highest on record at the Ashbourne gauging station. [PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Henmore Brook had a very large peak flow during Storm Babet (second highest on the gauging station record), with a short 3&ndash;hour lag from peak rainfall to peak flow; the brook on its floodplain contributed to internal flooding of properties to the west of Ashbourne. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overbanking of Henmore Brook during the storm caused river flooding that inundated the adjacent residential community in central Ashbourne. Floodwaters entered homes in the Almshouses on School Lane and properties on Coachmans Close and affected non&ndash;residential buildings including St Oswald&#8217;s Church Centre (hall), Kingdom Hall and the Bridge Public House on Dig Street. [PAG,IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from Henmore Brook overtopped flood defences and caused internal property flooding in the adjacent Ashbourne community (properties on School Lane, Coachmans Close and nearby non&ndash;residential buildings). Flood warnings were issued for the Henmore Brook at Ashbourne during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as the primary source of flooding during Storm Babet; overtopping of Henmore Brook banks and a retaining wall allowed flow to escape the channel and inundate adjacent streets and properties (notably School Lane and Coachmans Close). [IMG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Banks of Henmore Brook overtopped, sending water northwards along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and properties; overtopping adjacent to School Lane bridge also directed flows that flooded nearby buildings. Flood levels exceeded the previous 1% AEP design standard at this location and at Coachmans Close. The embankment protecting the Almshouses did not overtop but was purposefully lowered after Storm Babet's peak to allow trapped water to drain and was subsequently reinstated. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as the subject of post&ndash;event actions: the Environment Agency is updating the Henmore Brook flood model to investigate the current standard of protection provided by defences and to test whether water can be stored earlier in the Ashbourne Flood Storage Reservoir during lower&ndash;magnitude events. These actions follow flooding on 20 October 2023. (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak flow was the second highest in the station's record; lag time between peak rainfall and peak river flow recorded in Ashbourne was 3 hours. [PAG,MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 properties [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 purpose built flood defences overtopped [IMG,PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>16 internally flooded properties (community total) [IMG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 residential properties; up to 15 cm depth [PAG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Henmore Brook,England,"Responded rapidly to Storm Babet (short lag between peak rainfall and peak flow) and produced high flows that contributed to flooding in Ashbourne; peak flow during Storm Babet was the second highest on record at the Ashbourne gauging station. [PAG,MUL] (p3)
Henmore Brook had a very large peak flow during Storm Babet (second highest on the gauging station record), with a short 3–hour lag from peak rainfall to peak flow; the brook on its floodplain contributed to internal flooding of properties to the west of Ashbourne. [PAG] (p4)
Overbanking of Henmore Brook during the storm caused river flooding that inundated the adjacent residential community in central Ashbourne. Floodwaters entered homes in the Almshouses on School Lane and properties on Coachmans Close and affected non–residential buildings including St Oswald’s Church Centre (hall), Kingdom Hall and the Bridge Public House on Dig Street. [PAG,IMG] (p5)
Flooding from Henmore Brook overtopped flood defences and caused internal property flooding in the adjacent Ashbourne community (properties on School Lane, Coachmans Close and nearby non–residential buildings). Flood warnings were issued for the Henmore Brook at Ashbourne during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p6)
Identified as the primary source of flooding during Storm Babet; overtopping of Henmore Brook banks and a retaining wall allowed flow to escape the channel and inundate adjacent streets and properties (notably School Lane and Coachmans Close). [IMG] (p7)
Banks of Henmore Brook overtopped, sending water northwards along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and properties; overtopping adjacent to School Lane bridge also directed flows that flooded nearby buildings. Flood levels exceeded the previous 1% AEP design standard at this location and at Coachmans Close. The embankment protecting the Almshouses did not overtop but was purposefully lowered after Storm Babet's peak to allow trapped water to drain and was subsequently reinstated. [PAG] (p8)
Described as the subject of post–event actions: the Environment Agency is updating the Henmore Brook flood model to investigate the current standard of protection provided by defences and to test whether water can be stored earlier in the Ashbourne Flood Storage Reservoir during lower–magnitude events. These actions follow flooding on 20 October 2023. (p9)","Peak flow was the second highest in the station's record; lag time between peak rainfall and peak river flow recorded in Ashbourne was 3 hours. [PAG,MUL] (p3)
16 properties [PAG] (p4)
3 purpose built flood defences overtopped [IMG,PAG] (p6)
