10 residential properties; up to 15 cm depth [PAG] (p8)",GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,52.8605665,-1.6356159,"<div id=""html_0261268e6b4d0762552e925156162168"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hilton Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hilton Brook is the main river flowing through the community where internal property flooding occurred during Storm Babet; the text and map link watercourse locations and flooded streets, so the brook is likely a contributing source to the internal flooding in Hilton. [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflow of Hilton Brook onto its floodplain caused internal flooding of properties in Hilton during Storm Babet; most flooded homes were west of the brook. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The community sits on the Hilton Brook floodplain; most flooded homes were west of Hilton Brook. Internal flooding of properties in the community occurred during Storm Babet. A flood relief channel and engineered high ground adjacent to Uttoxeter Road are identified along the brook. [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hilton Brook overflowed upstream of Hilton village, overtopped its channel (upstream of the relief channel) and caused flooding onto Uttoxeter Road and into nearby properties. A blocked culvert beneath Uttoxeter Road was reported to have contributed to upstream overtopping. The Sutton Mill gauge on Hilton Brook recorded its highest&ndash;ever level during the event. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overflowed (about 600 m upstream of the Uttoxeter Road crossing) and overtopped upstream of the village; a blocked culvert beneath Uttoxeter Road caused the channel to overtop and flood onto Uttoxeter Road, with floodwater flowing westwards and along flow routes to Main Street and Mill Lane, internally flooding local properties. Internal depths reached almost 1 m at some properties; some semi&ndash;detached properties are known to flood frequently; one property had a sump and pump; a few properties only flooded externally. [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hilton Brook overtopped/caused flooding at Sutton on the Hill and Uttoxeter Road (Hilton), leading to property flooding. Sandbags provided to some properties were overwhelmed by high flood levels. The flood relief channel was inspected and found to be operating as designed; investigations are exploring redirecting floodwater that currently escapes upstream back into the Hilton Brook channel to reduce future property flooding. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 residential properties; 2 commercial properties [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 homes; 3 commercial properties [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak gauge level 1.84 m at Sutton Mill (highest recorded at that gauge) [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties on Uttoxeter Road; 1 home on Mill Lane; five homes and three commercial properties (reported) [PAG,IMG] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Hilton Brook,England,"Hilton Brook is the main river flowing through the community where internal property flooding occurred during Storm Babet; the text and map link watercourse locations and flooded streets, so the brook is likely a contributing source to the internal flooding in Hilton. [IMG] (p9)
Overflow of Hilton Brook onto its floodplain caused internal flooding of properties in Hilton during Storm Babet; most flooded homes were west of the brook. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
The community sits on the Hilton Brook floodplain; most flooded homes were west of Hilton Brook. Internal flooding of properties in the community occurred during Storm Babet. A flood relief channel and engineered high ground adjacent to Uttoxeter Road are identified along the brook. [PAG,IMG] (p11)
Hilton Brook overflowed upstream of Hilton village, overtopped its channel (upstream of the relief channel) and caused flooding onto Uttoxeter Road and into nearby properties. A blocked culvert beneath Uttoxeter Road was reported to have contributed to upstream overtopping. The Sutton Mill gauge on Hilton Brook recorded its highest–ever level during the event. [PAG] (p12)
Overflowed (about 600 m upstream of the Uttoxeter Road crossing) and overtopped upstream of the village; a blocked culvert beneath Uttoxeter Road caused the channel to overtop and flood onto Uttoxeter Road, with floodwater flowing westwards and along flow routes to Main Street and Mill Lane, internally flooding local properties. Internal depths reached almost 1 m at some properties; some semi–detached properties are known to flood frequently; one property had a sump and pump; a few properties only flooded externally. [PAG,IMG] (p13)
Hilton Brook overtopped/caused flooding at Sutton on the Hill and Uttoxeter Road (Hilton), leading to property flooding. Sandbags provided to some properties were overwhelmed by high flood levels. The flood relief channel was inspected and found to be operating as designed; investigations are exploring redirecting floodwater that currently escapes upstream back into the Hilton Brook channel to reduce future property flooding. [PAG] (p14)","4 residential properties; 2 commercial properties [PAG,IMG] (p10)
5 homes; 3 commercial properties [PAG,IMG] (p11)
Peak gauge level 1.84 m at Sutton Mill (highest recorded at that gauge) [PAG] (p12)
7 properties on Uttoxeter Road; 1 home on Mill Lane; five homes and three commercial properties (reported) [PAG,IMG] (p13)",GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,52.8826914,-1.8231664,"<div id=""html_066debf12416035441ff7c0d9bbef9d0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Dove</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Dove sub&ndash;catchment experienced hydrological effects during Storm Babet (changes to river flows and levels across the Dove catchment). (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Dove sub&ndash;catchment experienced extreme rainfall during Storm Babet which drove high river flows in the catchment; Henmore Brook is a tributary of the Dove and contributed to flooding in Ashbourne. (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1.9% to 3.9% AEP (recorded rainfall equating to these AEPs across stations in the catchment) (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Dove,England,"The Dove sub–catchment experienced hydrological effects during Storm Babet (changes to river flows and levels across the Dove catchment). (p1)
The Dove sub–catchment experienced extreme rainfall during Storm Babet which drove high river flows in the catchment; Henmore Brook is a tributary of the Dove and contributed to flooding in Ashbourne. (p3)",1.9% to 3.9% AEP (recorded rainfall equating to these AEPs across stations in the catchment) (p3),GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,53.01416210959004,-1.737360993864126,"<div id=""html_bcb4043b40a43673e13c14b90a7899e4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>School Lane Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Banks adjacent to the bridge overtopped, enabling flood water to flow toward the Kingdom Hall and northwards along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and properties, contributing to internal flooding. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As a short&ndash;term action following the flood on 20 October 2023, de&ndash;silting and removal of vegetation from School Lane Bridge are scheduled for 2024 to address issues identified during the event. (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 residential properties; up to 15 cm depth (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,School Lane Bridge,England,"Banks adjacent to the bridge overtopped, enabling flood water to flow toward the Kingdom Hall and northwards along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and properties, contributing to internal flooding. (p8)
As a short–term action following the flood on 20 October 2023, de–silting and removal of vegetation from School Lane Bridge are scheduled for 2024 to address issues identified during the event. (p9)",10 residential properties; up to 15 cm depth (p8),GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,53.0139355,-1.7374501,"<div id=""html_22f11866828f2093321560875585f4ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>School Lane, Ashbourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on School Lane (including the Almshouses) experienced internal flooding during Storm Babet; the area sits on the Henmore Brook floodplain. [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Almshouses complex on School Lane (majority of properties in the complex) were part of the community that flooded; properties experienced internal flooding during the event. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from Henmore Brook overtopped the banks and a retaining wall, flowed north along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and entered properties through doorways, causing internal flooding and prompting lowering of a protective embankment post&ndash;peak to drain trapped water (subsequently reinstated). [PAG,IMG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water overtopped Henmore Brook banks and a retaining wall, flowed north along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and entered properties through doorways; embankment protecting the Almshouses was purposefully lowered after the peak to release trapped water and later reinstated. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 residential properties and 2 non&ndash;residential properties internally flooded on School Lane [PAG,IMG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 residential properties; up to 15 cm internal flood depth [PAG] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"School Lane, Ashbourne",England,"Properties on School Lane (including the Almshouses) experienced internal flooding during Storm Babet; the area sits on the Henmore Brook floodplain. [PAG,IMG] (p4)
The Almshouses complex on School Lane (majority of properties in the complex) were part of the community that flooded; properties experienced internal flooding during the event. [PAG] (p5)
Floodwater from Henmore Brook overtopped the banks and a retaining wall, flowed north along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and entered properties through doorways, causing internal flooding and prompting lowering of a protective embankment post–peak to drain trapped water (subsequently reinstated). [PAG,IMG] (p7)
Flood water overtopped Henmore Brook banks and a retaining wall, flowed north along School Lane into the Almshouses courtyard and entered properties through doorways; embankment protecting the Almshouses was purposefully lowered after the peak to release trapped water and later reinstated. [PAG] (p8)","10 residential properties and 2 non–residential properties internally flooded on School Lane [PAG,IMG] (p7)
10 residential properties; up to 15 cm internal flood depth [PAG] (p8)",GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,5,2023-10-18,53.0146836,-1.737059,"<div id=""html_a0988c3d130ddfd901de7bae9079add8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coachmans Close, Ashbourne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Coachmans Close, north of Henmore Brook, suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet; parts of Coachmans Close are also identified on long&ndash;term mapping as having a high surface&ndash;water flood risk. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Coachmans Close were flooded during the event; properties experienced internal flooding as part of the community&ndash;wide flooding adjacent to Henmore Brook. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Coachmans Close were flooded during the event. Some parts of Coachmans Close are shown on the long&ndash;term surface water flood risk map as high risk (>3.3% AEP); more typically School Lane, Coachmans Close and Kingdom Hall have low (0.1&ndash;1% AEP) surface water flood chance. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed over the embankment north of the properties and flood defence wall to the east and south of Coachmans Close overtopped along its length, resulting in internal flooding of properties. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood levels in this location exceeded the previous 1% AEP design standard during the event. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood defences around Coachmans Close were identified as having low sections and embankment height issues during the response to the 20 October 2023 flooding. Short&ndash;term works planned for summer 2024 include raising the lowest sections of the defence wall to a consistent crest level and raising the embankment to match the coping stones. (p9)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 residential properties internally flooded to a reported depth of 0.5 m [PAG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"Coachmans Close, Ashbourne",England,"Residential properties on Coachmans Close, north of Henmore Brook, suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet; parts of Coachmans Close are also identified on long–term mapping as having a high surface–water flood risk. [PAG] (p4)
Residential properties on Coachmans Close were flooded during the event; properties experienced internal flooding as part of the community–wide flooding adjacent to Henmore Brook. [PAG] (p5)
Residential properties on Coachmans Close were flooded during the event. Some parts of Coachmans Close are shown on the long–term surface water flood risk map as high risk (>3.3% AEP); more typically School Lane, Coachmans Close and Kingdom Hall have low (0.1–1% AEP) surface water flood chance. [PAG] (p6)
Floodwater flowed over the embankment north of the properties and flood defence wall to the east and south of Coachmans Close overtopped along its length, resulting in internal flooding of properties. [PAG] (p7)
Flood levels in this location exceeded the previous 1% AEP design standard during the event. [PAG] (p8)
Flood defences around Coachmans Close were identified as having low sections and embankment height issues during the response to the 20 October 2023 flooding. Short–term works planned for summer 2024 include raising the lowest sections of the defence wall to a consistent crest level and raising the embankment to match the coping stones. (p9)",3 residential properties internally flooded to a reported depth of 0.5 m [PAG] (p7),GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
