5 homes; 3 commercial properties; 7 properties on Uttoxeter Road; 1 home on Mill Lane [PAG,IMG] (p13)",GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_5_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-5-dove-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-MDW,1,2018-05-29,51.3688234,0.5484471,"<div id=""html_15adc742118cee71a062bef3f55b5c1c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nelson Terrace, Chatham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The most extensive flooding occurred at Nelson Terrace, causing internal flooding to properties and resulting in displacement of residents from their homes. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Nelson Terrace experienced flooding during the event; gullies were reported blocked (inspected 27 Feb 2018 but could not all be cleaned due to parked vehicles) and flooding at Nelson Terrace was driven by overland flow from Capstone Road. Subsequent cleaning of two out of four gullies was carried out in August 2018. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Nelson Terrace was caused by overland flow from Capstone Road; the historic valley watercourses are now part of an underground sewerage system that was not designed to convey surrounding land drainage, contributing to flooding and surcharge of the foul system. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A major surface water flood incident occurred at Nelson Terrace: council officers visited to establish causes and extent; a Community Impact Assessment was undertaken; follow&ndash;up site meetings with impacted residents were delayed because residents were unable to return to the properties for approximately six months. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Major surface water flood incident: properties were flooded, officers visited to establish causes and extent, a Community Impact Assessment was undertaken, residents were unable to return to properties for approximately 6 months; sandbags were provided and a rest centre put on standby; follow&ndash;up site meetings and advice on property&ndash;level protection measures were provided during recovery. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident at Nelson Terrace escalated to emergency status; communication failures meant the council was not immediately informed and partners responded according to their own internal procedures rather than a coordinated response. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 6 months (residents unable to return) [PAG] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Medway,"Nelson Terrace, Chatham",England,"The most extensive flooding occurred at Nelson Terrace, causing internal flooding to properties and resulting in displacement of residents from their homes. (p10)
Nelson Terrace experienced flooding during the event; gullies were reported blocked (inspected 27 Feb 2018 but could not all be cleaned due to parked vehicles) and flooding at Nelson Terrace was driven by overland flow from Capstone Road. Subsequent cleaning of two out of four gullies was carried out in August 2018. (p12)
Flooding at Nelson Terrace was caused by overland flow from Capstone Road; the historic valley watercourses are now part of an underground sewerage system that was not designed to convey surrounding land drainage, contributing to flooding and surcharge of the foul system. [PAG] (p13)
A major surface water flood incident occurred at Nelson Terrace: council officers visited to establish causes and extent; a Community Impact Assessment was undertaken; follow–up site meetings with impacted residents were delayed because residents were unable to return to the properties for approximately six months. [PAG] (p14)
Major surface water flood incident: properties were flooded, officers visited to establish causes and extent, a Community Impact Assessment was undertaken, residents were unable to return to properties for approximately 6 months; sandbags were provided and a rest centre put on standby; follow–up site meetings and advice on property–level protection measures were provided during recovery. [PAG] (p15)
A flood incident at Nelson Terrace escalated to emergency status; communication failures meant the council was not immediately informed and partners responded according to their own internal procedures rather than a coordinated response. (p17)",approximately 6 months (residents unable to return) [PAG] (p14),GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29.html,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29,Medway flooding 29 May 2018,Medway Council S19 Flood Investigation Report,078/Medway/Flood/Investigation.pdf
GB-MDW,1,2018-05-29,51.366974,0.548746,"<div id=""html_bc83a790aabfecdca71418c470457beb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Capstone Road, Chatham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flow from Capstone Road was observed to cause flooding at lower&ndash;lying Nelson Terrace. Hydraulic overloading occurred in the Capstone Road area causing water to be released laterally through the highway construction which lifted the road; ingress of surface water into the foul system caused the foul system to surcharge. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hydraulic overloading of the sewer system in Capstone Road caused water pressure to be released laterally through the highway construction, lifting the road. Ingress of surface water into the foul sewer contributed to surcharge of the system. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Emergency survey of highways drainage infrastructure was carried out at Capstone Road after the flood event. A carriageway repair was undertaken. Southern Water CCTV surveys at Capstone Road identified a lining repair requirement at the junction with Hopewell Drive (works completed Oct&ndash;Nov 2018). Gully inspections were also carried out following the event. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Emergency survey of highways drainage infrastructure following the flooding event; carriageway repair carried out at Capstone Road; CCTV survey of several lengths of sewer identified a need for lining repair at the junction of Capstone Road and Hopewell Drive (another pipe constructed too close to the soffit, not causing obstruction to flow); lining works completed Oct&ndash;Nov 2018; less serious defects (e.g. cracking on pipe crown) recorded in Southern Water's asset database for future AMP planning. [PAG] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Medway,"Capstone Road, Chatham",England,"Overland flow from Capstone Road was observed to cause flooding at lower–lying Nelson Terrace. Hydraulic overloading occurred in the Capstone Road area causing water to be released laterally through the highway construction which lifted the road; ingress of surface water into the foul system caused the foul system to surcharge. [PAG] (p12)
Hydraulic overloading of the sewer system in Capstone Road caused water pressure to be released laterally through the highway construction, lifting the road. Ingress of surface water into the foul sewer contributed to surcharge of the system. [PAG] (p13)
Emergency survey of highways drainage infrastructure was carried out at Capstone Road after the flood event. A carriageway repair was undertaken. Southern Water CCTV surveys at Capstone Road identified a lining repair requirement at the junction with Hopewell Drive (works completed Oct–Nov 2018). Gully inspections were also carried out following the event. [TAB,PAG] (p15)
Emergency survey of highways drainage infrastructure following the flooding event; carriageway repair carried out at Capstone Road; CCTV survey of several lengths of sewer identified a need for lining repair at the junction of Capstone Road and Hopewell Drive (another pipe constructed too close to the soffit, not causing obstruction to flow); lining works completed Oct–Nov 2018; less serious defects (e.g. cracking on pipe crown) recorded in Southern Water's asset database for future AMP planning. [PAG] (p16)",,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29.html,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29,Medway flooding 29 May 2018,Medway Council S19 Flood Investigation Report,078/Medway/Flood/Investigation.pdf
GB-MDW,1,2018-05-29,51.365171,0.5460414,"<div id=""html_eae3a10fbb09b8d09c294cf68f799657"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hopewell Drive, Chatham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hopewell Drive was a surveyed location where structural deficiencies were suspected following flooding. CCTV identified that another pipe had been constructed too close to the soffit at the junction with Capstone Road (stated as not obstructing flow); lining repair works at that junction were completed Oct&ndash;Nov 2018 and defects were recorded for future asset planning. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as an area impacted by flooding where structural deficiencies were suspected; CCTV survey found lining repair was needed at the junction with Capstone Road because another pipe had been constructed too close to the soffit (not causing obstruction); lining works completed Oct&ndash;Nov 2018 and defects recorded for future AMP prioritisation. [PAG] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Medway,"Hopewell Drive, Chatham",England,"Hopewell Drive was a surveyed location where structural deficiencies were suspected following flooding. CCTV identified that another pipe had been constructed too close to the soffit at the junction with Capstone Road (stated as not obstructing flow); lining repair works at that junction were completed Oct–Nov 2018 and defects were recorded for future asset planning. [TAB,PAG] (p15)
Identified as an area impacted by flooding where structural deficiencies were suspected; CCTV survey found lining repair was needed at the junction with Capstone Road because another pipe had been constructed too close to the soffit (not causing obstruction); lining works completed Oct–Nov 2018 and defects recorded for future AMP prioritisation. [PAG] (p16)",,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29.html,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29,Medway flooding 29 May 2018,Medway Council S19 Flood Investigation Report,078/Medway/Flood/Investigation.pdf
GB-MDW,1,2018-05-29,51.4071779,0.5012466,"<div id=""html_bbca0b406b26ac075c5921712560464a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grasmere Grove, Wainscott</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A new highways drainage system was proposed for Grasmere Grove in response to the flooding incident (action recorded as achieved in the report). [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Medway,"Grasmere Grove, Wainscott",England,A new highways drainage system was proposed for Grasmere Grove in response to the flooding incident (action recorded as achieved in the report). [TAB] (p15),,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29.html,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29,Medway flooding 29 May 2018,Medway Council S19 Flood Investigation Report,078/Medway/Flood/Investigation.pdf
GB-MDW,1,2018-05-29,51.37265326623865,0.5349773493032547,"<div id=""html_d41594d5a0d72a5f1c9eede713c4f569"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Medway</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 29 May 2018 heavy rainfall across Medway caused rapid overland and surface water flow in topographically susceptible areas, leading to multiple reports of internal and external property flooding and highway flooding. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface water flooding reported across the Medway administrative area during the rainfall event; reports included highway flooding incidents and some property flooding (property specifics not published). [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread surface water flooding occurred across the Medway administrative area on 29 May 2018; multiple flood incidents were reported to the council and observed during site inspections, and the area has a history of localised flooding and drainage problems during heavy/prolonged rainfall. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 29 May 2018 Medway experienced widespread flooding largely attributed to excessive overland flow and sewer capacity exceedance; impermeable urban surfaces increased runoff and drainage systems were overwhelmed beyond design capacity. (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Medway,Medway,England,"On 29 May 2018 heavy rainfall across Medway caused rapid overland and surface water flow in topographically susceptible areas, leading to multiple reports of internal and external property flooding and highway flooding. [PAG] (p4)
Widespread surface water flooding reported across the Medway administrative area during the rainfall event; reports included highway flooding incidents and some property flooding (property specifics not published). [PAG] (p9)
Widespread surface water flooding occurred across the Medway administrative area on 29 May 2018; multiple flood incidents were reported to the council and observed during site inspections, and the area has a history of localised flooding and drainage problems during heavy/prolonged rainfall. (p10)
On 29 May 2018 Medway experienced widespread flooding largely attributed to excessive overland flow and sewer capacity exceedance; impermeable urban surfaces increased runoff and drainage systems were overwhelmed beyond design capacity. (p12)",,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29.html,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29,Medway flooding 29 May 2018,Medway Council S19 Flood Investigation Report,078/Medway/Flood/Investigation.pdf
GB-MDW,1,2018-05-29,51.3819167,0.526559,"<div id=""html_71cb651ecd5e14cd02841afb895d48b3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chatham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Chatham experienced extensive surface water flooding during the event, notably at Nelson Terrace where properties were internally flooded and residents displaced. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area has experienced overland runoff and sewer&ndash;flow issues prompting investigation (SWMP) into options such as constructing an upstream bund to attenuate overland runoff and sewer flows. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Medway,Chatham,England,"Chatham experienced extensive surface water flooding during the event, notably at Nelson Terrace where properties were internally flooded and residents displaced. (p10)
Area has experienced overland runoff and sewer–flow issues prompting investigation (SWMP) into options such as constructing an upstream bund to attenuate overland runoff and sewer flows. (p18)",,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29.html,GB-MDW_1_2018-05-29,Medway flooding 29 May 2018,Medway Council S19 Flood Investigation Report,078/Medway/Flood/Investigation.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.58744439191329,-0.6828508825522218,"<div id=""html_7decbab81748ac9cee55f9e9eb4069dd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Queen and Albert (former)</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cellar of the Queen and Albert public house was flooded during the event; nearby properties experienced flooding to floorboards, gardens and drives. [PAG] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Queen and Albert (former),England,"Cellar of the Queen and Albert public house was flooded during the event; nearby properties experienced flooding to floorboards, gardens and drives. [PAG] (p16)",,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.61063486564572,-0.5955778826581319,"<div id=""html_25b9fd4ebff46086c6045c6092d90b43"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Thames Valley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the period of flooding the Environment Agency was engaged responding to flood incidents elsewhere across the Thames Valley, which prevented an immediate EA response to the incident reported in Wooburn Green. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Thames Valley,England,"During the period of flooding the Environment Agency was engaged responding to flood incidents elsewhere across the Thames Valley, which prevented an immediate EA response to the incident reported in Wooburn Green. (p17)",,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.5863662,-0.6839538,"<div id=""html_5bff7a9099b4cdf8b633994e2a20c69c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Wye</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wye's channel through the Wooburn Green area split as it passed under Windsor Hill, with flows directed west toward the A4094 and SW around the eastern boundary. During the event these river channels contributed to flooding in the Wooburn Green area (including the area around Wooburn Manor Park). (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wye burst its banks and flowed across the right bank, across fields and toward the houses on Windsor Hill, delivering floodwater into the built&ndash;up area. A fallen tree was mapped on the east bank upstream of the main bridge and may have obstructed flow, exacerbating localized flooding. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wye burst its banks when high winter rainfall combined with a channel blockage (a fallen tree) prevented flow. The river broke its banks on the right bank and flowed across the adjacent field towards Windsor Hill, filling the field and causing downstream flooding of properties. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Wye levels were very high and, combined with a tree blocking the channel upstream of Windsor Hill bridge, the river breached its right bank upstream of the blockage. Floodwater flowed into the adjacent field and towards Windsor Hill, inundating properties and infrastructure. [PAG,TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A tree in the channel upstream of Windsor Hill bridge blocked the free flow of water during high flows, causing the river to overtop the right bank and flow into the adjacent field, across the road and up to a house on the north side of Windsor Hill. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,River Wye,England,"The River Wye's channel through the Wooburn Green area split as it passed under Windsor Hill, with flows directed west toward the A4094 and SW around the eastern boundary. During the event these river channels contributed to flooding in the Wooburn Green area (including the area around Wooburn Manor Park). (p7)
The River Wye burst its banks and flowed across the right bank, across fields and toward the houses on Windsor Hill, delivering floodwater into the built–up area. A fallen tree was mapped on the east bank upstream of the main bridge and may have obstructed flow, exacerbating localized flooding. (p14)
The River Wye burst its banks when high winter rainfall combined with a channel blockage (a fallen tree) prevented flow. The river broke its banks on the right bank and flowed across the adjacent field towards Windsor Hill, filling the field and causing downstream flooding of properties. [PAG] (p15)
River Wye levels were very high and, combined with a tree blocking the channel upstream of Windsor Hill bridge, the river breached its right bank upstream of the blockage. Floodwater flowed into the adjacent field and towards Windsor Hill, inundating properties and infrastructure. [PAG,TAB] (p16)
A tree in the channel upstream of Windsor Hill bridge blocked the free flow of water during high flows, causing the river to overtop the right bank and flow into the adjacent field, across the road and up to a house on the north side of Windsor Hill. (p17)",,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.58618636925599,-0.6803642725773973,"<div id=""html_26d371ab51a472bb0024a549b8bbe9b9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Windsor Hill Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A tree lodged in the channel of the River Wye upstream of Windsor Hill Bridge obstructed flow during high flows; this blockage contributed to the river overtopping its right bank and sending water into the adjacent field, road and up to the house on the north side of Windsor Hill. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A tree in the River Wye channel upstream of Windsor Hill Bridge blocked flow; during high flows water overtopped the right bank and flowed into the adjacent field, onto the road and up to a house on the north side of Windsor Hill. Removal of the tree allowed water levels to recede. (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Windsor Hill Bridge,England,"A tree lodged in the channel of the River Wye upstream of Windsor Hill Bridge obstructed flow during high flows; this blockage contributed to the river overtopping its right bank and sending water into the adjacent field, road and up to the house on the north side of Windsor Hill. (p17)
A tree in the River Wye channel upstream of Windsor Hill Bridge blocked flow; during high flows water overtopped the right bank and flowed into the adjacent field, onto the road and up to a house on the north side of Windsor Hill. Removal of the tree allowed water levels to recede. (p21)",,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.58678588969138,-0.6813744205427654,"<div id=""html_eaa3e9bce412af2364f47b359e51e63a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Windsor Hill, Wooburn Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding forced full closure of the road; closure disrupted access and transport through the area. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Windsor Hill road was closed due to flooding, causing loss of highway access over the period of the event. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Wye flowed across fields toward the houses on Windsor Hill, and additional rapid overland flow ran down the steep Windsor Hill, increasing flood risk to properties on the hill. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Wye flowed across the field and down Windsor Hill. The field filled with water up to waist depth; horses had to be removed. Residents moved cars to safety and pumped water out of front gardens. Water approached houses from the road and the rear; properties had water up to their floorboards and gardens and drives were flooded. The Queen and Albert public house had its cellar flooded. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Wye broke its banks and flowed down into the field towards Windsor Hill. The field was inundated to about waist depth, horses were removed for safety, residents moved cars to safety, water was pumped from front gardens, and properties experienced flooding to floorboards, gardens and drives. The road was closed (reports show closures from 07 Feb, remaining closed through 11 Feb, reopened 12 Feb). [PAG,TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the River Wye's right bank caused floodwater to flow into the adjacent field, across the road and up to the house on the north side of Windsor Hill; the road experienced deep water and was closed by TfB/BCC to prevent vehicles pushing water towards houses; residents undertook protective measures and sought assistance from the EA. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Windsor Hill road and adjacent properties were flooded: the road was closed between 6 and 12 February 2014; water surrounded properties on the north side of Windsor Hill, residents pumped to try to prevent ingress, and water rose up to the floorboards in a number of properties. (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 houses (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Windsor Hill, Wooburn Green",England,"Flooding forced full closure of the road; closure disrupted access and transport through the area. (p4)
Windsor Hill road was closed due to flooding, causing loss of highway access over the period of the event. (p5)
Floodwater from the River Wye flowed across fields toward the houses on Windsor Hill, and additional rapid overland flow ran down the steep Windsor Hill, increasing flood risk to properties on the hill. (p14)
Floodwater from the River Wye flowed across the field and down Windsor Hill. The field filled with water up to waist depth; horses had to be removed. Residents moved cars to safety and pumped water out of front gardens. Water approached houses from the road and the rear; properties had water up to their floorboards and gardens and drives were flooded. The Queen and Albert public house had its cellar flooded. [PAG] (p15)
Floodwater from the River Wye broke its banks and flowed down into the field towards Windsor Hill. The field was inundated to about waist depth, horses were removed for safety, residents moved cars to safety, water was pumped from front gardens, and properties experienced flooding to floorboards, gardens and drives. The road was closed (reports show closures from 07 Feb, remaining closed through 11 Feb, reopened 12 Feb). [PAG,TAB] (p16)
Overtopping of the River Wye's right bank caused floodwater to flow into the adjacent field, across the road and up to the house on the north side of Windsor Hill; the road experienced deep water and was closed by TfB/BCC to prevent vehicles pushing water towards houses; residents undertook protective measures and sought assistance from the EA. (p17)
Windsor Hill road and adjacent properties were flooded: the road was closed between 6 and 12 February 2014; water surrounded properties on the north side of Windsor Hill, residents pumped to try to prevent ingress, and water rose up to the floorboards in a number of properties. (p21)",2 houses (p4),GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.5854333,-0.6838976,"<div id=""html_ea623d13d60c62434e5cb1a0430829e8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wooburn Manor Park, Wooburn Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Wye channels split after flowing under Windsor Hill and flowed around then along the southern boundary of the Wooburn Manor Park residential area, resulting in flooding of the residential area and adjacent land. (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,"Wooburn Manor Park, Wooburn Green",England,"River Wye channels split after flowing under Windsor Hill and flowed around then along the southern boundary of the Wooburn Manor Park residential area, resulting in flooding of the residential area and adjacent land. (p7)",,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.86619751869193,-0.8610230642396219,"<div id=""html_bdbf84f51a92f75cc303731e65f1e922"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckinghamshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Buckinghamshire County Council (BCC) implemented the closure of Windsor Hill (with TfB) between 06 Feb 2014 and 12 Feb 2014 as part of the immediate response; the flooding was caused by River Wye overtopping its right bank after a tree blocked the channel, leading to water on the road and surrounding properties on the north side of Windsor Hill. [TAB] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Buckinghamshire,England,"Buckinghamshire County Council (BCC) implemented the closure of Windsor Hill (with TfB) between 06 Feb 2014 and 12 Feb 2014 as part of the immediate response; the flooding was caused by River Wye overtopping its right bank after a tree blocked the channel, leading to water on the road and surrounding properties on the north side of Windsor Hill. [TAB] (p21)",,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.58813049847262,-0.683046263483928,"<div id=""html_906f4df7c2b3de2804c2fccd71353f1c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wooburn Green</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Windsor Hill within Wooburn Green caused the Windsor Hill road to be closed for several days; residential properties experienced internal flooding to floorboard level and the public house cellar flooded. (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Wooburn Green area caused the closure of Windsor Hill (a Class A/B highway) and prompted a formal section 19 investigation by the county authority. [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the River Wye affected the Wooburn Green area. The River Wye, which runs through Wooburn Green, splits under Windsor Hill; one channel flows toward the A4094 and the other flows around the eastern boundary then along the southern boundary of the Wooburn Manor Park residential area before rejoining. These channel routes were implicated in the local inundation of the Wooburn Green locality during the event. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The region of Wooburn Green was flooded, with inundation affecting areas around Wooburn Manor Park and other low&ndash;lying areas adjacent to the River Wye's channels. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater from the River Wye breached its banks and flowed across the lower&ndash;lying fields between the river and the property Salan, inundating those fields and flowing onto Windsor Hill road toward houses on Windsor Hill. Runoff down the steep Windsor Hill road also contributed to flood routing into the settlement. Photographs and resident records document historic inundation along Windsor Hill and the intervening fields. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area of Wooburn Green (particularly Windsor Hill) was affected when the River Wye breached its banks and flooded adjoining fields and properties &mdash; causing inundation to gardens, drives and floorboards, horse evacuation, vehicle moves and road closures. [PAG,TAB] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The fluvial event (caused by prolonged rainfall and worsened by a tree blocking the River Wye channel upstream of Windsor Hill Bridge) led to river overtopping that produced deep water on roads and flow into fields and up to houses in the Wooburn Green area; residents called the Environment Agency, took protective measures for their properties and requested assistance; highways authorities closed the road to reduce the risk of vehicles pushing water into houses. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>road closed for 5 to 6 days; 2 houses with water up to floorboards; 1 public house cellar flooded (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Wooburn Green,England,"Flooding at Windsor Hill within Wooburn Green caused the Windsor Hill road to be closed for several days; residential properties experienced internal flooding to floorboard level and the public house cellar flooded. (p4)
Flooding in the Wooburn Green area caused the closure of Windsor Hill (a Class A/B highway) and prompted a formal section 19 investigation by the county authority. [PAG] (p5)
Flooding from the River Wye affected the Wooburn Green area. The River Wye, which runs through Wooburn Green, splits under Windsor Hill; one channel flows toward the A4094 and the other flows around the eastern boundary then along the southern boundary of the Wooburn Manor Park residential area before rejoining. These channel routes were implicated in the local inundation of the Wooburn Green locality during the event. [PAG] (p6)
The region of Wooburn Green was flooded, with inundation affecting areas around Wooburn Manor Park and other low–lying areas adjacent to the River Wye's channels. [PAG] (p7)
Floodwater from the River Wye breached its banks and flowed across the lower–lying fields between the river and the property Salan, inundating those fields and flowing onto Windsor Hill road toward houses on Windsor Hill. Runoff down the steep Windsor Hill road also contributed to flood routing into the settlement. Photographs and resident records document historic inundation along Windsor Hill and the intervening fields. (p13)
Area of Wooburn Green (particularly Windsor Hill) was affected when the River Wye breached its banks and flooded adjoining fields and properties — causing inundation to gardens, drives and floorboards, horse evacuation, vehicle moves and road closures. [PAG,TAB] (p16)
The fluvial event (caused by prolonged rainfall and worsened by a tree blocking the River Wye channel upstream of Windsor Hill Bridge) led to river overtopping that produced deep water on roads and flow into fields and up to houses in the Wooburn Green area; residents called the Environment Agency, took protective measures for their properties and requested assistance; highways authorities closed the road to reduce the risk of vehicles pushing water into houses. (p17)",road closed for 5 to 6 days; 2 houses with water up to floorboards; 1 public house cellar flooded (p4),GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-BKM,13,2014-02-06,51.580302363167256,-0.6897622577637851,"<div id=""html_fc677ce6c2d28766e0a84b3ebabc2797"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wooburn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Wooburn Green (part of Wooburn) was flooded; the flood triggered a section 19 investigation and was associated with local transport disruption (Windsor Hill road closure). [PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The two River Wye channels rejoin near the SW corner of Wooburn Manor Park and then follow a SW route along the A4094 through Wooburn, causing river flooding along that route through Wooburn. [PAG] (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",Buckinghamshire,Wooburn,England,"Wooburn Green (part of Wooburn) was flooded; the flood triggered a section 19 investigation and was associated with local transport disruption (Windsor Hill road closure). [PAG] (p5)
The two River Wye channels rejoin near the SW corner of Wooburn Manor Park and then follow a SW route along the A4094 through Wooburn, causing river flooding along that route through Wooburn. [PAG] (p7)",,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06.html,GB-BKM_13_2014-02-06,Wooburn Green flooding 06 Feb 2014 - 12 Feb 2014,Wooburn Green February 2014,018/Buckinghamshire County/018/2014/Buckinghamshire County/section-19-final-report-wooburn-green-31118.pdf
GB-CAM,23,2020-12-23,52.3397734,-0.0765153,"<div id=""html_322db05412bcbd7a2706937df02616a3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wheatfields</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal residential property flooding occurred in Wheatfields. [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Wheatfields,England,Internal residential property flooding occurred in Wheatfields. [TAB] (p12),,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23,St Ives flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Ives,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Ives-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,23,2020-12-23,52.3275492,-0.0702756,"<div id=""html_a7ec7fc1046cda8d28e8ddf18b8d6367"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Warners Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding to garage and outbuildings at Warner's park; occupants did not have to leave premises. [TAB] (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Warners Park,England,External flooding to garage and outbuildings at Warner's park; occupants did not have to leave premises. [TAB] (p13),,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23,St Ives flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Ives,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Ives-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,23,2020-12-23,52.3339194,-0.061815,"<div id=""html_2f80458c41197f7403ae928e698a71fe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marley Gap Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Indicative flooded areas are shown adjacent to Marley Gap Brook on the December 2020 map, with flooding concentrated along and near this watercourse and contributing to local inundation of streets and properties in St Ives. [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with Marley Gap Brook prompted maintenance and cleansing works on the relevant watercourse to improve conveyance and reduce future flood risk. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Contractors carried out significant maintenance and cleansing works on Marley Gap Brook (relevant sections) to improve conveyance as part of the flood investigation and response. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Marley Gap Brook exceeded channel capacity during the event, contributing to fluvial flooding in the area; the brook is identified with capacity issues, RMA operational issues and riparian maintenance issues in the investigation summary. [TAB] (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Marley Gap Brook,England,"Indicative flooded areas are shown adjacent to Marley Gap Brook on the December 2020 map, with flooding concentrated along and near this watercourse and contributing to local inundation of streets and properties in St Ives. [PAG,IMG] (p11)
Flooding associated with Marley Gap Brook prompted maintenance and cleansing works on the relevant watercourse to improve conveyance and reduce future flood risk. [TAB] (p14)
Contractors carried out significant maintenance and cleansing works on Marley Gap Brook (relevant sections) to improve conveyance as part of the flood investigation and response. (p15)
Marley Gap Brook exceeded channel capacity during the event, contributing to fluvial flooding in the area; the brook is identified with capacity issues, RMA operational issues and riparian maintenance issues in the investigation summary. [TAB] (p17)",,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23,St Ives flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Ives,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Ives-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,23,2020-12-23,52.3282706,-0.0594201,"<div id=""html_f9cc59fa12bf90b3b4d5358eaf084e29"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harrison Way, St Ives</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Commercial premises on Harrison Way were reported to have internal flooding as part of the December 2020 events. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Issues on the watercourse in the Harrison Way area were identified during inspections (upstream/downstream checks). A fallen tree downstream of Harrison Way led to bank damage for which bank repair work was planned, indicating local flood&ndash;related bank failure and obstruction to flow affecting the immediate area. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Used as a reference point for Parsons Drove Drain inspections (upstream vs downstream); a fallen tree downstream of Harrison Way required bank repair works, indicating local channel obstruction and bank damage connected to flooding impacts and subsequent repair actions. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Harrison Way, St Ives",England,"Commercial premises on Harrison Way were reported to have internal flooding as part of the December 2020 events. (p10)
Issues on the watercourse in the Harrison Way area were identified during inspections (upstream/downstream checks). A fallen tree downstream of Harrison Way led to bank damage for which bank repair work was planned, indicating local flood–related bank failure and obstruction to flow affecting the immediate area. [TAB] (p14)
