GB-CAM,23,2020-12-23,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_1609a9a366bdd653a0cfd79e3255d91a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local flooding reported in St Ives (within Cambridgeshire) with multiple LLFA flood reports; flooding events persisted for short durations on affected properties/areas. [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in St Ives (within Cambridgeshire) produced multiple indicative flooded areas concentrated along local watercourses, resulting in internal and external property flooding reported to the LLFA. [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Engaged in discussions regarding maintenance of highway gullies and liaised over riparian responsibilities for sections of ordinary watercourse; involved in arranging/removing silt at Stocks Bridge to improve conveyance. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>96 flood reports; duration between 5 hours and 2 days [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>96 flood reports; 32 properties internally flooded; 64 properties externally flooded; 15 commercial premises internally flooded [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"Local flooding reported in St Ives (within Cambridgeshire) with multiple LLFA flood reports; flooding events persisted for short durations on affected properties/areas. [IMG] (p9)
Flooding in St Ives (within Cambridgeshire) produced multiple indicative flooded areas concentrated along local watercourses, resulting in internal and external property flooding reported to the LLFA. [PAG,IMG] (p11)
Engaged in discussions regarding maintenance of highway gullies and liaised over riparian responsibilities for sections of ordinary watercourse; involved in arranging/removing silt at Stocks Bridge to improve conveyance. [TAB] (p14)","96 flood reports; duration between 5 hours and 2 days [IMG] (p9)
96 flood reports; 32 properties internally flooded; 64 properties externally flooded; 15 commercial premises internally flooded [PAG,IMG] (p11)",GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23,St Ives flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Ives,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Ives-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,23,2020-12-23,52.3358157,-0.0753866,"<div id=""html_bbb2be878ea616c714b189d277708ead"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Ives</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding began on 23 December 2020 due to intense rainfall and existing ground conditions; rapid runoff led to significant surface water flooding across the town, inundation of foul drainage and overwhelming of Anglian Water pumping stations. As a result foul water backed up into properties and there was a loss of toilet facilities. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>96 flood reports were received by the LLFA for December 2020. Flooding duration was reported between 5 hours and 2 days. 32 properties reported internal flooding. 64 further properties reported external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. 15 commercial premises reported internal flooding (in listed St Ives locations). (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding across the town led to internal flooding of residential properties and commercial premises, and external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. Commercial premises internally flooded were reported on Harrison Way, Bramley Road, Harding Way, Edison Road, Needingworth Road and Parson's Drove. [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple reports of internal residential property flooding within St Ives (for example at Wheatfields), indicating the town experienced property flooding. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External flooding affected roads, gardens and fields in the town; streets and public areas experienced inundation during the event. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense antecedent and extreme rainfall caused watercourses throughout St Ives to exceed channel capacity, producing significant surface water (fluvial&ndash;related) flooding across the town. Anglian Water assets were overwhelmed by watercourse flooding, which led to foul water systems backing up. (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>96 flood reports; 32 properties; 64 properties; 15 commercial premises (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>96 flood reports; 32 properties internally flooded; 64 properties externally flooded; 15 commercial premises internally flooded [PAG,IMG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,St Ives,England,"Flooding began on 23 December 2020 due to intense rainfall and existing ground conditions; rapid runoff led to significant surface water flooding across the town, inundation of foul drainage and overwhelming of Anglian Water pumping stations. As a result foul water backed up into properties and there was a loss of toilet facilities. (p7)
96 flood reports were received by the LLFA for December 2020. Flooding duration was reported between 5 hours and 2 days. 32 properties reported internal flooding. 64 further properties reported external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. 15 commercial premises reported internal flooding (in listed St Ives locations). (p10)
Flooding across the town led to internal flooding of residential properties and commercial premises, and external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. Commercial premises internally flooded were reported on Harrison Way, Bramley Road, Harding Way, Edison Road, Needingworth Road and Parson's Drove. [PAG,IMG] (p11)
Multiple reports of internal residential property flooding within St Ives (for example at Wheatfields), indicating the town experienced property flooding. (p12)
External flooding affected roads, gardens and fields in the town; streets and public areas experienced inundation during the event. [TAB] (p14)
Intense antecedent and extreme rainfall caused watercourses throughout St Ives to exceed channel capacity, producing significant surface water (fluvial–related) flooding across the town. Anglian Water assets were overwhelmed by watercourse flooding, which led to foul water systems backing up. (p17)","96 flood reports; 32 properties; 64 properties; 15 commercial premises (p10)
96 flood reports; 32 properties internally flooded; 64 properties externally flooded; 15 commercial premises internally flooded [PAG,IMG] (p11)",GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_23_2020-12-23,St Ives flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 24 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Ives,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Ives-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.377656,-0.5373621,"<div id=""html_d3da0cb2e4a9bec706a1b7d0e196c32c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brawns Spinney</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of flattened grass adjacent to the watercourse in Brawns Spinney observed during the investigation are considered evidence of overtopping of banks under heavy flows. (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Brawns Spinney,England,Areas of flattened grass adjacent to the watercourse in Brawns Spinney observed during the investigation are considered evidence of overtopping of banks under heavy flows. (p10),,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.56628789856925,-0.2027211766370756,"<div id=""html_663c6a66bb8adcd9c80517ebf4d4bc54"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Nene</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Nene rose in level so that properties adjacent came close to being flooded; rear gardens were affected by the raised river levels. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Raised water levels in the River Nene may have contributed to the flooding by preventing the ordinary watercourse from discharging to the river, exacerbating flooding in the area. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Nene is identified as the main river causing or contributing to flooding adjacent to Denford; the report recommends consideration of flood defence works at the River Nene and assessment of contributory factors at outfalls to the main river. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,River Nene,England,"River Nene rose in level so that properties adjacent came close to being flooded; rear gardens were affected by the raised river levels. (p11)
Raised water levels in the River Nene may have contributed to the flooding by preventing the ordinary watercourse from discharging to the river, exacerbating flooding in the area. (p15)
The River Nene is identified as the main river causing or contributing to flooding adjacent to Denford; the report recommends consideration of flood defence works at the River Nene and assessment of contributory factors at outfalls to the main river. (p18)",,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.3787932,-0.5392686,"<div id=""html_65b1a286f749fdbaf6ca9a51854e8dde"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Duck End, Denford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water from intense rainfall on a saturated catchment flowed over ground following natural contours into Duck End, causing internal flooding of at least one property and contributing to flooding in adjacent streets (Chapel Lane, Freeman's Lane). [PAG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High volumes of surface water flowed to Duck End from the south and east. Internal flooding affected at least one property to ground&ndash;floor living areas. Two properties required continuous efforts by residents and the Fire Service to prevent internal flooding. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Large area of ponding throughout Duck End. Flood water at Duck End was reported to include foul water from the public sewerage system; a nearby highway gully in poor condition impaired drainage at a low point. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding of at least one property; internal flooding experienced four times in one year [PAG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 2 properties (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Duck End, Denford",England,"Excess surface water from intense rainfall on a saturated catchment flowed over ground following natural contours into Duck End, causing internal flooding of at least one property and contributing to flooding in adjacent streets (Chapel Lane, Freeman's Lane). [PAG,MUL] (p5)
High volumes of surface water flowed to Duck End from the south and east. Internal flooding affected at least one property to ground–floor living areas. Two properties required continuous efforts by residents and the Fire Service to prevent internal flooding. (p8)
Large area of ponding throughout Duck End. Flood water at Duck End was reported to include foul water from the public sewerage system; a nearby highway gully in poor condition impaired drainage at a low point. (p11)","internal flooding of at least one property; internal flooding experienced four times in one year [PAG,MUL] (p5)
1 property; 2 properties (p8)",GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.3787834,-0.5409771,"<div id=""html_eaf8815e565a197d3b90aaf9c3ce2bad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chapel Lane, Denford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water, produced by intense rainfall on a saturated catchment, flowed over ground following natural contours into areas in and around Chapel Lane. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A culvert in the rear garden of a property on Chapel Lane collapsed during the flood incident and at the time of investigation the route was an open ditch; flows from upstream ditches reached the rear garden, flowed to the front of the property and entered the surface water public sewerage system. Replacement pipework is planned (may require consent to ensure capacity). (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ponding developed throughout Chapel Lane as the ordinary watercourse overspilled; drainage through a rear garden and to the front of a property on Chapel Lane collapsed under the high flow, causing water to find an overland route toward property thresholds (residents and Fire Service had to force water away to prevent internal flooding). (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Chapel Lane, Denford",England,"Excess surface water, produced by intense rainfall on a saturated catchment, flowed over ground following natural contours into areas in and around Chapel Lane. (p6)
A culvert in the rear garden of a property on Chapel Lane collapsed during the flood incident and at the time of investigation the route was an open ditch; flows from upstream ditches reached the rear garden, flowed to the front of the property and entered the surface water public sewerage system. Replacement pipework is planned (may require consent to ensure capacity). (p9)
Ponding developed throughout Chapel Lane as the ordinary watercourse overspilled; drainage through a rear garden and to the front of a property on Chapel Lane collapsed under the high flow, causing water to find an overland route toward property thresholds (residents and Fire Service had to force water away to prevent internal flooding). (p10)",,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.3795647,-0.5404243,"<div id=""html_611754887e41f036b6e734a4d457f04e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Freeman's Lane, Denford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Excess surface water, produced by intense rainfall on a saturated catchment, flowed over ground following natural contours into areas in and around Freeman's Lane. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear gardens of properties on Freeman's Lane were affected by ponding from the overspilling ordinary watercourse; a property on Freeman's Lane would have been flooded without continuous efforts by residents and the Fire Service to force water away. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear gardens of properties on Freeman's Lane were affected by ponding because the ordinary watercourse could not flow away quickly enough. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Freeman's Lane, Denford",England,"Excess surface water, produced by intense rainfall on a saturated catchment, flowed over ground following natural contours into areas in and around Freeman's Lane. (p6)
Rear gardens of properties on Freeman's Lane were affected by ponding from the overspilling ordinary watercourse; a property on Freeman's Lane would have been flooded without continuous efforts by residents and the Fire Service to force water away. (p10)
Rear gardens of properties on Freeman's Lane were affected by ponding because the ordinary watercourse could not flow away quickly enough. (p11)",one property (p10),GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.3792134,-0.5427976,"<div id=""html_4899159509f3b0d2957f894c4f0781f1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Denford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Where the ordinary watercourse meets the River Nene, flooding occurred affecting the rear gardens of residential properties and the highway of High Street; the ordinary watercourse culvert beneath High Street is identified as a factor in this flooding. (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"High Street, Denford",England,"Where the ordinary watercourse meets the River Nene, flooding occurred affecting the rear gardens of residential properties and the highway of High Street; the ordinary watercourse culvert beneath High Street is identified as a factor in this flooding. (p10)",,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.3784952,-0.5407047,"<div id=""html_8c3c664e15a40f6c8451d6bf4995db27"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Spinney Rise, Denford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Large areas of agricultural fields south of Spinney Rise were submerged in flood water during the flooding incident. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage accepting runoff from fields south of Spinney Rise lacked capacity for the flows experienced; the drainage route runs through residential property before entering the ordinary watercourse, increasing the risk of flooding to residential properties. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Spinney Rise, Denford",England,"Large areas of agricultural fields south of Spinney Rise were submerged in flood water during the flooding incident. (p11)
Drainage accepting runoff from fields south of Spinney Rise lacked capacity for the flows experienced; the drainage route runs through residential property before entering the ordinary watercourse, increasing the risk of flooding to residential properties. (p15)",,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,16,2012-11-21,52.37839832652422,-0.5420279639211473,"<div id=""html_844ee6991af68d93f48cfa9ebbbc07bc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Denford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall falling on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow overland following natural contours, resulting in internal flooding of property/properties in Denford (areas in and around Duck End, Chapel Lane and Freeman's Lane were affected). [PAG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall onto a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours into areas in and around Duck End (Denford). Internal flooding of at least one property at Duck End was reported; this property had experienced internal flooding four times in one year. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant rainfall on the Denford catchment on 21 November 2012 produced high volumes of surface water that flowed into parts of the village and resulted in internal flooding at Duck End (within Denford). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding across the village: rear gardens of properties and the highway (High Street) were affected; ponding occurred in Duck End and Chapel Lane; rear gardens of properties on Freeman's Lane were affected; agricultural fields south of Spinney Rise were submerged; foul water from the public sewer was present in flood water at Duck End. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in Denford; NCC can suggest possible causes and make recommendations to mitigate flood risk but lacks the mandate or funding under the FandWMA to tackle all identified causes. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall on a saturated catchment overwhelmed existing drainage systems and watercourses in Denford, causing water to pond, flow into areas and drainage systems unable to manage the additional water, and resulting in localised flooding across the village. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall onto a saturated catchment and insufficient drainage/ordinary watercourse capacity led to overtopping of the ordinary watercourse east of the village. Overtopping produced a high&ndash;velocity flow into Denford that spanned the full highway width; existing drainage systems and watercourses were unable to cope, causing ponding and flood flows into other areas. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overspill of land drainage led to flooding of neighbouring properties, the public highway and the public sewerage system; flows from high ground contributed to properties being affected. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties adjacent to the River Nene in Denford are affected by flood risk from the main river; the report recommends the Environment Agency consider flood defence works at the River Nene to reduce the likelihood of flooding and recommends documents to residents affected by surface water runoff from neighbouring land and emergence of groundwater. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>internal flooding of at least one property; internal flooding experienced four times in one year [PAG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least one property; four times in one year [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 2 properties (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Denford,England,"Intense rainfall falling on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow overland following natural contours, resulting in internal flooding of property/properties in Denford (areas in and around Duck End, Chapel Lane and Freeman's Lane were affected). [PAG,MUL] (p5)
Intense rainfall onto a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours into areas in and around Duck End (Denford). Internal flooding of at least one property at Duck End was reported; this property had experienced internal flooding four times in one year. [PAG] (p6)
Significant rainfall on the Denford catchment on 21 November 2012 produced high volumes of surface water that flowed into parts of the village and resulted in internal flooding at Duck End (within Denford). (p8)
Flooding across the village: rear gardens of properties and the highway (High Street) were affected; ponding occurred in Duck End and Chapel Lane; rear gardens of properties on Freeman's Lane were affected; agricultural fields south of Spinney Rise were submerged; foul water from the public sewer was present in flood water at Duck End. (p11)
Flooding occurred in Denford; NCC can suggest possible causes and make recommendations to mitigate flood risk but lacks the mandate or funding under the FandWMA to tackle all identified causes. [PAG] (p12)
Intense, short–duration rainfall on a saturated catchment overwhelmed existing drainage systems and watercourses in Denford, causing water to pond, flow into areas and drainage systems unable to manage the additional water, and resulting in localised flooding across the village. (p14)
Intense rainfall onto a saturated catchment and insufficient drainage/ordinary watercourse capacity led to overtopping of the ordinary watercourse east of the village. Overtopping produced a high–velocity flow into Denford that spanned the full highway width; existing drainage systems and watercourses were unable to cope, causing ponding and flood flows into other areas. (p15)
Overspill of land drainage led to flooding of neighbouring properties, the public highway and the public sewerage system; flows from high ground contributed to properties being affected. (p16)
Properties adjacent to the River Nene in Denford are affected by flood risk from the main river; the report recommends the Environment Agency consider flood defence works at the River Nene to reduce the likelihood of flooding and recommends documents to residents affected by surface water runoff from neighbouring land and emergence of groundwater. (p18)","internal flooding of at least one property; internal flooding experienced four times in one year [PAG,MUL] (p5)
at least one property; four times in one year [PAG] (p6)
1 property; 2 properties (p8)",GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21.html,GB-NNH_16_2012-11-21,Denford flooding 21 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Duck End, Denford, 21st Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Denford-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,33,2012-11-25,52.4214163,-0.6588565,"<div id=""html_4bd725c58a5d145c4760e7b62f96343a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grafton Road, Grafton Underwood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the junctions on Grafton Road (south at Warkton Road and north at Geddington Road). Water backed up at the junction with Geddington Road, causing road junction flooding; reverse flow flooded the rear garden and patio of a property (did not reach dwelling threshold); culvert blockages and inability of run&ndash;off to enter Alledge Brook led to manhole cover overtopping on the highway. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway sections on Grafton Road were flooded at junctions with Geddington Road and with Warkton Road (junction with Geddington Road flooded to an unspecified depth; junction with Warkton Road flooded to an unknown depth). (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The highway drainage system and the culverts beneath the highway serving Alledge Brook at the junction of Grafton Road and Geddington Road did not operate satisfactorily, causing the road junction to flood. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Grafton Road, Grafton Underwood",England,"Flooding occurred at the junctions on Grafton Road (south at Warkton Road and north at Geddington Road). Water backed up at the junction with Geddington Road, causing road junction flooding; reverse flow flooded the rear garden and patio of a property (did not reach dwelling threshold); culvert blockages and inability of run–off to enter Alledge Brook led to manhole cover overtopping on the highway. (p10)
Highway sections on Grafton Road were flooded at junctions with Geddington Road and with Warkton Road (junction with Geddington Road flooded to an unspecified depth; junction with Warkton Road flooded to an unknown depth). (p11)
The highway drainage system and the culverts beneath the highway serving Alledge Brook at the junction of Grafton Road and Geddington Road did not operate satisfactorily, causing the road junction to flood. (p15)",,GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25.html,GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25,Grafton Underwood flooding 25 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Grafton Underwood, 25th Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grafton-Underwood-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,33,2012-11-25,52.41484467421323,-0.6439122699742177,"<div id=""html_591cd6960f215765c6da1d9fdb8f3060"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Geddington Road, Grafton Underwood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water backed up at the junction of Geddington Road and Grafton Road causing flooding of the road junction and observed failures to the tarmac footway. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The highway at the junction of Geddington Road and Grafton Road flooded (depth not provided in the text). (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The highway drainage system and culverts beneath the highway serving Alledge Brook at the junction of Grafton Road and Geddington Road did not operate satisfactorily, causing the road junction to flood. (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Geddington Road, Grafton Underwood",England,"Water backed up at the junction of Geddington Road and Grafton Road causing flooding of the road junction and observed failures to the tarmac footway. (p10)
The highway at the junction of Geddington Road and Grafton Road flooded (depth not provided in the text). (p11)
The highway drainage system and culverts beneath the highway serving Alledge Brook at the junction of Grafton Road and Geddington Road did not operate satisfactorily, causing the road junction to flood. (p15)",,GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25.html,GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25,Grafton Underwood flooding 25 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Grafton Underwood, 25th Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grafton-Underwood-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,33,2012-11-25,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_689530a11391aa3bf691bfcd844f3fbe"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties where water crossed the threshold of buildings (internal flooding reported for three properties in the village). (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties (p7)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,Internal flooding of properties where water crossed the threshold of buildings (internal flooding reported for three properties in the village). (p7),three properties (p7),GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25.html,GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25,Grafton Underwood flooding 25 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Grafton Underwood, 25th Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grafton-Underwood-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,33,2012-11-25,52.41332318081368,-0.6433490061221884,"<div id=""html_a3c60c48e7e613947965512708fd5115"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Grafton Underwood</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties caused by overland flow of excess surface water following intense rainfall on a saturated catchment; drainage systems ineffective so water flowed over ground following natural contours into properties. [PAG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow overland following natural contours into properties, resulting in internal flooding. Flooding was reported to have caused internal flooding of properties and had occurred on more than one occasion in the previous five years. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties: two properties had ground&ndash;floor internal living areas flooded by surface water overland flows; one property experienced a back&ndash;up of effluent into a ground&ndash;floor toilet (sewer/back&ndash;up). [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water run&ndash;off from agricultural land to the east flowed into rear gardens and into properties. Run&ndash;off from fields overtopped drainage ditches and flowed onto the highway at the junction of Warkton Road and Grafton Road. Water backed up at the junction of Grafton Road and Geddington Road causing flooding of the road junction and failure/damage to the tarmac footway. Reverse flow flooded the rear garden and patio of a property at the northeast of the village (did not reach the dwelling threshold). Culvert blockages in the highway at the junction were evidenced and culverts beneath Geddington Road operated unsatisfactorily, allowing water onto the highway. A property at the east had its conservatory flooded to a shallow depth and water flowed to the kitchen; works (trench, driveway reshaping) were carried out to divert flows. A property at the southeast experienced approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen (groundwater emergence) and temporary rear patio inundation; another southeast property had a ground&ndash;floor toilet flooded. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties in the southeast of the village were flooded: one property had groundwater emerge through weaknesses in the floor slab causing approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen and surface water runoff from fields to the east temporarily flooded the rear patio; another property experienced water backing up and flooding a ground floor toilet; the garden and patio of the property referenced in 3.3.2 flooded to a depth of approximately 4. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in Grafton Underwood were flooded after intense, short&ndash;duration rainfall on a saturated catchment. Existing drainage systems and watercourses were unable to cope, causing water to flow into other areas and overwhelm local drainage infrastructure, resulting in flooding of properties. [PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the village were flooded after intense short&ndash;duration rainfall on a saturated catchment. Existing drainage systems and watercourses were unable to cope, causing water to flow into other areas and into properties; surface water and emergence of groundwater occurred within dwellings. (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [PAG,MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties; two properties; one property [MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least two residential properties; approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen of one property [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen; approximately 4 (depth) for garden and patio [PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Grafton Underwood,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties caused by overland flow of excess surface water following intense rainfall on a saturated catchment; drainage systems ineffective so water flowed over ground following natural contours into properties. [PAG,MUL] (p5)
Intense, short–duration rainfall on a saturated catchment caused excess surface water to flow overland following natural contours into properties, resulting in internal flooding. Flooding was reported to have caused internal flooding of properties and had occurred on more than one occasion in the previous five years. [PAG] (p6)
Internal flooding of properties: two properties had ground–floor internal living areas flooded by surface water overland flows; one property experienced a back–up of effluent into a ground–floor toilet (sewer/back–up). [MUL] (p8)
Surface water run–off from agricultural land to the east flowed into rear gardens and into properties. Run–off from fields overtopped drainage ditches and flowed onto the highway at the junction of Warkton Road and Grafton Road. Water backed up at the junction of Grafton Road and Geddington Road causing flooding of the road junction and failure/damage to the tarmac footway. Reverse flow flooded the rear garden and patio of a property at the northeast of the village (did not reach the dwelling threshold). Culvert blockages in the highway at the junction were evidenced and culverts beneath Geddington Road operated unsatisfactorily, allowing water onto the highway. A property at the east had its conservatory flooded to a shallow depth and water flowed to the kitchen; works (trench, driveway reshaping) were carried out to divert flows. A property at the southeast experienced approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen (groundwater emergence) and temporary rear patio inundation; another southeast property had a ground–floor toilet flooded. [PAG] (p10)
Residential properties in the southeast of the village were flooded: one property had groundwater emerge through weaknesses in the floor slab causing approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen and surface water runoff from fields to the east temporarily flooded the rear patio; another property experienced water backing up and flooding a ground floor toilet; the garden and patio of the property referenced in 3.3.2 flooded to a depth of approximately 4. [PAG] (p11)
Properties in Grafton Underwood were flooded after intense, short–duration rainfall on a saturated catchment. Existing drainage systems and watercourses were unable to cope, causing water to flow into other areas and overwhelm local drainage infrastructure, resulting in flooding of properties. [PAG] (p14)
Properties in the village were flooded after intense short–duration rainfall on a saturated catchment. Existing drainage systems and watercourses were unable to cope, causing water to flow into other areas and into properties; surface water and emergence of groundwater occurred within dwellings. (p15)","3 properties [PAG,MUL] (p5)
3 properties [PAG] (p6)
three properties; two properties; one property [MUL] (p8)
at least two residential properties; approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen of one property [PAG] (p10)
approximately 50mm of water in the kitchen; approximately 4 (depth) for garden and patio [PAG] (p11)",GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25.html,GB-NNH_33_2012-11-25,Grafton Underwood flooding 25 Nov 2012,"Flood Investigation Report: Grafton Underwood, 25th Novermber 2012",090/North Northamptonshire/Grafton-Underwood-FIR.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3189468,-0.0887842,"<div id=""html_4df59b46a1299097eb01152b642c84f0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bourne Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the Caterham Bourne, additional groundwater emergence and surcharging sewers caused Bourne Park to flood. A temporary flood storage area was created in the park to hold the flood waters. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rising groundwater entered the foul sewer network causing manholes to overflow and surcharge raw sewage both internally and externally, particularly around Bourne Park and upstream in Whyteleafe. [TAB] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewer surcharging led to foul (sewage) flooding across the park; aerial imagery documents widespread foul flooding of the open park area on 14 February 2014. A culvert in Bourne Park became silted and blocked following the flood (photo July 2014). [IMG,MUL] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Bourne Park had demountable flood barriers installed to control emerging groundwater associated with the flooding incident. [TAB] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Bourne Park,England,"High flows in the Caterham Bourne, additional groundwater emergence and surcharging sewers caused Bourne Park to flood. A temporary flood storage area was created in the park to hold the flood waters. (p6)
Rising groundwater entered the foul sewer network causing manholes to overflow and surcharge raw sewage both internally and externally, particularly around Bourne Park and upstream in Whyteleafe. [TAB] (p23)
Foul sewer surcharging led to foul (sewage) flooding across the park; aerial imagery documents widespread foul flooding of the open park area on 14 February 2014. A culvert in Bourne Park became silted and blocked following the flood (photo July 2014). [IMG,MUL] (p29)
Bourne Park had demountable flood barriers installed to control emerging groundwater associated with the flooding incident. [TAB] (p55)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.326033100000004,-0.10282522497173253,"<div id=""html_929ba529f7310b2f37d94aa741f7d737"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harris Primary Academy</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The open ditch of the bourne at the school overtopped; the school was closed during the flooding and a temporary flood storage area was erected in the adjacent field as part of local flood response measures. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Temporary flood storage barriers were deployed around the school field to protect the building and grounds. A wall associated with the school also impeded post&ndash;flood pumping (blocking flows) during response activities. [IMG,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Temporary flood storage barriers were installed around the school field as a response measure during the December 2013&ndash;January 2014 flooding. [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 10&ndash;11 Feb 2014 the academy was used to construct/host Flood Support Areas (FSAs) to support evacuees during the Bourne flooding. [IMG,MUL] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Used as a site for construction of temporary FSAs (flood storage/assembly areas) as part of the evacuation and protection measures for the threatened Kenley waterworks and surrounding communities. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>School closed as a precaution because of the flooding situation in the area. [TAB] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Harris Primary Academy,England,"The open ditch of the bourne at the school overtopped; the school was closed during the flooding and a temporary flood storage area was erected in the adjacent field as part of local flood response measures. (p7)
Temporary flood storage barriers were deployed around the school field to protect the building and grounds. A wall associated with the school also impeded post–flood pumping (blocking flows) during response activities. [IMG,MUL] (p32)
Temporary flood storage barriers were installed around the school field as a response measure during the December 2013–January 2014 flooding. [IMG] (p33)
On 10–11 Feb 2014 the academy was used to construct/host Flood Support Areas (FSAs) to support evacuees during the Bourne flooding. [IMG,MUL] (p38)
Used as a site for construction of temporary FSAs (flood storage/assembly areas) as part of the evacuation and protection measures for the threatened Kenley waterworks and surrounding communities. (p39)
School closed as a precaution because of the flooding situation in the area. [TAB] (p54)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3732125,-0.1062877,"<div id=""html_aab1d01c85c36b1db9859c25df9ad42c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Rose and Crown</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater levels at the Rose and Crown site rose markedly during the event, with a prolonged increase recorded and elevated groundwater remaining high through March 2014. (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>12 m rise (16%) over a 50 day period (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Rose and Crown,England,"Groundwater levels at the Rose and Crown site rose markedly during the event, with a prolonged increase recorded and elevated groundwater remaining high through March 2014. (p24)",12 m rise (16%) over a 50 day period (p24),GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.326478981987,-0.10453525580302624,"<div id=""html_703b8a144ad6d0b91994efe7f7efab9a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kenley Cricket Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kenley Cricket ground experienced fluvial&ndash;type flooding when large flows in the open sections of the Caterham Bourne overwhelmed the banks, resulting in flooding of the ground; debris and insufficient culvert capacity contributed to surcharging and upstream overflow. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Small areas of shallow flooding occurred on the cricket ground caused by overtopping of the Bourne and elevated groundwater levels. [IMG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bourne flowing alongside the ground overtopped in January 2014, producing shallow flooding of the cricket ground; mitigation included temporary sandbag walls and pumping (Kenley WTW noted as pumping location). Downstream culvert/trash screen issues were observed. [IMG,MUL] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Kenley Cricket Ground,England,"The Kenley Cricket ground experienced fluvial–type flooding when large flows in the open sections of the Caterham Bourne overwhelmed the banks, resulting in flooding of the ground; debris and insufficient culvert capacity contributed to surcharging and upstream overflow. [TAB] (p22)
Small areas of shallow flooding occurred on the cricket ground caused by overtopping of the Bourne and elevated groundwater levels. [IMG] (p32)
The bourne flowing alongside the ground overtopped in January 2014, producing shallow flooding of the cricket ground; mitigation included temporary sandbag walls and pumping (Kenley WTW noted as pumping location). Downstream culvert/trash screen issues were observed. [IMG,MUL] (p33)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3255991,-0.0999898,"<div id=""html_a332da9ec5be84adb53bffa6705d7704"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kenley Hotel</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of the commercial premises as Godstone Road filled with bourne water. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Kenley Hotel,England,Internal flooding of the commercial premises as Godstone Road filled with bourne water. (p26),,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.335260582420595,-0.11463751357798312,"<div id=""html_3332f97cd290ac086b8cdf6787ce8c26"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Liddell Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear of Liddell Court was flooded in February 2014 with standing water adjacent to the building; sandbags and temporary barriers were used to protect the property. [IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Building evacuated because of flooding; flood&ndash;alleviation measures (HVPs and sandbags) were employed in the area to reduce water levels. [TAB] (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Three HVPs [TAB] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Liddell Court,England,"Rear of Liddell Court was flooded in February 2014 with standing water adjacent to the building; sandbags and temporary barriers were used to protect the property. [IMG] (p34)
Building evacuated because of flooding; flood–alleviation measures (HVPs and sandbags) were employed in the area to reduce water levels. [TAB] (p54)",Three HVPs [TAB] (p54),GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.33482558692051,-0.11294909983820317,"<div id=""html_98870c2c40150360f338e751885c2d00"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nicholas Court</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Nicholas Court (part of Dale Road evacuations) was evacuated because of flooding; fire appliances were deployed to manage water levels. [TAB] (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 LFB appliances [TAB] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Nicholas Court,England,Nicholas Court (part of Dale Road evacuations) was evacuated because of flooding; fire appliances were deployed to manage water levels. [TAB] (p54),8 LFB appliances [TAB] (p54),GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,54.7023545,-3.2765753,"<div id=""html_89d44f2acd23aae121b3106423d70d63"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>UK</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged, exceptional rainfall across December 2013&ndash;January 2014 (notably December 2013 and January 2014) led to widespread flood conditions in parts of the UK, with particularly intense impacts across southern England; the report cites this two&ndash;month period as the wettest on record in parts of southern England and Kent. [IMG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,UK,England,"Prolonged, exceptional rainfall across December 2013–January 2014 (notably December 2013 and January 2014) led to widespread flood conditions in parts of the UK, with particularly intense impacts across southern England; the report cites this two–month period as the wettest on record in parts of southern England and Kent. [IMG] (p20)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.339275,-0.1172902,"<div id=""html_5cabb2c704c00e1648982ddbad24683a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Cross</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater was pumped from Dale Road into an open section of the bourne north of the railway. A pedestrian underpass was deliberately flooded to divert floodwaters away from properties. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Served as the downstream discharge/receiving location for pumped floodwater from the Kenley WTW emergency works; pipes were extended to this open section north of the railway line to relieve the WTW site. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The pedestrian/road underpass at Purley Cross was deliberately allowed to inundate and was cordoned off, used temporarily as a balancing pond to store floodwater and reduce downstream flow toward Purley Oaks. [IMG] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley Cross,England,"Floodwater was pumped from Dale Road into an open section of the bourne north of the railway. A pedestrian underpass was deliberately flooded to divert floodwaters away from properties. (p7)
Served as the downstream discharge/receiving location for pumped floodwater from the Kenley WTW emergency works; pipes were extended to this open section north of the railway line to relieve the WTW site. (p31)
The pedestrian/road underpass at Purley Cross was deliberately allowed to inundate and was cordoned off, used temporarily as a balancing pond to store floodwater and reduce downstream flow toward Purley Oaks. [IMG] (p35)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3550556,-0.0643103753173489,"<div id=""html_544cec29dab0ccd12eadad572f2973ff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding around the route of the Caterham Bourne (Kenley and Purley). Residential properties were affected at Bourne Park Close, Brighton Road, Dale Road, Foxley Hill Road, Godstone Road, Lansdown Road and Purley Park Road. Bourne Park flooded (temporary flood storage created) and the Bourne View Flood Storage Area was used though some issues were identified. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Council coordinated a multi&ndash;agency response, mobilised pumps and temporary defences to protect communities; number of flooded properties was reported as low due to early mobilisation; active short&ndash; and long&ndash;term recovery and mitigation planning (clearance, dredging/de&ndash;silting, inspection, investigating Dale Road, and catchment&ndash;scale alleviation/attenuation measures). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding across the London Borough of Croydon between December 2013 and January 2014. Flooding resulted from high groundwater (which drove the Caterham Bourne), foul sewer flooding due to groundwater ingress and emergency pumping stressing the sewer system, and surface water flooding from heavy rainfall and saturated soils. Croydon Council declared a Major Incident on 6 February 2014 and implemented a large pumping operation to protect homes, businesses and critical infrastructure. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The borough experienced extreme rainfall in December 2013 and January 2014 which led to flooding issues investigated by the Council (Croydon Borough&ndash;wide flood investigation covering impacts attributed to surface water/urban runoff and watercourse surcharging). [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The bourne moves into the more urbanised parts of Croydon; exceptionally high groundwater in December 2013&ndash;January 2014 led to high fluvial flows, ingress of groundwater into the foul sewer network, saturated soils and extreme pressure on surface water drainage, contributing to urban flooding in Croydon. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread high groundwater led to basement and garden flooding across the borough. Roads flooded due to bourne water surcharging from culverts (e.g. Dale Road/Godstone Road) and relief pumping; a row of commercial businesses between Kenley Hotel and Westbourne Florist experienced internal flooding as the road filled with water. Emergency pumping and flood protection measures were mobilised to protect properties. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas within the London Borough of Croydon along the Caterham Bourne were investigated because they experienced flooding. Gardens in Bourne Park Close were reported flooded with foul sewage (photograph captioned July 2014), prompting remedial action (new fencing) following the event. [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced widespread flooding in December 2013&ndash;January 2014; early mobilisation of pumps and use of temporary attenuation areas prevented significant property flooding; multi&ndash;agency response and a major incident declaration were used to protect communities. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Communities in Croydon were threatened by flooding associated with the Caterham Bourne and combined sources of surface water/urban flooding; mitigation options and maintenance programmes are proposed to reduce future threats. (p46)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Croydon,England,"Significant flooding around the route of the Caterham Bourne (Kenley and Purley). Residential properties were affected at Bourne Park Close, Brighton Road, Dale Road, Foxley Hill Road, Godstone Road, Lansdown Road and Purley Park Road. Bourne Park flooded (temporary flood storage created) and the Bourne View Flood Storage Area was used though some issues were identified. [MUL] (p6)
Council coordinated a multi–agency response, mobilised pumps and temporary defences to protect communities; number of flooded properties was reported as low due to early mobilisation; active short– and long–term recovery and mitigation planning (clearance, dredging/de–silting, inspection, investigating Dale Road, and catchment–scale alleviation/attenuation measures). (p8)
Significant flooding across the London Borough of Croydon between December 2013 and January 2014. Flooding resulted from high groundwater (which drove the Caterham Bourne), foul sewer flooding due to groundwater ingress and emergency pumping stressing the sewer system, and surface water flooding from heavy rainfall and saturated soils. Croydon Council declared a Major Incident on 6 February 2014 and implemented a large pumping operation to protect homes, businesses and critical infrastructure. (p9)
The borough experienced extreme rainfall in December 2013 and January 2014 which led to flooding issues investigated by the Council (Croydon Borough–wide flood investigation covering impacts attributed to surface water/urban runoff and watercourse surcharging). [TAB,PAG] (p19)
The bourne moves into the more urbanised parts of Croydon; exceptionally high groundwater in December 2013–January 2014 led to high fluvial flows, ingress of groundwater into the foul sewer network, saturated soils and extreme pressure on surface water drainage, contributing to urban flooding in Croydon. [PAG] (p21)
Widespread high groundwater led to basement and garden flooding across the borough. Roads flooded due to bourne water surcharging from culverts (e.g. Dale Road/Godstone Road) and relief pumping; a row of commercial businesses between Kenley Hotel and Westbourne Florist experienced internal flooding as the road filled with water. Emergency pumping and flood protection measures were mobilised to protect properties. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p26)
Areas within the London Borough of Croydon along the Caterham Bourne were investigated because they experienced flooding. Gardens in Bourne Park Close were reported flooded with foul sewage (photograph captioned July 2014), prompting remedial action (new fencing) following the event. [PAG,IMG] (p28)
Experienced widespread flooding in December 2013–January 2014; early mobilisation of pumps and use of temporary attenuation areas prevented significant property flooding; multi–agency response and a major incident declaration were used to protect communities. (p44)
Communities in Croydon were threatened by flooding associated with the Caterham Bourne and combined sources of surface water/urban flooding; mitigation options and maintenance programmes are proposed to reduce future threats. (p46)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.2822065,-0.0783882,"<div id=""html_eca6bbc6096f8c396eb3a67e0a5863f3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Caterham Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flow path of the groundwater&ndash;fed Caterham Bourne originated near Caterham Station, contributing to downstream flows. (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Caterham Station,England,"A flow path of the groundwater–fed Caterham Bourne originated near Caterham Station, contributing to downstream flows. (p6)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.2978716,-0.0710372,"<div id=""html_0db8f37e492152ddfb0eb737beff3f6b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wapses Lodge Roundabout</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two emerging flow paths combined at the Wapses Lodge Roundabout on the A22; this is where the Environment Agency map the start of the Caterham Bourne Main River, concentrating flows that contributed to flooding downstream. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flow&ndash;control weir was constructed upstream of Wapses Lodge roundabout during the event to manage and control flood flows. (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Wapses Lodge Roundabout,England,"Two emerging flow paths combined at the Wapses Lodge Roundabout on the A22; this is where the Environment Agency map the start of the Caterham Bourne Main River, concentrating flows that contributed to flooding downstream. (p6)
A flow–control weir was constructed upstream of Wapses Lodge roundabout during the event to manage and control flood flows. (p40)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.2967412,-0.0667991,"<div id=""html_742c6a8756b4550e5a8b88fbb8392e1a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woldingham Road, Caterham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Emerging groundwater flows were observed east of the railway aqueduct over Woldingham Road, forming one of the flow pathways of the Caterham Bourne. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>As water began to emerge from elevated ground in January&ndash;February 2014, emergency storage areas were constructed in the fields alongside Woldingham Road; these were later formalised into major attenuation structures used to control water discharged downstream. (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Woldingham Road, Caterham",England,"Emerging groundwater flows were observed east of the railway aqueduct over Woldingham Road, forming one of the flow pathways of the Caterham Bourne. (p6)
As water began to emerge from elevated ground in January–February 2014, emergency storage areas were constructed in the fields alongside Woldingham Road; these were later formalised into major attenuation structures used to control water discharged downstream. (p40)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3180775,-0.0871242,"<div id=""html_8e157c37b82749238987ab7291212d7b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bourne Park Close, Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties at Bourne Park Close were affected by flooding during the Caterham Bourne event driven primarily by high groundwater emergence (with combined influences from surface water and sewers). (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Garden flooding caused by very high groundwater levels. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gardens were flooded by foul sewage; photographic evidence shows new fencing installed as a post&ndash;event repair. [PAG,IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gardens along Bourne Park Close were surrounded by rising groundwater mixed with surcharging foul sewer and were flooded by foul sewage; residents later installed new fencing (photo July 2014). Groundwater springs were observed emerging on higher ground east of the street, and a resident redirected water toward the Caterham Bourne channel. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater emergence and sewer flooding at Bourne Park Close caused flooding to properties on the street. [TAB] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers were surcharging, which prevented clean&ndash;up operations. [TAB,MUL] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Bourne Park Close, Kenley",England,"Residential properties at Bourne Park Close were affected by flooding during the Caterham Bourne event driven primarily by high groundwater emergence (with combined influences from surface water and sewers). (p6)
Garden flooding caused by very high groundwater levels. [TAB] (p26)
Gardens were flooded by foul sewage; photographic evidence shows new fencing installed as a post–event repair. [PAG,IMG] (p28)
Gardens along Bourne Park Close were surrounded by rising groundwater mixed with surcharging foul sewer and were flooded by foul sewage; residents later installed new fencing (photo July 2014). Groundwater springs were observed emerging on higher ground east of the street, and a resident redirected water toward the Caterham Bourne channel. [IMG] (p29)
Groundwater emergence and sewer flooding at Bourne Park Close caused flooding to properties on the street. [TAB] (p54)
Sewers were surcharging, which prevented clean–up operations. [TAB,MUL] (p55)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3389331,-0.1163358,"<div id=""html_c44b829ef642de04c87a34b92a6037dd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brighton Road, Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Brighton Road were affected by flooding associated with the Caterham Bourne event, primarily due to exceptional groundwater rise and with potential contribution from surface water/sewer interactions. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Emergence and movement of groundwater onto the surface: groundwater flowed across fields and across a road intersection producing visible standing water patches; flow continued into adjacent fields. Houses are present near the flow paths but the image notes they are not directly affected. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding from Bourne water. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Brighton Road, Purley",England,"Residential properties on Brighton Road were affected by flooding associated with the Caterham Bourne event, primarily due to exceptional groundwater rise and with potential contribution from surface water/sewer interactions. (p6)
Emergence and movement of groundwater onto the surface: groundwater flowed across fields and across a road intersection producing visible standing water patches; flow continued into adjacent fields. Houses are present near the flow paths but the image notes they are not directly affected. (p18)
Property flooding from Bourne water. [TAB,MUL] (p26)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3343562,-0.1139064,"<div id=""html_0679910fa8772a17f48c8d61bc3d7461"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dale Road, Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Dale Road were impacted by flooding during the Caterham Bourne event driven by exceptionally high groundwater, with combined surface water/sewer influences possible. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Deepest flooding of approximately 1 metre occurred at this location; properties were inundated and residents were evacuated. Intensive pumping operations were required in the area. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flood problems associated with the bourne rising; recovery and restoration actions were identified including channel and culvert clearance, dredging and de&ndash;silting, more regular maintenance/inspection, and working with Thames Water on asset maintenance. Authorities planned investigations into influences on the flooding at Dale Road. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road flooding caused by a surcharging Bourne culvert; relief pumping was carried out. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Street and adjacent properties were inundated; emergency responders constructed sandbag walls, used boats to evacuate residents and water was pumped out of the street during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road and adjacent areas were flooded, prompting response officers to build sandbag walls and to pump water out; pumped water was diverted to a short open section of the Bourne north of the railway line. [IMG,PAG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dale Road experienced flooding related to an undersized/managed culvert; the investigation recommends clarifying asset ownership and assessing downstream implications of changing the culvert size to reduce future flood impact. [TAB] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road and adjacent residential buildings remained flooded; evacuations carried out (Liberty Court and Nicholas Court); relief measures (pumps/HVPs and sandbags) used and emergency services operated to manage water levels. [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Dale Road remained flooded across the response period: pumping at Dale Road was operating at capacity; the Army laid sandbags; water levels were significantly reduced by 17 February but later increased slightly and remained stable. Tesco Underpass became full. Environment Agency pumps later replaced LFB HVPs at Dale Road. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Site experienced flooding; an investigation continued into the exact cause and water levels at the site were reported to be falling during the post&ndash;event response. [TAB,PAG] (p56)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 metre flood depth; 56 properties evacuated (Dale Road and Foxley Hill Road) (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 LFB appliances; three HVPs [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 LFB appliances on site [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Dale Road, Purley",England,"Residential properties on Dale Road were impacted by flooding during the Caterham Bourne event driven by exceptionally high groundwater, with combined surface water/sewer influences possible. (p6)
Deepest flooding of approximately 1 metre occurred at this location; properties were inundated and residents were evacuated. Intensive pumping operations were required in the area. (p7)
Experienced flood problems associated with the bourne rising; recovery and restoration actions were identified including channel and culvert clearance, dredging and de–silting, more regular maintenance/inspection, and working with Thames Water on asset maintenance. Authorities planned investigations into influences on the flooding at Dale Road. (p8)
Road flooding caused by a surcharging Bourne culvert; relief pumping was carried out. [TAB,MUL] (p26)
Street and adjacent properties were inundated; emergency responders constructed sandbag walls, used boats to evacuate residents and water was pumped out of the street during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p34)
Road and adjacent areas were flooded, prompting response officers to build sandbag walls and to pump water out; pumped water was diverted to a short open section of the Bourne north of the railway line. [IMG,PAG] (p35)
Dale Road experienced flooding related to an undersized/managed culvert; the investigation recommends clarifying asset ownership and assessing downstream implications of changing the culvert size to reduce future flood impact. [TAB] (p50)
Road and adjacent residential buildings remained flooded; evacuations carried out (Liberty Court and Nicholas Court); relief measures (pumps/HVPs and sandbags) used and emergency services operated to manage water levels. [TAB,MUL] (p54)
Dale Road remained flooded across the response period: pumping at Dale Road was operating at capacity; the Army laid sandbags; water levels were significantly reduced by 17 February but later increased slightly and remained stable. Tesco Underpass became full. Environment Agency pumps later replaced LFB HVPs at Dale Road. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p55)
Site experienced flooding; an investigation continued into the exact cause and water levels at the site were reported to be falling during the post–event response. [TAB,PAG] (p56)","1 metre flood depth; 56 properties evacuated (Dale Road and Foxley Hill Road) (p7)
8 LFB appliances; three HVPs [TAB,MUL] (p54)
8 LFB appliances on site [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p55)",GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3352618,-0.1149157,"<div id=""html_71de8dbf82ad888764252e22dbb03e15"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Foxley Hill Road, Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Foxley Hill Road were affected by flooding during the Caterham Bourne event, primarily due to groundwater emergence exacerbated by heavy rainfall and possible sewer/surface water interactions. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Deepest flooding of approximately 1 metre occurred at this location; properties were inundated and residents were evacuated. Intensive pumping operations were required in the area. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding caused by bourne water surcharging from a culvert in Dale Road. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater flowed from Dale Road toward Foxley Hill Road causing inundation of the street and flooding at the rear of residential properties (photographed at Liddell Court). Sandbags were deployed to protect buildings. [IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Foxley Hill Road experienced impacts during the flooding incident, with electrical supply disrupted. [TAB] (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 metre flood depth; 56 properties evacuated (Dale Road and Foxley Hill Road) (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 properties [TAB] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Foxley Hill Road, Purley",England,"Residential properties on Foxley Hill Road were affected by flooding during the Caterham Bourne event, primarily due to groundwater emergence exacerbated by heavy rainfall and possible sewer/surface water interactions. (p6)
