GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3770376,-0.095461,"<div id=""html_0fb119a4998585ea15c60afdbaa9d863"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lansdowne Road, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Lansdown Road and Purley Park Road were flooded due to exceptionally high groundwater levels; surface water and surcharging sewers also contributed to the flooding. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement flooding resulting from very high groundwater levels. [TAB] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Lansdowne Road, Croydon",England,"Residential properties on Lansdown Road and Purley Park Road were flooded due to exceptionally high groundwater levels; surface water and surcharging sewers also contributed to the flooding. (p6)
Basement flooding resulting from very high groundwater levels. [TAB] (p26)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3218516,-0.092752,"<div id=""html_a4893ace8500982e99abdfad1227e304"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bourne View, Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Open section of the Caterham Bourne produced very large flows that overwhelmed the river banks at Bourne View, causing fluvial flooding of the area; high flows and debris contributed to blockages of trash screens and some downstream culvert/manhole surcharging and upstream overflow. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the Bourne and surcharging of the culverted watercourse caused standing floodwater in Bourne View and flooding of rear gardens. Properties were issued sandbags early; residents experienced garden flooding from high groundwater but reported no internal (property) flooding. The street was photographed flooded on 9 February 2014 and warning signs/utility response were present. [IMG,MUL] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels at Bourne View were elevated during the event and later noted to have dropped, indicating prior flood impact and ongoing risk. [TAB] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers were surcharging. [TAB] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Bourne View, Kenley",England,"Open section of the Caterham Bourne produced very large flows that overwhelmed the river banks at Bourne View, causing fluvial flooding of the area; high flows and debris contributed to blockages of trash screens and some downstream culvert/manhole surcharging and upstream overflow. [TAB] (p22)
High flows in the Bourne and surcharging of the culverted watercourse caused standing floodwater in Bourne View and flooding of rear gardens. Properties were issued sandbags early; residents experienced garden flooding from high groundwater but reported no internal (property) flooding. The street was photographed flooded on 9 February 2014 and warning signs/utility response were present. [IMG,MUL] (p30)
Water levels at Bourne View were elevated during the event and later noted to have dropped, indicating prior flood impact and ongoing risk. [TAB] (p54)
Sewers were surcharging. [TAB] (p55)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3425635,-0.1018549,"<div id=""html_ca7dbde7809a0217d93e90b13fb1eac2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Downs Road, Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Basement flooding of properties caused by very high groundwater. [TAB,MUL] (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Purley Downs Road, Purley",England,"Basement flooding of properties caused by very high groundwater. [TAB,MUL] (p26)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.316926,-0.0856417,"<div id=""html_6e4479888da46cde09610cc63168d886"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bushey Close, Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A commercial site off Bushey Close was flooded with combined surface water and foul sewage caused by high groundwater entering and surcharging the sewer system. [IMG] (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Bushey Close, Kenley",England,A commercial site off Bushey Close was flooded with combined surface water and foul sewage caused by high groundwater entering and surcharging the sewer system. [IMG] (p29),,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3263474,-0.1059934,"<div id=""html_ed896c5e7a10d7fabebdf80084644604"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Roke Road, Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewers surcharged and discharged onto the highway along Little Roke Road (sewer flooding onto the road surface). [IMG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Little Roke Road, Kenley",England,Sewers surcharged and discharged onto the highway along Little Roke Road (sewer flooding onto the road surface). [IMG] (p33),,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3285987,-0.1076367,"<div id=""html_90ec118bd0aaf178d3c884aac462878b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Road, Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Some surcharging from the bourne culvert where the route takes a sharp right&ndash;angle turn between Lower Road and Little Roke Avenue; this surcharging was noted but described as not significant. [IMG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Lower Road, Kenley",England,Some surcharging from the bourne culvert where the route takes a sharp right–angle turn between Lower Road and Little Roke Avenue; this surcharging was noted but described as not significant. [IMG] (p33),,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3276426,-0.1078354,"<div id=""html_40630debe45a032f745a460cf464adda"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Little Roke Avenue, Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surcharging associated with the bourne culvert at the sharp right&ndash;angle turn between Lower Road and Little Roke Avenue was observed in this area; the issue was reported as not significant. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Little Roke Avenue, Kenley",England,Surcharging associated with the bourne culvert at the sharp right–angle turn between Lower Road and Little Roke Avenue was observed in this area; the issue was reported as not significant. (p33),,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.2715316,-0.3414523511290909,"<div id=""html_13352c66215535d987d45ed5bec34de6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Surrey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding was experienced in parts of Surrey (Kenley, Purley, Woldingham and Whyteleafe) where the Caterham Bourne flows before entering the London Borough of Croydon. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surrey experienced multiple flood incidents across a large area during the same period; cross&ndash;boundary coordination and differing procedures with Croydon caused response inefficiencies. (p44)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Surrey,England,"Significant flooding was experienced in parts of Surrey (Kenley, Purley, Woldingham and Whyteleafe) where the Caterham Bourne flows before entering the London Borough of Croydon. (p9)
Surrey experienced multiple flood incidents across a large area during the same period; cross–boundary coordination and differing procedures with Croydon caused response inefficiencies. (p44)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,50.99916185320384,-1.1369855447202502,"<div id=""html_e9c2be62f23f7847398c91e0dd4766ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>South England</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Southern England experienced record rainfall in January 2014 (the wettest January since 1910) and very high December 2013 totals; these exceptional rainfall conditions led to flooding across parts of southern England (including Kent and areas around Croydon). [IMG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,South England,England,Southern England experienced record rainfall in January 2014 (the wettest January since 1910) and very high December 2013 totals; these exceptional rainfall conditions led to flooding across parts of southern England (including Kent and areas around Croydon). [IMG] (p20),,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.2070749,0.7210362,"<div id=""html_30c46189c46a04eff94b6e1b84093969"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Environment Agency reported the two&ndash;month period (Dec 2013&ndash;Jan 2014) as the wettest on record for Kent; exceptional rainfall in this period produced flood conditions in the Kent/South London area. (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Kent,England,The Environment Agency reported the two–month period (Dec 2013–Jan 2014) as the wettest on record for Kent; exceptional rainfall in this period produced flood conditions in the Kent/South London area. (p20),,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.2393372,-0.0344318,"<div id=""html_13a2f1cc497a1a883fe6dc55262a58ee"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tandridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Emergency measures and relief pumping were being carried out at the Tandridge end of the catchment as part of the response to rapidly rising groundwater and bourne flows. [MUL] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Communities in Tandridge were identified as being threatened by intermittent rises and flooding of the Caterham Bourne and combined flood sources; mitigation and cross&ndash;boundary management are recommended. (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer systems were surcharging in Tandridge during the incident, contributing to flooding risk and sewer flooding impacts. [TAB] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Tandridge,England,"Emergency measures and relief pumping were being carried out at the Tandridge end of the catchment as part of the response to rapidly rising groundwater and bourne flows. [MUL] (p26)
Communities in Tandridge were identified as being threatened by intermittent rises and flooding of the Caterham Bourne and combined flood sources; mitigation and cross–boundary management are recommended. (p46)
Sewer systems were surcharging in Tandridge during the incident, contributing to flooding risk and sewer flooding impacts. [TAB] (p54)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.33766666433047,-0.11694467799549156,"<div id=""html_1e6da220e1e4d76bde7173fcb97bbd7c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Underpass</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Purley Cross pedestrian underpass was deliberately flooded and used as temporary storage, cordoned off for safety; this intervention and associated pumping operations were used to protect Purley town centre and to manage downstream flows toward Purley Oaks during the February 2014 incident. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 8 Feb 2014 the underpass became inundated and was used as a balancing pond to store flood water during the incident. [IMG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Used as a temporary flood water storage area during the event; flood water levels at the underpass were monitored 24/7 on an hourly basis by the London Highways Alliance Contractor. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the underpass required pumping operations to remove floodwater. [TAB] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The underpass became full of floodwater. [TAB] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley Underpass,England,"The Purley Cross pedestrian underpass was deliberately flooded and used as temporary storage, cordoned off for safety; this intervention and associated pumping operations were used to protect Purley town centre and to manage downstream flows toward Purley Oaks during the February 2014 incident. [IMG] (p35)
On 8 Feb 2014 the underpass became inundated and was used as a balancing pond to store flood water during the incident. [IMG] (p38)
Used as a temporary flood water storage area during the event; flood water levels at the underpass were monitored 24/7 on an hourly basis by the London Highways Alliance Contractor. (p42)
Flooding of the underpass required pumping operations to remove floodwater. [TAB] (p54)
The underpass became full of floodwater. [TAB] (p55)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.322754,-0.1037854,"<div id=""html_24fe2935f420461ae217d948f5563aa5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kenley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding from the groundwater&ndash;fed Caterham Bourne affected Kenley. The bourne rose along its route through Whyteleafe into Kenley, travelling through a mixture of culverts and open ditches and causing local inundation along the A22 corridor. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding was experienced in the Kenley and Purley area as the bourne flowed through these locations prior to entering Croydon. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas in Kenley were affected by overflow from the Caterham Bourne: open sections produced fluvial&ndash;type flooding that overwhelmed banks and inundated local assets, specifically the Kenley Cricket ground. Insufficient culvert capacity and surcharge of manholes led to upstream backing up and overflows in the Kenley area. Debris&ndash;blocked trash screens contributed to local backups. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the bourne in February 2014 threatened Kenley, with reports of potential for catastrophic flooding in the Purley and Kenley area as high flows caused the culverted bourne to surcharge. [IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters reached the main building at the water treatment works (WTW); water was pumped from Kenley WTW as part of response and temporary defences were erected to protect infrastructure and pipes leading off down the A22 (photographed February 2014). [IMG,MUL] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kenley waterworks was identified as at risk: a robust evacuation plan was developed for when trigger levels were reached at the Kenley waterworks, including construction of temporary flood storage/assembly areas to protect the water treatment works. [MUL] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kenley Water Treatment Works (WTW) at Godstone Road was threatened with shutdown due to inundation from the Bourne and rising groundwater; boreholes at Kenley required increased abstraction to try to reduce groundwater levels. [PAG,MUL] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kenley water treatment works (Godstone Road) was inundated/threatened by flooding from the Bourne, risking shutdown; sandbags and pumps were deployed around key assets and pumps installed to remove water; contingency plans were made to supply customers from other works. The London Fire Brigade was notified on 6 February 2014 that rising flood water in the Kenley area threatened the integrity of the WTW. [PAG,MUL] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rising floodwater in the Kenley area threatened the integrity of the Kenley Water Treatment Works, prompting high&ndash;volume pump deployments, sustained LFB flood management activities and a dedicated police presence; a critical situation at the Kenley Water Treatment Works required temporary transfer of incident management. [MUL] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kenley water treatment works (WTW) was identified as being at flood risk; the report recommends developing protection measures for the WTW that do not increase flood risk elsewhere and to record WTW failure on the council's risk register. [TAB] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kenley WTW had surcharging issues; measures including sandbags and demountable flood defences were put in place and the works were reported as stable under ongoing monitoring. [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels at Kenley WTW rose above critical level during heavy rainfall on 14 February 2014; pumping operated at maximum capacity and significant groundwater pumping was undertaken to lower the water table. LFB high&ndash;volume pumps at Kenley WTW were later replaced by Environment Agency pumps. Status was later recorded as 'Green'. [TAB,MUL] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Kenley,England,"Significant flooding from the groundwater–fed Caterham Bourne affected Kenley. The bourne rose along its route through Whyteleafe into Kenley, travelling through a mixture of culverts and open ditches and causing local inundation along the A22 corridor. (p6)
Significant flooding was experienced in the Kenley and Purley area as the bourne flowed through these locations prior to entering Croydon. (p9)
Areas in Kenley were affected by overflow from the Caterham Bourne: open sections produced fluvial–type flooding that overwhelmed banks and inundated local assets, specifically the Kenley Cricket ground. Insufficient culvert capacity and surcharge of manholes led to upstream backing up and overflows in the Kenley area. Debris–blocked trash screens contributed to local backups. [TAB] (p22)
Flooding from the bourne in February 2014 threatened Kenley, with reports of potential for catastrophic flooding in the Purley and Kenley area as high flows caused the culverted bourne to surcharge. [IMG] (p31)
Floodwaters reached the main building at the water treatment works (WTW); water was pumped from Kenley WTW as part of response and temporary defences were erected to protect infrastructure and pipes leading off down the A22 (photographed February 2014). [IMG,MUL] (p32)
Kenley waterworks was identified as at risk: a robust evacuation plan was developed for when trigger levels were reached at the Kenley waterworks, including construction of temporary flood storage/assembly areas to protect the water treatment works. [MUL] (p39)
Kenley Water Treatment Works (WTW) at Godstone Road was threatened with shutdown due to inundation from the Bourne and rising groundwater; boreholes at Kenley required increased abstraction to try to reduce groundwater levels. [PAG,MUL] (p41)
The Kenley water treatment works (Godstone Road) was inundated/threatened by flooding from the Bourne, risking shutdown; sandbags and pumps were deployed around key assets and pumps installed to remove water; contingency plans were made to supply customers from other works. The London Fire Brigade was notified on 6 February 2014 that rising flood water in the Kenley area threatened the integrity of the WTW. [PAG,MUL] (p42)
Rising floodwater in the Kenley area threatened the integrity of the Kenley Water Treatment Works, prompting high–volume pump deployments, sustained LFB flood management activities and a dedicated police presence; a critical situation at the Kenley Water Treatment Works required temporary transfer of incident management. [MUL] (p43)
The Kenley water treatment works (WTW) was identified as being at flood risk; the report recommends developing protection measures for the WTW that do not increase flood risk elsewhere and to record WTW failure on the council's risk register. [TAB] (p50)
Kenley WTW had surcharging issues; measures including sandbags and demountable flood defences were put in place and the works were reported as stable under ongoing monitoring. [TAB,MUL] (p54)
Water levels at Kenley WTW rose above critical level during heavy rainfall on 14 February 2014; pumping operated at maximum capacity and significant groundwater pumping was undertaken to lower the water table. LFB high–volume pumps at Kenley WTW were later replaced by Environment Agency pumps. Status was later recorded as 'Green'. [TAB,MUL] (p55)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3385156,-0.115893,"<div id=""html_03308a1544346830749883b8bf558df2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Kenley and Purley around the route of the Caterham Bourne experienced significant flooding driven by exceptionally high groundwater levels and combined surface&ndash;water/sewer influences; residential properties in these areas were affected. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Caterham Bourne flowed into Croydon along the A22 towards Purley and the area experienced significant flooding during the event. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The EA issued a Groundwater Flood Alert for Purley on 8 January 2014 following rapid rises in groundwater in December 2013&ndash;January 2014. Rising groundwater caused emergence of springs and flooding of gardens and basements and also influenced sewer ingress and pressure on surface water drainage. [MUL] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area at risk and a recipient area for pumped floodwater (pumped to Purley Cross/open section north of the railway) as part of the emergency response; report states there was potential for catastrophic flooding in Purley if the Kenley WTW shut down due to the bourne surcharging. (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kenley/Purley area experienced flooding that required emergency response from police and other services and local flood&ndash;management activities to minimise property damage. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Purley underpass flooded and was used as a temporary attenuation area during the December 2013&ndash;January 2014 event to prevent property flooding. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding in the Purley area and on the railway; high water volumes at nearby Purley Oaks Pond led to pump failures and later deployment of pumps to reduce levels. [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the heavy rainfall on 14 February 2014, properties in Purley were affected by flooding and evacuations: nine properties were evacuated. [TAB] (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>47,000 homes (drinking water supply at risk if Kenley WTW shut down) (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 pumps to be installed; 2 pumps working at full capacity [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>9 properties evacuated [TAB] (p55)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley,England,"Areas of Kenley and Purley around the route of the Caterham Bourne experienced significant flooding driven by exceptionally high groundwater levels and combined surface–water/sewer influences; residential properties in these areas were affected. [MUL] (p6)
The Caterham Bourne flowed into Croydon along the A22 towards Purley and the area experienced significant flooding during the event. (p9)
The EA issued a Groundwater Flood Alert for Purley on 8 January 2014 following rapid rises in groundwater in December 2013–January 2014. Rising groundwater caused emergence of springs and flooding of gardens and basements and also influenced sewer ingress and pressure on surface water drainage. [MUL] (p21)
Area at risk and a recipient area for pumped floodwater (pumped to Purley Cross/open section north of the railway) as part of the emergency response; report states there was potential for catastrophic flooding in Purley if the Kenley WTW shut down due to the bourne surcharging. (p31)
The Kenley/Purley area experienced flooding that required emergency response from police and other services and local flood–management activities to minimise property damage. (p43)
Purley underpass flooded and was used as a temporary attenuation area during the December 2013–January 2014 event to prevent property flooding. (p44)
Localised flooding in the Purley area and on the railway; high water volumes at nearby Purley Oaks Pond led to pump failures and later deployment of pumps to reduce levels. [TAB,MUL] (p54)
Following the heavy rainfall on 14 February 2014, properties in Purley were affected by flooding and evacuations: nine properties were evacuated. [TAB] (p55)","47,000 homes (drinking water supply at risk if Kenley WTW shut down) (p31)
3 pumps to be installed; 2 pumps working at full capacity [TAB,MUL] (p54)
9 properties evacuated [TAB] (p55)",GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.28272115629364,-0.03568237756323519,"<div id=""html_b83bdff9ab53d265c4311d4beb4b401e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woldingham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Exceptional rainfall caused groundwater to rise and emerge in Woldingham, producing flow pathways that contributed to the Caterham Bourne; very high groundwater levels were recorded. [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sources of the Caterham Bourne rose above ground in Woldingham and Caterham, causing local flooding in these areas as the bourne flowed prior to entering Croydon. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Groundwater emergence and flow across fields and onto/across a road, producing visible standing water and surface flooding paths across agricultural land adjacent to the road; flow paths continued into neighbouring fields. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rapid rise in groundwater recorded at the Woldingham observation borehole: levels rose sharply between late December 2013 and late January 2014, approaching ground level and contributing to sustained flow in the upper reaches of the Caterham Bourne through early 2014. [PAG,IMG] (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fields alongside Woldingham Road experienced emerging water in January&ndash;February 2014; Surrey County Council, TDC and the Army constructed a series of emergency storage areas in these fields which were later formalised into major attenuation structures to control water discharged downstream. (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>groundwater levels increased by over 22 metres between late December 2013 and late January 2014 [MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 22.7 m rise (≈26%) between 23 December 2013 and 23 January 2014 [PAG,IMG] (p25)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Woldingham,England,"Exceptional rainfall caused groundwater to rise and emerge in Woldingham, producing flow pathways that contributed to the Caterham Bourne; very high groundwater levels were recorded. [MUL] (p6)
Sources of the Caterham Bourne rose above ground in Woldingham and Caterham, causing local flooding in these areas as the bourne flowed prior to entering Croydon. (p9)
Groundwater emergence and flow across fields and onto/across a road, producing visible standing water and surface flooding paths across agricultural land adjacent to the road; flow paths continued into neighbouring fields. [IMG] (p18)
Rapid rise in groundwater recorded at the Woldingham observation borehole: levels rose sharply between late December 2013 and late January 2014, approaching ground level and contributing to sustained flow in the upper reaches of the Caterham Bourne through early 2014. [PAG,IMG] (p25)
Fields alongside Woldingham Road experienced emerging water in January–February 2014; Surrey County Council, TDC and the Army constructed a series of emergency storage areas in these fields which were later formalised into major attenuation structures to control water discharged downstream. (p40)","groundwater levels increased by over 22 metres between late December 2013 and late January 2014 [MUL] (p6)
approximately 22.7 m rise (≈26%) between 23 December 2013 and 23 January 2014 [PAG,IMG] (p25)",GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.28197,-0.0771032,"<div id=""html_76e76dfb004a4608e01b46cdbf0bceb9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Caterham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sources of the Caterham Bourne rose above ground in Woldingham and Caterham, causing local flooding in these areas as the bourne flowed prior to entering Croydon. (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Caterham,England,"Sources of the Caterham Bourne rose above ground in Woldingham and Caterham, causing local flooding in these areas as the bourne flowed prior to entering Croydon. (p9)",,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.3101495,-0.0795445,"<div id=""html_2b4edaed9790926b9ab919898a2041d6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Whyteleafe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High groundwater caused sources of the Caterham Bourne to rise above ground in Woldingham (and Caterham), producing flows through Woldingham and Whyteleafe that contributed to downstream flooding into Croydon. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Emergency works were carried out on a demolition site where the culverted Bourne had been damaged; a 24&ndash;hour highways/flood crew operated in Whyteleafe during the event and community flood surgeries were held to keep residents informed. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding led to evacuations of properties in Whyteleafe. [TAB] (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties [TAB] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Whyteleafe,England,"High groundwater caused sources of the Caterham Bourne to rise above ground in Woldingham (and Caterham), producing flows through Woldingham and Whyteleafe that contributed to downstream flooding into Croydon. (p9)
Emergency works were carried out on a demolition site where the culverted Bourne had been damaged; a 24–hour highways/flood crew operated in Whyteleafe during the event and community flood surgeries were held to keep residents informed. (p40)
Flooding led to evacuations of properties in Whyteleafe. [TAB] (p54)",6 properties [TAB] (p54),GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.34761998515787,-0.10246447872690746,"<div id=""html_6ed6b128dbc2dd907423177eb009ddac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Purley Oaks</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged rise in groundwater levels in the lower catchment consistent with groundwater flooding; elevated groundwater levels remained high through March 2014. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Target of downstream flows from upstream pumping and natural flow in the Bourne; measures upstream (pumping and use of the Purley Cross underpass as a balancing pond) were implemented to alleviate flows toward Purley Oaks. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the balancing pond led to shallow inundation of the highway in the Purley Oaks area on 14 February 2014; the event triggered a multi&ndash;agency emergency response and local temporary defences. [IMG,MUL] (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater was pumped from the balancing pond at Purley Oaks into a separate drainage system as part of LFB operations to remove floodwater and reduce local flood risk. (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>water level increased by approximately 9.2 m (19%) over a 63&ndash;day period (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one occasion (14 February 2014) [IMG,MUL] (p36)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Purley Oaks,England,"Prolonged rise in groundwater levels in the lower catchment consistent with groundwater flooding; elevated groundwater levels remained high through March 2014. (p24)
Target of downstream flows from upstream pumping and natural flow in the Bourne; measures upstream (pumping and use of the Purley Cross underpass as a balancing pond) were implemented to alleviate flows toward Purley Oaks. [IMG] (p35)
Flooding from the balancing pond led to shallow inundation of the highway in the Purley Oaks area on 14 February 2014; the event triggered a multi–agency emergency response and local temporary defences. [IMG,MUL] (p36)
Floodwater was pumped from the balancing pond at Purley Oaks into a separate drainage system as part of LFB operations to remove floodwater and reduce local flood risk. (p43)","water level increased by approximately 9.2 m (19%) over a 63–day period (p24)
one occasion (14 February 2014) [IMG,MUL] (p36)",GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-CRY,1,2013-12-01,51.32383691472678,-0.09611657177253966,"<div id=""html_2a38a6521298de343fc66f2e3e69a8df"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kenley Treatment Works</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kenley Water Treatment Works was at significant risk from Caterham Bourne flows and rising groundwater; relief efforts concentrated on preventing high floodwaters reaching the works to avoid a shutdown (which would have had wider consequences). High&ndash;volume pumping and other measures were deployed to protect the works; the situation became critical during the incident and was a focus of multi&ndash;agency response. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Waterworks was the focus of protection efforts during the flood: defences were erected and pumps installed to protect the asset; Thames Water assisted and attended sites of foul (sewer) flooding associated with the event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy and prolonged rainfall threatened the Kenley Water Treatment Works; significant pumping was required to prevent its closure and to avoid property flooding. This pumping increased volumes entering the sewer system as routes were sought to remove water from the risk area. [TAB] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows in the bourne caused the culverted watercourse to surcharge and flood the water treatment works in February 2014; floodwaters reached the main building and the works were threatened with shutdown. Emergency response included erection of demountable flood defences, sandbags and high&ndash;volume pumps, and pumping floodwater away from the site along Godstone Road to an open section at Purley Cross. Pumping was impeded by a wall constructed since the 2001 flood. [IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels at the Waterworks reached critical levels on 14 February 2014, triggering the temporary commencement of the evacuation plan. [IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water was pumped from Kenley WTW into an open section of the bourne north of the railway as an emergency management measure; this pumping directed water into the watercourse to help relieve downstream pressure during the flood response. [IMG] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rising floodwater in the Kenley area threatened to compromise the integrity of the Kenley Water Treatment Works. High&ndash;volume pumps were deployed to remove floodwater from the bourne around the WTW to a point downstream with greater capacity; water was also pumped from the balancing pond at Purley Oaks into a separate drainage system. Flood&ndash;fighting operations were maintained 24 hours a day to protect the WTW and local communities. (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kenley Water Treatment Works experienced surcharging issues and flood risk; sandbags and demountable flood defences were provided and the site was monitored as 'stable' during the event. [TAB,MUL] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water levels rose above critical on 14 February causing pumping to run at maximum capacity; significant groundwater pumping was undertaken to lower the water table. Control was escalated to London Gold and evacuation plans (for up to 220 properties) were drawn up but later reversed. Environment Agency pumps later replaced LFB high&ndash;volume pumps and the site status was later recorded as 'Green'. [TAB,MUL] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood water management required on site: pumping operations were deployed and coordinated, site status recorded as 'Green', and water levels reported stable during monitoring. [TAB,PAG] (p56)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 47,000 people; hundreds of properties [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>47,000 homes; 3 high&ndash;volume pumps [IMG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>220 properties (evacuation planned); 9 properties evacuated [TAB,MUL] (p55)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 pumps [TAB,PAG] (p56)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Kenley Treatment Works,England,"Kenley Water Treatment Works was at significant risk from Caterham Bourne flows and rising groundwater; relief efforts concentrated on preventing high floodwaters reaching the works to avoid a shutdown (which would have had wider consequences). High–volume pumping and other measures were deployed to protect the works; the situation became critical during the incident and was a focus of multi–agency response. [MUL] (p7)
The Waterworks was the focus of protection efforts during the flood: defences were erected and pumps installed to protect the asset; Thames Water assisted and attended sites of foul (sewer) flooding associated with the event. (p8)
Heavy and prolonged rainfall threatened the Kenley Water Treatment Works; significant pumping was required to prevent its closure and to avoid property flooding. This pumping increased volumes entering the sewer system as routes were sought to remove water from the risk area. [TAB] (p23)
High flows in the bourne caused the culverted watercourse to surcharge and flood the water treatment works in February 2014; floodwaters reached the main building and the works were threatened with shutdown. Emergency response included erection of demountable flood defences, sandbags and high–volume pumps, and pumping floodwater away from the site along Godstone Road to an open section at Purley Cross. Pumping was impeded by a wall constructed since the 2001 flood. [IMG] (p31)
Water levels at the Waterworks reached critical levels on 14 February 2014, triggering the temporary commencement of the evacuation plan. [IMG] (p34)
Water was pumped from Kenley WTW into an open section of the bourne north of the railway as an emergency management measure; this pumping directed water into the watercourse to help relieve downstream pressure during the flood response. [IMG] (p35)
Rising floodwater in the Kenley area threatened to compromise the integrity of the Kenley Water Treatment Works. High–volume pumps were deployed to remove floodwater from the bourne around the WTW to a point downstream with greater capacity; water was also pumped from the balancing pond at Purley Oaks into a separate drainage system. Flood–fighting operations were maintained 24 hours a day to protect the WTW and local communities. (p43)
Kenley Water Treatment Works experienced surcharging issues and flood risk; sandbags and demountable flood defences were provided and the site was monitored as 'stable' during the event. [TAB,MUL] (p54)
Water levels rose above critical on 14 February causing pumping to run at maximum capacity; significant groundwater pumping was undertaken to lower the water table. Control was escalated to London Gold and evacuation plans (for up to 220 properties) were drawn up but later reversed. Environment Agency pumps later replaced LFB high–volume pumps and the site status was later recorded as 'Green'. [TAB,MUL] (p55)
Flood water management required on site: pumping operations were deployed and coordinated, site status recorded as 'Green', and water levels reported stable during monitoring. [TAB,PAG] (p56)","approximately 47,000 people; hundreds of properties [MUL] (p7)
47,000 homes; 3 high–volume pumps [IMG] (p31)
220 properties (evacuation planned); 9 properties evacuated [TAB,MUL] (p55)
2 pumps [TAB,PAG] (p56)",GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01.html,GB-CRY_1_2013-12-01,Croydon flooding December 2013 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Caterham Bourne Flood Investigation,030/Croydon/030/2014/Croydon/Caterham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – October 2014 2.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3470843,0.7291323,"<div id=""html_66ed1b64a8dbec7ca64ddcdc24340ba6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Stumble Inn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gully adjacent to the property was cleansed and made free&ndash;flowing as part of post&ndash;flood drainage works. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,The Stumble Inn,England,Gully adjacent to the property was cleansed and made free–flowing as part of post–flood drainage works. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3410952,0.7301857,"<div id=""html_8c1a7540fc25a947ed69c27a5849fa06"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pizza Hut</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gully behind the Pizza Hut premises was cleansed and is free&ndash;flowing as part of post&ndash;May flood drainage works. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Pizza Hut,England,Gully behind the Pizza Hut premises was cleansed and is free–flowing as part of post–May flood drainage works. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3408313,0.8226145,"<div id=""html_63988b6a2860e035c6bbce5696d14d4d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Banks Farm</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from the May 2018 flood affected highway drainage on Conyer Road between Stone Chimney Farm and Banks Farm; gullies were cleansed as post&ndash;flood maintenance to restore drainage flow. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Banks Farm,England,Surface water from the May 2018 flood affected highway drainage on Conyer Road between Stone Chimney Farm and Banks Farm; gullies were cleansed as post–flood maintenance to restore drainage flow. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.327839157755044,0.7993861372392495,"<div id=""html_4d54091838ff9864c8fa20ca1b9136ad"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>George Inn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies outside The George Pub on London Road were cleansed as post&ndash;May&ndash;2018 flood maintenance to restore highway drainage after surface water flooding. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,George Inn,England,Gullies outside The George Pub on London Road were cleansed as post–May–2018 flood maintenance to restore highway drainage after surface water flooding. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3349971,0.765362,"<div id=""html_c3c657b03504735864173a48d197e7fb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Fox and Goose</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An overgrown watercourse behind the Fox and Goose caused drainage from nearby highway gullies to back up, contributing to a build&ndash;up of water on this part of The Street. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Fox and Goose,England,"An overgrown watercourse behind the Fox and Goose caused drainage from nearby highway gullies to back up, contributing to a build–up of water on this part of The Street. (p33)",,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3116061,0.8743053,"<div id=""html_a7455d5252f970d06139c4d46362f5ae"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Ship Inn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gully immediately outside the property was cleansed but was recorded as requiring CCTV inspection. (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,The Ship Inn,England,Gully immediately outside the property was cleansed but was recorded as requiring CCTV inspection. (p37),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3269213,0.65908,"<div id=""html_8a67796fbd04f1d61823eeb5a9c14404"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Stockbury Roundabout</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding began near the Stockbury Roundabout by around 12:00 on 29 May and contributed to severe flooding that led to closure of the nearby A249 by 15:30. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Stockbury Roundabout,England,Surface water flooding began near the Stockbury Roundabout by around 12:00 on 29 May and contributed to severe flooding that led to closure of the nearby A249 by 15:30. (p30),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3751826,0.7269844,"<div id=""html_177a5828a2f7f574f0addabc7eb04c53"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sheerstone, Iwade</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to residential properties; vehicles damaged and written off; Environment Agency carried out overpumping from the nearby watercourse as part of the response. [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river at the Sheerstone Road gauge reached a depth of 1.6 m (recorded at c.12:50), causing the Iwade Stream to flow out of bank through adjacent gardens and onto the road; properties on Sheerstone Road experienced internal flooding as part of the event. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water began to overwhelm the road culvert at Sheerstone; screen was inspected (found little debris) and a 6"" pump was used to overpump from one side of the culvert to the other to manage flows. Photographs show muddy water and a partially blocked bar screen; images also show flooding to the front and rear of a property on Sheerstone Road. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater inundated the front and rear of properties and the street; muddy water covered the carriageway and encroached onto driveways and patios, partially submerging vehicles and rendering the road impassable. (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties; 3 cars [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 properties (across Sheerstone Road, Springvale and The Waterway); 1.6 m depth recorded at Sheerstone Road (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (front and rear flooded) (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Sheerstone, Iwade",England,"Internal flooding to residential properties; vehicles damaged and written off; Environment Agency carried out overpumping from the nearby watercourse as part of the response. [TAB] (p7)
