Highway gullies inspected and cleansed; all found free–flowing. One gully was initially inaccessible due to a parked car; gully outside 129F was cleansed and free–flowing. (p37)",,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3143419,0.8754955,"<div id=""html_e1796ebe0f91e4fedb78279daba5d074"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lower Road, Faversham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies on Station Road between Lower Road and London Road were cleansed and restored to free&ndash;flowing condition as part of drainage works following the May 2018 flood. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Lower Road, Faversham",England,Gullies on Station Road between Lower Road and London Road were cleansed and restored to free–flowing condition as part of drainage works following the May 2018 flood. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3122355,0.9102962,"<div id=""html_f704534e0c07063bcd453c3afe2f5616"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Graveney Road, Faversham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies were cleansed and are reported as free&ndash;flowing as part of drainage works undertaken after the May 2018 flood. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Graveney Road, Faversham",England,Gullies were cleansed and are reported as free–flowing as part of drainage works undertaken after the May 2018 flood. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.31174521007382,0.8797562059534899,"<div id=""html_f56afecd299320cb2eb2b98fea26231c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mount Field, Faversham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies were cleansed with no further issues reported as part of drainage works following the May 2018 flooding. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Mount Field, Faversham",England,Gullies were cleansed with no further issues reported as part of drainage works following the May 2018 flooding. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3257541,0.8815262,"<div id=""html_0f9246527b46711d6cea5789c2e4b63c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Seager Road, Faversham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies inspected and cleansed; most found free&ndash;flowing. Catch pits were identified as requiring cleansing. One jammed gully lid and one parked car were recorded as obstructions. A gully outside The Ship Inn was noted as cleansed but requiring CCTV inspection; gullies at the junction with Oare Road were cleansed and found free&ndash;flowing. (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Seager Road, Faversham",England,Gullies inspected and cleansed; most found free–flowing. Catch pits were identified as requiring cleansing. One jammed gully lid and one parked car were recorded as obstructions. A gully outside The Ship Inn was noted as cleansed but requiring CCTV inspection; gullies at the junction with Oare Road were cleansed and found free–flowing. (p37),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.31095429623419,0.8808229752703248,"<div id=""html_adabcc0242f15a65a9c42a9d4122cdcf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Upper St Anns Road, Faversham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Post&ndash;29 May 2018 surface water flooding response: highway gullies on Upper St Anns Road were cleansed as maintenance and restored to free&ndash;flowing condition. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Upper St Anns Road, Faversham",England,Post–29 May 2018 surface water flooding response: highway gullies on Upper St Anns Road were cleansed as maintenance and restored to free–flowing condition. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3305307,0.7713006,"<div id=""html_caf73b558f57d6a71a5e75265afb171a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Panteny Lane, Bapchild</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gullies near NSL signs on Panteny Lane were cleansed as part of post&ndash;29 May 2018 maintenance following the May flooding. [TAB] (p35)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Panteny Lane, Bapchild",England,Gullies near NSL signs on Panteny Lane were cleansed as part of post–29 May 2018 maintenance following the May flooding. [TAB] (p35),,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3367451,0.8013698,"<div id=""html_c8a73e92a5d0bfe13f7efff285e06e5f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Swale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Swale area was affected by flooding. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flood events were recorded across the district on 29 May 2018, with clustering of event locations near Sittingbourne and along the main east&ndash;west corridor towards Faversham. Both urban and adjacent rural locations within the district were affected. [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 29 May 2018 intensive rainfall produced surface runoff in parts of the district (notably the Iwade catchment underlain by less permeable soils). Highway drainage dependent on soakaways and infiltration features was overwhelmed, causing localized surface water/overland flow and flooding of carriageways and low&ndash;lying areas. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall across Swale led to high flows in the Iwade Stream and surface water flooding; water from an adjacent field overtopped a low bund and flooded Meadow Brown View and Purple Emperor Grove on the south&ndash;western edge of Iwade; overland runoff from fields and the highway caused flooding at Culnells Farm businesses (Iwade area). (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Teynham, within the borough of Swale, experienced surface water/urban flooding on 29 May 2018 that affected properties in the village and parish; drainage was overwhelmed causing water to pond in low&ndash;lying areas and reach depths sufficient to enter properties. (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread post&ndash;flood highway drainage maintenance: gullies and soakaways across the Swale area were inspected and cleansed following the May 2018 flood to restore free flow in the highway drainage network. (p35)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>13 properties (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Swale,England,"The Swale area was affected by flooding. (p1)
Multiple flood events were recorded across the district on 29 May 2018, with clustering of event locations near Sittingbourne and along the main east–west corridor towards Faversham. Both urban and adjacent rural locations within the district were affected. [IMG] (p9)
On 29 May 2018 intensive rainfall produced surface runoff in parts of the district (notably the Iwade catchment underlain by less permeable soils). Highway drainage dependent on soakaways and infiltration features was overwhelmed, causing localized surface water/overland flow and flooding of carriageways and low–lying areas. (p12)
Heavy rainfall across Swale led to high flows in the Iwade Stream and surface water flooding; water from an adjacent field overtopped a low bund and flooded Meadow Brown View and Purple Emperor Grove on the south–western edge of Iwade; overland runoff from fields and the highway caused flooding at Culnells Farm businesses (Iwade area). (p21)
Teynham, within the borough of Swale, experienced surface water/urban flooding on 29 May 2018 that affected properties in the village and parish; drainage was overwhelmed causing water to pond in low–lying areas and reach depths sufficient to enter properties. (p29)
Widespread post–flood highway drainage maintenance: gullies and soakaways across the Swale area were inspected and cleansed following the May 2018 flood to restore free flow in the highway drainage network. (p35)",13 properties (p29),GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.2070749,0.7210362,"<div id=""html_dc14426365629db620dcab8a4d7064dd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kent</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Kent experienced extensive surface water and fluvial (river) flooding that resulted in the inundation of properties. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the county on 29 May 2018 that met the criteria for a significant flood event; prompted a multi&ndash;agency response and a formal investigation due to the extent of impacts and public interest. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood events on 29 May 2018 occurred in the Sittingbourne&ndash;Faversham area of Kent; the mapped locations show distributed flooding within this part of the county, including clusters near Sittingbourne and along the corridor toward Faversham. [IMG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kent County Council carried out post&ndash;flood investigations and response actions across the county (e.g. inspections, cleansing and liaison on repairs and planning breaches) following the flooding event. (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Kent,England,"Parts of Kent experienced extensive surface water and fluvial (river) flooding that resulted in the inundation of properties. (p5)
Widespread flooding across the county on 29 May 2018 that met the criteria for a significant flood event; prompted a multi–agency response and a formal investigation due to the extent of impacts and public interest. (p6)
Flood events on 29 May 2018 occurred in the Sittingbourne–Faversham area of Kent; the mapped locations show distributed flooding within this part of the county, including clusters near Sittingbourne and along the corridor toward Faversham. [IMG] (p9)
Kent County Council carried out post–flood investigations and response actions across the county (e.g. inspections, cleansing and liaison on repairs and planning breaches) following the flooding event. (p38)",,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.279213,0.77177495,"<div id=""html_fbfb3db60668876b86cdc09deef00def"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Doddington</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding in Doddington including properties and highway; Old Lenham Road had properties affected and The Street experienced extensive highway flooding. [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water flowed down Old Lenham Road onto The Street; the highway was inundated and 8 properties experienced internal flooding. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected the village; vehicles driving through flood water may have generated bow waves that caused additional damage to properties in the village. (p25)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The community experienced flooding; local authorities and the River Stour Countryside Management Partnership are exploring NFM measures to manage run&ndash;off and reduce future flood risk; a community workshop was held to identify project opportunities. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The extreme storm produced rapid overland flows that led to flooding in Doddington; the event extremity here was over a 1 in 115 year intensity and led to proposals for natural flood management to intercept overland flows. (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Doddington,England,"Widespread flooding in Doddington including properties and highway; Old Lenham Road had properties affected and The Street experienced extensive highway flooding. [TAB] (p7)
Water flowed down Old Lenham Road onto The Street; the highway was inundated and 8 properties experienced internal flooding. (p24)
Surface water flooding affected the village; vehicles driving through flood water may have generated bow waves that caused additional damage to properties in the village. (p25)
The community experienced flooding; local authorities and the River Stour Countryside Management Partnership are exploring NFM measures to manage run–off and reduce future flood risk; a community workshop was held to identify project opportunities. (p38)
The extreme storm produced rapid overland flows that led to flooding in Doddington; the event extremity here was over a 1 in 115 year intensity and led to proposals for natural flood management to intercept overland flows. (p40)","8 properties [TAB] (p7)
8 properties (p24)",GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29.html,GB-KEN_8_2018-05-29,Swale flooding 29 May 2018,Flood Investigation Report: Flooding Affecting the Swale Area On 29 May 2018,063/Kent/Flooding-report-affecting-the-Swale-area-of-Kent-on-29-May-2018.pdf
GB-KEN,8,2018-05-29,51.3135702,0.786795,"<div id=""html_aeef7cf4b8a1be6ec5ecd7e7edc5e552"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lynsted</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected residential properties on The Street in Lynstead (sic). [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and foul water entered properties in the village; five properties on The Street were affected. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm runoff from surrounding higher ground collected in a low&ndash;lying depression in the village (on The Street). Highway drainage discharged to a nearby pond which reached capacity and overtopped, flooding the highway. Surface water infiltrated the Southern Water pumping station causing pump failure and foul sewer overflows, all contributing to local property flooding. [IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the flooding the community and landowners will work with KCC and the River Stour Countryside Management Partnership to explore NFM to manage run&ndash;off; Southern Water will investigate measures to reduce flood risk to the local pumping station. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows and rapid surface water runoff impacted the area; local drainage and watercourse capacity were overwhelmed. The pumping station at Lynsted was identified as requiring protection, indicating it was affected or at risk during the event. (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [TAB] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>five properties (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [IMG] (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,Lynsted,England,"Flooding affected residential properties on The Street in Lynstead (sic). [TAB] (p7)
Surface water runoff and foul water entered properties in the village; five properties on The Street were affected. (p26)
Storm runoff from surrounding higher ground collected in a low–lying depression in the village (on The Street). Highway drainage discharged to a nearby pond which reached capacity and overtopped, flooding the highway. Surface water infiltrated the Southern Water pumping station causing pump failure and foul sewer overflows, all contributing to local property flooding. [IMG] (p27)
Following the flooding the community and landowners will work with KCC and the River Stour Countryside Management Partnership to explore NFM to manage run–off; Southern Water will investigate measures to reduce flood risk to the local pumping station. (p38)
Overland flows and rapid surface water runoff impacted the area; local drainage and watercourse capacity were overwhelmed. The pumping station at Lynsted was identified as requiring protection, indicating it was affected or at risk during the event. (p40)","5 properties [TAB] (p7)
five properties (p26)
