GB-CAM,24,2020-08-13,52.2193679,-0.2634328,"<div id=""html_ef1903e9683d0147639451e041b6a259"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Willow Close, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residents' properties caused by a blind outfall of a surface water sewer located in a back garden; highway surface water appeared to drain to this sewer and contributed to flooding, prompting investigation and remedial works. [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Willow Close, St Neots",England,"Internal flooding of residents' properties caused by a blind outfall of a surface water sewer located in a back garden; highway surface water appeared to drain to this sewer and contributed to flooding, prompting investigation and remedial works. [TAB] (p15)",,GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13.html,GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13,St Neots flooding 13 Aug 2020 - 16 Aug 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-August-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,24,2020-08-13,52.22758,-0.2703789,"<div id=""html_11f74ec11374bae669ddd7640abbece9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced intense rainfall on 13 and 16 August 2020 that put extreme pressure on local drainage systems and produced high water volumes in watercourses and drainage systems, increasing flood risk across the settlement. Hardened, dry ground preceding the events reduced soakage and worsened surface water runoff. [MUL] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Between 13 and 16 August 2020 intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage networks in the town. Surface water sewers and road drainage were inundated, causing flooding from highways gullies, surface water on roads and surcharging of sewers. Floodwaters followed local topography and entered properties in areas at risk of surface water flooding. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Across the town seven properties were reported to have flooded internally (Drake Road; Monarch Road; Marchioness Way &mdash; 3 properties; Howitts Lane (Eynesbury); Hempsals (Eaton Ford)). Six further properties reported external flooding (Ackerman Street &mdash; 2 properties; Bishops Road (Eynesbury); The Broad Walk (Eynesbury); Ridgeway (Eynesbury)). [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Neots area recorded multiple instances of residential flooding across suburbs (Eaton Socon, Eynesbury and Eaton Ford) with houses, gardens and outbuildings affected. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Two consecutive extreme rainfall events on 13 and 16 August 2020 produced very intense storms that overwhelmed local drainage, causing surface water flooding across St Neots. Highways drainage and sewer systems came under extreme pressure; investigation and maintenance actions were initiated and a residents Flood Liaison Action Group (FLAG) was set up to work with risk management authorities. (p19)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>7 properties internal; 6 properties external [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,St Neots,England,"Experienced intense rainfall on 13 and 16 August 2020 that put extreme pressure on local drainage systems and produced high water volumes in watercourses and drainage systems, increasing flood risk across the settlement. Hardened, dry ground preceding the events reduced soakage and worsened surface water runoff. [MUL] (p7)
Between 13 and 16 August 2020 intense rainfall overwhelmed drainage networks in the town. Surface water sewers and road drainage were inundated, causing flooding from highways gullies, surface water on roads and surcharging of sewers. Floodwaters followed local topography and entered properties in areas at risk of surface water flooding. (p8)
Across the town seven properties were reported to have flooded internally (Drake Road; Monarch Road; Marchioness Way — 3 properties; Howitts Lane (Eynesbury); Hempsals (Eaton Ford)). Six further properties reported external flooding (Ackerman Street — 2 properties; Bishops Road (Eynesbury); The Broad Walk (Eynesbury); Ridgeway (Eynesbury)). [PAG,IMG] (p10)
St Neots area recorded multiple instances of residential flooding across suburbs (Eaton Socon, Eynesbury and Eaton Ford) with houses, gardens and outbuildings affected. [TAB] (p11)
Two consecutive extreme rainfall events on 13 and 16 August 2020 produced very intense storms that overwhelmed local drainage, causing surface water flooding across St Neots. Highways drainage and sewer systems came under extreme pressure; investigation and maintenance actions were initiated and a residents Flood Liaison Action Group (FLAG) was set up to work with risk management authorities. (p19)","7 properties (p8)
7 properties internal; 6 properties external [PAG,IMG] (p10)",GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13.html,GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13,St Neots flooding 13 Aug 2020 - 16 Aug 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-August-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,24,2020-08-13,52.2177279,-0.2644645,"<div id=""html_611ed91dd8b21730e5fdc7a45b95deba"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eynesbury</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was reported within Eynesbury (Howitts Lane is named as a location where internal flooding occurred). (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Howitts Lane reported internal flooding. External flooding was reported at Bishops Road, The Broad Walk and at Ridgeway. One property on Ridgeway reported no internal flooding itself but that neighbouring properties had flooded. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gardens, outbuildings (including garages) and nearby gardens were flooded; some reports also note flooding inside other nearby homes. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties (water entered houses and gardens or outbuildings) [TAB,PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was investigated after an extreme rainfall event. The LLFA liaised with the Highway Authority to arrange cleansing and surveying of the highway surface water drainage system and wrote to riparian landowners requesting ditch maintenance. Ditch clearance was carried out (April 2021) to gain access to a culvert; cleansing of the culvert was scheduled for 2021&ndash;22. The Highway Authority included gullies in a gully&ndash;jetting programme (April&ndash;June 2021) and increased gully maintenance frequency. [IMG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding to residents investigated; contributory cause identified as a blind outfall for a surface water sewer in a resident's back garden that drained highway surface water. Highways Projects arranged rerouting of highway drainage into the Anglian Water surface water sewer to alleviate the issue. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Part of 7 properties reported flooded internally (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property internal; 3 properties external [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Eynesbury,England,"Internal property flooding was reported within Eynesbury (Howitts Lane is named as a location where internal flooding occurred). (p9)
One property on Howitts Lane reported internal flooding. External flooding was reported at Bishops Road, The Broad Walk and at Ridgeway. One property on Ridgeway reported no internal flooding itself but that neighbouring properties had flooded. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Gardens, outbuildings (including garages) and nearby gardens were flooded; some reports also note flooding inside other nearby homes. [TAB] (p11)
Internal flooding of properties (water entered houses and gardens or outbuildings) [TAB,PAG] (p12)
Internal property flooding was investigated after an extreme rainfall event. The LLFA liaised with the Highway Authority to arrange cleansing and surveying of the highway surface water drainage system and wrote to riparian landowners requesting ditch maintenance. Ditch clearance was carried out (April 2021) to gain access to a culvert; cleansing of the culvert was scheduled for 2021–22. The Highway Authority included gullies in a gully–jetting programme (April–June 2021) and increased gully maintenance frequency. [IMG] (p14)
Internal flooding to residents investigated; contributory cause identified as a blind outfall for a surface water sewer in a resident's back garden that drained highway surface water. Highways Projects arranged rerouting of highway drainage into the Anglian Water surface water sewer to alleviate the issue. [TAB] (p15)","Part of 7 properties reported flooded internally (p9)
1 property internal; 3 properties external [PAG,IMG] (p10)",GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13.html,GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13,St Neots flooding 13 Aug 2020 - 16 Aug 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-August-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,24,2020-08-13,52.218525,-0.2902765,"<div id=""html_23fc90d1bc0d72aecd9c1fe13d9ed7cb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eaton Socon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple properties within Eaton Socon experienced internal flooding as surface water drainage systems were unable to convey intense rainfall and water followed local topography into properties. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties reported at Drake Road and Monarch Road, and three properties on Marchioness Way. External flooding was reported at Ackerman Street (two properties). [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple residential locations in Eaton Socon experienced flooding of inside houses, gardens and outbuildings (including garages) as recorded in the Central Eaton and North West Eaton sections. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in Central Eaton Socon; surface water drainage system surcharged during the storm event and surface water manholes were damaged by surcharge. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding was investigated on site; Anglian Water jet&ndash;cleaned the drainage system along the B1428 and the route of the surface water drainage from Drake Road was cleared. A blockage on the outfall from the highways surface water drainage system to Duloe Brook was investigated and cleared. Highway cleansing and gully&ndash;jetting works were scheduled/increased as part of the response. (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (collective for the three Eaton Socon streets); part of 7 properties reported flooded internally (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties internal; 2 properties external [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Eaton Socon,England,"Multiple properties within Eaton Socon experienced internal flooding as surface water drainage systems were unable to convey intense rainfall and water followed local topography into properties. (p9)
Internal flooding of residential properties reported at Drake Road and Monarch Road, and three properties on Marchioness Way. External flooding was reported at Ackerman Street (two properties). [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Multiple residential locations in Eaton Socon experienced flooding of inside houses, gardens and outbuildings (including garages) as recorded in the Central Eaton and North West Eaton sections. [TAB] (p11)
Internal flooding of properties in Central Eaton Socon; surface water drainage system surcharged during the storm event and surface water manholes were damaged by surcharge. [TAB] (p12)
Internal flooding was investigated on site; Anglian Water jet–cleaned the drainage system along the B1428 and the route of the surface water drainage from Drake Road was cleared. A blockage on the outfall from the highways surface water drainage system to Duloe Brook was investigated and cleared. Highway cleansing and gully–jetting works were scheduled/increased as part of the response. (p13)","3 properties (collective for the three Eaton Socon streets); part of 7 properties reported flooded internally (p9)
5 properties internal; 2 properties external [PAG,IMG] (p10)",GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13.html,GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13,St Neots flooding 13 Aug 2020 - 16 Aug 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-August-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,24,2020-08-13,52.2262531,-0.2787842,"<div id=""html_01a1e91dda1ffe93e02459cdcd9c1440"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eaton Ford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water drainage failure led to internal flooding of at least one property (Hempsals, Eaton Ford) as overland flows followed local topography. (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within Eaton Ford (HEMPSALS) floodwater entered properties &mdash; inside houses and outbuildings (including garages) were flooded. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of houses and flooding of gardens and outbuildings (including garages) at properties in Eaton Ford (HEMPSALS address cited). (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding investigated by RMA/LLFA; local surface water drainage issues being pursued by the Highway Authority and measures such as gully maintenance/jetting scheduled to reduce risk. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (internal flooding at Hempsals, Eaton Ford) (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Eaton Ford,England,"Surface water drainage failure led to internal flooding of at least one property (Hempsals, Eaton Ford) as overland flows followed local topography. (p9)
Within Eaton Ford (HEMPSALS) floodwater entered properties — inside houses and outbuildings (including garages) were flooded. [TAB] (p11)
Internal flooding of houses and flooding of gardens and outbuildings (including garages) at properties in Eaton Ford (HEMPSALS address cited). (p12)
Internal flooding investigated by RMA/LLFA; local surface water drainage issues being pursued by the Highway Authority and measures such as gully maintenance/jetting scheduled to reduce risk. [TAB] (p15)","1 property (internal flooding at Hempsals, Eaton Ford) (p9)",GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13.html,GB-CAM_24_2020-08-13,St Neots flooding 13 Aug 2020 - 16 Aug 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-August-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,31,2016-01-02,52.3844435,-0.5892047,"<div id=""html_a035864cb69d7258ad0b139b120ef02d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Windmill Close, Woodford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at Windmill Close was caused by moderate rainfall on a saturated catchment; land drainage systems could not convey the rainwater, so excess surface water flowed following natural contours to low points around private property. Field ditches to the south of Windmill Close may have been altered, directing surface water towards private property. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding entered the building envelope of a residential property at Windmill Close; residents affected and the property has experienced repeated internal flooding incidents in the last five years. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Affected residents were spoken to as part of a Flood Incident Investigation regarding a flooding incident on 2 January 2016; the location was investigated for flood risk (mapping indicated Windmill Close is not shown to be at risk of surface water or fluvial flooding). [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed into rear gardens from scrub land to the west, ponding around houses and entering air bricks; ground and paving were saturated/damp during the period described. [TAB,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed from scrub land beyond the rear boundary fence toward the rear of houses, ponded in gardens and around houses and entered air bricks. This caused continual saturated ground and dampness in rear gardens and other external areas. Residents excavated an informal ditch; following that excavation flooding was experienced at a different property on Windmill Close (and at one on Paddock Road) on early January 2016. [PAG,MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall over a saturated catchment and ineffective land drainage caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours to low points around private properties in Windmill Close. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Paddock Road/Windmill Close development was identified for review of planning, flood risk assessment and drainage design; developers were to be contacted if surface water drainage measures were absent or ineffective. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Documented residents' photographs relate to a flood incident affecting a property on Windmill Close, indicating the property was impacted by the flood. (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Runoff from the surrounding catchment flowed into ditches immediately south of the street, producing ponding and active, turbid flow adjacent to properties. Temporary defensive measures (an informal ditch and sandbags) were placed at the rear of properties to divert water away from the fence line. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An informal, temporary ditch has been constructed at the rear of residential properties and contains pooled/muddy water. The ditch runs parallel to rear boundary fences and shows recent vegetation clearance, indicating local surface water runoff/drainage measures to manage waterlogging at property boundaries. [IMG,PAG] (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sandbags were placed at the rear of a residential property to protect against floodwater entering the rear of the property. (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties; 50mm depth; 5 incidents (2012&ndash;December 2015); 1 property (02/01/2016); 1 property (03/01/2016); 1 property (07/01/2016); 1 property (09/03/2016) [TAB,MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>three properties; approximately 50mm depth; approximately five times; one different property on Windmill Close affected on 02/01/16, 03/01/16 and 07/01/16 [PAG,MUL] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Windmill Close, Woodford",England,"Flooding at Windmill Close was caused by moderate rainfall on a saturated catchment; land drainage systems could not convey the rainwater, so excess surface water flowed following natural contours to low points around private property. Field ditches to the south of Windmill Close may have been altered, directing surface water towards private property. (p7)
Internal flooding entered the building envelope of a residential property at Windmill Close; residents affected and the property has experienced repeated internal flooding incidents in the last five years. [MUL] (p9)
Affected residents were spoken to as part of a Flood Incident Investigation regarding a flooding incident on 2 January 2016; the location was investigated for flood risk (mapping indicated Windmill Close is not shown to be at risk of surface water or fluvial flooding). [PAG] (p10)
Surface water flowed into rear gardens from scrub land to the west, ponding around houses and entering air bricks; ground and paving were saturated/damp during the period described. [TAB,MUL] (p12)
Surface water flowed from scrub land beyond the rear boundary fence toward the rear of houses, ponded in gardens and around houses and entered air bricks. This caused continual saturated ground and dampness in rear gardens and other external areas. Residents excavated an informal ditch; following that excavation flooding was experienced at a different property on Windmill Close (and at one on Paddock Road) on early January 2016. [PAG,MUL] (p14)
Moderate rainfall over a saturated catchment and ineffective land drainage caused excess surface water to flow over ground following natural contours to low points around private properties in Windmill Close. (p15)
The Paddock Road/Windmill Close development was identified for review of planning, flood risk assessment and drainage design; developers were to be contacted if surface water drainage measures were absent or ineffective. (p24)
Documented residents' photographs relate to a flood incident affecting a property on Windmill Close, indicating the property was impacted by the flood. (p38)
Runoff from the surrounding catchment flowed into ditches immediately south of the street, producing ponding and active, turbid flow adjacent to properties. Temporary defensive measures (an informal ditch and sandbags) were placed at the rear of properties to divert water away from the fence line. [IMG,PAG] (p43)
An informal, temporary ditch has been constructed at the rear of residential properties and contains pooled/muddy water. The ditch runs parallel to rear boundary fences and shows recent vegetation clearance, indicating local surface water runoff/drainage measures to manage waterlogging at property boundaries. [IMG,PAG] (p44)
Sandbags were placed at the rear of a residential property to protect against floodwater entering the rear of the property. (p47)","one property (p7)
one property [MUL] (p9)
three properties; 50mm depth; 5 incidents (2012–December 2015); 1 property (02/01/2016); 1 property (03/01/2016); 1 property (07/01/2016); 1 property (09/03/2016) [TAB,MUL] (p12)
three properties; approximately 50mm depth; approximately five times; one different property on Windmill Close affected on 02/01/16, 03/01/16 and 07/01/16 [PAG,MUL] (p14)",GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02.html,GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02,Woodford flooding 02 Jan 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Windmill Close, Woodford, 2nd JANUARY 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood-Investigation-Woodford-January-2016.pdf
GB-NNH,31,2016-01-02,52.3846789,-0.5886541,"<div id=""html_40e8a0a981519e9e9de533a1f54e45ac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Paddock Road, Woodford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as part of the flood incident investigated on 2 January 2016; excess surface water from rainfall on a saturated catchment flowed to low points around private property in the area, contributing to internal flooding of a property within the incident area. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Run&ndash;off from agricultural field ditches flows toward the southwest corner of the Paddock Road housing development. A recently excavated shallow informal ditch (from that corner, along the east boundary of the scrub land) collects this water and conveys it along the rear boundaries of properties, contributing to flooding of those properties. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from scrub land beyond the rear boundary fence flowed toward the rear of properties, ponded in gardens and around houses (approximately 50mm) and flowed into air bricks. Flooding affected the garage of the property on Paddock Road and caused continual saturated ground and dampness around the property. [PAG,MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Land drainage systems downstream of the Paddock Road housing estate were unable to convey rainwater effectively to larger watercourses, contributing to excess surface water flowing across ground toward private properties. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The formal ditch to the west of Paddock Road and associated drainage/pipework were identified for ownership clarification and investigation; route and condition of pipework were to be checked and maintained to address flood risk. Planning and surface water drainage for the adjacent Paddock Road/Windmill Close development were to be reviewed. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Informal temporary ditch to the rear of properties is waterlogged and contains turbid, flowing water used as an ad&ndash;hoc drainage route; muddy, obstructed channel indicates surface water runoff and poor drainage adjacent to property boundaries. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Informal temporary ditch to the rear of properties was waterlogged and carrying muddy, flowing water, indicating runoff/drainage overload affecting rear gardens/properties. [IMG,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A single line/row of white sandbags was placed at the garage threshold of a residential property to prevent floodwater ingress into the garage. [PAG] (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property on Paddock Road; approximately 50mm depth [PAG,MUL] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Paddock Road, Woodford",England,"Identified as part of the flood incident investigated on 2 January 2016; excess surface water from rainfall on a saturated catchment flowed to low points around private property in the area, contributing to internal flooding of a property within the incident area. (p7)
Run–off from agricultural field ditches flows toward the southwest corner of the Paddock Road housing development. A recently excavated shallow informal ditch (from that corner, along the east boundary of the scrub land) collects this water and conveys it along the rear boundaries of properties, contributing to flooding of those properties. (p11)
Surface water runoff from scrub land beyond the rear boundary fence flowed toward the rear of properties, ponded in gardens and around houses (approximately 50mm) and flowed into air bricks. Flooding affected the garage of the property on Paddock Road and caused continual saturated ground and dampness around the property. [PAG,MUL] (p14)
Land drainage systems downstream of the Paddock Road housing estate were unable to convey rainwater effectively to larger watercourses, contributing to excess surface water flowing across ground toward private properties. (p15)
The formal ditch to the west of Paddock Road and associated drainage/pipework were identified for ownership clarification and investigation; route and condition of pipework were to be checked and maintained to address flood risk. Planning and surface water drainage for the adjacent Paddock Road/Windmill Close development were to be reviewed. [PAG] (p24)
Informal temporary ditch to the rear of properties is waterlogged and contains turbid, flowing water used as an ad–hoc drainage route; muddy, obstructed channel indicates surface water runoff and poor drainage adjacent to property boundaries. [IMG,PAG] (p45)
Informal temporary ditch to the rear of properties was waterlogged and carrying muddy, flowing water, indicating runoff/drainage overload affecting rear gardens/properties. [IMG,PAG] (p46)
A single line/row of white sandbags was placed at the garage threshold of a residential property to prevent floodwater ingress into the garage. [PAG] (p47)","one property (p7)
one property on Paddock Road; approximately 50mm depth [PAG,MUL] (p14)",GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02.html,GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02,Woodford flooding 02 Jan 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Windmill Close, Woodford, 2nd JANUARY 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood-Investigation-Woodford-January-2016.pdf
GB-NNH,31,2016-01-02,52.3855383,-0.5905013,"<div id=""html_d54c44d8af08baec1b71d80d3ee29b02"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mill Road, Woodford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Mapping indicates a localised area at high risk of surface water flooding to the rear of properties on Mill Road. A surface water public sewer manhole is shown in front of No. 97 Mill Road (with no pipework shown from that point). Ditches discharge to a pipe in a brick headwall at Mill Road, which is part of the flow path conveying water that affects rear boundaries of properties. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overtopped a ditch to the east and flowed into the rear garden causing minor ponding in December 2015. On 11/01/16 the overtopping increased, the pond became much deeper and extended across the width of the garden (ponding located in a depression several metres from the house). [MUL] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ownership, route and condition of pipework in Mill Road (from the headwall at the end of the formal ditch west of Paddock Road) were identified as unclear and requiring investigation and maintenance to manage flood risk. [PAG] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Mill Road, Woodford",England,"Mapping indicates a localised area at high risk of surface water flooding to the rear of properties on Mill Road. A surface water public sewer manhole is shown in front of No. 97 Mill Road (with no pipework shown from that point). Ditches discharge to a pipe in a brick headwall at Mill Road, which is part of the flow path conveying water that affects rear boundaries of properties. [PAG] (p11)
Surface water overtopped a ditch to the east and flowed into the rear garden causing minor ponding in December 2015. On 11/01/16 the overtopping increased, the pond became much deeper and extended across the width of the garden (ponding located in a depression several metres from the house). [MUL] (p14)
Ownership, route and condition of pipework in Mill Road (from the headwall at the end of the formal ditch west of Paddock Road) were identified as unclear and requiring investigation and maintenance to manage flood risk. [PAG] (p24)",,GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02.html,GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02,Woodford flooding 02 Jan 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Windmill Close, Woodford, 2nd JANUARY 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood-Investigation-Woodford-January-2016.pdf
GB-NNH,31,2016-01-02,52.38440789599423,-0.585276260482043,"<div id=""html_4f2ee8e57d8af1fbeb2316f10d55577d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woodford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Windmill Close in Woodford is the subject of the Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation report, indicating flooding occurred at this location and was investigated. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Subject of a Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation (report title references Windmill Close, Woodford). (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface water flooding occurred in Woodford on 2 January 2016: moderate rainfall on a saturated catchment with inadequate land drainage caused excess surface water to flow to low points around private properties; one property experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident in the village (Windmill Close) resulted in internal flooding of a residential property; the affected property has flooded on more than one occasion within the past five years. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall over a saturated catchment led to land drainage systems being unable to convey rainwater, producing excess surface water that flowed to low points around private properties in Woodford. [PAG] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residents installed temporary, informal drainage systems to protect their properties from flooding during the Flood Incident; residents were identified as being at risk and advised to take protective actions. (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Woodford,England,"Windmill Close in Woodford is the subject of the Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation report, indicating flooding occurred at this location and was investigated. (p1)
Subject of a Northamptonshire County Council flood investigation (report title references Windmill Close, Woodford). (p2)
Localised surface water flooding occurred in Woodford on 2 January 2016: moderate rainfall on a saturated catchment with inadequate land drainage caused excess surface water to flow to low points around private properties; one property experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p7)
A flood incident in the village (Windmill Close) resulted in internal flooding of a residential property; the affected property has flooded on more than one occasion within the past five years. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
Moderate rainfall over a saturated catchment led to land drainage systems being unable to convey rainwater, producing excess surface water that flowed to low points around private properties in Woodford. [PAG] (p15)
Residents installed temporary, informal drainage systems to protect their properties from flooding during the Flood Incident; residents were identified as being at risk and advised to take protective actions. (p16)","one property (p7)
one property [PAG,MUL] (p9)",GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02.html,GB-NNH_31_2016-01-02,Woodford flooding 02 Jan 2016,"Flood Investigation Report: Windmill Close, Woodford, 2nd JANUARY 2016",090/North Northamptonshire/Flood-Investigation-Woodford-January-2016.pdf
GB-NNH,19,2020-08-16,52.30242255568699,-0.6853308955248743,"<div id=""html_0a10ccba38fdbb3bf100ace00394df8b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Knox Mews</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer flooding to external areas of a property at Knox Mews. [TAB] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Knox Mews,England,Sewer flooding to external areas of a property at Knox Mews. [TAB] (p11),,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Newcomen Road, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Newcomen Road, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,19,2020-08-16,52.3035135,-0.6853421,"<div id=""html_1644e613b4b56d33e71fdc5774012eff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Newcomen Road, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense, short&ndash;duration heavy rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around properties, causing internal flooding of multiple neighbouring buildings on Newcomen Road. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of buildings occurred during a single flood incident; water entered buildings (crossed thresholds) on Newcomen Road. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties experienced flooding to rear gardens from surcharging manhole covers (sewer flooding) which flowed across gardens into rear alleys; in other properties surface water ran down adjacent streets and open space into rear gardens and entered properties through rear/kitchen doors and by seepage through rear walls. One property had whole&ndash;ground&ndash;floor inundation reaching electrical sockets, causing loss of electricity and damage to appliances. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The north side of Newcomen Road was flooded by surface water flowing south from Gordon Road, across a public open space and through rear garden gates/fences into rear of dwellings. Water entered properties via rear doors, air bricks and other voids, causing internal flooding and prompting some residents to board up back gates to try to prevent recurrence. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed south beneath gates and fencing of rear gardens on the north side of Newcomen Road towards the rear of dwellings. Water entered dwellings via rear doors, air bricks and other voids/weaknesses. Standing water increased in the natural valley so lower properties experienced deeper internal flooding. The foul public sewer at the rear of properties on the south side (also accepting roof water) was exceeded; manhole covers were forcibly lifted and strong flows discharged from open manholes, producing overland flow that contributed to water in the back alley. Roof gutters were also reported overtopping, adding to surface runoff. [PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland/surface water flow from the north entering rear gardens; residents have installed improvised barriers (plastic sheeting, wooden planks, bricks) at the base of walls/gates to block or divert water from entering rear gardens. There is also a significant build&ndash;up of fallen leaves that could affect drainage at the carriageway channel, increasing risk of surface water run&ndash;off into properties. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland water flow from the north entering rear gardens; residents made attempts to prevent water entering rear gardens. Significant build&ndash;up of fallen leaves along the carriageway channel noted, which could impair gully/road drainage and exacerbate surface water runoff into properties' rear gardens. [PAG] (p39)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>250mm (assumed to reach electrical sockets); 15mm depth in one property [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties (on Newcomen Road) reported internal flooding; area total 11 properties flooded internally in Wellingborough (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Newcomen Road, Wellingborough",England,"Intense, short–duration heavy rainfall overwhelmed surface water drainage. Excess surface water flowed over ground to low points around properties, causing internal flooding of multiple neighbouring buildings on Newcomen Road. (p6)
Internal flooding of buildings occurred during a single flood incident; water entered buildings (crossed thresholds) on Newcomen Road. (p8)
