GB-NNH,19,2020-08-16,52.3027574,-0.6838398,"<div id=""html_32dbd704d4bb8026fd237bb683d03ea0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Knox Road, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Sewer flooding to external areas of properties on Knox Road. [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Knox Road, Wellingborough",England,"Sewer flooding to external areas of properties on Knox Road. [TAB,MUL] (p11)",,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Newcomen Road, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Newcomen Road, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,19,2020-08-16,52.3044869,-0.6836382,"<div id=""html_133807de175ba2a984858e9c4ae662ee"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mill Road, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Strong surface water flows reached the junction of Mill Road and Gordon Road; the junction acted as a low point where drainage was overwhelmed and water flowed over kerbs into the nearby public open space and then into rear gardens of properties on Newcomen Road. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Photographs show the low point on Mill Road (opposite the junction with Gordon Road) with wet pavement and storm drains present. The images document surface water accumulation/damp conditions at this low point and the presence of a gully/drain at the carriageway edge adjacent to private rear gardens (Newcomen Road). [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Mill Road, Wellingborough",England,"Strong surface water flows reached the junction of Mill Road and Gordon Road; the junction acted as a low point where drainage was overwhelmed and water flowed over kerbs into the nearby public open space and then into rear gardens of properties on Newcomen Road. (p18)
Photographs show the low point on Mill Road (opposite the junction with Gordon Road) with wet pavement and storm drains present. The images document surface water accumulation/damp conditions at this low point and the presence of a gully/drain at the carriageway edge adjacent to private rear gardens (Newcomen Road). [IMG,PAG] (p37)",,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Newcomen Road, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Newcomen Road, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,19,2020-08-16,52.3042884,-0.685832,"<div id=""html_32137e8f2ab7d94fa76d254f704f9462"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gordon Road, Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ran down Gordon Street, across public open space and into rear gardens, contributing to flooding of properties' rear ground&ndash;floor areas. [TAB] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flows from higher in the catchment moved south along roads; strong flows reached the junction of Gordon Road and Mill Road. Drainage systems higher in the catchment were exceeded, contributing to flows that overwhelmed lower drainage and routed water into the public open space south of the junction. (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Gordon Road, Wellingborough",England,"Surface water ran down Gordon Street, across public open space and into rear gardens, contributing to flooding of properties' rear ground–floor areas. [TAB] (p17)
Surface water flows from higher in the catchment moved south along roads; strong flows reached the junction of Gordon Road and Mill Road. Drainage systems higher in the catchment were exceeded, contributing to flows that overwhelmed lower drainage and routed water into the public open space south of the junction. (p18)",,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Newcomen Road, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Newcomen Road, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,19,2020-08-16,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_4e59599ec4934a4b868a48f5874ca243"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Met Office issued a yellow thunderstorm warning covering Northamptonshire for 16 August 2020; Gov.uk forecast indicated localised flooding was possible from surface water and small rivers in the area, with properties potentially flooding and travel disruption possible. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding occurred in parts of the catchment within Northamptonshire, affecting highway drainage and developments and prompting multi&ndash;agency actions to cleanse drains, assess capacity and review planning and drainage measures to reduce flood risk. (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"Met Office issued a yellow thunderstorm warning covering Northamptonshire for 16 August 2020; Gov.uk forecast indicated localised flooding was possible from surface water and small rivers in the area, with properties potentially flooding and travel disruption possible. (p10)
Surface water flooding occurred in parts of the catchment within Northamptonshire, affecting highway drainage and developments and prompting multi–agency actions to cleanse drains, assess capacity and review planning and drainage measures to reduce flood risk. (p27)",,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Newcomen Road, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Newcomen Road, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,19,2020-08-16,52.30189,-0.6937309,"<div id=""html_f241417a41a6286e36c0f1580000e317"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wellingborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding from intense heavy rainfall led to internal flooding of multiple nearby buildings (on Newcomen Road) when local drainage systems were unable to convey the runoff, causing water to flow to low points around properties. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area (Newcomen Road within Wellingborough) experienced internal flooding of buildings during the single flood incident. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report describes a flood incident in the area of Wellingborough involving surface water / urban flooding linked to highway and private drainage discharging to the public surface water sewer. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>In this area of Wellingborough (including Newcomen Road), internal flooding affected properties; community impacts included displaced vulnerable residents and property damage such as replaced flooring. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas of Wellingborough were affected by surface water/urban flooding. Several properties and external areas held relatively deep flood water with no effective means to drain; Fire and Rescue pumped water from affected properties and external areas to safe areas. Drainage capacity issues were reported. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>This area experienced flooding caused by intense heavy rainfall over a relatively short period. Properties were affected; details of affected properties and the immediate response were shared by the Emergency Planning Team with the Lead Local Flood Authority, and Section 19 flood incident investigations were instructed where thresholds were met. [PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense heavy rainfall caused surface water drainage systems across the catchment to be unable to collect and convey rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around properties. These low points flooded because there were no effective overland flow routes to divert water to safe areas, creating a reliance on buried drainage systems (some of which required maintenance/cleansing). There was no prior warning and no community or property&ndash;level resilience measures in place to be deployed. [PAG] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>more than five buildings (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>11 properties reported to have flooded internally in this area of Wellingborough (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Wellingborough,England,"Surface water flooding from intense heavy rainfall led to internal flooding of multiple nearby buildings (on Newcomen Road) when local drainage systems were unable to convey the runoff, causing water to flow to low points around properties. (p6)
Area (Newcomen Road within Wellingborough) experienced internal flooding of buildings during the single flood incident. (p8)
The report describes a flood incident in the area of Wellingborough involving surface water / urban flooding linked to highway and private drainage discharging to the public surface water sewer. (p15)
In this area of Wellingborough (including Newcomen Road), internal flooding affected properties; community impacts included displaced vulnerable residents and property damage such as replaced flooring. (p18)
Areas of Wellingborough were affected by surface water/urban flooding. Several properties and external areas held relatively deep flood water with no effective means to drain; Fire and Rescue pumped water from affected properties and external areas to safe areas. Drainage capacity issues were reported. (p20)
This area experienced flooding caused by intense heavy rainfall over a relatively short period. Properties were affected; details of affected properties and the immediate response were shared by the Emergency Planning Team with the Lead Local Flood Authority, and Section 19 flood incident investigations were instructed where thresholds were met. [PAG] (p21)
Intense heavy rainfall caused surface water drainage systems across the catchment to be unable to collect and convey rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow over ground to low points around properties. These low points flooded because there were no effective overland flow routes to divert water to safe areas, creating a reliance on buried drainage systems (some of which required maintenance/cleansing). There was no prior warning and no community or property–level resilience measures in place to be deployed. [PAG] (p22)","more than five buildings (p6)
more than five buildings (p8)
11 properties reported to have flooded internally in this area of Wellingborough (p18)",GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16.html,GB-NNH_19_2020-08-16,Wellingborough flooding 16 Aug 2020,"Flood Investigation Report: Newcomen Road, Wellingborough, 16th August 2020","090/North Northamptonshire/Flood Investigation Report - Newcomen Road, Wellingborough.pdf"
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.26475616425703,-0.7507282363301858,"<div id=""html_07480085ee220a33384e5c6a524a2284"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Earls Barton Library and Community Centre</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the adjacent roadway and sidewalks in front of the Jobcentre Plus building, likely impeding access to the premises. [IMG] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Earls Barton Library and Community Centre,England,"Floodwater covered the adjacent roadway and sidewalks in front of the Jobcentre Plus building, likely impeding access to the premises. [IMG] (p37)",,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.265402,-0.7534214,"<div id=""html_a4832bc05d7244be5457e7fbefca2013"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Square, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location identified as the subject of the Flood Investigation Report &mdash; the Square in Earls Barton was investigated for flooding. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall overwhelmed drainage so excess surface water flowed over ground and along the carriageway and kerbs into low points on the public highway and around private property, causing internal flooding to at least one property. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage systems could not collect and carry rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow over ground and along the carriageway/kerbs/footways toward low points on the public highway and around private property, resulting in internal flooding of a property. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding crossed the threshold of a property located at The Square; the property experienced internal flooding during the incident and has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a low point receiving surface water sewers from High Street and West Street; investigation noted road gullies on approaches and carriageway/footway thresholds that influence water behaviour. These factors were associated with surface water flooding observed during the incident. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water inundation of the highway and external flooding of private property caused by insufficient highway drain capacity (undated). On 16/09/2015 surface water from High Street and West Way overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square; water entered the doorway of a property on The Square and flowed into several rooms, nearly reaching the electrical cupboard. [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water built up at the corner of Station Road and Broad Street and flowed into the doorway of a property at The Square; internal flooding reached approximately 25 mm depth through rooms, required cleaning up and temporarily affected the business. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Arrangement of kerbs and levels allowed surface water flowing off the carriageway to reach the footway and enter doorways of properties, making areas vulnerable to surface water flooding during the event. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater overflowed onto the pavement and street area in front of a stone building; sandbags were placed at the building base to protect against ingress; debris and displaced paving material concentrated near the building entrance; construction materials and a traffic cone were affected by the floodwater. [IMG,PAG] (p34)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property; 25 mm depth of water (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"The Square, Earls Barton",England,"Location identified as the subject of the Flood Investigation Report — the Square in Earls Barton was investigated for flooding. (p1)
Moderate rainfall overwhelmed drainage so excess surface water flowed over ground and along the carriageway and kerbs into low points on the public highway and around private property, causing internal flooding to at least one property. [PAG] (p6)
Drainage systems could not collect and carry rainwater, causing excess surface water to flow over ground and along the carriageway/kerbs/footways toward low points on the public highway and around private property, resulting in internal flooding of a property. [PAG] (p7)
Internal flooding crossed the threshold of a property located at The Square; the property experienced internal flooding during the incident and has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. [PAG] (p9)
Identified as a low point receiving surface water sewers from High Street and West Street; investigation noted road gullies on approaches and carriageway/footway thresholds that influence water behaviour. These factors were associated with surface water flooding observed during the incident. [PAG] (p10)
Surface water inundation of the highway and external flooding of private property caused by insufficient highway drain capacity (undated). On 16/09/2015 surface water from High Street and West Way overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square; water entered the doorway of a property on The Square and flowed into several rooms, nearly reaching the electrical cupboard. [TAB,PAG] (p11)
Surface water built up at the corner of Station Road and Broad Street and flowed into the doorway of a property at The Square; internal flooding reached approximately 25 mm depth through rooms, required cleaning up and temporarily affected the business. (p12)
Arrangement of kerbs and levels allowed surface water flowing off the carriageway to reach the footway and enter doorways of properties, making areas vulnerable to surface water flooding during the event. (p13)
Floodwater overflowed onto the pavement and street area in front of a stone building; sandbags were placed at the building base to protect against ingress; debris and displaced paving material concentrated near the building entrance; construction materials and a traffic cone were affected by the floodwater. [IMG,PAG] (p34)","1 property [PAG] (p6)
1 property [PAG] (p7)
1 property [PAG] (p9)
1 property; 25 mm depth of water (p12)",GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.2670253,-0.7502767,"<div id=""html_b0b384fef81428886501553e88a0a6f5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>High Street, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water sewers flow from High Street to The Square; several road gullies on High Street were observed blocked with leaves and silt during the flood investigation, and runoff from private car parks and hardstanding was seen to flow onto the public highway during the incident. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface&ndash;water runoff from higher ground on High Street contributed to flows along carriageways and over road gullies; this runoff overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square and contributed to accumulation and entry of surface water into nearby property (notably on 16/09/2015). Repeated overtopping from High Street (and West Way) has been reported historically. [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed from High Street north to south across the crossroads junction toward the corner of Station Road and Broad Street, contributing to accumulation of water at that junction. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Approximately six times every year for last five years [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"High Street, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water sewers flow from High Street to The Square; several road gullies on High Street were observed blocked with leaves and silt during the flood investigation, and runoff from private car parks and hardstanding was seen to flow onto the public highway during the incident. (p10)
Surface–water runoff from higher ground on High Street contributed to flows along carriageways and over road gullies; this runoff overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square and contributed to accumulation and entry of surface water into nearby property (notably on 16/09/2015). Repeated overtopping from High Street (and West Way) has been reported historically. [TAB,PAG] (p11)
Surface water flowed from High Street north to south across the crossroads junction toward the corner of Station Road and Broad Street, contributing to accumulation of water at that junction. (p12)","Approximately six times every year for last five years [TAB,PAG] (p11)",GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.2657751,-0.7550298,"<div id=""html_7e3f343f52718042e208d6b2afa35a77"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Street, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water sewers direct flows from West Street into the centre of Earls Barton; limited inspection noted runoff from private car parks and other hardstanding on West Street flowing to the public highway where drainage was limited or non&ndash;existent, contributing to surface water flooding during the incident. (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"West Street, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water sewers direct flows from West Street into the centre of Earls Barton; limited inspection noted runoff from private car parks and other hardstanding on West Street flowing to the public highway where drainage was limited or non–existent, contributing to surface water flooding during the incident. (p10)",,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.2646634,-0.7511289,"<div id=""html_40268fc097db265a37247c1d39583446"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Broad Street, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Described as the low point where surface water sewers discharge and identified on flood mapping as high risk; several road gullies on Broad Street were seen to be blocked with leaves and silt during the flood investigation, implicating Broad Street in the surface water flooding observed. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water continued east along Broad Street to the low point opposite Churchill Road, with water reported flowing out of road gullies at low areas; overtopping of kerbs at the low point flooded the footway and the road and footways at low points of Broad Street were flooded. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Arrangement of kerbs and levels allowed surface water flowing off the carriageway to reach the footway and enter doorways of properties, making areas vulnerable to surface water flooding during the event. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater covered the road and intersection, extended onto sidewalks and up to residential doorsteps on the south side of Broad Street, impeding vehicle movement and pedestrian access; parked vehicles were partially in standing floodwater. [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Water emerging from a kerbside (offlet) gully and flowing onto the road and pavement, causing localised surface water flooding that pooled along the pavement and extended into the carriageway, affecting pedestrian movement and vehicle passage and posing a hazard. [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised footway flooding on the south side of Broad Street opposite Churchill Road, with water pooling around a drain and wet pavement causing pedestrian inconvenience and indicating ineffective drainage/blockage. [IMG,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Footway flooding on the south side of Broad Street opposite Churchill Road. Roof drainage downpipe discharging into a blocked highway gully contributed to surface water accumulation on the pavement, creating localized flooding and risk of water ingress to adjacent properties. [IMG,PAG] (p43)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 parked cars [IMG,PAG] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Broad Street, Earls Barton",England,"Described as the low point where surface water sewers discharge and identified on flood mapping as high risk; several road gullies on Broad Street were seen to be blocked with leaves and silt during the flood investigation, implicating Broad Street in the surface water flooding observed. [PAG] (p10)
Surface water continued east along Broad Street to the low point opposite Churchill Road, with water reported flowing out of road gullies at low areas; overtopping of kerbs at the low point flooded the footway and the road and footways at low points of Broad Street were flooded. (p12)
Arrangement of kerbs and levels allowed surface water flowing off the carriageway to reach the footway and enter doorways of properties, making areas vulnerable to surface water flooding during the event. (p13)
Floodwater covered the road and intersection, extended onto sidewalks and up to residential doorsteps on the south side of Broad Street, impeding vehicle movement and pedestrian access; parked vehicles were partially in standing floodwater. [IMG,PAG] (p37)
Water emerging from a kerbside (offlet) gully and flowing onto the road and pavement, causing localised surface water flooding that pooled along the pavement and extended into the carriageway, affecting pedestrian movement and vehicle passage and posing a hazard. [IMG,PAG] (p38)
Localised footway flooding on the south side of Broad Street opposite Churchill Road, with water pooling around a drain and wet pavement causing pedestrian inconvenience and indicating ineffective drainage/blockage. [IMG,PAG] (p42)
Footway flooding on the south side of Broad Street opposite Churchill Road. Roof drainage downpipe discharging into a blocked highway gully contributed to surface water accumulation on the pavement, creating localized flooding and risk of water ingress to adjacent properties. [IMG,PAG] (p43)","2 parked cars [IMG,PAG] (p37)",GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.2524261,-0.7470122,"<div id=""html_6a7944b06b3313da6db0d8cb089e54cf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple surface&ndash;water flooding incidents affecting the highway: on 09/04/1998 the road was closed for 48 hours due to surface water flooding; further flooding recorded near traffic signals on 21/12/2012 and 23/12/2012; on 16/09/2015 surface water from High Street/West Way overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square contributing to highway accumulation. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overtopped lowered kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square, flowed onto the south&ndash;side footway of the crossroads and into the doorway of an adjacent property (at The Square). Footways at the corner were flooded. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>48 hours [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Station Road, Earls Barton",England,"Multiple surface–water flooding incidents affecting the highway: on 09/04/1998 the road was closed for 48 hours due to surface water flooding; further flooding recorded near traffic signals on 21/12/2012 and 23/12/2012; on 16/09/2015 surface water from High Street/West Way overtopped kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square contributing to highway accumulation. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
Surface water overtopped lowered kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square, flowed onto the south–side footway of the crossroads and into the doorway of an adjacent property (at The Square). Footways at the corner were flooded. (p12)","48 hours [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)",GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.2677619,-0.7563716,"<div id=""html_d524aff32f56e4f60d9898b317d9c8ea"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Way, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from higher ground on West Way flowed along the carriageway towards The Square, overtopping kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square and contributing to surface water entering property at The Square (water entered several rooms and nearly reached the electrical cupboard) on 16/09/15. [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff was reported flowing from higher ground on West Way along the carriageway and over road gullies toward The Square and the low point at the corner of Station Road and Broad Street, contributing to flooding at that junction. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>six times every year for five years [TAB,PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"West Way, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water runoff from higher ground on West Way flowed along the carriageway towards The Square, overtopping kerbs at the corner of Station Road and The Square and contributing to surface water entering property at The Square (water entered several rooms and nearly reached the electrical cupboard) on 16/09/15. [TAB,PAG] (p11)
Surface water runoff was reported flowing from higher ground on West Way along the carriageway and over road gullies toward The Square and the low point at the corner of Station Road and Broad Street, contributing to flooding at that junction. (p12)","six times every year for five years [TAB,PAG] (p11)",GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.2665922,-0.7474644,"<div id=""html_81b531f9828b73fd4d137b5ff3e377f8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Churchill Road, Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from Churchill Road contributed to flooding at low points (notably Broad Street/The Square), and was reported as a source of surface water that flooded the south footway at the low point of Broad Street on 14/09/15. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from Churchill Road joined flows on Broad Street, contributing to the accumulation of water at the low point opposite Churchill Road and downstream flooding of footways. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction of Churchill Road with Broad Street was inundated; floodwater covered the intersection area and flowed from kerb offlet gullies, indicating drainage was overwhelmed and access was impeded. [IMG] (p37)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Churchill Road, Earls Barton",England,"Surface water from Churchill Road contributed to flooding at low points (notably Broad Street/The Square), and was reported as a source of surface water that flooded the south footway at the low point of Broad Street on 14/09/15. [TAB] (p11)
Surface water runoff from Churchill Road joined flows on Broad Street, contributing to the accumulation of water at the low point opposite Churchill Road and downstream flooding of footways. (p12)
The junction of Churchill Road with Broad Street was inundated; floodwater covered the intersection area and flowed from kerb offlet gullies, indicating drainage was overwhelmed and access was impeded. [IMG] (p37)",,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_a82d427945226d393aa5c6f9a505823b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A surface water flooding incident occurred within the county at The Square, Earls Barton, where drainage capacity was exceeded and at least one property suffered internal flooding. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding crossed the threshold of a property at The Square, Earls Barton. The affected property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"A surface water flooding incident occurred within the county at The Square, Earls Barton, where drainage capacity was exceeded and at least one property suffered internal flooding. (p6)
Internal flooding crossed the threshold of a property at The Square, Earls Barton. The affected property has experienced internal flooding on more than one occasion in the last five years. (p8)","1 property (p6)
one property (p8)",GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,17,2015-09-16,52.263397,-0.7455269,"<div id=""html_9b57f2e1f3bf25168f743cfaf3e3b83c"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Earls Barton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Earls Barton (the village containing The Square) is the locality investigated in the flood report. [PAG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Square in Earls Barton was the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council Section 19 flood investigation, indicating a flooding incident occurred at The Square that is examined in the report. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall led to drainage being unable to collect and carry rainwater, with surface water following natural contours to low points on the public highway and around private property in the village, resulting in internal flooding of a property. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised surface water flooding following moderate rainfall led to surface water flowing to low points on the public highway in The Square and caused internal flooding of one property within Earls Barton. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Square, within Earls Barton, experienced internal flooding of a property on 16th September 2015; the incident is part of repeated internal flooding incidents in the village over the last five years. [PAG] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incident investigation of the town identified surface water flooding issues during the event: public and surface water sewers conveyed flows into the town centre (The Square/Broad Street), road gullies were observed blocked and runoff from hardstanding and private car parks was seen entering the public highway. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Moderate rainfall could not be collected or conveyed by drainage. Surface water flowed from private property and the highway on higher ground, following natural contours to low points in the village, causing flooding of low&ndash;lying areas, footways and properties; highway drainage and public sewers were relied upon and were vulnerable to blockage and exceedance. [PAG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water overtopped kerbs and flowed towards private property; highway drainage capacity was identified as insufficient in areas creating flood risk to properties. Actions recommended include regular highway drainage cleansing, asset capacity assessments and review of carriageway/kerb/footway levels to reduce overtopping and runoff to properties. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [PAG] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Earls Barton,England,"Earls Barton (the village containing The Square) is the locality investigated in the flood report. [PAG] (p1)
The Square in Earls Barton was the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council Section 19 flood investigation, indicating a flooding incident occurred at The Square that is examined in the report. (p2)
Moderate rainfall led to drainage being unable to collect and carry rainwater, with surface water following natural contours to low points on the public highway and around private property in the village, resulting in internal flooding of a property. [PAG] (p6)
Localised surface water flooding following moderate rainfall led to surface water flowing to low points on the public highway in The Square and caused internal flooding of one property within Earls Barton. [PAG] (p7)
The Square, within Earls Barton, experienced internal flooding of a property on 16th September 2015; the incident is part of repeated internal flooding incidents in the village over the last five years. [PAG] (p9)
Flood incident investigation of the town identified surface water flooding issues during the event: public and surface water sewers conveyed flows into the town centre (The Square/Broad Street), road gullies were observed blocked and runoff from hardstanding and private car parks was seen entering the public highway. (p10)
Moderate rainfall could not be collected or conveyed by drainage. Surface water flowed from private property and the highway on higher ground, following natural contours to low points in the village, causing flooding of low–lying areas, footways and properties; highway drainage and public sewers were relied upon and were vulnerable to blockage and exceedance. [PAG] (p13)
Surface water overtopped kerbs and flowed towards private property; highway drainage capacity was identified as insufficient in areas creating flood risk to properties. Actions recommended include regular highway drainage cleansing, asset capacity assessments and review of carriageway/kerb/footway levels to reduce overtopping and runoff to properties. (p20)","1 property [PAG] (p6)
1 property [PAG] (p7)
1 property [PAG] (p9)",GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16.html,GB-NNH_17_2015-09-16,Earls Barton flooding 16 Sep 2015,"Flood Investigation Report: The Square, Earls Barton, 16th September 2015",090/North Northamptonshire/Earls-Barton-FIR.pdf
GB-NNH,14,2014-05-24,52.4812301,-0.4894626,"<div id=""html_0b52bdc86dee92b9a81eb8ede64a4c20"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lyveden Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse (and its tributary immediately east of Lytham Park) was identified as contributing to surface water runoff and flooding within the local area; the report recommends confirming ownership and maintenance responsibilities for sections of this tributary to reduce future surface water collection and conveyance issues. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The tributary of Lyveden Brook (start of a culverted section) was the location of a flood incident; drainage works were carried out at the culvert inlet on the north side of Benefield Road during the investigation and the culverted section connects downstream to open water east of Lytham Park. [IMG] (p47)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Lyveden Brook,England,"The watercourse (and its tributary immediately east of Lytham Park) was identified as contributing to surface water runoff and flooding within the local area; the report recommends confirming ownership and maintenance responsibilities for sections of this tributary to reduce future surface water collection and conveyance issues. (p18)
The tributary of Lyveden Brook (start of a culverted section) was the location of a flood incident; drainage works were carried out at the culvert inlet on the north side of Benefield Road during the investigation and the culverted section connects downstream to open water east of Lytham Park. [IMG] (p47)",,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24.html,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24,Oundle flooding 24 May 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Benefield Road, Oundle, 24th May 2014",090/North Northamptonshire/Benefield-Road-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,14,2014-05-24,52.4822863,-0.4956477,"<div id=""html_f402553d2ce16c50efbf36d77d3a4a22"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Benefield Road, Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense short&ndash;duration rainfall and inadequate drainage caused excess surface water to flow over ground and follow contours to low points around private property on Benefield Road, resulting in internal flooding of at least one property; local kerb/footway levels and surface features exacerbated the flow. [PAG,MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of a residential property (water crossed the threshold). Flooding was reported near the junction of Benefield Road and Lytham Park and has occurred on more than one occasion. [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from the carriageway flowed down the carriageway channel into the low point adjacent to a culverted watercourse and then over front gardens into residential property on the Benefield Road/Lytham Park development. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flowed down the carriageway channel from east and west to a low point on the south side adjacent to a culverted watercourse; a shallow wave of surface water moved from the carriageway across the footway and toward nearby properties, entering at least one property's front doorway. The resident took measures to sweep flood water back to the door and divert flows to lower areas to the east; kerbing at the low point was laid flush with the footway which contributed to flow across the footway. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Heavy rainfall combined with the layout and levels of the carriageway, kerb and footway, and the layout/levels of the property's front approach and steps, channelled surface water toward the property's front doorway threshold. Highway drainage effectiveness and inability to discharge satisfactorily contributed to increased accumulation of surface water at the lowest point of Benefield Road. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A flood incident occurred at the low point of Benefield Road (adjacent to the flood incident), indicating highway drainage failure/insufficient capacity; this prompted recommendations for highway works including full height kerbing, re&ndash;levelled footway and review of developer&ndash;implemented highway works. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ponded at a low point on Benefield Road during the flood incident; a roadside ditch was observed in&ndash;filled with small aggregate, restricting drainage and contributing to water ponding over the carriageway. [IMG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road gullies located at the low point, together with flush kerbs and a backward&ndash;sloping footway, concentrated runoff and reduced drainage effectiveness. These features were recorded as causing surface water to pond at the low point during the 24 May 2014 event. [IMG,PAG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water ponding occurred at a low point on Benefield Road. Drainage works and excavation were carried out at the location during the investigation; sandbags were placed at the entrance to a nearby residential property to prevent water ingress. Road gullies and the flush&ndash;kerb/backward&ndash;sloping footway at the low point are recorded as features at the flood location. The start of a culverted section of a tributary of Lyveden Brook is located on the north side of Benefield Road (downstream) and was investigated in relation to the incident. [IMG,PAG] (p46)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at the north side of Benefield Road where the culvert entrance of the tributary is located; drainage works were undertaken at this spot during the flood investigation. [IMG] (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG,MUL] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Benefield Road, Oundle",England,"Intense short–duration rainfall and inadequate drainage caused excess surface water to flow over ground and follow contours to low points around private property on Benefield Road, resulting in internal flooding of at least one property; local kerb/footway levels and surface features exacerbated the flow. [PAG,MUL] (p6)
Internal flooding of a residential property (water crossed the threshold). Flooding was reported near the junction of Benefield Road and Lytham Park and has occurred on more than one occasion. [PAG,MUL] (p9)
Surface water from the carriageway flowed down the carriageway channel into the low point adjacent to a culverted watercourse and then over front gardens into residential property on the Benefield Road/Lytham Park development. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p11)
Surface water flowed down the carriageway channel from east and west to a low point on the south side adjacent to a culverted watercourse; a shallow wave of surface water moved from the carriageway across the footway and toward nearby properties, entering at least one property's front doorway. The resident took measures to sweep flood water back to the door and divert flows to lower areas to the east; kerbing at the low point was laid flush with the footway which contributed to flow across the footway. (p12)
Heavy rainfall combined with the layout and levels of the carriageway, kerb and footway, and the layout/levels of the property's front approach and steps, channelled surface water toward the property's front doorway threshold. Highway drainage effectiveness and inability to discharge satisfactorily contributed to increased accumulation of surface water at the lowest point of Benefield Road. (p17)
A flood incident occurred at the low point of Benefield Road (adjacent to the flood incident), indicating highway drainage failure/insufficient capacity; this prompted recommendations for highway works including full height kerbing, re–levelled footway and review of developer–implemented highway works. (p20)
Surface water ponded at a low point on Benefield Road during the flood incident; a roadside ditch was observed in–filled with small aggregate, restricting drainage and contributing to water ponding over the carriageway. [IMG] (p43)
Road gullies located at the low point, together with flush kerbs and a backward–sloping footway, concentrated runoff and reduced drainage effectiveness. These features were recorded as causing surface water to pond at the low point during the 24 May 2014 event. [IMG,PAG] (p45)
Surface water ponding occurred at a low point on Benefield Road. Drainage works and excavation were carried out at the location during the investigation; sandbags were placed at the entrance to a nearby residential property to prevent water ingress. Road gullies and the flush–kerb/backward–sloping footway at the low point are recorded as features at the flood location. The start of a culverted section of a tributary of Lyveden Brook is located on the north side of Benefield Road (downstream) and was investigated in relation to the incident. [IMG,PAG] (p46)
Flooding occurred at the north side of Benefield Road where the culvert entrance of the tributary is located; drainage works were undertaken at this spot during the flood investigation. [IMG] (p47)","one property [PAG,MUL] (p6)
one property [PAG,MUL] (p9)",GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24.html,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24,Oundle flooding 24 May 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Benefield Road, Oundle, 24th May 2014",090/North Northamptonshire/Benefield-Road-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,14,2014-05-24,52.4825287,-0.4840986,"<div id=""html_51ea90f785d78cda5c9a9df4108a28af"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lytham Park, Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding affected a residential property near the junction of Benefield Road and Lytham Park; the area was the focus of a formal flood investigation following the incident and residents reported previous flood occurrences. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A property adjacent to the Lytham Park development was flooded by surface water runoff from the highway/drainage catchment; localised drainage works were installed by the developer (including a new ACO channel at the back of the footway) to collect highway runoff and reduce the risk of recurrence. [PAG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential property within the Benefield Road / Lytham Park development was flooded when surface water from the Benefield Road carriageway flowed over front gardens into properties. [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A shallow wave of surface water flowed from Benefield Road across the footway, across the front garden and steps to a property on Benefield Road/Lytham Park and into the property's front doorway and internal hallway; the resident swept and diverted water to reduce internal impact, aided by wooden laminate flooring in the hallway which eased clean&ndash;up. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas immediately east of Lytham Park (the location of the Lyveden Brook tributary) were implicated in surface water/runoff problems during the event; the report recommends confirming ownership and maintenance of the watercourse there to address flooding and surface water collection. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised drainage works were being carried out on the Lytham Park housing development at the time of the investigation because surface water runoff/overland flow from or around the development had contributed to flooding; ENC was asked to review whether watercourse works and other measures were satisfactorily designed and implemented during construction. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Ditch immediately west of Lytham Park was observed in&ndash;filled with small aggregate and overgrown, restricting conveyance. The obstructed ditch and outlet arrangement contributed to localised surface water pooling at the low point during the 24 May 2014 event. [IMG] (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The route of a culverted watercourse installed as part of the Lytham Park development (south of Benefield Road, east of Lytham Park) was involved in the flood incident; drainage works were recorded along the culverted route and where the culvert discharges to open water east of Lytham Park. [IMG,PAG] (p47)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Lytham Park, Oundle",England,"Internal flooding affected a residential property near the junction of Benefield Road and Lytham Park; the area was the focus of a formal flood investigation following the incident and residents reported previous flood occurrences. [MUL] (p9)
A property adjacent to the Lytham Park development was flooded by surface water runoff from the highway/drainage catchment; localised drainage works were installed by the developer (including a new ACO channel at the back of the footway) to collect highway runoff and reduce the risk of recurrence. [PAG] (p10)
Residential property within the Benefield Road / Lytham Park development was flooded when surface water from the Benefield Road carriageway flowed over front gardens into properties. [TAB,MUL] (p11)
A shallow wave of surface water flowed from Benefield Road across the footway, across the front garden and steps to a property on Benefield Road/Lytham Park and into the property's front doorway and internal hallway; the resident swept and diverted water to reduce internal impact, aided by wooden laminate flooring in the hallway which eased clean–up. (p12)
Areas immediately east of Lytham Park (the location of the Lyveden Brook tributary) were implicated in surface water/runoff problems during the event; the report recommends confirming ownership and maintenance of the watercourse there to address flooding and surface water collection. (p18)
Localised drainage works were being carried out on the Lytham Park housing development at the time of the investigation because surface water runoff/overland flow from or around the development had contributed to flooding; ENC was asked to review whether watercourse works and other measures were satisfactorily designed and implemented during construction. (p21)
Ditch immediately west of Lytham Park was observed in–filled with small aggregate and overgrown, restricting conveyance. The obstructed ditch and outlet arrangement contributed to localised surface water pooling at the low point during the 24 May 2014 event. [IMG] (p45)
The route of a culverted watercourse installed as part of the Lytham Park development (south of Benefield Road, east of Lytham Park) was involved in the flood incident; drainage works were recorded along the culverted route and where the culvert discharges to open water east of Lytham Park. [IMG,PAG] (p47)",one property [MUL] (p9),GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24.html,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24,Oundle flooding 24 May 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Benefield Road, Oundle, 24th May 2014",090/North Northamptonshire/Benefield-Road-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,14,2014-05-24,52.4826971,-0.4852475,"<div id=""html_c2bfd8dc746cb75b969ff16faf1d480b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Wakerley Close, Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overgrown ditch systems west of Lytham Park at Wakerley Close were heavily vegetated and obstructed, limiting flow and maintenance access. These obstructions were identified as contributing to localised surface water flooding during the 24 May 2014 event. [IMG] (p45)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,"Wakerley Close, Oundle",England,"Overgrown ditch systems west of Lytham Park at Wakerley Close were heavily vegetated and obstructed, limiting flow and maintenance access. These obstructions were identified as contributing to localised surface water flooding during the 24 May 2014 event. [IMG] (p45)",,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24.html,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24,Oundle flooding 24 May 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Benefield Road, Oundle, 24th May 2014",090/North Northamptonshire/Benefield-Road-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,14,2014-05-24,52.3106138,-0.8659129831915817,"<div id=""html_cb3d9acaaabd6adee9ccb5e96b31f799"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Northamptonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Within Northamptonshire (LLFA area) an event of intense short&ndash;duration rainfall produced surface water runoff that caused flooding at Benefield Road, Oundle, including internal flooding of a property; the event prompted a formal Section 19 investigation by the Northamptonshire LLFA. (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected Benefield Road, Oundle. Heavy rainfall combined with the layout and levels of the carriageway, kerb and footway adjacent to the property, and the layout/levels of the property's front approach and steps, channelled surface water towards the front doorway threshold causing the property to flood. (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Northamptonshire,England,"Within Northamptonshire (LLFA area) an event of intense short–duration rainfall produced surface water runoff that caused flooding at Benefield Road, Oundle, including internal flooding of a property; the event prompted a formal Section 19 investigation by the Northamptonshire LLFA. (p6)
Surface water flooding affected Benefield Road, Oundle. Heavy rainfall combined with the layout and levels of the carriageway, kerb and footway adjacent to the property, and the layout/levels of the property's front approach and steps, channelled surface water towards the front doorway threshold causing the property to flood. (p16)",one property (p6),GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24.html,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24,Oundle flooding 24 May 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Benefield Road, Oundle, 24th May 2014",090/North Northamptonshire/Benefield-Road-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-NNH,14,2014-05-24,52.480678744170966,-0.46597423959156464,"<div id=""html_c32a983d737adcbcad43d813045aec98"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Oundle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Benefield Road in Oundle was the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council Section 19 flood investigation, indicating flooding affected the location. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in Oundle included an incident on Benefield Road where one property experienced internal flooding; residents were spoken to about the 24th May 2014 event and previous incidents. [MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Lyveden Brook experienced excessive flows that overtopped banks and affected adjoining properties in October 2013 (river flooding). Separately, surface water flowing along Benefield Road inundated front gardens and residential properties in the Oundle area on 05/08/2013, 22/05/2014 and 24/05/2014. [MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported routes of surface water were recorded during the event; surface water flowed along those reported routes causing flooding in Oundle during the incident. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Oundle (specifically Benefield Road) experienced surface water flooding on 24th May 2014 due to heavy rainfall and local carriageway/kerb/footway levels that channelled water towards property thresholds; highway drainage discharge issues increased surface water collection at the lowest point. (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water collected on highway and low&ndash;lying parts of the town linked to ineffective highway drainage and runoff, prompting a community flood planning response and recommendations for property&ndash;level and community&ndash;wide measures. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water run&ndash;off and inadequacies in historic drainage/watercourse capacity caused flooding in the town, prompting town&ndash;wide measures: re&ndash;excavation of ditches, repair/replacement of piped/culvert sections, dredging/trimming of watercourses, replacement of structures and vegetation clearance; community&ndash;level response actions (flood warden and village flood plan) were recommended. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property [MUL] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Northamptonshire,Oundle,England,"Benefield Road in Oundle was the subject of a Northamptonshire County Council Section 19 flood investigation, indicating flooding affected the location. (p2)
Flooding in Oundle included an incident on Benefield Road where one property experienced internal flooding; residents were spoken to about the 24th May 2014 event and previous incidents. [MUL] (p9)
Lyveden Brook experienced excessive flows that overtopped banks and affected adjoining properties in October 2013 (river flooding). Separately, surface water flowing along Benefield Road inundated front gardens and residential properties in the Oundle area on 05/08/2013, 22/05/2014 and 24/05/2014. [MUL] (p11)
Reported routes of surface water were recorded during the event; surface water flowed along those reported routes causing flooding in Oundle during the incident. (p13)
Parts of Oundle (specifically Benefield Road) experienced surface water flooding on 24th May 2014 due to heavy rainfall and local carriageway/kerb/footway levels that channelled water towards property thresholds; highway drainage discharge issues increased surface water collection at the lowest point. (p17)
Surface water collected on highway and low–lying parts of the town linked to ineffective highway drainage and runoff, prompting a community flood planning response and recommendations for property–level and community–wide measures. (p18)
Surface water run–off and inadequacies in historic drainage/watercourse capacity caused flooding in the town, prompting town–wide measures: re–excavation of ditches, repair/replacement of piped/culvert sections, dredging/trimming of watercourses, replacement of structures and vegetation clearance; community–level response actions (flood warden and village flood plan) were recommended. (p20)",one property [MUL] (p9),GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24.html,GB-NNH_14_2014-05-24,Oundle flooding 24 May 2014,"Flood Investigation Report: Benefield Road, Oundle, 24th May 2014",090/North Northamptonshire/Benefield-Road-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,15,2020-12-23,52.2930668,-0.2204357,"<div id=""html_ac05c6d0ebe4726dacde65e7704d7bb2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Buckden Marina</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused loss of access and egress to flood&ndash;resilient properties at Buckden Marina. [TAB] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Buckden Marina,England,Flooding caused loss of access and egress to flood–resilient properties at Buckden Marina. [TAB] (p9),,GB-CAM_15_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_15_2020-12-23,Buckden flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Buckden,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Buckden-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,15,2020-12-23,52.29113182607577,-0.24652795065864616,"<div id=""html_b07371666ac5d6481e9587f636698b81"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Recreation Ground</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected the amenity land (Recreation Ground). [TAB] (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Recreation Ground,England,Flooding affected the amenity land (Recreation Ground). [TAB] (p9),,GB-CAM_15_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_15_2020-12-23,Buckden flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Buckden,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Buckden-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,15,2020-12-23,52.2965399,-0.2547368,"<div id=""html_7fa69c093abc4c60b12ecedec960e71d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>A1 at Buckden</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding inundated the A1 corridor: the northern underpass beneath the A1 was flooded; the road between Buckden Marina and the level crossing flooded, cutting off an exit from the village; the A1 was flooded and impassable for some hours. (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,A1 at Buckden,England,"Surface water flooding inundated the A1 corridor: the northern underpass beneath the A1 was flooded; the road between Buckden Marina and the level crossing flooded, cutting off an exit from the village; the A1 was flooded and impassable for some hours. (p11)",,GB-CAM_15_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_15_2020-12-23,Buckden flooding 23 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: Buckden,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/Buckden-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
