Hatfield experienced very intense, localised rainfall at about 18:15 on 23 June 2016 (estimated on the radar map to be within the high–intensity band). This extreme short–duration rainfall coincided with the incident and is associated with localised surface–water/flash flooding in parts of Hatfield. [IMG] (p7)","1 property (p1)
1 property (p4)",GB-HRT_2_2016-06-23.html,GB-HRT_2_2016-06-23,Hatfield flooding 23 Jun 2016,"Section 19 Flood Investigation: Deerswood Avenue, Hatfield",056/Hertfordshire/deerswood-avenue-hatfield-flood-investigation-report.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.09126695,-1.4108604769704165,"<div id=""html_3c5333f934e7ea55235e4613d6c387b0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Roecliffe Caravan Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warnings were issued for the River Ure at Roecliffe caravan park on 15 Feb 2020, again between 21&ndash;24 Feb, and were reissued on the weekend of 28&ndash;29 Feb 2020. [MUL] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Roecliffe Caravan Park,England,"Flood warnings were issued for the River Ure at Roecliffe caravan park on 15 Feb 2020, again between 21–24 Feb, and were reissued on the weekend of 28–29 Feb 2020. [MUL] (p30)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.222475,-1.6568504,"<div id=""html_d98deb76acb84982ae81b59cd4c9b6cf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Beavers</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The low point near Beavers' butchers (on Silver Street) collected the floodwater route; the fire brigade pumped water from this low point through to Shooting Holme. (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Beavers,England,The low point near Beavers' butchers (on Silver Street) collected the floodwater route; the fire brigade pumped water from this low point through to Shooting Holme. (p40),,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.2221885,-1.6595269,"<div id=""html_ad4c3166ab41b14d1aa7816180aa343a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brewery</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Swinney Beck breached opposite Theakston's brewery and floodwaters flowed from behind the brewery down Red Lane and into the town; NYCC highways attended and committed to clearing highway drains where the flooding started behind the brewery. (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>silt 170mm deep in The Oaks box culvert (culvert depth 1200mm) (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Brewery,England,Swinney Beck breached opposite Theakston's brewery and floodwaters flowed from behind the brewery down Red Lane and into the town; NYCC highways attended and committed to clearing highway drains where the flooding started behind the brewery. (p40),silt 170mm deep in The Oaks box culvert (culvert depth 1200mm) (p40),GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.7023545,-3.2765753,"<div id=""html_624dab1f4f8b8ff98aad0c675313be18"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>UK</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>February 2020 produced record&ndash;high monthly rainfall in multiple catchments (Pennine&ndash;fed Swale, Ure, Nidd, Wharfe, Aire, Calder and Don, and lower Ouse, Rye, Derwent and Hull further east) which led to widespread river and surface water flooding across those catchments and is the subject of this investigation. (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,UK,England,"February 2020 produced record–high monthly rainfall in multiple catchments (Pennine–fed Swale, Ure, Nidd, Wharfe, Aire, Calder and Don, and lower Ouse, Rye, Derwent and Hull further east) which led to widespread river and surface water flooding across those catchments and is the subject of this investigation. (p23)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.0664403,-2.2915997,"<div id=""html_ba8a4246fc6a4f27ae1f045caa237c3b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Tems Beck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Tems Beck was one of the watercourses identified as contributing to fluvial flood risk at Gildersleets during the February 2020 event; it flows in a south&ndash;easterly direction to its confluence with the River Ribble approximately 130m east of the properties and was therefore a source of flood hazard to the settlement. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Map annotations show flood flows originating to the west of the railway associated with the local watercourses (including Tems Beck) and contributing to flow paths that converge near Far End Barn, indicating Tems Beck was part of the flood flow causing local inundation risk. [IMG] (p39)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Tems Beck,England,"Tems Beck was one of the watercourses identified as contributing to fluvial flood risk at Gildersleets during the February 2020 event; it flows in a south–easterly direction to its confluence with the River Ribble approximately 130m east of the properties and was therefore a source of flood hazard to the settlement. (p12)
Map annotations show flood flows originating to the west of the railway associated with the local watercourses (including Tems Beck) and contributing to flow paths that converge near Far End Barn, indicating Tems Beck was part of the flood flow causing local inundation risk. [IMG] (p39)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.0959797,-2.1650086,"<div id=""html_a15173da9b28d7e4d3b298ca197ecbc4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Malham Tarn</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Malham Tarn on the upper Aire catchment recorded substantial rainfall during the February events, contributing to persistent high flows and elevated flood risk in the Aire catchment. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Malham Tarn,England,"Malham Tarn on the upper Aire catchment recorded substantial rainfall during the February events, contributing to persistent high flows and elevated flood risk in the Aire catchment. (p26)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.2958002,-1.5625698,"<div id=""html_f1cab86efb247c6834126fdbdf4ef4fa"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bedale Beck</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pronounced peak in river levels at Leeming associated with February storm activity (sharp rise during Storm Ciara period). [IMG] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Bedale Beck,England,Pronounced peak in river levels at Leeming associated with February storm activity (sharp rise during Storm Ciara period). [IMG] (p28),,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7381373,-1.0000834,"<div id=""html_aeea208ba52c93e930a51fc6b16fdf5d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Drax Power Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding damaged a railway embankment at Hirst Courtney/West Bank that carried wood pellet fuel; the railway was operated as single&ndash;track during repairs. This damage had the potential to cause significant disruption to fuel deliveries and operations at Drax Power Station. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Operations at the power station were put at risk by flood impacts to the rail supply route (damage to the railway embankment), with potential for significant disruption to fuel deliveries; aerial imagery also shows the power station in proximity to floodwaters and at risk from inundation. (p34)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Drax Power Station,England,"Flooding damaged a railway embankment at Hirst Courtney/West Bank that carried wood pellet fuel; the railway was operated as single–track during repairs. This damage had the potential to cause significant disruption to fuel deliveries and operations at Drax Power Station. [PAG] (p33)
Operations at the power station were put at risk by flood impacts to the rail supply route (damage to the railway embankment), with potential for significant disruption to fuel deliveries; aerial imagery also shows the power station in proximity to floodwaters and at risk from inundation. (p34)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7354024,-1.3564686,"<div id=""html_7443005bebddbc837ee9963efe132bac"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Allerton Ings</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The monitoring series for Allerton Ings rose during the storm events in February and remained elevated through the month; relative to other sites it remained lower (series remained below ~2 m) but still showed clear storm&ndash;driven increases. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warnings for the River Aire at Allerton Ings were issued multiple times in February 2020 (on 8 February, again on 16 February and between 21&ndash;24 February), indicating the site was subject to river flooding risk during 01 Feb 2020 &ndash; 29 Feb 2020. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Allerton Ings,England,"The monitoring series for Allerton Ings rose during the storm events in February and remained elevated through the month; relative to other sites it remained lower (series remained below ~2 m) but still showed clear storm–driven increases. [IMG] (p29)
Flood warnings for the River Aire at Allerton Ings were issued multiple times in February 2020 (on 8 February, again on 16 February and between 21–24 February), indicating the site was subject to river flooding risk during 01 Feb 2020 – 29 Feb 2020. (p30)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7974185,-1.5437941,"<div id=""html_bf69d99dae34af0af9b73991f4823e6d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Leeds Metropolitan District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Monitoring at Allerton Ings (Leeds) showed elevated water depths through February associated with Storm Ciara and subsequent storms; levels did not return to pre&ndash;storm values by the end of the month, indicating prolonged high water in the washlands. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Elevated river levels passed through Leeds on multiple occasions in February 2020; the Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme (weirs) were actively operated to manage levels (weirs were lowered once on 24 Feb 2020 at 16:00). The scheme reduces the floodplain through the centre of Leeds, affecting upstream levels while increasing flows downstream toward Lemonroyd weir. [PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood peaks passed through the city on the dates listed, indicating river flooding affected Leeds during the February 2020 events. [PAG,MUL] (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood peaks passed through Leeds on 9, 16, 22, 23 and 24 February 2020. The Leeds flood alleviation scheme weirs were operated during the period and were lowered on 24 February 2020 at 16:00. [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Leeds Metropolitan District,England,"Monitoring at Allerton Ings (Leeds) showed elevated water depths through February associated with Storm Ciara and subsequent storms; levels did not return to pre–storm values by the end of the month, indicating prolonged high water in the washlands. [IMG] (p29)
Elevated river levels passed through Leeds on multiple occasions in February 2020; the Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme (weirs) were actively operated to manage levels (weirs were lowered once on 24 Feb 2020 at 16:00). The scheme reduces the floodplain through the centre of Leeds, affecting upstream levels while increasing flows downstream toward Lemonroyd weir. [PAG] (p43)
Flood peaks passed through the city on the dates listed, indicating river flooding affected Leeds during the February 2020 events. [PAG,MUL] (p53)
Flood peaks passed through Leeds on 9, 16, 22, 23 and 24 February 2020. The Leeds flood alleviation scheme weirs were operated during the period and were lowered on 24 February 2020 at 16:00. [IMG,PAG] (p54)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.704139,-2.482178,"<div id=""html_672337d8641988fd73ceb7bb9a120aff"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pennines</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Pennine&ndash;fed catchments received exceptionally high rainfall in February 2020 (320&ndash;375% of monthly LTA), producing very high river levels that contributed to widespread river and surface&ndash;water flooding across the catchments and downstream communities. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged, persistent and exceptionally heavy rainfall across February 2020 affecting Pennine&ndash;fed catchments. The Pennine fed catchments received very large accumulations of rainfall over the month, and experienced bands of particularly intense rainfall associated with named storms (Ciara 8&ndash;9 Feb, Dennis 15&ndash;16 Feb, Jorge 28 Feb&ndash;1 Mar). (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storm Ciara produced intense flows that resulted in significant flooding over the western Pennines and localised flooding across the county. (p49)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 25 properties internally flooded (Craven District); 44 properties internally flooded (reported to Harrogate Borough Council); 27 properties internally flooded (Selby District) (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Pennines,England,"Pennine–fed catchments received exceptionally high rainfall in February 2020 (320–375% of monthly LTA), producing very high river levels that contributed to widespread river and surface–water flooding across the catchments and downstream communities. (p8)
Prolonged, persistent and exceptionally heavy rainfall across February 2020 affecting Pennine–fed catchments. The Pennine fed catchments received very large accumulations of rainfall over the month, and experienced bands of particularly intense rainfall associated with named storms (Ciara 8–9 Feb, Dennis 15–16 Feb, Jorge 28 Feb–1 Mar). (p24)
Storm Ciara produced intense flows that resulted in significant flooding over the western Pennines and localised flooding across the county. (p49)",up to 25 properties internally flooded (Craven District); 44 properties internally flooded (reported to Harrogate Borough Council); 27 properties internally flooded (Selby District) (p8),GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.1525499,-2.1305249,"<div id=""html_04f70f444c8c176acc1883d0749215c8"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Littondale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Littondale (on the Wharfe) recorded very high rainfall totals during the February events, contributing to high river flows and elevated flood risk across the Wharfe catchment. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Littondale,England,"Littondale (on the Wharfe) recorded very high rainfall totals during the February events, contributing to high river flows and elevated flood risk across the Wharfe catchment. (p26)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.1643472,-2.3079822,"<div id=""html_71bca33d02eddae553aad3bda9a5fa88"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ribblesdale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood incidents were reported in the Ribblesdale area, with the Environment Agency reporting incidents predominantly on the River Wharfe and River Ure; Horton in Ribblesdale was reported as surrounded by floodwater. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Ribblesdale,England,"Flood incidents were reported in the Ribblesdale area, with the Environment Agency reporting incidents predominantly on the River Wharfe and River Ure; Horton in Ribblesdale was reported as surrounded by floodwater. (p30)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7095433,-1.0693049,"<div id=""html_9f81fad358a95c4dc83a13dd78ad31b4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hirst Marsh</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warnings were issued for the River Aire at Hirst Marsh on 10 Feb 2020; alerts for the Aire at Hirst Marsh remained in place during the 16&ndash;20 Feb period. [MUL] (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Hirst Marsh,England,Flood warnings were issued for the River Aire at Hirst Marsh on 10 Feb 2020; alerts for the Aire at Hirst Marsh remained in place during the 16–20 Feb period. [MUL] (p30),,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.8034294,-1.5829169,"<div id=""html_d68d22f92d7d124b38e4bda598e38b69"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Aire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from the main River Aire caused inundation of the lower Aire and its washlands; the event was widespread across multiple authority areas and has prompted an Environment Agency review of flows and washland capacity. [PAG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire and its associated washlands were involved in the February 2020 flood events; this has prompted the Environment Agency to undertake a review and further assessment of flows and washland capacity (work noted as already in progress since the February 2020 events). (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Consecutive storms and saturated floodplain caused washlands around Hirst Courtney to reach capacity; River Aire overtopped into the communities of West Bank and Hirst Courtney, and high river levels prevented Black Drain from draining to the river, causing flooding along the Hirst Road corridor between the A19 and Carlton. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Lower Aire area was identified as an exception to otherwise isolated incidents and described as a worst&ndash;affected area: flooding concentrated along the Hirst Road corridor from the A19 at Chapel Haddlesey to Carlton Bridge in Selby District. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding associated with the River Aire affected low&ndash;lying land to the south of Chapel Haddlesey and contributed to flood risk/observed flow paths for Chapel Haddlesey and nearby settlements (Hirst Courtney, West Bank, Carlton Chapel Haddlesey). The presence of the weir/old lock influences upstream headwater levels and the flood behaviour at the village. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Aire catchment experienced extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced exceptionally high monthly rainfall in February 2020 and intense short&ndash;term rainfall during Storm Ciara and other periods; locations in the Aire catchment recorded 106% to 128% of the February LTA in 48 hours. Persistent rainfall occurred throughout the month and during named storms. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Aire catchment (Thornton Moor and Upper Aire locations) experienced persistent rainfall and elevated river flows during February, with sustained runoff particularly around 21 February that increased catchment saturation and contributed to flow peaks. [IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple monitoring sites on the Aire recorded significant and, at some sites, record&ndash;high peaks during February storm and rainfall events (levels rose during Storm Ciara, rose further during Storm Dennis, and reached monthly/record peaks on 25 Feb). [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During February the River Aire recorded significant and sustained high flows and water levels driven by successive storms (Ciara, Dennis, Jorge) and saturated catchments. Monitoring sites along the Aire recorded major peaks (including monthly peaks on 25 February) and prolonged elevated levels through the month. [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire and its catchments experienced widespread flood alerts and flood warnings throughout February 2020 (flood alert for the upper and middle Aire on 8 February; flood warnings for the Aire at Crosshills, Hirst Marsh and West Haddesley on 10 February; warnings for Allerton Ings, Hirst Courtney, Gowdall, Kellington, Hensall, Temple Hirst and repeated alerts for lower/middle/upper Aire catchments across mid&ndash; to late&ndash;February), indicating significant river flooding risk across the Aire catchment during 01 Feb 2020 &ndash; 29 Feb 2020. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire flooded its floodplain, inundating agricultural land and encroaching on infrastructure; flooding contributed to failure/damage of floodbank at Temple Hirst (requiring emergency repairs) and impacted transport routes and assets in the vicinity. [IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The middle and Lower Aire experienced sustained high water levels and successive peaks during the late&ndash;February 2020 rainfall period; washlands did not empty between peaks and tidal/low&ndash;tide elevations within the channel caused rising baseline levels and a noted 'surge' in water levels. (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodplain filling of the River Aire over several days raised river levels and contributed to the high levels and flooding observed at Snaith (culminating in high levels on 25 Feb 2020). Flood peaks also passed through Leeds on several February dates. [PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Operation of the Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme (weirs) and the high flows during the February 2020 event increased downstream flows and water levels on the River Aire. The most notable modeled increase was at Thwaite's Mill; downstream water level increases reduced with distance and washlands overtopped at Chapel Haddlesey producing flat&ndash;topped gauge peaks. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hydrographs across multiple monitoring sites on the River Aire show episodic surges and multiple flood pulses between 9&ndash;27 Feb 2020. Those river stage peaks correspond with recorded flooding incidents across communities in western and southern parts of North Yorkshire during February 2020. [IMG] (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Consecutive storms and prolonged rain caused the River Aire to be very high; washlands around Hirst Courtney reached capacity and there was overtopping of the river into the communities of West Bank and Hirst Courtney on 25th February. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>High flows on the River Aire during February 2020 caused inundation of its washlands and contributed to raised water levels downstream, prompting further assessment of flows and washland capacity. (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood peaks on the River Aire passed through Leeds during the February 2020 heavy rainfall, producing distinct flood peaks in the city. (p53)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire had flood peaks passing through Leeds and its floodplains filled over several days, contributing to the high levels observed at Snaith; the Leeds FAS weirs on the Aire were operated during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire propagated elevated flows downstream during the event; water&ndash;level changes at Leeds propagated to downstream gauges (approx. 5 hours to Castleford) and the main flood peak was recorded further downstream at Snaith. [IMG] (p55)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>27 properties internally flooded (Selby District), mostly along the Hirst Road corridor between the A19 and Carlton (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>320% to 375% of the monthly long term average (LTA) rainfall; 106% to 128% of the February LTA recorded in 48 hours [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Chapel Haddesely 3.43 m on 25/02/2020; Beal Weir 4.10 m on 25/02/2020; Birkin Westholme Washalnds 5.40 m on 25/02/2020; Roal Dyke 5.95 m (monthly peak); West Bank pond 3.38 m (highest on record); Wheelan Dyke 4.59 m (highest on record) [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Chapel Haddesely peak 3.43 m (25/02/2020); Beal Weir (Beal Weir bridge) peak 4.10 m (25/02/2020); Birkin West Holme Washlands peak 5.40 m (25/02/2020); Roall Dyke 5.95 m; West Bank Pond 3.38 m (highest on record); Wheelan Dyke 4.59 m (highest on record). [IMG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak stage at Carlton Bridge recorded 6.052 m AOD (early Feb) and 6.228 m AOD (25 Feb); Chapel Haddlesey peaks 3.436 m and 3.437 m [TAB,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>over 26 separate communities; Harrogate Borough Council recorded 44 incidents; Selby recorded 27 properties; Craven recorded 25 properties [IMG] (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,River Aire,England,"Flooding from the main River Aire caused inundation of the lower Aire and its washlands; the event was widespread across multiple authority areas and has prompted an Environment Agency review of flows and washland capacity. [PAG] (p2)
The River Aire and its associated washlands were involved in the February 2020 flood events; this has prompted the Environment Agency to undertake a review and further assessment of flows and washland capacity (work noted as already in progress since the February 2020 events). (p3)
Consecutive storms and saturated floodplain caused washlands around Hirst Courtney to reach capacity; River Aire overtopped into the communities of West Bank and Hirst Courtney, and high river levels prevented Black Drain from draining to the river, causing flooding along the Hirst Road corridor between the A19 and Carlton. (p8)
The Lower Aire area was identified as an exception to otherwise isolated incidents and described as a worst–affected area: flooding concentrated along the Hirst Road corridor from the A19 at Chapel Haddlesey to Carlton Bridge in Selby District. (p11)
Fluvial flooding associated with the River Aire affected low–lying land to the south of Chapel Haddlesey and contributed to flood risk/observed flow paths for Chapel Haddlesey and nearby settlements (Hirst Courtney, West Bank, Carlton Chapel Haddlesey). The presence of the weir/old lock influences upstream headwater levels and the flood behaviour at the village. (p18)
The Aire catchment experienced extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)
Experienced exceptionally high monthly rainfall in February 2020 and intense short–term rainfall during Storm Ciara and other periods; locations in the Aire catchment recorded 106% to 128% of the February LTA in 48 hours. Persistent rainfall occurred throughout the month and during named storms. [PAG] (p24)
The Aire catchment (Thornton Moor and Upper Aire locations) experienced persistent rainfall and elevated river flows during February, with sustained runoff particularly around 21 February that increased catchment saturation and contributed to flow peaks. [IMG] (p26)
Multiple monitoring sites on the Aire recorded significant and, at some sites, record–high peaks during February storm and rainfall events (levels rose during Storm Ciara, rose further during Storm Dennis, and reached monthly/record peaks on 25 Feb). [PAG] (p28)
During February the River Aire recorded significant and sustained high flows and water levels driven by successive storms (Ciara, Dennis, Jorge) and saturated catchments. Monitoring sites along the Aire recorded major peaks (including monthly peaks on 25 February) and prolonged elevated levels through the month. [IMG] (p29)
The River Aire and its catchments experienced widespread flood alerts and flood warnings throughout February 2020 (flood alert for the upper and middle Aire on 8 February; flood warnings for the Aire at Crosshills, Hirst Marsh and West Haddesley on 10 February; warnings for Allerton Ings, Hirst Courtney, Gowdall, Kellington, Hensall, Temple Hirst and repeated alerts for lower/middle/upper Aire catchments across mid– to late–February), indicating significant river flooding risk across the Aire catchment during 01 Feb 2020 – 29 Feb 2020. (p30)
The River Aire flooded its floodplain, inundating agricultural land and encroaching on infrastructure; flooding contributed to failure/damage of floodbank at Temple Hirst (requiring emergency repairs) and impacted transport routes and assets in the vicinity. [IMG] (p34)
The middle and Lower Aire experienced sustained high water levels and successive peaks during the late–February 2020 rainfall period; washlands did not empty between peaks and tidal/low–tide elevations within the channel caused rising baseline levels and a noted 'surge' in water levels. (p42)
Floodplain filling of the River Aire over several days raised river levels and contributed to the high levels and flooding observed at Snaith (culminating in high levels on 25 Feb 2020). Flood peaks also passed through Leeds on several February dates. [PAG] (p43)
Operation of the Leeds Flood Alleviation Scheme (weirs) and the high flows during the February 2020 event increased downstream flows and water levels on the River Aire. The most notable modeled increase was at Thwaite's Mill; downstream water level increases reduced with distance and washlands overtopped at Chapel Haddlesey producing flat–topped gauge peaks. (p44)
Hydrographs across multiple monitoring sites on the River Aire show episodic surges and multiple flood pulses between 9–27 Feb 2020. Those river stage peaks correspond with recorded flooding incidents across communities in western and southern parts of North Yorkshire during February 2020. [IMG] (p48)
Consecutive storms and prolonged rain caused the River Aire to be very high; washlands around Hirst Courtney reached capacity and there was overtopping of the river into the communities of West Bank and Hirst Courtney on 25th February. (p49)
High flows on the River Aire during February 2020 caused inundation of its washlands and contributed to raised water levels downstream, prompting further assessment of flows and washland capacity. (p50)
Flood peaks on the River Aire passed through Leeds during the February 2020 heavy rainfall, producing distinct flood peaks in the city. (p53)
The River Aire had flood peaks passing through Leeds and its floodplains filled over several days, contributing to the high levels observed at Snaith; the Leeds FAS weirs on the Aire were operated during the event. [IMG,PAG] (p54)
The River Aire propagated elevated flows downstream during the event; water–level changes at Leeds propagated to downstream gauges (approx. 5 hours to Castleford) and the main flood peak was recorded further downstream at Snaith. [IMG] (p55)","27 properties internally flooded (Selby District), mostly along the Hirst Road corridor between the A19 and Carlton (p8)
320% to 375% of the monthly long term average (LTA) rainfall; 106% to 128% of the February LTA recorded in 48 hours [PAG] (p24)
Chapel Haddesely 3.43 m on 25/02/2020; Beal Weir 4.10 m on 25/02/2020; Birkin Westholme Washalnds 5.40 m on 25/02/2020; Roal Dyke 5.95 m (monthly peak); West Bank pond 3.38 m (highest on record); Wheelan Dyke 4.59 m (highest on record) [PAG] (p28)
Chapel Haddesely peak 3.43 m (25/02/2020); Beal Weir (Beal Weir bridge) peak 4.10 m (25/02/2020); Birkin West Holme Washlands peak 5.40 m (25/02/2020); Roall Dyke 5.95 m; West Bank Pond 3.38 m (highest on record); Wheelan Dyke 4.59 m (highest on record). [IMG] (p29)
Peak stage at Carlton Bridge recorded 6.052 m AOD (early Feb) and 6.228 m AOD (25 Feb); Chapel Haddlesey peaks 3.436 m and 3.437 m [TAB,PAG] (p42)
over 26 separate communities; Harrogate Borough Council recorded 44 incidents; Selby recorded 27 properties; Craven recorded 25 properties [IMG] (p48)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.8674307,-2.4188743,"<div id=""html_231a8515a624c73a174d855a65bd60ec"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Ribble</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ribble recorded a highest&ndash;ever level of 2.87 m at Pennybridge on 9 February 2020; this peak (with other river peaks) resulted in flooding of properties in the Settle, Gargrave and Skipton areas and contributed to flooding in Pateley Bridge, Masham and Boroughbridge. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Ribble recorded extreme high flows during February 2020 with a highest&ndash;ever recorded level at the Pennybridge gauge and additional monthly peaks at other gauging locations; these peaks corresponded with named storms and a major rainfall event. [IMG] (p27)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,River Ribble,England,"The River Ribble recorded a highest–ever level of 2.87 m at Pennybridge on 9 February 2020; this peak (with other river peaks) resulted in flooding of properties in the Settle, Gargrave and Skipton areas and contributed to flooding in Pateley Bridge, Masham and Boroughbridge. (p8)
River Ribble recorded extreme high flows during February 2020 with a highest–ever recorded level at the Pennybridge gauge and additional monthly peaks at other gauging locations; these peaks corresponded with named storms and a major rainfall event. [IMG] (p27)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.0322022,-1.5638727,"<div id=""html_44f81c1fb40b7dd33e660c73a36c56f1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Nidd</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Nidd's catchment received very high rainfall in February 2020, contributing to elevated flows within the Pennine catchments that fed the flood event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Nidd catchment experienced extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Upper Nidd catchment recorded intense short&ndash;duration rainfall within the month (notably during Storm Ciara), and the Nidd catchment as a whole received exceptionally high monthly totals in February 2020. Persistent heavy rainfall occurred throughout the month and during named storms. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Nidd experienced elevated runoff and increased river levels during the February storms (notably around the 8&ndash;9 February and subsequent events), reflecting strong catchment response and contributing to county&ndash;wide river flow peaks through the month. [IMG] (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Nidd recorded extreme peaks in February 2020: at Pateley Bridge the Nidd reached a highest&ndash;ever peak of 3.93 m on 9 February (Storm Ciara), and at Knaresborough it peaked at 2.1 m on the same day. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A severe flood warning was issued for the Nidd at Pateley Bridge on 9 February 2020; flood warnings were issued for the Nidd at Knaresborough and for Knaresborough caravan park on multiple dates in February, and alerts remained in place for the upper and lower Nidd. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Upper catchment experienced very high river flows during Storms Ciara and Denis in February 2020, contributing to localised river flooding in the Nidd catchment. (p49)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>320% to 375% of the monthly long term average (LTA) rainfall; 106% to 128% of the February LTA recorded in 48 hours (upper Nidd) [PAG] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,River Nidd,England,"The River Nidd's catchment received very high rainfall in February 2020, contributing to elevated flows within the Pennine catchments that fed the flood event. (p8)
The Nidd catchment experienced extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)
Upper Nidd catchment recorded intense short–duration rainfall within the month (notably during Storm Ciara), and the Nidd catchment as a whole received exceptionally high monthly totals in February 2020. Persistent heavy rainfall occurred throughout the month and during named storms. [PAG] (p24)
River Nidd experienced elevated runoff and increased river levels during the February storms (notably around the 8–9 February and subsequent events), reflecting strong catchment response and contributing to county–wide river flow peaks through the month. [IMG] (p26)
River Nidd recorded extreme peaks in February 2020: at Pateley Bridge the Nidd reached a highest–ever peak of 3.93 m on 9 February (Storm Ciara), and at Knaresborough it peaked at 2.1 m on the same day. (p27)
A severe flood warning was issued for the Nidd at Pateley Bridge on 9 February 2020; flood warnings were issued for the Nidd at Knaresborough and for Knaresborough caravan park on multiple dates in February, and alerts remained in place for the upper and lower Nidd. (p30)
Upper catchment experienced very high river flows during Storms Ciara and Denis in February 2020, contributing to localised river flooding in the Nidd catchment. (p49)",320% to 375% of the monthly long term average (LTA) rainfall; 106% to 128% of the February LTA recorded in 48 hours (upper Nidd) [PAG] (p24),GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.910881,-1.6910531,"<div id=""html_f41b678bcb9fe27363aae0db7e48e991"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Wharfe</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Wharfe recorded a highest&ndash;ever peak (3.91 m at Netherside Hall) on 9 February 2020; the high river levels contributed to flooding of properties in the Settle, Gargrave and Skipton areas and elsewhere in the catchment (including Pateley Bridge, Masham and Boroughbridge). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Exceptional rainfall during February 2020 across the Wharfe/Aire/Swinney catchments caused river flooding within the catchment, impacting communities and infrastructure in the area. (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Catchment received exceptionally high cumulative rainfall during February 2020 (wettest February on record for the area) with persistent rainfall and intense bands during the named storms across the Pennine ridge. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Prolonged and repeated rainfall through February produced four significant high&ndash;flow peaks on the River Wharfe corresponding with Storms Ciara, Dennis and Jorge and the intense 21 February rainfall, resulting in elevated river levels and high flows across the Wharfe catchment. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Wharfe recorded very high peaks during February storms. The Wharfe at Netherside Hall recorded a highest&ndash;ever peak of 3.91 m on 9 Feb, and the Wharfe at Kettlewell peaked at 2.39 m on 10 Feb; the river showed four significant peaks corresponding to the named storms and the 21 Feb rainfall event. [IMG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Environment Agency reported that incidents on Friday night and Saturday morning were predominantly on the River Wharfe (part of the February flooding incidents across the county). (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Storms Ciara and Denis produced intense rainfall in the Wharfe's upper catchment, contributing to localised flooding during the February 2020 events. (p49)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 25 properties internally flooded (Craven District); 44 properties internally flooded (reported to Harrogate Borough Council) (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,River Wharfe,England,"The River Wharfe recorded a highest–ever peak (3.91 m at Netherside Hall) on 9 February 2020; the high river levels contributed to flooding of properties in the Settle, Gargrave and Skipton areas and elsewhere in the catchment (including Pateley Bridge, Masham and Boroughbridge). (p8)
Exceptional rainfall during February 2020 across the Wharfe/Aire/Swinney catchments caused river flooding within the catchment, impacting communities and infrastructure in the area. (p23)
Catchment received exceptionally high cumulative rainfall during February 2020 (wettest February on record for the area) with persistent rainfall and intense bands during the named storms across the Pennine ridge. [PAG] (p24)
Prolonged and repeated rainfall through February produced four significant high–flow peaks on the River Wharfe corresponding with Storms Ciara, Dennis and Jorge and the intense 21 February rainfall, resulting in elevated river levels and high flows across the Wharfe catchment. (p26)
The Wharfe recorded very high peaks during February storms. The Wharfe at Netherside Hall recorded a highest–ever peak of 3.91 m on 9 Feb, and the Wharfe at Kettlewell peaked at 2.39 m on 10 Feb; the river showed four significant peaks corresponding to the named storms and the 21 Feb rainfall event. [IMG] (p27)
The Environment Agency reported that incidents on Friday night and Saturday morning were predominantly on the River Wharfe (part of the February flooding incidents across the county). (p30)
Storms Ciara and Denis produced intense rainfall in the Wharfe's upper catchment, contributing to localised flooding during the February 2020 events. (p49)",up to 25 properties internally flooded (Craven District); 44 properties internally flooded (reported to Harrogate Borough Council) (p8),GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.3895364,-1.6709991,"<div id=""html_7f1d0c393e0f9e4522b1764f2508be9f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Swale</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Swale's catchment received very high rainfall in February 2020, contributing to elevated river flows within the Pennine catchments that led to downstream flooding during the event. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Swale catchment received extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced exceptionally high cumulative rainfall during February 2020 (wettest February on record for the catchment) with prolonged and repeated heavy rainfall associated with Storm Ciara (8&ndash;9 Feb), Storm Dennis (15&ndash;16 Feb) and Storm Jorge (28 Feb). [IMG,PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Swale catchment experienced very high February rainfall and a notable rainfall event at Ivelet on 21 Feb; the report links the prolonged/repeated February rainfall to significant river flow peaks affecting rivers including the Swale, contributing to elevated flows and flood risk. (p26)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warnings were issued for the Swale (including around Richmond and at Topcliffe) and flood alerts were issued for upper and lower Swale during mid&ndash; and late&ndash;February 2020. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River flooding led to overtopping/closures impacting the A684 and the bridge at Morton/Swale, contributing to repeated road closures in February. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,River Swale,England,"The River Swale's catchment received very high rainfall in February 2020, contributing to elevated river flows within the Pennine catchments that led to downstream flooding during the event. (p8)
The Swale catchment received extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)
Experienced exceptionally high cumulative rainfall during February 2020 (wettest February on record for the catchment) with prolonged and repeated heavy rainfall associated with Storm Ciara (8–9 Feb), Storm Dennis (15–16 Feb) and Storm Jorge (28 Feb). [IMG,PAG] (p24)
The Swale catchment experienced very high February rainfall and a notable rainfall event at Ivelet on 21 Feb; the report links the prolonged/repeated February rainfall to significant river flow peaks affecting rivers including the Swale, contributing to elevated flows and flood risk. (p26)
Flood warnings were issued for the Swale (including around Richmond and at Topcliffe) and flood alerts were issued for upper and lower Swale during mid– and late–February 2020. (p30)
River flooding led to overtopping/closures impacting the A684 and the bridge at Morton/Swale, contributing to repeated road closures in February. (p33)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.6884889,-1.8393557,"<div id=""html_5909e26c2a2ced6f1cb786ed05c9f267"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Calder</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Calder catchment received extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded exceptionally high monthly rainfall during February 2020 and intense 48&ndash;hour rainfall during Storm Ciara and other periods; locations in the Calder catchment received 106% to 128% of the February LTA in 48 hours. Rainfall was prolonged and persistent across the month. [PAG] (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant contributing flows from the River Calder increased levels in the lower Aire and were cited as a contributing cause of the very high levels at Snaith (notably around 25 Feb 2020). [PAG] (p43)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant contributing flows from the River Calder increased downstream levels and were cited as a material contributor to the high flood levels at Snaith on 25 February 2020. [IMG,PAG] (p54)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>320% to 375% of the monthly long term average (LTA) rainfall; 106% to 128% of the February LTA recorded in 48 hours [PAG] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,River Calder,England,"The Calder catchment received extreme rainfall in February 2020, producing very high river flows and causing river flooding across the catchment during the period. [PAG] (p23)
Recorded exceptionally high monthly rainfall during February 2020 and intense 48–hour rainfall during Storm Ciara and other periods; locations in the Calder catchment received 106% to 128% of the February LTA in 48 hours. Rainfall was prolonged and persistent across the month. [PAG] (p24)
Significant contributing flows from the River Calder increased levels in the lower Aire and were cited as a contributing cause of the very high levels at Snaith (notably around 25 Feb 2020). [PAG] (p43)
Significant contributing flows from the River Calder increased downstream levels and were cited as a material contributor to the high flood levels at Snaith on 25 February 2020. [IMG,PAG] (p54)",320% to 375% of the monthly long term average (LTA) rainfall; 106% to 128% of the February LTA recorded in 48 hours [PAG] (p24),GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.8103182,-1.0426146,"<div id=""html_00fa458d24ad96c7267ca563e2cb7ec4"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Ouse</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The lower Ouse catchment received exceptionally high rainfall during February 2020 (part of the same prolonged rainfall sequence driven by named storms). This contributed to flooding in the lower Ouse area during the February 2020 event. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the Ouse corridor affected roads (e.g. the road from Tollerton to Newton on Ouse) leading to closures. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ouse reached high peak stages at Skelton during February 2020 (recorded peaks), producing elevated river levels associated with the regional flooding events. [TAB,PAG] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,River Ouse,England,"The lower Ouse catchment received exceptionally high rainfall during February 2020 (part of the same prolonged rainfall sequence driven by named storms). This contributed to flooding in the lower Ouse area during the February 2020 event. (p24)
