GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.88432508022958,-0.5364144843155703,"<div id=""html_74841258d50f1c8b464f6d0dd1a98399"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>East Riding of Yorkshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of the lower Aire affected the East Riding authority area, prompting a parallel investigation process and regular progress meetings between the East Riding and North Yorkshire councils to coordinate community engagement and messaging. (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,East Riding of Yorkshire,England,"Flooding of the lower Aire affected the East Riding authority area, prompting a parallel investigation process and regular progress meetings between the East Riding and North Yorkshire councils to coordinate community engagement and messaging. (p2)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.006741961160174,-1.5144704578826116,"<div id=""html_10f9a123ef2e00db2331d5a40b150924"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harrogate District</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Masham (in Harrogate Borough) is recorded as one of the worst&ndash;affected settlements during February 2020 and was a focus of the investigation for that district. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rivers within Harrogate district (notably the Nidd and Ure) saw four significant flow peaks in February 2020 corresponding to storms Ciara, Dennis, Jorge and the rainfall event on 21 February; notable recorded peaks included the Nidd at Pateley Bridge and Ure at Boroughbridge. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding of residential and commercial premises across the district; flooding also affected farming assets, roads and bridges; emergency services responded to flood&ndash;related call&ndash;outs including vehicles in floodwater and manning pumps. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area has a history of localised flooding driven by Swinney Beck; during periods of intense rainfall (such as February) the beck overtops and produces flow exceedance down town streets, increasing local flood risk and prompting reactive highway cleansing and response measures. (p40)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>44 addresses (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Harrogate District,England,"Masham (in Harrogate Borough) is recorded as one of the worst–affected settlements during February 2020 and was a focus of the investigation for that district. [IMG] (p11)
Rivers within Harrogate district (notably the Nidd and Ure) saw four significant flow peaks in February 2020 corresponding to storms Ciara, Dennis, Jorge and the rainfall event on 21 February; notable recorded peaks included the Nidd at Pateley Bridge and Ure at Boroughbridge. (p27)
Internal property flooding of residential and commercial premises across the district; flooding also affected farming assets, roads and bridges; emergency services responded to flood–related call–outs including vehicles in floodwater and manning pumps. (p32)
Area has a history of localised flooding driven by Swinney Beck; during periods of intense rainfall (such as February) the beck overtops and produces flow exceedance down town streets, increasing local flood risk and prompting reactive highway cleansing and response measures. (p40)",44 addresses (p32),GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.9825271,-1.3852499932684972,"<div id=""html_fe015da4bcd73f5593cfa1cb22ee7584"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Yorkshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The region experienced prolonged and persistent heavy rainfall throughout February 2020 (wettest February on record for multiple local catchments), with intense rainfall during named storms which produced widespread, sustained high runoff across catchments in Yorkshire. (p24)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Across the Yorkshire area soils were saturated and groundwater levels rose at most index sites, with more than half ending the month exceptionally high; the Environment Agency had groundwater flood warnings in force. [PAG] (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Yorkshire,England,"The region experienced prolonged and persistent heavy rainfall throughout February 2020 (wettest February on record for multiple local catchments), with intense rainfall during named storms which produced widespread, sustained high runoff across catchments in Yorkshire. (p24)
Across the Yorkshire area soils were saturated and groundwater levels rose at most index sites, with more than half ending the month exceptionally high; the Environment Agency had groundwater flood warnings in force. [PAG] (p29)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.697448543088825,-1.0268934330649808,"<div id=""html_e40e2cbbb16fda1dee6abfa3a1012c64"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Carlton Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carlton Bridge lies at the eastern end of the Hirst Road corridor identified as one of the worst&ndash;affected parts of the Lower Aire area; it was recorded as impacted by the February 2020 flooding in Selby District. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple high peak stages recorded at Carlton Bridge during February, showing it reached the highest recorded level in February on 25 Feb and indicating significant river flooding at this location. [TAB,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Observed gradual increase in river stage at the Carlton Bridge gauge during the event; hydrographs show water levels rising and this increase was exacerbated by tidal cycles. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carlton Bridge stage record shows frequent jagged peaks between 9&ndash;27 Feb 2020; peaks are synchronized with Crown Point, indicating multiple high&ndash;flow events on the River Aire that contributed to flooding in the Lower Aire corridor during February 2020. [IMG] (p48)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak stages recorded: 6.052 m AOD (initial peak), 5.926 m AOD (17 Feb), 6.228 m AOD (25 Feb highest in February) [TAB,PAG] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Carlton Bridge,England,"Carlton Bridge lies at the eastern end of the Hirst Road corridor identified as one of the worst–affected parts of the Lower Aire area; it was recorded as impacted by the February 2020 flooding in Selby District. (p11)
Multiple high peak stages recorded at Carlton Bridge during February, showing it reached the highest recorded level in February on 25 Feb and indicating significant river flooding at this location. [TAB,PAG] (p42)
Observed gradual increase in river stage at the Carlton Bridge gauge during the event; hydrographs show water levels rising and this increase was exacerbated by tidal cycles. (p44)
Carlton Bridge stage record shows frequent jagged peaks between 9–27 Feb 2020; peaks are synchronized with Crown Point, indicating multiple high–flow events on the River Aire that contributed to flooding in the Lower Aire corridor during February 2020. [IMG] (p48)","Peak stages recorded: 6.052 m AOD (initial peak), 5.926 m AOD (17 Feb), 6.228 m AOD (25 Feb highest in February) [TAB,PAG] (p42)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.9921491,-1.5391039,"<div id=""html_85fe66af889a41f2f9eb68bfe0aaa101"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Harrogate</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Harrogate Borough was affected by the significant flood events in the Aire catchment; these February 2020 events prompted formal Section 19 investigations by the lead local flood authority. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Harrogate,England,Harrogate Borough was affected by the significant flood events in the Aire catchment; these February 2020 events prompted formal Section 19 investigations by the lead local flood authority. (p1),,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.058640597310216,-2.2962422752192233,"<div id=""html_7374f74df88aba66b5028d9a8e344c19"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gildersleets</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The community was identified for a feasibility review of flood alleviation or improved resilience measures, indicating it experienced flood impacts that require consideration of mitigation/resilience actions. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded river flooding during February 2020; identified by NYCC as one of the worst&ndash;affected settlements in Craven. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Suffered direct flooding to properties; significant depths of floodwater occurred on the adjacent A65, with flows driven towards properties by passing vehicles. [IMG,PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Five properties experienced internal flooding. Flood depths on the nearby A65 produced flows that were driven toward the properties; vehicle passage on the A65 exacerbated flows. [PAG] (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A submitted photograph documents floodwater overtopping and bypassing a protective bund at Gildersleets; water streamed through trees and into the area behind the bund, causing inundation of land and likely flooding of properties/areas protected by the bund. [IMG,PAG] (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An unnamed ordinary watercourse flooded onto the A65, overspilling and flowing through properties after the watercourse network was overwhelmed. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The community experienced flooding during February 2020, prompting recommendations to review feasibility of flood alleviation or improved property/community resilience measures. (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [IMG,PAG] (p31)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties [PAG] (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Gildersleets,England,"The community was identified for a feasibility review of flood alleviation or improved resilience measures, indicating it experienced flood impacts that require consideration of mitigation/resilience actions. (p2)
Recorded river flooding during February 2020; identified by NYCC as one of the worst–affected settlements in Craven. (p11)
Suffered direct flooding to properties; significant depths of floodwater occurred on the adjacent A65, with flows driven towards properties by passing vehicles. [IMG,PAG] (p31)
Five properties experienced internal flooding. Flood depths on the nearby A65 produced flows that were driven toward the properties; vehicle passage on the A65 exacerbated flows. [PAG] (p32)
A submitted photograph documents floodwater overtopping and bypassing a protective bund at Gildersleets; water streamed through trees and into the area behind the bund, causing inundation of land and likely flooding of properties/areas protected by the bund. [IMG,PAG] (p39)
An unnamed ordinary watercourse flooded onto the A65, overspilling and flowing through properties after the watercourse network was overwhelmed. (p49)
The community experienced flooding during February 2020, prompting recommendations to review feasibility of flood alleviation or improved property/community resilience measures. (p50)","5 properties [IMG,PAG] (p31)
5 properties [PAG] (p32)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.222008,-1.6564736,"<div id=""html_16e75d0d390893a2dfdb199f9f86898a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Masham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following the flood event, authorities have continued monitoring of Swinney Beck in Masham and provided responsive services, indicating local flood impacts and ongoing risk management in the town. (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of properties in the Masham area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded river flooding during February 2020; identified by NYCC as one of the worst&ndash;affected settlements in Harrogate Borough. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ure at Masham recorded a monthly peak during the storms, reaching 3.5 m on 9 Feb. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Masham was the focus of repeated flood warnings during February 2020: Swinney Beck at Masham had flood warnings on 9 February, 15 February, 21&ndash;24 February and again at the end of February (28&ndash;29 Feb); the River Ure at Masham was also the subject of warnings on 8 February, 16 February and 21&ndash;24 February, indicating repeated river flooding risk to Masham during 01 Feb 2020 &ndash; 29 Feb 2020. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding reported; extent of flooding documented in contemporaneous reports of Storm Ciara impacts. (p32)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Swinney Beck breached through a resident's garden and flowed down Red Lane, Park Square and Church Street to the low point at Silver Street; flows overtopped the beck and tracked along roads, overwhelming gullies and requiring NYCC/brigade intervention (fire brigade pumped water through to the Shooting Holme). NYCC committed to clearing highway drains on the flow route and inspected the Oaks box culvert. (p40)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Swinney Beck capacity was exceeded due to high river flows; NYCC&ndash;owned structures were inspected and no significant defects or obstructions were found. The exceeded capacity led to flooding in Masham. (p49)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The town experienced flooding associated with Swinney Beck during February 2020, prompting monitoring and responsive actions by authorities. (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>44 properties reported to Harrogate Borough Council (affecting Pateley Bridge, Masham, Boroughbridge) (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>170mm silt in The Oaks box culvert; box culvert depth 1200mm (p40)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Masham,England,"Following the flood event, authorities have continued monitoring of Swinney Beck in Masham and provided responsive services, indicating local flood impacts and ongoing risk management in the town. (p2)
Flooding of properties in the Masham area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)
Recorded river flooding during February 2020; identified by NYCC as one of the worst–affected settlements in Harrogate Borough. (p11)
The River Ure at Masham recorded a monthly peak during the storms, reaching 3.5 m on 9 Feb. (p27)
Masham was the focus of repeated flood warnings during February 2020: Swinney Beck at Masham had flood warnings on 9 February, 15 February, 21–24 February and again at the end of February (28–29 Feb); the River Ure at Masham was also the subject of warnings on 8 February, 16 February and 21–24 February, indicating repeated river flooding risk to Masham during 01 Feb 2020 – 29 Feb 2020. (p30)
Property flooding reported; extent of flooding documented in contemporaneous reports of Storm Ciara impacts. (p32)
Swinney Beck breached through a resident's garden and flowed down Red Lane, Park Square and Church Street to the low point at Silver Street; flows overtopped the beck and tracked along roads, overwhelming gullies and requiring NYCC/brigade intervention (fire brigade pumped water through to the Shooting Holme). NYCC committed to clearing highway drains on the flow route and inspected the Oaks box culvert. (p40)
Swinney Beck capacity was exceeded due to high river flows; NYCC–owned structures were inspected and no significant defects or obstructions were found. The exceeded capacity led to flooding in Masham. (p49)
The town experienced flooding associated with Swinney Beck during February 2020, prompting monitoring and responsive actions by authorities. (p50)","44 properties reported to Harrogate Borough Council (affecting Pateley Bridge, Masham, Boroughbridge) (p8)
170mm silt in The Oaks box culvert; box culvert depth 1200mm (p40)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7130761,-1.0725282,"<div id=""html_a0dd2f83aad3cb8ec5421e81b2f76c4a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hirst Courtney</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Washlands reached capacity and the River Aire overtopped into the community on 25 February 2020; flooding at Hirst Courtney was attributed in part to Black Drain being unable to drain into the Aire because of high river levels. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warnings and alerts were in place for the River Aire at Hirst Courtney during the February event (alerts remained 16&ndash;20 February and further warnings issued between 21&ndash;24 February). (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location of internal property flooding (part of the 18 properties recorded in Selby District) and site of railway embankment damage caused by flooding, which led to single&ndash;track railway operations and risk to deliveries to Drax Power Station. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Railway embankment at Hirst Courtney / West Banks was damaged during the flood event; railway was repaired to single&ndash;track operations to maintain fuel deliveries, with risk of significant disruption to nearby infrastructure and operations. (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the River Aire into the community on 25 February 2020 after washlands reached capacity, resulting in flooding of the settlement. [MUL] (p49)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 properties (concentrated around Hirst Courtney, Carlton and Acaster) [PAG] (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Hirst Courtney,England,"Washlands reached capacity and the River Aire overtopped into the community on 25 February 2020; flooding at Hirst Courtney was attributed in part to Black Drain being unable to drain into the Aire because of high river levels. (p8)
Flood warnings and alerts were in place for the River Aire at Hirst Courtney during the February event (alerts remained 16–20 February and further warnings issued between 21–24 February). (p30)
Location of internal property flooding (part of the 18 properties recorded in Selby District) and site of railway embankment damage caused by flooding, which led to single–track railway operations and risk to deliveries to Drax Power Station. [PAG] (p33)
Railway embankment at Hirst Courtney / West Banks was damaged during the flood event; railway was repaired to single–track operations to maintain fuel deliveries, with risk of significant disruption to nearby infrastructure and operations. (p34)
Overtopping of the River Aire into the community on 25 February 2020 after washlands reached capacity, resulting in flooding of the settlement. [MUL] (p49)","18 properties (concentrated around Hirst Courtney, Carlton and Acaster) [PAG] (p33)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.709324,-1.0228333,"<div id=""html_d28ea264b1ca2576336871df0794ac58"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Carlton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Carlton formed the other boundary of the Hirst Road corridor where most of the district's 27 internally flooded properties occurred. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Concentration of internal property flooding (part of the 18 properties recorded in Selby District) and local road flooding/closures (references to Carlton / Hirst Courtney and Hirst Road, Carlton) during the storms. (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Highway (Hirst Road) in Carlton required verge and carriageway repairs and sandbagging was used to protect at&ndash;risk communities. (p36)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>27 properties (mostly along the Hirst Road corridor between the A19 and Carlton) (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>18 properties (concentrated around Hirst Courtney, Carlton and Acaster) (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Carlton,England,"Carlton formed the other boundary of the Hirst Road corridor where most of the district's 27 internally flooded properties occurred. (p8)
Concentration of internal property flooding (part of the 18 properties recorded in Selby District) and local road flooding/closures (references to Carlton / Hirst Courtney and Hirst Road, Carlton) during the storms. (p33)
Highway (Hirst Road) in Carlton required verge and carriageway repairs and sandbagging was used to protect at–risk communities. (p36)","27 properties (mostly along the Hirst Road corridor between the A19 and Carlton) (p8)
18 properties (concentrated around Hirst Courtney, Carlton and Acaster) (p33)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.069473,-2.276127,"<div id=""html_d5f09b3a0b2d64945668c66299d870fa"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Settle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of properties in the Settle area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded flood incidents in February 2020 concentrated near Settle; incidents are associated with main rivers and low&ndash;lying valley locations adjacent to the town. [IMG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas surrounding Settle experienced river flooding that led to properties being inundated. (p31)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 25 properties internally flooded across Craven District (affecting Settle, Gargrave, Skipton) (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>10 properties (across areas surrounding Settle and Giggleswick, including Gildersleets) (p31)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Settle,England,"Flooding of properties in the Settle area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)
