GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.960537735975684,-2.017616471159667,"<div id=""html_f5ac1b3cadd3fe19fbb8093b18ce136e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Skipton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of properties in the Skipton area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding led to people being trapped in a vehicle; emergency crews rescued occupants and attended to animals affected by the flooding. (p31)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>up to 25 properties internally flooded across Craven District (affecting Settle, Gargrave, Skipton) (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Skipton,England,"Flooding of properties in the Skipton area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)
Flooding led to people being trapped in a vehicle; emergency crews rescued occupants and attended to animals affected by the flooding. (p31)","up to 25 properties internally flooded across Craven District (affecting Settle, Gargrave, Skipton) (p8)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.08647706778406,-1.760107260368611,"<div id=""html_322728816e5ca23505072b77aa3446e3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pateley Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of properties in the Pateley Bridge area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The gauge on the Nidd at Pateley Bridge recorded a highest&ndash;ever peak of 3.93 m on 9 February 2020 during Storm Ciara, representing an extreme river&ndash;level event at that location. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flood warning issued for the Nidd at Pateley Bridge (river levels threatened severe flooding). (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Incidents of property flooding reported (residential and business properties flooded). (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>44 properties reported to Harrogate Borough Council (affecting Pateley Bridge, Masham, Boroughbridge) (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Pateley Bridge,England,"Flooding of properties in the Pateley Bridge area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)
The gauge on the Nidd at Pateley Bridge recorded a highest–ever peak of 3.93 m on 9 February 2020 during Storm Ciara, representing an extreme river–level event at that location. (p27)
Severe flood warning issued for the Nidd at Pateley Bridge (river levels threatened severe flooding). (p30)
Incidents of property flooding reported (residential and business properties flooded). (p32)","44 properties reported to Harrogate Borough Council (affecting Pateley Bridge, Masham, Boroughbridge) (p8)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.0943398,-1.3947113,"<div id=""html_4e4a79801a010685a4e806728eefb7fc"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Boroughbridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding of properties in the Boroughbridge area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Ure at Boroughbridge recorded a monthly peak of 15.6 m on 10 Feb during the February storm sequence. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Incidents of property flooding reported (residential and business properties flooded). (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>44 properties reported to Harrogate Borough Council (affecting Pateley Bridge, Masham, Boroughbridge) (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Boroughbridge,England,"Flooding of properties in the Boroughbridge area caused by exceptionally high river levels during the February 2020 storms. (p8)
The River Ure at Boroughbridge recorded a monthly peak of 15.6 m on 10 Feb during the February storm sequence. (p27)
Incidents of property flooding reported (residential and business properties flooded). (p32)","44 properties reported to Harrogate Borough Council (affecting Pateley Bridge, Masham, Boroughbridge) (p8)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7127557,-1.0559087,"<div id=""html_1738566e5b8e941d2f93db55f22eb0b6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>West Bank</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the River Aire into the community, causing flooding of the community on the event date. (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>West Bank pond recorded a peak level of 3.38 m during the February events (25 February monthly peak), noted as the highest level on record for this site (typical range 0.27&ndash;1.00 m), indicating record high inundation at this pond/area. [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced rises during Storm Ciara and Storm Dennis and reached the highest level on record during the February event. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Railway embankment damaged by flooding, disrupting rail deliveries of wood pellet fuel to Drax Power Station; repairs implemented with single&ndash;track railway operations. [PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Railway embankment damage at West Banks (associated with Hirst Courtney) affected rail operations delivering wood pellet fuel; repairs allowed single&ndash;track running but posed disruption risk. (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local highway (Hirst Road) in West Bank experienced flooding requiring sandbag removal and verge and carriageway repairs. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The West Bank area experienced river flooding; local residents constructed an approx. 300 m impermeable membrane and rubble bund along Hurst Road to hold back water. The unauthorised defence created a residual breach risk, prompted local dispute about possible exacerbation of flooding to properties south of the river, and was retained/reinforced to reduce immediate risk. (p45)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overtopping of the River Aire into the West Bank community on 25 February 2020 after washlands reached capacity, causing flooding of the area. [PAG,MUL] (p49)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3.38 m peak (highest on record); typical range 0.27&ndash;1.00 m [PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Peak depth 3.38 m (highest level on record; recorded during February 2020) [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,West Bank,England,"Overtopping of the River Aire into the community, causing flooding of the community on the event date. (p8)
West Bank pond recorded a peak level of 3.38 m during the February events (25 February monthly peak), noted as the highest level on record for this site (typical range 0.27–1.00 m), indicating record high inundation at this pond/area. [PAG] (p28)
Experienced rises during Storm Ciara and Storm Dennis and reached the highest level on record during the February event. [IMG,PAG] (p29)
Railway embankment damaged by flooding, disrupting rail deliveries of wood pellet fuel to Drax Power Station; repairs implemented with single–track railway operations. [PAG] (p33)
Railway embankment damage at West Banks (associated with Hirst Courtney) affected rail operations delivering wood pellet fuel; repairs allowed single–track running but posed disruption risk. (p34)
Local highway (Hirst Road) in West Bank experienced flooding requiring sandbag removal and verge and carriageway repairs. (p36)
The West Bank area experienced river flooding; local residents constructed an approx. 300 m impermeable membrane and rubble bund along Hurst Road to hold back water. The unauthorised defence created a residual breach risk, prompted local dispute about possible exacerbation of flooding to properties south of the river, and was retained/reinforced to reduce immediate risk. (p45)
Overtopping of the River Aire into the West Bank community on 25 February 2020 after washlands reached capacity, causing flooding of the area. [PAG,MUL] (p49)","3.38 m peak (highest on record); typical range 0.27–1.00 m [PAG] (p28)
Peak depth 3.38 m (highest level on record; recorded during February 2020) [IMG,PAG] (p29)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7287,-1.121041,"<div id=""html_734d146a346d4cd67d24e809bf9e1138"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Chapel Haddlesey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Location at the A19 junction formed the upstream extent of the Hirst Road corridor flooding during February 2020, affecting local transport connections in the village area. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Aire at Chapel Haddesely recorded a significant peak (3.43 m) on 25 February 2020 during the February storm sequence, representing a monthly high in river level and contributing to elevated flood risk in the local area. (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Site of A19 closure on 19 Feb when the River Aire overtopped; the area experienced road closures as flooding impacted the A19 and surrounding routes (A19 Chapel Haddlesey to Wand Lane). [MUL] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwaters inundated surrounding fields and submerged the nearby A19; aerial imagery shows flooding affecting areas around the village and some residential areas, causing transport disruption and community risk. [IMG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Damage was caused to the A19 at Chapel Haddlesey, requiring embankment and road reconstruction as a result of the flooding. (p36)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire at Chapel Haddlesey recorded multiple peak stages during February 2020 (notably 3.436m, 3.37m and 3.437m), indicating repeated high river levels and sustained river flooding during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p42)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gauge at Chapel Haddlesey showed flat&ndash;topped peaks caused by overtopping of embankments and flooding of washlands; the stage record therefore does not fully reflect storage and floodplain behaviours during the event. (p44)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Stage at Chapel Haddlesey rose notably during the 9&ndash;27 Feb 2020 period, indicating river flood pulses on the River Aire during the February 2020 event that were associated with local flooding incidents. [IMG] (p48)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3.436 m; 3.37 m; 3.437 m (recorded peak stages) [TAB,PAG] (p42)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Chapel Haddlesey,England,"Location at the A19 junction formed the upstream extent of the Hirst Road corridor flooding during February 2020, affecting local transport connections in the village area. (p11)
River Aire at Chapel Haddesely recorded a significant peak (3.43 m) on 25 February 2020 during the February storm sequence, representing a monthly high in river level and contributing to elevated flood risk in the local area. (p28)
Site of A19 closure on 19 Feb when the River Aire overtopped; the area experienced road closures as flooding impacted the A19 and surrounding routes (A19 Chapel Haddlesey to Wand Lane). [MUL] (p33)
Floodwaters inundated surrounding fields and submerged the nearby A19; aerial imagery shows flooding affecting areas around the village and some residential areas, causing transport disruption and community risk. [IMG] (p34)
Damage was caused to the A19 at Chapel Haddlesey, requiring embankment and road reconstruction as a result of the flooding. (p36)
The River Aire at Chapel Haddlesey recorded multiple peak stages during February 2020 (notably 3.436m, 3.37m and 3.437m), indicating repeated high river levels and sustained river flooding during the event. [TAB,PAG] (p42)
Gauge at Chapel Haddlesey showed flat–topped peaks caused by overtopping of embankments and flooding of washlands; the stage record therefore does not fully reflect storage and floodplain behaviours during the event. (p44)
Stage at Chapel Haddlesey rose notably during the 9–27 Feb 2020 period, indicating river flood pulses on the River Aire during the February 2020 event that were associated with local flooding incidents. [IMG] (p48)","3.436 m; 3.37 m; 3.437 m (recorded peak stages) [TAB,PAG] (p42)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.1855566,-1.3320382890988745,"<div id=""html_85463da45cea181faf4b0579f0986ccb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Dalton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Dalton area (including the industrial estate) was recorded in Hambleton district as affected by the February 2020 flooding, with Dalton Bridge cited as impacted infrastructure. (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road closures and severe disruption to the industrial estate due to flooding and the closure of Dalton Bridge (reported 26 February 2020). (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Dalton,England,"The Dalton area (including the industrial estate) was recorded in Hambleton district as affected by the February 2020 flooding, with Dalton Bridge cited as impacted infrastructure. (p11)
Road closures and severe disruption to the industrial estate due to flooding and the closure of Dalton Bridge (reported 26 February 2020). (p33)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.7173936,-1.0834287,"<div id=""html_2c809c6635f482c1df8bc02478cda5ba"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Temple Hirst</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused damage to a flood bank which required immediate emergency repairs; the collapse prompted notifications to those at risk. (p33)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Temple Hirst,England,Flooding caused damage to a flood bank which required immediate emergency repairs; the collapse prompted notifications to those at risk. (p33),,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.3774549,-2.1000335,"<div id=""html_83bd367dff46673d4928ebbd929d9ae1"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Ivelet</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The gauge at Ivelet recorded substantial storm&ndash;period runoff that led to raised river levels on the Swale during the report period, reflecting localised high flows associated with the 15&ndash;16 February event and the later 21 February rainfall. (p26)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Ivelet,England,"The gauge at Ivelet recorded substantial storm–period runoff that led to raised river levels on the Swale during the report period, reflecting localised high flows associated with the 15–16 February event and the later 21 February rainfall. (p26)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.1471677,-2.0474661,"<div id=""html_d2ecd8b5d8eda6386744403b6ebfc9e7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kettlewell</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warnings for Kettlewell Beck at Kettlewell were in place (21&ndash;25 February), indicating the village was at risk from river/beck flooding during the event. (p30)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Kettlewell,England,"Flood warnings for Kettlewell Beck at Kettlewell were in place (21–25 February), indicating the village was at risk from river/beck flooding during the event. (p30)",,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.0101765,-1.4651279311880052,"<div id=""html_b1e44a06d0f05808cf413ae6971b419e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Knaresborough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River Nidd flows at Knaresborough peaked during Storm Ciara, reaching 2.1 m on 9 Feb. (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood warnings were issued for the River Nidd at Knaresborough and for the Knaresborough caravan park during February 2020. (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was reported affecting residential and commercial properties in Knaresborough during February; incidents formed part of wider district impacts that required emergency service responses (e.g. vehicles in floodwater, pumping). (p32)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>approximately 44 addresses (across Harrogate district) (p32)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Knaresborough,England,"River Nidd flows at Knaresborough peaked during Storm Ciara, reaching 2.1 m on 9 Feb. (p27)
Flood warnings were issued for the River Nidd at Knaresborough and for the Knaresborough caravan park during February 2020. (p30)
Internal property flooding was reported affecting residential and commercial properties in Knaresborough during February; incidents formed part of wider district impacts that required emergency service responses (e.g. vehicles in floodwater, pumping). (p32)",approximately 44 addresses (across Harrogate district) (p32),GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,54.298462,-1.5500293,"<div id=""html_fe2b04810f7f7dd4b55a743382f32556"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Leeming</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced high river levels where Bedale Beck peaked during the February storm sequence. [IMG] (p28)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Leeming,England,Experienced high river levels where Bedale Beck peaked during the February storm sequence. [IMG] (p28),,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
GB-NYK,3,2020-02-01,53.785097,-1.099040720243205,"<div id=""html_8395b8236a47f5e657e84064e86c4125"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Selby</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The River Aire running through Selby district recorded significant peaks and sustained high water levels across February; associated washlands and ing areas in the Selby area showed prolonged elevated depths following Storm Ciara and later storms, indicating localised flooding and high water retention in the district's floodplain areas. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Selby town experienced flooding impacts to its roads (examples cited include Ousegate and local riverside areas such as The Ramparts), with emergency response to affected roads and properties during February. (p33)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Monitoring sites in the Selby area recorded peaks including Chapel Haddesely 3.43 m, Beal Weir 4.10 m, Birkin West Holme 5.40 m, plus record highs at Roall Dyke 5.95 m, West Bank Pond 3.38 m and Wheelan Dyke 4.59 m. [IMG,PAG] (p29)</p></div></div></body></div>",North Yorkshire,Selby,England,"The River Aire running through Selby district recorded significant peaks and sustained high water levels across February; associated washlands and ing areas in the Selby area showed prolonged elevated depths following Storm Ciara and later storms, indicating localised flooding and high water retention in the district's floodplain areas. [IMG,PAG] (p29)
Selby town experienced flooding impacts to its roads (examples cited include Ousegate and local riverside areas such as The Ramparts), with emergency response to affected roads and properties during February. (p33)","Monitoring sites in the Selby area recorded peaks including Chapel Haddesely 3.43 m, Beal Weir 4.10 m, Birkin West Holme 5.40 m, plus record highs at Roall Dyke 5.95 m, West Bank Pond 3.38 m and Wheelan Dyke 4.59 m. [IMG,PAG] (p29)",GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01.html,GB-NYK_3_2020-02-01,North Yorkshire flooding 01 Feb 2020 - 29 Feb 2020,Flood Investigation Report: February 2020,089/North Yorkshire County/Section 19 Report on the February Flooding Events in the Aire Catch.pdf
