GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.3550556,-0.0643103753173489,"<div id=""html_288138efc85eccb517bfcd901868a6d5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding associated with the Merstham/Coulsdon Bourne occurred in the south of the borough (South Coulsdon) during February&ndash;March 2014, affecting local railway infrastructure and residential gardens. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Croydon were affected by the Merstham Bourne flooding in February&ndash;March 2014: gardens were flooded and transport infrastructure was disrupted (railway line flooded), with identified risks to residential properties and transport if maintenance is not carried out. (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Croydon,England,"Flooding associated with the Merstham/Coulsdon Bourne occurred in the south of the borough (South Coulsdon) during February–March 2014, affecting local railway infrastructure and residential gardens. (p5)
Parts of Croydon were affected by the Merstham Bourne flooding in February–March 2014: gardens were flooded and transport infrastructure was disrupted (railway line flooded), with identified risks to residential properties and transport if maintenance is not carried out. (p6)",,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.315543,-0.137974,"<div id=""html_d3faa91dbe23540e98c262667bbdaaaa"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coulsdon South Station</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Railway infrastructure around South Coulsdon Station was flooded for several weeks during the February&ndash;March 2014 event. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood flow paths (known and assumed) converge near Coulsdon South Station and the approximate flooded area is adjacent to the station; flooding occurred in the residential area immediately downstream of these flow paths, placing infrastructure and nearby developments at risk. [IMG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding from an overwhelmed culvert flowing north towards Coulsdon South station inundated the railway, causing signals to trip and slow line speeds and disruption to rail services. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An overwhelmed culvert near the station caused pooling and led to flooding of the railway track (water discharged from the culvert beneath the line inundated the track). [IMG] (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Coulsdon South Station,England,"Railway infrastructure around South Coulsdon Station was flooded for several weeks during the February–March 2014 event. (p5)
Flood flow paths (known and assumed) converge near Coulsdon South Station and the approximate flooded area is adjacent to the station; flooding occurred in the residential area immediately downstream of these flow paths, placing infrastructure and nearby developments at risk. [IMG,MUL] (p16)
Flooding from an overwhelmed culvert flowing north towards Coulsdon South station inundated the railway, causing signals to trip and slow line speeds and disruption to rail services. [PAG] (p17)
An overwhelmed culvert near the station caused pooling and led to flooding of the railway track (water discharged from the culvert beneath the line inundated the track). [IMG] (p18)",,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.3120373,-0.140708,"<div id=""html_e718b1b78acfe83b0b08f44d15cb9ebf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Reddown Road, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gardens at a number of residential properties in Reddown Road (which back onto the London to Brighton main line) were flooded for several weeks during the February&ndash;March 2014 event. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Overland flows from the Bourne passed through an open ditch between the Brighton main line railway and the rear gardens of residential properties on Reddown Road during the February&ndash;March 2014 event, affecting the gardens adjacent to the ditch and conveying floodwater toward the drainage network. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Back gardens of properties along Reddown Road were flooded when water escaped from the underground Merstham Bourne (culvert damage) and flowed through gardens; floodwaters reached depths up to 30 cm and persisted for several weeks before fully receding in late March 2014. [MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Merstham Bourne overflowed into residential gardens and patios in Reddown Road; gardens were inundated with standing water and an open culvert section was overwhelmed, contributing to water backing up toward the railway embankment. [PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Merstham Bourne flowed between residential gardens on Reddown Road, bringing high water adjacent to properties and vegetation. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Numbers 20, 20A and 18 had water entering gardens; water also flowed along backs of Nos.16 and 8A; flood depths up to 30 cm [MUL] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Reddown Road, Croydon",England,"Gardens at a number of residential properties in Reddown Road (which back onto the London to Brighton main line) were flooded for several weeks during the February–March 2014 event. (p5)
Overland flows from the Bourne passed through an open ditch between the Brighton main line railway and the rear gardens of residential properties on Reddown Road during the February–March 2014 event, affecting the gardens adjacent to the ditch and conveying floodwater toward the drainage network. (p10)
Back gardens of properties along Reddown Road were flooded when water escaped from the underground Merstham Bourne (culvert damage) and flowed through gardens; floodwaters reached depths up to 30 cm and persisted for several weeks before fully receding in late March 2014. [MUL] (p16)
Merstham Bourne overflowed into residential gardens and patios in Reddown Road; gardens were inundated with standing water and an open culvert section was overwhelmed, contributing to water backing up toward the railway embankment. [PAG] (p17)
Merstham Bourne flowed between residential gardens on Reddown Road, bringing high water adjacent to properties and vegetation. [IMG] (p18)","Numbers 20, 20A and 18 had water entering gardens; water also flowed along backs of Nos.16 and 8A; flood depths up to 30 cm [MUL] (p16)",GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.303623,-0.1432914,"<div id=""html_ee02744591408b1ad43f1b41d2a8bb38"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Woodplace Lane, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The railway line adjacent to Woodplace Lane was flooded as part of the February&ndash;March 2014 event, contributing to transport disruption. [TAB] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Railway adjacent to Woodplace Lane was flooded as part of a 530 metre flooded section caused by an overwhelmed culvert, contributing to signalling trips and service disruption. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Woodplace Lane, Croydon",England,"The railway line adjacent to Woodplace Lane was flooded as part of the February–March 2014 event, contributing to transport disruption. [TAB] (p6)
Railway adjacent to Woodplace Lane was flooded as part of a 530 metre flooded section caused by an overwhelmed culvert, contributing to signalling trips and service disruption. (p17)",,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.29222196667048,-0.1478854264709371,"<div id=""html_fc9443a24805f7c4949d040318ca10a5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Netherne Drive, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The railway line at/near Star Bridge (Nethern Drive) was within the 530 m stretch flooded during February&ndash;March 2014, causing disruption to rail infrastructure. [TAB] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A 530 metre length of railway between Woodplace Lane and Star Bridge (Nethern Drive) was flooded when an overwhelmed culvert allowed the Merstham Bourne to flow north. Flooding caused signals to trip, slow line speeds and disruption to rail travel. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Netherne Drive, Croydon",England,"The railway line at/near Star Bridge (Nethern Drive) was within the 530 m stretch flooded during February–March 2014, causing disruption to rail infrastructure. [TAB] (p6)
A 530 metre length of railway between Woodplace Lane and Star Bridge (Nethern Drive) was flooded when an overwhelmed culvert allowed the Merstham Bourne to flow north. Flooding caused signals to trip, slow line speeds and disruption to rail travel. (p17)",,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.3389331,-0.1163358,"<div id=""html_3de76d5c0f0b22cfa9be39ee8883473f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brighton Road, Purley</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Bourne&#8217;s route follows the line of the A23/railway valley; during the February&ndash;March 2014 event flows were conveyed along this corridor into the surface water drainage network toward Purley Cross, with valley topography and concentrated runoff exacerbating flow accumulation along the A23/railway corridor. (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Brighton Road, Purley",England,"The Bourne’s route follows the line of the A23/railway valley; during the February–March 2014 event flows were conveyed along this corridor into the surface water drainage network toward Purley Cross, with valley topography and concentrated runoff exacerbating flow accumulation along the A23/railway corridor. (p10)",,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.3172473,-0.1383468,"<div id=""html_5cf8490873ac263ee21f30f76239efc6"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Marlpit Lane, Croydon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced flow from the Merstham Bourne connecting to the sewer causing foul/sewer&ndash;related flooding; tankers were deployed to try to remove water at Marlpit Lane but were ineffective while the bourne continued to flow; works were carried out on open ditch sections to increase capacity and storage. [TAB] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,"Marlpit Lane, Croydon",England,Experienced flow from the Merstham Bourne connecting to the sewer causing foul/sewer–related flooding; tankers were deployed to try to remove water at Marlpit Lane but were ineffective while the bourne continued to flow; works were carried out on open ditch sections to increase capacity and storage. [TAB] (p19),,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.2715316,-0.3414523511290909,"<div id=""html_20b080ee682fe3305216d1876070fd9e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Surrey</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas south of the Croydon borough boundary in Surrey experienced flooding near the north end of Merstham Tunnel during the event. (p17)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Surrey,England,Areas south of the Croydon borough boundary in Surrey experienced flooding near the north end of Merstham Tunnel during the event. (p17),,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CRY,2,2014-02-01,51.3199716,-0.1393506,"<div id=""html_316c50c8e7bcb29b7acb7623f437105f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Coulsdon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Investigation focused on impacts from the ephemeral Merstham (Coulsdon) Bourne at the open ditch section near Coulsdon South Station where the bourne flows adjacent to the railway and rear gardens; the event involved overland/ephemeral watercourse flow into the surface water drainage network affecting this area. (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised flooding occurred in the Coulsdon area where the Merstham Bourne emerges from a damaged culvert; floodwaters affected residential gardens adjacent to Network Rail land and created a local flood risk that required monitoring by the Environment Agency because recent ground&ndash;floor flats nearby could be threatened. [IMG,MUL] (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected gardens in residential areas (water up to 30 cm in gardens), coming very close to properties and posing a serious potential risk; residential buildings were not entered by floodwater. (p21)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>gardens flooded to depths up to 30 cm; 0 residential properties flooded (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Croydon,Coulsdon,England,"Investigation focused on impacts from the ephemeral Merstham (Coulsdon) Bourne at the open ditch section near Coulsdon South Station where the bourne flows adjacent to the railway and rear gardens; the event involved overland/ephemeral watercourse flow into the surface water drainage network affecting this area. (p10)
Localised flooding occurred in the Coulsdon area where the Merstham Bourne emerges from a damaged culvert; floodwaters affected residential gardens adjacent to Network Rail land and created a local flood risk that required monitoring by the Environment Agency because recent ground–floor flats nearby could be threatened. [IMG,MUL] (p16)
Flooding affected gardens in residential areas (water up to 30 cm in gardens), coming very close to properties and posing a serious potential risk; residential buildings were not entered by floodwater. (p21)",gardens flooded to depths up to 30 cm; 0 residential properties flooded (p21),GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01.html,GB-CRY_2_2014-02-01,Croydon flooding February 2014 - March 2014,Croydon Flood Investigations January - March 2014: Merstham Bourne Flood Investigation: Final report,030/Croydon/030/2015/Croydon/Merstham Bourne S19 Flood Investigation Report – February 2015.pdf
GB-CAM,11,2014-08-08,52.56213480587854,0.07786022820345838,"<div id=""html_ffd0a4e7ec718afe2c29a8bb1446b36b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Beetroot Factory</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The drainage ditch alongside the footpath next to the beetroot factory was silted and full of rubbish, which is thought to have exacerbated flooding; CCC cleared this ditch as a one&ndash;off gesture of goodwill after the flood. [TAB] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The open drainage ditch alongside the footpath next to the beetroot factory was silted and full of rubbish and suffered blockages (including flytipping) that exacerbated local flooding during the 2014 event. The ditch was cleared by CCC as a one&ndash;off; Anglian Water and others cleared systems that outfall into this ditch. [TAB,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Anglian Water system outfalls into a drainage ditch alongside the footpath next to the Beetroot Factory; the ditch was silted and full of rubbish and was cleared by Cambridgeshire County Council, while Anglian Water cleared the connected system to improve the situation. (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Beetroot Factory,England,"The drainage ditch alongside the footpath next to the beetroot factory was silted and full of rubbish, which is thought to have exacerbated flooding; CCC cleared this ditch as a one–off gesture of goodwill after the flood. [TAB] (p6)
The open drainage ditch alongside the footpath next to the beetroot factory was silted and full of rubbish and suffered blockages (including flytipping) that exacerbated local flooding during the 2014 event. The ditch was cleared by CCC as a one–off; Anglian Water and others cleared systems that outfall into this ditch. [TAB,PAG] (p7)
The Anglian Water system outfalls into a drainage ditch alongside the footpath next to the Beetroot Factory; the ditch was silted and full of rubbish and was cleared by Cambridgeshire County Council, while Anglian Water cleared the connected system to improve the situation. (p8)",,GB-CAM_11_2014-08-08.html,GB-CAM_11_2014-08-08,March flooding 08 Aug 2014 - 09 Aug 2014,Flood Investigation Report: March,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2019/Cambridgeshire County/March-flood-investigation-2019-v2.pdf
GB-CAM,11,2014-08-08,52.5596511,0.0806259,"<div id=""html_bba0ae9e705c48a98be2797ae1e84a79"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Norwood Road, March</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>External property flooding. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and sewer surcharge caused flooding to the road and adjacent properties; highways gullies were jetted and gully maintenance frequency increased as a response. [TAB] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff and an overwhelmed surface water sewer during the August 2014 extreme rainfall led to local flooding and runoff issues on and around Norwood Road. Gullies were jetted and their maintenance frequency increased; a non&ndash;return valve was fitted to the SW sewer but capacity limits meant the system could still become overwhelmed. Blockages in the open ditch alongside the footpath (near the beetroot factory) and flytipping aggravated the flooding; remedial cleansing to the outfall ditch was undertaken. [TAB,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Linked to flood incident reports FI/14/006 (2014) and referenced in the report as part of the 2014 flooding investigations; actions by LLFA/Highways were taken in response to these incidents. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>depths reported by residents vary from 2 inches internally to 12 inches externally (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2&#8220; internally; 12&#8221; externally [TAB] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Norwood Road, March",England,"External property flooding. (p5)
Surface water runoff and sewer surcharge caused flooding to the road and adjacent properties; highways gullies were jetted and gully maintenance frequency increased as a response. [TAB] (p6)
