GB-IOW,4,2023-10-25,50.695008,-1.3045189,"<div id=""html_a489302a08377cbbcca557cf3736036e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Carisbrooke Road, Newport</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rear gardens backing onto the Lukely Brook were flooded as the river channel was exceeded; combined river flooding, drain blockage and foul sewer overflows were reported by residents, leading to internal flooding of properties. Properties are identified as being within Flood Zone 3. [MUL] (p48)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 9 properties [MUL] (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,"Carisbrooke Road, Newport",England,"Rear gardens backing onto the Lukely Brook were flooded as the river channel was exceeded; combined river flooding, drain blockage and foul sewer overflows were reported by residents, leading to internal flooding of properties. Properties are identified as being within Flood Zone 3. [MUL] (p48)",at least 9 properties [MUL] (p48),GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25,Newport flooding 25 Oct 2023,Newport Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation report Newport September 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,4,2023-10-25,50.7112214,-1.3116596,"<div id=""html_b821dd12dd6d92d4cc6f01f7b5843be9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Crossways, Newport</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Intense rainfall produced surface water runoff flowing down Crossways (downhill from west to east), contributing to local surface&ndash;water flooding and runoff&ndash;driven inundation. (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,"Crossways, Newport",England,"Intense rainfall produced surface water runoff flowing down Crossways (downhill from west to east), contributing to local surface–water flooding and runoff–driven inundation. (p58)",,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25,Newport flooding 25 Oct 2023,Newport Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation report Newport September 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,4,2023-10-25,50.7093665,-1.3052402,"<div id=""html_05ee396198f1af79c7aaf74df4576233"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Sherwood Road, Newport</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on the east of Sherwood Road experienced impacts from surface water runoff during the intense rainfall, contributing to local inundation risk. [PAG] (p58)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,"Sherwood Road, Newport",England,"Properties on the east of Sherwood Road experienced impacts from surface water runoff during the intense rainfall, contributing to local inundation risk. [PAG] (p58)",,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25,Newport flooding 25 Oct 2023,Newport Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation report Newport September 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,4,2023-10-25,50.67108245,-1.3328042802764226,"<div id=""html_62f458baf66c38fe147b0cb25b9a4fd3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Isle of Wight</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of the Isle of Wight (notably the Newport area) have concentrated flood risk along the River Medina and its tributaries, with much of the river corridor in Flood Zone 3. Reported internal flood incidents (Oct 2023) in the Newport study area cluster near these mapped flood zones; tidal locking is noted as a mechanism that can exacerbate upstream fluvial flooding. [IMG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The study area is on the Isle of Wight and the Newport area experienced flooding in October 2023 (community volunteers responded). Parts of the Isle of Wight (including Newport and areas around main rivers/brooks) are therefore affected by river and surface water flood risk and are covered by EA flood warning areas. [IMG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>River and surface&ndash;water flooding affected locations on the island (Lukely Brook, River Medina, Carisbrooke, Hunny Hill, Gunville), triggering flood warnings, emergency calls and on&ndash;the&ndash;ground actions to reduce flood risk (debris screen clearance, removal of blockages, sandbag distribution, sluice operation). [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The 25 October 2023 surface water flood event affected locations within the Isle of Wight, notably Newport where internal property flooding was recorded and mapped by JBA. [IMG] (p38)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Gunville: 22 reported internal incidents; Alvington Manor View: 5 reported internal incidents; Carisbrooke High Street: 9 reported internal incidents [IMG] (p38)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Isle of Wight,England,"Parts of the Isle of Wight (notably the Newport area) have concentrated flood risk along the River Medina and its tributaries, with much of the river corridor in Flood Zone 3. Reported internal flood incidents (Oct 2023) in the Newport study area cluster near these mapped flood zones; tidal locking is noted as a mechanism that can exacerbate upstream fluvial flooding. [IMG] (p18)
The study area is on the Isle of Wight and the Newport area experienced flooding in October 2023 (community volunteers responded). Parts of the Isle of Wight (including Newport and areas around main rivers/brooks) are therefore affected by river and surface water flood risk and are covered by EA flood warning areas. [IMG] (p28)
River and surface–water flooding affected locations on the island (Lukely Brook, River Medina, Carisbrooke, Hunny Hill, Gunville), triggering flood warnings, emergency calls and on–the–ground actions to reduce flood risk (debris screen clearance, removal of blockages, sandbag distribution, sluice operation). [TAB] (p35)
The 25 October 2023 surface water flood event affected locations within the Isle of Wight, notably Newport where internal property flooding was recorded and mapped by JBA. [IMG] (p38)",Gunville: 22 reported internal incidents; Alvington Manor View: 5 reported internal incidents; Carisbrooke High Street: 9 reported internal incidents [IMG] (p38),GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25,Newport flooding 25 Oct 2023,Newport Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation report Newport September 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,4,2023-10-25,50.6913105,-1.3163555978208235,"<div id=""html_596d2c8c20ce4f345e12bf1387ac0898"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Newport</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties in the town caused by a combined impact of river (fluvial), surface water and sewer sources; steep topography and heavy urbanisation exacerbated runoff and flooding. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred across Newport driven by a combined impact of fluvial (river), surface water and sewer sources; steep topography and heavy urbanisation contributed to the flooding during the event. (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report includes multiple flood maps and data for Newport (Environment Agency Flood Map for Planning; Risk of Flooding from Surface Water maps; JBA groundwater flood map; Environment Agency historic flood map) and a line graph of tide and river levels, indicating Newport is exposed to surface&ndash;water, groundwater, river and tidal/coastal flooding and is the focus of flood risk assessment and warning/alert areas. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported internal property flooding across the town (heatmap of reported internal property flooding is referenced). (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties in the town, causing internal inundation of homes. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties within the town. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood risk is concentrated along the course of the River Medina and its tributaries through central Newport (most of which lies in Flood Zone 3). Reported internal flood incidents (as of Oct 2023) cluster near these flood zones. Tidal influence (tidal locking) can inhibit river discharge at high tide and cause river levels to rise upstream, increasing fluvial flooding risk in Newport. [IMG,PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Study area intersected by multiple watercourses and covered by EA Flood Alert/Warning areas. The text records flooding in October 2023 when community volunteers cleared and unblocked drains and cleared roads in Newport. [IMG,PAG] (p27)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Newport was subject to flooding during the October 2023 event; community volunteers helped to unblock drains and clear roads during that flooding. The Newport study area is covered by Environment Agency flood alert and flood warning areas linked to nearby watercourses. [IMG,PAG] (p28)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A storm on 25 October 2023 produced heavy rainfall and prompted a fluvial flow estimate for the event; gauged data were used to assess river/flow response affecting Newport. [PAG] (p30)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 25 October 2023 peak river levels coincided with a high tide (tidal locking) in downstream areas of Newport, raising river levels and likely obstructing sewer outfalls. Tide locking and elevated river levels exacerbated flooding impacts across Newport; internal property flooding is reported in the town (see heatmap and photographs referenced). [IMG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gunville area (within Newport) generated multiple flood rescue calls; sandbags were made available at a Newport car park (Lugley Street); Local Authority set up a hub for help, guidance and information at Ambassadors Snooker Hall in Gunville (Newport) on 29 October as part of the recovery. [TAB,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread flooding across the town on 25 October 2023, including multiple properties experiencing internal flooding; heatmap and source&ndash;pathway&ndash;receptor mapping used to analyse the event. [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Widespread internal property flooding across Newport during the 25 October 2023 event. JBA&#8217;s heatmap and community surveys identified dense clusters of internal flooding requiring source&ndash;pathway&ndash;receptor analysis, and localised incidents elsewhere in the town (see examples below). [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from fields west of Forest Hills and insufficient drainage produced overland flow that inundated roads and gardens in the Gunville area. The Gunville Stream became fast flowing and exceeded channel capacity, causing localised river flooding and internal flooding of properties backing onto the stream (Ash Lane). Roads and junctions were inundated (notably Broadwood Lane, Forest Hills, Arthur Moody Drive / Park Close area); rear gardens were flooded and sandbags were used on Forest Hills. Site visit on 27 February 2024 found Ash Lane properties remaining uninhabited due to flood water damage and large electrical appliances showing clear flood damage. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report maps internal property flooding in the Newport study area (heatmap) with incidents concentrated near the watercourse and Carisbrooke Mill Pond; flood flows from local rivers and surface water pathways contributed to internal flooding of properties during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p50)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>At least 56 residential and commercial properties experienced internal flooding; foul sewage was reported entering gardens; drainage systems were overwhelmed causing high surface runoff; river channel breaches contributed to flooding and widespread property damage and impacts on residents' mental health. (p86)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred in Newport on 25th October 2023, prompting a Section 19 investigation and a package of recommended measures (improved flood zone and watercourse mapping, a community flood plan and training, SuDS and enhanced asset monitoring) to reduce future flood risk. [TAB,PAG] (p87)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 56 properties [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>56 properties [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 56 properties [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Four calls received between 05:15 and 06:02 [TAB,MUL] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 reported internal incidents in Gunville; 5 reported internal incidents in Alvington Manor View; 9 reported internal incidents in Carisbrooke High Street [PAG] (p37)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>Gunville: 22 reported internal incidents; Alvington Manor View: 5 reported internal incidents; Carisbrooke High Street: 9 reported internal incidents [IMG,PAG] (p38)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least 5 properties [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>56 residential and commercial properties (p86)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Newport,England,"Internal flooding of residential properties in the town caused by a combined impact of river (fluvial), surface water and sewer sources; steep topography and heavy urbanisation exacerbated runoff and flooding. [PAG] (p4)
Flooding occurred across Newport driven by a combined impact of fluvial (river), surface water and sewer sources; steep topography and heavy urbanisation contributed to the flooding during the event. (p5)
The report includes multiple flood maps and data for Newport (Environment Agency Flood Map for Planning; Risk of Flooding from Surface Water maps; JBA groundwater flood map; Environment Agency historic flood map) and a line graph of tide and river levels, indicating Newport is exposed to surface–water, groundwater, river and tidal/coastal flooding and is the focus of flood risk assessment and warning/alert areas. [PAG] (p7)
Reported internal property flooding across the town (heatmap of reported internal property flooding is referenced). (p8)
Internal flooding of residential properties in the town, causing internal inundation of homes. [PAG] (p11)
Internal flooding of residential properties within the town. [PAG] (p12)
Flood risk is concentrated along the course of the River Medina and its tributaries through central Newport (most of which lies in Flood Zone 3). Reported internal flood incidents (as of Oct 2023) cluster near these flood zones. Tidal influence (tidal locking) can inhibit river discharge at high tide and cause river levels to rise upstream, increasing fluvial flooding risk in Newport. [IMG,PAG] (p18)
Study area intersected by multiple watercourses and covered by EA Flood Alert/Warning areas. The text records flooding in October 2023 when community volunteers cleared and unblocked drains and cleared roads in Newport. [IMG,PAG] (p27)
Newport was subject to flooding during the October 2023 event; community volunteers helped to unblock drains and clear roads during that flooding. The Newport study area is covered by Environment Agency flood alert and flood warning areas linked to nearby watercourses. [IMG,PAG] (p28)
A storm on 25 October 2023 produced heavy rainfall and prompted a fluvial flow estimate for the event; gauged data were used to assess river/flow response affecting Newport. [PAG] (p30)
On 25 October 2023 peak river levels coincided with a high tide (tidal locking) in downstream areas of Newport, raising river levels and likely obstructing sewer outfalls. Tide locking and elevated river levels exacerbated flooding impacts across Newport; internal property flooding is reported in the town (see heatmap and photographs referenced). [IMG] (p33)
Gunville area (within Newport) generated multiple flood rescue calls; sandbags were made available at a Newport car park (Lugley Street); Local Authority set up a hub for help, guidance and information at Ambassadors Snooker Hall in Gunville (Newport) on 29 October as part of the recovery. [TAB,MUL] (p35)
Widespread flooding across the town on 25 October 2023, including multiple properties experiencing internal flooding; heatmap and source–pathway–receptor mapping used to analyse the event. [PAG] (p37)
Widespread internal property flooding across Newport during the 25 October 2023 event. JBA’s heatmap and community surveys identified dense clusters of internal flooding requiring source–pathway–receptor analysis, and localised incidents elsewhere in the town (see examples below). [IMG,PAG] (p38)
Surface water runoff from fields west of Forest Hills and insufficient drainage produced overland flow that inundated roads and gardens in the Gunville area. The Gunville Stream became fast flowing and exceeded channel capacity, causing localised river flooding and internal flooding of properties backing onto the stream (Ash Lane). Roads and junctions were inundated (notably Broadwood Lane, Forest Hills, Arthur Moody Drive / Park Close area); rear gardens were flooded and sandbags were used on Forest Hills. Site visit on 27 February 2024 found Ash Lane properties remaining uninhabited due to flood water damage and large electrical appliances showing clear flood damage. [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p41)
The report maps internal property flooding in the Newport study area (heatmap) with incidents concentrated near the watercourse and Carisbrooke Mill Pond; flood flows from local rivers and surface water pathways contributed to internal flooding of properties during the event. [PAG,IMG] (p50)
At least 56 residential and commercial properties experienced internal flooding; foul sewage was reported entering gardens; drainage systems were overwhelmed causing high surface runoff; river channel breaches contributed to flooding and widespread property damage and impacts on residents' mental health. (p86)
Flooding occurred in Newport on 25th October 2023, prompting a Section 19 investigation and a package of recommended measures (improved flood zone and watercourse mapping, a community flood plan and training, SuDS and enhanced asset monitoring) to reduce future flood risk. [TAB,PAG] (p87)","at least 56 properties [PAG] (p4)
56 properties [PAG] (p11)
at least 56 properties [PAG] (p12)
Four calls received between 05:15 and 06:02 [TAB,MUL] (p35)
22 reported internal incidents in Gunville; 5 reported internal incidents in Alvington Manor View; 9 reported internal incidents in Carisbrooke High Street [PAG] (p37)
Gunville: 22 reported internal incidents; Alvington Manor View: 5 reported internal incidents; Carisbrooke High Street: 9 reported internal incidents [IMG,PAG] (p38)
at least 5 properties [PAG,IMG,MUL] (p41)
56 residential and commercial properties (p86)",GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25,Newport flooding 25 Oct 2023,Newport Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation report Newport September 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,4,2023-10-25,50.6978776,-1.3223774,"<div id=""html_478d175a72b630c85b920466f9486727"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Gunville</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Localised street and garden flooding in the Gunville study area (source&ndash;pathway&ndash;receptor mapping for Gunville and multiple photographs in the Gunville area are referenced). [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding in the Gunville area generated emergency calls and required flood rescue response; a local recovery hub was later established in Gunville (Ambassadors Snooker Hall). [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Streets and properties within Gunville (including Broadwood Lane, Ash Lane and Forest Hills) were affected by surface water runoff and localised river flooding during the event, causing road inundation and internal property flooding. (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from fields west of Forest Hills produced overland flows across the Gunville area; water pooled between Arthur Moody Drive and Park Close and at the junction of Broadwood Lane and Forest Hills, inundating parts of Broadwood Lane. The Gunville Stream was fast&ndash;flowing and exceeded channel capacity on 25 October, increasing local flood levels. The land adjacent to the Gunville Stream is in Flood Zone 3, indicating a high probability of river flooding during intense rainfall. [PAG,IMG] (p41)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gunville experienced flooding linked to surface water runoff from adjacent undeveloped land and limited sewer network capacity; measures such as retrofitting SuDS (basins, swales), investigating culvert capacity/condition and other drainage improvements are recommended to mitigate surface water flood risk. (p81)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The report found fluvial (river) flooding to be a leading source of flooding in parts of Gunville; some properties are at risk from river&ndash;related flooding. (p83)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 calls [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least five properties (p39)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least five properties [PAG,IMG] (p41)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Gunville,England,"Localised street and garden flooding in the Gunville study area (source–pathway–receptor mapping for Gunville and multiple photographs in the Gunville area are referenced). [PAG] (p8)
Flooding in the Gunville area generated emergency calls and required flood rescue response; a local recovery hub was later established in Gunville (Ambassadors Snooker Hall). [TAB] (p35)
Streets and properties within Gunville (including Broadwood Lane, Ash Lane and Forest Hills) were affected by surface water runoff and localised river flooding during the event, causing road inundation and internal property flooding. (p39)
Surface water runoff from fields west of Forest Hills produced overland flows across the Gunville area; water pooled between Arthur Moody Drive and Park Close and at the junction of Broadwood Lane and Forest Hills, inundating parts of Broadwood Lane. The Gunville Stream was fast–flowing and exceeded channel capacity on 25 October, increasing local flood levels. The land adjacent to the Gunville Stream is in Flood Zone 3, indicating a high probability of river flooding during intense rainfall. [PAG,IMG] (p41)
Gunville experienced flooding linked to surface water runoff from adjacent undeveloped land and limited sewer network capacity; measures such as retrofitting SuDS (basins, swales), investigating culvert capacity/condition and other drainage improvements are recommended to mitigate surface water flood risk. (p81)
The report found fluvial (river) flooding to be a leading source of flooding in parts of Gunville; some properties are at risk from river–related flooding. (p83)","4 calls [TAB] (p35)
at least five properties (p39)
at least five properties [PAG,IMG] (p41)",GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25,Newport flooding 25 Oct 2023,Newport Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation report Newport September 2024.pdf
GB-IOW,4,2023-10-25,50.6911525,-1.3128845,"<div id=""html_53cc480f5c581fdc8ca3026ed1faaf97"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Carisbrooke</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Carisbrooke catchment responded rapidly to the storm. The Carisbrooke Mill gauge (within Carisbrooke area) recorded a high fluvial peak on 25 October 2023; fluvial flow for the event was estimated between a 1 in 20 and 1 in 50&ndash;year event. [TAB,PAG] (p33)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Received a Flood Warning on 25 October 2023 (Carisbrooke on the Lukely Brook at 05:13) and was subject to the resulting local response (sandbags made available from Carisbrooke High Street Car Park; stock reported insufficient). [PAG] (p34)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood rescue operations were carried out in Carisbrooke with multiple calls for assistance (Castle Street and Carisbrooke High Street). A Flood Warning was also issued covering Carisbrooke. [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Local residents and properties in the Carisbrooke area experienced fluvial flooding during the event, with significant negative impacts on wellbeing and properties. (p48)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flooding from the Lukely Brook exceeded the channel and fords (Spring Lane and Castle Street), causing water to enter rear gardens and flow into properties on Carisbrooke Road and nearby streets. Flooding was driven by channel exceedance combined with blocked drains and foul sewer overflows; surface runoff from High Street also contributed. High water velocities at the Castle Street ford overtopped a flood barrier and caused the Lukely Brook to flow out of bank towards nearby properties. Water ingress affected property interiors and electrical circuits, leaving some residents unable to use electricity until properties dried. [IMG,MUL] (p50)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 call at Castle Street (07:40); 3 calls at Carisbrooke High Street (09:06&ndash;10:53) [TAB] (p35)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>at least nine properties experienced internal flooding (p48)</p></div></div></body></div>",Isle of Wight,Carisbrooke,England,"The Carisbrooke catchment responded rapidly to the storm. The Carisbrooke Mill gauge (within Carisbrooke area) recorded a high fluvial peak on 25 October 2023; fluvial flow for the event was estimated between a 1 in 20 and 1 in 50–year event. [TAB,PAG] (p33)
Received a Flood Warning on 25 October 2023 (Carisbrooke on the Lukely Brook at 05:13) and was subject to the resulting local response (sandbags made available from Carisbrooke High Street Car Park; stock reported insufficient). [PAG] (p34)
Flood rescue operations were carried out in Carisbrooke with multiple calls for assistance (Castle Street and Carisbrooke High Street). A Flood Warning was also issued covering Carisbrooke. [TAB] (p35)
Local residents and properties in the Carisbrooke area experienced fluvial flooding during the event, with significant negative impacts on wellbeing and properties. (p48)
Fluvial flooding from the Lukely Brook exceeded the channel and fords (Spring Lane and Castle Street), causing water to enter rear gardens and flow into properties on Carisbrooke Road and nearby streets. Flooding was driven by channel exceedance combined with blocked drains and foul sewer overflows; surface runoff from High Street also contributed. High water velocities at the Castle Street ford overtopped a flood barrier and caused the Lukely Brook to flow out of bank towards nearby properties. Water ingress affected property interiors and electrical circuits, leaving some residents unable to use electricity until properties dried. [IMG,MUL] (p50)","1 call at Castle Street (07:40); 3 calls at Carisbrooke High Street (09:06–10:53) [TAB] (p35)
at least nine properties experienced internal flooding (p48)",GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25.html,GB-IOW_4_2023-10-25,Newport flooding 25 Oct 2023,Newport Section 19 Flood Investigation,059/Isle of Wight/Section 19 flood investigation report Newport September 2024.pdf
GB-LAN,10,2015-12-26,53.77336385418381,-2.7948849448514395,"<div id=""html_4f20229f9aef59ce4be4ee35adc09d45"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Mary's RC Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>St Mary's RC Primary School suffered internal flooding (floodwater entered the building). (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (the school) (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,St Mary's RC Primary School,England,St Mary's RC Primary School suffered internal flooding (floodwater entered the building). (p12),1 property (the school) (p12),GB-LAN_10_2015-12-26.html,GB-LAN_10_2015-12-26,Preston flooding 26 Dec 2015,Preston District Flood Report Recommended Actions,069/Lancashire County/preston-final-report.pdf
GB-LAN,10,2015-12-26,53.752895,-2.6995215,"<div id=""html_56018bfb809e398e43ba8e16b5cf8ec3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Avenham Park</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Nearby public conveniences were submerged to half the height of the building and the external children's play area was submerged. (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Avenham Park,England,Nearby public conveniences were submerged to half the height of the building and the external children's play area was submerged. (p13),1 property (p13),GB-LAN_10_2015-12-26.html,GB-LAN_10_2015-12-26,Preston flooding 26 Dec 2015,Preston District Flood Report Recommended Actions,069/Lancashire County/preston-final-report.pdf
