GB-LAN,1,2015-12-01,53.763842666379,-2.202641672904807,"<div id=""html_d134ea258f057d8d86289571901c70bb"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cliviger</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in the community. (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Cliviger,England,Internal flooding of properties in the community. (p23),8 properties (p23),GB-LAN_1_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_1_2015-12-01,Burnley District flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,Burnley District Flood Report: Recommended Action,069/Lancashire County/burnley-final-report.pdf
GB-LAN,1,2015-12-01,53.7707997,-2.1928663,"<div id=""html_5b4abe27576e32d60bc21e52fe4fd7c5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mereclough</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties in the community (same location referenced as Cliviger (Mereclough)). (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties (p23)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Mereclough,England,Internal flooding of properties in the community (same location referenced as Cliviger (Mereclough)). (p23),8 properties (p23),GB-LAN_1_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_1_2015-12-01,Burnley District flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,Burnley District Flood Report: Recommended Action,069/Lancashire County/burnley-final-report.pdf
GB-LAN,1,2015-12-01,53.79974561874378,-2.3119807895673565,"<div id=""html_9be7c418e2a5021bfec98e59ca3d494d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Padiham</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties across the town; critical infrastructure was affected (police station, fire station, medical centre and a hospice). [TAB] (p24)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>147 properties [TAB] (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,Padiham,England,"Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties across the town; critical infrastructure was affected (police station, fire station, medical centre and a hospice). [TAB] (p24)",147 properties [TAB] (p24),GB-LAN_1_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_1_2015-12-01,Burnley District flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,Burnley District Flood Report: Recommended Action,069/Lancashire County/burnley-final-report.pdf
GB-HEF,27,2020-02-16,52.034241,-2.6094462,"<div id=""html_edd416fd2fe61235245aa0f667626551"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Pentaloe Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Impacted by high River Wye levels which cause the brook to spill out of its channel and flood Woolhope Road; properties located directly adjacent to the brook were recorded as internally flooded during the February 2020 event. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties internally flooded (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Pentaloe Brook,England,Impacted by high River Wye levels which cause the brook to spill out of its channel and flood Woolhope Road; properties located directly adjacent to the brook were recorded as internally flooded during the February 2020 event. (p2),4 properties internally flooded (p2),GB-HEF_27_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_27_2020-02-16,Mordiford flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Mordiford,055/Herefordshire County/26/Mordiford.pdf
GB-HEF,27,2020-02-16,52.2355219,-2.7449348,"<div id=""html_61c3cfa2e1ddbf45d435a25ae4dedc92"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Lugg</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the connected catchment (Penteloe Brook discharges into the River Lugg in the village); the catchment&ndash;level fluvial flooding contributed to internal flooding of properties in Mordiford. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,River Lugg,England,Part of the connected catchment (Penteloe Brook discharges into the River Lugg in the village); the catchment–level fluvial flooding contributed to internal flooding of properties in Mordiford. (p2),4 properties (p2),GB-HEF_27_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_27_2020-02-16,Mordiford flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Mordiford,055/Herefordshire County/26/Mordiford.pdf
GB-HEF,27,2020-02-16,52.0329935,-2.6275176,"<div id=""html_4eafd1ea0ef9b0f5d926ad968e55cabf"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Mordiford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The village core (centred on Mordiford) is shown on the maps as being within Flood Zone 3 (highest probability) along the main river corridor. Flood defence lines and areas &#8216;benefiting from flood defences&#8217; are shown running along the river through the village, with the school and much of the village centre located within or adjacent to defended areas. The mapping identifies the river as the primary source of flood risk affecting the village and local infrastructure (e.g. The Mill, Garlands Farm vicinity). [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Four properties recorded internal flooding during February 2020; the affected properties are in or adjacent to Flood Zone 3 and are located directly adjacent to the Pentaloe/Penteloe Brook. Some low&ndash;threshold properties in the locality are at risk during flash floods. Additionally, six listed properties/structures are located within the Flood Zone. (p2)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 internally flooded properties; 6 listed properties/structures located within the Flood Zone (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Herefordshire,Mordiford,England,"The village core (centred on Mordiford) is shown on the maps as being within Flood Zone 3 (highest probability) along the main river corridor. Flood defence lines and areas ‘benefiting from flood defences’ are shown running along the river through the village, with the school and much of the village centre located within or adjacent to defended areas. The mapping identifies the river as the primary source of flood risk affecting the village and local infrastructure (e.g. The Mill, Garlands Farm vicinity). [IMG] (p1)
Four properties recorded internal flooding during February 2020; the affected properties are in or adjacent to Flood Zone 3 and are located directly adjacent to the Pentaloe/Penteloe Brook. Some low–threshold properties in the locality are at risk during flash floods. Additionally, six listed properties/structures are located within the Flood Zone. (p2)",4 internally flooded properties; 6 listed properties/structures located within the Flood Zone (p2),GB-HEF_27_2020-02-16.html,GB-HEF_27_2020-02-16,Mordiford flooding 16 Feb 2020,Herefordshire 2019/20 Section 19 Report: Mordiford,055/Herefordshire County/26/Mordiford.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.2645462,-1.1842435,"<div id=""html_e3b7d759d147a54d4ec13125656d3749"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Millwood Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Millwood Brook flows into the River Poulter through Creswell and experienced elevated flows as part of the Idle and Torne sub&ndash;catchment response during Storm Babet, contributing to increased downstream river flows. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Millwood Brook,England,"Millwood Brook flows into the River Poulter through Creswell and experienced elevated flows as part of the Idle and Torne sub–catchment response during Storm Babet, contributing to increased downstream river flows. (p1)",,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.1707398,-1.2716381,"<div id=""html_a2c983f2c28b025ab0e6053f81d20701"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Merril Sick</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Merril Sick flows into the River Meden near Pleasley and formed part of the catchment response during Storm Babet, contributing to elevated flows in the River Meden in that area. (p1)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Merril Sick,England,"Merril Sick flows into the River Meden near Pleasley and formed part of the catchment response during Storm Babet, contributing to elevated flows in the River Meden in that area. (p1)",,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.2445377,-1.1109569,"<div id=""html_b52f1aca87e82ff7b5456099397a162e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Poulter</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of the Idle and Torne sub&ndash;catchment hydrology described for Storm Babet; included in the report's hydrological analysis (headwaters and flows through the sub&ndash;catchment were considered). (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Recorded a peak flow on 20 Oct 2023 (about 18 hours after peak rainfall) and produced one of the highest ranked flows/levels on record, contributing to flooding in nearby communities such as Langwith. (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Poulter,England,"Part of the Idle and Torne sub–catchment hydrology described for Storm Babet; included in the report's hydrological analysis (headwaters and flows through the sub–catchment were considered). (p1)
Recorded a peak flow on 20 Oct 2023 (about 18 hours after peak rainfall) and produced one of the highest ranked flows/levels on record, contributing to flooding in nearby communities such as Langwith. (p3)",,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.396828,-0.9397523,"<div id=""html_da14d7591a2fbfd2f680ea12f530aa04"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Idle</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Idle sub&ndash;catchment experienced heavy rainfall during 19&ndash;21 Oct 2023, producing the highest ranked flows/levels on record at surrounding stations and contributing to flooding in nearby communities (including internal flooding of properties in Clowne). (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 residential properties; 1 commercial property (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,River Idle,England,"The Idle sub–catchment experienced heavy rainfall during 19–21 Oct 2023, producing the highest ranked flows/levels on record at surrounding stations and contributing to flooding in nearby communities (including internal flooding of properties in Clowne). (p3)",17 residential properties; 1 commercial property (p3),GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.2771274,-1.2599185,"<div id=""html_fff369093e5baa12b20fb600a7e6fedd"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road, Clowne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The watercourse diverts beneath Station Road via a 600mm culvert and flows adjacent to properties before diverting under Station Road. Residential properties adjacent to Station Road are within the part of the community where internal flooding occurred; culvert blockage/overtopping at these culverted sections is described as exacerbating flooding and therefore may have affected properties adjacent to Station Road. [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential properties on Station Road during Storm Babet; properties were inundated by surface water causing internal property damage. [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Station Road experienced internal flooding caused primarily by the adjacent ordinary watercourse (culvert under Station Road may have been overwhelmed). Flood depths caused garden walls at properties along Station Road to collapse. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater depth caused garden walls at properties along Station Road to collapse; floodwater overwhelmed sandbags and entered properties. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 homes and 1 commercial property (grouped across Station Road, Creswell Road and Hickinwood Lane) [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 homes and 1 commercial property (across Station Road, A616 and Hickingwood Lane) [IMG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"Station Road, Clowne",England,"The watercourse diverts beneath Station Road via a 600mm culvert and flows adjacent to properties before diverting under Station Road. Residential properties adjacent to Station Road are within the part of the community where internal flooding occurred; culvert blockage/overtopping at these culverted sections is described as exacerbating flooding and therefore may have affected properties adjacent to Station Road. [PAG,IMG] (p4)
Internal flooding of residential properties on Station Road during Storm Babet; properties were inundated by surface water causing internal property damage. [IMG,PAG] (p5)
Properties on Station Road experienced internal flooding caused primarily by the adjacent ordinary watercourse (culvert under Station Road may have been overwhelmed). Flood depths caused garden walls at properties along Station Road to collapse. [IMG] (p6)
Floodwater depth caused garden walls at properties along Station Road to collapse; floodwater overwhelmed sandbags and entered properties. [PAG] (p7)","17 homes and 1 commercial property (grouped across Station Road, Creswell Road and Hickinwood Lane) [IMG,PAG] (p5)
17 homes and 1 commercial property (across Station Road, A616 and Hickingwood Lane) [IMG] (p6)",GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.2813791,-1.2491307,"<div id=""html_0f24d24a4d677e523f3104f5d4a7c62b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hickingwood Lane, Clowne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Homes adjacent to Hickingwood Lane are described as forming part of the mainly residential community where internal flooding occurred. It is plausible that some properties adjacent to Hickingwood Lane were flooded during Storm Babet, but the report does not explicitly enumerate flooded properties by street. [PAG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Hickinwood Lane flooded internally during the Storm Babet incident; these properties are included among the seventeen homes and one commercial property reported to have flooded internally. [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Hickingwood Lane experienced internal flooding. Surface water runoff from saturated ground north of Hickingwood Lane flowed towards the ordinary watercourse, increasing levels and contributing to flooding. (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>part of 17 homes and 1 commercial property [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 homes and 1 commercial property (across Station Road, A616 and Hickingwood Lane) (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"Hickingwood Lane, Clowne",England,"Homes adjacent to Hickingwood Lane are described as forming part of the mainly residential community where internal flooding occurred. It is plausible that some properties adjacent to Hickingwood Lane were flooded during Storm Babet, but the report does not explicitly enumerate flooded properties by street. [PAG] (p4)
Properties on Hickinwood Lane flooded internally during the Storm Babet incident; these properties are included among the seventeen homes and one commercial property reported to have flooded internally. [IMG] (p5)
Properties on Hickingwood Lane experienced internal flooding. Surface water runoff from saturated ground north of Hickingwood Lane flowed towards the ordinary watercourse, increasing levels and contributing to flooding. (p6)","part of 17 homes and 1 commercial property [IMG] (p5)
17 homes and 1 commercial property (across Station Road, A616 and Hickingwood Lane) (p6)",GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.2779259,-1.2543447,"<div id=""html_5ef8099302b110f5967ce4e168438e75"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Creswell Road, Clowne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties on the A616 (Creswell Road) during Storm Babet; properties were inundated by surface water causing internal property damage. [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 homes and 1 commercial property (grouped across Station Road, Creswell Road and Hickinwood Lane) [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,"Creswell Road, Clowne",England,"Internal flooding of properties on the A616 (Creswell Road) during Storm Babet; properties were inundated by surface water causing internal property damage. [IMG,PAG] (p5)","17 homes and 1 commercial property (grouped across Station Road, Creswell Road and Hickinwood Lane) [IMG,PAG] (p5)",GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.118504,-1.556618,"<div id=""html_aa76cf271b78423962bde0c81b3d8fe3"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Derbyshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Extreme rainfall associated with Storm Babet produced peak river flows/levels (the highest ranked flows or levels on record at surrounding stations) in the Idle and Torne catchment. Communities within Derbyshire were affected; for example, in Clowne 17 residential properties and 1 commercial property suffered internal flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 residential properties; 1 commercial property [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Derbyshire,England,"Extreme rainfall associated with Storm Babet produced peak river flows/levels (the highest ranked flows or levels on record at surrounding stations) in the Idle and Torne catchment. Communities within Derbyshire were affected; for example, in Clowne 17 residential properties and 1 commercial property suffered internal flooding. [PAG,IMG] (p4)","17 residential properties; 1 commercial property [PAG,IMG] (p4)",GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-DBY,6,2023-10-18,53.2746354,-1.2685349,"<div id=""html_0cc345827dbd241960f79096fd8d8947"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Clowne</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties north of the village centre adjacent to an unnamed ordinary watercourse (flows through Harlesthorpe Dam); most flooded properties were east of the watercourse. [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Seventeen residential properties and one commercial property north of the village centre suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet. Flooding was associated with an unnamed ordinary watercourse that flows through Harlesthorpe Dam and through culverted sections in the community; culvert blockage and overtopping of the watercourse are identified as contributory factors. Most flooded properties were east of the watercourse. [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of residential and one commercial property in the Clowne community during Storm Babet. Floodwater affected properties on Station Road, the A616 (Creswell Road) and Hickinwood Lane; flooding was concentrated along the local watercourse and culverted watercourse shown in the surface&ndash;water risk map. [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>In Clowne the ordinary watercourse and associated surface water runoff caused internal flooding of properties (including 17 homes and 1 commercial property across Station Road, A616 and Hickingwood Lane). Culverts under roads may have been overwhelmed and garden walls along Station Road collapsed due to water depth. [IMG] (p6)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 residential properties; 1 commercial property [PAG] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 residential properties; 1 commercial property [PAG,IMG] (p4)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 homes; 1 commercial property [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 homes and 1 commercial property [IMG] (p6)</p></div></div></body></div>",Derbyshire,Clowne,England,"Internal flooding of residential and commercial properties north of the village centre adjacent to an unnamed ordinary watercourse (flows through Harlesthorpe Dam); most flooded properties were east of the watercourse. [PAG] (p3)
Seventeen residential properties and one commercial property north of the village centre suffered internal flooding during Storm Babet. Flooding was associated with an unnamed ordinary watercourse that flows through Harlesthorpe Dam and through culverted sections in the community; culvert blockage and overtopping of the watercourse are identified as contributory factors. Most flooded properties were east of the watercourse. [PAG,IMG] (p4)
Internal flooding of residential and one commercial property in the Clowne community during Storm Babet. Floodwater affected properties on Station Road, the A616 (Creswell Road) and Hickinwood Lane; flooding was concentrated along the local watercourse and culverted watercourse shown in the surface–water risk map. [IMG] (p5)
In Clowne the ordinary watercourse and associated surface water runoff caused internal flooding of properties (including 17 homes and 1 commercial property across Station Road, A616 and Hickingwood Lane). Culverts under roads may have been overwhelmed and garden walls along Station Road collapsed due to water depth. [IMG] (p6)","17 residential properties; 1 commercial property [PAG] (p3)
17 residential properties; 1 commercial property [PAG,IMG] (p4)
17 homes; 1 commercial property [IMG] (p5)
17 homes and 1 commercial property [IMG] (p6)",GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18.html,GB-DBY_6_2023-10-18,Derbyshire flooding 18 Oct 2023 - 21 Oct 2023,Derbyshire County Council Section 19 Investigation Report – Storm Babet 18/10/23 – 21/10/23,034/Derbyshire County/034/2023/Derbyshire County/section-6-idle-and-torne-catchment-final-storm-babet-dcc-section-19-report.pdf
GB-KTT,2,2013-07-01,51.4096275,-0.3062621,"<div id=""html_61e6179a8f9075fa5214ef0eb47e4403"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kingston Upon Thames</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The local authority carried out emergency response to flooding at the underpass and carried out Section 19 investigations into the July 2013 and June 2019 events. [MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Kingston experienced flooding during the investigated incidents, with surface&ndash;water and sewer&ndash;related flooding affecting streets, properties and infrastructure; incidents led to investigation and post&ndash;2013 mitigation measures. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Experienced two flooding incidents in July 2013 which triggered a Section 19 investigation by the local authority. [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Urban flooding occurred at the Kingston underpass within the town, causing local road inundation and traffic disruption on 10 June 2019. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The hydrological catchment and RoFSW mapping show parts of Kingston Upon Thames at risk of surface water flooding; the report states previous flood events in July 2013 were attributed to surface water flooding affecting the area. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A Flood Alert and Flood Guidance Statement on 10 June 2019 identified the Beverly Brook area of Kingston upon Thames as at risk, stating flooding of highways and land was possible and urban areas were at risk, with associated transport disruption predicted. [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at the Kingsgate Road railway bridge/underpass in Kingston led to closure/traffic diversions and prompted the 2013 Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report and subsequent measures (diversions, signage, alarms reviewed). [PAG] (p25)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>two flooding incidents [PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 underpass affected [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Thames,Kingston Upon Thames,England,"The local authority carried out emergency response to flooding at the underpass and carried out Section 19 investigations into the July 2013 and June 2019 events. [MUL] (p3)
Kingston experienced flooding during the investigated incidents, with surface–water and sewer–related flooding affecting streets, properties and infrastructure; incidents led to investigation and post–2013 mitigation measures. [MUL] (p5)
Experienced two flooding incidents in July 2013 which triggered a Section 19 investigation by the local authority. [PAG] (p7)
Urban flooding occurred at the Kingston underpass within the town, causing local road inundation and traffic disruption on 10 June 2019. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
The hydrological catchment and RoFSW mapping show parts of Kingston Upon Thames at risk of surface water flooding; the report states previous flood events in July 2013 were attributed to surface water flooding affecting the area. [IMG] (p13)
A Flood Alert and Flood Guidance Statement on 10 June 2019 identified the Beverly Brook area of Kingston upon Thames as at risk, stating flooding of highways and land was possible and urban areas were at risk, with associated transport disruption predicted. [TAB,PAG] (p19)
Flooding at the Kingsgate Road railway bridge/underpass in Kingston led to closure/traffic diversions and prompted the 2013 Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report and subsequent measures (diversions, signage, alarms reviewed). [PAG] (p25)","two flooding incidents [PAG] (p7)
1 underpass affected [IMG,PAG] (p11)",GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc.html,GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc,Kingston Upon Thames flooding July 2013 and Kingston Upon Thames flooding 10 Jun 2019,"Kingston Underpass Flood Risk Investigation – Addendum to Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report, 2013",065/Kingston upon Thames/Kingston/Underpass/v1.2.pdf
GB-KTT,2,2013-07-01,51.413073592908496,-0.30338557452959425,"<div id=""html_d4e81188cc5074c54d51d8568819a424"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The underpass was flooded during reported events; required emergency response and removal of water (TWUL attended to restart pumps and remove water). [MUL] (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kingston underpass (section of Kingsgate Road beneath the railway bridge) flooded on multiple occasions, affecting a critical transport route. Flooding under the bridge made the highway infrastructure impassable and prompted Section 19 investigations. [IMG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Kingston underpass (A308 carriageway running under the railway) is the subject of the flood incident investigation &mdash; site where road underpass was inundated and investigated as a flood location. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Underpass inundated with deep standing water that covered the road at the lowest point, rendering the underpass impassable; traffic lights and road signage were partially submerged and traffic was disrupted. [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water accumulation affected the underpass; the report links previous July 2013 flood events at the site to surface water flooding (site is within the underpass hydrological catchment shown on the map). [IMG] (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Rainwater ponded in the underpass during the June 2019 intense rainfall; the underpass flooded due to its position at a local topographical low and experienced greater severity of ponding than would be expected from the recorded TBR rainfall totals. [PAG] (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified in the 2013 Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report as being affected during the 2013 flood when the nearby TWUL pumping station failed, contributing to flooding at the railway bridge. (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple flooding reports affected the Kingston underpass and prompted a flood risk investigation; the underpass experienced flooding related to drainage failure requiring response actions. [PAG,MUL] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The underpass was inundated by surface water and sewer&ndash;related flooding. Flooding was exacerbated by proximity to the primary flow path and by failure of the nearby TWUL pumping station; pumps had to be re&ndash;started and water removed. [PAG,MUL] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The FEH (Flood Estimation Handbook) analysis prepared for the Kingston Underpass shows short&ndash;duration extreme rainfall and return&ndash;period estimates used to assess the June 2019 event; the assessment concludes the June 2019 event at the underpass is approximately under a 1&ndash;in&ndash;5&ndash;year return period, indicating a surface&ndash;water flooding impact at the underpass for that event. [TAB] (p26)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 flood incidents (July 2013); 1 flood report (July 2019) [IMG,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 underpass [IMG,PAG] (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Thames,Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge,England,"The underpass was flooded during reported events; required emergency response and removal of water (TWUL attended to restart pumps and remove water). [MUL] (p3)
The Kingston underpass (section of Kingsgate Road beneath the railway bridge) flooded on multiple occasions, affecting a critical transport route. Flooding under the bridge made the highway infrastructure impassable and prompted Section 19 investigations. [IMG,MUL] (p8)
The Kingston underpass (A308 carriageway running under the railway) is the subject of the flood incident investigation — site where road underpass was inundated and investigated as a flood location. [TAB,PAG,MUL] (p9)
Underpass inundated with deep standing water that covered the road at the lowest point, rendering the underpass impassable; traffic lights and road signage were partially submerged and traffic was disrupted. [IMG,PAG] (p11)
Surface water accumulation affected the underpass; the report links previous July 2013 flood events at the site to surface water flooding (site is within the underpass hydrological catchment shown on the map). [IMG] (p13)
Rainwater ponded in the underpass during the June 2019 intense rainfall; the underpass flooded due to its position at a local topographical low and experienced greater severity of ponding than would be expected from the recorded TBR rainfall totals. [PAG] (p18)
Identified in the 2013 Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report as being affected during the 2013 flood when the nearby TWUL pumping station failed, contributing to flooding at the railway bridge. (p21)
Multiple flooding reports affected the Kingston underpass and prompted a flood risk investigation; the underpass experienced flooding related to drainage failure requiring response actions. [PAG,MUL] (p22)
The underpass was inundated by surface water and sewer–related flooding. Flooding was exacerbated by proximity to the primary flow path and by failure of the nearby TWUL pumping station; pumps had to be re–started and water removed. [PAG,MUL] (p23)
The FEH (Flood Estimation Handbook) analysis prepared for the Kingston Underpass shows short–duration extreme rainfall and return–period estimates used to assess the June 2019 event; the assessment concludes the June 2019 event at the underpass is approximately under a 1–in–5–year return period, indicating a surface–water flooding impact at the underpass for that event. [TAB] (p26)","2 flood incidents (July 2013); 1 flood report (July 2019) [IMG,MUL] (p8)
1 underpass [IMG,PAG] (p11)",GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc.html,GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc,Kingston Upon Thames flooding July 2013 and Kingston Upon Thames flooding 10 Jun 2019,"Kingston Underpass Flood Risk Investigation – Addendum to Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report, 2013",065/Kingston upon Thames/Kingston/Underpass/v1.2.pdf
GB-KTT,2,2013-07-01,51.5575525,-0.781404,"<div id=""html_fb1635bc0b0552de92614aa9c6463f12"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Thames</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flood risk from the Thames / local watercourses was identified as a potential contributing source to the flooding in the Kingston area during the investigated incidents. [MUL] (p5)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Thames,River Thames,England,Fluvial (river) flood risk from the Thames / local watercourses was identified as a potential contributing source to the flooding in the Kingston area during the investigated incidents. [MUL] (p5),,GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc.html,GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc,Kingston Upon Thames flooding July 2013 and Kingston Upon Thames flooding 10 Jun 2019,"Kingston Underpass Flood Risk Investigation – Addendum to Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report, 2013",065/Kingston upon Thames/Kingston/Underpass/v1.2.pdf
GB-KTT,2,2013-07-01,51.4142271,-0.3010781,"<div id=""html_cb77d43efc0c79546be4894366a3775e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kingsgate Road, Kingston Upon Thames</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>A section of Kingsgate Road (under the railway bridge) flooded during the reported incidents, affecting the highway and prompting investigation into repeated impacts to critical infrastructure. (p8)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 flooding incidents (July 2013); 1 reported flood (July 2019) (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kingston upon Thames,"Kingsgate Road, Kingston Upon Thames",England,"A section of Kingsgate Road (under the railway bridge) flooded during the reported incidents, affecting the highway and prompting investigation into repeated impacts to critical infrastructure. (p8)",2 flooding incidents (July 2013); 1 reported flood (July 2019) (p8),GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc.html,GB-KTT_2_2013-07-01_etc,Kingston Upon Thames flooding July 2013 and Kingston Upon Thames flooding 10 Jun 2019,"Kingston Underpass Flood Risk Investigation – Addendum to Kingsgate Road Railway Bridge Flood Investigation Report, 2013",065/Kingston upon Thames/Kingston/Underpass/v1.2.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.9192877,1.3015545,"<div id=""html_281b8d97b808badb2503d286dc3de869"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brownshill, Cromer</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage system overload and surcharging, drainage/outfall blockage and surface water run&ndash;off (including water washed off the public highway by vehicles) led to flood water reaching the location and entering properties. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Brownshill was internally flooded. [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Run&ndash;off from intense rainfall was directed into the combined drainage network which was already overloaded; infiltration of surface water into the foul system caused it to surcharge and directed flood water towards properties in Brownshill. Floodwater entered properties internally via toilets and sinks. [TAB,PAG] (p17)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant rainfall was directed into the foul (combined) sewer system causing the foul system to surcharge elsewhere; this surcharging contributed to flooding at properties in Brownshill. (p18)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,"Brownshill, Cromer",England,"Drainage system overload and surcharging, drainage/outfall blockage and surface water run–off (including water washed off the public highway by vehicles) led to flood water reaching the location and entering properties. (p3)
One property on Brownshill was internally flooded. [TAB] (p15)
Run–off from intense rainfall was directed into the combined drainage network which was already overloaded; infiltration of surface water into the foul system caused it to surcharge and directed flood water towards properties in Brownshill. Floodwater entered properties internally via toilets and sinks. [TAB,PAG] (p17)
Significant rainfall was directed into the foul (combined) sewer system causing the foul system to surcharge elsewhere; this surcharging contributed to flooding at properties in Brownshill. (p18)","5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)
1 property [TAB] (p15)",GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.9255383,1.3054149,"<div id=""html_5463b92027f4581bc1d23cb6dfbe39c0"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cromwell Road, Cromer</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage system overload and surcharging, drainage/outfall blockage and surface run&ndash;off (including water washed off the public highway by vehicles) caused flood water to reach the road and enter nearby properties. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Cromwell Road was internally flooded. [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant rainfall was directed into the local ditch/watercourse causing it to surcharge, which contributed to flooding at the affected property on Cromwell Road. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant rainfall directed into the nearby ditch/watercourse caused it to surcharge; the surcharging contributed to flooding at a property on Cromwell Road by allowing water to enter the building via low thresholds at the entrances. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant rainfall was directed into the adjacent ditch/watercourse causing it to surcharge; the surcharging contributed to flooding of a property by entering the building via low thresholds at the entrances. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,"Cromwell Road, Cromer",England,"Drainage system overload and surcharging, drainage/outfall blockage and surface run–off (including water washed off the public highway by vehicles) caused flood water to reach the road and enter nearby properties. (p3)
One property on Cromwell Road was internally flooded. [TAB] (p8)
Significant rainfall was directed into the local ditch/watercourse causing it to surcharge, which contributed to flooding at the affected property on Cromwell Road. [TAB] (p11)
Significant rainfall directed into the nearby ditch/watercourse caused it to surcharge; the surcharging contributed to flooding at a property on Cromwell Road by allowing water to enter the building via low thresholds at the entrances. [PAG] (p12)
Significant rainfall was directed into the adjacent ditch/watercourse causing it to surcharge; the surcharging contributed to flooding of a property by entering the building via low thresholds at the entrances. [IMG] (p13)","5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)
1 property [TAB] (p8)",GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.9305997,1.3012183,"<div id=""html_2d1de68ced24d017df271d75bf57593a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bond Street, Cromer</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding driven by overloaded/surcharging drainage, blocked outfalls and surface run&ndash;off from roads (including vehicle wash&ndash;off) led to flood water reaching the street and properties. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Partial obstruction of the foul drainage system by debris or silt caused failure of the upstream drainage system, contributing to flooding at properties on Bond Street. [IMG] (p22)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,"Bond Street, Cromer",England,"Flooding driven by overloaded/surcharging drainage, blocked outfalls and surface run–off from roads (including vehicle wash–off) led to flood water reaching the street and properties. (p3)
Partial obstruction of the foul drainage system by debris or silt caused failure of the upstream drainage system, contributing to flooding at properties on Bond Street. [IMG] (p22)",5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3),GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.9209943,1.3114299,"<div id=""html_569381bbc1240475b1c9fe7d821f8156"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Station Road, Cromer</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water run&ndash;off and overloaded/surcharging drainage (and associated blockages) resulted in flood water reaching the road and entering properties. (p3)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,"Station Road, Cromer",England,Surface water run–off and overloaded/surcharging drainage (and associated blockages) resulted in flood water reaching the road and entering properties. (p3),5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3),GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.9310174,1.3011496,"<div id=""html_5529f59d84a7b45e97deecb5f917e309"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Church Street, Cromer</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding caused by an overloaded/surcharging drainage system, outfall blockage and surface run&ndash;off (including wash&ndash;off from the highway) led to flood water entering properties on/near the street. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Church Street was internally flooded. [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface run&ndash;off from the highway (road camber) flowed onto property accesses and contributed to flooding of properties. The local surface water drainage was partially obstructed and/or overloaded so flows could not be accommodated and flood water entered properties (through low thresholds at entrances). [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred at Church Street on 08/08/2017 and affected properties on that street. (p12)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,"Church Street, Cromer",England,"Flooding caused by an overloaded/surcharging drainage system, outfall blockage and surface run–off (including wash–off from the highway) led to flood water entering properties on/near the street. (p3)
One property on Church Street was internally flooded. [TAB] (p8)
Surface run–off from the highway (road camber) flowed onto property accesses and contributed to flooding of properties. The local surface water drainage was partially obstructed and/or overloaded so flows could not be accommodated and flood water entered properties (through low thresholds at entrances). [TAB] (p11)
Flooding occurred at Church Street on 08/08/2017 and affected properties on that street. (p12)","5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)
1 property [TAB] (p8)",GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.9289607,1.3062974,"<div id=""html_b9f355675c47f5d15e2221ba3e27bb9e"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Overstrand Road, Cromer</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Drainage system overload, surcharging and blockages combined with surface run&ndash;off from the road (including vehicle wash&ndash;off) led to flood water reaching and entering properties adjacent to the road. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property on Overstrand Road was internally flooded; residents were visited afterwards by agencies to offer advice and gather information. [TAB] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Run&ndash;off from significant rainfall was directed towards the surface water drainage network which was already overloaded; the drainage system was also partially obstructed by debris/silt, reducing its efficiency and directing flood water towards affected properties. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding occurred on Overstrand Road on 08/08/2017 and affected the road and nearby properties. (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The surface water drainage system was partially obstructed by debris or silt, causing the ditch/watercourse to overflow and flood into nearby properties. [IMG] (p13)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p8)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,"Overstrand Road, Cromer",England,"Drainage system overload, surcharging and blockages combined with surface run–off from the road (including vehicle wash–off) led to flood water reaching and entering properties adjacent to the road. (p3)
One property on Overstrand Road was internally flooded; residents were visited afterwards by agencies to offer advice and gather information. [TAB] (p8)
Run–off from significant rainfall was directed towards the surface water drainage network which was already overloaded; the drainage system was also partially obstructed by debris/silt, reducing its efficiency and directing flood water towards affected properties. [TAB] (p11)
Flooding occurred on Overstrand Road on 08/08/2017 and affected the road and nearby properties. (p12)
The surface water drainage system was partially obstructed by debris or silt, causing the ditch/watercourse to overflow and flood into nearby properties. [IMG] (p13)","5 properties (internal flooding across the listed locations) (p3)
1 property [TAB] (p8)",GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.835652,1.1276598,"<div id=""html_8c7843dbb085afa1363f86bd23aa1722"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>North Norfolk</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The properties affected by the reported incidents were concentrated within the North Norfolk catchment; flooding across these catchments was caused by obstructed or overloaded surface water drainage and increased surface run&ndash;off which resulted in internal and external property flooding. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>North Norfolk District Council was recommended to assess the use of permissive powers under the Land Drainage Act 1991 to maintain a watercourse in relation to Cromwell Road following the flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 properties affected; 5 internal; 12 external (p3)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,North Norfolk,England,"The properties affected by the reported incidents were concentrated within the North Norfolk catchment; flooding across these catchments was caused by obstructed or overloaded surface water drainage and increased surface run–off which resulted in internal and external property flooding. (p3)
North Norfolk District Council was recommended to assess the use of permissive powers under the Land Drainage Act 1991 to maintain a watercourse in relation to Cromwell Road following the flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p5)",17 properties affected; 5 internal; 12 external (p3),GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.666667,1.0,"<div id=""html_ffb7f8bec2ab54f71f536756711a106f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Norfolk</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Norfolk County Council, acting as the Lead Local Flood Authority, is investigating the significant flooding incident under Section 19 of the Flood and Water Management Act 2010. [IMG,PAG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties and impacts to people, property and infrastructure within the catchment. The investigation records 1 incident of internal flooding in the catchment and recommends RMAs to review assets and communicate with affected residents. [PAG] (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 incident (100%) of internal flooding in this catchment [PAG] (p20)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,Norfolk,England,"Norfolk County Council, acting as the Lead Local Flood Authority, is investigating the significant flooding incident under Section 19 of the Flood and Water Management Act 2010. [IMG,PAG] (p5)
Internal flooding of properties and impacts to people, property and infrastructure within the catchment. The investigation records 1 incident of internal flooding in the catchment and recommends RMAs to review assets and communicate with affected residents. [PAG] (p20)",1 incident (100%) of internal flooding in this catchment [PAG] (p20),GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-NFK,1,2016-01-01,52.925941949999995,1.3023365942523295,"<div id=""html_356a687fdd252d25e6ccc0cde6dc8486"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cromer</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Urban surface water flooding and associated contamination in parts of the town; prompted emergency response and mitigation activities documented in images. (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Between 1 January 2016 and 31 December 2017 there were 17 reported flooding incidents for Cromer which led to internal flooding of 5 properties (plus 2 further unconfirmed internal reports). Flooding causes reported included overloaded or surcharging drainage systems, drainage/outfall blockages, surface water washed off public highways by vehicles, and surface run&ndash;off from roads; flood water entered properties via unprotected building features (low thresholds, unprotected air bricks and service conduits). (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding affected the town, impacting people, property and infrastructure and triggering a Section 19 investigation. [IMG] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Multiple residential properties and multiple commercial properties in Cromer were internally flooded. [PAG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood reports (both external and internal) were clustered mainly in the central and northern parts of Cromer during the event, particularly along the central flow line. Reported incidents were concentrated near key infrastructure (hospital and schools) and in more densely populated urban areas. [IMG,PAG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal flooding of properties within Cromer (Cromwell Road, Church Street, Overstrand Road) during the reported incidents. [TAB,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Cromer experienced surface water/urban flooding: properties on Church Street and Overstrand Road were flooded on 08/08/2017 due to highway run&ndash;off, obstructed/overloaded surface water drains and low thresholds allowing entry; a property on Cromwell Road was flooded on 23/06/2016 when heavy rainfall caused the local ditch/watercourse to surcharge. [TAB,MUL] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Cromer were affected by flooding during events on 23/06/2016 and 08/08/2017, including impacts on streets listed (Church Street, Overstrand Road and Cromwell Road). [MUL] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flood reports aggregate around the town centre and more built&ndash;up areas of Cromer; watercourses and low&ndash;lying zones near the town are shown in 1&ndash;in&ndash;100 flood risk areas. Main roads and bridges through the town intersect flood&ndash;prone areas, indicating local infrastructure vulnerability. [IMG,PAG] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>An internal flood affected a property in Cromer (on Brownshill). [TAB] (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Brownshill area of Cromer experienced flooding on 23/06/2016 when significant rainfall overwhelmed the combined drainage network and caused foul&ndash;system surcharge, resulting in internal property flooding (water entering through toilets and sinks). [TAB] (p17)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>17 reports; 5 internal properties; 2 unconfirmed internal instances (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties [TAB,MUL] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>1 property [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Norfolk,Cromer,England,"Urban surface water flooding and associated contamination in parts of the town; prompted emergency response and mitigation activities documented in images. (p1)
Between 1 January 2016 and 31 December 2017 there were 17 reported flooding incidents for Cromer which led to internal flooding of 5 properties (plus 2 further unconfirmed internal reports). Flooding causes reported included overloaded or surcharging drainage systems, drainage/outfall blockages, surface water washed off public highways by vehicles, and surface run–off from roads; flood water entered properties via unprotected building features (low thresholds, unprotected air bricks and service conduits). (p3)
Significant flooding affected the town, impacting people, property and infrastructure and triggering a Section 19 investigation. [IMG] (p5)
Multiple residential properties and multiple commercial properties in Cromer were internally flooded. [PAG] (p6)
Flood reports (both external and internal) were clustered mainly in the central and northern parts of Cromer during the event, particularly along the central flow line. Reported incidents were concentrated near key infrastructure (hospital and schools) and in more densely populated urban areas. [IMG,PAG] (p7)
Internal flooding of properties within Cromer (Cromwell Road, Church Street, Overstrand Road) during the reported incidents. [TAB,MUL] (p8)
Parts of Cromer experienced surface water/urban flooding: properties on Church Street and Overstrand Road were flooded on 08/08/2017 due to highway run–off, obstructed/overloaded surface water drains and low thresholds allowing entry; a property on Cromwell Road was flooded on 23/06/2016 when heavy rainfall caused the local ditch/watercourse to surcharge. [TAB,MUL] (p11)
Parts of Cromer were affected by flooding during events on 23/06/2016 and 08/08/2017, including impacts on streets listed (Church Street, Overstrand Road and Cromwell Road). [MUL] (p12)
Flood reports aggregate around the town centre and more built–up areas of Cromer; watercourses and low–lying zones near the town are shown in 1–in–100 flood risk areas. Main roads and bridges through the town intersect flood–prone areas, indicating local infrastructure vulnerability. [IMG,PAG] (p14)
An internal flood affected a property in Cromer (on Brownshill). [TAB] (p15)
The Brownshill area of Cromer experienced flooding on 23/06/2016 when significant rainfall overwhelmed the combined drainage network and caused foul–system surcharge, resulting in internal property flooding (water entering through toilets and sinks). [TAB] (p17)","17 reports; 5 internal properties; 2 unconfirmed internal instances (p3)
3 properties [TAB,MUL] (p8)
1 property [TAB] (p15)",GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01.html,GB-NFK_1_2016-01-01,Cromer flooding 01 Jan 2016 - 31 Dec 2017,Investigation Report Into the Flooding In Cromer In 2016-2017,084/Norfolk/084/2016/Norfolk/Cromer/16.pdf
GB-LAN,27,2015-12-01,53.8042646,-2.3174942,"<div id=""html_6d5bb551fec810aee3d822917eeff678"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>St Leonard's Primary School</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Hosted a drop&ndash;in event on 03 Feb 2016 to provide advice and support to residents and businesses affected by the December 2015 floods. (p9)</p></div></div></body></div>",Lancashire,St Leonard's Primary School,England,Hosted a drop–in event on 03 Feb 2016 to provide advice and support to residents and businesses affected by the December 2015 floods. (p9),,GB-LAN_27_2015-12-01.html,GB-LAN_27_2015-12-01,South Ribble flooding 01 Dec 2015 - 31 Dec 2015,South Ribble District Flood Report: Recommended Actions,069/Lancashire County/south-ribble-final-report.pdf
