During the intense storm on 20 July 2021 surface water flowed into Finchams Close producing surface water flooding. Misconnections or ingress to the foul/sewer network and riparian maintenance issues were noted as contributing factors to the flooding here. [TAB] (p20)",15–45cm (depth in Lonsdale); 15–20cm (depth in Bakers Lane and Bartlow Road); residents displaced for 1–8 months (p12),GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20.html,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20,Linton flooding 20 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Linton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2021/Cambridgeshire County/Linton-July-2021-Flood-Investigation-v1.2.pdf
GB-CAM,29,2021-07-20,52.0966141,0.2901661,"<div id=""html_736d60e91b162b31154a5e1d583944ef"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Bakers Lane, Linton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Reported internal property flooding as part of the south&ndash;east residential cluster (grouped with Lonsdale, Bartlow Road and Finchams Close); flooding was rapid ('flash'). [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Floodwater that overtopped the retaining wall flowed into properties on Bakers Lane, causing internal and external flooding to properties and gardens. [TAB] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from a construction site flowed across the site and surrounding area, creating a flood risk; developer put in measures (tankers on standby, rearranging stored materials) to reduce immediate flooding risk to the basin. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>During the 20 July 2021 storm surface water flowed along Bakers Lane producing surface water flooding. Misconnections or ingress of rainwater into the foul/sewer network and riparian maintenance issues were identified as contributing factors. [TAB] (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15&ndash;20cm (reported depth in Bakers Lane) [TAB] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Bakers Lane, Linton",England,"Reported internal property flooding as part of the south–east residential cluster (grouped with Lonsdale, Bartlow Road and Finchams Close); flooding was rapid ('flash'). [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Floodwater that overtopped the retaining wall flowed into properties on Bakers Lane, causing internal and external flooding to properties and gardens. [TAB] (p12)
Surface water from a construction site flowed across the site and surrounding area, creating a flood risk; developer put in measures (tankers on standby, rearranging stored materials) to reduce immediate flooding risk to the basin. (p14)
During the 20 July 2021 storm surface water flowed along Bakers Lane producing surface water flooding. Misconnections or ingress of rainwater into the foul/sewer network and riparian maintenance issues were identified as contributing factors. [TAB] (p20)",15–20cm (reported depth in Bakers Lane) [TAB] (p12),GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20.html,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20,Linton flooding 20 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Linton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2021/Cambridgeshire County/Linton-July-2021-Flood-Investigation-v1.2.pdf
GB-CAM,29,2021-07-20,52.097124,0.2847983,"<div id=""html_5540f8b444fcc4be351bd245413a8b24"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Lamb Fair Court, Linton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>One property was flooded from a surface water manhole in its garden. (p11)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>one property (p11)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Lamb Fair Court, Linton",England,One property was flooded from a surface water manhole in its garden. (p11),one property (p11),GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20.html,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20,Linton flooding 20 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Linton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2021/Cambridgeshire County/Linton-July-2021-Flood-Investigation-v1.2.pdf
GB-CAM,29,2021-07-20,52.097722,0.2989635,"<div id=""html_428f9b2949686a7f969a4fd7c1df1d4d"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Horseheath Road, Linton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Large volumes of surface water from surrounding arable fields and carriageways flowed onto and along Horseheath Road. Highway drainage was minimal and inundated, causing water to enter a construction site through the temporary site entrance and main entrance and to continue westerly along the road into Lonsdale. [PAG] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Large volumes of surface water flowed westerly down Horseheath Road due to a large upstream surface water catchment, absence of crops and minimal highway drainage. Surface water entered the construction site south of Horseheath Road via the temporary entrance, and other flows continued along the road into the development site and further into Lonsdale. [PAG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water runoff from the fields and highway above these roads contributed to flooding; the LLFA is working with Highways and landowners to investigate measures to reduce runoff and whether additional attenuation capacity can be introduced. Highway drainage (gullies) were inspected and works orders raised for cleaning. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water from the 20 July 2021 event flooded the highway; highway gullies were blocked/required cleaning and highway drainage on Horseheath Road was reviewed. The road was added to a grip&ndash;cutting programme to improve surface water removal. [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Horseheath Road, Linton",England,"Large volumes of surface water from surrounding arable fields and carriageways flowed onto and along Horseheath Road. Highway drainage was minimal and inundated, causing water to enter a construction site through the temporary site entrance and main entrance and to continue westerly along the road into Lonsdale. [PAG] (p11)
Large volumes of surface water flowed westerly down Horseheath Road due to a large upstream surface water catchment, absence of crops and minimal highway drainage. Surface water entered the construction site south of Horseheath Road via the temporary entrance, and other flows continued along the road into the development site and further into Lonsdale. [PAG] (p12)
Surface water runoff from the fields and highway above these roads contributed to flooding; the LLFA is working with Highways and landowners to investigate measures to reduce runoff and whether additional attenuation capacity can be introduced. Highway drainage (gullies) were inspected and works orders raised for cleaning. (p13)
Surface water from the 20 July 2021 event flooded the highway; highway gullies were blocked/required cleaning and highway drainage on Horseheath Road was reviewed. The road was added to a grip–cutting programme to improve surface water removal. [TAB] (p15)",,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20.html,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20,Linton flooding 20 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Linton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2021/Cambridgeshire County/Linton-July-2021-Flood-Investigation-v1.2.pdf
GB-CAM,29,2021-07-20,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_8bbf1cf3096b30b8f7802dcbc123e156"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas within the county experienced surface water flooding that prompted the LLFA to undertake walkovers and inspections. In response, works orders were raised to clean gullies in affected areas, a new recording system was introduced to monitor all drainage works, and a cyclic gully&ndash;cleaning programme (every other year) was established. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>County&ndash;wide response included increasing the frequency of gully maintenance to every second year and undertaking highway drainage investigations to reduce surface water runoff after flooding; these policy/maintenance changes were made following the flooding. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Following flooding in the area, gully cleaning was increased and the frequency of gully maintenance for gullies across Cambridgeshire was changed (actioned from July 2021); highway gullies were cleaned in areas where flooding was experienced to improve surface water removal. [TAB] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"Areas within the county experienced surface water flooding that prompted the LLFA to undertake walkovers and inspections. In response, works orders were raised to clean gullies in affected areas, a new recording system was introduced to monitor all drainage works, and a cyclic gully–cleaning programme (every other year) was established. (p13)
County–wide response included increasing the frequency of gully maintenance to every second year and undertaking highway drainage investigations to reduce surface water runoff after flooding; these policy/maintenance changes were made following the flooding. [TAB] (p14)
Following flooding in the area, gully cleaning was increased and the frequency of gully maintenance for gullies across Cambridgeshire was changed (actioned from July 2021); highway gullies were cleaned in areas where flooding was experienced to improve surface water removal. [TAB] (p15)",,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20.html,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20,Linton flooding 20 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Linton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2021/Cambridgeshire County/Linton-July-2021-Flood-Investigation-v1.2.pdf
GB-CAM,29,2021-07-20,52.097523,0.2893014,"<div id=""html_05b5368f4e06e5c74fe02d53c9b88f74"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Linton</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Linton has a floodplain associated with the River Granta and large areas are at significant risk of surface water flooding. The 20 July 2021 flooding occurred largely in areas already identified as at risk; some properties around the village centre are located within a flood zone. The village has also experienced previous flooding events in 2014, 2017 and 2019. [MUL] (p5)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20 July 2021 the village experienced flooding largely from surface water; inundation occurred in parts of the village already identified as at risk from surface water, including areas around the village centre and properties located within mapped flood zones. [PAG,IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant surface&ndash;water flooding occurred across parts of the village on 20 July 2021 after an intense rainfall event; flooding affected large areas already identified as at risk from surface water, with inundation extending into built&ndash;up parts of Linton. [PAG,IMG] (p7)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Significant flooding occurred on 20 July 2021 from intense, convective rainfall. The event produced localised, intense surface water/flash flooding that resulted in internal and external flooding of properties; local flood reports were submitted to the Lead Local Flood Authority (LLFA) and Linton Parish Council documenting affected properties. [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20 July 2021 the LLFA received 23 flood reports from Linton. Properties experienced rapid ('flash') water entry with water levels receding relatively quickly. Overall 17 properties were reported to have flooded internally and a further 6 properties experienced external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. At least 5 properties were likely to be uninhabitable for 1&ndash;12 months. Three localised risk areas were identified (High Street; Balsham Road/Lamb Fair Court; Lonsdale/Bakers Lane/Bartlow Road/Finchams Close). (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Village&ndash;wide reports of property flooding: internal and external flooding of residential properties and gardens/outbuildings; multiple rapid&ndash;onset (flash) surface&ndash;water flood incidents with water entering properties and receding relatively quickly. At least some properties rendered uninhabitable. [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water flooding affected the town, with reports that residents had to leave their properties for periods of between 1 and 8 months. The LLFA and the highway authority attended to investigate reports, inspected highway drainage, and raised works orders to clean gullies. Further work was initiated with Highways and landowners to reduce surface water runoff from fields and highway and to investigate introducing additional attenuation capacity. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>On 20 July 2021 an intense convective storm produced surface water flooding across Linton. Rainfall exceeded drainage capacity so surface water flowed along roads and across fields toward the village low points; drainage networks were overwhelmed. A partially constructed development (stripped of vegetation and without a completed surface water network) increased runoff and contributed to the flooding. (p20)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 flood reports [PAG] (p8)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 flood reports; 17 properties flooded internally; 6 properties with external flooding; at least 5 properties uninhabitable (p9)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>23 flood reports; 17 properties flooded internally; 6 properties with external flooding; at least 5 properties likely uninhabitable for 1&ndash;12 months [PAG,IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Linton,England,"Linton has a floodplain associated with the River Granta and large areas are at significant risk of surface water flooding. The 20 July 2021 flooding occurred largely in areas already identified as at risk; some properties around the village centre are located within a flood zone. The village has also experienced previous flooding events in 2014, 2017 and 2019. [MUL] (p5)
On 20 July 2021 the village experienced flooding largely from surface water; inundation occurred in parts of the village already identified as at risk from surface water, including areas around the village centre and properties located within mapped flood zones. [PAG,IMG] (p6)
Significant surface–water flooding occurred across parts of the village on 20 July 2021 after an intense rainfall event; flooding affected large areas already identified as at risk from surface water, with inundation extending into built–up parts of Linton. [PAG,IMG] (p7)
Significant flooding occurred on 20 July 2021 from intense, convective rainfall. The event produced localised, intense surface water/flash flooding that resulted in internal and external flooding of properties; local flood reports were submitted to the Lead Local Flood Authority (LLFA) and Linton Parish Council documenting affected properties. [PAG] (p8)
On 20 July 2021 the LLFA received 23 flood reports from Linton. Properties experienced rapid ('flash') water entry with water levels receding relatively quickly. Overall 17 properties were reported to have flooded internally and a further 6 properties experienced external flooding of gardens, outbuildings and roads. At least 5 properties were likely to be uninhabitable for 1–12 months. Three localised risk areas were identified (High Street; Balsham Road/Lamb Fair Court; Lonsdale/Bakers Lane/Bartlow Road/Finchams Close). (p9)
Village–wide reports of property flooding: internal and external flooding of residential properties and gardens/outbuildings; multiple rapid–onset (flash) surface–water flood incidents with water entering properties and receding relatively quickly. At least some properties rendered uninhabitable. [PAG,IMG] (p10)
Surface water flooding affected the town, with reports that residents had to leave their properties for periods of between 1 and 8 months. The LLFA and the highway authority attended to investigate reports, inspected highway drainage, and raised works orders to clean gullies. Further work was initiated with Highways and landowners to reduce surface water runoff from fields and highway and to investigate introducing additional attenuation capacity. (p13)
On 20 July 2021 an intense convective storm produced surface water flooding across Linton. Rainfall exceeded drainage capacity so surface water flowed along roads and across fields toward the village low points; drainage networks were overwhelmed. A partially constructed development (stripped of vegetation and without a completed surface water network) increased runoff and contributed to the flooding. (p20)","23 flood reports [PAG] (p8)
23 flood reports; 17 properties flooded internally; 6 properties with external flooding; at least 5 properties uninhabitable (p9)
23 flood reports; 17 properties flooded internally; 6 properties with external flooding; at least 5 properties likely uninhabitable for 1–12 months [PAG,IMG] (p10)",GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20.html,GB-CAM_29_2021-07-20,Linton flooding 20 Jul 2021,Flood Investigation Report: Linton,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2021/Cambridgeshire County/Linton-July-2021-Flood-Investigation-v1.2.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2237528,-0.2899064,"<div id=""html_f2502b91e6da9734c34e9497bc59a013"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Duloe Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flows in Duloe Brook exceeded channel capacity and caused flooding of adjacent properties (notably upstream at Squires Court) and contributed to surcharging of local drainage where it meets the River Great Ouse. [PAG] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Duloe Brook,England,Flows in Duloe Brook exceeded channel capacity and caused flooding of adjacent properties (notably upstream at Squires Court) and contributed to surcharging of local drainage where it meets the River Great Ouse. [PAG] (p16),,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2267541,-0.2692814,"<div id=""html_cd0b1d0287be3e52339985fb709e3d8f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Hen Brook</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial flows from Wintringham Brook and Hen Brook contributed to river flooding into St Neots, inundating gardens and outbuildings. Surveyed impacts include garden/outbuilding inundation associated with Hen Brook. (p18)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The open section of Hen Brook at Henbrook Park was subject to riparian maintenance (documented May 2021) as part of actions arising from the flood investigation into the December 2020 event; the brook is documented within the area of concern and maintained to reduce future flood risk to nearby residences. [IMG] (p23)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>60 cm (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Hen Brook,England,"Fluvial flows from Wintringham Brook and Hen Brook contributed to river flooding into St Neots, inundating gardens and outbuildings. Surveyed impacts include garden/outbuilding inundation associated with Hen Brook. (p18)
The open section of Hen Brook at Henbrook Park was subject to riparian maintenance (documented May 2021) as part of actions arising from the flood investigation into the December 2020 event; the brook is documented within the area of concern and maintained to reduce future flood risk to nearby residences. [IMG] (p23)",60 cm (p18),GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.1278302,-0.4250206,"<div id=""html_8438dc394f75caa2ae9640d5fd4110f7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>River Great Ouse</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The river/catchment is identified as the geographic source being managed; strategic projects within the River Great Ouse catchment are being considered to reduce flood risk to St Neots. (p24)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,River Great Ouse,England,The river/catchment is identified as the geographic source being managed; strategic projects within the River Great Ouse catchment are being considered to reduce flood risk to St Neots. (p24),,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2262764430708,-0.2747681620114428,"<div id=""html_d2c5170ad6cd119828167c5417ab6323"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Riverside Carpark</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Areas on the river side of the defences at the Riverside Carpark experienced flooding when river flows exceeded channel capacity; the carpark is noted as functioning as a flood storage basin on the river side of the defences. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The carpark was affected by river flooding, triggering review and update of procedures for closing its flood gate (actions undertaken by the Environment Agency and Huntingdonshire District Council). [TAB] (p19)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Riverside Carpark,England,"Areas on the river side of the defences at the Riverside Carpark experienced flooding when river flows exceeded channel capacity; the carpark is noted as functioning as a flood storage basin on the river side of the defences. (p13)
The carpark was affected by river flooding, triggering review and update of procedures for closing its flood gate (actions undertaken by the Environment Agency and Huntingdonshire District Council). [TAB] (p19)",,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2267582,-0.2683418,"<div id=""html_6a88106ba1b3b55a22bd7dedc69f8239"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Brook Street, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Brook Street area reported external flooding to gardens/outbuildings with eight external flood reports recorded during the event (no internal property flooding recorded in the table). [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Community flood reports indicate external flooding affecting properties in the Brook Street area during the December 2020 event. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding extended to Brook Street but anecdotal evidence indicates depths did not prevent access/egress and properties were not flooded internally in this event. (p14)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding investigated; foul sewer surcharge into toilets and baths reported and inspected. [TAB,PAG] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Investigations were carried out into internal flooding in the Brook Street area (site visits to inspect internal flooding and speak to residents). Responses and follow&ndash;up actions were recorded to address flooding impacts in this locality. (p20)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected the Brook Street area during 23 Dec 2020 &ndash; 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues and RMA operational issues as contributors in this area. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>8 properties experienced external flooding [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Brook Street, St Neots",England,"The Brook Street area reported external flooding to gardens/outbuildings with eight external flood reports recorded during the event (no internal property flooding recorded in the table). [TAB] (p11)
Community flood reports indicate external flooding affecting properties in the Brook Street area during the December 2020 event. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Flooding extended to Brook Street but anecdotal evidence indicates depths did not prevent access/egress and properties were not flooded internally in this event. (p14)
Internal property flooding investigated; foul sewer surcharge into toilets and baths reported and inspected. [TAB,PAG] (p19)
Investigations were carried out into internal flooding in the Brook Street area (site visits to inspect internal flooding and speak to residents). Responses and follow–up actions were recorded to address flooding impacts in this locality. (p20)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected the Brook Street area during 23 Dec 2020 – 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues and RMA operational issues as contributors in this area. [TAB] (p28)","8 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)
8 properties experienced external flooding [PAG,IMG] (p12)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2280217,-0.2635661,"<div id=""html_220a4f5016b4a93d5e756d02f1272db2"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridge Street, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area C (Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area) experienced both internal and external flooding: four internal property flood reports and nine external flood reports to gardens/outbuildings were recorded for the event. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected properties in the Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area during the December 2020 event, with both internal and external property impacts reported. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties on Manor Farm Road, Victoria Court (Cambridge Street) and adjacent Cambridge Street reported garden/outbuildings flooding with reported depths of 30 cm (Manor Farm Road), ~15 cm (Victoria Court) and 20 cm (Cambridge Street); no evacuations reported for these specific reports. (p15)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area experienced internal flooding; reports included foul sewer surcharge into toilets and baths. RMAs attended site to inspect features and speak to residents. [IMG,PAG] (p21)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Piped section of Fox Brook runs behind properties on Cambridge Street and Shaftesbury Avenue and was investigated for collapse or blockage under the Land Drainage Act (1991). LLFA attended to investigate internal flooding at nearby properties. Routine and emergency maintenance/clearance of the trash screen on Fox Brook at the junction of Kings Lane and Cambridge Street was undertaken. Anglian Water was tasked to investigate the resilience of their structures in the area because high water levels in the main rivers were reducing discharge efficiency, potentially causing backups into the piped watercourse and contributing to internal/property flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected the Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area during 23 Dec 2020 &ndash; 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues, RMA operational issues and riparian maintenance issues as contributors to the flooding. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 9 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties flooded internally; 9 properties experienced external flooding [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>30 cm; ~15 cm; 20 cm; durations 2 days, 2 days, 1 day (Manor Farm Road, Victoria Court and Cambridge Street) (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Cambridge Street, St Neots",England,"Area C (Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area) experienced both internal and external flooding: four internal property flood reports and nine external flood reports to gardens/outbuildings were recorded for the event. [TAB] (p11)
Flooding affected properties in the Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area during the December 2020 event, with both internal and external property impacts reported. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Properties on Manor Farm Road, Victoria Court (Cambridge Street) and adjacent Cambridge Street reported garden/outbuildings flooding with reported depths of 30 cm (Manor Farm Road), ~15 cm (Victoria Court) and 20 cm (Cambridge Street); no evacuations reported for these specific reports. (p15)
Properties in the Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area experienced internal flooding; reports included foul sewer surcharge into toilets and baths. RMAs attended site to inspect features and speak to residents. [IMG,PAG] (p21)
Piped section of Fox Brook runs behind properties on Cambridge Street and Shaftesbury Avenue and was investigated for collapse or blockage under the Land Drainage Act (1991). LLFA attended to investigate internal flooding at nearby properties. Routine and emergency maintenance/clearance of the trash screen on Fox Brook at the junction of Kings Lane and Cambridge Street was undertaken. Anglian Water was tasked to investigate the resilience of their structures in the area because high water levels in the main rivers were reducing discharge efficiency, potentially causing backups into the piped watercourse and contributing to internal/property flooding. [IMG,PAG] (p22)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected the Cambridge Street / Cambridge Gardens area during 23 Dec 2020 – 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues, RMA operational issues and riparian maintenance issues as contributors to the flooding. [TAB] (p28)","4 properties; 9 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)
4 properties flooded internally; 9 properties experienced external flooding [PAG,IMG] (p12)
30 cm; ~15 cm; 20 cm; durations 2 days, 2 days, 1 day (Manor Farm Road, Victoria Court and Cambridge Street) (p15)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2285533,-0.2614565,"<div id=""html_65119243e402bbfb64f2c1dd8bf91c83"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridge Gardens, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Cambridge Gardens is part of the Area C Cambridge Street/Cambridge Gardens cluster that recorded flooding during the event: combined counts for the area show four internal property flood reports and nine external flood reports (gardens/outbuildings). [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Gardens and outbuildings in Cambridge Gardens were inundated during the December 2020 Fox Brook event, with reported external depths around ~15cm and reports ranging 10&ndash;40cm at some properties; no records of evacuations for these entries. [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 9 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties; depths ~15cm and 10&ndash;40cm; durations 1&ndash;2 days [TAB,PAG] (p15)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Cambridge Gardens, St Neots",England,"Cambridge Gardens is part of the Area C Cambridge Street/Cambridge Gardens cluster that recorded flooding during the event: combined counts for the area show four internal property flood reports and nine external flood reports (gardens/outbuildings). [TAB] (p11)
Gardens and outbuildings in Cambridge Gardens were inundated during the December 2020 Fox Brook event, with reported external depths around ~15cm and reports ranging 10–40cm at some properties; no records of evacuations for these entries. [TAB,PAG] (p15)","4 properties; 9 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)
3 properties; depths ~15cm and 10–40cm; durations 1–2 days [TAB,PAG] (p15)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2267325,-0.2757977,"<div id=""html_83c637940fe81841acc6e6108c98fa6b"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>The Paddock, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Surface water drains surcharged within the road, causing surface water flooding in December 2020; flooding within the road reached approximately 15cm depth. A local pumping station ran at full capacity on 24&ndash;25 Dec to reduce sustained high surface water levels. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal garden and outbuildings were flooded; residents did not have to leave their premises. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15cm (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 days; 3 days; 15cm [TAB] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"The Paddock, St Neots",England,"Surface water drains surcharged within the road, causing surface water flooding in December 2020; flooding within the road reached approximately 15cm depth. A local pumping station ran at full capacity on 24–25 Dec to reduce sustained high surface water levels. (p13)
Internal garden and outbuildings were flooded; residents did not have to leave their premises. [TAB] (p14)","15cm (p13)
4 days; 3 days; 15cm [TAB] (p14)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2359469,-0.2820342,"<div id=""html_8dff3a9c006f00a0d7612dc358720813"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Crosshall Road, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties in the Crosshall Road area were flooded. On 23 Dec 2020 rapid&ndash;onset surface water flowed across fields causing little lead time for response, so property&ndash;level barriers were not deployed and vehicles were not removed, resulting in property damage. On 24 Dec 2020 the River Great Ouse exceeded channel capacity causing fluvial flooding on the wet side of the defences; fluvial depths reported at significant depth. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal garden and outbuildings flooded; occupants reported having to leave their premises during the flooding. [TAB] (p14)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>50&ndash;90cm (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>5 days; 2 days; 50&ndash;60cm; 90cm [TAB] (p14)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Crosshall Road, St Neots",England,"Properties in the Crosshall Road area were flooded. On 23 Dec 2020 rapid–onset surface water flowed across fields causing little lead time for response, so property–level barriers were not deployed and vehicles were not removed, resulting in property damage. On 24 Dec 2020 the River Great Ouse exceeded channel capacity causing fluvial flooding on the wet side of the defences; fluvial depths reported at significant depth. (p13)
Internal garden and outbuildings flooded; occupants reported having to leave their premises during the flooding. [TAB] (p14)","50–90cm (p13)
5 days; 2 days; 50–60cm; 90cm [TAB] (p14)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
