GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2228621,-0.2884655,"<div id=""html_03c65d7f7644335591a177049ba4867f"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Squires Court, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties reported flooding when flows in Duloe Brook exceeded channel capacity (throttle under the Great North Road increased upstream risk). Internal gardens/outbuildings were flooded; one household reported displacement for greater than one month. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>four reports of internal garden/outbuildings flooding; reported depths: 10 cm, 6 cm, 10 cm, ~15 cm; one household displaced for &gt;1 month [TAB] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Squires Court, St Neots",England,Properties reported flooding when flows in Duloe Brook exceeded channel capacity (throttle under the Great North Road increased upstream risk). Internal gardens/outbuildings were flooded; one household reported displacement for greater than one month. [TAB] (p16),"four reports of internal garden/outbuildings flooding; reported depths: 10 cm, 6 cm, 10 cm, ~15 cm; one household displaced for >1 month [TAB] (p16)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2214547,-0.2864776,"<div id=""html_3ea6cdd5aad19c19c5295a42d6c944ed"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Nene Road, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected gardens/outbuildings and car park drainage surcharged when river levels were high, indicating surface water drainage could not discharge into the brook/river at the confluence. [TAB] (p16)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>2 reports of garden/outbuildings flooded [TAB] (p16)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Nene Road, St Neots",England,"Flooding affected gardens/outbuildings and car park drainage surcharged when river levels were high, indicating surface water drainage could not discharge into the brook/river at the confluence. [TAB] (p16)",2 reports of garden/outbuildings flooded [TAB] (p16),GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2295014,-0.2588795,"<div id=""html_3ccb2d32123e44849d498a323e6bdd5a"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Kings Lane, St Neots</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The junction location was the site of a trash screen that required clearance and ongoing maintenance to maintain flow and reduce local flooding; identified as a focal point for emergency clearance works. [IMG] (p21)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,"Kings Lane, St Neots",England,The junction location was the site of a trash screen that required clearance and ongoing maintenance to maintain flow and reduce local flooding; identified as a focal point for emergency clearance works. [IMG] (p21),,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.3729017,-0.04493599422000649,"<div id=""html_eb78193a1a0d750f04328706b75bd790"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Cambridgeshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Parts of Cambridgeshire (notably the St Neots area) experienced fluvial and surface water flooding during the winter 2020&ndash;21 event; risk maps show high surface&ndash;water and river&ndash;flood risk in and around St Neots. [PAG,IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding began in St Neots on 23 December 2020; a major incident was declared that day and tactical response was coordinated by Cambridgeshire Police. The event lasted between 1 and 5 days, with predominantly surface water flooding on 23&ndash;24 December followed by fluvial (river) flooding on 26 December. Several River Great Ouse gauges upstream of St Neots exceeded 1998 levels and reached the highest levels on record; upstream river flow was recorded at 34.29 m3/s (about 200% of the Long Term Average). [IMG] (p10)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>34 flood reports; upstream river flow 34.29 m3/s (200% of Long Term Average) [IMG] (p10)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Cambridgeshire,England,"Parts of Cambridgeshire (notably the St Neots area) experienced fluvial and surface water flooding during the winter 2020–21 event; risk maps show high surface–water and river–flood risk in and around St Neots. [PAG,IMG] (p6)
Flooding began in St Neots on 23 December 2020; a major incident was declared that day and tactical response was coordinated by Cambridgeshire Police. The event lasted between 1 and 5 days, with predominantly surface water flooding on 23–24 December followed by fluvial (river) flooding on 26 December. Several River Great Ouse gauges upstream of St Neots exceeded 1998 levels and reached the highest levels on record; upstream river flow was recorded at 34.29 m3/s (about 200% of the Long Term Average). [IMG] (p10)",34 flood reports; upstream river flow 34.29 m3/s (200% of Long Term Average) [IMG] (p10),GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.371044,-0.2235779,"<div id=""html_f5ac71a4549e55d4803fbcd135f2cbd9"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Huntingdonshire</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Severe flooding occurred across the district in December 2020 (area&ndash;wide fluvial flooding that affected communities including St Neots). (p18)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Huntingdonshire,England,Severe flooding occurred across the district in December 2020 (area–wide fluvial flooding that affected communities including St Neots). (p18),,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.218525,-0.2902765,"<div id=""html_5d766a8d37b68ac74ec8c199c79c2023"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eaton Socon</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Eaton Socon is identified on the flood risk maps as lying in low&ndash;lying, river&ndash; and surface&ndash;water&ndash;prone areas and formed part of the St Neots flooding event that began 23 December 2020. [PAG,IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area D (Eaton Socon North) recorded both internal and external property flooding: four internal property flood reports and three external flood reports to gardens/outbuildings were recorded during the event. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Eaton Socon (North) area experienced flooding during the December 2020 incident, affecting both internal and external premises of properties. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area affected by Duloe Brook overtopping/exceeding channel capacity; properties in Squires Court and locations at the Duloe Brook&ndash;River Great Ouse confluence experienced flooding and surcharging of car&ndash;park drainage when river levels were high. (p16)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>LLFA attended to investigate internal flooding, inspected relevant features and spoke with residents and representatives; actions were taken to determine next steps for risk management authorities. Follow&ndash;up included increased gully maintenance and investigations by Anglian Water into asset resilience. [TAB] (p22)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected Eaton Socon (North) during 23 Dec 2020 &ndash; 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues and RMA operational issues as contributors in this area. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties; 3 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>4 properties flooded internally; 3 properties experienced external flooding [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Eaton Socon,England,"Eaton Socon is identified on the flood risk maps as lying in low–lying, river– and surface–water–prone areas and formed part of the St Neots flooding event that began 23 December 2020. [PAG,IMG] (p6)
Area D (Eaton Socon North) recorded both internal and external property flooding: four internal property flood reports and three external flood reports to gardens/outbuildings were recorded during the event. [TAB] (p11)
The Eaton Socon (North) area experienced flooding during the December 2020 incident, affecting both internal and external premises of properties. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Area affected by Duloe Brook overtopping/exceeding channel capacity; properties in Squires Court and locations at the Duloe Brook–River Great Ouse confluence experienced flooding and surcharging of car–park drainage when river levels were high. (p16)
LLFA attended to investigate internal flooding, inspected relevant features and spoke with residents and representatives; actions were taken to determine next steps for risk management authorities. Follow–up included increased gully maintenance and investigations by Anglian Water into asset resilience. [TAB] (p22)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected Eaton Socon (North) during 23 Dec 2020 – 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues and RMA operational issues as contributors in this area. [TAB] (p28)","4 properties; 3 external flood reports [TAB] (p11)
4 properties flooded internally; 3 properties experienced external flooding [PAG,IMG] (p12)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2177279,-0.2644645,"<div id=""html_02b0c9b3e1653808da4a1ea1dd3556e7"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eynesbury</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Part of St Neots that was affected when flooding began on 23 December 2020. Flooding was caused by intense rainfall and high River Great Ouse levels, with surface water flooding evident across low&ndash;lying areas shown on the council map. The event was declared a major incident (23 December 2020) and response was coordinated by Cambridgeshire Police. Flooding was exacerbated by prolonged high river levels that reduced discharge capacity of contributory watercourses and connected drainage. [PAG,IMG] (p6)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding affected properties in the area: internal property flooding reported and external flooding to gardens/outbuildings. Identified as one of five key risk areas in St Neots during the December 2020 event. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Property flooding reported in the Eynesbury (East) key risk area: internal flooding of residential properties and external flooding of gardens/outbuildings affecting local premises. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Internal property flooding was investigated by the LLFA; officers attended site to inspect features, speak to residents and and determine next steps for Risk Management Authorities. [IMG] (p23)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected Eynesbury (East) during 23 Dec 2020 &ndash; 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies RMA operational issues as a relevant issue in this area. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (internal); 2 properties (external) [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>3 properties (internal); 2 properties (external) [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Eynesbury,England,"Part of St Neots that was affected when flooding began on 23 December 2020. Flooding was caused by intense rainfall and high River Great Ouse levels, with surface water flooding evident across low–lying areas shown on the council map. The event was declared a major incident (23 December 2020) and response was coordinated by Cambridgeshire Police. Flooding was exacerbated by prolonged high river levels that reduced discharge capacity of contributory watercourses and connected drainage. [PAG,IMG] (p6)
Flooding affected properties in the area: internal property flooding reported and external flooding to gardens/outbuildings. Identified as one of five key risk areas in St Neots during the December 2020 event. [TAB] (p11)
Property flooding reported in the Eynesbury (East) key risk area: internal flooding of residential properties and external flooding of gardens/outbuildings affecting local premises. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
Internal property flooding was investigated by the LLFA; officers attended site to inspect features, speak to residents and and determine next steps for Risk Management Authorities. [IMG] (p23)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected Eynesbury (East) during 23 Dec 2020 – 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies RMA operational issues as a relevant issue in this area. [TAB] (p28)","3 properties (internal); 2 properties (external) [TAB] (p11)
3 properties (internal); 2 properties (external) [PAG,IMG] (p12)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-CAM,25,2020-12-23,52.2262531,-0.2787842,"<div id=""html_2653b5bd25d4d98206e6d3d581e7b3a5"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Eaton Ford</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Area A (Eaton Ford West) recorded internal flooding affecting properties; six internal property flood reports were recorded for the event. [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Flooding at The Paddocks in December 2020 was reported as surface water flooding caused by surface water drains surcharging within the road rather than fluvial inundation; nearby Riverside Carpark functions as flood storage when gates are deployed but the December 2020 event at The Paddock was due to surcharging drains. [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The Eaton Ford area experienced both rapid&ndash;onset surface water flooding and subsequent fluvial flooding on the river ('wet' side of the defences). Surface water sewer surcharging occurred in defended areas; parts of the area on the river side of the defences (including Riverside carpark and north of the B1428) were flooded. (p13)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Properties experienced internal flooding; site visits were carried out (March/May 2021) to investigate internal flooding, inspect features and speak to residents. High main&ndash;river levels reduced discharge efficiency affecting the local pumping station and outfalls, contributing to flooding. [TAB] (p19)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Fluvial (river) flooding affected Eaton Ford (East) during 23 Dec 2020 &ndash; 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues, RMA operational issues and riparian maintenance issues as contributors to the flooding impact in this area. [TAB] (p28)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties [TAB] (p11)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>6 properties flooded internally; flood water depth reported at approximately 15 cm in the road [PAG,IMG] (p12)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>15cm; 50&ndash;90cm (p13)</p></div></div></body></div>",Cambridgeshire,Eaton Ford,England,"Area A (Eaton Ford West) recorded internal flooding affecting properties; six internal property flood reports were recorded for the event. [TAB] (p11)
Flooding at The Paddocks in December 2020 was reported as surface water flooding caused by surface water drains surcharging within the road rather than fluvial inundation; nearby Riverside Carpark functions as flood storage when gates are deployed but the December 2020 event at The Paddock was due to surcharging drains. [PAG,IMG] (p12)
The Eaton Ford area experienced both rapid–onset surface water flooding and subsequent fluvial flooding on the river ('wet' side of the defences). Surface water sewer surcharging occurred in defended areas; parts of the area on the river side of the defences (including Riverside carpark and north of the B1428) were flooded. (p13)
Properties experienced internal flooding; site visits were carried out (March/May 2021) to investigate internal flooding, inspect features and speak to residents. High main–river levels reduced discharge efficiency affecting the local pumping station and outfalls, contributing to flooding. [TAB] (p19)
Fluvial (river) flooding affected Eaton Ford (East) during 23 Dec 2020 – 26 Dec 2020. Table 3 identifies capacity issues, RMA operational issues and riparian maintenance issues as contributors to the flooding impact in this area. [TAB] (p28)","6 properties [TAB] (p11)
6 properties flooded internally; flood water depth reported at approximately 15 cm in the road [PAG,IMG] (p12)
15cm; 50–90cm (p13)",GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23.html,GB-CAM_25_2020-12-23,St Neots flooding 23 Dec 2020 - 26 Dec 2020,Flood Investigation Report: St Neots,021/Cambridgeshire County/021/2020/Cambridgeshire County/St-Neots-December-2020-Flood-Investigation.pdf
GB-KEN,1,2014-05-21,51.2241407,1.3968996,"<div id=""html_ba15079049a7c12e69366a34e5aa59ee"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Albert Road, Deal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Residential properties on Albert Road were damaged by the flood event. [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Road and adjacent residential properties were flooded following heavy rainfall; internal flooding and property damage occurred and vehicles were damaged. [IMG] (p2)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Foul sewers surcharged (due to Golf Road pumping station failure) and excess foul and rainwater discharged onto Albert Road; water could not escape quickly and the surface water sewer lacked capacity, resulting in inundation of the road; road drains were subsequently cleared of foul effluent. (p3)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>Identified as a low point where overland flow from the south is trapped by the railway embankment, creating local flood risk during the flood event; properties here are at risk from surface water/overland flow (noted that properties do not have basements). (p4)</p></div><i>Quantified impacts</i><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 properties [IMG] (p1)</p></div><div class=""nugget-popup quant""><p>22 properties flooded internally; approximately 30 cm deep floodwater on the road [IMG] (p2)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Albert Road, Deal",England,"Residential properties on Albert Road were damaged by the flood event. [IMG] (p1)
Road and adjacent residential properties were flooded following heavy rainfall; internal flooding and property damage occurred and vehicles were damaged. [IMG] (p2)
Foul sewers surcharged (due to Golf Road pumping station failure) and excess foul and rainwater discharged onto Albert Road; water could not escape quickly and the surface water sewer lacked capacity, resulting in inundation of the road; road drains were subsequently cleared of foul effluent. (p3)
Identified as a low point where overland flow from the south is trapped by the railway embankment, creating local flood risk during the flood event; properties here are at risk from surface water/overland flow (noted that properties do not have basements). (p4)","22 properties [IMG] (p1)
22 properties flooded internally; approximately 30 cm deep floodwater on the road [IMG] (p2)",GB-KEN_1_2014-05-21.html,GB-KEN_1_2014-05-21,Deal flooding 21 May 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Albert Road – Deal Dover,063/Kent/Albert-Road-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf
GB-KEN,1,2014-05-21,51.2249359,1.3950971,"<div id=""html_c3237f559e9fe7b8bb58c806e1b69946"" style=""width: 100.0%; height: 100.0%;""><head><meta charset=""utf-8""><link href=""../../../../output/html/style.css"" rel=""stylesheet""/></head><body class=""readout""><div><h1 class='h-popup'>Matthews Close, Deal</h1><div class=""nugget-popup""><p>The ditch at the back of Matthews Close (an ordinary watercourse) is part of the local drainage system; surface water sewers discharge into this ditch which is then culverted to the Penfield Sewer. Improvement works on the ditch had commenced but were incomplete at the time of the flood; the works did not prevent the culvert from functioning and inspection showed the culvert was working during the flood. (p4)</p></div></div></body></div>",Kent,"Matthews Close, Deal",England,The ditch at the back of Matthews Close (an ordinary watercourse) is part of the local drainage system; surface water sewers discharge into this ditch which is then culverted to the Penfield Sewer. Improvement works on the ditch had commenced but were incomplete at the time of the flood; the works did not prevent the culvert from functioning and inspection showed the culvert was working during the flood. (p4),,GB-KEN_1_2014-05-21.html,GB-KEN_1_2014-05-21,Deal flooding 21 May 2014,Flood Investigation Report: Albert Road – Deal Dover,063/Kent/Albert-Road-section-19-flood-investigation.pdf
